window.APP_DATA = {"domains":[{"n":1,"title":"Bootstrapping and Maintaining a Google Cloud Organization","short":"Organization & IaC","weight":"~17%","desc":"Resource hierarchy, org policies, IAM and service accounts, Terraform and Kubernetes-native config management, environments.","lessons":[{"id":"d1l1","num":1,"title":"Resource hierarchy, folders & cost grouping","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Folders are the attachment point for IAM and Organization Policy inheritance and a natural boundary for billing/cost aggregation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>Google's landing-zone guidance cautions against mirroring the org chart; prefer <strong>environment-based</strong> (or sensitivity-based) folders when the pain is policy consistency per environment <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Labels</strong> help filter billing exports but are <strong>not</strong> an IAM/Org Policy attachment point <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>A Cloud Billing <strong>budget can be scoped to a folder</strong> and sums spend of every nested project <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q43\">Exam 3 · Q43</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Apply security policy consistently to all prod projects\" + \"track prod vs dev cost\" → one <strong>Production</strong> folder + one <strong>Development</strong> folder <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Visibility into total department spend, alert if department exceeds budget\" → department folder + <strong>one budget scoped to the folder</strong> at the full budget <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q43\">Exam 3 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>Only pick per-project budgets when the question asks <em>which project</em> overspent <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q43\">Exam 3 · Q43</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"20 business units\" in the stem tempts BU folders — but the stated problems are environment problems <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>Full budget on <em>each</em> project → nothing fires until one project alone eats the whole budget; budget ÷ N projects → false/missed alarms <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q43\">Exam 3 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>A \"common services project\" per BU centralizes shared infra; it is not a policy or cost boundary <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Mechanism</th>\n<th>Policy/IAM inheritance?</th>\n<th>Cost aggregation?</th>\n<th>Use for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Folder</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Yes (folder-scoped budgets/reports)</td>\n<td>Environment or department grouping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Label</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Filter in billing export only</td>\n<td>Tagging/breakdowns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Common-services project</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Shared VPC host, logging, security tooling</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","summary":"This lesson covers how folders, labels and projects group resources in a Google Cloud organization, and which of them carry IAM, Organization Policy and cost boundaries. Exam questions here test whether you can choose an environment-based folder layout and a folder-scoped budget instead of options that only look right.","objectives":["After this lesson you can explain why folders are the attachment point for IAM and Organization Policy inheritance and for cost aggregation.","After this lesson you can choose between environment-based and business-unit folder layouts based on the problem the question describes.","After this lesson you can set up a single Cloud Billing budget scoped to a folder to track total department spend.","After this lesson you can tell folders, labels and common-services projects apart by what each one actually controls."],"takeaways":["Consistent security policy plus prod vs dev cost tracking → one Production folder and one Development folder.","Labels only filter billing exports; they are not an IAM or Org Policy attachment point.","Department total spend with an alert → department folder + one budget scoped to that folder at the full amount.","Use per-project budgets only when the question asks which project overspent.","Don't let \"20 business units\" in the question pull you toward BU folders when the stated problems are environment problems."],"questions":["e3q37","e3q43"]},{"id":"d1l2","num":2,"title":"Organization policies","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Org policies inherit org → folder → project; a lower level can override the parent <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li><code>iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation</code> blocks creating user-managed SA keys; <code>iam.disableServiceAccountKeyUpload</code> blocks uploading an externally generated public key — a different action <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a>. A newer managed variant <code>iam.managed.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation</code> exists <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>There is no downloadable \"default key\": Google-managed keys can't be downloaded; only user-managed keys can be created/exported <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q47\">Exam 5 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li><code>gcp.resourceLocations</code> restricts where resources are created; Secret Manager <strong>automatic replication requires <code>global</code></strong>, so under a US/EU-only policy use <strong>user-managed replication</strong> with every replica in an allowed region <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q9\">Exam 3 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li><code>compute.restrictXpnProjectLienRemoval</code> makes lien removal on a Shared VPC host project require <code>resourcemanager.projects.updateLiens</code> <strong>at the organization level</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q7\">Exam 4 · Q7</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Eliminate JSON key risk org-wide with minimal overhead → enforce <code>constraints/iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation</code> at the <strong>org</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>A key is unavoidable for a 3rd-party app → override the constraint <strong>on that project only</strong>, then create the key <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q47\">Exam 5 · Q47</a>. Even narrower: tag-based exemption of specific service accounts <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Regulatory \"resources only in region X\" across the org → org policy with allowed regions in an <code>in: allowed_values</code> list <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q63\">Exam 5 · Q63</a>.</li>\n<li>Org policy blocks a create → first change the <em>resource configuration</em> to comply, don't relax the policy <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q9\">Exam 3 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>Teams self-manage project IAM, need lien removal restricted to org level → <code>compute.restrictXpnProjectLienRemoval</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q7\">Exam 4 · Q7</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Removing the policy at org/folder \"fixes the error\" but drops the guardrail for everyone <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Custom roles stripping <code>iam.serviceAccountKeys.*</code> or restricting <code>serviceAccountKeyAdmin</code> to admins only <em>reduce/concentrate</em> risk — they don't eliminate it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>Detect-and-delete approaches (log-based alerts, hourly Asset Inventory scans) are reactive, not preventive <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q63\">Exam 5 · Q63</a>.</li>\n<li>Revoking <code>updateLiens</code> at project level doesn't help when teams can re-grant it to themselves; VPC-SC has nothing to do with liens <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q7\">Exam 4 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Allowing <code>global</code> makes secret creation succeed but doesn't keep replicas in-region <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q9\">Exam 3 · Q9</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Constraint</th>\n<th>Blocks</th>\n<th>Typical stem</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation</code></td>\n<td>Creating new SA keys</td>\n<td>\"Remove JSON key risk\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>iam.disableServiceAccountKeyUpload</code></td>\n<td>Uploading external public keys</td>\n<td>Distractor for the above</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>gcp.resourceLocations</code></td>\n<td>Resources in non-allowed locations</td>\n<td>Data residency / \"error … in [global]\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>compute.restrictXpnProjectLienRemoval</code></td>\n<td>Lien removal on Shared VPC host w/o org-level permission</td>\n<td>Protect Shared VPC host from deletion</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","summary":"This lesson covers Organization Policy constraints, how they inherit and are overridden, and the specific constraints the exam keeps coming back to: service account keys, resource locations and Shared VPC liens. The right answer is usually a preventive guardrail set at the right level, with the narrowest possible exception.","objectives":["After this lesson you can explain how org policies inherit from org to folder to project and how a lower level overrides a parent.","After this lesson you can choose the right constraint to block service account key creation, restrict resource locations, or protect a Shared VPC host project.","After this lesson you can handle an unavoidable exception with a project-level override instead of removing the guardrail.","After this lesson you can fix a policy-blocked create by changing the resource configuration, for example Secret Manager user-managed replication."],"takeaways":["Remove JSON key risk org-wide → enforce iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation at the organization (not ...KeyUpload).","A third-party app that truly needs a key → override the constraint on that one project only. No downloadable \"default key\" exists.","Data residency → gcp.resourceLocations with allowed regions. Secret Manager under that policy → user-managed replication in allowed regions.","Lien removal on a Shared VPC host restricted to the org level → compute.restrictXpnProjectLienRemoval.","Detect-and-delete approaches (log alerts, Asset Inventory scans) react after the fact; org policies prevent."],"questions":["e2q8","e3q9","e4q7","e4q22","e5q47","e5q63"]},{"id":"d1l3","num":3,"title":"IAM, service accounts & workload identity","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Workload Identity Federation for GKE gives each workload (Kubernetes ServiceAccount) its own identity with short-lived credentials — no keys <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Impersonation setup: Google SA + KSA, grant KSA <code>roles/iam.workloadIdentityUser</code> on the GSA, annotate KSA with <code>iam.gke.io/gcp-service-account</code>, run Pods as the KSA <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>. Current guidance generally prefers direct IAM grants to federated principals where supported, but if the stem says \"impersonation\", use this <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Node service accounts are shared by every Pod on the node → violates least privilege <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Kubernetes Secrets are only base64-encoded; mounting a key in a Secret still distributes a long-lived credential <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>For a CI tool on a Compute Engine VM (Jenkins + Terraform), attach a service account with the needed IAM permissions to the VM <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q33\">Exam 5 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>Time-boxed access → <strong>group</strong> + IAM binding with an <strong>IAM Condition</strong> (date/time expression); access ends automatically <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q62\">Exam 5 · Q62</a>.</li>\n<li>Grant roles to <strong>groups</strong>, not individuals <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q56\">Exam 4 · Q56</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q62\">Exam 5 · Q62</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Change risk recommendations</strong> (Recommender/Active Assist) surface risk of an IAM policy change <em>before</em> applying it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q44\">Exam 3 · Q44</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Terraform pipeline in ephemeral GKE Pods → (C) create GSA with needed roles + (A) KSA via Workload Identity Federation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Avoid long-lived credentials, per-workload permissions, multi-tenant GKE\" → Workload Identity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Access only during September\" → group + IAM Condition <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q62\">Exam 5 · Q62</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Assess risk of this IAM change before production\" → change risk recommendations <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q44\">Exam 3 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Restrict a team to its namespace\" → Kubernetes <strong>RBAC</strong>, not IAP <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Node SA option looks attractive as the \"keyless\" choice — but it's shared identity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>. Only defensible if the cluster is dedicated to the one pipeline.</li>\n<li>JSON key in the CI tool's secret store is still a long-lived key <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Recent change recommendations\" = after-the-fact; deprecation/breaking-changes recommender = different risk; Project Owner = no assessment at all <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q44\">Exam 3 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>Firewall rules control traffic, not IAM access; manual role removal or Cloud Scheduler revocation per user is more fragile than conditions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q62\">Exam 5 · Q62</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Pick</th>\n<th>Not</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Pod → Google APIs</td>\n<td>Workload Identity Federation for GKE</td>\n<td>Node SA, JSON key in Secret</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VM workload → Google APIs</td>\n<td>Attached service account</td>\n<td><code>GOOGLE_CREDENTIALS</code> key, <code>gcloud auth application-default login</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Temporary human access</td>\n<td>Group + IAM Condition</td>\n<td>Custom role removed manually</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Namespace-level access in GKE</td>\n<td>Kubernetes RBAC</td>\n<td>IAP (HTTP(S) apps only)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","summary":"This lesson covers how workloads and people get access to Google Cloud without long-lived credentials: Workload Identity Federation for GKE, attached service accounts on VMs, and group-based, time-boxed IAM. The exam often offers convenient options (node service accounts, JSON keys in Secrets) that break least privilege.","objectives":["After this lesson you can set up Workload Identity Federation for GKE with a Google service account, a Kubernetes ServiceAccount, the workloadIdentityUser role and the KSA annotation.","After this lesson you can explain why node service accounts and JSON keys stored in Kubernetes Secrets violate least privilege.","After this lesson you can grant temporary access by combining a group with an IAM Condition.","After this lesson you can use change risk recommendations to assess an IAM change before applying it."],"takeaways":["Pods → Google APIs: Workload Identity Federation for GKE. Never a node SA shared by all Pods, never a JSON key in a Secret.","When the question requires impersonation: GSA + KSA, grant roles/iam.workloadIdentityUser to the KSA, annotate with iam.gke.io/gcp-service-account.","CI tool on a Compute Engine VM → attach a service account with the needed roles to the VM.","\"Access only during September\" → group + IAM Condition with a date/time expression. Grant roles to groups, not individuals.","Restricting a team to its namespace → Kubernetes RBAC. IAP is for HTTP(S) apps."],"questions":["e2q20","e2q47","e3q44","e4q3","e4q56","e5q33","e5q62"]},{"id":"d1l4","num":4,"title":"Audit logs & centralized logging access","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Data Access audit log config inherits downward; a child <strong>cannot disable</strong> a log type a parent enabled — it can only add <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q5\">Exam 4 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>The supported way to narrow an enabled config is <strong>exempted principals</strong> (by identity, not by resource branch) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q5\">Exam 4 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>Org-level enablement automatically covers future projects; per-project enablement doesn't <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q5\">Exam 4 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Log views</strong> slice a single log bucket into IAM-grantable subsets with no data duplication; <code>_AllLogs</code> gives full visibility. <code>_Default</code> view exists only on the <code>_Default</code> bucket and isn't project-scoped <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q46\">Exam 4 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li>Data Access audit logs require <code>roles/logging.privateLogViewer</code>; <code>roles/logging.viewer</code> can't see them <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q56\">Exam 4 · Q56</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Company-wide Data Access logging except one folder → enable at <strong>org</strong>, add that folder's principals to <strong>exempted principals</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q5\">Exam 4 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>Central log project, each team sees only its logs, ops sees all, low cost → per-team <strong>log views</strong> + ops on <code>_AllLogs</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q46\">Exam 4 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li>Security team needs read-only Data Access logs → <code>privateLogViewer</code> granted to a <strong>group</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q56\">Exam 4 · Q56</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Enable everywhere except the folder\" (per-resource) is the literal reading but doesn't scale and isn't how exclusion works <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q5\">Exam 4 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>BigQuery export per team duplicates storage; Logs Writer is the wrong direction of permission <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q46\">Exam 4 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li><code>logging.viewer</code> \"sounds enough\" — it isn't for Data Access logs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q56\">Exam 4 · Q56</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers how Data Access audit log settings inherit through the hierarchy and how to give teams scoped access to centrally stored logs. Expect questions about excluding part of the org from audit logging, log views on a central bucket, and the IAM role needed to read Data Access logs.","objectives":["After this lesson you can explain why a child resource cannot disable a Data Access log type that a parent enabled.","After this lesson you can narrow an org-level audit config with exempted principals and describe the limits of that approach.","After this lesson you can design per-team log views on a central log bucket, with ops on _AllLogs.","After this lesson you can choose roles/logging.privateLogViewer over roles/logging.viewer for Data Access logs."],"takeaways":["Audit config inherits downward and only adds; children can't turn off what a parent enabled.","Enabling at the org automatically covers future projects; per-project enablement does not.","Exempted principals are identity-based: they are exempt wherever the org config applies, not just inside one folder.","Each team sees only its logs in a central project → log views (no data duplication); ops gets _AllLogs.","Security team reads Data Access logs → roles/logging.privateLogViewer granted to a group."],"questions":["e4q5","e4q46","e4q56"]},{"id":"d1l5","num":5,"title":"Multi-project monitoring (metrics scopes)","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>scoping project</strong> hosts a <strong>metrics scope</strong> listing monitored projects; it also stores alert policies, dashboards, uptime checks. Metric data stays in the source project <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q31\">Exam 1 · Q31</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q57\">Exam 1 · Q57</a>.</li>\n<li>Monitoring Viewer on the scoping project is enough to view all monitored projects' data — no role on workload projects needed <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q31\">Exam 1 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>Google recommends a <strong>new or resource-free</strong> project as the scoping project <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q31\">Exam 1 · Q31</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q57\">Exam 1 · Q57</a>.</li>\n<li>Ordinary folders don't auto-populate scope membership (app-enabled folders are a separate mechanism) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q57\">Exam 1 · Q57</a>.</li>\n<li>Metrics scopes are what Workspaces became <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q31\">Exam 1 · Q31</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Prod-only alerting, least privilege → new monitoring project, add only prod projects, grant read on the scope <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q31\">Exam 1 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>Per-environment dashboards → <strong>one dedicated scoping project per environment</strong>, each containing that environment's projects <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q57\">Exam 1 · Q57</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Hosting the scope in an existing prod workload project \"works\" but isn't recommended (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q31\">Exam 1 · Q31</a> option C, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q57\">Exam 1 · Q57</a> options C/D).</li>\n<li>Per-project scopes fragment the view; Project Viewer is too broad <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q31\">Exam 1 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>One scope with all environments + filters works but isn't the best match <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q57\">Exam 1 · Q57</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers Cloud Monitoring metrics scopes, which let a single scoping project view metrics from many monitored projects. Exam questions test where to host the scope, how to split scopes by environment, and the minimum role needed to view data.","objectives":["After this lesson you can explain what a scoping project stores and that metric data stays in the source project.","After this lesson you can choose a new, resource-free project as the scoping project.","After this lesson you can design one scoping project per environment for per-environment dashboards and alerts.","After this lesson you can grant Monitoring Viewer on the scoping project as the least-privilege way to see all monitored projects."],"takeaways":["Scoping project holds the metrics scope, alert policies, dashboards and uptime checks; metric data stays in each source project.","Monitoring Viewer on the scoping project is enough; no roles are needed on the workload projects.","Google recommends a new or resource-free project as the scoping project, not an existing prod workload project.","Per-environment dashboards → one dedicated scoping project per environment.","Ordinary folders don't automatically add projects to a metrics scope."],"questions":["e1q31","e1q57"]},{"id":"d1l6","num":6,"title":"Infrastructure as Code with Terraform","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Terraform code belongs in a <strong>VCS (Git)</strong> with branches, reviews, merge to main; object versioning, network shares and Drive are not VCS <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q4\">Exam 1 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q8\">Exam 5 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Peer review + tests (<code>terraform validate</code>/<code>plan</code>) before merge prevent conflicts; \"daily merges\" don't <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q8\">Exam 5 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>New stack on every apply = Terraform can't see shared state → use a <strong>remote <code>gcs</code> backend</strong> (with locking) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q45\">Exam 5 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>State in source control: no locking, plaintext secrets, documented anti-pattern <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Foundation Toolkit</strong> provides Google-maintained Terraform modules for project + foundational resource provisioning <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q40\">Exam 5 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>Repo layout: Terraform <strong>one shared repo, directories per env</strong>; Kustomize/Config Sync <strong>one shared repo, overlays per env</strong>; app code <strong>one repo per app, feature branches</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q26\">Exam 4 · Q26</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Version and share IaC with a growing team\" → Git + PR/merge workflow <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q4\">Exam 1 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q8\">Exam 5 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Duplicate stacks / exactly one stack\" → gcs backend <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q45\">Exam 5 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Fast, reliable, repeatable project provisioning\" → CFT modules <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q40\">Exam 5 · Q40</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Cloud Storage object versioning ≠ code versioning <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q4\">Exam 1 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>Cleanup pipeline treats the symptom; destroy-then-apply causes downtime <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>Custom Terraform module from scratch is declarative but slower/riskier than CFT; gcloud scripts are imperative (no state/drift detection) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>Double-encoding environment (repo per env <em>and</em> branch per env) breaks single source of truth; separate repos+branches fight Kustomize's base/overlay design <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q26\">Exam 4 · Q26</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Storage for…</th>\n<th>Right tool</th>\n<th>Wrong tool</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>.tf</code> source</td>\n<td>Git + PRs</td>\n<td>GCS versioned bucket, Drive, network share</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>terraform.tfstate</code></td>\n<td>GCS backend (remote, locked)</td>\n<td>Git repo, local disk</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","summary":"This lesson covers how to manage Terraform as a team: source in Git with reviews, state in a remote locked backend, and Google-maintained modules for project provisioning. It also covers repository layout for infrastructure, Kubernetes config and application code.","objectives":["After this lesson you can keep Terraform code in Git with branches, peer review and validate/plan checks before merge.","After this lesson you can diagnose duplicate stacks as missing shared state and fix it with a remote gcs backend.","After this lesson you can choose Cloud Foundation Toolkit modules for fast, repeatable project provisioning.","After this lesson you can pick a repository layout: shared infra repo with per-environment directories or overlays, and one repo per app with feature branches."],"takeaways":[".tf source → Git + pull requests. A versioned GCS bucket, Drive or network share is not a VCS.","terraform.tfstate → remote gcs backend with locking. Never in Git (no locking, plaintext secrets).","\"A new stack appears on every apply\" means Terraform can't see shared state.","Fast, reliable project provisioning → Cloud Foundation Toolkit modules, not hand-written modules or gcloud scripts.","Don't encode the environment twice (repo per env and branch per env); it breaks the single source of truth."],"questions":["e1q4","e2q17","e4q18","e4q26","e5q8","e5q40","e5q45"]},{"id":"d1l7","num":7,"title":"Kubernetes-native config: Config Connector, Config Sync, Policy Controller","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Config Connector</strong>: Kubernetes CRDs that represent <strong>Google Cloud resources</strong>; it's a controller with a continuous reconciliation loop (corrects drift); Google-supported <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q10\">Exam 4 · Q10</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q27\">Exam 4 · Q27</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q38\">Exam 5 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Crossplane</strong>: CRD-based too, but community project → no Google Cloud Support path <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q10\">Exam 4 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Config Sync</strong>: continuously syncs/reconciles cluster objects from Git (installs things and keeps them matching) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q24\">Exam 5 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Policy Controller</strong> (Gatekeeper-based): admission control + audit of constraints — decides what is <em>allowed</em>, doesn't install resources <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Anthos Config Management</strong> = Config Sync + Policy Controller; constraint templates/parameters in Git → synced and enforced across clusters <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q37\">Exam 5 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>Pods and Jobs run once; Cloud Build runs on triggers — neither is a reconciler <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q27\">Exam 4 · Q27</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"CRDs\" + \"Google Cloud Support\" → Config Connector <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q10\">Exam 4 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>\"GitOps devs create cloud resources\" + \"reconcile periodically / prevent drift\" → Config Connector <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q27\">Exam 4 · Q27</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q38\">Exam 5 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Git is source of truth, install &amp; keep network policies + DaemonSet consistent across clusters\" → Config Sync <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q24\">Exam 5 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Constraint templates + parameters in GitHub, auto-applied\" → ACM (Policy Controller + Config Sync) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q37\">Exam 5 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Prevent Pods that violate a security standard\" → Policy Controller (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a> trap note).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Enforce\" ≠ Policy Controller automatically — enforcing <em>existence</em> is Config Sync <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>Config Connector synced to Git still only manages GCP resources, not policy constraints <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>Monitoring alerts + Cloud Run functions remediation = custom plumbing duplicating Config Sync <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>Anthos Service Mesh is traffic/mTLS, irrelevant to policy sync <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Manages</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>Reconciles?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Config Connector</td>\n<td>Google Cloud resources (as CRDs)</td>\n<td>K8s manifests (often via GitOps)</td>\n<td>Yes (controller loop)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Config Sync</td>\n<td>Any K8s objects in cluster</td>\n<td>Git repo</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Policy Controller</td>\n<td>Constraints (allow/deny + audit)</td>\n<td>Constraint templates</td>\n<td>Admission + audit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Crossplane</td>\n<td>Cloud resources (CRDs)</td>\n<td>K8s</td>\n<td>Yes, but no Google support</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Terraform via Cloud Build</td>\n<td>Cloud resources</td>\n<td><code>.tf</code></td>\n<td>No — per trigger</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","summary":"This lesson covers the Kubernetes-native tools for managing configuration: Config Connector for Google Cloud resources, Config Sync for GitOps sync of cluster objects, and Policy Controller for admission control and audit. Many questions turn on which of these three a scenario actually needs.","objectives":["After this lesson you can choose Config Connector when you need Google Cloud resources as Kubernetes CRDs with Google support and drift correction.","After this lesson you can choose Config Sync when you need to install Kubernetes objects from Git and keep them matching Git across clusters.","After this lesson you can choose Policy Controller when you need to decide which resources are allowed, using admission control and audit.","After this lesson you can tell reconcilers (controllers) apart from one-off runners such as Pods, Jobs and Cloud Build triggers."],"takeaways":["CRDs + Google Cloud Support, or periodic drift correction for cloud resources → Config Connector (Crossplane has no Google support path).","Git as source of truth, install and keep objects consistent across clusters → Config Sync.","Prevent Pods that violate a security standard → Policy Controller. Enforcing that something exists is Config Sync.","Constraint templates in Git applied automatically → Config Sync + Policy Controller (formerly branded Anthos Config Management).","Cloud Build, Pods and Jobs run once per trigger; they don't reconcile drift."],"questions":["e2q18","e4q10","e4q27","e4q33","e5q24","e5q37","e5q38"]},{"id":"d1l8","num":8,"title":"CI/CD tooling & artifact storage","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Helm 3 (3.8+) charts can be stored as <strong>OCI artifacts</strong> in Artifact Registry (Docker-format repos); IAM-governed and VPC-SC-supported <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q1\">Exam 2 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Packer</strong> builds VM images for multiple clouds from one config; <strong>Cloud Deploy</strong> promotes releases to GKE/Cloud Run/attached clusters; <strong>kpt</strong> manages Kubernetes config packages <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Build <strong>default pool</strong> runs outside your VPC; <strong>private pools</strong> are managed, peered into your VPC, reach private resources without public exposure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q16\">Exam 4 · Q16</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Public + private Helm charts, native access control, VPC-SC → Artifact Registry OCI <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q1\">Exam 2 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Multi-cloud VM image pipeline → Cloud Build + Packer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>CI build must hit private VPC APIs, no public exposure, low overhead → Cloud Build private pools <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q16\">Exam 4 · Q16</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>GitHub Enterprise / self-hosted chart server on GKE / GCS bucket repo → not native IAM + VPC-SC <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q1\">Exam 2 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Build + Cloud Deploy is the \"standard pairing\" but builds no VM images <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>External LB + Cloud Armor is still public; internal LB doesn't give default-pool workers a path into the VPC; Spinnaker adds overhead <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q16\">Exam 4 · Q16</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Tool</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>VM images across clouds</td>\n<td>Packer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Container release promotion (GKE/Cloud Run)</td>\n<td>Cloud Deploy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>K8s config packages</td>\n<td>kpt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Helm charts + images, IAM + VPC-SC</td>\n<td>Artifact Registry (OCI)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Builds reaching private VPC</td>\n<td>Cloud Build private pool</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","summary":"This lesson covers choosing the right CI/CD tool for each job: Artifact Registry for Helm charts and images, Packer for multi-cloud VM images, Cloud Deploy for release promotion, kpt for config packages, and Cloud Build private pools for builds that need to reach private networks.","objectives":["After this lesson you can store Helm charts as OCI artifacts in Artifact Registry with IAM and VPC Service Controls.","After this lesson you can match Packer, Cloud Deploy and kpt to the problems they solve.","After this lesson you can use Cloud Build private pools so builds reach private VPC resources without public exposure."],"takeaways":["Helm charts with native IAM and VPC-SC → Artifact Registry (OCI). Not a GCS bucket, GitHub Enterprise or a self-hosted chart server.","Multi-cloud VM image pipeline → Cloud Build + Packer. Cloud Deploy builds no VM images.","Release promotion to GKE or Cloud Run → Cloud Deploy. Kubernetes config packages → kpt.","CI must reach private VPC APIs → Cloud Build private pool. The default pool runs outside your VPC."],"questions":["e2q1","e2q14","e4q16"]},{"id":"d1l9","num":9,"title":"Environments & GKE multi-tenancy","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Separate <strong>dev</strong> (write/break code), <strong>test/staging</strong> (config changes, experiments, load tests in a production-like env) and <strong>prod</strong> (validated changes only); commonly separate projects under env folders <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q32\">Exam 1 · Q32</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q5\">Exam 5 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>GKE charges a per-cluster management fee → cluster count drives cost <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>Recommended layout: <strong>cluster per environment</strong>, <strong>namespace per application</strong>; namespaces scope policy, RBAC + network policies enforce it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q4\">Exam 3 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>Release channels: <strong>Stable</strong> = most mature; <strong>Regular</strong> = default balance; <strong>Rapid</strong> = earliest; <strong>Extended</strong> = longer minor-version support, not \"extra stable\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q52\">Exam 3 · Q52</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Dev/experiments/load tests hurting prod → separate dev env + separate test env <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q32\">Exam 1 · Q32</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q5\">Exam 5 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>Many teams, dev + prod, minimum cost, team isolation → one shared dev cluster + one shared prod cluster, namespace per team, <strong>RBAC</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>Sensitive data, several apps, low admin overhead → cluster per env, namespace per app <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q4\">Exam 3 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Prioritize stability\" for staging + prod → <strong>Stable for both</strong>; \"balance freshness &amp; stability\" → Regular for both <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q52\">Exam 3 · Q52</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A single scaled-down dev env shared with testers gives unrepresentative load results <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q32\">Exam 1 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Freezing prod to one release a year concentrates risk <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q32\">Exam 1 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Cluster per app/team multiplies fees and overhead <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q4\">Exam 3 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>Staging doesn't have to be on a faster channel than prod <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q52\">Exam 3 · Q52</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers how to separate dev, test and prod, and how to lay out GKE clusters and namespaces for many teams at minimum cost. It also covers choosing GKE release channels based on what the question prioritizes.","objectives":["After this lesson you can describe what belongs in dev, test/staging and prod environments.","After this lesson you can design a cluster-per-environment, namespace-per-team/app layout using RBAC and network policies.","After this lesson you can explain why cluster count drives GKE cost.","After this lesson you can pick the GKE release channel (Stable, Regular, Rapid, Extended) that matches the stated priority."],"takeaways":["Experiments or load tests hurting prod → separate dev and test environments; test should be production-like.","Many teams, minimum cost → one shared dev cluster and one shared prod cluster, namespace per team, RBAC.","Cluster per app or per team multiplies the per-cluster management fee and admin overhead.","\"Prioritize stability\" → Stable for both staging and prod; Extended means longer support, not extra stability."],"questions":["e1q32","e2q47","e3q4","e3q52","e5q5"]},{"id":"d1l10","num":10,"title":"Networking between departments","html":"<ul>\n<li>Many departments, many apps, minimal management, flexibility → <strong>VPC per department + VPC Network Peering</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q56\">Exam 2 · Q56</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Private Service Connect</strong> exposes one published service at a time — not general VPC-to-VPC connectivity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q56\">Exam 2 · Q56</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud VPN</strong> encrypts over the public internet, suited to hybrid links, not linking Google Cloud VPCs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q56\">Exam 2 · Q56</a>.</li>\n<li>VPC per application = unmanageable at the stated scale <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q56\">Exam 2 · Q56</a>.</li>\n<li>See ⚠️ section — this answer is option-relative.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers connecting VPC networks across many departments and how to rule out options that solve a different problem. The exam's answer here only holds relative to the options given, so learn the reasoning, not just the pick.","objectives":["After this lesson you can explain why VPC per department beats VPC per application at scale.","After this lesson you can tell VPC Network Peering, Private Service Connect and Cloud VPN apart by what each one connects.","After this lesson you can recognize the limits of peering meshes and the alternatives landing-zone guidance points to."],"takeaways":["Among the listed options, many departments with minimal management → VPC per department + VPC Network Peering.","Private Service Connect exposes one published service at a time; it isn't general VPC-to-VPC connectivity.","Cloud VPN encrypts over the public internet for hybrid links, not for linking Google Cloud VPCs.","Caveat: peering is non-transitive and limited per network; landing-zone guidance often uses Shared VPC or hub-and-spoke."],"questions":["e2q56"]},{"id":"d1l11","num":11,"title":"Developer environments: Cloud Workstations","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Workstation <strong>configurations</strong> are templates (image, IDE, machine type, storage) shared with a team; changes apply on next start <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q58\">Exam 1 · Q58</a>.</li>\n<li>Patching: updated base images, rebuild custom images, <code>runningTimeout</code> forces refresh; custom dependencies remain your responsibility <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q58\">Exam 1 · Q58</a>.</li>\n<li><code>idleTimeout</code> = stop after inactivity (cost); <code>runningTimeout</code> = stop after fixed runtime even if active <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Private Workstations cluster keeps environments off public networks <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Code OSS <strong>preview link</strong> gives authenticated browser access to an app on a workstation port — no public IP, bastion, or IAP tunnel to the underlying VM <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>Workstations support attached GPUs; grant developers <strong>Cloud Workstations User</strong>. Cloud Shell can't get GPUs (Google-owned VM) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q27\">Exam 3 · Q27</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Standardized team env + IDEs + ongoing patching → Cloud Workstations config per team <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q58\">Exam 1 · Q58</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q57\">Exam 5 · Q57</a>.</li>\n<li>Same IDE, private, MOST cost-effective → private cluster + <code>idleTimeout</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>View web app running in workstation → preview link <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>Managed Java IDE + GPUs → GPU workstation per developer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q27\">Exam 3 · Q27</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Golden-VM snapshots, checklists/scripts, laptop images → no managed refresh <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q58\">Exam 1 · Q58</a>.</li>\n<li>Scheduled VM shutdowns ignore developer activity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Per-dev projects with Editor role = broad permissions + DIY IDE <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q27\">Exam 3 · Q27</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers Cloud Workstations as the managed way to give teams standardized, patched and private development environments. Exam questions focus on configurations, timeouts for cost and patching, preview links, and GPU support.","objectives":["After this lesson you can use workstation configurations as team templates that apply on next start.","After this lesson you can tell idleTimeout (cost) apart from runningTimeout (forced refresh).","After this lesson you can give developers browser access to an app on a workstation port with a preview link.","After this lesson you can provide GPU development environments with Cloud Workstations instead of Cloud Shell."],"takeaways":["Standardized team environments with ongoing patching → one Cloud Workstations configuration per team.","Most cost-effective private setup → private Workstations cluster + idleTimeout.","runningTimeout stops a workstation after a fixed runtime even when it's active, forcing a refresh to updated images.","View a web app running on a workstation → Code OSS preview link; no public IP, bastion or IAP tunnel needed.","Need GPUs → GPU workstation with the Cloud Workstations User role; Cloud Shell can't get GPUs."],"questions":["e1q58","e3q7","e3q10","e3q27","e5q57"]},{"id":"d1l12","num":12,"title":"Capacity, quotas, secrets & encryption","html":"<ul>\n<li>Quotas are enforced <strong>per project per region</strong> → multi-region capacity planning = verify quota in <strong>each</strong> target region <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q17\">Exam 1 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>Machine-type choice is sizing, not capacity planning; Cloud Trace is latency debugging <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q17\">Exam 1 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Run: a <strong>scaling target</strong> (CPU %, concurrency) says <em>when</em> to scale; <strong>resource limits / max instances + quotas</strong> say <em>how far</em> — verify quotas support the range <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q58\">Exam 3 · Q58</a>. Request-based scaling uses <strong>concurrent requests</strong>, and Cloud Run CPU targets <em>are</em> customizable <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q58\">Exam 3 · Q58</a>.</li>\n<li>Sensitive data with bounded exposure on breach → keys in <strong>Cloud KMS</strong>, rotated frequently <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q60\">Exam 2 · Q60</a>. SSO is user auth; per-instance build-time secrets spread secrets into artifacts <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q60\">Exam 2 · Q60</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers capacity planning with regional quotas, setting Cloud Run scaling within limits, and protecting sensitive data with Cloud KMS. These are short decision-rule questions where the trap is an option that solves a neighboring problem.","objectives":["After this lesson you can plan multi-region capacity by checking quota in each target region.","After this lesson you can tell a Cloud Run scaling target (when to scale) apart from max instances, limits and quotas (how far it can scale).","After this lesson you can choose Cloud KMS-managed, regularly rotated keys for sensitive data, and explain what rotation does and doesn't protect."],"takeaways":["Quotas are enforced per project per region → verify quota in every target region.","Machine-type choice is sizing, not capacity planning; Cloud Trace is for latency debugging.","Cloud Run: scaling target says when, max instances/limits and quotas say how far. CPU targets are customizable.","Key rotation protects new encryptions; older key versions still decrypt existing data until it is re-encrypted."],"questions":["e1q17","e2q60","e3q58"]},{"id":"d1l13","num":13,"title":"SRE incident readiness","html":"<ul>\n<li>Reduce customer disruption → <strong>current playbooks</strong> + <strong>automation</strong> that analyzes impact and suggests mitigations to on-call <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q55\">Exam 3 · Q55</a>.</li>\n<li>Everyone modifying prod during incidents = uncoordinated; exclusive unrestricted on-call permissions ≠ least privilege; alerts on internal behavior alone aren't necessarily actionable/symptom-based <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q55\">Exam 3 · Q55</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers preparing teams for incidents in a way that reduces customer disruption. The exam favors current playbooks plus automation that helps on-call engineers, over options that sound decisive but break coordination or least privilege.","objectives":["After this lesson you can identify current playbooks and impact-analysis automation as the best way to reduce customer disruption.","After this lesson you can reject uncoordinated production changes during incidents.","After this lesson you can explain why unrestricted on-call permissions and alerts on internal behavior alone are weak answers."],"takeaways":["Reduce customer disruption → keep playbooks current + automation that analyzes impact and suggests mitigations to on-call.","Everyone changing prod during an incident is uncoordinated, not faster.","Exclusive, unrestricted on-call permissions break least privilege.","Alerts on internal behavior alone aren't necessarily actionable or symptom-based."],"questions":["e3q55"]}],"ref":{"cheat":"<ul>\n<li>Policy consistency/cost by environment → <strong>prod &amp; dev folders</strong>, not BU folders or labels.</li>\n<li>Department total budget → <strong>folder + one folder-scoped budget</strong>.</li>\n<li>Kill JSON key risk org-wide → <strong><code>iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation</code> at org</strong> (not <code>…KeyUpload</code>).</li>\n<li>Key truly required → <strong>override constraint on that project only</strong>; no \"default key\" exists.</li>\n<li>Data residency → <strong><code>gcp.resourceLocations</code> / <code>in: allowed_values</code></strong>; secrets → <strong>user-managed replication</strong> in allowed regions.</li>\n<li>Shared VPC host lien protection → <strong><code>compute.restrictXpnProjectLienRemoval</code></strong>.</li>\n<li>Pods → Google APIs → <strong>Workload Identity Federation for GKE</strong> (GSA + KSA + <code>workloadIdentityUser</code> + annotation).</li>\n<li>Never: JSON key in K8s Secret, node SA shared by all Pods.</li>\n<li>Temporary access → <strong>group + IAM Condition (date/time)</strong>.</li>\n<li>Pre-change IAM risk → <strong>change risk recommendations</strong>.</li>\n<li>Namespace isolation → <strong>RBAC</strong>; IAP is for HTTP(S) apps.</li>\n<li>Exclude a folder from org-level Data Access logs → <strong>exempted principals</strong>; children can't disable.</li>\n<li>Data Access logs → <strong><code>logging.privateLogViewer</code> to a group</strong>.</li>\n<li>Per-team view of central logs → <strong>log views</strong>; ops → <code>_AllLogs</code>.</li>\n<li>Multi-project monitoring → <strong>dedicated resource-free scoping project</strong> per boundary; Monitoring Viewer there.</li>\n<li>Terraform code → <strong>Git + PRs</strong>; state → <strong>GCS backend</strong>; duplicate stacks = missing remote state.</li>\n<li>New projects fast &amp; repeatable → <strong>Cloud Foundation Toolkit modules</strong>.</li>\n<li>Repos: infra shared w/ env dirs/overlays; apps repo-per-app w/ feature branches.</li>\n<li>CRDs + Google support / periodic drift correction for cloud resources → <strong>Config Connector</strong>.</li>\n<li>Install &amp; keep K8s objects matching Git → <strong>Config Sync</strong>; what's allowed → <strong>Policy Controller</strong>.</li>\n<li>Helm charts + VPC-SC → <strong>Artifact Registry (OCI)</strong>; multi-cloud VM images → <strong>Packer</strong>.</li>\n<li>CI needs private VPC → <strong>Cloud Build private pools</strong>.</li>\n<li>GKE: cluster per env, namespace per app/team; stability → <strong>Stable</strong> for staging &amp; prod.</li>\n<li>Dev envs → <strong>Cloud Workstations</strong>; cost → <code>idleTimeout</code>; view app → preview link; GPUs → workstation, not Cloud Shell.</li>\n<li>Multi-region capacity → <strong>check quota in every region</strong>; Cloud Run → limits + quotas, not just a scaling target.</li>\n</ul>","rules":[{"lesson":"d1l1","title":"Resource hierarchy, folders & cost grouping","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Apply security policy consistently to all prod projects\" + \"track prod vs dev cost\" → one <strong>Production</strong> folder + one <strong>Development</strong> folder <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Visibility into total department spend, alert if department exceeds budget\" → department folder + <strong>one budget scoped to the folder</strong> at the full budget <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q43\">Exam 3 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>Only pick per-project budgets when the question asks <em>which project</em> overspent <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q43\">Exam 3 · Q43</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l2","title":"Organization policies","html":"<ul>\n<li>Eliminate JSON key risk org-wide with minimal overhead → enforce <code>constraints/iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation</code> at the <strong>org</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>A key is unavoidable for a 3rd-party app → override the constraint <strong>on that project only</strong>, then create the key <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q47\">Exam 5 · Q47</a>. Even narrower: tag-based exemption of specific service accounts <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Regulatory \"resources only in region X\" across the org → org policy with allowed regions in an <code>in: allowed_values</code> list <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q63\">Exam 5 · Q63</a>.</li>\n<li>Org policy blocks a create → first change the <em>resource configuration</em> to comply, don't relax the policy <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q9\">Exam 3 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>Teams self-manage project IAM, need lien removal restricted to org level → <code>compute.restrictXpnProjectLienRemoval</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q7\">Exam 4 · Q7</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l3","title":"IAM, service accounts & workload identity","html":"<ul>\n<li>Terraform pipeline in ephemeral GKE Pods → (C) create GSA with needed roles + (A) KSA via Workload Identity Federation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Avoid long-lived credentials, per-workload permissions, multi-tenant GKE\" → Workload Identity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Access only during September\" → group + IAM Condition <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q62\">Exam 5 · Q62</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Assess risk of this IAM change before production\" → change risk recommendations <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q44\">Exam 3 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Restrict a team to its namespace\" → Kubernetes <strong>RBAC</strong>, not IAP <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l4","title":"Audit logs & centralized logging access","html":"<ul>\n<li>Company-wide Data Access logging except one folder → enable at <strong>org</strong>, add that folder's principals to <strong>exempted principals</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q5\">Exam 4 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>Central log project, each team sees only its logs, ops sees all, low cost → per-team <strong>log views</strong> + ops on <code>_AllLogs</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q46\">Exam 4 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li>Security team needs read-only Data Access logs → <code>privateLogViewer</code> granted to a <strong>group</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q56\">Exam 4 · Q56</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l5","title":"Multi-project monitoring (metrics scopes)","html":"<ul>\n<li>Prod-only alerting, least privilege → new monitoring project, add only prod projects, grant read on the scope <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q31\">Exam 1 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>Per-environment dashboards → <strong>one dedicated scoping project per environment</strong>, each containing that environment's projects <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q57\">Exam 1 · Q57</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l6","title":"Infrastructure as Code with Terraform","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Version and share IaC with a growing team\" → Git + PR/merge workflow <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q4\">Exam 1 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q8\">Exam 5 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Duplicate stacks / exactly one stack\" → gcs backend <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q45\">Exam 5 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Fast, reliable, repeatable project provisioning\" → CFT modules <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q40\">Exam 5 · Q40</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l7","title":"Kubernetes-native config: Config Connector, Config Sync, Policy Controller","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"CRDs\" + \"Google Cloud Support\" → Config Connector <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q10\">Exam 4 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>\"GitOps devs create cloud resources\" + \"reconcile periodically / prevent drift\" → Config Connector <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q27\">Exam 4 · Q27</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q38\">Exam 5 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Git is source of truth, install &amp; keep network policies + DaemonSet consistent across clusters\" → Config Sync <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q24\">Exam 5 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Constraint templates + parameters in GitHub, auto-applied\" → ACM (Policy Controller + Config Sync) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q37\">Exam 5 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Prevent Pods that violate a security standard\" → Policy Controller (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a> trap note).</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l8","title":"CI/CD tooling & artifact storage","html":"<ul>\n<li>Public + private Helm charts, native access control, VPC-SC → Artifact Registry OCI <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q1\">Exam 2 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Multi-cloud VM image pipeline → Cloud Build + Packer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>CI build must hit private VPC APIs, no public exposure, low overhead → Cloud Build private pools <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q16\">Exam 4 · Q16</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l9","title":"Environments & GKE multi-tenancy","html":"<ul>\n<li>Dev/experiments/load tests hurting prod → separate dev env + separate test env <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q32\">Exam 1 · Q32</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q5\">Exam 5 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>Many teams, dev + prod, minimum cost, team isolation → one shared dev cluster + one shared prod cluster, namespace per team, <strong>RBAC</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>Sensitive data, several apps, low admin overhead → cluster per env, namespace per app <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q4\">Exam 3 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Prioritize stability\" for staging + prod → <strong>Stable for both</strong>; \"balance freshness &amp; stability\" → Regular for both <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q52\">Exam 3 · Q52</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l11","title":"Developer environments: Cloud Workstations","html":"<ul>\n<li>Standardized team env + IDEs + ongoing patching → Cloud Workstations config per team <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q58\">Exam 1 · Q58</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q57\">Exam 5 · Q57</a>.</li>\n<li>Same IDE, private, MOST cost-effective → private cluster + <code>idleTimeout</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>View web app running in workstation → preview link <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q10\">Exam 3 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>Managed Java IDE + GPUs → GPU workstation per developer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q27\">Exam 3 · Q27</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"traps":[{"lesson":"d1l1","title":"Resource hierarchy, folders & cost grouping","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"20 business units\" in the stem tempts BU folders — but the stated problems are environment problems <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>Full budget on <em>each</em> project → nothing fires until one project alone eats the whole budget; budget ÷ N projects → false/missed alarms <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q43\">Exam 3 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>A \"common services project\" per BU centralizes shared infra; it is not a policy or cost boundary <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q37\">Exam 3 · Q37</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Mechanism</th>\n<th>Policy/IAM inheritance?</th>\n<th>Cost aggregation?</th>\n<th>Use for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Folder</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Yes (folder-scoped budgets/reports)</td>\n<td>Environment or department grouping</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Label</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Filter in billing export only</td>\n<td>Tagging/breakdowns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Common-services project</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Shared VPC host, logging, security tooling</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"lesson":"d1l2","title":"Organization policies","html":"<ul>\n<li>Removing the policy at org/folder \"fixes the error\" but drops the guardrail for everyone <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q8\">Exam 2 · Q8</a>.</li>\n<li>Custom roles stripping <code>iam.serviceAccountKeys.*</code> or restricting <code>serviceAccountKeyAdmin</code> to admins only <em>reduce/concentrate</em> risk — they don't eliminate it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q22\">Exam 4 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>Detect-and-delete approaches (log-based alerts, hourly Asset Inventory scans) are reactive, not preventive <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q63\">Exam 5 · Q63</a>.</li>\n<li>Revoking <code>updateLiens</code> at project level doesn't help when teams can re-grant it to themselves; VPC-SC has nothing to do with liens <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q7\">Exam 4 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Allowing <code>global</code> makes secret creation succeed but doesn't keep replicas in-region <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q9\">Exam 3 · Q9</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Constraint</th>\n<th>Blocks</th>\n<th>Typical stem</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation</code></td>\n<td>Creating new SA keys</td>\n<td>\"Remove JSON key risk\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>iam.disableServiceAccountKeyUpload</code></td>\n<td>Uploading external public keys</td>\n<td>Distractor for the above</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>gcp.resourceLocations</code></td>\n<td>Resources in non-allowed locations</td>\n<td>Data residency / \"error … in [global]\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>compute.restrictXpnProjectLienRemoval</code></td>\n<td>Lien removal on Shared VPC host w/o org-level permission</td>\n<td>Protect Shared VPC host from deletion</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"lesson":"d1l3","title":"IAM, service accounts & workload identity","html":"<ul>\n<li>Node SA option looks attractive as the \"keyless\" choice — but it's shared identity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>. Only defensible if the cluster is dedicated to the one pipeline.</li>\n<li>JSON key in the CI tool's secret store is still a long-lived key <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Recent change recommendations\" = after-the-fact; deprecation/breaking-changes recommender = different risk; Project Owner = no assessment at all <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q44\">Exam 3 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>Firewall rules control traffic, not IAM access; manual role removal or Cloud Scheduler revocation per user is more fragile than conditions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q62\">Exam 5 · Q62</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Pick</th>\n<th>Not</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Pod → Google APIs</td>\n<td>Workload Identity Federation for GKE</td>\n<td>Node SA, JSON key in Secret</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>VM workload → Google APIs</td>\n<td>Attached service account</td>\n<td><code>GOOGLE_CREDENTIALS</code> key, <code>gcloud auth application-default login</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Temporary human access</td>\n<td>Group + IAM Condition</td>\n<td>Custom role removed manually</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Namespace-level access in GKE</td>\n<td>Kubernetes RBAC</td>\n<td>IAP (HTTP(S) apps only)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"lesson":"d1l4","title":"Audit logs & centralized logging access","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Enable everywhere except the folder\" (per-resource) is the literal reading but doesn't scale and isn't how exclusion works <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q5\">Exam 4 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>BigQuery export per team duplicates storage; Logs Writer is the wrong direction of permission <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q46\">Exam 4 · Q46</a>.</li>\n<li><code>logging.viewer</code> \"sounds enough\" — it isn't for Data Access logs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q56\">Exam 4 · Q56</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l5","title":"Multi-project monitoring (metrics scopes)","html":"<ul>\n<li>Hosting the scope in an existing prod workload project \"works\" but isn't recommended (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q31\">Exam 1 · Q31</a> option C, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q57\">Exam 1 · Q57</a> options C/D).</li>\n<li>Per-project scopes fragment the view; Project Viewer is too broad <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q31\">Exam 1 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>One scope with all environments + filters works but isn't the best match <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q57\">Exam 1 · Q57</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l6","title":"Infrastructure as Code with Terraform","html":"<ul>\n<li>Cloud Storage object versioning ≠ code versioning <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q4\">Exam 1 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>Cleanup pipeline treats the symptom; destroy-then-apply causes downtime <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q17\">Exam 2 · Q17</a>.</li>\n<li>Custom Terraform module from scratch is declarative but slower/riskier than CFT; gcloud scripts are imperative (no state/drift detection) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q18\">Exam 4 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>Double-encoding environment (repo per env <em>and</em> branch per env) breaks single source of truth; separate repos+branches fight Kustomize's base/overlay design <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q26\">Exam 4 · Q26</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Storage for…</th>\n<th>Right tool</th>\n<th>Wrong tool</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>.tf</code> source</td>\n<td>Git + PRs</td>\n<td>GCS versioned bucket, Drive, network share</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>terraform.tfstate</code></td>\n<td>GCS backend (remote, locked)</td>\n<td>Git repo, local disk</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"lesson":"d1l7","title":"Kubernetes-native config: Config Connector, Config Sync, Policy Controller","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Enforce\" ≠ Policy Controller automatically — enforcing <em>existence</em> is Config Sync <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>Config Connector synced to Git still only manages GCP resources, not policy constraints <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>Monitoring alerts + Cloud Run functions remediation = custom plumbing duplicating Config Sync <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>Anthos Service Mesh is traffic/mTLS, irrelevant to policy sync <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Manages</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>Reconciles?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Config Connector</td>\n<td>Google Cloud resources (as CRDs)</td>\n<td>K8s manifests (often via GitOps)</td>\n<td>Yes (controller loop)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Config Sync</td>\n<td>Any K8s objects in cluster</td>\n<td>Git repo</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Policy Controller</td>\n<td>Constraints (allow/deny + audit)</td>\n<td>Constraint templates</td>\n<td>Admission + audit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Crossplane</td>\n<td>Cloud resources (CRDs)</td>\n<td>K8s</td>\n<td>Yes, but no Google support</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Terraform via Cloud Build</td>\n<td>Cloud resources</td>\n<td><code>.tf</code></td>\n<td>No — per trigger</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"lesson":"d1l8","title":"CI/CD tooling & artifact storage","html":"<ul>\n<li>GitHub Enterprise / self-hosted chart server on GKE / GCS bucket repo → not native IAM + VPC-SC <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q1\">Exam 2 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Build + Cloud Deploy is the \"standard pairing\" but builds no VM images <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q14\">Exam 2 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>External LB + Cloud Armor is still public; internal LB doesn't give default-pool workers a path into the VPC; Spinnaker adds overhead <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q16\">Exam 4 · Q16</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Tool</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>VM images across clouds</td>\n<td>Packer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Container release promotion (GKE/Cloud Run)</td>\n<td>Cloud Deploy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>K8s config packages</td>\n<td>kpt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Helm charts + images, IAM + VPC-SC</td>\n<td>Artifact Registry (OCI)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Builds reaching private VPC</td>\n<td>Cloud Build private pool</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"lesson":"d1l9","title":"Environments & GKE multi-tenancy","html":"<ul>\n<li>A single scaled-down dev env shared with testers gives unrepresentative load results <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q32\">Exam 1 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Freezing prod to one release a year concentrates risk <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q32\">Exam 1 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Cluster per app/team multiplies fees and overhead <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q47\">Exam 2 · Q47</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q4\">Exam 3 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>Staging doesn't have to be on a faster channel than prod <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q52\">Exam 3 · Q52</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d1l11","title":"Developer environments: Cloud Workstations","html":"<ul>\n<li>Golden-VM snapshots, checklists/scripts, laptop images → no managed refresh <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q58\">Exam 1 · Q58</a>.</li>\n<li>Scheduled VM shutdowns ignore developer activity <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q7\">Exam 3 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Per-dev projects with Editor role = broad permissions + DIY IDE <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q27\">Exam 3 · Q27</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"questionable":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Q</th>\n<th>Issue</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q33\">Exam 5 · Q33</a></td>\n<td>Answer (attach SA to Jenkins VM) is fine, but the explanation is wrong in places: it says \"Workload Identity is designed for VM-to-Google Cloud communication\" (VMs use the attached SA via the metadata server; Workload Identity is a GKE/federation feature), and claims <code>gcloud auth application-default login</code> needs \"manual key rotation\" (it's an interactive <em>user</em> credential flow — the real problem is using a human's credentials in automation). Also note the tension with <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q20\">Exam 2 · Q20</a>: a shared node SA is rejected there because many Pods share it; a dedicated Jenkins VM doesn't have that problem.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q60\">Exam 2 · Q60</a></td>\n<td>KMS key rotation creates a new primary version for <strong>new</strong> encryptions; old versions stay enabled to decrypt existing data, so rotation alone doesn't shrink what a compromised old version exposes unless data is re-encrypted. Treat as \"best of the options\"; the reasoning overstates rotation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q56\">Exam 2 · Q56</a></td>\n<td>VPC Peering is non-transitive and has per-network peering limits, so a mesh across \"a large number of departments\" is not low-maintenance in practice; Google landing-zone guidance commonly points to Shared VPC or hub-and-spoke (e.g., Network Connectivity Center). Correct only relative to the listed options.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q5\">Exam 4 · Q5</a></td>\n<td>Exempted principals are identity-based: those principals become exempt wherever the org-level config applies, not just inside the CustomerService folder. Works only if those principals are used solely by that folder's projects.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q18\">Exam 2 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q24\">Exam 5 · Q24</a></td>\n<td>The \"correct\" option has Cloud Build <em>deploy</em> the resources <strong>and</strong> Config Sync sync them — redundant in pure GitOps (Config Sync applies from Git). E5's explanation reframes Cloud Build as validating/rendering before the repo. Pick it for \"Config Sync\", not for the Cloud Build deploy step.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q26\">Exam 4 · Q26</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q33\">Exam 4 · Q33</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q37\">Exam 5 · Q37</a></td>\n<td>\"Anthos Config Management\" is legacy branding; the components are now Config Sync and Policy Controller. Concepts still hold.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q4\">Exam 1 · Q4</a></td>\n<td>Cites Cloud Source Repositories as a Git host; CSR is closed to new customers — use GitHub/GitLab/Secure Source Manager. The VCS principle is unaffected.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q3\">Exam 4 · Q3</a></td>\n<td>Explanation itself notes current guidance prefers direct IAM grants to Workload Identity principals over GSA impersonation; the answer is right only because the stem demands impersonation.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},"glossary":[{"term":"_AllLogs view","definition":"A built-in log view that gives access to all logs in a log bucket.","exam_context":"Give the central ops team _AllLogs while each team gets its own log view.","lesson":"d1l4","domain":1},{"term":"Artifact Registry (OCI)","definition":"Google Cloud's managed artifact store; Helm 3.8+ charts can be stored as OCI artifacts in Docker-format repositories, with IAM access control and VPC Service Controls support.","exam_context":"Public and private Helm charts with native IAM and VPC-SC → Artifact Registry, not a GCS bucket or a self-hosted chart server.","lesson":"d1l8","domain":1},{"term":"Change risk recommendations","definition":"Recommender (Active Assist) insights that show the risk of an IAM policy change before you apply it.","exam_context":"\"Assess the risk of this IAM change before production\" → change risk recommendations, not recent-change recommendations, which come after the fact.","lesson":"d1l3","domain":1},{"term":"Cloud Billing budget (folder-scoped)","definition":"A budget whose scope is a folder, so it adds up spend across every project nested under that folder.","exam_context":"Department total spend with an alert → one budget scoped to the department folder, not a full budget on each project.","lesson":"d1l1","domain":1},{"term":"Cloud Build private pool","definition":"A managed pool of Cloud Build workers peered into your VPC, so builds can reach private resources without exposing them publicly.","exam_context":"CI must call private VPC APIs with low overhead → private pool. The default pool runs outside your VPC.","lesson":"d1l8","domain":1},{"term":"Cloud Deploy","definition":"A managed service that promotes releases through targets such as GKE, Cloud Run and attached clusters.","exam_context":"Often offered as the \"standard pairing\" with Cloud Build, but it builds no VM images; choose Packer for those.","lesson":"d1l8","domain":1},{"term":"Cloud Foundation Toolkit","definition":"A set of Google-maintained Terraform modules for provisioning projects and foundational resources.","exam_context":"\"Fast, reliable, repeatable project provisioning\" → CFT modules, not custom modules from scratch or gcloud scripts.","lesson":"d1l6","domain":1},{"term":"Cloud KMS","definition":"Google Cloud's key management service for creating, storing and rotating encryption keys.","exam_context":"Sensitive data with bounded exposure → keys in Cloud KMS, rotated regularly. Rotation applies to new encryptions; old versions still decrypt existing data.","lesson":"d1l12","domain":1},{"term":"Cloud VPN","definition":"Encrypted tunnels over the public internet, typically used for hybrid connectivity.","exam_context":"A distractor for connecting Google Cloud VPCs to each other.","lesson":"d1l10","domain":1},{"term":"Cloud Workstations","definition":"Managed development environments created from shared configurations, with a choice of IDE and support for private clusters and GPUs.","exam_context":"Standardized, patched team dev environments → a Cloud Workstations configuration per team, not golden-VM snapshots or laptop images.","lesson":"d1l11","domain":1},{"term":"compute.restrictXpnProjectLienRemoval","definition":"An Organization Policy constraint that makes lien removal on a Shared VPC host project require resourcemanager.projects.updateLiens at the organization level.","exam_context":"Teams self-manage project IAM but lien removal must be org-only → this constraint. Project-level revocation and VPC-SC don't solve it.","lesson":"d1l2","domain":1},{"term":"Config Connector","definition":"A Kubernetes controller that represents Google Cloud resources as CRDs and continuously reconciles them to correct drift; Google-supported.","exam_context":"\"CRDs + Google Cloud Support\" or \"devs create cloud resources via GitOps with drift correction\" → Config Connector (not Crossplane).","lesson":"d1l7","domain":1},{"term":"Config Sync","definition":"A GitOps tool that continuously syncs Kubernetes objects from a Git repo into clusters and keeps them matching.","exam_context":"\"Install network policies and a DaemonSet and keep them consistent across clusters from Git\" → Config Sync, not Policy Controller.","lesson":"d1l7","domain":1},{"term":"Crossplane","definition":"A community project that also manages cloud resources through Kubernetes CRDs.","exam_context":"A distractor when the question requires Google Cloud Support; it has no Google support path.","lesson":"d1l7","domain":1},{"term":"Data Access audit logs","definition":"Audit logs that record reads of resource data and configuration; they must be enabled and their config inherits down the hierarchy.","exam_context":"Enabled at a parent, they can't be disabled by a child; enabling at the org also covers future projects.","lesson":"d1l4","domain":1},{"term":"Exempted principals","definition":"Identities listed in an audit log config so their access isn't logged for that log type.","exam_context":"The supported way to narrow an org-level Data Access config. They're identity-based, so they're exempt everywhere the config applies, not just in one folder.","lesson":"d1l4","domain":1},{"term":"Folder","definition":"A resource hierarchy node that groups projects; IAM and Organization Policy inherit through it, and it can serve as a cost-aggregation boundary.","exam_context":"Policy consistency and cost tracking per environment → Production and Development folders, not business-unit folders or labels.","lesson":"d1l1","domain":1},{"term":"gcp.resourceLocations","definition":"An Organization Policy constraint that restricts the locations where resources can be created, using allowed values.","exam_context":"Data residency across the org → this constraint with allowed regions. An error mentioning [global] for Secret Manager means switch to user-managed replication.","lesson":"d1l2","domain":1},{"term":"GKE release channels","definition":"Channels that control GKE version upgrades: Rapid (earliest), Regular (default balance), Stable (most mature) and Extended (longer minor-version support).","exam_context":"\"Prioritize stability\" → Stable for both staging and prod. Extended is not \"extra stable\".","lesson":"d1l9","domain":1},{"term":"IAM Condition","definition":"An expression on an IAM role binding, such as a date/time range, that limits when or where the binding applies.","exam_context":"Temporary access (\"only during September\") → group + IAM Condition; access ends automatically without manual removal.","lesson":"d1l3","domain":1},{"term":"iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation","definition":"An Organization Policy constraint that blocks creating user-managed service account keys.","exam_context":"Remove JSON key risk org-wide → enforce at the org; if a key is unavoidable, override on that one project only.","lesson":"d1l2","domain":1},{"term":"iam.disableServiceAccountKeyUpload","definition":"An Organization Policy constraint that blocks uploading an externally generated public key to a service account.","exam_context":"A common distractor for key-creation questions; it blocks a different action.","lesson":"d1l2","domain":1},{"term":"kpt","definition":"A tool for managing Kubernetes configuration as packages.","exam_context":"Matches questions about Kubernetes config packages, not VM images or release promotion.","lesson":"d1l8","domain":1},{"term":"Kubernetes RBAC","definition":"Kubernetes role-based access control that grants permissions on cluster resources, scoped to namespaces or the whole cluster.","exam_context":"Restrict a team to its namespace → RBAC. IAP only protects HTTP(S) apps.","lesson":"d1l9","domain":1},{"term":"Label","definition":"A key/value tag on resources that can be used to filter and break down billing exports.","exam_context":"Not an IAM or Org Policy attachment point; a distractor when the question needs policy inheritance.","lesson":"d1l1","domain":1},{"term":"Log view","definition":"A subset of a log bucket that can be granted through IAM, so different teams see different logs without duplicating data.","exam_context":"Central log project where each team sees only its logs at low cost → per-team log views, not per-team BigQuery exports.","lesson":"d1l4","domain":1},{"term":"Metrics scope","definition":"The list of monitored projects whose metrics a scoping project can view; the successor to Monitoring Workspaces.","exam_context":"Multi-project monitoring → a metrics scope in a dedicated scoping project; ordinary folders don't add projects automatically.","lesson":"d1l5","domain":1},{"term":"Monitoring Viewer","definition":"An IAM role that grants read access to Cloud Monitoring data.","exam_context":"Granting it on the scoping project is enough to view all monitored projects; Project Viewer is too broad.","lesson":"d1l5","domain":1},{"term":"Node service account","definition":"The service account attached to GKE nodes, whose credentials every Pod on the node can use.","exam_context":"Looks like the keyless answer but is a shared identity that violates least privilege for Pods.","lesson":"d1l3","domain":1},{"term":"Organization policy","definition":"A guardrail made of constraints, set at the org, folder or project, that inherits down the hierarchy; a lower level can override the parent.","exam_context":"Preventive answer over detect-and-delete. When a policy blocks a create, change the resource configuration rather than relaxing the policy.","lesson":"d1l2","domain":1},{"term":"Packer","definition":"A tool that builds VM images for multiple clouds from one configuration.","exam_context":"Multi-cloud VM image pipeline → Cloud Build + Packer.","lesson":"d1l8","domain":1},{"term":"Policy Controller","definition":"A Gatekeeper-based admission controller that enforces and audits constraints defined by constraint templates; it decides what is allowed but doesn't install resources.","exam_context":"\"Prevent Pods that violate a security standard\" → Policy Controller; constraint templates in Git → Policy Controller + Config Sync.","lesson":"d1l7","domain":1},{"term":"Preview link","definition":"A Code OSS feature in Cloud Workstations that gives authenticated browser access to an app on a workstation port.","exam_context":"View a web app running in a workstation → preview link; no public IP, bastion or IAP tunnel needed.","lesson":"d1l11","domain":1},{"term":"Private Service Connect","definition":"A way to expose a single published service to consumers in other networks.","exam_context":"A distractor for department-to-department networking; it isn't general VPC-to-VPC connectivity.","lesson":"d1l10","domain":1},{"term":"Quota (per project per region)","definition":"Limits on resource usage that Google Cloud enforces per project and per region.","exam_context":"Multi-region capacity planning → verify quota in each target region; machine-type choice is sizing, not capacity planning.","lesson":"d1l12","domain":1},{"term":"roles/iam.workloadIdentityUser","definition":"The IAM role granted to a Kubernetes ServiceAccount on a Google service account so it can impersonate that GSA.","exam_context":"Part of the impersonation setup with the iam.gke.io/gcp-service-account annotation, used when the question asks for impersonation.","lesson":"d1l3","domain":1},{"term":"roles/logging.privateLogViewer","definition":"A Logging role that can read private logs, including Data Access audit logs.","exam_context":"Security team needs read-only Data Access logs → privateLogViewer to a group; roles/logging.viewer can't see them.","lesson":"d1l4","domain":1},{"term":"runningTimeout / idleTimeout","definition":"Cloud Workstations settings: idleTimeout stops a workstation after inactivity; runningTimeout stops it after a fixed runtime even when active.","exam_context":"Cost savings → idleTimeout. Forcing refresh to patched images → runningTimeout.","lesson":"d1l11","domain":1},{"term":"Scoping project","definition":"The project that hosts a metrics scope plus its alert policies, dashboards and uptime checks; metric data stays in the source projects.","exam_context":"Use a new or resource-free project, one per environment boundary, not an existing prod workload project.","lesson":"d1l5","domain":1},{"term":"Secret Manager replication","definition":"How a secret's replicas are placed: automatic replication requires the global location; user-managed replication lets you pick specific regions.","exam_context":"Under a gcp.resourceLocations policy that excludes global → use user-managed replication with replicas only in allowed regions.","lesson":"d1l2","domain":1},{"term":"Terraform gcs backend","definition":"A remote Terraform state backend in Cloud Storage that shares state across runs and supports locking.","exam_context":"A new stack on every apply means missing shared state → configure a gcs backend. Never keep state in Git.","lesson":"d1l6","domain":1},{"term":"VPC Network Peering","definition":"A connection between two VPC networks for private traffic; it is non-transitive and has per-network limits.","exam_context":"Selected for VPC-per-department connectivity only relative to the listed options; landing-zone guidance often favors Shared VPC or hub-and-spoke.","lesson":"d1l10","domain":1},{"term":"Workload Identity Federation for GKE","definition":"A GKE feature that gives each Kubernetes ServiceAccount its own identity with short-lived credentials to call Google APIs, with no keys.","exam_context":"Pods → Google APIs, avoid long-lived credentials, per-workload permissions → Workload Identity, not a node SA or a JSON key in a Secret.","lesson":"d1l3","domain":1},{"term":"Workstation configuration","definition":"A Cloud Workstations template defining image, IDE, machine type and storage, shared with a team; changes apply on next start.","exam_context":"Standardized team environment with ongoing patching → one configuration per team.","lesson":"d1l11","domain":1}]},{"n":2,"title":"Building and Implementing CI/CD Pipelines","short":"CI/CD Pipelines","weight":"~23%","desc":"Cloud Build, Cloud Deploy, Artifact Registry, deployment strategies, secrets and software supply-chain security.","lessons":[{"id":"d2l1","num":1,"title":"Cloud Build: triggers, config, performance","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Trigger event types</strong> you need to tell apart:</li>\n<li><strong>Push to a branch</strong>: runs automatically when a commit lands on the matching branch, such as <code>main</code>. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a></li>\n<li><strong>Push new tag</strong>: takes a regex on the tag name, so <code>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+</code> matches only semver release tags. <code>.*</code> would also match dev tags. The tag has to be <em>pushed</em> to GitHub, because a local tag does not fire the trigger. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q1\">Exam 3 · Q1</a></li>\n<li><strong>Pull request</strong>: runs on every PR open or update, and the result shows on the PR before reviewers approve. That makes it the gate for \"test every change before it is accepted\". <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a></li>\n<li><strong>Included files filter</strong>: limits which changed files fire the trigger (monorepo or per-module scoping). It has <strong>nothing to do with approval</strong>. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a></li>\n<li><strong>Trigger approval</strong>: the build is <em>submitted</em> automatically but stays pending until a principal with <code>roles/cloudbuild.builds.approver</code> approves it. <code>roles/cloudbuild.builds.editor</code> does <strong>not</strong> include the approve permission. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q38\">Exam 3 · Q38</a></li>\n<li><strong>Substitutions</strong>: <code>$TAG_NAME</code> holds the Git tag on trigger-invoked builds, so you can put it in the image name. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q35\">Exam 1 · Q35</a></li>\n<li><strong>Parallel steps (<code>waitFor</code>)</strong>: a step with no <code>waitFor</code> waits for <em>all previous steps</em>. To run steps concurrently, give <strong>every</strong> independent step <code>waitFor: ['-']</code>, and give the dependent step <code>waitFor: [foo, bar]</code>. Putting <code>['-']</code> on the first step only changes nothing. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q2\">Exam 4 · Q2</a></li>\n<li><strong>Build notifications</strong>: Cloud Build publishes every build state change to the <strong><code>cloud-builds</code> Pub/Sub topic</strong>. A <strong>push subscription</strong> POSTs those messages to a webhook with no code. (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q49\">Exam 1 · Q49</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q2\">Exam 5 · Q2</a> ❌)</li>\n<li><strong>Speeding up builds</strong>:</li>\n<li>Docker builds: pull the previous image from Artifact Registry and pass it with <code>--cache-from</code>. Put frequently changing files (app source) <strong>late</strong> in the Dockerfile and stable inputs (dependency manifests) early. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q60\">Exam 3 · Q60</a></li>\n<li>Other intermediate artifacts: cache them in <strong>Cloud Storage</strong> between builds. It is cheap and low effort. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q54\">Exam 2 · Q54</a></li>\n<li><strong>Private pools</strong>:</li>\n<li>Use a private pool <strong>connected to your VPC</strong> when tests must reach private APIs and the traffic must stay private. Creating the pool alone does <em>not</em> connect it to your VPC. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q14\">Exam 3 · Q14</a></li>\n<li>Use a private pool plus a dedicated service account for schema migrations against a private-IP AlloyDB. Watch for <strong>non-transitive VPC peering</strong>. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q16\">Exam 3 · Q16</a></li>\n<li>Use private pools with <strong>VPC Service Controls</strong> to prevent data exfiltration and keep builds off public IPs at scale. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q61\">Exam 5 · Q61</a></li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Build is for builds, not recurring batch jobs.</strong> A scheduled decrypt-and-load job belongs in a <strong>Cloud Run job</strong> with a scheduler. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q48\">Exam 3 · Q48</a></li>\n<li><strong>The pipeline broke right after a YAML change that is in Git</strong>: <code>git diff</code> the previous and current config. Do not abandon CI, switch registries, or point Error Reporting at a YAML file, because Error Reporting only handles app exceptions. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q15\">Exam 1 · Q15</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>If the question says…</th>\n<th>Choose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Build automatically on main, and a change-control team must approve pushes to main\"</td>\n<td>Push-to-branch trigger on <code>main</code> <strong>plus GitHub branch protection</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Every new tag builds automatically, but execution waits for group X\"</td>\n<td>Tag trigger with <strong>required approval</strong>, give group X <code>cloudbuild.builds.approver</code>, give the build SA <code>artifactregistry.writer</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q38\">Exam 3 · Q38</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Apply Terraform when a release (semver tag) is ready\"</td>\n<td><strong>Tag</strong> trigger with regex <code>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q1\">Exam 3 · Q1</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Test every change before it's accepted / feature branches continuously\"</td>\n<td><strong>PR trigger</strong>, scoped to the module folder <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Send build info to a 3rd-party webhook, minimal effort\"</td>\n<td>Pub/Sub <strong>push subscription on <code>cloud-builds</code></strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q49\">Exam 1 · Q49</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q2\">Exam 5 · Q2</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Faster builds, low cost/effort\"</td>\n<td><strong>Caching</strong> (<code>--cache-from</code> for Docker, GCS for other artifacts), not a bigger machine <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q54\">Exam 2 · Q54</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q60\">Exam 3 · Q60</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Two independent steps should run concurrently\"</td>\n<td><code>waitFor: ['-']</code> on <strong>both</strong>, and the dependent step waits on their ids <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q2\">Exam 4 · Q2</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Tests must reach private VPC APIs privately, low ops\"</td>\n<td><strong>Private pool</strong> peered to the VPC <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q14\">Exam 3 · Q14</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>\"Approve\" in the stem does not always mean Cloud Build approval.</strong> If the approval is about <em>pushes to a branch</em>, it belongs to source control (branch protection). Cloud Build approval adds a manual step to <em>every build</em>. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a></li>\n<li><strong>A bigger <code>machineType</code> is the speed trap.</strong> It costs much more per minute and does not parallelize sequential steps. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q54\">Exam 2 · Q54</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q2\">Exam 4 · Q2</a></li>\n<li><strong>Splitting work into more steps does not reduce total time,</strong> because steps run sequentially by default. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q54\">Exam 2 · Q54</a></li>\n<li><strong>A \"final step that POSTs to the webhook\" still means writing custom code,</strong> and it never runs for failed builds. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q49\">Exam 1 · Q49</a></li>\n<li><strong>Branch-push triggers with a semver regex match <em>branch</em> names, not tags.</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q1\">Exam 3 · Q1</a></li>\n</ul>","summary":"Cloud Build is the managed CI engine at the center of most Domain 2 questions. This lesson covers which trigger type fits which requirement, how trigger approval differs from source-control approval, how to run steps in parallel, how to get build notifications, and how to make builds faster or reach private networks.","objectives":["After this lesson you can choose between push-to-branch, push-new-tag, and pull request triggers based on the wording of a requirement.","After this lesson you can tell Cloud Build trigger approval apart from GitHub branch protection and name the role that can approve builds.","After this lesson you can configure waitFor so that independent build steps run concurrently.","After this lesson you can choose caching, private pools, or Pub/Sub notifications to meet speed, network, or integration requirements."],"takeaways":["Release on a semver tag means a push-new-tag trigger with a strict semver regex, and the tag must be pushed to the remote.","Auto-submit but wait for a human means trigger approval plus roles/cloudbuild.builds.approver; builds.editor cannot approve.","Approval of pushes or merges to main belongs to GitHub branch protection, not to Cloud Build approval.","Parallel steps need waitFor: ['-'] on every independent step; the default is to wait for all previous steps.","Faster builds come from caching (--cache-from for Docker, Cloud Storage for other artifacts), not from a bigger machineType."],"questions":["e1q15","e1q35","e1q49","e2q2","e2q54","e3q1","e3q14","e3q16","e3q38","e3q48","e3q60","e4q2","e4q32","e5q2","e5q61"]},{"id":"d2l2","num":2,"title":"Pipeline identity and IAM","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>kubectl</code> builder deploying to GKE</strong>: grant <code>roles/container.developer</code> to the <strong>existing Cloud Build service account</strong>. You cannot \"specify a role in <code>cloudbuild.yaml</code>\", and you don't need a new SA or a credential-fetch step. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q19\">Exam 1 · Q19</a></li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Deploy access to one pipeline only</strong>: apply a policy file binding <code>roles/clouddeploy.developer</code> <strong>on that delivery pipeline</strong> (<code>gcloud deploy delivery-pipelines set-iam-policy</code>). Don't grant broadly and then deny, don't use <code>automations.*</code> permissions, and remember that a <em>target</em> is not a <em>pipeline</em>. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q6\">Exam 3 · Q6</a></li>\n<li><strong>Terraform state bucket 403 during <code>init</code></strong>: grant <code>roles/storage.objectAdmin</code> <strong>on that bucket only</strong>. Not project Owner, not local state, and don't create a new bucket, because a 403 means access was denied, not that the bucket is missing. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q24\">Exam 4 · Q24</a></li>\n<li><strong>Terraform running on Jenkins on a GCE VM</strong>: use the VM's <strong>attached service account</strong> through ADC and the metadata server. No JSON key in <code>GOOGLE_CREDENTIALS</code>, and no <code>gcloud auth application-default login</code>, which is for humans. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q49\">Exam 4 · Q49</a></li>\n<li><strong>Pipeline component in GKE that signs with a KMS key</strong>: use <strong>Workload Identity</strong>, not an SA JSON key stored as a Kubernetes Secret. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q30\">Exam 4 · Q30</a></li>\n<li><strong>\"No static credentials and no federation with external IdPs\"</strong>: use Cloud Build's own SA. That rules out GitHub Actions with an SA key, an SSH deploy key, <em>and</em> Workload Identity Federation for GitHub. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q38\">Exam 3 · Q38</a></li>\n<li><strong>AlloyDB migration</strong>: use a dedicated SA registered as an IAM database user, with the needed PostgreSQL grants. An IAM role alone does not grant DB privileges. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q16\">Exam 3 · Q16</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>On Google Cloud:</strong> use the workload's attached or managed identity, scoped to the exact resource, with a role that matches the work.</li>\n<li><strong>Least privilege:</strong> a <strong>resource-scoped</strong> role always beats <code>roles/owner</code>, even though Owner \"works\". <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q24\">Exam 4 · Q24</a></li>\n<li><strong>\"Keyless\" can still mean federation.</strong> Read the constraints carefully. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q38\">Exam 3 · Q38</a></li>\n</ul>","summary":"Pipelines need identities, and the exam rewards the identity that is attached, keyless, and scoped to the exact resource. This lesson covers which service account and role to use for GKE deploys, Cloud Deploy access, Terraform state, Jenkins on Compute Engine, and KMS signing from GKE.","objectives":["After this lesson you can grant the Cloud Build service account the right role to deploy to GKE with kubectl.","After this lesson you can scope Cloud Deploy and Cloud Storage permissions to a single pipeline or bucket.","After this lesson you can choose between an attached service account, Workload Identity, and Workload Identity Federation based on the constraints in a question.","After this lesson you can explain why an IAM role alone does not grant database privileges for an AlloyDB migration."],"takeaways":["kubectl deploys from Cloud Build: grant roles/container.developer to the existing Cloud Build service account.","A resource-scoped role, such as roles/storage.objectAdmin on the state bucket, always beats roles/owner.","Workloads on a GCE VM use the attached service account through ADC; never a JSON key or user ADC login.","A GKE workload that signs with a KMS key uses Workload Identity, not an SA key stored as a Kubernetes Secret.","\"No static credentials and no external IdP federation\" rules out Workload Identity Federation and points to Cloud Build's own service account."],"questions":["e1q19","e3q6","e3q16","e3q38","e4q24","e4q30","e4q49"]},{"id":"d2l3","num":3,"title":"Pipeline design, testing stages and delivery process","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Canonical container pipeline for trunk-based development</strong>:\n  1. Cloud Build runs <strong>unit tests on push</strong>.\n  2. Build the image <strong>once</strong> and push it to a central registry.\n  3. Deploy <em>that</em> image to a test environment and run <strong>integration and acceptance tests</strong>.\n  4. Deploy to prod and run <strong>smoke tests</strong>. (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q21\">Exam 2 · Q21</a> ❌, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q25\">Exam 5 · Q25</a>)</li>\n<li><strong>Git hooks are not a pipeline gate.</strong> Client-side hooks aren't cloned and can be bypassed. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q21\">Exam 2 · Q21</a></li>\n<li><strong>Rebuilding for prod after the tests</strong> means the artifact you ship is not the one you tested. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q21\">Exam 2 · Q21</a></li>\n<li><strong>Shortening MTTR</strong> (choose two): <strong>blue/green</strong> through a CD server (rollback becomes a traffic switch) plus a <strong>CI server running unit tests on every commit</strong>. Local integration tests are not enforced. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q42\">Exam 1 · Q42</a></li>\n<li><strong>Replacing a CAB without hurting delivery</strong> (DORA): <strong>peer review at check-in backed by automated tests</strong> plus <strong>fast feedback</strong> to developers. A senior manager approver, self-merge without review, or bigger batches all lose. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q28\">Exam 2 · Q28</a></li>\n<li><strong>A runtime config failure</strong> (missing env var) needs an <strong>integration test that starts the container with its real config</strong>. Canary, static analysis and audit logs don't catch it. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q17\">Exam 4 · Q17</a></li>\n<li><strong>Concurrent feature branches breaking a shared Dev cluster</strong>: build per feature branch and deploy into a <strong>dynamic namespace per branch</strong> (Config Sync) in the autoscaling Dev cluster, then delete it afterwards. A cluster per branch is costlier. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q8\">Exam 4 · Q8</a></li>\n<li><strong>Reducing toil and duration in Cloud Deploy</strong> (choose two):</li>\n<li><strong>Automate the low-risk dev→test promotion approval.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Notify the team the moment a manual step is reached.</strong></li>\n<li>Not: scripting pipeline creation (a one-time task), more engineers, or smaller steps. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q51\">Exam 4 · Q51</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q31\">Exam 5 · Q31</a></li>\n<li><strong>API versioning lifecycle</strong>: introduce the new version, announce deprecation, contact the remaining users, give best-effort support, then deactivate. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q50\">Exam 5 · Q50</a></li>\n<li><strong>Unreliable synchronous dependency with a tight SLO</strong>: a <strong>circuit breaker plus fallback</strong> (store the data in a DB). Retries eat the latency budget, and a bigger queue just adds latency. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q8\">Exam 3 · Q8</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Acceptance tests <em>after</em> the production deploy</strong> make customers the test audience. Option C in <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q21\">Exam 2 · Q21</a> differs from D only in that last step.</li>\n<li><strong>Rollback capability is recovery, not an approval process.</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q28\">Exam 2 · Q28</a></li>\n<li><strong>Post-merge tests can't gate acceptance.</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a></li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers the canonical order of stages in a container pipeline and the process choices that the exam ties to DORA research. It also covers how to cut MTTR and toil, how to replace a change advisory board, and how to handle feature branches, API deprecation, and unreliable dependencies.","objectives":["After this lesson you can order the stages of a trunk-based container pipeline, from unit tests to production smoke tests.","After this lesson you can explain why you build an image once and promote the same artifact through environments.","After this lesson you can choose process changes that shorten MTTR and replace a CAB without slowing delivery.","After this lesson you can choose the right test type or resilience pattern for a given failure mode."],"takeaways":["Pipeline order: unit tests on push, build once, push to the registry, integration and acceptance tests in a test environment, then prod with smoke tests.","Git hooks are not a pipeline gate, and rebuilding for prod means you ship an untested artifact.","Replace a CAB with peer review at check-in backed by automated tests, plus fast feedback to developers.","Reduce Cloud Deploy toil by auto-approving the low-risk dev to test promotion and notifying the team when a manual step is reached.","Use a circuit breaker with a fallback for an unreliable synchronous dependency under a tight SLO; retries eat the latency budget."],"questions":["e1q42","e2q21","e2q28","e3q8","e4q8","e4q17","e4q32","e4q51","e5q25","e5q31","e5q50"]},{"id":"d2l4","num":4,"title":"Cloud Deploy","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>What it does</strong>: models a <strong>delivery pipeline</strong> across ordered <strong>targets</strong> and manages releases and rollouts. A <strong>rollback</strong> is a new rollout of a previously successful release. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q20\">Exam 3 · Q20</a></li>\n<li><strong>Manual QA before any prod traffic, no automated tests</strong>: <strong>two stages (QA, then Prod) with the standard strategy</strong>, and <code>requireApproval: true</code> on the prod target. The approval is what enforces \"QA done\". <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q12\">Exam 3 · Q12</a></li>\n<li><strong>Risky release with automatic rollback</strong>: a <strong>canary strategy</strong> with phases (for example 5%, then more), <strong>verification / deploy analysis on Cloud Monitoring telemetry</strong>, and an automation rule that <strong>rolls back on failed verification</strong>. \"Automatic\" means no human runs <code>rollout undo</code>. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q35\">Exam 3 · Q35</a></li>\n<li><strong>Per-environment differences with otherwise identical manifests</strong> (2 vs 5 replicas): in <code>skaffold.yaml</code>, use <strong>multiple profiles (one per target)</strong>, each pointing at a <strong>Kustomize overlay</strong> over a shared base. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q47\">Exam 3 · Q47</a></li>\n<li><strong>Canary on GKE through a CD pipeline</strong> is the answer when load testing was incomplete. Cloud Deploy manages the Deployment and Service during the canary. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a></li>\n<li><strong>Retention</strong>: use <strong>Artifact Registry cleanup policies</strong> (with dry run), not a scheduled Cloud Build delete job or manual deletion. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q20\">Exam 3 · Q20</a></li>\n<li><strong>Not for VM images across clouds</strong>: for custom Compute Engine images in a multi-cloud setup, use <strong>Cloud Build + Packer</strong>. Cloud Deploy and kpt are Kubernetes and serverless oriented. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q26\">Exam 5 · Q26</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision table: Skaffold manifests for per-target variation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q47\">Exam 3 · Q47</a></h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Multiple profiles + <code>manifests.kustomize</code></td>\n<td>✅ Shared base, tiny overlays, one profile per target</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Single profile + kustomize</td>\n<td>❌ Only one overlay can be active</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multiple profiles + <code>rawYaml</code></td>\n<td>⚠️ Works, but duplicates full manifests</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Single profile + <code>rawYaml</code></td>\n<td>❌ No variation possible</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Canary is not a substitute for a preceding QA environment.</strong> It still exposes production users. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q12\">Exam 3 · Q12</a></li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Deploy does not create the missing tests.</strong> You supply the verification. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q20\">Exam 3 · Q20</a></li>\n<li><strong>A dedicated rollback pipeline in Cloud Build</strong> means hand-rolling what Cloud Deploy provides. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q20\">Exam 3 · Q20</a></li>\n</ul>","summary":"Cloud Deploy is Google Cloud's managed continuous delivery service for GKE and serverless targets. This lesson covers delivery pipelines, targets, releases, rollouts and rollbacks, how to enforce manual QA with approvals, how to automate canary rollback, and how to vary manifests per environment with Skaffold profiles and Kustomize.","objectives":["After this lesson you can model a delivery pipeline as ordered targets and explain releases, rollouts and rollbacks.","After this lesson you can enforce a manual QA gate with requireApproval on the production target.","After this lesson you can design a canary rollout that rolls back automatically on failed verification.","After this lesson you can use Skaffold profiles with Kustomize overlays to handle per-target differences."],"takeaways":["Manual QA before prod: two stages (QA then Prod) with the standard strategy and requireApproval: true on prod.","Automatic rollback: canary phases plus verification on Cloud Monitoring telemetry plus an automation rule that rolls back on failure.","Per-environment differences: one Skaffold profile per target, each pointing at a Kustomize overlay over a shared base.","A rollback is a new rollout of a previously successful release; don't hand-build a rollback pipeline in Cloud Build.","Cloud Deploy is not for multi-cloud VM images; use Cloud Build with Packer for those."],"questions":["e2q19","e3q12","e3q20","e3q35","e3q47","e5q26"]},{"id":"d2l5","num":5,"title":"Artifact Registry and image references","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Artifact Registry is the managed registry.</strong> Container Registry is <strong>retired</strong> and <strong>never stored Helm charts</strong>. Artifact Registry Docker repositories are OCI, so they hold images <em>and</em> Helm 3.8+ charts, with native GKE, Cloud Run, IAM and VPC-SC integration. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q12\">Exam 4 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q10\">Exam 1 · Q10</a></li>\n<li><strong>Repository modes</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q34\">Exam 3 · Q34</a>:</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Mode</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n<th>Caches upstream?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard</td>\n<td>You push artifacts</td>\n<td>No, uploads are manual</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Remote</strong></td>\n<td>Proxies an upstream (Docker Hub and so on) and caches on first pull</td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Virtual</strong></td>\n<td>One endpoint fronting several repos (routing only)</td>\n<td>No, not by itself</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>\"Cache every dependency + single endpoint + low effort\" → <strong>remote repos fronted by one virtual repo</strong>.\n- <strong>Location matters for pulls</strong>: <code>gcr.io</code> and <code>us.gcr.io</code> map to the US multi-region, <code>eu.gcr.io</code> to Europe, and <code>asia.gcr.io</code> to Asia. Store images <strong>near the cluster that pulls them</strong> (push once, pull often). <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q58\">Exam 2 · Q58</a>\n- <strong>Tags vs digests</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Tag</th>\n<th>Digest (<code>sha256:…</code>)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Mutable?</td>\n<td>Yes, it can be moved</td>\n<td>No, content-derived</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use it to…</td>\n<td><strong>Select a release version</strong> (put <code>$TAG_NAME</code> in the image name) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q35\">Exam 1 · Q35</a></td>\n<td><strong>Pin or roll back to exact bytes</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q37\">Exam 4 · Q37</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gotcha</td>\n<td>Re-pointing <code>latest</code> doesn't change the Pod spec, so no rollout happens <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q37\">Exam 4 · Q37</a></td>\n<td>Known only after the push, so you can't match it to a Git tag <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q35\">Exam 1 · Q35</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Roll back to a known-good image</strong> by referencing its <strong>digest</strong>. Rebuilding from an old Git tag gives a <em>different</em> artifact. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q37\">Exam 4 · Q37</a></li>\n<li><strong>Registry events</strong>: Artifact Registry publishes to the <strong><code>gcr</code> Pub/Sub topic</strong> on upload, tag and delete. A deploy tool's <strong>native Pub/Sub trigger</strong> (Spinnaker) is the low-effort \"deploy on new image\". <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q43\">Exam 1 · Q43</a></li>\n<li><strong>Who pushed an image?</strong> Enable Artifact Registry <strong>Data Write audit logs</strong> and look at <code>Docker-PutManifest</code> and the caller identity. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q15\">Exam 3 · Q15</a></li>\n</ul>","summary":"Artifact Registry is the managed home for container images, Helm charts and language packages, and Container Registry is retired. This lesson covers repository modes, registry location, tags versus digests, registry Pub/Sub events, retention, and audit logging of pushes.","objectives":["After this lesson you can choose between standard, remote and virtual Artifact Registry repositories.","After this lesson you can decide when to reference an image by tag and when by digest.","After this lesson you can trigger deployments from registry events on the gcr Pub/Sub topic.","After this lesson you can find out who pushed an image using Data Write audit logs."],"takeaways":["Cache upstream dependencies through one endpoint with remote repositories fronted by a virtual repository.","Pin or roll back to exact bytes with a digest; select a release version with a tag such as $TAG_NAME.","Re-pointing latest does not change the Pod spec, so no rollout happens.","Store images in a registry location near the cluster that pulls them: push once, pull often.","Retention means Artifact Registry cleanup policies with a dry run, not a scheduled delete job."],"questions":["e1q10","e1q35","e1q43","e2q58","e3q15","e3q34","e4q12","e4q37"]},{"id":"d2l6","num":6,"title":"Deployment strategies","html":"<h3>Comparison table</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Strategy</th>\n<th>Real users exposed?</th>\n<th>Rollback</th>\n<th>Use it when the stem says…</th>\n<th>Cite</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Recreate</strong></td>\n<td>All, with downtime</td>\n<td>Slow redeploy</td>\n<td>(almost never the answer)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q22\">Exam 2 · Q22</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Rolling update</strong></td>\n<td>Gradually all</td>\n<td>Roll back through the fleet (<code>kubectl rollout undo</code>), mixed versions</td>\n<td>Default K8s, no special need</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q22\">Exam 2 · Q22</a>, Q30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Partitioned rolling update</strong> (StatefulSet <code>partition</code>)</td>\n<td>Pods with ordinal ≥ partition</td>\n<td>Raise or lower the partition</td>\n<td>\"Phased rollout to <strong>half the pods</strong>\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q39\">Exam 2 · Q39</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q19\">Exam 5 · Q19</a> ❌</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Blue/green</strong></td>\n<td>All, at cutover</td>\n<td><strong>Instant</strong>: switch traffic back, the old version is still running</td>\n<td>\"<strong>Instant</strong> rollback\", \"simplify releases\", \"shortest rollback\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q22\">Exam 2 · Q22</a>, Q30, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q42\">Exam 1 · Q42</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Canary</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Small %</strong>, growing on evidence</td>\n<td>Shift traffic back</td>\n<td>\"Not fully load-tested\", \"progressively widen exposure\", \"limit blast radius\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a>, Q22, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q58\">Exam 5 · Q58</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>A/B testing</strong></td>\n<td>A defined segment, by condition</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Comparing variants for user segments (experimentation)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q22\">Exam 2 · Q22</a>, Q30</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Shadow</strong></td>\n<td><strong>None</strong>, a mirrored copy of full traffic</td>\n<td>N/A</td>\n<td>\"<strong>Full production load before launch</strong>\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q21\">Exam 4 · Q21</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Key facts and rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blue/green answers <em>how fast you can undo</em>; canary answers <em>how many users you risk</em>.</strong> You can combine them, for example blue/green deployment with canary testing. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q22\">Exam 2 · Q22</a></li>\n<li><strong>Blue/green on GKE</strong> = two Deployments plus one Service.</li>\n<li><strong>Rollback = change the Service selector back</strong> to the old version (for example <code>version: blue</code>). This also keeps the failing Deployment intact for debugging.</li>\n<li>Not <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> (that's for rolling updates), not scaling green to zero, and not deleting the image. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q59\">Exam 4 · Q59</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q29\">Exam 5 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q52\">Exam 5 · Q52</a></li>\n<li><strong>Canary analysis baseline</strong> (Spinnaker/Kayenta): compare against a <strong>freshly created deployment of the <em>current</em> production version</strong>, started at the same time. Long-running prod has warm caches, which skews the comparison. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q45\">Exam 1 · Q45</a></li>\n<li><strong>Header- or attribute-based routing plus arbitrary % splits</strong> (Android user-agent → Android microservice): <strong>GKE + Cloud Service Mesh VirtualService</strong> (headers decide <em>who</em>, weights decide <em>how many</em>). (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q21\">Exam 5 · Q21</a> ❌)</li>\n<li>Cloud Run splits only by revision %.</li>\n<li>App Engine splits by IP or cookie.</li>\n<li><strong>Urgent security patch on a global GCE fleet</strong>: a <strong>short canary in the MIG updater, then a full rollout</strong>. All-at-once is too risky, overnight regional rolls are too slow, and duplicating the fleet costs too much. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q58\">Exam 5 · Q58</a></li>\n<li><strong>Validate MIG changes without losing capacity</strong>: set autoscaling to <strong>\"Only scale out\"</strong> during the change. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q56\">Exam 5 · Q56</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>\"Instantly revert\" means blue/green, not canary.</strong> Canary is about gradual exposure. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q30\">Exam 2 · Q30</a></li>\n<li><strong>Blue/green that switches <em>all</em> traffic and only then watches metrics</strong> exposes everyone. Untested load means canary. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a></li>\n<li><strong>A rolling update is not a canary.</strong> It does not hold traffic back based on metrics. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a></li>\n<li><strong>Pod management policy <code>Parallel</code></strong> controls scaling order, not updates. A <strong>ReplicaSet</strong> can't stop at 50%. <strong>NoExecute taints</strong> evict Pods. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q39\">Exam 2 · Q39</a></li>\n<li><strong>Plain Service selectors can't split traffic by percentage.</strong> \"Gradually move traffic via the selector\" is wrong. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q35\">Exam 3 · Q35</a></li>\n</ul>","summary":"Deployment strategy questions turn on a few keywords: instant rollback, limited blast radius, full load with no users exposed, or half the pods. This lesson compares recreate, rolling, partitioned rolling, blue/green, canary, A/B and shadow deployments and shows how each is implemented on GKE, Compute Engine and with a service mesh.","objectives":["After this lesson you can map requirement wording to the right deployment strategy.","After this lesson you can roll back a blue/green deployment on GKE by switching the Service selector.","After this lesson you can choose the right platform for header-based routing combined with percentage splits.","After this lesson you can roll out an urgent patch to a MIG fleet safely and quickly."],"takeaways":["Instant rollback means blue/green; limiting how many users you risk means canary.","Blue/green rollback on GKE is changing the Service selector back, not kubectl rollout undo.","Full production load with zero users exposed means a shadow deployment.","A phased rollout to half the pods means a StatefulSet rolling update partition.","Header-based routing plus arbitrary % splits means GKE with a Cloud Service Mesh VirtualService."],"questions":["e1q42","e1q45","e2q19","e2q22","e2q30","e2q36","e2q39","e3q35","e4q21","e4q59","e5q19","e5q21","e5q29","e5q52","e5q56","e5q58"]},{"id":"d2l7","num":7,"title":"Cloud Run revisions and traffic","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Goal</th>\n<th>Command or pattern</th>\n<th>Cite</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Developers/QA test a revision <strong>before any customer traffic</strong></td>\n<td><code>gcloud run deploy SVC --no-traffic --tag dev</code>, then test at <code>https://dev---SVC-xxx.a.run.app</code> (three hyphens)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q5\">Exam 2 · Q5</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Test thoroughly in staging <em>and</em> prod with minimal consumer impact</td>\n<td>Tagged revision with no traffic, test it, then shift traffic progressively. Repeat in prod</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q29\">Exam 2 · Q29</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Canary to ~10% of clients, least effort</td>\n<td>Deploy with no traffic, then <strong>traffic split 90/10 between revisions of the same service</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q4\">Exam 4 · Q4</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Live-traffic test <strong>and</strong> manual QA, with rollback (choose two)</td>\n<td><strong>Tagged <code>--no-traffic</code> revision</strong> (QA URL) + <strong>traffic split</strong> (live %). The old revision stays deployed, so rollback is moving traffic back</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q36\">Exam 4 · Q36</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>--to-revisions LATEST=1</code></strong> sends 1% of <em>customers</em> to an untested revision. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q5\">Exam 2 · Q5</a></li>\n<li><strong>Identity tokens and <code>run.invoker</code> don't change routing,</strong> and there is no <code>.private.run.app</code> URL. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q5\">Exam 2 · Q5</a></li>\n<li><strong>A tag with zero traffic is not private access.</strong> The tagged URL is public if the service is public. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q5\">Exam 2 · Q5</a></li>\n<li><strong>10% of requests ≠ 10% of distinct clients.</strong> Session affinity is best effort. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q4\">Exam 4 · Q4</a></li>\n<li><strong>A separate service plus a load balancer or app-level forwarding</strong> is extra work for no gain. (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q4\">Exam 4 · Q4</a>, Q36)</li>\n</ul>","summary":"Cloud Run gives you revisions, revision tags and traffic splitting, which together cover most testing and canary scenarios with very little effort. This lesson covers deploying with no traffic, testing at a tagged URL, splitting traffic between revisions, and rolling back by moving traffic.","objectives":["After this lesson you can deploy a Cloud Run revision with --no-traffic and a tag, then test it at its tag URL.","After this lesson you can run a canary by splitting traffic between revisions of the same service.","After this lesson you can roll back a Cloud Run release by moving traffic back to the previous revision."],"takeaways":["QA before any customer traffic: gcloud run deploy --no-traffic --tag, then test at the tag---service URL.","Canary with least effort: a traffic split such as 90/10 between revisions of the same service.","--to-revisions LATEST=1 sends real customers to an untested revision.","A tagged revision with zero traffic is not private; its URL is public if the service is public.","10% of requests is not 10% of distinct clients, because session affinity is best effort."],"questions":["e2q5","e2q29","e4q4","e4q36"]},{"id":"d2l8","num":8,"title":"Secrets management","html":"<h3>Cloud KMS vs Secret Manager</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th><strong>Secret Manager</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Cloud KMS</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Stores</td>\n<td>Secret <em>values</em> (API keys, passwords, certs) with versions</td>\n<td>Cryptographic <em>keys</em> (encrypt, decrypt, sign)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rotation of a secret</td>\n<td>Add a new version, with no config to touch</td>\n<td>You must re-encrypt and re-embed the ciphertext</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use for</td>\n<td>Cloud Build <code>availableSecrets.secretManager</code>, Cloud Run secrets</td>\n<td>Signing keys for attestations <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q30\">Exam 4 · Q30</a>, imported decryption keys <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q48\">Exam 3 · Q48</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mnemonic</td>\n<td>\"An API key is a secret to <em>store</em>\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q60\">Exam 4 · Q60</a></td>\n<td>\"A key to <em>manage</em>\"</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Cloud Build secrets: evolution seen in the bank</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Legacy</strong>: Cloud Storage + KMS-encrypted secrets, pipeline granted access via IAM. This was the answer when no Secret Manager option existed. (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q9\">Exam 5 · Q9</a>; see ⚠️)</li>\n<li><strong>KMS ciphertext in the build config</strong>: <code>availableSecrets.inline</code> plus <code>secretEnv</code>, with the build SA granted <code>cloudkms.cryptoKeyDecrypter</code>. The top-level <code>secrets</code> field is the older legacy form. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q45\">Exam 2 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q46\">Exam 3 · Q46</a></li>\n<li><strong>Recommended</strong>: <strong>Secret Manager</strong>. Grant <code>roles/secretmanager.secretAccessor</code> to the build SA and reference the secret under <strong><code>availableSecrets.secretManager</code></strong>. Rotation needs no config change. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q46\">Exam 3 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q65\">Exam 5 · Q65</a></li>\n</ol>\n<h3>Cloud Run: env var vs volume</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Delivery</th>\n<th>When the value is read</th>\n<th>Pick it when…</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Environment variable</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Once, at instance start</strong>. Google recommends pinning a specific version</td>\n<td>Static secret, simplest integration (\"store an API key securely\") <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q60\">Exam 4 · Q60</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q30\">Exam 5 · Q30</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Volume mount (<code>latest</code>)</strong></td>\n<td><strong>On each file read</strong></td>\n<td>Secret <strong>rotates</strong> and the app must always see the current value without redeploying. The app must re-read the file <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Never / traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Never store secrets in Git</strong>, whether plaintext, base64 (\"encoding ≠ encryption\"), or encrypted with the key in another repo. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q46\">Exam 3 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q65\">Exam 5 · Q65</a></li>\n<li><strong>Never store the decryption key in the same bucket as the ciphertext.</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q45\">Exam 2 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q48\">Exam 3 · Q48</a></li>\n<li><strong>Never prompt humans for secrets at build time,</strong> because it breaks automation. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q65\">Exam 5 · Q65</a></li>\n<li><strong>Never bake secrets into images or source.</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a></li>\n<li><strong>Default GCS encryption at rest ≠ a secret store.</strong> Any authorized reader sees plaintext. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q45\">Exam 2 · Q45</a></li>\n<li><strong>The <code>latest</code> alias does not make an env var refresh.</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a></li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Run already decrypts Secret Manager values,</strong> so \"mount, then decrypt in the app\" is wrong. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q60\">Exam 4 · Q60</a></li>\n<li><strong>Build-time secrets don't provide runtime secrets.</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a></li>\n</ul>","summary":"Secret handling in pipelines and at runtime is a frequent source of trap answers. This lesson contrasts Secret Manager with Cloud KMS, traces how Cloud Build secret handling evolved to availableSecrets.secretManager, and explains when Cloud Run should expose a secret as an environment variable or as a volume.","objectives":["After this lesson you can choose between Secret Manager and Cloud KMS for a given requirement.","After this lesson you can give a Cloud Build pipeline access to secrets through availableSecrets.secretManager and secretAccessor.","After this lesson you can decide between an environment variable and a volume mount for a Cloud Run secret.","After this lesson you can recognize insecure secret patterns such as secrets in Git, base64 encoding, or the key next to the ciphertext."],"takeaways":["Build secrets: Secret Manager with availableSecrets.secretManager and roles/secretmanager.secretAccessor on the build service account.","An API key is a secret to store (Secret Manager); KMS manages keys.","A rotating Cloud Run secret that the app must always see is a volume mount of latest; a static key can be an env var.","The latest alias does not make an environment variable refresh; env vars are read once at instance start.","Never put secrets in Git or images, never treat base64 as encryption, and never store the key next to the ciphertext."],"questions":["e1q21","e2q45","e3q28","e3q46","e3q48","e4q28","e4q30","e4q60","e5q9","e5q30","e5q65"]},{"id":"d2l9","num":9,"title":"Supply-chain security: Binary Authorization and Artifact Analysis","html":"<h3>Which control answers which question</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Requirement wording</th>\n<th>Control</th>\n<th>Why not the others</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Only trusted/approved images can be <strong>deployed</strong>\", \"guardrail\", \"can't bypass testing\"</td>\n<td><strong>Binary Authorization</strong> (deploy-time admission)</td>\n<td>— (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q7\">Exam 1 · Q7</a>, Q39, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a>, Q47, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q34\">Exam 5 · Q34</a>, Q43)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Only images <strong>from repo X</strong>\"</td>\n<td>BinAuthz policy with an <strong>allowlist name pattern</strong> (<code>admissionWhitelistPatterns</code>) and default deny</td>\n<td>Custom builder, manifest checks and tags are bypassable or custom <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q10\">Exam 1 · Q10</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Only images <strong>built by our Cloud Build</strong>\", low effort</td>\n<td>Require the <strong><code>built-by-cloud-build</code></strong> attestor, which Cloud Build creates and signs automatically</td>\n<td>Your own KMS keyring and attestor duplicates this <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q41\">Exam 3 · Q41</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Only images that <strong>passed the load test</strong>\" (custom gate)</td>\n<td>Pipeline signs an attestation with a <strong>Cloud KMS</strong> key, authenticated via <strong>Workload Identity</strong></td>\n<td>A human signer breaks automation; an SA JSON key breaks keyless auth <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q30\">Exam 4 · Q30</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"<strong>Trace</strong> a running image to source/build\"</td>\n<td>Cloud Build <strong>signed build provenance</strong> (SLSA L3) stored via Container Analysis API, <strong>verified by BinAuthz</strong></td>\n<td>Scanning ≠ provenance <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q31\">Exam 3 · Q31</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Find/report <strong>CVEs</strong>\", replace VM vuln tools</td>\n<td><strong>Artifact Analysis</strong> (Container Analysis) scanning</td>\n<td>Dockerfile linting doesn't see installed packages <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q50\">Exam 2 · Q50</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q14\">Exam 5 · Q14</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"No critical vulns may <strong>reach the registry</strong>\"</td>\n<td>Cloud Build + <strong>On-Demand Scanning before push</strong>, failing the build on CRITICAL</td>\n<td>Automatic scanning runs <em>after</em> push, too late <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q24\">Exam 3 · Q24</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"<strong>Who</strong> can deploy\"</td>\n<td>IAM / K8s RBAC</td>\n<td>Doesn't check <em>what</em> runs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q7\">Exam 1 · Q7</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q47\">Exam 4 · Q47</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Detect bad behavior in running pods</td>\n<td>Falco / Prisma / Twistlock (runtime)</td>\n<td>That's shift-right, not a guardrail <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q47\">Exam 4 · Q47</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q34\">Exam 5 · Q34</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Binary Authorization is managed and based on the Kritis spec.</strong> Kritis itself is open source and self-operated, so with \"low overhead\" pick BinAuthz. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a></li>\n<li><strong>Supports dry-run, and enforcement events go to Cloud Audit Logs.</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a></li>\n<li><strong>Emergency or debug Pod with an unsigned image</strong>: add the label <strong><code>image-policy.k8s.io/break-glass: \"true\"</code></strong> to that Pod. It is scoped to one Pod and automatically logged.</li>\n<li>Not <code>ALWAYS_ALLOW</code>, not disabling BinAuthz, and not a permanent allowlist entry. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q30\">Exam 3 · Q30</a></li>\n<li><strong>Artifact Analysis behavior</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q15\">Exam 3 · Q15</a>:</li>\n<li>It scans on <strong>push, by digest</strong>. Adding a tag does not rescan.</li>\n<li>Enabling scanning does <strong>not</strong> retroactively scan existing images. Re-push them.</li>\n<li>Continuous analysis updates findings, but images must be pulled within <strong>30 days</strong> to stay fresh.</li>\n<li>Audit logs can't recreate upload records that were never collected.</li>\n<li><strong>Scanning detects; Binary Authorization enforces.</strong> A scan result can become an attestation that BinAuthz then requires. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q39\">Exam 1 · Q39</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q47\">Exam 4 · Q47</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Treating an <em>artifact</em> problem as an <em>access</em> problem</strong> (RBAC, branch protection, a source-IP admission controller). <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q7\">Exam 1 · Q7</a></li>\n<li><strong>Restricting who can push to Artifact Registry</strong> does not control what GKE runs. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q41\">Exam 3 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a></li>\n<li><strong>Web Security Scanner and private clusters</strong> are not image-trust controls. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q39\">Exam 1 · Q39</a></li>\n<li><strong>COS nodes and auto-upgrades harden hosts,</strong> not image provenance. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q31\">Exam 3 · Q31</a></li>\n</ul>","summary":"Supply-chain questions ask which control enforces what can run versus which control only reports. This lesson covers Binary Authorization policies, attestors and break-glass, build provenance, and Artifact Analysis scanning, both automatic and on-demand.","objectives":["After this lesson you can choose Binary Authorization when a question requires that only trusted images can be deployed.","After this lesson you can choose between the built-by-cloud-build attestor, a custom KMS-signed attestation, and an allowlist name pattern.","After this lesson you can explain when Artifact Analysis scans images and why On-Demand Scanning is needed to block vulnerabilities before push.","After this lesson you can deploy an unsigned debug Pod safely with the break-glass label."],"takeaways":["Only trusted or tested images can deploy means Binary Authorization; scanning only reports and IAM only controls who.","Images built by Cloud Build means requiring the built-by-cloud-build attestor; no custom KMS keyring is needed.","A custom gate attestation is signed by the pipeline with a Cloud KMS key, authenticated through Workload Identity.","Block critical vulnerabilities before they reach the registry with On-Demand Scanning in Cloud Build; automatic scanning runs after push.","Automatic scanning works by digest on push: tags don't trigger a rescan, and enabling it doesn't scan existing images."],"questions":["e1q7","e1q10","e1q39","e2q50","e3q15","e3q24","e3q30","e3q31","e3q41","e4q30","e4q44","e4q47","e5q14","e5q34"]},{"id":"d2l10","num":10,"title":"Terraform and IaC in pipelines","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Replacing an immutable instance template that a MIG uses</strong> (error \"already being used by\"): add <strong><code>lifecycle { create_before_destroy = true }</code></strong> on the template, which needs unique names via <code>name_prefix</code>. Terraform then creates the new template, repoints the MIG and deletes the old one in <strong>one apply</strong>. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q55\">Exam 1 · Q55</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q53\">Exam 5 · Q53</a></li>\n<li>Wrong answers: destroying the MIG (disruptive), separate pipeline runs (extra executions), and state rm/import (doesn't remove the blocker).</li>\n<li><code>create_before_destroy</code> does not by itself roll the new machine type out to existing VMs. That depends on the MIG update policy. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q55\">Exam 1 · Q55</a></li>\n<li><strong>State backend 403</strong>: give the build SA bucket-scoped <code>roles/storage.objectAdmin</code>. Keep remote state, which is shared and lockable. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q24\">Exam 4 · Q24</a></li>\n<li><strong>Authentication</strong>: on GCE, use the attached SA through ADC. Avoid SA keys and avoid user ADC login. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q49\">Exam 4 · Q49</a></li>\n<li><strong>Triggering</strong>: a semver <strong>tag</strong> trigger to apply releases <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q1\">Exam 3 · Q1</a>. Run IaC module integration tests on <strong>every PR</strong>, scoped per folder <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Multi-cloud VM images</strong>: use <strong>Packer</strong> orchestrated by Cloud Build. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q26\">Exam 5 · Q26</a></li>\n</ul>","summary":"Terraform shows up in pipelines through state backends, authentication, triggering, and a recurring instance template replacement error. This lesson covers create_before_destroy, remote state permissions, keyless authentication, and how to trigger IaC applies and tests.","objectives":["After this lesson you can fix the instance template \"already being used by\" error with create_before_destroy and name_prefix.","After this lesson you can resolve a 403 on a Terraform state bucket with a bucket-scoped role.","After this lesson you can authenticate Terraform on Compute Engine through the attached service account.","After this lesson you can choose tag and PR triggers for IaC applies and module tests."],"takeaways":["A template in use by a MIG: lifecycle { create_before_destroy = true } with name_prefix, replaced in one apply.","create_before_destroy does not by itself roll a new machine type out to existing VMs; the MIG update policy does.","A state bucket 403 means grant bucket-scoped roles/storage.objectAdmin; keep shared, lockable remote state.","Apply on a semver tag trigger and run IaC module tests on every PR, scoped per folder.","Multi-cloud VM images are built with Packer orchestrated by Cloud Build."],"questions":["e1q55","e3q1","e4q24","e4q32","e4q49","e5q26","e5q53"]},{"id":"d2l11","num":11,"title":"Jenkins, Spinnaker and other third-party tools","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Hosting Jenkins for an app that is entirely on GCP</strong>: use <strong>Compute Engine VMs</strong>. Jenkins needs a persistent controller; Cloud Run functions are time-boxed; on-prem K8s adds a hybrid credential path; workstations are unmanaged. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q52\">Exam 2 · Q52</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q16\">Exam 5 · Q16</a></li>\n<li><strong>Jenkins deployments are slow to <em>start</em> while CPU and memory look normal</strong>: <strong>increase the executors</strong> (execution slots) on the agents, not on the controller. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q57\">Exam 3 · Q57</a></li>\n<li><strong>Build fleet that must avoid public IPs and exfiltration at scale</strong>: use Cloud Build private pools + VPC-SC rather than Jenkins on VMs (managed, autoscaling). Spot VMs can be preempted. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q61\">Exam 5 · Q61</a></li>\n<li><strong>Spinnaker</strong>: a <strong>Pub/Sub trigger</strong> on the <code>gcr</code> topic to deploy on each new image <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q43\">Exam 1 · Q43</a>. <strong>Kayenta</strong> canary analysis against a fresh baseline <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q45\">Exam 1 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Kritis vs Binary Authorization</strong>: same idea, but BinAuthz is managed. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a></li>\n</ul>","summary":"The exam still includes Jenkins and Spinnaker scenarios alongside managed GCP tools. This lesson covers where to host Jenkins, how to fix slow-starting Jenkins jobs, when managed Cloud Build private pools beat a self-hosted fleet, and how Spinnaker integrates with Pub/Sub and canary analysis.","objectives":["After this lesson you can choose Compute Engine as the host for a Jenkins controller serving a GCP-only application.","After this lesson you can diagnose slow Jenkins job starts and fix them by adding executors.","After this lesson you can configure Spinnaker to deploy on new images and run Kayenta canary analysis correctly.","After this lesson you can explain when to prefer managed Cloud Build private pools or Binary Authorization over self-operated tools."],"takeaways":["Jenkins for an app entirely on GCP runs on Compute Engine VMs, because it needs a persistent controller.","Jobs slow to start while CPU and memory look normal: increase executors on the agents.","Spinnaker deploys on each new image through a Pub/Sub trigger on the gcr topic.","Kayenta canary analysis compares against a freshly created baseline of the current production version.","Kritis and Binary Authorization share an idea, but Binary Authorization is managed, so pick it for low overhead."],"questions":["e1q43","e1q45","e2q52","e3q57","e4q44","e5q16","e5q61"]}],"ref":{"cheat":"<ul>\n<li>\"Only trusted/approved/tested images can <strong>deploy</strong>\" → <strong>Binary Authorization</strong>. Scanning only <em>reports</em>, and IAM/RBAC only controls <em>who</em>.</li>\n<li>Images built by Cloud Build → require the <strong><code>built-by-cloud-build</code></strong> attestor. No custom KMS needed.</li>\n<li>Custom gate attestation → the <strong>pipeline</strong> signs with a <strong>Cloud KMS</strong> key via <strong>Workload Identity</strong>. No humans, no JSON keys.</li>\n<li>Only images from repo X → BinAuthz <strong>allowlist name pattern</strong>.</li>\n<li>Debug Pod with an unsigned image → <strong>break-glass label</strong> on that Pod only.</li>\n<li>Trace an image to source/build → <strong>signed build provenance + BinAuthz verification</strong>.</li>\n<li>Block vulns <em>before</em> push → <strong>On-Demand Scanning</strong> in Cloud Build. Automatic scanning runs after push. Enabling it doesn't scan old images, and tags don't trigger rescans.</li>\n<li>Secrets in builds → <strong>Secret Manager</strong> + <code>availableSecrets.secretManager</code> + <code>secretAccessor</code>. Never Git, base64, or the key next to the ciphertext.</li>\n<li>Cloud Run secret that <strong>rotates</strong> → <strong>volume mount</strong> of <code>latest</code>. Static API key → env var is fine.</li>\n<li>API key → <strong>Secret Manager</strong>, not KMS. KMS manages <em>keys</em>.</li>\n<li>Webhook on build events → Pub/Sub <strong>push subscription on <code>cloud-builds</code></strong>. Deploy on new image → Pub/Sub <strong><code>gcr</code></strong> topic.</li>\n<li>Faster builds → <strong>cache</strong> (<code>--cache-from</code> + changing files late, or GCS). Not a bigger machine.</li>\n<li>Parallel steps → <code>waitFor: ['-']</code> on <strong>every</strong> independent step.</li>\n<li>Release on semver → <strong>tag</strong> trigger <code>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+</code>. Test before accept → <strong>PR</strong> trigger. Approval of merges → <strong>GitHub branch protection</strong>.</li>\n<li>Auto-submit but wait for a human → trigger <strong>approval</strong> + <code>cloudbuild.builds.approver</code>.</li>\n<li>Private VPC APIs, private DBs, or no public IPs → Cloud Build <strong>private pool</strong> (+ VPC peering / VPC-SC).</li>\n<li>Pin or roll back exact content → <strong>digest</strong>. Select a version → <strong>tag</strong> in the image name.</li>\n<li>Cache upstream images through one endpoint → <strong>remote repos + virtual repo</strong>. Retention → <strong>cleanup policies</strong>. Helm + images → <strong>Artifact Registry</strong>.</li>\n<li>Push once, pull often → registry <strong>near the cluster</strong> (<code>eu.gcr.io</code> for Europe).</li>\n<li>Instant rollback → <strong>blue/green</strong>. On GKE, rollback = <strong>flip the Service selector</strong>, not <code>rollout undo</code>.</li>\n<li>Unproven under load / widen gradually → <strong>canary</strong>. Full prod load with zero users exposed → <strong>shadow</strong>. Half the pods → StatefulSet <strong>partition</strong>.</li>\n<li>Header-based routing + % split → <strong>GKE + Cloud Service Mesh VirtualService</strong>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Run: QA first → <code>--no-traffic --tag</code> (URL <code>tag---svc</code>). Live % → <strong>traffic split</strong>. Rollback → move traffic back.</li>\n<li>Cloud Deploy: QA then prod → <strong>two stages + <code>requireApproval</code></strong>. Auto rollback → <strong>canary + verification + automation</strong>. Per-env diffs → <strong>profiles + Kustomize overlays</strong>. Access → <code>clouddeploy.developer</code> <strong>on the pipeline</strong>.</li>\n<li>Terraform: template in use → <strong><code>create_before_destroy</code></strong>. State 403 → bucket-scoped <strong><code>storage.objectAdmin</code></strong>. On a GCE VM → <strong>attached SA</strong> (ADC).</li>\n<li>Jenkins for a GCP app → <strong>Compute Engine VM</strong>. Slow to start with idle CPU → <strong>more executors</strong>.</li>\n<li>Pipeline order: unit tests in CI → build once → push → test env (integration + acceptance) → prod + smoke tests. Git hooks are not gates.</li>\n<li>Replace a CAB → <strong>peer review + automated tests + fast feedback</strong>. Reduce Cloud Deploy toil → <strong>auto-approve dev→test + notify on manual steps</strong>.</li>\n</ul>","rules":[{"lesson":"d2l1","title":"Cloud Build: triggers, config, performance","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>If the question says…</th>\n<th>Choose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Build automatically on main, and a change-control team must approve pushes to main\"</td>\n<td>Push-to-branch trigger on <code>main</code> <strong>plus GitHub branch protection</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Every new tag builds automatically, but execution waits for group X\"</td>\n<td>Tag trigger with <strong>required approval</strong>, give group X <code>cloudbuild.builds.approver</code>, give the build SA <code>artifactregistry.writer</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q38\">Exam 3 · Q38</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Apply Terraform when a release (semver tag) is ready\"</td>\n<td><strong>Tag</strong> trigger with regex <code>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q1\">Exam 3 · Q1</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Test every change before it's accepted / feature branches continuously\"</td>\n<td><strong>PR trigger</strong>, scoped to the module folder <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Send build info to a 3rd-party webhook, minimal effort\"</td>\n<td>Pub/Sub <strong>push subscription on <code>cloud-builds</code></strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q49\">Exam 1 · Q49</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q2\">Exam 5 · Q2</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Faster builds, low cost/effort\"</td>\n<td><strong>Caching</strong> (<code>--cache-from</code> for Docker, GCS for other artifacts), not a bigger machine <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q54\">Exam 2 · Q54</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q60\">Exam 3 · Q60</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Two independent steps should run concurrently\"</td>\n<td><code>waitFor: ['-']</code> on <strong>both</strong>, and the dependent step waits on their ids <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q2\">Exam 4 · Q2</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Tests must reach private VPC APIs privately, low ops\"</td>\n<td><strong>Private pool</strong> peered to the VPC <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q14\">Exam 3 · Q14</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"lesson":"d2l2","title":"Pipeline identity and IAM","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>On Google Cloud:</strong> use the workload's attached or managed identity, scoped to the exact resource, with a role that matches the work.</li>\n<li><strong>Least privilege:</strong> a <strong>resource-scoped</strong> role always beats <code>roles/owner</code>, even though Owner \"works\". <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q24\">Exam 4 · Q24</a></li>\n<li><strong>\"Keyless\" can still mean federation.</strong> Read the constraints carefully. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q38\">Exam 3 · Q38</a></li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l4","title":"Cloud Deploy","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Multiple profiles + <code>manifests.kustomize</code></td>\n<td>✅ Shared base, tiny overlays, one profile per target</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Single profile + kustomize</td>\n<td>❌ Only one overlay can be active</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Multiple profiles + <code>rawYaml</code></td>\n<td>⚠️ Works, but duplicates full manifests</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Single profile + <code>rawYaml</code></td>\n<td>❌ No variation possible</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"lesson":"d2l6","title":"Deployment strategies","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Blue/green answers <em>how fast you can undo</em>; canary answers <em>how many users you risk</em>.</strong> You can combine them, for example blue/green deployment with canary testing. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q22\">Exam 2 · Q22</a></li>\n<li><strong>Blue/green on GKE</strong> = two Deployments plus one Service.</li>\n<li><strong>Rollback = change the Service selector back</strong> to the old version (for example <code>version: blue</code>). This also keeps the failing Deployment intact for debugging.</li>\n<li>Not <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> (that's for rolling updates), not scaling green to zero, and not deleting the image. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q59\">Exam 4 · Q59</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q29\">Exam 5 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q52\">Exam 5 · Q52</a></li>\n<li><strong>Canary analysis baseline</strong> (Spinnaker/Kayenta): compare against a <strong>freshly created deployment of the <em>current</em> production version</strong>, started at the same time. Long-running prod has warm caches, which skews the comparison. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q45\">Exam 1 · Q45</a></li>\n<li><strong>Header- or attribute-based routing plus arbitrary % splits</strong> (Android user-agent → Android microservice): <strong>GKE + Cloud Service Mesh VirtualService</strong> (headers decide <em>who</em>, weights decide <em>how many</em>). (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q21\">Exam 5 · Q21</a> ❌)</li>\n<li>Cloud Run splits only by revision %.</li>\n<li>App Engine splits by IP or cookie.</li>\n<li><strong>Urgent security patch on a global GCE fleet</strong>: a <strong>short canary in the MIG updater, then a full rollout</strong>. All-at-once is too risky, overnight regional rolls are too slow, and duplicating the fleet costs too much. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q58\">Exam 5 · Q58</a></li>\n<li><strong>Validate MIG changes without losing capacity</strong>: set autoscaling to <strong>\"Only scale out\"</strong> during the change. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q56\">Exam 5 · Q56</a></li>\n</ul>"}],"traps":[{"lesson":"d2l1","title":"Cloud Build: triggers, config, performance","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>\"Approve\" in the stem does not always mean Cloud Build approval.</strong> If the approval is about <em>pushes to a branch</em>, it belongs to source control (branch protection). Cloud Build approval adds a manual step to <em>every build</em>. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q2\">Exam 2 · Q2</a></li>\n<li><strong>A bigger <code>machineType</code> is the speed trap.</strong> It costs much more per minute and does not parallelize sequential steps. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q54\">Exam 2 · Q54</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q2\">Exam 4 · Q2</a></li>\n<li><strong>Splitting work into more steps does not reduce total time,</strong> because steps run sequentially by default. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q54\">Exam 2 · Q54</a></li>\n<li><strong>A \"final step that POSTs to the webhook\" still means writing custom code,</strong> and it never runs for failed builds. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q49\">Exam 1 · Q49</a></li>\n<li><strong>Branch-push triggers with a semver regex match <em>branch</em> names, not tags.</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q1\">Exam 3 · Q1</a></li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l3","title":"Pipeline design, testing stages and delivery process","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Acceptance tests <em>after</em> the production deploy</strong> make customers the test audience. Option C in <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q21\">Exam 2 · Q21</a> differs from D only in that last step.</li>\n<li><strong>Rollback capability is recovery, not an approval process.</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q28\">Exam 2 · Q28</a></li>\n<li><strong>Post-merge tests can't gate acceptance.</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q32\">Exam 4 · Q32</a></li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l4","title":"Cloud Deploy","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Canary is not a substitute for a preceding QA environment.</strong> It still exposes production users. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q12\">Exam 3 · Q12</a></li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Deploy does not create the missing tests.</strong> You supply the verification. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q20\">Exam 3 · Q20</a></li>\n<li><strong>A dedicated rollback pipeline in Cloud Build</strong> means hand-rolling what Cloud Deploy provides. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q20\">Exam 3 · Q20</a></li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l6","title":"Deployment strategies","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>\"Instantly revert\" means blue/green, not canary.</strong> Canary is about gradual exposure. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q30\">Exam 2 · Q30</a></li>\n<li><strong>Blue/green that switches <em>all</em> traffic and only then watches metrics</strong> exposes everyone. Untested load means canary. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a></li>\n<li><strong>A rolling update is not a canary.</strong> It does not hold traffic back based on metrics. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q19\">Exam 2 · Q19</a></li>\n<li><strong>Pod management policy <code>Parallel</code></strong> controls scaling order, not updates. A <strong>ReplicaSet</strong> can't stop at 50%. <strong>NoExecute taints</strong> evict Pods. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q39\">Exam 2 · Q39</a></li>\n<li><strong>Plain Service selectors can't split traffic by percentage.</strong> \"Gradually move traffic via the selector\" is wrong. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q35\">Exam 3 · Q35</a></li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l7","title":"Cloud Run revisions and traffic","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong><code>--to-revisions LATEST=1</code></strong> sends 1% of <em>customers</em> to an untested revision. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q5\">Exam 2 · Q5</a></li>\n<li><strong>Identity tokens and <code>run.invoker</code> don't change routing,</strong> and there is no <code>.private.run.app</code> URL. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q5\">Exam 2 · Q5</a></li>\n<li><strong>A tag with zero traffic is not private access.</strong> The tagged URL is public if the service is public. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q5\">Exam 2 · Q5</a></li>\n<li><strong>10% of requests ≠ 10% of distinct clients.</strong> Session affinity is best effort. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q4\">Exam 4 · Q4</a></li>\n<li><strong>A separate service plus a load balancer or app-level forwarding</strong> is extra work for no gain. (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q4\">Exam 4 · Q4</a>, Q36)</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l8","title":"Secrets management","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Never store secrets in Git</strong>, whether plaintext, base64 (\"encoding ≠ encryption\"), or encrypted with the key in another repo. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q46\">Exam 3 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q65\">Exam 5 · Q65</a></li>\n<li><strong>Never store the decryption key in the same bucket as the ciphertext.</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q45\">Exam 2 · Q45</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q48\">Exam 3 · Q48</a></li>\n<li><strong>Never prompt humans for secrets at build time,</strong> because it breaks automation. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q65\">Exam 5 · Q65</a></li>\n<li><strong>Never bake secrets into images or source.</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a></li>\n<li><strong>Default GCS encryption at rest ≠ a secret store.</strong> Any authorized reader sees plaintext. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q45\">Exam 2 · Q45</a></li>\n<li><strong>The <code>latest</code> alias does not make an env var refresh.</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a></li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Run already decrypts Secret Manager values,</strong> so \"mount, then decrypt in the app\" is wrong. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q60\">Exam 4 · Q60</a></li>\n<li><strong>Build-time secrets don't provide runtime secrets.</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a></li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d2l9","title":"Supply-chain security: Binary Authorization and Artifact Analysis","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Treating an <em>artifact</em> problem as an <em>access</em> problem</strong> (RBAC, branch protection, a source-IP admission controller). <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q7\">Exam 1 · Q7</a></li>\n<li><strong>Restricting who can push to Artifact Registry</strong> does not control what GKE runs. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q41\">Exam 3 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q44\">Exam 4 · Q44</a></li>\n<li><strong>Web Security Scanner and private clusters</strong> are not image-trust controls. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q39\">Exam 1 · Q39</a></li>\n<li><strong>COS nodes and auto-upgrades harden hosts,</strong> not image provenance. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q31\">Exam 3 · Q31</a></li>\n</ul>"}],"questionable":"<p>These are graded \"correct\" in the bank but conflict with other questions or current Google guidance. Know the graded answer, but don't generalize from it.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q21\">Exam 1 · Q21</a> and <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q9\">Exam 5 · Q9</a>: Cloud Storage + Cloud KMS for pipeline secrets.</strong>\n   - The answer is the best of the four options offered, but none of them is Secret Manager. Secret Manager is the current recommended store <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q46\">Exam 3 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q65\">Exam 5 · Q65</a>.\n   - <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q9\">Exam 5 · Q9</a>'s claim that \"Cloud KMS simplifies <strong>secret</strong> rotation\" confuses rotating a key with rotating a secret value.\n   - Pick GCS+KMS only when Secret Manager isn't offered.</li>\n<li><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q45\">Exam 2 · Q45</a>: KMS-encrypted secrets inline in the build config.</strong>\n   - This is valid among its options, but <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q46\">Exam 3 · Q46</a> calls it higher-maintenance than Secret Manager, because every rotation needs a re-encrypt and re-embed.\n   - It is a dated pattern, not the preferred one.</li>\n<li><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q19\">Exam 5 · Q19</a> ❌: the explanation says partitioned rolling updates are \"directly supported within Kubernetes Deployment objects\".</strong>\n   - That is wrong. <code>partition</code> is a <strong>StatefulSet</strong> RollingUpdate field (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q39\">Exam 2 · Q39</a> says so explicitly).\n   - It also dismisses StatefulSets as \"not for phased rollouts\", which contradicts where the feature actually lives.\n   - The answer letter is right; the reasoning is wrong.</li>\n<li><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q21\">Exam 5 · Q21</a> ❌: the explanation claims Anthos Service Mesh \"does not manage non-GKE VM workloads\".</strong>\n   - The parallel question <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a> rejects the Compute Engine option for a different reason: the option only configures a % split and has no user-agent rule.\n   - Rely on the <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q36\">Exam 2 · Q36</a> reasoning: header routing is the deciding criterion.</li>\n<li><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q30\">Exam 5 · Q30</a>: the explanation says \"Cloud Run does not support arbitrary filesystem secret mounts\".</strong>\n   - Cloud Run <em>does</em> mount Secret Manager secrets as volumes (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q28\">Exam 3 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q28\">Exam 4 · Q28</a> depend on it).\n   - Option II is wrong because of the needless app-side \"decrypt\" step <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q60\">Exam 4 · Q60</a>, not because of mounting.</li>\n<li><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q14\">Exam 5 · Q14</a>: the explanation recommends integrating with \"Google Container Registry\".</strong>\n   - Container Registry is retired <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q12\">Exam 4 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q10\">Exam 1 · Q10</a>.\n   - Read \"Container Analysis\" as today's <strong>Artifact Analysis</strong> on Artifact Registry.</li>\n<li><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q53\">Exam 5 · Q53</a>: the explanation says <code>create_before_destroy</code> lets \"new ones with the updated machine type spin up\", avoiding downtime.</strong>\n   - <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q55\">Exam 1 · Q55</a> explicitly notes that it does <strong>not</strong> guarantee rollout to existing VMs, which depends on the MIG update configuration.\n   - It also faults option B for \"mixing instance types\", which is not the real reason. The real reason is extra pipeline runs.</li>\n<li><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q52\">Exam 5 · Q52</a>: the reasoning against option B (\"roll back green\") is weak.</strong>\n   - It says this \"reverts to an outdated version\". The stronger reason, consistent with <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q59\">Exam 4 · Q59</a>, is that switching the selector to blue is instant and leaves green intact for developers to debug.</li>\n<li><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q12\">Exam 3 · Q12</a> and <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q8\">Exam 4 · Q8</a>: the bank's own explanations admit ambiguity.</strong>\n   - <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q12\">Exam 3 · Q12</a>: option C (two stages plus canary) is \"technically defensible\".\n   - <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q8\">Exam 4 · Q8</a>: option A's \"main-branch merge requests\" wording is ambiguous.\n   - Choose the simplest option that literally matches the stem.</li>\n</ol>"},"glossary":[{"term":"A/B testing","definition":"A release approach that routes a defined user segment to a variant based on conditions, in order to compare variants.","exam_context":"Pick it for experimentation across user segments, not for safe rollout or fast rollback.","lesson":"d2l6","domain":2},{"term":"Artifact Analysis","definition":"Google Cloud's vulnerability scanning and metadata service for images in Artifact Registry, formerly called Container Analysis. It scans images on push, by digest, and keeps findings up to date through continuous analysis.","exam_context":"Choose it to find or report CVEs; remember that it only detects, enabling it does not scan existing images, and adding a tag does not trigger a rescan.","lesson":"d2l9","domain":2},{"term":"Artifact Registry","definition":"Google Cloud's managed registry for container images, Helm charts and other packages, with IAM, VPC-SC, GKE and Cloud Run integration. It replaces the retired Container Registry.","exam_context":"Pick it whenever a question needs to store images and Helm charts together; Container Registry never stored Helm charts.","lesson":"d2l5","domain":2},{"term":"Artifact Registry cleanup policies","definition":"Rules on an Artifact Registry repository that automatically delete or keep artifacts, with a dry-run mode to preview their effect.","exam_context":"The answer to image retention; a scheduled Cloud Build delete job or manual deletion is the trap.","lesson":"d2l4","domain":2},{"term":"availableSecrets.secretManager","definition":"The Cloud Build config field that references Secret Manager secret versions and exposes them to build steps through secretEnv.","exam_context":"The recommended way to use secrets in builds, paired with roles/secretmanager.secretAccessor on the build service account; rotation needs no config change.","lesson":"d2l8","domain":2},{"term":"Binary Authorization","definition":"A managed deploy-time admission control for GKE and Cloud Run that only admits images matching a policy, such as required attestations or allowlisted name patterns. It is based on the Kritis spec, supports dry run, and logs enforcement to Cloud Audit Logs.","exam_context":"Choose it whenever the stem says only trusted, approved or tested images can be deployed; IAM, RBAC and scanning do not control what runs.","lesson":"d2l9","domain":2},{"term":"Blue/green deployment","definition":"A strategy that runs the old (blue) and new (green) versions side by side and switches all traffic at cutover, so rollback is switching traffic back.","exam_context":"Pick it for \"instant rollback\" or \"shortest rollback\"; on GKE the rollback is flipping the Service selector, not kubectl rollout undo.","lesson":"d2l6","domain":2},{"term":"Break-glass label","definition":"The Pod label image-policy.k8s.io/break-glass: \"true\", which lets a single Pod bypass Binary Authorization enforcement while the event is automatically logged.","exam_context":"The answer for an emergency or debug Pod with an unsigned image; ALWAYS_ALLOW, disabling Binary Authorization, or a permanent allowlist entry are traps.","lesson":"d2l9","domain":2},{"term":"built-by-cloud-build attestor","definition":"An attestor that Cloud Build creates and whose attestations it signs automatically for images it builds.","exam_context":"Require it in a Binary Authorization policy for \"only images built by our Cloud Build\" with low effort; building your own KMS keyring and attestor duplicates it.","lesson":"d2l9","domain":2},{"term":"Canary deployment","definition":"A strategy that exposes a small percentage of real traffic to the new version and widens exposure as evidence shows it is healthy.","exam_context":"Pick it for \"not fully load-tested\", \"progressively widen exposure\" or \"limit blast radius\"; it does not replace a preceding QA environment.","lesson":"d2l6","domain":2},{"term":"Circuit breaker","definition":"A resilience pattern that stops calling a failing dependency and serves a fallback instead, so failures don't consume the caller's latency budget.","exam_context":"The answer for an unreliable synchronous dependency under a tight SLO; more retries or a bigger queue add latency.","lesson":"d2l3","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud Build","definition":"Google Cloud's managed CI service that runs build steps defined in a build config, typically cloudbuild.yaml, in response to triggers.","exam_context":"It is for builds, not recurring batch jobs; a scheduled job belongs in a Cloud Run job with a scheduler.","lesson":"d2l1","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud Build private pool","definition":"A pool of dedicated Cloud Build workers that can be peered to your VPC and used with VPC Service Controls, so builds reach private resources without public IPs.","exam_context":"Choose it when tests must reach private VPC APIs or private-IP databases, or when builds must avoid public IPs and exfiltration; creating the pool alone does not connect it to your VPC.","lesson":"d2l1","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud Build trigger","definition":"A rule that starts a build on a source event: a push to a branch, a push of a new tag matching a regex, or a pull request. Included-files filters can scope it to parts of a repository.","exam_context":"Semver release means a tag trigger, test-before-accept means a PR trigger, and the included-files filter has nothing to do with approval.","lesson":"d2l1","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud Build trigger approval","definition":"A trigger setting where builds are submitted automatically but stay pending until a principal with roles/cloudbuild.builds.approver approves them.","exam_context":"Use it for \"builds start automatically but must wait for group X\"; approval of pushes to a branch is GitHub branch protection instead, and builds.editor cannot approve.","lesson":"d2l1","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud Deploy","definition":"Google Cloud's managed continuous delivery service that models a delivery pipeline across ordered targets and manages releases, rollouts, approvals and rollbacks for GKE and serverless targets.","exam_context":"Choose it for promotion, approvals and automated canary rollback on GKE or Cloud Run; it does not create your tests and is not for multi-cloud VM images.","lesson":"d2l4","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud Deploy automation","definition":"Rules in Cloud Deploy that act on rollouts without a human, such as promoting a release or rolling back after failed verification.","exam_context":"Combine canary phases, verification and an automation rule for \"automatic rollback\"; also used to auto-approve low-risk dev to test promotions to cut toil.","lesson":"d2l4","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud KMS","definition":"Google Cloud's service for managing cryptographic keys used to encrypt, decrypt and sign data.","exam_context":"Use it for signing keys and decryption keys, not to store API keys; rotating a KMS key is not rotating a secret value.","lesson":"d2l8","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud Run revision tag","definition":"A named tag on a Cloud Run revision that gives it its own URL of the form tag---service, so it can be tested without receiving production traffic.","exam_context":"Deploy with --no-traffic --tag to let QA test before any customer traffic; a tagged URL is not private if the service is public.","lesson":"d2l7","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud Run traffic splitting","definition":"Routing fixed percentages of requests to different revisions of the same Cloud Run service.","exam_context":"The least-effort canary (for example 90/10) and rollback mechanism on Cloud Run; it splits by revision percentage only, not by header.","lesson":"d2l7","domain":2},{"term":"Cloud Service Mesh VirtualService","definition":"A service mesh routing resource that routes requests by headers or attributes and splits traffic by weights.","exam_context":"Pick GKE with Cloud Service Mesh when routing must use headers such as user agent plus arbitrary % splits.","lesson":"d2l6","domain":2},{"term":"cloud-builds Pub/Sub topic","definition":"The Pub/Sub topic to which Cloud Build publishes every build state change.","exam_context":"Send build info to a third-party webhook with a push subscription on this topic; a final build step that POSTs needs custom code and never runs for failed builds.","lesson":"d2l1","domain":2},{"term":"create_before_destroy","definition":"A Terraform lifecycle setting that creates a replacement resource before destroying the old one; with name_prefix it lets an in-use instance template be replaced in one apply.","exam_context":"The fix for the \"already being used by\" instance template error; it does not by itself roll the new config out to existing MIG VMs.","lesson":"d2l10","domain":2},{"term":"Image digest","definition":"An immutable, content-derived sha256 identifier for an image that always refers to the same bytes.","exam_context":"Use a digest to pin or roll back to a known-good image; it can't be matched to a Git tag because it is only known after the push.","lesson":"d2l5","domain":2},{"term":"Image tag","definition":"A mutable, human-readable label on an image, such as a version number or latest, that can be moved to point at different content.","exam_context":"Use a tag such as $TAG_NAME to select a release version; re-pointing latest does not trigger a Kubernetes rollout.","lesson":"d2l5","domain":2},{"term":"Kayenta","definition":"Spinnaker's automated canary analysis component, which compares metrics from the canary against a baseline.","exam_context":"The baseline must be a freshly created deployment of the current production version, not long-running prod with warm caches.","lesson":"d2l11","domain":2},{"term":"Kritis","definition":"An open-source, self-operated admission control spec and implementation for deploy-time image policy, on which Binary Authorization is based.","exam_context":"With \"low overhead\" in the stem, pick the managed Binary Authorization over self-operated Kritis.","lesson":"d2l9","domain":2},{"term":"Kustomize overlay","definition":"A set of patches applied on top of a shared base of Kubernetes manifests to produce environment-specific variants.","exam_context":"Use one Skaffold profile per Cloud Deploy target, each pointing at a Kustomize overlay, for per-environment differences such as replica counts.","lesson":"d2l4","domain":2},{"term":"On-Demand Scanning","definition":"An Artifact Analysis API that scans an image for vulnerabilities on request, for example inside a build before the image is pushed.","exam_context":"Use it in Cloud Build to fail the build on critical vulnerabilities so they never reach the registry; automatic scanning runs after push.","lesson":"d2l9","domain":2},{"term":"Packer","definition":"A tool that builds machine images, which can be orchestrated by Cloud Build.","exam_context":"The answer for custom Compute Engine or multi-cloud VM images; Cloud Deploy and kpt are Kubernetes and serverless oriented.","lesson":"d2l10","domain":2},{"term":"Remote repository","definition":"An Artifact Registry repository mode that proxies an upstream source such as Docker Hub and caches artifacts on first pull.","exam_context":"For \"cache every dependency through one endpoint\", use remote repositories fronted by a virtual repository.","lesson":"d2l5","domain":2},{"term":"requireApproval","definition":"A Cloud Deploy target setting that makes rollouts to that target wait for manual approval.","exam_context":"Set it on the prod target, after a QA stage with the standard strategy, to enforce \"QA done\" before any prod traffic.","lesson":"d2l4","domain":2},{"term":"roles/cloudbuild.builds.approver","definition":"The IAM role that grants permission to approve or reject Cloud Build builds that require approval.","exam_context":"Grant it to the group that must approve builds; roles/cloudbuild.builds.editor does not include the approve permission.","lesson":"d2l1","domain":2},{"term":"roles/clouddeploy.developer","definition":"A Cloud Deploy IAM role for working with delivery pipelines and releases.","exam_context":"Bind it on the specific delivery pipeline with set-iam-policy to limit access to one pipeline; a target is not a pipeline.","lesson":"d2l2","domain":2},{"term":"roles/container.developer","definition":"A GKE IAM role that grants access to Kubernetes API objects inside clusters.","exam_context":"Grant it to the existing Cloud Build service account so the kubectl builder can deploy; you can't set roles in cloudbuild.yaml.","lesson":"d2l2","domain":2},{"term":"roles/secretmanager.secretAccessor","definition":"The IAM role that allows reading secret payloads from Secret Manager.","exam_context":"Grant it to the build or runtime service account that needs the secret, rather than embedding the secret anywhere.","lesson":"d2l8","domain":2},{"term":"Secret Manager","definition":"Google Cloud's managed store for versioned secret values such as API keys, passwords and certificates.","exam_context":"The recommended store for pipeline and Cloud Run secrets; rotation is adding a new version. Pick Cloud Storage plus KMS only when Secret Manager isn't offered.","lesson":"d2l8","domain":2},{"term":"Shadow deployment","definition":"A strategy that mirrors a copy of production traffic to the new version without returning its responses to users.","exam_context":"Pick it for \"full production load before launch\" with no users exposed.","lesson":"d2l6","domain":2},{"term":"Signed build provenance","definition":"Verifiable metadata that Cloud Build generates and signs about how an image was built (SLSA level 3), stored through the Container Analysis API.","exam_context":"Use it, verified by Binary Authorization, to trace a running image back to its source and build; scanning is not provenance.","lesson":"d2l9","domain":2},{"term":"Skaffold profile","definition":"A named configuration in skaffold.yaml that changes how manifests are rendered, for example by selecting a Kustomize overlay.","exam_context":"Use multiple profiles, one per Cloud Deploy target, with manifests.kustomize; a single profile allows no per-target variation.","lesson":"d2l4","domain":2},{"term":"StatefulSet partition","definition":"A StatefulSet RollingUpdate field that updates only Pods with an ordinal greater than or equal to the partition value.","exam_context":"The answer for a phased rollout to half the pods; it is a StatefulSet field, not a Deployment field.","lesson":"d2l6","domain":2},{"term":"Substitution variables","definition":"Values such as $TAG_NAME that Cloud Build fills into the build config, with $TAG_NAME holding the Git tag on trigger-invoked builds.","exam_context":"Use $TAG_NAME in the image name to tie an image to its release tag.","lesson":"d2l1","domain":2},{"term":"Virtual repository","definition":"An Artifact Registry repository mode that provides a single endpoint in front of several repositories; it routes requests but does not cache by itself.","exam_context":"Combine it with remote repositories for a single endpoint that caches upstream dependencies.","lesson":"d2l5","domain":2},{"term":"waitFor","definition":"A Cloud Build step field that lists which steps must finish first; waitFor: ['-'] starts the step immediately, and with no waitFor a step waits for all previous steps.","exam_context":"To run independent steps concurrently, put waitFor: ['-'] on every one of them; setting it on the first step only changes nothing.","lesson":"d2l1","domain":2},{"term":"Workload Identity","definition":"A GKE feature that lets Kubernetes service accounts act as IAM service accounts without key files.","exam_context":"Use it for GKE pipeline components that call Google APIs, such as signing attestations with Cloud KMS, instead of an SA JSON key in a Kubernetes Secret.","lesson":"d2l2","domain":2}]},{"n":3,"title":"Applying Site Reliability Engineering Practices","short":"SRE Practices","weight":"~18%","desc":"SLIs, SLOs and error budgets, incident management, blameless postmortems, toil and capacity planning.","lessons":[{"id":"d3l1","num":1,"title":"SLIs: what to measure and where to measure it","html":"<h3>Key concepts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SLI = good events / valid events.</strong> The denominator must cover the same scope as the SLI. For a home page latency SLI, count home page requests under 100 ms and divide by <strong>home page requests only</strong>, not by all app requests <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>An SLI measures <strong>what users get</strong>, not what a backend is doing. Queue size, I/O wait and PD throughput are <em>causes</em>. The availability SLI is the share of report requests that return a successful response <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q38\">Exam 2 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Availability as a ratio, not a count.</strong> \"Successful API calls ÷ attempted API calls\" beats \"successful uploads per minute\", because the per-minute count is throughput <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Separate SLIs for separate expectations.</strong> \"99.9% available and ≤200 ms for 95% of requests\" calls for an availability SLO and a latency SLO, each built on its own SLI and monitored in Cloud Monitoring <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q17\">Exam 3 · Q17</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>SLI type selector</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario wording</th>\n<th>SLI type</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Page is served but <strong>some widgets are missing</strong>, or the response is degraded or partial</td>\n<td><strong>Quality</strong>: non-degraded responses / total responses</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Requests fail or return 5xx</td>\n<td><strong>Availability</strong>: successful / valid requests</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q38\">Exam 2 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q29\">Exam 3 · Q29</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Fast enough\": N ms threshold</td>\n<td><strong>Latency</strong>: requests under threshold / valid requests</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data updated recently enough (pipelines)</td>\n<td><strong>Freshness</strong>. A distractor when the issue is incomplete content</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Where to measure</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Requirement</th>\n<th>Measure at</th>\n<th>Reject</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>User's view, <strong>no app code changes</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Synthetic clients</strong> plus <strong>existing request logs</strong></td>\n<td>\"Add new metrics\" (that needs instrumentation), modifying code, \"<strong>only</strong> proxy logs\" (too narrow)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q14\">Exam 1 · Q14</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Catch <strong>CDN, LB misconfiguration or global network</strong> failures</td>\n<td><strong>Client-side instrumentation</strong> plus a <strong>synthetic client</strong></td>\n<td>Server logs, server metrics and GKE health checks share the LB SLI's blind spot</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q34\">Exam 1 · Q34</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>If the service returns \"something, but incomplete\", choose a <strong>quality</strong> SLI <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>If an option counts infra health (\"healthy microservices / total microservices\"), reject it. That is backend health, not user experience <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>If an option correlates strongly with failures, such as a queue metric, it can serve as a leading-indicator <strong>alert</strong>. It is still not the SLI <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q38\">Exam 2 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>To extend coverage upstream of your infrastructure, measure <strong>outside</strong> the infrastructure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q34\">Exam 1 · Q34</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Percentile at the threshold</strong> (\"calculate the percentile that corresponds to 100 ms\") is close in spirit, but it is not the recommended good/valid form. <strong>Median + p90</strong> only describes the shape of the distribution <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Average latency</strong> hides the tail. A low average does not mean 95% of requests are fast <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q17\">Exam 3 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q19\">Exam 4 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>Alerting on <strong>average latency &gt; 150 ms</strong> is not an SLO <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q17\">Exam 3 · Q17</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers how to choose Service Level Indicators (SLIs): what to measure, how to express it as a ratio, and where to collect it. Exam questions test whether you can pick the SLI type that matches a symptom and a measurement point that sees what users actually experience.","objectives":["After this lesson you can express an SLI as good events divided by valid events, with a correctly scoped denominator.","After this lesson you can choose between availability, latency, quality and freshness SLIs based on scenario wording.","After this lesson you can pick a measurement point (synthetic clients, request logs, client-side instrumentation) that fits the stated constraints.","After this lesson you can reject backend-health and average-latency options that do not reflect user experience."],"takeaways":["SLI = good events / valid events, and the denominator covers the same scope as the numerator (home page requests only for a home page SLI).","Partial or degraded responses call for a quality SLI, failed responses for availability, and speed for latency.","Queue size, I/O wait and disk throughput are causes, not SLIs. A correlated metric can still be a leading-indicator alert.","No code changes allowed: use synthetic clients plus existing request logs. To catch CDN, LB or network failures: client-side instrumentation plus a synthetic client.","Average latency hides the tail, so it is never the answer for a '95% of requests' goal."],"questions":["e1q14","e1q25","e1q34","e1q47","e2q38","e3q17","e3q29","e4q19"]},{"id":"d3l2","num":2,"title":"Setting SLO targets","html":"<h3>Latency SLO from measured percentiles (worked examples)</h3>\n<p>Rule: <strong>anchor the target to current measured performance, then add a modest margin.</strong> Never set it tighter than current performance. Never set it exactly equal (that leaves zero error budget, a tripwire). Never loosen it so far that it stops reflecting what users are happy with.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Measured (28-day)</th>\n<th>Too tight</th>\n<th>Exact match (zero budget)</th>\n<th><strong>Correct</strong></th>\n<th>Too loose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q49\">Exam 2 · Q49</a></td>\n<td>p90 120 ms / p95 275 ms</td>\n<td>100 / 250</td>\n<td>120 / 275</td>\n<td><strong>150 / 300</strong></td>\n<td>250 / 400</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q11\">Exam 4 · Q11</a></td>\n<td>p90 160 ms / p95 300 ms</td>\n<td>150 / 290</td>\n<td>160 / 300</td>\n<td><strong>190 / 330</strong></td>\n<td>300 / 450</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The cue in both questions is \"customers are currently satisfied\". That makes the current measurement the anchor.</p>\n<h3>Cloud Monitoring SLO configuration <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q35\">Exam 4 · Q35</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q36\">Exam 5 · Q36</a></h3>\n<p>Goal: \"requests handled in under 300 ms at least 90% of the time per calendar month.\"</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Metric:</strong> speed means <strong>latency</strong>. Availability measures success, not speed.</li>\n<li><strong>Evaluation:</strong> \"X% of requests faster than N ms\" means <strong>request-based</strong> evaluation.</li>\n<li><strong>Calendar month</strong> is the <strong>compliance period</strong>. It says nothing about the evaluation method.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Evaluation method</th>\n<th>Counts</th>\n<th>Use when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Request-based</strong></td>\n<td>Good requests / total requests</td>\n<td>The goal is stated per request: \"90% of requests &lt; 300 ms\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Windows-based</strong></td>\n<td>Good time windows / total windows</td>\n<td>The goal is stated per interval (\"p95 &lt; 300 ms in 90% of 10-min windows\"), or the raw metric is already a percentile</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>Trap: \"90% <strong>of the time</strong>\" pulls toward windows-based evaluation, but the subject of the sentence is requests <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q35\">Exam 4 · Q35</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Don't over-deliver on the SLO</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Meet the SLO, but don't greatly exceed it.</strong> Users depend on the reliability they actually <em>observe</em>, which creates a hidden, tighter implicit SLO. The source cites Google's Chubby example: planned outages keep dependents from assuming the service never fails <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q33\">Exam 2 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>SLO consistently met, customers happy, toil low: <strong>raise deployment velocity or accepted risk</strong>, and <strong>move engineers to services that need reliability</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q31\">Exam 2 · Q31</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q44\">Exam 5 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>Under 5% of the budget used for 6 months, and stakeholders confirmed the SLO is right: <strong>release more often or take more risk</strong>, and <strong>announce planned downtime</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q33\">Exam 2 · Q33</a>. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q49\">Exam 5 · Q49</a> keys this differently (see ⚠️).</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Do</th>\n<th>Don't</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>SLO met, users happy, low toil</td>\n<td>Raise velocity, reassign engineers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q31\">Exam 2 · Q31</a></td>\n<td>Tighten the SLO, put reliability ahead of features, rework SLIs without a trigger</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SLO greatly exceeded, SLO confirmed appropriate</td>\n<td>Riskier or faster releases, planned downtime <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q33\">Exam 2 · Q33</a></td>\n<td>Tighten the SLO to match observed reliability, add capacity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SLO met but <strong>users unhappy</strong></td>\n<td>Tighten the SLO or improve SLI coverage (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q31\">Exam 2 · Q31</a> trap note)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SLI falls short of the SLO</td>\n<td>Fix the service <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q2\">Exam 1 · Q2</a></td>\n<td>Lower the SLO to match the SLI</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Cost of an extra nine (worked math) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q10\">Exam 2 · Q10</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q46\">Exam 5 · Q46</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>99.9% → 99.99% on $1,000,000 revenue, at a cost of $2,000:</li>\n<li>Difference = 99.99 − 99.9 = <strong>0.09 percentage points</strong>.</li>\n<li>Value = 0.0009 × $1,000,000 = <strong>$900</strong> &lt; $2,000, so <strong>not worth it</strong>.</li>\n<li>Alternative path <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q46\">Exam 5 · Q46</a>: 99.9% allows about 8.76 h of downtime a year and 99.99% about 0.876 h. That saves about 7.88 h, and at $1M ÷ 8,760 h ≈ $114/h, 7.88 h is worth about $900.</li>\n<li>Distractors are off by a factor of 10: <strong>$1,000</strong> treats the gain as 0.1%, the full unavailability at the old target. <strong>$9,000</strong> treats it as 0.9%.</li>\n<li>The decision flips with the inputs. At $10M revenue the same gain is worth $9,000, which exceeds $2,000 <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q10\">Exam 2 · Q10</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers setting SLO targets: anchoring latency SLOs to measured percentiles, configuring SLOs in Cloud Monitoring, and deciding what to do when you exceed or miss an SLO. It also covers the arithmetic for judging whether an extra nine of availability is worth its cost.","objectives":["After this lesson you can set a latency SLO from measured percentiles by adding a modest margin to current performance.","After this lesson you can choose request-based or windows-based evaluation and tell the compliance period apart from the evaluation method.","After this lesson you can decide what to do when an SLO is greatly exceeded, met with unhappy users, or missed.","After this lesson you can calculate the value of an extra nine of availability and compare it with its cost."],"takeaways":["Latency SLO = current measured value + modest margin: 120/275 ms becomes 150/300, and 160/300 becomes 190/330. Never tighter than, or exactly equal to, current performance.","'X% of requests under N ms' means a latency metric with request-based evaluation. A calendar month is the compliance period, not the evaluation method.","Don't greatly exceed the SLO. If it is met, users are happy and toil is low, raise release velocity or accepted risk and move engineers to services that need them.","If the SLI falls short of the SLO, fix the service. Don't lower the SLO to match.","Value of an extra nine = difference in availability x revenue: 0.09% x $1M = $900, which is less than a $2,000 cost, so not worth it."],"questions":["e1q2","e2q10","e2q31","e2q33","e2q49","e4q11","e4q35","e5q36","e5q44","e5q46","e5q49"]},{"id":"d3l3","num":3,"title":"Error budgets, burn rate and error budget policy","html":"<h3>Key concepts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Error budget = 1 − SLO target.</strong> You cannot request or grant budget on demand <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q34\">Exam 4 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Each SLO has its own budget.</strong> Spare budget on one SLO cannot be moved to another <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q34\">Exam 4 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>A 99% target means a 1% budget. Measured 99.5% means half the budget has been used this month <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Chain:</strong> SLI (what you measure) → SLO (target + compliance period) → error budget (permitted failure) → <strong>burn rate</strong> (how fast the budget is being spent) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q27\">Exam 2 · Q27</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Detecting budget consumption <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q27\">Exam 2 · Q27</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Define an SLO and alert on <code>select_slo_burn_rate</code>.</strong> A sustained burn rate above 1 means the service will miss the SLO for the period. The source recommends pairing a <strong>fast-burn</strong> and a <strong>slow-burn</strong> alert.</li>\n<li>Reject SLI threshold alerts, raw service mesh metrics and uptime-check failure alerts. An uptime-check alert says something is down <em>now</em>. It does not say how fast the budget is going.</li>\n<li>If the question asks to be notified <strong>as soon as an endpoint is unreachable</strong>, uptime-check alerting fits.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Error budget policy, agreed in advance <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q53\">Exam 2 · Q53</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q17\">Exam 5 · Q17</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>To make engineering pivot from features to reliability automatically after an SLO miss, <strong>work with all stakeholders to establish an error budget policy ahead of time</strong>.</li>\n<li>Reject \"reliability <strong>always</strong> beats features\": the absolute removes the balancing mechanism. Reject \"cap releases to once a week\": that treats a symptom. Reject \"a Jenkins plugin that blocks releases\": it can implement a policy, but it is unilateral and only blocks.</li>\n<li>Pattern: <strong>absolutes (\"always X\") and single-tool fixes are the standard distractors.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Budget exhausted or nearly exhausted</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario</th>\n<th>Correct</th>\n<th>Why the distractors fail</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Policy freezes deploys, budget exhausted, the biggest customer urgently wants a feature, tests pass</td>\n<td><strong>Wait for the budget to replenish</strong></td>\n<td>Rerunning tests, deploying now, or a <strong>canary</strong> are all still production deployments. Rollout safety ≠ release permission</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q11\">Exam 3 · Q11</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>99.9% SLO, quarterly budget nearly gone, features due next week</td>\n<td><strong>Postpone and work on reliability</strong> until the budget replenishes</td>\n<td>Auto-rollback still burns budget. A staged rollout still ships. Postponing to build a <em>feature backlog</em> wastes the freeze</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Budget used up for the rolling window, product team about to launch</td>\n<td><strong>Tell the team, then negotiate: launch freeze vs. knowingly accepting worse UX</strong></td>\n<td>\"Make sure tests pass\", \"escalate for more budget\", \"reallocate from other SLOs\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q34\">Exam 4 · Q34</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Budget surplus plus other goals</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>99% target, 99.5% measured, limited engineers, goals are <strong>reduce technical debt and keep ops costs low</strong>: <strong>identify, quantify and eliminate toil through automation</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q23\">Exam 5 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>Trap: the surplus budget tempts you toward \"assign engineers to the feature backlog\". That would be right only if the debt and cost goals were removed <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>N+1 redundancy adds cost. Working to \"minimize the remaining error budget\" pushes the wrong way.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Shared ownership of reliability <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q56\">Exam 1 · Q56</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q55\">Exam 5 · Q55</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Joint ownership means <strong>both teams collaboratively define, agree on and publish the SLOs</strong>. In <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q56\">Exam 1 · Q56</a> it also means shared code review and a <strong>shared on-call rotation</strong>.</li>\n<li>Reject options where one team <em>unilaterally</em> sets SLOs for another to meet. Reject handing <em>all</em> on-call to developers: \"transferring the pager is not sharing ownership\" <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q56\">Exam 1 · Q56</a>.</li>\n<li>In <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q55\">Exam 5 · Q55</a> the best available option is \"SLO-Focused Collaboration\": shared SLO definition and publication.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers error budgets, burn rate alerting and the error budget policy that decides what happens when the budget runs out. The exam tests whether you respect a budget freeze, set policy with stakeholders in advance, and use surplus budget in line with the stated goals.","objectives":["After this lesson you can derive an error budget from an SLO target and work out how much of it has been used.","After this lesson you can detect budget consumption with an SLO burn-rate alert instead of threshold or uptime-check alerts.","After this lesson you can apply an error budget policy when the budget is exhausted, nearly exhausted or in surplus.","After this lesson you can describe shared reliability ownership between development and SRE teams."],"takeaways":["Error budget = 1 - SLO target. Each SLO has its own budget, and budget cannot be requested, granted or moved between SLOs.","Detect budget consumption with an SLO plus a select_slo_burn_rate alert, ideally a fast-burn and a slow-burn pair.","Agree on an error budget policy with all stakeholders ahead of time. Absolutes ('reliability always wins') and single-tool fixes are distractors.","Budget exhausted and the policy says freeze: wait for it to replenish. A canary or passing tests is still a production deployment.","Shared ownership means both teams define, agree on and publish the SLOs together. Handing the pager to developers is not sharing."],"questions":["e1q56","e2q23","e2q27","e2q53","e3q11","e3q42","e4q34","e5q17","e5q23","e5q55"]},{"id":"d3l4","num":4,"title":"Incident management (IMAG)","html":"<h3>Roles</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Role</th>\n<th>Owns</th>\n<th>Does not</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Incident Commander (IC)</strong></td>\n<td>Overall command and coordination. Holds every role not yet delegated. Sets up the \"room\"</td>\n<td>Hands-on mitigation once an OL exists. Answering stakeholder email</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Operations Lead (OL)</strong></td>\n<td>Hands-on technical response: diagnose, mitigate, change the system</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Communications Lead (CL)</strong></td>\n<td>All stakeholder updates, <strong>internal and external</strong></td>\n<td>Splitting audiences, or pushing internal updates onto the IC</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>IMAG has <strong>exactly three</strong> roles. \"Engineering Lead\", \"Customer Impact Assessor\" and \"External Customer Communications Lead\" are distractors <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q6\">Exam 1 · Q6</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Sequence after roles are assigned</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>IC has declared the incident and assigned an OL and a CL. <strong>Next step: set up a dedicated, shared communication channel</strong> for responders and leads <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q27\">Exam 1 · Q27</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q6\">Exam 5 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>Mitigation belongs to the OL and stakeholder updates to the CL. The postmortem comes after resolution.</li>\n<li>If <strong>no OL had been assigned</strong>, the IC holds that role and would work on mitigation directly (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q27\">Exam 1 · Q27</a> trap note).</li>\n<li>Memory hook: <em>the IC sets up the room, the OL fixes, the CL tells, and the postmortem comes last.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Communications Lead <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q20\">Exam 1 · Q20</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Large incident, no ETA, both internal stakeholders and customers emailing: send <strong>periodic updates to all stakeholders, each committing to a \"next update\" time</strong>.</li>\n<li>Reject serving only one audience, delegating internal emails to someone else, or forwarding them to the IC.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Living document vs. postmortem <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a></h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Artifact</th>\n<th>When</th>\n<th>Audience</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Incident state (\"living\") document</strong></td>\n<td><strong>During</strong> the incident, updated in real time</td>\n<td>Responders</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Postmortem</strong></td>\n<td><strong>After</strong> resolution</td>\n<td>Stakeholders and the wider org</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>After a multi-hour outage, the first step in summarizing for stakeholders is to <strong>write a postmortem and distribute it</strong>. Phoning people or asking the \"responsible engineer\" for an apology are wrong.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Mitigate first, diagnose later</h3>\n<p>Rule: <strong>stop the bleeding before you study the wound.</strong> If the symptom started right after a change, roll the change back.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario</th>\n<th>First action</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Canary experiment → spike in 500s and latency</td>\n<td><strong>Revert the canary</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q26\">Exam 1 · Q26</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>New release → all users see login latency</td>\n<td><strong>Roll back the release</strong>. Not more monitoring, not bigger VMs, not another release</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q51\">Exam 2 · Q51</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q15\">Exam 5 · Q15</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Users in one region can't connect (multi-region GKE)</td>\n<td><strong>Redirect traffic to healthy regions</strong>. Logs and metrics come next</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q42\">Exam 2 · Q42</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cloud Run canary breaching the SLO</td>\n<td><strong>Send 100% of traffic to the previous revision</strong>. Not a redeploy, not Cloud Trace first</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q56\">Exam 3 · Q56</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Blue/green on GKE, green failing, blue healthy, keep green running for investigation</td>\n<td><strong>Change the Service selector to <code>version: blue</code></strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q59\">Exam 1 · Q59</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Some Pods in one zone erroring after a rollout</td>\n<td>Key: restore the previous Pod template and roll the affected Pods (see ⚠️)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q13\">Exam 3 · Q13</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>Blue/green trap: <strong>rolling back traffic ≠ rolling back a Deployment.</strong> A Deployment rollback changes the failing workload. Removing <code>version: green</code> from the selector <em>broadens</em> it to both versions <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q59\">Exam 1 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>Exceptions: if no previous version or revision exists, diagnosis or a redeploy of the known-good version becomes the first step <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q26\">Exam 1 · Q26</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q56\">Exam 3 · Q56</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>OL removing a bad node from the LB pool <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q31\">Exam 4 · Q31</a></h3>\n<p>Pool runs at about 70% capacity and one node returns 5xx on every request. The order is:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Communicate your intent</strong> to the incident team.</li>\n<li><strong>Run a load analysis</strong> to see whether the rest of the pool can absorb the traffic, and <strong>scale only if needed</strong>.</li>\n<li>Once any new nodes are healthy, <strong>drain the bad node and remove it</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Reject \"always add a node first\" (it skips the analysis). Reject removing the node first and scaling reactively. Reject communicating last.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Suspected compromised GKE node <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q1\">Exam 4 · Q1</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cordon</strong> (stop new scheduling), then <strong>drain</strong> gracefully. Controller-managed Pods get recreated on other nodes.</li>\n<li>Per the explanation, cordon and drain is <em>not</em> full security isolation. Google's guidance also restricts the node VM's network access and <strong>preserves the suspected Pod</strong> for investigation.</li>\n<li>Reject a taint that blocks scheduling elsewhere, scaling the Deployment to 0 (loses evidence), or restarting the node.</li>\n<li>Hook: <em>cordon stops arrivals, drain evacuates workloads, and network controls restrict communication.</em></li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers incident management using Google's IMAG model: the three roles, what happens first after roles are assigned, and how responders communicate. It also covers the 'mitigate first' rule for common GCP rollback and traffic-shifting scenarios.","objectives":["After this lesson you can name the three IMAG roles and what each one owns.","After this lesson you can order early incident steps, starting with a dedicated shared communication channel.","After this lesson you can pick the mitigation that stops user impact first: rollback, traffic shift or selector change.","After this lesson you can sequence safe operational actions such as removing a bad node from a load-balanced pool or isolating a suspect GKE node."],"takeaways":["IMAG has exactly three roles: Incident Commander, Operations Lead and Communications Lead. Any other 'lead' is a distractor.","Once the IC has assigned an OL and a CL, the next step is a dedicated shared communication channel. The OL fixes, the CL tells, and the postmortem comes last.","The CL sends periodic updates to all stakeholders, internal and external, and each one commits to a 'next update' time.","Mitigate first: revert the canary, roll back the release, send 100% of traffic to the previous Cloud Run revision, or redirect traffic to healthy regions.","Blue/green on GKE: change the Service selector to version: blue to restore traffic and keep green for investigation."],"questions":["e1q6","e1q20","e1q26","e1q27","e1q44","e1q59","e2q42","e2q51","e3q13","e3q56","e4q1","e4q31","e5q6","e5q15"]},{"id":"d3l5","num":5,"title":"Postmortems","html":"<h3>When to write one: triggers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q7\">Exam 2 · Q7</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Objective triggers: <strong>data loss</strong>, user-visible impact, on-call intervention (rollback, rerouting), resolution time above a threshold, and <strong>monitoring failure</strong> (a human had to discover the problem).</li>\n<li>A <strong>stakeholder request</strong> is also a trigger. This item keyed <em>internal</em> (see ⚠️).</li>\n<li><strong>Not</strong> triggers: monitoring detecting a failed instance, or CD automatically rolling back a bad release. Those are automation working as intended.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>What goes in it</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Include</th>\n<th>Exclude</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Root cause or contributing causes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q40\">Exam 2 · Q40</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q44\">Exam 2 · Q44</a></td>\n<td>A list of people responsible <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q40\">Exam 2 · Q40</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q53\">Exam 1 · Q53</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Action items to prevent recurrence <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q40\">Exam 2 · Q40</a></td>\n<td>Personal opinion comparing severity with past incidents <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q40\">Exam 2 · Q40</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>How it could have been worse</strong> (near misses) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q43\">Exam 4 · Q43</a></td>\n<td>Copies of every affected design doc <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q40\">Exam 2 · Q40</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Resolution and lessons learned <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q53\">Exam 1 · Q53</a></td>\n<td>Banning internal component names. You need the technical specifics <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q43\">Exam 4 · Q43</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>End-user or customer data. Keeping it out matches Google practice, but that rule alone is incomplete <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q43\">Exam 4 · Q43</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Blameless means fixing the system, not the person</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Preventing a severe incident caused by a software change: <strong>make tests that catch this class of error a required gate before every release</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q12\">Exam 1 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q37\">Exam 2 · Q37</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q11\">Exam 5 · Q11</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q48\">Exam 5 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>Reject escalating the engineers to management (blame). Reject following up with reviewers and prescribing personal practices (people-focused, doesn't persist). Reject an on-call call-out policy (that improves <em>response</em>, not <em>prevention</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Ask what failed in the system, not who failed.</strong> One-on-ones to find \"who approved and pushed\" are attribution. Barring the committing engineer is punitive <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q44\">Exam 2 · Q44</a>.</li>\n<li>Interviewing people to <strong>rebuild the timeline</strong> without assigning fault is fine (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q44\">Exam 2 · Q44</a> trap note).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Action item ownership <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q35\">Exam 2 · Q35</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q20\">Exam 5 · Q20</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Exactly one owner per action item, with collaborators as needed.</strong></li>\n<li>Reject multiple owners (diffused responsibility), \"collaborators only, to stay blameless\" (that misapplies blamelessness, which governs the analysis of causes, not forward-looking accountability), and making the team lead own everything.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Sharing and culture</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Postmortem for another team's manager: write a blameless one with root causes, resolution, lessons and <strong>prioritized</strong> action items, and <strong>publish it on the org-wide portal</strong>, not just to the manager <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q53\">Exam 1 · Q53</a>.</li>\n<li>A good postmortem policy is <strong>blameless in content</strong> (cause, how it could have been worse, prevention) and <strong>open in process</strong> (all participants author it and it is shared as widely as possible) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q43\">Exam 4 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>To make postmortems well received: <strong>senior leadership acknowledges and takes part</strong>, and <strong>good postmortems are rewarded and celebrated</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q39\">Exam 4 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>Reject a dedicated postmortem team (separates the analysis from the people closest to the incident), new hires running them, or a forum for critiquing past postmortems.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers blameless postmortems: when to write one, what to put in it, and how to make sure its action items prevent recurrence. Exam questions reward fixing the system rather than the person, and sharing postmortems widely.","objectives":["After this lesson you can list objective postmortem triggers and recognize automation working as intended as a non-trigger.","After this lesson you can choose what a postmortem should include and exclude.","After this lesson you can pick system-level preventive actions over people-focused ones.","After this lesson you can describe action item ownership and the practices that build a healthy postmortem culture."],"takeaways":["Triggers include data loss, user-visible impact, on-call intervention, long resolution time and monitoring failure. Automatic CD rollback or automated failure detection is not a trigger.","Include root cause, action items and how it could have been worse. Never include a list of people responsible.","To prevent a recurrence, make tests that catch this class of error a required gate before every release.","Each action item has exactly one owner, plus collaborators as needed.","Postmortem culture grows when senior leadership takes part and good postmortems are rewarded, and when postmortems are published org-wide."],"questions":["e1q12","e1q53","e2q7","e2q35","e2q37","e2q40","e2q44","e4q39","e4q43","e5q11","e5q20","e5q48"]},{"id":"d3l6","num":6,"title":"Toil and automation","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Toil:</strong> recurring manual work, such as deleting and recreating a crashing VM by hand. <strong>Fix:</strong> a <strong>managed instance group of size 1 with health-check autohealing</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q22\">Exam 1 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>Reject a load balancer with health checks (routes around the failure but doesn't recreate the VM), SMS alerts (faster <em>awareness</em>, same manual work) and a bug filing (useful in parallel, but not a toil reducer).</li>\n<li>A GKE Pod goes bad (403s) after about 5 h and the fix is a month away: <strong>add an HTTP liveness probe</strong>. Reject a cron job that kills old Pods (duplicates a built-in feature), a human babysitter, or an alert only <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q52\">Exam 4 · Q52</a>.</li>\n<li>Toil removal is the right spend when the goals are less debt and lower cost <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q23\">Exam 5 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fast notification of a VM shutdown:</strong> create a <strong>log-based alert</strong> on <code>protoPayload.methodName = v1.compute.instances.stop</code> with a notification channel. Reject hourly BigQuery queries (lag), app-side detection (can't anticipate abrupt termination) and randomized Scheduler health checks <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q52\">Exam 1 · Q52</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Shared Terraform code:</strong> use Git with <strong>peer review and unit tests before integration</strong>, and treat the integrated repo as the single source of truth. Reject daily self-merges plus a bucket upload as \"master\", and Drive folders or zip archives <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q23\">Exam 1 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>VM telemetry IAM, least privilege:</strong> grant the service account <strong><code>logging.logWriter</code> + <code>monitoring.metricWriter</code></strong> and nothing broader. Viewer access for the ops and security teams is a separate grant to <em>them</em> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q40\">Exam 4 · Q40</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers recognizing toil and replacing it with automation that uses built-in GCP features. It also covers related operational hygiene: fast log-based alerts, peer-reviewed infrastructure code, and least-privilege telemetry permissions.","objectives":["After this lesson you can identify toil and pick the built-in GCP feature that eliminates it.","After this lesson you can choose self-healing mechanisms such as MIG autohealing and Kubernetes liveness probes.","After this lesson you can set up a log-based alert for fast notification of infrastructure events.","After this lesson you can apply least privilege to VM telemetry and source control practices to shared Terraform code."],"takeaways":["Recreating a crashing VM by hand is toil. Fix it with a managed instance group of size 1 with health-check autohealing.","A Pod that degrades after hours needs an HTTP liveness probe, not a cron job or a human babysitter.","For fast notification of a VM stop, use a log-based alert on protoPayload.methodName = v1.compute.instances.stop.","VM telemetry service accounts get logging.logWriter + monitoring.metricWriter and nothing broader.","Shared Terraform belongs in Git with peer review and unit tests before integration."],"questions":["e1q22","e1q23","e1q52","e2q23","e4q40","e4q52","e5q23"]},{"id":"d3l7","num":7,"title":"Capacity planning and scaling","html":"<h3>Availability through horizontal scale <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q2\">Exam 1 · Q2</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A stateless API on one VM runs out of memory under load, the SLI misses the SLO, and the goal is availability: <strong>add instances across zones behind a load balancer that actively distributes traffic</strong>.</li>\n<li>A bigger VM fixes capacity but stays a single-zone SPOF. <strong>Passive failover</strong> removes the zone SPOF, but the idle standby doesn't relieve load on the primary. Lowering the SLO changes nothing.</li>\n<li>Passive failover would be the better answer only if the root cause were a zone or hardware failure with no capacity problem.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Compounding growth and zone-loss headroom (worked math) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q40\">Exam 1 · Q40</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>10% month-over-month for 6 months: 1.1⁶ ≈ <strong>1.77×</strong>, which is about <strong>77% growth, not 60%</strong> (the simple-interest trap).</li>\n<li>30% CPU today × 1.77 ≈ <strong>53%</strong> of today's capacity.</li>\n<li>With 3 zones and one lost, <strong>2/3 of capacity remains</strong>, so 53% ÷ 0.667 ≈ <strong>80% utilization</strong>. That works but isn't roomy, so validate it.</li>\n<li><strong>Answer:</strong> confirm the <strong>max node pool size</strong>, enable <strong>HPA</strong>, then <strong>load test</strong>.</li>\n<li>Reject \"the cluster autoscaler handles it regardless\" (it is bounded by max limits and only reacts to pending pods), \"30% is plenty\", and \"+60% up front\" (wrong math and over-provisioning).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Risk catalog values (worked math) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q37\">Exam 1 · Q37</a></h3>\n<p>Setup: 3 zones with users split evenly, existing failover takes 10 min, DB failure about once a quarter, detection in 5 min. The new DB's failover takes <strong>twice as long</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n<th>Reasoning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>MTTD</td>\n<td><strong>5 min</strong></td>\n<td>The detection setup is unchanged</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MTTR</td>\n<td><strong>20 min</strong></td>\n<td>Equals failover duration (10 × 2)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MTBF</td>\n<td><strong>90 days</strong></td>\n<td>Once per quarter, unchanged</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>User impact</td>\n<td><strong>33%</strong></td>\n<td>1 ÷ number of zones (3). It would be 50% only with 2 zones</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Rule: change only the field the new property drives.</p>\n<h3>Autoscaler selection</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>How many Pods (load rises and falls, unpredictable traffic, stateless)</td>\n<td><strong>HPA</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q57\">Exam 4 · Q57</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How many nodes</td>\n<td><strong>Cluster autoscaler</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q12\">Exam 2 · Q12</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How big each Pod is (steady load, mis-sized requests)</td>\n<td><strong>VPA</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q12\">Exam 2 · Q12</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q57\">Exam 4 · Q57</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Daily cycle, want \"only the Pods <strong>and nodes</strong> needed\"</td>\n<td><strong>HPA + cluster autoscaler</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q12\">Exam 2 · Q12</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unpredictable traffic, consistent UX, efficient cluster</td>\n<td><strong>HPA</strong> (CA only matters once something changes pod demand)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q57\">Exam 4 · Q57</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>Trap: a cron job scaling on a fixed schedule can't track unpredictable traffic <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q57\">Exam 4 · Q57</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Scaling signal for an LB-fronted GKE frontend <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q9\">Exam 1 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q12\">Exam 5 · Q12</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>HPA on the GCLB request-count metric</strong> from Cloud Monitoring.</li>\n<li>Reject probe response times (they measure health, not load), VPA + CA (that is right-sizing, not replica count), and NGINX stats (they duplicate the LB signal and add maintenance).</li>\n<li><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q9\">Exam 1 · Q9</a> says to use GKE's <strong>native</strong> custom metrics support. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q12\">Exam 5 · Q12</a> says to install the custom metrics adapter (see ⚠️).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pub/Sub consumer backlog <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q60\">Exam 1 · Q60</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q51\">Exam 5 · Q51</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Oldest unacked age went from 347 ms to 8,074 ms and undelivered messages from 5 to 14,705, while CPU went from 20% to 30% and memory stayed at 10%. Answer: <strong>scale out Pod replicas</strong>.</li>\n<li>Low CPU does not mean enough throughput. Reject a shorter ack deadline (redeliveries), a virtual waiting room (delays customers) and bigger Pod limits (Pods aren't resource-bound).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Preparing for peak or launch</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario</th>\n<th>First or best action</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Just migrated, peak season coming, \"what <strong>first</strong>?\"</td>\n<td><strong>Load test</strong> to profile performance and scaling. Re-platforming to Cloud Run, multi-region Terraform and pre-provisioning should come <em>after</em> the data</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q15\">Exam 4 · Q15</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Big marketing event, prepare for failures (choose 2)</td>\n<td><strong>Collect the right metrics and alert at meaningful thresholds</strong> + <strong>review capacity needs and plan quotas</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q19\">Exam 4 · Q19</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>New multi-region autoscaling MIG with large instances, plan capacity</td>\n<td><strong>Verify resource needs fit project quota in each region</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q50\">Exam 4 · Q50</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q32\">Exam 5 · Q32</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q19\">Exam 4 · Q19</a> rejects adding service mesh just before the event (new risk), <strong>average</strong> latency (hides the tail) and alerting on <strong>every</strong> failure (noise).</li>\n<li><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q50\">Exam 4 · Q50</a> rejects Cloud Trace for sizing, a hard-coded <code>n2-highcpu-96</code>, and an internal LB.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Cost-efficient bursty batch compute <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q55\">Exam 4 · Q55</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cloud Storage bucket + IAM</strong> for third-party uploads. A <strong>Cloud Run function on the object-finalized trigger</strong> scales up an <strong>autoscaling MIG</strong> whose image <strong>terminates instances when processing is done</strong>.</li>\n<li>Reject a self-managed SFTP VM (always on), an always-on GKE watcher, and doing heavy CPU work inside the function (bounded CPU).</li>\n<li>Hook: <em>tiny trigger, big compute, and the big compute turns itself off.</em></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>GKE maintenance for an interruption-intolerant legacy DB <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q60\">Exam 5 · Q60</a></h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Use a <strong>maintenance exclusion</strong> with scope <strong>\"No minor or node upgrades\"</strong>.</li>\n<li>The Stable channel still upgrades. <code>max-surge-upgrade=0</code> still upgrades. The PDB option is rejected (see ⚠️ for the explanation's error).</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers capacity planning and scaling: horizontal scaling for availability, compounding growth math, zone-loss headroom, risk catalog values and choosing the right GKE autoscaler. It also covers preparing for peak events through load testing and quota checks.","objectives":["After this lesson you can calculate compound growth and utilization after losing a zone.","After this lesson you can fill in risk catalog values (MTTD, MTTR, MTBF, user impact) from a scenario.","After this lesson you can choose between HPA, VPA and the cluster autoscaler, and pick the right scaling signal.","After this lesson you can decide the first step before a peak event or launch, such as load testing or verifying regional quota."],"takeaways":["Availability under load means multiple instances across zones behind a load balancer that distributes traffic. A bigger VM is still a single-zone SPOF.","Growth compounds: 1.1^6 is about 1.77x. Judge headroom after losing a zone (divide by 2/3 with 3 zones), then confirm max node pool size, enable HPA and load test.","Risk catalog: MTTR = failover time, user impact = 1 / number of zones. Change only the field the new property drives.","HPA sets Pod count, the cluster autoscaler sets node count, VPA sets Pod size. Daily cycles need HPA + CA. For an LB-fronted GKE frontend, scale on the load balancer request-count metric.","Low CPU with a growing Pub/Sub backlog means scale out replicas. Before a peak, load test first, and verify quota per region for multi-region MIGs."],"questions":["e1q2","e1q9","e1q37","e1q40","e1q60","e2q12","e4q15","e4q19","e4q50","e4q55","e4q57","e5q12","e5q32","e5q51","e5q60"]},{"id":"d3l8","num":8,"title":"Production readiness and the SRE engagement model","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>PRR finds the service can't meet its SLOs:</strong> decide on reliability improvements and <strong>get them done before handover</strong>. Reject lowering the SLOs, launching without SLOs, and sending support back to developers. That last one is legitimate only if dev can't make the fixes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q29\">Exam 1 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Early operational input on a new product</strong> (data ingestion with unpredictable volume and high cost): <strong>review the design with the product team early in the design phase</strong>. Prototype load tests, staging metrics and production logs all come after design is fixed <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q38\">Exam 4 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Service in active development, productionalization (choose 2):</strong> keyed <strong>monitoring/alerting + early SRE design consultation</strong> (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q19\">Exam 3 · Q19</a>, see ⚠️).</li>\n<li><strong>API version retirement</strong> (least disruption): <strong>introduce the new version → announce deprecation → deprecate → contact remaining users → best-effort support → turn down</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q34\">Exam 2 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>Rules: the replacement exists before any announcement, outreach comes after the announcement, and <strong>turn-down is always last</strong>. Support after turn-down is illogical.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers production readiness reviews and how SRE engages with development teams across a service's lifecycle. It also covers the least-disruptive sequence for retiring an API version.","objectives":["After this lesson you can respond correctly when a production readiness review shows a service can't meet its SLOs.","After this lesson you can explain why SRE input belongs early, in the design phase.","After this lesson you can order the steps of an API version retirement from introducing the new version to turn-down."],"takeaways":["If a PRR shows SLOs can't be met, make the reliability improvements before handover. Don't lower the SLOs or launch without them.","Give early operational input by reviewing the design with the product team during the design phase. Load tests and staging metrics come later.","API retirement: introduce the new version, announce deprecation, deprecate, contact remaining users, give best-effort support, then turn down.","Turn-down is always the last step, and the replacement must exist before any announcement."],"questions":["e1q29","e2q34","e3q19","e4q38"]}],"ref":{"cheat":"<ul>\n<li>SLI = <strong>good / valid</strong> events, with the denominator scoped to the same thing as the numerator <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li>Partial or degraded responses call for a <strong>quality</strong> SLI. Failed responses call for <strong>availability</strong>. Speed calls for <strong>latency</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>SLIs measure user outcomes. Queue size, I/O wait and disk throughput are causes, not SLIs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q38\">Exam 2 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>No code changes allowed: use <strong>synthetic probes + existing request logs</strong>. To cover CDN and LB failures: <strong>client instrumentation + synthetic probes</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q14\">Exam 1 · Q14</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q34\">Exam 1 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>Latency SLO = measured value <strong>+ modest margin</strong>: 120/275 → 150/300, and 160/300 → 190/330 <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q49\">Exam 2 · Q49</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q11\">Exam 4 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>\"X% of requests &lt; N ms\" means a <strong>latency metric + request-based</strong> evaluation. The calendar month is the compliance period <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q35\">Exam 4 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>Extra nine: value = <strong>Δ availability × revenue</strong>. 0.09% × $1M = $900 &lt; $2K, so no <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q10\">Exam 2 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>Detect budget consumption with <strong>SLO + <code>select_slo_burn_rate</code> alert</strong>, fast-burn and slow-burn <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q27\">Exam 2 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li>Set up an <strong>error budget policy with all stakeholders in advance</strong>. \"Always\", cadence caps and lone pipeline plugins are distractors <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q53\">Exam 2 · Q53</a>.</li>\n<li>Budget exhausted and the policy says freeze: <strong>wait</strong>. Canaries and passing tests don't lift a freeze <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q11\">Exam 3 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>Budget nearly gone: postpone the release and <strong>do reliability work</strong>, not feature-backlog work <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q42\">Exam 3 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>Budget gone and a launch pending: <strong>tell the team and let them choose freeze vs. worse UX</strong>. No borrowing or reallocating budget <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q34\">Exam 4 · Q34</a>.</li>\n<li>SLO beaten by a wide margin with happy users: <strong>ship faster or riskier, move engineers elsewhere</strong>, don't tighten the SLO <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q31\">Exam 2 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>Surplus budget but the goals are debt and cost: <strong>eliminate toil</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q23\">Exam 2 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li>IMAG roles are exactly <strong>IC, OL, CL</strong>. After assigning them, the IC <strong>opens the shared comms channel</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q6\">Exam 1 · Q6</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q27\">Exam 1 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li>CL sends <strong>periodic updates to everyone</strong>, each with a <strong>\"next update\" time</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q20\">Exam 1 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Mitigate first</strong>: roll back the release or canary, shift traffic to the previous revision, region or blue env, then diagnose <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q26\">Exam 1 · Q26</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q42\">Exam 2 · Q42</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q56\">Exam 3 · Q56</a>.</li>\n<li>Blue/green: <strong>flip the Service selector</strong> to keep the failing env for investigation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q59\">Exam 1 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>Bad node in a pool: <strong>communicate → load analysis → scale if needed → drain/remove</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q31\">Exam 4 · Q31</a>.</li>\n<li>Living doc <strong>during</strong> the incident, <strong>blameless postmortem after</strong>, published widely <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q44\">Exam 1 · Q44</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q53\">Exam 1 · Q53</a>.</li>\n<li>Postmortem prevention action = an <strong>automated test gate before release</strong>, not people-focused fixes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q12\">Exam 1 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>Postmortem content: <strong>root cause + action items + how it could have been worse</strong>, never a list of culprits <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q40\">Exam 2 · Q40</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q43\">Exam 4 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>One owner per action item</strong> plus collaborators. Culture comes from <strong>leadership participation + rewarding good postmortems</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q35\">Exam 2 · Q35</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q39\">Exam 4 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>Toil fixes: <strong>MIG autohealing</strong> for crashing VMs, a <strong>liveness probe</strong> for Pods that degrade over time <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q22\">Exam 1 · Q22</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q52\">Exam 4 · Q52</a>.</li>\n<li>Scaling: <strong>HPA</strong> for Pod count, <strong>CA</strong> for nodes, <strong>VPA</strong> for Pod size. Daily cycles need HPA + CA <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q12\">Exam 2 · Q12</a>.</li>\n<li>Growth compounds: 1.1⁶ ≈ 1.77. Judge headroom <strong>after losing a zone</strong> (×1.5 for 3 zones) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q40\">Exam 1 · Q40</a>.</li>\n<li>Risk catalog: MTTR = failover time, impact = <strong>1/zones</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q37\">Exam 1 · Q37</a>.</li>\n<li>Capacity planning for a multi-region MIG means <strong>checking quota per region</strong>. For a new or just-migrated app, <strong>load test first</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q50\">Exam 4 · Q50</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q15\">Exam 4 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>PRR shows SLOs can't be met: <strong>fix the service before handover</strong>. Give SRE input <strong>early, at design</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q29\">Exam 1 · Q29</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q38\">Exam 4 · Q38</a>.</li>\n</ul>","rules":[{"lesson":"d3l1","title":"SLIs: what to measure and where to measure it","html":"<ul>\n<li>If the service returns \"something, but incomplete\", choose a <strong>quality</strong> SLI <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>If an option counts infra health (\"healthy microservices / total microservices\"), reject it. That is backend health, not user experience <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q25\">Exam 1 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>If an option correlates strongly with failures, such as a queue metric, it can serve as a leading-indicator <strong>alert</strong>. It is still not the SLI <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q38\">Exam 2 · Q38</a>.</li>\n<li>To extend coverage upstream of your infrastructure, measure <strong>outside</strong> the infrastructure <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q34\">Exam 1 · Q34</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"traps":[{"lesson":"d3l1","title":"SLIs: what to measure and where to measure it","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Percentile at the threshold</strong> (\"calculate the percentile that corresponds to 100 ms\") is close in spirit, but it is not the recommended good/valid form. <strong>Median + p90</strong> only describes the shape of the distribution <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q47\">Exam 1 · Q47</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Average latency</strong> hides the tail. A low average does not mean 95% of requests are fast <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q17\">Exam 3 · Q17</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q19\">Exam 4 · Q19</a>.</li>\n<li>Alerting on <strong>average latency &gt; 150 ms</strong> is not an SLO <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q17\">Exam 3 · Q17</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"questionable":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Issue</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q49\">Exam 5 · Q49</a></strong> vs <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q33\">Exam 2 · Q33</a></strong></td>\n<td>Same scenario, different keys. Exam 2 keys <strong>B + E</strong> (riskier releases + announced planned downtime) and cites the SRE book's Chubby planned-outage practice. Exam 5 keys <strong>B + D</strong> (add SLIs) and calls planned downtime \"anti-SRE\", which contradicts the Chubby example its counterpart cites. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q33\">Exam 2 · Q33</a>'s own explanation says D (more SLIs) fits only when SLIs miss user journeys, which the stem doesn't say. <strong>Prefer B + E</strong>, but expect either key to show up.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q60\">Exam 5 · Q60</a></strong></td>\n<td>Answer IV (maintenance exclusion) is fine, but the explanation says a PDB with <code>maxUnavailable: 100%</code> \"would effectively prevent any Pod disruptions.\" That is backwards: 100% max unavailable permits all Pods to be disrupted.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q12\">Exam 5 · Q12</a></strong> vs <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q9\">Exam 1 · Q9</a></strong></td>\n<td>Exam 5 keys \"install the Stackdriver custom metrics adapter\". <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q9\">Exam 1 · Q9</a> says that adapter step has been superseded by GKE's native custom metrics support and treats self-managed adapters as extra overhead. Pick the option with the LB request-count metric. If both adapter and native versions appear, choose native.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q13\">Exam 3 · Q13</a></strong></td>\n<td>The bank's own trap and takeaway say restoring the Pod template on a <em>shared</em> Deployment can't roll back just one zone. C is valid only if each zone has its own Deployment, which the stem doesn't state. Learn the key, but know that it is shaky.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q19\">Exam 3 · Q19</a></strong></td>\n<td>The bank flags it: the key is <strong>A + E</strong>, but Google's lifecycle guidance directly supports <strong>A + D</strong> (redundancy during active development). The item is ambiguous.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q7\">Exam 2 · Q7</a></strong></td>\n<td>The key is internal-stakeholder request (C) over external (A). Google's SRE text says \"any stakeholder\", and the bank admits the internal/external split is the item's weak point. The solid lesson is rejecting D and E (automation working is not a trigger).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q46\">Exam 5 · Q46</a></strong></td>\n<td>The answer ($900) is right, but the explanation says the $1,000 distractor comes from \"rounding\". The real error is using 0.1% (the old unavailability) instead of the 0.09-point difference <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q10\">Exam 2 · Q10</a>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q32\">Exam 5 · Q32</a></strong></td>\n<td>The answer (verify quota) is right, but the claim that internal load balancers are strictly regional and can't span regions is presented without qualification. Don't rely on it as a general fact. The rejection of option III holds anyway because it doesn't address quota or capacity.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q6\">Exam 5 · Q6</a></strong></td>\n<td>The answer (dedicated channel) matches <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q27\">Exam 1 · Q27</a>. The claim that Google \"explicitly defines this as an early, mandatory step before mitigation actions begin\" overstates the source. The reasoning in <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q27\">Exam 1 · Q27</a> is that mitigation belongs to the OL.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},"glossary":[{"term":"Action item owner","definition":"The single person accountable for a postmortem action item, supported by collaborators as needed.","exam_context":"Exactly one owner per item. Multiple owners or 'collaborators only' are distractors.","lesson":"d3l5","domain":3},{"term":"API deprecation sequence","definition":"The least-disruptive order for retiring an API version: introduce new, announce, deprecate, contact remaining users, best-effort support, turn down.","exam_context":"The replacement exists before any announcement, and turn-down is always last.","lesson":"d3l8","domain":3},{"term":"Availability SLI","definition":"The ratio of successful requests to valid (attempted) requests.","exam_context":"Choose it when requests fail or return 5xx. A per-minute success count is throughput, not availability.","lesson":"d3l1","domain":3},{"term":"Blameless postmortem","definition":"A postmortem that asks what failed in the system, not who failed.","exam_context":"Reject options that list culprits, escalate engineers to management or prescribe personal practices. Rebuilding the timeline through interviews is fine.","lesson":"d3l5","domain":3},{"term":"Blue/green deployment","definition":"Running two environments side by side and switching traffic between them, on GKE through the Service selector.","exam_context":"Change the selector to version: blue to restore traffic and keep green running for investigation. Rolling back traffic is not rolling back a Deployment.","lesson":"d3l4","domain":3},{"term":"Burn rate","definition":"How fast the error budget is being consumed relative to the rate that would use it up exactly over the compliance period.","exam_context":"Alert with select_slo_burn_rate on fast-burn and slow-burn windows. A sustained rate above 1 means the SLO will be missed.","lesson":"d3l3","domain":3},{"term":"Client-side instrumentation","definition":"Measurement collected in the user's client, outside your own infrastructure.","exam_context":"Use it to catch CDN, load balancer or global network failures that server logs and LB metrics can't see.","lesson":"d3l1","domain":3},{"term":"Cluster autoscaler","definition":"The GKE autoscaler that adds or removes nodes when Pods are pending or nodes are underused.","exam_context":"It is bounded by max node pool size and only reacts to pending Pods. Combine it with HPA for daily cycles.","lesson":"d3l7","domain":3},{"term":"Communications Lead (CL)","definition":"The incident role that owns all stakeholder updates, internal and external.","exam_context":"Sends periodic updates to all audiences, each with a committed 'next update' time.","lesson":"d3l4","domain":3},{"term":"Compliance period","definition":"The time span over which SLO compliance is measured, such as a calendar month or rolling window.","exam_context":"'Calendar month' sets the compliance period and says nothing about request-based vs. windows-based evaluation.","lesson":"d3l2","domain":3},{"term":"Compound growth","definition":"Growth that multiplies on the previous period's value, such as 10% per month, giving 1.1^6 (about 1.77x) over 6 months.","exam_context":"The simple-interest answer (60%) is the trap. Then judge headroom after losing a zone.","lesson":"d3l7","domain":3},{"term":"Cordon and drain","definition":"Marking a GKE node unschedulable (cordon) and then gracefully evicting its Pods (drain).","exam_context":"For a suspected compromised node, cordon then drain, and also restrict network access and preserve the suspect Pod. It is not full isolation on its own.","lesson":"d3l4","domain":3},{"term":"Cost of an extra nine","definition":"The value of raising availability by one nine, calculated as the difference in availability times revenue.","exam_context":"99.9% to 99.99% on $1M is a 0.09-point gain worth $900. The $1,000 and $9,000 distractors are off by a factor of 10.","lesson":"d3l2","domain":3},{"term":"Error budget","definition":"The amount of unreliability an SLO allows, equal to 1 - SLO target.","exam_context":"Each SLO has its own budget, and budget can't be requested, granted or reallocated from another SLO.","lesson":"d3l3","domain":3},{"term":"Error budget policy","definition":"An agreement, made in advance with all stakeholders, on what happens (for example, a release freeze) when the budget is exhausted.","exam_context":"When the budget is gone and the policy says freeze, wait. Canaries and passing tests don't lift the freeze.","lesson":"d3l3","domain":3},{"term":"Freshness SLI","definition":"An SLI that measures whether data was updated recently enough, typical for pipelines.","exam_context":"A distractor when the real problem is incomplete or degraded content.","lesson":"d3l1","domain":3},{"term":"Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA)","definition":"The GKE autoscaler that changes the number of Pod replicas based on a metric.","exam_context":"Use it for unpredictable, stateless traffic. For an LB-fronted frontend, scale on the load balancer request-count metric.","lesson":"d3l7","domain":3},{"term":"Implicit SLO","definition":"The tighter reliability level users come to depend on when a service greatly exceeds its stated SLO.","exam_context":"The reason not to over-deliver: release faster or take more risk instead of tightening the SLO.","lesson":"d3l2","domain":3},{"term":"Incident Commander (IC)","definition":"The person in overall command of an incident who holds every role not yet delegated.","exam_context":"After assigning an OL and a CL, the IC's next step is a dedicated shared communication channel, not hands-on mitigation.","lesson":"d3l4","domain":3},{"term":"Incident Management at Google (IMAG)","definition":"Google's incident response framework with exactly three roles: Incident Commander, Operations Lead and Communications Lead.","exam_context":"Roles such as 'Engineering Lead' or 'Customer Impact Assessor' are distractors.","lesson":"d3l4","domain":3},{"term":"Incident state document","definition":"A living document that responders update in real time during an incident.","exam_context":"Use it during the incident. The postmortem is written after resolution for the wider org.","lesson":"d3l4","domain":3},{"term":"Latency SLI","definition":"The share of valid requests served faster than a threshold, expressed as good/valid.","exam_context":"For 'fast enough' goals, prefer it over average latency or a percentile-at-threshold calculation.","lesson":"d3l1","domain":3},{"term":"Liveness probe","definition":"A Kubernetes health check that restarts a container when it stops responding correctly.","exam_context":"For Pods that go bad after hours, add an HTTP liveness probe rather than a cron job that kills old Pods.","lesson":"d3l6","domain":3},{"term":"Load testing","definition":"Running controlled traffic against a service to profile its performance and scaling behavior.","exam_context":"For a just-migrated app ahead of peak season it is the first step, before re-platforming or pre-provisioning.","lesson":"d3l7","domain":3},{"term":"Log-based alert","definition":"A Cloud Monitoring alert that fires on matching log entries.","exam_context":"For fast notification of a VM stop, alert on protoPayload.methodName = v1.compute.instances.stop.","lesson":"d3l6","domain":3},{"term":"Maintenance exclusion","definition":"A GKE setting that blocks upgrades during a defined window, with a scope such as 'No minor or node upgrades'.","exam_context":"Use it for interruption-intolerant workloads. The Stable channel and max-surge-upgrade=0 still upgrade.","lesson":"d3l7","domain":3},{"term":"MIG autohealing","definition":"A managed instance group feature that recreates VMs failing a health check.","exam_context":"A size-1 MIG with autohealing replaces manual VM recreation. A load balancer only routes around the failure.","lesson":"d3l6","domain":3},{"term":"Mitigate first","definition":"The rule of stopping user impact before diagnosing root cause, usually by rolling back the most recent change.","exam_context":"Revert the canary or release, or shift traffic to the previous revision, region or blue environment, before reaching for logs or traces.","lesson":"d3l4","domain":3},{"term":"MTBF","definition":"Mean time between failures, such as 90 days for a once-a-quarter failure.","exam_context":"Leave it unchanged unless the scenario changes failure frequency.","lesson":"d3l7","domain":3},{"term":"MTTD","definition":"Mean time to detect a failure.","exam_context":"Stays the same when the detection setup doesn't change.","lesson":"d3l7","domain":3},{"term":"MTTR","definition":"Mean time to recover, which in a risk catalog equals the failover duration.","exam_context":"If the new database fails over twice as slowly (10 min to 20 min), only MTTR changes.","lesson":"d3l7","domain":3},{"term":"Operations Lead (OL)","definition":"The incident role that owns the hands-on technical response: diagnosis, mitigation and system changes.","exam_context":"When removing a bad node, the OL communicates intent, runs a load analysis, scales if needed, then drains and removes it.","lesson":"d3l4","domain":3},{"term":"Planned downtime","definition":"An announced, deliberate outage that stops dependents from assuming the service never fails, as in Google's Chubby example.","exam_context":"When an SLO is greatly exceeded and confirmed appropriate, announced planned downtime plus riskier releases is the preferred answer.","lesson":"d3l2","domain":3},{"term":"Postmortem","definition":"A written record of an incident covering root cause, resolution, lessons learned and action items, produced after resolution.","exam_context":"Write one after a multi-hour outage and publish it widely, not just to one manager.","lesson":"d3l5","domain":3},{"term":"Postmortem trigger","definition":"A condition that requires a postmortem, such as data loss, user-visible impact, on-call intervention, long resolution or monitoring failure.","exam_context":"Automation working as intended (automatic CD rollback, monitoring detecting a failed instance) is not a trigger.","lesson":"d3l5","domain":3},{"term":"Production Readiness Review (PRR)","definition":"An SRE assessment of whether a service is ready to be supported in production.","exam_context":"If it shows SLOs can't be met, make the reliability fixes before handover. Don't lower the SLOs.","lesson":"d3l8","domain":3},{"term":"Quality SLI","definition":"The ratio of non-degraded responses to total responses, for services that can return partial results.","exam_context":"Pick it when a page is served but some widgets or content are missing. Freshness is the distractor.","lesson":"d3l1","domain":3},{"term":"Request-based SLO","definition":"A Cloud Monitoring SLO evaluation method that counts good requests over total requests.","exam_context":"Use it when the goal is stated per request ('90% of requests under 300 ms'), even if the wording says 'of the time'.","lesson":"d3l2","domain":3},{"term":"Service Level Indicator (SLI)","definition":"A measure of the service users receive, expressed as good events divided by valid events.","exam_context":"Reject options that measure backend causes (queue size, I/O wait) or infrastructure health instead of user outcomes.","lesson":"d3l1","domain":3},{"term":"Service Level Objective (SLO)","definition":"A target value for an SLI over a compliance period.","exam_context":"Set it from current measured performance plus a modest margin. Don't tighten it to match observed reliability or loosen it to match a failing SLI.","lesson":"d3l2","domain":3},{"term":"Synthetic client","definition":"An automated probe that sends requests to the service the way a user would, from outside the serving infrastructure.","exam_context":"Pair it with existing request logs when code changes aren't allowed, or with client-side instrumentation to catch CDN and LB failures.","lesson":"d3l1","domain":3},{"term":"Toil","definition":"Recurring manual operational work that automation could eliminate, such as recreating a crashing VM by hand.","exam_context":"When goals are reducing technical debt and ops cost, spend surplus budget on finding and automating toil away.","lesson":"d3l6","domain":3},{"term":"Uptime check","definition":"A Cloud Monitoring probe that checks whether an endpoint is reachable.","exam_context":"Right for 'notify as soon as an endpoint is unreachable', but wrong for tracking how fast the error budget is spent.","lesson":"d3l3","domain":3},{"term":"Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA)","definition":"The GKE autoscaler that adjusts the resource requests of each Pod.","exam_context":"Use it for steady load with mis-sized requests. It doesn't set replica count.","lesson":"d3l7","domain":3},{"term":"Windows-based SLO","definition":"A Cloud Monitoring SLO evaluation method that counts good time windows over total windows.","exam_context":"Fits goals stated per interval, or a raw metric that is already a percentile.","lesson":"d3l2","domain":3}]},{"n":4,"title":"Implementing Observability Practices and Troubleshooting Issues","short":"Observability & Troubleshooting","weight":"~23%","desc":"Cloud Logging and Monitoring, log-based metrics, alerting, Trace/Profiler/Error Reporting and troubleshooting.","lessons":[{"id":"d4l1","num":1,"title":"Cloud Logging: sinks, aggregated sinks and destinations","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>sink</strong> routes log entries that match a filter to <strong>one destination</strong>. Two destinations need at least two sinks <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q16\">Exam 2 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>An <strong>aggregated sink</strong> on a <strong>folder</strong> or <strong>organization</strong> covers all child projects, <strong>including projects created later</strong>. A project-level sink covers only its own project <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q9\">Exam 2 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q16\">Exam 2 · Q16</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>Filtering an org-level sink by project ID does <strong>not</strong> follow future projects, and filters are limited to 20,000 characters <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q16\">Exam 2 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>The Log Router itself costs nothing extra, but you still pay for storage at the destination <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>New sinks to Cloud Storage can take hours to start routing and are processed hourly, so they are a poor fit for real-time needs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Destination comparison</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Destination</th>\n<th>Use it when the question says…</th>\n<th>Don't use it for…</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cloud Storage bucket</strong></td>\n<td>\"archive\", \"cheapest\", \"years\", \"immutable\" (add a <strong>retention policy + Bucket Lock</strong>)</td>\n<td>real-time dashboards (it is processed hourly)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q9\">Exam 2 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q43\">Exam 2 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>BigQuery dataset</strong></td>\n<td>SQL analysis, live dashboard in Data Studio/Looker Studio, \"central repository for analysis\"</td>\n<td>locked retention (BigQuery expiration can be changed) or the cheapest cold archive</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q9\">Exam 2 · Q9</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pub/Sub topic</strong></td>\n<td>an external <strong>SIEM</strong> or third-party tool that \"ingests via Pub/Sub\", or streaming to another system</td>\n<td>storage: retention is only weeks (31 days max)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q43\">Exam 2 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q53\">Exam 4 · Q53</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cloud Logging (log) bucket</strong></td>\n<td>centralize logs and still query and alert natively in Cloud Logging, or set custom retention (for example, 365 days)</td>\n<td>consumers that can read only Pub/Sub</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q53\">Exam 4 · Q53</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Every current <strong>and future</strong> project in the org\" → <strong>org-level aggregated sink</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q9\">Exam 2 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Only projects in the Prod folder\" → <strong>folder-level aggregated sink</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Dev logs → dev_dataset, Prod logs → prod_dataset, fewest sinks, future projects\" → <strong>one aggregated sink per folder</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q16\">Exam 2 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>\"7-year regulatory retention, guard against human error\" → <strong>org aggregated sink → Cloud Storage + locked retention policy</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q9\">Exam 2 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Archive 7 years at lowest cost\" → <strong>named sink → Cloud Storage bucket</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q43\">Exam 2 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Security team wants alerting plus near-real-time analysis on the logs\" (no external SIEM) → <strong>folder sink → Cloud Logging bucket</strong>, not Pub/Sub <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>\"SIEM ingests only through Pub/Sub\" → <strong>org sink → Pub/Sub topic</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Interactive, shareable, real-time dashboard with quarterly aggregation\" → <strong>sink → BigQuery → Data Studio</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>\"GKE + Cloud Run logs into BigQuery, low cost and complexity\" → <strong>native Cloud Logging collection + sink to BigQuery</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Check <strong>scope</strong> and <strong>destination</strong> separately. Each distractor usually gets one of them right <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q9\">Exam 2 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Repeat for each folder\" still misses folders created later. The organization level is the only fully automatic scope <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>Pub/Sub <em>can</em> be a sink destination, but it is transport, not storage <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q43\">Exam 2 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Exporting to BigQuery and then to CSV and Google Sheets gives a <strong>static snapshot</strong>, not a live dashboard. Sheets also refreshes no more often than every 15 minutes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>Avoid custom apps that write logs directly to BigQuery or Cloud Storage, App Engine \"pullers\", Datadog relays and manual uploads. Use native collection plus a sink <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q43\">Exam 2 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers how the Log Router uses sinks to send log entries to destinations, and how aggregated sinks at the folder or organization level cover many projects at once. Exam questions often hinge on choosing the right scope (project, folder, org) and the right destination (Cloud Storage, BigQuery, Pub/Sub, log bucket) for a stated requirement.","objectives":["After this lesson you can explain what a sink is and why two destinations require two sinks.","After this lesson you can choose between project, folder and organization aggregated sinks, including for projects created in the future.","After this lesson you can match Cloud Storage, BigQuery, Pub/Sub and Cloud Logging buckets to archive, analysis, SIEM and native-query requirements.","After this lesson you can spot distractors that get scope right but destination wrong (or the reverse)."],"takeaways":["One sink routes to exactly one destination; folder or org aggregated sinks automatically include future child projects.","Cheapest multi-year or immutable archive: sink to Cloud Storage with a retention policy plus Bucket Lock.","External SIEM that ingests via Pub/Sub: org sink to a Pub/Sub topic; Pub/Sub is transport (max 31 days), never storage.","Live, shareable SQL dashboards on logs: sink to BigQuery, then Data Studio/Looker Studio; a CSV/Sheets export is only a static snapshot.","Prefer native collection plus a sink over custom apps, pullers or third-party relays that write logs elsewhere."],"questions":["e1q28","e2q9","e2q16","e2q25","e2q43","e3q25","e4q9","e4q53"]},{"id":"d4l2","num":2,"title":"Cloud Logging: exclusions, log buckets, retention and cost","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>An <strong>exclusion filter</strong> stops matching entries from being ingested and stored. It is <strong>not</strong> an access control <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>A sink's <strong>export</strong> sends a <em>copy</em> and does not stop the original from being kept in <code>_Default</code>. To capture entries elsewhere <em>and</em> keep them out of Cloud Logging, you need <strong>sink + exclusion</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q16\">Exam 1 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>For high-volume workloads, the cost driver is <strong>ingestion volume</strong>, not retention length <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>Log-based metrics are still evaluated on entries that exclusions drop, so metrics recover visibility into excluded logs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>A custom log bucket can have its retention set (for example, <strong>365 days</strong>). Combine it with a sink, then give the consumer access <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q53\">Exam 4 · Q53</a>.</li>\n<li>Disabling the <code>_Default</code> sink (<code>gcloud logging sinks update _Default --disabled</code>) stops <strong>all</strong> routing into <code>_Default</code> for the project, including system logs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Cut cost of terabytes/day, keep critical ops/security info\" → <strong>exclude all non-ERROR logs + build log-based metrics for key indicators</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>\"GKE dev cluster: drop app logs (devs use <code>kubectl logs</code>), keep operational logs\" → <code>gcloud container clusters update --logging=SYSTEM</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Keep logs 1 year for client to import, minimal code changes\" (Cloud Run) → <strong>custom log bucket with 365-day retention + sink + client access</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q53\">Exam 4 · Q53</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Retention and sharing\" → a bucket-and-sink problem, not a code problem <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q53\">Exam 4 · Q53</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Distractor</th>\n<th>Why it's wrong</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Exclude only DEBUG</td>\n<td>INFO (the bulk of the volume) is still ingested</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shorten <code>_Default</code> retention to 15 days</td>\n<td>Doesn't reduce ingestion, and you lose investigation data</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sample across all severities</td>\n<td>Drops ERROR/security entries too</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--logging=WORKLOAD</code></td>\n<td>That is the app-log source, the opposite of the goal. <code>SYSTEM</code> is required on all cluster types</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exclusion <code>resource.type=\"k8s_container\"</code></td>\n<td>Also matches system Pods and filters after collection. Excluding GKE logs can leave nothing to share with Support</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pub/Sub for 1-year retention</td>\n<td>31-day maximum</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q53\">Exam 4 · Q53</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Answer flip: use an exclusion filter (instead of <code>--logging=SYSTEM</code>) if you must keep <em>part</em> of the app logs, or on <strong>Autopilot</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a>.</p>\n</blockquote>","summary":"This lesson explains exclusion filters, the _Default sink, custom log buckets and retention, and what actually drives Cloud Logging cost. The exam tests whether you know that ingestion volume, not retention, is the cost lever and that a sink copies logs rather than removing them.","objectives":["After this lesson you can use exclusion filters to reduce ingestion cost while keeping critical ERROR and security data.","After this lesson you can combine a sink with an exclusion to capture logs elsewhere and keep them out of Cloud Logging.","After this lesson you can configure a custom log bucket with a specific retention (e.g., 365 days) and share it via a sink.","After this lesson you can choose between --logging=SYSTEM and exclusion filters for GKE log volume."],"takeaways":["Cost of high-volume logging is driven by ingestion; exclude non-ERROR logs and keep visibility with log-based metrics.","A sink sends a copy; to keep entries out of _Default you need sink + exclusion.","Exclusion filters are not access control; they drop entire entries before storage.","GKE: drop app logs but keep operational logs with gcloud container clusters update --logging=SYSTEM; use an exclusion instead on Autopilot or to keep part of app logs.","Long retention for sharing is a log bucket + sink problem, not a code change; Pub/Sub cannot hold logs for a year."],"questions":["e1q16","e3q33","e4q6","e4q41","e4q53"]},{"id":"d4l3","num":3,"title":"Sensitive data / PII in logs","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Goal in the stem</th>\n<th>Mechanism</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Capture the PII entries securely <strong>and</strong> stop retaining them in Cloud Logging</td>\n<td><strong>Advanced filter → sink to Cloud Storage + exclusion with the same filter</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q16\">Exam 1 · Q16</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stop specific fields from being written to <em>new</em> entries <strong>as fast as possible</strong> (Compute Engine, logging agent)</td>\n<td>Agent-side Fluentd <strong><code>filter_record_transformer</code></strong> (built-in filter, <code>remove_keys</code>)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keep the log, hide <strong>one field</strong> from most people, let one group see it</td>\n<td>Mark <code>jsonPayload.&lt;field&gt;</code> as a <strong>restricted field</strong> on the log bucket + grant <strong>Log Field Accessor</strong> (<code>roles/logging.fieldAccessor</code>) to that group</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Collect GKE app logs but never expose PII</td>\n<td><strong>Sensitive Data Protection</strong> inspection/redaction <em>before</em> storing (a preprocessing integration, not a built-in Cloud Logging toggle)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Encryption ≠ redaction.</strong> An encrypted bucket still shows PII to authorized readers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Sampling</strong> reduces volume but does not remove PII, and <strong>disabling logging</strong> breaks the \"must collect\" requirement <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Exclusion</strong> to hide a field drops the whole entry and breaks mandatory capture <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>A conditional IAM binding (<code>resource.name.extract(\".../buckets/{bucket}/\") == \"_Default\"</code>) is real syntax, but on its own it hides nothing <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li><code>fluent-plugin-record-reformer</code> is an add-on gem framed as an output plugin. The built-in filter is the answer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Wait for developers to patch\" is the long-term root-cause fix, not the <em>fastest</em> one <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>Gating a field behind an environment variable plus CODEOWNERS controls <em>whether</em> data is written, not <em>who can view</em> it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers the different ways to handle PII and sensitive data in logs: excluding whole entries, scrubbing fields at the agent, restricting field visibility, and redacting before storage. Exam stems distinguish between 'stop retaining', 'stop writing fast' and 'hide from most people', and each maps to a different mechanism.","objectives":["After this lesson you can capture sensitive entries securely in Cloud Storage while excluding them from Cloud Logging.","After this lesson you can hide a single field from most users with restricted fields and the Log Field Accessor role.","After this lesson you can choose agent-side field removal as the fastest way to stop new sensitive fields being written.","After this lesson you can reject encryption, sampling and exclusion when the requirement is redaction or field-level access."],"takeaways":["Capture securely and stop retaining in Logging: advanced filter sink to Cloud Storage + exclusion with the same filter.","Hide one field but keep the entry: mark it as a restricted field on the log bucket and grant roles/logging.fieldAccessor to the allowed group.","Fastest way to stop a field being written: remove it at the logging agent, not wait for a code patch.","Encryption is not redaction, sampling does not remove PII, and exclusion drops the whole entry.","Prefer native options (restricted fields, agent-side removal) over custom Sensitive Data Protection preprocessing when they fit."],"questions":["e1q16","e2q41","e3q2","e4q6"]},{"id":"d4l4","num":4,"title":"Log-based metrics","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Log-based metrics turn a log filter into a time series that you can chart and alert on in Cloud Monitoring <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q10\">Exam 5 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>User-defined log-based metric types are <strong>counter</strong> and <strong>distribution</strong>. <strong>Boolean is not a supported metric type</strong> (a Boolean <em>label</em> is) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q59\">Exam 3 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>You can <strong>extract a field as a label</strong> (for example, <code>matchedFieldName</code>) and group charts by it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q59\">Exam 3 · Q59</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"How often does log event X happen over time\" (cache misses) → <strong>log-based metric + Cloud Monitoring dashboard</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q10\">Exam 5 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Requests from a specific IP\" with Ops Agent installed → <strong>Ops Agent logging receiver (access logs) + log-based metric</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q3\">Exam 2 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q28\">Exam 5 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Cloud Armor blocked requests for <code>/api</code> prefix, broken down by parameter name\" → <strong>counter metric filtered on <code>configuredAction=\"DENY\"</code> + <code>requestUrl</code> prefix, label = <code>matchedFieldName</code></strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q59\">Exam 3 · Q59</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A Data Studio/Looker Studio link to Cloud Logging is not a native path (it typically needs a BigQuery export first) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>Profiler cannot read logs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>A BigQuery sink plus scheduled queries is overkill for a simple frequency chart <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>An Ops Agent <strong>metrics receiver</strong> reports fixed metric sets and <strong>can't break traffic down by client IP</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q3\">Exam 2 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li><code>networksecurity.googleapis.com/https/request_count</code> with <code>blocked=true</code> gives totals only, with no URL or parameter dimension <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q59\">Exam 3 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>Filtering on <code>configuredAction=\"ALLOW\"</code> selects permitted traffic, which is the wrong half <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q59\">Exam 3 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>A log-based metric + alert tells you <strong>that</strong> requests are slow, not <strong>where</strong> they are slow <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q18\">Exam 3 · Q18</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson explains log-based metrics, which turn a log filter into a Cloud Monitoring time series you can chart and alert on. The exam uses them for 'how often does X happen' questions and for breaking traffic down by fields such as client IP or Cloud Armor parameter name.","objectives":["After this lesson you can create counter and distribution log-based metrics from a log filter.","After this lesson you can extract a log field as a metric label and group charts by it.","After this lesson you can pair the Ops Agent logging receiver with a log-based metric to count requests per client IP.","After this lesson you can explain why log-based metrics show that something happens, not where latency comes from."],"takeaways":["Frequency of a log event over time: log-based metric + Cloud Monitoring dashboard, not BigQuery scheduled queries or Profiler.","User-defined log-based metric types are counter and distribution; Boolean is not a metric type (only a label type).","Extract fields as labels (e.g., matchedFieldName) to break down Cloud Armor DENY requests by parameter.","Ops Agent metrics receivers report fixed metric sets and cannot break down by client IP; use access logs + a log-based metric.","Log-based metrics still count entries dropped by exclusion filters."],"questions":["e1q18","e2q3","e3q18","e3q59","e5q10","e5q28"]},{"id":"d4l5","num":5,"title":"Log collection: Ops Agent, GKE, Cloud Run","html":"<h3>Compute Engine</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>VMs <strong>do not</strong> have the agent by default. \"New app's logs missing\" → first confirm the <strong>Ops Agent is installed</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q55\">Exam 2 · Q55</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Logs missing, legacy agent\" → first check whether the agent <strong>process is running</strong> (<code>ps ax | grep fluentd</code>) before reinstalling or checking scopes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q51\">Exam 1 · Q51</a>.</li>\n<li>Writing logs requires the <code>logging.write</code> scope, which <code>cloud-platform</code> already covers. <code>monitoring.write</code> belongs to the monitoring agent <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q51\">Exam 1 · Q51</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Agent running + public IP + no logs + user-managed SA\" → grant the SA <strong>Logs Writer</strong> (<code>roles/logging.logWriter</code>). Don't export keys, switch to the default SA, or enable Private Google Access (PGA only matters for VMs with no external IP) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q25\">Exam 4 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Every current <strong>and future</strong> VM, keep agent <strong>up to date</strong>\" → <strong>agent policy</strong> via gcloud. A one-off install from the dashboard, a script over an Asset Inventory snapshot, or baking the agent into an image won't keep it current <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q26\">Exam 2 · Q26</a>.</li>\n<li>Developers need app logs with least config and least privilege → logging agent (today: <strong>Ops Agent</strong>) + <strong>Logs Viewer</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q46\">Exam 1 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q3\">Exam 5 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>The legacy logging and monitoring agents are end-of-support. The <strong>Ops Agent</strong> collects both logs and metrics <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q46\">Exam 1 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q51\">Exam 1 · Q51</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Troubleshooting order for missing VM logs</strong> (synthesized from <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q55\">Exam 2 · Q55</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q51\">Exam 1 · Q51</a> and <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q25\">Exam 4 · Q25</a>): agent installed? → agent process running? → service account has Logs Writer? → (only if no external IP) network path / PGA.</p>\n<h3>GKE</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The built-in node agent collects only <strong>stdout/stderr</strong> + system logs, not files inside containers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q48\">Exam 1 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Third-party app writes to <code>/var/log/app_messages.log</code>, can't be modified\" → <strong>sidecar that tails the file to stdout + shared volume</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q48\">Exam 1 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li>Trap: a custom Fluentd DaemonSet can't see a file inside a container without a host-mounted volume, and it adds a second agent to maintain. Pick it only if the file is already on a host path <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q48\">Exam 1 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li><code>--logging=SYSTEM</code> = operational logs only. <code>WORKLOAD</code> = application logs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Cloud Run</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>stdout/stderr capture is automatic, and there is <strong>no customer-installable agent</strong>. A line becomes <code>jsonPayload</code> only if it is written as a single valid JSON object <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q20\">Exam 4 · Q20</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Make logs structured JSON\" → change the app to use the <strong>Cloud Logging client library and emit JSON</strong>. A Fluent Bit sidecar or \"agent converts text to JSON\" are wrong <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q20\">Exam 4 · Q20</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers how logs get collected on Compute Engine (Ops Agent), GKE (node agent, stdout/stderr) and Cloud Run (automatic capture). Exam questions often present missing logs or unstructured logs and ask for the first troubleshooting step or the least-effort fix.","objectives":["After this lesson you can troubleshoot missing VM logs in order: agent installed, agent running, service account has Logs Writer, then network path.","After this lesson you can keep the Ops Agent installed and current on all current and future VMs with an agent policy.","After this lesson you can collect file-based logs from an unmodifiable GKE container using a sidecar.","After this lesson you can produce structured JSON logs on Cloud Run with the Cloud Logging client library."],"takeaways":["Compute Engine VMs do not have a logging agent by default; the Ops Agent collects both logs and metrics.","Agent running but no logs with a user-managed SA: grant roles/logging.logWriter; don't export keys or switch to the default SA.","Current and future VMs with the agent kept up to date: Ops Agent agent policy, not images or one-off scripts.","GKE only collects stdout/stderr; tail a file to stdout with a sidecar and shared volume.","Cloud Run has no installable agent; a line becomes jsonPayload only if it is a single valid JSON object."],"questions":["e1q46","e1q48","e1q51","e2q26","e2q55","e4q20","e4q25","e4q41","e5q3"]},{"id":"d4l6","num":6,"title":"Cloud Monitoring metrics: kinds, types and built-in metrics","html":"<h3>Metric kind / value type</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Choice</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Latency experienced by <strong>all</strong> users (spread, percentiles, tail)</td>\n<td><code>valueType=DISTRIBUTION</code>, <code>metricKind=GAUGE</code> (custom metric), <strong>Heatmap</strong> chart</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q1\">Exam 1 · Q1</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q13\">Exam 5 · Q13</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Running total</td>\n<td>CUMULATIVE (scalar, shown as a Line chart), which is <em>not</em> a distribution</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q1\">Exam 1 · Q1</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>METRIC_KIND_UNSPECIFIED</code></td>\n<td>API placeholder, never a valid choice</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q1\">Exam 1 · Q1</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q13\">Exam 5 · Q13</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>Scalar types (DOUBLE, INT64) collapse each interval into one number and hide the tail <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q1\">Exam 1 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>\"No load balancer\" means there are no managed LB latency metrics, so the app must emit its own custom metric <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q1\">Exam 1 · Q1</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Built-in metrics that were tested</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question asks</th>\n<th>Metric</th>\n<th>Distractor</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>App Engine (flex) current connections, whole app</td>\n<td><code>flex/connections/current</code></td>\n<td><code>flex/instance/connections/current</code> (per instance); <code>tcp_ssl_proxy/*</code> (load balancer metrics)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q33\">Exam 1 · Q33</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Which <strong>Pods</strong> cause node DiskPressure (no exec)</td>\n<td><code>container/ephemeral_storage/used_bytes</code> in Metrics Explorer, grouped by pod</td>\n<td><code>node/ephemeral_storage/used_bytes</code> (shows which nodes, not which Pods); <code>df</code> (needs exec)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q23\">Exam 4 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q39\">Exam 5 · Q39</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Containers using the most CPU/memory (hybrid GKE)</td>\n<td>Built-in <strong>Kubernetes Engine monitoring</strong> (Cloud Operations for GKE)</td>\n<td>Prometheus \"collecting logs\", custom metrics + groups, logs → BigQuery</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q38\">Exam 1 · Q38</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q42\">Exam 5 · Q42</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Getting app metrics in</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>New GKE app, centralized app-level metrics, minimal setup → <strong>OpenTelemetry client libraries with a Cloud Monitoring exporter</strong>. Writing directly to the Monitoring API is the <em>most</em> manual path. Pub/Sub has no metric semantics, and log-based metrics are awkward for histograms or gauges <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q24\">Exam 1 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li>Custom metrics are for data Cloud Monitoring does <strong>not</strong> already collect. Container CPU and memory are already system metrics <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q38\">Exam 1 · Q38</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers Cloud Monitoring metric kinds and value types, the built-in metrics that appear on the exam, and how to get application metrics in. Expect questions that ask you to pick the right metric descriptor for latency or the right built-in metric for a GKE or App Engine symptom.","objectives":["After this lesson you can choose DISTRIBUTION value type with GAUGE kind and a heatmap to show latency for all users.","After this lesson you can explain why scalar types hide tail latency and why METRIC_KIND_UNSPECIFIED is never valid.","After this lesson you can identify which Pods cause node DiskPressure using container ephemeral storage metrics.","After this lesson you can use OpenTelemetry with a Cloud Monitoring exporter for new application metrics."],"takeaways":["Latency for all users: custom metric with valueType=DISTRIBUTION, metricKind=GAUGE, shown as a Heatmap.","Which Pods cause DiskPressure without exec: container/ephemeral_storage/used_bytes grouped by pod, not the node metric.","Container CPU/memory are already system metrics via Kubernetes Engine monitoring; don't build custom metrics for them.","New GKE app metrics with minimal setup: OpenTelemetry client libraries with a Cloud Monitoring exporter.","App Engine flex whole-app connections: flex/connections/current, not the per-instance metric."],"questions":["e1q1","e1q24","e1q33","e1q38","e4q23","e5q13","e5q39","e5q42"]},{"id":"d4l7","num":7,"title":"Prometheus on GKE and hybrid metrics","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus (GMP)</strong> with <strong>managed collection</strong> stores data in Cloud Monitoring's backend (global, highly available). You query it with <strong>PromQL</strong> in Metrics Explorer (or Grafana pointed at it), and it covers custom workload metrics <strong>and</strong> GKE default metrics <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q15\">Exam 2 · Q15</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q22\">Exam 3 · Q22</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q32\">Exam 3 · Q32</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q13\">Exam 4 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>The Ops Agent's Prometheus receiver is for <strong>Compute Engine VMs</strong>. For GKE, Google recommends managed collection <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q22\">Exam 3 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>GMP data is <strong>not</strong> queried from BigQuery <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q13\">Exam 4 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Bindplane</strong> is Google's documented OpenTelemetry pipeline that brings on-prem/hybrid telemetry into Cloud Monitoring, at no extra cost to Cloud Observability customers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q50\">Exam 3 · Q50</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Already emit Prometheus metrics on GKE, least maintenance\" → <strong>enable GMP managed collection</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q32\">Exam 3 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Many clusters, global PromQL view, low overhead\" → <strong>GMP</strong>, not hierarchical or cross-service federation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q15\">Exam 2 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Multi-project GKE Prometheus + flexible query\" → <strong>GMP managed collection + Metrics Explorer, with a metrics scope for cross-project</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q22\">Exam 3 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>\"HA, custom + default GKE metrics, all clusters\" → <strong>managed collection + query in Cloud Monitoring</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q13\">Exam 4 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>\"On-prem K8s + several GCP projects, OpenTelemetry, one place, easy to support\" → <strong>scoping project + Bindplane</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q50\">Exam 3 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>\"GMP managed collection, metrics missing, <strong>no errors</strong>\" → validate the <strong>port in the <code>PodMonitoring</code> resource</strong> (exporter/scrape config). Check the write quota only if you see HTTP <strong>429</strong> errors <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q21\">Exam 3 · Q21</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Distractor</th>\n<th>Why not</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Self-run Prometheus / Prometheus Operator + remote-write / Thanos / Grafana</td>\n<td>You operate every tier, so overhead is not minimized</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q13\">Exam 4 · Q13</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q32\">Exam 3 · Q32</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q50\">Exam 3 · Q50</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hierarchical federation</td>\n<td>Works, but self-managed. Correct only if a managed service is forbidden</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q15\">Exam 2 · Q15</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cross-service federation</td>\n<td>Combines one service's metrics with another's; it is not a global roll-up</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q15\">Exam 2 · Q15</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Workload metrics within Cloud Observability for GKE\"</td>\n<td>Not the Prometheus-compatible PromQL/Grafana path the team relies on</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q15\">Exam 2 · Q15</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Self-deployed OTel Collector for Prometheus metrics on GKE</td>\n<td>Valid, but it is extra infrastructure. GMP is the primary recommendation</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q32\">Exam 3 · Q32</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>roles/monitoring.servicesViewer</code></td>\n<td>SLO/service read-only. Ingestion needs Monitoring Metric Writer</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q21\">Exam 3 · Q21</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Installing Grafana</td>\n<td>Optional UI that fixes nothing about scraping</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q21\">Exam 3 · Q21</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","summary":"This lesson covers Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus (GMP) on GKE and bringing hybrid or on-prem telemetry into Cloud Monitoring with Bindplane. The exam favors managed, low-overhead options over self-run Prometheus, federation or Thanos stacks.","objectives":["After this lesson you can choose GMP managed collection for Prometheus metrics on GKE with least maintenance.","After this lesson you can query GMP data with PromQL in Metrics Explorer across projects via a metrics scope.","After this lesson you can troubleshoot missing GMP metrics by checking the PodMonitoring port or 429 quota errors.","After this lesson you can bring on-prem OpenTelemetry telemetry into a scoping project using Bindplane."],"takeaways":["Prometheus on GKE, low ops, global PromQL view: GMP managed collection, not hierarchical or cross-service federation.","GMP stores data in Cloud Monitoring's backend; query with PromQL in Metrics Explorer or Grafana, not BigQuery.","The Ops Agent Prometheus receiver is for Compute Engine VMs; GKE uses managed collection.","Missing GMP metrics with no errors: check the port in the PodMonitoring resource; check write quota only on HTTP 429.","On-prem K8s + multiple GCP projects via OpenTelemetry: scoping project + Bindplane."],"questions":["e2q15","e3q21","e3q22","e3q32","e3q50","e4q13"]},{"id":"d4l8","num":8,"title":"Metrics scopes, dashboards and sharing","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>metrics scope</strong> lets a <strong>scoping project</strong> view metrics from multiple monitored projects. \"Workspaces\" are the legacy name <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q50\">Exam 1 · Q50</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>A metrics scope consolidates <strong>metrics, not logs</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Dashboards are project-scoped and governed by that project's IAM <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q54\">Exam 4 · Q54</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Dashboards showing only one folder's projects (Dev/Staging/Prod)\" → <strong>a new scoping project per folder</strong> (don't reuse an app project) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q54\">Exam 5 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Share one chart with the SRE team only, least privilege\" → <strong>share chart by URL + <code>roles/monitoring.viewer</code></strong> on the scoping project <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q50\">Exam 1 · Q50</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q1\">Exam 5 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Dev team should keep using <em>the same live</em> dashboard\" → <strong><code>roles/monitoring.viewer</code> + dashboard URL</strong>, not <code>roles/viewer</code> (too broad) and not a JSON/CSV export <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q51\">Exam 3 · Q51</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Share a dashboard with a <strong>partner team</strong>\" (no access to your project) → <strong>export the dashboard JSON</strong> so they can import their own copy <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q54\">Exam 4 · Q54</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Sharing comparison</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n<th>Why the others lose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Internal team, same project, live data</td>\n<td>URL + Monitoring Viewer</td>\n<td>Dashboard Configuration Viewer can't read metric data (charts error out); a project ID alone doesn't deliver the chart <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q50\">Exam 1 · Q50</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q1\">Exam 5 · Q1</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Internal team continues an investigation</td>\n<td>URL + Monitoring Viewer</td>\n<td><code>roles/viewer</code> is a broad basic role; exports are static <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q51\">Exam 3 · Q51</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>External/partner team</td>\n<td>Export JSON definition</td>\n<td>The URL needs project access; a single MQL query is only a fragment; Data Studio is a rebuild <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q54\">Exam 4 · Q54</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","summary":"This lesson covers metrics scopes for viewing metrics across projects, and how to share charts and dashboards with least privilege. Exam questions test whether you share by URL with Monitoring Viewer internally or export dashboard JSON for external teams.","objectives":["After this lesson you can use a scoping project and metrics scope to view metrics from many monitored projects.","After this lesson you can design one scoping project per folder for environment-specific dashboards.","After this lesson you can share a live chart or dashboard internally with roles/monitoring.viewer and the URL.","After this lesson you can share a dashboard with a partner team by exporting its JSON definition."],"takeaways":["A metrics scope consolidates metrics, not logs; 'Workspaces' is the legacy name.","Dashboards only for one folder's projects: create a new scoping project per folder, don't reuse an app project.","Internal live sharing: dashboard URL + roles/monitoring.viewer; roles/viewer is too broad and exports are static.","Dashboard Configuration Viewer sees definitions only, so charts fail to render.","Partner team without project access: export the dashboard JSON so they import their own copy."],"questions":["e1q50","e2q25","e3q51","e4q54","e5q1","e5q54"]},{"id":"d4l9","num":9,"title":"Alerting, notification channels, uptime checks and synthetic monitors","html":"<h3>Notification channels</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Native channels</strong>: email, <strong>SMS</strong>, mobile app push, PagerDuty, Slack, webhooks, Pub/Sub. \"SMS for critical alerts\" → team members <strong>add and verify phone numbers as SMS channels</strong>, then attach them to the policies <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q3\">Exam 1 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li>Webhooks are for systems with no purpose-built integration. Don't chain Slack→SMS or a webhook→SMS gateway <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q3\">Exam 1 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Webhook channels require public endpoints.</strong> For an alerting platform reachable <strong>only inside the VPC</strong> → <strong>Pub/Sub channel + pull subscription</strong> consumed from inside the VPC. A push subscription can't reach a private address. A Cloud Run function with Direct VPC egress works but is not the recommended pattern <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q53\">Exam 3 · Q53</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>SLO / alert design</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Notify before the error budget runs out\" → <strong>SLO error-budget burn-rate alert</strong>, not a latency threshold, log-anomaly alert or daily emailed report <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q39\">Exam 3 · Q39</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Alerts for self-healing restarts (recover in under a minute) are burning out staff\" → <strong>stop alerting on conditions that need no human action</strong>. Don't change the SLO, spread pages across time zones, or file an incident report per alert <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q48\">Exam 4 · Q48</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Probing</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Recurring test of a <strong>multi-step business journey</strong> (checkout) + alert on failure</td>\n<td><strong>Synthetic monitor</strong> (a Cloud Run function) + alerting policy</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q26\">Exam 3 · Q26</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Endpoint availability / response check</td>\n<td>Uptime check (stateless, independent requests)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q26\">Exam 3 · Q26</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Availability of <strong>internal-only</strong> APIs in a private VPC</td>\n<td><strong>Private uptime checks</strong> (they use Service Directory). No relay function or VPN needed</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q40\">Exam 3 · Q40</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Detect logged errors (passive)</td>\n<td>Log-based alert (doesn't execute any test)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q26\">Exam 3 · Q26</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>Traps: Workflows + Cloud Tasks (Cloud Tasks isn't a cron scheduler) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q26\">Exam 3 · Q26</a>. Liveness/readiness probes are kubelet restart/routing signals, not an alertable availability metric <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q40\">Exam 3 · Q40</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q32\">Exam 2 · Q32</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Service Health</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Dashboard must distinguish our failures from Google Cloud incidents\" → enable <strong>Personalized Service Health event annotations</strong> on the dashboard <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q5\">Exam 3 · Q5</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers notification channels, SLO burn-rate alerting, alert fatigue, uptime checks, synthetic monitors and Service Health annotations. The exam asks you to pick the native channel or probe type that fits constraints like SMS, private networks or multi-step user journeys.","objectives":["After this lesson you can configure native notification channels, including SMS and Pub/Sub for private alerting platforms.","After this lesson you can alert before the error budget is exhausted using SLO burn-rate alerts.","After this lesson you can choose between uptime checks, private uptime checks and synthetic monitors.","After this lesson you can reduce alert fatigue by not paging on conditions that need no human action."],"takeaways":["SMS alerts: team members add and verify phone numbers as native SMS channels; don't chain Slack or webhooks to SMS.","Webhooks need public endpoints; for a VPC-only alerting platform use a Pub/Sub channel + pull subscription.","'Before the error budget runs out' means an SLO burn-rate alert.","Multi-step business journey: synthetic monitor + alerting policy; internal-only endpoints: private uptime checks via Service Directory.","Distinguish your failures from Google Cloud incidents with Personalized Service Health annotations on the dashboard."],"questions":["e1q3","e2q32","e3q5","e3q26","e3q39","e3q40","e3q53","e4q48"]},{"id":"d4l10","num":10,"title":"Trace, Profiler and Error Reporting","html":"<h3>Comparison</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Answers</th>\n<th>Choose when the stem says…</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cloud Trace</strong> (OpenTelemetry)</td>\n<td><strong>Where</strong> time goes across services: per-request spans and waterfall</td>\n<td>\"downstream dependencies\", \"which service is slow\", \"root cause of latency\" across microservices</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q54\">Exam 1 · Q54</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q11\">Exam 2 · Q11</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q32\">Exam 2 · Q32</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q18\">Exam 3 · Q18</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cloud Profiler</strong></td>\n<td>Which <strong>code inside one process</strong> burns CPU/memory</td>\n<td>\"CPU spiked after release\", \"which function allocates most memory\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q11\">Exam 2 · Q11</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Error Reporting</strong></td>\n<td>Groups failures and exceptions</td>\n<td>\"failed requests increased\", \"exceptions\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q11\">Exam 2 · Q11</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Metrics / Prometheus</strong></td>\n<td><strong>That</strong> something is slow (aggregate numbers)</td>\n<td>trends, alerting</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q11\">Exam 2 · Q11</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q32\">Exam 2 · Q32</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cloud Debugger</strong></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><strong>Never.</strong> Deprecated in 2022 and shut down in May 2023</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q54\">Exam 1 · Q54</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>Rule of thumb: <strong>metrics say that it's slow, traces say where, Profiler says which code, Error Reporting says what failed</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q11\">Exam 2 · Q11</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q32\">Exam 2 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Time spent waiting on a dependency is mostly idle time, so a CPU profile misses it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q11\">Exam 2 · Q11</a>.</li>\n<li>VPC Flow Logs, health probes and a Dataflow metrics pipeline can't attribute per-request latency <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q32\">Exam 2 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>Logging call durations yourself gives only the caller's side, needs code changes and needs manual correlation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q54\">Exam 1 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li>Scaling up (HPA) or rolling back are actions to take <em>after</em> the root cause is known. They are not diagnosis <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q18\">Exam 3 · Q18</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Trace specifics</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Correlate logs with a slow trace</strong>: make sure the logging config writes the <strong>trace ID from the OTel context into each structured log entry</strong>, then use <strong>\"Show logs\"</strong> in the Cloud Trace details view. Querying Logs Explorer by trace ID or joining in BigQuery assumes the correlation already exists and is slower <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q36\">Exam 3 · Q36</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Apigee</strong>: trace only the affected proxy with a <strong>proxy-scoped trace configuration override</strong>. An environment-wide trace adds overhead to every proxy <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q49\">Exam 3 · Q49</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Run + OTel agent, inconsistent traces</strong> → <strong>instance-based billing (CPU always allocated)</strong>, because request-based billing throttles CPU after the response. Adding vCPUs adds capacity, not availability <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q3\">Exam 3 · Q3</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Error Reporting</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>It is enabled automatically on App Engine flexible. To <strong>customize</strong> error details → build <strong><code>ReportedErrorEvent</code></strong> objects yourself through the Error Reporting API (or as matching log entries). Installing the library gives only default capture, and moving to GCE or GKE is irrelevant <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q46\">Exam 2 · Q46</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson compares Cloud Trace, Cloud Profiler, Error Reporting and metrics, which each answer a different diagnostic question. Exam stems use keywords like 'downstream dependency', 'which function' or 'exceptions' to point to the right tool, and Cloud Debugger is never correct.","objectives":["After this lesson you can pick Trace, Profiler, Error Reporting or metrics based on the diagnostic question.","After this lesson you can correlate logs with traces by writing the trace ID into structured log entries.","After this lesson you can fix inconsistent Cloud Run traces with instance-based billing.","After this lesson you can customize Error Reporting details with ReportedErrorEvent objects."],"takeaways":["Metrics say that it's slow, Trace says where, Profiler says which code, Error Reporting says what failed.","Cloud Debugger is shut down (May 2023) and is never the answer.","Logs to trace correlation: put the OTel trace ID in each structured log entry, then use 'Show logs' in Trace.","Cloud Run + OTel with inconsistent traces: instance-based billing (CPU always allocated).","Apigee: use a proxy-scoped trace configuration override rather than tracing the whole environment."],"questions":["e1q8","e1q36","e1q54","e2q11","e2q32","e2q46","e3q3","e3q18","e3q36","e3q49"]},{"id":"d4l11","num":11,"title":"Network observability and troubleshooting","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Forensic capture of an intermittent malicious process</td>\n<td><strong>VPC Flow Logs, sampling 1.0</strong>, on the <strong>implicated env only</strong> (prod frontend + backend subnets), no test-first rollout</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q11\">Exam 1 · Q11</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Record <strong>each</strong> source IP hitting a port an existing firewall rule allows, fewest steps</td>\n<td><strong>Enable firewall rules logging</strong> on that rule (a passthrough NLB keeps client IPs)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q13\">Exam 2 · Q13</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Which <strong>layer</strong> of a cross-VPC path is broken, <strong>no exec access</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Connectivity Tests</strong> (Network <em>Intelligence</em> Center): simulates routes, firewalls, forwarding rules and peering</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Full packet payload inspection</td>\n<td>Packet Mirroring (needs collectors behind an internal NLB; doesn't write to Cloud Logging)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q13\">Exam 2 · Q13</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>VPC Flow Logs sample packets and aggregate them per connection, are enabled per subnet, and don't explain <em>why</em> traffic was denied. Firewall rules logging covers denials <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q13\">Exam 2 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>Toolbox or debug containers and <code>traceroute</code> violate a \"no exec\" constraint <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>The answer flips to Flow Logs for broad analysis (volume trends, top talkers) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q13\">Exam 2 · Q13</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers VPC Flow Logs, firewall rules logging, Connectivity Tests and Packet Mirroring for network troubleshooting. The exam tests which tool records the right granularity, explains denials, or finds a broken path without exec access.","objectives":["After this lesson you can enable VPC Flow Logs with high sampling on only the implicated subnets for forensics.","After this lesson you can record each source IP hitting an allowed port with firewall rules logging.","After this lesson you can diagnose a broken cross-VPC path with Connectivity Tests in Network Intelligence Center.","After this lesson you can choose Packet Mirroring when full payload inspection is required."],"takeaways":["Each source IP on a port an existing rule allows, fewest steps: enable firewall rules logging on that rule.","Broken path with no exec access: Connectivity Tests (Network Intelligence Center) simulates routes, firewalls and peering.","VPC Flow Logs are per subnet, sampled and aggregated; sampling 1.0 is the most complete setting, not a per-packet guarantee.","Flow Logs fit broad analysis (volume trends, top talkers); they don't explain why traffic was denied.","Packet Mirroring is for full payloads and needs collectors behind an internal load balancer."],"questions":["e1q11","e2q4","e2q13"]},{"id":"d4l12","num":12,"title":"Troubleshooting performance and resource problems","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Symptom</th>\n<th>Most probable cause / action</th>\n<th>Distractors</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cloud SQL timeouts after an app update, <strong>same user count</strong></td>\n<td>Check whether <strong>Cloud SQL connections increased</strong></td>\n<td>Serial port logs (boot/OS), Profiler (in-process), Web Security Scanner (vuln scanner, not DDoS)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>After a spike, latency stays high; <strong>Cloud SQL and Cloud Storage requests equally slow</strong>; no errors</td>\n<td>Shared layer = App Engine instances → <strong>keep more idle instances</strong></td>\n<td>Multi-region GCS, Cloud SQL HA (that's availability, not latency), migrate to GCE</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q57\">Exam 2 · Q57</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Half the Pods <code>OOMKilled</code>, HPA at minimum replicas</td>\n<td><strong>Raise the container memory limit</strong></td>\n<td>Bigger nodes, more nodes, higher HPA max (it doesn't trigger scaling)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q23\">Exam 3 · Q23</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Node DiskPressure, which Pods, no exec</td>\n<td><code>container/ephemeral_storage/used_bytes</code></td>\n<td>node metric, <code>df</code></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q23\">Exam 4 · Q23</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q39\">Exam 5 · Q39</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Need CPU/memory utilization for an app inside and outside GCP, minimal setup</td>\n<td>Cloud Profiler library (keyed answer; see ⚠️)</td>\n<td>Debugger (dead), custom scrape endpoint, third-party APM</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>Method: start with the signal closest to the symptom, run diagnostics before making changes, and match the fix to the resource boundary that is failing <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q51\">Exam 1 · Q51</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q23\">Exam 3 · Q23</a>.</li>\n<li><code>OOMKilled</code> can also be node-level memory exhaustion. Confirm which boundary was exceeded <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q23\">Exam 3 · Q23</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers a method for troubleshooting performance and resource problems such as database timeouts, persistent latency, OOMKilled Pods and DiskPressure. The exam rewards starting from the signal closest to the symptom and matching the fix to the resource boundary that is failing.","objectives":["After this lesson you can investigate Cloud SQL timeouts after a release by checking connection counts.","After this lesson you can identify a shared serving layer as the cause when all backends are equally slow.","After this lesson you can fix OOMKilled Pods by raising container memory limits instead of scaling nodes.","After this lesson you can apply a diagnose-before-change approach to resource problems."],"takeaways":["DB timeouts with the same user count after an update: check whether Cloud SQL connections increased.","Cloud SQL and Cloud Storage equally slow after a spike: the shared App Engine layer; keep more idle instances.","OOMKilled Pods with HPA at minimum: raise the container memory limit; bigger nodes or higher HPA max don't help.","Start with the signal closest to the symptom, run diagnostics before changing anything, and fix the failing boundary."],"questions":["e1q8","e1q36","e1q51","e2q57","e3q23","e4q23","e5q39"]},{"id":"d4l13","num":13,"title":"IAM roles for observability","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Role</th>\n<th>Grants</th>\n<th>Use when</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>roles/logging.configWriter</code> (Logs Configuration Writer)</td>\n<td>Create and manage sinks + log-based metric configs</td>\n<td>\"Let team members <strong>export logs</strong>\"</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q13\">Exam 1 · Q13</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>roles/logging.viewer</code> (Logs Viewer)</td>\n<td>Read ordinary logs</td>\n<td>Developers troubleshoot app logs</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q46\">Exam 1 · Q46</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q3\">Exam 5 · Q3</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>roles/logging.privateLogViewer</code> (Private Logs Viewer)</td>\n<td>+ private logs such as <strong>Data Access audit logs</strong></td>\n<td>Only if they need Data Access logs</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q46\">Exam 1 · Q46</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>roles/logging.logWriter</code> (Logs Writer)</td>\n<td>Write log entries</td>\n<td>VM service account whose agent can't write</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q25\">Exam 4 · Q25</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>roles/logging.fieldAccessor</code> (Log Field Accessor)</td>\n<td>See <strong>restricted fields</strong> on a bucket</td>\n<td>PII field visible only to the security team</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>roles/monitoring.viewer</code> (Monitoring Viewer)</td>\n<td>Read metric data + dashboards</td>\n<td>Share a chart or dashboard (least privilege)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q50\">Exam 1 · Q50</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q51\">Exam 3 · Q51</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q1\">Exam 5 · Q1</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monitoring Dashboard Configuration Viewer</td>\n<td>Dashboard <strong>definitions only</strong>, no metric data</td>\n<td>Almost never (charts won't render)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q50\">Exam 1 · Q50</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>roles/monitoring.servicesViewer</code></td>\n<td>Read services/SLOs</td>\n<td>Not for ingestion problems</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q21\">Exam 3 · Q21</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monitoring Metric Writer</td>\n<td>Write metrics</td>\n<td>Ingestion identity</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q21\">Exam 3 · Q21</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A custom role with <code>logging.sinks.list</code> + <code>logging.sinks.get</code> is read-only and can't <strong>create</strong> a sink <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q13\">Exam 1 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>Access Context Manager (context perimeters) and Organization Policy (resource constraints) don't grant permission for an action <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q13\">Exam 1 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li><code>roles/viewer</code> works but is too broad <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q51\">Exam 3 · Q51</a>.</li>\n<li>Don't export service account keys, and don't fall back to the default Compute Engine SA. Grant the missing role to the attached SA <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q25\">Exam 4 · Q25</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers the Cloud Logging and Cloud Monitoring IAM roles that appear on the exam and the least-privilege choice for each task. Many questions are decided by picking the narrowest predefined role that actually grants the needed action.","objectives":["After this lesson you can grant Logs Configuration Writer to let users create sinks and export logs.","After this lesson you can choose between Logs Viewer and Private Logs Viewer based on whether Data Access audit logs are needed.","After this lesson you can fix agent write failures by granting Logs Writer to the attached service account.","After this lesson you can choose Monitoring Viewer over Dashboard Configuration Viewer or roles/viewer for sharing dashboards."],"takeaways":["Let team members export logs: roles/logging.configWriter; a custom role with sinks.list/get can't create sinks.","Private Logs Viewer adds private logs such as Data Access audit logs; Logs Viewer suffices for ordinary logs.","Agent can't write logs: grant roles/logging.logWriter to the attached SA; never export keys or fall back to the default SA.","Restricted PII fields: roles/logging.fieldAccessor; metric ingestion identity: Monitoring Metric Writer, not servicesViewer.","Access Context Manager and Organization Policy don't grant permissions; roles/viewer works but is too broad."],"questions":["e1q13","e1q46","e1q50","e3q21","e3q51","e4q6","e4q25","e5q1","e5q3"]}],"ref":{"cheat":"<ul>\n<li><strong>One sink = one destination</strong>; folder or org <strong>aggregated sinks</strong> cover future projects.</li>\n<li>Archive cheaply for years → sink → <strong>Cloud Storage</strong>; immutable → <strong>retention policy + Bucket Lock</strong>.</li>\n<li>External SIEM → <strong>Pub/Sub</strong> (it is transport, with ≤31-day retention, never an archive).</li>\n<li>Keep querying and alerting in Logging → sink to a <strong>Cloud Logging bucket</strong>; 1-year retention → custom log bucket with 365 days.</li>\n<li>Live, shareable SQL dashboard on logs → sink → <strong>BigQuery → Data Studio/Looker Studio</strong>, with no CSV step.</li>\n<li>Capture elsewhere <strong>and</strong> keep out of Logging → <strong>sink + exclusion</strong>.</li>\n<li>Cut cost → <strong>exclude non-ERROR + log-based metrics</strong>; retention length isn't the cost driver.</li>\n<li>GKE: drop app logs but keep ops logs → <code>--logging=SYSTEM</code>.</li>\n<li>Hide one PII field → <strong>restricted field + Log Field Accessor</strong>; strip at the source fast → filter in the agent.</li>\n<li>\"How often does log X happen\" or \"requests by IP\" → <strong>log-based metric</strong> (counter/distribution, not Boolean).</li>\n<li>Latency for all users → <strong>DISTRIBUTION + GAUGE + Heatmap</strong>.</li>\n<li>Missing VM logs → agent installed? → running? → SA has <strong>Logs Writer</strong>?</li>\n<li>Current + future VMs, agent kept current → <strong>Ops Agent agent policy</strong>.</li>\n<li>GKE file logs from an unmodifiable app → <strong>sidecar tail to stdout + shared volume</strong>.</li>\n<li>Cloud Run structured logs → <strong>client library emitting JSON</strong> (no agent on Cloud Run).</li>\n<li>Prometheus on GKE, low ops, global PromQL → <strong>Managed Service for Prometheus</strong>; missing metrics with no errors → check the <code>PodMonitoring</code> port.</li>\n<li>On-prem OTel + multiple projects → <strong>scoping project + Bindplane</strong>.</li>\n<li>Slow across services → <strong>Trace</strong>; hot code → <strong>Profiler</strong>; exceptions → <strong>Error Reporting</strong>; Debugger is dead.</li>\n<li>Logs ↔ trace → put the <strong>trace ID in structured logs</strong>, then <strong>\"Show logs\"</strong> in Trace.</li>\n<li>Cloud Run OTel background export → <strong>instance-based billing (CPU always allocated)</strong>.</li>\n<li>Share a dashboard internally → <strong>URL + <code>roles/monitoring.viewer</code></strong>; partner team → <strong>export JSON</strong>.</li>\n<li>Folder-only dashboards → <strong>one scoping project per folder</strong>.</li>\n<li>SMS alerts → native <strong>SMS channel</strong>; private webhook → <strong>Pub/Sub channel + pull subscription</strong>.</li>\n<li>\"Before the error budget runs out\" → <strong>burn-rate alert</strong>; self-healing, non-actionable → <strong>don't page</strong>.</li>\n<li>Business journey → <strong>synthetic monitor</strong>; private endpoint → <strong>private uptime check</strong>; GCP incident context → <strong>Service Health annotations</strong>.</li>\n<li>Each source IP on an allowed port → <strong>firewall rules logging</strong>; broken path with no exec → <strong>Connectivity Tests</strong>.</li>\n<li>Which Pods cause DiskPressure → <code>container/ephemeral_storage/used_bytes</code>; <code>OOMKilled</code> at min replicas → raise the <strong>memory limit</strong>.</li>\n<li>DB timeouts with flat load after a release → check <strong>Cloud SQL connections</strong>; equal slowness across backends → the <strong>shared serving layer</strong> (App Engine idle instances).</li>\n</ul>","rules":[{"lesson":"d4l1","title":"Cloud Logging: sinks, aggregated sinks and destinations","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Every current <strong>and future</strong> project in the org\" → <strong>org-level aggregated sink</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q9\">Exam 2 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Only projects in the Prod folder\" → <strong>folder-level aggregated sink</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Dev logs → dev_dataset, Prod logs → prod_dataset, fewest sinks, future projects\" → <strong>one aggregated sink per folder</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q16\">Exam 2 · Q16</a>.</li>\n<li>\"7-year regulatory retention, guard against human error\" → <strong>org aggregated sink → Cloud Storage + locked retention policy</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q9\">Exam 2 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Archive 7 years at lowest cost\" → <strong>named sink → Cloud Storage bucket</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q43\">Exam 2 · Q43</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Security team wants alerting plus near-real-time analysis on the logs\" (no external SIEM) → <strong>folder sink → Cloud Logging bucket</strong>, not Pub/Sub <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>\"SIEM ingests only through Pub/Sub\" → <strong>org sink → Pub/Sub topic</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Interactive, shareable, real-time dashboard with quarterly aggregation\" → <strong>sink → BigQuery → Data Studio</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>\"GKE + Cloud Run logs into BigQuery, low cost and complexity\" → <strong>native Cloud Logging collection + sink to BigQuery</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l2","title":"Cloud Logging: exclusions, log buckets, retention and cost","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Cut cost of terabytes/day, keep critical ops/security info\" → <strong>exclude all non-ERROR logs + build log-based metrics for key indicators</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a>.</li>\n<li>\"GKE dev cluster: drop app logs (devs use <code>kubectl logs</code>), keep operational logs\" → <code>gcloud container clusters update --logging=SYSTEM</code> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Keep logs 1 year for client to import, minimal code changes\" (Cloud Run) → <strong>custom log bucket with 365-day retention + sink + client access</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q53\">Exam 4 · Q53</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Retention and sharing\" → a bucket-and-sink problem, not a code problem <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q53\">Exam 4 · Q53</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l4","title":"Log-based metrics","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"How often does log event X happen over time\" (cache misses) → <strong>log-based metric + Cloud Monitoring dashboard</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q10\">Exam 5 · Q10</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Requests from a specific IP\" with Ops Agent installed → <strong>Ops Agent logging receiver (access logs) + log-based metric</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q3\">Exam 2 · Q3</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q28\">Exam 5 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Cloud Armor blocked requests for <code>/api</code> prefix, broken down by parameter name\" → <strong>counter metric filtered on <code>configuredAction=\"DENY\"</code> + <code>requestUrl</code> prefix, label = <code>matchedFieldName</code></strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q59\">Exam 3 · Q59</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l7","title":"Prometheus on GKE and hybrid metrics","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Already emit Prometheus metrics on GKE, least maintenance\" → <strong>enable GMP managed collection</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q32\">Exam 3 · Q32</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Many clusters, global PromQL view, low overhead\" → <strong>GMP</strong>, not hierarchical or cross-service federation <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q15\">Exam 2 · Q15</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Multi-project GKE Prometheus + flexible query\" → <strong>GMP managed collection + Metrics Explorer, with a metrics scope for cross-project</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q22\">Exam 3 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>\"HA, custom + default GKE metrics, all clusters\" → <strong>managed collection + query in Cloud Monitoring</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q13\">Exam 4 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>\"On-prem K8s + several GCP projects, OpenTelemetry, one place, easy to support\" → <strong>scoping project + Bindplane</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q50\">Exam 3 · Q50</a>.</li>\n<li>\"GMP managed collection, metrics missing, <strong>no errors</strong>\" → validate the <strong>port in the <code>PodMonitoring</code> resource</strong> (exporter/scrape config). Check the write quota only if you see HTTP <strong>429</strong> errors <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q21\">Exam 3 · Q21</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l8","title":"Metrics scopes, dashboards and sharing","html":"<ul>\n<li>\"Dashboards showing only one folder's projects (Dev/Staging/Prod)\" → <strong>a new scoping project per folder</strong> (don't reuse an app project) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q54\">Exam 5 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Share one chart with the SRE team only, least privilege\" → <strong>share chart by URL + <code>roles/monitoring.viewer</code></strong> on the scoping project <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q50\">Exam 1 · Q50</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q1\">Exam 5 · Q1</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Dev team should keep using <em>the same live</em> dashboard\" → <strong><code>roles/monitoring.viewer</code> + dashboard URL</strong>, not <code>roles/viewer</code> (too broad) and not a JSON/CSV export <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q51\">Exam 3 · Q51</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Share a dashboard with a <strong>partner team</strong>\" (no access to your project) → <strong>export the dashboard JSON</strong> so they can import their own copy <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q54\">Exam 4 · Q54</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"traps":[{"lesson":"d4l1","title":"Cloud Logging: sinks, aggregated sinks and destinations","html":"<ul>\n<li>Check <strong>scope</strong> and <strong>destination</strong> separately. Each distractor usually gets one of them right <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q9\">Exam 2 · Q9</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Repeat for each folder\" still misses folders created later. The organization level is the only fully automatic scope <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q9\">Exam 4 · Q9</a>.</li>\n<li>Pub/Sub <em>can</em> be a sink destination, but it is transport, not storage <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q43\">Exam 2 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q25\">Exam 2 · Q25</a>.</li>\n<li>Exporting to BigQuery and then to CSV and Google Sheets gives a <strong>static snapshot</strong>, not a live dashboard. Sheets also refreshes no more often than every 15 minutes <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q28\">Exam 1 · Q28</a>.</li>\n<li>Avoid custom apps that write logs directly to BigQuery or Cloud Storage, App Engine \"pullers\", Datadog relays and manual uploads. Use native collection plus a sink <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q43\">Exam 2 · Q43</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q25\">Exam 3 · Q25</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l2","title":"Cloud Logging: exclusions, log buckets, retention and cost","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Distractor</th>\n<th>Why it's wrong</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Exclude only DEBUG</td>\n<td>INFO (the bulk of the volume) is still ingested</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shorten <code>_Default</code> retention to 15 days</td>\n<td>Doesn't reduce ingestion, and you lose investigation data</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sample across all severities</td>\n<td>Drops ERROR/security entries too</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q33\">Exam 3 · Q33</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--logging=WORKLOAD</code></td>\n<td>That is the app-log source, the opposite of the goal. <code>SYSTEM</code> is required on all cluster types</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exclusion <code>resource.type=\"k8s_container\"</code></td>\n<td>Also matches system Pods and filters after collection. Excluding GKE logs can leave nothing to share with Support</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pub/Sub for 1-year retention</td>\n<td>31-day maximum</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q53\">Exam 4 · Q53</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Answer flip: use an exclusion filter (instead of <code>--logging=SYSTEM</code>) if you must keep <em>part</em> of the app logs, or on <strong>Autopilot</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q41\">Exam 4 · Q41</a>.</p>\n</blockquote>"},{"lesson":"d4l3","title":"Sensitive data / PII in logs","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Encryption ≠ redaction.</strong> An encrypted bucket still shows PII to authorized readers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Sampling</strong> reduces volume but does not remove PII, and <strong>disabling logging</strong> breaks the \"must collect\" requirement <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Exclusion</strong> to hide a field drops the whole entry and breaks mandatory capture <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li>A conditional IAM binding (<code>resource.name.extract(\".../buckets/{bucket}/\") == \"_Default\"</code>) is real syntax, but on its own it hides nothing <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li><code>fluent-plugin-record-reformer</code> is an add-on gem framed as an output plugin. The built-in filter is the answer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>\"Wait for developers to patch\" is the long-term root-cause fix, not the <em>fastest</em> one <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a>.</li>\n<li>Gating a field behind an environment variable plus CODEOWNERS controls <em>whether</em> data is written, not <em>who can view</em> it <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l4","title":"Log-based metrics","html":"<ul>\n<li>A Data Studio/Looker Studio link to Cloud Logging is not a native path (it typically needs a BigQuery export first) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>Profiler cannot read logs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>A BigQuery sink plus scheduled queries is overkill for a simple frequency chart <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>An Ops Agent <strong>metrics receiver</strong> reports fixed metric sets and <strong>can't break traffic down by client IP</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q3\">Exam 2 · Q3</a>.</li>\n<li><code>networksecurity.googleapis.com/https/request_count</code> with <code>blocked=true</code> gives totals only, with no URL or parameter dimension <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q59\">Exam 3 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>Filtering on <code>configuredAction=\"ALLOW\"</code> selects permitted traffic, which is the wrong half <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q59\">Exam 3 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>A log-based metric + alert tells you <strong>that</strong> requests are slow, not <strong>where</strong> they are slow <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q18\">Exam 3 · Q18</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d4l7","title":"Prometheus on GKE and hybrid metrics","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Distractor</th>\n<th>Why not</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Self-run Prometheus / Prometheus Operator + remote-write / Thanos / Grafana</td>\n<td>You operate every tier, so overhead is not minimized</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q13\">Exam 4 · Q13</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q32\">Exam 3 · Q32</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q50\">Exam 3 · Q50</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hierarchical federation</td>\n<td>Works, but self-managed. Correct only if a managed service is forbidden</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q15\">Exam 2 · Q15</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cross-service federation</td>\n<td>Combines one service's metrics with another's; it is not a global roll-up</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q15\">Exam 2 · Q15</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Workload metrics within Cloud Observability for GKE\"</td>\n<td>Not the Prometheus-compatible PromQL/Grafana path the team relies on</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q15\">Exam 2 · Q15</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Self-deployed OTel Collector for Prometheus metrics on GKE</td>\n<td>Valid, but it is extra infrastructure. GMP is the primary recommendation</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q32\">Exam 3 · Q32</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>roles/monitoring.servicesViewer</code></td>\n<td>SLO/service read-only. Ingestion needs Monitoring Metric Writer</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q21\">Exam 3 · Q21</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Installing Grafana</td>\n<td>Optional UI that fixes nothing about scraping</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q21\">Exam 3 · Q21</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"lesson":"d4l13","title":"IAM roles for observability","html":"<ul>\n<li>A custom role with <code>logging.sinks.list</code> + <code>logging.sinks.get</code> is read-only and can't <strong>create</strong> a sink <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q13\">Exam 1 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li>Access Context Manager (context perimeters) and Organization Policy (resource constraints) don't grant permission for an action <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q13\">Exam 1 · Q13</a>.</li>\n<li><code>roles/viewer</code> works but is too broad <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q51\">Exam 3 · Q51</a>.</li>\n<li>Don't export service account keys, and don't fall back to the default Compute Engine SA. Grant the missing role to the attached SA <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q25\">Exam 4 · Q25</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"questionable":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question</th>\n<th>Issue</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q8\">Exam 1 · Q8</a></strong> (Cloud Profiler for \"system resource utilization\" metrics, hybrid)</td>\n<td>Profiler is code-level CPU/heap profiling attributed to functions. Other explanations in this bank say so <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q18\">Exam 1 · Q18</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q11\">Exam 2 · Q11</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q36\">Exam 1 · Q36</a>, and it does not produce system resource-utilization metrics. The bank's own memory hook adds \"(or the Ops/Monitoring agent)\". Profiler is the least-bad option listed, but on a real exam an <strong>Ops Agent / Cloud Monitoring</strong> option would beat it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q3\">Exam 5 · Q3</a></strong> explanation</td>\n<td>Says Private Logs Viewer is for \"admin activity and system logs\". In fact, private logs are mainly <strong>Data Access audit logs</strong>, as <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q46\">Exam 1 · Q46</a> correctly states, and Admin Activity logs are readable with Logs Viewer. The answer (A) is right; the reasoning is wrong.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q13\">Exam 5 · Q13</a></strong> explanation</td>\n<td>Rejects INT64 because latency is \"floating-point\" and calls DELTA \"rate-based\". These are weak reasons. The stronger reasoning in <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q1\">Exam 1 · Q1</a> is that any scalar loses the distribution. The keyed answer (GAUGE + DISTRIBUTION + Heatmap) stands. The claim in <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q1\">Exam 1 · Q1</a> that DELTA-kind DISTRIBUTION is disallowed for custom metrics is stated without detail, so don't lean on it beyond this question.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q28\">Exam 5 · Q28</a></strong> explanation</td>\n<td>Rejects the Ops Agent metrics receiver only because it \"likely requires more configuration\". The real reason, given in <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q3\">Exam 2 · Q3</a>, is that a metrics receiver <strong>can't break down by client IP</strong> at all.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q4\">Exam 2 · Q4</a></strong> option text</td>\n<td>The keyed option says \"Network <strong>Connectivity</strong> Center\" runs Connectivity Tests. Connectivity Tests belong to <strong>Network Intelligence Center</strong>, as the explanation itself admits. Pick it by capability, but know the real product name.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q11\">Exam 1 · Q11</a></strong> vs <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q13\">Exam 2 · Q13</a></strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q11\">Exam 1 · Q11</a> treats Flow Logs at sampling 1.0 as capturing every flow. <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q13\">Exam 2 · Q13</a> says Flow Logs have a dynamic, non-adjustable primary sampling and can't guarantee every IP. Both keyed answers are defensible in context. Know that \"sampling 1.0\" is the most complete Flow Logs setting, not a guarantee of every packet.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a></strong>, <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q16\">Exam 1 · Q16</a></strong>, <strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q51\">Exam 1 · Q51</a></strong></td>\n<td>These are built on the <strong>legacy Fluentd-based logging agent</strong>, which is end-of-support per <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q46\">Exam 1 · Q46</a>. Learn the principle (scrub at the agent; check that the agent process is running) rather than the Fluentd plugin and process names.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q2\">Exam 3 · Q2</a></strong></td>\n<td>The keyed answer describes Sensitive Data Protection redacting \"before storing in Cloud Logging\". The explanation admits this is a custom preprocessing integration, not a Cloud Logging setting. If a question offers native options such as restricted fields <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q6\">Exam 4 · Q6</a> or agent-side removal <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q41\">Exam 2 · Q41</a>, prefer those when they fit.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q1\">Exam 5 · Q1</a></strong> explanation</td>\n<td>Says Monitoring Viewer via a project ID \"shares all dashboards\", while via a URL it doesn't. The role grants the same access either way; the URL only <em>delivers</em> the chart. The answer is right; the over-sharing argument is not.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},"glossary":[{"term":"_Default sink","definition":"The project's built-in sink that routes logs into the _Default log bucket.","exam_context":"Disabling it stops all routing into _Default, including system logs, so it's rarely the right way to cut volume.","lesson":"d4l2","domain":4},{"term":"Agent policy","definition":"A gcloud-managed policy that installs the Ops Agent on matching current and future VMs and keeps it up to date.","exam_context":"Pick it over images, one-off installs or scripts when the stem says 'current and future VMs' and 'keep up to date'.","lesson":"d4l5","domain":4},{"term":"Aggregated sink","definition":"A sink created at the folder or organization level that routes matching logs from all child projects, including projects created later.","exam_context":"If the stem says 'current and future projects', pick an org (or folder) aggregated sink; per-project or per-folder repetition misses new resources.","lesson":"d4l1","domain":4},{"term":"BigQuery sink destination","definition":"Routing logs to a BigQuery dataset for SQL analysis and live dashboards in Data Studio/Looker Studio.","exam_context":"Pick it for analysis and live shareable dashboards; not for locked retention or cheapest cold archive.","lesson":"d4l1","domain":4},{"term":"Bindplane","definition":"Google's documented OpenTelemetry pipeline for bringing on-prem and hybrid telemetry into Cloud Monitoring.","exam_context":"On-prem Kubernetes + several projects, one place, easy to support: scoping project + Bindplane.","lesson":"d4l7","domain":4},{"term":"Bucket Lock","definition":"A Cloud Storage feature that locks a bucket's retention policy so objects cannot be deleted or the policy shortened.","exam_context":"Regulatory multi-year retention 'guarding against human error' means sink to Cloud Storage with a locked retention policy.","lesson":"d4l1","domain":4},{"term":"Burn-rate alert","definition":"An SLO alert that fires based on how fast the error budget is being consumed.","exam_context":"'Notify before the error budget runs out' means a burn-rate alert, not a latency threshold or daily report.","lesson":"d4l9","domain":4},{"term":"Cloud Debugger","definition":"A deprecated debugging service shut down in May 2023.","exam_context":"Never the correct answer.","lesson":"d4l10","domain":4},{"term":"Cloud Profiler","definition":"A continuous profiler that attributes CPU and memory use to functions inside a process.","exam_context":"'Which function burns CPU/memory' means Profiler; it can't read logs and misses time waiting on dependencies.","lesson":"d4l10","domain":4},{"term":"Cloud Trace","definition":"A distributed tracing service that shows per-request spans and latency across services.","exam_context":"'Which service is slow' or 'downstream dependencies' means Trace; correlate logs by writing the trace ID into structured logs.","lesson":"d4l10","domain":4},{"term":"Connectivity Tests","definition":"A Network Intelligence Center tool that simulates routes, firewalls, forwarding rules and peering to find where a path breaks.","exam_context":"Broken cross-VPC path with no exec access; the real product is Network Intelligence Center, not Network Connectivity Center.","lesson":"d4l11","domain":4},{"term":"Distribution metric","definition":"A metric value type that records a spread of values (histogram) per interval rather than a single number.","exam_context":"Latency for all users: DISTRIBUTION value type with GAUGE kind displayed as a heatmap; scalar types hide the tail.","lesson":"d4l6","domain":4},{"term":"Error Reporting","definition":"A service that groups application errors and exceptions; custom details can be sent as ReportedErrorEvent objects.","exam_context":"Pick it for 'exceptions' or 'failed requests increased'; customize details by building ReportedErrorEvent objects via the API.","lesson":"d4l10","domain":4},{"term":"Exclusion filter","definition":"A filter that stops matching log entries from being ingested and stored in Cloud Logging.","exam_context":"Use it to cut ingestion cost (e.g., exclude non-ERROR) or with a sink to keep data out of Logging; it is not access control and drops whole entries.","lesson":"d4l2","domain":4},{"term":"Firewall rules logging","definition":"Logging enabled on an individual firewall rule that records connections the rule allows or denies.","exam_context":"Record each source IP hitting a port an existing rule allows, with fewest steps: enable logging on that rule.","lesson":"d4l11","domain":4},{"term":"GKE logging components (SYSTEM / WORKLOAD)","definition":"Cluster settings that choose which log sources GKE collects: SYSTEM for operational logs and WORKLOAD for application logs.","exam_context":"Drop app logs but keep operational logs with --logging=SYSTEM; use an exclusion filter instead on Autopilot or to keep part of app logs.","lesson":"d4l2","domain":4},{"term":"Instance-based billing","definition":"A Cloud Run billing mode where CPU stays allocated outside request handling.","exam_context":"Inconsistent traces from a Cloud Run OTel agent: switch to instance-based billing, not more vCPUs.","lesson":"d4l10","domain":4},{"term":"Kubernetes Engine monitoring","definition":"Built-in Cloud Monitoring for GKE (Cloud Operations for GKE) that provides system metrics like container CPU and memory.","exam_context":"Find containers using most CPU/memory with built-in metrics; don't create custom metrics for data already collected.","lesson":"d4l6","domain":4},{"term":"Log bucket","definition":"A Cloud Logging storage container (such as _Default or a custom bucket) with configurable retention that keeps logs queryable and alertable natively.","exam_context":"Centralize logs while still querying and alerting in Logging, or keep logs 365 days for sharing: sink to a custom log bucket.","lesson":"d4l2","domain":4},{"term":"Log Field Accessor (roles/logging.fieldAccessor)","definition":"An IAM role that lets principals see restricted fields on a log bucket.","exam_context":"Paired with restricted fields to let only one group see a PII field.","lesson":"d4l13","domain":4},{"term":"Log-based metric","definition":"A Cloud Monitoring metric derived from log entries that match a filter, which you can chart and alert on.","exam_context":"Answer for 'how often does log event X happen' or 'requests by client IP'; it shows that something happens, not where latency comes from.","lesson":"d4l4","domain":4},{"term":"Logs Configuration Writer (roles/logging.configWriter)","definition":"An IAM role to create and manage sinks and log-based metric configurations.","exam_context":"'Let team members export logs' means Logs Configuration Writer; read-only sink permissions can't create sinks.","lesson":"d4l13","domain":4},{"term":"Logs Writer (roles/logging.logWriter)","definition":"An IAM role that allows writing log entries.","exam_context":"Agent running but no logs with a user-managed SA: grant Logs Writer to that SA instead of exporting keys.","lesson":"d4l13","domain":4},{"term":"Managed Service for Prometheus (GMP)","definition":"Google Cloud's managed Prometheus that stores data in Cloud Monitoring's global backend and is queried with PromQL.","exam_context":"Prometheus on GKE with least maintenance or a global PromQL view: GMP managed collection, not federation or Thanos.","lesson":"d4l7","domain":4},{"term":"Metric kind","definition":"The Cloud Monitoring property describing how values relate over time: GAUGE, DELTA or CUMULATIVE.","exam_context":"CUMULATIVE is a running total, not a distribution; METRIC_KIND_UNSPECIFIED is never a valid answer.","lesson":"d4l6","domain":4},{"term":"Metrics scope","definition":"The set of monitored projects whose metrics a scoping project can view; formerly called a Workspace.","exam_context":"Consolidates metrics, not logs; for folder-only dashboards create a new scoping project per folder.","lesson":"d4l8","domain":4},{"term":"Monitoring Metric Writer","definition":"An IAM role that allows writing metric data to Cloud Monitoring.","exam_context":"The ingestion identity role; roles/monitoring.servicesViewer is SLO read-only and doesn't fix ingestion.","lesson":"d4l13","domain":4},{"term":"Monitoring Viewer (roles/monitoring.viewer)","definition":"An IAM role that grants read access to metric data and dashboards.","exam_context":"Least-privilege way to share a live chart or dashboard by URL; Dashboard Configuration Viewer can't render charts and roles/viewer is too broad.","lesson":"d4l13","domain":4},{"term":"Notification channel","definition":"A destination for alert notifications such as email, SMS, mobile push, PagerDuty, Slack, webhooks or Pub/Sub.","exam_context":"Use native SMS channels for SMS; for VPC-only alert platforms use a Pub/Sub channel + pull subscription since webhooks need public endpoints.","lesson":"d4l9","domain":4},{"term":"OOMKilled","definition":"A container termination status meaning it exceeded its memory limit (or the node ran out of memory).","exam_context":"Half the Pods OOMKilled at minimum replicas: raise the container memory limit, not node size or HPA max.","lesson":"d4l12","domain":4},{"term":"OpenTelemetry","definition":"An open standard and set of libraries for emitting metrics and traces that can export to Cloud Monitoring and Cloud Trace.","exam_context":"New GKE app metrics with minimal setup: OTel client libraries with a Cloud Monitoring exporter.","lesson":"d4l6","domain":4},{"term":"Ops Agent","definition":"Google's current agent for Compute Engine VMs that collects both logs and metrics, replacing the end-of-support legacy logging and monitoring agents.","exam_context":"VMs don't have it by default; missing VM logs start with 'is the Ops Agent installed and running?'.","lesson":"d4l5","domain":4},{"term":"Packet Mirroring","definition":"A feature that copies full packets to collectors behind an internal load balancer for payload inspection.","exam_context":"Only for full payload inspection; it doesn't write to Cloud Logging.","lesson":"d4l11","domain":4},{"term":"Personalized Service Health","definition":"A service that surfaces Google Cloud incidents affecting your projects, including as dashboard event annotations.","exam_context":"Distinguish your own failures from Google Cloud incidents on a dashboard by enabling its annotations.","lesson":"d4l9","domain":4},{"term":"PodMonitoring","definition":"The GMP managed-collection custom resource that defines which Pods and ports are scraped.","exam_context":"Missing GMP metrics with no errors: validate the port in PodMonitoring; check write quota only on HTTP 429.","lesson":"d4l7","domain":4},{"term":"Private Logs Viewer (roles/logging.privateLogViewer)","definition":"An IAM role that adds access to private logs, mainly Data Access audit logs, on top of Logs Viewer.","exam_context":"Grant it only when Data Access audit logs are needed; Logs Viewer suffices for app troubleshooting.","lesson":"d4l13","domain":4},{"term":"Pub/Sub sink destination","definition":"Routing logs to a Pub/Sub topic so external systems such as a SIEM can consume them as a stream.","exam_context":"Choose it when a third-party tool ingests via Pub/Sub; reject it for storage because retention is at most 31 days.","lesson":"d4l1","domain":4},{"term":"Restricted field","definition":"A log field (e.g., jsonPayload.<field>) marked on a log bucket so only principals with Log Field Accessor can view it.","exam_context":"Hide one PII field while keeping the entry: restricted field + roles/logging.fieldAccessor, not an exclusion or encryption.","lesson":"d4l3","domain":4},{"term":"Sensitive Data Protection","definition":"Google Cloud's service for inspecting and redacting sensitive data such as PII.","exam_context":"Appears as custom preprocessing before logs are stored; prefer native options like restricted fields or agent-side removal when they fit.","lesson":"d4l3","domain":4},{"term":"Sidecar log container","definition":"A helper container sharing a volume with the app container that tails a log file to stdout so GKE collects it.","exam_context":"Unmodifiable GKE app writing to a file: sidecar tailing to stdout + shared volume.","lesson":"d4l5","domain":4},{"term":"Sink","definition":"A Log Router rule that sends log entries matching a filter to exactly one destination: Cloud Storage, BigQuery, Pub/Sub or a Cloud Logging bucket.","exam_context":"Two destinations require two sinks; check scope and destination separately because distractors usually get only one right.","lesson":"d4l1","domain":4},{"term":"Structured logging (jsonPayload)","definition":"Log entries written as single valid JSON objects so Cloud Logging stores them as jsonPayload fields.","exam_context":"On Cloud Run there is no agent; use the Cloud Logging client library to emit JSON.","lesson":"d4l5","domain":4},{"term":"Synthetic monitor","definition":"A Cloud Monitoring test implemented as a Cloud Run function that runs a multi-step user journey on a schedule.","exam_context":"Recurring test of a business journey like checkout plus alerting: synthetic monitor, not an uptime check or log-based alert.","lesson":"d4l9","domain":4},{"term":"Uptime check","definition":"A Cloud Monitoring probe that sends independent requests to check endpoint availability; private uptime checks use Service Directory for internal endpoints.","exam_context":"Internal-only APIs in a VPC: private uptime checks, no relay function or VPN needed.","lesson":"d4l9","domain":4},{"term":"VPC Flow Logs","definition":"Sampled, per-connection network flow records enabled per subnet.","exam_context":"Good for forensics (sampling 1.0 on implicated subnets) and broad trends; they don't explain denials or guarantee every packet.","lesson":"d4l11","domain":4}]},{"n":5,"title":"Optimizing Performance and Cost","short":"Performance & Cost","weight":"~19%","desc":"Compute pricing models, network tiers, billing export and labels, rightsizing and logging cost control.","lessons":[{"id":"d5l1","num":1,"title":"Compute pricing: CUDs, flexible commitments, reservations, Spot VMs","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Committed use discounts (CUDs)</strong> give lower prices in return for a <strong>1-year or 3-year</strong> commitment. The 3-year term gives the bigger discount. A CUD is a <strong>billing arrangement only</strong>: the VMs run unchanged, so performance is not affected <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q7\">Exam 5 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Resource-based commitments</strong> cover predictable, steady usage, and they are <strong>tied to one region</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q45\">Exam 3 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Compute flexible commitments</strong> commit to a <strong>minimum hourly spend</strong>, not to a number of instances. They apply <strong>across regions and projects within the same Cloud Billing account</strong>, so the discount follows workloads when they move <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q45\">Exam 3 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>A commitment does not reserve capacity.</strong> Reservations hold capacity in specific zones, unused reserved capacity is still charged, and flexible commitments cannot have reservations attached (you create them separately) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q45\">Exam 3 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Spot VMs</strong> replace preemptible VMs. Compute Engine can reclaim them at any time, they are <strong>excluded from the Compute Engine SLA</strong>, and they may not be available again right away. The discount is large (the bank cites 60–90%) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q5\">Exam 1 · Q5</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q54\">Exam 3 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Instance groups (MIG or unmanaged) do not lower prices.</strong> MIGs provide autoscaling, autohealing and updates. Unmanaged groups only let you load balance VMs you manage yourself <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q7\">Exam 5 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>Sustained use discounts (SUDs) do not appear in any question in this bank.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>If the workload is stable, fixed in size, business-critical, and \"no performance impact\" is required → choose <strong>CUDs</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q7\">Exam 5 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>If total demand is steady or growing but instances will <strong>move between regions</strong>, on a multi-year horizon → choose a <strong>3-year compute flexible commitment</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q45\">Exam 3 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>If the question also needs <strong>assured capacity in specific zones</strong> → add <strong>reservations</strong> (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q45\">Exam 3 · Q45</a>, option B becomes correct).</li>\n<li>If the work can be interrupted (resumable or checkpointed, no completion-time SLO) and must stay on VMs → choose <strong>Spot VMs</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q54\">Exam 3 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li>If the architecture survives losing a node (quorum, replication, stateless with external coordination) → it is a <strong>Spot candidate</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q5\">Exam 1 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>If the workload is \"unsuitable for containerization\" → rule out <strong>Cloud Run and GKE</strong>, which both run containers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q54\">Exam 3 · Q54</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Comparison</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Lever</th>\n<th>Lowers price?</th>\n<th>Interruption risk</th>\n<th>Scope</th>\n<th>Best for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Resource-based CUD</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td>One region</td>\n<td>Steady, predictable usage in one region</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compute flexible CUD</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td>All regions and projects in the billing account</td>\n<td>Steady spend that moves between regions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reservation</td>\n<td>No (unused capacity is still billed)</td>\n<td>None</td>\n<td>Specific zone(s)</td>\n<td>Guaranteed capacity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spot VM</td>\n<td>Yes, the biggest discount</td>\n<td>Can be reclaimed at any time, no SLA</td>\n<td>n/a</td>\n<td>Fault-tolerant or resumable batch work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MIG / unmanaged group</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>n/a</td>\n<td>n/a</td>\n<td>Operations and load balancing, not cost</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Spot VMs for a business-critical workload.</strong> It is the most tempting distractor, but the discount comes from accepting interruption <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>\"The data is in durable storage, so Spot is fine.\"</strong> Durable inputs and outputs do not protect work in progress. A GPU render job with no checkpoint loses its progress when preempted <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q5\">Exam 1 · Q5</a>. See ⚠️ below.</li>\n<li><strong>\"Scalable\" is not the same as \"tolerates losing a node.\"</strong> A scalable cache is not necessarily safe on Spot <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q5\">Exam 1 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Treating a commitment as a capacity guarantee,</strong> or adding reservations the question never asked for <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q45\">Exam 3 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>A 3-year CUD for a resumable job with no SLO.</strong> It works, but Spot VMs are cheaper <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q54\">Exam 3 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Picking Spot does not make the application recoverable.</strong> The application still needs its own retry and resume logic <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q54\">Exam 3 · Q54</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson compares the main ways to lower Compute Engine prices: committed use discounts (resource-based and compute flexible), reservations, and Spot VMs. Exam questions usually give you a workload profile (stable or interruptible, one region or moving between regions, capacity needs) and ask which pricing lever fits without risking performance or availability.","objectives":["After this lesson you can choose between resource-based CUDs, compute flexible commitments and Spot VMs for a given workload profile.","After this lesson you can explain why a commitment is only a billing discount and does not reserve capacity.","After this lesson you can tell when a workload is a safe Spot VM candidate (it survives losing a node or can resume work).","After this lesson you can rule out options that do not lower price, such as MIGs and unmanaged instance groups."],"takeaways":["Stable, fixed-size, business-critical workload that needs no performance impact: choose a CUD, never Spot.","Steady spend but instances move between regions: choose a compute flexible commitment, and the 3-year term for a multi-year horizon.","A commitment is not capacity. If the question asks for guaranteed capacity in specific zones, add reservations (unused reserved capacity is still billed).","Spot VMs give the biggest discount but can be reclaimed at any time and have no SLA. Use them only for interruptible or resumable work, or architectures that survive losing a node (such as quorum).","MIGs and unmanaged instance groups do not lower prices. They provide operations features and load balancing."],"questions":["e1q5","e1q30","e3q45","e3q54","e5q7"]},{"id":"d5l2","num":2,"title":"Network Service Tiers and load balancer scope","html":"<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Premium Tier</strong> is the default. Traffic rides Google's global backbone, which gives better performance and costs more. <strong>Standard Tier</strong> hands traffic to ordinary ISP transit networks, which costs less but has lower performance and availability, and it is <strong>regional</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Global external IP addresses and the global (Envoy-based) external Application Load Balancer require Premium Tier.</strong> The <strong>regional</strong> external Application Load Balancer (also Envoy-based) works in either tier and needs a <strong>proxy-only subnet</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>.</li>\n<li>Standard Tier with a regional load balancer gives up the global anycast IP and <strong>Cloud CDN</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>If the goal is to <strong>lower network cost</strong> for internet-facing traffic (for example, a game backend serving players) → choose <strong>Standard Tier</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q4\">Exam 5 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>If the backends are in one region and the users are local (europe-west2, UK only) → choose <strong>Standard Tier with a regional load balancer</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q22\">Exam 5 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>If users are worldwide, backends are in several regions, or you need Cloud CDN → choose <strong>Premium Tier with a global load balancer</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Comparison: what each \"network\" option actually does</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>What it solves</th>\n<th>Lowers egress cost?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard Tier</td>\n<td>Pricing and routing of internet traffic</td>\n<td><strong>Yes</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shared VPC host project</td>\n<td>Central network administration and IAM</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Private GKE cluster</td>\n<td>Security (nodes get internal IPs only)</td>\n<td>No, and NAT can add cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>External Application Load Balancer as Ingress</td>\n<td>Distributing HTTP traffic</td>\n<td>No, and it has its own charges</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cloud NAT</td>\n<td>Outbound internet access for private VMs</td>\n<td>No, and it does not raise bandwidth</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q4\">Exam 5 · Q4</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q14\">Exam 4 · Q14</a></p>\n<h3>Traps</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>\"Standard Tier + global load balancer.\"</strong> It looks like the best of both, but the combination is invalid (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>, option C).</li>\n<li><strong>\"Premium + regional.\"</strong> The scope is right but the tier is the expensive one (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>, option B).</li>\n<li><strong>\"HTTP load balancer as Ingress.\"</strong> It improves traffic distribution, not the price per GB <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers Premium and Standard Network Service Tiers and how tier choice limits load balancer scope. The exam tests whether you can cut internet egress cost with Standard Tier while avoiding invalid combinations such as Standard Tier with a global load balancer.","objectives":["After this lesson you can compare Premium Tier and Standard Tier on cost, performance and scope.","After this lesson you can identify which load balancers and IP addresses require Premium Tier.","After this lesson you can pick the right tier and load balancer scope for local versus worldwide users.","After this lesson you can reject network options that do not reduce egress cost (Shared VPC, private clusters, Cloud NAT, a load balancer used as Ingress)."],"takeaways":["To lower internet-facing network cost, choose Standard Tier.","Backends in one region with local users: Standard Tier plus a regional load balancer.","Global external IPs and the global external Application Load Balancer require Premium Tier, so Standard plus global is an invalid combination.","Standard Tier with a regional load balancer gives up the global anycast IP and Cloud CDN.","Shared VPC, private GKE clusters, Cloud NAT and a load balancer used as Ingress do not reduce egress cost."],"questions":["e1q41","e2q24","e4q14","e5q4","e5q22"]},{"id":"d5l3","num":3,"title":"Global serving for latency (Cloud Run)","html":"<ul>\n<li>If users are worldwide and should reach the <strong>nearest region</strong> → <strong>deploy the service to every region</strong>, behind <strong>one global anycast IP on a global external HTTP(S) load balancer</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q59\">Exam 5 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>A single region behind a global load balancer is not enough. Neither is Cloud Armor, which is a security control, not a latency tool <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q59\">Exam 5 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>This is the opposite of the cost rule in section 2: when <strong>latency for global users</strong> is the goal, choose global + Premium; when <strong>cost for local users</strong> is the goal, choose regional + Standard.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers how to serve a Cloud Run service to users worldwide with low latency. It is the counterpart to the cost rule in lesson 2: when global latency is the goal, you deploy to many regions behind one global anycast IP on a Premium Tier global load balancer.","objectives":["After this lesson you can design a multi-region Cloud Run deployment that routes users to the nearest region.","After this lesson you can explain why one region behind a global load balancer, or Cloud Armor, does not solve latency.","After this lesson you can decide between the global latency design and the regional cost design based on the question's goal."],"takeaways":["Worldwide users who should reach the nearest region: deploy the service to every region behind one global anycast IP on a global external HTTP(S) load balancer.","A single region behind a global load balancer still serves far-away users from far away.","Cloud Armor is a security control, not a latency tool.","Regional load balancers cannot share one global anycast IP; a global IP needs the global, Premium Tier load balancer.","Latency for global users points to global plus Premium; cost for local users points to regional plus Standard."],"questions":["e5q59"]},{"id":"d5l4","num":4,"title":"VM network throughput","html":"<ul>\n<li>The <strong>bandwidth cap applies to the whole instance</strong>, not to each vNIC. It scales with the <strong>vCPU count</strong> (the bank cites about 2 Gbps per vCPU) up to a limit set by the machine series. <strong>Tier_1 networking</strong> on qualifying machine types also raises the cap <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q14\">Exam 4 · Q14</a>.</li>\n<li>If monitoring shows a VM is pinned at its maximum throughput → <strong>resize to a larger machine type</strong> (n2-standard-2 → n2-standard-8) <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q14\">Exam 4 · Q14</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q41\">Exam 5 · Q41</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Distractor</th>\n<th>Why it fails</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Add more vNICs</td>\n<td>Bandwidth is counted per instance, so extra NICs do not add bandwidth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cloud NAT</td>\n<td>Provides outbound connectivity for private VMs, not throughput</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ops Agent</td>\n<td>Adds observability only, not capacity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>","summary":"This lesson explains how Compute Engine network bandwidth is capped per instance and scales with vCPU count. The exam presents a VM pinned at its maximum throughput and offers distractors such as adding NICs, Cloud NAT or the Ops Agent.","objectives":["After this lesson you can explain that the egress bandwidth cap applies to the whole instance and scales with vCPUs up to a machine-series limit.","After this lesson you can choose the right fix for a VM that is pinned at its throughput limit.","After this lesson you can explain why extra vNICs, Cloud NAT and the Ops Agent do not raise throughput."],"takeaways":["The bandwidth cap is per instance, not per vNIC, and scales with vCPU count (the bank cites about 2 Gbps per vCPU).","VM pinned at maximum throughput: resize to a larger machine type (for example n2-standard-2 to n2-standard-8).","Tier_1 networking on qualifying machine types also raises the cap.","Adding vNICs, Cloud NAT or the Ops Agent does not add bandwidth."],"questions":["e4q14","e5q41"]},{"id":"d5l5","num":5,"title":"Cost attribution: labels + billing export to BigQuery","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Labels</strong> are key/value pairs that are <strong>forwarded to Cloud Billing</strong>. <strong>Billing export to BigQuery</strong> writes cost line items, including their labels, into a dataset you can query <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q18\">Exam 5 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>If several systems share <strong>one project</strong> → the project cannot separate them. <strong>Label each system's resources, export billing to BigQuery, and group cost by label</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Not billing dimensions:</strong> instance <strong>metadata</strong>, <strong>Cloud Logging exports</strong> (they contain log entries, not charges) and <strong>VM names</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q18\">Exam 5 · Q18</a>.</li>\n<li>The console billing reports <em>can</em> group by label, <strong>but only after the labels exist</strong>. Without labels, a \"cost breakdown report\" cannot split costs by system (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>, option A).</li>\n<li>Good practice: apply labels at provisioning time (through IaC), keep watching for unlabeled spend, and use a separate allocation rule for shared costs such as networking <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q48\">Exam 2 · Q48</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers how to attribute cloud cost to systems or teams that share one project, using labels and Cloud Billing export to BigQuery. Exam questions test which metadata actually reaches billing data and which tempting options (metadata, log exports, VM names) do not.","objectives":["After this lesson you can split costs between systems that share one project by using labels and billing export to BigQuery.","After this lesson you can identify which attributes are billing dimensions and which are not.","After this lesson you can describe good labeling practice, such as applying labels through IaC at provisioning time.","After this lesson you can query labels in the billing export correctly (labels are a repeated key/value field)."],"takeaways":["Cost per system inside one project: label each system's resources, export billing to BigQuery, and group cost by label.","Labels are forwarded to Cloud Billing; instance metadata, Cloud Logging exports and VM names are not billing dimensions.","Console billing reports can group by label, but only after the labels exist.","In the BigQuery billing export, labels is a repeated key/value field, so use UNNEST(labels) and filter on the key.","Apply labels at provisioning time through IaC and watch for unlabeled spend."],"questions":["e2q48","e5q18"]},{"id":"d5l6","num":6,"title":"Rightsizing and resource utilization","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>VM rightsizing:</strong> the <strong>VM instance rightsizing recommender</strong> (Active Assist, available through the <strong>Recommender API</strong>) analyzes CPU and memory over the <strong>last 8 days</strong>, using data Compute Engine already collects. <strong>No agent is needed</strong> for a CPU-based recommendation. Query it and apply the result <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q58\">Exam 4 · Q58</a>.</li>\n<li>Installing the Ops Agent, by hand or through an Agent Policy, is optional. It only becomes necessary when you need finer guest or memory metrics <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q58\">Exam 4 · Q58</a>.</li>\n<li>Picking a machine type by reading a dashboard yourself is subjective, takes more steps, and is not Google's recommended practice <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q58\">Exam 4 · Q58</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Run utilization:</strong> <strong>Cloud Monitoring</strong> already has built-in container CPU and memory utilization metrics, with no setup. There is no VM, so the Ops Agent cannot be installed <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q6\">Exam 2 · Q6</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q27\">Exam 5 · Q27</a>.</li>\n<li>Look at <strong>peak</strong> utilization, not just the average, before you resize <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q6\">Exam 2 · Q6</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Rule:</strong> if Google already collects the data and a recommender exists → use the recommender or the built-in metric, not your own pipeline <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q58\">Exam 4 · Q58</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q6\">Exam 2 · Q6</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers how to find over- or under-provisioned resources using data Google already collects. For VMs that means the rightsizing recommender in Active Assist; for Cloud Run it means Cloud Monitoring's built-in container metrics. The exam rewards the managed, no-setup option over building your own pipeline or installing agents.","objectives":["After this lesson you can use the VM instance rightsizing recommender through the Recommender API to resize VMs.","After this lesson you can explain when the Ops Agent is optional and when it cannot be used at all (Cloud Run).","After this lesson you can check Cloud Run CPU and memory utilization with built-in Cloud Monitoring metrics.","After this lesson you can explain why peak utilization matters, not just the average, before resizing."],"takeaways":["VM rightsizing: query the rightsizing recommender (Recommender API), which uses the last 8 days of CPU and memory data and needs no agent for CPU-based recommendations.","The Ops Agent is optional for rightsizing and only needed for finer guest or memory metrics.","Cloud Run utilization: use Cloud Monitoring's built-in container CPU and memory metrics; there is no VM, so no Ops Agent.","Check peak utilization, not just the average, before resizing.","If Google already collects the data and a recommender exists, use it instead of a custom pipeline or manual dashboard reading."],"questions":["e2q6","e4q58","e5q27"]},{"id":"d5l7","num":7,"title":"Logging cost control (GKE)","html":"<ul>\n<li>Scenario: a dev cluster has verbose application logs, developers read them with <code>kubectl logs</code> (not Cloud Logging), and GKE operational logs must be kept. The bank's answer is an <strong>exclusion filter on the <code>_Default</code> sink</strong> for <code>k8s_container</code> logs <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q35\">Exam 5 · Q35</a>.</li>\n<li>What each option does, per the bank's explanation:</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--logging=SYSTEM</code></td>\n<td>Sends system logs only; workload logs no longer go to Cloud Logging</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>--logging=WORKLOAD</code></td>\n<td>Does not reduce verbosity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Disable the <code>_Default</code> sink</td>\n<td>Too broad: GKE operational logs are lost too</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exclusion filter on <code>_Default</code></td>\n<td>Targeted cut in log volume, which lowers cost</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>⚠️ The bank's answer is questionable. See the final section.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers how to reduce Cloud Logging cost from verbose GKE workload logs while keeping GKE operational logs. The bank's answer is disputed, so the lesson focuses on what each option (logging component settings, sink changes, exclusion filters) actually does and which one the guide recommends for the exam.","objectives":["After this lesson you can explain what the GKE --logging=SYSTEM and --logging=WORKLOAD settings do.","After this lesson you can explain why disabling the _Default sink is too broad.","After this lesson you can choose a log-cost reduction that keeps GKE operational logs when developers read logs with kubectl logs."],"takeaways":["Dev GKE cluster, developers read logs with kubectl logs, system logs must be kept: prefer --logging=SYSTEM.","--logging=SYSTEM sends system logs only, so workload logs stop going to Cloud Logging.","Disabling the _Default sink is too broad because it also drops GKE operational logs.","The _Required sink keeps audit logs even if _Default is disabled.","kubectl logs does not depend on Cloud Logging."],"questions":["e5q35"]},{"id":"d5l8","num":8,"title":"Performance tools: Monitoring vs Profiler vs Trace vs logs","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question asked</th>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>How much CPU or memory is the service using? (cost/sizing)</td>\n<td><strong>Cloud Monitoring</strong> built-in metrics</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q6\">Exam 2 · Q6</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Which <strong>code path or function</strong> is hot or slow, in production?</td>\n<td><strong>Cloud Profiler</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q42\">Exam 4 · Q42</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q64\">Exam 5 · Q64</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Where does latency go across a request or downstream calls?</td>\n<td><strong>Cloud Trace</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q6\">Exam 2 · Q6</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q29\">Exam 4 · Q29</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A custom aggregate that is only found in log content</td>\n<td><strong>Logs-based metric</strong></td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q6\">Exam 2 · Q6</a></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Is the resource saturated? Alert on it</td>\n<td>Resource metric (e.g., CPU utilization)</td>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q42\">Exam 4 · Q42</a></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Cloud Profiler facts</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Profiler is a <strong>statistical (sampling)</strong> profiler that runs <strong>continuously</strong> and <strong>attributes CPU and heap to source code</strong>. Overhead is under 5% at collection time and commonly under 0.5% amortized, so it is safe to leave on in production <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q42\">Exam 4 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>It is managed by Google. In Go, call <code>profiler.Start</code> from <code>cloud.google.com/go/profiler</code> early in <code>main</code>. On App Engine standard, the service name and version are inferred automatically <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q42\">Exam 4 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>Results are viewed as <strong>flame graphs</strong>. Use Profiler to compare different algorithms in staging and production <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q64\">Exam 5 · Q64</a>.</li>\n<li>Profiler needs a <strong>language-specific agent or library inside the application</strong>. It is <strong>not</strong> paired with the Ops Agent <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q6\">Exam 2 · Q6</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision rules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>If the problem is intermittent, only at peak, cannot be reproduced locally, and both overhead and management must be minimal → choose <strong>Cloud Profiler</strong>. A self-hosted profiler on Compute Engine adds management work, and running <code>go tool pprof</code> by hand is not continuous <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q42\">Exam 4 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>If Profiler shows the <strong>heap climbing until each restart</strong> on GKE → <strong>raise the container's memory limit in the deployment</strong>. Adding bigger nodes does not change the limit that triggers the OOM kill, raising the CPU limit targets the wrong resource, and Trace measures latency, not memory <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q29\">Exam 4 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Wall-clock vs CPU time</strong> (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q45\">Exam 4 · Q45</a>, choose two), as the bank teaches it:</li>\n<li>A <strong>large gap</strong> → increase the <strong>CPU</strong> allocation (not memory or disk).</li>\n<li>A <strong>small gap</strong> plus latency <strong>burning the error budget</strong> (from Trace) → the code is CPU-bound, so <strong>mark the application for code optimization</strong>.</li>\n<li>\"Low heap usage\" is <strong>not</strong> a signal that the application needs tuning.</li>\n<li>⚠️ See the final section.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson maps performance questions to the right tool: Cloud Monitoring for utilization, Cloud Profiler for hot code paths, Cloud Trace for request latency, and logs-based metrics for values only found in log content. It also covers how the exam expects you to act on Profiler findings such as heap growth and wall-clock versus CPU time.","objectives":["After this lesson you can choose between Cloud Monitoring, Cloud Profiler, Cloud Trace and logs-based metrics for a given performance question.","After this lesson you can explain why Cloud Profiler is safe to run continuously in production and how it is enabled.","After this lesson you can give the exam's expected responses to Profiler findings (heap growth, wall-clock versus CPU time gaps).","After this lesson you can spot where the exam answer differs from real-world diagnosis."],"takeaways":["Utilization: Cloud Monitoring. Hot code paths: Cloud Profiler. Latency across services: Cloud Trace. Aggregates only in log content: logs-based metric.","Cloud Profiler is a managed, continuous, sampling profiler with low overhead, viewed as flame graphs; in Go you call profiler.Start early in main.","Profiler needs a language-specific agent or library in the application; it is not paired with the Ops Agent.","Heap climbing until restart on GKE: the exam answer is to raise the container memory limit, but treat it as a stopgap for a likely memory leak.","Wall-clock versus CPU time (exam answer): a large gap means add CPU; a small gap with latency burning the error budget means optimize the code."],"questions":["e2q6","e4q29","e4q42","e4q45","e5q64"]},{"id":"d5l9","num":9,"title":"Capacity planning: measure first","html":"<ul>\n<li>If the question asks what to do <strong>\"first\"</strong> to prepare for a peak or busy season → <strong>run a load test</strong> to learn how the system scales and where it bottlenecks. Autoscaling thresholds, pre-provisioning and DR sizing should all be based on those results <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q59\">Exam 2 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>Traps: doubling last season's capacity is an arbitrary multiplier; turning on autoscaling before you know the right metric and thresholds; a DR runbook is a different concern from normal peak traffic <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q59\">Exam 2 · Q59</a>.</li>\n<li>Google's peak-event guidance says to start load testing <strong>3–5 months</strong> before the event <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q59\">Exam 2 · Q59</a>.</li>\n</ul>","summary":"This lesson covers capacity planning for a peak event or busy season. When a question asks what to do first, the exam expects you to measure with a load test before setting autoscaling thresholds, pre-provisioning or sizing anything.","objectives":["After this lesson you can identify load testing as the first step in preparing for a peak event.","After this lesson you can explain why arbitrary capacity multipliers and premature autoscaling settings are traps.","After this lesson you can separate peak-traffic planning from disaster recovery planning."],"takeaways":["Asked what to do first before a peak season: run a load test to learn how the system scales and where it bottlenecks.","Base autoscaling thresholds, pre-provisioning and capacity sizing on load test results.","Doubling last season's capacity is an arbitrary multiplier, not a plan.","A DR runbook addresses a different concern than normal peak traffic.","Google's peak-event guidance says to start load testing 3 to 5 months before the event."],"questions":["e2q59"]}],"ref":{"cheat":"<ul>\n<li>Stable, fixed, business-critical, and \"no performance impact\" → <strong>CUD</strong>. Never Spot.</li>\n<li>Steady total usage but instances move between regions → <strong>compute flexible CUD</strong>; a longer horizon → <strong>3 years</strong>.</li>\n<li>A CUD is a discount, not capacity. You need guaranteed capacity → <strong>add a reservation</strong>.</li>\n<li>Interruptible or resumable work, no SLO, VM-only (no containers) → <strong>Spot VMs</strong>.</li>\n<li>Spot needs the system to survive losing a node (e.g., <strong>quorum</strong>); durable storage alone is not enough.</li>\n<li>MIGs and unmanaged groups do <strong>not</strong> lower prices.</li>\n<li>Cut internet egress cost → <strong>Standard Tier</strong>. Not Shared VPC, a private cluster, or a load balancer Ingress.</li>\n<li>One region and local users → <strong>Standard Tier + regional load balancer</strong>. A global load balancer or global IP needs <strong>Premium</strong>.</li>\n<li>Global users routed to the nearest region → <strong>deploy to each region + global HTTP(S) load balancer with an anycast IP</strong>.</li>\n<li>VM pinned at its throughput limit → <strong>larger machine type</strong> (bandwidth scales with vCPUs). Not NICs, NAT or Ops Agent.</li>\n<li>Cost per system in one project → <strong>labels + billing export to BigQuery + GROUP BY label</strong>.</li>\n<li>VM rightsizing → <strong>Recommender API (rightsizing recommender)</strong>, which needs no agent and uses the last 8 days of data.</li>\n<li>Cloud Run CPU or memory use → <strong>Cloud Monitoring built-in metrics</strong>. No setup, no Ops Agent.</li>\n<li>Hot code paths in production, low overhead → <strong>Cloud Profiler</strong> (<code>profiler.Start</code>), viewed as flame graphs.</li>\n<li>Latency across services → <strong>Trace</strong>; code hotspots → <strong>Profiler</strong>; utilization → <strong>Monitoring</strong>.</li>\n<li>Heap climbs until restart on GKE → <strong>raise the container memory limit</strong> (not node size, CPU or Trace).</li>\n<li>Wall-clock vs CPU time: big gap → add CPU (exam answer); small gap + error budget burning → optimize the code.</li>\n<li>Prepare for a busy season: <strong>load test first</strong>, then size autoscaling and capacity.</li>\n<li>Dev GKE logs read with <code>kubectl logs</code>, system logs must be kept → prefer <code>--logging=SYSTEM</code> (the bank says exclusion filter; see ⚠️).</li>\n</ul>","rules":[{"lesson":"d5l1","title":"Compute pricing: CUDs, flexible commitments, reservations, Spot VMs","html":"<ul>\n<li>If the workload is stable, fixed in size, business-critical, and \"no performance impact\" is required → choose <strong>CUDs</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q7\">Exam 5 · Q7</a>.</li>\n<li>If total demand is steady or growing but instances will <strong>move between regions</strong>, on a multi-year horizon → choose a <strong>3-year compute flexible commitment</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q45\">Exam 3 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li>If the question also needs <strong>assured capacity in specific zones</strong> → add <strong>reservations</strong> (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q45\">Exam 3 · Q45</a>, option B becomes correct).</li>\n<li>If the work can be interrupted (resumable or checkpointed, no completion-time SLO) and must stay on VMs → choose <strong>Spot VMs</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q54\">Exam 3 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li>If the architecture survives losing a node (quorum, replication, stateless with external coordination) → it is a <strong>Spot candidate</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q5\">Exam 1 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li>If the workload is \"unsuitable for containerization\" → rule out <strong>Cloud Run and GKE</strong>, which both run containers <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q54\">Exam 3 · Q54</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d5l2","title":"Network Service Tiers and load balancer scope","html":"<ul>\n<li>If the goal is to <strong>lower network cost</strong> for internet-facing traffic (for example, a game backend serving players) → choose <strong>Standard Tier</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q4\">Exam 5 · Q4</a>.</li>\n<li>If the backends are in one region and the users are local (europe-west2, UK only) → choose <strong>Standard Tier with a regional load balancer</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>, <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q22\">Exam 5 · Q22</a>.</li>\n<li>If users are worldwide, backends are in several regions, or you need Cloud CDN → choose <strong>Premium Tier with a global load balancer</strong> <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d5l8","title":"Performance tools: Monitoring vs Profiler vs Trace vs logs","html":"<ul>\n<li>If the problem is intermittent, only at peak, cannot be reproduced locally, and both overhead and management must be minimal → choose <strong>Cloud Profiler</strong>. A self-hosted profiler on Compute Engine adds management work, and running <code>go tool pprof</code> by hand is not continuous <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q42\">Exam 4 · Q42</a>.</li>\n<li>If Profiler shows the <strong>heap climbing until each restart</strong> on GKE → <strong>raise the container's memory limit in the deployment</strong>. Adding bigger nodes does not change the limit that triggers the OOM kill, raising the CPU limit targets the wrong resource, and Trace measures latency, not memory <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q29\">Exam 4 · Q29</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Wall-clock vs CPU time</strong> (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q45\">Exam 4 · Q45</a>, choose two), as the bank teaches it:</li>\n<li>A <strong>large gap</strong> → increase the <strong>CPU</strong> allocation (not memory or disk).</li>\n<li>A <strong>small gap</strong> plus latency <strong>burning the error budget</strong> (from Trace) → the code is CPU-bound, so <strong>mark the application for code optimization</strong>.</li>\n<li>\"Low heap usage\" is <strong>not</strong> a signal that the application needs tuning.</li>\n<li>⚠️ See the final section.</li>\n</ul>"}],"traps":[{"lesson":"d5l1","title":"Compute pricing: CUDs, flexible commitments, reservations, Spot VMs","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Spot VMs for a business-critical workload.</strong> It is the most tempting distractor, but the discount comes from accepting interruption <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q30\">Exam 1 · Q30</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>\"The data is in durable storage, so Spot is fine.\"</strong> Durable inputs and outputs do not protect work in progress. A GPU render job with no checkpoint loses its progress when preempted <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q5\">Exam 1 · Q5</a>. See ⚠️ below.</li>\n<li><strong>\"Scalable\" is not the same as \"tolerates losing a node.\"</strong> A scalable cache is not necessarily safe on Spot <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q5\">Exam 1 · Q5</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Treating a commitment as a capacity guarantee,</strong> or adding reservations the question never asked for <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q45\">Exam 3 · Q45</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>A 3-year CUD for a resumable job with no SLO.</strong> It works, but Spot VMs are cheaper <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q54\">Exam 3 · Q54</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Picking Spot does not make the application recoverable.</strong> The application still needs its own retry and resume logic <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q54\">Exam 3 · Q54</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},{"lesson":"d5l2","title":"Network Service Tiers and load balancer scope","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>\"Standard Tier + global load balancer.\"</strong> It looks like the best of both, but the combination is invalid (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>, option C).</li>\n<li><strong>\"Premium + regional.\"</strong> The scope is right but the tier is the expensive one (<a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>, option B).</li>\n<li><strong>\"HTTP load balancer as Ingress.\"</strong> It improves traffic distribution, not the price per GB <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>.</li>\n</ul>"}],"questionable":"<ul>\n<li><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q35\">Exam 5 · Q35</a> (logging).</strong> The bank's answer is weak in two ways.</li>\n<li>The filter <code>severity &gt;= DEBUG</code> matches essentially <strong>all</strong> container logs at DEBUG or above. It does not, as the explanation claims, exclude only \"DEBUG and lower.\" The explanation misreads the filter.</li>\n<li>Developers read logs with <code>kubectl logs</code>, which does not depend on Cloud Logging. GKE operational logs must be kept. That fits <strong><code>--logging=SYSTEM</code></strong> (option I), which many sources treat as the intended answer.</li>\n<li>Disabling <code>_Default</code> also does not remove <em>all</em> logging, because the <code>_Required</code> sink still keeps audit logs.</li>\n<li>On the exam, prefer <strong>SYSTEM</strong> for this exact scenario.</li>\n<li><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q45\">Exam 4 · Q45</a>, option A (large wall-clock/CPU gap → add CPU).</strong> Cloud Profiler's own concepts describe wall time much larger than CPU time as time spent <strong>waiting</strong> (I/O, locks, downstream calls). Adding CPU usually does not fix that.</li>\n<li>The bank's answer matches the published sample question, so remember it for the exam.</li>\n<li>It is not a general rule for real diagnosis.</li>\n<li><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e4q29\">Exam 4 · Q29</a> (heap grows until restart → raise the memory limit).</strong> A heap that grows steadily after a new release suggests a <strong>memory leak</strong>. Raising the limit only delays the OOM kill.</li>\n<li>It is the expected exam answer. The explanation itself mentions a parallel effort to fix the code.</li>\n<li>Treat it as a stopgap, not a fix.</li>\n<li><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q5\">Exam 1 · Q5</a> (Spot VM workload).</strong> Google's Spot documentation lists batch and <strong>rendering / media-processing</strong> jobs as typical Spot workloads. Rejecting option D (a GPU render job that reads from and writes to a bucket) because \"checkpointing isn't stated\" is a stretch.</li>\n<li>Compare <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e3q54\">Exam 3 · Q54</a>, where resumable processing makes Spot correct.</li>\n<li>Learn the exam's decisive cue: \"sufficient quorum\" is stated explicitly. Do not conclude that rendering is a poor fit for Spot.</li>\n<li><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q59\">Exam 5 · Q59</a>, reasoning for option III.</strong> The real reason option III fails is that <strong>regional external load balancers cannot share one global anycast IP</strong>; global IPs require the global, Premium-Tier load balancer <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>. The explanation's \"latency within continents\" argument is not the real reason. The answer itself (II) is fine.</li>\n<li><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q18\">Exam 5 · Q18</a>, sample SQL.</strong> In the billing export, <code>labels</code> is a repeated key/value field. You need <code>UNNEST(labels)</code> and a filter on <code>key = 'system'</code>. Selecting <code>labels.system</code> directly will not work. The answer (B) is still correct.</li>\n<li><strong><a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e5q4\">Exam 5 · Q4</a>, option IV.</strong> \"The HTTP load balancer is Premium-only and therefore more expensive\" is an overstatement. A regional external Application Load Balancer can run in Standard Tier <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e2q24\">Exam 2 · Q24</a>. The correct reason to reject option IV is that it solves traffic distribution, not cost <a class=\"cite\" href=\"#/q/e1q41\">Exam 1 · Q41</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},"glossary":[{"term":"Active Assist","definition":"Google Cloud's portfolio of recommenders that analyze usage data and suggest actions such as rightsizing VMs.","exam_context":"When the question asks for Google's recommended way to resize VMs, pick the Active Assist rightsizing recommender over reading dashboards yourself.","lesson":"d5l6","domain":5},{"term":"Billing export to BigQuery","definition":"A Cloud Billing feature that writes cost line items, including resource labels, into a BigQuery dataset you can query.","exam_context":"Combined with labels, it is the answer for splitting cost between systems that share one project.","lesson":"d5l5","domain":5},{"term":"Cloud Armor","definition":"Google Cloud's security service that protects applications behind load balancers.","exam_context":"It is a distractor in latency questions: it is a security control, not a way to serve users from a nearer region.","lesson":"d5l3","domain":5},{"term":"Cloud CDN","definition":"Google's content delivery network that caches content at the edge for external Application Load Balancers.","exam_context":"Standard Tier with a regional load balancer gives up Cloud CDN; if CDN is needed, choose Premium Tier with a global load balancer.","lesson":"d5l2","domain":5},{"term":"Cloud Monitoring built-in metrics","definition":"Metrics that Google Cloud collects automatically, such as container CPU and memory utilization for Cloud Run, with no setup.","exam_context":"For Cloud Run utilization or sizing questions, use these metrics; the Ops Agent cannot be installed because there is no VM.","lesson":"d5l6","domain":5},{"term":"Cloud NAT","definition":"A managed service that gives private VMs outbound internet access without external IP addresses.","exam_context":"A distractor for both egress cost and throughput: it neither lowers network cost nor raises VM bandwidth.","lesson":"d5l4","domain":5},{"term":"Cloud Profiler","definition":"A managed, continuous, statistical (sampling) profiler that attributes CPU and heap usage to source code with low overhead, shown as flame graphs.","exam_context":"Pick it when you must find hot or slow code paths in production with minimal overhead and management.","lesson":"d5l8","domain":5},{"term":"Cloud Trace","definition":"A distributed tracing service that shows where latency goes across a request and its downstream calls.","exam_context":"Use it for latency across services; it measures latency, not memory or CPU usage.","lesson":"d5l8","domain":5},{"term":"Committed use discount (CUD)","definition":"A lower price in return for a 1-year or 3-year commitment; it is a billing arrangement only, so VMs run unchanged.","exam_context":"Stable, fixed, business-critical workloads that need no performance impact get CUDs, never Spot VMs.","lesson":"d5l1","domain":5},{"term":"Compute flexible commitment","definition":"A spend-based commitment to a minimum hourly spend that applies across regions and projects in the same Cloud Billing account.","exam_context":"Choose it (3-year for a multi-year horizon) when total usage is steady but instances move between regions.","lesson":"d5l1","domain":5},{"term":"Container memory limit","definition":"The memory limit set on a container in a Kubernetes deployment; exceeding it causes the container to be OOM-killed.","exam_context":"When Profiler shows heap climbing until each restart on GKE, the exam answer is to raise this limit, not node size or CPU (it is a stopgap if the code leaks).","lesson":"d5l8","domain":5},{"term":"Exclusion filter","definition":"A filter on a log sink that drops matching log entries so they are not stored, reducing log volume and cost.","exam_context":"Appears as the bank's answer for verbose GKE dev logs, but for that exact scenario the guide recommends --logging=SYSTEM instead.","lesson":"d5l7","domain":5},{"term":"Flame graph","definition":"A visualization of profiling data that shows which functions consume the most CPU or memory.","exam_context":"Cloud Profiler presents results as flame graphs, useful for comparing algorithms in staging and production.","lesson":"d5l8","domain":5},{"term":"GKE logging components (--logging)","definition":"GKE cluster setting that selects which logs go to Cloud Logging; SYSTEM sends system logs only, WORKLOAD adds workload logs.","exam_context":"For a dev cluster where developers use kubectl logs and system logs must be kept, prefer --logging=SYSTEM; WORKLOAD does not reduce verbosity.","lesson":"d5l7","domain":5},{"term":"Global anycast IP","definition":"A single global external IP address served from many locations, routing users to the nearest healthy backend.","exam_context":"Needed to route worldwide users to the nearest region; it requires the global, Premium Tier load balancer.","lesson":"d5l3","domain":5},{"term":"Global external Application Load Balancer","definition":"Google's global, Envoy-based HTTP(S) load balancer that serves backends in many regions behind one anycast IP.","exam_context":"Requires Premium Tier; use it for worldwide users and multi-region backends, not for local cost savings.","lesson":"d5l2","domain":5},{"term":"Labels","definition":"Key/value pairs attached to resources that are forwarded to Cloud Billing and appear in billing data.","exam_context":"Labels, not metadata, VM names or log exports, are how you attribute cost to systems; apply them at provisioning time through IaC.","lesson":"d5l5","domain":5},{"term":"Load testing","definition":"Running controlled traffic against a system to learn how it scales and where it bottlenecks.","exam_context":"When asked what to do first before a peak season, run a load test before setting autoscaling or capacity.","lesson":"d5l9","domain":5},{"term":"Logs-based metric","definition":"A Cloud Monitoring metric derived from the content of log entries.","exam_context":"Use it only when the value you need exists only in log content; for CPU or memory use built-in metrics.","lesson":"d5l8","domain":5},{"term":"Managed instance group (MIG)","definition":"A group of identical VMs that provides autoscaling, autohealing and rolling updates.","exam_context":"MIGs do not lower prices, so they are wrong answers to pricing questions.","lesson":"d5l1","domain":5},{"term":"Network Service Tiers","definition":"A Google Cloud setting that chooses how internet traffic is routed and priced: Premium Tier or Standard Tier.","exam_context":"Cost goal points to Standard Tier; global latency goal or global load balancer points to Premium Tier.","lesson":"d5l2","domain":5},{"term":"Ops Agent","definition":"Google's agent installed on VMs to collect guest-level logs and metrics.","exam_context":"A frequent distractor: not needed for CPU-based rightsizing, impossible on Cloud Run, unrelated to Profiler, and does not add throughput.","lesson":"d5l6","domain":5},{"term":"Premium Tier","definition":"The default network tier, which carries traffic on Google's global backbone for better performance at higher cost.","exam_context":"Required for global external IPs and the global external Application Load Balancer.","lesson":"d5l2","domain":5},{"term":"Proxy-only subnet","definition":"A subnet reserved for the Envoy proxies used by regional Envoy-based load balancers.","exam_context":"The regional external Application Load Balancer needs one and works in either network tier.","lesson":"d5l2","domain":5},{"term":"Recommender API","definition":"The API that exposes Active Assist recommendations, including the VM instance rightsizing recommender.","exam_context":"Query it to rightsize VMs; it uses data Compute Engine already collects, so no agent is needed for CPU-based recommendations.","lesson":"d5l6","domain":5},{"term":"Reservation","definition":"A Compute Engine feature that holds VM capacity in specific zones; unused reserved capacity is still charged.","exam_context":"Add a reservation only when the question needs assured capacity, because commitments do not reserve capacity.","lesson":"d5l1","domain":5},{"term":"Resource-based commitment","definition":"A committed use discount for predictable, steady usage of specific resources, tied to one region.","exam_context":"Fits steady usage in one region; if workloads move between regions, choose a compute flexible commitment instead.","lesson":"d5l1","domain":5},{"term":"Rightsizing recommender","definition":"The VM instance rightsizing recommender analyzes the last 8 days of CPU and memory data and suggests a better machine type.","exam_context":"The recommended answer for resizing VMs, ahead of manual dashboard reading or installing agents first.","lesson":"d5l6","domain":5},{"term":"Spot VM","definition":"A heavily discounted VM (the bank cites 60 to 90 percent) that Compute Engine can reclaim at any time and that is excluded from the Compute Engine SLA.","exam_context":"Choose it for interruptible or resumable work, or systems that survive losing a node; never for business-critical workloads.","lesson":"d5l1","domain":5},{"term":"Standard Tier","definition":"A regional network tier that hands traffic to ordinary ISP transit networks, costing less with lower performance and availability.","exam_context":"The answer for cutting internet egress cost; it cannot be combined with a global load balancer or global anycast IP.","lesson":"d5l2","domain":5},{"term":"Tier_1 networking","definition":"A higher-bandwidth networking option on qualifying Compute Engine machine types that raises the per-instance bandwidth cap.","exam_context":"Along with a larger machine type, a valid way to raise VM throughput; extra vNICs are not.","lesson":"d5l4","domain":5},{"term":"VM network bandwidth cap","definition":"The per-instance egress bandwidth limit, which scales with vCPU count up to a limit set by the machine series.","exam_context":"A VM pinned at max throughput needs a larger machine type, not more vNICs, Cloud NAT or the Ops Agent.","lesson":"d5l4","domain":5},{"term":"Wall-clock time vs CPU time","definition":"Profiler measurements of elapsed time versus time actually spent executing on the CPU.","exam_context":"Exam answer: a large gap means add CPU, a small gap with error budget burning means optimize the code; in real diagnosis a large gap usually means waiting on I/O or locks.","lesson":"d5l8","domain":5}]}],"questions":{"e1q1":{"id":"e1q1","exam":1,"n":1,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You manage an application that exposes an HTTP endpoint directly, without a load balancer in front of it. HTTP response latency is critical to the user experience, and you want visibility into the latencies that all of your users are experiencing. You use Cloud Monitoring. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. In your application, create a metric with <code>metricKind</code> set to <code>DELTA</code> and <code>valueType</code> set to <code>DOUBLE</code>. In Cloud Monitoring’s Metrics Explorer, use a Stacked Bar chart to visualize the metric.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. In your application, create a metric with <code>metricKind</code> set to <code>CUMULATIVE</code> and <code>valueType</code> set to <code>DOUBLE</code>. In Cloud Monitoring’s Metrics Explorer, use a Line chart to visualize the metric.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. In your application, create a metric with <code>metricKind</code> set to <code>GAUGE</code> and <code>valueType</code> set to <code>DISTRIBUTION</code>. In Cloud Monitoring’s Metrics Explorer, use a Heatmap chart to visualize the metric.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. In your application, create a metric with <code>metricKind</code> set to <code>METRIC_KIND_UNSPECIFIED</code> and <code>valueType</code> set to <code>INT64</code>. In Cloud Monitoring’s Metrics Explorer, use a Stacked Area chart to visualize the metric.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. In your application, create a metric with</strong><code>**metricKind**</code><strong>set to</strong><code>**GAUGE**</code><strong>and</strong><code>**valueType**</code><strong>set to</strong><code>**DISTRIBUTION**</code><strong>. In Cloud Monitoring’s Metrics Explorer, use a Heatmap chart to visualize the metric.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard requirement is understanding the latency experienced by <em>all</em> users — not a single summary number like an average or a total. That rules out any scalar value type (DOUBLE, INT64) up front, because a single DOUBLE or INT64 value per time interval collapses every request’s latency into one number, discarding the shape of the distribution (how many requests were fast, how many were slow, whether there’s a long tail). DISTRIBUTION is the only value type built to preserve that per-request spread as a histogram. Once DISTRIBUTION is established as the required value type, the metric kind must also be a combination Cloud Monitoring actually permits for a custom (<code>custom.googleapis.com</code>) metric: DELTA-kind DISTRIBUTION metrics are explicitly disallowed for custom metrics, leaving GAUGE as the valid, practical choice for an application self-reporting a snapshot of recent latencies. The visualization then has to match the data: a Heatmap is purpose-built to render a single distribution-valued time series over time, which is exactly what this metric produces.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“understand what HTTP latencies all of your users are experiencing” is a hard constraint — it demands the full distribution/spread of latency values, not an aggregate like a sum or average, which immediately rules out scalar value types.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“without using a load balancer” is a technical signal — it establishes that no managed load-balancer latency metric is automatically available, so the application itself must create and report this custom metric.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The metricKind/valueType pairing in each option is a decisive technical signal on its own: only one pairing (GAUGE + DISTRIBUTION) is both semantically correct for latency data and a permitted combination for a custom metric.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A DISTRIBUTION-valued metric lets Cloud Monitoring bucket incoming latency measurements into a histogram, so instead of losing information to averaging, you retain the full shape of the data — letting you later compute arbitrary percentiles (p50, p90, p99) and see multimodal patterns an average would hide. Setting <code>metricKind</code> to GAUGE is the correct, custom-metric-compatible choice for an application reporting this distribution as periodic snapshots of recent request latencies (DELTA is not a valid kind for DISTRIBUTION-valued custom metrics). In Metrics Explorer, a Heatmap chart is specifically designed to display one distribution-valued time series, plotting time on the X-axis, latency buckets on the Y-axis, and using color intensity to represent how many requests fell into each bucket — giving you exactly the “latency experienced by all users” view the scenario asks for.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Incorrect:</strong> DOUBLE is a scalar value type, so this metric could only ever report a single number per interval (e.g., a sum or count of latency), not the distribution across users; it can’t show the spread of experienced latencies at all. It’s also a disallowed combination for a custom metric, since DELTA-kind metrics are not permitted with custom.googleapis.com metrics for several value types. A Stacked Bar chart compounds the mismatch by being built for comparing discrete scalar categories, not rendering distribution data.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Incorrect:</strong> CUMULATIVE + DOUBLE would track something like total accumulated latency or total request count over time, which is a running total, not per-request distribution data — it tells you nothing about the spread of individual latencies. A Line chart reinforces that this option is designed to show a scalar trend over time, not a histogram.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Incorrect:</strong> <code>METRIC_KIND_UNSPECIFIED</code> is not a usable kind for an actual custom metric definition — it’s a placeholder/default value in the API, not a deliberate choice you’d make when creating a metric. INT64 is again a scalar type, incapable of representing a distribution, and a Stacked Area chart is built for comparing multiple scalar series over time, not visualizing one distribution’s bucketed values.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming that any numeric metric type (DOUBLE or INT64) is “good enough” for latency monitoring as long as you’re collecting <em>something</em> — especially since CUMULATIVE/DOUBLE (Option B) sounds plausible for “tracking latency over time.” The decisive distinction the exam is testing is between <em>aggregate</em> numeric reporting (which erases per-request detail) and <em>distribution-valued</em> reporting (which preserves it) — only the latter can answer “what are all my users experiencing,” since an average or sum can look identical whether every user had the same latency or half had near-zero latency and half had extreme outliers.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>When you need to understand the full experience across a population of events (requests, users, transactions) rather than a single rolled-up number, distribution-valued metrics and histogram-style visualization (heatmaps, percentile charts) are the correct tool — averages and simple counters are appropriate for trend-watching a single aggregate quantity, not for characterizing variability or spotting a problematic tail of slow requests hiding behind a healthy-looking mean.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>In practice, teams exposing an HTTP endpoint without a managed load balancer (which would otherwise provide built-in request-latency distribution metrics automatically) instrument the application directly with a DISTRIBUTION-valued custom metric, then build a Heatmap or percentile-based dashboard in Cloud Monitoring to catch latency regressions — a p99 spike that would be completely invisible in a mean-latency line chart often reveals the exact subset of requests (e.g., those hitting a cold cache or a specific downstream dependency) causing real user pain.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Latency across all users” → DISTRIBUTION value type (never a scalar) → GAUGE kind for a custom metric → Heatmap chart to visualize it.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Whenever a Cloud Monitoring question asks you to understand the full spread or experience of a measurement across many events rather than a single aggregate, reach for a DISTRIBUTION-valued metric (GAUGE kind for custom metrics) visualized with a Heatmap — scalar value types (DOUBLE, INT64) and their matching chart types (Line, Bar, Stacked Area) are built for trend-watching single numbers, not for representing per-event variability.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/charts/charting-distribution-metrics\">Google Cloud — About distribution-valued metrics: </a>Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of using DISTRIBUTION-valued metrics to capture HTTP latency spread, and Heatmap charts as the designed visualization for a single distribution-valued time series</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/api/v3/kinds-and-types\"><strong>Google Cloud — Metric kinds and value types (custom metrics):</strong></a> Supports: the valid metricKind/valueType combinations, including that DELTA-kind DISTRIBUTION metrics are not permitted for custom (<code>custom.googleapis.com</code>) metrics, making GAUGE the correct kind here</p>","lessons":["d4l6"]},"e1q2":{"id":"e1q2","exam":1,"n":2,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You maintain a stateless, web-based API running on a single Compute Engine instance in the <code>europe-west2-a</code> zone. The Service Level Indicator (SLI) for availability is falling short of the defined Service Level Objective (SLO). A postmortem determined that requests are timing out because the API receives a high volume of traffic and the instance is running out of memory. You need to improve service availability. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Adjust the SLO so that it matches the currently measured SLI.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Migrate the service to a larger Compute Engine instance type with more memory.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Deploy additional service instances across other zones and distribute traffic across all of them using a load balancer.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Deploy additional service instances across other zones and configure them as failover targets to take over if the primary instance becomes unavailable.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Deploy additional service instances across other zones and distribute traffic across all of them using a load balancer.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The must-have constraints are: the root cause is resource exhaustion under high request volume (out-of-memory timeouts), and the goal is improved <em>availability</em> specifically — not just fixing one symptom. A single instance, however large, remains a single point of failure tied to one zone; if that zone has an outage, the service goes down regardless of its spec. The only option that addresses both the capacity problem (spreading request load across multiple instances relieves per-instance memory pressure) and the availability problem (removing the single-zone, single-instance dependency) is horizontal scaling across zones combined with active load balancing, since all instances share the traffic continuously rather than sitting idle waiting for a failure.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“stateless” is a hard enabler — it’s a technical signal that the service can safely run on multiple concurrent instances with no session-affinity or shared-state concerns, which is what makes load-balanced horizontal scaling viable.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“running out of memory” under “high number of requests” is the root cause — it points toward a capacity/scaling fix, not merely a redundancy fix.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“improve service availability” is the stated objective — it’s a sequence/scope instruction that the chosen fix must raise the measured SLI, not just paper over the gap or address only half the problem (capacity or resilience alone).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Running multiple stateless instances across different zones behind a load balancer accomplishes two things simultaneously: it distributes incoming requests so no single instance is overwhelmed to the point of exhausting memory (directly fixing the postmortem’s identified cause of the timeouts), and it removes the single-zone, single-instance fragility, so a zonal failure no longer takes the whole service down. Because load balancing is active (all instances serve traffic all the time), this also means capacity scales with demand rather than sitting unused until disaster strikes — which is what actually raises the measured availability SLI toward the SLO.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Lowering the SLO to match the current SLI doesn’t improve availability at all — it just redefines the target downward to make the problem disappear on paper. This conflicts directly with the stated goal of improving service availability and ignores the user-facing timeout issue entirely.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A bigger instance with more memory would likely reduce or eliminate the out-of-memory timeouts, addressing the capacity symptom. However, it leaves the service as a single instance in a single zone — a zonal outage still causes a full availability loss, so it doesn’t resolve the broader availability objective as completely as distributing across zones does.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Adding failover instances in other zones does remove the single-zone dependency, but a passive failover instance sits idle until the primary fails — it does nothing to relieve the primary instance’s memory pressure under normal high-traffic conditions, so the root-cause timeouts identified in the postmortem would persist until an actual failover event occurs. This would be the better choice only if the postmortem’s root cause were purely a zonal-outage risk rather than sustained resource exhaustion under normal load.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is picking D because “failover in case the primary instance is unavailable” sounds like the textbook high-availability pattern. But the postmortem’s root cause here isn’t an unavailable primary instance — it’s an overloaded one. A passive failover design doesn’t touch that root cause at all; only active load distribution does. If the scenario instead described a hardware/zone failure with no capacity issue, D would become the stronger answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>For a stateless service experiencing availability loss from resource exhaustion, the SRE-aligned fix is horizontal scaling with active load balancing, not vertical scaling (bigger instance) or passive redundancy (failover) alone — those each solve only one dimension (capacity or resilience) of a two-dimensional availability problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Teams facing this pattern typically deploy a managed instance group spanning multiple zones behind an external or internal load balancer, paired with autoscaling based on CPU/memory or request-based signals, so capacity grows automatically with demand and a single zone’s failure only removes a fraction of total capacity rather than the entire service — directly improving the measured SLI against the error-budget-driven SLO.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Timeouts from overload + availability goal → scale horizontally across zones with active load balancing, not a bigger box or an idle backup.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a postmortem ties availability loss to resource exhaustion under load, look for the option that both multiplies capacity and spreads it across failure domains with active traffic distribution — vertical scaling and passive failover each only solve half the problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instance-groups\"><strong>Google Cloud — Managed instance groups for highly available, scalable applications:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of distributing stateless workloads across multiple zones with active load balancing to address both capacity and availability</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/reliability\"><strong>Google Cloud Architecture Framework — Reliability pillar:</strong></a> Supports: why horizontal scaling plus load balancing is preferred over vertical scaling or passive failover for improving availability against an SLO</p>","lessons":["d3l2","d3l7"]},"e1q3":{"id":"e1q3","exam":1,"n":3,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You support an application running on Google Cloud and want to configure SMS notifications for your team for the most critical alerts in Cloud Monitoring. You have already identified which alerting policies should trigger these notifications. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Download and configure a third-party integration between Cloud Monitoring and an SMS gateway. Have your team members add their phone numbers to the external tool.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Select the Webhook notification option for each alerting policy, and configure it to call a third-party integration tool. Have your team members add their phone numbers to the external tool.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Have your team members add and verify their phone numbers as SMS notification channels in Cloud Monitoring. Select the SMS notification type for each alerting policy, and choose the appropriate verified phone numbers from the list.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Configure a Slack notification for each alerting policy. Set up a Slack-to-SMS integration to forward Slack messages as SMS. Have your team members add their phone numbers to the external integration.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Have your team members add and verify their phone numbers as SMS notification channels in Cloud Monitoring. Select the SMS notification type for each alerting policy, and choose the appropriate verified phone numbers from the list.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The must-have constraint is getting SMS notifications working for specific, already-identified alerting policies with the least unnecessary complexity. Cloud Monitoring natively supports SMS as a first-class notification channel type: you verify a phone number as a notification channel, then attach that channel directly to an alerting policy. Since this native capability exists and directly satisfies the requirement, any option that introduces a third-party gateway, a webhook relay, or a chained Slack-to-SMS bridge is solving the same problem with more moving parts, more external dependencies, and more points of failure than necessary — none of which the scenario calls for.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“SMS notifications... in Cloud Monitoring” is a hard constraint — it specifies the notification medium and the platform, pointing toward checking whether that platform has native support before reaching for an external tool.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“You have already identified the alerting policies you want to configure this for” is a scope/sequence signal — the task is purely about wiring up notification delivery to existing policies, not building new infrastructure or integrations.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“most critical alerts” implies reliability matters — an extra external hop (webhook → third-party tool → SMS, or Slack → SMS bridge) introduces more failure points between the alert firing and the message reaching someone’s phone than a direct, native channel does.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Monitoring lets you add SMS as a notification channel directly in the Alerting section of the console: each team member’s phone number is entered and verified there, after which it appears as a selectable SMS channel. When creating or editing an alerting policy, you choose the SMS notification type and select the relevant verified phone numbers from that list, and Cloud Monitoring delivers the SMS itself when the policy fires — no external gateway, webhook, or chat-platform bridge required. This is the simplest path that fully satisfies the stated need, using functionality Cloud Monitoring already provides out of the box.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A third-party SMS gateway integration could technically deliver text messages, but it duplicates a capability Cloud Monitoring already provides natively, adding an unnecessary external dependency, additional configuration and account management outside Google Cloud, and another potential point of failure for critical alerts — with no corresponding benefit given the requirement as stated.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Webhook notifications are the correct tool when you need to push alert data into a system that doesn’t have a purpose-built Cloud Monitoring integration (a custom incident-management tool, for example), triggering custom logic on receipt. Using a webhook here to reach a third-party SMS relay solves a problem that native SMS support already solves more directly, so it’s solving the right kind of problem with the wrong tool for this scenario.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> Slack notifications are well suited for team-visible, non-urgent alert awareness, and Slack itself has a native Cloud Monitoring integration. But chaining a Slack-to-SMS bridge on top adds two additional integration layers (Cloud Monitoring → Slack → external SMS bridge) purely to reach a destination (SMS) that Cloud Monitoring can already notify directly — this is unnecessary complexity for the stated goal.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming that because Options A, B, and D all plausibly end in an SMS being delivered, they’re all “valid” answers that differ only in implementation detail — when the real test is whether you know Cloud Monitoring has native SMS support at all. Engineers unfamiliar with that native capability are prone to reach for an external integration pattern (webhook, Slack bridge, third-party gateway) by habit, when the simplest and most reliable answer is already built into the product.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Before reaching for a webhook, custom integration, or a chained notification path through another platform, check whether the target notification channel is natively supported — Cloud Monitoring supports several channel types out of the box (email, SMS, mobile app push, PagerDuty, Slack, and more), and using the native channel is almost always preferable to replicating that functionality through an indirect integration, since it reduces operational surface area and failure points for alerts that matter.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Teams running critical production services typically configure multiple native notification channels for their highest-severity alerting policies — for example, both a PagerDuty channel for on-call paging and an SMS channel as a backup — specifically because Google’s own guidance notes SMS isn’t a fully reliable channel type on its own and recommends pairing it with another channel type, which is only a realistic practice once you’re using the native, directly configurable channels rather than a chain of external integrations.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Before building a custom path to a notification medium, check whether Cloud Monitoring already supports it natively — SMS, like email and Slack, is a built-in notification channel type, not something you need to bridge to.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a Cloud Monitoring alerting question describes a notification requirement, first ask whether that channel type is natively supported before considering webhooks or third-party integrations — native channels (email, SMS, PagerDuty, Slack, mobile push) are simpler, more reliable, and the expected answer whenever they directly satisfy the stated need.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/support/notification-options\">Google Cloud — Create and manage notification channels: </a>Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of adding and verifying an SMS phone number as a native notification channel, then selecting it when configuring an alerting policy</p>","lessons":["d4l9"]},"e1q4":{"id":"e1q4","exam":1,"n":4,"domain":1,"html":"<p>Your DevOps team is growing, and you have recently adopted Terraform to manage your cloud infrastructure. You need a way to version your Terraform configuration files and share them with the rest of the team. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Store the Terraform configuration files in a version-control system, and establish procedures for committing new changes and merging them into the main branch.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Store the Terraform configuration files in a shared network drive with a separate subfolder for each release, and have each team member work on a different set of files.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Store the Terraform configuration files in a Cloud Storage bucket with object versioning enabled, and grant every team member access so they can download the files.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Store the Terraform configuration files in a shared Google Drive folder that syncs automatically to each team member’s machine, and use a file-naming convention to track each new version.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Store the Terraform configuration files in a version-control system, and establish procedures for committing new changes and merging them into the main branch.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard requirement here is two-fold: true code <em>versioning</em> (a history of changes, who made them, and the ability to diff/revert) and controlled <em>collaboration</em> among multiple engineers editing the same codebase. A version-control system (VCS) such as Git is purpose-built for both: it tracks every change as a commit, supports branching, and uses merge workflows (pull/merge requests, code review) to reconcile concurrent edits safely. The other three options each substitute a generic file-storage or file-sync mechanism for an actual VCS, which satisfies “a copy of the files exists somewhere shared” but not “versioned, collaboratively editable code” — the distinction the question is testing.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“implement code versioning” is a hard constraint — it demands real revision history, not just file storage.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“share code with other team members” is a collaboration requirement — it implies concurrent, multi-author editing, not read-only distribution.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“team of Infrastructure DevOps Engineers is growing” signals scale — the solution must hold up as more people edit the same code, favoring conflict-resolution tooling over manual coordination.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A VCS (Git, typically hosted on Cloud Source Repositories, GitHub, or GitLab) stores every change as a discrete, reversible commit tied to an author and timestamp, which is the textbook definition of “code versioning.” Branching and merge workflows let multiple engineers work in parallel on the same Terraform configuration without overwriting each other’s changes, and establishing a push/merge procedure (e.g., feature branches plus pull requests into <code>main</code>) adds the review gate needed to keep shared infrastructure code trustworthy as the team scales. This is also the foundation Google Cloud’s own recommended practices build on: CI/CD pipelines for Terraform (via Cloud Build or similar) are triggered from VCS commits, so starting with a VCS is the prerequisite for everything that follows in a mature IaC workflow.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> A network shared folder with per-release subfolders provides no real diff/merge capability and no commit history; “everyone works on different files” is a manual, error-prone substitute for concurrency control, and it doesn’t scale as the team grows — it directly fails the versioning requirement.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Cloud Storage object versioning does preserve prior versions of an object, so it has a surface-level resemblance to “versioning.” But it has no concept of commits, authorship, diffs, branches, or merges — it’s built for object/blob storage, not collaborative source-code management, so it can’t support the “sharing and working together” half of the requirement.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> Google Drive sync plus a file-naming convention is a document-collaboration pattern, not a code-collaboration one. Simultaneous edits to the same <code>.tf</code> file risk silent overwrites or sync conflicts, and naming conventions are a manual, unenforceable stand-in for real version history.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is conflating <em>object/file versioning</em> (Option C) with <em>source-code versioning</em> (Option A) because both use the word “version.” Cloud Storage versioning would only become the right tool if the question were about retaining historical copies of a single uploaded artifact (e.g., a state file backup), not about a team collaboratively authoring and merging code.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Infrastructure as Code should be treated exactly like application code: it belongs in a VCS with branching, code review, and merge policies, because infrastructure changes carry the same risk of conflicting or regressive edits as application changes do. “Storage with history” (object versioning, file shares) is not a substitute for “collaboration with history” (a VCS), which is the broader principle this question is probing.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Teams that skip a VCS for Terraform typically discover the cost later: untracked manual edits to shared <code>.tf</code> files cause state drift, nobody can tell who changed a security group rule or why, and rolling back a bad change means hand-reconstructing it from memory. Adopting Git from day one with a merge-request workflow turns every infrastructure change into a reviewable, auditable, revertible unit — which is also what lets you later wire Terraform into a CI/CD pipeline (plan on pull request, apply on merge).</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>If the question is about <em>collaboratively versioning code</em> (not just storing file copies), the answer is a version-control system — full stop.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Whenever a DevOps scenario mentions a growing team needing to version and share <em>code</em> (including Terraform/IaC), look for the option describing a VCS with branch/merge procedures — file shares, Drive sync, and object-storage versioning are all distractors dressed up as “versioning” but lack collaborative merge capability.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/terraform/best-practices/general-style-structure\"><strong>Google Cloud — Infrastructure as Code with Terraform (best practices):</strong></a> Supports: why Terraform configurations should live in a VCS with branching/merge workflows as the basis for IaC management</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/source-repositories/docs\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Source Repositories overview:</strong></a> Supports: Google Cloud’s own recommended Git-based version-control service for storing and collaborating on infrastructure code</p>","lessons":["d1l6"]},"e1q5":{"id":"e1q5","exam":1,"n":5,"domain":5,"html":"<p>Your organization wants to lower Compute Engine spend, and after evaluating the options, you decide to use Spot VMs for part of the workload. Which of the following applications is best suited to run on Spot VMs?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. A horizontally scalable in-memory caching system.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. The organization’s public-facing website.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. A distributed, eventually consistent NoSQL database cluster with sufficient quorum.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. A GPU-accelerated video-rendering platform that reads source files from and writes rendered output to a storage bucket.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. A distributed, eventually consistent NoSQL database cluster with sufficient quorum.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard constraint behind every Spot VM decision is fault tolerance: Spot VMs can be reclaimed by Google Cloud at any time with minimal notice, so the workload must be able to absorb the sudden loss of one or more nodes without an interruption in service or loss of data. Each option needs to be evaluated against that bar. A quorum-based, eventually consistent distributed database is explicitly designed for this: it tolerates the loss of individual nodes as long as enough replicas remain to satisfy quorum, and the remaining nodes keep serving reads/writes while the cluster heals. That property — architected redundancy that absorbs node loss gracefully — is exactly what Spot VM eligibility requires, and it’s the one option here that has it built in by design (“sufficient quorum” is stated explicitly).</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“distributed, eventually consistent... with sufficient quorum” is the decisive technical signal — it states outright that the architecture tolerates losing individual nodes without breaking availability or consistency guarantees.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“scalable in-memory caching system” is a preference/ambiguous signal, not a hard constraint — scalability alone says nothing about whether losing a cache node causes a correctness problem or just a performance dip, so it needs more information than is given to be confidently fault-tolerant.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“public-facing website” and “retrieves and stores videos in a storage bucket” are both exclusion signals once examined: a public-facing site typically needs continuous availability (ruling out unannounced interruption), and a GPU render job mid-frame has in-progress compute state that external bucket storage doesn’t protect unless the job is explicitly checkpointed — which isn’t stated.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Spot VMs are intended for workloads that are interruption-tolerant: batch processing, fault-tolerant distributed systems, and horizontally scalable services where losing an individual instance doesn’t jeopardize the overall system’s correctness or availability. A NoSQL cluster built with quorum-based replication is the textbook example — losing a node (because it was preempted) simply means the cluster operates with one fewer replica until a new node rejoins, while quorum ensures reads and writes remain consistent and available throughout. This makes it uniquely well-matched to Spot VMs’ core trade-off: significant cost savings in exchange for the possibility of sudden, uncoordinated instance termination.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A caching system being “scalable” doesn’t by itself guarantee it tolerates node loss safely — many caching layers treat a lost node as cache data loss requiring expensive cache-miss recomputation or even serving stale/absent data to the application, and the question gives no indication the cache has quorum, replication, or warm-standby behavior equivalent to the NoSQL cluster’s explicit quorum guarantee. Without that stated property, it’s a weaker fit than C.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> A production-facing website generally has an availability expectation that directly conflicts with Spot VMs’ lack of an SLA and the possibility of simultaneous, uncoordinated preemption across instances — user-facing traffic shouldn’t be exposed to that risk as the primary serving layer.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Reading inputs from and writing outputs to a storage bucket protects data-at-rest, but it does nothing for an in-progress GPU render job: if the instance is preempted mid-render, that render’s progress is lost and must restart from the beginning (GPUs attached to preemptible/Spot instances only persist for the life of the instance), making this workload interruption-costly unless the question specified checkpoint/resume logic, which it doesn’t.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming “stores data in durable storage” is sufficient for Spot VM eligibility (Option D) — durability of inputs/outputs is not the same as fault tolerance of in-flight compute work. The distinguishing signal the exam wants you to catch is whether the <em>in-progress state of the workload itself</em> survives a node being pulled out from under it, which only the quorum-based database explicitly guarantees here.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Spot VM suitability hinges on one question: if this instance disappears right now, does the system keep working correctly? Workloads with built-in redundancy and graceful degradation (quorum-based clusters, horizontally scalable stateless services, restartable batch jobs) say yes; workloads with irreplaceable in-flight state or hard availability requirements (user-facing production traffic, long-running unsaved compute) say no.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Teams commonly run distributed data stores’ replica nodes on Spot VMs specifically because the replication/quorum layer already exists to handle planned maintenance and hardware failures — Spot preemption is just another instance of the same failure mode the system was built to tolerate — turning an existing resilience investment directly into cost savings, often in the 60-90% range versus standard VM pricing.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>If a workload’s architecture already assumes nodes can vanish without breaking it (quorum, replication, statelessness with external coordination), it’s a Spot VM candidate; if losing a node loses real work or breaks an SLA, it isn’t.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When matching workloads to Spot VMs, look past “has durable storage” or “is scalable” and ask specifically whether the stated architecture tolerates losing an individual node mid-operation without data loss or availability impact — a quorum-based distributed system states that tolerance explicitly and is the strongest match.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/spot\"><strong>Google Cloud — Spot VMs overview:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (Spot VMs can be reclaimed at any time, making fault-tolerant/interruption-tolerant workloads the right fit) and the terminology update from the legacy Preemptible VM model</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/preemptible\"><strong>Google Cloud — Preemptible VM instances (legacy) and GPU preemption behavior:</strong></a> Supports: the distractor elimination for Option D — GPUs on preemptible/Spot instances persist only for the life of the instance, so in-progress render work is lost on preemption</p>","lessons":["d5l1"]},"e1q6":{"id":"e1q6","exam":1,"n":6,"domain":3,"html":"<p>Your organization follows Site Reliability Engineering practices. You are acting as Incident Commander for a new, customer-impacting incident. To run an effective incident response, you need to immediately delegate two other incident management roles. Which two roles should you assign? (Choose two.)</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Operations Lead</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Engineering Lead</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Communications Lead</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Customer Impact Assessor</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>E. External Customer Communications Lead</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Operations Lead</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Communications Lead</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Google’s Incident Management at Google (IMAG) framework, which formalizes SRE incident response, defines exactly three core roles for a well-run incident: Incident Commander (IC), Operations Lead (OL), and Communications Lead (CL). The scenario already assigns you the IC role, so the question is really asking which of the framework’s other canonical roles exist to delegate — and only Operations Lead and Communications Lead are part of that defined structure. Everything else in the option list is a plausible-sounding but non-canonical title: delegating to roles the framework doesn’t define would leave you without the specific, well-understood division of labor the IMAG structure is designed to provide during the time-pressured opening minutes of an incident.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“immediately assign two incident management roles” is a hard constraint signaling a two-answer MSQ drawn from a known, named set of roles rather than an open-ended staffing decision.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Site Reliability Engineering practices” is a technical signal that points specifically to Google’s IMAG role taxonomy as the authoritative framework to apply, not a generic or company-specific incident-response structure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“to assist you” (the IC) in “effective incident response” implies the two roles must report to and relieve the IC of defined categories of work — technical mitigation and stakeholder communication — which is precisely the IC/OL/CL hierarchy’s design.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>In IMAG, the IC retains overall authority and, by default, personally holds any role not yet delegated — so the first priority in a growing incident is to hand off the two other defined roles so the IC can focus on coordination and decision-making rather than execution. The Operations Lead takes ownership of the hands-on technical response: diagnosing the problem, applying mitigations, and directing the team actually modifying the system. The Communications Lead takes ownership of keeping stakeholders — internal teams, leadership, and (when needed) customers — informed with accurate, timely updates, which both frees the IC from fielding status questions and ensures messaging stays consistent. Together, these two delegated roles cover the two major categories of work an incident generates (fixing it, and communicating about it), which is exactly why IMAG defines no more and no fewer than these three roles for a standard incident.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Incorrect:</strong> “Engineering Lead” is not a defined role in Google’s IMAG framework. It’s the kind of title that sounds plausible by analogy to common organizational roles, but it doesn’t correspond to a specific, recognized incident-management responsibility within this framework — the technical mitigation function it might suggest is already owned by the Operations Lead.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Incorrect:</strong> “Customer Impact Assessor” is not one of the IMAG roles. Assessing customer impact is something that naturally falls under the Communications Lead’s remit (to inform accurate messaging) or the Operations Lead’s technical assessment, rather than being split out as its own formally defined role.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option E — Incorrect:</strong> “External Customer Communications Lead” is a plausible-sounding but non-canonical variant of the Communications Lead role. IMAG’s Communications Lead already covers both internal and external communications as a single unified role; splitting external customer communications into a separate title isn’t part of the standard framework.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is being pulled toward option titles that sound organizationally reasonable — “Engineering Lead,” “Customer Impact Assessor” — because they resemble roles you might see in a generic corporate incident process. The exam is specifically testing recall of IMAG’s precise, minimal three-role structure (IC, OL, CL); any title outside that exact set should be treated as a distractor, however sensible it sounds in isolation.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>IMAG deliberately keeps its core role set small — Incident Commander, Operations Lead, Communications Lead — because during an active incident, clarity about who owns what (and who to report to) matters more than granular role specialization. The IC can further delegate sub-tasks or pull in subject matter experts as needed, but those are informal extensions of the OL’s technical work, not separately named roles in the framework itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>In practice, a growing on-call team typically keeps IC, OL, and CL as separate rotations so that a single person isn’t trying to simultaneously fix the problem, coordinate the team, and answer stakeholder questions during a high-pressure incident — splitting these concerns early (exactly as this question describes doing “immediately”) is what lets each function operate without one bottlenecking the others, directly improving time-to-mitigation.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>IMAG has exactly three roles: IC, Ops Lead, Comms Lead — if a title isn’t one of those three, it isn’t a canonical incident management role.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Whenever a question asks which roles an Incident Commander should delegate under SRE/IMAG practices, the answer set is always drawn from Operations Lead and Communications Lead — any other-sounding title (Engineering Lead, Impact Assessor, etc.) is a distractor built to sound plausible without being part of the defined framework.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/static/pdf/IncidentManagementGuide.pdf\"><strong>Google SRE — Incident Management Guide:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism — IMAG’s three core roles (Incident Commander, Operations Lead, Communications Lead) and their reporting relationship to the IC</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/incident-response/\">Google SRE Workbook — Chapter 9, Incident Response: </a>Supports: the detailed responsibilities of the Operations Lead and Communications Lead roles within an active incident</p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e1q7":{"id":"e1q7","exam":1,"n":7,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your organization has moved to a container-based development model. Your team builds many applications that flow continuously through an automated build pipeline into a Kubernetes cluster running in production. The security auditor is worried that a developer or operator could bypass automated testing and push code changes straight to production without the required approval. What should you do to enforce that approval step?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Configure the build system to use protected branches that require pull request approval before merging.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use an admission controller to verify that incoming requests to the cluster originate from approved sources.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use Kubernetes Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) to restrict cluster access to only approved users.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Enable Binary Authorization on the Kubernetes cluster, and configure the build pipeline as an attestor.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Enable Binary Authorization on the Kubernetes cluster, and configure the build pipeline as an attestor.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The must-have constraint is specifically about the deploy-time gate: the auditor’s concern is that someone could circumvent automated testing and push an image to the production cluster without the testing/approval pipeline having actually run on it. That means the control needs to be enforced at the point where workloads are admitted to run in the cluster, and it needs to verify that a specific trusted process (the pipeline) actually vetted the image — not just that a person was authorized to act, or that a process was followed earlier upstream. Binary Authorization is purpose-built for exactly this: it blocks deployment of any container image to the cluster unless it carries a cryptographically verifiable attestation from a designated, trusted attestor (here, the build pipeline), closing the gap between “testing happened” and “the tested artifact is what’s actually running.”</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“circumvent automated testing and push code changes to production without approval” is a hard constraint — it specifically calls out bypassing the pipeline’s own gate, not just unauthorized human access.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“enforce approvals” at deploy time, into “a Kubernetes cluster in the production environment,” is a technical signal pointing at an admission-time control over the cluster itself, not a source-control or identity-access control upstream of it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“configured as an attestor” (in the correct option) is the decisive mechanism: it ties the approval cryptographically to the pipeline’s own attestation, which is what actually prevents circumvention rather than merely restricting who can act.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Binary Authorization enforces deploy-time policy on a Kubernetes cluster by requiring every container image to carry one or more valid attestations, signed by designated attestors, before the cluster’s admission controller will allow it to run. Configuring the automated build pipeline itself as the attestor means an image can only receive the required signature if it has actually passed through that pipeline’s testing and build steps — so even someone with legitimate <code>kubectl</code> access can’t deploy an image that skipped the pipeline, because the cluster will reject any unattested image outright. This directly closes the exact gap the auditor raised: it’s not about restricting who can act, but about guaranteeing what gets admitted to run.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong> Protected branches with required pull request approval is a legitimate control, but it operates on source code merges in the version-control system, not on what gets deployed to the cluster. It does nothing to stop someone from building and pushing a container image directly to the cluster outside the normal pipeline, so it doesn’t close the specific circumvention risk described.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A generic admission controller that checks the <em>source</em> of incoming requests (e.g., which service account or IP issued the deployment call) addresses <em>who</em> is making the request, but it doesn’t verify that the image itself was actually built and tested by the approved pipeline. A user with a legitimately “approved” source could still push an untested image; this doesn’t enforce the testing/approval gate itself.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> Kubernetes RBAC controls which authenticated users or service accounts can perform which actions against the cluster API (an identity/authorization concern). It’s valuable for least-privilege access but says nothing about whether a given container image passed through automated testing — an authorized user under RBAC could still deploy an image that skipped the pipeline entirely.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating this as an access-control problem (who is allowed to deploy) rather than a supply-chain-integrity problem (what is allowed to run). RBAC and source-restricted admission controllers both sound like reasonable “enforce approvals” answers because they gate <em>actors</em> , but the auditor’s concern is about <em>artifacts</em> bypassing testing — which only an attestation-based control like Binary Authorization actually verifies.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Software supply chain security distinguishes between controlling who can take an action (identity and access management, e.g., RBAC) and verifying that an artifact meets a required provenance or quality bar before it’s trusted to run (attestation-based admission control, e.g., Binary Authorization). When a scenario specifically worries about bypassing automated testing, the answer sits in the second category, because the first category can’t detect or prevent a legitimately authorized actor from deploying an unvetted artifact.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>In practice, teams configure their CI/CD pipeline (e.g., Cloud Build) to run its test and security-scan stages and, only on success, sign the resulting image with an attestor key tied to Binary Authorization policy; the GKE cluster’s policy is then set to reject any image lacking that attestation. The operational payoff is that even a well-intentioned “hotfix” pushed manually during an incident gets blocked unless it goes through the pipeline — trading a small amount of deployment friction for a hard guarantee that production only ever runs tested, approved artifacts.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Stop untested images from running” → Binary Authorization with the pipeline as attestor; “control who can act” → RBAC; they solve different problems.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question frames the risk as bypassing automated testing/approval before something reaches production in Kubernetes, look for an attestation-based admission control (Binary Authorization) rather than an identity/access control (RBAC) or a source-control gate (branch protection) — those control different stages and different risks.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Binary Authorization overview:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of blocking unattested images from deployment and configuring a build pipeline as a trusted attestor</p>","lessons":["d2l9"]},"e1q8":{"id":"e1q8","exam":1,"n":8,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your team is designing a new application that will be deployed both inside and outside Google Cloud. You need to collect detailed metrics, such as system resource utilization, and you want to rely on a centralized Google Cloud service while keeping the setup work required to a minimum. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Import the Cloud Profiler client library into the application, and configure it to report CPU and memory profiling data to Cloud Profiler for analysis.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Import the Cloud Debugger client library into the application, and configure it to emit debug log messages that include timing information.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Instrument the application code with a custom timing library, and publish the resulting metrics through a health-check endpoint that Cloud Monitoring scrapes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Deploy a third-party Application Performance Monitoring (APM) tool at both locations, and configure it to export the collected data to a central data store for analysis.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Import the Cloud Profiler client library into the application, and configure it to report CPU and memory profiling data to Cloud Profiler for analysis.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The must-have constraints are threefold: the solution must work for a hybrid deployment (both inside and outside Google Cloud), it must funnel data into a <em>centralized Google Cloud service</em> , and it must require <em>minimal setup work</em>. Testing each option against all three in sequence narrows the field fast. A custom timing library with a scraped health-check endpoint (C) and a third-party APM tool with a hand-built export pipeline (D) both satisfy the hybrid and centralization requirements only at the cost of significant custom engineering — which directly violates the “minimize work” constraint. A Debugger-based approach (B) fails on a more fundamental level: it targets the wrong kind of telemetry (application state inspection, not resource metrics) and, as of this writing, the underlying service no longer exists to send data to. Cloud Profiler is the only option that is a lightweight library import, works identically whether the host is a GCP VM or an external server, and reports directly into a centralized Google Cloud service — satisfying all three constraints simultaneously.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“both inside and outside Google Cloud Platform” is a hard constraint — it rules out any approach that depends on being hosted on GCP infrastructure to work.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“centralized GCP services while minimizing the amount of work” is a dual hard constraint — it eliminates any option requiring custom instrumentation, a hand-rolled scrape/export pipeline, or installing and maintaining separate tooling.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“detailed metrics such as system resource utilization” is a technical signal pointing toward resource-consumption data (CPU, memory) rather than application logic/state data, which is what distinguishes a profiling tool from a debugging tool.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Profiler is a statistical, low-overhead continuous profiler: you add its client library to the application, and it samples CPU time, heap (memory) allocation, and other resource-consumption data directly from the running process, then reports that data to a centralized Cloud Profiler backend in Google Cloud. Because the profiling agent lives inside the application process itself rather than depending on GCP-specific host infrastructure, it works the same way whether the application is deployed on Compute Engine, GKE, or a server outside Google Cloud entirely — satisfying the hybrid-deployment requirement. Setup is limited to importing the library and enabling it (typically a few lines of initialization code), which is about as little work as instrumentation gets, and the reporting destination is natively a centralized Google Cloud service, with no custom export pipeline to build or maintain.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Outdated:</strong> Cloud Debugger (formerly Stackdriver Debugger) was deprecated in May 2022 and the service was fully shut down in May 2023; it no longer exists to import or configure. Even setting aside its retirement, Debugger was never designed for metrics collection — it’s a snapshot-based tool for inspecting application state at a code location without stopping execution, not a system for aggregating resource-utilization data. “Emit debug messages with timing information” describes manual log instrumentation, which is also a distinct, heavier-effort pattern than what the question is asking for.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> This is a legitimate pattern in principle (expose a metrics endpoint for scraping), but it requires writing and maintaining custom timing instrumentation, building and exposing an endpoint, and configuring a scrape/collection pipeline into Cloud Monitoring — none of which is “minimal work” compared to simply importing a managed profiling library.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> Deploying and maintaining a separate third-party APM tool at every location, plus building a custom export process into centralized storage, is the most labor-intensive of the four options. It’s a reasonable architecture if you specifically need APM-style distributed tracing or vendor-specific features unavailable in GCP, but it directly conflicts with the stated goal of minimizing setup work while using centralized GCP services natively.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is pattern-matching “needs timing/debug-style instrumentation” to the Debugger product purely because of its name, without checking (a) whether Debugger actually collects system resource metrics (it doesn’t — it inspects application state) and (b) whether the service is still current (it isn’t — it was retired in 2023). Exam questions referencing legacy Stackdriver sub-products should always be checked against current product status before being treated as a viable answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud’s observability tooling splits cleanly by data type: Cloud Monitoring handles metrics, Cloud Logging handles logs, Cloud Trace handles distributed request latency, and Cloud Profiler handles continuous code-level CPU/memory profiling — all under the Google Cloud Observability umbrella (formerly Stackdriver). When a question asks for “resource utilization” metrics with minimal setup across hybrid environments, the profiling or monitoring-agent tools (which run as lightweight in-process or host-level agents reporting to a managed backend) are almost always a better fit than custom scrape pipelines or third-party tools requiring manual central aggregation.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Teams running hybrid workloads commonly pair Cloud Profiler (for in-process CPU/memory profiling) with the Ops Agent (for host-level OS metrics like disk and network) on both GCP and non-GCP hosts, giving them a single centralized view in Google Cloud Observability without building or operating any custom collection infrastructure — freeing engineering time for the application itself rather than observability plumbing.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Need resource-utilization metrics, minimal setup, hybrid deployment, centralized in GCP → Cloud Profiler (or the Ops/Monitoring agent), not a deprecated Debugger, custom scrape pipeline, or third-party APM tool.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks for low-effort, centralized collection of resource-utilization metrics across GCP and non-GCP hosts, favor the managed, library-based Google Cloud Observability tool (Profiler or the Monitoring/Ops agent) over any option requiring custom instrumentation, a hand-built export pipeline, or a separate third-party tool — and always sanity-check any legacy-sounding Stackdriver sub-product name against current service status.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/about-profiler\">Google Cloud — Cloud Profiler overview: </a>Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (lightweight client library reporting CPU/memory profiling data to a centralized Google Cloud service, usable from any deployment location)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/deprecations/debugger-deprecation\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Debugger deprecation notice:</strong></a> Supports: the elimination of Option B as both an outdated and now-nonexistent service</p>","lessons":["d4l10","d4l12"]},"e1q9":{"id":"e1q9","exam":1,"n":9,"domain":3,"html":"<p>Your team has recently deployed an NGINX-based application to Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) and exposed it to the public through an HTTP(S) Google Cloud Load Balancer (GCLB) Ingress. You want to scale the application’s frontend deployment using an appropriate Service Level Indicator (SLI). What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Configure the horizontal pod autoscaler (HPA) to scale based on the average response time reported by the Liveness and Readiness probes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Configure the vertical pod autoscaler (VPA) in GKE, and enable the cluster autoscaler so the cluster grows as pod resource requests expand.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use GKE’s native custom metrics support for the horizontal pod autoscaler, and configure it to scale based on the request count metric that the GCLB publishes to Cloud Monitoring.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Expose the NGINX stats endpoint, and configure the horizontal pod autoscaler to scale based on the request metrics it reports.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Use GKE’s native custom metrics support for the horizontal pod autoscaler, and configure it to scale based on the request count metric that the GCLB publishes to Cloud Monitoring.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The decisive constraint is “appropriate SLI” for a user-facing frontend sitting behind a load balancer. SRE practice treats request-driven services as best measured by signals tied directly to actual user traffic — request volume, latency, error rate — rather than proxy signals internal to the pod (CPU/memory via VPA) or health-check mechanics never designed to represent load (Liveness/Readiness probe timing). The GCLB’s request-count metric is the authoritative, end-to-end signal of real traffic hitting the frontend, published automatically to Cloud Monitoring with no extra instrumentation. GKE’s native custom metrics support lets the HPA consume that metric directly, without standing up and maintaining a separate adapter — making it the option that is simultaneously the most accurate SLI and the least operationally burdensome to implement.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“appropriate Service Level Indicator (SLI)” is a hard constraint — it requires the chosen signal to genuinely represent user-facing load/experience, immediately disqualifying any metric that only indirectly correlates with it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“HTTP Google Cloud Load Balancer (GCLB) ingress” is a technical signal — it establishes that a load-balancer-level request metric already exists and is the natural source of truth for traffic volume to the frontend.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“scale the deployment of the application’s frontend” is a scope signal pointing toward horizontal scaling (more replicas serving more concurrent requests) rather than vertical scaling (bigger pods), since request-driven frontends typically scale by replica count, not per-pod resources.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A horizontal pod autoscaler driven by the GCLB’s request-count metric scales replica count in direct proportion to real incoming traffic — exactly the SLI that matters for a public-facing frontend’s user experience. GKE’s native custom metrics support lets the HPA read this metric straight from Cloud Monitoring, eliminating the previously required middleman (the custom-metrics-stackdriver-adapter) along with its service accounts, Workload Identity bindings, and ongoing maintenance burden; Google has stated this also reduces metric-read latency so the HPA reacts to real traffic bursts faster. The result is a scaling signal that is both technically accurate (tied to actual request volume) and operationally simple to set up and keep running.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Liveness and Readiness probe response times measure whether a pod is alive and ready to serve traffic, not how much traffic it’s currently handling. A pod can report fast, healthy probe responses while still being overwhelmed by concurrent user requests, so this signal doesn’t represent the SLI the scenario actually cares about.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> VPA with cluster autoscaler addresses a different problem — right-sizing each pod’s CPU/memory requests and growing cluster capacity to accommodate that — not scaling the number of frontend replicas in response to request volume. It would be the better choice if the issue were individual pods being under-provisioned for their per-request resource needs, not a need to handle more concurrent requests.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Exposing NGINX’s own stats endpoint and scaling on self-reported request metrics is workable, but it duplicates a signal the GCLB already provides upstream, adds instrumentation and metric-exporting work to maintain, and reflects only what reaches NGINX rather than the full picture the load balancer sees (including, for example, requests the load balancer handles before reaching any pod). It’s a reasonable fallback only when no load-balancer-level metric is available — which isn’t the case here.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “Liveness/Readiness probe timing” as a legitimate load signal because it sounds “metric-like” and is already built into the deployment — but probe latency measures pod health, not request volume, and conflating the two is a common misunderstanding of what an SLI for a request-driven service should actually represent. A second trap is assuming the Stackdriver/Prometheus adapter is still a required step for custom-metric autoscaling in GKE; that operational overhead has since been removed by native custom metrics support, which is the current correct mechanism to cite.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>For request-driven services, the right SLI is almost always something derived from the request path itself — request rate, latency, or error rate — captured as close to the real user-facing boundary as possible (here, the load balancer). Scaling decisions should be driven by that SLI directly rather than by proxy signals (resource utilization, health-check timing) that can diverge from actual user-facing load.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Teams running public HTTP frontends on GKE commonly scale on load-balancer request-rate metrics specifically because it lets capacity track real traffic patterns (diurnal cycles, marketing-driven spikes) more faithfully than CPU-based scaling alone, which can lag behind or misrepresent load for I/O-bound or request-queuing workloads; native custom metrics support further reduces the operational risk of that setup by removing a previously-required adapter component from the critical scaling path.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For a request-driven frontend, scale on a real request-volume signal from the load balancer — not on probe timing, not on pod resource usage, and (where a native path exists) not through a self-managed metrics adapter either.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When asked to pick an “appropriate SLI” to drive autoscaling for a user-facing, request-driven service, favor a traffic-based metric sourced as close to the user-facing boundary as possible (load-balancer request count) over resource-utilization or health-check-derived signals — and prefer GKE’s native metrics path over a manually maintained adapter wherever the native option covers the required metric source.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/gke-now-supports-custom-metrics-natively\"><strong>Google Cloud — GKE native support for custom metrics (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler):</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of scaling HPA on Cloud Monitoring/load-balancer metrics without deploying a separate custom-metrics adapter</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/horizontal-pod-autoscaling\"><strong>Google Cloud — Configuring horizontal Pod autoscaling in GKE:</strong></a> Supports: autoscaling based on load balancer traffic and external/custom metrics as the appropriate signal for request-driven frontend workloads</p>","lessons":["d3l7"]},"e1q10":{"id":"e1q10","exam":1,"n":10,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your application services run on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). You want to guarantee that only images from your centrally managed Artifact Registry repository in the <code>altostrat-images</code> project can be deployed to the cluster, while keeping the development effort required to achieve this to a minimum. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create a custom builder for Cloud Build that only pushes images to the <code>altostrat-images</code> Artifact Registry repository.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Configure a Binary Authorization policy that includes an allowlist name pattern matching the <code>altostrat-images</code> Artifact Registry repository.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Add logic to the deployment pipeline that checks that every manifest references only images from the <code>altostrat-images</code> Artifact Registry repository.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Tag every image in the <code>altostrat-images</code> Artifact Registry repository, and verify that tag is present whenever an image is deployed.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Configure a Binary Authorization policy that includes an allowlist name pattern matching the</strong><code>**altostrat-images**</code><strong>Artifact Registry repository.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard requirement is enforcement at the point of deployment — “only images from X can be deployed” is a guarantee about what the cluster will actually admit, not about what a build pipeline happens to produce or what a separate script happens to check. That distinction eliminates any approach that only controls the image-push side (where images get created) or that lives outside the cluster’s own admission path (a pipeline-side check that can be bypassed by anyone with direct <code>kubectl</code> access). The second constraint — minimizing development effort — favors a declarative policy configuration over building and maintaining custom tooling, scripts, or tagging conventions. Binary Authorization is the GKE-native admission control mechanism built exactly for this: a short, declarative policy restricts which image name patterns the cluster will admit, with no custom code to write or maintain.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“only images from your centrally-managed... registry... can be deployed” is a hard constraint — it specifies admission-time enforcement on the cluster, not a build-time convention or a pipeline-side check that a bypass could evade.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“to the cluster” is a technical signal — it points toward a Kubernetes/GKE admission control mechanism rather than a CI/CD pipeline step that happens before the cluster is ever involved.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“while minimizing development time” is a preference that rules out options requiring custom code, scripts, or manual conventions in favor of a declarative, policy-based control.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Binary Authorization enforces its policy at image admission into the GKE cluster: a policy with a default rule set to deny deployment, combined with an allowlist name pattern scoped to the <code>altostrat-images</code> repository, means the cluster will only admit images whose registry path matches that pattern, and will block anything else regardless of how someone attempted to deploy it (kubectl, a different pipeline, a compromised CI job). This is implemented as a short YAML policy rather than custom code, satisfying the “minimize development time” goal, and it closes the loop that build-side or pipeline-side controls leave open: it doesn’t matter where an image was built or pushed from — only images matching the approved registry pattern can ever run on the cluster.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong> A custom Cloud Build builder restricted to pushing only to the <code>altostrat-images</code> repository controls one path by which images get created, but it does nothing to stop a different image — built elsewhere, pulled from a public registry, or pushed by another pipeline — from being deployed directly to the cluster. It secures the build side, not the deploy side, which is what the requirement actually asks for.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Adding manual validation logic to the deployment pipeline that inspects manifests is a workable idea in principle, but it requires writing and maintaining custom checking code, and it only guards deployments that go through that specific pipeline — anyone with direct cluster access can bypass it entirely. It also conflicts with the “minimize development time” goal compared to a declarative policy.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Tagging approved images and checking for that tag at deploy time is a weak, convention-based control: tags are mutable and can be applied to any image by anyone with push access, so this doesn’t actually guarantee the image originates from the approved registry. It also requires building and maintaining custom tagging and verification logic rather than relying on built-in enforcement.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating Option A (controlling the build pipeline’s push destination) as equivalent to controlling what the cluster will run — but restricting where a trusted pipeline publishes images says nothing about what else might be deployed from outside that pipeline. The scenario’s “only images from X can be deployed” is a cluster-admission guarantee, which only an admission-control mechanism like Binary Authorization can actually provide.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Restricting <em>where images can be built or pushed to</em> and restricting <em>what a cluster will admit to run</em> are two different controls that address different threat models — the first narrows your trusted build surface, the second guarantees enforcement regardless of how an image arrived. When a requirement is phrased as “only X can be deployed,” it’s asking for the second: an admission-control policy evaluated at deploy time, not a convention enforced earlier in the pipeline.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Teams commonly layer both controls in production — a locked-down CI/CD pipeline that only pushes trusted images to one or two approved repositories, and a Binary Authorization policy on the cluster restricting admission to exactly those repository paths — so that even a misconfigured or compromised alternate deployment path can’t introduce an unapproved image, because the cluster itself refuses to run anything outside the allowlisted registry pattern.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Only X can be deployed to the cluster” means enforce it at the cluster’s admission boundary (Binary Authorization), not at the build pipeline or with a convention like tagging.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a GKE question asks you to guarantee that only images from a specific registry can be deployed — especially with minimal custom engineering — reach for a Binary Authorization policy with a name-pattern restriction rather than a build-pipeline control or a custom pipeline/tagging check, since only admission control actually governs what the cluster will run.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs/policy-yaml-reference\"><strong>Google Cloud — Binary Authorization Policy YAML reference:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of restricting admitted images to a registry path pattern via a declarative policy, and the current “allowlist” terminology for this feature</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/container-registry/docs/deprecations/container-registry-deprecation\"><strong>Google Cloud — Container Registry deprecation and transition to Artifact Registry:</strong></a> Supports: the terminology update reflecting that Container Registry (GCR) is fully shut down and Artifact Registry is the current, official image registry product</p>","lessons":["d2l5","d2l9"]},"e1q11":{"id":"e1q11","exam":1,"n":11,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You operate a real-time multiplayer gaming workload on Compute Engine, with separate production and testing environments, each in its own VPC network. Within each VPC, the frontend and backend servers sit on distinct subnets. You suspect a malicious process is periodically communicating from your production frontend instances, and you need to capture network traffic so you can investigate. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Enable VPC Flow Logs on the frontend and backend subnets of the production VPC network only, with a sampling rate of 0.5.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Enable VPC Flow Logs on the frontend and backend subnets of the production VPC network only, with a sampling rate of 1.0.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Enable VPC Flow Logs on the frontend and backend subnets of both the testing and production VPC networks, with a sampling rate of 0.5. Roll the change out to testing before production.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Enable VPC Flow Logs on the frontend and backend subnets of both the testing and production VPC networks, with a sampling rate of 1.0. Roll the change out to testing before production.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Enable VPC Flow Logs on the frontend and backend subnets of the production VPC network only, with a sampling rate of 1.0.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two decisions drive this question: <em>where</em> to enable Flow Logs and <em>at what sampling rate</em>. Scope is a must-have constraint — the suspected malicious activity is specifically in production, so that’s the environment that needs instrumenting; testing is unaffected and enabling logging there adds cost and noise without investigative value. Both the frontend and backend subnets need coverage because a compromised frontend instance could be communicating laterally to the backend tier, or the backend could be the actual source/destination of interest — restricting to frontend alone would blind you to that path. Rate is the second decision: this is a forensic investigation of an intermittent, low-frequency malicious process, so you need every relevant flow captured, not a statistical sample that could simply miss the event. That requires a sampling rate of 1.0 (100%), not a reduced rate chosen for cost/volume optimization.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“suspect there is a malicious process communicating intermittently” — a security-investigation/forensic scenario, not routine capacity monitoring; intermittent/low-frequency activity is a hard constraint favoring full sampling, since a partial sample could simply miss the event.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“production frontend servers” — identifies where the suspicious activity is observed, but doesn’t by itself prove the backend is irrelevant; the question’s broader setup (two tiers in one VPC) is a technical signal that traffic could cross tiers.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“ensure that network traffic is captured for analysis” — “ensure” is a completeness directive, ruling out any sampled (less-than-100%) approach as insufficiently reliable for analysis.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>VPC Flow Logs record metadata about IP traffic sent from and received by VM instances, and the sampling rate parameter controls what fraction of eligible flows are actually logged — a setting of 1.0 logs 100% of flows, which is what a security investigation needs to avoid missing the very traffic you’re hunting for. Scoping the logs to the production VPC’s frontend and backend subnets targets exactly the environment and both tiers where the suspicious communication could be occurring, without burdening the unrelated testing environment with unnecessary logging overhead and cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Correct scope (production frontend and backend only) but a 0.5 sampling rate captures only half of flows on average. For an intermittent, suspected-malicious process, that’s a coin flip on whether the relevant traffic is even recorded — it directly undermines “ensure traffic is captured.”</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> Enabling Flow Logs across both testing and production is scope creep; the incident is confined to production, and testing isn’t implicated in the scenario. The 0.5 sampling rate repeats the same flaw as Option A — incomplete capture for a forensic use case — and “roll out to testing before production” is a standard safe-change-management practice that is irrelevant and even counterproductive here, since it delays instrumenting the one environment that actually needs it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> This does get the sampling rate right (1.0), but it unnecessarily expands scope to the testing VPC, which adds cost and log volume with no investigative benefit, and again inserts a testing-first rollout that delays getting visibility into the actual incident in production. It would only be the better choice if the scenario indicated the testing environment might also be compromised or that the source of the malicious traffic was unclear across environments — neither of which is stated here.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “capture traffic for analysis” as if it just means “turn on Flow Logs” and then picking the default or cost-conscious sampling rate (0.5) out of habit, since 0.5 is a common middle-ground choice in cost-optimization scenarios. For a security/forensic scenario with intermittent activity, the correct instinct flips: you prioritize capture completeness over cost, which is the deciding detail between A/B and the rate-pairing in C/D.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>VPC Flow Logs sampling rate is a cost-vs-completeness lever: lower rates (e.g., 0.5 or lower) reduce log volume and expense for routine monitoring or capacity analysis, where a representative sample is good enough, while a rate of 1.0 is reserved for cases — security investigations, compliance, incident forensics — where missing even one flow could mean missing the thing you’re looking for. The mental model is: sample for trends, capture fully for investigations.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>In practice, a team investigating a suspected compromise would enable full-rate Flow Logs on the implicated VPC’s subnets, route the logs to Cloud Logging (or export to BigQuery/Cloud Storage for deeper analysis), and correlate flow metadata with other signals (firewall logs, IDS alerts) to identify the destination IPs and ports the malicious process is talking to — then use that to scope containment (firewall rule changes, instance isolation) without having wasted logging spend instrumenting the unrelated testing environment.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Security/forensic investigation → sampling rate 1.0, scoped only to the implicated environment and every subnet traffic could cross.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a DevOps Engineer question frames a scenario as “suspected malicious activity, need to capture traffic for analysis,” default to full (1.0) VPC Flow Logs sampling and scope it to exactly the environment/subnets involved — reduced sampling rates and broadened scope are both classic distractors built for cost-optimization scenarios, not security investigations.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/flow-logs\"><strong>Google Cloud — VPC Flow Logs overview:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (sampling rate controls the fraction of flows logged; 1.0 logs all flows) and the rationale for full capture during investigation</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/validating-filtering-network-traffic-flow-logs\"><strong>Google Cloud — Using VPC Flow Logs for network monitoring, forensics, and security:</strong></a> Supports: the use case of enabling Flow Logs specifically for security investigation and traffic analysis, including scoping guidance</p>","lessons":["d4l11"]},"e1q12":{"id":"e1q12","exam":1,"n":12,"domain":3,"html":"<p>Your organization follows Site Reliability Engineering principles. You’re writing the postmortem for an incident, triggered by a software change, that had a severe impact on users. You want to prevent incidents of this severity from recurring. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Identify the engineers responsible for the incident, and escalate the matter to their senior management.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Ensure that test cases capable of catching this class of error run successfully as a required step before future software releases.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Follow up individually with the employees who reviewed the change, and prescribe practices they should personally follow going forward.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Create a policy requiring on-call teams to immediately call engineers and management to discuss a plan of action whenever an incident occurs.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Ensure that test cases capable of catching this class of error run successfully as a required step before future software releases.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard requirement is a <em>systemic</em> fix that prevents the same class of incident from recurring, consistent with SRE’s blameless-postmortem culture, which treats incidents as failures of process and tooling rather than failures of individuals. That constraint immediately filters out any remedy aimed at specific people (assigning blame, individually retraining reviewers) in favor of a remedy embedded in the engineering process itself. Among the remaining candidates, the real distinguishing question is whether the fix addresses <em>prevention</em> (stopping the defect from reaching production) or merely improves <em>response</em> (how quickly the team reacts once an incident is already happening). Since the incident was triggered by a software change, a prevention mechanism gated into the release process — automated tests that specifically catch this error type — directly closes the gap that let the defect ship, which is the strongest and most durable fix available.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“triggered by a software change” is a technical signal — it points the remediation toward the software delivery pipeline (testing, release gating) rather than toward incident communication or escalation processes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“prevent severe incidents from happening in the future” is a hard constraint — it demands a preventive fix, not merely a faster or better-coordinated response after an incident starts.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Site Reliability Engineering principles” and the postmortem context are a technical/cultural signal — SRE postmortems are explicitly blameless, which disqualifies any option centered on identifying or penalizing individuals.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Adding automated test cases that specifically catch this error type, and requiring them to pass before any future release, closes the exact gap that allowed the triggering defect to reach production in the first place — it converts a one-time painful lesson into a permanent, automated guardrail that every future change must clear. This is systemic and durable: it doesn’t depend on any individual remembering a lesson or following a manual practice, and it directly targets prevention (stopping the defect class from shipping) rather than response (how the team reacts once something has already gone wrong), which is precisely what “prevent severe incidents from happening” calls for.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Identifying and escalating individual engineers to senior management is the definition of a blame-oriented response, which directly conflicts with SRE’s blameless-postmortem principle. It also does nothing systemic — it neither fixes the underlying defect class nor prevents a different engineer from introducing a similar one later.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Prescribing personal practices to specific reviewers individually is still people-focused rather than process-focused, and it doesn’t scale or persist: it depends on those particular individuals remembering and consistently applying the guidance, with no systemic enforcement if they don’t, and it implicitly singles out specific people in a way blameless postmortems avoid.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> A policy for immediate incident-response escalation improves how quickly the organization reacts and coordinates once an incident is already underway — a legitimate and valuable practice, but one addressing incident <em>response</em> , not <em>prevention</em>. It would be the better choice if the goal were reducing time-to-mitigation or improving coordination during incidents, not stopping this class of incident from occurring in the first place.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is that Options A and C can look like reasonable “accountability” measures on first read, especially since the incident was caused by a change someone wrote and someone else reviewed — but SRE postmortems are explicitly designed to resist that instinct, because blaming or individually retraining people doesn’t fix the process gap that let the defect through, and it discourages the open, honest incident reporting that effective postmortems depend on.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A blameless postmortem’s value lies in converting an incident into a systemic, durable improvement — typically a change to tooling, process, or automated gating — rather than a judgment about who was at fault. The decisive test for any proposed postmortem action item is whether it would have prevented this specific incident (or its class) regardless of which individuals were involved, which is exactly what an automated, release-gating test case achieves and people-focused remedies do not.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Teams following this practice typically add a regression test derived directly from the incident’s root cause to their CI pipeline, so every subsequent release is automatically checked against the exact failure mode that caused the outage — turning a costly, user-impacting incident into a permanent improvement to release safety, with far more leverage than any one-off conversation or escalation could provide.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>A good postmortem action item fixes the process (an automated test gating future releases), not the person — blameless means systemic, not individual.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When an SRE postmortem question asks how to prevent a recurrence, favor action items that are systemic, automated, and embedded in the delivery pipeline (like release-gating tests) over anything aimed at identifying, escalating, or individually retraining specific people — and distinguish prevention-focused fixes from response/coordination-focused ones, since only prevention stops the incident class from recurring.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/\"><strong>Google SRE Book — Postmortem Culture: Learning from Failure:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of blameless, systemic action items (such as adding regression tests) as the proper output of a postmortem, and the exclusion of individual-blame remedies</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide, Section 3 (SRE practices):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment — conducting blameless postmortems and applying their lessons is explicitly part of applying site reliability engineering practices</p>","lessons":["d3l5"]},"e1q13":{"id":"e1q13","exam":1,"n":13,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You manage an application that writes its logs to Cloud Logging. You need to grant some team members the ability to export logs. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Grant the team members the IAM role Logs Configuration Writer (<code>roles/logging.configWriter</code>).</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Configure Access Context Manager to allow only these members to export logs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create and grant a custom IAM role that includes the <code>logging.sinks.list</code> and <code>logging.sinks.get</code> permissions.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Create an Organization Policy in IAM that allows only these members to create log exports.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Grant the team members the IAM role Logs Configuration Writer (</strong><code>**roles/logging.configWriter**</code><strong>).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard requirement is granting specific individuals the ability to perform one well-defined action: create and manage log exports (sinks) in Cloud Logging. Google Cloud’s IAM model favors predefined roles scoped to exactly this kind of capability over building custom mechanisms, so the first question is whether a predefined role already covers it — and it does. The Logs Configuration Writer role is purpose-built to grant read/write access to sink (export) and log-based metric configurations, which is precisely “the ability to export logs.” That makes every other option either the wrong IAM mechanism (Access Context Manager, Organization Policy) or an under-permissioned attempt to replicate what the predefined role already does more simply.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“give some team members the ability to export logs” is a hard constraint — it specifies a particular IAM capability (creating/managing log sinks) for a defined set of individuals, pointing toward standard IAM role assignment rather than organization-wide policy or network/context-based controls.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“some team members” (not all, not a blanket rule) is a scope signal — it indicates principal-level role binding is the right granularity, not a project- or org-wide policy mechanism.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The permissions named in the custom-role option, <code>logging.sinks.list</code> and <code>logging.sinks.get</code>, are a decisive technical signal: these are read-only permissions (viewing existing sinks), not the <code>logging.sinks.create</code> permission actually required to export logs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>The Logs Configuration Writer predefined role grants the permissions needed to create, update, delete, get, and list log sinks — sinks being the mechanism Cloud Logging uses to export logs to destinations like Cloud Storage, BigQuery, or Pub/Sub. Granting this role to the specific team members directly satisfies “the ability to export logs” using Google’s own purpose-built, maintained role, with no custom role definition or additional infrastructure to build and keep in sync as Cloud Logging’s permission set evolves.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Access Context Manager defines context-aware access perimeters (e.g., restricting access based on IP range, device policy, or identity context) for resources within VPC Service Controls. It isn’t an IAM role-granting mechanism and isn’t designed to control which principals can perform a specific Cloud Logging action like creating a sink — it solves a network/context-boundary problem, not a permissions-granting one.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> <code>logging.sinks.list</code> and <code>logging.sinks.get</code> only allow viewing existing sinks’ names and configurations — they do not include <code>logging.sinks.create</code>, so a principal with only these permissions cannot actually create a log export. Building a custom role here also means manually maintaining a permission set that the predefined role already covers and keeps current.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> Organization Policy constraints govern resource configuration guardrails across an organization (e.g., restricting which services or regions can be used) — they are not an IAM mechanism for granting specific principals permission to perform an action like creating a log sink. This misapplies a governance/constraint tool to what is fundamentally an access-grant problem.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is Option C’s apparent precision — naming specific <code>logging.sinks.*</code> permissions makes it look like the more deliberate, least-privilege choice. But listing and getting sinks only lets you view configuration that already exists; the actual verb needed is <code>create</code>, which neither listed permission provides. Spotting that the named permissions are read-only, not write/create, is the key to eliminating this distractor.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>When a requirement maps cleanly to “grant specific people a specific capability,” the IAM-first instinct should be to check whether a predefined role already covers exactly that capability before reaching for a custom role, and well before considering governance tools like Organization Policy or network/context tools like Access Context Manager — those solve different classes of problems (configuration guardrails and context-aware network access, respectively) than principal-level permission grants.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Teams commonly grant Logs Configuration Writer narrowly — to a platform or observability team responsible for setting up log routing to BigQuery for analysis or to Cloud Storage for long-term retention — while keeping broader Logging Admin or Owner-level access restricted, since the predefined role’s scope (sinks and log-based metrics) is exactly the surface area that export-configuration work touches, without granting unrelated capabilities like managing log buckets or viewing private logs.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Give someone the ability to export logs” maps directly to the Logs Configuration Writer role — not a custom role missing the <code>create</code> permission, and not a network- or org-level policy tool.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When an IAM question asks how to grant a specific capability to specific people, check for a predefined role that matches the capability by name and permission set before considering a custom role, Organization Policy, or Access Context Manager — and when a custom-role option is offered, verify it actually includes the create/write permission the scenario requires, not just read permissions.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/roles-permissions/logging\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Logging roles and permissions:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism — the Logs Configuration Writer role’s permissions for creating and managing log sinks (exports), and the elimination of Option C’s read-only permission set</p>","lessons":["d4l13"]},"e1q14":{"id":"e1q14","exam":1,"n":14,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You support a high-traffic web application running on Google Cloud. You need to measure the application’s reliability from the user’s perspective, without making any engineering changes to the application itself. What should you do? (Choose two.)</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Review the application’s current metrics, and add new ones as needed.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Modify the application code to capture additional information about user interactions.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Analyze only the web proxy logs, and capture the response time of each request.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Create synthetic clients that simulate a user journey through the application.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>E. Use current and historical request logs to trace customer interactions with the application.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Create synthetic clients that simulate a user journey through the application.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>E. Use current and historical request logs to trace customer interactions with the application.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard constraint that filters every option is “without making any engineering changes to it” — any approach that requires touching the application’s code is immediately disqualified, regardless of how good the resulting data would be. Against that filter, synthetic clients and request-log analysis both operate entirely outside the application: synthetic clients are external callers that exercise the live application exactly as a real user would, and request logs are already generated by the existing infrastructure (load balancer, proxy, or web server) without any instrumentation added to the app. Both independently produce a user-perspective view of reliability — synthetic clients proactively, by simulating real journeys on a schedule, and request logs retrospectively, by revealing how actual users experienced the system — which is why they work together as a complete answer rather than overlapping redundantly.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“without making any engineering changes to it” is a hard constraint — it eliminates any option that requires modifying, instrumenting, or redeploying the application’s own code.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“from a user perspective” is a technical signal — it requires the measurement to reflect the actual experience of someone using the application (end-to-end journeys, real request outcomes), not internal system metrics alone.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Choose two” with “only” appearing in one distractor (Option C) is a cardinality/scope signal — it flags that option as artificially narrowed in a way that should be checked against the other candidates before being accepted.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Synthetic clients simulate real user journeys — logging in, navigating, completing a transaction — against the live, unmodified application, producing proactive, repeatable measurements of whether the application behaves correctly and performantly from an end-user’s point of view, entirely from outside the app. Historical and current request logs, which the infrastructure already generates as requests flow through it, let you trace what actually happened to real customer requests over time — successes, failures, latencies — giving a retrospective, ground-truth view of user-experienced reliability without adding any logging or instrumentation to the application code. Together, these two approaches cover both proactive (synthetic) and observed (historical) measurement of user-facing reliability, satisfying the requirement comprehensively while respecting the no-engineering-changes constraint.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> “Add new [metrics] as needed” implies instrumenting the application to emit additional metrics it doesn’t currently produce, which is itself an engineering change to the app — directly conflicting with the stated constraint, however useful it might be if that constraint weren’t present.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> This explicitly states modifying the application code, which is the most direct possible violation of “without making any engineering changes to it.”</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Analyzing web proxy logs for response time doesn’t require any code changes, so it clears the hard constraint, but the word “only” artificially narrows the approach to a single data source and a single dimension (response time), omitting other reliability signals like error rates or full request outcomes that the available request logs already capture more completely — making it a weaker, incomplete choice next to Option E.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is Option A, which sounds like sound SRE practice (“review your metrics, add what’s missing”) but quietly smuggles in an engineering change via “add new ones as needed” — a reminder to check every clause in a distractor against the stated constraints, not just its opening phrase. A second trap is treating Option C as acceptable because it avoids code changes, while missing that its own wording (“only,” single metric) makes it a narrower, inferior version of the broader request-log analysis in Option E.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Measuring user-perceived reliability without modifying the system under test is the defining feature of black-box observability techniques: synthetic monitoring (proactively exercising the system from outside) and analysis of existing infrastructure-level logs (retrospectively observing real traffic) are the two canonical black-box approaches, as opposed to white-box techniques (custom application metrics, code-level instrumentation) that require the engineering changes this scenario rules out.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>SRE teams commonly pair these two techniques in practice: synthetic checks catch availability or journey-breaking regressions proactively, often before real users are affected, while mining request logs for success/failure rates and latency distributions establishes the actual SLI used to track an SLO against real traffic — giving both an early-warning signal and a ground-truth reliability measurement, without ever touching the application’s own codebase.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>To measure user-perceived reliability with zero application code changes, look outside the app: simulate the user from the outside (synthetic clients) and read what already happened from the outside (request logs).</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a scenario rules out engineering/code changes but asks for user-perspective reliability measurement, favor black-box techniques — synthetic monitoring and existing infrastructure/request logs — over any option that mentions adding, modifying, or instrumenting application code, however reasonable that option sounds in isolation.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/uptime-checks\"><strong>Google Cloud — Monitoring overview and Uptime checks (synthetic monitoring):</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of using external synthetic clients to measure application reliability from a user perspective without application code changes</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/https/https-logging-monitoring\"><strong>Google Cloud — HTTP(S) Load Balancing logging and monitoring:</strong></a> Supports: the use of existing load balancer/proxy request logs to trace historical customer interactions and reliability without instrumenting the application</p>","lessons":["d3l1"]},"e1q15":{"id":"e1q15","exam":1,"n":15,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You have a CI/CD pipeline that uses Cloud Build to build Docker images and push them to Docker Hub. Your code is versioned in Git. After making a change to the Cloud Build YAML configuration, you notice that the pipeline is no longer building any new artifacts. Following Site Reliability Engineering practices, what should you do to resolve the issue?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Disable the CI pipeline, and revert to building and pushing the artifacts manually.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Change the CI pipeline to push the artifacts to Artifact Registry instead of Docker Hub.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Upload the configuration YAML file to Cloud Storage, and use Error Reporting to identify and fix the issue.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Run a Git diff between the previous and current versions of the Cloud Build configuration file to find and fix the bug.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Run a Git diff between the previous and current versions of the Cloud Build configuration file to find and fix the bug.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The must-have constraint is diagnosing and fixing the actual root cause — the pipeline broke immediately after a configuration change, which is a direct, available clue pointing to exactly where the defect was introduced. SRE troubleshooting practice favors using the evidence closest to the failure before reaching for broader or unrelated remedies: since the code (including the Cloud Build YAML) is already versioned in Git, comparing the last known-working configuration against the current one is the fastest, most direct way to isolate precisely what changed and caused the failure. Every other option either abandons the automation entirely, changes something unrelated to the reported symptom, or applies a tool that doesn’t actually inspect configuration files — none of which engages with the root cause the way a direct diff does.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“After making a change in the Cloud Build YAML configuration... no new artifacts are being built” is a hard constraint — it establishes a clear before/after causal link between a specific recent change and the failure, which is the strongest available diagnostic signal.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“You use Git for code versioning” is a technical signal — it tells you the tooling needed to directly compare the working and broken configuration states is already in place and requires no new setup.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“following Site Reliability Engineering practices” is a scope signal — it points toward a root-cause diagnostic approach consistent with blameless, evidence-based troubleshooting, rather than a workaround that sidesteps the problem or discards the automation.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Since the pipeline failure began immediately after a specific, identifiable change to the Cloud Build configuration, and that configuration is version-controlled in Git, running a diff between the last working revision and the current one directly surfaces the exact lines that changed — which is almost always where the bug was introduced in a “it broke right after I changed X” scenario. This lets you pinpoint and correct the specific misconfiguration (a malformed step, an incorrect trigger condition, a broken substitution variable, etc.) with minimal guesswork, restoring the pipeline to a working state while preserving the intended change where possible. It’s the most targeted, lowest-effort path to root cause given the information already available.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Disabling the pipeline and reverting to manual builds abandons the automation entirely rather than fixing it — it’s a workaround that sacrifices the benefits of CI/CD (consistency, speed, reduced human error) instead of addressing the actual defect, and it does nothing to prevent the same misconfiguration from recurring once the pipeline is eventually re-enabled.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Switching the artifact destination from Docker Hub to Artifact Registry doesn’t address the reported symptom at all — the pipeline isn’t failing because of where it pushes images, it’s failing to build anything in the first place, almost certainly due to the YAML change itself. This is a distractor that changes something unrelated to the actual failure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Incorrect:</strong> Error Reporting is designed to aggregate and surface exceptions and stack traces from running application code, not to analyze static configuration files; uploading a YAML file to Cloud Storage and pointing Error Reporting at it doesn’t correspond to how that service actually works, so this approach can’t meaningfully diagnose a Cloud Build configuration problem.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is reaching for a tool-sounding “observability” answer (Option C) because the scenario involves troubleshooting, without checking whether that tool’s actual function matches the problem — Error Reporting ingests application exceptions, not static config files, so it has no mechanism to inspect or validate YAML syntax or pipeline logic. The fastest, most defensible fix is almost always the one that directly uses the evidence already pointing at the cause (the recent, version-controlled change), rather than introducing a new, mismatched tool.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>When a system breaks immediately following a specific, identifiable change, the most effective troubleshooting first step is to examine that exact change before considering broader remedies — version control is one of the most direct diagnostic tools available precisely because it preserves the before/after state needed to isolate a regression quickly, which is far more targeted than disabling the system, changing unrelated components, or applying a monitoring tool outside its intended scope.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Teams running CI/CD pipelines routinely rely on this exact pattern when a pipeline regresses after a configuration commit: a quick <code>git diff</code> or <code>git log -p</code> against the pipeline definition file isolates the change, often revealing a typo, an incorrect step ordering, or a broken trigger condition within minutes — turning what could be an extended outage of the delivery pipeline into a short, well-understood fix, and reinforcing why keeping CI/CD configuration under the same version control as application code is a core DevOps practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“It broke right after a specific change, and that change is in version control” → diff the before/after states first, before reaching for unrelated fixes or abandoning the pipeline.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a CI/CD pipeline fails immediately after a configuration change and that configuration is version-controlled, the SRE-aligned first move is always to compare the before/after states directly (a Git diff) to isolate the root cause — not to disable the pipeline, swap an unrelated component, or apply a monitoring tool that doesn’t inspect configuration files.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/troubleshooting\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Build configuration overview and troubleshooting builds:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of diagnosing a Cloud Build YAML regression by examining the configuration change that preceded the failure</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\">Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide, Section 2 (CI/CD pipelines): </a>Supports: domain alignment — diagnosing and resolving CI/CD pipeline failures is explicitly part of building and implementing CI/CD pipelines</p>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e1q16":{"id":"e1q16","exam":1,"n":16,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You run an application on Compute Engine and collect its logs through Cloud Logging. You discover that some personally identifiable information (PII) is leaking into certain log entry fields, and every affected entry begins with the text <code>userinfo</code>. You need to capture these entries in a secure location for later review while preventing them from being retained in Cloud Logging. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create a basic log filter matching <code>userinfo</code>, and then configure a log export in the Google Cloud console with Cloud Storage as the sink.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use a Fluentd filter plugin with the logging agent to strip log entries containing <code>userinfo</code>, and then copy those entries to a Cloud Storage bucket.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create an advanced log filter matching <code>userinfo</code>, configure a log export in the Google Cloud console with Cloud Storage as the sink, and then configure a log exclusion using <code>userinfo</code> as the filter.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Use a Fluentd filter plugin with the logging agent to strip log entries containing <code>userinfo</code>, create an advanced log filter matching <code>userinfo</code>, and then configure a log export in the Google Cloud console with Cloud Storage as the sink.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Create an advanced log filter matching</strong><code>**userinfo**</code><strong>, configure a log export in the Google Cloud console with Cloud Storage as the sink, and then configure a log exclusion using</strong><code>**userinfo**</code><strong>as the filter.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The scenario has two distinct, must-have requirements: capture the matching entries somewhere secure for later review, and stop them from being retained in Cloud Logging. Cloud Logging’s architecture provides a dedicated mechanism for each: a sink with a filter routes matching entries to an external destination like Cloud Storage (satisfying capture), while a log exclusion independently stops matching entries from being written into Cloud Logging’s own storage (satisfying prevention). Because these are two separate, purpose-built controls operating at the log-routing layer, the correct approach configures both of them together — and because the filter needs to match text appearing within a specific field rather than a simple resource-type or severity match, it must be an advanced filter, not a basic one.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“capture these log entries in a secure location for later review” is a hard constraint — it requires the matching entries to still exist somewhere accessible, ruling out any approach that simply discards them.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“prevent them from leaking to [Cloud Logging]” is a second, independent hard constraint — it requires suppressing retention specifically within Cloud Logging’s own storage, which is a distinct action from exporting a copy elsewhere.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“All PII entries begin with the text userinfo” is a technical signal pointing toward a text-pattern match on log content, which requires an advanced log filter (capable of matching on field content) rather than a basic filter (typically limited to simpler criteria like resource type or severity).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>An advanced log filter matching <code>userinfo</code> precisely targets the affected entries based on their content. Configuring a sink with that filter and Cloud Storage as the destination exports a copy of every matching entry to a secure, access-controlled bucket for later review, satisfying the capture requirement. Separately configuring a log exclusion with the same <code>userinfo</code> filter tells Cloud Logging not to ingest and store matching entries in its own log storage going forward, directly satisfying the leak-prevention requirement. Because sinks and exclusions are independent, complementary mechanisms within the same log-routing pipeline, applying both together achieves exactly what the scenario asks for — a secure copy retained outside Cloud Logging, and no retention of the sensitive data inside it.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A basic log filter and export sink would capture matching entries in Cloud Storage, partially satisfying the first requirement, but a basic filter isn’t built to reliably match arbitrary text patterns within log entry fields, and — critically — this option includes no exclusion step at all, so the PII would continue to be retained in Cloud Logging’s own storage, failing the leak-prevention requirement entirely.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Modifying the logging agent’s pipeline to strip matching entries via a Fluentd filter addresses prevention at the collection layer rather than within Cloud Logging’s routing controls, which is a heavier, host-level change requiring agent configuration management across every instance, and it isn’t the mechanism Cloud Logging itself provides for this exact use case. It also doesn’t by itself establish a clean, Cloud Logging-native secure copy — the “copy the entries” step is left vague and disconnected from the filtering logic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> This combines the agent-side Fluentd filtering of Option B with the filter-and-export logic of Option A, but still doesn’t configure a log exclusion — so even with the entries removed at the agent before shipping, there’s no guarantee within Cloud Logging’s own routing that a similar future leak would be suppressed, and it layers unnecessary agent-side complexity on top of controls Cloud Logging already provides natively.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming that exporting matching entries to Cloud Storage (Option A) is sufficient on its own, since “capture them in Cloud Storage” sounds like it solves the whole problem — but exporting a copy via a sink does not stop the original entries from also being retained in Cloud Logging’s default storage. Export and exclusion are separate, additive controls, and missing the exclusion step leaves the leak-prevention half of the requirement completely unaddressed.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>In Cloud Logging’s routing model, a sink determines where a copy of matching log entries is sent (which can include external destinations like Cloud Storage, BigQuery, or Pub/Sub), while an exclusion filter determines what is withheld from being written into Cloud Logging’s own log storage. These are independent levers: you can export without excluding (keeping entries in both places), exclude without exporting (discarding entries entirely), or do both together — which is the pattern needed whenever sensitive data must be preserved somewhere secure while being kept out of the general logging system.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Teams handling accidental PII leaks in logs commonly use exactly this sink-plus-exclusion pattern: route the sensitive entries to a tightly access-controlled Cloud Storage bucket (often with additional encryption, retention, and audit controls) for compliance review and root-cause analysis, while excluding them from the organization’s general-purpose log buckets so they aren’t visible to the broader set of engineers who have standard log-viewing access — addressing both the operational need to investigate and the compliance need to minimize exposure.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Capture sensitive logs elsewhere with a sink; stop them from staying in Cloud Logging with an exclusion — you need both, not just one.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Whenever a Cloud Logging question asks you to both preserve certain log entries somewhere secure and stop them from being retained in Cloud Logging itself, look for the option that pairs an advanced filter plus export sink (capture) with a matching log exclusion (prevention) — an export alone never stops the data from also sitting in Cloud Logging’s own storage.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/export/configure_export_v2\">Google Cloud — Configure log sinks: </a>Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of using an advanced log filter and a Cloud Storage sink to export matching PII entries to a secure location</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/exclusions\"><strong>Google Cloud — Configure log exclusions:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of using a log exclusion filter to prevent matching entries from being retained in Cloud Logging’s own storage</p>","lessons":["d4l2","d4l3"]},"e1q17":{"id":"e1q17","exam":1,"n":17,"domain":1,"html":"<p>You need to deploy a new production service that will scale automatically using a Managed Instance Group (MIG) and that must run across multiple regions. Each instance requires a large amount of resources, and you need to plan for capacity ahead of the deployment. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use the <code>n1-highcpu-96</code> machine type in the MIG’s instance configuration.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Monitor Cloud Trace results to determine how many resources are required.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Confirm that the resource requirements fall within the available quota limits in each target region.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Deploy the service in a single region, and use a global load balancer to route traffic to that region.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Confirm that the resource requirements fall within the available quota limits in each target region.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The decisive requirement is capacity planning specifically for a multi-region deployment of resource-heavy instances — and Google Cloud resource quotas (CPUs, specific machine-type families, IP addresses, and similar) are allocated and enforced per project per region. That means a deployment plan is incomplete, and genuinely at risk of failing partway through rollout, until you’ve confirmed each target region actually has enough available quota to support the planned instance sizes and counts. None of the other options engages with this region-specific capacity constraint: picking a machine type addresses sizing but not whether that sizing is actually obtainable in every region, monitoring request latency data addresses a completely different concern, and consolidating to one region directly contradicts the multi-region requirement rather than solving the planning problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“plan for capacity” is a hard constraint — it specifically calls for a capacity-planning action, not a sizing choice, a monitoring tool, or an architectural simplification.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“deployed over multiple regions” is a hard constraint — it rules out any option that collapses the deployment into a single region, since quotas and capacity must be confirmed independently in each of several regions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“needs a large number of resources for each instance” is a technical signal — it raises the real risk of hitting regional quota ceilings (CPU quotas, specific machine-type quotas) precisely because large instances consume quota quickly, making quota validation materially important here.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud quotas are enforced at the project-and-region level, so a MIG configured to scale instances with large resource footprints across several regions can succeed in one region and fail to provision in another purely because that region’s quota hasn’t been raised to accommodate the planned instance count and size. Confirming available quota in every target region before deployment is the direct, necessary capacity-planning step: it surfaces where a quota increase request is needed ahead of time, preventing a scaling event or initial rollout from silently failing or being capped well below the intended capacity in one or more regions.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Choosing a specific machine type like <code>n1-highcpu-96</code> is a sizing decision that might suit a high-CPU workload, but selecting a machine type says nothing about whether enough of that machine type’s resources are actually available under quota in each target region — it addresses “what to request” without addressing “can you actually get it,” which is the core of capacity planning here.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Cloud Trace captures distributed request latency data to help diagnose where time is spent across a request’s lifecycle — it’s a performance-debugging and observability tool, not a capacity-planning or quota-forecasting tool. It provides no information about CPU/resource quota availability in a given region.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Deploying in a single region behind a global load balancer directly abandons the stated requirement to run the service across multiple regions. It might simplify quota management by concentrating everything in one place, but it does so by ignoring an explicit constraint of the scenario rather than solving the capacity-planning problem within it.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating machine-type selection (Option A) as equivalent to capacity planning, since picking “enough CPU per instance” feels like it addresses “a large number of resources for each instance.” But sizing an individual instance and confirming that your project can actually obtain that many instances of that size, in every region you’re deploying to, are two different steps — quota validation is the one capacity planning specifically requires.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Capacity planning for a multi-region deployment must account for the fact that cloud resource quotas are regional and finite: successfully designing an architecture (machine type, autoscaling policy, region selection) is necessary but not sufficient — you must also confirm the underlying capacity is actually allocated and available in every region the architecture depends on, requesting quota increases ahead of deployment wherever a gap exists.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Teams rolling out a resource-intensive service across several regions routinely check (and often pre-request increases to) CPU and machine-family quotas in each region as part of their launch checklist, specifically because discovering a quota shortfall during a live scaling event — rather than during planning — turns a routine autoscaling action into a production incident where the MIG simply can’t add the instances it needs.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Multi-region capacity planning always includes a region-by-region quota check — architecture and sizing decisions alone don’t guarantee the capacity is actually available where you need it.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Whenever a question asks about capacity planning for a deployment spanning multiple regions or projects, look for the option that validates available resource quota in each target location — quotas are regional, so a plan isn’t complete until you’ve confirmed the numbers actually fit.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/quotas/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Resource quotas overview:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of quotas being enforced per project and region, and the need to validate and request increases ahead of a multi-region deployment</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instance-groups\"><strong>Google Cloud — Managed instance groups (MIGs) overview:</strong></a> Supports: the architectural context of autoscaling MIGs across multiple regions and why per-region resource availability directly affects scaling outcomes</p>","lessons":["d1l12"]},"e1q18":{"id":"e1q18","exam":1,"n":18,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You support an application that stores product information in a cache. For every cache miss, an entry is logged in Cloud Logging. You want to visualize how often cache misses occur over time. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Link Cloud Logging as a data source in Data Studio, and filter the report on the cache-miss entries.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Configure Cloud Profiler to identify and visualize when the cache misses occur, based on the logs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create a logs-based metric in Cloud Logging, and build a dashboard for that metric in Cloud Monitoring.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Configure BigQuery as a sink for Cloud Logging, and create a scheduled query that filters the cache-miss logs and writes them to a separate table.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Create a logs-based metric in Cloud Logging, and build a dashboard for that metric in Cloud Monitoring.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The must-have requirement is a time-series visualization of how often a specific, filterable log event occurs — this is exactly the use case Cloud Logging and Cloud Monitoring were designed to handle together natively, with no custom pipeline or external BI tool required. A logs-based metric converts a log filter (matching the cache-miss entries) into a countable, time-series metric directly within Cloud Logging, and Cloud Monitoring can then chart that metric on a dashboard with essentially no additional setup. Every other option either uses a tool built for a different purpose (code profiling) or routes the data through a heavier, more indirect pipeline (BigQuery scheduled queries, an external BI connector) to arrive at a visualization Cloud Monitoring already provides directly.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“visualize how often a cache miss happens over time” is a hard constraint — it specifically calls for a time-series count visualization, which logs-based metrics plus Cloud Monitoring dashboards are purpose-built to deliver.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“an entry is logged” for every cache miss is a technical signal — it establishes that the event is already captured as a discrete, filterable log entry, meaning no new application instrumentation is needed, only a filter on existing logs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The absence of any stated requirement for long-term archival, complex cross-dataset analysis, or external reporting is an exclusion signal — it rules out heavier options (BigQuery pipelines, external BI tools) built for needs beyond simple frequency visualization.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A logs-based metric lets you define a filter (matching the cache-miss log entries) directly in Cloud Logging and have it automatically counted as a time-series metric, with no code changes, data export jobs, or external tooling required. Because that metric now exists as a standard Cloud Monitoring metric, you can immediately chart it on a Cloud Monitoring dashboard to see cache-miss frequency over time, set up alerting on it if desired, and correlate it with other operational metrics — all using the native, tightly integrated path between Cloud Logging and Cloud Monitoring that exists specifically for this kind of “turn a log event into a trend line” use case.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong> Data Studio is a general-purpose reporting and dashboarding tool, but it has no native connector that reads directly from Cloud Logging — getting log data into it typically requires an intermediate step (such as exporting to BigQuery first), making “link Cloud Logging as a source” not a straightforward or native path, and introducing more setup than the logs-based metric approach for what is fundamentally a simple frequency chart.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Incorrect:</strong> Cloud Profiler is a continuous code-level profiling tool that samples CPU and memory usage within a running application to show where execution time and resources are spent — it has no capability to ingest or visualize arbitrary Cloud Logging entries, so it cannot address this requirement at all.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Routing logs to BigQuery via a sink and running scheduled queries to isolate cache-miss entries into a separate table is a viable data pipeline, but it’s considerably more infrastructure than the question calls for — it requires building and maintaining a sink, a scheduled query, and a separate visualization layer on top of the resulting table, when a logs-based metric and Cloud Monitoring dashboard deliver the same time-series visualization with built-in, native tooling and far less setup.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is reaching for a heavier data-engineering solution (BigQuery sink plus scheduled queries, Option D) because it sounds thorough and “production-grade,” when the actual requirement — visualize frequency over time — is already a first-class, built-in capability of Cloud Logging and Cloud Monitoring together. When a log-based count-over-time visualization is all that’s needed, the native logs-based metric path is almost always the better-fit answer over building a custom data pipeline.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Logging and Cloud Monitoring are designed to interoperate directly: any log event that can be isolated with a filter can be promoted to a logs-based metric and immediately visualized, alerted on, or combined with other metrics in Cloud Monitoring, without needing to export the data elsewhere first. Reaching for an external pipeline or BI tool is appropriate only when the need goes beyond what this native integration provides — richer cross-dataset analysis, long-term structured querying, or reporting formats Cloud Monitoring doesn’t support.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Teams commonly use logs-based metrics for exactly this kind of operational signal — cache misses, specific error codes, retry events — because it turns an existing log line into an actionable, chartable, alertable metric in minutes, letting them set an alert if cache-miss frequency crosses a threshold (signaling a caching layer problem) without having to build and maintain any additional data infrastructure.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“How often does a specific log event happen over time?” → logs-based metric in Cloud Logging, charted in Cloud Monitoring — not a profiler, not an external BI tool, not a custom BigQuery pipeline.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks to visualize the frequency of a specific, filterable log event over time, default to a Cloud Logging logs-based metric paired with a Cloud Monitoring dashboard — it’s the native, lowest-effort path, and heavier options (BigQuery pipelines, external BI tools, profiling tools) are usually distractors solving a different or larger problem than what’s actually being asked.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/logs-based-metrics\">Google Cloud — Logs-based metrics overview: </a>Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of converting a log filter into a time-series metric directly within Cloud Logging</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/dashboards\"><strong>Google Cloud — Create and manage dashboards in Cloud Monitoring:</strong></a> Supports: visualizing a logs-based metric’s frequency over time on a Cloud Monitoring dashboard</p>","lessons":["d4l4"]},"e1q19":{"id":"e1q19","exam":1,"n":19,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your team uses Cloud Build for all CI/CD pipelines. You want to use the <code>kubectl</code> builder in Cloud Build to deploy new images to Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), and you need to authenticate to GKE while keeping the development effort to a minimum. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Grant the Kubernetes Engine Developer role to the Cloud Build service account.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Specify the Kubernetes Engine Developer role for Cloud Build in the <code>cloudbuild.yaml</code> file.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create a new service account with the Kubernetes Engine Developer role, and use it to run Cloud Build.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Add a separate step in Cloud Build that retrieves service account credentials and passes them to <code>kubectl</code>.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Grant the Kubernetes Engine Developer role to the Cloud Build service account.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard requirement is authenticating Cloud Build’s <code>kubectl</code> builder to the GKE cluster while minimizing development effort — meaning the solution should use existing, built-in mechanisms rather than custom scripting or additional infrastructure. Cloud Build already runs every build under its own default service account, and the <code>kubectl</code> builder is designed to pick up that identity’s credentials automatically (via the GKE cluster’s environment variables for zone/region and cluster name) with no manual credential handling required. That makes IAM role assignment on the existing Cloud Build service account the single, minimal-effort action needed — every other option either grants the permission in the wrong place (a YAML file isn’t an IAM mechanism), introduces unnecessary new infrastructure (a separate service account to run Cloud Build itself), or adds custom pipeline logic to do something the builder already handles natively.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“minimizing development effort” is a hard constraint — it rules out any option requiring custom scripting, credential-handling steps, or additional infrastructure beyond what Cloud Build and the <code>kubectl</code> builder already provide.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“the kubectl builder for Cloud Build” is a technical signal — it specifies a tool that is built to authenticate using the Cloud Build service account’s existing identity, pointing directly at an IAM-role-grant solution rather than a manual credential-passing one.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“authenticate to GKE” is a scope signal — it’s specifically an identity/authorization task, which in Google Cloud is solved through IAM role bindings, not through application configuration files like <code>cloudbuild.yaml</code>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Every Cloud Build job runs under a Google-managed Cloud Build service account by default, and the <code>kubectl</code> builder is specifically designed to authenticate to a GKE cluster using that identity’s IAM permissions — it reads the cluster, zone/region, and project details from environment variables and handles the credential exchange itself. Granting the Kubernetes Engine Developer role (<code>roles/container.developer</code>) to that existing service account gives it exactly the permissions needed to interact with Kubernetes API objects in the cluster, which is all that’s required: no new service account, no custom credential-retrieval step, and no configuration-file-level role declaration (which isn’t how IAM permissions are granted in the first place). This is the smallest possible change that fully satisfies the authentication requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Incorrect:</strong> IAM roles are granted through Identity and Access Management policy bindings on a principal (a user, group, or service account) — they are not something you “specify” inside a <code>cloudbuild.yaml</code> build configuration file. This option describes a mechanism that doesn’t correspond to how GCP IAM actually works, so it can’t function as described.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Creating a new service account and configuring Cloud Build to run under it would work, but it’s unnecessary additional effort and infrastructure when the default Cloud Build service account already exists and only needs an IAM role granted to it. This adds a service-account creation and configuration step that doesn’t improve on simply granting the role to the existing identity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Manually retrieving service account credentials and passing them to <code>kubectl</code> in a separate build step duplicates functionality the <code>kubectl</code> builder already performs automatically via the Cloud Build service account’s identity. This is significantly more development effort (writing and maintaining custom credential-handling logic) for no functional benefit over simply granting the role.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is Option B, which sounds plausible because <code>cloudbuild.yaml</code> is where you configure <em>what</em> a build does — but IAM role grants are an identity/access-management concept entirely separate from build step configuration, and conflating the two is a common misunderstanding of where permissions actually live in Google Cloud. The decisive distinction is that authentication/authorization is an IAM concern (a policy binding on a principal), never something declared inside the pipeline’s own YAML definition.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>When a CI/CD tool needs to interact with another Google Cloud service, the lowest-effort and most maintainable authentication pattern is almost always to grant the pipeline’s existing runtime identity (its default service account) the specific IAM role needed for the target service — rather than creating new service accounts, manually handling credentials, or trying to configure permissions through the pipeline’s own configuration file, which has no authority over IAM.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Teams running Cloud Build-to-GKE deployment pipelines commonly grant the Cloud Build service account the Kubernetes Engine Developer role exactly once during pipeline setup, after which every subsequent build’s <code>kubectl</code> builder step authenticates and deploys without any further credential management — keeping the pipeline simple to maintain and avoiding the security and operational overhead of managing separate service account keys or custom authentication logic.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Authenticating a GCP CI/CD tool to another GCP service is almost always “grant the existing service account the right IAM role” — not a new service account, not a config-file permission, and not manual credential plumbing.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a Cloud Build question asks how to authenticate a builder (like <code>kubectl</code>) to another Google Cloud service with minimal effort, look for the option that grants the appropriate IAM role directly to Cloud Build’s existing default service account — that’s almost always simpler and more correct than creating new service accounts, writing custom credential-handling steps, or trying to set permissions inside the build configuration file itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/api-server-authentication\"><strong>Google Cloud — Authenticating to the Kubernetes API server:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of granting <code>roles/container.developer</code> to the service account used to authenticate to a GKE cluster</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/iam\"><strong>Google Cloud — IAM roles for Kubernetes Engine:</strong></a> Supports: the Kubernetes Engine Developer role’s purpose and scope as the minimal-effort permission grant for deploying to GKE</p>","lessons":["d2l2"]},"e1q20":{"id":"e1q20","exam":1,"n":20,"domain":3,"html":"<p>Your organization follows Site Reliability Engineering practices. You are acting as Communications Lead for a large, ongoing incident affecting customer-facing applications, and there is still no estimated resolution time. You’re receiving emails from internal stakeholders asking for updates, as well as emails from customers asking what’s happening. You want to efficiently keep everyone affected informed. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Focus on responding to internal stakeholders at least every 30 minutes, and commit to “next update” times in those communications.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Provide periodic updates to all stakeholders in a timely manner, and commit to a “next update” time in every communication.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Delegate responding to internal stakeholder emails to another member of the incident response team, and focus on providing responses directly to customers.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Forward all internal stakeholder emails to the Incident Commander and let them manage internal communications, while you focus on providing responses directly to customers.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Provide periodic updates to all stakeholders in a timely manner, and commit to a “next update” time in every communication.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The must-have constraint is efficiently keeping <em>everyone affected</em> informed — both internal stakeholders and customers are named explicitly, so any approach that serves one audience while ignoring or offloading the other fails to meet the stated scope. The role described (handling communications for the incident) is exactly the Communications Lead function in Google’s incident management framework, whose core responsibility is managing information flow to all audiences, not just one. Given “no estimated time for a resolution,” the efficient and expectation-setting move for every audience is a predictable cadence of periodic updates, each one committing to when the next update will arrive — this single practice scales naturally across however many stakeholders are asking, replacing a flood of one-off replies with a structured broadcast rhythm.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“efficiently provide updates to everyone affected by the outage” is a hard constraint — it names both audiences (internal stakeholders and customers) and requires covering both, ruling out any option that narrows focus to just one.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“no estimated time for a resolution” is a technical signal — it specifically calls for a practice that manages expectations under uncertainty, which is exactly what committing to a “next update” time accomplishes regardless of whether the underlying issue is resolved yet.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“in charge of Communications” is a scope/role signal — it identifies this as the Communications Lead function, whose defined responsibility spans all stakeholder communications, not a narrower subset.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Periodic, scheduled updates replace a reactive, one-off-reply approach (which doesn’t scale as email volume grows) with a predictable broadcast cadence that efficiently reaches both internal stakeholders and customers from a single communications effort. Committing to a “next update” time in every communication is the specific technique that manages expectations when there’s no resolution ETA — it tells every recipient exactly when they’ll hear more, reducing the anxious, repeated “any updates?” follow-ups that consume further Communications Lead time, and keeping both audiences appropriately informed throughout a prolonged incident without requiring a reply to be drafted for every individual email received.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> This narrows focus to internal stakeholders only, leaving customers without updates entirely — directly conflicting with “everyone affected by the outage,” which explicitly includes customers as well as internal stakeholders.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Delegating internal communications away and focusing solely on customers still results in only one audience receiving direct attention from the person responsible for Communications; more fundamentally, it abandons the periodic-update structure in favor of ad hoc delegation, which doesn’t establish the predictable update cadence the uncertain-ETA situation calls for.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Handing internal stakeholder communications to the Incident Commander pulls that person away from their actual role — commanding and coordinating the incident response — to handle a function that belongs to Communications. It also still results in only customers receiving direct attention from the Communications Lead, again failing to cover both named audiences from the role actually responsible for them.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming that splitting the two audiences between different people (Options C and D) is a reasonable division of labor under high email volume — but IMAG’s Communications Lead role exists precisely so that one role owns messaging across all audiences, and reassigning audience-specific communications to the Incident Commander or another unspecified team member either misuses a different role’s time or abandons the stated requirement to serve “everyone affected.” The efficient fix for volume isn’t reassignment — it’s switching from one-off replies to scheduled, scalable periodic broadcasts.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Under prolonged uncertainty (no ETA), the single highest-leverage communications practice is a predictable update cadence with an explicit commitment to the next update time — it substitutes structure for individualized responses, scales to any number of recipients without added effort per person, and directly reduces stakeholder anxiety by replacing silence (or reactive, inconsistent replies) with a reliable rhythm of information.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Teams running a major incident commonly post status-page or broadcast updates on a fixed interval (e.g., “next update in 30 minutes”) to both internal channels and public status pages simultaneously, which lets the Communications Lead spend their limited time crafting one accurate, well-reviewed update rather than fielding a growing queue of individual emails — directly reducing the operational load that incident communications would otherwise place on the broader response effort.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Serving “everyone affected” under incident communications means one scheduled update reaching all audiences, each one promising when the next update lands — not splitting audiences apart or handing part of the job to a role that doesn’t own it.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When an SRE incident-communications question describes multiple stakeholder groups and an uncertain resolution time, favor the option that delivers periodic updates to all named audiences with an explicit “next update” commitment — options that narrow focus to one audience or reassign communications to an unrelated role (like the Incident Commander) are distractors that fail the “everyone affected” requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/static/pdf/IncidentManagementGuide.pdf\"><strong>Google SRE — Incident Management Guide:</strong></a> Supports: the Communications Lead role’s responsibility for managing information flow to all stakeholders, and the practice of periodic, scheduled updates during a prolonged incident</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/incident-response/\">Google SRE Workbook — Chapter 9, Incident Response: </a>Supports: the rationale for committing to a next-update cadence as a core communications practice when an incident’s resolution time is unknown</p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e1q21":{"id":"e1q21","exam":1,"n":21,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your application artifacts are built and deployed through a CI/CD pipeline. You want the pipeline to access application secrets securely, and you also want to be able to rotate those secrets easily in the event of a security breach. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Prompt developers for secrets at build time, and instruct them not to store secrets at rest.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Store secrets in a separate configuration file in Git, and give select developers access to that file.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Store secrets in Cloud Storage, encrypted with a key from Cloud KMS, and grant the CI/CD pipeline access to Cloud KMS through IAM.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Encrypt the secrets and store them in the source code repository, store the decryption key in a separate repository, and grant the pipeline access to that repository.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Store secrets in Cloud Storage, encrypted with a key from Cloud KMS, and grant the CI/CD pipeline access to Cloud KMS through IAM.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The two must-have requirements are secure, automated access for the pipeline and easy rotation in a breach scenario — which together rule out anything requiring manual human intervention at build time or anything that puts secrets inside version control, where history is hard to fully purge and access can’t be centrally revoked. Each option has to be checked against both requirements independently: automation compatibility, and how cleanly a compromised secret can be rotated. A centralized, encrypted store with IAM-controlled access satisfies both — the pipeline authenticates automatically via its service account, the secret value and the encryption key are managed independently of any code repository, and rotating either the secret or revoking access is a quick operation in a dedicated system built for exactly this.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“securely access application secrets” is a hard constraint — it requires automated, access-controlled retrieval by the pipeline itself, ruling out any human-in-the-loop step.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“more easily rotate secrets in case of a security breach” is a second hard constraint — it demands that updating or revoking a secret be fast and centralized, which disqualifies anything embedding secrets in version-controlled files (where rotation means purging history and redeploying across every clone).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“CI/CD pipeline” as the consumer is a technical signal pointing toward IAM-based service-to-service access (the pipeline’s own identity authenticating to a secrets store), rather than developer-managed access controls on a shared file.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Storing secrets in Cloud Storage with Cloud KMS encryption keeps the sensitive values out of source control entirely, encrypted at rest with keys that are centrally managed and access-controlled. Granting the CI/CD pipeline’s service account IAM access to the relevant Cloud KMS key and Cloud Storage object lets the pipeline retrieve and decrypt secrets automatically during a build with no manual step, satisfying secure access. For rotation, updating the stored secret value or rotating the KMS key is a contained, centralized operation — and if a breach occurs, revoking IAM access or rotating the key immediately cuts off further use of the compromised secret, without needing to rewrite Git history or coordinate changes across every repository that might reference it.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Prompting developers for secrets at build time breaks automation entirely — a CI/CD pipeline is meant to run unattended, and requiring a human to supply a secret defeats the purpose of continuous delivery while also providing no mechanism for secure storage or rotation at all.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> Storing secrets in a Git configuration file, even with restricted developer access, puts sensitive values into version control history, where they persist indefinitely across clones and forks; rotating a compromised secret would require not just updating the file but scrubbing history everywhere it was ever committed, which is neither secure nor easy.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Encrypting secrets and storing them alongside code, with the decryption key in a separate repository, is a workable but weaker pattern than using a dedicated key-management service: both the encrypted secrets and the key end up living in source control systems not purpose-built for secret lifecycle management, lacking the centralized audit logging, fine-grained IAM controls, and key-rotation tooling that Cloud KMS provides natively. Rotation here still means committing new encrypted values and managing key access through repository permissions rather than a dedicated IAM/KMS control plane.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is Option D looking secure at first glance because the secrets are “encrypted” — but encryption alone doesn’t solve the rotation and access-revocation problem if the encrypted blob and its key are still managed through source-control permissions rather than a centralized key-management system built for exactly this lifecycle (issuing, rotating, and revoking access to keys and secrets independently of code history).</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Secrets belong in a system designed around their specific lifecycle — centralized storage, encryption at rest, fine-grained IAM-based access, and the ability to rotate or revoke instantly — never in source control, even encrypted, because version control systems are optimized for code history and collaboration, not for the access-revocation and audit guarantees a compromised secret response actually requires.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>While Cloud Storage encrypted with a Cloud KMS key is a valid and functional pattern, Google Cloud also offers Secret Manager, a service purpose-built for exactly this use case — centralized secret storage, automatic versioning, IAM-based per-secret access control, and built-in audit logging — which many CI/CD pipelines use directly for application secrets precisely because it provides this same security and rotation model with less infrastructure to manage than assembling Cloud Storage and Cloud KMS by hand.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Secrets for an automated pipeline belong in a centralized, IAM-controlled, encrypted store — never in source control, encrypted or not — because only a dedicated secret-management approach makes both secure automated access and fast rotation possible.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a CI/CD question asks how to securely provide and easily rotate secrets, favor a centralized, encrypted secret store with IAM-based access over anything that puts secrets (even encrypted ones) into source control or requires a human to supply them at build time — the decisive test is whether a compromised secret can be rotated and revoked without touching repository history.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kms/docs/key-management-service\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud KMS overview:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of encrypting secrets with centrally managed, rotatable Cloud KMS keys and controlling access via IAM</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/secret-manager/docs/overview\">Google Cloud — Secret Manager overview: </a>Supports: the current, purpose-built Google Cloud service for application secret storage, access control, and rotation referenced as the broader best-practice context</p>","lessons":["d2l8"]},"e1q22":{"id":"e1q22","exam":1,"n":22,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You support a production service running on a single Compute Engine instance. You regularly spend time recreating the service by deleting the crashing instance and creating a new one from the relevant image. You want to reduce the time spent on this manual work while following Site Reliability Engineering principles. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. File a bug with the development team so they can find the root cause of the crashing instance.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Create a managed instance group with a single instance, and use health checks to determine the system’s status.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Place a load balancer in front of the Compute Engine instance, and use health checks to determine the system’s status.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Create a Cloud Monitoring dashboard with SMS alerts so you can begin recreating the crashed instance promptly after it crashes.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Create a managed instance group with a single instance, and use health checks to determine the system’s status.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard requirement is eliminating manual, repetitive toil — specifically the recurring human effort of deleting a crashed instance and recreating it from an image — which is exactly the kind of work SRE principles define as toil to be automated away. That immediately rules out anything that still requires a person to notice the crash and act (filing a bug, watching a dashboard and reacting to an alert) in favor of a mechanism that performs the recreation automatically. A managed instance group (MIG) is Google Cloud’s native automation for exactly this: it continuously monitors instance health and automatically recreates any instance that fails its health check, even when the group size is just one, turning a manual recovery procedure into a fully automated self-healing loop.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“reduce the time spent performing manual operations” is a hard constraint — it specifically targets reducing human toil, not just detecting or being notified of failures faster.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“following Site Reliability Engineering principles” is a technical signal — SRE explicitly treats recurring manual recovery work as toil to be eliminated through automation, pointing toward a self-healing mechanism rather than better alerting.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“deleting the crashing instance and creating a new instance based on the relevant image” describes a repeatable, mechanical recovery procedure — a technical signal that this is precisely the kind of task a managed instance group’s auto-healing is built to automate.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A managed instance group, even with a target size of one, continuously applies health checks to its member instance and automatically deletes and recreates that instance from the configured instance template whenever it fails its health check — this is functionally identical to the manual recreate-from-image procedure described in the scenario, except performed automatically by the platform with no human action required. This directly and completely eliminates the recurring manual toil the question identifies, replacing a human-driven recovery loop with a system-driven one, which is the core SRE objective of reducing time spent on repetitive operational work.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong> Filing a bug to investigate the root cause of the crashes is a legitimate and valuable parallel action (and good SRE postmortem practice), but it does nothing to reduce the manual recreation work happening in the meantime — the instance will keep crashing and still require manual recreation until the underlying bug is found and fixed, which could take considerable time.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A load balancer with health checks can detect that an unhealthy instance shouldn’t receive traffic and can route around it, but a load balancer does not recreate or replace the failed instance itself — the crashed instance still needs to be manually deleted and recreated. This addresses traffic routing, not the automated recovery the scenario actually needs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A monitoring dashboard with SMS alerts speeds up human awareness of a crash, letting you react faster, but it still requires a person to receive the alert and manually perform the same delete-and-recreate steps — it reduces detection latency, not the manual operational work itself, which is the specific time sink the question asks you to reduce.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is Option D, which looks like it addresses “reduce time spent” because faster notification does shorten the overall incident timeline — but faster awareness of a problem is not the same as eliminating the manual labor of fixing it. The distinguishing requirement is toil reduction through automation, not faster human response, which only the managed instance group’s self-healing actually provides.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>SRE treats manual, repetitive, automatable operational work as toil, and the standard response to toil is not to detect it faster or notify about it sooner, but to automate it away entirely. When a scenario describes a human repeatedly performing the same mechanical recovery steps, the SRE-aligned answer is almost always the platform feature that performs that exact recovery automatically — here, a managed instance group’s health-check-driven auto-healing — rather than any improvement to alerting or escalation around the manual process.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Teams running even single-instance production services commonly wrap them in a managed instance group specifically to gain this auto-healing behavior at essentially no architectural cost — the group still runs just one instance, but a crashed or unresponsive instance is automatically replaced from the same instance template within minutes, freeing the on-call engineer from being paged for routine instance failures and letting their attention go toward investigating recurring root causes instead of performing the recovery itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Recurring manual delete-and-recreate work is toil — automate it with a managed instance group’s health-check-based auto-healing, not with better alerts or faster human reaction.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question describes an engineer regularly performing the same manual recovery steps and asks how to reduce that time under SRE principles, look for the option that automates the recovery itself (a managed instance group with health checks) rather than options that only improve detection, notification, or routing around the problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instance-groups/autohealing-instances-in-migs\"><strong>Google Cloud — Autohealing instances in a managed instance group:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of automatic, health-check-driven instance recreation, including for a single-instance managed instance group</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/eliminating-toil/\"><strong>Google SRE Book — Eliminating Toil:</strong></a> Supports: the SRE principle of automating recurring manual operational work rather than improving detection or response speed around it</p>","lessons":["d3l6"]},"e1q23":{"id":"e1q23","exam":1,"n":23,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You are responsible for writing and updating the Terraform configurations that define your organization’s infrastructure. Two new engineers will soon contribute to the same codebase. You need to choose tooling and establish a workflow that prevents team members from overwriting one another’s changes. You also need to make sure the latest version always contains every update. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A.</p>\n<p>Keep the code in a Git-based version control system.</p>\n<p>Define a workflow in which each developer merges their own changes at the end of the working day.</p>\n<p>Bundle the code and upload it to a versioned Cloud Storage bucket, designating that upload as the current master version.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B.</p>\n<p>Keep the code in a Git-based version control system.</p>\n<p>Define a workflow that requires peer code review and unit testing to validate integrity and functionality before changes are integrated.</p>\n<p>Define a workflow in which the fully integrated code in the repository is treated as the current master version.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C.</p>\n<p>Keep the code as plain-text files in Google Drive, arranged in a defined folder hierarchy.</p>\n<p>At the end of each day, verify that every change is reflected in the files within that hierarchy.</p>\n<p>Rename the folder hierarchy using a predefined naming convention that increments the version number.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D.</p>\n<p>Keep the code as plain-text files in Google Drive, arranged in a defined folder hierarchy.</p>\n<p>At the end of each day, verify that every change is reflected in the files within that hierarchy, then create a new .zip archive using a predefined naming convention.</p>\n<p>Upload the .zip archive to a versioned Cloud Storage bucket and accept it as the latest version.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Keep the code in a Git-based version control system; define a workflow that requires peer code review and unit testing to validate integrity and functionality before changes are integrated; define a workflow in which the fully integrated code in the repository is treated as the current master version.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Separate the two must-haves from the quality measures. The must-haves are that concurrent contributors cannot silently overwrite each other, and that the latest version reliably contains every update. Peer review and testing before integration are the quality measures that make the shared codebase trustworthy. Work through each requirement. Preventing overwrites requires a tool with branching, merging, and conflict detection. Capturing all updates requires a single integration point that every change flows through. Only a Git-based repository with a gated merge process and one authoritative main line meets both. File shares and zip snapshots fail the first requirement, so they are eliminated. Option A has the right tool but a weak process and a second source of truth, so it loses to B.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“prevent you from overwriting each other’s code”</strong> (hard constraint): this calls for version control with branch, merge, and conflict-detection semantics, not folders or file copies.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“capture all updates in the latest version”</strong> (hard constraint): it needs one authoritative integration line, which means the integrated repository itself must be the master.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Terraform templates that define your infrastructure”</strong> (technical signal): infrastructure code should be handled with the same review-and-test discipline as application code.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“two new engineers”</strong> (technical signal): with several contributors, the integration process matters as much as the tool.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Git gives every engineer an isolated working copy and records each change as a commit. Concurrent edits are reconciled through merges, and conflicting edits to the same lines are surfaced explicitly instead of silently replacing one another. Adding peer review and unit testing before integration means only reviewed, validated changes reach the shared line. Treating the fully integrated repository as the master means the latest version is by definition the sum of everything merged. No separate packaging step can drift out of sync with it. This matches Google’s guidance for Terraform on Google Cloud. Infrastructure code lives in version control, development happens on short-lived branches, changes return to a protected main branch through pull requests, and main represents the latest approved code.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> Git is the right tool, and the daily merge does give some integration cadence, so it is tempting. It loses on two points. Each developer merging their own work at the end of the day has no review or test gate, so untested infrastructure changes can enter the shared line. Packaging code into a bucket and declaring that upload the master creates a second source of truth that can lag behind or diverge from the repository.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement.</strong> A shared Drive folder offers no branching, merge, or conflict-detection mechanism. Renaming a folder to increment a version is a naming convention, not version control. It does nothing to stop two engineers from editing the same file at once and overwriting each other, which is the core requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement.</strong> It has the same underlying weakness as C. A versioned bucket keeps older zip snapshots, but a daily snapshot of a shared folder cannot reconcile concurrent edits or show which change came from whom. It adds manual packaging steps without solving the overwrite problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option A, because both A and B use Git and it is easy to stop reading at “Git-based.” The resolving distinction is that the tool alone is not the answer. The process decides whether changes are reviewed and validated before integration, and whether the repository is the single source of truth. If Option A required reviewed, tested merges into a protected main branch, it would converge on Option B. Its bucket upload would still be an unnecessary second source of truth.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Version control exists to solve concurrent change. Each contributor works in isolation, integration happens through explicit merges, and the history is a complete, attributable record that supports rollback. Treating infrastructure as code means applying the same practices used for application code: review, automated validation, and one authoritative branch. Version control tracks the source code, and file storage or archive snapshots do not substitute for it.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>In practice, the team hosts the Terraform repository on a Git host, such as GitHub, GitLab, or Secure Source Manager. They protect the main branch and require pull requests with at least one reviewer. A CI pipeline, for example in Cloud Build, runs validation and a plan on each pull request, so reviewers see the proposed infrastructure impact before merging. The result is fewer conflicting or untested changes reaching production, and every infrastructure change is auditable and reversible. A Cloud Storage bucket does appear in Terraform workflows, but as the remote location for Terraform state, not as the home of the source code.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For shared Terraform code, use Git plus reviewed, tested merges into one authoritative main line. Folders and zip snapshots are not version control.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks how several engineers can safely share and evolve infrastructure code without overwriting each other, choose Git-based version control with peer review and testing before integration, and treat the integrated repository as the single source of truth. Reject any option that relies on file shares, folder renaming, or manually packaged archives as the source of truth.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/terraform/best-practices/version-control\"><strong>Google Cloud: Best practices for version control (Terraform for Google Cloud):</strong></a> Supports: the correct answer, covering infrastructure code in version control, a protected main branch as the latest approved code, branch-and-pull-request workflow, and Option A’s lack of review gating.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/solutions/managing-infrastructure-as-code\"><strong>Google Cloud: Managing infrastructure as code with Terraform, Cloud Build, and GitOps:</strong></a> Supports: reviewing proposed infrastructure changes before merging, and the distinct role of a Cloud Storage bucket as Terraform state storage rather than source-code storage (Options A and D).</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\">Google Cloud: Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 1.2, Managing infrastructure): </a>Supports: domain alignment with infrastructure-as-code tooling, GitOps, Terraform, and Google-recommended practices for making infrastructure changes.</p>","lessons":["d3l6"]},"e1q24":{"id":"e1q24","exam":1,"n":24,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your team is designing a new application for deployment to Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). You need to set up monitoring that collects and aggregates various application-level metrics in a centralized location, using Google Cloud services while keeping the setup effort to a minimum. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Publish the various metrics from the application directly to the Cloud Monitoring API, and then view these custom metrics in Cloud Monitoring.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Install the Pub/Sub client libraries, push the various metrics from the application to Pub/Sub topics, and then view the aggregated metrics in Cloud Monitoring.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Install the OpenTelemetry client libraries in the application, configure Cloud Monitoring as the export destination for the metrics, and then view the application’s metrics in Cloud Monitoring.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Emit all metrics as application-specific log messages, route these messages from the containers to the Cloud Logging collector, and then view the metrics in Cloud Monitoring.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Install the OpenTelemetry client libraries in the application, configure Cloud Monitoring as the export destination for the metrics, and then view the application’s metrics in Cloud Monitoring.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The two must-have requirements are centralization in a Google Cloud service and minimal setup effort. Each option needs to be judged on how much custom engineering it demands before metrics actually show up, aggregated, in a central place. Writing directly against the raw Cloud Monitoring API (Option A) means building and maintaining your own instrumentation, batching, and labeling logic by hand. Routing metrics through Pub/Sub (Option B) introduces a messaging layer with no built-in aggregation or metrics semantics, requiring a custom consumer to actually get the data into Monitoring. Emitting metrics as log lines and relying on log-based metrics (Option D) works for simple counters but is an indirect, limited path for richer metric types. OpenTelemetry is the one option that provides standardized instrumentation APIs plus a ready-made, officially supported exporter to Cloud Monitoring — meaning the “collect and centralize” work is handled by existing, maintained tooling rather than custom code.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“collect and aggregate various application-level metrics in a centralized location” is a hard constraint — it requires both breadth of metric types and a single centralized destination, favoring a general-purpose instrumentation standard over narrow, single-purpose mechanisms.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“while minimizing the amount of work required to set up monitoring” is a hard constraint — it rules out any option requiring you to build custom plumbing (direct API calls, a Pub/Sub consumer, custom log-parsing rules) when an existing, maintained library and exporter already does the job.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“use Google Cloud Platform services” is a technical signal — it points toward an option with a direct, native integration into a GCP observability service, which the OpenTelemetry-to-Cloud-Monitoring exporter provides out of the box.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>OpenTelemetry provides a vendor-neutral, standardized set of client libraries for instrumenting an application’s metrics (counters, gauges, histograms, and more), and Google Cloud provides an officially supported exporter that sends that telemetry directly to Cloud Monitoring. Installing the OpenTelemetry libraries and configuring the Cloud Monitoring exporter means the application code only needs to call standard OpenTelemetry APIs to record metrics — all the work of formatting, batching, and shipping that data to a centralized Google Cloud service is handled by existing, maintained libraries rather than custom integration code, directly satisfying both the centralization and minimal-effort requirements.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Publishing metrics directly to the Cloud Monitoring API does centralize the data in the right place, but it requires writing and maintaining custom code to construct time series, handle labels and resource types, manage batching, and deal with API quirks — considerably more setup effort than using an existing instrumentation library with a ready-made exporter.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Pub/Sub is a messaging and event-delivery service, not a metrics aggregation or storage system — pushing metrics into Pub/Sub topics doesn’t by itself get them into Cloud Monitoring or aggregate them in any way. A separate consumer would still need to be built to read from Pub/Sub and write into Monitoring, which is strictly more work than instrumenting directly with a metrics-aware library.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Emitting metrics as log messages and relying on log-based metrics can work for simple event counts, but it’s an indirect path for anything beyond basic counters (histograms, gauges with multiple dimensions are awkward or impossible to represent cleanly this way), and it requires designing consistent log formats and filter expressions rather than using a purpose-built metrics API.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating Option A as the “most direct” answer because it names the Cloud Monitoring API explicitly — but direct API access isn’t the same as low-effort setup; it actually represents the most manual-integration-heavy path of the four. The real signal to follow is which option offloads the integration work to existing, maintained tooling (an instrumentation library with a built-in exporter) rather than which option sounds most literally tied to the target service.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>When minimizing setup effort for application metrics is a stated goal, favor a standardized instrumentation library with a maintained, purpose-built exporter over hand-rolled API integration, a repurposed messaging system, or an indirect channel like log parsing — the library-plus-exporter pattern concentrates the integration complexity in code you don’t have to write or maintain yourself.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Teams instrumenting new GKE workloads commonly standardize on OpenTelemetry specifically because it decouples the application’s instrumentation code from any particular backend — the same OpenTelemetry-instrumented code can export to Cloud Monitoring today and, with only an exporter configuration change, to a different observability backend later, which is a meaningful advantage over metrics logic tightly coupled to one vendor’s raw API.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For centralized application metrics with minimal setup, reach for a standardized instrumentation library with a built-in exporter (OpenTelemetry → Cloud Monitoring) — not a raw API integration, a messaging system, or log-based metrics.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks for centralized application-level metrics in a Google Cloud service with minimal setup effort, favor OpenTelemetry (or another maintained instrumentation library) with its native exporter over direct API calls, messaging-system detours, or log-based metric extraction — those alternatives all shift integration work onto you that the instrumentation library already handles.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/instrumentation/choose-approach\"><strong>Google Cloud — Choose an approach to application instrumentation:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of using OpenTelemetry client libraries with Cloud Monitoring as the export destination for application metrics</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/instrumentation/opentelemetry\"><strong>Google Cloud — OpenTelemetry and Cloud Monitoring:</strong></a> Supports: the native, officially supported integration between OpenTelemetry-instrumented applications and Cloud Monitoring as a centralized metrics destination</p>","lessons":["d4l6"]},"e1q25":{"id":"e1q25","exam":1,"n":25,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You operate a high-traffic consumer web application built on a microservices architecture. The application’s home page assembles several independent widgets — for example, current weather conditions, live stock quotes, and breaking news headlines. For each widget, the request-handling thread calls a dedicated backend microservice and then renders the home page from the responses it collects. These backend microservices intermittently fail, and when that happens the home page is still served, but with one or more widgets missing. Your users tolerate an occasional missing widget and would rather see a partially populated page than no page at all, but satisfaction drops sharply if this partial-content condition becomes too frequent. You want to define a Service Level Indicator (SLI) that can support a Service Level Objective (SLO) for this experience. Which SLI should you select?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. A quality SLI, calculated as the ratio of non-degraded responses to total responses.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. An availability SLI, calculated as the ratio of healthy microservices to the total number of microservices.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. A freshness SLI, calculated as the proportion of widgets refreshed within the preceding 10 minutes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. A latency SLI, calculated as the ratio of microservice calls completing in under 100 ms to total microservice calls.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. A quality SLI, calculated as the ratio of non-degraded responses to total responses.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Start from the hard requirement: the SLO must track user-perceived experience, specifically how often the home page is served in a degraded (partial-content) state. A quality SLI is purpose-built for exactly this: it measures the proportion of responses that are complete and correct versus those that are “good enough but downgraded.” Every other option measures a different layer of the system (infrastructure health, data staleness, or response speed), none of which directly capture “how often did the user see missing content.”</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“serves the homepage with some missing content” — a technical signal pointing to degraded-response measurement, not a binary up/down signal.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“users would rather have some content served instead of no content at all” — a preference clue ruling out a strict availability definition (success vs. failure), since the response is still technically “successful.”</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“unhappy if this degraded mode occurs too frequently” — a cardinality/threshold clue: the SLO needs a metric that can quantify frequency of degradation, which is exactly what a quality SLI’s good/total ratio provides.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>No mention of response time anywhere in the scenario — an exclusion clue against latency.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A quality SLI is defined as the ratio of responses that meet the full, non-degraded bar to the total number of responses served. In this scenario, a “good” event is a home-page response where all widgets loaded successfully, and a “bad” (but still-served) event is a response where one or more widgets are missing. This directly operationalizes “degraded mode occurring too frequently” as a percentage, which is exactly what an SLO needs: a target like “99% of home-page loads should be non-degraded.”</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Possible but not best.</strong> An availability SLI measured as “healthy microservices / total microservices” describes the health of backend infrastructure, not the experience of the end user viewing the home page. A single unhealthy weather microservice would disproportionately swing this ratio even though 99% of users might still get a fully populated page if that microservice recovers quickly, so it doesn’t correlate well with the thing users actually notice.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different use case.</strong> Freshness SLIs measure whether data was updated recently enough (a data-processing/pipeline concern), not whether a request returned complete content. Even if every widget’s underlying data were perfectly fresh, a failed microservice call would still produce a degraded page, which freshness would never capture.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different use case.</strong> A latency SLI measures how fast calls complete, not whether they succeeded or returned complete content. The scenario describes failures that produce missing content, not slow responses; a microservice could fail instantly (well under 100 ms) and still cause degradation, so latency would show “healthy” even during a degraded user experience.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is the availability SLI (Option B), because “microservices occasionally fail” sounds like a classic availability problem. The resolving distinction is that availability in SRE is normally scoped to the end-to-end user-facing request (the home-page response itself succeeded), not to the fleet of backend dependencies behind it. If the scenario instead asked you to track whether individual backend microservices were reachable for an internal health dashboard, Option B would become defensible.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>In the SRE SLI taxonomy, request/response services are typically measured with three complementary SLI types: availability (did the request get a response at all), latency (was it fast enough), and quality (was the response complete/correct, as opposed to degraded). A system can be “available” while still being degraded — which is precisely why quality exists as its own SLI category rather than being folded into availability.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Choosing a quality SLI here means engineering teams get paged or review dashboards based on the rate of degraded home-page loads, not on transient individual-microservice blips. This keeps the error budget aligned with real user pain, avoids false alarms when a non-critical microservice has a brief hiccup that self-heals before affecting many users, and lets the team prioritize fixing whichever widget microservice contributes the most to degraded-response volume.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>If the system can still return “something” but it’s incomplete, measure it with a quality SLI, not an availability SLI.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Whenever a question describes a service that returns a technically successful but incomplete or lower-fidelity response, reach for a quality SLI (good/total non-degraded ratio) rather than availability, latency, or freshness — those measure whether a response happened, how fast it was, or how current the data was, not whether the response itself was whole.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/\"><strong>Google — The Site Reliability Workbook: Implementing SLOs (SLI menu, including the quality/degraded-response SLI type)</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (quality SLI as the ratio of non-degraded to total responses)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/service-monitoring\"><strong>Google Cloud — Concepts in service monitoring (definitions of availability SLI and latency SLI used to eliminate Options B and D)</strong></a> Supports: option elimination for availability and latency SLI definitions</p>","lessons":["d3l1"]},"e1q26":{"id":"e1q26","exam":1,"n":26,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You are running an experiment to determine whether users respond positively to a new feature in your web application. Soon after you release the feature as a canary, users begin receiving a spike in HTTP 500 errors, and your monitoring reports show higher latency. You want to reduce the impact on users as quickly as possible. What should you do FIRST?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Revert the experimental canary rollout.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Begin tracking latency, traffic, errors, and saturation.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Collect information for the incident’s postmortem document.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Track down where the 500 errors originate and what is causing the latency increase.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Revert the experimental canary rollout.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The stem sets one governing constraint, which is to reduce user impact as quickly as possible, and one sequencing instruction, which is FIRST. A recent change, the canary, is the obvious suspect because the errors and latency appeared shortly after it shipped. Work backward from the constraint. Which action stops the harm fastest and with the least uncertainty? Reverting the canary removes the suspect change right away. Monitoring, evidence gathering, and diagnosis all leave users affected while they run. They are valid incident activities, but they are premature as the first step. The decision method is to mitigate first, then investigate and document.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Shortly after deploying the feature as a canary release”</strong> (technical signal): the timing correlates the degradation with a recent, easily reversible change.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“spike in the number of 500 errors” and “increased latency”</strong> (technical signal): users are being harmed right now, and monitoring has already detected the problem.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“quickly minimize the negative impact on users”</strong> (hard constraint): the priority is mitigation speed, not understanding.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“first”</strong> (sequence instruction): it asks for the opening action only, which must be the one that stops the impact.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A canary release is designed to expose a new version to a small portion of traffic so problems can be caught early with limited impact. The standard response when that canary misbehaves is to roll it back, which restores the known-good version and removes the faulty change from the user path. Google’s SRE guidance on incidents follows the same pattern. Once a recent change is identified as the likely cause, reversing it is the fastest mitigation, and the rollback is the “undo button” for a progressive rollout. It does not require a diagnosis, and a rollback is low-risk because it returns the system to a state that was previously stable. Cloud Deploy’s canary strategy also supports rollback, so this mitigation is built into the tooling.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> The four golden signals (latency, traffic, errors, and saturation) are sound monitoring practice, and it is tempting because the stem mentions latency and errors. It loses on two counts. Monitoring already detected the problem, so “begin” tracking adds little, and watching signals does nothing to stop users from receiving errors.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement.</strong> Postmortem data collection is a necessary follow-up activity, but it happens during or after mitigation, not instead of it. Doing it first prolongs the user impact, which directly conflicts with the requirement to reduce it quickly.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> Root-cause investigation is valuable and will be needed, but it is slower and its outcome is uncertain. Users keep experiencing errors during that work. Because the canary can be rolled back without knowing the exact failing component, diagnosis is deferred until after mitigation.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option D, because engineers instinctively want to understand a failure before acting, and “find the root cause” sounds like the rigorous answer. The resolving distinction is that mitigation and root-cause analysis are separate tasks with separate priorities. Mitigation comes first, and the diagnosis can be done safely after the canary is out of the user path. If the rollback were not possible, for example because there was no previous version to return to, then targeted diagnosis or another mitigation such as shifting traffic would become the first step.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Incident response has a standard order: detect, mitigate, investigate, and then learn. Mitigation reduces or removes user impact using the fastest safe action, such as rolling back a recent change, redirecting traffic, or adding capacity. Root-cause analysis and postmortems come after impact has stopped. A mitigation does not need to explain the failure; it only needs to restore healthy service reliably. Progressive rollouts such as canaries exist precisely so that a rollback can limit the blast radius.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>In practice, an on-call engineer who sees error and latency alerts correlated with a canary stage reverts the canary through the delivery tooling, for example by rolling back the rollout in Cloud Deploy or shifting traffic fully back to the stable revision. Error rate and latency return toward baseline, and the incident is contained within minutes. The team then preserves logs, metrics, and traces from the failed canary for diagnosis and records the timeline for a blameless postmortem. The cost of this approach is a delayed feature experiment, which is far cheaper than prolonged user-facing errors.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Stop the bleeding before you study the wound: roll back the likely cause first, then diagnose and document.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question describes user-facing degradation right after a recent release and asks what to do first, pick the fastest safe mitigation, usually a rollback. Monitoring, root-cause tracing, and postmortem notes are real work, but they come after impact is contained.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/gcp/incident-management-at-google-adventures-in-sre-land\"><strong>Google Cloud Blog: Incident management at Google, Adventures in SRE-land:</strong></a> Supports: the correct answer, covering rollback as the SRE’s mitigation for a bad change in a progressive rollout and the principle of stopping impact before root-cause work (Options B, C, and D).</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/deployment-strategies/canary\">Google Cloud: Use a canary deployment strategy (Cloud Deploy): </a>Supports: the canary release concept as a progressive rollout that limits user exposure, and rollback support within Cloud Deploy deployment strategies.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud: Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 3.3, Mitigating incident impact on users):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment with rollback strategies, draining or redirecting traffic, and adding capacity as incident-mitigation actions.</p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e1q27":{"id":"e1q27","exam":1,"n":27,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You are the on-call engineer for an infrastructure service that many other systems depend on. An alert tells you the service is failing to serve most of its requests, and every dependent system, which together serve hundreds of thousands of users, is affected. Following your Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) incident management protocol, you declare yourself Incident Commander (IC) and bring in two experienced teammates as Operations Lead (OL) and Communications Lead (CL). What should you do next?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Work out ways to reduce the impact on users and roll the mitigations out to production.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Reach out to the owners of the affected services and give them a status update on the incident.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Set up a shared communication channel for the incident responders and leads to talk to one another.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Open a postmortem document, record the incident details, share the draft internally, and request input from internal stakeholders.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Set up a shared communication channel for the incident responders and leads to talk to one another.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>This is a sequencing question, so first identify what has already happened and who owns each remaining task. The IC has declared the incident and assigned the OL and CL, so the command structure exists. The remaining tasks belong to different roles: system changes belong to the OL, stakeholder updates belong to the CL, and the postmortem belongs to the aftermath. The IC’s own job is coordination, and coordination needs a shared place where the leads and responders can talk. That makes establishing the channel the IC’s next action, and it enables everything else.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“declare yourself Incident Commander ... Operations Lead ... Communications Lead”</strong> (technical signal): the roles are already delegated, so the open item is enabling coordination, not doing the delegated work yourself.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“What should you do next?”</strong> (sequence instruction): the answer must be the step that follows role assignment, not a later-phase or delegated activity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“hundreds of thousands of users” and “all of its dependent systems”</strong> (technical signal): this is a major incident that calls for structured command and a single coordination point, not ad hoc responses.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Google’s incident management model separates duties. The IC leads, and the CL and OL report to the IC, whose job is to coordinate the response, communicate, and stay in control. For coordination to work, interested parties need to know where to interact with the IC, so a recognized command post, such as a war room, is part of the protocol. A shared channel or call is that command post. With it in place, the OL can report mitigation work, the CL can gather facts for updates, and the IC can make decisions from one source of truth. Without it, delegated work is scattered and conflicting changes become more likely.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> Mitigation is the right priority for the incident, so this is tempting. However, applying mitigations is the Operations Lead’s responsibility, and the IC delegates instead of working hands-on. The operations team should be the only group changing the system, and its work needs to be coordinated through the command post. If the IC dives into mitigation first, command and control are lost.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case.</strong> Updating affected service owners is valuable, but it belongs to the Communications Lead. Doing it yourself defeats the point of having delegated that role, and the CL needs the coordination channel to obtain accurate information anyway.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement.</strong> A postmortem is a post-incident activity. The service is still failing for hundreds of thousands of users, so drafting and circulating a postmortem now is out of sequence and diverts attention from the response. Only a working record of events should be kept at this stage, so it is available later.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option A, because “stop the user impact first” is a core SRE principle and the stem describes a severe outage. The resolving distinction is that the principle governs what the response team does, while the IC’s role is to enable that response through delegation and coordination. If no OL had been assigned, the IC would absorb the undelegated roles by default, and then working on mitigation directly would become the right next step.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Structured incident management rests on three ideas: coordinate the response, communicate between responders, the organization, and the outside world, and keep control of the incident. A single leader assigns roles, and people in those roles work to the leader instead of freelancing. A recognized command post gives everyone one place to coordinate. The structure matters because unmanaged incidents suffer from unclear roles, uncoordinated changes, and poor communication, all of which prolong impact.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>In practice, the IC opens a dedicated incident channel or call, invites the OL, the CL, and responders, and states who holds each role. The OL then runs mitigation, such as rolling back a recent change or redirecting traffic, and reports each action in the channel. The CL pulls verified status from the same place and updates affected service owners. Everything done in the channel also forms the record used later for the postmortem. The result is faster mitigation with fewer conflicting changes and clearer messages to dependent teams.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>The IC sets up the room, the OL fixes, the CL tells, and the postmortem comes last.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When an IC has already declared an incident and assigned the OL and CL, the next step is to establish the shared command post or channel where the team coordinates. Mitigation belongs to the OL, outbound updates belong to the CL, and postmortems come after resolution, so reject options that have the IC doing those tasks or jump ahead of the response.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/managing-incidents/\"><strong>Google SRE Book: Chapter 14, Managing Incidents:</strong></a> Supports: the correct answer, covering the separation of incident roles, the operations team as the only group modifying the system, and the recognized command post where parties interact with the incident commander (Options A and B).</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/incident-response/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook: Chapter 9, Incident Response:</strong></a> Supports: the IC, CL, and OL hierarchy, the IC delegating roles and assuming undelegated ones by default, and the principles of coordinating, communicating, and controlling an incident (the answer-switch condition in Exam Trap).</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud: Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 3, Applying site reliability engineering practices, including 3.3 Mitigating incident impact on users):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment with SRE practices for managing and mitigating incidents.</p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e1q28":{"id":"e1q28","exam":1,"n":28,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your VM utilization logs are currently stored in Cloud Logging. You need to provide an interactive, easy-to-share dashboard that updates in real time and presents the data aggregated by quarter. You want to use Google Cloud solutions. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A.</p>\n<p>Route the VM utilization logs from Cloud Logging to BigQuery.</p>\n<p>Build a dashboard in Data Studio.</p>\n<p>Share the dashboard with your stakeholders.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B.</p>\n<p>Route the VM utilization logs from Cloud Logging to Pub/Sub.</p>\n<p>Forward the logs from Pub/Sub to a Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system.</p>\n<p>Build the dashboards in the SIEM system and share them with your stakeholders.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C.</p>\n<p>Route the VM utilization logs from Cloud Logging to BigQuery.</p>\n<p>Export the logs from BigQuery to a CSV file.</p>\n<p>Import the CSV file into Google Sheets.</p>\n<p>Build a dashboard in Google Sheets and share it with your stakeholders.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D.</p>\n<p>Route the VM utilization logs from Cloud Logging to a Cloud Storage bucket.</p>\n<p>Enable the Cloud Storage API so the logs can be pulled programmatically.</p>\n<p>Build a custom data visualization application.</p>\n<p>Display the pulled logs on a custom dashboard.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. 1. Route the VM utilization logs from Cloud Logging to BigQuery. 2. Build a dashboard in Data Studio. 3. Share the dashboard with your stakeholders.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Separate the must-haves from the preference. The must-haves are an interactive, easily shared dashboard, a continuously updated data path, and quarterly aggregation over log data. The preference is to stay within Google Cloud solutions. Work through the pipeline: the logs need a destination that can aggregate large volumes by period, and the dashboard needs a tool that reads from that destination live and can be shared. BigQuery with Data Studio does both. Each other option breaks the chain somewhere. One uses a security system for a utilization dashboard, one takes a static snapshot, and one requires building a custom application.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“easy-to-share interactive ... dashboard”</strong> (hard constraint): this calls for a visualization tool built for sharing reports, not a spreadsheet or a hand-built application.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“updated in real time”</strong> (hard constraint): the data path must stay live end to end, so a streaming log sink and a live connector fit and a one-time export does not.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“aggregated on a quarterly basis”</strong> (technical signal): this needs an analytical engine that can group large log volumes by period, which points to BigQuery.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“use Google Cloud Platform solutions”</strong> (preference): it favors native Google Cloud services over an external third-party system.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Logging uses log sinks to route entries to supported destinations, including BigQuery. Sinks stream log data into BigQuery in small batches, and entries are typically visible there within about a minute, so the data stays near real time. Once in BigQuery, the logs can be aggregated by quarter with SQL, for example in a view that groups utilization by quarter. Data Studio connects directly to BigQuery as a data source, so the dashboard queries live data instead of a copy. It also lets you tune how fresh that data is, from every minute up to every few hours. Stakeholders can then be given access to the same interactive report, which satisfies the sharing requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case.</strong> Routing logs through Pub/Sub is designed for streaming to external or third-party systems, such as SIEM tools like Splunk. A SIEM targets security event analysis, so it is a heavy, tangential fit for a utilization dashboard. It also moves the logs off Google Cloud, which conflicts with the stated preference.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement.</strong> It starts correctly by routing logs to BigQuery, which makes it tempting. The CSV export then turns the data into a static snapshot, so the dashboard cannot update in real time. Google Sheets also refreshes connected data no more often than every 15 minutes, and the manual export and import steps make the process fragile.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> Cloud Storage is intended for long-term archival of logs. New sinks to Cloud Storage can take hours to begin routing, and these sinks are processed hourly, so this path is poorly suited to real-time needs. A custom visualization application also has to be built, secured, and maintained. That is far more effort than a managed tool that already offers interactive, shareable dashboards.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option C, because it shares its first step with the correct answer and Google Sheets feels familiar for sharing. The resolving distinction is the CSV hop. It breaks the live connection between the logs and the dashboard, so it can only produce a snapshot. Option C would become defensible only if the stakeholders wanted a static, point-in-time quarterly report instead of a continuously updated dashboard.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Observability dashboards follow a standard pipeline: telemetry is collected, routed to an analytical store, and visualized through a tool that reads from that store. The routing destination should match the purpose. Analytics and aggregation go to BigQuery, archival goes to Cloud Storage, and streaming to external systems goes through Pub/Sub. Freshness depends on keeping every hop live, so any manual export or file hand-off turns a live pipeline into a snapshot.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>In practice, you create a log sink with a filter for the relevant VM utilization logs and send it to a write-enabled BigQuery dataset. You then define a view or query that aggregates utilization by quarter and point a Data Studio data source at it. Data Studio’s default BigQuery refresh interval is several hours, so you would lower the data freshness setting to a few minutes to approach real time. The trade-off is that shorter refresh intervals mean more BigQuery queries, and the data freshness setting exists precisely to balance up-to-date data against cost and performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Logs that must be queried, aggregated, and shown live go through a sink to BigQuery and then to a Data Studio dashboard, with no snapshot exports in between.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks for an interactive, shareable, continuously updated dashboard over Cloud Logging data, route the logs to BigQuery and visualize them in Data Studio. Reject options that pass through a CSV file, a security-focused SIEM, or a custom-built application.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/install/logs_export\"><strong>Google Cloud: Route logs to supported destinations (Cloud Logging):</strong></a> Supports: the correct answer’s routing step, and the elimination of Options B and D, covering BigQuery for analysis, Pub/Sub for third-party integrations such as Splunk, and Cloud Storage for long-term storage with hourly sink processing.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/export/bigquery\"><strong>Google Cloud: View logs routed to BigQuery (Cloud Logging):</strong></a> Supports: the real-time requirement, covering log sinks that stream data into BigQuery in small batches with entries typically visible within one minute.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/data-studio/manage-data-freshness\"><strong>Google Cloud: Manage data freshness (Data Studio):</strong></a> Supports: the live-dashboard requirement and the elimination of Option C, covering configurable BigQuery refresh intervals and the slower refresh rates of Google Sheets.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud: Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 4.2, Managing and analyzing logs):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment with exporting and retaining logs by routing to BigQuery, Pub/Sub, and Cloud Storage.</p>","lessons":["d4l1"]},"e1q29":{"id":"e1q29","exam":1,"n":29,"domain":3,"html":"<p>Your organization follows SRE principles and practices. You are preparing to assume operational responsibility for a new service built by the Development Team, and you run a Production Readiness Review (PRR) as part of the handover. When the analysis phase ends, you conclude that the service, as it stands, cannot satisfy its Service Level Objectives (SLOs). You want to be confident the service can meet its SLOs once it is running in production. What should you do next?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Lower the SLO targets to values the service can currently achieve so that it can go live.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Inform the development team that they will need to provide ongoing production support for the service.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Determine the reliability improvements to recommend and have them completed before the handover.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Launch the service in production without SLOs and define them later, once operational data has been gathered.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Determine the reliability improvements to recommend and have them completed before the handover.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Treat the requirement as fixed: the service must be able to meet its SLOs in production. The SLOs reflect what users need, and the PRR analysis shows the service falls short of them. That leaves the service as the thing to change. Requirement to capability to elimination gives a clear path. The only capability that closes the gap between the service and its SLOs is improving the service’s reliability. Lowering the target, dropping SLOs, or pushing support back to developers each leaves the service unchanged and the gap open. The correct next step is to turn the analysis findings into recommended improvements and complete them before the SRE team takes over.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“the service cannot currently meet its SLOs”</strong> (technical signal): the PRR analysis has found a reliability gap, so the next phase is to fix the service, not to move forward as is.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“ensure that the service can meet its SLOs in production”</strong> (hard constraint): the SLOs stay fixed and the service must become capable of meeting them.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“What should you do next?”</strong> (sequence instruction): it follows the analysis phase of the PRR, which in the SRE engagement model leads into improvements and refactoring.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“taking over the management of a new service”</strong> (technical signal): SRE accepts responsibility only after the service is deemed ready, so improvements come before handover.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A PRR is meant to verify that a service meets accepted standards of production setup and operational readiness, and to improve its reliability before the SRE team takes responsibility for it. After the analysis phase surfaces gaps, the next phase is improvements and refactoring. SRE identifies the changes needed, such as bug fixes, reliability and design changes, automation, and monitoring and alerting, and works with the development team to get them done. Only after sufficient improvements are made and the service is deemed ready does SRE assume production responsibilities. This directly serves the goal, because it makes the service capable of meeting the SLOs instead of changing what the SLOs say.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement.</strong> Weakening the SLO targets to match current performance is tempting because it makes the gap disappear on paper. It does not make the service more reliable, and it disconnects the SLO from what users need. The requirement was to ensure the service can meet its SLOs, not to redefine them.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> Having developers keep supporting the service is a legitimate outcome in some engagements, since SRE does not take on every service. It is not the next step after a failed analysis, though. It sets aside the SLO gap instead of resolving it, and it does not make the service able to meet its SLOs in production.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement.</strong> Launching with no SLOs removes the measure of success instead of improving the service. It gives up the reliability target the organization defined and exposes users to an unreliable service with no objective to hold it to. Collecting data is useful, but data collection does not itself raise reliability.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option B, because it sounds like a sensible SRE decision. SRE teams do decline to take on services that are not ready, and developer-led support is a recognized fallback. The resolving distinction is that the stem asks what to do to ensure the service meets its SLOs, which calls for improving the service, not deciding who runs it. Option B would become the better answer if the stem said the development team could not make the needed improvements and SRE had to decide on support responsibility.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An SRE engagement is gated by readiness. A review analyzes a service against production standards, and the findings drive concrete improvements. Support is handed over only after those improvements make the service ready. SLOs are a fixed expression of required reliability during this process, and the service is adjusted to meet them. They are revisited through the normal SLO review cycle, not rewritten to fit a service that falls short.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>In practice, the SRE team writes up the findings from the analysis, such as missing monitoring, weak failure handling, or manual operational steps, as a prioritized list of improvements for the development team. The teams agree on which items must be finished before handover and which can follow afterward. Once those are done, the service is re-assessed and taken into production support. This costs development time up front but avoids launching a service that predictably exhausts its error budget and generates incidents and toil for the on-call team.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>When the service misses its SLOs, fix the service before handover; do not change the target, skip the target, or push the problem back.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>If a readiness review shows a service cannot meet its SLOs, the next step is to define and complete reliability improvements before SRE takes over. Reject options that weaken or remove the SLOs, or that sidestep the gap by shifting support responsibility.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/evolving-sre-engagement-model/\"><strong>Google SRE Book: Chapter 32, The Evolving SRE Engagement Model:</strong></a> Supports: the correct answer, covering PRR objectives, improvement before handover, and SRE assuming responsibility only after the service is deemed ready (Options A, B, and D).</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/gcp/how-sres-find-the-landmines-in-a-service-cre-life-lessons\">Google Cloud Blog: How SREs find the landmines in a service (CRE life lessons): </a>Supports: the SRE entrance review (PRR) identifying bug fixes, process changes, and needed service behavior before onboarding, and the four improvement axes of bugs, reliability, automation, and monitoring and alerting.</p>","lessons":["d3l8"]},"e1q30":{"id":"e1q30","exam":1,"n":30,"domain":5,"html":"<p>You need to run a business-critical workload on a fixed set of Compute Engine instances for the next several months. The workload is stable, and the instances are provisioned with exactly the resources it needs. You want to reduce the cost of running this workload without affecting its performance. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Buy committed use discounts for the instances.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Move the instances into a managed instance group.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Convert the instances to Spot VMs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Place the instances in an unmanaged instance group.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Buy committed use discounts for the instances.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Start with the two hard constraints: the cost must go down, and performance must not change. The workload is also described as business-critical, stable, and fixed in size, so interruption and resizing are both unwanted. Now test each option on one question: does it change the price, and does it leave the VMs running exactly as they are? Only a committed use discount changes the price while leaving the running instances untouched. The group options change how instances are organized, not what they cost, and Spot VMs lower the price by accepting interruption, which this workload cannot tolerate.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“lower the costs”</strong> (hard constraint): the chosen action must change pricing, not just management.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“without any performance implications”</strong> (hard constraint): the instances must keep running as they do today, with no interruption risk.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“business-critical”</strong> (exclusion): it rules out any capacity that Compute Engine can reclaim at any time.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“stable with the exact amount of resources allocated” and “fixed set of instances”</strong> (technical signal): steady, predictable usage is the exact profile that committed use discounts are designed to reward.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Committed use discounts (CUDs) trade a commitment for lower prices on Compute Engine usage. Resource-based commitments are described as ideal for predictable, steady-state usage, which matches this workload. Because the instances need exactly the resources already allocated, the commitment can be sized to match them. A commitment is a pricing arrangement applied to usage, so the instances themselves are unchanged and performance is unaffected. The discount comes from the billing side alone.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case.</strong> Managed instance groups provide autoscaling, autohealing, and automated updates for groups of identical VMs built from an instance template. That is valuable for scalable or self-healing workloads. It is tempting because MIGs are a recommended practice. It lowers no price, though, and migrating a fixed, stable fleet into a MIG adds change without a cost benefit.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement.</strong> Spot VMs offer large discounts, and they are the current successor to preemptible VMs, which makes this option attractive for a pure cost goal. Compute Engine can preempt Spot VMs at any time, and they are excluded from the Compute Engine SLA. That directly conflicts with a business-critical workload and the “no performance implications” requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case.</strong> An unmanaged instance group only collects VMs you manage yourself so they can be load balanced as a unit. It has no effect on pricing, so it does not address the goal.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option C, because Spot VMs are the best-known way to cut Compute Engine costs steeply. The resolving distinction is that the discount comes from accepting interruption, which this workload cannot afford. Spot VMs would become the right answer if the workload were fault-tolerant, such as a restartable batch job. One production caveat: CUD terms are 1 year or 3 years, with the longer term giving the bigger discount, so before buying you would confirm the workload will run for the commitment term. The exam treats the stable, fixed profile as the deciding signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud cost optimization separates two levers. Pricing models change what you pay for the same capacity, and architecture or operations choices change how capacity is organized and managed. Steady, predictable capacity suits commitment-based pricing. Interruptible capacity such as Spot VMs suits fault-tolerant work. Grouping constructs like managed and unmanaged instance groups address availability and operations, not price. Spot VMs are the current successor to preemptible VMs, so the same trade-off of low cost for reclaim risk applies.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team running a long-lived, always-on service on a fixed fleet reviews its steady usage, then purchases a resource-based commitment that matches the vCPU and memory it consistently uses in a region and project. The discount applies automatically to matching usage, the VMs keep running unchanged, and the monthly bill drops. The trade-off is the commitment itself, which is why it is sized to the stable baseline rather than to peak or uncertain demand. A commitment alone does not reserve capacity in a zone; reservations can be attached if guaranteed capacity is also needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Steady, predictable, must-not-fail capacity means commit for a discount; interruptible capacity means Spot VMs; groups manage instances but do not discount them.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks how to lower Compute Engine cost for a stable, always-on, business-critical workload without risking performance, choose committed use discounts. Reject Spot VMs for anything that cannot tolerate interruption, and reject instance-group options because they change management, not price.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/committed-use-discounts-overview\">Google Cloud: Committed use discounts overview (Compute Engine): </a>Supports: the correct answer, covering CUDs as discounted prices in return for 1-year or 3-year commitments, and commitments as a pricing agreement that does not by itself reserve capacity.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/spot\">Google Cloud: Spot VMs: </a>Supports: the elimination of Option C, covering Spot VMs as the latest version of preemptible VMs, preemptible at any time, and excluded from the Compute Engine SLA.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instance-groups\">Google Cloud: Instance groups (managed and unmanaged): </a>Supports: the elimination of Options B and D, covering MIGs as autoscaling, autohealing, and update tooling for identical VMs, and unmanaged groups as a way to load balance VMs you manage yourself.</p>","lessons":["d5l1"]},"e1q31":{"id":"e1q31","exam":1,"n":31,"domain":1,"html":"<p>You are designing a strategy for monitoring your production Google Cloud projects with Cloud Monitoring metrics scopes. One requirement is to identify and respond to production issues quickly, without false alerts coming from development and staging projects. You also want to follow the principle of least privilege when you give the relevant team members access to the monitoring setup. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Give the relevant team members read access to every production project. Set up a separate metrics scope inside each project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Assign the relevant team members the Project Viewer IAM role on every production project. Set up a separate metrics scope inside each project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Select an existing production project to host the metrics scope. Add the production projects to this metrics scope. Give the relevant team members read access to the metrics scope.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Create a new Google Cloud project for monitoring and host a metrics scope in it. Add the production projects to this metrics scope. Give the relevant team members read access to the metrics scope.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Create a new Google Cloud project for monitoring and host a metrics scope in it. Add the production projects to this metrics scope. Give the relevant team members read access to the metrics scope.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Separate the requirements by what they force. Two are must-haves. Monitoring must show only production, so a single metrics scope should contain only the production projects, which also keeps development and staging out of alerting. Access must follow least privilege, so team members should receive read-level monitoring access, not broad roles on production projects. A third consideration shapes the best choice: where the scope is hosted. The decision path is as follows. A single production-only scope removes the per-project options. Of the two single-scope options, the one hosted in a dedicated monitoring project keeps monitoring configuration and access separate from production workloads, so it is the best fit.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“without false alerts from development and staging projects”</strong> (hard constraint): the scope must list only production projects, because alerting policies query the projects in the metrics scope.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“quickly identify and react to production environment issues”</strong> (technical signal): this calls for one consolidated production view instead of checking each project separately.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“principle of least privilege”</strong> (hard constraint): grant only read-level monitoring access, at the place where monitoring is configured, not broad access to each production project.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“relevant team members”</strong> (technical signal): access should go to a defined group, scoped narrowly to what they need.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A metrics scope lets one project, called the scoping project, chart and monitor time-series data stored in other projects. The scoping project also stores the alerting policies, dashboards, and uptime checks you create. Adding only the production projects to the scope gives a single production view and ensures alerts evaluate only production data. Access control follows the scoping project. A team member with the Monitoring Viewer role on the scoping project can view the time-series data of every project in the scope without needing any other permissions. That means no role on any production project is required, which is least privilege in practice. Google also recommends using a new project, or one without resources, as the scoping project when monitoring multiple projects. A dedicated project stores no time-series data of its own, so no filtering is needed to separate it from monitored data.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement.</strong> Separate metrics scopes in each project leave production fragmented, so there is no single view for fast detection. Read access to every production project is also broader than monitoring needs, since the team only needs to see monitoring data.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement.</strong> It has the same fragmentation problem as Option A. The Project Viewer role is a broad basic role that grants read access across a project’s resources, which is far more than monitoring requires and contradicts least privilege.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> It does provide a single production-only view and grants read access to the scope, which makes it tempting. However, it hosts the scope in a project that runs production resources. That mixes monitoring configuration with workloads, and the host project’s own time-series data must be filtered when you want to separate it from monitored data. Google’s recommended practice is a new or resource-free scoping project.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option C, because it differs from the correct answer only in where the scoping project lives. The resolving distinction is that a dedicated project cleanly separates monitoring from production workloads. Option C would become acceptable if an organizational policy prevented creating additional projects, but even then it would be a compromise, not the best design.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A metrics scope is a read-time construct. Metric data always stays in the project where it originates, and the scope only defines which projects a scoping project can query. Every project hosts a metrics scope by default, and metrics scopes are what Workspaces became in Cloud Monitoring. A scope can be shared by many viewers, but anyone viewing through the scoping project sees data from all projects in it. A permission granted on a scoping project can therefore reach the metrics of every monitored project, so scope membership should be designed deliberately.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>An SRE organization creates a monitoring project, and an administrator adds the production projects to its metrics scope, which requires administrative rights on both the scoping project and each added project. Alerting policies and dashboards are created in that project, so they evaluate production data only. On-call engineers receive a read role on the monitoring project and see production health in one place, without gaining access to production workloads. Development teams can have their own separate scoping projects, which keeps the two audiences isolated from each other.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For multi-project monitoring, use one dedicated scoping project that contains only the projects you want to watch, and grant viewer access on that project alone.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks how to monitor several projects together under least privilege, choose a dedicated monitoring project whose metrics scope contains only the target projects, with read-only access granted on that project. Reject options that fragment the view across per-project scopes or hand out broad roles on the workload projects.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/settings\"><strong>Google Cloud: Metrics scopes overview (Cloud Monitoring):</strong></a> Supports: the correct answer, covering scoping and monitored projects, the Monitoring Viewer role on the scoping project as sufficient to view monitored data, and the recommendation to use a new or resource-free project as the scoping project (Options A, B, and C).</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/settings/multiple-projects\"><strong>Google Cloud: Configure a metrics scope (Cloud Monitoring):</strong></a> Supports: adding projects to a metrics scope, the administrative role required to modify it, and read roles on the scoping project letting principals view data from projects in the scope.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/operations/multi-project-cloud-monitoring-made-easier/\"><strong>Google Cloud Blog: Multi-project Cloud Monitoring made easier:</strong></a> Supports: the current terminology, covering Workspaces converted to metrics scopes and the scoping project concept.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud: Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 1.1, Designing the overall resource hierarchy):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment with multi-project monitoring and logging and IAM roles.</p>","lessons":["d1l5"]},"e1q32":{"id":"e1q32","exam":1,"n":32,"domain":1,"html":"<p>Your company is experiencing bugs, outages, and slowness in its production systems. Developers build new features and fix bugs directly in the production environment, and configuration changes and experiments are also carried out there, which causes outages for users. Testers run load tests in production as well, which frequently slows the production systems. You need to redesign the environment so that production has fewer bugs and outages and testers can load test new features. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Add an automated test script that runs in production to detect failures as soon as they occur.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Build a development environment with reduced server capacity and restrict access to developers and testers.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Lock down the production environment so developers cannot modify it, and set up a single controlled update each year.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Create a development environment for writing code and a test environment for configuration changes, experiments, and load testing.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Create a development environment for writing code and a test environment for configuration changes, experiments, and load testing.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>List the causes the stem gives, then match each to the change that removes it. Development work, configuration changes, and experiments in production cause the bugs and outages. Load testing in production causes the slowness. The redesign must also give testers a place to load test new features. The decision method is to move each risky activity out of production and into an environment suited to it. Only the option that provides separate non-production environments for coding and for configuration, experiments, and load testing satisfies all of these. Options that only detect problems, share one thin environment, or freeze production each fail at least one requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Developers build new features and fix bugs directly in the production environment” and “configuration changes and experiments are also carried out there”</strong> (technical signals): these activities are the root cause of the outages, so they must move out of production.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“testers run load tests in production” and “enable testers to load test new features”</strong> (hard constraint): testers need an environment of their own for load testing, so the solution cannot simply forbid changes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“reduce the number of bugs and outages in production”</strong> (hard constraint): the fix must prevent problems from reaching production, not merely detect them after the fact.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Separating environments isolates the blast radius of risky work. In a development environment, developers can write and break code without affecting users. In a test environment, configuration changes, experiments, and load tests run without competing with production traffic or slowing it down. Changes are promoted to production only after they have been exercised elsewhere, which cuts the bugs and outages that reach users. This also follows Google’s guidance on structuring environments, where development, testing, and production can carry different policies and access controls, with stricter controls in production.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> Automated tests or probes in production can detect failures quickly, and that has value. It is reactive, though, so users still experience every failure. It also leaves development, experiments, configuration changes, and load testing running in production, which are the causes of the problems.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> Moving development out of production helps, which makes this tempting. But a single environment with reduced capacity has to serve coding, configuration experiments, and load tests together, so load tests would disturb developers. A smaller environment also produces load results that do not represent production capacity. The option does not say where configuration work and experiments should happen either.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement.</strong> Locking down production stops direct changes, but it gives developers and testers nowhere to work and offers testers no way to load test new features. A single update per year also bundles a year of changes into one release, which concentrates risk instead of reducing it.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option B, because it also moves development out of production and restricts access. The resolving distinction is that development and testing have different needs. Coding needs a place that can be broken freely, while load testing needs a production-like environment and must not interfere with other work. Option B would be adequate only if the stem asked solely to move development out of production, with no need for load testing or for rehearsing configuration changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Environment separation is a basic delivery practice. Development is where code changes quickly, test or staging is where changes are validated under conditions resembling production, and production is changed in a controlled way. Each environment can have its own access rules and policies, with the strictest controls in production. In Google Cloud, environments are commonly isolated using separate projects, often grouped under environment folders, so that IAM and organization policies can differ by environment. A test environment is most useful when it mirrors production closely enough for its results to carry over.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team creates development, test (non-production), and production projects, grouped under environment folders in the organization. Developers have broad rights in development, narrower rights in test, and no direct changes in production. Changes reach production through a pipeline after passing tests, and load tests run in a test environment that resembles production. Outages from experiments and slowdowns from load tests disappear from production. The trade-off is the cost and upkeep of the extra environments, which can be reduced by creating test environments only when needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Build in development, break things safely in test, and let only validated changes reach production.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question describes development, experiments, or load tests disrupting production, the fix is to separate environments by purpose and keep production for validated changes. Reject options that only detect failures in production, squeeze all non-production work into one shared environment, or freeze production with no place to test.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/landing-zones/decide-resource-hierarchy\"><strong>Google Cloud: Decide a resource hierarchy for your Google Cloud landing zone:</strong></a> Supports: the correct answer, covering hierarchies based on application environments such as development, production, and testing, with separate and stricter policies and access for production.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/creating-managing-folders\"><strong>Google Cloud: Create and manage folders:</strong></a> Supports: using folders to group projects by environment, such as development, production, and test, and to apply environment-specific policies.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/blueprints/security-foundations/organization-structure\"><strong>Google Cloud: Enterprise foundations blueprint, organization structure:</strong></a> Supports: the foundation concept and Option B’s weakness, covering separate production, nonproduction, and development environments, with nonproduction holding a copy of production for testing workloads before promotion.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud: Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 1.4, Managing multiple environments):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment with managing multiple environments such as staging and production, including ephemeral environments and configuration and policy management.</p>","lessons":["d1l9"]},"e1q33":{"id":"e1q33","exam":1,"n":33,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You support an application running on App Engine that is used worldwide and accessed from many different types of devices. You want to measure the number of connections, and you are using Cloud Monitoring for App Engine. Which metric should you use?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. flex/connections/current</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. tcp_ssl_proxy/new_connections</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. tcp_ssl_proxy/open_connections</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. flex/instance/connections/current</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. flex/connections/current</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Work through three filters. First, the metric must come from App Engine, because the stem says you are using Cloud Monitoring for App Engine. Second, it must report the number of connections as a current count, not a rate of connections being created. Third, the stem describes the application as a whole, so the metric should describe the application and not a single instance. The first filter removes the load balancer metrics, and the third separates the two remaining App Engine candidates.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Cloud Monitoring for App Engine”</strong> (hard constraint): the metric must be an App Engine metric, which excludes metrics owned by other services such as Cloud Load Balancing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“the number of connections”</strong> (technical signal): this calls for a current connection count (a gauge), not a count of newly created connections.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“an application ... used globally and accessed from various device types”</strong> (technical signal): the scope is the application overall, not one instance, so a version-level metric fits better than a per-instance metric.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>The metric appengine.googleapis.com/flex/connections/current reports the number of currently active connections per App Engine flexible environment version. It is recorded against the application-level monitored resource (gae_app), so it gives one figure for the application across its instances. That matches a question about how many connections the application is handling. The metric is a gauge, so it reflects connections open now, which is what “the number of connections” asks for.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case.</strong> tcp_ssl_proxy/new_connections is a Cloud Load Balancing metric for TCP and SSL proxy load balancers, and it counts connections created. It therefore belongs to a different service and measures creations, not the current number of connections.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case.</strong> tcp_ssl_proxy/open_connections reports the current number of outstanding connections through a TCP or SSL proxy load balancer. It is the right kind of measure, so it is tempting, but it describes the load balancer, not App Engine, and the question asks about App Engine metrics.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> flex/instance/connections/current is an App Engine metric for current active connections, but it is reported per instance. Getting an application-level number from it means aggregating across instances. It fits only when you need to see how connections are spread across individual instances.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option D, because its name differs from the correct answer only by the word “instance,” and both metrics appear with the display name “Connections” in the metric reference. The resolving distinction is scope: the metric without “instance” is version-level, and the one with it is instance-level. Option D would become correct if the question asked which instance is carrying the most connections or how load is distributed across instances.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A Google Cloud metric type has the form of a service domain followed by a metric path, and the service domain tells you which product emits the metric. Choosing the right metric means matching three things: the owning service, the kind of measurement, and the level it is recorded at. A gauge describes the current state, whereas a count of new events is a different measurement. A metric recorded at the application or version level is a different series from one recorded per instance, even when the names are similar. The flex/ prefix in the metric path marks metrics of App Engine’s flexible environment.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>In Metrics Explorer, you pick the App Engine application resource and the connections metric, then group or filter by version to chart connection counts over time. You can place that chart on a dashboard or base an alerting policy on sustained high values. If the aggregate looks unhealthy, you then switch to the instance-level metric to see whether load is concentrated on specific instances. Starting from the aggregate keeps the first view simple and uses the finer-grained metric only for drill-down.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Match the service, then the measure, then the scope: App Engine, current count, whole application.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When asked which monitoring metric to use, first match the metric’s service domain to the product in the stem, then check whether it is a current count or a rate, and finally match its scope to the question. Reject load balancer metrics for App Engine questions, and prefer the version-level metric over its per-instance twin when the question concerns the application as a whole.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/api/metrics_gcp_a_b\"><strong>Google Cloud: Google Cloud metrics, A or B (Cloud Monitoring):</strong></a> Supports: the correct answer and Option D, covering the App Engine metric types, including the version-level and instance-level flexible environment connection metrics.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/api/metrics_gcp_i_o\"><strong>Google Cloud: Google Cloud metrics, I to O (Cloud Monitoring):</strong></a> Supports: the elimination of Options B and C, covering Cloud Load Balancing, including its TCP and SSL proxy metrics, as metrics owned by a different service domain.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/api/metrics_gcp\"><strong>Google Cloud: Google Cloud metrics overview (Cloud Monitoring):</strong></a> Supports: the foundation concept, covering the metric type format and the service domain identifying which product emits a metric.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud: Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 4.3, Managing metrics, dashboards, and alerts):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment with collecting metrics from platforms and analyzing metrics using the Metrics Explorer.</p>","lessons":["d4l6"]},"e1q34":{"id":"e1q34","exam":1,"n":34,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You support a multi-region web service that runs on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) behind a global external Application Load Balancer. For legacy reasons, user requests first pass through a third-party Content Delivery Network (CDN), which then forwards traffic to the load balancer. You have already implemented an availability Service Level Indicator (SLI) at the load balancer. You now want broader coverage so that a load balancer misconfiguration, a CDN failure, or another global networking catastrophe would also be detected. Where should you measure this new SLI? (Choose two.)</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. In your application servers’ logs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Through instrumentation built directly into the client.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. In metrics exported from your application servers.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Through GKE health checks on your application servers.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>E. Using a synthetic client that periodically sends simulated user requests.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Through instrumentation built directly into the client.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>E. Using a synthetic client that periodically sends simulated user requests.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The decisive constraint is where the new SLI sits in the request path. The failure modes named in the question are a CDN failure, a load balancer misconfiguration, and a global networking catastrophe. All of them happen before a request reaches the application tier, and the existing SLI at the load balancer cannot see the CDN hop at all. Any new measurement point at or behind the load balancer repeats the same blind spot. Only a vantage point outside Google Cloud, at the user’s edge of the path, can observe the whole chain. That leaves two candidates: real client-side telemetry, and a synthetic client that sends probe requests along the same public path. They are complementary rather than redundant. One reports what real users experience, and the other probes on a schedule even when real traffic is low.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“load balancer misconfiguration, CDN failure, or other global networking catastrophe” is a hard constraint. It names failures upstream of the application tier, so the measurement point has to sit upstream too.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“requests first pass through a third-party CDN” is a technical signal. It shows the CDN hop lies outside your infrastructure and outside the current load balancer SLI, so only an external vantage point can observe it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“already implemented... at the load balancer” together with “increase coverage” is an exclusion signal. A new SLI measured at or behind that point adds no coverage for the named failure modes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Choose two” is a cardinality instruction. It points to two complementary external vantage points, not two variations of a downstream signal.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Client-side instrumentation records the outcome of each request from the user’s own device. If the CDN is down or the load balancer is misconfigured, the client records the failure directly, even though no server ever saw the request. A synthetic client sends simulated user requests on a schedule across the full public path (CDN, then load balancer, then GKE). It detects an outage even during quiet traffic periods and gives a consistent, controlled signal that does not depend on real users encountering the problem first. Used together, they give a real-user view and a proactive view of the same end-to-end path, which together cover the failure modes that no server-side signal can reveal.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Application server logs only record requests that actually arrived. In a CDN failure or load balancer misconfiguration, requests never reach the servers, so the logs stay quiet during exactly the outages in question. It is tempting because logs are detailed, but the detail is irrelevant when the failure happens upstream.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Metrics exported from application servers share the same blind spot. They reflect only traffic that reached the application, so they would look healthy during a CDN or load balancer failure. They are tempting as a richer signal than logs, but they sit behind the failure points.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> GKE health checks assess whether pods and nodes are healthy inside the cluster. They have no visibility into the third-party CDN or the load balancer’s configuration, so a global networking failure upstream would not change their results. They are tempting because they sound like availability measurement, but they measure the cluster, not the user’s path.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming that any additional availability signal increases coverage. Options A, C, and D are all real, useful signals, but each sits at or behind the same point in the request path as the load balancer SLI, so none extends visibility upstream. The test is whether the measurement point lies upstream of the failure modes named in the stem, not whether the signal is a different type. If the question had asked about detecting application-level errors for requests that did reach the servers, the server-side options would become strong candidates.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An SLI is only as trustworthy as its vantage point. A signal measured downstream of a failure point cannot detect failures upstream of it, however detailed it is. SRE practice therefore recommends measuring as close to the user’s experience as practical. For paths that include infrastructure you do not control, such as a third-party CDN, this means measuring from outside your own infrastructure through real client telemetry and external synthetic probing.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team that puts a third-party CDN in front of its own load balancer can pair client-side reporting of request outcomes with external synthetic checks that traverse the full public path. During a CDN outage, server dashboards, logs, and cluster health checks all stay green, while the client telemetry and the synthetic probes flag the failure within minutes. This lets the team declare the incident and engage the CDN vendor sooner, and it keeps the reported availability SLI honest.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>To catch failures upstream of your infrastructure, measure from upstream of your infrastructure: real client telemetry plus external synthetic probes.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks how to extend SLI coverage to failures in a CDN, a load balancer, or global networking, look for measurement points outside your own infrastructure, meaning client-side instrumentation and synthetic probing. Eliminate server logs, server metrics, and cluster health checks, because they share the blind spot of the SLI you already have.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — Implementing SLOs:</strong></a> Supports: the principle that SLIs should be measured as close to the user’s experience as practical, and the trade-offs of each measurement point (server-side logs and metrics, load balancer, client-side instrumentation, synthetic probing)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/uptime-checks\"><strong>Google Cloud — Uptime checks:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of using an external synthetic client to probe the full public request path</p>","lessons":["d3l1"]},"e1q35":{"id":"e1q35","exam":1,"n":35,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your application images are built with Cloud Build and pushed to Artifact Registry. You want to be able to choose a specific version of your application to deploy, based on the release version tagged in source control. What should you do when you push the image?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Record the image digest in the source control tag.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Pass the source control tag as a parameter in the image name.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Have Cloud Build embed the release version tag in the application image.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Use Artifact Registry digest versioning to match the image to the source control tag.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Pass the source control tag as a parameter in the image name.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Start from the deployment side. To deploy “a particular version,” the deployment must be able to name that version in the image reference it uses. The version comes from the source control tag, so that tag has to become part of the image reference at the moment the image is pushed. The push step is where the reference is set, which makes it the only place the link can be created. Test each option against one question: after the push, can I select the image by the Git release tag alone? Only an image published under that tag in its name can be selected that way.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“specify a particular version of your application for deployment”</strong> (hard constraint): the image must be addressable by a version identifier at deployment time.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“based on the release version tagged in source control”</strong> (hard constraint): the identifier must come from the Git tag, so that value has to appear in the image reference.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“when you push the image”</strong> (sequence instruction): the action must happen at push time, which is when the image’s name and tag are assigned.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A container image is referenced by name plus either a tag or a digest. Image tags are the common human-readable way to refer to different revisions, and Google’s guidance describes tagging images with a version identifier at build time as a common approach. Cloud Build supports this directly. For builds invoked by a trigger, the default substitution $TAG_NAME holds the name of the Git tag, and you can use it in the image name in the build config, so the pushed image carries the release version. For a manual build, you can supply the same value through the substitutions flag. Deployment can then reference the image by that tag, and redeploying an earlier release means pointing back to its tag.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best</strong>. This runs in the wrong direction. A digest is computed by the registry from the image content, so it is only known after the push, and writing it back into source control adds a post-build step. It also sets nothing on the pushed image, so you would have to look up the digest from the Git tag before every deployment instead of simply deploying the tagged image.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> Embedding the version inside the image, for example as a label or a file, helps identify what is running. Deployment still resolves an image through its registry reference, so embedded content does not let you select a version. The image must still be pushed under a tag for that to work.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Incorrect.</strong> A digest is a content-derived hash, not a versioning scheme you can configure to match a Git tag. There is no digest versioning feature that maps an image to a source control tag.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractors are the digest-based options, because digests are immutable and tags are mutable, so deploying by digest is often the better practice for guaranteeing exact content. The resolving distinction is that this question asks how to select a version by its Git release tag, and a digest cannot carry or be matched to that tag. The answer would shift if the requirement were to guarantee the exact bytes deployed even if a tag were later reassigned. In that case you would deploy by digest, ideally in addition to tagging.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An image has three identifiers. The name locates the repository, a tag is a mutable human-readable pointer to a revision, and the digest is an immutable hash of the content. Tagging at build time with the source control version ties an artifact to the code that produced it, which supports traceability and straightforward rollbacks. Tags can be moved to new images, so strict reproducibility requires resolving to a digest. Google Container Registry has been superseded by Artifact Registry, which also hosts images for the gcr.io domain through gcr.io repositories.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team creates a release tag such as v1.4.0 in Git, which fires a Cloud Build trigger on tags. The build config builds the image and pushes it to an Artifact Registry repository under a name ending in the tag value, using the $TAG_NAME substitution. Deployment manifests or a delivery pipeline then reference that exact tag, so promoting or rolling back is a matter of choosing a release tag. The trade-off is that tags can be reassigned, so teams that need stronger guarantees also record or deploy the resulting digest.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Push the image under the release tag in its name, because tags select versions and digests only identify content.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks how to deploy a specific version identified by a source control tag, tag the image with that release version in its name at push time. Reject digest-based options as the way to match a Git tag, and reject options that bury the version inside the image, since neither makes the version selectable by reference.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/configuring-builds/substitute-variable-values\">Google Cloud: Substituting variable values (Cloud Build): </a>Supports: the correct answer, covering the default $TAG_NAME substitution holding the Git tag name for triggered builds, and using substitutions in image names pushed to Artifact Registry.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/using-container-images\"><strong>Google Cloud: About container image digests:</strong></a> Supports: the option distinctions, covering tagging images with a version identifier at build time, tags as mutable references, and digests as immutable content hashes (Options A and D).</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/transition/transition-from-gcr\"><strong>Google Cloud: Transition from Container Registry (Artifact Registry):</strong></a> Supports: the current terminology, covering Artifact Registry as the recommended successor to Container Registry and its support for the gcr.io domain through gcr.io repositories.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud: Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 2.1, Designing pipelines):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment with CI/CD of applications and artifact management with Artifact Registry.</p>","lessons":["d2l1","d2l5"]},"e1q36":{"id":"e1q36","exam":1,"n":36,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You support an application running on Compute Engine that uses a Cloud SQL instance to store and retrieve data. Following an update to the application, users report errors containing database timeout messages. The number of concurrent active users has not changed. You need to determine the MOST probable cause of the database timeouts. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Examine the serial port logs of the Compute Engine instance.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use Cloud Profiler to visualize resource utilization across the application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Check whether the number of connections to the Cloud SQL instance has increased.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Use Web Security Scanner to determine whether the Cloud SQL instance is under a Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attack.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Check whether the number of connections to the Cloud SQL instance has increased.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Build the diagnosis from the evidence. The symptom is database timeouts, the trigger is an application update, and the user load is unchanged. With load constant, the most probable cause is a change in how the application uses the database. The next question is which database resource an application consumes per request and can exhaust. That resource is connections. So check the signal closest to the symptom, the connection count on the Cloud SQL instance. The other options inspect different layers, such as the VM’s operating system, code-level CPU and memory, or web vulnerabilities, and none of them speaks to the database boundary where the timeouts occur.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Following an update to the application”</strong> (technical signal): the problem began with a change, which points to the application’s new behavior as the trigger.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“The number of concurrent active users has not changed”</strong> (exclusion): this rules out load growth and also makes an external attack an unlikely explanation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“database timeout messages”</strong> (technical signal): the failure sits on the path between the application and the database, which makes connection availability a prime suspect.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“MOST probable cause”</strong> (decision word): choose the check best supported by the evidence, not merely a possible one.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Every database connection consumes client-side and server-side resources, and Cloud SQL imposes overall connection limits that cannot be exceeded. An application update can change connection behavior, for example by opening a new connection per request, leaking connections, or enlarging connection pools across instances. That can push the instance toward its limit even though the number of users is the same. Requests then wait for a connection or fail, which users see as timeouts. Comparing the instance’s connection count before and after the update directly confirms or rules out this cause. Google’s guidance is that creating and using fewer connections reduces overhead and helps you stay under the limit.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case.</strong> Serial port output captures operating system, BIOS, and other system-level messages and is useful for crashes, failed boots, and startup or shutdown problems. Here the VM is running and serving users, and the error is at the database layer, so these logs are unlikely to explain the timeouts.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> Cloud Profiler continuously gathers CPU usage and memory-allocation information and attributes it to code. It could expose an inefficient code path, which makes it tempting after an update. It does not show how many connections the application holds against Cloud SQL, so it is less likely to reveal the cause of connection-related timeouts.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Incorrect.</strong> Web Security Scanner scans web applications for vulnerabilities such as cross-site scripting and outdated libraries, so it cannot tell you whether a DDoS attack is underway. A stable user count and onset right after an application update also point away from an attack.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option B, because profiling tools sound like the natural answer to a performance problem after a deployment. The resolving distinction is where each tool looks. A profiler measures resource use inside the application process, whereas the symptom appears at the database boundary, where connection exhaustion cannot be seen from a CPU or memory profile. Option B would become the better first step if the symptom were high CPU on the application VM or slow responses without any database error.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Effective troubleshooting correlates a change with a symptom and then inspects the metric nearest to that symptom. A database connection limit is a finite shared resource, and many concurrent application instances multiply whatever each one holds. A constant user count does not mean constant connection usage, since connection handling is application behavior and can change independently of load. Each diagnostic tool answers a different question: profilers cover code-level CPU and memory, serial output covers VM system behavior, vulnerability scanners cover security flaws, and database connection metrics cover the application-to-database path.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>In practice, you open the Cloud SQL instance’s monitoring and compare connection counts before and after the release. If counts climbed, you review how the new code obtains and releases connections and how large each pool is relative to the number of application instances. Typical fixes include closing connections reliably, right-sizing pools, and using connection pooling. A rollback is the fastest mitigation if impact is severe. The cost of skipping this check is chasing code or infrastructure suspects while users keep hitting the limit.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>When the load stays flat but database timeouts appear after a release, check connections first.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question describes database timeouts that begin after an application change with unchanged user load, look for what the change did to the application’s database usage, and connection count is the classic answer. Match each tool to its layer, and reject profilers, VM boot logs, and security scanners for a connection-level symptom.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/mysql/manage-connections\"><strong>Google Cloud: Manage database connections (Cloud SQL):</strong></a> Supports: the correct answer, covering the resources each connection consumes, Cloud SQL’s connection limits that cannot be exceeded, and using fewer connections to stay under the limit.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/about-profiler\"><strong>Google Cloud: Cloud Profiler overview:</strong></a> Supports: the elimination of Option B, covering Cloud Profiler as a low-overhead profiler for CPU usage and memory allocation in production applications.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/concepts-web-security-scanner-overview\"><strong>Google Cloud: Overview of Web Security Scanner (Security Command Center):</strong></a> Supports: the elimination of Option D, covering Web Security Scanner as a scanner for web application vulnerabilities such as cross-site scripting and outdated libraries.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/viewing-serial-port-output\"><strong>Google Cloud: Viewing serial port output (Compute Engine):</strong></a> Supports: the elimination of Option A, covering serial port output as system-level output for troubleshooting crashes, failed boots, and startup or shutdown issues.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\">Google Cloud: Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 4.5, Troubleshooting issues): </a>Supports: domain alignment with troubleshooting application, infrastructure, and performance and latency issues.</p>","lessons":["d4l10","d4l12"]},"e1q37":{"id":"e1q37","exam":1,"n":37,"domain":3,"html":"<p>Your application runs across three Google Cloud zones, and its users are split among them. You can fail over from one zone to another, but doing so interrupts service for 10 minutes for the affected users. A database failure occurs about once every quarter, and your monitoring detects it within five minutes. You are documenting the reliability risks of a planned real-time messaging feature. For each risk, you record Mean Time to Detect (MTTD) in minutes, Mean Time to Repair (MTTR) in minutes, Mean Time Between Failures (MTBF) in days, and the percentage of users impacted. The messaging feature relies on a new database system whose zonal failover takes twice as long to complete successfully. You need to capture the risk of this new database failing in a single zone. Which set of values should you record for the new database failover risk?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. MTTD: 5 minutes, MTTR: 10 minutes, MTBF: 90 days, User impact: 33%</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. MTTD: 5 minutes, MTTR: 20 minutes, MTBF: 90 days, User impact: 33%</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. MTTD: 5 minutes, MTTR: 10 minutes, MTBF: 90 days, User impact: 50%</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. MTTD: 5 minutes, MTTR: 20 minutes, MTBF: 90 days, User impact: 50%</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. MTTD: 5 minutes, MTTR: 20 minutes, MTBF: 90 days, User impact: 33%</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Start by separating what the scenario states from what it changes. The detection time, failure frequency, and zone layout are stated as facts about the existing setup. The only thing the new system changes is how long failover takes. Test each of the four fields against that change. Detection time depends on monitoring, not on failover, so it stays at 5. Failure frequency has no stated change, so the quarterly cadence carries over. Repair time is the failover duration, so it doubles. User impact depends on how many users sit in the failing zone, which the zone layout fixes. Only the repair time and the layout-derived impact need calculating, and the result leaves one option.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Twice as long to successfully fail over”</strong> is a technical signal. It scales only the repair component, taking MTTR from 10 to 20 minutes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Once per quarter”</strong> is a technical signal. A quarter is about 90 days, so MTBF is 90.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Detect it within five minutes”</strong> is a hard constraint. MTTD stays at 5 because nothing about the new database changes detection.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Three zones” with “users divided between the zones”</strong> is a hard constraint. One zone failing affects one of three equal groups, about 33%.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Failing in one zone”</strong> is a scoping instruction. It limits the impact to a single zone’s share of users, not all users.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>In a reliability risk catalog, each risk is described by how long it takes to detect, how long it takes to repair, how often it occurs, and what share of users it affects. For a database failure handled by zonal failover, repair is the failover itself. The current failover takes 10 minutes, so a system that takes twice as long gives an MTTR of 20 minutes. Detection is unchanged at 5 minutes, and failures are assumed to keep their quarterly cadence of roughly 90 days. Users are split across three zones, so a single-zone failure affects about one third of them, or 33%. Option B is the only one with all four values consistent with this reasoning.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Incorrect:</strong> It keeps MTTR at 10 minutes, which is the old system’s failover time. It is tempting because the other three values are right, but it ignores the stated doubling of failover time.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Incorrect:</strong> It makes two errors. It leaves MTTR at 10 minutes and sets impact to 50%. The 50% figure fits a two-zone split, not the three zones in the scenario, which makes it a tempting but wrong calculation.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> It gets MTTR right at 20 minutes but sets impact to 50%. With users divided across three zones, one zone’s failure affects about 33% of them. The 50% figure ignores the stated zone count.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is option D, because it gets the doubled repair time right and fails only on the impact percentage. The distinction is that impact is a function of how many zones users are divided across, not of how severe the outage is. The answer would switch to D only if the product were deployed across two zones instead of three. A second trap is assuming that a slower failover also changes detection time or failure frequency, which it does not.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A reliability risk can be scored by how long it takes to detect, how long it takes to repair, how often it recurs, and what share of users it hurts. Together, these determine the expected user-facing unavailability the risk contributes. For a failure with a fixed recovery procedure, the recovery procedure’s duration sets repair time, and the failure’s blast radius sets impact. This model applies when failures are independent and users are evenly distributed. If users were unevenly distributed across zones, the impact would be the affected zone’s actual share, not a simple equal split.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Suppose your team adds a database with a slower failover. Recording MTTR as 20 minutes instead of 10 doubles the expected downtime each time that failure occurs. Over a quarterly failure cycle, that can push the feature’s expected unavailability past its availability target. This lets you decide, before launch, whether to invest in faster failover, add zones to shrink per-zone impact, or negotiate a looser objective for the feature.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Detection and frequency come from the existing setup, repair time comes from the failover duration, and impact is one divided by the number of zones.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a risk-catalog question changes one property, change only the field it drives and leave the rest alone. For zonal failover, repair time equals failover duration and impact equals one over the number of zones.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/\">Google SRE Workbook — Implementing SLOs (risk catalog with time to detect, time to repair, time between failures, and impact): </a>Supports: how detection time, repair time, failure frequency, and user impact are combined to evaluate reliability risks, which is the basis for the correct values and for rejecting the other options.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/embracing-risk\"><strong>Google SRE Book — Embracing Risk:</strong></a> Supports: the underlying principle that reliability risk is weighed by the expected impact and duration of failures against availability targets.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 3, Applying site reliability engineering practices):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment with balancing change, velocity, and reliability, and with mitigating incident impact on users.</p>","lessons":["d3l7"]},"e1q38":{"id":"e1q38","exam":1,"n":38,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You support an e-commerce application that runs on a large Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster deployed both on-premises and on Google Cloud. The application consists of microservices running in containers. You want to identify the containers that consume the most CPU and memory. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use Cloud Monitoring’s Kubernetes Engine monitoring.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Use Prometheus to gather and aggregate per-container logs, then analyze the results in Grafana.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use the Cloud Monitoring API to create custom metrics, then organize the containers into groups.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Use Cloud Logging to export application logs to BigQuery, aggregate the logs per container, and then analyze CPU and memory consumption.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Use Cloud Monitoring’s Kubernetes Engine monitoring.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Separate what the task needs from how it could be built. The task needs per-container CPU and memory consumption, and it must work across both on-premises and Google Cloud deployments. CPU and memory consumption are numeric measurements over time, so they are metrics, not log content. The decision path is therefore to look for a solution that already produces container-level resource metrics for Kubernetes in both locations. If one exists as a built-in capability, it beats any option that requires building a collection pipeline. The options built on logs or custom instrumentation each require extra work to approximate what built-in Kubernetes monitoring already provides.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“identify the containers that consume the most CPU and memory”</strong> (hard constraint): the solution must expose per-container resource utilization that can be compared across containers.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“deployed both on-premises and on Google Cloud”</strong> (technical signal): the monitoring must cover both locations, so a solution that works across them with little extra setup fits best.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“CPU and memory”</strong> (technical signal): these are resource metrics, which makes log-based approaches indirect.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>By default, GKE clusters send system metrics to Cloud Monitoring, and those system metrics describe low-level resources such as CPU, memory, and storage for containers, pods, and nodes. Google Distributed Cloud clusters, the on-premises counterpart, are also configured by default to monitor system and container metrics in Cloud Monitoring. Its predefined dashboards chart container CPU usage and memory usage per container. That gives one place to rank containers by consumption across both environments, with no pipeline to build. Google also does not charge for ingesting GKE system metrics, which lowers the cost of this approach.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Incorrect.</strong> Prometheus is a systems monitoring and alerting toolkit that collects and stores metrics as time series, not logs. The option misdescribes what Prometheus does. Even if reworded to collect metrics, you would be deploying and operating your own Prometheus and Grafana stack across both environments, when built-in monitoring already provides the metrics.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> Custom metrics are meant for measurements Cloud Monitoring does not already collect, and container CPU and memory are already available as system metrics. Groups help organize resources for viewing, but they do not rank containers by consumption, so this adds work without adding capability.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> Application logs record events and messages, not measurements of resource usage. CPU and memory consumption would appear in them only if the application logged those figures itself. Exporting logs to BigQuery and aggregating them per container would be a heavy, indirect substitute for metrics that already exist.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option B, because Prometheus with Grafana is the well-known Kubernetes monitoring stack and it feels like the natural answer. The resolving distinction is that the option says Prometheus collects logs, which it does not, and that it requires operating a separate stack to get data Cloud Monitoring already provides. Option B would become reasonable if the requirement were to use open-source tooling or to collect application-specific custom metrics, and Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus can feed such metrics into Cloud Monitoring.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Observability data comes in three kinds: metrics, logs, and traces. Resource consumption is a metric, meaning a numeric measurement recorded over time, whereas logs record discrete events. The preferred approach is to use built-in managed telemetry first and add custom collection only for gaps. Terminology has also changed. The Stackdriver brand was replaced by Google Cloud’s operations suite, and its monitoring product is now Cloud Monitoring, with GKE integration documented as observability for GKE. Cloud Monitoring can show Kubernetes metrics from both GKE and on-premises clusters in one place.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>An SRE team opens the Kubernetes dashboards in Cloud Monitoring, charts per-container CPU and memory usage, and sorts or groups by container, namespace, or cluster to find the heaviest consumers. They then use those findings to right-size container requests and limits and to set alerts on utilization approaching limits. The built-in system metrics add no ingestion charge on GKE, while additional collection, such as application or Prometheus metrics, is charged by samples. This avoids the operational burden of running and scaling a separate monitoring stack in two environments.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Resource usage is a metric, so use the built-in Kubernetes metrics in Cloud Monitoring before building logs or custom pipelines.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When asked how to find which containers use the most CPU and memory in GKE, including hybrid deployments, use Cloud Monitoring’s built-in Kubernetes metrics. Reject logs-based answers, since logs are events and not resource measurements, and reject custom-metric or self-managed stack answers when the built-in metrics already cover the need.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/observability\"><strong>Google Cloud: Observability for GKE:</strong></a> Supports: the correct answer, covering GKE sending system metrics to Cloud Monitoring and logs to Cloud Logging by default, and Managed Service for Prometheus as an option for user-defined metrics (Options B and C).</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/kubernetes-engine/installing\">Google Cloud: Configure metrics collection (GKE): </a>Supports: GKE system metrics covering low-level resources such as CPU, memory, and storage, being sent to Cloud Monitoring, and no charge for their ingestion.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/anthos/clusters/docs/on-prem/1.15/reference/predefined-dashboards\"><strong>Google Cloud: Use predefined dashboards (Google Distributed Cloud clusters):</strong></a> Supports: the on-premises coverage, covering predefined dashboards that chart container CPU usage and memory usage per container for Google Distributed Cloud clusters in Cloud Monitoring.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://prometheus.io/docs/introduction/overview/\">Prometheus: Overview: </a>Supports: the elimination of Option B, covering Prometheus as a monitoring and alerting toolkit that collects and stores metrics as time series data.</p>","lessons":["d4l6"]},"e1q39":{"id":"e1q39","exam":1,"n":39,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You manage production deployments to a set of Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) clusters. You want to ensure that only images successfully built by your trusted CI/CD pipeline can be deployed to production. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Turn on Web Security Scanner for the clusters.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Turn on Artifact Analysis vulnerability scanning in Artifact Registry.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Configure the GKE clusters as private clusters.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Configure the GKE clusters with Binary Authorization.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Configure the GKE clusters with Binary Authorization.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Start from what the requirement demands. It has two parts: the control must establish where an image came from, specifically that your trusted pipeline built it successfully, and it must enforce that fact at deployment by blocking anything that cannot show it. A control that only detects problems, or that only restricts network access, does not tie an image to its build origin or block a deployment. The decision method is to ask of each option whether it records provenance at build time and checks it at deploy time. Only one of the four does both.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“only images successfully built by your trusted CI/CD pipeline”</strong> (hard constraint): the control must verify an image’s build origin, not just its contents.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“can be deployed to production”</strong> (hard constraint): enforcement must happen at deployment, which means blocking, not reporting.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“a set of Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) clusters”</strong> (technical signal): the control must apply on the cluster deployment path, where images are admitted.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Binary Authorization is a deploy-time security control that ensures only trusted container images are deployed on GKE. It works by enforcing a policy, and one common policy requires signed attestations. An attestor signs an image after your pipeline builds it, and at deploy time the admission check verifies the attestation, so unsigned images are blocked. Cloud Build integrates directly. When a build produces images, Cloud Build automatically creates the built-by-cloud-build attestor in your project and, once the images are successfully built, signs and creates attestations for them. A production policy that requires that attestation therefore admits only images your pipeline built successfully. That matches the requirement precisely.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case.</strong> Web Security Scanner crawls deployed web applications to identify vulnerabilities such as cross-site scripting and outdated libraries. It evaluates running applications, not the origin of the images, and it cannot stop an image from being deployed.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> Artifact Analysis scans images in Artifact Registry for known vulnerabilities and can feed results into a delivery process, so it is tempting as an image trust control. Scanning alone does not show that your pipeline built the image, and it does not block a deployment. It becomes useful here only as an input to an attestation policy.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case.</strong> Private clusters use nodes with only internal IP addresses, isolating them from the public internet and limiting control plane access. That is a network isolation control. It says nothing about which images are admitted to run on the nodes.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option B, because vulnerability scanning sounds like image trust. The resolving distinction is that scanning finds known vulnerabilities in an image’s contents, whereas the requirement concerns provenance and enforcement. Option B would become part of the answer if the requirement were to block images with serious vulnerabilities. In that case a scan result would be turned into an attestation, and Binary Authorization would require it at deploy time.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Software supply chain security rests on two steps: produce evidence about an artifact where it is built, and verify that evidence where it is deployed. Evidence takes the form of a signed attestation tied to a specific image, and verification is an admission check that allows or denies the workload. Detection tools and network controls complement this but do not replace it. Policies can also be introduced gradually, for example by first auditing violations before enforcing, and they typically include a break-glass path for emergencies.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team builds images in Cloud Build and pushes them to Artifact Registry, and each successful build is automatically attested by the built-by-cloud-build attestor. They then enable Binary Authorization on the production GKE clusters with a policy requiring that attestation, usually trialing it in an audit mode first to see which deployments would have been blocked. An image pushed by hand or built outside the pipeline is rejected at admission. The result is a verifiable chain from source to production, at the cost of maintaining the policy and attestors and planning for emergency exceptions.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Sign at build and verify at deploy: Binary Authorization admits only images with a trusted attestation.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks to ensure that only images from a trusted pipeline run in GKE, choose Binary Authorization with attestations. Reject vulnerability scanners and network isolation, since scanners report on contents and private clusters restrict network access, and neither enforces build provenance at deployment.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://codelabs.developers.google.com/gating-deployments-with-binary-auth\"><strong>Google Cloud: Gating Deployments with Binary Authorization (codelab):</strong></a> Supports: the correct answer, covering policies requiring signed attestations at deploy time, and Cloud Build automatically creating the built-by-cloud-build attestor and signing attestations for successfully built images.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/analysis\"><strong>Google Cloud: Artifact Analysis and vulnerability scanning:</strong></a> Supports: the elimination of Option B and the current terminology, covering Artifact Analysis (formerly Container Analysis) scanning images automatically when they are pushed to Artifact Registry.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/private-cluster-concept\"><strong>Google Cloud: About private clusters (GKE):</strong></a> Supports: the elimination of Option C, covering private clusters as nodes with only internal IP addresses that are isolated from the public internet.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/security-command-center/docs/concepts-web-security-scanner-overview\"><strong>Google Cloud: Overview of Web Security Scanner (Security Command Center):</strong></a> Supports: the elimination of Option A, covering Web Security Scanner as a vulnerability scanner for web applications on App Engine, GKE, and Compute Engine.</p>","lessons":["d2l9"]},"e1q40":{"id":"e1q40","exam":1,"n":40,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You are carrying out a semi-annual capacity planning review for your primary service. You anticipate user growth of 10% month over month for the next six months. The service is fully containerized and runs on a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) Standard regional cluster spread across three zones, with the cluster autoscaler enabled. The service currently uses about 30% of its total provisioned CPU capacity, and it must stay resilient if a single zone fails. You want users to see minimal negative impact from either the growth or a zone failure, without paying for capacity you do not need. How should you prepare for the projected growth?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Confirm the maximum node pool size, enable a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler, and then run a load test to validate the expected resource requirements.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Rely on the cluster autoscaler, since a GKE cluster with it enabled scales automatically regardless of the growth rate.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Take no capacity action, since 30% utilization leaves ample headroom for this rate of growth.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Add 60% more node capacity up front to cover six months of 10% growth, and then run a load test to confirm the capacity is sufficient.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Confirm the maximum node pool size, enable a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler, and then run a load test to validate the expected resource requirements.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Separate the must-haves from the preference. The must-haves are surviving a zone failure and keeping user impact minimal as load grows. The preference is avoiding unnecessary cost. Start with the arithmetic. Growth of 10% per month compounds to 1.1^6, about 1.77 times current load, so 30% utilization becomes about 53% of today’s capacity. If one of three zones fails, only two thirds of capacity remains, so that load would run at about 80% utilization. That is workable but not roomy, so headroom is not guaranteed and must be validated. This points away from doing nothing and away from guessing a fixed number. The remaining decision is between trusting automation blindly and configuring and testing it.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“10% month-over-month for six months”</strong> is a technical signal. It compounds to about 77% growth, not 60%.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Regional cluster on three zones” and “resilience against the failure of a zone”</strong> is a hard constraint. After a zone loss only two thirds of capacity remains, so planned utilization must be judged against that reduced capacity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Cluster autoscaler enabled”</strong> is a technical signal. Nodes are added only within configured limits and only when pods cannot be scheduled.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Minimal negative impact” while “avoiding unnecessary costs”</strong> is a hard constraint paired with a preference. Capacity should be elastic and validated, not over-provisioned in advance.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>The cluster autoscaler adds nodes only when pods are pending because no node has room, and it stops at the node pool maximum, so you must confirm that maximum covers peak demand including a zone failure. For a regional cluster, node limits can apply per zone or across zones depending on how they are set, which is worth checking. Pod counts must also rise with load, and a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler does that by scaling replicas on observed utilization, which in turn creates the pending pods the cluster autoscaler responds to. A load test then checks that the expected resource needs, including the reduced capacity during a zone loss, are met. Together these steps keep capacity elastic, which avoids paying for idle nodes while protecting users.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Incorrect:</strong> It is tempting because the cluster autoscaler does add nodes automatically. The claim fails because it only reacts to unschedulable pods, so it cannot scale if pod counts do not grow, and it cannot exceed the configured maximum node pool size. Quotas can also limit it. Saying it works regardless of growth rate is false.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> It is tempting because 30% looks low. It ignores both compounding growth and the zone-failure requirement. Load reaches about 53% of current capacity, and a zone loss leaves roughly 80% utilization, so calling this significant headroom is not supported and nothing is validated.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Adding capacity and load testing could keep users safe. It loses on two points. The 60% figure comes from adding 10% six times, which understates compounding growth of about 77%. It also pays for six months of unused capacity now, which conflicts with avoiding unnecessary costs when autoscaling can add it only as needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is option B, because enabling the cluster autoscaler feels like it settles capacity. The distinction is that the autoscaler is bounded by configured limits and driven by pending pods, so it needs the maximum checked and pod autoscaling in place. The answer would shift toward option D only if demand were sudden and unforecastable enough that scale-up delay could hurt users, which is not the case with a steady, predicted growth rate. Another trap is the simple-interest error in option D: six months at 10% is not 60%.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Capacity planning combines forecast demand, a failure-tolerance requirement, and elasticity. Growth rates compound, so forecasts multiply rather than add. Resilience to losing one of N zones means usable capacity is reduced to N minus 1 over N of the total, and headroom must be judged against that reduced capacity. Autoscaling should be layered, with pod-level scaling creating demand for node-level scaling, and both bounded by limits that must be checked. Load testing turns assumptions into evidence. This model works for steady growth. Spiky or bursty demand needs additional buffer because scale-up takes time.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team confirms the node pool maximum covers about 1.8 times current load with one zone down, enables a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler on the main workloads, and runs a load test with a simulated zone loss. The result is that nodes are added gradually as users grow, with cost tracking real demand. The load test surfaces any gap, such as a quota or a limit that is too low, before users feel it rather than during an outage.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Compound the growth, judge headroom against capacity after losing a zone, and pair pod autoscaling with node limits and a load test.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a capacity question combines growth, zone resilience, and cost, choose elastic autoscaling with verified limits and a load test. Reject answers that assume automation has no limits, ignore failure capacity, or provision everything up front.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/cluster-autoscaler\"><strong>Google Cloud — GKE cluster autoscaler:</strong></a> Supports: the autoscaler adding nodes only for unschedulable pods and staying within configured node pool limits, which rules out option B and supports checking the maximum in option A.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/horizontalpodautoscaler\"><strong>Google Cloud — GKE Horizontal Pod Autoscaler:</strong></a> Supports: scaling replica counts based on observed utilization, which is the pod-level scaling that drives node scaling in option A.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 3, Applying site reliability engineering practices):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment with managing the service lifecycle, including capacity planning and autoscaling.</p>","lessons":["d3l7"]},"e1q41":{"id":"e1q41","exam":1,"n":41,"domain":5,"html":"<p>You are responsible for the backend of a mobile game that runs on a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster, and the application serves HTTP requests from players. You need to put a solution in place that lowers the network cost. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Set up the VPC network as a Shared VPC host project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Set up your network services to use the Standard Network Service Tier.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Set up your Kubernetes cluster as a private cluster.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Set up an external Application Load Balancer as the Ingress.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Set up your network services to use the Standard Network Service Tier.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The single requirement is lower network cost for a workload that serves HTTP traffic to internet users. Treat the options as levers and ask which one changes what you pay for traffic between your service and the internet. Shared VPC, private clusters, and a load balancer Ingress each solve a different problem, such as administration, isolation, or traffic distribution, and none of them is priced as a cost reduction on user-facing traffic. Network Service Tiers is the one lever whose purpose is to trade performance for cheaper egress. That elimination leaves one decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Reduce the network cost”</strong> is a hard constraint. It is the only goal, so the answer must change how traffic is priced.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Serving HTTP requests from users”</strong> is a technical signal. Responses to users leave Google Cloud as internet egress, which is where tier pricing applies.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Mobile phone game”</strong> is a context signal. It implies user traffic over the internet, not internal service-to-service traffic.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Network Service Tiers lets you choose how traffic between the internet and your Google Cloud resources is carried. Premium Tier, the default, uses Google’s global backbone and costs more. Standard Tier routes traffic over regular ISP transit networks, which is priced lower. Because the game’s backend sends responses to players as internet egress, moving to Standard Tier directly reduces that cost. Two boundaries apply. Standard Tier gives lower performance and availability than Premium, and it is regional, so it fits workloads where higher latency is tolerable. Premium is used by default unless you choose otherwise.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong> A Shared VPC host project lets multiple projects share one network for centralized administration and policy. It is tempting because it is a networking feature in an organizational setting. It changes how networks are managed, not how internet egress is priced, so it does not reduce this cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> A private cluster gives nodes internal IP addresses only, which improves security by reducing exposure. It is tempting because it sounds like a network configuration choice. It does not lower the price of egress to users, and it can add cost, for example for NAT to reach the internet.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> An external Application Load Balancer as Ingress distributes HTTP traffic and gives routing and scaling features. It is tempting because the workload serves HTTP. It does not make egress cheaper and has its own charges, so it is a traffic management choice rather than a cost reduction.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is option D, because an HTTP load balancer is the standard front door for HTTP workloads on GKE and feels like an optimization. The distinction is that it improves distribution and availability, not the price per gigabyte of egress. The answer would switch only if the question asked about balancing traffic or global routing. A second trap is assuming Standard Tier is free of trade-offs. It reduces cost by giving up the performance of Google’s backbone, which is acceptable here because cost is the stated priority.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud network cost is driven mostly by egress to the internet, and the route traffic takes determines the price. Carrying traffic over a provider’s private backbone costs more than handing it to the public internet early. A cost question about user-facing traffic therefore points to routing and pricing choices, not to access control or administration. This model applies to internet egress. Internal traffic between services in the same region follows different pricing, and security or organizational features rarely change any of it.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team running a regional game backend switches its external addresses and load balancing to Standard Tier. Egress charges to players drop, and the team accepts slightly higher latency and lower availability targets in exchange. If players are spread worldwide and latency matters for gameplay, they would measure the effect first and might keep Premium for latency-sensitive paths while using Standard elsewhere.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For cheaper user-facing egress, change the network tier, not the network layout.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks to cut network cost for internet-facing traffic, look for the routing and pricing option, which is Standard Tier. Reject features that solve administration, isolation, or traffic distribution.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/network-tiers/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Network Service Tiers overview:</strong></a> Supports: Premium Tier optimizing for performance and Standard Tier optimizing for cost, which supports option B and its performance trade-off.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/network-tiers/pricing\"><strong>Google Cloud — Network Service Tiers pricing:</strong></a> Supports: Standard Tier egress being priced differently from Premium Tier egress, which is the cost mechanism behind option B.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 5, Optimizing performance and cost):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment with infrastructure cost planning that explicitly names network tiers.</p>","lessons":["d5l2"]},"e1q42":{"id":"e1q42","exam":1,"n":42,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You release a new version of an internal application during a weekend maintenance window, when user traffic is minimal. After the window closes, you discover that one of the new features does not work as expected in production. The outage lasts a long time before you roll back the release and deploy a fix. You want to change your release process to shorten the mean time to recovery (MTTR) so that future outages are not prolonged. What should you do? (Choose two.)</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Require two separate peers to review code changes before they are merged.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Adopt a blue/green deployment strategy when releasing new code through a continuous delivery (CD) server.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Add a code linting tool that checks coding standards before any code is accepted into the repository.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Require developers to run automated integration tests in their local development environments before a release.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>E. Set up a continuous integration (CI) server, add a unit test suite to the code, and have the CI server run the tests on every commit to verify changes.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Adopt a blue/green deployment strategy when releasing new code through a continuous delivery (CD) server.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>E. Set up a continuous integration (CI) server, add a unit test suite to the code, and have the CI server run the tests on every commit to verify changes.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Separate the must-have from the framing. The goal is a shorter recovery time after a bad release, and the incident shows two weaknesses: the broken feature reached production, and getting back to a good state took too long. Choose one action for each weakness. For fast recovery, you need a release mechanism that makes returning to the previous version quick and low-risk. For fast detection and repair, you need automated, enforced feedback on every change so defects surface early and a fix can ship through a trusted pipeline. Then eliminate options that improve code hygiene or rely on unenforced manual steps without affecting recovery. Two options remain.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Reduce the mean time to recovery”</strong> is a hard constraint. The chosen actions must shorten recovery, not only prevent problems.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“After an extended outage, you roll back ... and deploy a fix”</strong> is a technical signal. Both rolling back and redeploying took too long, so the release mechanism and the verification of fixes are the targets.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Choose two”</strong> is a cardinality instruction. Select exactly two options, ideally covering different parts of the problem.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Release process”</strong> is a scope signal. The change should live in the delivery pipeline, not in individual habits or style rules.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Option B addresses recovery directly. In a blue/green deployment, two identical environments exist, and the new version is deployed to the idle one while the current one keeps serving users. Traffic moves to the new version only when it is ready, and if a problem appears, traffic switches back to the previous environment almost instantly. This turns an extended rollback into a quick switch, which is the largest cut to MTTR. Option E strengthens the other half. A CI server that runs unit tests on every commit gives immediate, automated, consistent feedback, so regressions are caught close to where they were introduced. It also verifies a fix quickly and reliably before it is released. The two options work together: E reduces how often bad changes reach production and speeds up fixes, while B limits how long users are affected when one does. They are independent controls, and together they cover both prevention and recovery.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong> Requiring two peer reviewers improves code quality and shares knowledge. It is tempting because reviews catch defects. It happens before merging, depends on human attention, and does nothing to speed up rollback or fix verification, and it can slow delivery.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> A linting tool enforces style and standards. It is tempting because it is automated and runs before acceptance. It does not detect functional defects such as a feature that fails in production, and it has no effect on recovery speed.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Automated integration tests are valuable, so the idea is sound. It loses because running them in local environments is left to individual developers, is not enforced centrally, and may differ from the production setup. It is also not part of the release mechanism, so it does not make recovery faster the way a pipeline-level control does.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is option D, because automated integration tests sound more thorough than unit tests. The distinction is where and how they run: tests that depend on each developer’s local environment are inconsistent and unenforced, while a CI server runs them the same way on every commit. The answer would shift toward integration testing only if the option said they run automatically in the pipeline against a production-like environment. A second trap is treating quality gates such as peer review and linting as recovery measures. They are preventive and do not shorten recovery.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Mean time to recovery improves when you can detect problems fast and restore service fast. Detection improves with automated testing and monitoring, and restoration improves with deployment strategies that make reverting cheap, such as blue/green, canary, or traffic splitting. Prevention reduces how often failures happen, but it does not substitute for recovery speed. Blue/green has costs, including running two environments and handling data or schema compatibility, so it fits best when instant rollback matters more than the duplicated capacity.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team routes releases through a pipeline that builds, runs unit tests on each commit, deploys to the idle environment, and only then shifts traffic. When a broken feature appears, an operator shifts traffic back within minutes, and the team fixes the defect with a new commit that passes the same automated tests before redeploying. The result is a short disruption instead of an extended outage, at the cost of keeping a second environment available during releases.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>To recover faster, make rollback a traffic switch and make every change pass automated tests before it ships.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For MTTR questions, pick one option that makes reverting instant, such as blue/green, and one that gives automated, enforced feedback, such as CI tests. Reject style rules, peer review, and unenforced local testing, which do not change recovery speed.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/application-deployment-and-testing-strategies\">Google Cloud Architecture Center — Application deployment and testing strategies: </a>Supports: blue/green deployment keeping the previous environment available and allowing a quick switch back, which supports option B.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/devops\"><strong>Google Cloud Architecture Center — DevOps capabilities (DORA), including continuous integration, test automation, and continuous delivery:</strong></a> Supports: running automated tests on each commit through a CI process as a practice that speeds feedback and release reliability, which supports option E.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\">Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 2, Building and implementing CI/CD pipelines): </a>Supports: domain alignment with deployment strategies such as blue/green and with troubleshooting and mitigating deployment issues.</p>","lessons":["d2l3","d2l6"]},"e1q43":{"id":"e1q43","exam":1,"n":43,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your team builds application container images and pushes them to Artifact Registry. You need an automated pipeline that deploys the application each time a new image is published, and you want to keep the development effort as low as possible. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Configure Cloud Build to start a Spinnaker pipeline.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Configure Cloud Pub/Sub to start a Spinnaker pipeline.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Configure a custom builder in Cloud Build to start a Jenkins pipeline.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Configure Cloud Pub/Sub to start a custom deployment service running on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE).</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Configure Cloud Pub/Sub to start a Spinnaker pipeline.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Separate the must-have from the preference. The must-have is that the trigger is the image update itself, not a source commit or a manual step. The preference is minimal development effort, which favors managed, built-in integrations over anything custom. Work through it as requirement, capability, elimination, decision. The registry already emits an event when an image changes, so you need something that listens to that event, and Spinnaker can start a pipeline directly from a Pub/Sub subscription. That path needs configuration but no new code. Each remaining option adds a build step, a custom builder, or a service you would have to write and run.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Deploys the application when the image is updated”</strong> is a hard constraint. The trigger must be the image publish event, not a code change.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Minimizing the development effort”</strong> is a preference that works as a tiebreaker. It rules out anything requiring custom code or a custom service.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Images are built and pushed to the registry”</strong> is a technical signal. The image already exists when the pipeline starts, so a build step is unnecessary for triggering.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Automated pipeline”</strong> is a technical signal. It points to an event-driven trigger rather than a manual or polling approach.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Artifact Registry publishes messages to a Pub/Sub topic named gcr whenever an image is uploaded, tagged, or deleted. Spinnaker supports a Pub/Sub trigger type that subscribes to a Google Pub/Sub subscription and starts a pipeline when a message arrives. Its Google Pub/Sub integration documents registry-originated messages as a supported source. Connecting the two is a configuration task: create a subscription on the topic, enable Google Pub/Sub in Spinnaker, and add a Pub/Sub trigger to the pipeline. The image reference in the message can be used as the artifact to deploy. No custom code is needed, which is why it is the lowest-effort fit. One boundary matters: the topic delivers all registry events, so the pipeline should constrain the trigger to the push events and image it cares about.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong> Cloud Build starts builds from source events such as commits or tags, and Spinnaker can react to Cloud Build results. That chain is tied to a build happening, not to an image appearing in the registry. It is tempting because Cloud Build is a natural place to produce the image, but it would miss images pushed by other means. It also adds a build dependency the requirement does not call for.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A custom builder in Cloud Build can call Jenkins, so the outcome is achievable. It is tempting because Jenkins is a well-known deployment tool. It loses on effort, because it requires writing and maintaining a custom builder, running a Jenkins server, and still has no direct link to the image update event.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Pub/Sub is the right trigger source, and that makes this option look close. However, writing, hosting, securing, and operating your own deployment service on GKE is the highest-effort path. It directly conflicts with minimizing development effort when an existing tool can consume the same event.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is option A, because Cloud Build feels like the natural pipeline entry point when images are built there. The distinction is that the requirement is event-driven on the image update, not on a source change. The answer would shift toward Cloud Build only if the requirement were to deploy on every source commit. A second trap is option D, which uses the correct Pub/Sub event but ignores the effort constraint. A terminology note: Container Registry has been superseded by Artifact Registry, and Artifact Registry publishes to the same gcr topic, so the Pub/Sub approach is unchanged. Google’s native managed continuous delivery service is Cloud Deploy, but it is not offered here, so the best choice among the given options is B.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Event-driven pipelines start from a signal that something changed, not from polling or a manual step. A registry publishes change events to a message bus, and a deployment system subscribes to that bus and acts on it. Using a deployment tool’s built-in trigger for that event beats writing glue code, because the integration is already tested and maintained. The pattern holds whenever the source of the change can publish events. It weakens when you need to filter heavily or enforce ordering and approvals, which the deployment tool must then handle.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team creates a subscription on the registry’s topic and adds a Pub/Sub trigger to a Spinnaker pipeline with a constraint on the image name. Every new tagged image then starts a deployment with no code to maintain. The operational result is a short, auditable path from image publish to rollout, and the main ongoing cost is managing Spinnaker itself rather than custom services. Because the topic carries all registry events, the team also adds payload filtering so tag deletions do not start deployments.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Image changed in the registry means a Pub/Sub event, and the least-effort consumer is the deployment tool’s built-in Pub/Sub trigger.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a deployment must start from an artifact update and effort must be minimal, pick the managed event source feeding the tool’s native trigger. Reject options that add build steps, custom builders, or self-written services.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/configure-notifications\">Google Cloud — Artifact Registry: Configure Pub/Sub notifications: </a>Supports: the registry publishing image upload and tag events to the gcr topic, and that Artifact Registry uses the same topic as Container Registry, which makes the Pub/Sub trigger valid for option B.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://spinnaker.io/docs/setup/other_config/triggers/google/\"><strong>Spinnaker — Google Cloud Pub/Sub triggers:</strong></a> Supports: Spinnaker starting pipelines from Pub/Sub messages originating from Google Cloud systems such as the container registry, which separates B from A, C, and D.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 2, Building and implementing CI/CD pipelines):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment with designing pipelines, pipeline triggers, and artifact management with Artifact Registry.</p>","lessons":["d2l5","d2l11"]},"e1q44":{"id":"e1q44","exam":1,"n":44,"domain":3,"html":"<p>A serious outage prevented every user of your service from accessing it for several hours. After extended incident response, the service recovered and access was restored. You now need to give relevant stakeholders a summary of the incident, following Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) recommended practices. What should you do first?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Phone each stakeholder individually to describe what occurred.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Write a postmortem and distribute it to the stakeholders.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Send the incident state document to every stakeholder.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Ask the engineer responsible to write an apology email to all stakeholders.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Write a postmortem and distribute it to the stakeholders.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Separate what is required from what is tempting. The requirement is a stakeholder-facing incident summary that follows SRE practice, and the word “first” asks for the opening step after recovery. The preference implied by SRE practice is a blameless, documented, learning-focused record. Work from requirement to capability to elimination. The SRE artifact that summarizes an incident for stakeholders and captures cause, impact, response, and follow-up actions is the postmortem. Each other option either skips documentation, uses a document meant for a different audience and moment, or assigns blame. That leaves one decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Following Site Reliability Engineering recommended practices”</strong> is a hard constraint. It points to blameless postmortems rather than informal or punitive communication.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“After several hours of incident management, the service returned to normal”</strong> is a sequence signal. Response is over, so the next step is the after-action write-up, not more live coordination.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Incident summary to relevant stakeholders”</strong> is a technical signal. The deliverable is a written, shareable summary of the incident.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“First”</strong> is a sequence instruction. It asks for the initial step toward the summary, which is producing the document before distributing or discussing it.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>SRE practice treats a postmortem as the standard written record of an incident. It describes impact, root causes, the timeline, what went well and badly, and action items to prevent recurrence. A postmortem is written once the incident is resolved and the facts are understood, then shared so stakeholders get a consistent, accurate account and the organization learns from it. It is blameless, focusing on systems and processes rather than individuals. Writing it first means every later communication, whether a meeting, a call, or an update, draws on one agreed source of truth.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Calling stakeholders does communicate, and it is tempting because it feels personal and quick. It produces no durable record, delivers inconsistent accounts to different people, and happens before the facts and causes are analyzed. It does not match the SRE expectation of a written summary.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> The incident state document, also called the living incident document, is a working record that the incident team updates in real time during the response. It is valid for coordinating responders. It is tempting because it already exists and contains incident detail. It is raw, in-progress, and written for responders, so it is not a polished summary for stakeholders and does not include analysis or follow-up actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Making one engineer apologize contradicts the blameless culture SRE recommends. Outages come from system and process weaknesses, and punishing or singling out an individual discourages honest reporting and does not address root causes.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is option C, because the incident state document is a real SRE artifact and holds all the incident details. The distinction is audience and timing. It is a live working tool for responders, while the postmortem is the after-action summary for stakeholders. The answer would shift to C only if the question asked what to maintain during the incident. Option A is the other trap, since talking to people feels responsible but skips the documentation step.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Incident handling has two phases with different artifacts. During the incident, a living document tracks status and coordinates the response. After resolution, a blameless postmortem analyzes what happened and drives preventive actions. Blamelessness matters because people report problems openly only when they are not punished for them, which improves learning. This model applies to significant incidents. Minor events may warrant a lighter review, but the principle of documenting and learning remains the same.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>After a multi-hour outage, the incident commander gathers the timeline from the incident document, the team writes a postmortem with impact, root cause, and tracked action items, and it is shared with stakeholders and reviewed. The result is one consistent explanation, assigned follow-up work, and fewer repeat incidents. Leadership gets a clear account without relying on side conversations, and engineers stay willing to report problems.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>During the incident you keep a living document, and after it you write a blameless postmortem for stakeholders.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks what to do after an incident is resolved under SRE practice, choose the blameless postmortem. Reject ad hoc communication, live incident documents, and blame-based actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/\">Google SRE Book — Postmortem Culture: Learning from Failure: </a>Supports: the postmortem as the standard written record after an incident and the principle of blamelessness, which supports option B and rules out option D.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/managing-incidents/\"><strong>Google SRE Book — Managing Incidents:</strong></a> Supports: the living incident document as a real-time coordination tool used during the response, which distinguishes option C as a different use case.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 3, Applying site reliability engineering practices):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment with mitigating incident impact on users and applying SRE practices.</p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e1q45":{"id":"e1q45","exam":1,"n":45,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You use Spinnaker to deploy your application, and your pipeline includes a canary deployment stage. The application keeps an in-memory cache that is populated with objects when the application starts. You want to automate the comparison between the canary version and the production version. How should you configure the canary analysis?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Compare the canary against a newly created deployment of the current production version.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Compare the canary against a newly created deployment of the previous production version.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Compare the canary against the already running deployment of the current production version.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Compare the canary against the averaged performance of a sliding window of earlier production versions.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Compare the canary against a newly created deployment of the current production version.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The must-have is a fair, like-for-like comparison that isolates the effect of the new code. The scenario supplies the key detail: an in-memory cache filled at startup. That means a long-running instance and a freshly started instance behave differently regardless of the code version. Work through requirement, capability, elimination, decision. A valid baseline must run the same code as production while matching the canary in age and conditions. Any comparison target that differs in running time, code version, or traffic period adds noise that cannot be separated from the canary’s real effect. Only one option meets that bar.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“In-memory cache that loads objects at start time”</strong> is a decisive technical signal. A freshly started canary has a cold cache, while a long-running instance has a warm one, which skews performance metrics.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Automate the comparison of the canary version against the production version”</strong> is a hard constraint. The comparison target must produce statistically fair results.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Canary deployment stage in the pipeline”</strong> is a context signal. Spinnaker’s canary analysis is designed to run a canary and a baseline side by side.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Spinnaker’s automated canary analysis, built on Kayenta, compares the canary against a baseline rather than against the live production instances. The baseline is a new deployment of the current production version, started at the same time as the canary, with the same number of instances and configuration. Because both start together, they have the same cache warmup state, so differences in metrics reflect the code change instead of uptime. In this application, the cold cache is the very effect that would distort a comparison against long-running production instances. A boundary to note: the baseline runs the same version production runs, so it tests the new code, not production’s accumulated state.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Incorrect:</strong> A new deployment of the previous production version is a baseline of the wrong code. It is tempting because it is freshly started, like the canary, which would match cache state. It is not the version users currently run, so the analysis would compare the canary with something that is not production and could miss regressions relative to what is live.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Comparing against the existing production deployment is the intuitive approach, and it is tempting for that reason. The existing instances have run for a long time with warm caches, so latency and resource metrics would differ from a freshly started canary for reasons unrelated to the code. This is exactly the unfairness the cache detail points to.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A sliding window of earlier versions provides a long history, which is tempting for statistical stability. It mixes several code versions and different traffic periods, and none of it matches the canary’s start time, so it cannot isolate the effect of the new release.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is option C, because comparing a canary with the live production deployment is the most natural reading of a canary. The distinction is that the comparison must hold everything constant except the code, and uptime and cache state are not constant. The answer would switch to C only if the application had no warmup or startup effects and the production instances were otherwise comparable. A second trap is option B, which looks fair because it is freshly started but tests the wrong code version.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A valid experiment changes one variable and controls the rest. In canary analysis, the canary carries the changed code, and the baseline is the control. For the control to be valid, it should match the canary in code version relevant to production, start time, scale, and traffic exposure. Anything that depends on instance age, such as caches, JIT warmup, or connection pools, makes long-running instances a poor control. This model applies to automated comparison of metrics. Where an application has no age-dependent behavior, the difference matters less, but the baseline pattern remains the safer default.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A pipeline deploys three canary instances with the new image and three baseline instances with the current production image, routes a small share of traffic to each, and runs the analysis on matching metrics for a fixed period. A score above the threshold promotes the release, and a low score stops it. Teams often add a test where the canary runs the same version as production, which should always pass, to confirm the analysis configuration is sound. The cost is temporary extra instances while the analysis runs.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Compare the canary to a fresh copy of today’s production, not to the instances that have been running for weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For canary analysis questions, pick a baseline that runs the current production version and starts alongside the canary. Reject comparisons against long-running instances, older versions, or historical averages.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/devops-sre/canary-analysis-lessons-learned-and-best-practices-from-google-and-waze\"><strong>Google Cloud Blog — Canary analysis: Lessons learned and best practices from Google and Waze:</strong></a> Supports: comparing the canary against a baseline with the same version and configuration as production rather than against production itself, citing cache warmup as a skewing factor, which supports option A and rules out options C and D.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.infoq.com/news/2018/04/kayenta\"><strong>InfoQ — Kayenta: An Open Source Canary Analysis Tool from Netflix and Google:</strong></a> Supports: creating a brand new baseline cluster so metrics are free of effects from long-running processes, which reinforces the elimination of option C.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 2, Building and implementing CI/CD pipelines):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment with deployment strategies such as canary and with defining success metrics based on application telemetry.</p>","lessons":["d2l6","d2l11"]},"e1q46":{"id":"e1q46","exam":1,"n":46,"domain":4,"html":"<p>A group of application servers runs on Compute Engine. You need a secure approach that requires the LEAST configuration and lets developers easily reach application logs for troubleshooting. How should you implement this on Google Cloud?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Install the Cloud Logging agent on the application servers. Grant the developers the Logs Viewer IAM role so they can read the logs in Cloud Logging.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Install the Cloud Logging agent on the application servers. Grant the developers the Private Logs Viewer IAM role so they can read the logs in Cloud Logging.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Install the Cloud Monitoring agent on the application servers. Grant the developers the Monitoring Viewer IAM role so they can read the metrics in Cloud Monitoring.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Install the gsutil tool on the application servers and write a script that uploads the application logs to a Cloud Storage bucket, scheduled with cron every five minutes. Grant the developers the Storage Object Viewer IAM role on the bucket so they can read the logs.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Install the Cloud Logging agent on the application servers. Grant the developers the Logs Viewer IAM role so they can read the logs in Cloud Logging.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Separate the must-haves from the preference. The must-haves are that developers can read application logs and that access stays secure. The preference is the least configuration. Work through requirement, capability, elimination, decision. Application logs on a VM need an agent to reach Cloud Logging, so the choice must include a logging collector. Access should follow least privilege, so the role should grant only what developers need to read ordinary logs. Option C collects the wrong data type, and option D replaces managed collection with custom scripting. Of the two remaining logging options, the one with the narrower role is the secure fit.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Application logs”</strong> is a technical signal. The goal is logs, not metrics, so a metrics-oriented agent and role do not meet it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Secure”</strong> is a hard constraint. It calls for the narrowest role that still lets developers read the logs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“LEAST amount of configuration”</strong> is a preference. A managed agent and a predefined role beat custom scripts and schedules.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Easily access ... for troubleshooting”</strong> is a technical signal. Developers should use the Logs Explorer rather than fetch files from a bucket.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A logging agent running on each VM collects application logs and sends them to Cloud Logging, which stores and indexes them without extra infrastructure. The Logs Viewer role gives read access to the log data in the default log buckets, except Data Access audit logs, which is what developers need for troubleshooting. Granting it at the project level takes a single IAM binding, and developers browse and query logs in the Logs Explorer. This combines managed collection with least-privilege access and almost no custom configuration. One boundary: the VMs must be allowed to write logs, so their service account needs permission to do so, which is typically a default setup step.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> It collects logs the same way, so it works for troubleshooting. The Private Logs Viewer role adds access to private logs such as Data Access audit logs, which developers do not need. It is tempting because it sounds more complete, but it grants more than necessary and weakens least privilege.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> The monitoring agent collects metrics, and the Monitoring Viewer role shows metrics and dashboards. It is a valid combination for performance monitoring. It is tempting because the two products share a brand and an observability goal. It does not deliver application logs, so it fails the core requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Scripted uploads to Cloud Storage can make logs available. It is tempting because Storage roles are simple. It requires writing, scheduling, and maintaining scripts, introduces a five-minute delay, and gives developers files to download instead of searchable logs. That is the opposite of least configuration and easy troubleshooting.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is option B, because it is identical to the answer except for the role, and a broader role can look like the safer way to ensure access. The distinction is least privilege: Logs Viewer already covers ordinary application logs, and Private Logs Viewer adds sensitive log types. The answer would switch to B only if developers needed to read Data Access audit logs. A terminology note: the legacy logging and monitoring agents have reached end of support, and the Ops Agent is the current agent that collects both logs and metrics. The item keeps the separate agent names so the distinction between logs and metrics in option C stays meaningful. Today’s equivalent of the answer is the Ops Agent with the Logs Viewer role.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Centralized logging separates collection, storage, and access. An agent collects and ships logs, a managed service stores and indexes them, and IAM roles control who can read them. Least privilege means granting the narrowest role that satisfies the task. Logs and metrics are different telemetry types with different tools and roles, so matching the data type to the task comes first. This model fits routine application troubleshooting. Regulated or sensitive logs may need stricter role separation or restricted log views.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team installs the agent through its VM image or instance configuration, so every server in the pool ships logs automatically. The developers group receives the Logs Viewer role on the project, and engineers filter by service and severity in the Logs Explorer during an incident. The result is faster troubleshooting with no scripts to maintain. Sensitive audit data stays restricted to the smaller group that holds the private log role.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Logs need a logging agent and the Logs Viewer role, while metrics belong to monitoring and private logs belong to audit needs.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks for developer access to application logs with minimal setup, choose the managed logging agent plus the narrowest viewer role. Reject metrics tools, broader roles than needed, and custom upload scripts.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/access-control\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Logging access control with IAM:</strong></a> Supports: Logs Viewer covering log data except Data Access logs and Private Logs Viewer adding private logs, which separates option A from option B.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/deprecations/logging-agent\"><strong>Google Cloud Observability — Legacy Monitoring and Logging agents end of support:</strong></a> Supports: the legacy agents having reached end of support and the Ops Agent being the supported alternative, which explains the terminology note.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 4, Implementing observability practices and troubleshooting issues):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment with collecting logs using the Ops Agent and managing and analyzing logs.</p>","lessons":["d4l5","d4l13"]},"e1q47":{"id":"e1q47","exam":1,"n":47,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You support a high-traffic web application and want to make sure the home page loads quickly. As a first step, you decide to define a Service Level Indicator (SLI) for home page request latency, with 100 ms as the acceptable page load time. What is the Google-recommended way to calculate this SLI?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Group request latencies into buckets, and then calculate the percentile that corresponds to 100 ms.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Group request latencies into buckets, and then calculate the median and the 90th percentile.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Count the home page requests that complete in under 100 ms, and then divide that count by the total number of home page requests.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Count the home page requests that complete in under 100 ms, and then divide that count by the total number of requests to the whole web application.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Count the home page requests that complete in under 100 ms, and then divide that count by the total number of home page requests.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Start with the requirement: an SLI for home page latency with a stated threshold of 100 ms. Then ask what shape a Google-recommended SLI takes. It is a ratio of good events to valid events, expressed as a percentage, so it can later be compared with an objective. Apply that shape in two steps. First, define good: a home page request faster than 100 ms. Second, define valid: only home page requests, because the SLI describes the home page. Check each option against both steps. Two options fail on the threshold or the formula, one fails on the denominator, and one satisfies both.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Acceptable page load time set to 100 ms”</strong> is a hard constraint. The calculation must classify each request against this threshold.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Home page request latency”</strong> is a scoping instruction. The SLI covers home page requests only, so the denominator must exclude other traffic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Google-recommended way of calculating”</strong> is a technical signal. It points to the standard SLI equation of good events divided by valid events.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“As a first step”</strong> is a context signal. It suggests a simple, well-defined indicator to be paired with an objective later.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Google’s SRE guidance defines an SLI as the proportion of valid events that were good, which yields a value between 0 and 100 percent. For latency, a good event is a request served faster than the threshold, and valid events are the requests the SLI is meant to describe. Counting home page requests under 100 ms and dividing by all home page requests gives exactly that ratio. The result is directly usable as the basis for a service level objective, such as a target percentage of fast requests, and it maps naturally to error budgets. It also reads in plain terms: the share of home page loads that met the target.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Looking up the percentile at 100 ms is close in spirit, because it approximates the share of requests at or below the threshold. It is tempting for that reason. It is not the recommended calculation, because bucketing limits precision to bucket boundaries, a percentile is a summary of the distribution rather than a good-over-valid ratio, and it is harder to tie to a percentage-based objective.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Incorrect:</strong> The median and the 90th percentile describe the shape of the latency distribution. It is tempting because percentiles are common in latency reporting. Neither value is compared with the 100 ms threshold, so the calculation never classifies requests as good or bad and does not produce an SLI in the required form.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> The numerator is correct, but the denominator includes all web application requests. It is tempting because it differs from the answer by only one phrase. It dilutes the home page measurement with unrelated traffic, so the result no longer represents home page latency.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is option D, because it looks nearly identical to the answer and differs only in the denominator. The distinction is that valid events must match what the SLI describes, which here is home page requests. The answer would switch to D only if the SLI were defined for the whole application’s request latency. Option A is the second trap, since a percentile at the threshold seems equivalent but is not the recommended form of the calculation.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A good SLI is expressed as a ratio of good events to valid events. This lets one definition work across latency, availability, and quality, and it maps directly to a percentage objective and an error budget. Thresholds turn a continuous measurement such as latency into a good or bad classification for each event. Scoping matters: the valid event set should match the user journey being measured, so unrelated traffic does not mask problems. This approach works for request-driven services. Other workloads, such as batch jobs, use different event definitions but keep the same ratio shape.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team counts home page requests served under 100 ms from its load balancer or application metrics and divides by all home page requests. It then sets an objective such as 99 percent of home page loads under the threshold over a rolling window. If the ratio drops below the objective, the error budget burns and the team prioritizes reliability work. Excluding other pages keeps a slow checkout flow from hiding a healthy home page, and the reverse.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>An SLI is good events over valid events, so count the fast home page requests and divide by home page requests only.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When asked how to calculate an SLI, answer with good events divided by valid events, and check that the denominator matches the scope the SLI describes. Reject answers that report distribution summaries or use a broader denominator.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — Implementing SLOs:</strong></a> Supports: the SLI equation of good events divided by valid events and its use for latency, which supports option C and rules out options B and D.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/\"><strong>Google SRE Book — Service Level Objectives:</strong></a> Supports: the definition of an SLI as a measure of service level, with latency thresholds and percentiles discussed as indicators and objectives, which clarifies why option A and option B are not the recommended calculation.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 3, Applying site reliability engineering practices):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment with defining SLIs, SLOs, and SLAs under balancing change, velocity, and reliability.</p>","lessons":["d3l1"]},"e1q48":{"id":"e1q48","exam":1,"n":48,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Several applications run on a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster, and you use GKE’s built-in integration with Cloud Logging and Cloud Monitoring. You are about to put a new containerized application into production that a third party wrote and that cannot be modified or reconfigured. The application writes its log output to /var/log/app_messages.log, and you want those entries to appear in Cloud Logging. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Rely on the default GKE logging agent configuration.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Deploy a Fluentd DaemonSet to the GKE cluster, and then define a custom input and output configuration that tails the log file in the application’s pods and writes to Cloud Logging.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Install Kubernetes on Compute Engine and redeploy your applications, and then customize the built-in logging configuration to tail the log file in the application’s pods and write to Cloud Logging.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Write a script that tails the log file inside the pod and writes the entries to standard output, run it as a sidecar container in the application’s pod, and configure a volume shared between the containers so the script can read /var/log in the application container.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Write a script that tails the log file inside the pod and writes the entries to standard output, run it as a sidecar container in the application’s pod, and configure a volume shared between the containers so the script can read /var/log in the application container.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The must-haves are that the application stays untouched and that its file-based logs reach Cloud Logging. Start from how GKE collects logs. The built-in node agent picks up what containers write to standard output and standard error, and nothing else. The application writes to a file inside its own container, so the managed agent never sees it. The decision then becomes how to turn that file into a stream the managed agent already handles, or how to add a separate collector that can reach the file. A file inside one container is not visible to other containers or to the node unless a volume is shared. That favors changing the pod around the application, not the application itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Written by a third party and cannot be modified or reconfigured”</strong> is a hard constraint. The solution must work around the application, for example with a companion container.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Writes its log information to /var/log/app_messages.log”</strong> is a technical signal. The logs are in a file, not on standard output, so default collection misses them.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Using GKE’s built-in logging integration”</strong> is a technical signal. The managed agent already ships standard output and standard error, so converting the file into that stream reuses it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Containerized application”</strong> is a scoping signal. The file lives in the container’s filesystem and is isolated unless a volume is shared.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Kubernetes documents a streaming sidecar pattern for applications that cannot write to standard output. The application container writes to a file on a shared volume, and a sidecar container in the same pod reads that file and prints it to its own standard output. The kubelet and the existing node-level logging agent then collect the sidecar’s output like any other container log, so the entries reach Cloud Logging with no new collector to operate. The script is essentially a single tail command. The application’s code and configuration are unchanged, and only the pod specification gains a sidecar and a shared volume. One boundary: mounting a shared volume at /var/log hides anything already at that path in the application image, and the sidecar adds some resource use to each pod.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Incorrect:</strong> The default configuration collects container standard output and standard error plus system logs. It does not read arbitrary files inside a container, so the application’s file never reaches Cloud Logging. It is tempting because the managed integration is already in place.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A custom Fluentd DaemonSet can be made to read files, which is why it is tempting, and Google publishes guidance on customizing Fluentd for GKE. A node-level agent reads files that exist on the node, and the container’s log file is not visible there unless a host-mounted volume exposes it, which means changing the pod specification anyway. It also adds a second logging agent to maintain and configure beside the managed one, so it is more work for the same pod change.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Moving to self-managed Kubernetes on Compute Engine abandons the GKE environment that is already in use, and the logging challenge remains because a file inside a pod still needs a shared volume or sidecar. It is tempting only because custom configuration is possible there. The effort and operational burden are far out of proportion.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is option B, because a Fluentd DaemonSet is the familiar way to customize logging on GKE and is the answer some practice sources list. The distinction is that a node-level agent only works on what is visible at the node, which for containers means standard output and standard error, so a file inside the container needs a shared volume or a sidecar to be reachable. The answer would switch to B if the application’s file were already on a host-mounted path that the DaemonSet could read. The word “script” in option D is also a trap, because it looks like development effort, but it is a one-line tail command and a sidecar in the pod specification.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Container platforms are designed around logs as streams written to standard output and standard error, which a node-level agent collects without touching the application. When an application writes to files instead, you must either bridge the file to a stream or run a collector that can see the file. A sidecar shares the pod’s volumes and network, so it is the natural place to bridge. This model works for any file, socket, or journal source. A sidecar that runs its own logging agent is an alternative when you need parsing or custom routing, at the cost of more resources and configuration.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team adds an emptyDir volume mounted at /var/log in the application container and the sidecar, and the sidecar runs a tail command on the log file. The entries appear in Cloud Logging under the sidecar container’s name, can be filtered like any other container logs, and the vendor’s application is untouched. The operational costs are a little extra CPU and memory per pod, and care that log rotation does not leave the shared volume growing without bound.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>If the app writes to a file and cannot change, add a sidecar that streams the file to standard output.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For file-based logs from an unchangeable app on GKE, choose a sidecar that tails the file to standard output through a shared volume. Reject answers that rely on default collection or on a node agent that cannot see inside the container.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/logging/\"><strong>Kubernetes — Logging Architecture:</strong></a> Supports: node-level logging agents handling container standard output and standard error, and the sidecar pattern in which a container streams application logs to its own standard output so the existing agent collects them, which supports option D and the elimination of options A and B.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 4, Implementing observability practices and troubleshooting issues):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment with collecting and importing logs and optimizing logs by source.</p>","lessons":["d4l5"]},"e1q49":{"id":"e1q49","exam":1,"n":49,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your multi-step Cloud Build pipeline builds your application and deploys it to Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). You want to connect the pipeline to a third-party monitoring platform by sending the build information to a webhook with an HTTP POST. You also want to keep development effort as low as possible. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Add logic to every Cloud Build step that sends the build information to the webhook with an HTTP POST.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Add a final step to the Cloud Build pipeline that sends the build information to the webhook with an HTTP POST.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use Cloud Logging to create a log-based metric from the Cloud Build logs, and then create an alerting policy that uses a webhook notification channel.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Create a Pub/Sub push subscription on the Cloud Build cloud-builds topic so that the build information is sent to the webhook with an HTTP POST.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Create a Pub/Sub push subscription on the Cloud Build cloud-builds topic so that the build information is sent to the webhook with an HTTP POST.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The must-have is that build information reaches a third-party webhook as an HTTP POST. The preference is minimal development effort. Work through requirement, capability, elimination, decision. Ask which approach already produces the build information and the HTTP POST without new code. Cloud Build already publishes build state changes as messages, and a Pub/Sub push subscription already delivers messages as HTTP POST requests to an endpoint. Joining those two managed pieces is configuration. The other options require writing and maintaining logic inside the pipeline or repurposing a metrics and alerting feature for a job it was not designed to do.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“HTTP POST of the build information to a webhook”</strong> is a technical signal. Pub/Sub push delivery is itself an HTTP POST to a webhook endpoint.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Minimize the development effort”</strong> is a preference that works as a tiebreaker. It favors configuration over custom pipeline code.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Multiple step pipeline”</strong> is a technical signal. Per-step logic would multiply the work and still miss overall build status events.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Build information”</strong> is a technical signal. Cloud Build’s own notifications carry the build resource and status, so you do not need to assemble it.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Build publishes a message to the cloud-builds Pub/Sub topic whenever a build’s state changes, such as when it is created, starts working, or completes. Each message carries the build resource and its status. A push subscription on that topic makes Pub/Sub send each message to a configured HTTPS endpoint as a POST request, so the webhook receives build updates with no pipeline code. Setup amounts to creating the topic if needed, granting the required permissions, and creating the subscription. Two boundaries apply. The push request wraps the message in a JSON envelope, with the build data base64-encoded inside, so the receiving platform must accept that format, or you can choose unwrapped delivery to send the raw data. The endpoint must also be an HTTPS address that Pub/Sub can reach and that returns a success code.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Adding POST logic to every step works. It is tempting because it uses only Cloud Build. It requires the most effort, since the code must be written, kept consistent, and maintained in each step. It also does not capture overall build state changes cleanly.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A final step is less work than adding logic to every step, which makes it tempting. It still requires custom code, and it only runs when earlier steps succeed, so failed builds may never report. It would also need to assemble the build information itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> A log-based metric with an alerting policy and a webhook notification channel can notify a webhook. It is tempting because alerting policies support webhooks. It is built to alert on conditions in logs, not to forward build information, so it adds metric and policy configuration and the alert payload would not be the build data the monitoring platform expects.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is option B, because a final pipeline step seems like the obvious lightweight integration. The distinction is that it still means writing and maintaining code inside the pipeline, and it can miss failed builds, whereas the Pub/Sub notification is managed and fires on every state change. The answer would switch toward B only if Pub/Sub were unavailable or if the webhook needed a custom payload that Pub/Sub could not provide. Option C is the second trap, because log-based alerting sounds like a monitoring integration but delivers alerts, not build records.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Event-driven integration lets a source publish state changes and any number of consumers react without changing the source. Messaging services decouple producers from consumers, and push delivery turns messages into HTTP calls for webhook-style receivers. Prefer using the event stream a managed service already emits over adding code to the process that generates the events. This model works well for status and lifecycle events. Fine-grained custom data from inside a build step still requires explicit logic in that step.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team creates a push subscription on the build topic that targets the monitoring vendor’s HTTPS endpoint with authentication enabled. Every build creation, progress, success, and failure then appears in the monitoring platform, including builds that fail early. The team maintains no scripts, and its main ongoing task is confirming the receiver handles the Pub/Sub message envelope. If the vendor cannot parse it, they can enable unwrapped delivery or place a small relay in front.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Build events are already published to Pub/Sub, so a push subscription turns them into webhook calls without code.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a managed service already emits events and you need them in an external HTTP endpoint with low effort, choose Pub/Sub with a push subscription. Reject custom code in the pipeline and alerting features that do not carry the data.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/subscribe-build-notifications\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Build: Subscribe to build notifications:</strong></a> Supports: Cloud Build publishing build state changes to the cloud-builds topic and supporting push subscriptions that deliver messages to an endpoint, which supports option D and rules out options A and B.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/push\"><strong>Google Cloud — Pub/Sub push subscriptions:</strong></a> Supports: push delivery sending messages to a webhook as an HTTPS POST request, in wrapped or unwrapped form, which supports the mechanism and the format boundary in option D.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 2, Building and implementing CI/CD pipelines):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment with implementing and managing pipelines, including auditing and tracking deployments with Cloud Build.</p>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e1q50":{"id":"e1q50","exam":1,"n":50,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You built a Cloud Monitoring chart for CPU utilization on a dashboard in the scoping project of your metrics scope. You want to share the chart with your Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) team and nobody else, and you must follow the principle of LEAST privilege. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Give the SRE team the scoping project’s project ID, and grant the team the Monitoring Viewer IAM role on that project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Give the SRE team the scoping project’s project ID, and grant the team the Monitoring Dashboard Configuration Viewer IAM role on that project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use the chart’s share-by-URL option and send the link to the SRE team, and grant the team the Monitoring Viewer IAM role on the scoping project.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Use the chart’s share-by-URL option and send the link to the SRE team, and grant the team the Monitoring Dashboard Configuration Viewer IAM role on the scoping project.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Use the chart’s share-by-URL option and send the link to the SRE team, and grant the team the Monitoring Viewer IAM role on the scoping project.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two separate things must happen for the SRE team to see the chart: they need a way to reach that specific chart, and they need permission to read what it displays. Treat these as two requirements and check each option against both. A link delivers the chart, but a link alone grants no access. A role grants access, and least privilege asks for the narrowest role that still lets the chart render its data. A chart shows metric data, so a role limited to dashboard configuration may not be enough. Work through requirement, capability, elimination, decision, and only one option meets both parts without extra privilege or a missing piece.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Share the chart with the SRE team only”</strong> is a scoping instruction. The sharing method should deliver the specific chart and the access should be limited to that group.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Principle of LEAST privilege”</strong> is a hard constraint. The role must be the narrowest one that still allows the chart to display data.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Chart ... CPU utilization”</strong> is a technical signal. A chart displays metric data, so the viewer needs permission to read metrics, not only the dashboard’s definition.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Dashboard within your scoping project”</strong> is a technical signal. Permissions are granted on the project that holds the metrics scope.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Sharing the chart’s URL gives the team a direct link to the exact chart they need. Access is a separate control, because a link does not by itself grant permission to view the data behind it. The Monitoring Viewer role gives read-only access to Cloud Monitoring data and configuration, including custom dashboards, and it is the narrow predefined role that lets the chart show its metric data. It does not allow editing or administration, so it respects least privilege among the options offered. Granting it to a group for the SRE team keeps the access limited to those people. One boundary: Monitoring Viewer applies to the whole project’s monitoring data, not only this chart, so a tighter scope would require a custom role.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> The role is correct, and the team could eventually find the chart, which is why it is tempting. A project ID does not deliver the chart. The team would have to navigate to the project and locate the dashboard themselves, so the specific sharing requirement is not met cleanly.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> The Monitoring Dashboard Configuration Viewer role grants read-only access to dashboard configurations. It is narrower, which makes it attractive for least privilege. It does not provide the permissions to read the underlying metric data, so the chart would not display what the team needs. The project ID also does not deliver the chart.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> It delivers the chart with a link, which is right, but pairs it with the configuration-only role. The SRE team could open the dashboard definition but not see the CPU data. Least privilege is not satisfied by a role that is too narrow to do the job.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is option D, because the dashboard-specific role looks like the most precise least-privilege choice. The distinction is that least privilege means the minimum needed to accomplish the task, and viewing a chart’s data requires metric read permission that the configuration role lacks. The answer would switch to D only if the team needed to see dashboard definitions without viewing any metric values. Option A is the second trap, because the role is right and the sharing method only looks equivalent. A terminology note: workspaces have been replaced by metrics scopes, and the project that hosts the scope is the scoping project. The old Dashboard Viewer role corresponds to the Monitoring Dashboard Configuration Viewer role, which is how the options name it.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Sharing involves two layers: delivery, which tells people where something is, and authorization, which decides whether they may see it. Least privilege picks the narrowest role that still allows the task to succeed, not the narrowest role in existence. Granting roles to a group rather than individuals keeps access manageable as team membership changes. Predefined roles are broad by design, and when even the narrowest predefined role is too wide, a custom role with specific permissions is the fix.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>An engineer adds the SRE team’s Google group to the scoping project with the Monitoring Viewer role, then pastes the chart link into the team’s channel. Anyone in the group can open the chart and see live CPU data but cannot change dashboards or alerting. If the link is forwarded to someone outside the group, that person still sees a permission error, so the data stays limited to the team.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>A link delivers the chart and a role authorizes the data, so send the link and grant the narrowest role that can read metrics.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For sharing monitoring content with least privilege, deliver it with a direct link and grant the narrowest role that can read the underlying data. Reject answers that skip the link or choose a role too narrow to display the data.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/access-control\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Monitoring: Control access with IAM:</strong></a> Supports: Monitoring Viewer granting read-only access to Monitoring data and configuration including custom dashboards, and the dashboard configuration viewer role being limited to dashboard configurations, which supports option C and rules out options B and D.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/charts/share-dashboards\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Monitoring: Share a custom dashboard:</strong></a> Supports: shared links requiring the recipient to have permission to view the dashboard, which explains why a link and a role are both needed.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\">Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 4, Implementing observability practices and troubleshooting issues): </a>Supports: domain alignment with managing dashboards, including creating, filtering, and sharing them.</p>","lessons":["d4l8","d4l13"]},"e1q51":{"id":"e1q51","exam":1,"n":51,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You run an application on a virtual machine (VM) created from a custom Debian image that has the Cloud Logging agent installed. The VM has the cloud-platform access scope, and the application writes its log output through syslog. You want to view these logs in the Logs Explorer in the Google Cloud console, but syslog does not appear in the “All logs” list. What should you do first?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Look in the Logs Explorer for the agent’s test log entry.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Install the latest version of the Cloud Logging agent.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Confirm that the VM’s service account access scopes include the monitoring.write scope.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Connect to the VM over SSH and run the command: ps ax | grep fluentd.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Connect to the VM over SSH and run the command: ps ax | grep fluentd.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The symptom is that a log stream that should exist is missing, and the agent is the component responsible for delivering it. The first troubleshooting step should be the cheapest check that splits the problem in half: is the collector running at all? Work through requirement, capability, elimination, decision. If the agent process is not running, nothing else matters yet. If it is running, move on to credentials, scopes, or configuration. The remaining options either look at downstream results that depend on the agent running, change the installation before diagnosing it, or check a scope that does not apply to the problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Syslog is not showing up”</strong> is a technical signal. Something between the application and Cloud Logging is broken, and the agent is the first suspect.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Image has the agent installed”</strong> is a technical signal. Installed does not mean running, so the process state is the next thing to verify.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Cloud-platform scope”</strong> is a hard constraint that eliminates scope problems. This scope already allows writing logs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“First”</strong> is a sequence instruction. It calls for the quickest, lowest-impact diagnostic before any change.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>On a Linux VM, the legacy Cloud Logging agent runs as a fluentd-based process. Running ps ax | grep fluentd on the VM shows immediately whether that process exists. Google’s troubleshooting guidance says that if you suspect the agent is not working, you should check that it is running and then try to send a test message. A missing process points to a stopped or disabled agent, which you can then restart or investigate, for example through the service status and the agent’s own log file. If the process is present, you know to look at credentials, scopes, or configuration next. The command is read-only, fast, and gives a clear branching result.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> The agent’s test log entry is a real verification method, and it is tempting because it uses the console you are already in. Google lists it as a way to verify a new installation. Here you already know the log is missing, and an absent test entry would not explain why. Checking the process directly is the more informative and more direct first step.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Reinstalling or upgrading can fix some agent problems, which makes it tempting. It is a change made before any diagnosis, takes more effort, and could simply reinstall the same configuration problem. Diagnostics should come before changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Incorrect:</strong> The monitoring.write scope is for the Monitoring agent. Writing logs requires logging.write, and the cloud-platform scope already covers it. Checking this scope would not reveal the cause and is based on the wrong agent.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is option A, because it appears in the official guidance and feels like a verification step. The distinction is purpose and order. It confirms that a fresh installation worked, while the process check answers whether the agent is running now, which is the question behind the symptom. The answer would switch to A if the scenario said the agent had just been installed and you needed to confirm the installation. Option C is a second trap because a scope problem is a common cause of missing telemetry, but here the scope is already broad and the named scope belongs to a different agent. A terminology note: the legacy logging and monitoring agents have reached end of support, and the Ops Agent is their supported successor. This item keeps the legacy agent because the fluentd process check is specific to it.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>When telemetry is missing, troubleshoot along the path from source to destination: the application writes the log, the agent collects and ships it, the credentials and permissions allow the write, and the backend stores and displays it. Start with the cheapest check that eliminates the largest part of the path, which is usually whether the collector process is running. Make changes only after diagnosis. This approach works for most agent-based pipelines. Once the agent is confirmed running, the next checks follow the path: agent logs, scopes, and service account permissions.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>An engineer connects to the VM, runs the process check, and finds no agent process. They check the service status and the agent’s log file, see that the agent was disabled by instance metadata on a copied image, and re-enable it. Logs begin to appear within minutes. Had the process been running, the engineer would have moved on to credentials and scopes, saving a needless reinstall.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Missing logs mean check whether the agent process is running before you check anything else.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For missing telemetry questions, choose the least invasive diagnostic that tests the collector first, such as confirming the agent process is running. Reject reinstalls, unrelated scopes, and checks that only make sense after the agent is known to work.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/agent/troubleshooting\">Google Cloud — Troubleshoot the Logging agent: </a>Supports: checking that the agent is running with ps ax | grep fluentd when you suspect it is not working, the test log entry being a separate installation check, and the scope guidance showing logging.write for the Logging agent and monitoring.write for the Monitoring agent, which supports option D and rules out options A and C as first steps.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/deprecations/logging-agent\"><strong>Google Cloud Observability — Legacy Monitoring and Logging agents end of support:</strong></a> Supports: the legacy agents having reached end of support, which explains the terminology note.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 4, Implementing observability practices and troubleshooting issues):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment with collecting logs and troubleshooting observability issues.</p>","lessons":["d4l5","d4l12"]},"e1q52":{"id":"e1q52","exam":1,"n":52,"domain":3,"html":"<p>An application runs on a single Compute Engine VM in one region. Occasional VM shutdowns interrupt users’ ongoing work. You need a solution that can be implemented quickly and reliably notifies you about shutdown events.</p>\n<p>Which approach should you choose?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. In Logs Explorer, create a log-based alert for VM log entries with <code>protoPayload.methodName</code> set to <code>v1.compute.instances.stop</code>.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Route the VM logs to BigQuery and schedule hourly queries that find entries with <code>protoPayload.methodName</code> set to <code>v1.compute.instances.stop</code>.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Modify the application to detect when its host VM is about to shut down and send a notification from the application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Use Cloud Scheduler to trigger health checks of the VM at randomized intervals, with no more than one hour between checks.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. In Logs Explorer, create a log-based alert for VM log entries with</strong><code>**protoPayload.methodName**</code><strong>set to</strong><code>**v1.compute.instances.stop**</code><strong>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The requirements are prompt notification and reliable detection; quick implementation favors an existing managed capability. A directly connects matching VM audit events to alert notifications. B and D introduce polling delays, while C depends on the affected application anticipating its host’s shutdown.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Shutdown events” — Technical signal:</strong> Look for VM lifecycle events in audit logs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Reliably notifies you” — Hard constraint:</strong> Finding records alone is insufficient; the solution needs an alerting policy and notification channel.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Implemented quickly” — Preference:</strong> A managed alerting capability avoids application changes and custom checking logic.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Google documents <code>v1.compute.instances.stop</code> as a Compute Engine stop operation. A log-based alert can match its audit entries and send notifications through configured channels. Logs Explorer provides the workflow to create this policy directly. </p>\n<p>The essential filter is:</p>\n<pre><code>      1. resource.type=\"gce_instance\"\n  2. protoPayload.methodName=\"v1.compute.instances.stop\"\n</code></pre>\n<p>Configure a notification channel and scope the filter to the relevant VM. This is the strongest listed approach for detecting the specified stop operation.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Hourly queries can identify matching historical records, making this tempting for analysis. However, detection can wait until the next query execution, and the option does not configure notifications. It fits periodic reporting better than prompt operational alerting.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Incorrect:</strong> Application logic might recognize some graceful shutdown signals, but it cannot reliably anticipate every unexpected VM termination or guarantee that it can send a notification before execution stops. It also requires development and deployment work.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Cloud Scheduler can invoke a target that performs a custom check. However, intervals approaching one hour delay detection and can miss outages that resolve between checks. Scheduler invokes work on a schedule; checking health and sending notifications require additional logic. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>BigQuery can find the same log records, but scheduled analysis introduces a waiting period. If the requirement changed to an <strong>hourly historical report of stop operations</strong> , B would become appropriate.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Event-based monitoring reacts to recorded events; polling observes state at intervals and can miss changes between observations.</p>\n<p>Coverage depends on the event filter. <code>**v1.compute.instances.stop**</code><strong>does not represent every possible VM shutdown cause.</strong> Guest OS terminations, host errors, and maintenance terminations use other event names. Production coverage should include the relevant additional events.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>An operations team scopes the alert to the application VM and routes notifications to its on-call channel. This reduces manual log inspection and speeds investigation of stop operations. Alerting improves detection; restoring availability still requires a separate recovery action.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For prompt notification about a recorded VM lifecycle event, match the event in logs and attach a notification channel.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When an operational event already appears in logs, prefer a managed log-based alert over hourly queries or application-based prediction. Check that the filter covers the failure causes you need to detect.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud Logging — Configure log-based alerting policies</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Creating alerts in Logs Explorer, matching log entries, and configuring notification channels.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/alerting/log-based-alerts\">Official documentation</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud Monitoring — Event types: Virtual machine terminations</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: The stop-event filter and the distinction between stop operations and other termination causes.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/dashboards/event-types#vm-terminations\">Official documentation</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud Scheduler — About Cloud Scheduler</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Scheduled target invocation and why custom health-check logic is required.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/scheduler/docs/overview\">Official documentation</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Objective 4.3, configuring alerting and alerting policies.</p>","lessons":["d3l6"]},"e1q53":{"id":"e1q53","exam":1,"n":53,"domain":3,"html":"<p>Your organization is adopting Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) culture and principles. A service you support recently suffered a limited outage. A manager on another team asks you for a formal account of what happened so that they can act on remediation work. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Write a postmortem covering the root causes, the resolution, lessons learned, and a prioritized list of action items, and share it only with the manager.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Write a postmortem covering the root causes, the resolution, lessons learned, and a prioritized list of action items, and publish it on the engineering organization’s document portal.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Write a postmortem covering the root causes, the resolution, lessons learned, the people responsible, and a list of action items for each person, and share it only with the manager.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Write a postmortem covering the root causes, the resolution, lessons learned, the people responsible, and a list of action items for each person, and publish it on the engineering organization’s document portal.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Write a postmortem covering the root causes, the resolution, lessons learned, and a prioritized list of action items, and publish it on the engineering organization’s document portal.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two decisions separate the four options, and SRE practice settles both. The first is content: whether the postmortem assigns blame to individuals or focuses on systems and process. The second is audience: whether it goes to one requester or is shared widely so the organization can learn from it. Work through requirement, capability, elimination, decision. The stated context is adopting SRE culture, so blamelessness and shared learning are the standards to apply. Naming people responsible and assigning actions per person fails the first test. Sending it to only one manager fails the second. Only one combination passes both.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Implement SRE culture and principles”</strong> is a hard constraint. It points to blameless postmortems that are shared for learning.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Formal explanation of what happened”</strong> is a technical signal. The artifact is a postmortem with causes, resolution, lessons learned, and action items.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Another team ... so they can action remediations”</strong> is a technical signal. The action items must be clear, prioritized, and visible to those who will act.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“The people responsible” and “action items for each person”</strong> is an exclusion signal. It frames the outcome around individuals, which conflicts with blamelessness.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Share with the manager only” versus “document portal”</strong> is a scoping instruction. It decides whether the learning stays private or is shared broadly.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>SRE postmortems are written to record impact, causes, the response, what went well and badly, and follow-up actions, and they are blameless. They focus on contributing factors in systems and processes rather than on individuals, because people report problems openly only when they are not punished for them. They are also shared widely, so other teams can learn from the incident and avoid repeating it. A prioritized list of action items tells the receiving team where to start. Publishing on an organization-wide portal gives the requesting manager access while making the learning available to everyone. Option B has the right content and the right audience.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> The content is correct, which makes it tempting, and it does serve the manager’s request. Sending it to one person keeps the lessons from the rest of the organization. SRE treats postmortems as shared learning artifacts, so limiting the audience wastes most of their value.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> It names the people responsible and assigns actions per person, which makes the document about blame instead of system causes. It also restricts the audience to one manager. It fails on both content and sharing.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> The audience is right because it is published widely, but naming responsible individuals and assigning per-person actions contradicts blamelessness. It is tempting because it looks accountable and thorough. The effect is that people become less willing to surface problems honestly, which weakens future reliability.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is option D, because publishing widely is correct and per-person action items look like accountability. The distinction is that SRE places accountability on fixing systems with owned action items, not on assigning fault to people. The answer would switch to D only if the culture were blame-oriented, which contradicts the stated goal. Option A is the second trap, since it satisfies the requester but ignores the shared-learning purpose.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A blameless postmortem assumes that people acted reasonably on the information they had and that failures reflect gaps in systems, tooling, or process. This encourages honest reporting, which in turn produces better fixes. Wide sharing converts a single team’s incident into organizational knowledge. Action items should be specific, prioritized, and tracked, with owners assigned to the work, not to the fault. This model fits incidents of any size. Genuinely sensitive information, such as security details, may need redaction or restricted sharing, but the default is open and blameless.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>The on-call engineer writes the postmortem with a timeline, root causes, and prioritized actions, and posts it on the engineering portal. The manager’s team picks up the actions relevant to them and tracks them to completion. Other teams read it and check whether they share the same weakness. Over time, engineers report near misses more readily because past postmortems have focused on fixing systems, not on faulting individuals.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>A postmortem is blameless and shared widely, with action items for the system and not blame for the people.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks how to communicate an incident under SRE principles, choose the blameless postmortem shared broadly. Reject versions that name people responsible or keep the document private.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/\">Google SRE Book — Postmortem Culture: Learning from Failure: </a>Supports: blameless postmortems focused on systems and process, with wide sharing for organizational learning, which supports option B and rules out options A, C, and D.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/postmortem-culture/\">Google SRE Workbook — Postmortem Culture: Learning from Failure: </a>Supports: postmortem content such as root causes, impact, lessons learned, and action items, and the practice of sharing them openly.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 3, Applying site reliability engineering practices):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment with managing the service lifecycle and mitigating incident impact on users.</p>","lessons":["d3l5"]},"e1q54":{"id":"e1q54","exam":1,"n":54,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You support a Node.js application that runs in production on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). The application sends several HTTP requests to other applications that it depends on. You want to anticipate which of those dependencies could cause performance problems. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Instrument all of the applications with Cloud Profiler.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Instrument all of the applications with Cloud Trace, and review the HTTP requests between services.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Use Cloud Debugger to examine how the logic runs inside each application, and instrument all of the applications.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Modify the Node.js application to log the time of each HTTP request and response to its dependencies, and use Cloud Logging to find the dependencies that perform poorly.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Instrument all of the applications with Cloud Trace, and review the HTTP requests between services.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The goal is to see which dependencies are slow, which is a question about time spent across service boundaries. Start by naming the signal you need: the latency of each call between services, attributed to the service that handled it. Then match each option to the signal it produces. Some tools look inside a single process, one produces partial and manual data from only one side, and one is built to follow a request across services and show where time goes. Work through requirement, capability, elimination, decision, and the cross-service tool is the only one that answers the question directly.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Several HTTP requests to dependent applications”</strong> is a technical signal. The performance risk sits in calls between services, so the data must follow requests across them.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Anticipate which dependent applications might cause performance issues”</strong> is a hard constraint. You need comparative latency by dependency, not a single application’s internals.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Instrument all applications”</strong> is a technical signal in the options. Tracing the full chain requires every service to participate so spans connect into one trace.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Running in production on GKE”</strong> is a context signal. The solution must work on live traffic with low overhead and without code stepping.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Trace is a distributed tracing system that records how long a request takes and how long each operation within it takes, including calls to other services. When each application is instrumented, the spans from each service join into a single trace, and the trace view shows which downstream call consumed the time. You can compare latency by dependency and see changes over time, which lets you spot dependencies that are getting slower before they cause outages. Node.js is supported, and OpenTelemetry libraries with a Cloud Trace exporter are the common way to instrument. One boundary: tracing shows where time is spent but not why a function is slow, so a profiler is the follow-up tool for in-process causes.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong> A profiler samples CPU and memory use of the code within a single application. It is tempting because it is a performance tool that can be added to every application. It shows which functions consume resources, not how long calls between services take, so it cannot rank dependencies by latency.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Outdated:</strong> Cloud Debugger inspected the state of a running application at selected code locations. It is tempting because it appears to show what each application is doing. It is a debugging tool for application state, not a measure of inter-service latency, and the service was shut down in May 2023, so it is no longer available.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Logging request and response times can show how long the Node.js application waits for each dependency, so it can work. It is tempting because it uses a familiar tool. It requires modifying the application and measures only the caller’s view, so you have no breakdown of where time goes inside the dependencies. Correlating logs across services is manual and does not give a request-level, end-to-end picture.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is option D, because logging call durations feels like a direct way to rank dependencies. The distinction is that it gives one side of each call, requires code changes, and needs manual correlation, while tracing is built for end-to-end request paths. The answer would switch to D only if tracing instrumentation were not possible for the application. Option A is the second trap, since profiling sounds like performance analysis but looks inside one process. A terminology note: Cloud Debugger has been shut down, and the open source Snapshot Debugger is an archived alternative, so option C is outdated as well as a different use case.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Distributed tracing follows a request through every service it touches and records a span for each operation, linked by a shared trace identifier propagated in request headers. This lets you see the critical path and attribute latency to specific services. Profiling, logging, and debugging each answer different questions: profiling shows resource use in code, logging records discrete events, and debugging inspects state. Tracing is the right tool when the question is where time goes across a chain of calls. It requires instrumentation in each service, or the trace breaks at the uninstrumented hop.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team adds the OpenTelemetry SDK with the Cloud Trace exporter to each service, deploys it, and reviews the trace heatmap and the slowest traces in the console. They notice that one dependency’s latency creeps up during peak hours, and they raise it with its owner and add capacity before it breaches the service objective. Sampling keeps the overhead small in production, and linking trace identifiers to logs helps with root-cause analysis.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>To find which dependency is slow, trace the request across services, and use a profiler only for what happens inside one service.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks which service in a call chain is slow, choose distributed tracing across all services. Reject profilers, debuggers, and one-sided logging that cannot give an end-to-end latency breakdown.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/trace/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Trace overview:</strong></a> Supports: Trace recording how long requests and operations such as calls to other services take, the Node.js and OpenTelemetry support, and trace and span analysis for latency, which supports option B.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/deprecations/debugger-deprecation\"><strong>Google Cloud Observability — Cloud Debugger deprecation:</strong></a> Supports: Cloud Debugger having been deprecated and shut down on May 31, 2023, which marks option C as outdated.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 4, Implementing observability practices and troubleshooting issues):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment with capturing and analyzing distributed traces using tracing frameworks such as OpenTelemetry.</p>","lessons":["d4l10"]},"e1q55":{"id":"e1q55","exam":1,"n":55,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your organization provisions a Compute Engine managed instance group (MIG) through a Terraform-based CI/CD pipeline. The MIG runs an application using an instance template that generates unique names with <code>name_prefix</code>. The MIG’s Terraform resource directly references that template resource.</p>\n<p>After you modify the machine type in the instance-template configuration, <code>terraform apply</code> fails with this error:</p>\n<pre><code>      1. Error waiting for Deleting Instance Template:\n  2. The instance_template resource\n  3. 'projects/example-project/global/instanceTemplates/app-template-202610310001'\n  4. is already being used by\n  5. 'projects/example-project/regions/us-central1/instanceGroupManagers/app-mig'\n</code></pre>\n<p>You must replace the template and update the MIG to reference its replacement while minimizing application disruption and additional pipeline executions.</p>\n<p>Which action should you take?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p><strong>A.</strong> Destroy the MIG, replace the instance template with one containing the new machine type, and recreate the MIG using the replacement template.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>B.</strong> Perform separate pipeline executions to provision another instance template, switch the MIG to that template, and remove the previous template.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>C.</strong> Temporarily remove the MIG from Terraform state, apply the template replacement, and import the existing MIG back into state afterward.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>D.</strong> Configure <code>create_before_destroy = true</code> inside a <code>lifecycle</code> block on the instance-template resource, and run the pipeline again.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Add a</strong><code>**lifecycle**</code><strong>block to the instance-template resource and set</strong><code>**create_before_destroy = true**</code><strong>, then rerun the pipeline.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The error identifies a replacement-ordering problem: Terraform attempts to delete a template that the MIG still references. Preserve the MIG and let Terraform coordinate the replacement within one apply operation. D addresses that ordering directly; A unnecessarily rebuilds the group, B requires additional runs, and C changes state bookkeeping without removing the cloud dependency.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Already being used by” — Technical signal:</strong> The existing MIG reference blocks template deletion.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“</strong><code>**name_prefix**</code><strong>” — Technical signal:</strong> The original and replacement templates can coexist under different names.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Directly references the template resource” — Technical signal:</strong> Terraform can track the dependency and update the reference.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Minimizing application disruption” — Preference:</strong> Avoid tearing down the MIG.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Number of pipeline runs” — Preference:</strong> Prefer dependency-aware replacement within one apply.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Instance templates are immutable, so changing the machine type requires a replacement. Add this block inside the existing instance-template resource:</p>\n<pre><code>      1. lifecycle {\n  2.   create_before_destroy = true\n  3. }\n</code></pre>\n<p>With the stated unique naming and direct reference, Terraform can:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>Create the replacement template.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Update the MIG to reference it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Delete the original template after the reference changes.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The Google provider documentation explicitly recommends this configuration for instance templates used by instance group managers. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Rebuilding the group removes the dependency but unnecessarily disrupts application capacity. Replacing the template does not require destroying the MIG.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> The sequence is valid, but splitting it across pipeline runs adds coordination and intermediate configurations. D lets Terraform manage the required ordering declaratively.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Incorrect:</strong> Removing the MIG from state leaves the actual group and its template reference intact. The deletion blocker remains. If the MIG configuration stays present, Terraform can also plan to create another group, potentially encountering an existing-name conflict. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>B describes the right operational sequence but misses that Terraform can execute it within one apply. Separate runs become reasonable when a changed requirement mandates independently approved creation, cutover, and cleanup stages.</p>\n<p>Also, <code>**create_before_destroy**</code><strong>does not guarantee a disruption-free rollout of the new machine type to every existing VM</strong> ; that depends on the MIG’s update configuration. </p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Distinguish <strong>replacement ordering</strong> , <strong>resource naming</strong> , and <strong>workload rollout</strong>. Lifecycle rules control replacement ordering; unique names permit old and new resources to coexist; the MIG’s update policy controls how existing VMs adopt the new configuration. A fixed conflicting name could prevent creation even with <code>create_before_destroy</code> enabled. </p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Use the lifecycle fix alongside an appropriate proactive MIG update policy when existing VMs must receive the new machine type automatically. Configure surge capacity, availability limits, and health checks to control disruption. Additional surge VMs require available capacity and can temporarily increase compute costs. </p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For an immutable resource with live dependents: create its replacement, redirect the dependents, then retire the original.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When Terraform cannot delete an in-use template, look for <code>create_before_destroy</code>, compatible unique naming, and a direct resource reference. Resolve the dependency ordering while treating the application’s rolling update as a separate concern.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-google/blob/main/website/docs/r/compute_instance_template.html.markdown\"><strong>Terraform Google Provider — Instance Templates: Using with Instance Group Manager</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Template immutability, unique naming, and the create–update–delete sequence.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/meta-arguments/lifecycle\"><strong>HashiCorp Terraform — Lifecycle Reference</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: <code>create_before_destroy</code> behavior and coexistence constraints.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/cli/commands/state/rm\"><strong>HashiCorp Terraform — terraform state rm</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Why removing state does not remove the actual MIG or its cloud dependencies.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instance-groups/rolling-out-updates-to-managed-instance-groups\"><strong>Google Compute Engine — Automatically Apply VM Configuration Updates in a MIG</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Proactive versus opportunistic updates, disruption controls, and surge-capacity trade-offs.</p>","lessons":["d2l10"]},"e1q56":{"id":"e1q56","exam":1,"n":56,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You are leading a DevOps transformation for an organization that operates a production service. The DevOps team maintains the service infrastructure and handles on-call incidents. The Software Development team authors, submits, and reviews application code. Neither team has documented or published service level objectives (SLOs).</p>\n<p>You want both teams to share accountability for service reliability. Which assignment of responsibilities best establishes this operating model?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p><strong>A.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>DevOps team:</strong> Maintain the infrastructure, handle on-call incidents, and review application code.</p>\n<p><strong>Software Development team:</strong> Author and submit code for DevOps review, and unilaterally establish and publish SLOs that DevOps is responsible for achieving.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>B.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>DevOps team:</strong> Maintain the infrastructure and review application code.</p>\n<p><strong>Software Development team:</strong> Author and submit code for DevOps review, assume all on-call responsibilities, and unilaterally establish and publish SLOs that DevOps is responsible for achieving.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>C.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>DevOps team:</strong> Maintain the service infrastructure.</p>\n<p><strong>Software Development team:</strong> Author and submit application code for review.</p>\n<p><strong>Both teams:</strong> Review application code, participate in a shared on-call rotation, and collaboratively define, agree to, and publish the service’s SLOs.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p><strong>D.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>DevOps team:</strong> Maintain the infrastructure and retain all on-call responsibilities.</p>\n<p><strong>Software Development team:</strong> Author, submit, and review application code, and establish and publish the service’s SLOs independently.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>DevOps team: Manage the service infrastructure.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Software Development team: Write and submit application code for review.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Both teams: Participate in code reviews, share on-call incident support through a rotation, and jointly agree on, adopt, and publish SLOs for the service.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The decisive requirement is <strong>shared accountability for reliability</strong>. Look for collaboration across preventive work, incident response, and reliability targets. C addresses all three; A, B, and D retain unilateral SLO decisions and assign incident support exclusively to one team.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Joint-ownership model” — Hard constraint:</strong> Both teams must participate in reliability responsibilities and decisions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Neither team has published…SLOs” — Technical signal:</strong> The model must establish explicit, agreed reliability targets.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Both teams…take responsibility” — Hard constraint:</strong> Moving operational duties entirely from one team to the other is insufficient.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>C connects development decisions with production outcomes. Shared reviews bring application and operational knowledge into changes; shared on-call participation exposes both teams to production problems; jointly accepted SLOs establish a common reliability goal. Google’s SRE guidance explicitly supports shared service ownership between development and SRE teams. </p>\n<p>The rotation should include appropriate training, runbooks, and escalation paths so participants can respond effectively and sustainably. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Adding operational expertise to code review is useful, but developers remain outside on-call support and impose targets on DevOps without joint agreement. Collaboration in one activity does not establish the requested ownership model.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> Developer on-call participation is valuable, but this option transfers <strong>all</strong> incident support to developers while leaving SLO achievement assigned to DevOps. It redistributes duties without establishing shared reliability decisions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> This preserves the existing separation between application development and production support. Publishing SLOs adds documentation, but unilateral target setting leaves the teams without a jointly accepted reliability commitment.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Google’s SLO guidance calls for agreement among product stakeholders, developers, and the team responsible for production reliability. That directly distinguishes C from all three distractors. </p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Transferring the pager is not the same as sharing ownership.</strong> Developer-only on-call can be appropriate for a developer-operated service, but B still retains unilateral SLO decisions and divided accountability. Its operational reassignment does not satisfy this scenario.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Shared ownership combines <strong>common outcomes, agreed decision rules, and participation in maintaining the service</strong>. Teams can retain specialist responsibilities—such as infrastructure administration—while sharing reliability accountability. An SLO defines the reliability target; an agreed error-budget policy helps translate that target into decisions about feature delivery and reliability work. </p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A service team can introduce a trained rotation spanning developers and DevOps, review production incidents together, and prioritize corrective work using agreed reliability goals. This gives developers direct operational feedback and creates a path for recurring incidents to become engineering improvements rather than repeated support work. </p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Shared reliability ownership means shared targets, shared operational feedback, and shared improvement work.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For joint-ownership questions, select the model that combines collaborative engineering, shared incident responsibility, and mutually accepted SLOs. Specialist duties can remain separate while accountability for the service is shared.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/how-sre-relates/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — How SRE Relates to DevOps</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Shared service ownership, collaboration, and reducing organizational silos.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — Implementing SLOs</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Stakeholder agreement, documented SLOs, and error-budget policies; explains why unilateral target setting weakens the alternatives.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/on-call/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — On-Call</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Developer participation in production support, sustainable rotations, training, and escalation procedures.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Domain alignment with “Applying site reliability engineering practices,” particularly objective 3.1 concerning reliability and SLOs.</p>","lessons":["d3l3"]},"e1q57":{"id":"e1q57","exam":1,"n":57,"domain":1,"html":"<p>An organization operates two applications in separate Google Cloud projects for development, staging, and production. The projects are grouped by environment:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Development folder:</strong> <code>app-one-dev</code> and <code>app-two-dev</code></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Staging folder:</strong> <code>app-one-staging</code> and <code>app-two-staging</code></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Production folder:</strong> <code>app-one-prod</code> and <code>app-two-prod</code></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You must provide dashboards for each environment that show metrics from both projects in that environment and omit metrics from the other environments.</p>\n<p>Which configuration of scoping projects and metrics scopes best follows Google’s recommended practices?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p><strong>A.</strong> Provision a single dedicated monitoring project, include all six workload projects in its metrics scope, and store every environment’s dashboards in that project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>B.</strong> Provision a dedicated monitoring project for each environment folder. Include only that environment’s two workload projects in its metrics scope, and store the environment’s dashboards in the corresponding monitoring project.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p><strong>C.</strong> Designate <code>app-one-prod</code> as the organization’s central scoping project. Add the remaining five workload projects to its metrics scope, and store all environment dashboards there.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>D.</strong> Designate <code>app-one-dev</code>, <code>app-one-staging</code>, and <code>app-one-prod</code> as their environments’ scoping projects. Add the matching <code>app-two</code> project to each metrics scope, and store the dashboards in those existing workload projects.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Create a new, dedicated scoping project for each folder. Add only that folder’s two workload projects to the corresponding metrics scope, and create its dashboards in that scoping project.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>First identify the required grouping: <strong>both applications together, with separate views for each environment</strong>. Then apply the architectural preference: Google recommends a new project, or one without resources, as the scoping project for cross-project monitoring. B combines the intended grouping with that recommendation. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Both projects in the corresponding folder” — Hard constraint:</strong> Each environment’s view must cover both applications.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Exclude metrics…in the other folders” — Hard constraint:</strong> Dashboards must avoid mixing environments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Google-recommended practices” — Selection criterion:</strong> Distinguish a technically functional configuration from the recommended scoping-project design.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>B creates three dedicated monitoring hosts, each with the appropriate workload membership:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Development:</strong> Add <code>app-one-dev</code> and <code>app-two-dev</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Staging:</strong> Add <code>app-one-staging</code> and <code>app-two-staging</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Production:</strong> Add <code>app-one-prod</code> and <code>app-two-prod</code>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Dashboard configurations reside in their respective scoping projects. Each scope also includes its own scoping project; the two workload projects are its additional monitored projects. Keeping these hosts free of application workloads follows Google’s documented recommendation. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A central dedicated project supports cross-project monitoring. However, its scope includes every environment, so environment-specific charts require appropriate filtering. It would better fit a requirement for consolidated visibility across all environments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> This also permits centralized monitoring with filters, but additionally uses an application’s production project as the monitoring host instead of a dedicated project.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> This can satisfy the environment-specific display requirement. Existing workload projects can host metrics scopes, but this choice does not follow the recommendation to use new or resource-free scoping projects. <strong>Its weakness is the architectural preference, not technical impossibility.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Do not assume that an existing project cannot be a scoping project. D works technically. Also, A can produce environment-specific dashboards with filters; <strong>B is the best match to the stated recommendation, rather than the only functional design</strong>. Google does not require one metrics scope per folder universally.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A <strong>scoping project</strong> hosts monitoring configuration; its <strong>metrics scope</strong> determines which projects’ metrics are available for charting. Chart filters further narrow that available data.</p>\n<p>Ordinary folder organization does not automatically configure scope membership. <strong>App-enabled folders</strong> have a separate management-project mechanism that can synchronize membership; the question does not specify that setup. </p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Create a dedicated production monitoring project, add the two production workload projects, and save the production dashboards there. When another production workload project is introduced, explicitly update the scope so its metrics become available. This makes the monitoring membership an intentional configuration choice. </p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Use dedicated scoping projects and match monitored-project membership to the intended monitoring boundary.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For cross-project monitoring questions, distinguish <strong>scope membership</strong> , <strong>dashboard filtering</strong> , and <strong>the choice of monitoring host</strong>. Here, dedicated hosts with environment-specific membership make B the strongest answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/settings\"><strong>Google Cloud Monitoring — Metrics scopes overview</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Scoping-project behavior, dashboard ownership, filtering, and the recommendation for new or resource-free scoping projects.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/settings/multiple-projects\"><strong>Google Cloud Monitoring — Configure a metrics scope</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Explicitly adding monitored projects and controlling which projects’ metrics are available.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/settings/metrics-scope-app-enabled-folders\"><strong>Google Cloud Monitoring — Metrics scopes for app-enabled folders</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: The distinction between ordinary folders and automatically managed scopes for app-enabled folders.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Domain alignment with objective 1.1, which explicitly includes multi-project monitoring and logging.</p>","lessons":["d1l5"]},"e1q58":{"id":"e1q58","exam":1,"n":58,"domain":1,"html":"<p>Your organization requires developers on the same team to use a standardized development environment that supports commonly used integrated development environments (IDEs). You are responsible for maintaining software updates and security patches for these environments.</p>\n<p>Which Google-recommended approach should you use to provide a consistent development environment for each team?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Take daily Compute Engine snapshots of a fully patched “golden” VM and make them available for developers to update their machines.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Publish a software-update checklist and supporting scripts, and have developers apply the updates to their machines.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Build an image containing the latest dependencies and install it on developers’ laptops.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Define a Cloud Workstations configuration for each development team.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Define a Cloud Workstations configuration for each development team.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The requirements combine a shared environment template, IDE support, and ongoing maintenance. The appropriate solution must provide a reproducible baseline and a reliable way to distribute changes. D provides managed workstation templates; A, B, and C leave environment adoption or subsequent maintenance dependent on individual machine-update workflows.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Developers on the same team” — Hard constraint:</strong> Standardization must support team-specific requirements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Standardized development environment” — Technical signal:</strong> Look for a reusable configuration that defines the environment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Commonly used … IDEs” — Hard constraint:</strong> The solution must support developer tooling.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Maintaining software updates and security patches” — Hard constraint:</strong> Initial provisioning alone does not satisfy the full lifecycle requirement.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Workstations configurations act as templates. They define the container image, IDE, machine settings, and storage used by developers’ workstations. Administrators share a configuration with a team, and developers create their individual workstations from that common definition. Configuration changes apply when the workstations next start. </p>\n<p>For patch maintenance, use updated base images, regularly rebuild custom images, and configure a running timeout to support refreshes between sessions. <strong>Custom packages and dependencies remain the organization’s responsibility</strong> ; selecting Cloud Workstations does not automatically maintain every added dependency. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong> Snapshots capture disk contents and support restoration. A snapshot of a patched machine preserves a useful baseline, which makes this approach tempting. However, publishing snapshots does not ensure that developers adopt them or keep their environments synchronized. Snapshots primarily address disk backup and recovery. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Checklists and scripts can document and automate updates. However, the proposed process relies on developers executing them correctly and consistently. It provides no managed provisioning or refresh mechanism to maintain the required team baseline.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A common laptop image can standardize the initial installation. However, the option does not establish an ongoing process for distributing patches and maintaining consistency after installation. Laptop imaging would require additional endpoint-management processes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>A common image can establish consistency at installation time, but ongoing consistency also requires a maintained update process. If the task were limited to provisioning an initial software baseline on laptops, C would fit.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A reproducible development environment combines an approved template with a controlled lifecycle: define the tools and dependencies, provision environments from that definition, update it, and refresh the environments. A shared starting point alone does not prevent configuration drift.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team maintains a Java development image containing the approved JDK, IDE, and build tools. After validating a security update, it publishes the revised image and updates the team’s workstation configuration. Refreshing workstations through this process reduces individual setup work and machine-specific build differences.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Team-wide development consistency requires a shared template and a managed refresh process.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question combines standardized developer environments, IDE support, and ongoing updates, look for Cloud Workstations configurations. Remember that updates require workstation refreshes and that custom dependencies still need maintenance.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Workstations overview</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Configuration templates, IDE access, team provisioning, and application of configuration changes.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/workstations/docs/overview\">Official documentation</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Workstations security best practices</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Updated base images, running timeouts, custom-image rebuilds, and responsibility for added dependencies.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/workstations/docs/set-up-security-best-practices\">Official documentation</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud — About archive and standard disk snapshots</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Snapshot backup and restoration capabilities and the distinction relevant to option A.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/disks/snapshots\">Official documentation</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Objective 1.5, enabling secure cloud development environments using tools such as Cloud Workstations. <a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\">services.google.com</a></p>","lessons":["d1l11"]},"e1q59":{"id":"e1q59","exam":1,"n":59,"domain":3,"html":"<p>An application running on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) uses separate blue and green Deployments in the same namespace. Production traffic reaches the application through an Ingress and a Service. The relevant manifest excerpts are:</p>\n<pre><code>      1. apiVersion: apps/v1\n  2. kind: Deployment\n  3. metadata:\n  4.   name: app-green\n  5. spec:\n  6.   selector:\n  7.     matchLabels:\n  8.       app: my-app\n  9.       version: green\n  10.   template:\n  11.     metadata:\n  12.       labels:\n  13.         app: my-app\n  14.         version: green\n  15.     # Container configuration omitted.\n  16. ---\n  17. apiVersion: apps/v1\n  18. kind: Deployment\n  19. metadata:\n  20.   name: app-blue\n  21. spec:\n  22.   selector:\n  23.     matchLabels:\n  24.       app: my-app\n  25.       version: blue\n  26.   template:\n  27.     metadata:\n  28.       labels:\n  29.         app: my-app\n  30.         version: blue\n  31.     # Container configuration omitted.\n  32. ---\n  33. apiVersion: v1\n  34. kind: Service\n  35. metadata:\n  36.   name: app-svc\n  37. spec:\n  38.   selector:\n  39.     app: my-app\n  40.     version: green\n  41.   ports:\n  42.     - port: 80\n  43.       targetPort: 8080\n  44. ---\n  45. apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\n  46. kind: Ingress\n  47. metadata:\n  48.   name: app-ingress\n  49. spec:\n  50.   defaultBackend:\n  51.     service:\n  52.       name: app-svc\n  53.       port:\n  54.         number: 80\n</code></pre>\n<p>The green Deployment is serving a newly released application version. Monitoring now indicates widespread request failures that were not observed during testing. The blue Deployment continues to run the previous stable version, is healthy, and already has enough capacity to serve production traffic.</p>\n<p>You must restore service promptly while keeping the failing release running for developer investigation.</p>\n<p>Which action best meets these requirements?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p><strong>A.</strong> Deploy the failing application version from <code>app-green</code> to <code>app-blue</code> as well.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>B.</strong> Revert the application version running in <code>app-green</code> to the previous stable release.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>C.</strong> Broaden the <code>app-svc</code> selector by removing <code>version: green</code> and retaining only <code>app: my-app</code>.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>D.</strong> Change the <code>app-svc</code> selector to require <code>app: my-app</code> and <code>version: blue</code>.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Update the</strong><code>**app-svc**</code><strong>selector to match both</strong><code>**app: my-app**</code><strong>and</strong><code>**version: blue**</code><strong>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Recovery must satisfy two constraints: use the already healthy release and preserve the failing release for investigation. Changing the Service selector redirects production traffic without requiring an application rollout. D satisfies both constraints; replacing either Deployment or admitting both versions into the backend pool does not.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Blue/green deployment strategy” — Technical signal:</strong> A separate release environment is available for traffic switching.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Previous stable release…healthy…sufficient capacity” — Technical signal:</strong> Blue can accept production traffic without first being rebuilt or scaled.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Restore service quickly” — Preference:</strong> Reuse the prepared environment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Preserving the failing release” — Hard constraint:</strong> Keep the green workload available for investigation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>The Service selects</strong><code>**version: green**</code><strong>— Technical signal:</strong> This selector is the relevant traffic-switching control.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Change the Service’s selector to:</p>\n<pre><code>      1. spec:\n  2.   selector:\n  3.     app: my-app\n  4.     version: blue\n</code></pre>\n<p>Both requirements must match, selecting blue Pods and excluding green Pods. Kubernetes reconciles the Service’s EndpointSlices with the matching Pods. The Ingress still references <code>app-svc</code> on port <code>80</code>, while neither Deployment’s Pod template changes. Green therefore remains available for investigation.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Incorrect:</strong> Updating blue to the failing version removes the prepared stable fallback. It also leaves the production Service selecting green, so it does not perform the required recovery.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> A Deployment rollback can restore an earlier Pod template, but it changes the failing workload and can replace its running Pods. That conflicts with preserving the running release for investigation and requires a rollout that D avoids. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Both Deployments’ Pods have <code>app: my-app</code>. Removing the version requirement makes both colors eligible backends, so requests can continue reaching the failing green release. This does not establish an exclusive switch to stable blue. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Distinguish <strong>rolling back traffic</strong> from <strong>rolling back a Deployment</strong>. D switches traffic to an existing stable environment. B modifies the failing environment itself. Keeping the failing workload running makes the traffic switch the stronger recovery action.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A Deployment controls the desired workload; a Service selector determines which Pods belong to its backend set. These controls are separate: changing Service membership does not trigger a Deployment rollout. Blue and green are labels—their operational roles come from the deployed versions and current routing configuration. </p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>After applying the selector change, verify that <code>app-svc</code> resolves to blue backends and that production error rates recover. Developers can investigate green without rebuilding it. Allow for controller and load-balancer reconciliation rather than assuming the cutover completes instantaneously.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>When a healthy standby is ready, switch the Service selector to recover while preserving the failing workload.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For blue/green incident recovery, identify the healthy environment and the object selecting production backends. Switch routing to the stable version while keeping the failing Deployment intact; broadening a selector admits additional versions rather than replacing the failing backend set.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/\"><strong>Kubernetes — Service</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Pod selection and automatic reconciliation of Service EndpointSlices.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/working-with-objects/labels/\"><strong>Kubernetes — Labels and Selectors</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: AND semantics across selector requirements and why removing the version requirement admits both colors.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/\"><strong>Kubernetes — Deployments</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Pod-template rollouts and the distinction between Deployment rollback and Service traffic switching.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Objective 3.3, mitigating incident impact through traffic redirection and rollback strategies.</p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e1q60":{"id":"e1q60","exam":1,"n":60,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You are responding to an incident affecting an inventory-update microservice deployed on a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) Autopilot cluster.</p>\n<p>An online retailer publishes order events to Pub/Sub. Multiple replicas of the microservice can consume events from the same pull subscription and process inventory updates concurrently in a warehouse system.</p>\n<p>A promotional campaign causes a sudden increase in orders. Inventory updates become delayed, and customers can place orders for products that have already sold out. Monitoring shows these changes from normal operation:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Average Pod CPU utilization:</strong> Normally 20% of the CPU limit; currently 30%.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Average Pod memory utilization:</strong> Normally 10% of the memory limit; currently 10%.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Average oldest unacknowledged message age:</strong> Normally 347 milliseconds; currently 8,074 milliseconds.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Average undelivered message count:</strong> Normally 5 messages; currently 14,705 messages.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Average acknowledgment latency:</strong> Normally 312 milliseconds; currently 354 milliseconds.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Which action should you take to reduce inventory-processing delays while minimizing customer impact?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p><strong>A.</strong> Shorten the Pub/Sub subscription’s acknowledgment deadline.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>B.</strong> Add a virtual waiting room that limits incoming store traffic to its normal pre-campaign rate.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>C.</strong> Scale out the inventory-processing microservice by increasing its Pod replica count.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p><strong>D.</strong> Raise the CPU and memory limits configured for each inventory-processing Pod.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Increase the number of Pod replicas running the inventory-processing microservice.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The sharp increases in backlog size and message age indicate that aggregate consumer throughput is insufficient for the increased order volume. CPU and memory measurements do not show corresponding pressure against their reported limits.</p>\n<p>Because processing can be distributed across replicas, increasing the number of consumers directly addresses the throughput shortfall while allowing customers to continue shopping. Google recommends adding subscriber processes or containers when backlog count and age increase together. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Shared pull subscription” — Technical signal:</strong> Additional subscriber replicas can participate in processing the same backlog.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Distributed across multiple Pod replicas” — Technical signal:</strong> Horizontal scaling is explicitly supported.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Backlog count and oldest-message age increase sharply — Technical signal:</strong> Consumers are falling behind incoming work.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>CPU reaches only 30%; memory remains at 10% — Technical signal:</strong> Increasing per-Pod resource limits is not supported by the reported averages.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Minimize…impact on customers” — Preference:</strong> Increase processing capacity before introducing customer-facing admission restrictions.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Additional Pods provide more subscriber workers, increasing potential concurrent message processing. This can increase inventory-update throughput and drain the backlog.</p>\n<p>The important boundary is downstream capacity: the warehouse system, connection pools, and other shared dependencies must accommodate the additional concurrency. Scale and verify that completed processing increases and backlog age falls; these measurements do not establish an exact replica count. GKE supports horizontal Pod autoscaling on Autopilot, including scaling from external metrics. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Incorrect:</strong> The acknowledgment deadline governs when an unacknowledged message becomes eligible for redelivery. Shortening it does not add processing capacity and can increase redelivery and repeated work if messages are still being processed. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Admission control can reduce incoming demand, but it delays customers and does not increase consumer throughput. It becomes more appropriate if a hard downstream capacity limit prevents effective scaling.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> More resources per Pod can help resource-constrained workers. Here, reported utilization is well below the limits, while the workload explicitly supports additional replicas. Raising limits alone does not necessarily increase subscriber concurrency.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Low CPU utilization does not imply sufficient application throughput.</strong> Consumers can fall behind because concurrency is limited or workers spend time waiting for I/O.</p>\n<p>Conversely, averages do not conclusively rule out individual overloaded Pods or downstream bottlenecks. C is the strongest action from the supplied evidence, followed by verification that scaling improves throughput.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Horizontal scaling adds workers; vertical scaling increases resources available to each worker. A backlog shrinks when aggregate successful processing exceeds incoming work long enough to catch up.</p>\n<p>Also, the question reports CPU utilization relative to <strong>limits</strong>. Percentage-based CPU targets in an HPA use <strong>requests</strong> , so the reported 30% should not be treated as an HPA target measurement.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Increase consumer replicas during the incident, then configure HPA using backlog signals alongside a complementary signal such as resource utilization. Google recommends multiple scaling signals and regional Pub/Sub backlog metrics for resilience. Bound scaling by tested downstream capacity to avoid moving the bottleneck into the warehouse system. </p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Growing backlog plus parallelizable consumers suggests adding workers, even when CPU utilization is modest.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When backlog size and age rise together, compare consumer throughput with demand. If processing can run across replicas and per-Pod resource exhaustion is not evident, scale consumers horizontally and confirm that the backlog begins draining.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/monitoring\"><strong>Google Cloud Pub/Sub — Monitor Pub/Sub in Cloud Monitoring</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Backlog diagnosis, adding subscriber containers, and acknowledgment-deadline redelivery behavior.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/horizontalpodautoscaler\"><strong>Google Kubernetes Engine — Horizontal Pod Autoscaling</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Autopilot workload scaling, external metrics, and request-based utilization targets.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/metrics-autoscaling-best-practices\"><strong>Google Cloud Pub/Sub — Best Practices for Using Pub/Sub Metrics as a Scaling Signal</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Multiple scaling signals and recommended regional backlog metrics.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Objective 3.3, mitigating incident impact by adding capacity, and objective 3.2 on autoscaling.</p>","lessons":["d3l7"]},"e2q1":{"id":"e2q1","exam":2,"n":1,"domain":1,"html":"<p>Your organization packages its containerized applications using Helm, and your deployments depend on a mix of publicly hosted and internally maintained charts. Your security team has raised a concern that depending directly on a public Helm chart repository introduces risk. You want a single, uniform way to manage every chart that provides built-in access control and works with VPC Service Controls. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Store both the public and private charts as OCI artifacts in Artifact Registry.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Store both the public and private charts in GitHub Enterprise, using Google Workspace as the identity provider for authentication.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Store both the public and private charts in a Git repository, use Cloud Build to synchronize the repository’s contents into a Cloud Storage bucket, and point Helm at that bucket as a chart repository using the https://[bucket].storage-googleapis.com/[helmchart] URL.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Deploy a self-managed Helm chart repository server on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), using a Cloud Storage bucket as its storage backend.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Store both the public and private charts as OCI artifacts in Artifact Registry.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The must-have constraints are threefold: one uniform store for both chart types, <em>native</em> (platform-managed) access control, and VPC Service Controls support. Walking requirement → capability: Helm 3+ can push and pull charts as OCI artifacts, and Artifact Registry’s Docker-format repositories natively store OCI artifacts — including Helm charts — so one repository type covers both public (re-hosted/mirrored) and private charts. Artifact Registry access is governed entirely through IAM, satisfying “native access control” without any extra identity layer. And Artifact Registry is a fully supported VPC Service Controls product, so the repository can sit inside a service perimeter. No other option satisfies all three constraints with a Google-managed, IAM-integrated, VPC-SC-eligible service.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“references both public and private charts” — a hard constraint that the solution must handle both chart sources through one mechanism.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“using a public Helm repository as a dependency is a risk” — signals the fix should bring external dependencies under organizational control, not simply re-point to another external SaaS.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“manage all charts uniformly” — preference for a single, consistent storage model rather than a mix of tools.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“native access control” — excludes solutions that require bolting on a custom or third-party authentication/authorization layer.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“VPC Service Controls” — a hard technical signal: the destination must be a Google Cloud service with documented VPC-SC support, not a self-hosted workload or external SaaS product.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Since Helm 3.8, Helm charts can be packaged and distributed as OCI artifacts, and Artifact Registry’s Docker-format repositories implement the OCI Distribution Specification, so they can store Helm charts the same way they store container images. Access to an Artifact Registry repository is controlled entirely by IAM roles and bindings — no separate auth system to build or maintain — which is exactly what “native access control” calls for. Artifact Registry is also one of the Google Cloud services with full VPC Service Controls integration, so the repository (and the organization’s private and locally-cached public charts inside it) can be placed behind a service perimeter to prevent unauthorized data exfiltration. This combination directly removes the security team’s concern: instead of pulling from a public Helm repository at deploy time, charts are pulled from a single governed, perimeter-protected registry.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — <em>Violates Requirement</em></strong>. GitHub Enterprise is a third-party SaaS product, not a Google Cloud resource, so it falls outside what VPC Service Controls can protect — service perimeters govern Google Cloud API access, not external platforms. Using Google Workspace as an identity provider addresses authentication, but that’s a federated-identity layer bolted on top of a third-party tool, not “native” access control on a Google Cloud artifact store, and it does nothing to satisfy the VPC-SC requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — <em>Outdated / Technically Possible but Not Best</em>.</strong> This is the classic pre-OCI Helm chart repository pattern (an index.yaml-based repo served over plain HTTPS from a public bucket URL). It adds a Cloud Build synchronization pipeline as extra moving parts (working against “manage uniformly”), and because Helm’s classic chart-repository protocol just performs HTTP GETs against that URL, access control isn’t natively enforced through IAM in the way Helm-to-Artifact-Registry authentication is — the bucket URL shown is a public-style endpoint, not an IAM-gated pull path.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — <em>Technically Possible but Not Best</em>.</strong> Running your own chart repository server (e.g., ChartMuseum-style) on GKE means you now own patching, scaling, and — critically — building your own access-control layer in front of it, which is the opposite of “native access control.” A self-managed workload on GKE isn’t itself a VPC-SC-protected API resource the way a managed Artifact Registry repository is, so the security requirement isn’t met natively either.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “native access control” and “VPC Service Controls” as if any Google Cloud-adjacent service (GKE, Cloud Storage via Cloud Build) automatically qualifies. Running infrastructure <em>on</em> Google Cloud (Option D) or storing files <em>in</em> a Cloud Storage bucket (Option C) is not the same as using a service with first-class IAM integration and documented VPC Service Controls support. If the question had not mentioned VPC Service Controls at all, Option C’s bucket-based approach might be a reasonable lower-effort answer for simple public/private separation — but the explicit VPC-SC requirement rules it out as written.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Since Helm 3, any OCI-compliant registry can serve as a Helm chart repository, which is why “Docker format” repositories in Artifact Registry can hold Helm charts alongside container images — OCI is a shared distribution protocol for both. When a Google Cloud exam question asks for “native” access control plus VPC Service Controls, read that as a cue to choose a managed Google Cloud service that is explicitly on the VPC-SC supported-products list, rather than a self-hosted workload or an external SaaS tool, however well-integrated that tool appears.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team consolidates all Helm charts — including re-hosted copies of commonly used public charts — into one or more Artifact Registry repositories inside a service perimeter. The operational result is that every deployment pulls from a single, audited, IAM-governed source, eliminating the supply-chain risk of pulling directly from the public internet at deploy time, and giving security a single control point for access reviews and data-exfiltration prevention. The trade-off is an added step in the chart lifecycle: public charts must be deliberately mirrored into the registry (and kept up to date) rather than consumed live from upstream.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Helm 3 + OCI + Artifact Registry” = one managed, IAM-governed, VPC-SC-eligible home for every chart, public or private.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question pairs “uniform chart/artifact management” with “native access control” and “VPC Service Controls,” look for the managed Google Cloud artifact store (Artifact Registry) over any self-hosted server or third-party SaaS — only a native, VPC-SC-supported service satisfies both the access-control and perimeter requirements at once.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/helm/manage-charts\"><strong>Google Cloud — Manage Helm charts in Artifact Registry:</strong></a> Supports: Artifact Registry’s Docker-format repositories natively store Helm 3 charts as OCI artifacts, which is the mechanism behind the correct answer</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/securing-with-vpc-sc\"><strong>Google Cloud — Protect Artifact Registry repositories in a service perimeter:</strong></a> Supports: Artifact Registry’s full VPC Service Controls integration, which eliminates Options B and D</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\">Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Exam Guide — Section 1.3, Designing a CI/CD architecture stack: </a>Supports: domain alignment via the “Artifact Registry configuration” and “Security of CI/CD tooling” considerations</p>","lessons":["d1l8"]},"e2q2":{"id":"e2q2","exam":2,"n":2,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You’re building a CI/CD pipeline in Cloud Build that produces an application container image, with the source code hosted in GitHub. Company policy requires that production image builds run only against the main branch, and that the change control team must approve every push to the main branch. You also want the build itself to require as little manual intervention as possible. What should you do? (Choose two.)</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create a Cloud Build trigger and set its repository event to Pull request.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Add an OWNERS file to the trigger’s Included files filter.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create a Cloud Build trigger and set its repository event to Push to a branch, scoped to the main branch.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Configure a branch protection rule on the main branch in the GitHub repository.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>E. Enable the manual Approval option on the Cloud Build trigger.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Create a Cloud Build trigger and set its repository event to Push to a branch, scoped to the main branch.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Configure a branch protection rule on the main branch in the GitHub repository.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The scenario bundles two separate controls that must each land in the right system. “Builds only run against main” is a Cloud Build trigger-configuration concern — walking requirement → capability, a trigger whose event is Push to a branch, scoped with a branch regex matching only <code>main</code>, fires exactly when (and only when) commits land on main, fully automatically. “The change control team approves all pushes to main” is a source-control concern — it’s about gating what’s allowed to reach the main branch in the first place, which is exactly what a GitHub branch protection rule (requiring a pull request plus required approving reviews) enforces. Together, C handles the build-automation half and D handles the human-approval half, each in the system built for it — and neither one duplicates or interferes with the other’s job, which is why both are needed and why no third option is required.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“production image builds are only run against the main branch” — a hard constraint on trigger scope: points to a Cloud Build trigger event configured for pushes to a specific branch.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“the change control team approves all pushes to the main branch” — a hard constraint on who can get code into main: points to a source-control-level gate (GitHub), not a build-level gate.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“as automated as possible” — a decisive preference that disqualifies any option that inserts a recurring manual step into the build execution itself.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“(Choose two)” — confirms the two requirements are solved by two distinct, complementary mechanisms rather than one option doing double duty.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A Cloud Build trigger with the repository event set to Push to a branch starts a build automatically whenever a commit lands on the matched branch (here, main) — once configured, it requires no further human action per build, satisfying “as automated as possible” for the build itself. Separately, GitHub branch protection rules let repository administrators require that changes reach a protected branch only via a pull request, and that the pull request receive a specified number of approving reviews before it can be merged — this is precisely how a “change control team” enforces sign-off on everything that reaches main. Because the approval happens at the merge/PR stage (outside Cloud Build) and the build trigger fires automatically afterward (inside Cloud Build), the two together deliver full automation for the build while still enforcing human approval exactly where the policy requires it — on the path into main.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — <em>Different Use Case</em>.</strong> A Pull request event triggers a build when a PR is opened or updated, which is well suited to running checks against proposed changes before merge — but the requirement is that <em>production</em> image builds run against the main branch, which means after code has been approved and merged, not on every PR update against a feature branch.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — <em>Incorrect</em>. </strong>The Included files filter controls whether a trigger fires based on which files changed (commonly used to scope triggers in a monorepo) — it has nothing to do with approvals. Pairing an OWNERS file with this filter doesn’t make Cloud Build enforce code-owner review; that capability (requiring review from code owners) lives in GitHub’s branch protection settings, not in a Cloud Build trigger’s file filter.</p>\n<p><strong>Option E — <em>Violates Requirement</em>. </strong>Cloud Build’s trigger-level approval setting pauses every single triggered build until a user with the Cloud Build Approver role manually approves it inside Cloud Build. That’s a real feature, but it adds a recurring manual step to every production build run — directly working against “as automated as possible” — and it approves the act of <em>building</em> , not the act of <em>pushing to main</em> , which is what the change control team is actually meant to govern.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is reaching for Cloud Build’s own approval feature (E) because the word “approve” appears in both the requirement and the Cloud Build UI. But the requirement’s approval is scoped to “pushes to the main branch” — a source-control event — not to build executions. Enabling Cloud Build approval would satisfy a differently-worded requirement (“require sign-off before every production build runs”), and it would do so at the cost of automation; the question explicitly prioritizes automation for the build step while placing the approval burden on the one place it belongs — the gate into main.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A secure, automated CI/CD pipeline typically separates two layers of control: a <em>code-governance</em> layer (who can change what, and under what review requirements — enforced by the source-control platform, here GitHub) and a <em>build-automation</em> layer (what happens once governed, approved code lands — enforced by the CI/CD tool, here Cloud Build). Approval requirements are usually best enforced as early and as close to the source of truth as possible (the merge gate), leaving the downstream build pipeline free to run without further manual checkpoints.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team configures GitHub so that main is protected — no direct pushes, pull requests required, and at least one approval from a designated change-control reviewer — while Cloud Build watches main with a push-triggered pipeline that builds and pushes the production image the moment a PR lands. The operational result is a clean audit trail (every production image traces back to an approved, merged PR) with zero added latency in the build step itself. The trade-off is that this design puts all the protection at the merge gate; if someone with sufficient repository permissions could bypass the branch protection rule (for example, an admin with “include administrators” unchecked), the build would faithfully build whatever reaches main, so the strength of this design depends on the branch protection rule being configured to apply to everyone, including admins.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“GitHub gates who merges, Cloud Build automates what builds” — approval belongs on the branch protection rule, automation belongs on the push trigger.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question splits “only build from this branch” from “require approval before changes land,” expect two separate answers: a Cloud Build push trigger scoped to the branch for automation, and a GitHub (or equivalent source-control) branch protection/required-review rule for the human approval — never a Cloud Build trigger-level approval setting standing in for source-control governance.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/create-github-app-triggers\"><strong>Google Cloud — Create and manage GitHub App triggers:</strong></a> Supports: the distinction between the Push to a branch and Pull request trigger events, and how a branch-scoped push trigger automates production builds</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/builds/triggers/create/manual\">Google Cloud — gcloud builds triggers create manual (--require-approval reference): </a>Supports: Cloud Build’s manual approval feature pauses each triggered build until a Cloud Build Approver approves it, clarifying why Option E governs build execution rather than branch access</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-protected-branches/about-protected-branches\"><strong>GitHub Docs — About branch protection rules:</strong></a> Supports: branch protection rules can require a pull request and a specified number of approving reviews before changes reach a protected branch, which is the mechanism behind the correct answer’s change-control requirement</p>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e2q3":{"id":"e2q3","exam":2,"n":3,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You’re running a web application on a Compute Engine managed instance group, and Ops Agent is already installed on every instance. You’ve noticed suspicious activity coming from one particular IP address and need a way to see, in Cloud Monitoring, how many requests that IP address is generating — while keeping the operational overhead of the solution as low as possible. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Add a logging receiver to the Ops Agent configuration to collect the web server’s access logs, then create a logs-based metric to count requests from the IP address.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Write a custom script that scrapes the web server’s log files and pushes IP-address request counts to the Cloud Monitoring API.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Modify the application code so it reports IP-address request counts directly to the Cloud Monitoring API.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Add a metrics receiver to the Ops Agent configuration to collect web server metrics.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Add a logging receiver to the Ops Agent configuration to collect the web server’s access logs, then create a logs-based metric to count requests from the IP address.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard requirement is visibility into requests <strong>from one specific IP address</strong> , and the preference is <strong>minimal operational overhead</strong>. The decisive fact is that client IP addresses live in the web server’s access log entries, not in a predefined metric. Walking requirement → capability: to turn log content into something chartable in Cloud Monitoring, you need a logs-based metric, and to get the access logs into Cloud Logging in the first place without writing or running anything custom, you need Ops Agent’s logging receiver (a configuration change, not code or scripting). A metrics receiver, by contrast, only reports predefined metric sets (like connection counts) and has no concept of “count requests matching this IP.” That eliminates D on capability grounds and B/C on overhead grounds.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“suspicious activity from a specific IP address” — a hard constraint: the solution must be able to isolate and count by an arbitrary field value (client IP) buried in log content.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“view the number of requests... in Cloud Monitoring” — the output must land in Monitoring as a chartable/alertable metric, not just remain as raw log entries.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“minimal operational overhead” — a decisive preference that eliminates any option requiring custom code, scripts, or ongoing maintenance.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Ops Agent is installed on all instances” — a setup clue: the agent is already present, so the lowest-overhead path is a configuration change to that agent, not a new tool.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Ops Agent’s logging receivers collect log files (such as a web server’s access log, which records the client IP for every request) and ship them to Cloud Logging. Once those log entries are in Cloud Logging, a user-defined logs-based metric can be created with a filter that matches the specific IP address; Cloud Logging then automatically maintains a counter-type time series from matching log entries, and that time series is directly visible and chartable in Cloud Monitoring. The entire solution is two configuration/filter changes — no code to write, deploy, or maintain, which is exactly what “minimal operational overhead” is asking for.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — <em>Technically Possible but Not Best</em>.</strong> A custom scraping script could eventually get IP-level counts into Cloud Monitoring, but it means writing, deploying, scheduling, and maintaining a bespoke tool — the opposite of minimal operational overhead, and it duplicates functionality Ops Agent and Cloud Logging already provide natively.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — <em>Technically Possible but Not Best</em>.</strong> Changing the application to self-report metrics works for data the app doesn’t already expose elsewhere, but here the needed data (client IP per request) is already captured passively in the access log. Modifying, testing, and redeploying application code to duplicate that is unnecessary effort and ongoing maintenance burden compared to a log-based metric.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — <em>Violates Requirement</em>.</strong> A metrics receiver collects a fixed set of metrics exposed by the host or a supported application (for example, general request/connection counts), not an arbitrary dimension like “requests from IP X.” There’s no way to configure a metrics receiver to break down traffic by client IP, so it can’t satisfy the core requirement regardless of overhead.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is conflating “metrics receiver” with “a way to get request metrics.” Both A and D mention Ops Agent and both sound metric-related, but only logs carry the per-request client IP detail needed here — metrics receivers report aggregated, predefined values, not arbitrary field-level breakdowns from request data. Seeing “Ops Agent” and “metrics” in the same answer can tempt a quick read into D; the question’s real pivot word is “specific IP address,” which only log content (and therefore a logs-based metric) can filter on.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>In Google Cloud’s observability stack, logs and metrics serve different purposes: metrics are numeric time series optimized for trends and alerting but are limited to whatever dimensions were defined in advance, while logs carry arbitrary structured or unstructured detail about each individual event. Logs-based metrics are the bridge between the two — they let you mine log content for a pattern (like a specific IP, error code, or user ID) and turn matches into a proper Cloud Monitoring time series, without having to predict that need when metrics were first designed.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A security or SRE team facing a suspected scraping or abuse pattern adds a filter-based logs-based metric for the offending IP, gets a chartable, alertable signal within minutes, and can later promote that same technique into a broader rate-limiting or WAF rule once the pattern is confirmed. The main trade-off is cost and scale: logs-based metrics are calculated from log volume, so a very high-traffic log stream with many ad hoc filters can add to logging ingestion costs — which is why they’re best used for targeted, investigative queries like this one rather than as a permanent high-cardinality metrics pipeline.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“IP in the logs, not in the metrics” — when you need to slice by something only visible in raw log content, reach for a logs-based metric, not a metrics receiver.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks to surface a value buried inside log content (an IP, a status code, a username) as a Cloud Monitoring metric with minimal effort, the answer is almost always “ingest the logs (via Ops Agent’s logging receiver) + create a logs-based metric” — never a metrics receiver, and never custom code unless the data literally doesn’t exist anywhere else.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/logs-based-metrics\">Google Cloud — Log-based metrics overview: </a>Supports: how a logs-based metric filters log entries (such as those matching a specific IP) into a counter time series visible in Cloud Monitoring</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/agent/ops-agent/configuration\"><strong>Google Cloud — Configure the Ops Agent:</strong></a> Supports: the distinction between logging receivers (which collect log files like web server access logs) and metrics receivers (which collect predefined metric sets), explaining why Option D cannot satisfy the requirement</p>","lessons":["d4l4"]},"e2q4":{"id":"e2q4","exam":2,"n":4,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You’re setting up connectivity between Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) clusters that live in separate VPCs, and you’ve found that nodes in Cluster A cannot reach nodes in Cluster B. You suspect the root cause is a network misconfiguration rather than an application issue. You need to pinpoint exactly which layer of the network path is failing, but you don’t have execute access into any workloads or nodes to run commands directly. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Install a toolbox container on a node in Cluster A, and use it to confirm that the routes to Cluster B are configured correctly.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use Network Connectivity Center to run a Connectivity Test from a Cluster A endpoint to a Cluster B endpoint.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Attach a debug container and run the traceroute command in both directions — from Cluster A to Cluster B and from Cluster B to Cluster A — to identify the point where the paths fail in common.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Enable VPC Flow Logs in both VPCs and monitor the logs for packet drops.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Use Network Connectivity Center to run a Connectivity Test from a Cluster A endpoint to a Cluster B endpoint.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two constraints decide this question: you need to identify the <em>specific layer</em> where the path breaks (routing? firewall? forwarding rule?), and you explicitly cannot exec into any workload or node. Walking requirement → capability: anything requiring you to install, attach, or run something inside a container or on a node (A and C) fails the no-exec constraint outright, regardless of how useful the output would be. That leaves a choice between a static, hop-by-hop configuration analysis tool (B) and raw traffic telemetry (D). A Connectivity Test simulates the packet’s path through every relevant configuration layer — routes, firewall rules, forwarding rules, peering — and reports exactly which hop blocks it, without touching a single VM or pod. VPC Flow Logs, by contrast, record accepted-connection flow statistics at the NIC level and don’t inherently capture <em>why</em> or <em>where</em> a blocked packet was dropped, so they can’t reliably answer “which layer is broken” on their own.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“identify the layer at which the network connectivity is broken” — a hard constraint requiring a tool that decomposes the path into discrete configuration layers, not just overall reachability.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“do not have execute access to workloads and nodes” — a hard exclusion constraint that eliminates any answer requiring exec, attach, SSH, or a debug/toolbox container running inside the cluster’s compute.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“suspect... due to the network configuration” — signals the tool should analyze configuration (routes, firewalls), not application behavior.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“different VPCs” — a technical anchor indicating the path likely crosses VPC boundaries (peering, routing, or a connectivity hub), which is exactly the kind of multi-hop scenario a path-analysis tool is built to evaluate.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A Connectivity Test performs static (and, where applicable, dynamic) analysis of the configured network path between a source and destination, examining routes, firewall rules (VPC, hierarchical, and regional network firewall policies), and forwarding rules at each hop, then reports the specific point of failure — for example, “blocked by firewall rule X in VPC B” or “no valid route beyond this point.” None of this requires logging into a VM, exec’ing into a pod, or installing any tooling on the cluster’s nodes; it’s entirely a control-plane, configuration-level analysis. That makes it the only option that satisfies both the “identify the layer” requirement and the “no execute access” constraint at the same time.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — <em>Violates Requirement</em>. </strong>Installing a toolbox container on a node and inspecting routes from inside it requires exactly the kind of node-level execute access the question says you don’t have. The approach might work technically, but it’s disqualified by the stated constraint before its usefulness is even relevant.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — <em>Violates Requirement</em>.</strong> A debug container (whether attached to a pod or run against a node) still requires creating and executing a process within the cluster’s compute context to run <code>traceroute</code>. That’s execute-level interaction with workloads/nodes in all but name, so it fails the same constraint as Option A — and running it in both directions only adds more of exactly the access the scenario rules out.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — <em>Technically Possible but Not Best</em>. </strong>VPC Flow Logs don’t require exec access, so they clear that bar, but they’re the wrong tool for the job as described. Flow Logs primarily report metadata about connections that were established (bytes, packets, 5-tuple) at the VM network interface; capturing why traffic was denied is the job of a separate feature, VPC firewall rules logging. Even with both enabled, you’d be inferring the failure layer indirectly from traffic patterns across two VPCs, rather than getting a direct, authoritative answer the way a Connectivity Test provides in a single targeted check.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The sharpest trap in this question is the product name itself: the Connectivity Test capability actually lives under <strong>Network Intelligence Center</strong> , not <strong>Network Connectivity Center</strong> — the latter is a separate Google Cloud product for building hub-and-spoke connectivity (VPC spokes, VPN, Interconnect, router-appliance spokes) between networks. The two names are easy to swap under exam pressure. Learn to recognize the capability by what it does — simulate a path and report the failing configuration layer without touching any compute resource — rather than by memorizing the exact product name, since official practice material (and even real exam distractors) can blur the two.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud networking troubleshooting splits into two broad approaches: configuration analysis (reasoning about what the network <em>should</em> do, based on routes, firewall rules, and peering/hub configuration) and traffic observation (recording what actually happened to real packets, via Flow Logs or firewall rule logging). When you can’t execute commands inside the environment, configuration-analysis tools like Connectivity Tests become the primary diagnostic path, because they work entirely from the control plane’s view of how traffic <em>would</em> flow, hop by hop, layer by layer.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team with strict least-privilege policies — no engineer has SSH or <code>kubectl exec</code> rights into production nodes — runs a Connectivity Test the moment cross-cluster traffic breaks, gets an immediate answer such as “blocked by a firewall rule in the destination VPC,” and hands that to the owning team to fix, all without ever touching a live workload. The trade-off is that a Connectivity Test reasons about configuration and, in many cases, synthetic probe packets — it won’t catch an application-layer bug (like a misbehaving service inside a healthy, correctly-routed network path), so it complements rather than replaces application-level troubleshooting.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>No exec access + “which layer broke” = Connectivity Test (Network Intelligence Center), not Flow Logs and not a debug/toolbox container.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Whenever a GKE or VPC networking question pairs “identify where in the path connectivity fails” with “no access to exec into VMs or pods,” the answer is a configuration-analysis path test — functionally, a Connectivity Test — never a debug container, toolbox pod, or raw flow-log monitoring.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/network-intelligence-center/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Network Intelligence Center overview:</strong></a> Supports: Connectivity Tests is described as a diagnostics tool for checking connectivity between network endpoints, and it is organized under Network Intelligence Center (clarifying the naming distinction flagged in the Exam Trap)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/network-intelligence-center/docs/connectivity-tests/concepts/overview\">Google Cloud — Connectivity Tests overview: </a>Supports: the mechanism behind the correct answer — Connectivity Tests analyzes routes, VPC firewall rules, and regional/hierarchical firewall policies to assess reachability without requiring access to the endpoints themselves</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/firewall/docs/vpc-firewall-rules-logging-overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — VPC firewall rules logging overview:</strong></a> Supports: why Option D’s VPC Flow Logs alone are not the direct tool for identifying denied/dropped traffic, since logging denied connections is a distinct feature from Flow Logs</p>","lessons":["d4l11"]},"e2q5":{"id":"e2q5","exam":2,"n":5,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your company operates a public Cloud Run service named <code>booking-engine</code>, accessible through HTTPS at https://booking-engine-abcdef.a.run.app.</p>\n<p>Developers need to validate new revisions before those revisions receive customer traffic through the service’s standard URL. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Deploy using <code>gcloud run deploy booking-engine --no-traffic --tag dev</code> and test the revision at https://dev---booking-engine-abcdef.a.run.app.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Run <code>gcloud run services update-traffic booking-engine --to-revisions LATEST=1</code> and test through https://booking-engine-abcdef.a.run.app.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Include the header supplied by <code>curl -H \"Authorization:Bearer $(gcloud auth print-identity-token)\"</code> when requesting https://booking-engine-abcdef.a.run.app to test privately.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Grant developers <code>roles/run.invoker</code> on <code>booking-engine</code> and test through https://booking-engine-abcdef.private.run.app.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Deploy using</strong><code>**gcloud run deploy booking-engine --no-traffic --tag dev**</code><strong>and test the revision at</strong><a href=\"https://dev---booking-engine-abcdef.a.run.app\"><strong>https://dev---booking-engine-abcdef.a.run.app</strong></a><strong>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The requirement concerns selecting an unreleased revision while preserving customer routing. Choose revision-specific access with no production traffic allocation. Authentication changes address who may invoke the service; a traffic split deliberately exposes customers to the new revision.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“New revisions” — technical signal:</strong> Developers need revision-specific routing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Before … customer traffic” — sequence constraint:</strong> Keep the new revision outside the standard traffic allocation during testing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Public Cloud Run service” — contextual signal:</strong> Adding authentication headers alone does not select a different revision.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p><code>--no-traffic</code> deploys the revision without assigning it traffic from the standard service URL. <code>--tag dev</code> provides a dedicated URL that routes testing requests directly to that revision. Google documents this pattern for validating revisions before migrating customer traffic. </p>\n<p><strong>URL correction:</strong> The supplied option’s tagged hostname needs <strong>three hyphens</strong> after <code>dev</code>: <code>dev---booking-engine-abcdef.a.run.app</code>. The revised option includes that correction. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> <code>LATEST=1</code> assigns 1% of service traffic to the latest revision. This is a customer-facing canary rollout, so customers can encounter the revision before developer validation finishes. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> An identity token authenticates the caller when authentication is required. It does not change revision routing; requests to the standard URL follow the configured traffic allocation. It also does not make the existing public service private. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Incorrect:</strong> Cloud Run Invoker authorizes service invocation. Granting it does not create a <code>.private.run.app</code> endpoint or select an unreleased revision. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Zero production traffic allocation does not mean private access.</strong> A traffic tag on this public service provides separate routing, without adding developer-only authentication. If the requirement instead called for testing with 1% of real customer traffic, option B would fit. </p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Deployment, routing, and authorization are separate controls: deployment creates a version, routing selects the version that receives a request, and authorization determines whether the caller may invoke it.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Developers can run smoke tests against the <code>dev</code> URL while customers continue using the standard endpoint. After validation, the team can gradually migrate traffic to the tested revision. </p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Use a revision tag with zero standard traffic allocation to test before routing customers to it.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Choose <strong>A</strong> , with the corrected three-hyphen URL. Use <code>--no-traffic</code> and a revision tag for pre-release testing; configure authentication separately when developer-only access is required.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud — Rollbacks, gradual rollouts, and traffic migration</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Tagged revision testing, <code>--no-traffic</code>, URL format, and percentage-based rollouts.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/rollouts-rollbacks-traffic-migration\">Official documentation</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud — Authenticate developers</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Cloud Run Invoker permissions and identity-token authentication.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/authenticating/developers\">Official documentation</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud — Allowing public (unauthenticated) access</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Service-level public access controls and their distinction from revision routing.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/authenticating/public\">Official documentation</a></p>","lessons":["d2l7"]},"e2q6":{"id":"e2q6","exam":2,"n":6,"domain":5,"html":"<p>You’ve built a serverless application on Cloud Run and deployed it to production. You want to understand the application’s resource utilization so you can make cost-optimization decisions. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use Cloud Trace’s distributed tracing capability to monitor the application’s resource utilization.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use Cloud Profiler together with Ops Agent to monitor the application’s CPU and memory utilization.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use Cloud Monitoring to track the application’s container-level CPU and memory utilization.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Use Google Cloud Observability to create logs-based metrics that monitor the application’s resource utilization.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Use Cloud Monitoring to track the application’s container-level CPU and memory utilization.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The requirement is to see <em>resource utilization</em> (CPU and memory) for a <em>Cloud Run</em> service, in order to drive cost decisions, with the implicit preference of using what’s already available rather than adding tooling. Walking requirement → capability: Cloud Run is automatically and natively integrated with Cloud Monitoring, which already collects container CPU utilization and container memory utilization for every revision with zero setup. That single fact resolves the question — no agent to install, no code to instrument, no custom metric to build. The other three options each reach for a tool built for a different job (request-path latency, code-level hotspot profiling, or log-derived custom metrics) when the data needed is already a standard, built-in metric.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“serverless application... Cloud Run” — a setup constraint: the solution should fit a fully managed environment where you don’t control or access underlying VMs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“identify the resource utilization... for cost optimization” — a hard constraint: the goal is CPU/memory usage data specifically, not latency breakdowns or code-level hotspots.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“deployed to your production environment” — implies a solution that works passively, without modifying running application code.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Each distractor names a real Google Cloud tool, but tied to the wrong kind of signal (trace = latency, profiler = code hotspots, logs-based metrics = custom/derived data) rather than the native utilization metric itself.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Run ships with built-in integration into Cloud Monitoring: as soon as a service or revision runs, metrics including container CPU utilization and container memory utilization are captured automatically and are visible both on the Cloud Run console pages and in Cloud Monitoring’s Metrics Explorer, with no agent installation or code changes required (since Cloud Run is fully managed, there’s no VM to install an agent on in the first place). Those exact metrics are what’s needed to judge whether a service’s configured CPU/memory allocation is over- or under-provisioned — the foundation of a cost-optimization decision for a serverless workload.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — <em>Different Use Case</em>.</strong> Cloud Trace’s distributed tracing captures request latency as it flows through a request’s call graph — it tells you <em>where time goes</em> , not how much CPU or memory a container is consuming. It’s the right tool for diagnosing slow requests, not for sizing compute resources.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — <em>Incorrect / Violates Requirement</em>. </strong>Cloud Profiler is a continuous, statistical profiler that attributes CPU and memory <em>allocation</em> to specific lines of application code, and it requires a language-specific profiling agent built into the application itself — not Ops Agent, which is a host-level telemetry agent installed on Compute Engine VMs (or similar hosts) to collect system logs and metrics. Cloud Run doesn’t expose the underlying VM for you to install Ops Agent on, so this pairing doesn’t apply to the scenario. Even where Cloud Profiler is used with Cloud Run, it’s aimed at code-level optimization, not service-level cost sizing.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — <em>Technically Possible but Not Best</em>.</strong> Logs-based metrics are the documented way to derive <em>custom</em> metrics from log content when the data you need isn’t already captured as a metric. CPU and memory utilization for Cloud Run are already first-class, built-in Cloud Monitoring metrics, so manufacturing them from logs would be redundant extra engineering effort for data that’s already available directly.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is picking a tool because it’s plausible for “observability” in general, without checking whether it produces the <em>specific</em> data type asked for. Trace, Profiler, and logs-based metrics are all legitimate, frequently-correct answers in other Cloud Run observability scenarios — but here the question asks specifically for CPU/memory resource utilization, which is a metric Cloud Run already reports natively. If the question instead asked “which function in your code is consuming the most CPU,” Cloud Profiler would become the right answer; if it asked “where is latency being introduced across downstream calls,” Cloud Trace would be correct.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Think of Cloud Run’s observability stack as layered by question type: “is my service healthy and how much is it costing me to run” → built-in Cloud Monitoring metrics (CPU, memory, instance count, billable instance time); “where is time being spent across a request” → Cloud Trace; “which code path is hot” → Cloud Profiler; “what happened, in detail, for this one request” → Cloud Logging (and logs-based metrics when you need a custom aggregate from that detail). Resource-utilization-for-cost questions point straight at the first layer.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team notices rising Cloud Run bills and opens the service’s Cloud Monitoring CPU and memory utilization charts; seeing memory consistently sitting far below the configured limit, they reduce the memory allocation per instance, which lowers the billable resource cost per request without touching application code. The trade-off to watch is under-provisioning: cutting CPU or memory too aggressively based on average utilization can cause cold-start slowdowns or out-of-memory errors under traffic spikes, so teams typically look at peak (not just average) utilization before resizing.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Resource utilization, no setup needed” — Cloud Run’s CPU/memory metrics are already in Cloud Monitoring by default; reach for Trace or Profiler only when the question is about latency or code hotspots instead.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a Cloud Run question asks for CPU/memory resource utilization — especially for cost decisions — default to Cloud Monitoring’s built-in container metrics; it requires no agent and no code changes, which beats any option that proposes extra tooling (Profiler, Trace, or custom logs-based metrics) to recreate data that’s already being collected.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/monitoring\"><strong>Google Cloud — Monitor Cloud Run:</strong></a> Supports: Cloud Run is automatically integrated with Cloud Monitoring, and container CPU utilization and container memory utilization are built-in metrics requiring no setup</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Profiler documentation:</strong></a> Supports: Cloud Profiler continuously attributes CPU usage and memory allocation to application source code via a language-specific profiling agent, distinguishing its code-level purpose (and agent model) from Ops Agent and from service-level resource-utilization monitoring</p>","lessons":["d5l6","d5l8"]},"e2q7":{"id":"e2q7","exam":2,"n":7,"domain":3,"html":"<p>Your team is writing a postmortem after an incident on your externally facing application. The team wants to improve its postmortem policy by adding triggers that indicate when an incident requires a postmortem. Based on Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) practices, which triggers should the policy define? (Choose two.)</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. A request for a postmortem comes from an external stakeholder.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. An incident results in data loss.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. A request for a postmortem comes from an internal stakeholder.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. The monitoring system detects that an instance of your application has failed.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>E. The CD pipeline detects a problem with a release and automatically rolls it back.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. An incident results in data loss.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. A request for a postmortem comes from an internal stakeholder.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The decision method is to separate triggers that are defined in advance and objective from triggers that reflect the system working as designed. Google’s SRE guidance lists objective triggers such as user-visible downtime or degradation beyond a threshold, data loss of any kind, on-call engineer intervention, long resolution time, and a monitoring failure. It adds that any stakeholder may also request a postmortem. Option B matches the objective list directly. Options D and E describe automation succeeding (monitoring detecting a failure, a pipeline catching and reversing a bad release), which is the opposite of a failure that needs organizational learning. That leaves a stakeholder request as the second trigger, and the A versus C split is a scoping judgment explained in the Exam Trap.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“triggers that indicate whether an incident requires a postmortem” is a hard constraint. You need criteria that can be defined before an incident, not reasons a postmortem would be useful.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Based on Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) practices” is a hard constraint. The answers must align with Google’s published SRE criteria rather than general incident-management folklore.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Choose two” is a cardinality instruction. The stem expects one objective trigger and one request-based trigger.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“external facing application” is context only. It describes who uses the application, not who can trigger the team’s own postmortem policy.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Option B is the clearest match. Data loss of any kind is an explicit SRE postmortem trigger, because losing data is a serious, often irreversible failure even if the service recovers quickly. Option C reflects the SRE guidance that, beyond the objective triggers, any stakeholder may request a postmortem for an event. The two selections are independent triggers that work together. One is an objective criterion that fires without anyone asking, and the other is a request-based path that catches events the objective criteria miss. Together they cover the two mechanisms the SRE guidance describes: criteria defined before an incident, and stakeholder requests on top of them.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A is <em>Technically Possible but Not Best</em>. </strong>SRE guidance does say any stakeholder may request a postmortem, so an external request is not absurd. It loses to C because the policy is the team’s internal governance and postmortems are internal documents that contain operational detail. A request from outside the company would normally reach the team through an internal stakeholder such as support or management, who then request the postmortem.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D is <em>Incorrect</em>.</strong> The SRE trigger is a monitoring failure, meaning monitoring did not detect the problem and a person had to discover it manually. Here monitoring worked, and a single failed instance is routine in most managed deployments and is typically absorbed by redundancy without user impact.</p>\n<p><strong>Option E is a <em>Different Use Case</em></strong>. The SRE trigger is on-call engineer intervention, such as a manual rollback or traffic reroute. An automated pipeline rollback is a control working as designed with no human intervention and no stated user impact. It is better handled by release-quality review than by a postmortem trigger.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The weak point of this item is A versus C, so it is worth stating plainly. Google’s SRE text says “any stakeholder,” which does not distinguish internal from external, and this item’s key rests on that distinction. The defensible reading is that the policy is internal governance, so an internal stakeholder request is the one that belongs in it. Do not treat this as a rule that external requests are never honored. The stronger exam skill is rejecting D and E, which sound like incident signals but describe automation succeeding. The answer would change if the stem said an on-call engineer manually rolled back a release, because on-call intervention is a listed trigger.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A postmortem policy has two parts: objective criteria defined before an incident, and discretionary requests from stakeholders. Objective criteria remove debate about whether an incident was “big enough” and keep the practice blameless and consistent. Postmortems cost time, so triggers aim at events that carry learning value, such as user impact, data loss, human intervention, slow resolution, or failures of the detection system itself. Events that the system handled automatically and invisibly usually carry less learning value.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team writes a policy that requires a postmortem for any data loss, any user-visible degradation past a set threshold, any manual intervention by on-call, and any incident where monitoring failed to alert, and lets managers or partner teams request one for anything else. The outcome is predictable triage: no one argues about whether to write one, and effort goes to incidents with real lessons. The trade-off is that too many triggers or low thresholds create postmortem fatigue, so teams tune thresholds as they learn what is worth the effort.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Postmortems are triggered by failures (data loss, user impact, human intervention, slow fix, monitoring failure) or by a stakeholder request, not by automation doing its job.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For SRE postmortem-trigger questions, pick the listed objective triggers such as data loss, user-visible impact, on-call intervention, long resolution time and monitoring failure, plus a stakeholder request. Reject options where monitoring or CI/CD automation simply worked as intended.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/\">Google SRE Book — Postmortem Culture: Learning from Failure: </a>Supports: the list of common postmortem triggers (including data loss of any kind) and the statement that any stakeholder may request a postmortem, which together justify Options B and C and eliminate D and E</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/reliability/conduct-postmortems\"><strong>Google Cloud Architecture Framework — Conduct thorough postmortems:</strong></a> Supports: the recommendation to define postmortem criteria before an incident occurs, and the same set of objective triggers, including on-call interventions and monitoring failures</p>","lessons":["d3l5"]},"e2q8":{"id":"e2q8","exam":2,"n":8,"domain":1,"html":"<p>A third-party application requires a service account key in order to work. When you try to create the key in your project, you get an error saying the organization policy constraint iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation is enforced. You need to get the third-party application working while following Google-recommended security practices. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Enable the default service account key and download it.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Remove the iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation policy at the organization level, then create a key.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Turn off the service account key creation policy on the project’s parent folder, then download the default key.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Add a rule to the project’s organization policy that turns off the iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation constraint, then create a key.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Add a rule to the project’s organization policy that turns off the iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation constraint, then create a key.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>There are two requirements in tension: the application needs a key, and the fix must follow Google-recommended security practice. Walking requirement → capability: the constraint blocks key creation, so something has to change before a key can exist, but the recommended practice is to treat keys as an exception and limit which projects can create them. The answer is therefore the narrowest exemption that still unblocks you, which is the single project that needs the key. Organization-wide and folder-wide changes lift the guardrail for many projects that never asked for it. Options A and C also rely on a “default key” that you cannot simply download.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“needs to have a service account key to work properly” is a hard constraint. A keyless alternative is not available here, so a key must be created.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“following Google-recommended security practices” is a hard constraint. It points to the narrowest-scope, exception-based approach.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“when you try to export the key from your cloud project” is a scoping signal. The project is the unit that needs the exemption.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“organization policy constraint... is enforced” is a technical signal. The fix is to change that policy’s scope, not to work around it.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Google’s guidance says to use organization policy constraints to limit which projects can create service account keys, and to treat key use as the exception rather than the norm. Organization policies inherit down the resource hierarchy and can be overridden at a lower level. Google’s own troubleshooting guidance for this exact error says that if a key is needed, you can disable the constraint for your project. Adding a rule to the project’s policy that turns off enforcement lets the one project create the key while every other project in the organization stays protected. Once the exemption exists, you can create the key, ideally with the gcloud CLI writing it directly to a secure location rather than leaving a copy in a downloads folder.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A is <em>Incorrect</em>. </strong>Service accounts have user-managed keys that you create and Google-managed keys that you cannot download, so there is no “default key” to enable and download. Nothing in this option changes the enforced constraint either, so key creation would still be blocked.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B is <em>Violates Requirement</em>. </strong>Removing the policy at the organization level would unblock you, but it removes the guardrail for every project in the company, which is the opposite of limiting key creation to the projects that need it. It is tempting because it is the most direct way to make the error disappear.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C is <em>Incorrect</em> on two counts. </strong>Turning the policy off at the parent folder is wider than necessary and affects every project in that folder, and “download the default key” repeats the false premise of Option A. A key still has to be created after the policy allows it.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is choosing the most direct way to make the error go away (removing the constraint, Option B) instead of the narrowest compliant exception. The deciding word is “recommended”: Google prefers avoiding keys entirely, and when a key is unavoidable, exempting only the project that needs it. If the application could use workload identity federation, an attached service account, or impersonation instead, the better answer would be to avoid keys altogether, but the stem states a key is required. One further detail: the supplied error text had a typo in the constraint name, so the revised item uses the correct name, iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation. Google’s documentation also describes a newer managed variant, iam.managed.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation, and the legacy constraint name used here remains valid.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Organization policy constraints are inherited from organization to folder to project, and a policy set at a lower level can override the one above it. That makes scope the main design choice: apply restrictions broadly by default, then grant narrowly scoped exceptions with clear ownership. Google also supports finer exemptions using resource tags on individual service accounts, which is narrower than a project-level exemption. Service account keys are long-lived credentials that can leak, which is why they are restricted by default.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A security team enforces the key-creation constraint across the organization and handles each legitimate exception by exempting only the requesting project, then tracking that key’s owner and lifetime. The result is a small, auditable set of keys instead of keys scattered across every project. The trade-off is operational work, because each exception needs review, and the exempted project stays open to key creation until someone narrows it again. Teams usually pair exceptions with Cloud Audit Logs monitoring of key creation and with a plan to migrate to keyless authentication later.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Need a key despite the constraint? Exempt the smallest scope that works, the project and not the organization or folder.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When an organization policy blocks something you legitimately need, pick the narrowest-scope override on the resource that needs it. For service account keys specifically, Google’s order of preference is to avoid keys first, and when that is impossible, to exempt only the requesting project.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/best-practices-for-managing-service-account-keys\">Google Cloud — Best practices for managing service account keys: </a>Supports: using organization policy constraints to limit which projects can create service account keys, and treating key use as an exception, which is the reasoning behind Option D over B and C</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/troubleshoot-org-policies\"><strong>Google Cloud — Troubleshoot organization policy errors for service accounts:</strong></a> Supports: the exact error message in the stem, and the options to turn off enforcement for the organization or exempt a single project (or tagged service accounts), with the project-level exemption being the narrower choice</p>","lessons":["d1l2"]},"e2q9":{"id":"e2q9","exam":2,"n":9,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your organization operates under strict regulatory requirements that mandate retaining all logs generated across the company for a period of seven years. You want to keep the logging architecture as simple as possible by relying on fully managed services, and you need to guard against any future gaps in log collection or accidental loss of stored logs caused by misconfiguration or human error. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Configure an aggregated Cloud Logging sink at the organization level that routes all logs to a BigQuery dataset.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Configure an aggregated Cloud Logging sink at the organization level that routes all logs to a Cloud Storage bucket governed by a seven-year retention policy with Bucket Lock enabled.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Configure an individual Cloud Logging export sink in each project that routes that project’s logs to a BigQuery dataset.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Configure an individual Cloud Logging export sink in each project that routes that project’s logs to a Cloud Storage bucket governed by a seven-year retention policy with Bucket Lock enabled.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Configure an aggregated Cloud Logging sink at the organization level that routes all logs to a Cloud Storage bucket governed by a seven-year retention policy with Bucket Lock enabled.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two separate requirements are stacked here, and each one eliminates half the option set. The must-have constraint on <em>capture</em> is “never miss future logs with minimal effort” — that points to sink <strong>scope</strong>. The must-have constraint on <em>storage</em> is “logs cannot be deleted early, even by mistake, for seven years” — that points to storage <strong>destination</strong>. Walking it through: an organization-level aggregated sink automatically captures every current and future project under that node, so there’s no per-project step to forget (eliminates C and D). A Cloud Storage bucket with a locked retention policy makes the seven-year retention a property of the bucket itself, enforced by the platform rather than by a person remembering not to delete something (eliminates A and C again). Only B satisfies both constraints simultaneously.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“store all organization logs” + “seven years” — a hard constraint on retention duration and scope (org-wide, not per-project).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“minimize logging infrastructure complexity by using managed services” — a preference that rules out hand-rolled or per-resource solutions in favor of a single managed construct.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“avoid any future loss of log capture... due to misconfiguration or human error” — this is the decisive phrase. It signals that the design must be self-enforcing (platform-guaranteed), not dependent on someone configuring things correctly every time.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“What should you do?” with four single-sentence options — single-select, best-fit question.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>An aggregated sink created at the organization level (with the “include children” behavior) automatically pulls in log entries from every project in the resource hierarchy, including projects created after the sink exists. That directly satisfies “avoid future loss of log capture” — there’s no manual step tied to onboarding a new project. Pointing that sink at a Cloud Storage bucket with a locked retention policy then satisfies the storage side: once the policy is locked, the retention period can only be increased, never reduced or removed, and the bucket itself cannot be deleted until every object has satisfied its retention period. That turns “seven years, guaranteed” into an enforced platform property rather than a process everyone has to follow correctly.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — <em>Technically Possible but Not Best</em>. </strong>The organization-level aggregated sink correctly solves the capture problem, but BigQuery datasets don’t offer an equivalent of Bucket Lock: a dataset or table’s expiration settings can be changed or removed by anyone with the right permissions, so the “immune to misconfiguration or human error” requirement for the stored data is not met.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — <em>Violates Requirement</em>.</strong> Per-project sinks require someone to remember to add a sink every time a new project is created; forgetting even one project means silent log loss, which is precisely the failure mode the question asks you to eliminate. It also inherits BigQuery’s lack of a retention lock.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — <em>Violates Requirement</em>.</strong> The storage destination here is correct (Cloud Storage with a locked seven-year policy), but the sink scope is still per-project, so it carries the same capture-gap risk as Option C. A good storage choice doesn’t compensate for an architecture that can silently miss entire projects.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is evaluating the sink location and the storage destination independently instead of together. A candidate who only reads for “seven-year retention and Bucket Lock” might pick D because it has the right storage half; a candidate who only reads for “minimize complexity, avoid missing new projects” might pick A because it has the right scope half. The question requires both halves to be correct at once. If the requirement dropped “avoid future loss of log capture due to... human error” and only asked about immutable storage, a project-level sink (D) could become defensible for a single-project scope — but as written, org-wide guarantees are explicit.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Think of this as two independent axes: <em>where logs are collected from</em> (sink scope: project vs. folder/organization, aggregated vs. per-resource) and <em>how logs are protected once stored</em> (destination type and its immutability guarantees). Cloud Logging sinks solve the first axis; Cloud Storage retention policies plus Bucket Lock solve the second. BigQuery and Pub/Sub are valid sink destinations for analytics or streaming use cases, but neither offers an immutable, lockable retention guarantee comparable to Bucket Lock — so when a question emphasizes tamper-proof long-term retention, Cloud Storage with Bucket Lock is the Google-recommended pattern.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A compliance team configures exactly this pattern (org-level aggregated sink → Cloud Storage with a locked retention policy) once, centrally. The operational result is that no engineer anywhere in the company can shorten the retention window or delete the archive, intentionally or by mistake, and no new team can “forget” to wire up logging when they spin up a new project. The trade-off is reduced flexibility: once locked, the organization cannot even shorten retention later if a regulation changes, so the retention period is typically set conservatively and reviewed carefully before locking.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Org-wide capture, Storage for the lock” — aggregated sink at the org/folder level for guaranteed scope, Cloud Storage with Bucket Lock for guaranteed immutability.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question combines “don’t miss future resources” with “don’t let anyone alter/delete this later,” look for an aggregated (org/folder-level) sink paired with a destination that offers a lockable retention guarantee — that’s Cloud Storage with Bucket Lock, not BigQuery or Pub/Sub.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/export/aggregated_sinks\"><strong>Google Cloud — Aggregated sinks:</strong></a> Supports: organization-level aggregated sinks automatically capture current and future child-resource logs, eliminating the per-project configuration risk in Options C and D</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/bucket-lock\"><strong>Google Cloud — Bucket Lock:</strong></a> Supports: locked retention policies cannot be reduced or removed and the bucket cannot be deleted until all objects meet the retention period, which is why Cloud Storage (Option B) is correct over BigQuery (Option A)</p>","lessons":["d4l1"]},"e2q10":{"id":"e2q10","exam":2,"n":10,"domain":3,"html":"<p>Your organization wants to raise an application’s availability target from 99.9% to 99.99%, and the improvement would cost $2,000. The application currently generates $1,000,000 in revenue. You need to decide whether the higher availability is worth the investment over a single year of use. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Calculate the value of the improved availability as $900, and conclude that the improvement is not worth the investment.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Calculate the value of the improved availability as $1,000, and conclude that the improvement is not worth the investment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Calculate the value of the improved availability as $1,000, and conclude that the improvement is worth the investment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Calculate the value of the improved availability as $9,000, and conclude that the improvement is worth the investment.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Calculate the value of the improved availability as $900, and conclude that the improvement is not worth the investment.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>This is a two-step decision: first find the value of the extra availability, then compare it with the cost. The value step comes from the SRE method of treating availability as a share of revenue, so the improvement is the difference between the two targets applied to annual revenue. The decision step is a straight comparison: spend more than the value gained and the investment loses money. Computing $900 and comparing it with the $2,000 cost settles the answer, and only option A pairs the correct value with the correct conclusion.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“from 99.9% to 99.99%” is the calculation input. The improvement is the gap between the two targets, not either target on its own.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“current revenue is $1,000,000” is the calculation base. The value of availability is proportional to the revenue the service generates.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“for a single year of usage” is a hard constraint. The value ($900) and the cost ($2,000) are compared over the same one-year period.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“worth the investment” is the decision rule. Worth it only when the value gained exceeds the cost.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Going from 99.9% to 99.99% availability is an improvement of 0.09 percentage points (99.99% − 99.9% = 0.09%). Applied to $1,000,000 of revenue, the incremental value is 0.0009 × $1,000,000 = $900. Google’s SRE guidance frames the same trade-off: if the cost of gaining one more nine is less than the incremental revenue, the investment pays off, and if it costs more, the cost exceeds the projected revenue gain. Here the cost of $2,000 is greater than the $900 value, so the improvement is not worth it for one year.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B is <em>Incorrect</em>. </strong>The $1,000 figure is a miscalculation, and it comes from treating the gain as 0.1% of revenue, which is the unavailability at the old target rather than the difference between the two targets. Its conclusion (not worth it) happens to be right, but the supporting number is wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C is <em>Incorrect</em>. </strong>It combines the same wrong $1,000 value with the wrong conclusion. Even at $1,000, the investment would still cost twice what it returns.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D is <em>Incorrect</em>.</strong> The $9,000 figure comes from an order-of-magnitude slip, 0.9% rather than 0.09%, which makes the improvement look worth buying. It is tempting because it flips the decision, but the arithmetic does not support it.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is the decimal placement in the percentage difference. 99.99% − 99.9% is 0.09 percentage points, and it is easy to read as 0.1% ($1,000) or 0.9% ($9,000). If the revenue were $10,000,000 instead, the same improvement would be worth $9,000, which would exceed the $2,000 cost. The decision flips with revenue and cost, so always recompute both.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Each extra nine of availability costs more than the previous one but protects a smaller slice of revenue, so reliability has diminishing returns. SRE therefore treats availability targets as a business decision, not a technical goal to maximize. The value of an availability improvement is the revenue protected by the difference between the targets, and an investment makes sense only when that value exceeds the cost of reaching the target. The comparison should use the same time period for both sides.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team weighing a multi-zone redesign multiplies the availability gain by the service’s annual revenue and compares it with the engineering and infrastructure cost before committing. When the value is smaller than the cost, they keep the current target and spend the error budget on feature work instead. The trade-off is that revenue is only part of the picture, because reputation, contractual penalties, and indirect effects can also matter, so teams use the calculation as a starting point rather than the final word.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Value of an extra nine = the difference in availability (here 0.09%) × revenue; invest only if that value beats the cost.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For availability-versus-cost questions, subtract the two targets, multiply by revenue to get the value, and compare it with the cost over the same period. Watch the decimal placement, because wrong answers are usually off by a factor of 10.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/embracing-risk/\">Google SRE Book — Embracing Risk: </a>Supports: the framing of availability in nines, the link between availability and revenue, and the principle that reliability investments should be weighed against the value they protect</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Exam Guide — Section 3.1, Balancing change, velocity, and reliability of the service:</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment via “Defining SLIs (e.g., availability, latency), SLOs, and SLAs” and “Opportunity cost of risk and reliability (e.g., number of ‘nines’)”</p>","lessons":["d3l2"]},"e2q11":{"id":"e2q11","exam":2,"n":11,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your application’s performance on Google Cloud has declined since the most recent release. You suspect that calls to downstream dependencies are making some requests take longer to complete, and you need to investigate the application to find the cause. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Set up Error Reporting in your application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Set up Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus in your application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Set up Cloud Profiler in your application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Set up Cloud Trace in your application.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Set up Cloud Trace in your application.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The must-have constraint is diagnosing <em>slow requests</em> caused by <em>downstream dependencies</em> , which means the evidence needed is per-request timing broken down by the calls a request makes. Walking requirement → capability: only distributed tracing records a request as a tree of timed spans across service and dependency boundaries, so you can see which hop consumed the time. Error Reporting groups failures, Prometheus aggregates numeric metrics, and Profiler samples resource use inside a single process, so none of them attributes latency to a specific downstream call. That leaves Cloud Trace as the only tool built for this question.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“performance... has degraded” and “take longer to complete” — a hard constraint pointing to latency, not errors or resource consumption.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“downstream dependencies” — a technical signal that the investigation must follow a request across service boundaries, which is the defining use case of distributed tracing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“since the last release” — a contextual clue that you want to compare latency before and after the change, which trace data supports over time.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“investigate the issue with your application” — implies per-request, root-cause-level detail rather than an aggregate trend.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Trace is a distributed tracing system that tracks request latency and helps you find performance bottlenecks across services. It records each request as a trace made of spans, so you can measure end-to-end latency, compare latency trends over time, and pinpoint which downstream dependency is causing delays. Instrumenting the application (OpenTelemetry is the recommended approach) lets Trace show where a slow request spent its time, such as waiting on a database call or another service, which is exactly the evidence needed to confirm or rule out the suspected dependency.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — <em>Different Use Case</em>. </strong>Error Reporting aggregates and groups application error events by root cause, usually from stack traces or reported errors. A request can be slow without throwing any error, so a latency regression caused by a sluggish dependency may never show up there.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — <em>Technically Possible but Not Best</em>.</strong> Managed Service for Prometheus collects and stores numeric metrics that you query with PromQL. If you had already exposed a latency histogram per dependency, it could show that something is slow, but it gives aggregate numbers rather than per-request, per-hop timing, and it requires you to define and emit those metrics yourself. It tells you that latency rose, not which call within a request caused it.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — <em>Different Use Case</em>. </strong>Cloud Profiler continuously gathers CPU usage and memory-allocation data and attributes it to source code. That finds hot code inside your own process. Time spent waiting on a downstream dependency is mostly idle time, not CPU work, so a CPU or heap profile would largely miss it.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating every observability product as interchangeable “performance tooling.” Profiler sounds like the right fit for “performance degraded,” but it answers “which of my code consumes CPU or memory,” while the stem points to waiting on other services. If the question had said “CPU usage spiked after the release” or “which function is allocating the most memory,” Profiler would be correct; if it had said “the number of failed requests increased,” Error Reporting would fit better.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Match the telemetry signal to the question. Metrics tell you how much and how often (rates, utilization, aggregate latency), logs tell you what happened in a specific event, errors tell you what failed, profiles tell you where your own code spends CPU and memory, and traces tell you where a single request spent its time across components. A latency problem involving dependencies is a trace-shaped question because the answer is a timeline of calls.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>After a release, an engineer opens Trace Explorer, filters to slow requests, and sees that most of the added time sits in one span for a call to an internal pricing service. That points the investigation at that dependency (or at how the new release calls it) instead of the application’s own code. The trade-off is that tracing needs instrumentation and context propagation through every service, and sampling means rare slow requests might not always be captured.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Slow requests across dependencies means Trace; hot code means Profiler; failures means Error Reporting; numeric trends means metrics.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question describes a latency regression and suspects downstream or dependent services, choose distributed tracing (Cloud Trace). Reserve Profiler for CPU and memory in your own code, Error Reporting for exceptions, and metrics services for aggregate numeric trends.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/trace/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Trace overview:</strong></a> Supports: Trace tracks request latency across services and helps pinpoint which downstream dependencies cause delays, which is why Option D is correct</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/error-reporting\"><strong>Google Cloud — Error Reporting overview:</strong></a> Supports: Error Reporting aggregates and groups error events by root cause, which distinguishes it from latency analysis (Option A)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/profiler/docs\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Profiler documentation:</strong></a> Supports: Profiler gathers CPU usage and memory-allocation data attributed to source code, which makes it a different use case from dependency latency (Option C)</p>","lessons":["d4l10"]},"e2q12":{"id":"e2q12","exam":2,"n":12,"domain":3,"html":"<p>Your company runs an ecommerce website built from JVM-based applications in a microservice architecture on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). Traffic rises during the day and falls overnight. Your operations team currently runs enough Pods to cover the evening peak at all times. You want to automate scaling so that only the Pods and nodes needed for the current load are running. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Set up the Vertical Pod Autoscaler and leave the node pool at a fixed size.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Set up the Vertical Pod Autoscaler and turn on the cluster autoscaler.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Set up the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler and leave the node pool at a fixed size.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Set up the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler and turn on the cluster autoscaler.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Set up the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler and turn on the cluster autoscaler.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The requirement has two layers that both have to flex with the daily load curve: the number of Pods, and the number of nodes that host them. Walking requirement → capability: load that varies with time of day is a replica-count problem, so Pods should scale out and in, which is what the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler does. The cost saving only materializes if the nodes go away when the Pods do, and a fixed node pool keeps paying for peak capacity regardless of Pod count. That requires the cluster autoscaler to shrink and grow the node pool. Only option D covers both layers in the right way.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“load increases during the day and decreases during the night” is a hard constraint. Demand swings up and down daily, which calls for scaling the number of replicas.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“run enough Pods to handle the evening peak load” describes the problem to remove, because capacity is sized for peak and wasted off-peak.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“only running enough Pods and nodes for the load” is a hard constraint. Both Pods and nodes must scale, so a static node pool is disqualified.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“microservice architecture” supports horizontal scaling, since stateless replicated services are the natural fit for adding and removing Pods.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler changes the number of Pods in response to CPU or memory consumption or custom metrics, so replicas rise during the day and fall at night. The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler does not change the number of nodes in a GKE cluster, and Google’s documentation points to the cluster autoscaler for that job. The cluster autoscaler resizes a node pool between the minimum and maximum you set: when Pods can’t be scheduled it adds nodes, and when nodes are under-used and their Pods fit elsewhere it removes them. The two work as a chain, where replicas go up, pending Pods trigger new nodes, and the reverse happens as load drops, so you pay for only what the current load needs.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A is <em>Different Use Case</em>.</strong> The Vertical Pod Autoscaler adjusts the CPU and memory requests of individual Pods, which is aimed at steady-state right-sizing. Google’s guidance says to use the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler for workloads with sudden spikes in demand, and the fixed node pool means off-peak savings would never be realized.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B is <em>Different Use Case</em></strong>. Adding the cluster autoscaler fixes the node side, but the Pod side still uses the Vertical Pod Autoscaler, which resizes Pods rather than changing replica counts. Right-sized Pods are still running around the clock, so the daily swing in load is not followed.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C is <em>Technically Possible but Not Best</em>. </strong>The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler reduces the number of running Pods at night, which is the right Pod-side choice. With a static node pool, though, the idle nodes keep running and billing, so you do not meet the requirement to run only the nodes you need.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is stopping after the Pod layer. Option C looks almost complete because the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler is correct, but the stem explicitly asks for “Pods and nodes,” so the node layer has to scale too. A second trap is treating the Vertical Pod Autoscaler as the general-purpose “autoscaling” answer. Vertical autoscaling would be the better fit if the load were steady but the Pods were over- or under-provisioned, and the question would then be about right-sizing rather than following a daily cycle.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Kubernetes autoscaling works in layers. Horizontal Pod autoscaling changes how many Pods run, vertical Pod autoscaling changes how much CPU and memory each Pod requests, and cluster autoscaling changes how many nodes exist to host them. The cluster autoscaler decides based on the resource requests of Pods, not their live utilization, which is why explicit resource requests on workloads matter. A cost-efficient design for variable load usually pairs Pod-level scaling with node-level scaling.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>An ecommerce team sets a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler on its storefront Deployment and enables the cluster autoscaler on the node pool with sensible minimum and maximum sizes. Overnight the Pod count drops, nodes drain and are removed, and the bill shrinks. The trade-off is that scale-down can briefly disrupt Pods that are rescheduled to other nodes, so replicas should tolerate restarts, and scale-up has a short delay while new nodes start, so some headroom or a higher minimum may be needed for sudden traffic spikes.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Horizontal for how many Pods, cluster autoscaler for how many nodes, and vertical for how big each Pod is.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For GKE workloads whose load rises and falls over time and where both Pod and node costs matter, pick the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler together with the cluster autoscaler. Rule out any option that leaves the node pool static or that relies only on the Vertical Pod Autoscaler.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/horizontalpodautoscaler\"><strong>Google Cloud — About horizontal Pod autoscaling:</strong></a> Supports: the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler scales the number of Pods with load, does not change the number of nodes, and relies on the cluster autoscaler for node scaling (Options C and D)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/cluster-autoscaler\"><strong>Google Cloud — About GKE cluster autoscaling:</strong></a> Supports: the cluster autoscaler adds nodes when Pods can’t be scheduled and removes under-used nodes down to the node pool minimum, which is the node-layer scaling the question requires</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/verticalpodautoscaler\"><strong>Google Cloud — Vertical Pod autoscaling:</strong></a> Supports: the Vertical Pod Autoscaler is for analyzing and setting CPU and memory resources per Pod and for steady-state right-sizing, with horizontal autoscaling recommended for sudden spikes (Options A and B)</p>","lessons":["d3l7"]},"e2q13":{"id":"e2q13","exam":2,"n":13,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You manage an application that runs on Compute Engine. The application uses a custom HTTP server to expose an API, and other applications reach that API through an internal passthrough Network Load Balancer. A firewall rule permits traffic to the API port from 0.0.0.0/0. You need Cloud Logging to record each IP address that accesses the API, using as few steps as possible. What should you do first?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Enable Packet Mirroring on the VPC network.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Install the Ops Agent on the Compute Engine instances.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Enable logging on the firewall rule.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Enable VPC Flow Logs on the subnet.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Enable logging on the firewall rule.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard requirement is a record of every client IP that reaches the API, delivered to Cloud Logging. The preference is the fewest steps, and the stem asks what to do <em>first</em>. Walking requirement → capability: the API traffic already passes an ingress firewall rule, and a connection record from that rule contains the source IP and lands in Cloud Logging. The load balancer is a passthrough type, so backend VMs receive packets with the client’s original source address intact, which means the rule sees real client IPs. Turning on logging for that existing rule is one setting. The other options add infrastructure, depend on application behavior, or lose the “each IP” guarantee.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“log each IP address that accesses the API” is a hard constraint. The record must carry the source IP of every accessing client, not a sampled view.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“fewest number of steps” is a hard preference. A single setting on an existing resource beats installing or deploying anything.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“A firewall rule permits traffic to the API port from 0.0.0.0/0” is a technical signal. An existing allow rule already matches this traffic, so logging can be switched on for it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“internal passthrough Network Load Balancer” is a technical signal. Passthrough balancing leaves the client source IP unchanged at the backend, so the logged IP is the real client.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“custom HTTP server” is a technical signal. Nothing says it writes access logs, so you cannot rely on application logs for client IPs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>When logging is enabled on a VPC firewall rule, Google Cloud creates a connection record each time the rule allows or denies traffic. Each record includes the source and destination IP addresses, protocol and ports, the connection time, and a reference to the rule that handled it. Those records are written to Cloud Logging, which is exactly the destination required. Because the existing rule allows the API port from 0.0.0.0/0, enabling logging on it captures each new client connection to the API. Internal passthrough Network Load Balancers deliver packets to backends with the original source and destination IP addresses unchanged, so the recorded source is the actual client. One boundary to keep in mind: firewall rules logging is connection-based and records only TCP and UDP connections, which is sufficient for an HTTP API.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A is a <em>Different Use Case</em>.</strong> Packet Mirroring clones full packets (headers and payloads) from instances and forwards them to collector instances behind an internal passthrough Network Load Balancer for inspection by security or monitoring tools. It requires a collector deployment and a policy, and it does not write IP records to Cloud Logging by itself. It is tempting because it can see every packet, but it is far more than “fewest steps.”</p>\n<p><strong>Option B is <em>Technically Possible but Not Best</em>. </strong>The Ops Agent can ship log files to Cloud Logging, but it only forwards what the application writes. The stem describes a custom HTTP server with no mention of access logging, so you would also have to make the server log client IPs and configure a logging receiver for that file, across every instance. That is many steps, and it depends on application behavior.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D is <em>Technically Possible but Not Best</em>.</strong> VPC Flow Logs also record IP addresses and write to Cloud Logging, but they sample packets (the primary sampling rate is dynamic and cannot be adjusted) and aggregate sampled packets over an interval into one entry per connection. That does not guarantee a record of each IP, and it is enabled per subnet, which covers far more traffic than the API port.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is choosing VPC Flow Logs (D) because “log IP addresses” sounds like its textbook use. The words “each” and “fewest steps” push away from a sampled, aggregated, subnet-wide feed and toward a connection record from the rule that already governs this traffic. The answer would change to D if the requirement were broader network analysis, such as traffic volume trends, top talkers, or visibility into protocols beyond TCP and UDP. It would change to B if the stem said the application already wrote access logs and you also needed request details such as paths or status codes.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud gives several network visibility tools that answer different questions. Firewall rules logging records what a specific rule allowed or denied per connection. VPC Flow Logs provides sampled, aggregated flow statistics for subnets and other resources. Packet Mirroring copies full traffic to an analysis tool. Agent-based logging captures what applications write. Choosing among them means matching the evidence you need to the tool’s granularity, scope, and setup effort. Load balancer type also matters here, because passthrough balancing preserves the client address while proxy-based balancing would replace it.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team investigating who is calling an internal API turns on logging for the ingress allow rule, then filters the resulting records in Logs Explorer by destination port to list the distinct client IPs. The result is immediate visibility with no deployment. The cost consideration is volume: logging a rule open to 0.0.0.0/0 on a busy port can generate many records and increase Cloud Logging charges, so teams review retention and exclusions. Existing long-lived connections that were already active when logging was enabled do not produce a new entry until they are re-established.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Need the source IPs hitting a port you already allow? Turn on logging for the firewall rule that allows it.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks for each client IP reaching a service with minimal effort, look for logging on the existing firewall rule rather than flow logs (sampled), Packet Mirroring (collector required), or agents (depends on application logging). Choose the broader tools only when the stem asks for network-wide analysis or other protocols.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/firewall/docs/vpc-firewall-rules-logging-overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — VPC firewall rules logging overview:</strong></a> Supports: logging is enabled per firewall rule, records are connection records containing source and destination IPs, only TCP and UDP connections are logged, and records are viewed in Cloud Logging (Option C)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/flow-logs\"><strong>Google Cloud — VPC Flow Logs:</strong></a> Supports: flow logs sample packets, aggregate them by connection over an interval, and are enabled per subnet or wider scope, which is why they do not guarantee a record of each IP (Option D)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/passthrough-network-load-balancer\"><strong>Google Cloud — Passthrough Network Load Balancer overview:</strong></a> Supports: backend VMs receive load-balanced packets with the original source and destination IP addresses unchanged, so logged source addresses are the real clients</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/packet-mirroring\"><strong>Google Cloud — Packet Mirroring:</strong></a> Supports: Packet Mirroring clones traffic to collector instances behind an internal passthrough Network Load Balancer for inspection, which makes it a different use case from simple client-IP logging (Option A)</p>","lessons":["d4l11"]},"e2q14":{"id":"e2q14","exam":2,"n":14,"domain":1,"html":"<p>You’re building a CI/CD pipeline for an application that runs in your company’s multi-cloud environment. The application is deployed from custom Compute Engine images, and from the equivalent image types on the other cloud providers. You need a solution that can build these images and make them available in your current environment, and that will still work as your environment changes. Which solution stack should you use?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud Build together with Packer.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud Build together with Cloud Deploy.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) together with Cloud Deploy.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud Build together with kpt.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Cloud Build together with Packer.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two hard requirements decide this item: the deployable artifact is a custom VM image, and the same approach must work across several cloud providers and stay adaptable. Walking requirement → capability: you need an automation engine to run the pipeline and an image builder that targets many platforms from one definition. Cloud Build is the managed pipeline runner. Packer is the open source tool built to produce VM images for multiple platforms from a single source configuration, with a separate builder per cloud. Only option A has a component that actually builds VM images, so B, C and D fail on the first requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“custom Compute Engine images and the equivalent in other cloud providers” is a hard constraint. The artifact is a VM image, not a container image or Kubernetes manifest.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“multi-cloud environment” is a hard constraint. The tool must not be tied to a single provider’s image format.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“adaptable to future changes” is a preference. A vendor-neutral builder with per-cloud plugins fits better than a Kubernetes- or Google Cloud-specific tool.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“build and deploy the images” signals that the stack needs an image-building tool, since a pure delivery service would only cover the second half.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Packer defines an image once in a template and uses platform-specific builders to produce matching images on each cloud. For Google Cloud, the googlecompute builder creates a Compute Engine image, and for other providers you add the corresponding builders to the same workflow. Cloud Build documents this pairing: a Packer community builder image runs <code>packer build</code> as a build step, and the resulting image appears in the Compute Engine Images page. Cloud Build supplies the triggers, managed execution and automation, and Packer supplies the multi-cloud image logic. If the company later adds or changes a provider, you add or swap a Packer builder rather than redesigning the pipeline. One prerequisite applies: the build service account needs permissions such as Compute Engine Instance Admin, plus Artifact Registry Writer if you store the Packer builder image there.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B</strong> , Cloud Build with Cloud Deploy, is a <em>Different Use Case</em>. Cloud Deploy is a continuous delivery service that promotes releases across targets, and its supported target types are GKE, Cloud Run and GKE Enterprise or attached clusters. It can be extended through custom targets, but that means writing your own deployment logic, and nothing in this pairing creates VM images for multiple clouds. It is tempting because Cloud Build plus Cloud Deploy is the standard Google Cloud CI/CD pairing.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C,</strong> GKE with Cloud Deploy, is <em>Violates Requirement</em>. GKE is a container orchestration platform, and the application here is deployed from VM images. This stack contains no image builder at all, and it ties the solution to Kubernetes rather than the multi-cloud VM-image workflow described.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D,</strong> Cloud Build with kpt, is a <em>Different Use Case</em>. kpt is a toolchain for managing, customizing and applying Kubernetes resource configuration packages, so it operates on Kubernetes manifests rather than VM images. It would be a sensible pairing for managing Kubernetes configuration, but not for producing Compute Engine images or their equivalents elsewhere.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is that Cloud Deploy, kpt and Packer all appear together in the exam guide’s CI/CD architecture objective, so any of them can sound plausible under time pressure. Cloud Deploy and kpt are Kubernetes- and Cloud Run-oriented, while Packer is the one that builds VM images across clouds. The answer would switch to B if the application were containerized and delivered through staged promotion across GKE or Cloud Run targets. It would switch toward kpt if the problem were managing and customizing Kubernetes configuration packages.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An image-based deployment pipeline has two separate jobs: <em>build the immutable artifact</em> (a baked VM image) and <em>deliver it to an environment</em>. Different tools own each job, and the artifact type decides which tools apply. Container images and Kubernetes manifests point to Kubernetes-centric delivery tools, while baked VM images point to an image builder such as Packer. A vendor-neutral builder with per-cloud plugins is also what keeps a multi-cloud pipeline adaptable.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team keeps one Packer template per application image in Git. A Cloud Build trigger runs the Packer build on each merge and publishes a new Compute Engine image to the current environment. When the company later adds another cloud, the team adds that provider’s builder to the same workflow rather than creating a second pipeline. The trade-off is operating the Packer builder yourself, because it is a community builder image that you build and store in Artifact Registry, and each additional cloud needs its own credentials and permissions.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>VM images across clouds means Packer, containers on Kubernetes means Cloud Deploy, and Kubernetes configuration packages means kpt.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question describes baked VM images in a multi-cloud environment, choose Cloud Build running Packer. Cloud Deploy and kpt are the answers when the artifacts are containers or Kubernetes configuration.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/building/build-vm-images-with-packer\"><strong>Google Cloud — Building VM images using Packer:</strong></a> Supports: Packer creates identical VM images for multiple platforms from a single source configuration, and Cloud Build runs it through a Packer builder to produce Compute Engine images (Option A)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/deploy-to-custom-targets\"><strong>Google Cloud — Deploy to custom target types (Cloud Deploy):</strong></a> Supports: Cloud Deploy’s standard targets are GKE, Cloud Run and GKE attached clusters, with custom targets for anything else, which is why Options B and C do not fit a VM-image workflow</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cncf.io/blog/2026/07/02/reintroducing-kpt-your-toolchain-for-infrastructure-automation\"><strong>CNCF — Reintroducing kpt, your toolchain for infrastructure automation:</strong></a> Supports: kpt works on packages of Kubernetes Resource Model files, which is why Option D addresses Kubernetes configuration rather than VM images</p>","lessons":["d1l8"]},"e2q15":{"id":"e2q15","exam":2,"n":15,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your company runs its services on multiple, globally distributed Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) clusters. Your operations team has built workload monitoring on Prometheus-based tooling for metrics, alerts, and dashboards, but that setup has no way to view metrics across all clusters at once. You need a scalable solution that supports global Prometheus querying and keeps management overhead low. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Set up Prometheus cross-service federation to centralize data access.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Set up workload metrics collection within Google Cloud Observability for GKE.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Set up Prometheus hierarchical federation to centralize data access.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Set up Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Set up Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Three requirements decide this item. The solution must keep Prometheus-style tooling, give a single global query view across clusters, and cost little to operate. Walking requirement → capability: federation can aggregate data, but you still run and scale the Prometheus servers at every tier yourself. A managed Prometheus-compatible service removes that burden and exposes one global query endpoint, so existing Prometheus workflows keep working. Options that are self-operated or not built for cross-cluster querying fail the overhead or the global-view requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“global Prometheus querying” is a hard constraint. The answer must keep Prometheus-style querying (PromQL) and apply it across all clusters at once.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“minimize management overhead” is a hard preference. A managed service beats any design where your team runs and scales Prometheus servers.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“scalable” is a hard constraint. The design has to handle many clusters without redesign as the fleet grows.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Prometheus-based tooling for metrics, alerts, and dashboards” is a technical signal. The existing tooling should carry over rather than be rebuilt.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus gives you Prometheus with the global, multi-cloud, cross-project infrastructure of Cloud Monitoring behind it. Data lands in Monarch, the same globally scalable datastore Google uses for its own monitoring, so metrics from every cluster can be queried together with PromQL in Cloud Monitoring, Grafana, or any tool that calls the Prometheus query API. Collection is handled by managed collectors on GKE, which are automatically deployed, scaled, sharded, and maintained, and existing Grafana dashboards and PromQL-based alerts continue to work. That combination delivers the global view and low operational burden the stem asks for.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A is a <em>Different Use Case</em>.</strong> Cross-service federation lets one service’s Prometheus server pull selected series from another service’s server, so both datasets can be used together in a single server, for example combining service metrics with the cluster metrics of the platform they run on. It is not a design for rolling up many clusters into one global view, and it still leaves you running the servers.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B is <em>Technically Possible but Not Best</em>.</strong> Workload metrics collection within Google Cloud Observability for GKE sends metrics into Cloud Monitoring, which is globally scoped. It does not by itself give you the Prometheus-compatible collection and query path (PromQL through the Prometheus API, Grafana, rule evaluation) that the team’s existing tooling depends on, so it is a different and larger migration than the managed Prometheus service.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C is <em>Technically Possible but Not Best</em>. </strong>Hierarchical federation is the Prometheus project’s own pattern for scale: local servers hold detailed data and higher-level global servers pull aggregated series from them, which gives an aggregate global view. It meets the global-view and scale requirements, but you still deploy, size, secure, and operate every tier of Prometheus servers, which contradicts “minimize management overhead.”</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is Option C, because hierarchical federation is the textbook open-source answer to “global view across many Prometheus servers” and it genuinely works. The deciding phrase is “minimize management overhead”: federation scales, but you operate it. The answer would flip to C if the requirement were to stay fully self-managed, for example for a policy against managed telemetry services. A second trap is picking B because “workload metrics” and “Google Cloud” sound native, when the question is specifically about keeping Prometheus-based querying and tooling.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Prometheus on its own is a per-server, local-storage system, so global views require either federation (pulling aggregated series upward through tiers) or a shared backend that all collectors write to. Federation keeps you in the operations business, while a managed backend moves storage, scaling, and cross-cluster querying to the provider and leaves you with collection and queries. Google Cloud Observability is the current name for the former operations suite, and Managed Service for Prometheus sits within it, using the same backend and APIs as Cloud Monitoring.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team enables managed collection on each regional GKE cluster, points Grafana at the managed query endpoint, and reuses the PromQL dashboards and alert rules it already had. An SRE can then chart request rates across all regions in one query instead of logging into separate Prometheus servers. The trade-off is that cost shifts from running servers to per-sample ingestion charges, so teams watch metric cardinality and use filters or sampling intervals to control spend.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Many clusters, one PromQL view, no servers to run: managed Prometheus beats federation.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks for global Prometheus querying across clusters with minimal overhead, choose Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus. Federation (hierarchical for scale, cross-service for correlating services) is the self-managed alternative and carries operational burden.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/managed-prometheus\"><strong>Google Cloud — Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus:</strong></a> Supports: managed Prometheus built on the global Monarch datastore, with managed collection and global PromQL querying across clusters (Option D)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://prometheus.io/docs/prometheus/latest/federation/\"><strong>Prometheus — Federation:</strong></a> Supports: hierarchical federation scales Prometheus with higher-level servers pulling aggregated data from local servers, and cross-service federation lets one service’s server pull selected data from another’s, which distinguishes Options C and A</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/monitoring\"><strong>Google Cloud — Observability for GKE:</strong></a> Supports: GKE clusters send system metrics to Cloud Monitoring by default and can enable Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus, which frames the role of Option B relative to Option D</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Exam Guide — Section 4.1, Instrumenting and collecting telemetry:</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment via “Collecting metrics (e.g., from applications, platforms, networking, Cloud Service Mesh, Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus, hybrid/multi-cloud environments)”</p>","lessons":["d4l7"]},"e2q16":{"id":"e2q16","exam":2,"n":16,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You are creating Cloud Logging sinks that export log entries to BigQuery for later analysis. Your organization has a folder named Dev that contains development projects and a folder named Prod that contains production projects. Log entries from development projects must be exported to dev_dataset, and log entries from production projects must be exported to prod_dataset. You want to create as few log sinks as possible, and you want the sinks to apply to projects created in the future. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create a single aggregated log sink at the organization level.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Create a log sink in every project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create two aggregated log sinks at the organization level, each filtering by project ID.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Create an aggregated log sink in each of the Dev and Prod folders.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Create an aggregated log sink in each of the Dev and Prod folders.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Three constraints work together. The logs must be split into two different datasets, the number of sinks must be minimal, and the sinks must automatically cover projects that do not exist yet. Walking requirement → capability: a sink routes to one destination, so two destinations require at least two sinks. The scope of each sink should follow how projects are already grouped, which is the folder. An aggregated sink attached to a folder routes the logs of the folder’s child projects, so new projects placed in that folder are covered without further configuration. That gives the minimum of two sinks with no maintenance, and only option D meets all three.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“Dev... must be exported to dev_dataset, and Prod... to prod_dataset” is a hard constraint. Two destinations mean a single sink cannot do the job.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“minimize the number of log sinks” is a hard preference. A per-project design is ruled out, and two sinks is the floor.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“apply to future projects” is a hard constraint. Scope must be defined by hierarchy placement, not by a fixed list of project names.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“folder named Dev... folder named Prod” is a technical signal. The folders are the natural attachment points for aggregated sinks.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Aggregated sinks let you combine and route logs generated by resources in an organization or folder to a central location. You create one with the folder as the parent and with children included, so log entries from projects inside the folder flow to the sink’s destination, and a BigQuery dataset is a supported destination. One sink on Dev pointing at dev_dataset and one on Prod pointing at prod_dataset yields exactly two sinks. A project created later inside either folder is a child of that folder, so its logs are routed without anyone touching the sink. Remember the prerequisites: the datasets must exist and be write-enabled, and each sink’s writer identity needs permission to write to its dataset.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A is <em>Violates Requirement</em>. </strong>A sink has a single destination, so one organization-level sink would send everything to one dataset and could not separate development from production logs. It is tempting because one sink is the smallest possible number, but it cannot satisfy the split.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B is <em>Violates Requirement</em>.</strong> A sink in each project multiplies the number of sinks and requires someone to create a new sink every time a project is added, which fails both “minimize” and “future projects.”</p>\n<p><strong>Option C is <em>Technically Possible but Not Best</em>.</strong> Two organization-level sinks match the minimum count, but filtering by project ID only matches the projects named in the filter. Each new project would require editing a filter, and filters are limited to 20,000 characters, so the design neither applies to future projects automatically nor scales cleanly. It is tempting because the sink count is the same as in D.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is the pair of answers that both look like they use the “minimum” number of sinks: A with one and C with two. A fails because one sink cannot feed two datasets, and C fails because project-ID filtering is a list of names rather than a rule that follows folder membership. The answer would change to A if all logs, development and production alike, were to go to a single dataset. It would change toward B only if the requirement were per-project isolation of log routing.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Log routing has two independent decisions: scope (which resources’ logs a sink sees) and destination (where matching logs go). Scope is set by where the sink is attached in the resource hierarchy, and aggregated sinks extend a sink’s reach to the resources below that node, including ones created later. Each sink has one destination, so the number of distinct destinations sets the minimum number of sinks. Filters refine what a sink matches but are a poor tool for expressing hierarchy membership.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team attaches one sink to the Dev folder and one to the Prod folder, each writing to its own BigQuery dataset with partitioned tables. When a team spins up a new project in Prod, its logs appear in prod_dataset immediately, and analysts keep the two environments separate in permissions and cost. The trade-off is that sinks at a folder are broad, so noisy logs from every project in the folder flow to BigQuery unless exclusion filters are added to control volume and storage cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>One destination per sink, so two datasets mean two sinks, and attach each to the folder that holds the projects.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When logs must be split across destinations and cover future projects, attach one aggregated sink per destination at the folder that contains the right projects. Avoid per-project sinks and project-ID filters, which do not follow the hierarchy.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/export/aggregated_sinks\">Google Cloud — Collate and route organization- and folder-level logs to supported destinations (aggregated sinks): </a>Supports: aggregated sinks route logs from the resources in a folder or organization to a destination, can be created with a folder as parent with children included, support BigQuery datasets, and filter by project only by naming it (Options D, A, and C)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/export/configure_export\"><strong>Google Cloud — Route logs to supported destinations:</strong></a> Supports: a log entry that matches a sink is routed to that sink’s destination, and the BigQuery destination dataset must be write-enabled, which explains why one sink cannot feed two datasets</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\">Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Exam Guide — Section 4.2, Managing and analyzing logs: </a>Supports: domain alignment via “Exporting and retaining logs (e.g., routing to BigQuery, Pub/Sub, Cloud Storage)”</p>","lessons":["d4l1"]},"e2q17":{"id":"e2q17","exam":2,"n":17,"domain":1,"html":"<p>Your organization uses Terraform to provision and manage infrastructure as code through a CI/CD pipeline. You observe that each time a configuration change is applied, an entirely new copy of the infrastructure stack is provisioned in your Google Cloud project rather than the existing stack being updated, resulting in duplicate resources and unnecessary spend. You need to ensure that the pipeline maintains exactly one instance of the infrastructure stack at all times, in line with Google-recommended practices. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Build an additional pipeline whose sole purpose is to identify and delete infrastructure stacks that are no longer required.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Verify that the pipeline reads and writes the terraform.tfstate file to and from Cloud Storage using Terraform’s gcs backend.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Verify that the pipeline reads and writes the terraform.tfstate file to and from a source control repository.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Modify the pipeline so that it tears down all existing infrastructure prior to applying the newest configuration.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Verify that the pipeline reads and writes the terraform.tfstate file to and from Cloud Storage using Terraform’s gcs backend.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Separate the hard constraint from the symptom first: the requirement is “exactly one stack at all times, Google-recommended practice” — not “clean up extra stacks” and not “guarantee freshness at any cost.” Work backward from the symptom: a brand-new stack appearing on every apply means Terraform has no memory of what it already created, which only happens when it can’t read a consistent, shared state file. That isolates the fix to state storage and rules out anything that reacts to duplicates after the fact (A), stores state somewhere unsafe for concurrent pipeline use (C), or avoids the problem by destroying everything every run (D). The decision converges on configuring a locked, remote backend — the <code>gcs</code> backend — so every pipeline execution sees the same state.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“a new copy... is created each time a change... is made” — technical signal that Terraform is not recognizing prior resources, pointing at state visibility rather than a cleanup gap.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“ensure that only a single instance... exists at a time” — hard constraint that rules out reactive or destructive workarounds in favor of a practice that prevents duplication from happening at all.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“You want to follow Google-recommended practices” — exclusion signal: the answer must be the documented best practice, not merely a workaround that happens to achieve the stated goal.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“within a CI/CD pipeline” — preference/context clue: pipeline runs are typically ephemeral, which is exactly the condition under which unshared local state gets lost between runs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Terraform decides what to create, update, or leave alone by diffing your configuration against its state file, which maps configuration to real-world resource IDs. If that state lives only on the ephemeral pipeline runner’s local disk (or isn’t being reliably written back), each run starts from a blank slate and <code>terraform apply</code> concludes nothing exists yet — so it creates a full new stack. Configuring the <code>gcs</code> backend directs Terraform to read and write <code>terraform.tfstate</code> from a persistent Cloud Storage bucket on every run, and the backend also provides state locking, so concurrent or repeated pipeline executions converge on updating the one real stack instead of spawning another.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A cleanup pipeline can reduce the cost impact of stacks that already exist, but it does nothing to stop the root cause, so new duplicates will keep appearing between cleanup runs. It’s an operational patch layered on top of a broken workflow, not the Google-recommended fix.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Source control is not a valid remote state backend. It offers no state locking, so overlapping pipeline runs can corrupt or desynchronize state, and committed state files commonly expose sensitive resource attributes in plaintext. This is a documented anti-pattern, and critically, state drifting between source control and reality is a plausible cause of the very symptom described — not a solution to it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Destroying and recreating the entire stack on every deployment technically prevents duplicates, but at the cost of full service downtime and unnecessary resource churn on every change — the opposite of balancing delivery speed with reliability, and not how Google recommends operating persistent infrastructure with Terraform.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The visible symptom — “multiple stacks exist” — pulls you toward a deletion/cleanup answer (A) because that directly addresses what you can see. The trap is treating the symptom as the problem instead of asking why Terraform keeps failing to recognize the stack it already built. If the scenario were changed so that state was already correctly centralized in a GCS backend but the team intentionally spins up ephemeral per-branch environments that pile up by design, a cleanup pipeline (A) would become the right answer instead.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Terraform’s state file is the single source of truth linking your <code>.tf</code> configuration to actual deployed resources. Whether separate executions of a pipeline “remember” prior infrastructure depends entirely on whether they share that same state consistently. A remote backend with locking (such as <code>gcs</code>) is what turns Terraform from a per-run, stateless tool into a safe, idempotent automation component for CI/CD.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team wires <code>terraform apply</code> into Cloud Build on every merge to main but never adds a <code>backend</code> block. Every merge spins up a parallel, fully duplicated environment, multiplying compute and storage spend and leaving no clear “real” environment. Adding a <code>backend \"gcs\" {}</code> block pointing at a versioned, access-controlled bucket fixes the duplication immediately and, as a side benefit, protects against race conditions from two pipeline runs applying at once.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>No shared remote state means Terraform forgets what it already built, so every apply looks like day one.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a Terraform-driven pipeline keeps creating duplicate infrastructure instead of updating it, suspect the state backend first. Google’s recommended practice is a locked, remote GCS backend — not source control, and not a destroy-and-rebuild or after-the-fact cleanup workaround.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/terraform/resource-management/store-state\"><strong>Google Cloud — Store Terraform state in a Cloud Storage bucket:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (why local/unshared state causes duplicate stacks, and how the gcs backend fixes it) and the elimination of Option C (source control is not the documented remote-state solution)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\">Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide: </a>Supports: domain alignment to Section 1.2, “Managing infrastructure,” which explicitly lists infrastructure-as-code tooling including Terraform</p>","lessons":["d1l6"]},"e2q18":{"id":"e2q18","exam":2,"n":18,"domain":1,"html":"<p>Your team runs workloads on three Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) environments: development, staging, and production. Your GitHub repositories serve as the single source of truth for cluster configuration. You must keep all three environments consistent and, following Google-recommended practices, enforce and install a set of network policies and a logging DaemonSet across every cluster in these environments. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use Cloud Deploy to roll out the network policies and the DaemonSet, and configure Cloud Monitoring to raise an alert whenever either one diverges from the configuration stored in the repository.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use Cloud Deploy to roll out the DaemonSet and Policy Controller to define the network policies, then rely on Cloud Monitoring to detect divergence from the repository and Cloud Run functions to remediate it.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use Cloud Build to render and deploy the network policies and the DaemonSet, and configure Config Sync to keep the configurations of all three environments synchronized.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Use Cloud Build to render and deploy the network policies and the DaemonSet, and configure Policy Controller to enforce the configurations across all three environments.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Use Cloud Build to render and deploy the network policies and the DaemonSet, and configure Config Sync to keep the configurations of all three environments synchronized.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Start by separating the hard constraints from the noise. The hard constraints are: Git is the source of truth, all three clusters must stay consistent, and the same resources (network policies and a DaemonSet) must be both installed and kept enforced. “Install and keep consistent from Git” is the GitOps pattern, so the decision reduces to asking which option contains a component that continuously reconciles cluster state to the repository. Option C does through Config Sync; A only notifies, B builds a bespoke remediation chain, and D uses a component built for admission control, not for installing resources. Requirement → capability → elimination → decision: Git-driven consistency → continuous reconciliation → Config Sync.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“GitHub repositories as your source of truth” — technical signal for GitOps; the answer must pull configuration from Git and reconcile clusters to it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“ensure that the three environments are consistent” — hard constraint requiring continuous convergence, not one-time deployment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“enforce and install” — sequence/capability signal: the solution must both create the resources and keep them in place, which requires a reconciler.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Google-recommended practices” — exclusion signal that disfavors custom drift-detection plumbing assembled from alerts and functions.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Config Sync is the GitOps service included with GKE. It continuously monitors a central source of truth, such as a Git repository, and automatically reconciles each cluster’s state to match it. Pointing the dev, staging, and production clusters at the repository means the network policies and the logging DaemonSet are installed everywhere and any manual change or deletion is automatically reverted, which is exactly the consistency requirement. Cloud Build’s role in this option is the CI side: rendering the manifests (for example with Kustomize or Helm) so they can be committed or deployed as rendered output, while Config Sync handles the continuous delivery and drift correction.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Cloud Deploy can deliver the manifests, and Cloud Monitoring can alert on divergence. It is tempting because it looks like a complete deploy-plus-observe loop. It loses because an alert only tells a human about drift after it happens; nothing enforces or restores the desired state, so consistency depends on manual intervention.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> This adds automated remediation, which makes it look more complete than A. It loses on two counts: Policy Controller is an admission-control and audit engine built on Gatekeeper that validates or rejects requests against constraints, and is not the tool for installing network policy resources. The Monitoring-plus-Cloud Run functions drift-correction chain is also custom plumbing that duplicates what Config Sync does natively, so it is not the Google-recommended approach.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> Policy Controller is valuable for enforcing guardrails, such as rejecting noncompliant objects at admission time and auditing existing ones, so it is tempting for the word “enforce”. But it does not synchronize resources from Git or deliver a DaemonSet, so it cannot, on its own, guarantee that the required network policies and DaemonSet exist and match the repository across clusters.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest trap is Option D, because “enforce” maps naturally onto “Policy Controller”. The subtle distinction is that Policy Controller enforces what is allowed (admission and audit of constraints), while Config Sync enforces what exists and matches Git (reconciliation). If the question instead asked to prevent teams from creating Pods that violate a security standard, Policy Controller would become the right answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>GitOps means the desired state lives in version control and an in-cluster agent continuously pulls and reconciles actual state to it, which makes drift self-healing rather than something to be detected and fixed by people. Policy engines and reconcilers solve different problems: a reconciler makes the cluster match a declared state, while a policy engine accepts or rejects changes against rules. Mature setups often combine them, with Config Sync delivering both workloads and the policy constraints themselves.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team registers its dev, staging, and production clusters in a fleet and configures Config Sync to read a single repository with per-environment overlays. When an engineer manually deletes a NetworkPolicy in production during an incident, Config Sync restores it from Git automatically, and the change history for every policy lives in pull requests. The result is auditable, consistent security posture with far less operational toil than alert-and-fix workflows.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Git as the source of truth plus “keep clusters matching it” means Config Sync; Policy Controller decides what is allowed, not what gets installed.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question pairs a Git source of truth with consistency across multiple GKE clusters, look for Config Sync as the continuous reconciler. Treat Policy Controller as the admission and audit guardrail, and be wary of answers that rely on alerts or custom functions to handle drift.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/config-sync/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Config Sync overview:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (continuous reconciliation of clusters to a Git source of truth, drift prevention, and fleet-wide consistency) and the elimination of Options A and B</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/enterprise/policy-controller/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Policy Controller overview:</strong></a> Supports: the distinction that Policy Controller enforces constraints through admission control and auditing, which eliminates Option D as the mechanism for installing resources from Git</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide:</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment to Section 1.4, “Managing multiple environments,” which covers managing configuration and policy and managing GKE clusters across an enterprise</p>","lessons":["d1l7"]},"e2q19":{"id":"e2q19","exam":2,"n":19,"domain":2,"html":"<p>The latest version of your containerized application has passed testing and is ready for production on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). You were unable to fully load-test the new version in pre-production, so you need to make sure it does not introduce performance problems once it is deployed. The deployment must also be automated. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Deploy through a continuous delivery pipeline that uses a canary deployment, watch for performance issues in Cloud Monitoring, and increase traffic to the new version as the metrics support it.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Deploy through a continuous delivery pipeline that uses a blue/green deployment, move traffic to the new version, and watch for performance issues in Cloud Monitoring.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Deploy with kubectl, use Config Connector to gradually shift traffic between the versions, and watch for performance issues in Cloud Monitoring.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Deploy with kubectl with the spec.updateStrategy.type field set to RollingUpdate, watch for performance issues in Cloud Monitoring, and run the kubectl rollback command if any issues appear.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Deploy through a continuous delivery pipeline that uses a canary deployment, watch for performance issues in Cloud Monitoring, and increase traffic to the new version as the metrics support it.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two must-have constraints decide this item: the deployment must be automated, and the release must be safe given that load testing was incomplete. Walking requirement → capability: because performance under real load is unknown, the strategy has to expose the new version to a small share of production traffic first, then widen exposure only when metrics look healthy. That is the canary pattern. Automation points to a continuous delivery pipeline rather than hand-run commands. Options that shift all traffic at once, rely on manual <code>kubectl</code>, or use a tool that is not a traffic manager fail one of those two constraints, leaving only option A.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“could not fully load-test the new version in your pre-production environment” is a hard constraint. Performance risk is unresolved until real traffic hits the new version, so exposure must be limited at first.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“ensure that the application does not have performance problems after deployment” is a hard constraint. The strategy must include a metrics-driven check before the rollout widens.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Your deployment must be automated” is a hard constraint. This favors a continuous delivery pipeline over manual <code>kubectl</code> steps.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Use Cloud Monitoring to look for performance issues” is a technical signal. Every option monitors; the difference is how much traffic is exposed while you watch.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A canary deployment sends a small percentage of traffic to the new version while the current version keeps serving the rest, so any performance regression affects only a fraction of users. You watch Cloud Monitoring, and if latency and resource metrics hold, you raise the percentage in stages until the new version carries all traffic. Cloud Deploy supports this on GKE, where a delivery pipeline stage defines canary phases with percentages and can run verification at each phase, and it manages the underlying Deployment and Service resources during the rollout. The pipeline makes the progression repeatable and automated, while the staged percentages give the real-traffic evidence that pre-production load testing could not.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B is <em>Technically Possible but Not Best</em>.</strong> Blue/green runs the new version alongside the old one and switches traffic to it. That gives fast rollback, but the option migrates traffic to the new version and only then looks for problems, so every user is exposed to any performance issue at once. It is tempting because it is also automatable and easy to reverse, but it does not limit the blast radius of an untested-under-load release.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C is <em>Violates Requirement</em>.</strong> Config Connector lets you manage Google Cloud resources through Kubernetes configuration, so it is not a mechanism for gradually shifting traffic between application versions. Pairing it with manual <code>kubectl</code> also fails the requirement for an automated deployment.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D is <em>Violates Requirement</em>.</strong> A rolling update replaces Pods gradually, but it does not hold back traffic according to performance metrics, and it starts from a manual <code>kubectl</code> deployment rather than an automated pipeline. The option’s details are also off: Kubernetes Deployments set their update strategy under <code>.spec.strategy.type</code>, not <code>spec.updateStrategy.type</code>, and the rollback command is <code>kubectl rollout undo</code>, since <code>kubectl rollback</code> is not a command.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is choosing blue/green (B) because it sounds like the safest, most mature strategy. Its safety comes from fast reversal after a problem is seen, whereas this question needs protection before most users are affected. If the stem said the new version had been fully load-tested but you needed zero downtime and an instant switch-back, blue/green would be the better answer. A second trap is treating a rolling update as a canary. It replaces Pods incrementally, but it does not tie the progression to the performance metrics you are watching.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Progressive delivery limits risk by controlling how much traffic a new version receives and by gating each increase on evidence. The main strategies differ in blast radius and reversal speed: rolling updates replace instances gradually, blue/green swaps whole environments, and canary exposes a slice of real traffic first. When you cannot validate a release under production-like load beforehand, the strategy should turn production itself into the test, in small, observed steps. Automation matters because manual traffic changes are slow and error-prone.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team defines a Cloud Deploy pipeline with canary phases such as 10%, then 50%, then 100%, and watches latency and error dashboards in Cloud Monitoring between phases. If a phase shows degraded latency, they stop and roll back while only a small share of users saw the problem. The trade-off is a slower rollout and the need to run two versions side by side, which also requires the application to tolerate mixed versions, for example with compatible database schemas.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Unproven under load means start small and widen on evidence: a canary through an automated pipeline.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question says the release could not be fully tested for performance and the deployment must be automated, choose a canary deployment through a continuous delivery pipeline with metrics-gated ramp-up. Blue/green fits when instant switch-back matters more than limiting exposure, and manual <code>kubectl</code> options fail the automation requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/deployment-strategies/canary/gke/service-networking\"><strong>Google Cloud — Canary deployments to GKE and GKE attached clusters using service-based networking (Cloud Deploy):</strong></a> Supports: Cloud Deploy canary strategies define percentage phases in a delivery pipeline stage and can enable verification on each phase, which is the automated, metrics-gated rollout in Option A</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/config-connector/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Config Connector overview:</strong></a> Supports: Config Connector manages Google Cloud resources through Kubernetes tooling and APIs, which is why it is not a traffic-ramping mechanism for application versions (Option C)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/run-application/update-deployment-rolling/\">Kubernetes — Update a Deployment Without Downtime: </a>Supports: rolling updates are configured under <code>.spec.strategy</code> on a Deployment and are rolled back with <code>kubectl rollout undo</code>, which clarifies the field and command errors in Option D</p>","lessons":["d2l4","d2l6"]},"e2q20":{"id":"e2q20","exam":2,"n":20,"domain":1,"html":"<p>Your organization is building a CI/CD pipeline that deploys Google Cloud resources with Terraform. The CI/CD tooling runs on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), and each pipeline run executes in its own ephemeral Pod. You need to make sure that the pipelines running in these Pods hold the right Identity and Access Management (IAM) permissions to carry out the Terraform deployments, and you want to follow Google-recommended practices for identity management. Which two actions should you take? (Choose two.)</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create a Kubernetes service account, associate it with the Pods, and use Workload Identity Federation for GKE to authenticate as the Google service account.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Generate a JSON key for the Google service account, save it as a Kubernetes secret, mount it into the Pods, and set the GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS environment variable.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create a Google service account and grant it the IAM permissions that the deployments require.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Generate a JSON key for the Google service account, keep it in the CI/CD tool’s secret management store, and configure Terraform to authenticate with that key.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>E. Grant the required IAM permissions to the Google service account used by the Compute Engine VM instances that host the Pods.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Create a Kubernetes service account, associate it with the Pods, and use Workload Identity Federation for GKE to authenticate as the Google service account.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Create a Google service account and grant it the IAM permissions that the deployments require.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Identify the hard constraints: the workload is on GKE, Pods are ephemeral, and the identity approach must follow Google-recommended practice, which means no long-lived credentials. That eliminates anything built around exported service account keys (B, D). The remaining requirement is a least-privilege identity for the pipeline rather than a shared node identity, which eliminates E. For a multi-select item, check that the two answers are complementary rather than redundant: one creates and permissions the identity (C), and the other connects the Pods to it keylessly (A). Together they form the complete chain from Pod to Google Cloud IAM.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“ephemeral Pod for each pipeline run” — technical signal: credentials must be obtained automatically at runtime, and nothing long-lived should be distributed to short-lived Pods.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“appropriate Identity and Access Management (IAM) permissions” — hard constraint: the identity must carry only the roles Terraform needs, which points to a dedicated, purpose-built service account.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Google-recommended practices for identity management” — exclusion signal: service account keys and node-level identities are the documented anti-patterns on GKE.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“(Choose two.)” — cardinality instruction: the answer is a pair of steps that work together, not two alternatives.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Option C establishes the identity: a dedicated Google service account, with just the IAM roles required for the Terraform deployments, is what the pipeline will act as, and it keeps those permissions separate from every other workload. Option A connects the Pods to that identity without any exported secret. With Workload Identity Federation for GKE enabled, a Kubernetes ServiceAccount assigned to the Pods is granted the ability to act as the Google service account (by impersonating it), and GKE exchanges the Pod’s identity for short-lived credentials automatically. The two options are interdependent: A has nothing to authenticate as without C, and C’s permissions are unreachable from the Pods without A. The set is complete because it covers identity creation, permission assignment, and keyless Pod authentication.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> It works technically and is tempting because it is a familiar pattern, but it relies on a long-lived exported JSON key. Keys are static credentials that can be leaked, copied, or left unrotated, and they are exactly what keyless mechanisms replace. Kubernetes secrets are only base64-encoded by default, which does not change that.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Moving the key into the CI/CD tool’s secret store improves storage slightly, but it is still a long-lived exported key requiring rotation and carrying leak risk. It also bypasses the native identity that GKE provides to Pods, so it is not the recommended practice.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option E — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Granting permissions to the node VMs’ service account does let the Pods obtain credentials via the metadata server, which is why it is tempting as the “no keys” choice. However, every Pod on those nodes would inherit the same broad permissions, which violates least privilege and blurs workload isolation. Workload Identity Federation for GKE exists specifically to give each workload its own identity.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The most dangerous distractor is E, because it is keyless and therefore looks like it satisfies the “no keys” instinct. The distinction is scope: node-level identity is shared by all Pods on the node, while Workload Identity Federation for GKE scopes identity per Kubernetes ServiceAccount. If the scenario stated that the cluster was dedicated entirely to this one pipeline and per-workload isolation was not required, E would become a defensible choice.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Prefer short-lived, automatically issued credentials bound to a workload’s own identity over static secrets. On GKE, that means mapping a Kubernetes ServiceAccount to a Google Cloud identity so that the platform vouches for the Pod. Least privilege then comes from granting that dedicated identity only the roles its task requires. Note that Google’s current guidance also allows granting IAM roles directly to the Kubernetes ServiceAccount principal without impersonation, but the exam item asks for a Google service account, so the impersonation pattern in A and C is the matching approach here.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team runs Terraform jobs as ephemeral Pods in a CI namespace. They create a dedicated Google service account with only the roles needed for the target projects, bind the job’s Kubernetes ServiceAccount to it, and deploy. There are no keys to rotate, no secrets to leak from the cluster, and audit logs attribute each Terraform change to a single, purpose-built identity, which simplifies incident review and permission tightening.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>On GKE, give each workload its own identity and let the platform issue short-lived credentials; never ship keys to Pods.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For workloads on GKE that need Google Cloud access, choose Workload Identity Federation for GKE with a dedicated, least-privilege service account. Reject exported JSON keys, wherever they are stored, and reject node-level service accounts shared by every Pod.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity\"><strong>Google Cloud — Authenticate to Google Cloud APIs from GKE workloads:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (A and C), covering how Workload Identity Federation for GKE lets Kubernetes ServiceAccounts access Google Cloud with granted IAM permissions and without keys, and why this is preferred over keys (B, D) and node-level identity (E)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide:</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment to Section 1.1, “Designing the overall resource hierarchy for an organization,” which includes Identity and Access Management (IAM) roles and creating and managing service accounts</p>","lessons":["d1l3"]},"e2q21":{"id":"e2q21","exam":2,"n":21,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You need to build a CI/CD pipeline in Google Cloud for a containerized application. Your development team practices trunk-based development with a central Git repository. You want every new version of the application to pass through all of your tests in the pipeline so that quality improves. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. First, install a Git hook that requires developers to run unit tests before pushing code to the central repository. Second, trigger Cloud Build to build the application container, deploy it to a testing environment, and run integration tests. Third, if the integration tests pass, deploy the container to production and run acceptance tests.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. First, install a Git hook that requires developers to run unit tests before pushing code to the central repository, and build a container if all tests pass. Second, trigger Cloud Build to deploy the container to a testing environment and run integration tests and acceptance tests. Third, if all tests pass, tag the code as production ready, then trigger Cloud Build to build and deploy the application container to production.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. First, trigger Cloud Build to build the application container and run unit tests using that container. Second, if the unit tests pass, deploy the container to a testing environment and run integration tests. Third, if the integration tests pass, have the pipeline deploy the container to production, then run acceptance tests.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. First, trigger Cloud Build to run unit tests whenever code is pushed, and if all unit tests pass, build the application container and push it to a central registry. Second, trigger Cloud Build to deploy the container to a testing environment and run integration tests and acceptance tests. Third, if all tests pass, have the pipeline deploy the application to production and run smoke tests.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. First, trigger Cloud Build to run unit tests whenever code is pushed, and if all unit tests pass, build the application container and push it to a central registry. Second, trigger Cloud Build to deploy the container to a testing environment and run integration tests and acceptance tests. Third, if all tests pass, have the pipeline deploy the application to production and run smoke tests.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two must-have constraints decide this item: all tests must run <em>in the pipeline</em> , and every test must pass before the release reaches production. Walking requirement → capability: a pipeline-run test is enforced for every push, while a developer-side hook is not. Test order should also run from cheapest to most expensive, so a failure stops the pipeline early. Finally, the artifact that passes the test stages should be the one that is deployed. Only option D puts unit tests in the pipeline, builds and stores a container once, runs integration and acceptance tests before production, and limits production to quick post-deploy checks.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“run all your tests in the pipeline” is a hard constraint. Testing cannot depend on a step outside the pipeline, such as a local developer hook.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“trunk-based development” is a technical signal. Commits land frequently on one shared branch, so automated checks must trigger on each push rather than rely on individual discipline.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“any new versions of the application” is a hard constraint. Every version must pass the full set of tests, which means testing before production exposure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“improve the quality” is a preference. The order of stages should catch defects early and keep untested code away from customers.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Running unit tests in Cloud Build on every push makes the quality gate part of the shared pipeline, so it applies to every commit no matter whose machine produced it. Building the container only after unit tests pass avoids packaging code that already failed. Storing the image in a central registry gives the later stages one artifact to deploy. Deploying that container to a testing environment lets integration tests and acceptance tests exercise it in a production-like setting before any customer is affected. Only if all of those pass does the pipeline deploy to production, where short smoke tests confirm the release started correctly. This matches the shape Google documents for containerized apps: Cloud Build builds and tests, the container goes to Artifact Registry, it is deployed to staging, and then the same container is promoted to production.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A is <em>Violates Requirement</em>.</strong> It relies on a Git hook for unit tests, so those tests are not run in the pipeline. It also deploys to production before running acceptance tests, so a version that would fail acceptance reaches customers first.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B is <em>Violates Requirement</em>.</strong> Its unit-test gate is again a Git hook rather than a pipeline step. It also builds the production container a second time after the tests pass, so the artifact that reaches production is not the artifact that was tested. It is tempting because the test environment stage correctly includes both integration and acceptance tests.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C is <em>Violates Requirement</em>.</strong> Unit tests run in Cloud Build and integration tests run in a test environment, which is close to right. The flaw is the final step: it deploys to production and only then runs acceptance tests, so acceptance testing is not a gate for the release. Running unit tests inside the already-built container is also a slower point to catch failures than before the build.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is Option C, which looks nearly identical to D until the last step. Reading quickly, “Cloud Build, test environment, integration tests, production” seems right, but acceptance tests placed after production make customers the test audience. A second trap is treating a Git hook as a pipeline control. Git’s own documentation notes that client-side hooks are not copied when a repository is cloned and can be bypassed (for example with <code>--no-verify</code>), and that policy enforcement belongs on the server side. A pre-push hook would be a reasonable add-on if the goal were faster local feedback, but it cannot satisfy “in the pipeline.”</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A trustworthy CI/CD pipeline orders its checks from fast and cheap to slow and broad: unit tests first, then integration tests against a deployed test environment, then acceptance tests, and only then production. Two principles support this. First, quality gates must live where they cannot be skipped, which is the shared pipeline. Second, build the artifact once and promote that same artifact through the environments, so what you tested is exactly what you ship. Post-deploy smoke tests are brief sanity checks, not a replacement for pre-production acceptance testing.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team configures a Cloud Build trigger on pushes to the main branch. Unit tests run first; if they pass, the container is built and pushed to Artifact Registry, deployed to a staging cluster, and checked by integration and acceptance suites, with production deployment gated on success. Developers still get a quick local feedback loop, but the guarantee comes from the pipeline. The trade-off is pipeline run time: long acceptance suites delay releases, so teams parallelize tests and keep unit tests fast so that trunk-based commits are not blocked.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Gates belong in the pipeline, run fast tests first, build once, and never put customers before acceptance tests.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For “run all tests in the pipeline” questions, choose the option with CI-triggered unit tests, a single container build stored in a registry, integration and acceptance tests in a test environment, and production last. Reject options that rely on Git hooks or run acceptance tests after production.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/app-development-and-delivery-with-cloud-code-gcb-cd-and-gke\">Google Cloud — CI/CD pipeline for developing and delivering containerized apps: </a>Supports: the recommended flow in which Cloud Build builds and tests, the container is stored in Artifact Registry and deployed to staging, and the same container is then promoted to production (Option D over A, B, and C)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Customizing-Git-Git-Hooks\"><strong>Pro Git — Customizing Git: Git Hooks:</strong></a> Supports: client-side hooks are not copied when a repository is cloned and can be bypassed, and policy enforcement belongs on the server side, which is why a Git hook is not a pipeline gate (Options A and B)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Exam Guide — Section 2.1, Designing pipelines:</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment via “CI/CD of applications and infrastructure” and “CI/CD pipeline triggers” within the section on building and implementing CI/CD pipelines including continuous testing</p>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e2q22":{"id":"e2q22","exam":2,"n":22,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You are defining the deployment and testing approach for a CI/CD pipeline on Google Cloud. The approach you choose must satisfy two requirements:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>It must simplify release deployments and keep the time needed to roll back a release as short as possible.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>It must let you validate changes against real production traffic while progressively widening the share of users exposed to the change.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Which deployment strategy and testing strategy should you select?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Recreate deployment combined with canary testing</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Blue/green deployment combined with canary testing</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Rolling update deployment combined with A/B testing</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Rolling update deployment combined with shadow testing</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Blue/green deployment combined with canary testing</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Treat the item as two independent filters, one per requirement, and keep only the option that passes both. Filter one concerns the deployment strategy: simple releases and the shortest rollback. Filter two concerns the testing strategy: real production traffic with gradually increasing user exposure. Evaluate each half separately, because every option pairs one deployment pattern with one testing pattern, and a single wrong half is enough to eliminate it. Only option B has a deployment half that gives near-instant rollback and a testing half that exposes users incrementally.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“minimize the duration of deployment rollbacks” — hard constraint on the deployment half: favors a strategy where rollback is a traffic switch rather than a redeployment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Reduce the complexity of release deployments” — preference on the deployment half: favors a clean cutover between two whole versions over a gradual in-place version mix.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“real production traffic” — hard constraint on the testing half: eliminates any approach that relies on simulated or staging traffic only.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“gradual increase in the number of affected users” — hard constraint on the testing half: describes progressively widening exposure, which is the defining behavior of canary testing and not of shadow testing (no users affected) or A/B testing (condition-based segments).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Blue/green deployment runs two identical environments: the current version (blue) keeps serving traffic while the new version (green) is deployed and verified, and a traffic switch at the load balancer then cuts over. That keeps releases simple, with no mixed-version fleet to reason about, and rollback is just pointing traffic back at the still-running blue environment, which is why it is documented as an instant rollback. Canary testing partially rolls out a change to a small share of live production traffic, compares it with a baseline, and increases exposure step by step, which satisfies the requirement for real traffic with a gradually growing number of affected users. The two strategies are complementary: blue/green provides the clean environments and fast fallback, and canary provides the progressive exposure.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Canary testing satisfies the testing half, but a recreate deployment shuts down the old version before starting the new one, which causes downtime and makes rollback slow because the previous version must be redeployed. It directly conflicts with minimizing rollback duration.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Rolling updates replace instances gradually in place, so rolling back means rolling forward again through the fleet, which is slower than a traffic switch, and the system runs mixed versions during the process. A/B testing also targets users under specific conditions to compare variants for experimentation, rather than progressively increasing the share of affected users, so both halves are a weaker fit.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Shadow testing mirrors real production traffic to the new version without affecting users’ responses, so there is no gradual increase in affected users at all, which contradicts the stated testing requirement. Pairing it with a rolling update also leaves rollback slower than a blue/green switch, so neither half matches.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is option C, because rolling updates are a common default on GKE and managed instance groups and A/B testing sounds like testing on real traffic with partial exposure. The distinction is that the question wants rollback speed (a traffic switch, not a fleet re-roll) and progressive exposure by percentage (canary, not condition-based experiments). If the requirement were instead to compare two variants for a specific user segment, A/B testing would become correct, and if the requirement were to test with no user impact at all, shadow testing would.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Deployment strategies define how a new version replaces the old one (recreate, rolling, blue/green), while testing strategies define how you gain confidence in it using live traffic (canary, A/B, shadow). They are separate decisions that are often combined. Rollback speed is determined by whether the old version is still running and traffic can be redirected, and risk is controlled by how much real traffic the new version receives. Blue/green’s cost is running duplicate environments, and it requires backward-compatible data and schema changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team deploys a new release to a green environment behind the same load balancer, then shifts 5 percent of traffic to it, watches error rates and latency against the baseline, and raises the percentage in stages. If a metric degrades, they redirect all traffic to the blue environment within seconds instead of redeploying. The cost is temporarily paying for two environments, which is an acceptable trade-off for fast recovery and lower blast radius.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Blue/green decides how fast you can undo a release; canary decides how many users you risk while testing it.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks for fast rollback plus gradually increasing exposure to real traffic, evaluate the deployment half and the testing half separately. Blue/green gives the instant rollback, and canary gives the progressive production exposure. Shadow means no user impact, and A/B means condition-based segments.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/application-deployment-and-testing-strategies\"><strong>Google Cloud — Application deployment and testing strategies:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (blue/green cutover with instant rollback via traffic redirection, and canary testing on live production traffic) and the elimination of the recreate, rolling, A/B, and shadow pairings</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide:</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment to Section 2.2, “Implementing and managing pipelines,” which lists deployment strategies (canary, blue/green, rolling, traffic splitting, feature flags)</p>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e2q23":{"id":"e2q23","exam":2,"n":23,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You operate a globally used service that has a 99% availability target, and your team has limited engineering capacity. For the current calendar month, measured availability is 99.5%. You need to keep the service within its defined availability goals while staying able to respond to business changes, including an upcoming launch of new features. You must also reduce technical debt and keep operational costs low, and you want to follow Google-recommended practices. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Provide N+1 redundancy for the service by provisioning additional compute capacity.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Identify, quantify, and eliminate toil by automating repetitive operational tasks.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Establish an error budget for the service’s availability target and work to minimize the remaining error budget.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Assign the available engineers to the feature backlog while making sure the service stays within its availability target.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Identify, quantify, and eliminate toil by automating repetitive operational tasks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Separate the must-haves from the context. The context is that the service already beats its target, so reliability is not the problem. The must-haves are three simultaneous goals: keep meeting the target and absorb upcoming change, reduce technical debt, and minimize operational cost, all with few engineers. Test each option against all three, not just the first. An option that adds cost, or that uses the spare reliability headroom for something other than debt and cost reduction, fails a requirement. Only the option that converts engineering time into lasting automation satisfies all of them together.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“99% target” versus “99.5% availability” — technical signal: the service is over-delivering, so the problem is not reliability and no new reliability spend is justified.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“limited engineering resources” — hard constraint: the chosen work must free capacity, not consume more of it ongoing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“reduce technical debt while minimizing operational costs” — hard constraint: the answer must lower recurring effort and cost, which feature work or added infrastructure does not.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“react to business changes, including the upcoming launch” — preference: the team needs spare capacity and headroom for change, which comes from removing manual work.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Toil is manual, repetitive, automatable operational work that scales with the service and brings no lasting value. Google SRE practice is to identify it, measure it, and eliminate it with engineering, keeping operational work below about half of each engineer’s time so the rest goes to projects that reduce future toil or add features. Automating toil directly reduces technical debt, lowers operating cost, and frees scarce engineers to support the launch, while the existing availability surplus gives the team room to invest in that automation safely. It satisfies every requirement at once, which none of the other options do.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> It is tempting because redundancy sounds like reliability protection ahead of a launch. But the service already exceeds its target, so extra capacity buys reliability nobody asked for while directly increasing operating cost, the opposite of what the question requires. It also does nothing about technical debt.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Error budgets are a core SRE tool, and the 99% target already implies a 1% budget, of which only half a point has been used this month. But the item asks for action on debt and cost, and “minimizing the remaining budget” would mean deliberately burning reliability headroom as an end in itself. Budgets govern how fast you may change things, not how you reduce toil or debt, so this does not solve the stated problem.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> The surplus budget does permit feature velocity, which is why this is the most tempting distractor. However, it directs scarce engineers to new features and leaves technical debt and operational cost unaddressed, so it meets only the first goal and ignores the other two.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is reading “99.5% against a 99% target” as a signal to spend the error budget on feature work, which leads to Option D. The surplus budget describes what is safe to do, but the question’s explicit requirements are debt reduction and low operational cost, so the best use of limited engineers is toil elimination. If the question dropped those two requirements and asked only how to take advantage of the surplus, Option D would be the right answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An SLO defines acceptable reliability, and the gap between it and 100% is the error budget that can be spent on change and risk. Toil work is a separate lever: automation turns recurring human effort into a one-time engineering investment, which compounds by freeing capacity. Reliability work, feature work, and toil reduction compete for the same limited engineering time, so the right choice depends on which constraint the scenario emphasizes.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A small team notices it spends hours each week on manual certificate renewals, ad hoc capacity changes, and ticket-driven access grants. By measuring that time and automating those tasks, they cut recurring on-call and ticket load and shrink the manual surface that creates inconsistencies. When the feature launch arrives, they have engineers available and a simpler system to change, without having paid for extra infrastructure.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>When reliability already exceeds the target and the goals are less debt and lower cost, spend engineering time on removing toil, not on more capacity or more features.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Read every stated goal in an SRE scenario, since the surplus-error-budget detail is a distraction when the question asks for debt and cost reduction. Choose toil elimination through automation when engineering capacity is limited and recurring manual work is the cost driver.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/eliminating-toil/\"><strong>Google SRE Book — Eliminating Toil:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (defining toil, capping operational work at about 50 percent, and using engineering project time to reduce future toil), and the elimination of Options A and D as responses to debt and cost goals</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/management-tools/identifying-and-tracking-toil-using-sre-principles\"><strong>Google Cloud Blog — Identifying and tracking toil using SRE principles:</strong></a> Supports: the link between identifying, measuring, and reducing toil and lower operating costs, and the role of an SLO and error budget in deciding when to invest in automation (distinguishing Option C)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide:</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment to Section 3, “Applying site reliability engineering practices,” covering balancing change, velocity, and reliability and managing the service lifecycle</p>","lessons":["d3l3","d3l6"]},"e2q24":{"id":"e2q24","exam":2,"n":24,"domain":5,"html":"<p>You are setting up the frontend tier of an application on Google Cloud. The frontend runs nginx on a managed instance group and sits behind an Envoy-based external Application Load Balancer. The entire application is deployed in the europe-west2 region and serves only users located in the United Kingdom. You need to pick the network tier and load balancing scope that minimize cost. Which combination should you choose?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Premium Tier with a global load balancer</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Premium Tier with a regional load balancer</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Standard Tier with a global load balancer</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Standard Tier with a regional load balancer</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Standard Tier with a regional load balancer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Start with the requirement that drives cost: users and backends are all in one region, so nothing in the scenario needs global reach or Google’s global backbone. Then work through the two decisions in order. First, decide which tier is cheapest while still workable: Standard. Second, check which load balancer scope is actually available in that tier. Because the question specifies an Envoy-based load balancer, the global option is eliminated by tier compatibility, which leaves the regional option as the only valid cheapest combination.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“most cost-effective” — hard constraint: the answer must optimize cost, which favors Standard Tier over Premium Tier.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“deployed entirely within the europe-west2 region” — technical signal: all backends are in a single region, so a global load balancer adds no value.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“only serves users based in the United Kingdom” — technical signal: there is no need for global anycast IPs or worldwide backbone ingress, and users are close to the region.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Envoy-based external ... load balancer” — technical signal: the Envoy-based global external Application Load Balancer runs only in Premium Tier, which constrains valid pairings.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Standard Tier is the cost-optimized tier: traffic enters and leaves Google’s network at a peering point near the region where the load balancer is configured, instead of riding Google’s global backbone. The regional external Application Load Balancer, which is Envoy-based, can be configured in either Premium or Standard Tier, and it serves backends from a single region, which matches this deployment exactly. Pairing it with Standard Tier gives the lowest-cost valid design for UK-only users in one region. One prerequisite to remember is that the regional external Application Load Balancer needs a proxy-only subnet in the region.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A global external Application Load Balancer in Premium Tier works and would serve these users, which makes it tempting as the “default” choice. It loses because you would pay for global capabilities, such as anycast IPs and worldwide backbone ingress, that a single-region, UK-only application never uses.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A regional load balancer is the right scope, and it does run in Premium Tier, so it is a close second. It loses on the cost requirement because Standard Tier is available for the same load balancer and is the lower-cost tier, and nothing in the scenario requires Premium Tier’s performance.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Incorrect:</strong> This combination is not available for an Envoy-based global load balancer. Global external Application Load Balancers use global external IP addresses and global forwarding rules, which require Premium Tier, so it cannot be built even though it sounds cheapest. The classic Application Load Balancer can operate regionally in Standard Tier, but then it is not functioning as a global load balancer and is not the Envoy-based type the question describes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is Option C, because “Standard Tier” pairs with the cost requirement and “global” pairs with the load balancer type, so the combination looks like the best of both. The distinction is that Standard Tier is regional only, so a global Envoy-based load balancer cannot use it. If the users were spread worldwide, had backends in multiple regions, or needed Cloud CDN, Premium Tier with a global load balancer would become the correct choice.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Network Service Tiers decide how traffic travels between the internet and your resources: Premium Tier uses Google’s global backbone for performance, while Standard Tier uses regular ISP networks to reduce cost. The tier also constrains which resources you can use, since global IP addresses and global load balancing require Premium Tier, whereas regional resources can use either. The general design rule is to buy global capability only when users or backends are actually global. Standard Tier trades some routing performance and feature breadth for lower cost, which is an acceptable trade when users sit near a single region.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A UK-focused retail site runs its nginx frontend in europe-west2 and creates a regional external Application Load Balancer using a Standard Tier regional IP address, plus the required proxy-only subnet. Because traffic enters near the London region and no global features are needed, networking costs stay low without changing the application. The team accepts that it has no global anycast address and cannot attach Cloud CDN, and it would move to Premium Tier only if the audience expanded internationally.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>One region and local users mean Standard Tier with a regional load balancer, because global load balancers require Premium Tier.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks for the cheapest network tier and the workload and users are in a single region, choose Standard Tier with a regional load balancer. Check pairing validity first: any global load balancer or global IP in the Envoy-based family forces Premium Tier.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/network-tiers/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Network Service Tiers overview:</strong></a> Supports: the cost-versus-performance distinction between tiers and the fact that global external IP addresses and the global external Application Load Balancer require Premium Tier while regional resources can use Standard Tier (eliminating Option C and justifying D over B)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/https\"><strong>Google Cloud — External Application Load Balancer overview:</strong></a> Supports: the regional external Application Load Balancer being configurable in Premium or Standard Tier and serving a single region, and the global mode being Premium only (supporting D and eliminating A)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide:</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment to Section 5.2, “Implementing FinOps practices for optimizing resource utilization and costs,” which lists infrastructure cost planning including network tiers</p>","lessons":["d5l2"]},"e2q25":{"id":"e2q25","exam":2,"n":25,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your company’s Google Cloud resource hierarchy has separate folders for production, test, and development. The cyber security team is reviewing the organization’s Google Cloud security posture and wants to find and resolve security issues faster. You need to consolidate the logs generated by Google Cloud services across all projects that sit only within the production folder, so the team can configure alerting and perform near-real-time analysis. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Enable the Workflows API and direct all logs into Cloud Logging.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Set up a central Cloud Monitoring metrics scope and add all relevant projects to it.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create an aggregated log sink on the production folder that routes to a Pub/Sub topic.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Create an aggregated log sink on the production folder that routes to a Cloud Logging bucket.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Create an aggregated log sink on the production folder that routes to a Cloud Logging bucket.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Split the requirement into three tests: scope (only production projects), content (logs, not metrics), and outcome (alerting and near-real-time analysis available without extra build-out). Apply scope first, since only a folder-level construct can bound the result to production. Then apply the content test, which removes anything that deals with metrics or unrelated services. Finally apply the outcome test to the remaining routing options: the destination that natively supports querying and alerting wins over a destination that merely transports data elsewhere.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“only inside your production folder” — hard constraint: the collection point must be attached at the production folder so test and development projects are excluded.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“centralize the logs ... from all projects” — technical signal: points to an aggregated sink, which gathers logs from every child project in one routing rule.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“alerting and near-real time analysis” — hard constraint: the destination must support alerting and analysis on its own, without additional consumer infrastructure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“logs generated by Google Cloud services” — exclusion signal: this is about log entries, which rules out metrics-focused or unrelated service options.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>An aggregated sink created on the production folder with child inclusion enabled routes matching log entries from every project in that folder to a single destination, and it does not touch the test and development folders. Choosing a Cloud Logging bucket as the destination keeps the logs inside Cloud Logging, where the security team can query them in Logs Explorer and configure log-based alerting policies, which meets the near-real-time analysis and alerting need with no additional pipeline. One prerequisite is that the sink’s writer identity must be granted permission to write to the destination bucket, and an inclusion filter can keep the bucket focused on security-relevant logs.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Incorrect:</strong> Workflows is a service for orchestrating steps across services and has no role in collecting or aggregating logs. It is tempting because “route all the logs to Cloud Logging” sounds like centralization, but each project already writes to its own Cloud Logging, so nothing is consolidated and nothing limits the scope to the production folder.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> A metrics scope lets one scoping project view metrics from multiple monitored projects, which is valuable for cross-project dashboards. It is tempting because it uses a Cloud Monitoring construct that spans projects, but it consolidates metrics rather than log entries, so it does not meet the log-centralization requirement.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A Pub/Sub topic does receive the routed logs in a streaming fashion, which makes it tempting for “near-real-time.” However, it is only a transport layer: you would still need a subscriber, storage, and an analysis and alerting layer to make the logs useful, whereas a Cloud Logging bucket provides querying and alerting natively.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is Option C, because streaming through Pub/Sub sounds like the natural fit for near-real-time. The distinction is that Pub/Sub moves logs somewhere else, while a Cloud Logging bucket keeps them where alerting and analysis already work. If the security team’s analysis happened in an external SIEM that needed a streaming feed, a Pub/Sub destination would become the correct choice.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>The Log Router evaluates sinks to send log entries to supported destinations such as log buckets, BigQuery datasets, Cloud Storage buckets, Pub/Sub topics, and other projects. A sink created at a folder or organization becomes an aggregated sink when it includes child resources, letting one rule collect from many projects. Destination choice decides what you can do next: buckets favor in-place analysis and alerting, BigQuery favors SQL analytics, Cloud Storage favors cheap archival, and Pub/Sub favors streaming to external systems.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A security team creates a dedicated logging project with a log bucket, then adds a folder-level aggregated sink on the production folder with child inclusion and a filter for audit and security logs. They grant the sink’s writer identity permission on the bucket and create log-based alerting policies for risky events such as IAM policy changes. New production projects are covered automatically, while test and development logs never enter the central bucket.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Scope the sink to the folder, then pick the destination that matches what you need to do with the logs.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For centralizing logs across many projects under one folder, use an aggregated sink on that folder with child inclusion. Choose a Cloud Logging bucket when the goal is in-place alerting and analysis, and choose Pub/Sub only when the logs must be streamed to an external consumer.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/export/aggregated_sinks\">Google Cloud — Collate and route organization- and folder-level logs to supported destinations (aggregated sinks): </a>Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (folder-level aggregated sinks with child inclusion, and the supported destinations including Cloud Logging buckets and Pub/Sub topics), and the elimination of Option A</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/workspaces\"><strong>Google Cloud — Metrics scopes overview:</strong></a> Supports: the distinction that a metrics scope lets a scoping project view metrics from multiple monitored projects, which eliminates Option B for a log-centralization requirement</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide:</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment to Section 4.2, “Managing and analyzing logs,” which covers exporting and retaining logs by routing to destinations such as BigQuery, Pub/Sub, and Cloud Storage</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l1","d4l8"]},"e2q26":{"id":"e2q26","exam":2,"n":26,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your organization needs to collect system logs from its Google Cloud project to power dashboards in Google Cloud Observability. You must configure every current and future Compute Engine instance to collect these system logs, and you must make sure the Ops Agent stays up to date. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use the Google Cloud CLI to install the Ops Agent on each VM listed in Cloud Asset Inventory.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. On the Cloud Monitoring VM Instances dashboard, select every VM whose agent status is Not detected, then choose the option to install agents.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use the Google Cloud CLI to create an agent policy.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Use a startup script to install the Ops Agent on the Compute Engine image.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Use the Google Cloud CLI to create an agent policy.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Pull out the two lifecycle requirements: coverage must include VMs that do not exist yet, and the agent must be kept current over time. Both describe a continuously enforced desired state, not a one-time installation. Test each option by asking what happens to a VM created next month and to a VM running an outdated agent. Any option that acts only on the VMs present today, or only on a fixed image, fails one of the tests. Only the option that declares a project-level policy for current and future VMs passes both.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“all current and future Compute Engine instances” — hard constraint: the mechanism must apply automatically to VMs created after setup, which excludes one-time actions against an existing list.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“ensure that the Ops Agent remains up to date” — hard constraint: requires ongoing lifecycle management of the agent, not just initial installation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“system logs ... dashboards” — technical signal: the Ops Agent is the agent that collects logs and metrics from Compute Engine VMs for use in Google Cloud Observability.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Use the Google Cloud CLI” — technical signal: agent policies are created through the <code>gcloud compute instances ops-agents policies</code> command group.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>An agent policy is a project-level definition that governs both existing and new VMs matching the criteria you specify, such as operating system, labels, or specific instances, and it automates installing and maintaining the Ops Agent. It builds on VM Manager’s OS Config capabilities, which keep applying the declared state, so a newly created VM that matches the policy receives the agent without anyone logging in. That satisfies the “current and future” requirement directly, and it is the mechanism Google provides for keeping the agent managed across a fleet. One prerequisite is that the OS Config agent must be present on the VMs, and it may need to be installed manually on older VMs.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Looping over the VMs listed in Cloud Asset Inventory does install the agent, which is tempting because it is scriptable and covers the existing fleet. It loses because the inventory is a snapshot, so VMs created later are not covered, and the script installs at one point in time with no ongoing upgrade management.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> The dashboard offers a convenient install action for VMs shown as Not detected. It loses on both lifecycle requirements: it is a manual, point-in-time action that does not reach future VMs, and VMs already running an older agent are not part of the Not detected set, so nothing keeps them current.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Baking the agent into the image covers only VMs created from that image, so VMs already running are untouched. It also freezes the agent version at the time the image was built, so the agent drifts out of date unless you keep rebuilding images and recreating VMs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The most tempting distractor is Option B, because it is the quickest visible fix and the dashboard states the problem directly. The distinction is that it remediates today’s gap, whereas the question demands a standing guarantee for future VMs and for currency. If the scenario asked only to fix a handful of existing VMs once, the dashboard install would become a reasonable answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Fleet management works best as declarative desired state: you state what every matching VM should have, and a control plane continuously reconciles reality to that state, rather than running imperative per-VM steps that go stale. Agent policies apply this idea to monitoring agents. For exam purposes, “current and future” plus “stay up to date” points to policy-based management. In production, check how upgrades are handled: Google’s documentation describes beta agent policies as able to upgrade the agent automatically, and for GA policies it points to OS Patch jobs for keeping the agent at the latest version.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team creates an agent policy that selects VMs by label, so new application VMs inherit the Ops Agent automatically at creation. Because agent policies apply only to the zone they are created in, the team defines one per zone that hosts VMs. System logs then appear in Cloud Logging and feed dashboards without per-VM toil, and compliance can be reviewed from the OS policies page.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Current and future” plus “stays up to date” means a policy, not a per-VM action or an image.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question requires agents on both existing and newly created VMs and asks that they be kept current, choose a policy-based, centrally managed approach such as an agent policy. Reject manual installs, inventory-driven scripts, and image baking, since each covers only a moment in time or only part of the fleet.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/agent/ops-agent/agent-policies-overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Agent policies overview (Ops Agent):</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (a policy governing existing and new VMs, built on VM Manager OS Config, created with the gcloud ops-agents policies commands) and the upgrade-handling nuance for beta versus GA policies</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/agent/installation\">Google Cloud — Installing the agent on individual VMs (VM Instances dashboard and agent status): </a>Supports: the elimination of Option B, showing that the dashboard install acts on selected VMs and that Not detected status means an agent is missing or not running</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide:</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment to Section 4.1, “Instrumenting and collecting telemetry,” which includes collecting and importing logs using the Ops Agent</p>","lessons":["d4l5"]},"e2q27":{"id":"e2q27","exam":2,"n":27,"domain":3,"html":"<p>Your team runs microservices on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). You want to detect when your error budget is being consumed so that you can protect customers and establish release policies. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Derive SLIs from metrics, and enable alerting policies to fire when the services fail to pass.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use the metrics from Cloud Service Mesh to gauge the health of the microservices.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Define an SLO, and create an alerting policy on select_slo_burn_rate.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Define an SLO and set up uptime checks for your services, and enable alerting policies to fire when the services fail to pass.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Define an SLO, and create an alerting policy on select_slo_burn_rate.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Work through the dependency chain the question implies. An error budget exists only when an SLO sets a target, so the answer must include defining an SLO. Next, “detect consumption” is about how quickly the budget is being used, not whether a single check passed or failed, so the alerting signal must be a rate of consumption. Finally, “protect customers” favors an early warning before the SLO is breached. Apply those three tests in order, and only one option carries an SLO and an alert on the burn rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“error budget” — technical signal: an error budget is derived from an SLO target, so an SLO must be defined and an SLI alone is not enough.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“detect consumption” — hard constraint: the alert must measure how fast the budget is being spent, which is the burn rate.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“protect customers” — preference: alerting on the rate of consumption warns before the SLO is violated, instead of after.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“define release policies” — technical signal: budget status gives an objective basis for deciding when to slow or pause releases.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>An SLO combines an SLI, a target, and a compliance period, and the error budget is the amount of failure the target permits. The burn rate describes how quickly that budget is being used, and Cloud Monitoring exposes it through the time-series selector <code>select_slo_burn_rate</code>, which takes the SLO and a lookback period. A sustained burn rate above one means the service will miss its SLO for that period. An alerting policy on this selector therefore notifies the team while there is still budget left, which protects customers, and the same signal gives an objective basis for release policies, such as slowing rollouts while the budget is burning fast. Google’s guidance recommends pairing a fast-burn alert with a slow-burn alert to cover both sudden and gradual consumption.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> SLIs are measurements, and alerting when services fail to pass reacts to threshold crossings on those measurements. It is tempting because SLIs are the foundation of reliability monitoring. But without an SLO target there is no error budget, so nothing can be detected as being consumed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Cloud Service Mesh telemetry gives visibility into service health, such as latency, traffic, and error rates, and it can be the data source for an SLI. It is tempting because it is native to GKE microservices. But raw health metrics have no objective or budget attached, so they do not detect consumption or inform release policy.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Defining an SLO is correct, but alerting when an uptime check fails is binary failure alerting on probe results. It is the closest distractor because it names both an SLO and alerting. It loses because the alert is not tied to the rate at which the error budget is being spent, so it does not give the early, proportional warning the question asks for.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is Option D, since “SLO plus alerting” looks like a complete answer. The subtle distinction is what the alert evaluates: a failed check says something is down now, while a burn-rate alert says the budget is being used up faster than the SLO can tolerate. If the question had asked to be notified as soon as an endpoint becomes unreachable, uptime-check alerting would be the better fit.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>The reliability chain runs from SLI (what you measure) to SLO (the target for it) to error budget (the permitted failure) to burn rate (how fast that allowance is being used). Alerting on burn rate ties monitoring directly to the objective, rather than to arbitrary thresholds on symptoms, and it lets teams trade release velocity against reliability using a shared measure. This is why error budget status is used to decide when to ship and when to focus on stability.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team defines an availability SLO for a checkout microservice over a rolling window, then creates a fast-burn alerting policy that pages on-call and a slow-burn policy that opens a ticket. Their release policy states that non-urgent rollouts pause while the fast-burn alert is active. Customers are protected because the team reacts to rapid budget loss early, and release decisions rest on an agreed measure instead of opinion. The cost is tuning thresholds and lookback windows so alerts stay actionable.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>The SLO sets the budget, and the burn rate shows how fast you are spending it, so alert on the rate.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks how to detect error budget consumption, look for an SLO plus an alerting policy based on burn rate. SLIs alone, raw mesh metrics, and uptime-check failures do not tell you how fast the budget is being used.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring/alerting-on-budget-burn-rate\"><strong>Google Cloud — Alerting on your burn rate (Cloud Monitoring SLO monitoring):</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (burn rate of the error budget, the select_slo_burn_rate time-series selector, and fast-burn and slow-burn alert types) and the elimination of Options A and D</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/service-mesh/docs/observability/design-slo\"><strong>Google Cloud — Design service level objectives (Cloud Service Mesh):</strong></a> Supports: how SLIs, SLO targets, compliance periods, and error budgets fit together for services running on GKE with Cloud Service Mesh, which distinguishes Option B</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\">Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide: </a>Supports: domain alignment to Section 3.1, “Balancing change, velocity, and reliability of the service,” which covers defining SLIs, SLOs, and SLAs and managing error budgets</p>","lessons":["d3l3"]},"e2q28":{"id":"e2q28","exam":2,"n":28,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your organization requires a change advisory board (CAB) to approve every change made to an existing service. You want to redesign this process so that it no longer has a negative effect on software delivery performance. Which two actions should you take? (Choose two.)</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Replace the CAB with a senior manager who provides continuous oversight from development through deployment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Allow developers to merge their own changes, provided the team’s deployment platform can roll back changes if problems are found.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Adopt a peer-review-based process for individual changes that is enforced at code check-in and backed by automated tests.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Group changes into larger, less frequent software releases.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>E. Make sure the team’s development platform gives developers fast feedback on the impact of their changes.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Adopt a peer-review-based process for individual changes that is enforced at code check-in and backed by automated tests.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>E. Make sure the team’s development platform gives developers fast feedback on the impact of their changes.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Begin with the hard constraint: whatever replaces the CAB must not slow delivery, so any option that keeps approval in the hands of people outside the team, or that increases risk per release, fails it. Then ask what control the CAB was providing, which is catching bad changes before they cause harm, and look for practices that deliver that control inside the team’s normal workflow. Because two answers are required, look for a complementary pair: one that gates changes at the point of check-in and one that shortens the loop for detecting problems. Only options C and E form that pair.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“change advisory board (CAB) to approve all changes” — technical signal: an external approval gate, which DORA research associates with worse delivery performance.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“eliminate any negative impact on the software delivery performance” — hard constraint: the replacement controls must be lightweight and owned by the team so they do not add waiting time.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“(Choose two.)” — cardinality instruction: select a complementary pair of practices, not two alternatives for the same job.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“existing service” — context clue: changes are ongoing and incremental, which favors per-change review and automation over scheduled approval meetings.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Option C replaces the CAB’s gatekeeping with peer review enforced when code is checked in and supported by automated tests. This keeps a genuine approval step and segregation of duties, since a second person reviews each change, while the review, comments, and approvals are recorded in the team’s development platform. Automated tests then catch many defects before a human spends time on them. Option E supplies the second half: when developers see the effect of their changes quickly, through continuous integration results, monitoring, and observability, bad changes are detected and corrected early instead of waiting for a committee. DORA’s research describes this same combination, peer review supplemented by automation for early detection, visibility, and fast feedback. The two work together rather than independently: C prevents and filters changes at check-in, and E detects and corrects what slips through, so the pair covers both prevention and detection without any external gate.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> A senior manager is another approval step performed by someone outside the team, so it keeps the same bottleneck under a different name. It is tempting because it appears to simplify the process, but DORA found that both CABs and senior-manager approvals have a negative impact on delivery performance.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Rollback capability does reduce the cost of mistakes, and self-merging removes waiting time, which makes this option appealing. It loses because it drops peer review, the control DORA identifies as the preferred approval mechanism, and it relies on recovery after problems reach production rather than on catching them at check-in.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Larger, less frequent releases carry more changes each, which makes defects harder to isolate, raises the risk of every release, and delays feedback. DORA’s guidance points the opposite way, toward working in small batches for shorter lead times and faster feedback loops.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is Option B, because it sounds like modern DevOps: trust developers and rely on rollback. The distinction is that rollback is a recovery mechanism, whereas the question asks for a replacement approval process, and peer review is the practice that serves that role. If the option had said developers merge their own changes after peer approval and passing automated checks, it would align with the recommended approach.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Change risk is best managed by making changes small, reviewing them close to where they are written, automating detection of problems, and shortening feedback loops, not by adding an approval layer far from the work. The role of risk and compliance functions shifts from gatekeeper to designer of the process and provider of information. Exam framing aside, this also applies in regulated environments, where peer review records in the development platform can serve as audit evidence.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team enforces branch protection so that every change needs at least one peer approval and passing automated tests before it can merge. After deployment, dashboards and alerts show the effect of each change within minutes. Approval history lives in the pull request record, which auditors can inspect, and lead time drops from the cycle of a weekly board meeting to hours. The main investment is keeping the test suite reliable, since flaky tests would erode trust in the gate.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Peer review gates the change and fast feedback catches what slips through, with no external gatekeeper.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When asked how to improve on a CAB or other external approval process, choose in-team peer review backed by automated tests, together with fast feedback on change impact. Reject swapping one external approver for another, merging with no review, and batching changes into larger releases.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://dora.dev/capabilities/streamlining-change-approval/\"><strong>DORA — Streamlining change approval:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism for options C and E (peer review enforced at check-in, supported by automation and fast feedback) and the elimination of Option A (CAB and senior-manager approvals hurting delivery performance)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/devops\"><strong>Google Cloud — DevOps capabilities (DORA):</strong></a> Supports: the capability overview, including streamlining change approval through peer review and working in small batches for shorter lead times and faster feedback, which eliminates Option D</p>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e2q29":{"id":"e2q29","exam":2,"n":29,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your organization is developing and releasing a microservice on Cloud Run that many internal applications depend on. You are shipping a new release and must test it thoroughly in both the staging and production environments, while keeping the impact on service consumers and on developers as low as possible. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Deploy the new release to the staging environment and split traffic so that 1% reaches the latest version. Run your tests against it. If they succeed, progressively roll the latest version out to both the staging and production environments.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Deploy the new release to the staging environment and split traffic so that 50% reaches the latest version. Run your tests against it. If they succeed, route all traffic to the latest version, then repeat the process in the production environment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Deploy the new release to the staging environment with a new-release tag and no traffic assigned. Run your tests against the tagged version. If they succeed, progressively roll out the tagged version, then repeat the process in the production environment.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Create a new environment tagged green to serve as the staging environment and deploy the new release there for testing. If the tests succeed, route all traffic to the green environment and delete the existing staging environment, then repeat the process in the production environment.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Deploy the new release to the staging environment with a new-release tag and no traffic assigned. Run your tests against the tagged version. If they succeed, progressively roll out the tagged version, then repeat the process in the production environment.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Rank the options by how much real user traffic the new version touches before it has been tested. The requirement to minimize user impact makes that the deciding variable, and the requirement to minimize developer impact penalizes anything that adds rebuilding or environment-management effort. Option C is the only one where testing happens with zero user exposure, and traffic only moves after the tests pass. Options A and B expose users first, and Option D creates heavy rework in the name of safety.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“minimize user and developer impact” — hard constraint with two sides: no untested exposure for users, and a lightweight workflow for developers.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“test the new version extensively” — technical signal: testing needs a stable, directly addressable endpoint, not a sampled share of live traffic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Cloud Run” — technical signal: revision tags and traffic splitting are native features, so no extra environment is needed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“staging and production environments” — sequence instruction: the same safe pattern must be repeated in each environment, in order.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Run lets you deploy a new revision with no traffic assigned and attach a tag to it. The tag gives that revision its own dedicated URL, so you can run extensive tests against it without any user or consuming application reaching it. After the tests pass, you shift traffic to the tagged revision in increments, watching error and latency signals, and you can send traffic back to the previous revision immediately if something looks wrong. Because this uses built-in revision features on the existing service, developers do not have to build or tear down parallel environments. Repeating the same sequence in production gives the same safety there.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> A 1% split limits blast radius, but real users are still routed to a version that has not been tested yet, which conflicts with minimizing user impact. It also states that the gradual rollout proceeds to both staging and production at once, which skips the separate validation of production. It is tempting because 1% sounds harmless, but “extensive testing” cannot be done reliably on a thin slice of live traffic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> Sending half of the traffic to an untested version is the highest-risk choice, and the jump from 50% straight to all traffic abandons a gradual rollout. It maximizes user impact, so it contradicts the main requirement.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A green environment gives strong isolation and would protect users, which makes it tempting. However, standing up a whole new environment, switching all traffic at once, and deleting the existing staging environment each cycle adds heavy and unnecessary work for developers, and deleting staging eliminates the stable baseline. It is a different pattern than what Cloud Run provides natively through tagged revisions and traffic splitting.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option A, because small-percentage traffic splitting is the textbook “gradual rollout” and the 1% figure looks safe. The subtle point is that the question asks for extensive testing with minimal user impact, which means testing must happen before any traffic is assigned. If the requirement were instead to validate behavior under real production load with limited exposure, a small initial traffic split would become the right first step.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Separating deploy from release is a core progressive-delivery idea: a new version can exist, be reachable for testing, and receive no user traffic until you decide to release it. Cloud Run implements this with immutable revisions, tags that create direct test URLs, and percentage-based traffic assignment. Testing first through a tag, then shifting traffic gradually, combines the safety of a staging check with the controlled exposure of a canary.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team deploys each release with no traffic and a tag, then runs integration and load tests against the tag’s URL while consuming applications continue using the stable revision. They then move traffic in steps while watching dashboards, and revert in seconds by sending traffic back to the previous revision if a metric degrades. Developers need only a single command per step rather than provisioning duplicate environments.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>On Cloud Run, deploy with no traffic and a tag, test on the tag’s URL, then shift traffic gradually.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a Cloud Run question asks for extensive testing with minimal user impact, choose a no-traffic revision with a tag, test via its direct URL, and then roll out gradually. Reject options that send untested code to real users, even at small percentages, or that rebuild whole environments.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/rollouts-rollbacks-traffic-migration\"><strong>Google Cloud — Rollbacks, gradual rollouts, and traffic migration (Cloud Run):</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (deploying a revision with no traffic, tagging it for a dedicated test URL, then gradually shifting traffic) and the elimination of Options A, B, and D</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide:</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment to Section 2.2, “Implementing and managing pipelines,” which covers deployment strategies such as canary, blue/green, rolling, and traffic splitting</p>","lessons":["d2l7"]},"e2q30":{"id":"e2q30","exam":2,"n":30,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your team recently moved an application onto Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) and is now preparing to ship an updated version. The release approach must let you revert to the earlier version immediately if the update proves faulty. Which deployment model fits best?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Roll out the update with a rolling deployment, then run tests on the new application after the rollout completes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use A/B testing, and run tests on the application at regular intervals after the rollout completes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use a canary deployment, and test the new application at regular intervals after the new version is live.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Use a blue/green deployment, and test the new application after the rollout completes.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Use a blue/green deployment, and test the new application after the rollout completes.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Only one requirement carries weight here: rollback must be instant. Turn that into a mechanical test. For each option, ask whether going back means reversing a process that took time (not instant) or flipping traffic to a version that is still fully running (instant). The testing phrases in each option are secondary, because the question asks which deployment model to use. Applying the test leaves a single option in which the previous version stays intact and live during the release.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“instantly roll back” — hard constraint: eliminates models where rollback means undoing a gradual replacement or redeploying the old version.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“previous version” — technical signal: the old version must still be running and reachable so traffic can be returned to it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Which deployment model” — scope clue: the question targets how a release replaces the old version, not how it is tested, which separates it from A/B testing and canary testing.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Blue/green deployment maintains two identical environments. The current version (blue) keeps serving users while the new version (green) is deployed alongside it, and the release happens by switching traffic from one to the other, usually at the load balancer or Service level. Because blue is left running until you choose to retire it, a rollback is simply a routing change back to blue, which is why Google documents blue/green as offering instant rollback along with zero-downtime cutover. On GKE this typically means two parallel Deployments with a traffic switch between them.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> A rolling deployment swaps Pods in batches, leaving a mix of old and new versions during the process. It is tempting because it is the Kubernetes default and can be reversed, but reversing means rolling back through the same staged replacement, which is neither instant nor clean.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> A/B testing sends defined user segments to a variant to compare behavior under specific conditions, so it is an experimentation technique and not a way to release a version with a full-fleet fallback. It is tempting because two versions run simultaneously, but it does not provide the instant, complete revert the question requires.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A canary deployment shifts a small share of live traffic to the new version, increasing it over time, and traffic can be moved back quickly, which makes it a close second. It loses because its rollback is described as fast rather than instant, and its design goal is progressive exposure of real users to a version still being evaluated.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The tempting wrong answer is canary (Option C), since it also supports quick reversion and is widely associated with safe releases. The deciding word is “instantly”: blue/green keeps a complete idle copy of the old version, whereas canary is built around gradual user exposure. If the question instead asked for progressively increasing the share of real traffic that reaches the new version, canary would be correct.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>How fast you can roll back depends on whether the prior version is still running and how traffic is directed to it. Blue/green keeps two full environments and swaps traffic between them, trading duplicate capacity cost for the fastest, simplest revert. Rolling and canary approaches conserve resources by replacing or shifting capacity incrementally, so reverting takes longer. Deployment models (how one version replaces another) are separate from testing strategies (how confidence is built), which is why the testing wording in each option does not change the answer. In production practice, you would usually verify the green environment before cutting traffic over, but the exam item turns on the deployment model.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team deploys the updated version as a second set of Pods beside the live one, checks it through an internal endpoint, and then repoints the Service or load balancer to it. If an unexpected error appears after cutover, they restore the original routing within moments and investigate without pressure. The cost is paying for duplicate capacity during the release, and data schema changes must stay compatible with both versions so the switch back is safe.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Instant rollback requires the old version to still be running, and only blue/green guarantees that.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>If a question stresses an instant revert of a release, choose blue/green. Choose canary when it stresses gradual exposure of real traffic, and remember that A/B testing is a segment-based experimentation technique, not a release model.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/architecture/application-deployment-and-testing-strategies\"><strong>Google Cloud — Application deployment and testing strategies:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (blue/green cutover with instant rollback by redirecting traffic) and the distinctions that eliminate the rolling, A/B, and canary options</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\">Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide: </a>Supports: domain alignment to Section 2.2, “Implementing and managing pipelines,” which lists deployment strategies such as canary, blue/green, rolling, and traffic splitting</p>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e2q31":{"id":"e2q31","exam":2,"n":31,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You support a service that has a well-defined Service Level Objective (SLO). Over the past six months, the service has consistently met its SLO and customer satisfaction has remained high. Most of its operational tasks are automated, so few repetitive tasks occur often. You want to optimize the balance between reliability and deployment velocity while following site reliability engineering (SRE) best practices. Which two actions should you take? (Choose two.)</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Tighten the service’s SLO.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Raise the service’s deployment velocity and/or accepted risk.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Reassign engineering time to other services that need greater reliability.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Persuade the product team to put reliability work ahead of new features.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>E. Revise how the service’s Service Level Indicators (SLIs) are implemented so they cover more of the service.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Raise the service’s deployment velocity and/or accepted risk.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Reassign engineering time to other services that need greater reliability.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Read the scenario as a set of diagnostic signals, and ask what each says about where effort is needed. The SLO is consistently met, so reliability is sufficient. Customers are consistently happy, so the SLO matches what users need. Toil is low, so operations are not consuming engineering time. None of the signals show a shortfall, which means the service is spending less of its error budget than it is allowed to. The sensible decisions are therefore to spend that surplus on speed and to move scarce engineering capacity to where reliability is lacking. Options that add reliability investment to an already healthy service, or that adjust measurement without a trigger, do not follow from the signals.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“consistently met its SLO” over six months — technical signal: reliability exceeds what is required, so error budget is going unused.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“customer satisfaction has been consistently high” — hard constraint on the answer: the current SLO is adequate for users, so there is no basis for raising the target.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Most of the service’s operational tasks are automated” — technical signal: toil is low, so there is no toil-reduction work to prioritize.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“optimize the balance between reliability and deployment velocity” — hard constraint: with reliability in surplus, the balance should move toward velocity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“(Choose two.)” — cardinality instruction: select two complementary actions that together redeploy the surplus.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Option B uses the surplus reliability directly. An SLO defines the reliability users need, and the error budget exists so it can be spent on change and risk; consistently leaving it unspent means releases are slower or more cautious than necessary. Releasing faster or accepting more risk converts that headroom into delivery speed while the SLO still defines the safe boundary. Option C addresses the other surplus, people. Engineers are scarce, and their time should go to the most important characteristics of the most important services, so with this service stable and toil low, capacity is better used on services that fall short of their reliability needs. The two actions are complementary and cover both resources the scenario leaves unspent, error budget and engineering time.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> A stricter SLO shrinks the error budget and increases the cost of reliability, yet customers are already happy at the current target. It is tempting because the team is beating the target easily, but it moves the balance toward reliability when the signals call for velocity, and it sets a bar users did not ask for.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Prioritizing reliability over new features is the right response to a missed SLO or exhausted error budget. Here the SLO is met and customers are satisfied, so this reverses the intended use of the budget and slows delivery with no benefit.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option E — Different Use Case:</strong> Increasing SLI coverage is valuable when indicators miss important user journeys, for example when customers are unhappy even though the SLO is met. The scenario shows the opposite, so there is no evidence the SLIs are inadequate, and the change does not address the reliability-versus-velocity balance.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The most tempting distractor is Option A, because “we are easily exceeding the target, so raise the bar” feels like continuous improvement. The distinction is that SLOs reflect user needs and cost, not engineering pride, and happy customers indicate the target is already right. If customers had been unhappy while the SLO was being met, tightening the SLO or improving SLI coverage (Option E) would become the correct direction.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An SLO sets the reliability level at which user happiness and cost are balanced, and the error budget is the tolerated unreliability that can be traded for velocity. Reliability beyond the target has diminishing value and real costs, such as slower releases and engineering time that could go elsewhere. SRE decision making combines three signals: whether the SLO is met, whether customers are happy, and how much toil exists. Different combinations point to different actions, and this combination points toward spending surplus rather than adding investment.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team whose service has comfortably exceeded its SLO for two quarters shortens release cycles, reduces canary soak times, and takes on a long-delayed migration while watching error budget burn-rate alerts. They also lend an engineer to a neighboring service that keeps missing its SLO. Feature delivery speeds up and reliability effort is focused where it is needed, with the SLO and burn-rate alerting acting as the guardrail if the added risk causes problems.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Surplus reliability is unspent budget, so spend it on velocity and send spare engineers where reliability is short.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a service consistently beats its SLO, users are happy, and toil is low, shift the balance toward velocity and move engineering capacity to services that need reliability. Tighten the SLO or prioritize reliability work only when the signals show unhappy users or a missed SLO.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — Implementing SLOs:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism for options B and C (SLOs reflect user happiness, engineering time should go to the most important services, and decisions about reliability versus functionality follow from SLO and error budget status), and the elimination of options A, D, and E</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\">Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide: </a>Supports: domain alignment to Section 3.1, “Balancing change, velocity, and reliability of the service,” which covers SLOs, error budgets, and the opportunity cost of risk and reliability</p>","lessons":["d3l2"]},"e2q32":{"id":"e2q32","exam":2,"n":32,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You run an application on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). For each request, the application calls several other services, and its responses are too slow. You need to determine which downstream service or services are responsible for the delay. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Analyze VPC Flow Logs along the path that the request takes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Examine the liveness and readiness probes of each service.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Build a Dataflow pipeline that analyzes service metrics in real time.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Use a distributed tracing framework, such as OpenTelemetry or Cloud Trace.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Use a distributed tracing framework, such as OpenTelemetry or Cloud Trace.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The decisive requirement is attribution: you must split one slow request into the time spent in each downstream service. That calls for data recorded per request, with each hop timed and linked to its caller. Check each option for whether it measures per-request, per-service duration or something else, such as network connections, container health, or fleet-wide aggregates. Only one option produces a per-request breakdown across services without extra construction.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“invokes multiple services per request” — technical signal: a single request’s latency is the sum of several service calls, so the answer must expose each call’s timing within that request.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“identify which downstream service or services” — hard constraint: the evidence must attribute delay to specific services, not just show that the application is slow overall.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“responds too slowly” — technical signal: this is a latency problem, not an availability or health problem.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Google Kubernetes Engine” — context clue: the runtime is supported by Cloud Trace, and instrumentation with OpenTelemetry works there.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Distributed tracing follows a request as it crosses services. Each operation is recorded as a span with a start, a duration, and a parent-child link to the span that triggered it, and the spans for a request form a trace. Viewing a trace shows exactly which downstream call consumed most of the time. Cloud Trace is Google Cloud’s distributed tracing system for this purpose, it runs with applications on GKE, and its Trace Explorer lets you examine aggregate latency and individual traces. Google recommends instrumenting applications with OpenTelemetry. One prerequisite is that the services must be instrumented and must propagate trace context, so that spans from different services join into the same trace.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong> VPC Flow Logs record network-level information about connections, which helps with traffic patterns and connectivity questions. It is tempting because the services communicate over the network, but flow records do not show application-level request latency or which spans belong to the same request.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Liveness and readiness probes tell Kubernetes whether a container should be restarted or receive traffic. They are tempting because they relate to service health, but a service can pass its probes and still be slow, and probes do not measure how long individual requests take.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A streaming pipeline could process service metrics and might show which service is slow in aggregate, which is why it is tempting. It would require building and maintaining custom infrastructure, and aggregated metrics do not tie time to specific requests or show where inside a request the delay occurred, which tracing provides natively.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option C, because “real-time analysis of metrics” sounds sophisticated and metrics do reveal that something is slow. The distinction is that metrics aggregate across requests, while the question needs a per-request view of where time is spent. If the goal were to detect anomalies continuously across a large fleet’s aggregated metrics, a streaming analytics pipeline could be justified.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Observability signals have complementary roles: metrics show that a problem exists and how large it is, logs explain discrete events, and traces show where time is spent along a request path. A trace is made of spans, and trace-context propagation between services is what stitches those spans together. Tracing has limits, since services must be instrumented and sampling can hide rare slow requests, but it is the standard tool for latency attribution in microservice architectures.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team instruments its GKE services with OpenTelemetry and exports spans to Cloud Trace. In Trace Explorer, the latency heatmap shows the slow requests, and a trace waterfall reveals that one inventory-lookup span takes most of the request time. The team fixes that service and uses the trace ID to jump to the related logs. The costs are instrumentation effort and a sampling choice that balances detail against volume.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Metrics tell you that it is slow, and traces tell you where.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a request fans out to several services and you must find which one adds latency, choose distributed tracing. Network flow logs, health probes, and custom metric pipelines do not give a per-request breakdown across services.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/trace/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Trace overview:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (Cloud Trace as a distributed tracing system for finding where request latency arises, support for GKE, the recommendation to instrument with OpenTelemetry, and Trace Explorer for analysis)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-liveness-readiness-startup-probes/\"><strong>Kubernetes — Configure liveness, readiness and startup probes:</strong></a> Supports: the elimination of Option B, since probes determine container restart and traffic-readiness decisions and not request latency</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/flow-logs\"><strong>Google Cloud — VPC Flow Logs overview:</strong></a> Supports: the elimination of Option A, since flow logs describe network-level connections and not application request latency</p>","lessons":["d4l9","d4l10"]},"e2q33":{"id":"e2q33","exam":2,"n":33,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You support a user-facing web application. When you analyze its error budget for the past six months, you find that it has never used more than 5% of its error budget in any given time window. You hold a Service Level Objective (SLO) review with business stakeholders and confirm that the SLO is set appropriately. You want the application’s SLO to reflect its observed reliability more closely. Which two steps can you take to further that goal while balancing velocity, reliability, and business needs? (Choose two.)</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Add more serving capacity to every zone the application runs in.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Make application releases more frequent or potentially riskier.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Tighten the SLO to match the application’s observed reliability.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Implement and measure additional Service Level Indicators (SLIs) for the application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>E. Announce planned downtime to use up more of the error budget, and make sure users are not depending on a tighter SLO.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Make application releases more frequent or potentially riskier.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>E. Announce planned downtime to use up more of the error budget, and make sure users are not depending on a tighter SLO.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Identify what is fixed and what can move. The SLO is fixed, because stakeholders just confirmed it matches business needs. What can move is the observed reliability, which is far higher than the SLO requires. The goal of aligning the two therefore has to be met from the observed side, by deliberately using some of the unspent error budget, and not by changing the target. Evaluate each option by asking whether it spends surplus budget safely or whether it adds reliability, changes the agreed target, or only changes measurement. Only two options spend the surplus in ways the business can accept.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“never consumed more than 5% of its error budget” — technical signal: reliability substantially exceeds the SLO, so most of the budget goes unused.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“confirm that the SLO is set appropriately” — hard constraint: the SLO target should stay as it is, so the gap must be closed by moving observed reliability.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“SLO to more closely reflect its observed reliability” — goal statement: reduce the over-delivery rather than reinforce it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“balancing velocity, reliability, and business needs” — preference: favor actions that convert surplus reliability into delivery speed without breaking business commitments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“(Choose two.)” — cardinality instruction: pick two complementary ways to spend the budget.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Option B spends the unused budget on change. Releases are a major source of outages, so more frequent or somewhat riskier releases use budget in exchange for velocity, and the SLO remains the boundary that limits how far to go. Option E addresses a less obvious risk of over-delivering: users come to depend on the reliability they actually observe, not the reliability promised, so an SLO that is greatly exceeded effectively becomes a tighter implicit SLO. Google describes this with its Chubby lock service, where SRE makes a service meet, but not significantly exceed, its SLO, and introduces planned outages so dependents do not assume it never fails. The two work together: B uses the surplus to speed delivery, and E uses part of it to reset user expectations and expose hidden dependencies. Both lower observed reliability toward the SLO while staying within the agreed budget, which makes the pair complete for the stated goal.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Extra capacity in every zone raises reliability and cost further, which widens the gap between the SLO and observed reliability, the opposite of the goal.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Matching the SLO to observed performance is tempting because it makes the two numbers agree immediately. But the SLO has just been validated against business needs, and SLOs should reflect what users and the business require, not what the system happens to deliver. Tightening shrinks the error budget, leaving less room for change and slowing velocity, and it commits the team to a level nobody asked for.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> More SLIs improve measurement coverage, which is valuable if existing indicators miss important user journeys. Nothing in the scenario suggests the SLIs are inadequate, and extra measurement does not close the gap between a validated SLO and the unused budget.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The most tempting distractor is Option C, because “make the SLO match observed reliability” reads like the literal request. The distinction is that the SLO review confirmed the target, so the question is asking how to bring behavior toward the target. If stakeholders had instead determined that users really needed higher reliability, tightening the SLO would become appropriate, and if the SLIs were found to miss key user journeys, Option D would become relevant.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An SLO sets the reliability users and the business actually need, and the error budget is the amount of unreliability that can be traded for other benefits. Persistently exceeding the SLO is not free: it slows delivery, raises cost, and teaches users to rely on performance that was never promised, which creates a de facto tighter SLO. SRE therefore treats unused budget as something to spend deliberately. In production, any planned downtime must stay within the budget, be announced in advance, and be coordinated with dependent teams.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team that has never used more than a small share of its error budget increases release frequency and shortens canary soak times. It also schedules announced, time-boxed maintenance events sized to use part of the budget, and tells dependent teams so they add retries and fallbacks. Delivery speeds up and downstream services become more resilient, with burn-rate alerting watching that the budget is not overspent.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Meet the SLO without greatly exceeding it, because unspent budget is wasted velocity and a hidden promise.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a service far outperforms an SLO that stakeholders have confirmed, close the gap by spending budget safely, through faster or riskier releases and announced planned downtime. Do not tighten the SLO, add capacity, or simply measure more.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/\"><strong>Google SRE Book — Service Level Objectives:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism for options B and E (users rely on actual performance, and the Chubby example of meeting but not significantly exceeding an SLO with planned outages), and the elimination of Option C</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/\">Google SRE Workbook — Implementing SLOs: </a>Supports: the use of error budgets to balance reliability against change and velocity, which eliminates Option A and distinguishes Option D</p>","lessons":["d3l2"]},"e2q34":{"id":"e2q34","exam":2,"n":34,"domain":3,"html":"<p>Your development team has built a new version of its service’s API. You need to roll out the new version with the least possible disruption to third-party developers and to the end users of third-party installed applications. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Introduce the new API version. Announce the deprecation of the old version. Deprecate the old version. Contact the remaining users of the old version. Provide best-effort support to users of the old version. Turn down the old version.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Announce the deprecation of the old version. Introduce the new API version. Contact the remaining users of the old version. Deprecate the old version. Turn down the old version. Provide best-effort support to users of the old version.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Announce the deprecation of the old version. Contact the remaining users of the old version. Introduce the new API version. Deprecate the old version. Provide best-effort support to users of the old version. Turn down the old version.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Introduce the new API version. Contact the remaining users of the old version. Announce the deprecation of the old version. Deprecate the old version. Turn down the old version. Provide best-effort support to users of the old version.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Introduce the new API version. Announce the deprecation of the old version. Deprecate the old version. Contact the remaining users of the old version. Provide best-effort support to users of the old version. Turn down the old version.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Because every option contains the same six steps, the question is purely about ordering, so derive the ordering rules from the constraint. Third-party developers and their installed applications cannot be upgraded on your schedule, so every step that pressures users must come after something that makes the pressure reasonable, and the only step that breaks clients must come last. That yields four dependencies: the replacement exists before anyone is asked to migrate, notice comes before the deprecation takes effect, outreach to remaining users follows the migration period, and support for stragglers is offered while the old version still runs, before it is turned down. Check each option for violations, and only one has none.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“least disruption to third-party developers and end users” — hard constraint: clients you do not control must keep working throughout, so the breaking step, turn down, must come last.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“third-party installed applications” — technical signal: installed apps update slowly and unpredictably, so a long overlap and direct outreach are needed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“deploy the new versions of the API” — sequence instruction: the new version must be available first so there is something to migrate to.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“remaining users” and “best-effort support” — sequence signals: these steps address stragglers after migration has begun and before the old version is removed.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Option A follows the dependency order. Introducing the new version first gives users a destination. Announcing the deprecation then gives them notice and a timeline, and deprecating the old version formally marks it as superseded. Contacting the remaining users comes next, because only after the migration period can you identify who has not moved. Providing best-effort support to those users helps them finish migrating while the old version still works, and turning it down last removes the old version only after migration has been supported, which keeps disruption to a minimum. This matches Google’s published API guidance that an older version must go through a reasonable, well-communicated deprecation period before it is shut down.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> It announces deprecation before the new version exists, so users are told to leave with nowhere to go. It also provides support after turning the old version down, when the users who needed help have already lost service.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> It announces deprecation and contacts users before introducing the replacement, so the outreach has nothing to offer them. Starting the process this way creates exactly the disruption the question is trying to avoid.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> It starts correctly by introducing the new version, which makes it the most tempting distractor. It then contacts the remaining users before any deprecation announcement, so there is no migration period from which anyone could be “remaining,” and it offers support only after the old version has been turned down.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is Option D, because it shares the correct first step and looks nearly identical to A. The errors are in the middle and end: outreach before announcement, and support after shutdown. Support after turn-down is illogical under any reading, so no single changed requirement would make D correct. As a rule of thumb, scan each sequence for the turn-down step, which must be last.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>When you run an API consumed by clients you do not control, the producer owns the migration timeline but not the clients’ release schedules. Safe lifecycle management therefore follows a pattern: ship the replacement, give notice, keep the old version working during an overlap, identify and help stragglers, and remove the old version last because removal is the only step that breaks clients. The exam’s six-step labels are framing for this pattern; Google’s API guidance (AIP-185) states the general requirement as a reasonable, well-communicated deprecation period before shutdown.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team publishes v2 beside v1 behind the same API gateway and announces a deprecation timeline with dates. It marks v1 as deprecated in documentation and responses, then uses request logs and API keys to find the callers who are still on v1 and contacts them directly. After helping those callers migrate, it turns v1 down once traffic is negligible. Partner applications keep working throughout, and the cost is running two versions in parallel during the overlap.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Replacement first and shutdown last, with notice before deprecation and outreach before turn-down.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For API version retirement questions, order the steps by dependency: new version, announcement, deprecation, outreach to remaining users, support, then turn-down. Reject any sequence that asks users to migrate before the replacement exists or that supports users after the old version is gone.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://google.aip.dev/185\"><strong>Google API Improvement Proposals — AIP-185: API Versioning:</strong></a> Supports: Google’s guidance that an older API version must go through a reasonable, well-communicated deprecation period before being shut down, which underpins the ordering in option A</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\">Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide: </a>Supports: domain alignment to Section 3.2, “Managing service lifecycle,” which covers service management from planning and deployment through maintenance and retirement</p>","lessons":["d3l8"]},"e2q35":{"id":"e2q35","exam":2,"n":35,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You are drafting the action items in a postmortem for an outage and assigning people to them. The outage has ended, but its root causes still need to be fixed, and you want the team to complete the action items promptly and efficiently. What is the best way to assign owners and collaborators to the action items?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Name one owner for each action item, and add collaborators where needed.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Name several owners for each item so the team is guaranteed to complete items quickly.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Name collaborators only, with no individual owner, so the postmortem stays blameless.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Name the team lead as the owner of every action item, because they lead the SRE team.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Name one owner for each action item, and add collaborators where needed.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Begin with the one hard requirement, which is that corrective work gets finished quickly and efficiently. Then picture each assignment model once the incident pressure has faded and the item competes with feature work. Speed depends on whether someone clearly knows the item is theirs to push, and on whether helpers know whom they are supporting. So separate the two roles in the question: an accountable owner and supporting collaborators. Screen each option for whether it keeps accountability single, present, and close to the work. Only one option meets all three conditions.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“address the root causes” — technical signal: the action items are corrective work that must be completed, and recording them alone does not reduce risk.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“quickly and efficiently” — hard constraint: the assignment model must remove ambiguity about who drives the item and avoid handoff delays.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“owners and collaborators” — technical signal: these are separate roles, so the answer must define both and keep them distinct.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“The outage is over” — context clue: with no incident urgency, follow-through depends on clear ownership while other priorities compete.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>One named owner is accountable for moving the item from idea to completion, which means a single person tracks status, chases dependencies, and escalates blockers. Collaborators contribute expertise or access, such as another team’s system, without sharing the accountability. This keeps decisions fast, because one person can proceed without waiting for agreement among several people. It also matches how SRE guidance treats postmortem action items: as prioritized, tracked work that needs a clear accountable person to reach closure. The pair of roles is complete for the question, since it covers who drives the work and who supports it.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> Several owners sounds like extra assurance, which is why it is tempting. But shared ownership spreads responsibility, so each person can reasonably assume another is handling the item, and coordination overhead rises. Nothing about it guarantees speed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Blameless postmortems avoid assigning fault for what happened. They do not remove accountability for completing the fix. With no individual owner, nobody is answerable for progress, so items tend to stall, which defeats the goal of addressing root causes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> The team lead is a single accountable person, so this option does not fail on ambiguity. It loses because routing every item through one person creates a bottleneck, and ownership follows seniority instead of who is best placed to do the work, which slows items that need hands-on expertise.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option C, because it applies the blameless principle to the wrong thing. Blamelessness concerns how the incident’s causes are analyzed and described, whereas ownership of a corrective action is forward-looking accountability. If the question had asked how to write the incident narrative without attributing fault to individuals, then not naming people would be the right approach.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Accountability and blame are different ideas: blame looks backward at fault, while ownership looks forward at who will deliver the change. Tasks shared among many people are prone to diffusion of responsibility, and the usual remedy is one accountable person supported by contributors, a pattern similar to the accountable and consulted roles in responsibility-assignment models. Good action items are also prioritized, tracked in the team’s normal work system, and reviewed on a regular cadence so they do not fade after the postmortem is published.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>After a memory-exhaustion outage, an action item to add a saturation alert is assigned to one engineer on the owning service team, with the observability team listed as a collaborator. The item has a priority and due date in the issue tracker and is checked in the weekly reliability review. The owner reports status and raises any blocker with the collaborating team, so the fix competes fairly with feature work and gets closed.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>One owner drives each item, and collaborators help without sharing the accountability.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For postmortem action items, choose a single accountable owner with collaborators as needed. Eliminate multiple owners, ownerless items justified by blamelessness, and blanket assignment to the team lead.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/postmortem-culture/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — Postmortem Culture: Learning from Failure:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism, since it explains what makes postmortem action items actionable for preventing recurrence, including clear priorities, and why options that blur accountability fall short</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.usenix.org/publications/login/spring2017/lunney\"><strong>USENIX ;login: — Postmortem Action Items: Plan the Work and Work the Plan (Google SRE):</strong></a> Supports: Google’s approach to categorizing, prioritizing, and tracking action items so that they are actually executed</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide:</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment to Section 3, “Applying site reliability engineering practices,” whose incident-related objectives (3.3) cover post-incident follow-through</p>","lessons":["d3l5"]},"e2q36":{"id":"e2q36","exam":2,"n":36,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your organization runs a containerized web application on-premises. As part of its migration to Google Cloud, you must choose a deployment strategy and a platform that satisfy all of the following acceptance criteria:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>The platform can route traffic from Android devices to an Android-specific microservice.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The platform supports arbitrary percentage-based traffic splitting.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The deployment strategy supports continuous testing of multiple versions of any microservice.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Release a canary of the application on Cloud Run, and use traffic splitting to send 10% of user traffic to the canary according to the revision tag.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Release a canary of the application on App Engine, and use traffic splitting to send a subset of user traffic to the new version according to the IP address.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Release a canary of the application on Compute Engine, and use Cloud Service Mesh with Compute Engine to send 10% of user traffic to the canary by configuring the virtual service.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Release a canary on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) with Cloud Service Mesh, and use traffic splitting to send 10% of user traffic to the new version according to the user-agent header configured in the virtual service.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Release a canary on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) with Cloud Service Mesh, and use traffic splitting to send 10% of user traffic to the new version according to the user-agent header configured in the virtual service.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Treat the three acceptance criteria as independent filters and require every option to pass all of them. Criterion 1 is the most discriminating, because it needs routing based on a property of the request, such as the kind of client, which is more than a percentage split. Criterion 2 then requires weighted routing with arbitrary percentages, and criterion 3 requires version-level control for each microservice, not just for the application as a whole. Applying the filters in that order leaves one option that identifies Android clients, applies weights, and works per microservice.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“direct traffic from Android devices to an Android-specific microservice” — hard constraint: the platform must make routing decisions from request attributes such as the user-agent header.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“arbitrary percentage-based traffic splitting” — hard constraint: weighted routing with freely chosen percentages is required, not fixed shards or per-user stickiness.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“continuous testing of multiple versions of any microservice” — technical signal: routing control must be available per microservice and per version, which points to a service mesh.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“containerized web application” — context clue: a container platform such as GKE is a natural fit and keeps migration effort low.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Service Mesh on GKE uses Istio-style traffic management. A VirtualService can match on request headers, such as the user-agent that identifies Android clients, and route those requests to a specific microservice, which satisfies criterion 1. The same resource assigns weights to destinations, so a canary can receive 10% or any other percentage and be adjusted gradually, which satisfies criterion 2. Because the rules are defined per service and per version subset, any number of microservice versions can run side by side and be tested continuously, which satisfies criterion 3. The routing happens in the mesh’s proxies, so the application code does not need to change.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Cloud Run assigns traffic by percentage across revisions and uses tags to give a revision its own test URL, which is tempting because it handles canaries well. However, its own traffic splitting does not inspect request headers such as user-agent, so it cannot steer Android clients to a dedicated microservice.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> App Engine splits traffic by IP address or cookie, and an IP address does not identify an Android device. Google also documents that IP-based splits are imprecise and that users on mobile networks can shift IP addresses during a session, so it fails criterion 1 and weakens criterion 2.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A service mesh with a virtual service is the right family of tool, which is why this is the strongest distractor. It loses because the option configures only a percentage split and gives no rule for identifying Android traffic, and it places a containerized microservice application on VMs, which adds management overhead compared with GKE.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is Option C, because it names the right tool, Cloud Service Mesh with a virtual service, and satisfies the percentage requirement. The subtle point is that criterion 1 asks for attribute-based routing, which only Option D states. If criterion 1 were removed and the question only required a gradual percentage rollout, Cloud Run’s revision-based traffic splitting would become the simpler and defensible answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Platform-native traffic splitting, such as App Engine versions or Cloud Run revisions, operates at the granularity of a service and divides traffic by percentage, cookie, or IP address. A service mesh moves routing into a proxy layer that can inspect request attributes, such as headers and paths, and apply weights per service and version. When requirements combine content-aware routing with fine-grained, per-microservice version control, the mesh is the appropriate layer.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A retailer moves its containerized storefront to GKE with Cloud Service Mesh. A virtual service routes requests whose user-agent indicates Android to the Android microservice, while a second rule sends 90% of the remaining traffic to the stable version and 10% to the canary. Operators adjust weights over time and run several versions in parallel. The trade-off is added mesh operations and proxy resource overhead, and user-agent values can be spoofed, so they suit routing but not security decisions.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Route by what the request says and by how much with a mesh virtual service, headers for who and weights for how many.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When requirements demand routing by a request attribute, such as device type, together with arbitrary weights per microservice version, choose a service mesh on GKE. Platform-native splitting alone gives percentages, cookies, or IP addresses, not header-aware routing.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/service-mesh/docs/tutorials/canary-deployment\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Service Mesh by example: canary deployments:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism, showing canary rollouts with weighted VirtualService routing on GKE using Cloud Service Mesh and Istio APIs</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://istio.io/latest/docs/reference/config/networking/virtual-service/\"><strong>Istio — VirtualService reference:</strong></a> Supports: the ability of a VirtualService to match requests on headers such as user-agent and to assign weights to destinations, which satisfies criteria 1 and 2 in option D and distinguishes option C</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/flexible/splitting-traffic\"><strong>Google Cloud — App Engine splitting traffic:</strong></a> Supports: the elimination of Option B, since App Engine splits by IP address or cookie and documents the limits of IP-based splitting for mobile users</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/rollouts-rollbacks-traffic-migration\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Run rollbacks, gradual rollouts, and traffic migration:</strong></a> Supports: the elimination of Option A, since Cloud Run splits traffic by percentage across revisions and uses tags for direct test URLs rather than request-header routing</p>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e2q37":{"id":"e2q37","exam":2,"n":37,"domain":3,"html":"<p>Your company applies Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) principles. You are writing the postmortem for an incident that was triggered by a software change and severely affected users. You want to prevent a severe incident like this from happening again. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Identify the engineers responsible for the incident and escalate the matter to senior management.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Make sure that test cases capable of catching errors of this kind pass before new software releases.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Follow up with the employees who reviewed the changes and prescribe the practices they should follow going forward.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Design a policy requiring on-call teams to immediately call engineers and management to agree on a plan of action whenever an incident occurs.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Make sure that test cases capable of catching errors of this kind pass before new software releases.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Start from the SRE principle that postmortems look for systemic causes, not culprits, and that their action items should reduce the chance of recurrence. Then ask where the failure entered: a software change reached users, so a control was missing between code and release. Test each option on two questions: does it target the system or specific people, and does it prevent the failure or only react to it? Options that point at individuals fail the first test, an option about response coordination fails the second, and only one option adds a systemic preventive control.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“follows Site Reliability Engineering principles” — technical signal: postmortems are blameless and focus on contributing causes in the system, not on individuals.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“triggered by a software change” — technical signal: the defect passed through the release process, so the fix belongs before release.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“prevent severe incident from happening in the future” — hard constraint: the action must lower the likelihood of recurrence, not just improve how the next incident is handled.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“writing a postmortem” — context clue: the deliverable is concrete action items that address root causes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>The incident shows a gap: a class of error was able to reach users because no release-time check caught it. Adding test cases that detect this kind of error, and requiring them to pass before new releases, converts the lesson into an automated, repeatable control. It works no matter who writes or reviews a change, which is what a systemic fix should do. In Google’s classification of postmortem action items, it is a prevention-type item, the type that aims to stop recurrence or reduce its impact, whereas items that only mitigate or improve response leave the underlying defect path open.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Naming responsible engineers and escalating them is the opposite of blameless practice. It is tempting because it appears to create accountability, but it discourages honest reporting and changes nothing in the system, so the same class of error can recur with a different author.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Following up with reviewers and prescribing practices sounds like process improvement, which makes it tempting. It still singles out individuals, and it relies on people remembering to apply guidance each time, which is a weaker and less reliable control than an automated test gate.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> A call-out policy improves coordination during an incident, which is valid incident management practice. It does not stop a faulty change from reaching users, and it would apply to every incident regardless of severity, so it fails the prevention requirement.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option C, because improving the review process feels like a systemic fix. The distinction is that it targets the people who reviewed the change and depends on their future diligence, while the SRE approach is to fix the system with controls that do not depend on any one person. If the question had asked how to shorten response time to future incidents, Option D would move up, since it addresses coordination rather than prevention.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A blameless postmortem identifies contributing causes without indicting individuals or teams, because people who fear punishment hide problems and the organization stops learning. The output is a set of concrete, owned, tracked action items, which Google categorizes as investigate, mitigate, repair, detect, and prevent. Prefer automated prevention, such as tests and pipeline gates, over reminders, training, or additional approvals. Google’s SRE Workbook also warns against postmortems whose action items are mostly mitigative and fail to address recurrence.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>After an outage caused by an unvalidated configuration change, a team writes a regression test that reproduces the failure pattern and makes it a required check in its release pipeline. The postmortem records the action item with an owner and a due date and tracks it to closure. Releases that would trigger the same class of failure are now blocked automatically, at the cost of maintaining the test and a slightly longer pipeline.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Fix the system, not the person, by turning the lesson into an automated release gate.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>In an SRE postmortem question about preventing recurrence, pick the systemic, automated control that closes the gap the change slipped through. Reject answers that blame or retrain individuals, and answers that only improve incident response.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/\"><strong>Google SRE Book — Postmortem Culture: Learning from Failure:</strong></a> Supports: the blameless-postmortem principle and the focus on contributing causes over individuals, which eliminates Options A and C</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/postmortem-culture/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — Postmortem Culture: Learning from Failure:</strong></a> Supports: the need for action items that prevent recurrence and not merely mitigate, which supports option B over option D</p>","lessons":["d3l5"]},"e2q38":{"id":"e2q38","exam":2,"n":38,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You are responsible for the reliability of a high-volume enterprise application. Many users report that an important part of its functionality, a data-intensive reporting feature, consistently fails with an HTTP 500 error. When you review the application’s dashboards, you see a strong correlation between the failures and a metric that tracks the size of an internal queue used to generate reports. You trace the failures to a reporting backend with high I/O wait times, and you resolve the problem quickly by resizing the backend’s persistent disk (PD). Now you need to create an availability Service Level Indicator (SLI) for the report generation feature. How should you define it?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. As the I/O wait times aggregated across all report generation backends.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. As the proportion of report generation requests that result in a successful response.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. As the application’s report generation queue size compared with a known-good threshold.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. As the reporting backend’s PD throughput capacity compared with a known-good threshold.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. As the proportion of report generation requests that result in a successful response.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Separate the diagnostic story from the actual ask. The troubleshooting details, queue size, I/O wait, and disk capacity, describe what caused one incident, whereas the requirement is an availability indicator for the feature. An availability SLI should answer one question: did users get a successful result when they asked for a report? Test each option on whether it counts successful outcomes against all valid attempts, or whether it measures an internal condition that merely correlated with failure. Only one option is a direct measure of the user-visible outcome, and the rest are cause-side signals.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“availability Service Level Indicator” — hard constraint: the indicator must express whether requests to the feature succeed, not how a component is behaving.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“report generation feature” — scope clue: the SLI should be limited to requests for this feature, not the whole application.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“consistently failing with an HTTP 500 error” — technical signal: the user-visible failure is a failed response, so success versus failure of responses is the natural basis for good and bad events.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“queue size,” “I/O wait times,” and “persistent disk” — exclusion signals: these are causes found during troubleshooting, and an SLI built on them would track one failure mode instead of availability.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>An SLI is most useful as a ratio of good events to valid events, and for availability the natural form is the proportion of requests that were served successfully. Defining it as the share of report generation requests that return a successful response measures exactly what users experience, and it moves down whatever the cause of failure is, whether disk I/O, a full queue, a software defect, or something not yet seen. That makes it a stable basis for an SLO and an error budget. One boundary is to define “valid” and “good” explicitly, for example by excluding health checks and by treating 5xx responses as bad events.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong> I/O wait time is a resource saturation signal that is valuable for diagnosing backend performance, and it is tempting because it was the cause here. But it does not say whether requests succeeded: high I/O wait may not break requests, and requests can fail with low I/O wait.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Queue size correlated strongly with failures, which makes it attractive as a signal and potentially useful as a leading indicator for alerting. It is still an internal proxy, and a threshold on it describes system state instead of user-visible success, so it can miss failures that have other causes and can fire when users are unaffected.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> Disk throughput capacity compared with a threshold is a capacity-planning and configuration measure, and resizing the disk was the fix for this incident. It says nothing directly about whether report requests succeed, and the “known-good” threshold is subjective.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option C, because the dashboard correlation makes the queue metric feel authoritative. The distinction is that SLIs measure the outcome users experience, while queue size is one of several possible causes of a bad outcome. If the question had asked for an early-warning alert that fires before users are affected, a queue-size threshold would be a reasonable leading indicator for that purpose.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An SLI is a carefully defined measure of an aspect of the service level that users receive, and the preferred form is good events divided by valid events. SLIs and SLOs should reflect user experience, so they track symptoms, while cause-based metrics such as saturation, queue depth, and I/O wait belong on diagnostic dashboards and in supporting alerts. Common SLI types for request-driven features are availability, latency, and quality, and each requires a precise definition of which events count as valid and which count as good.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team defines the report feature’s availability SLI as the number of report requests answered with a non-5xx status divided by all report requests, measured at the load balancer or in application metrics. It sets an SLO over a rolling window and alerts on error budget burn rate. I/O wait and queue depth stay on a diagnostic dashboard with warning alerts. The SLI drops no matter what breaks the feature, though it does not explain why, so the diagnostic signals remain necessary for troubleshooting.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>An SLI measures what users get, as good over valid events, not what a backend is doing.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When asked to define an availability SLI, choose the ratio of successful requests to valid requests for the feature in question. Do not pick resource, queue, or capacity metrics, which are causes that help diagnose problems but do not measure availability.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/\"><strong>Google SRE Book — Service Level Objectives:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism, including the definition of an SLI as a carefully defined measure of the service level provided and the guidance to build indicators around what users care about, which eliminates options A, C, and D</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — Implementing SLOs:</strong></a> Supports: the request-based SLI form, the proportion of good requests among all valid requests, and how SLIs feed SLOs and error budgets</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide:</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment to Section 3.1, “Balancing change, velocity, and reliability of the service,” which covers defining SLIs such as availability and latency, SLOs, and SLAs</p>","lessons":["d3l1"]},"e2q39":{"id":"e2q39","exam":2,"n":39,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You are ready to release a new feature of a web-based application to production. You want to use Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) to perform a phased rollout that first reaches half of the web server pods. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use a partitioned rolling update.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Use node taints with the NoExecute effect.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use a ReplicaSet in the Deployment specification.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Use a StatefulSet with the Parallel pod management policy.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Use a partitioned rolling update.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Convert the requirement into a capability: the update must stop partway, after exactly half the pods have the new version, and then be continued deliberately. So look for a native Kubernetes setting that defines a boundary inside a rolling update. Then test each option against what it actually controls. Taints control which pods may run on which nodes, ReplicaSets control how many pods exist, and pod management policy controls how pods are created or deleted when scaling. Only one option controls which subset of pods receives an update.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“phased rollout” — technical signal: the update must proceed in stages with a deliberate stopping point, not all at once.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“half of the web server pods” — hard constraint: the mechanism must let you choose the exact subset that receives the new version.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)” — context clue: the solution should use native Kubernetes workload settings available on GKE, without extra tooling.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A StatefulSet’s RollingUpdate strategy accepts a <code>partition</code> value. Pods with an ordinal greater than or equal to the partition are updated to the new template, while lower-ordinal pods stay on the previous revision. With N replicas, setting the partition to N/2 updates the upper half and leaves the lower half untouched, and lowering the partition afterwards, finally to 0, continues the rollout to the rest. GKE’s rolling update documentation describes partitioning as useful for staging an update, rolling out a canary, or performing a phased rollout, which is exactly the stated need. One boundary is that partitioning is a feature of StatefulSets, not of Deployments.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> A NoExecute taint evicts running pods that do not tolerate it from a node. It is tempting because removing pods from some nodes sounds like targeting a subset, but it is node-level scheduling and eviction control and does not deploy a new version to a chosen share of pods.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A ReplicaSet keeps a specified number of pod replicas running, and a Deployment uses ReplicaSets to perform rolling updates. Neither offers a setting that halts an update at half the pods. You could approximate a split by running two ReplicaSets with different replica counts, but that is a manual workaround and not a built-in phased rollout control.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> The Parallel pod management policy lets a StatefulSet launch or terminate pods without waiting on each other, and Kubernetes documents it as affecting scaling operations. It is tempting because it appears in the same resource type, but it provides no stopping point for an update and does not select a subset of pods.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option D, because it also involves a StatefulSet setting and “parallel” can sound like acting on subsets of pods. The distinction is that pod management policy governs how pods are created and deleted when scaling, whereas partition governs which pods receive an update. If the requirement were to scale a StatefulSet up or down quickly without ordered waiting, Parallel pod management would be the correct choice.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A rolling update replaces pods incrementally, and a phased rollout adds a controllable pause so that part of the fleet runs the new version while you observe it. Kubernetes expresses that pause through update-strategy parameters such as the StatefulSet partition. Exam framing differs from common production practice here: web servers are usually stateless and run as Deployments, where phased rollouts are typically done with two Deployments behind one Service, traffic splitting in a service mesh, or a delivery tool such as Cloud Deploy, because Deployments have no partition setting.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team running a StatefulSet with eight replicas sets the partition to four and applies the new template, so only the upper four pods update. They watch error rate and latency, ideally against an SLO burn-rate alert, then lower the partition to zero to finish the rollout, or revert the template if signals degrade. The blast radius stays limited to half the pods during the observation window, though the traffic share reaching the new version depends on how requests are distributed among pods.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>The partition sets the line: pods at or above that ordinal get the update, and the rest stay put.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a Kubernetes question asks for an update to reach only part of a workload’s pods before continuing, choose a partitioned rolling update. Taints control scheduling and eviction, ReplicaSets control replica counts, and pod management policy controls scaling behavior.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/statefulset/\">Kubernetes — StatefulSets (rolling updates, partitions, and pod management policy): </a>Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (the RollingUpdate partition that updates only pods at or above a given ordinal) and the elimination of Option D (pod management policy documented as affecting scaling operations)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/taint-and-toleration/\"><strong>Kubernetes — Taints and Tolerations:</strong></a> Supports: the elimination of Option B, since NoExecute taints evict non-tolerating pods from nodes and are scheduling controls, not rollout controls</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/replicaset/\"><strong>Kubernetes — ReplicaSet:</strong></a> Supports: the elimination of Option C, since a ReplicaSet maintains a number of pod replicas and Deployments build rolling updates on top of it without a built-in halfway stop</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\">Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide: </a>Supports: domain alignment to Section 2.2, “Implementing and managing pipelines,” which lists deployment strategies such as canary, blue/green, rolling, and traffic splitting</p>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e2q40":{"id":"e2q40","exam":2,"n":40,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You are writing the postmortem for an incident that had a severe impact on users, and you want to prevent similar incidents in the future. Which two of the following sections should you include in the postmortem? (Choose two.)</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. An explanation of the incident’s root cause.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. A list of the employees responsible for causing the incident.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. A list of action items to prevent the incident from recurring.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Your personal assessment of how the incident’s severity compares with past incidents.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>E. Copies of the design documents for every service the incident affected.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. An explanation of the incident’s root cause.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. A list of action items to prevent the incident from recurring.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The stated purpose is prevention of similar incidents, so the test for each section is whether it helps the organization understand why the failure happened or change something so it does not happen again. Sections that identify people, offer subjective rankings, or bulk up the document with reference material do not serve that purpose. Because the question asks for two answers, look for a complementary pair: one section that diagnoses and one that prescribes. That pair covers the learning loop, from understanding the cause to acting on it.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“prevent similar incidents in the future” — hard constraint: every included section must contribute to understanding causes or driving corrective change.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“postmortem” — technical signal: SRE postmortems are blameless learning documents, so sections that single out individuals are excluded.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“severely affected users” — context clue: a serious incident warrants a thorough causal analysis and concrete follow-up work.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“(Choose two.)” — cardinality instruction: pick two sections that together form a diagnosis-and-remediation pair.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>An explanation of the root cause, or more precisely the contributing causes, records why the incident happened and gives readers the understanding needed to find similar weaknesses elsewhere. A list of action items turns that understanding into work: concrete, prioritized changes with owners that reduce the chance or impact of recurrence. The two sections work together and neither is sufficient alone, since cause analysis without actions leaves the gap open, and actions without a causal explanation risk fixing the wrong thing. Together they are the minimum that delivers the question’s goal.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> A list of people responsible turns the postmortem into a blame exercise. It is tempting because accountability sounds useful, but SRE postmortems are blameless, since blaming individuals discourages honest reporting and leaves the underlying system gaps unaddressed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A severity comparison can add context, and teams often record impact and severity in a postmortem. A personal opinion on how it ranks against past incidents is subjective, so it does not explain the cause or lead to a fix, and it is not among the sections that serve the stated prevention goal.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option E — Different Use Case:</strong> Design documents are reference material for understanding a system, and a postmortem may link to specific relevant ones. Attaching copies for every impacted service adds bulk without analysis, and it does not explain this incident or prescribe changes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option B, because naming those involved feels like part of an honest account. The distinction is that postmortems analyze the system conditions that allowed the failure, and people appear in the timeline only as actors whose decisions the system made possible. If the question asked for the incident timeline, referring to roles or decisions made at each step would be appropriate, but a list of culprits would still not be.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A postmortem is a written record of an incident: its impact, the actions taken to resolve it, the contributing causes, and the follow-up actions to prevent recurrence. Blameless practice focuses on those causes without indicting individuals or teams, because people who fear punishment hide problems and organizations stop learning. Good action items are concrete, prioritized, owned, and tracked to completion, and they cover prevention as well as detection and mitigation.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>After an outage caused by a configuration change that overloaded a backend, the team writes a postmortem with a causal analysis that explains why the change passed review and why alerts fired late. The action items add a pre-release load check and a saturation alert, each with an owner and due date. Other teams read it and apply the same checks to their services, so the incident produces systemic improvement instead of a list of names.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>A postmortem explains why it happened and what will change, never who to blame.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For postmortem content questions, include the root-cause analysis and the action items that prevent recurrence. Exclude lists of people, subjective opinions, and bulk attachments that do not drive learning or change.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/\"><strong>Google SRE Book — Postmortem Culture: Learning from Failure:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism, since it describes postmortems as documentation of the incident, its root causes, and the follow-up actions to prevent recurrence, and it establishes the blameless principle that eliminates option B</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/postmortem-culture/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — Postmortem Culture: Learning from Failure:</strong></a> Supports: the need for actionable items that prevent recurrence, which supports option C and distinguishes the other options</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide:</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment to Section 3, “Applying site reliability engineering practices,” whose incident-related objectives (3.3) cover post-incident learning</p>","lessons":["d3l5"]},"e2q41":{"id":"e2q41","exam":2,"n":41,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You are running an application on Compute Engine and collecting its logs through Cloud Logging. You discover that some personally identifiable information (PII) is leaking into certain log entry fields. You want to stop these fields from being written to new log entries as quickly as possible. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use the filter-record-transformer Fluentd filter plugin to strip the fields from log entries in flight.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Use the fluent-plugin-record-reformer Fluentd output plugin to strip the fields from log entries in flight.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Wait for the application developers to patch the application, then confirm that new log entries no longer expose PII.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Stage log entries in Cloud Storage, then trigger a Cloud Run function that removes the fields and writes the entries to Cloud Logging through the Cloud Logging API.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Use the filter-record-transformer Fluentd filter plugin to strip the fields from log entries in flight.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two hard constraints decide this: the fields must never reach Cloud Logging in new entries, and the fix must take effect as fast as possible. The first constraint locates the control point. It has to sit in the path between the application’s log file and the write to Cloud Logging, which on Compute Engine is the logging agent running on the VM. The second constraint favors a configuration change on that agent over anything that needs code changes or a new pipeline. Check each option against both constraints, and only the one that edits records inside the agent’s pipeline satisfies both.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“PII is leaking into certain log entry fields” — technical signal: specific fields must be removed from records, which calls for record-level transformation and not dropping entire logs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“prevent these fields from being written in new log entries” — hard constraint: removal has to happen before the entry is written to Cloud Logging, so it must occur at the agent.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“as quickly as possible” — hard constraint: a configuration change on the existing agent beats waiting for a code release or building new infrastructure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“running an application on Compute Engine” — context clue: the Fluentd-based logging agent on the VM is the available interception point.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>The Cloud Logging agent, google-fluentd, is a modified version of the Fluentd log collector, so Fluentd plugins and configuration apply to it. Fluentd’s <code>filter_record_transformer</code> is built into Fluentd core and needs no installation, and its <code>remove_keys</code> parameter deletes the listed keys from each record as it passes through the pipeline. Adding a filter stanza that matches the affected log’s tag and restarting the agent stops the PII fields from being included in new entries immediately, without touching the application. Entries already written to Cloud Logging are not changed by this, so the application fix and any cleanup of existing data are separate follow-ups.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> The plugin name is almost identical to the correct one, which is why it is tempting. However, <code>fluent-plugin-record-reformer</code> is a separate gem that must be installed, and the option frames it as an output-stage plugin, while Fluentd already provides a built-in filter for deleting record keys in flight. It adds avoidable setup and is not the standard mechanism for this task.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Patching the application is the proper root-cause fix and should happen eventually, which makes it tempting. But waiting leaves PII flowing into new log entries until the release ships, which fails “as quickly as possible.”</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Staging entries in Cloud Storage and rewriting them with a function would eventually remove the fields before they reach Cloud Logging, but it places the raw PII in a storage bucket, adds latency and moving parts, and takes far longer to build than an agent configuration change.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is the pair of nearly identical plugin names in Options A and B. The distinguishing facts are that A names a built-in Fluentd filter whose <code>remove_keys</code> parameter exists for exactly this purpose, while B names an add-on plugin framed at the output stage. The other temptation is Option C, which is right for permanent remediation. If the question had asked for the long-term root-cause fix, patching the application would become the answer, and in practice you would do both.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Sensitive data is best controlled as early as possible, at the point where records are produced or collected, because anything written to a central log store is harder to remove and may be widely readable. Agent-side processing lets you filter or transform records before ingestion. The exam item is framed around the legacy Logging agent, which is Fluentd-based. Google recommends the Ops Agent as the current agent for Compute Engine, and it is configured through YAML receivers, processors, and pipelines. Google has also announced that standard maintenance for the legacy agents ended on September 28, 2026, with a 12-month migration grace period, so a real-world fix should plan for that move.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>An operator adds a <code>record_transformer</code> filter with <code>remove_keys</code> for the fields that carry email addresses and account numbers, rolls it out to the affected VMs through configuration management, and restarts the agent. New entries stop containing the fields within minutes while developers prepare the application fix. A configuration typo could interrupt log ingestion, so the change is tested on one VM first, and the team must separately decide what to do about the PII already stored.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Scrub at the source: remove the fields in the agent before the log entry is written.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When PII must stop appearing in new log entries immediately, change the agent’s configuration to drop the fields in flight, using the Fluentd record_transformer filter’s remove_keys on the legacy agent. Waiting for a code patch or building a separate cleanup pipeline is slower and leaves exposure in the meantime.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.fluentd.org/filter/record_transformer\">Fluentd — record_transformer filter plugin: </a>Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (filter_record_transformer is included in Fluentd core, and its remove_keys parameter deletes listed keys from records in flight) and the contrast with Option B’s separately installed plugin</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/agent/configuration\">Google Cloud — Logging agent configuration: </a>Supports: that the Logging agent google-fluentd is a modified Fluentd collector configured through Fluentd plugins and configuration files, which makes the Fluentd filter the applicable control point</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/agent\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Monitoring agent overview (legacy agent phase-out):</strong></a> Supports: the lifecycle note that the legacy Monitoring and Logging agents are being phased out in favor of the Ops Agent, with standard maintenance ending September 28, 2026</p>","lessons":["d4l3"]},"e2q42":{"id":"e2q42","exam":2,"n":42,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You support a popular mobile game application deployed on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) across several Google Cloud regions, and each region has multiple Kubernetes clusters. You receive a report that none of the users in one specific region can connect to the application. You want to resolve the incident in line with Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) practices. What should you do first?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Redirect user traffic from the affected region to the other regions that report no issues.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Use Cloud Monitoring to look for a spike in CPU or memory usage in the affected region.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Add a node pool of high-memory, high-CPU machine type instances to the cluster.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Use Cloud Logging to filter on the affected region’s clusters and inspect the error messages in the logs.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Redirect user traffic from the affected region to the other regions that report no issues.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The word “first” makes this a sequencing question, and the SRE rule for incidents is to limit user impact before investigating the cause. Sort the options into two groups: those that restore service for users now, and those that gather information or attempt a fix. Then ask whether a restoring option is available. Multiple healthy regions exist, so redirecting users is possible and fast. Every investigative or speculative action therefore comes later, because users remain unable to connect while you perform it.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“none of the users in a specific region can connect” — technical signal: total impact confined to one region, which makes the region a clear unit to isolate or drain.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“across several Google Cloud regions” with multiple clusters each — technical signal: redundancy exists, so healthy regions can serve the affected users.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“following Site Reliability Engineering practices” — hard constraint: mitigation takes priority over root-cause analysis during an incident.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“What should you do first?” — sequence instruction: the first action must reduce user impact, and diagnosis belongs after it.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Redirecting traffic away from the failing region restores service to users within the time it takes to change routing, without needing to know why the region failed. This follows Google’s SRE guidance that the first response in a major outage should be to make the system work as well as it can under the circumstances, for example by diverting traffic from a broken cluster to others, and not to chase the root cause immediately. It also preserves the failing region intact for investigation afterward. One prerequisite is that the remaining regions must have enough capacity headroom to absorb the redirected load, since overloading them would spread the outage.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Checking CPU and memory in the affected region is a sound diagnostic step, which is why it is tempting. But it only examines one hypothesis, and users remain unable to connect for as long as the investigation takes, so it belongs after mitigation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Adding larger instances assumes the problem is a capacity shortage, which has no supporting evidence. It takes time, adds cost, and may not address a connectivity failure at all, so it is a guess that leaves users down.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Inspecting logs is the reflexive first move in troubleshooting, and it will eventually be needed to find the cause. As a first action it delays relief for users, and it can be done more calmly once traffic is flowing to healthy regions.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option D, because looking at logs feels like the natural start of any investigation. The distinction is that SRE separates mitigation from diagnosis, and Google’s guidance explicitly tells responders to resist the instinct to start with root-cause hunting. If the question had asked what to do next, after traffic was moved, then inspecting logs and metrics in the affected region would be the right answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An incident has distinct phases: detect, mitigate, diagnose, fix, and learn. Mitigation reduces user impact using fast, reversible actions such as draining or redirecting traffic, rolling back a change, or adding capacity, and it does not require understanding the cause. Diagnosis and the permanent fix follow once users are protected. Multi-region redundancy exists so that one region’s failure can be absorbed by others, but that only works if capacity and routing controls were prepared in advance.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>An on-call engineer sets the affected region’s weight to zero in the global load balancer or traffic management layer and watches saturation in the healthy regions. Players reconnect within minutes through other regions, at somewhat higher latency. The team then examines logs and metrics in the isolated region, fixes the fault, and shifts traffic back gradually. The main risk is overloading the receiving regions, so their headroom is checked before and during the shift.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Stop the bleeding first, then find the cause.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>In “what should you do first” incident questions, choose the fast mitigation that restores user service, such as redirecting traffic or rolling back. Diagnostic steps like checking logs and metrics, and speculative fixes like adding capacity, come after impact is reduced.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/effective-troubleshooting/\"><strong>Google SRE Book — Effective Troubleshooting:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism, since it advises that the first response to a major outage is to make the system work as well as possible, for example by diverting traffic from a broken cluster, instead of immediately seeking the root cause, which eliminates options B, C, and D as first steps</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\">Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide: </a>Supports: domain alignment to Section 3.3, “Mitigating incident impact on users,” which lists draining or redirecting traffic, adding capacity, and rollback strategies</p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e2q43":{"id":"e2q43","exam":2,"n":43,"domain":4,"html":"<p>A government agency requires application logs to be archived for seven years. Cloud Logging needs to be configured so that the logs are exported and stored while keeping storage costs as low as possible. What should be done?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create a Cloud Storage bucket, and build the application so it sends its logs directly to that bucket.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Build an App Engine application that pulls the logs out of Cloud Logging and writes them into BigQuery.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create an export in Cloud Logging, and configure Pub/Sub to retain the logs in permanent storage for seven years.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Create a named sink in Cloud Logging, create a Cloud Storage bucket to hold the archived logs, and set that bucket as the sink’s export destination.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Create a named sink in Cloud Logging, create a Cloud Storage bucket to hold the archived logs, and set that bucket as the sink’s export destination.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two constraints matter here: a seven-year retention requirement, and minimizing storage cost. Those two together point squarely at long-term, infrequently accessed “cold” storage rather than an analytics-ready store or a messaging layer. Cloud Logging already has a native, built-in mechanism for this exact job — a sink that routes matching log entries to an external destination — so any option that bypasses that mechanism in favor of custom application code is adding unnecessary engineering effort without buying anything. Among the destinations a sink can target, Cloud Storage is the one purpose-built and priced for durable long-term archival; BigQuery is priced and optimized for querying, and Pub/Sub isn’t storage at all. The decision resolves to: use the native sink mechanism, and point it at the one destination actually designed for cheap, durable, multi-year retention.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“archive application logs for seven years” — a long-duration, infrequent-access retention requirement, which favors cold/archival storage over an analytics store.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“minimizing costs of storage” — a hard constraint that rules out any option carrying unnecessary compute, query, or engineering overhead on top of the raw storage cost.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“configure Stackdriver [Cloud Logging] to export and store the logs” — signals the intended mechanism is the native log export (sink) feature, not custom application-level logging code.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A Cloud Logging sink is the documented, built-in way to continuously route a filtered stream of log entries to an external destination without writing or maintaining any extra application code. Of the destination types a sink supports, Cloud Storage is explicitly the one intended for long-term log storage, and a Cloud Storage bucket can be set to a low-cost storage class suited to data that is rarely accessed but must be retained for years — directly satisfying both the seven-year retention requirement and the cost-minimization requirement in a single, fully managed configuration.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> writing logs directly from the application to a Cloud Storage bucket bypasses Cloud Logging’s native export mechanism entirely. It requires custom application code to implement and maintain, forfeits the filtering, reliability, and centralized routing Cloud Logging sinks already provide, and doesn’t actually “configure” Cloud Logging to export anything — it reinvents a feature that already exists.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> this also discards the native sink mechanism in favor of a custom App Engine application that has to be built and operated just to pull logs out. On top of that engineering overhead, BigQuery is priced and designed for analysis and querying, not for minimizing the cost of seven years of rarely-touched archival data — it is a more expensive and more complex choice than necessary for this requirement.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Incorrect:</strong> Pub/Sub is a messaging and streaming service, not a durable storage service, and its message retention tops out at a matter of weeks, nowhere near the seven years the agency requires. An export can legitimately target Pub/Sub, but Pub/Sub cannot hold onto those messages for anywhere close to the required duration, making this option technically infeasible as written.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “a valid sink destination” as interchangeable with “a place to store data long-term.” Pub/Sub is a perfectly legitimate export destination for streaming logs to other applications in near real time, which can make it look plausible here — but it was never built to retain data, only to deliver it, and its retention limits make it unusable for a multi-year archival mandate. The decisive move is checking whether the destination is actually a storage service at all, not just whether it can receive exported logs.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Logging’s export (sink) mechanism can route a filtered set of log entries to a Cloud Logging bucket, a Cloud Storage bucket, a BigQuery dataset, or a Pub/Sub topic. Each destination is optimized for a different purpose: Cloud Storage for durable, low-cost long-term retention; BigQuery for analysis and querying; Pub/Sub for streaming logs onward to other systems or applications. Matching the destination type to the actual need — in this case, cheap, durable, multi-year storage — is the core design decision behind configuring log exports well.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Routing a Cloud Logging sink to a Cloud Storage bucket, and configuring that bucket with a lower-cost storage class suited to infrequently accessed archival data, lets an organization meet a multi-year compliance retention mandate at a fraction of what the same volume of logs would cost sitting in an actively queried BigQuery table — while avoiding the ongoing operational burden of running and maintaining a custom log-export application.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For “store it cheaply for years,” use a Cloud Logging sink into a Cloud Storage bucket — Pub/Sub moves logs, it doesn’t keep them, and BigQuery is priced for querying, not cold archival.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a Cloud Logging question asks for low-cost, long-term log retention, favor the native sink-to-Cloud-Storage pattern over custom application code, BigQuery, or Pub/Sub; remember that Pub/Sub specifically cannot provide durable, multi-year storage at all.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/export/configure_export_v2\"><strong>Google Cloud — Configure and manage log exports (sinks):</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (a sink routing logs to a Cloud Storage bucket) and the elimination of Options A and B</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/subscription-message-retention\"><strong>Google Cloud — Configure message retention for a Pub/Sub subscription:</strong></a> Supports: the maximum retention limit that eliminates Option C as technically infeasible for seven-year storage</p>","lessons":["d4l1"]},"e2q44":{"id":"e2q44","exam":2,"n":44,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You support a service that recently suffered an outage. A new release that exhausted the service’s memory resources caused it, and you successfully rolled the release back to limit the impact on users. You are now responsible for the post-mortem and want to follow Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) practices while developing it. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Concentrate on building new features instead of preventing the outage from recurring.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Concentrate on identifying the contributing causes of the incident instead of the individual responsible for it.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Schedule one-on-one meetings with every engineer involved, and establish who approved and pushed the new release to production.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Use the Git history to locate the related code commit, and bar the engineer who made it from working on production services.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Concentrate on identifying the contributing causes of the incident instead of the individual responsible for it.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The impact has already been mitigated by the rollback, so the remaining question is what the post-mortem is for: learning and preventing recurrence. Apply the SRE test to each option by asking what it directs attention toward, systems and processes or individuals. The memory exhaustion reached production because some control failed to catch it, so the useful question is which controls were missing, not which person was involved. Only one option aims the investigation at causes in the system, and the others either drop the prevention goal or hunt for a person.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“follow Site Reliability Engineering practices when developing the post-mortem” — technical signal: SRE post-mortems are blameless and focus on learning from failure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“new release that exhausted the service memory resources” — technical signal: the defect passed through the release process, so the analysis should examine why existing controls did not catch it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“rolled back the release successfully to mitigate” — context clue: the incident is over, and the remaining work is understanding and preventing recurrence.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A blameless post-mortem identifies the contributing causes of an incident without indicting any individual or team for bad or inappropriate behavior. In this case that means asking questions such as why a memory-exhausting change passed testing, why the rollout did not surface the problem before full exposure, and why monitoring did not flag memory pressure sooner. The answers produce systemic action items, such as memory regression tests, resource-limit checks, or longer canary phases, that prevent a similar release from reaching users regardless of who wrote or approved it. Google’s experience is that a truly blameless post-mortem culture produces more reliable systems, partly because people who are not afraid of punishment report problems honestly.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> A post-mortem exists to document what happened and drive actions that reduce the chance or impact of recurrence. Prioritizing new features over that work leaves the underlying gap open, so the same class of outage can return.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Talking with the engineers involved is a normal way to gather facts, which makes this tempting. But the stated purpose is to establish who approved and pushed the release, which turns the exercise into a search for responsibility and discourages candid accounts.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Barring the engineer who made the commit is punitive and treats one person as the cause. It teaches others to hide mistakes, and it fixes nothing in the system, so the same change could be made by someone else.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option C, because collecting information from the people involved looks like diligence. The distinction is the objective: reconstructing what happened and why is blameless, while determining who approved and pushed the release is attribution of fault. If the same meetings aimed to rebuild the timeline and the decision context without assigning fault, they would be consistent with SRE practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>People respond to incentives, so if reporting a mistake leads to punishment, problems are hidden and the organization stops learning. Blameless post-mortems separate accountability for improving the system from blame for individuals, and they treat human error as a symptom of gaps in tooling, testing, review, or monitoring. A good post-mortem records impact, timeline, contributing causes, and concrete action items that have owners and are tracked to completion.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>After a release exhausts memory and is rolled back, the team writes a post-mortem that lists contributing causes: a leak in a new code path, no soak test, a canary period too short to show growth, and no alert on memory trend. Action items add a memory regression test and a resource-saturation alert and lengthen the canary stage, each with an owner and a due date. The document is shared widely so other teams can learn from it, and engineers continue to report issues honestly.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Ask what failed in the system, not who failed.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For SRE post-mortem questions, pick the blameless answer that analyzes contributing causes and drives systemic fixes. Reject answers that single out, question, or sideline individuals, and answers that skip prevention work.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/\">Google SRE Book — Postmortem Culture: Learning from Failure: </a>Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (blameless post-mortems that focus on contributing causes without indicting individuals or teams) and the elimination of Options C and D</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/postmortem-culture/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — Postmortem Culture: Learning from Failure:</strong></a> Supports: the link between a blameless post-mortem culture and more reliable systems, and the need for action items that prevent recurrence, which eliminates Option A</p>","lessons":["d3l5"]},"e2q45":{"id":"e2q45","exam":2,"n":45,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You use Cloud Build to build and deploy your application. You want to incorporate database credentials and other application secrets into the build pipeline securely, while keeping development effort to a minimum. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create a Cloud Storage bucket that relies on built-in encryption at rest, store the secrets in it, and grant Cloud Build access to the bucket.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Encrypt the secrets and keep them in the application repository, keep the decryption key in a separate repository, and grant Cloud Build access to that repository.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Encrypt the secrets on the client side and store them in a Cloud Storage bucket, keep the decryption key in the same bucket, and grant Cloud Build access to the bucket.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Use Cloud Key Management Service (Cloud KMS) to encrypt the secrets and reference them in your Cloud Build deployment configuration, and grant Cloud Build access to the key ring.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Use Cloud Key Management Service (Cloud KMS) to encrypt the secrets and reference them in your Cloud Build deployment configuration, and grant Cloud Build access to the key ring.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>There are two hard constraints: the secrets must be protected in a way that holds up under access to the places they are stored, and the solution must need little custom development. Evaluate protection by asking what someone who can read the storage location can see, and evaluate effort by asking whether Cloud Build can consume the secret natively or whether you must write your own retrieval and decryption logic. Options that store keys next to ciphertext, or that rely on default storage encryption, fail the protection test. Options that require hand-built cryptography fail the effort test. One option uses a managed key service that Cloud Build already integrates with.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“securely incorporate database credentials and other application secrets” — hard constraint: the secrets must never sit in readable form where broad access exists, and decryption must be controlled by access to a key.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“minimize the development effort” — hard constraint: the approach should use native Cloud Build integration, not custom encryption and decryption scripts.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Cloud Build” — technical signal: Cloud Build can decrypt Cloud KMS-encrypted values at build time and inject them into build steps.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“build pipeline” — context clue: the need is for build-time secret injection into specific steps.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud KMS lets you encrypt each secret with a key you control. You place the encrypted value in the Cloud Build configuration under <code>availableSecrets</code>, together with the name of the key that decrypts it, and a build step receives the decrypted value as an environment variable through <code>secretEnv</code>. Cloud Build decrypts it while the build runs, so the plaintext is not stored in the repository or in a bucket. The build’s service account needs the Cloud KMS CryptoKey Decrypter role on the key, which means access to the secret is governed by IAM on the key and not by who can read a file. The integration is native, so the development effort is a configuration change and not custom code. One boundary is that the ciphertext is only as safe as the key’s access controls, so key permissions must be kept tight.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Cloud Storage encrypts data at rest by default, which makes this sound secure, but that protects the disks and not against anyone authorized to read the bucket, who would see the secrets as plaintext objects. The build would also need custom steps to fetch and handle them.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> Keeping ciphertext in one repository and the key in another is hand-rolled key management. Repositories are not designed as key stores, many developers typically have read access, and the build needs custom decryption code, which adds effort without the controls of a managed key service.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Storing the decryption key in the same bucket as the encrypted secrets defeats the encryption, because anyone with access to the bucket gets both. Custom client-side cryptography also takes significant effort to build and maintain.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option A, because “built-in encryption at rest” sounds like the secure and low-effort choice. The distinction is that default storage encryption is automatic for all data and does not restrict what authorized readers can see, so it does not make a bucket a secret store. If the bucket held non-sensitive build artifacts, default encryption would be sufficient.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Encryption protects data only if access to the key is controlled separately from access to the ciphertext. Managed key services, such as Cloud KMS, provide that separation through IAM, and CI/CD systems with native integrations can decrypt at the point of use, a pattern called build-time secret injection. Secrets should never be stored in plaintext in source repositories or general-purpose storage. In practice, Google Cloud also offers Secret Manager as a dedicated store for secrets, and the exam guide lists both Cloud KMS and Secret Manager under pipeline configuration and secrets, so a new design should evaluate both, but Secret Manager is not among the options here.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team encrypts the database password with a Cloud KMS key and places the base64 ciphertext in the build configuration under <code>availableSecrets</code>. It grants the build service account the CryptoKey Decrypter role on that key, and the deployment step reads the value from an environment variable. Rotating the password means re-encrypting it and updating the configuration. Secrets never appear in plaintext in the repository, and the cost is managing key permissions and keeping the encrypted values current.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Encrypt with Cloud KMS, reference it in the build config, and let only the build account decrypt.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For secrets in a Cloud Build pipeline with minimal effort, use a managed key service that Cloud Build integrates with natively, so that access is controlled by IAM on the key. Reject plaintext in buckets or repositories, keys stored beside their ciphertext, and custom cryptography.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/securing-builds/use-encrypted-credentials\"><strong>Google Cloud — Use encrypted credentials from Cloud KMS (Cloud Build):</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (encrypting values with Cloud KMS, referencing them through availableSecrets with the key name, injecting them via secretEnv, and granting the build service account the CryptoKey Decrypter role) and the elimination of options A, B, and C</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide:</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment to Section 2.3, “Managing pipeline configuration and secrets,” which lists key management with Cloud KMS, secret management, and build versus runtime secret injection</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l8"]},"e2q46":{"id":"e2q46","exam":2,"n":46,"domain":4,"html":"<p>A trading application is written in Python and runs on App Engine flexible environment. The team wants to customize the error details that are sent to Error Reporting. What should be done?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Install the Error Reporting library for Python, and run the application on a Compute Engine VM instead.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Install the Error Reporting library for Python, and run the application on Google Kubernetes Engine instead.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Install the Error Reporting library for Python, and continue running the application on App Engine flexible environment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Use the Error Reporting API to write application errors as ReportedErrorEvent objects, generating properly formatted log entries in Cloud Logging.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Use the Error Reporting API to write application errors as ReportedErrorEvent objects, generating properly formatted log entries in Cloud Logging.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard constraint here is the word “customize.” Nothing in the scenario asks for a platform change, so any option that relocates the application off App Engine flexible is solving a problem that was never posed — that eliminates A and B immediately. Between the two remaining options, the question becomes: does installing the client library and letting it run give you control over <em>what</em> gets reported, or does it just give you default, automatic capture? Error Reporting’s default integrations (including the one built into App Engine flexible) and the client library’s convenience methods populate a fixed, standard set of fields. Full control over the content of an error event — the actual “customization” the scenario asks for — comes from constructing the ReportedErrorEvent payload yourself and submitting it, either directly through the Error Reporting API or as a correspondingly formatted Cloud Logging entry.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“hosted on App Engine flexible environment” — establishes the current platform; no requirement in the stem calls for changing it, so platform-migration options are suspect by default.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“customize the error information being sent” — the decisive phrase; it specifically asks for control over the content/shape of the error data, not just enabling error capture.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Single-select “What should you do?” — only one action is expected, so the correct option must fully satisfy the customization requirement on its own.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Reporting errors as ReportedErrorEvent objects — either by calling the Error Reporting API’s <code>report</code> method directly, or by writing a Cloud Logging entry whose payload is formatted to match the ReportedErrorEvent schema — gives the application full control over every field Error Reporting receives: the error message, service name and version, HTTP request context, affected user, and report location. This is the documented extensibility point for anything beyond default exception capture, and the API automatically turns these calls into properly structured log entries that Error Reporting parses and groups. That combination — direct construction of the event plus automatic, correctly formatted logging — is what “customizing the error information being sent” actually means in Error Reporting.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong> migrating the application to a Compute Engine VM is an unnecessary and disruptive platform change that the scenario never asked for, and it still doesn’t address customization — it only relocates where the same default library behavior would run.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> migrating to Google Kubernetes Engine has the identical problem as A — an unrequested platform migration that leaves the actual “customize the error data” requirement unresolved.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> installing the library and running on App Engine flexible is largely redundant, since Error Reporting is already automatically enabled there with no additional setup required. More importantly, the library’s convenience calls (such as reporting an exception or a simple message) populate only the standard default fields; they don’t give the application the fine-grained control over the event’s content that “customize” calls for.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is equating “install the client library” with “customize the error data.” The library is the easy path to default, automatic error capture — it is not, by itself, a customization mechanism. The moment a scenario asks specifically for custom error content, the correct move shifts from the convenience wrapper to directly building the underlying ReportedErrorEvent object, whether through the API or a matching Cloud Logging entry.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Error Reporting is built on top of Cloud Logging: it continuously analyzes log entries for a recognizable error structure (a stack trace, or a payload shaped like a ReportedErrorEvent) and groups matching entries into error events. There are two ways to produce that structure — let a platform integration or client library populate it automatically with standard fields, or construct it yourself for full control. The second path is the one the platform explicitly documents for custom error events.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A trading application might need each error enriched with operational context a default exception capture won’t include — which trading desk or order ID was affected, which upstream dependency call failed, or a custom severity classification tied to trade risk. Building the ReportedErrorEvent directly lets the team attach exactly that context to every error, which materially speeds up triage during a live incident on a time-sensitive system.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Installing the library gives you default error capture; constructing the ReportedErrorEvent yourself gives you customized error capture — when the ask is “customize,” go to the object, not just the install.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When an Error Reporting (or similar observability) question’s decisive word is “customize,” look past the “install the client library and run it somewhere” options and favor the one that directly constructs the underlying event or log payload — that’s where control over content actually lives.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/error-reporting/docs/formatting-error-messages\"><strong>Google Cloud — Format a log entry to report error events:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (ReportedErrorEvent via the Error Reporting API or a formatted Cloud Logging entry)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/error-reporting/docs/setup/app-engine-flexible-environment\"><strong>Google Cloud — Setting up Error Reporting on App Engine flexible environment:</strong></a> Supports: elimination of Options A, B, and C by confirming Error Reporting is automatically enabled on App Engine flexible with no additional setup</p>","lessons":["d4l10"]},"e2q47":{"id":"e2q47","exam":2,"n":47,"domain":1,"html":"<p>A company builds several applications that run on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), with each application owned by a separate team. The organization needs development and production environments for every team while keeping overall spend as low as possible, and no team should be able to reach another team’s environment. Which approach should be taken?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Provision a separate Google Cloud project for each team, and within every project create one GKE cluster for Development and a second for Production; grant each team IAM permissions scoped to its own clusters.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Provision a separate Google Cloud project for each team, and within every project create a single GKE cluster containing one Kubernetes namespace for Development and another for Production; grant each team IAM permissions scoped to its own cluster.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Provision one shared Development GKE cluster and one shared Production GKE cluster, each in its own project, and create a Kubernetes namespace per team inside every cluster; restrict each team to its own namespace by configuring Identity-Aware Proxy.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Provision one shared Development GKE cluster and one shared Production GKE cluster, each in its own project, and create a Kubernetes namespace per team inside every cluster; restrict each team to its own namespace by configuring Kubernetes role-based access control (RBAC).</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Provision one shared Development GKE cluster and one shared Production GKE cluster, each in its own project, and create a Kubernetes namespace per team inside every cluster; restrict each team to its own namespace by configuring Kubernetes role-based access control (RBAC).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two hard constraints govern this scenario: minimize cost, and enforce hard isolation between teams. Everything else (how many clusters, which access-control tool) is a means to those ends, not a requirement in itself. Start from cost: GKE bills a per-cluster management fee regardless of cluster size, so the number of clusters — not the number of projects — drives the cluster portion of the bill. Any design that creates a cluster (or cluster pair) per team scales cost linearly with team count; a design that shares two clusters (one Development, one Production) across all teams does not. That eliminates A and B as cost-optimal. From there, the remaining decision is which mechanism correctly restricts a team to only its own namespace inside a shared cluster — and only Kubernetes RBAC operates at that object level; IAP does not.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“while minimizing costs” — a hard constraint that rules out any option whose cluster count scales with the number of teams.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Different teams should not be able to access other teams’ environments” — a hard constraint requiring a mechanism that actually enforces namespace-level boundaries, not just convenience.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“configure Identity-Aware Proxy” vs. “configure Kubernetes Role-based access control (RBAC)” — the single technical-signal phrase that separates the two lowest-cost options (C and D) from each other.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Sharing one Development cluster and one Production cluster across every team caps the cluster-management-fee exposure at two clusters total, independent of how many teams exist. Namespaces then give each team its own logical partition for objects (Pods, Services, ConfigMaps, and so on) inside those shared clusters, at no additional infrastructure cost. Kubernetes RBAC is the mechanism built to authorize actions against those in-cluster objects: a Role scoped to a namespace, bound to a team’s users or group via a RoleBinding, lets GKE grant or deny access to exactly that namespace and nothing else. That combination — shared clusters, namespace partitioning, RBAC enforcement — satisfies both constraints simultaneously.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> a dedicated cluster pair per team does achieve isolation through project and IAM boundaries, but it multiplies the per-cluster management fee by the number of teams, directly violating the cost-minimization requirement.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> collapsing each team down to one cluster (instead of two) is an improvement over A, but it still creates one cluster per team rather than two shared clusters total, so cluster costs still scale with team count. It also relies on project-level IAM to gate the whole cluster rather than using RBAC to gate the namespace, which is coarser than necessary now that namespaces are already in play.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Incorrect:</strong> this option gets the cost structure right (two shared clusters, namespace per team) but names the wrong enforcement tool. Identity-Aware Proxy authorizes HTTPS requests to applications sitting behind Cloud Run, App Engine, or Cloud Load Balancing — it has no concept of a Kubernetes namespace and cannot authorize <code>kubectl</code> or Kubernetes API Server requests. It cannot deliver the stated isolation requirement at all.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is conflating two Google Cloud access-control tools that sound similar but operate at different layers: IAP governs identity-based access to HTTP(S) applications and infrastructure endpoints (VMs, load-balanced web apps), while Kubernetes RBAC governs authorization for objects inside the Kubernetes API itself. A scenario that explicitly says “namespace” is signaling the Kubernetes API layer — that should immediately point toward RBAC (or cluster-level IAM) and away from IAP. The distractor would only become defensible if the requirement shifted from “restrict access to a namespace” to “restrict access to a web application exposed from the cluster,” which is a materially different ask.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>GKE access control operates on two cooperating layers. Google Cloud IAM governs access at the Google Cloud resource level — primarily the project containing the cluster — and is what determines whether a principal can reach the cluster at all. Kubernetes RBAC governs access to objects inside the cluster’s API, scoped either to a single namespace (via a Role/RoleBinding) or cluster-wide (via a ClusterRole/ClusterRoleBinding). GKE checks RBAC policy first and falls back to IAM permissions, and in nearly all multi-tenant designs, RBAC is the right tool once you’ve decided to share a cluster across tenants. IAP sits in an entirely separate control plane for securing HTTP(S)-reachable applications, not the Kubernetes control plane.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Choosing shared clusters with namespace-scoped RBAC lowers the cluster-management bill and centralizes platform operations (upgrades, node pool management, security patching) into two cluster lifecycles instead of dozens — a real operational win for a platform team. The trade-off is that RBAC alone does not stop a noisy or misconfigured team from consuming a disproportionate share of shared node capacity; production designs typically pair namespace RBAC with ResourceQuotas and NetworkPolicies to keep teams from affecting each other’s reliability, not just their authorization boundary.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>IAP guards the front door to web applications; Kubernetes RBAC guards the namespace door inside the cluster — when the ask is “restrict access to a namespace,” reach for RBAC.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a GKE scenario asks for low-cost multi-team isolation, favor the design with the fewest clusters (shared Dev/Prod clusters, namespace per team) over one cluster or cluster pair per team, and verify the proposed access-control tool actually operates at the Kubernetes API/namespace level rather than at the HTTP application layer.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/role-based-access-control\"><strong>Google Cloud — Control access to GKE clusters using Kubernetes RBAC:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (namespace-scoped Role/RoleBinding authorization) and the elimination of Option C</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iap/docs/concepts-overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Identity-Aware Proxy overview:</strong></a> Supports: why IAP cannot enforce namespace-level access, eliminating Option C</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/pricing\"><strong>Google Cloud — Google Kubernetes Engine pricing:</strong></a> Supports: the per-cluster management fee basis for the cost-minimization requirement, eliminating Options A and B</p>","lessons":["d1l3","d1l9"]},"e2q48":{"id":"e2q48","exam":2,"n":48,"domain":5,"html":"<p>You manage several production systems that run on Compute Engine in the same Google Cloud project. Each system uses its own dedicated set of Compute Engine instances. You want to know how much it costs to run each system. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. In the Google Cloud console, use the cost breakdown report to visualize costs for each system.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Apply a label specific to its system to every instance, enable Cloud Billing export to BigQuery, and query costs by label.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Add metadata specific to its system to every instance, export Cloud Logging to BigQuery, and query costs based on the metadata.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Name each virtual machine (VM) after its system, export usage reports to a Cloud Storage bucket, and configure the bucket as a BigQuery source to query costs by VM name.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Apply a label specific to its system to every instance, enable Cloud Billing export to BigQuery, and query costs by label.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Cost reporting needs two things: billing data that contains the costs, and an attribute on each cost line that identifies the system. Work from that. First ask where cost data lives, which rules out options that rely on logs or other non-billing data. Then ask whether the billing data can distinguish the systems, given that all of them share one project and so project-level grouping cannot separate them. The answer must therefore add a per-system attribute that Cloud Billing carries through to its cost records, and then provide a way to aggregate by it.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“in the same Google Cloud project” — technical signal: project is not a usable grouping dimension, so a resource-level attribute is required.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“its own set of dedicated Compute Engine instances” — technical signal: each system maps cleanly onto a set of resources that can carry a system-specific label.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“how much it costs to run each of the systems” — hard constraint: the data source must be actual billed cost data, not usage or operational telemetry.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A label is a key-value pair that you attach to a resource, and Google Cloud forwards label information to the billing system so charges can be broken down by label. Labeling every instance with a value that identifies its system, such as <code>system=&lt;name&gt;</code>, adds the missing dimension. Enabling Cloud Billing export to BigQuery then writes billing line items, including their labels, into a dataset, and a query that sums cost grouped by the label gives the cost of each system. Google’s labels documentation describes this exact use, finding the cost of a particular service by exporting usage and labels to BigQuery. One boundary is that only consistently labeled resources can be attributed, so unlabeled spend will appear separately and should be monitored.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> The built-in billing reports are valuable, which makes this tempting. As stated, though, there is nothing in the data that identifies a system, because everything shares a project and no label has been applied, so the report cannot split costs per system.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Incorrect:</strong> Instance metadata describes configuration and is not part of billing data, and Cloud Logging exports contain log entries, not charges. Querying that data cannot produce costs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Naming VMs after systems and exporting usage reports builds an unusual pipeline around a naming convention. VM names are not how Cloud Billing attributes cost, and this path would require you to derive costs yourself, when Cloud Billing already exports cost data with labels to BigQuery.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option A, because the console’s billing reports are the quickest place to look at costs. The distinction is that a report can only group by dimensions present in the data, so the systems must be labeled first. If the instances were already labeled by system, the built-in billing reports, which can filter and group costs by resource label, would be a fast alternative to a BigQuery query.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Cost attribution works by attaching a dimension to each cost line item. Project, service, and SKU come with the billing data, while labels add custom dimensions such as system, team, or environment. Billing export to BigQuery makes those line items queryable at full detail, which supports custom grouping, dashboards, and chargeback or showback. Good practice is to define a consistent labeling scheme, apply it at provisioning time, and track unlabeled spend.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team sets a <code>system</code> label on every instance through its infrastructure-as-code templates and enables Cloud Billing export to BigQuery. A scheduled query sums monthly cost by label and feeds a dashboard, so each system’s owner can see their spend. Shared costs, such as networking, need a separate allocation rule, and a periodic check for unlabeled resources keeps the data trustworthy.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Label the resource, export the bill, and group the cost by label.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>To find costs per system within one project, apply system-specific labels to the resources, export Cloud Billing data to BigQuery, and query by label. Logs, metadata, and VM names are not billing dimensions, and built-in reports can only group by attributes that already exist.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/labels-overview\">Google Cloud — Labels overview (Resource Manager): </a>Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (labels are forwarded to billing, can be exported to BigQuery with usage, and are used to find the cost of a particular service), and the note that built-in billing reports can group by labels</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/export-data-bigquery\"><strong>Google Cloud — Export Cloud Billing data to BigQuery:</strong></a> Supports: how billing export writes cost data, including labels, to BigQuery so costs can be queried by label, which distinguishes options C and D</p>","lessons":["d5l5"]},"e2q49":{"id":"e2q49","exam":2,"n":49,"domain":3,"html":"<p>A team is defining Service Level Objectives (SLOs) for a high-traffic, multi-region web application whose customers expect the service to be consistently available and fast. Customers are currently satisfied with both availability and performance. Over the trailing 28-day window, measurements show a 90th-percentile latency of 120 ms and a 95th-percentile latency of 275 ms. Which latency SLO should the team publish?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Publish a 90th-percentile latency target of 100 ms and a 95th-percentile latency target of 250 ms.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Publish a 90th-percentile latency target of 120 ms and a 95th-percentile latency target of 275 ms.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Publish a 90th-percentile latency target of 150 ms and a 95th-percentile latency target of 300 ms.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Publish a 90th-percentile latency target of 250 ms and a 95th-percentile latency target of 400 ms.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Publish a 90th-percentile latency target of 150 ms and a 95th-percentile latency target of 300 ms.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The must-have constraint is that the published target has to leave room for normal variance — a service that is already running at 120 ms/275 ms cannot publish those exact numbers as its SLO without being perpetually one bad minute away from violating it. The second constraint, equally important, is that “customers are currently happy,” which anchors how much slack is appropriate: the target should stay close enough to today’s real performance that it still reflects what users actually experience, rather than drifting into territory where a real regression could hide behind a technically-met SLO. Evaluate each option against both constraints together, and only the option with a modest, proportionate buffer survives.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“Customers are currently happy with the application performance and availability” — a signal that the published target should stay close to current measured values, ruling out an excessively loose target.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“90th percentile of latency is 120ms and the 95th percentile of latency is 275ms” — the baseline the recommended SLO must be benchmarked against, not copied.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“What latency SLO would you recommend to the team to publish” — asks for a target the team will defend going forward, which implies the target needs headroom (an error budget) rather than being a snapshot of current measurement.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A target of 150 ms (p90) and 300 ms (p95) sits modestly above the measured 120 ms and 275 ms. That gap is the SLO’s error budget: it absorbs ordinary, expected variance in a high-traffic, multi-region system — traffic spikes, routine releases, minor infrastructure hiccups — without the service falling out of SLO on a routine basis. At the same time, the buffer is small enough that the target still tracks what satisfied customers are actually experiencing today, so a genuine regression (say, p90 latency drifting to 200 ms) would still trip the SLO well before users started noticing a meaningfully worse experience.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> a 100 ms / 250 ms target is tighter than the service’s own currently measured performance (120 ms / 275 ms). The service would already be out of SLO under normal, currently-satisfactory conditions, which makes the target unworkable from day one.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Incorrect:</strong> this sets the SLO to exactly the currently measured values, leaving zero buffer between achieved performance and the target. Any ordinary fluctuation in a high-traffic system would push the SLI past the SLO, triggering false alarms and unnecessary feature freezes despite customers being happy — this is the specific mistake SRE guidance warns against when it comes to choosing SLO targets.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> 250 ms / 400 ms is more than double the current p90 and nearly 1.5x the current p95. That much slack would let the service’s real performance degrade substantially — well past the point where “currently happy” customers would stay happy — while still reporting as fully within SLO, defeating the purpose of the SLO as an early-warning signal tied to actual user experience.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap in Option B is mistaking precision for rigor: measuring carefully and then publishing exactly what you measured feels disciplined, but it’s the canonical error SRE guidance calls out directly — a target with no buffer isn’t a target, it’s a tripwire. The trap in Option D is overcorrecting in the other direction, assuming “more margin is always safer,” when the stem’s “customers are currently happy” detail is precisely what tells you the margin shouldn’t be that large. The decisive test is always: does this target leave a believable, proportionate buffer over what’s actually being achieved?</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A latency SLO should be set with intentional headroom above currently observed performance — enough to function as a real error budget, but not so much that the target stops reflecting genuine user experience. That buffer is what makes the SLO useful as a decision-making tool: small, expected fluctuations stay within budget, while real regressions still get caught early, and it also gives engineering room to make trade-offs elsewhere (cost, maintainability) that might cost a little latency without breaching the SLO.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>With a 150 ms / 300 ms SLO in place, the team can ship a normal release that nudges p90 latency up a few milliseconds without burning through its error budget or triggering a reliability-first response — but a release or incident that pushes p90 toward 200 ms would still clearly violate the SLO, prompting investigation well before the degradation became severe enough for customers to complain.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>A latency SLO should sit a reasonable step above what’s currently measured — never exactly at it, and never so far above it that it stops meaning anything.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question gives you current measured percentiles and asks what SLO to publish, eliminate the option that matches those numbers exactly (no error budget) and the option far looser than them (no longer reflects real user experience); the modest-headroom option is almost always correct.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/\"><strong>Google SRE Book — Service Level Objectives (Choosing Targets):</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer reasoning and the elimination of Option B’s “set the target to current performance” anti-pattern</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — Implementing SLOs:</strong></a> Supports: practical guidance on setting initial reliability targets and buffers for an error budget</p>","lessons":["d3l2"]},"e2q50":{"id":"e2q50","exam":2,"n":50,"domain":2,"html":"<p>An organization has recently moved to a container-based application development workflow, and the team builds many applications that are deployed continuously through an automated pipeline into production. A recent security audit found that code reaching production could contain vulnerabilities, and that the team’s existing virtual machine (VM) vulnerability tooling no longer applies now that workloads run in containers. The security and patch level of every piece of code moving through the pipeline needs to be assured. What should be done?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Set up Artifact Analysis to scan container images and report Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVEs).</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Configure the containers in the build pipeline to automatically update themselves just before release.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Reconfigure the existing operating-system vulnerability scanning software to run inside the containers themselves.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Implement static code analysis tooling that scans the Dockerfiles used to build the containers.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Set up Artifact Analysis to scan container images and report Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVEs).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The audit hands over two hard facts: production code may contain vulnerabilities, and the old VM-based vulnerability tooling doesn’t translate to containers. That second fact should immediately rule out any option that tries to reuse or relocate the old tool rather than adopt a container-native replacement. What’s needed is a mechanism that inspects the actual software installed inside each built image — OS packages and language dependencies — against known vulnerability data, and that does so automatically as images flow through the existing continuous pipeline. That’s a different capability than linting build instructions or hoping containers patch themselves, and it’s exactly what a dedicated container vulnerability-scanning service is built to provide.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“existing tooling around virtual machine (VM) vulnerabilities no longer applies to the containerized environment” — an explicit fact that should disqualify any option built around reusing the old VM-based tool.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“ensure the security and patch level of all code running through the pipeline” — the solution needs to inspect the actual components that end up in the built artifact, not just the build instructions or a one-time update step.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“deployed continuously through an automated build pipeline” — favors a solution that triggers automatically as part of that pipeline rather than a manual or ad hoc process.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Artifact Analysis is Google Cloud’s purpose-built service for scanning container images for known vulnerabilities in both OS packages and language-level dependencies, identified by CVE ID. It triggers automatically whenever a new image is pushed to the registry the build pipeline publishes to, so every image moving through the pipeline is checked without any manual intervention. This directly replaces the VM-vulnerability tooling with its container-native equivalent and gives the team ongoing, automated visibility into the actual patch level of the software shipping in every container.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Incorrect:</strong> “containers updating themselves” before release isn’t a standard or verifiable security control. It provides no visibility into what vulnerabilities exist or existed, and containers are generally meant to be built as immutable artifacts from a known base rather than self-modifying at release time — this gives the team no actual detection or reporting capability, which is what the scenario requires.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> the audit has already explicitly stated that the existing VM vulnerability tooling doesn’t apply to the containerized environment. Trying to reconfigure that same software to run inside containers directly contradicts the constraint the scenario itself provides.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> static analysis of Dockerfiles can catch certain build-instruction issues (bad practices, risky base images named explicitly), but it doesn’t inspect the OS and language packages that actually end up installed inside the resulting image. It can’t, by itself, tell the team whether a vulnerable package version shipped in a given build.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap in Option C is overlooking information the scenario already gave: the audit states outright that the old VM tooling doesn’t work in containers, so picking an option built around that same tool means failing to apply a stated constraint. The trap in Option D is conflating “scanning the instructions used to build something” with “scanning what was actually built” — a Dockerfile check has no visibility into which vulnerable package versions actually got installed in the final image.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Container-native vulnerability management works by scanning the actual contents of a built image — its OS packages and language dependencies — against known vulnerability sources, rather than relying on agent-based tooling designed for a persistent VM operating system. Automating that scan to trigger on every image push embeds security visibility directly into the CI/CD pipeline instead of treating it as a separate, manual step layered on afterward.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>With Artifact Analysis enabled on the registry the pipeline publishes to, every new image is automatically scanned and any known CVEs in its OS or language packages are surfaced. The team can pair that with a deployment-time policy, such as Binary Authorization, to block images with unresolved critical vulnerabilities from reaching production — giving the organization the same kind of ongoing patch-level assurance for containers that the old tooling once provided for virtual machines.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>VM vulnerability tools scan installed OS packages on a running machine; Artifact Analysis scans installed OS and language packages inside a container image — same goal, container-native mechanism.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When VM-era vulnerability tooling stops applying after a move to containers, favor the option that scans the actual contents of the built image for known CVEs and integrates automatically with the registry and pipeline — not one that reuses the old tool, relies on self-updating containers, or only inspects the Dockerfile.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/artifact-analysis/docs/container-analysis\"><strong>Google Cloud — Artifact Analysis overview (formerly Container Analysis):</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of automated, pipeline-integrated container image vulnerability scanning.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/artifact-analysis/docs/vulnerability-scanning\"><strong>Google Cloud — Vulnerability scanning (Artifact Analysis):</strong></a> Supports: the OS-package and language-package CVE scanning capability that directly matches the correct answer</p>","lessons":["d2l9"]},"e2q51":{"id":"e2q51","exam":2,"n":51,"domain":3,"html":"<p>A web application hosted on Compute Engine provides a booking service to thousands of users. Shortly after a new feature is released, the monitoring dashboard shows every user experiencing elevated latency at login. The goal is to limit the impact of this incident on the service’s users. What should be done first?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Roll back the release that was just deployed.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Review the Cloud Monitoring data in more depth.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Increase the size of the virtual machines that run the login service.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Deploy another new release to see whether it resolves the problem.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Roll back the release that was just deployed.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two things anchor this question: the symptom began shortly after a new release, and the stated goal is to mitigate user impact, not to understand root cause. A tight timing correlation between a deploy and a new, universal symptom is strong evidence the release caused it, and when the likely cause of an incident is a recent change, SRE practice is to restore the last known-good state immediately rather than spend time investigating or risk introducing further change. Rolling back does exactly that: it’s the fastest, lowest-risk way to stop user impact given the evidence already in hand, which is what “what should you do first” is asking for.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“Shortly after the release of a new feature... all users are experiencing latency” — a strong timing correlation between the deploy and the symptom, pointing to the release as the likely cause.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“mitigate the impact of the incident on the users” — the explicit goal is impact reduction, not root-cause diagnosis.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“What should you do first?” — a sequencing constraint favoring the fastest, safest corrective action over further investigation or additional risky changes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Rolling back the release immediately restores the application to the state it was in before the latency began, directly addressing the most probable cause without requiring any further diagnosis first. This mitigates the impact on the thousands of affected users as quickly as possible, which is the explicit goal stated in the question — consistent with SRE incident-response priority of restoring service first and investigating root cause afterward, once the system is stable again.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> reviewing monitoring data is a reasonable diagnostic activity, but it doesn’t itself reduce user impact, and the evidence already available — the tight timing correlation with the release — is already strong enough to justify acting. Spending time investigating further before taking the indicated corrective action delays mitigation without a clear need.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> resizing the VMs assumes the problem is a capacity or resource constraint, but nothing in the scenario points to a load increase — the correlation is specifically with a code release, not traffic growth. Acting on that unverified assumption risks spending time and money without addressing the more probable actual cause.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> deploying another release introduces more unverified change into a system that’s already experiencing an incident, rather than first stabilizing it. This runs directly counter to standard incident practice, which calls for restoring a known-good state before introducing any further changes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap in Option B is that “investigate before acting” sounds like the careful, responsible choice, but the question isn’t asking what’s generally prudent — it’s asking what mitigates user impact first, and the evidence already on hand (the tight timing correlation with a release) is sufficient to justify an immediate rollback. Waiting to confirm what’s already strongly indicated only prolongs the impact the question is asking you to minimize.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A central tenet of SRE incident management is limiting the disruption of an incident and restoring normal operation as quickly as possible — which is exactly why every rollout is expected to have a well-understood, well-tested rollback mechanism. When a recent change is strongly implicated, rolling it back is the standard fastest path to restoring service; a full root-cause investigation follows afterward, typically through a postmortem, once the immediate user impact has been addressed.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Rolling back the suspect release the moment users begin experiencing latency right after a deploy stops the impact on thousands of active logins immediately. The team can then investigate what specifically in the new feature caused the regression in a calmer, lower-pressure setting, rather than leaving users degraded while that investigation happens live in production.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>When a problem starts right after a release, mitigate first by rolling it back — don’t investigate further or ship more changes before restoring the known-good state.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>A strong timing correlation between a deploy and a new symptom, combined with a question asking what to do “first” to mitigate user impact, points to rolling back the suspect change rather than further diagnosis or an additional deployment.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/incident-response/\"><strong>Google SRE Book — Chapter 14, Managing Incidents:</strong></a> Supports: the core principle that effective incident management limits disruption and restores service as quickly as possible</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/gcp/incident-management-at-google-adventures-in-sre-land\"><strong>Google Cloud Blog — Adventures in SRE-land: Incident management at Google:</strong></a> Supports: rollback as the standard, well-tested mechanism for restoring service once a recent change is implicated in an incident</p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e2q52":{"id":"e2q52","exam":2,"n":52,"domain":2,"html":"<p>An application runs entirely on Google Cloud, and the team needs to stand up Jenkins to deploy application releases into that environment. The goals are to streamline the release process, reduce operational toil, and keep user data secure. What should be done?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Run Jenkins on individual local workstations.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Run Jenkins on a Kubernetes cluster hosted on-premises.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Run Jenkins on Cloud Run functions.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Run Jenkins on Compute Engine virtual machines.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Run Jenkins on Compute Engine virtual machines.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The deployment target is already fully inside Google Cloud, so the natural baseline is to keep the CI/CD tooling in the same environment rather than introduce a second network or infrastructure boundary. From there, the decision narrows to whether the chosen Google Cloud compute option can actually run Jenkins: Jenkins is a persistent, stateful server process, which rules out any execution model built around short-lived, time-boxed invocations. What’s left is a platform that is both inside Google Cloud and capable of hosting a long-running server — which is exactly what Compute Engine is for, and it lets Jenkins use native IAM and private networking to reach its deployment targets without exporting credentials or data outside Google Cloud’s boundary.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“application runs entirely on Google Cloud” — the deployment target already lives in Google Cloud, favoring keeping Jenkins in that same environment rather than a separate network.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“streamline the release process, reduce operational toil, and keep user data secure” — three simultaneous requirements that together disqualify options adding manual maintenance, a second infrastructure environment, or an unsuitable execution model.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Single-select “What should be done?” — only one option needs to satisfy all three goals at once.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Compute Engine provides a persistent virtual machine, which is the kind of platform Jenkins’s controller process actually needs: a long-running server that keeps job queues, configuration, and plugin state alive between builds. Placing that VM inside the same Google Cloud project and VPC as the application lets Jenkins authenticate to deployment targets through an IAM service account and private networking, rather than external credentials crossing into another network — directly supporting the “keep user data secure” goal. Because everything stays in one managed cloud environment, the team also avoids maintaining a second piece of infrastructure (a local machine fleet or an on-premises cluster), which is what actually lowers operational toil.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> individual local workstations have no centralized management, no consistent security controls, and require ongoing manual upkeep (patching, availability, backup) on every machine — this works directly against both “lower operational toil” and “keep user data secure.”</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> hosting Jenkins on an on-premises Kubernetes cluster, while the application and its data live entirely in Google Cloud, creates a hybrid topology. Deployment credentials now have to cross from the on-premises network into Google Cloud, and the team has to operate and secure two separate environments instead of one — increasing rather than reducing toil and security exposure, even though Kubernetes itself is a perfectly capable orchestrator.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Incorrect:</strong> Cloud Run functions execute on a per-invocation basis with a hard maximum duration per call, after which the instance is terminated — a model built for short-lived, stateless work. Jenkins requires a continuously running controller process that persists state across builds, which this execution model cannot provide; this option is technically infeasible as described, not merely suboptimal.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming any Google Cloud service is interchangeable as long as it’s “serverless” or “cloud-native” sounding — Cloud Run functions looks appealing for low operational toil, but Jenkins’s architecture as a persistent server is fundamentally incompatible with a time-boxed, per-invocation execution model. A second trap is equating “Kubernetes” with “low toil” without registering that Option B explicitly places it on-premises, which reintroduces the exact hybrid-network toil and security problem the scenario is trying to avoid.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Jenkins runs as a traditional, stateful controller process, not a short-lived function — it needs a platform that supports long-running execution, persistent storage for configuration and build history, and a stable network identity for reaching deployment targets. A general-purpose VM platform like Compute Engine satisfies that directly, and co-locating it with the application inside the same cloud project lets it rely on native IAM and private networking instead of external credentials or VPN connectivity.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Running Jenkins on a Compute Engine VM with a narrowly scoped IAM service account lets the team grant Jenkins exactly the deployment permissions it needs on the target Google Cloud resources, avoid storing long-lived static credentials for an external network, and rely on Google Cloud’s own VM image and patching tooling — concretely reducing both the security exposure and the day-to-day operational burden compared to running a separate on-premises CI environment.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Jenkins needs a persistent server, not a function, and not a separate network — when the application lives in Google Cloud, put Jenkins in Google Cloud, on a VM.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For “where should Jenkins run” questions, eliminate FaaS platforms outright on execution-model grounds, and prefer keeping Jenkins inside the same cloud environment as its deployment targets over local workstations or on-premises infrastructure, which add toil and cross-network security risk.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/using-jenkins-for-distributed-builds-on-compute-engine\"><strong>Google Cloud — Using Jenkins for distributed builds on Compute Engine:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer pattern of running Jenkins on Compute Engine with an IAM service account for secure deployment access</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/quotas\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Run functions quotas (maximum function duration):</strong></a> Supports: the technical elimination of Option C, since Jenkins’s persistent controller process cannot run within a bounded per-invocation execution mode.</p>","lessons":["d2l11"]},"e2q53":{"id":"e2q53","exam":2,"n":53,"domain":3,"html":"<p>A team operates a large service governed by a clearly defined Service Level Objective (SLO), and engineers ship new releases of that service several times each week. In the event that a significant incident pushes the service outside its SLO, the goal is for engineering effort to pivot away from feature work and toward restoring reliability. Which action should be taken in advance of any such incident?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Work with every service stakeholder to establish a suitable error budget policy ahead of time.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Negotiate an agreement with the product team that service reliability will always take priority over shipping new features.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Negotiate with the development team to cut the release cadence down to once a week at most.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Introduce a plugin into the Jenkins pipeline that blocks new releases any time the service is outside its SLO.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Work with every service stakeholder to establish a suitable error budget policy ahead of time.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard constraint is timing: whatever mechanism makes the reliability-versus-features decision has to be agreed and in place <em>before</em> the incident, so that nobody is negotiating priorities while the service is already broken. The soft constraint is sustainability: the organization still releases multiple times a week under normal conditions, so the mechanism can’t be a permanent brake on velocity — it has to be conditional on an objective trigger. An error budget policy satisfies both: it is written and agreed in advance, and it only forces a reliability-first posture once the budget is actually spent, leaving normal release cadence untouched the rest of the time. Options that either impose a permanent constraint (B, C) or implement only a narrow technical fragment of the policy (D) fail one of these two requirements.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“deploys new releases of the service multiple times a week” — establishes that high release velocity is the normal, desired state, which rules out any option that permanently throttles releases as the default.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“If a major incident causes the service to miss its SLO” — defines an objective, data-driven trigger condition, which is exactly what an error budget policy is built to key off of.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“before a major incident occurs” — a hard constraint requiring the mechanism to be pre-agreed rather than negotiated reactively.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>An error budget policy is a document, agreed in advance by both the team building features and the team(s) accountable for reliability, that spells out exactly what happens as the error budget is consumed — up to and including redirecting engineering effort away from features and onto reliability work once the budget is exhausted. Because it is negotiated and signed off before any incident, it converts what would otherwise be an ad hoc, politically charged argument during a crisis into a pre-agreed, objective consequence that triggers automatically. That directly produces the outcome the scenario asks for: a clean shift in focus from features to reliability precisely when the SLO is missed, and normal behavior the rest of the time.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> committing to “always” prioritize reliability over features removes the balancing mechanism SRE is built around. Error budgets exist precisely so that reliability work is prioritized only when the data (budget burn) says it should be, not unconditionally — an absolute rule like this is neither what the scenario needs nor realistic for a product organization to accept.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> capping release frequency addresses the symptom the question never raised. The stated problem is what happens <em>after</em> a major incident causes an SLO miss, not that releases are currently too frequent; arbitrarily slowing down a healthy, well-functioning release cadence doesn’t target the actual trigger condition and sacrifices velocity even when the service is well within budget.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> an automated release gate tied to SLO status is a reasonable <em>implementation detail</em> that an error budget policy might eventually include, but introduced on its own it is a unilateral technical control rather than an agreement reached with stakeholders, and it only blocks releases — it does nothing to actually redirect engineering focus onto reliability work, run postmortems, or reallocate sprint capacity, which is the broader shift the scenario is asking for.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap options sound protective of reliability but each oversimplifies the SRE balancing act: B swings too far by making reliability win unconditionally, and D swings too narrow by automating only the release-blocking piece without the surrounding stakeholder agreement. The decisive test is whether the option is both collaboratively agreed upon <em>and</em> conditional on an objective trigger — only the error budget policy is both.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An error budget is the inverse of the SLO (roughly, 1 − SLO) — the quantified amount of unreliability a service is allowed to accumulate over a measurement window. On its own, that number is just a metric; the error budget policy is what gives it consequences, defining in advance what the team does as the budget is spent, including the point at which feature work pauses in favor of reliability work. This is the core SRE mechanism for resolving the structural tension between product development’s incentive to ship and reliability’s incentive to stay within SLO.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team that has a signed-off error budget policy going into a major incident can redirect the next sprint’s capacity to reliability fixes immediately and without internal conflict, because the response was already agreed before anyone had a deadline or a launch on the line. Teams that skip this step typically end up re-litigating the reliability-versus-features trade-off incident by incident, which slows the response, breeds inconsistency, and makes recurring SLO misses more likely.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks what to put in place <em>before</em> an incident to make the reliability-versus-features call automatic, the answer is an error budget policy agreed with stakeholders — not a blanket rule, a fixed release cadence, or a single pipeline gate.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>SRE questions about balancing velocity and reliability almost always resolve to establishing an error budget (and its policy) with stakeholders in advance; treat “always prioritize X” absolutes and single-tool technical fixes as the standard distractor pattern.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/embracing-risk/\"><strong>Google SRE Book — Embracing Risk (Motivation for Error Budgets):</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism and the rationale for why reliability work is prioritized conditionally rather than always</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/error-budget-policy/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — Example Error Budget Policy:</strong></a> Supports: what an error budget policy contains and how it operationalizes the shift from features to reliability work</p>","lessons":["d3l3"]},"e2q54":{"id":"e2q54","exam":2,"n":54,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Cloud Build is used to build an application, and the goal is to shorten the build time while keeping both cost and development effort as low as possible. What should be done?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cache intermediate build artifacts in Cloud Storage between builds.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Stand up multiple Jenkins agents to run the build in parallel.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Break the build into multiple smaller steps to cut down execution time.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Switch to a larger Cloud Build virtual machine (VM) using the machine-type option.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Cache intermediate build artifacts in Cloud Storage between builds.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>This question stacks three requirements at once: faster builds, lower cost, and low implementation effort. Any option that trades one of those for another fails the question, even if it technically speeds things up. Scaling to bigger compute or to a second CI system can address speed, but each does so by spending more money or more engineering effort — the opposite of what’s being asked. The option that actually attacks the real source of wasted build time — redoing work that hasn’t changed since the last build — is the one that satisfies all three constraints together, because avoiding redundant work is both cheap (object storage, not more compute) and simple to add (a couple of build steps) within the Cloud Build setup already in place.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“reduce the build time while minimizing cost and development effort” — a triple, simultaneous constraint; the right answer has to score well on all three, not just speed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“You use Cloud Build” — the scenario has already committed to Cloud Build as the CI tool, which should raise a flag against any option that introduces a separate CI system.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Single-select “What should you do?” — only one action needs to satisfy the full compound requirement.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Caching intermediate build artifacts — dependencies, compiled objects, or other reusable outputs — in a Cloud Storage bucket between builds is Google’s own documented technique for speeding up Cloud Build. By copying a previous build’s results from the bucket, reusing them, and writing updated results back, each new build only has to redo the work that actually changed rather than starting from scratch. That directly cuts build time. Cloud Storage is inexpensive relative to build compute, so the added cost is minimal, and the implementation is a small, well-documented addition of a few build steps to the existing configuration rather than a redesign — hitting all three goals at once.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> standing up multiple Jenkins agents means operating an entirely separate CI system alongside (or instead of) Cloud Build. That’s a significant increase in both development effort — integrating or migrating the pipeline — and ongoing cost — infrastructure to run and maintain Jenkins agents — directly working against two of the three stated goals.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Incorrect:</strong> simply splitting a build into more, smaller steps doesn’t by itself reduce total execution time. Cloud Build steps run sequentially unless explicitly configured to run in parallel, so breaking work into more steps leaves the total amount of work the same, and can even add overhead from pulling and starting more step containers, rather than solving the actual time cost.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> a larger machine type can genuinely speed up a CPU- or memory-bound build, but Cloud Build’s own pricing scales build-minute cost sharply with machine size — a high-CPU machine type can cost many times more per minute than the default. That directly conflicts with the “minimizing cost” requirement, even though it helps with raw speed.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Option D is the trap because “bigger machine, faster build” is the most intuitive lever to reach for — but the question explicitly also asks to minimize cost, and Cloud Build’s pricing makes that trade-off concrete and expensive. The question is testing whether the full compound requirement is being weighed, not just the speed half of it.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Google’s documented Cloud Build performance guidance centers on eliminating redundant work rather than adding more compute to brute-force through it. Techniques like Docker layer caching and caching directories or dependencies via Cloud Storage let each build reuse the results of the previous one, so only genuinely changed work has to be redone — typically both faster and cheaper than scaling up the build machine.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A build that re-downloads the same unchanged dependencies or recompiles unchanged code on every single commit wastes minutes every time it runs. Caching those results in Cloud Storage turns most builds into fast, incremental runs instead of full rebuilds, compounding savings in both developer wait time and Cloud Build billing as a team ships many times a day.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Don’t pay more compute to go faster — stop redoing the same work. Cache it.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a build-speed question also asks to minimize cost and effort, favor caching previous build results over scaling up build machines or standing up separate CI infrastructure.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/speeding-up-builds\"><strong>Google Cloud — Best practices for speeding up builds (Cloud Build):</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of caching directories and artifacts with Cloud Storage between builds</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/pricing\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Build pricing:</strong></a> Supports: the elimination of Option D by showing build-minute cost scaling sharply with machine type</p>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e2q55":{"id":"e2q55","exam":2,"n":55,"domain":4,"html":"<p>A team uses Cloud Logging to monitor applications running on Google Cloud. A newly deployed application’s logs are not showing up in Cloud Logging, and the issue needs to be troubleshot. What should be done?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Confirm that the Ops Agent has been installed on the virtual machine hosting the application.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Confirm that the account being used has the permissions needed to view Cloud Logging.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Confirm that port 25 has been opened in the firewall to allow traffic through to Cloud Logging.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Confirm that the application is using the required client library and that its service account key has the proper permissions.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Confirm that the Ops Agent has been installed on the virtual machine hosting the application.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The symptom is specific: logs for one newly deployed application are entirely missing, not that the console or dashboard is inaccessible. That points toward a data-collection problem rather than a viewing-permission problem. Compute Engine VMs do not have the agent responsible for collecting and forwarding logs installed by default — it has to be explicitly installed on each VM. Since this is a newly deployed application, quite possibly on a newly created VM, the single most common and most fundamental explanation for a total absence of logs is that this agent was simply never installed, which is exactly why it’s the first thing official Google diagnostic tooling checks when a VM’s logs or metrics go missing.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“recently deployed a new application” — a new or newly configured VM is a strong candidate for never having had the logging agent explicitly installed on it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“its logs are not appearing” — specifically a data problem for one application’s logs, not a description of being locked out of the console entirely.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Single-select “What should you do?” — asking for the first, most fundamental thing to check.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Compute Engine VM instances don’t ship with monitoring or logging agents pre-installed; the Ops Agent must be explicitly installed on a VM for its logs to be collected and sent to Cloud Logging. Because installing the agent is a separate, easy-to-forget step when standing up a new VM or application, it’s the standard, most common root cause whenever a VM’s logs are completely absent — and it’s precisely the first condition official Google troubleshooting tooling verifies when logs or metrics aren’t showing up for a Compute Engine instance.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> a permissions problem on the viewing account typically prevents access to the Cloud Logging console itself, producing an access-denied experience rather than selectively missing data for one new application while the dashboard is otherwise usable. The described symptom doesn’t match what an account-permission issue would look like.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Incorrect:</strong> port 25 is the standard SMTP mail-relay port and has no role in how a logging agent communicates with Cloud Logging. This option names the wrong protocol entirely and doesn’t correspond to any real mechanism involved here.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> checking the client library and service account permissions is a legitimate step for an application that writes logs directly through an API rather than relying on a VM-level agent, but given the scenario’s framing around “the hosting virtual machine,” the far more common and better-documented first failure mode for a VM-hosted application is simply that the collection agent itself was never installed.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Option C sounds like a reasonable “check the firewall” instinct, but the specific port it names is simply wrong for the service involved — a classic plausible-sounding-but-factually-incorrect distractor. Option B generalizes “logs not appearing” into “an access problem,” when the described symptom — one new application’s data missing, not a broad inability to use the dashboard — points somewhere more specific.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Compute Engine VMs are not monitored or logged automatically. The Ops Agent — the unified successor to the earlier, separate Stackdriver Logging and Monitoring agents — must be explicitly installed on a VM in order for its logs and metrics to be collected and sent to Cloud Logging and Cloud Monitoring. Because this installation step can easily be skipped when a new VM or deployment is created, it’s the standard first thing to verify whenever a VM’s data is unexpectedly absent.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>If a newly deployed application’s VM never had the Ops Agent installed, the application itself may be running fine and writing its own logs locally or to stdout, but nothing reaches Cloud Logging at all — from the console, it looks exactly like the application produces no logs, which is the symptom described. Installing and starting the agent on that VM typically resolves the issue immediately.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>No agent, no data — before chasing permissions or firewall ports, confirm the Ops Agent is actually installed and running on the VM.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a specific, newly deployed VM-hosted application’s logs are entirely missing from Cloud Logging, check first whether the Ops Agent is installed and running on that VM — it’s the most common root cause and the first condition official Google diagnostics verify.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/agent/ops-agent\"><strong>Google Cloud — Ops Agent overview:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism that the Ops Agent is what collects and sends a VM’s logs to Cloud Logging</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/agent/ops-agent/troubleshoot-find-info\"><strong>Google Cloud — Find troubleshooting information for the Ops Agent:</strong></a> Supports: confirming that checking whether the Ops Agent is installed and sending data is the standard first diagnostic step for missing VM logs</p>","lessons":["d4l5"]},"e2q56":{"id":"e2q56","exam":2,"n":56,"domain":1,"html":"<p>A company is standing up a new, cloud-native Google Cloud organization. It will initially be used by a small number of departments, then expand to support a large number of departments, each of which runs a large number of applications of varying sizes. The VPC network architecture needs to be designed so that it minimizes the amount of ongoing management required, while staying flexible enough for development teams to adapt quickly as their needs evolve. What should be done?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Give each department its own VPC, and connect those VPCs using VPC Network Peering.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Give each department its own VPC, and use Private Service Connect to connect those VPCs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Give each application its own VPC, and use Private Service Connect to connect those VPCs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Give each department its own VPC, and connect those VPCs using Cloud VPN.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Give each department its own VPC, and connect those VPCs using VPC Network Peering.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two constraints have to be satisfied together: minimal ongoing management, and enough flexibility for teams to adapt quickly without waiting on networking work. That combination favors a connectivity mechanism that operates at the whole-network level — connect two VPCs once, and every application in either one can reach the other without any further configuration — over a mechanism that has to be set up per application or per service. It also rules out any VPC-per-application structure outright, since the stated scale (“a large number of applications” per department, scaling to “a large number of departments”) would make that approach unmanageable regardless of which connectivity tool sits on top of it. Among the remaining whole-network options, the one actually built and documented for connecting Google Cloud VPCs to each other, running over Google’s own internal network, is VPC Network Peering.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“minimize the amount of management required” — disfavors anything that has to be configured per application or per service, and disfavors a per-application VPC structure outright.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“flexible enough for development teams to quickly adapt” — favors a connectivity layer that grants broad reachability once established, so new applications don’t require new networking configuration.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“expand... to a large number of departments” and “a large number of applications” — a scale signal that should be weighed against each option’s native fit for intra-Google-Cloud connectivity, not just whether it technically works.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>VPC Network Peering is Google Cloud’s native mechanism specifically for connecting two VPC networks so that resources in each can communicate with each other, using internal IP addresses over Google’s own network rather than the public internet. Once two departmental VPCs are peered, every application already running in either VPC — and every application added to either VPC afterward — can reach the other side without any additional per-application setup. That satisfies both requirements at once: one peering relationship per pair of departments covers all current and future applications (minimizing management), and development teams can add new applications freely without touching the network layer (supporting fast adaptation).</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Incorrect:</strong> Private Service Connect is documented as a way for consumers to privately access specific managed or published services, not as a mechanism for general inter-VPC network communication between customer-owned VPCs. Using it as the department-to-department connectivity layer would mean individually publishing and connecting each application or service as it’s created — more ongoing management as the application count grows, not less, directly working against the stated goal.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> giving every application its own VPC multiplies the number of VPCs (and the connections needed between them) far beyond what’s manageable, given each department already runs a large number of applications. This fails the “minimize management” requirement on its own, independent of which connectivity mechanism is layered on top of it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Cloud VPN is documented as encrypting traffic over the public internet and as being suited to low-volume data connections — a profile built for hybrid connectivity to an external network, not for linking VPCs that live entirely within Google Cloud. It adds tunnel and gateway configuration and encryption overhead without providing any connectivity advantage over native Peering for this all-cloud scenario.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming the newer-sounding “Private Service Connect” must be the more modern, correct choice over “plain” VPC Peering — but PSC solves a different problem (narrowly exposing one service to specific consumers), and official guidance is explicit that it does not provide general inter-VPC communication between customer networks. A second trap is treating a VPC per application as inherently more secure or well-isolated without weighing it against the explicit “minimize management” requirement at the stated scale.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>VPC Network Peering connects two entire VPC networks at the network level over Google’s internal backbone, giving every resource in each network reachability to every resource in the other — well suited to linking a department-scale set of VPCs with minimal per-workload configuration. Private Service Connect operates one layer down: it privately exposes and consumes individual services, which is the right tool for sharing one specific service narrowly, not for establishing broad reachability between two whole networks.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>With departmental VPCs peered, a development team can deploy a brand-new internal application in their department’s VPC and have it immediately reachable from another department’s VPC with zero additional networking work, instead of waiting on a networking team to publish a new Private Service Connect endpoint for every new service — which is exactly the kind of fast, low-friction adaptation the scenario is asking for.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Peering connects whole networks; Private Service Connect connects one published service at a time — for broad, low-maintenance department-to-department reachability, reach for Peering.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a VPC design question asks for minimal-management, broadly flexible connectivity between a moderate number of VPCs that live entirely within Google Cloud, favor native VPC Network Peering; rule out Private Service Connect for general inter-VPC connectivity (it’s for specific published services) and Cloud VPN for pure intra-Google-Cloud links (it’s built for internet-traversing, low-volume, hybrid connectivity).</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/vpc-peering\"><strong>Google Cloud — VPC Network Peering:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism for connecting whole VPC networks over Google’s internal network</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/private-service-connect\"><strong>Google Cloud — Private Service Connect overview:</strong></a> Supports: the elimination of Options B and C by confirming PSC’s purpose is private access to specific published/managed services, not general inter-VPC connectivity</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/network-connectivity/docs/vpn\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud VPN documentation:</strong></a> Supports: the elimination of Option D by confirming Cloud VPN traverses the public internet via IPsec and is intended for low-volume data connections</p>","lessons":["d1l10"]},"e2q57":{"id":"e2q57","exam":2,"n":57,"domain":4,"html":"<p>A web application runs on App Engine and relies on Cloud SQL and Cloud Storage for data storage. After a brief spike in site traffic, request latency rises sharply for every user, along with higher CPU usage and a larger number of application processes. Investigation turns up the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Traffic levels return to normal after the spike, but elevated latency persists.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Requests that read from the Cloud SQL database and requests that fetch images from Cloud Storage show the same degree of added latency.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Nothing about the website’s code or configuration changed around the time latency began rising.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The error rate seen by users has not increased.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Another traffic spike is expected in the next few days, and the goal is to prevent users from experiencing that latency again. What should be done?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Convert the Cloud Storage buckets to multi-regional storage.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Turn on high availability for the Cloud SQL instances.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Migrate the application off App Engine and onto Compute Engine.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Adjust the App Engine configuration to keep a larger pool of idle instances available.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Adjust the App Engine configuration to keep a larger pool of idle instances available.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The single most important piece of evidence is that Cloud SQL requests and Cloud Storage requests show the <em>same</em> elevated latency. If either backend were the bottleneck, only requests touching that specific backend would slow down — the fact that two unrelated services are equally affected means the shared layer every request passes through, the App Engine instances themselves, is the actual bottleneck. Combine that with latency persisting after load already returned to normal, and the picture is an App Engine deployment that scaled instances down after the spike and is now paying a provisioning/startup cost whenever it has to scale back up to meet ordinary traffic — a cost every request incurs regardless of which backend it ultimately calls. The fix has to act on that shared layer, proactively, before the next anticipated spike.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“Requests... from the Cloud SQL database and images from Cloud Storage show the same high latency” — the decisive clue: identical latency across two unrelated backends points to a shared upstream cause, not a backend-specific one.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“load levels returned to normal but users still experience high latency” — latency outlives the triggering spike, pointing to a lingering instance-capacity mismatch rather than an active load problem.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“No changes were made to the website” and “no increase in the number of errors” — rules out a code regression or an outage/overload condition; this is a pure latency issue tied to capacity, not correctness or availability.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>App Engine’s automatic scaler can reduce the number of running instances once traffic subsides, and bringing a new instance online to absorb renewed load takes measurable time to initialize. Configuring a minimum number of idle instances keeps a buffer of already-initialized, “warm” instances on standby at all times, so the next traffic spike is absorbed immediately instead of triggering a wave of cold instance starts. Because that initialization delay sits in front of every request — whichever backend it eventually calls — pre-warming capacity this way reduces latency uniformly for both Cloud SQL-backed and Cloud Storage-backed requests, exactly matching what was observed.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong> multi-regional Cloud Storage is about data durability and serving reads with low latency to users spread across multiple geographic regions, not about recovering from an App Engine instance-capacity shortfall. It also does nothing to explain or fix the matching latency seen on Cloud SQL requests, which share no infrastructure with a Cloud Storage bucket’s regional configuration.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Cloud SQL high availability exists specifically to reduce downtime during a zonal or instance failure by failing over to a standby instance — it is not a performance or latency feature, and there’s no sign of a Cloud SQL outage here (no increase in errors). It also can’t account for the identical latency on Cloud Storage requests, which an HA setting on Cloud SQL has no influence over at all.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> moving the application to Compute Engine would work eventually, but it means giving up App Engine’s managed autoscaling and deployment automation and rebuilding that capacity-management logic manually — a disproportionate, disruptive response when the same platform already exposes the exact configuration knob (idle instances) needed to solve this.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is reaching for a backend-specific fix because the stem names Cloud SQL and Cloud Storage explicitly — it’s tempting to “fix the database” or “fix the storage.” The clue that both backends show the identical latency increase is what rules that out: a real backend-specific issue would show up on only one of them. That detail should redirect attention to the one layer common to every request, the App Engine instances serving them.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>App Engine’s automatic scaling adjusts the number of running instances to match demand, scaling down aggressively once load drops to control cost. Provisioning a new instance when demand rises again isn’t instantaneous — it carries startup/initialization latency. Keeping a configured minimum of idle instances trades a predictable amount of extra instance-hours for the elimination of that startup latency on the next burst of traffic, a benefit that applies to every request regardless of destination, since it happens before the request ever reaches Cloud SQL or Cloud Storage.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Knowing a traffic spike is coming in the next few days, raising the minimum idle instance count ahead of time converts an unpredictable, user-facing latency spike into a small, known, budgeted increase in App Engine instance costs — a standard capacity-planning trade-off whenever the timing of increased demand can be anticipated, rather than leaving the autoscaler to react after the fact and re-incur the same cold-start penalty.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>When latency rises equally across unrelated backend services after a traffic spike and doesn’t recover once load normalizes, suspect the shared serving layer’s instance capacity, not either backend individually.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Identical latency across different backend services is the signal to look at the layer common to every request — here, App Engine’s instance count — rather than tuning either backend in isolation; use idle-instance configuration to pre-warm capacity ahead of an expected spike.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/appengine/docs/standard/python/how-instances-are-managed\"><strong>Google Cloud — How Instances Are Managed (App Engine):</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of keeping idle instances available to avoid provisioning latency on renewed traffic</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/sql/docs/high-availability\"><strong>Google Cloud — About high availability (Cloud SQL):</strong></a> Supports: the elimination of Option B by confirming HA addresses zonal/instance failover, not request latency</p>","lessons":["d4l12"]},"e2q58":{"id":"e2q58","exam":2,"n":58,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Some of your production services run on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) in a Europe region, while your build system runs in a US region. You want to push the container images from the build system to a scalable registry so that the bandwidth available for transferring the images to the cluster is as high as possible. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Push the images to Artifact Registry by using the gcr.io hostname.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Push the images to Artifact Registry by using the us.gcr.io hostname.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Push the images to Artifact Registry by using the eu.gcr.io hostname.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Push the images to a private image registry running on a Compute Engine instance in the same Europe region as the cluster.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Push the images to Artifact Registry by using the eu.gcr.io hostname.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two hard constraints define the answer: the registry must be a scalable service, and image transfer bandwidth to the cluster must be maximized. The second constraint tells you which side to optimize. Images are pushed once per build but pulled whenever nodes start or scale, so the pull path to the cluster matters most, which means the images should be stored close to the GKE cluster and not close to the build system. Apply that to each hostname option, then test the self-hosted option against the “scalable” requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“scalable registry” — hard constraint: the registry must be a managed service that scales without you operating it, which excludes a self-run server.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“maximize the bandwidth for transferring the images to the cluster” — hard constraint: the images must be stored near the cluster that pulls them.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“production services ... in a Europe region” with a build system in a US region — technical signal: producer and consumer are on different continents, and the optimization targets the consumer side.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“gcr.io,” “us.gcr.io,” and “eu.gcr.io” hostnames — technical signal: the hostname determines the multi-region where images are stored.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>The hostname selects the storage location. Artifact Registry supports the gcr.io domain through gcr.io repositories in multi-regional locations: gcr.io and us.gcr.io map to the us multi-region, eu.gcr.io maps to the europe multi-region, and asia.gcr.io maps to asia. Pushing to eu.gcr.io therefore stores the images in Europe, near the GKE cluster, so every pull travels a short distance with higher throughput and no intercontinental transfer. The build system pays the cross-ocean cost once per push, while the cluster benefits on every pull. Artifact Registry is a fully managed service, which meets the scalability requirement. One prerequisite is IAM: the build identity needs write access to the repository and the cluster’s nodes need read access.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> The gcr.io hostname maps to the US multi-region, so the images would be stored on the wrong continent and every pull would cross it. It is tempting because it is the default hostname, but it works against the bandwidth goal.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> The us.gcr.io hostname is also the US multi-region. It is tempting because the build system is in the US and pushes would be fast, but that optimizes the one-time push and not the repeated pulls the question is about.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A registry in the cluster’s region has the right proximity, but a self-run registry on a Compute Engine instance is not a scalable managed service. You would own capacity, availability, storage, patching, and authentication, and a single instance is a throughput bottleneck and a single point of failure.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest distractor is Option B, because “the build system is in the US” makes a US hostname feel natural. The distinction is which transfer matters: pushes happen once per build, and pulls happen on every deployment and scale-out. If the requirement had been to maximize build push speed because pulls were rare, the registry location near the build system would become the better choice.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Store artifacts close to the systems that consume them most often, since the read path usually dominates the write path for container images. Registry location choice balances latency, availability, and bandwidth cost. Exam framing and current practice differ in two ways. First, Container Registry is deprecated and superseded by Artifact Registry, which serves the gcr.io hostnames through gcr.io repositories, so this item is expressed with Artifact Registry. Second, the supplied region names (eu-west-1 and us-west-1) are not Google Cloud region IDs, so the revised item refers to the Europe and US geographies, which is all the answer depends on. In a new design, Google recommends creating a repository in the same region as your GKE cluster to reduce latency and network egress costs, which a pkg.dev repository allows.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A US-based build system pushes each image once to the Europe-hosted repository, and GKE nodes in Europe pull from it whenever pods are scheduled or the autoscaler adds nodes. Rollouts and scale-out start faster because pulls stay within Europe. The build pays a single cross-region transfer per image, and the team grants the build service account write access and the cluster’s node service account read access.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Push once, pull often: store the image next to the cluster that pulls it.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks to maximize image pull bandwidth to a cluster, choose the managed registry location nearest the cluster, not the build system. The hostname prefix (us, eu, asia) sets the storage multi-region, and a self-hosted registry fails the scalability requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/transition/gcr-repositories\"><strong>Google Cloud — Artifact Registry gcr.io repositories (hostname to location mapping and Container Registry transition):</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (gcr.io and us.gcr.io map to the us multi-region, eu.gcr.io maps to europe), the elimination of options A and B, and the terminology update from Container Registry to Artifact Registry</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/repositories\"><strong>Google Cloud — Artifact Registry repository overview (repository location):</strong></a> Supports: the guidance that repositories should be located near the GKE clusters and other services that consume them to reduce latency and egress costs, and the managed nature of the service that eliminates option D</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide:</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment to Section 2.1, “Designing pipelines,” which lists artifact management with Artifact Registry</p>","lessons":["d2l5"]},"e2q59":{"id":"e2q59","exam":2,"n":59,"domain":5,"html":"<p>An e-commerce application has recently been migrated to Google Cloud, and the team wants to get it ready for the upcoming busy season. What is the first thing that should be done to prepare?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Run load tests against the application to profile how its performance behaves under scaling.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Turn on autoscaling for the production clusters in anticipation of traffic growth.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Pre-provision twice the compute capacity that was used during last season, anticipating growth.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Write a runbook describing how to scale up the disaster recovery (DR) environment if growth occurs.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Run load tests against the application to profile how its performance behaves under scaling.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The decisive word in the question is “first.” Every one of the four options is a plausible activity somewhere in a busy-season readiness plan, so the question isn’t testing whether each is a good idea in isolation — it’s testing sequencing. Autoscaling thresholds, how much capacity to pre-provision, and how far a DR environment needs to scale are all decisions that should be derived from data about how the application actually behaves under load: where it bottlenecks, at what traffic level, and in which component. Until that data exists, any of those decisions is a guess. Load testing is the step that produces the data, so it has to come before the others, not alongside or after them.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“What is the first thing that should be done to prepare?” — a sequencing constraint; the correct option must be the foundational step everything else depends on, not merely a reasonable preparation activity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“recently been migrated to Google Cloud” — the application’s scaling behavior in this environment hasn’t necessarily been characterized yet, reinforcing the need to establish a performance baseline before making capacity commitments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Options B, C, and D are each specific infrastructure or process decisions, none of which states that performance data already exists to base that decision on.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Load testing reveals the application’s actual scaling characteristics and bottlenecks under simulated peak conditions — whether the constraint is compute, a database connection pool, a downstream API quota, or something else entirely. That information is a precondition for making every other preparation decision well: it tells you what signal and thresholds to configure for autoscaling, how much capacity actually needs to be reserved or pre-provisioned, and whether DR capacity needs to scale differently than production. Running it first means every subsequent decision is grounded in evidence rather than assumption.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> enabling autoscaling is a sound long-term practice, but configuring it effectively requires knowing which metric to scale on and what thresholds and instance limits actually match the application’s real behavior under load — exactly what a load test reveals. Turning it on without that information risks thresholds that are poorly matched to the application’s actual bottlenecks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> doubling last season’s compute is an arbitrary multiplier with no basis in measured need. It assumes growth will be linear and that compute is the binding constraint, when the real bottleneck could just as easily be a database, a third-party integration, or application code — something more compute alone wouldn’t fix, and something a load test would surface.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> a runbook for scaling the DR environment addresses disaster recovery — restoring service after a catastrophic failure — which is a related but separate concern from preparing production capacity for an expected, non-catastrophic increase in ordinary traffic. It doesn’t speak to the busy-season capacity question the scenario is actually asking about.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is evaluating each option only on “is this a sensible thing to do,” which makes B, C, and D all look reasonable. The question isn’t asking which options are valid preparation activities — it’s asking which one has to happen first so the others can be done well. Skipping past the diagnostic step straight to a specific infrastructure decision is the exact ordering mistake the question is testing for.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Capacity planning for an anticipated demand increase starts with establishing, through load testing under realistic simulated peak conditions, how the system actually scales and where its real limits are. Every subsequent decision — autoscaling configuration, capacity reservations, redundancy planning, DR sizing — should be derived from that evidence rather than assumed ahead of time.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Running load tests well ahead of a peak event — Google Cloud’s own peak-event guidance recommends starting three to five months out — surfaces bottlenecks like a saturated connection pool or an undersized API quota while there’s still time to fix them, and gives the team the concrete numbers needed to correctly size autoscaling policies and capacity reservations, instead of discovering those limits for the first time during the actual busy season.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Before tuning autoscaling, pre-provisioning compute, or updating a DR runbook — measure first. Load test to find out how the system actually scales.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks what to do “first” to prepare for anticipated higher demand, favor the step that produces the data every other decision should be based on — load testing — over any specific capacity or configuration action taken without it.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/support/docs/peak-events/conduct-load-testing\"><strong>Google Cloud — Conduct load testing (peak capacity event preparation):</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer reasoning that load testing is a foundational, early preparation step that surfaces scaling bottlenecks before an event</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/infra-reliability-guide/traffic-load\"><strong>Google Cloud — Manage traffic and load for your workloads in Google Cloud:</strong></a> Supports: why capacity decisions such as autoscaling configuration and provisioning should be derived from load-test results rather than assumed in advance</p>","lessons":["d5l9"]},"e2q60":{"id":"e2q60","exam":2,"n":60,"domain":1,"html":"<p>An application being deployed needs access to sensitive information, and that information must stay encrypted with the exposure kept to a minimum if a breach were to occur. What should be done?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Store the encryption keys in Cloud Key Management Service (KMS), and rotate those keys on a frequent schedule.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Inject the secret into the instance at creation time through an encrypted configuration management system.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Integrate the application with a single sign-on (SSO) system, keeping secrets from ever being exposed to the application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Use a continuous build pipeline that generates a different version of the secret for every instance of the application.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Store the encryption keys in Cloud Key Management Service (KMS), and rotate those keys on a frequent schedule.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The scenario sets two requirements that have to be satisfied together: the information must actually be encrypted, and exposure must be minimized specifically “if a breach occurs” — meaning the design has to assume a breach is possible and bound how much damage it can do, not just prevent it. That second requirement is the decisive one: it’s asking for a defense-in-depth property, not just an encryption mechanism. A centrally managed key service that encrypts the data satisfies the first requirement, and regularly rotating those keys is what satisfies the second, because rotation limits how much data any single compromised key version could ever expose. Options that only address how a secret is delivered or generated, rather than how the underlying encryption keys are managed over time, don’t provide that bounding property.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“ensure that this information is encrypted” — the chosen approach must directly protect the sensitive data through encryption, not merely restrict who can see it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“the risk of exposure is minimal if a breach occurs” — a hard requirement for limiting the blast radius of a compromise, not just preventing one.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Single-select “What should you do?” — only one mechanism needs to satisfy both requirements at once.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud KMS centrally creates and manages the cryptographic keys used to encrypt the sensitive information. Rotating those keys on a frequent schedule limits the cryptoperiod — the span of time a given key version is used to encrypt new data — so that if a key version is ever compromised, only the data encrypted during that version’s active window is at risk, not the full accumulated history of sensitive data the application has ever handled. That combination — managed encryption plus a bounded exposure window through rotation — directly satisfies both halves of the requirement at once.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> delivering a secret to an instance through an encrypted channel protects it in transit at creation time, but says nothing about how the underlying sensitive information is encrypted or how long that secret remains valid afterward. A secret injected once and never rotated stays exploitable for the entire lifetime of the instance, which does not minimize exposure if it’s later compromised.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> single sign-on governs how human users authenticate to a system. It has no bearing on how an application’s sensitive data is encrypted or how machine-level secrets are protected, so it doesn’t address either requirement the scenario actually states.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> generating a distinct secret version for every instance through a build pipeline means secrets get baked into build artifacts and spread across many build outputs and logs — more places a secret could leak, and more difficulty tracking which version is active where. That works against minimizing exposure rather than for it.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Options B and D both sound protective — an encrypted delivery channel, a fresh secret per instance — but neither one manages the lifecycle of the cryptographic material actually protecting the sensitive data over time; they’re adjacent practices to secret handling, not key management. The scenario specifically asks for bounded exposure in the event of a breach, which is exactly what a centrally managed, rotated key lifecycle is designed to deliver, not what secret delivery or build-time secret generation provides.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud KMS is Google Cloud’s centralized service for creating, managing, and using cryptographic keys to protect data, both in compatible Google Cloud services and in custom applications. A core discipline of key management is limiting a key’s cryptoperiod through regular rotation — the standard recommended practice precisely because it bounds how much data any single compromised key version could expose, rather than leaving one key protecting an ever-growing body of sensitive data indefinitely.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Rotating an application’s Cloud KMS keys on a regular schedule means that if an attacker ever obtains one key version, they can only read the data encrypted during that key’s active period — not months or years of accumulated sensitive data sitting behind a single, never-changed key — turning what could be a catastrophic breach into a bounded, time-limited incident.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Encrypting data protects it; rotating the keys protecting it is what actually limits how much a breach can expose — manage the keys, don’t just manage how the secret moves around.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks for both encrypting sensitive information and limiting exposure if a breach happens, look for the option that manages the cryptographic key lifecycle — a centralized key service plus regular rotation — rather than one that only addresses how a secret is delivered or generated.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kms/docs/key-rotation\"><strong>Google Cloud — Rotate a key (Cloud KMS):</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism of key rotation limiting the exposure window if a key is compromised</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kms/docs/key-management-service\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Key Management Service overview:</strong></a> Supports: Cloud KMS’s role as the centralized service for creating and managing the encryption keys used to protect application data</p>","lessons":["d1l12"]},"e3q1":{"id":"e3q1","exam":3,"n":1,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You have configured a Cloud Build pipeline to deploy Terraform configurations maintained in a GitHub repository. Developers work in short-lived branches and occasionally create tags during development. When a release is ready for deployment, you assign it a semantic version tag. You need the pipeline to apply the Terraform configuration whenever a new release becomes available, while minimizing operational overhead. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Configure a Cloud Build trigger for tag pushes matching <code>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+</code>.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Configure a Cloud Build trigger for branch pushes matching <code>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+</code>.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Configure a Cloud Build trigger for tag pushes matching <code>.*</code>.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Configure a Cloud Build trigger for branch pushes matching <code>.*</code>.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Configure a Cloud Build trigger for tag pushes matching</strong><code>**\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+**</code><strong>.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>First, identify the event that represents deployment readiness: a release tag. Then distinguish release tags from tags created during development. A tag trigger with a version pattern provides the most selective configuration offered, using Cloud Build’s built-in filtering instead of additional pipeline logic. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Semantic version tag” — Technical signal:</strong> Release identification depends on tag names.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Occasionally create tags during development” — Technical signal:</strong> Matching every tag would also invoke the deployment pipeline for development tags.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Whenever a new release becomes available” — Hard constraint:</strong> The trigger must respond to the release event.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Minimizing operational overhead” — Preference:</strong> Use native trigger filtering.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Build supports triggers for pushed tags and accepts regular expressions for matching their names. In <code>\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+</code>, each <code>\\d+</code> matches one or more digits, and each <code>\\.</code> matches a literal period. The expression therefore matches version strings such as <code>2.5.1</code>. </p>\n<p>Associate the trigger with the existing build configuration that applies Terraform. The tag must be pushed to GitHub; creating it locally does not invoke the trigger. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> The version pattern looks appropriate, but it filters branch names. Pushing a release tag does not satisfy a branch-push trigger.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> It invokes builds for release tags and development tags. Additional filtering would be needed to prevent development tags from reaching the deployment steps.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> It responds to pushes across all branches, including development branches, rather than using release tags as the deployment signal. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Selecting a tag trigger is only part of the decision: <code>.*</code> also includes development tags. Option C would fit if every pushed tag represented an approved deployment release.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A repository trigger combines an <strong>event type</strong> with a <strong>matching condition</strong>. Both must represent the intended deployment boundary.</p>\n<p>The supplied expression assumes development tags do not match the version pattern. Cloud Build performs regex searches, so it can also match a tag such as <code>test-2.5.1</code>. For strict release filtering, use anchors and a pattern tailored to your release naming convention; the supplied expression is not a complete semantic-version validator. </p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team could use development tags such as <code>experiment-networking</code> and release tags such as <code>2.5.1</code>. Filtering for version tags lets the release push invoke the existing Terraform pipeline automatically, reducing manual deployment work and unnecessary builds.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>When tags mark deployment readiness, match the release-tag convention in the trigger.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Identify what declares a release, then configure the corresponding trigger event and filter. Here, version-tag pushes provide the deployment signal, making A the best option.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/automating-builds/create-manage-triggers\"><strong>Google Cloud — Create and manage build triggers</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Tag versus branch events, repository matching, and the requirement to push tags to the remote repository.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/builds/triggers/create/github\"><strong>Google Cloud CLI — gcloud builds triggers create github</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Separate branch and tag patterns, regex search behavior, and the distinctions among options A–D.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/google/re2/wiki/Syntax\"><strong>Google RE2 — Syntax</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Digit classes, repetition, escaped literal characters, and matching boundaries.</p>","lessons":["d2l1","d2l10"]},"e3q2":{"id":"e3q2","exam":3,"n":2,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your team is deploying an application on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) that handles personally identifiable information (PII). You must collect the application’s logs in Cloud Logging while preventing sensitive user data from being exposed in those logs. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Apply log sampling to reduce the number of application log entries collected.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use Sensitive Data Protection to inspect log entries in real time and redact PII before storing them in Cloud Logging.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Turn off Cloud Logging for the application to prevent sensitive information from being collected.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Retain all application logs in an encrypted Cloud Storage bucket with restricted access.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Use Sensitive Data Protection to inspect log entries in real time and redact PII before storing them in Cloud Logging.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two requirements must be satisfied together: retain application logging and protect sensitive content. Redaction addresses the content while preserving useful diagnostic information. Sampling reduces volume, disabling logging removes observability, and encrypted storage does not remove sensitive values.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Collect the application’s logs in Cloud Logging” — Hard constraint:</strong> Logging must remain enabled, with Cloud Logging as the destination.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Preventing sensitive user data from being exposed” — Hard constraint:</strong> Protection must address the information contained in log entries.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Personally identifiable information” — Technical signal:</strong> Sensitive-data detection and de-identification are relevant capabilities.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Sensitive Data Protection supports synchronous content de-identification through the DLP API. Configure the relevant information types and redaction transformations, then send the sanitized content to Cloud Logging. Its text-redaction functionality can replace detected sensitive values with placeholders. </p>\n<p><strong>Implementation boundary:</strong> This requires integration into the application’s logging path or a preprocessing component. Enabling Sensitive Data Protection alone does not sanitize GKE logs. GKE’s default logging agent collects application output, so redaction must occur before unsanitized content reaches that collection path. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong> Sampling selects a subset of log entries to control volume. Retained entries can still contain PII because sampling does not redact their contents.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Disabling application log collection conflicts with the requirement to collect those logs in Cloud Logging. It also removes diagnostic evidence without changing what the application writes locally.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> This substitutes Cloud Storage for the required logging destination and retains the sensitive content. Encryption and restricted access protect stored data, but authorized readers still receive decrypted content. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Do not confuse encryption with redaction. Encryption protects stored information from unauthorized access; redaction removes sensitive values from the content that log readers receive.</p>\n<p>Also, interpret B as a preprocessing integration—not an automatic Cloud Logging redaction setting.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Privacy-preserving logging combines data minimization, sanitization, and access control. Avoid recording unnecessary personal information, redact sensitive content that must pass through the logging path, and restrict access to the resulting logs. Detection rules must cover the application’s actual sensitive-data types.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>An application logger can sanitize a message containing a customer’s email address before writing it to standard output. GKE then forwards the sanitized entry to Cloud Logging, preserving request status and diagnostic context while keeping the original address out of the stored message.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>To retain useful logs without exposing PII, sanitize sensitive content before ingestion.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question requires both logging and PII protection, select content redaction before storage. Sampling, disabling collection, and encryption each address a different concern.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/sensitive-data-protection/docs/redacting-sensitive-data\"><strong>Google Cloud — Redacting sensitive data from text</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Sensitive Data Protection redaction, information-type configuration, and replacement of detected values.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/about-logs\"><strong>Google Cloud — About GKE logs</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Application log collection, the default logging agent, and the need to sanitize content before collection.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/view/logging-query-language\"><strong>Google Cloud — Logging query language</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Sampling selects a fraction of log entries rather than redacting sensitive fields.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/storage/docs/encryption/default-keys\"><strong>Google Cloud — Standard Cloud Storage encryption</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Encryption at rest and automatic decryption for authorized readers.</p>","lessons":["d4l3"]},"e3q3":{"id":"e3q3","exam":3,"n":3,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your team is preparing to release a new API on Cloud Run. The application uses an OpenTelemetry agent to export distributed traces to Cloud Trace for analyzing request latency. However, trace collection has been inconsistent. Which configuration change should you make to address this issue?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Configure an HTTP health check for the Cloud Run service.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Enable instance-based billing to allocate CPU throughout each instance’s lifetime.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Increase the Cloud Run service’s CPU limit from 2 vCPUs to 4 vCPUs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Enable request-based billing to allocate CPU only during request processing.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Enable instance-based billing to allocate CPU throughout each instance’s lifetime.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The relevant distinction is when the tracing agent can execute. OpenTelemetry can perform background work after an HTTP response has been returned. Request-based CPU allocation can interrupt that work, making instance-based billing the best configuration among the options. Google explicitly identifies OpenTelemetry agents as a background-processing use case.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Cloud Run” — Technical signal:</strong> CPU availability depends on the service’s billing configuration.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“OpenTelemetry agent” — Technical signal:</strong> Telemetry processing can require background execution.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Trace collection has been inconsistent” — Problem signal:</strong> Investigate the agent’s execution conditions before increasing processing capacity.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Instance-based billing keeps CPU allocated throughout the container instance’s lifecycle, including periods without active requests. This allows the agent’s background processing and trace exports to continue after responses finish.</p>\n<p>This setting was previously called <strong>CPU always allocated</strong> ; instance-based billing is the current terminology. It does not prevent autoscaling or instance termination, so normal shutdown handling remains necessary. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong> HTTP health checks assess whether an instance can start, serve traffic, or needs restarting. They do not directly resolve background trace-export execution. They would fit a container-health problem.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Additional vCPUs can help CPU-bound workloads, but increasing capacity does not change when CPU is available. The scenario provides no evidence of CPU saturation. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> Request-based billing suits work completed within request handling. After a response, CPU access can be disabled or severely limited, which can suspend the agent’s background activity. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Do not confuse <strong>CPU capacity</strong> with <strong>CPU availability</strong>. Increasing the CPU limit cannot compensate for CPU being unavailable between requests. If the diagnosed problem were sustained CPU saturation, increasing vCPUs could be appropriate.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A serverless execution environment can keep a container in memory while restricting its ability to execute background code. Any asynchronous work that continues after the response must be compatible with that execution model. Cloud Run recommends instance-based billing for background activity. </p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>After an API response completes, the tracing agent might still need to export telemetry. Keeping CPU available lets that work proceed between requests. Evaluate the billing impact because instances are charged throughout their lifetimes, including idle periods. </p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For Cloud Run agents that work after responses, keep CPU available between requests.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Run plus background OpenTelemetry processing points to instance-based billing, formerly CPU always allocated. Choose more vCPUs when evidence identifies a capacity bottleneck.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/configuring/billing-settings\"><strong>Google Cloud — Billing settings for services</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Current terminology, CPU allocation behavior, billing implications, and OpenTelemetry background execution.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/tips/general\"><strong>Google Cloud — General development tips</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Background activity restrictions under request-based billing and the recommended configuration.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/container-contract\"><strong>Google Cloud — Container runtime contract</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: The distinction between configured CPU capacity and CPU allocation timing.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/configuring/healthchecks\"><strong>Google Cloud — Configure container health checks for services</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Health-check purposes and why option A addresses a different concern.</p>","lessons":["d4l10"]},"e3q4":{"id":"e3q4","exam":3,"n":4,"domain":1,"html":"<p>Your company handles highly sensitive user information. You are designing Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) infrastructure to host several applications in development and production environments. The architecture must prevent unauthorized access to data between applications while keeping administrative overhead low. Which design should you choose?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Deploy a single organization-wide cluster, with a separate namespace for each application and environment combination.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Deploy a dedicated cluster for each application, with separate namespaces for its development and production workloads.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Deploy a separate cluster for each environment, with each application assigned its own namespace within that cluster.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Deploy a single organization-wide cluster, with a separate namespace for each application.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Deploy a separate cluster for each environment, with each application assigned its own namespace within that cluster.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Balance environment separation with manageable application isolation. Separate clusters keep development and production apart, while application namespaces avoid the administrative burden of maintaining a cluster for every application. This combines Google’s recommendation to separate environment clusters with its namespace-based enterprise multi-tenancy model.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Highly sensitive user information” — Hard constraint:</strong> The design must support enforced access boundaries.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Development and production environments” — Technical signal:</strong> These environments warrant separate operational and security controls.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Between applications” — Hard constraint:</strong> Applications need individually scoped access policies.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Administrative overhead low” — Preference:</strong> Avoid unnecessary proliferation of clusters.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Separate development and production clusters allow independent cluster configuration and administration. Within each cluster, namespaces provide scopes for application-specific permissions and policies, reducing the number of clusters to manage. </p>\n<p><strong>Namespaces alone do not prevent unauthorized access.</strong> Enforce least-privilege Kubernetes RBAC and network policies, and restrict application permissions to underlying data. By default, Pods can communicate across namespaces; network policies must explicitly restrict unwanted traffic. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Separate namespaces can support application and environment policies, but development and production still share a control plane and cluster-wide components. This provides less environment separation than the recommended layout. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Dedicated application clusters provide stronger separation between applications, but increase cluster-management overhead. Development and production also remain together within each application’s cluster. This model is more appropriate when applications require dedicated cluster boundaries. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> This separates applications logically but provides no explicit separation between their development and production deployments. It offers the least suitable environment structure for this sensitive-data scenario. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Do not equate creating namespaces with enforcing security. Namespaces organize resources and scope policies; RBAC and network policies enforce the relevant access restrictions. Also, choosing the fewest clusters does not automatically provide the best balance of security and management effort.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Choose isolation boundaries according to trust and operational requirements. Clusters separate control planes and cluster-wide administration; namespaces support policy-controlled sharing within a cluster. Workloads requiring stronger isolation than shared-cluster policies provide may need dedicated clusters. </p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Create development and production clusters containing separate namespaces for billing and customer-profile applications. Apply namespace-scoped permissions and allow only required service communication. This preserves shared cluster administration while reducing opportunities for unintended cross-application access.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For shared enterprise GKE, separate environments by cluster and enforce application boundaries with namespace policies.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When the question combines development/production separation, application isolation, and low management overhead, choose one cluster per environment with application namespaces. Security still depends on correctly enforced policies.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/best-practices-continuous-integration-delivery-kubernetes\"><strong>Google Cloud — Best practices for continuous integration and delivery to Google Kubernetes Engine</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Separate clusters for development and production; distinctions affecting options A, B, and D.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/multitenancy-overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cluster multi-tenancy</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Namespace-based enterprise sharing, reduced management overhead, policy enforcement, and isolation limitations.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/network-policy\"><strong>Google Cloud — Control communication between Pods and Services using network policies</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Default Pod connectivity and enforcing communication restrictions between namespaces.</p>","lessons":["d1l9"]},"e3q5":{"id":"e3q5","exam":3,"n":5,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Following several production incidents, your company is building a Cloud Monitoring dashboard to investigate service failures. You need the dashboard to help distinguish problems originating in your service from disruptions affecting the Google Cloud services it depends on. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Define a log-based metric for Google Cloud service errors and display it on the dashboard.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Add a logs widget that displays system-error entries from Cloud Logging.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Configure an alerting policy based on system-error metrics.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Turn on Personalized Service Health event annotations for the dashboard.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Turn on Personalized Service Health event annotations for the dashboard.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The dashboard needs Google Cloud incident context alongside your service’s telemetry. Personalized Service Health annotations provide that context, allowing you to compare application failures with provider-reported disruptions. Generic error metrics, logs, and alerts describe symptoms without automatically establishing their origin. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Cloud Monitoring dashboard” — Technical signal:</strong> The solution should integrate incident information into the dashboard.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Distinguish problems originating in your service” — Hard constraint:</strong> Observing errors alone is insufficient; dependency-health context is needed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Google Cloud services it depends on” — Technical signal:</strong> Personalized Service Health supplies relevant Google Cloud disruption information.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Monitoring queries the Service Health API and displays relevant Google Cloud incidents as annotations on metric charts. These annotations show incident timing and duration, with details about affected services and locations. This lets you correlate service degradation with dependency disruptions. </p>\n<p>Enable the Service Health API and ensure viewers have permission to read its events, such as through <code>roles/servicehealth.viewer</code>. Select the events through the dashboard’s <strong>Annotations</strong> menu and pin the selection to persist it.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> An error metric helps quantify failures recorded in logs. However, a generic count does not automatically provide the provider-reported incident context needed to distinguish application problems from Google Cloud disruptions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> System-error logs support detailed investigation, but a generic logs widget does not directly overlay relevant Google Cloud incident timelines. It complements Service Health annotations. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> An alerting policy detects configured conditions and can notify responders. It does not, by itself, add the required dependency-health context to the dashboard. Personalized Service Health supports separate alerting workflows when notifications are also needed. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>An error involving a Google Cloud dependency is not automatically evidence of a provider incident. Use Service Health annotations for incident correlation. Option A would fit a requirement to count particular logged errors.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Effective troubleshooting combines application symptoms with dependency-health events. Matching timestamps, affected services, and locations helps narrow the investigation. Correlation does not prove causation, and the absence of an annotation does not independently prove that your application caused the failure.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>When request errors increase, compare the spike with the Service Health annotations. If a relevant incident affects the same dependency and location, inspect its details alongside your application telemetry. This can focus the investigation and reduce unnecessary application changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Use Service Health annotations to compare application symptoms with Google Cloud incident timelines.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a dashboard must distinguish application failures from Google Cloud dependency disruptions, enable Personalized Service Health annotations. Use logs and metrics to investigate the resulting correlation.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/dashboards/event-types\"><strong>Google Cloud — Event types</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Google Cloud incident annotations, relevance filtering, affected services, and incident duration.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/dashboards/show-events\"><strong>Google Cloud — Show events on a dashboard</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Annotation configuration, persistence, API prerequisites, permissions, and distinctions between event sources.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/service-health/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Personalized Service Health overview</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Project-relevant provider incidents and complementary logging and alerting integrations.</p>","lessons":["d4l9"]},"e3q6":{"id":"e3q6","exam":3,"n":6,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your company uses several Cloud Deploy delivery pipelines to release applications across different environments. The development team currently has no access to these pipelines. You must grant the team access exclusively to the development delivery pipeline while following Google-recommended access-control practices. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Grant the team <code>roles/clouddeploy.operator</code> through the Google Cloud console, and configure deny conditions for every pipeline except the development delivery pipeline.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Create a custom IAM role containing all <code>clouddeploy.automations.*</code> permissions through the Google Cloud console, and grant it through an allow policy scoped to the development delivery pipeline.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Define a policy file granting the team <code>roles/clouddeploy.operator</code>, and apply it to the development target.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Define a policy file granting the team <code>roles/clouddeploy.developer</code>, and apply it to the development delivery pipeline.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Define a policy file granting the team</strong><code>**roles/clouddeploy.developer**</code><strong>, and apply it to the development delivery pipeline.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Select both the appropriate permissions and the correct resource scope. The team needs access to one delivery pipeline, so attach the role binding directly to that pipeline. Google documents this exact approach using <code>roles/clouddeploy.developer</code> and a pipeline-level IAM policy. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Exclusively to the development delivery pipeline” — Hard constraint:</strong> Scope the grant to that pipeline.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Currently has no access” — Technical signal:</strong> A narrowly scoped grant is sufficient; existing broad access does not need to be counteracted.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Google-recommended access-control practices” — Preference:</strong> Use a suitable predefined role with the required resource scope.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Deploy supports IAM policies on individual delivery pipelines. Bind the development team’s group to <code>roles/clouddeploy.developer</code> in the policy file, then apply it to the development pipeline using <code>gcloud deploy delivery-pipelines set-iam-policy</code>. This grant does not extend to sibling pipelines. </p>\n<p>The Developer role provides deployment-configuration and release-related permissions without granting operational target-management permissions. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A broader grant followed by exclusions adds unnecessary access-control complexity. Since the team starts without access, grant permissions directly on the required pipeline. The Operator role itself is not inherently invalid for pipeline access; the proposed scoping approach is the problem. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Incorrect:</strong> <code>clouddeploy.automations.*</code> permissions manage automation resources. They do not supply the delivery-pipeline permissions required here. Restricting the policy’s scope cannot compensate for selecting the wrong permissions. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> Applying an Operator binding to a target grants access to configure that target, including updating or deleting it. It does not grant the requested access to the delivery pipeline. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>A <strong>development target</strong> and a <strong>development delivery pipeline</strong> are different resources. Choose the resource named in the requirement. Option C would fit a request to administer the development target.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An IAM role determines <strong>which actions</strong> a principal can perform; the resource receiving the binding determines <strong>where</strong> those permissions apply. Least privilege requires both an appropriate role and an appropriately narrow scope.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Bind a developer group to the development pipeline’s IAM policy. Team membership then controls who receives that access, while production pipelines receive no additional grants. This simplifies administration and preserves environment boundaries.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Match the role to the work and attach its binding to the exact resource requiring access.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For access to one Cloud Deploy pipeline, use a pipeline-level IAM binding. Here, select the predefined Developer role and apply the policy to the development delivery pipeline—not its target.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/securing/iam\"><strong>Google Cloud — Use IAM to restrict Cloud Deploy access</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Pipeline-specific Developer bindings, alternative Operator bindings, and the distinction between pipeline and target access.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/iam-roles-permissions\"><strong>Google Cloud — IAM roles and permissions</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Developer and Operator capabilities and the purpose of automation permissions.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/deploy/delivery-pipelines/set-iam-policy\"><strong>Google Cloud CLI — gcloud deploy delivery-pipelines set-iam-policy</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Applying a policy file to a specific delivery pipeline.</p>","lessons":["d2l2"]},"e3q7":{"id":"e3q7","exam":3,"n":7,"domain":1,"html":"<p>You are provisioning development environments for your company’s developers. Every developer must receive the same IDE setup, and the environments must not be exposed to public networks. You need the <strong>MOST</strong> cost-effective approach that preserves developer productivity. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Provision Compute Engine VMs with public IP addresses, configure Public NAT, and schedule the VMs to shut down.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Provision Compute Engine VMs without public IP addresses and schedule the VMs to shut down.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create a private Cloud Workstations cluster and configure <code>idleTimeout</code> in the workstation configuration.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Create a private Cloud Workstations cluster and configure <code>runningTimeout</code> in the workstation configuration.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Create a private Cloud Workstations cluster and configure</strong><code>**idleTimeout**</code><strong>in the workstation configuration.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The solution must combine consistent developer tooling, private access, and efficient resource use. Cloud Workstations configurations standardize the environment, while a private cluster restricts access. An inactivity timeout reduces unused compute time without imposing a shutdown solely because a fixed runtime or scheduled hour has been reached.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Same IDE setup” — Hard constraint:</strong> Use a reusable configuration that standardizes developer environments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Not … exposed to public networks” — Hard constraint:</strong> Provide private workstation access.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“MOST cost-effective” — Optimization requirement:</strong> Reduce unnecessary resource consumption.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Preserves developer productivity” — Hard constraint:</strong> Prefer activity-aware shutdown over stopping an actively used environment.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A workstation configuration defines the IDE, container image, machine settings, and other shared environment properties. The <code>idleTimeout</code> field determines how long a workstation can remain without user traffic before the configured idle action occurs; stopping is the default action. </p>\n<p>Private clusters prevent clients from connecting through the public internet. Complete the private design by disabling public IP addresses on workstation VMs and providing the required Private Service Connect endpoint and private connectivity for developers. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Assigning public IP addresses conflicts with the required private environment design. Adding Public NAT does not remove those addresses or make this equivalent to private workstation access. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Private VMs can host standardized IDEs, but require additional environment management. Scheduled shutdowns follow configured times rather than developer activity, so they can interrupt work or leave unused VMs running until the next shutdown. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> <code>runningTimeout</code> stops a workstation after a specified runtime, even if the developer remains active. It is useful for bounding session duration and facilitating updates, but is less suited to the inactivity-based cost control required here. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The documented inactivity parameter is <code>**idleTimeout**</code>; its spelling has been corrected in option C. Do not confuse it with <code>runningTimeout</code>, which applies regardless of activity.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An idle timeout responds to inactivity; a runtime limit responds to elapsed time; a schedule responds to the clock. Choose the mechanism that matches the operational goal. Set an appropriate inactivity threshold because background tasks without user interaction can still be considered idle.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Give developers workstations created from one configuration and automatically stop abandoned sessions. Developers retain consistent tooling while the organization reduces idle compute usage and repetitive setup work.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Use an idle timeout to reclaim unused development compute while preserving active sessions.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Consistent IDEs, private access, and activity-aware cost control point to a private Cloud Workstations cluster with <code>idleTimeout</code>. Fixed schedules and runtime limits can stop developers during active work.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/workstations/docs/reference/rest/v1/projects.locations.workstationClusters.workstationConfigs\"><strong>Google Cloud — WorkstationConfig REST resource</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Configuration templates, IDE standardization, <code>idleTimeout</code>, idle actions, and <code>runningTimeout</code>.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/workstations/docs/configure-vpc-service-controls-private-clusters\"><strong>Google Cloud — Configure VPC Service Controls and private clusters</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Private cluster access, disabling workstation public IP addresses, and private connectivity requirements.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/schedule-instance-start-stop\"><strong>Google Cloud — Scheduling a VM instance to start and stop</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Time-based VM shutdown behavior and its distinction from inactivity-based shutdown.</p>","lessons":["d1l11"]},"e3q8":{"id":"e3q8","exam":3,"n":8,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You operate Service A, a public-facing API with a response-time SLO of 500 ms. Service A synchronously calls Service B, an internal API that collects request information for transactions handled by Service A.</p>\n<p>Under heavy load, Service B becomes unreliable, returning HTTP 500 errors or causing connection timeouts. You need to protect Service A’s users from these failures. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Increase the capacity of the request queue preceding Service A’s worker thread pool.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Retry failed calls to Service B using exponential backoff.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Implement a circuit breaker with a fallback that stores the request data in a database.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Apply rate limiting in Service A to restrict outbound requests to Service B.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Implement a circuit breaker with a fallback that stores the request data in a database.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Prioritize the public API’s response-time objective. Repeatedly waiting for an unreliable supporting service can consume Service A’s latency budget and processing resources. A circuit breaker interrupts calls likely to fail, while a database fallback preserves the information Service B would normally collect. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Response-time SLO of 500 ms” — Performance constraint:</strong> Additional waiting and retries must fit a tight latency budget.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Synchronously calls” — Dependency signal:</strong> Service B’s delays directly affect Service A.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“HTTP 500 errors or … timeouts” — Failure signal:</strong> The dependency requires failure isolation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Collects request information” — Functional signal:</strong> A durable alternative can preserve this information while B is unavailable, assuming collection can be deferred.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A circuit breaker monitors dependency calls and opens when configured failure or slow-call thresholds are reached. While open, it rejects further calls quickly. After a waiting period, limited calls test whether the dependency has recovered. </p>\n<p>Service A handles rejected or failed calls through a fallback that writes the request information to a database. This allows the primary transaction path to continue without requiring immediate success from Service B.</p>\n<p>This solution assumes deferred collection is acceptable. Configure bounded call timeouts and a sufficiently fast fallback; a circuit breaker alone does not guarantee the 500 ms SLO. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Incorrect for This Requirement:</strong> A larger queue allows more requests to wait while workers remain occupied. It does not resolve Service B’s failures and can increase latency and memory consumption, working against the response-time objective. </p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Exponential backoff helps with transient failures, but synchronous retries introduce additional attempts and waiting within Service A’s limited response budget. Retries can also amplify an overloaded backend’s workload. Bounded retries may complement the solution, particularly during deferred processing. </p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Rate limiting can reduce overload and protect backend capacity. However, admitted requests can still encounter timeouts or errors, and rate limiting alone provides no alternative destination for their information. It is a useful complementary control. </p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>A circuit breaker <strong>does not itself store request data</strong>. Option C must be understood as a breaker combined with application-defined database fallback logic. The breaker detects and isolates failure; the fallback determines how Service A continues operating. </p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Circuit breakers have three principal states: closed permits normal calls, open rejects calls, and half-open permits recovery probes. They isolate unhealthy dependencies. Timeouts bound individual waits, while fallbacks provide acceptable behavior when the dependency cannot be used. </p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>When B becomes unhealthy, persist its request records through the fallback and process them later. Use transaction identifiers to prevent duplicate collection during replay, and test fallback latency under load. The alternative path must remain dependable when B fails.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>An unreliable supporting dependency and a tight response budget suggest a circuit breaker with a safe fallback.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Choose failure isolation when synchronous dependency failures threaten a public API’s latency. Combine the circuit breaker with bounded timeouts and a valid fallback; larger queues or retries alone do not provide that protection.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/circuit-breaker\"><strong>Microsoft Architecture Center — Circuit Breaker pattern</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Failure isolation, fast rejection, fallback handling, and distinction from retries.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://resilience4j.readme.io/docs/circuitbreaker\"><strong>Resilience4j — CircuitBreaker</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Failure thresholds, slow-call detection, and recovery states.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/addressing-cascading-failures\"><strong>Google SRE — Addressing Cascading Failures</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Queue latency, retry amplification, and deadline management.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/handling-overload/\"><strong>Google SRE — Handling Overload</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Capacity protection, throttling, and graceful degradation.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e3q9":{"id":"e3q9","exam":3,"n":9,"domain":1,"html":"<p>You administer Google Cloud resources for a healthcare company whose regulatory requirements mandate creating all resources in United States regions. When you attempt to create a Secret Manager secret, the operation returns this error:</p>\n<pre><code>      1. Constraint constraints/gcp.resourceLocations violated for [orgpolicy:projects/000000] attempting to create a secret in [global]\n</code></pre>\n<p>How should you resolve the failure while preserving the required location restrictions?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Delete the organization policy identified in the error message.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Configure automatic replication when creating the secret.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Configure user-managed replication when creating the secret.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Update the organization policy to permit the <code>global</code> location.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Configure user-managed replication when creating the secret.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Preserve the location restriction and change the secret’s replication configuration. Automatic replication requires <code>global</code> to be permitted. User-managed replication lets you explicitly select US locations, provided every selected location is allowed by the organization policy. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“United States regions” — Hard constraint:</strong> Every selected replica location must satisfy the geographic requirement.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><code>**constraints/gcp.resourceLocations**</code><strong>— Technical signal:</strong> The failure concerns resource placement, not IAM authorization.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><code>**[global]**</code><strong>— Technical signal:</strong> The attempted configuration conflicts with the permitted locations.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>User-managed replication stores the secret payload in locations you specify. Create the secret with replicas only in approved US regions.</p>\n<p>Secret Manager evaluates all selected replica locations against the resource-location policy. Creation succeeds only when every location is permitted. This resolves the placement conflict without weakening the policy. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Removing the policy does not configure compliant secret placement. It attempts to bypass the control enforcing the company’s location restrictions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Incorrect:</strong> Automatic replication requires the organization policy to allow <code>global</code>, so it does not resolve the reported failure. Its unrestricted replication also does not guarantee US-only payload storage. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Allowing <code>global</code> would permit automatic replication, but would not restrict replicas to US regions. Making secret creation succeed is insufficient unless the resulting placement remains compliant. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Do not relax an organization policy simply because it blocks resource creation. First select a resource configuration that satisfies it. Also, choosing user-managed replication is insufficient unless <strong>every configured replica location</strong> is approved.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Organization policies establish deployment guardrails; service-specific settings determine how a resource satisfies them. For Secret Manager, replication policies control secret-payload storage locations.</p>\n<p>Storage placement is distinct from broader processing and access requirements. Secret Manager’s regional service provides additional regional controls when requirements extend to data at rest, in use, and in transit. </p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Create an application credential with replicas confined to approved US regions. The existing policy continues rejecting disallowed placements, reducing the risk of accidental storage outside the permitted geography.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>When a location policy rejects <code>global</code>, explicitly place every secret replica in an approved region.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For this Secret Manager error, choose user-managed replication with permitted US locations. Keep the organization policy intact; enabling <code>global</code> would not enforce US-only replication.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/secret-manager/docs/choosing-replication\"><strong>Google Cloud — Choose a secret replication policy</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Automatic replication’s <code>global</code> requirement, user-managed replica placement, and validation of every selected location.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/organization-policy/reference/restrict-locations-supported-services\"><strong>Google Cloud — Services that support restricting resource locations</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Secret Manager’s enforcement of the resource-location organization policy.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/secret-manager/docs/secret-manager-secrets-comparison\"><strong>Google Cloud — Compare global and regional service</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: The distinction between replica placement and stricter regional data-residency controls.</p>","lessons":["d1l2"]},"e3q10":{"id":"e3q10","exam":3,"n":10,"domain":1,"html":"<p>You are using Code OSS in Cloud Workstations to develop a Node.js utility that serves a simple web page. The application is running on port 3000, and you have verified that the required firewall rules are configured. You now need to view the page from your local computer while following Google-recommended security practices. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Open the page using a browser hosted on a bastion VM.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Run <code>gcloud compute start-iap-tunnel</code> against the VM underlying the workstation.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Enable public IP addresses in the workstation configuration.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Open the application’s preview link in the Code OSS panel.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Open the application’s preview link in the Code OSS panel.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The application is already running, and the firewall configuration has been verified. The remaining requirement is secure browser access from your computer. Cloud Workstations provides built-in web access, making the editor’s preview the appropriate choice without introducing another VM or direct access path. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Code OSS in Cloud Workstations” — Technical signal:</strong> The development environment supports application previews.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Required firewall rules are configured” — Exclusion:</strong> Additional firewall configuration is not the requested solution.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Following Google-recommended security practices” — Hard constraint:</strong> Prefer the managed, authenticated workstation access path.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Workstations lets your local browser access HTTP applications running on workstation ports. The base editor redirects application links to the appropriate workstation URL. Access requires authentication and the <code>workstations.workstations.use</code> permission, included in the Cloud Workstations User role.</p>\n<p>Port 3000 is supported by default. If administrators restrict access through <code>allowedPorts</code>, that configuration must permit the application’s port. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A suitably connected bastion could host a browser, but it introduces another machine and access workflow. The existing workstation preview already provides the required browser access.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> IAP TCP forwarding supports connections to Compute Engine instances when appropriately configured. However, targeting the underlying VM introduces a separate access mechanism. Google recommends restricting access to underlying workstation resources and using the workstation gateway. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Public IP addresses are unnecessary for the managed preview and do not themselves provide authenticated application access. Google’s security guidance supports restricting public connectivity when appropriate. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Do not assume that viewing a remotely hosted development page requires a public IP address. The workstation’s authenticated web endpoint provides browser access without requiring direct public access to its VM.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Network reachability and user authorization are separate controls. A managed development gateway can route requests to an application while enforcing identity-based access. Opening additional network paths does not automatically improve that security model.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Keep the Node.js server running on port 3000 and open its preview from Code OSS. You can test the page in your local browser while retaining the workstation’s access controls and avoiding another VM to administer.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For a web application running in Cloud Workstations, use its authenticated preview before adding network access paths.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a Cloud Workstations application already runs successfully, use the editor’s preview for local browser testing. Bastions, VM-level tunnels, and public IP changes add unnecessary configuration for this scenario.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/workstations/docs/access-http-servers-running-on-workstations\"><strong>Google Cloud — Connect to HTTP servers running on a workstation</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Browser access, editor link forwarding, authentication, IAM permissions, and allowed ports.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/workstations/docs/set-up-security-best-practices\"><strong>Google Cloud — Set up security best practices</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Restricting direct VM access, limiting underlying-resource permissions, and controlling public connectivity.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/compute/start-iap-tunnel\"><strong>Google Cloud CLI — gcloud compute start-iap-tunnel</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: The purpose of IAP TCP forwarding and its distinction from workstation web preview.</p>","lessons":["d1l11"]},"e3q11":{"id":"e3q11","exam":3,"n":11,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You are responsible for your company’s main revenue-generating application. Its error budget policy suspends production deployments when the service approaches a breach of its service level objective (SLO). Recent service problems have exhausted the available error budget. A pending release includes a feature urgently needed by your largest customer, and you have been informed that all unit tests have passed. How should you proceed?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Wait for the error budget to be replenished before deploying the feature.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Repeat the unit tests and deploy the feature if they succeed.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Proceed with the feature deployment immediately.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Introduce the feature to a subset of users, then expand the rollout if no errors are reported.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Wait for the error budget to be replenished before deploying the feature.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The deployment freeze is the decisive constraint. The service has exhausted its error budget, so the established policy takes precedence over customer urgency and successful testing. Neither repeating tests nor reducing rollout size changes whether this feature release is permitted.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Suspends production deployments” — Hard constraint:</strong> The organization has already defined its response to reliability risk.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Exhausted the available error budget” — Technical signal:</strong> The service has reached the condition requiring the freeze.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Feature urgently needed” — Business pressure:</strong> Urgency does not establish an exception to the policy.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“All unit tests have passed” — Supporting evidence:</strong> Testing increases confidence but does not replenish the budget.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>An error budget policy translates measured reliability into release decisions. When the budget is depleted, postponing feature releases limits additional change risk and creates room for reliability improvements. Google’s guidance freezes changes until sufficient budget becomes available again. </p>\n<p>Google’s example policy permits exceptions for P0 issues and security fixes. The scenario describes a customer feature, with no qualifying exception identified.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> Passing the tests again does not lift the deployment freeze. Unit tests also cannot establish that every production interaction will behave correctly; some defects emerge only under real traffic. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Immediate deployment disregards the explicit error budget policy. The customer’s importance does not independently authorize bypassing that policy. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> A canary limits the number of users initially exposed to a change, but it remains a production deployment. It is useful when releases are permitted; it does not override an active freeze. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>A gradual rollout is normally a sound reliability practice, making D tempting. However, rollout safety and release permission are separate decisions. If the error budget had recovered and the freeze were lifted, a monitored canary would be an appropriate deployment strategy.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An error budget represents the failures permitted by an SLO over its measurement period. Its policy determines how remaining budget affects engineering priorities. Recovery depends on the defined measurement window and release criteria; successful tests do not erase previously observed failures. </p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Keep the tested feature ready while the team addresses the recent reliability problems. Once the policy permits feature releases again, deploy progressively and monitor the result. This protects the application’s existing revenue while preserving the work needed for the customer’s feature.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>A safer rollout reduces deployment risk, but it does not lift an error-budget freeze.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>An exhausted error budget plus an explicit deployment freeze means postponing feature releases. Customer urgency, passing tests, and canaries do not replace the policy’s release criteria.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/error-budget-policy/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — Example Error Budget Policy</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Deployment freezes, reliability prioritization, and exceptions for P0 issues or security fixes.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-best-practices/\"><strong>Google SRE Book — A Collection of Best Practices for Production Services</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Freezing feature changes when the budget is spent and resuming after budget recovery.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/canarying-releases/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — Canarying Releases</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Canary risk reduction, production exposure, and limitations of pre-production testing.</p>","lessons":["d3l3"]},"e3q12":{"id":"e3q12","exam":3,"n":12,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your company is adopting Cloud Deploy to deliver a web service running on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). The application currently has no automated tests. The Quality Assurance (QA) team must manually validate each new release before that version handles any production traffic. Which delivery pipeline design should you implement?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Configure a delivery pipeline with one stage and a standard deployment strategy.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Configure a delivery pipeline with one stage and a canary deployment strategy.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Configure a delivery pipeline with two stages and a canary deployment strategy.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Configure a delivery pipeline with two stages and a standard deployment strategy.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Configure a delivery pipeline with two stages and a standard deployment strategy.</strong></p>\n<p>D is the most direct design: deploy to a QA environment, complete manual validation, and then permit production deployment. However, the requirements do not strictly exclude C if the same QA gate precedes its production canary.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The decisive requirement is validation <strong>before any production exposure</strong>. Provide a non-production target where QA can exercise the release, followed by a production target protected by manual approval. Standard deployment supports this progression without introducing percentage-based rollout phases. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Manually validate each new release” — Hard constraint:</strong> A human decision must control progression to production.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Before … any production traffic” — Sequence constraint:</strong> QA must complete validation before production exposure begins.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“No automated tests” — Technical signal:</strong> The pipeline must accommodate manual testing rather than depend on an existing automated verification suite.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Implement the two stages as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>QA target:</strong> Deploy the release using the standard strategy and perform manual testing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Production target:</strong> Set <code>requireApproval: true</code>. An authorized reviewer approves the rollout only after QA succeeds.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Cloud Deploy supports ordered target progression and approval before deploying into a target. Two stages alone do not enforce QA completion—the approval gate is essential. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A single stage can require approval, but it does not provide both a deployed QA environment and a subsequent production target within this pipeline. Testing would need an additional arrangement outside the proposed progression.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> Using a production canary as the initial validation mechanism exposes some production traffic before QA completes. Canary phases do not substitute for a preceding QA environment. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Two stages can provide QA followed by production, and a production canary could begin after QA approval. This is feasible, but adds progressive rollout behavior that the scenario does not request; D directly implements the stated workflow. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Distinguish <strong>testing before production</strong> from <strong>testing with limited production exposure</strong>. A canary addresses the latter.</p>\n<p>The absence of automated tests does not prohibit canary deployment. Consequently, D is the straightforward intended answer, while C remains technically defensible with an appropriate approval gate.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Pipeline stages describe progression between deployment targets. Deployment strategies describe how a release reaches a target. Approval gates determine whether that deployment may proceed. These are separate controls that can be combined.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Deploy a release to <code>qa</code>, let testers verify its critical workflows, and approve the pending <code>production</code> rollout only after acceptance. If QA finds a defect, withhold approval so the existing production version continues serving users.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Validate in QA first, gate production with approval, and choose rollout strategy separately.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Manual QA before any production traffic points to QA and production stages with an approval gate. Standard deployment meets that requirement directly; canary deployment is an additional rollout choice.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/config-files\"><strong>Google Cloud — Configuration schema reference</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Ordered stages, deployment targets, and standard versus canary configuration.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/promote-release\"><strong>Google Cloud — Promote your release and manage approvals</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Manual approval gates, <code>requireApproval: true</code>, and approving or rejecting production rollouts.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/deployment-strategies\"><strong>Google Cloud — Use a deployment strategy</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Standard deployment behavior and canary deployments that progressively expose users to a release.</p>","lessons":["d2l4"]},"e3q13":{"id":"e3q13","exam":3,"n":13,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You are rolling out a new application version to a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster spanning multiple zones. Most of the rollout appears healthy, but some Pods in one zone are showing increased error rates. You need to revert the update only for the affected Pods while minimizing disruption to users. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Reduce the number of Pods in the affected zone, then deploy the new application version again.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Drain the affected nodes, then deploy the new application version on the remaining nodes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Restore the Deployment’s previous Pod template and use a rolling update to replace the Pods in the affected zone.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Run <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> for the entire Deployment, then redeploy the new version while excluding the affected zone.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Restore the Deployment’s previous Pod template and use a rolling update to replace the Pods in the affected zone.</strong></p>\n<p>However, C requires an unstated prerequisite: <strong>the affected Pods must belong to an independently managed Deployment scoped to that zone</strong>. Changing a Deployment’s template applies to its managed replicas across all zones—not just the zone experiencing errors. </p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The decisive requirement is rollback scope. A rolling update controls how Pods are replaced; it does not automatically restrict replacement to a geographic zone. First establish whether the affected zone has a separate Deployment. Without that boundary, C cannot provide the requested selective rollback.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Only for the affected Pods” — Hard constraint:</strong> Healthy portions must remain outside the rollback scope.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“One zone” — Technical signal:</strong> Placement and Deployment ownership must be distinguished.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Minimizing disruption” — Preference:</strong> Replace affected workloads gradually while preserving sufficient serving capacity.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>C describes an appropriate rollback mechanism <strong>when the Deployment already manages only the affected zone’s workload</strong>. Restore its previous template and use controlled rolling replacement.</p>\n<p>An architecture supporting this can combine separate Deployments with zone-specific node selectors or required node affinity. The conclusion follows from combining independent Deployment management with explicit Pod-placement constraints. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Incorrect:</strong> Scaling down and redeploying the new version does not restore the previous application version. It can reduce capacity without performing the required rollback.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Draining evicts workloads and makes nodes unavailable for new scheduling. It supports node maintenance or evacuation, but does not revert the application version and can affect additional workloads on those nodes. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> It explicitly rolls back the entire Deployment, including healthy replicas, before introducing another deployment. That exceeds the requested selective scope. Deployment rollback restores the selected Deployment’s previous template. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Rolling</strong> describes replacement behavior; it does not mean <strong>zone-selective</strong>. C becomes valid if the question specifies that each zone has its own independently managed Deployment.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Availability zones determine workload placement. Deployments determine the desired application version for their managed replicas. Selective recovery requires those management boundaries to match the desired recovery scope.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>With separate Deployments for zones A, B, and C, you can restore the previous version only in zone B while preserving the other Deployments. If one Deployment spans all three zones, reverting its template eventually affects its replicas throughout that Deployment.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For native Kubernetes Deployments, rollback scope follows the Deployment boundary.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>C is the intended answer only with an independently managed Deployment for the affected zone. A shared Deployment does not gain zone-specific rollback merely by restoring its previous Pod template.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/\"><strong>Kubernetes — Deployments</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Template-driven rollouts, Deployment-wide reconciliation, rollback behavior, and rolling-update controls.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/assign-pod-node/\"><strong>Kubernetes — Assigning Pods to Nodes</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Node selectors, required node affinity, and zone-based placement constraints.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/generated/kubectl_drain/\"><strong>Kubernetes — kubectl drain</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Node evacuation, Pod eviction, and why draining is distinct from application rollback.</p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e3q14":{"id":"e3q14","exam":3,"n":14,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You are configuring a continuous integration (CI) pipeline whose integration tests require access to APIs hosted in a private Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) network. Your security team requires that API traffic remain private. You need a solution that minimizes operational overhead. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use Cloud Build private pools configured to connect to your private VPC network.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Use Cloud Build to create a Compute Engine VM in your private VPC network, and execute the integration tests through a startup script.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use Cloud Build to run the pipeline, and configure a cross-region internal Application Load Balancer to provide access to the APIs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Use Cloud Build to run the pipeline, and configure a global external Application Load Balancer with a Google Cloud Armor security policy to provide access to the APIs.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Use Cloud Build private pools configured to connect to your private VPC network.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The solution must provide private connectivity while minimizing infrastructure management. Cloud Build private pools support access to private VPC resources and provide fully managed build workers. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Technical signal:</strong> Integration tests must call APIs inside a private VPC network.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Hard constraint:</strong> API traffic must remain private.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Preference:</strong> Minimize operational overhead.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Private pool workers run in a Google-managed service producer network. Configure a private connection between that network and your VPC so the workers can reach the APIs through internal IP addresses. Configure the necessary firewall access and run the integration tests on the private pool. </p>\n<p>Cloud Build manages the worker infrastructure and its scaling, avoiding the need to maintain your own test VMs. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A VM inside the VPC can reach private APIs. However, this approach adds VM provisioning, test execution coordination, result collection, and cleanup to the pipeline.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>C — Incorrect as a complete solution:</strong> An internal Application Load Balancer provides an internal endpoint, but creating it does not establish private connectivity from Cloud Build workers to your VPC. Additional networking would still be required.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>D — Violates Requirement:</strong> A global external Application Load Balancer exposes an external endpoint. A Cloud Armor policy can restrict requests, but it does not convert that endpoint into private VPC connectivity. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Creating a private pool alone does not automatically connect it to your VPC. The pool must be configured with the appropriate private network connection. </p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Build execution and network access are separate concerns. Cloud Build runs the tests; the private pool’s network configuration provides a route to the internal APIs.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>For example, integration tests can validate an internal orders API directly through its private address while Cloud Build manages the test workers.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>When Cloud Build needs private API access with minimal infrastructure management, choose a VPC-connected private pool.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Choose <strong>A</strong>. Private pools provide managed build execution with private VPC access; a load balancer alone does not establish that connectivity.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/private-pools/private-pools-overview\"><strong>Private pools overview</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Managed build workers, automatic scaling, and access to private network resources.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/private-pools/set-up-private-pool-to-use-in-vpc-network\"><strong>Set up environment to use private pools in a VPC network</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Private connections that allow communication through internal IP addresses.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/l7-internal\"><strong>Internal Application Load Balancer overview</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Internal endpoints and the network scope of cross-region internal load balancing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/https\"><strong>External Application Load Balancer overview</strong></a><br />\nSupports: External frontend IP addresses and Cloud Armor integration.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e3q15":{"id":"e3q15","exam":3,"n":15,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You store container images built by Cloud Build in Artifact Registry. You need to maintain vulnerability scanning coverage for both existing and newly uploaded images. You also need to identify the principal responsible for pushing each image. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Enable automatic vulnerability scanning for newly uploaded images and arrange periodic rescanning of all stored images. Use Cloud Audit Logs to track image uploads and identify the uploading principal.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Export vulnerability scan results to a Cloud Storage bucket, and use a custom script to analyze the results and notify the security team.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Configure automatic daily rescanning of images. Enable Cloud Audit Logs for scan operations, and use Logs Explorer to identify vulnerabilities.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Trigger automatic vulnerability scans when new image tags are created, and review the results. Use Cloud Audit Logs to track tag creation events.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Enable automatic vulnerability scanning for newly uploaded images and arrange periodic rescanning of all stored images. Use Cloud Audit Logs to track image uploads and identify the uploading principal.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>A addresses both requirements: vulnerability monitoring and upload attribution. However, its scanning language is shorthand: Artifact Analysis provides on-push scanning and continuous analysis, with additional steps needed to cover previously stored or inactive images. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Coverage requirement:</strong> Include existing images and future uploads.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Ongoing requirement:</strong> Detect newly disclosed vulnerabilities.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Audit requirement:</strong> Identify who performed each image push.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Implement A as follows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Enable automatic scanning.</strong> Enable the Container Scanning API and ensure scanning is enabled for the relevant repositories.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cover existing images.</strong> Push previously unscanned images again; enabling scanning does not automatically scan the existing inventory. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Maintain continuous analysis.</strong> Artifact Analysis updates findings as vulnerability information changes. Periodically pull retained images within the 30-day activity window to prevent their findings from becoming stale. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Enable upload auditing.</strong> Enable Artifact Registry <strong>Data Write audit logs</strong>. Inspect upload operations such as <code>Docker-PutManifest</code> and their caller identity. Data Access logging is generally disabled by default. </p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>B — Different Use Case:</strong> Processing exported findings supports reporting and notifications. It does not establish scanning coverage for every image or identify image uploaders.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>C — Incorrect:</strong> Continuous analysis updates vulnerability findings as new information arrives; it is not simply a configurable daily full rescan. Audit logs record operations and identities, while Artifact Analysis maintains vulnerability findings.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>D — Incorrect:</strong> Automatic initial scanning is based on the image’s <strong>digest</strong>. Adding or modifying a tag does not trigger a new scan. A tag creator also need not be the principal that originally uploaded the image. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Do not assume enabling scanning retroactively covers existing images. Similarly, enabling audit logging now cannot recreate upload records that were never collected.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Artifact Registry</strong> stores images.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Artifact Analysis</strong> maintains vulnerability findings.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Audit Logs</strong> records resource operations and caller identities.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>When Cloud Build pushes an image, the uploading principal is normally its build service account. To identify the human or source change behind that upload, correlate the registry event with the corresponding build records. </p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Use Artifact Analysis to understand image risk and audit logs to establish upload accountability.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Choose <strong>A</strong>. Include existing-image initialization, maintain the continuous-analysis activity window, and explicitly enable Data Write audit logging.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-analysis/docs/scanning-types\"><strong>Types of scanning</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Digest-based scanning, continuous analysis, tag behavior, and the 30-day activity window.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-analysis/docs/quickstart-scanning-os-automatically\"><strong>Scan OS packages automatically</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Enabling scanning and pushing existing images again for initial coverage.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/audit-logging\"><strong>Artifact Registry audit logging</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Upload operations, including <code>Docker-PutManifest</code>, and their Data Write classification.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/audit\"><strong>Cloud Audit Logs overview</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Caller identities and explicit enablement of Data Access audit logs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/configure-cloud-build\"><strong>Connect to Cloud Build</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Build service accounts uploading artifacts to Artifact Registry.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l5","d2l9"]},"e3q16":{"id":"e3q16","exam":3,"n":16,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your web application connects to an AlloyDB for PostgreSQL cluster through a private IP address accessible from your default VPC network. Before deploying a new application version, your Cloud Build CI/CD pipeline must execute a database schema migration.</p>\n<p>You need to implement this process following Google-recommended security practices. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Configure a Cloud Build private pool with a static external IP address. Restrict database connections to that address, and execute the migration script in the private pool.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Create a service account with the required database access permissions. Configure Cloud Build to use this service account, and execute the migration script in a private pool.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Store the database username and password in Secret Manager, and retrieve them when executing the migration script.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Store the database username and encrypted password in the application configuration file, and use these credentials to execute the migration through Cloud Build.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Create a service account with the required database access permissions. Configure Cloud Build to use this service account, and execute the migration script in a private pool.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The solution needs both <strong>private network connectivity</strong> and an appropriately authorized <strong>build identity</strong>. B addresses both components: a private pool for database access and a dedicated service account for the migration workload. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Technical signal:</strong> AlloyDB is accessed through a private IP address.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Execution requirement:</strong> The migration runs inside Cloud Build.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security preference:</strong> Use a dedicated identity with only the necessary permissions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Sequence requirement:</strong> Complete the migration before deploying the application.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Configure the private pool with a supported network path to the AlloyDB private endpoint. Private pools support access to resources in private networks, including databases. </p>\n<p>Configure Cloud Build to run as the dedicated service account. For IAM database authentication, enable that authentication method, register the service account as an AlloyDB database user, and grant the PostgreSQL privileges needed for the schema migration. <strong>Assigning an IAM role alone does not grant all required database privileges.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A — Incorrect:</strong> A static external IP address does not establish connectivity to the database’s private IP address. External address allowlisting does not solve the private network routing requirement. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Secret Manager is appropriate when using password-based authentication. However, retrieving credentials alone does not provide Cloud Build with connectivity to the private database. It is a supporting component rather than a complete solution.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> An encrypted password in a configuration file still requires secure decryption-key management and does not establish private connectivity. Secret Manager provides managed storage for passwords when they are required. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>A private pool is not automatically connected to every private endpoint. If AlloyDB uses private services access, account for <strong>non-transitive VPC peering</strong> : simply chaining the pool’s peering connection through your VPC to another peered network is insufficient. </p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Database access has three separate requirements:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Connectivity:</strong> A network path to the database.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Authentication:</strong> A recognized database identity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Authorization:</strong> Permission to execute the required SQL operations.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Run the versioned migration as a pipeline step under the migration service account. Make application deployment depend on successful migration completion, so a failed schema change stops the release.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Private connectivity reaches the database, while a scoped service identity controls migration access.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Choose <strong>B</strong>. Configure the private network path, run Cloud Build under a dedicated service account, and grant the necessary database privileges.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/private-pools/use-in-private-network\"><strong>Using Cloud Build in a private network</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Private database access and limitations of transitive peering.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/securing-builds/configure-user-specified-service-accounts\"><strong>Run builds with a user-managed service account</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Configuring a dedicated build identity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/alloydb/docs/database-users/manage-iam-auth?utm_source=chatgpt.com\"><strong>Manage</strong></a><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/alloydb/docs/database-users/manage-iam-auth\"><strong>IAM authentication</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Service account database users, IAM authentication, and PostgreSQL privileges.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/alloydb/docs/choose-alloydb-connectivity\"><strong>Choose a connectivity option</strong></a><br />\nSupports: AlloyDB private connectivity requirements and network topology considerations.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/securing-builds/use-secrets\"><strong>Use secrets from Secret Manager</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Securely providing passwords to Cloud Build when password authentication is used.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l1","d2l2"]},"e3q17":{"id":"e3q17","exam":3,"n":17,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You manage a critical API deployed on Cloud Run that handles an average of 10,000 requests per minute. Users expect <strong>99.9% availability</strong> and response times of <strong>200 milliseconds or less for at least 95% of requests</strong>.</p>\n<p>You need to define service level objectives (SLOs) that reflect these expectations and implement ongoing measurement and monitoring. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Configure Cloud Monitoring alerts to trigger when average API latency exceeds 150 milliseconds or the error rate exceeds 0.1%.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Define latency as the only SLO, requiring 99% of requests to complete within 100 milliseconds.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Define an availability SLO of 99% and a latency SLO requiring 90% of requests to complete within 500 milliseconds. Use Cloud Monitoring to measure compliance and alert on violations.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Define availability and latency SLOs using the corresponding service level indicators (SLIs). Use Cloud Monitoring to measure compliance and alert on violations.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Define availability and latency SLOs using the corresponding service level indicators (SLIs). Use Cloud Monitoring to measure compliance and alert on violations.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>D connects the user expectations to measurable indicators, explicit objectives, and operational monitoring. An SLO requires an SLI, a target, and a defined compliance period.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Availability requirement:</strong> At least 99.9% availability.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Latency requirement:</strong> At least 95% of requests complete within 200 milliseconds.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Operational requirement:</strong> Continuously measure performance and notify the team about problems.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Define two request-based SLOs over an agreed compliance period:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Availability:</strong> Successful eligible requests divided by total eligible requests, with a target of <strong>at least 99.9%</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Latency:</strong> Eligible requests completing within 200 milliseconds divided by total eligible requests, with a target of <strong>at least 95%</strong>. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Cloud Run automatically supplies request-count and request-latency metrics to Cloud Monitoring. Use suitable metrics to measure the SLIs, track compliance, and configure notification channels with SLO alerting. Burn-rate alerts can warn when the error budget is being consumed too quickly. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> These alerts can supplement monitoring, but average latency does not establish whether 95% of requests meet the 200-millisecond threshold. The option also does not explicitly define and measure the requested SLOs. Google’s SRE guidance explains that averages can conceal slow requests. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>B — Violates Requirement:</strong> It omits availability entirely. A stricter latency target does not replace the missing availability objective.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Both targets are weaker than required: 99% availability instead of 99.9%, and 90% within 500 milliseconds instead of 95% within 200 milliseconds.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Average latency and a percentile-based latency requirement are different measurements.</strong> A lower average does not guarantee that the required fraction of requests is fast enough. </p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An <strong>SLI</strong> measures service behavior. An <strong>SLO</strong> sets the acceptable target for that measurement over a specified period. An <strong>alerting policy</strong> determines when the team should respond. </p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Suppose 90% of requests take 50 milliseconds and 10% take 500 milliseconds. Average latency is only <strong>95 milliseconds</strong> , yet just <strong>90%</strong> meet the 200-millisecond threshold—the required latency SLO fails.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Measure successful requests and sufficiently fast requests, then set targets and alerts for both.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Choose <strong>D</strong>. Define separate availability and latency SLIs, apply the required targets over a compliance period, and monitor them with SLO-based alerts.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring\"><strong>Concepts in service monitoring</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Availability and latency SLIs, request-based SLOs, targets, and compliance periods.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/\"><strong>Service Level Objectives — Google SRE</strong></a><br />\nSupports: User-focused objectives and why average latency can conceal poor tail performance.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/monitoring\"><strong>Monitor Health and Performance — Cloud Run</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Automatic collection of request-count and request-latency metrics.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring/alerting-on-budget-burn-rate\"><strong>Alerting on your burn rate</strong></a><br />\nSupports: SLO-based alerting, notification channels, and error-budget burn monitoring.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l1"]},"e3q18":{"id":"e3q18","exam":3,"n":18,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your organization’s main ecommerce platform runs on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) and has started showing intermittent spikes in response latency. Customers have reported that the checkout flow sometimes takes noticeably longer to complete. You need to determine what is actually causing this performance degradation. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Increase the replica count for the application’s GKE deployment and turn on Horizontal Pod Autoscaling.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. In Cloud Logging, create a logs-based metric that counts slow requests, then configure a Cloud Monitoring alerting policy to notify you when the count crosses a defined threshold.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Review the rollout history in Cloud Deploy and roll back to an earlier application version to check whether the problem goes away.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Use Cloud Trace to examine distributed traces for the checkout flow, and inspect the trace waterfalls to pinpoint which service or operation is contributing the most latency.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Use Cloud Trace to examine distributed traces for the checkout flow, and inspect the trace waterfalls to pinpoint which service or operation is contributing the most latency.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard requirement in the stem is “identify the root cause” — not “reduce latency” and not “confirm a hunch.” That rules out anything that only reacts to symptoms or guesses at a cause without evidence. Cloud Trace is purpose-built to answer “where in a multi-service request is the time actually going?” by breaking a single checkout request down into spans per service/operation and rendering them as a waterfall, so you can see directly which hop is slow. Every other option either treats a symptom (A), detects that a problem exists without localizing it (B), or tests an unproven assumption by making a disruptive production change (C). Only D gives you evidence pointing at a specific component.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“intermittent periods of high ... latency”</strong> — points toward a tool that captures per-request timing data over time, not a static config check.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“sometimes very slow”</strong> — rules out a uniform, always-on problem; favors a tool that can show you the slow instances specifically, not just an aggregate count.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“identify the root cause”</strong> — a decisive requirement/exclusion signal: it eliminates any option that only mitigates symptoms or only detects that an issue occurred.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“checkout process”</strong> — implies a multi-step, likely multi-service flow, which is exactly what distributed tracing is designed to decompose.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Trace collects per-request latency data across the services involved in a transaction and displays it as a waterfall of spans, with each span showing how much time was spent in that specific service or operation. For an intermittent, checkout-specific slowdown, this lets you compare a slow trace against a fast one and see exactly which downstream call (a database query, a payment service, an external API) is adding the delay — which is precisely “identifying the root cause” rather than inferring it indirectly.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong> Horizontal Pod Autoscaling solves a capacity problem (not enough replicas for the load), which is a different failure mode than “one particular downstream call is slow.” Adding replicas might mask an overload-related symptom but tells you nothing about which service or operation is responsible, and if the real cause isn’t capacity, it won’t help at all.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A logs-based metric and alert is good for knowing <em>that</em> slow requests are occurring and getting notified when they cross a threshold, but a count of slow requests doesn’t tell you <em>where</em> in the request path the time is being lost — it’s a detection mechanism, not a localization mechanism.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Rolling back assumes, without evidence from the scenario, that a recent deployment introduced the regression. It’s a disruptive, all-or-nothing experiment: if the rollback doesn’t help, you’ve learned little and still have to diagnose the issue, and if it does help, you still don’t know which part of the previous change was the actual cause.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is picking an option that sounds like reasonable incident-response practice (scale up, roll back, alert) instead of one that specifically answers “where is the time going.” Google Cloud DevOps-track exams frequently pair a correct observability-analysis answer against plausible-sounding operational actions that would only be the right move after root cause is already known.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>In a distributed system, overall request latency is the sum of time spent across every service and network hop a request touches. Metrics and logs can tell you that latency increased in aggregate; only a distributed trace reconstructs the full path of an individual request and attributes latency to specific spans, which is why it’s the standard tool for localizing latency root causes in microservices-style architectures.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team that jumps straight to autoscaling for an intermittent latency complaint often burns on-call time and cloud spend without fixing anything, because the real cause (for example, a slow downstream payment-processor call or an unindexed database query) keeps happening regardless of replica count. Tracing the checkout flow first turns a vague “it’s sometimes slow” ticket into a specific, actionable fix targeted at the one slow hop.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Need to know <em>that</em> it’s slow → logs/metrics and alerts. Need to know <em>where</em> it’s slow → Cloud Trace and trace waterfalls.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question explicitly asks you to “identify the root cause” of latency in a multi-service or multi-step flow, look for the distributed-tracing answer (Cloud Trace, trace waterfalls, spans) over any option that only mitigates, detects, or guesses at the cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/trace/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Trace overview:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (distributed tracing, spans, and trace waterfalls used to localize latency)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/logs-based-metrics\"><strong>Google Cloud — Log-based metrics overview:</strong></a> Supports: the option B distinction (logs-based metrics and alerting are for detection/counting, not per-request root-cause localization)</p>","lessons":["d4l4","d4l10"]},"e3q19":{"id":"e3q19","exam":3,"n":19,"domain":3,"html":"<p>Your organization is actively developing a new web service and preparing it for production. As the SRE team lead, you <strong>MUST</strong> apply Google-recommended practices throughout productionalization. Which two activities should you prioritize next? <strong>(Choose two.)</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Establish monitoring and alerting for the service.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Begin preparing guidelines for using infrastructure systems.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Collaborate with the product team on the service’s design.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Implement redundant resources within the service environment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>E. Provide early SRE consultation to review architecture and design decisions in detail.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p>The supplied answer key selects:</p>\n<p><strong>A. Establish monitoring and alerting for the service.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>E. Provide early SRE consultation to review architecture and design decisions in detail.</strong></p>\n<p>However, <strong>this pair is not uniquely supported by the wording</strong>. Google’s guidance also directly supports D during active-development productionalization. </p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>A and E address operational visibility and early identification of architectural risks. Both are recommended practices. However, the scenario specifically identifies active development and productionalization, for which Google also explicitly recommends redundancy. Therefore, the supplied key is plausible, but eliminating D requires additional context. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Actively developing” — Technical signal:</strong> Identifies the service’s lifecycle stage.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Preparing it for production” — Required outcome:</strong> Calls for operational readiness work.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Google-recommended practices” — Hard constraint:</strong> Requires authoritative support for the selected activities.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Choose two” — Cardinality instruction:</strong> Requires a defensible distinction between the selected pair and the remaining options.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A:</strong> Monitoring and alerting provide visibility into service behavior and enable detection of operational problems.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>E:</strong> Early consultation identifies architectural risks while design changes are comparatively easier to make. Google’s early-engagement model extends through design and implementation, so this activity remains relevant during development.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These activities complement each other, but their validity does not establish that they are the only appropriate pair. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Infrastructure guidelines support consistent engineering decisions. Google associates this activity primarily with architecture and design, making it less directly aligned with the stated productionalization work. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Collaborative design is legitimate SRE work. The stem provides no specific design problem that makes codesign the immediate priority, although it should not be described as an incorrect practice. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Best Fit under a phase-specific interpretation:</strong> Google explicitly includes additional resources for redundancy in active-development productionalization. Consequently, D cannot be reliably eliminated, and A/D is another defensible selection. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Distinguish an intended answer key from a uniquely validated answer. Treating redundancy as unnecessary would introduce an assumption absent from the scenario.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>SRE combines early design influence with operational readiness. Lifecycle phases guide priorities, while consultation, documentation, monitoring, and resilience work can overlap.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>An SRE team could review architectural failure modes, establish actionable alerts, and validate redundant capacity before launch. Actual priorities depend on existing controls, reliability objectives, and identified risks.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Production readiness combines operational visibility, resilience, and early reliability review.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A/E is the supplied key, while A/D has direct lifecycle-specific support.</strong> This item needs clarification before it can be published as a uniquely graded two-answer question.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/engagement-model/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — SRE Engagement Model:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Monitoring, alerting, and redundancy during active development; infrastructure guidance during architecture and design.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/evolving-sre-engagement-model/\"><strong>Google SRE Book — The Evolving SRE Engagement Model:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Early consultation, collaborative design, and SRE participation during implementation.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Exam Guide:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Objective 3.2, managing the service lifecycle.</p>","lessons":["d3l8"]},"e3q20":{"id":"e3q20","exam":3,"n":20,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your company uses Cloud Build and Artifact Registry in a CI/CD pipeline that deploys container images to Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). Images are tagged with their corresponding commit hashes and promoted to production after passing tests in development and pre-production. A recent production release failed because an integration scenario had not been tested, leading to a disruptive manual rollback. During recovery, you also discovered numerous outdated, unused images in Artifact Registry. You need to improve deployment and rollback management and remove unnecessary images. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create a dedicated rollback pipeline in Cloud Build, and configure an Artifact Registry cleanup policy.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Manage application deployments with Cloud Deploy, and configure an Artifact Registry cleanup policy.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Deploy Cloud Service Mesh across the GKE clusters, and manually delete obsolete images from Artifact Registry.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Manage application deployments with Cloud Deploy, and schedule a Cloud Build job to delete obsolete container images.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Manage application deployments with Cloud Deploy, and configure an Artifact Registry cleanup policy.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two capabilities are required: controlled release management and automated artifact retention. Cloud Deploy manages deployment progression and rollback, while Artifact Registry cleanup policies manage image removal. B addresses both needs through their dedicated managed capabilities. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Improve deployment and rollback management” — Hard constraint:</strong> Requires repeatable release promotion and recovery.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Outdated, unused images” — Hard constraint:</strong> Requires artifact lifecycle management.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Integration scenario had not been tested” — Diagnostic signal:</strong> Deployment verification must include meaningful integration tests.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Commit hashes” — Technical signal:</strong> Build traceability already exists; tagging alone does not validate application behavior.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Deploy defines a delivery pipeline across target environments and manages releases and rollouts. A rollback creates another rollout using a previously successful release, providing a consistent recovery mechanism. It integrates with the existing Cloud Build and Artifact Registry workflow. </p>\n<p>Artifact Registry cleanup policies automatically remove versions matching deletion criteria and retain versions matching keep criteria. Configure retention to preserve production and rollback candidates, and preview deletion behavior with a dry run. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A Cloud Build pipeline can execute rollback commands, and its cleanup policy is appropriate. However, the team must implement and maintain its own release-selection and recovery workflow instead of using Cloud Deploy’s release-management capabilities. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> Cloud Service Mesh provides traffic management and can support canary or blue-green deployments. Those capabilities do not supply the requested release-management workflow, and manual image deletion leaves repository maintenance dependent on operators. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Cloud Deploy is appropriate, but a scheduled cleanup build requires custom deletion logic and job maintenance. Native cleanup policies already provide automated retention and removal. Custom jobs become useful when requirements exceed what policy conditions can express. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Adopting Cloud Deploy does not automatically create the missing integration tests. Configure deployment verification using tests that exercise the previously uncovered functionality; Cloud Deploy runs the tests you provide.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Release management and artifact retention must work together. A reproducible rollback needs both the previous deployment configuration and its container image. An image can be old yet still be necessary for recovery.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Continue building images with Cloud Build, promote releases through Cloud Deploy, and add integration verification to the relevant targets. Combine deletion rules for obsolete builds with keep rules protecting recovery images, then review cleanup results in dry-run mode before enabling deletion. </p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Use Cloud Deploy for release progression and rollback, and Artifact Registry policies for retention.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a scenario combines deployment recovery problems with accumulating images, pair managed delivery with native cleanup policies. Preserve the artifacts needed for rollback and explicitly configure the required tests.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Overview of Cloud Deploy:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Environment progression, release management, rollback, and integration with Cloud Build and Artifact Registry.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/verify-deployment\"><strong>Google Cloud Deploy — Verify your deployment:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Deployment verification using explicitly configured application tests.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/repositories/cleanup-policy\"><strong>Google Cloud Artifact Registry — Configure cleanup policies:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Automated deletion, keep rules, retention criteria, and dry-run validation.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/service-mesh/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Service Mesh overview:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Traffic management and progressive deployment capabilities; option C’s different operational focus.</p>","lessons":["d2l4"]},"e3q21":{"id":"e3q21","exam":3,"n":21,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your service runs on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), and you collect its metrics through Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus with managed collection. Following deployment, no metrics appear in Cloud Monitoring, although no errors have been reported. Which troubleshooting action should you take?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Check whether metric ingestion has exceeded the Cloud Monitoring API write quota.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Validate the application metrics port specified in the <code>PodMonitoring</code> resource.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Check whether a Grafana service is installed in the GKE cluster.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Verify that the service has been granted the <code>monitoring.servicesViewer</code> IAM role.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Validate the application metrics port specified in the</strong><code>**PodMonitoring**</code><strong>resource.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The combination of missing metrics and no reported errors points toward exporter or scrape configuration problems. Among the choices, validating the configured port directly tests whether managed collection can discover and scrape the application’s metrics endpoint. Google explicitly recommends this check for these symptoms. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Managed collection” — Technical signal:</strong> Application scraping is configured through resources such as <code>PodMonitoring</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“No metrics appear in Cloud Monitoring” — Diagnostic signal:</strong> Investigate the collection and ingestion path.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“No errors have been reported” — Diagnostic signal:</strong> A scrape configuration problem is a stronger match than an explicitly rejected API request.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A <code>PodMonitoring</code> resource identifies the application pods and endpoints to scrape. Its port must resolve to the intended metrics endpoint; when using a named port, the name must match the application’s declared container port. A mismatched reference can prevent collection even when the application itself runs normally. </p>\n<p>This is the best diagnostic check among the options, rather than proof that the port is necessarily the root cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Exhausted ingestion quota can prevent metrics from arriving. However, Google documents this condition with HTTP <code>429</code> quota-exceeded errors. Without that evidence, scrape configuration is the stronger starting hypothesis. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> Grafana is an optional query and visualization interface. Installing it does not configure application scraping or resolve metrics missing from Cloud Monitoring. Google’s troubleshooting process can use Metrics Explorer directly. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Incorrect:</strong> <code>roles/monitoring.servicesViewer</code> grants read-only access to service-monitoring resources and SLOs. It does not authorize metric ingestion. The relevant writing role is Monitoring Metric Writer, applied to the identity performing ingestion when required. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>An absence of errors does not establish that scraping is configured correctly. Conversely, if the scenario reported ingestion-related <code>429</code> errors, checking the write quota in A would become appropriate.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Metrics must be exposed, discovered, scraped, ingested, and queried. Troubleshooting should isolate the failing stage. A working application and a running collector do not establish that the collector has a valid target.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>For example, a pod might declare its metrics port as <code>metrics</code>, while <code>PodMonitoring</code> references <code>http-metrics</code>. Align the names, then inspect target status and verify that samples appear. This addresses collection configuration without unnecessary dashboard installation or IAM changes. </p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Silent missing metrics in managed collection call for checking the exporter and scrape configuration.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For managed Prometheus collection with no metrics and no errors, validate the <code>PodMonitoring</code> port, selectors, namespace, and exposed endpoint before pursuing unsupported quota or permission hypotheses.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/managed-prometheus/troubleshooting\"><strong>Google Cloud — Troubleshooting Managed Service for Prometheus:</strong></a>Supports: The missing-metrics symptom, valid-port checks, quota-error distinction, and troubleshooting independently of Grafana.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/managed-prometheus/setup-managed\"><strong>Google Cloud — Get started with managed collection:</strong></a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: <code>PodMonitoring</code> target selection, endpoint ports, and target-status verification.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/access-control\"><strong>Google Cloud Monitoring — Control access with IAM:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Monitoring Services Viewer versus Monitoring Metric Writer permissions.</p>","lessons":["d4l7","d4l13"]},"e3q22":{"id":"e3q22","exam":3,"n":22,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You operate applications on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) clusters distributed across several Google Cloud projects. You need a centralized, scalable approach to collect their Prometheus metrics and analyze them using a flexible query language. Which solution follows Google-recommended practices?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Deploy a Prometheus server on an administration cluster with cluster autoscaling enabled, and expose its endpoint for queries.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use the Ops Agent to collect metrics, and query the collected data through Metrics Explorer.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Configure Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus with managed collection, and query the collected metrics through Metrics Explorer.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Deploy a Prometheus server as an application sidecar in each cluster, and query the metrics through Cloud Monitoring.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Configure Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus with managed collection, and query the collected metrics through Metrics Explorer.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The solution must provide scalable collection, visibility across projects, and expressive querying. Managed Service for Prometheus supplies the managed backend and recommended Kubernetes collection approach, while Metrics Explorer supports PromQL. Configure cross-project visibility through a metrics scope rather than operating a central Prometheus server yourself.</p>\n<p>This aligns with objective 4.1, which explicitly includes collecting metrics through Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“GKE clusters” — Technical signal:</strong> Favors managed collection designed for Kubernetes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Several Google Cloud projects” — Hard constraint:</strong> Requires cross-project query visibility.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Centralized, scalable” — Hard constraint:</strong> Requires a shared monitoring backend that scales beyond individual servers.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Flexible query language” — Technical signal:</strong> PromQL provides expressive querying of Prometheus metrics.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Google recommends managed collection for Kubernetes environments. It handles collector configuration and ingestion scaling, avoiding the operational burden of maintaining Prometheus servers. </p>\n<p>Metrics Explorer supports PromQL queries against the managed metrics. To query across projects, add the relevant projects to the metrics scope of a designated scoping project and provide appropriate access. Enabling collection alone does not automatically establish cross-project visibility. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A central Prometheus server can collect and expose metrics, but you remain responsible for its configuration, storage, availability, and scaling. Cluster autoscaling does not remove those responsibilities. Managed collection directly addresses this operational burden. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> The Ops Agent can scrape Prometheus metrics from Compute Engine VMs. For Kubernetes workloads, Google recommends managed collection instead. Metrics Explorer is an appropriate query interface, but the proposed collector does not match the recommended GKE approach. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> Application-side Prometheus servers create separate collection and storage instances. As written, this does not establish ingestion into Cloud Monitoring’s shared backend; an explicit export integration would still be required. It also introduces per-application server management. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The Ops Agent supports Prometheus collection, so its name alone does not make B incorrect. The deployment environment resolves the choice: if these workloads ran on Compute Engine VMs, an Ops Agent configured with its Prometheus receiver could be appropriate. </p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Collection determines which metrics reach the backend; query scope determines which projects’ metrics a user can analyze. A centralized view does not require physically relocating every project’s metrics into one project—the metrics scope combines visibility at query time. </p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Enable managed collection on each cluster and define <code>PodMonitoring</code> resources for application metrics endpoints. Then query from a scoping project containing the participating projects. This provides consistent collection and shared analysis without maintaining a Prometheus server for every application. </p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For standard GKE Prometheus monitoring, use managed collection, PromQL, and a metrics scope for cross-project visibility.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Choose managed collection for Prometheus metrics on GKE. Use Metrics Explorer’s PromQL support and configure a multi-project metrics scope when the applications span projects.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/managed-prometheus\"><strong>Google Cloud — Managed Service for Prometheus:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Recommended Kubernetes collection, managed scaling, self-deployed collection trade-offs, and the Ops Agent’s VM use case.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/managed-prometheus/query-cm\"><strong>Google Cloud — Query using Cloud Monitoring:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Metrics Explorer, PromQL, shared backend access, and cross-project metrics scopes.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/managed-prometheus/setup-managed\"><strong>Google Cloud — Get started with managed collection:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Application endpoint scraping through <code>PodMonitoring</code> resources.</p>","lessons":["d4l7"]},"e3q23":{"id":"e3q23","exam":3,"n":23,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You manage a retail website composed of several microservices running in a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) Standard node pool with cluster autoscaling enabled. Each microservice has resource limits and a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) configured.</p>\n<p>During peak traffic, you receive alerts for one microservice. You discover that half of its Pods report <code>OOMKilled</code> container terminations, while its replica count remains at the HPA’s configured minimum. You need to resolve the issue. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Change the node pool’s machine type to one with more memory.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Increase the maximum number of nodes allowed in the node pool.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Increase the maximum replica count configured for the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Increase the memory resource limit configured for the affected microservice.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Increase the memory resource limit configured for the affected microservice.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The strongest indication is insufficient memory available to the affected containers. Increasing their memory limit directly addresses container-level OOM terminations. The replica count being at its minimum also indicates that raising the HPA’s maximum is unlikely to help. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Technical signal:</strong> <code>OOMKilled</code> indicates an out-of-memory termination.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Configuration clue:</strong> Each microservice has resource limits.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scaling clue:</strong> The workload remains at its minimum replica count, rather than reaching its maximum.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Kubernetes enforces each container’s configured memory limit. When a container exceeds that boundary, it can be terminated even when its node has free memory. Increase <code>resources.limits.memory</code> to accommodate the application’s peak requirements. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Larger nodes can address node-level memory exhaustion. However, additional node memory does not change an existing container memory limit. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>B — Different Use Case:</strong> Increasing the node pool maximum helps when unschedulable Pods require additional nodes and the existing maximum prevents expansion. It does not increase memory limits for running containers. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>C — Incorrect for the stated symptoms:</strong> Increasing <code>maxReplicas</code> raises the scaling ceiling; it does not itself trigger additional replicas. HPA scales according to configured metrics and does not adjust container memory limits. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p><code>OOMKilled</code> can also result from node-level memory exhaustion. In production, confirm which boundary was exceeded. For this question, <strong>D is the best fit</strong> given the configured limits and absence of evidence of node pressure. </p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>These settings control different resources:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Memory limit:</strong> Memory a container may consume.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>HPA:</strong> Number of workload replicas.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cluster autoscaler:</strong> Number of nodes available to schedule Pods. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Inspect the affected container’s termination details and memory consumption. After adjusting its limit, review memory requests for appropriate scheduling and investigate whether a memory leak contributed to the failures. </p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Match the corrective action to the resource boundary that is failing.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Choose <strong>D</strong>. Container memory exhaustion calls for an appropriate memory limit; increasing a scaling maximum does not automatically provide that memory.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/troubleshooting/oom-events\"><strong>Troubleshoot OOM events</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Container-level versus node-level OOM events, memory limits, and remediation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/horizontalpodautoscaler\"><strong>Horizontal Pod autoscaling</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Metric-driven replica scaling and its distinction from node scaling.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/cluster-autoscaler\"><strong>About GKE cluster autoscaling</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Node expansion for unschedulable Pods based on resource requests.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l12"]},"e3q24":{"id":"e3q24","exam":3,"n":24,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You are designing a CI/CD pipeline that packages an application into container images. Images must reach Artifact Registry only after a vulnerability scan confirms there are no critical findings. You also want to minimize ongoing pipeline maintenance. Which approach best satisfies both requirements?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Build and publish the image using a custom script on a VM. Use Artifact Analysis automatic scanning after publication and delete the image if critical vulnerabilities are found.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Build the image with Cloud Build. Run Artifact Analysis On-Demand Scanning before publication and push the image to Artifact Registry only if no critical vulnerabilities are found.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Build the image using Jenkins installed on a VM. Run Artifact Analysis On-Demand Scanning before publication and push the image to Artifact Registry only if no critical vulnerabilities are found.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Build and publish the image using Cloud Build. Use Artifact Analysis automatic scanning after publication and delete the image if critical vulnerabilities are found.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Build the image with Cloud Build. Run Artifact Analysis On-Demand Scanning before publication and push the image to Artifact Registry only if no critical vulnerabilities are found.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>First enforce the hard requirement: scanning and approval must precede publication. This eliminates A and D. Both remaining options support that sequence, but Cloud Build removes the need to operate a Jenkins installation and its VM, making B the stronger maintenance fit.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Only after a vulnerability scan confirms” — Sequence constraint:</strong> The security check must complete before pushing the image.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“No critical findings” — Hard constraint:</strong> Explicitly evaluate the scan results for <code>CRITICAL</code> severity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Minimize ongoing pipeline maintenance” — Preference:</strong> Favor managed build infrastructure over a self-hosted CI server.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>On-Demand Scanning can inspect a locally built image before it is uploaded to Artifact Registry. Configure the pipeline to build, scan, evaluate severity, and publish only after the check succeeds.</p>\n<p>The severity check must fail the build when <code>CRITICAL</code> findings exist, preventing the dependent push step from running. Merely running the scanner does not establish this enforcement. Google documents this Cloud Build integration directly. </p>\n<p>Cloud Build provides managed build workers, reducing infrastructure setup, upgrades, and scaling responsibilities. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Automatic scanning examines the image after upload. Deleting it afterward cannot satisfy a requirement that disallows publishing an image with critical vulnerabilities. The custom VM also introduces maintenance responsibilities.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Jenkins can implement the required pre-publication scanning sequence. However, hosting it on a VM requires maintaining the CI server and its infrastructure, making it less suitable than Cloud Build for the stated maintenance preference.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Cloud Build addresses infrastructure maintenance, but the scan occurs too late. Automatic on-push scanning begins when the image is uploaded, so subsequent deletion does not provide a pre-publication gate. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>“Automatic scanning” does not mean “scanning before upload.” If the requirement instead permitted post-upload detection and removal, D could fit. Here, the timing constraint requires an explicit gate before publication.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>The scanner detects findings; the pipeline enforces policy. A successful scan represents the supported vulnerabilities detected at that time. Automatic scanning can complement the initial gate by updating findings as new vulnerability information becomes available. </p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A commit-triggered build can stop at the severity check and retain diagnostic logs for the developer. After the vulnerable dependency is updated, the replacement image is rebuilt and rescanned before publication, avoiding a separate registry cleanup workflow.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Build, scan, enforce, then push; use managed build infrastructure to reduce maintenance.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When vulnerable images must never enter the registry, place the security decision before upload. If multiple options support that sequence, use the operational requirements—such as maintenance effort—to select the best implementation.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-analysis/docs/ods-cloudbuild\"><strong>Artifact Analysis — Use On-Demand Scanning in your Cloud Build pipeline:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Scanning before publication and failing builds based on selected vulnerability severities.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-analysis/docs/scanning-types\"><strong>Artifact Analysis — Types of scanning:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Local on-demand scans, automatic on-push scanning, and continuous analysis.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Overview of Cloud Build:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Managed build infrastructure and reduced infrastructure maintenance.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Objective 2.4, covering deployment-pipeline security, Artifact Analysis, and vulnerability scanning.</p>","lessons":["d2l9"]},"e3q25":{"id":"e3q25","exam":3,"n":25,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your company operates several applications on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) and Cloud Run. You intend to use BigQuery as the central repository for application logs. You need to consolidate log collection and analysis to understand system health and performance while keeping configuration complexity and costs low. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Modify each application to send its logs directly to BigQuery through the BigQuery API.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Collect the GKE and Cloud Run logs through Datadog, and forward them from Datadog to BigQuery.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use Cloud Logging to collect logs from GKE and Cloud Run, and configure a log sink that routes them to BigQuery.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Download the application logs from GKE and Cloud Run, and manually upload them to BigQuery through the Google Cloud console.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Use Cloud Logging to collect logs from GKE and Cloud Run, and configure a log sink that routes them to BigQuery.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>BigQuery is the required destination; simplicity and cost efficiency guide the collection method. Use the platforms’ existing logging integrations and configure routing centrally. This avoids adding delivery logic to every application, an intermediary platform, or recurring manual transfers.</p>\n<p>Exam objective 4.2 explicitly covers exporting and retaining logs through destinations such as BigQuery. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“GKE and Cloud Run” — Technical signal:</strong> Both platforms integrate with Cloud Logging.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“BigQuery as the central repository” — Hard constraint:</strong> Logs must reach BigQuery for centralized analysis.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Configuration complexity and costs low” — Preference:</strong> Favors native collection and managed routing over additional integrations.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>GKE’s managed logging integration collects system and application logs when the relevant collection settings are enabled. Cloud Run automatically collects supported application output, including <code>stdout</code> and <code>stderr</code>, along with platform-generated logs. </p>\n<p>A Cloud Logging sink filters incoming entries and routes matching logs to a BigQuery dataset. Authorize the sink’s writer identity to write to the destination, including the appropriate BigQuery permissions. This provides centralized routing without requiring each application to implement BigQuery ingestion. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Direct API writes can populate BigQuery, but each application must maintain its own delivery integration. Native collection and a sink avoid that repeated implementation work. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Datadog provides an additional observability platform, but this proposal inserts another collection and forwarding layer. The scenario does not require its capabilities, and Cloud Logging already supports the required BigQuery destination. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Manual uploads can transfer selected historical files, but repeated downloading and uploading creates operational work and delays analysis. A sink provides managed routing as new logs arrive. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>A central repository does not require every application to write directly to it. Separate log production from delivery. Also, native routing does not make storage free: Log Router has no additional charge, but destination storage charges still apply. </p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A logging architecture separates four responsibilities: producing events, collecting them, routing selected records, and analyzing stored data. Centralizing routing lets administrators change destinations and filters without updating every application.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Standardize structured application logs, verify collection from both platforms, and configure a sink filter that retains the records needed for troubleshooting and performance analysis. Filtering unnecessary entries reduces the data sent to BigQuery while keeping routing rules centrally maintained. </p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For GKE and Cloud Run logs destined for BigQuery, use native collection plus a Cloud Logging sink.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When supported Google Cloud services need centralized log analysis in BigQuery, prefer Cloud Logging and a sink. Direct application writes, extra platforms, and manual transfers add work unless another requirement specifically justifies them.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/about-logs\"><strong>Google Kubernetes Engine — About GKE logs:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Managed log collection, workload logging settings, structured logging, and BigQuery export.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/logging\"><strong>Google Cloud Run — Logging and viewing logs:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Automatic collection from supported application outputs and platform-generated logs.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/export/configure_export_v2\"><strong>Google Cloud Logging — Route logs to supported destinations:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: BigQuery sinks, inclusion filters, destination permissions, and managed routing instead of application-specific or manual delivery.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/products/observability/pricing\"><strong>Google Cloud Observability — Pricing:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: No additional Log Router charge and applicable destination storage charges.</p>","lessons":["d4l1"]},"e3q26":{"id":"e3q26","exam":3,"n":26,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your company is developing an ecommerce application for deployment on Google Cloud. You need to periodically exercise the production checkout process, verify that it works correctly, and confirm that failures generate automated notifications. You want to minimize ongoing administration while following Google-recommended practices. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create a Workflows workflow that validates checkout functionality, and use Cloud Tasks to invoke it periodically.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Implement a Cloud Run function for synthetic monitoring, and configure an alerting policy for test failures.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Create a log-based alerting policy in Cloud Logging that detects failure messages generated during checkout.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Configure an uptime check for the application, and create an alerting policy for check failures.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Implement a Cloud Run function for synthetic monitoring, and configure an alerting policy for test failures.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The mandatory requirements are active checkout testing and failure notifications. Minimizing administration favors managed test execution and alerting over custom orchestration. Select synthetic monitoring because the target is a business workflow, rather than endpoint availability alone.</p>\n<p>Exam objective 4.1 explicitly covers synthetic monitors that proactively probe application endpoints and workflows. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Periodically exercise the production checkout process” — Hard constraint:</strong> Requires scheduled execution of application functionality.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Verify that it works correctly” — Technical signal:</strong> Requires assertions about checkout behavior.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Failures generate automated notifications” — Hard constraint:</strong> Requires an alerting policy and notification channels.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Minimize ongoing administration” — Preference:</strong> Favors integrated scheduling, execution, and monitoring.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Monitoring synthetic monitors execute test scripts deployed as second-generation Cloud Run functions. The scripts can interact with web pages or APIs, perform checkout steps, and assert expected results. Cloud Monitoring manages recurring execution and records outcomes; an associated alerting policy sends failure notifications through configured channels. Google specifically identifies ecommerce checkout testing as a synthetic monitoring use case. </p>\n<p>Configure the function through a synthetic monitor so that scheduling and result collection are included.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Workflows can orchestrate validation, but Cloud Tasks dispatches individually configured tasks. It does not provide Cloud Scheduler’s recurring cron-job behavior. Building recurrence and monitoring around this approach adds administration. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> Log-based alerts notify you when incoming logs match specified criteria. They can detect recorded checkout errors, but they do not independently execute checkout transactions. This option therefore misses the active-testing requirement. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> Uptime checks validate endpoint responses and can generate alerts. However, their requests are independent and stateless; they do not directly reproduce a checkout journey involving a sequence of interactions. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>An application can return a successful HTTP response while checkout is broken. Uptime checks can validate response content, but that capability does not make them a scripted transaction test. If the requirement were only endpoint availability, D would be appropriate.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Match monitoring depth to the behavior being verified: endpoint checks assess individual responses, synthetic transactions exercise workflows, and log-based alerts detect recorded events. Each provides useful evidence, but they answer different operational questions.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Create a checkout test using controlled test data and meaningful success assertions. Connect its alerting policy to the operational notification channel, then perform a controlled failing test to verify delivery. This validates both the transaction checks and the notification path.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For a recurring user journey with failure alerts, choose a synthetic monitor.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Look for active testing of checkout, login, or another application workflow. Synthetic monitoring provides scripted validation with managed execution and integrated alerting; uptime checks and log alerts cover narrower needs.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/uptime-checks/introduction\"><strong>Google Cloud Monitoring — Synthetic monitoring overview:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Checkout testing, managed execution, and the distinction between synthetic monitors and uptime checks.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/synthetic-monitors/create\"><strong>Google Cloud Monitoring — Create a synthetic monitor:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Cloud Run function execution, test frameworks, scheduling, alerting policies, and notification channels.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/tasks/docs/comp-tasks-sched\"><strong>Google Cloud — Compare Cloud Tasks to Cloud Scheduler:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Individual task scheduling versus recurring execution; option A’s additional orchestration requirements.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/alerting/log-based-alerts\"><strong>Google Cloud Logging — Configure log-based alerting policies:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Notifications based on matching log entries; option C’s passive detection behavior.</p>","lessons":["d4l9"]},"e3q27":{"id":"e3q27","exam":3,"n":27,"domain":1,"html":"<p>Your company is developing a Java web application with AI capabilities. You must provide developers with a secure, managed, cloud-based Java integrated development environment (IDE). Each developer’s instance must have the GPUs required for development attached. Which approach follows Google-recommended practices?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create a dedicated development project with adequate GPU quota, and give developers access to Cloud Shell Editor for that project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Provision a dedicated GPU-equipped workstation for each developer using Cloud Workstations, and grant developers the Cloud Workstations User role.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Create an individual Google Cloud project for each developer, provision a GPU-equipped Compute Engine VM in each project, and grant the developer the Editor role.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Provision a GKE cluster with GPU-equipped nodes, create a dedicated namespace for each developer, and grant each developer access to their namespace.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Provision a dedicated GPU-equipped workstation for each developer using Cloud Workstations, and grant developers the Cloud Workstations User role.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The mandatory requirements are a managed development environment, Java tooling, attached GPUs, and secure developer access. Select the service designed to provide developer workstations with configurable hardware and scoped permissions. Cloud Shell does not meet the hardware requirement, while individual VMs and Kubernetes workspaces require additional environment management.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Managed, cloud-based Java … IDE” — Hard constraint:</strong> Requires a development environment, beyond compute infrastructure alone.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“GPUs … attached” — Hard constraint:</strong> The workstation must support configurable accelerator hardware.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Secure” — Hard constraint:</strong> Developer access should use permissions appropriate to workstation usage.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Google-recommended practices” — Preference:</strong> Favors the purpose-built managed service and appropriately scoped IAM roles.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Workstations configurations define the development tools, IDE image, machine settings, and GPU-compatible placement. Administrators can use a common configuration to provision consistent workstations for developers. GPU selection must match supported machine types and available zones. </p>\n<p>The Cloud Workstations User role, <code>roles/workstations.user</code>, provides workstation access. For a complete implementation, Google’s current access guide also lists Cloud Workstations Operation Viewer, <code>roles/workstations.operationViewer</code>, on the project. This additional setup detail does not change B as the best option. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Cloud Shell offers convenient browser-based development, making it tempting. However, its temporary backing VM is owned by Google and resides outside the developer’s projects. Allocating GPU quota to a development project does not attach GPUs to that Cloud Shell environment. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> GPU-equipped VMs can support Java development, but the company must configure and maintain the IDE environments. Project-level Editor access also grants broader permissions than workstation usage requires. Google permits basic roles in some development scenarios, so Editor is not universally prohibited; it is an inferior fit here. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> GPU-backed Kubernetes infrastructure can run development workloads, but the proposed cluster and namespaces do not themselves supply the requested Java IDE. A workspace platform would still need to be configured and maintained. Cloud Workstations directly provides reproducible development environments through workstation configurations.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Do not equate a managed browser editor with configurable development hardware. Cloud Shell’s project selection does not determine its backing VM’s GPU configuration. Without the GPU requirement, Cloud Shell could be suitable for some browser-based development tasks.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A managed development service combines configurable compute, development tooling, and access controls. Providing a GPU VM or container cluster supplies infrastructure; providing a developer workstation also requires configuring and maintaining the development environment.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Create a shared workstation configuration containing the approved Java tools and GPU settings, then provision individual workstations from it. Central configuration changes apply when workstations next start, reducing configuration drift and repeated setup work. An inactivity shutdown policy can also reduce idle compute spending.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For managed developer IDEs that need attached GPUs, consider Cloud Workstations with workstation-scoped access.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question combines managed cloud development, configurable GPUs, and controlled developer access, choose Cloud Workstations. The most specific blueprint objective is enabling secure cloud development environments. </p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/workstations/docs/create-configuration\"><strong>Google Cloud Workstations — Create a workstation configuration:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: IDE configuration, GPU-compatible placement, reusable environments, inactivity shutdown, and the distinction from assembling VM or Kubernetes workspaces.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/workstations/docs/access-control\"><strong>Google Cloud Workstations — Access control with IAM:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Cloud Workstations User and Operation Viewer roles.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/shell/docs/how-cloud-shell-works\"><strong>Google Cloud — How Cloud Shell works:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Google-owned backing VMs and why development-project GPU quota does not configure Cloud Shell hardware.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/using-iam-securely\"><strong>Google Cloud IAM — Use IAM securely:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Least privilege, narrow permission scope, and the qualified use of basic roles in development environments.</p>","lessons":["d1l11"]},"e3q28":{"id":"e3q28","exam":3,"n":28,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You are deploying a containerized application to Cloud Run. The application requires a password to connect to a database and retrieve data. Your company’s security policy requires the password to be rotated weekly. You need a solution that minimizes the ongoing effort required to manage password rotation. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p><strong>A.</strong> Deploy the application to Cloud Run. Each week, build a new container image with the updated database password configured as an environment variable in the Dockerfile. Deploy the image and migrate traffic to the new revision.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>B.</strong> Store the database password in Secret Manager. Deploy the application to Cloud Run and expose the secret’s <code>latest</code> version as an environment variable. Add a new secret version containing the updated password each week.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>C.</strong> Deploy the application to Cloud Run. Each week, build a new container image and supply the updated database password as a build-time secret. Deploy the image and migrate traffic to the new revision.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>D.</strong> Store the database password in Secret Manager. Deploy the application to Cloud Run and mount the secret’s <code>latest</code> version as a volume. Add a new secret version containing the updated password each week.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Store the database password in Secret Manager. Deploy the application to Cloud Run and mount the secret’s</strong><code>**latest**</code><strong>version as a volume. Add a new secret version containing the updated password each week.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The deciding factor is how the application receives updated credentials. A mounted secret supports runtime access to rotated values without requiring a weekly container rebuild and deployment.</p>\n<p>This question aligns with exam objective 2.3, which covers secret management and build-time versus runtime secret injection. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Weekly password rotation:</strong> Credentials change regularly.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Run:</strong> Supports Secret Manager integration.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Minimal management effort:</strong> Avoid coupling password changes to image builds and application deployments.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Run retrieves the secret value when the application reads the mounted file. Referencing <code>latest</code> therefore lets subsequent reads obtain the updated password. Google explicitly identifies mounted secrets as suitable for rotation. The application must reread the file rather than retain the password indefinitely in memory. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Rebuilding an image, deploying it, and shifting traffic every week adds unnecessary operational work.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Secret-backed environment variables are resolved when an instance starts. Updating <code>latest</code> does not refresh the variable in an already running instance. Google recommends selecting a specific secret version for environment variables. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>C — Different Use Case:</strong> Build-time secrets supply credentials to the build process. They do not establish runtime retrieval of the database password, and this option still requires weekly builds and deployments.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The <code>latest</code> alias does not make an environment variable refresh dynamically. Secret delivery and application consumption determine when a changed value takes effect.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A Secret Manager secret contains immutable, numbered versions. Rotation adds a new version, and <code>latest</code> refers to the most recently created version. Your application’s access strategy determines how that version reaches running workloads. </p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Coordinate the database credential change with publishing the new secret version, validate the password, and ensure the application reloads it when needed. Adding a version in Secret Manager alone does not update the database password. Google recommends a rotation workflow that updates the external system and Secret Manager together. </p>\n<p>For automation, a Secret Manager rotation schedule sends Pub/Sub notifications; a subscriber must perform the rotation steps. </p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For Cloud Run password rotation, mount <code>latest</code> and reread the file.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Choose <strong>D</strong>. Mounted secrets support updated values during runtime, while secret-backed environment variables are resolved at instance startup.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/configuring/services/secrets\"><strong>Configure secrets for services — Cloud Run</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Mounted secret behavior, environment variable resolution, and rotation suitability.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/secret-manager/docs/rotation-recommendations\"><strong>About rotation schedules — Secret Manager</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Secret versions, the <code>latest</code> alias, and coordination with external credential systems.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/secret-manager/docs/secret-rotation\"><strong>Create rotation schedules in Secret Manager</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Pub/Sub rotation notifications and the requirement for a subscriber to execute rotation.</p>","lessons":["d2l8"]},"e3q29":{"id":"e3q29","exam":3,"n":29,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You manage the reliability of an internally developed, distributed file-storage service that processes thousands of uploads and downloads each day. You need to select a service level indicator (SLI) and use it to support alerts for potential service problems. Which metric best represents the reliability experienced when using the service?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Mean response time of API operations such as <code>get</code>, <code>put</code>, and <code>list</code>.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Count of successfully completed file uploads and downloads per minute.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Mean size of the objects stored by the service.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Number of successful API calls divided by the total number of attempted API calls.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Number of successful API calls divided by the total number of attempted API calls.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The indicator should show whether users can successfully perform operations. A success ratio directly measures that outcome while accounting for changing request volume. Latency measures responsiveness, successful-operation counts measure throughput, and object size describes the workload. D therefore provides the strongest measure of operational availability.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Uploads and downloads” — Technical signal:</strong> Reliability should reflect whether requested operations succeed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Thousands … each day” — Technical signal:</strong> Request outcomes provide a useful population of measurable events.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Reliability experienced when using the service” — Hard constraint:</strong> Prefer a user-outcome measure over workload characteristics.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Support alerts” — Technical signal:</strong> The indicator should support comparison against a defined reliability objective.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Google’s SRE guidance recommends expressing SLIs as good events divided by total events. For request-driven availability:</p>\n<p>\\[ \\text{Availability SLI} = \\frac{\\text{Successful API calls}}{\\text{Total attempted API calls}} \\]</p>\n<p>Both counts must cover the same measurement period and request population. The result is dimensionless and can be expressed as a percentage. For example, \\(9{,}990/10{,}000 \\times 100 = 99.9\\%\\). </p>\n<p>For implementation, explicitly define eligible requests and successful completion so that the measurement reflects the service contract. Google commonly defines request-based availability using well-formed requests that succeed. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Latency is a valid SLI for responsiveness, but it does not directly measure successful completion. Requests can fail quickly while average latency remains low. Averages can also conceal slow requests in the distribution’s tail.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Successful operations per minute measures throughput. Without total attempts, it cannot distinguish reduced demand from increased failures or reveal the fraction of users whose operations fail.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> Average object size helps characterize storage workloads and capacity needs. It does not indicate whether uploads or downloads complete successfully.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>A large number of successful operations can coexist with many failures. The denominator reveals the fraction of attempted work that succeeds. If the requirement instead asked for successful processing volume per minute, B would be appropriate.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An SLI measures observed service behavior; an SLO sets the target for that behavior. Success ratio measures availability, while latency and durability capture additional aspects of storage reliability. One indicator does not fully describe every user expectation.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Instrument attempted and successful operations, then define an availability SLO using their ratio. Configure error-budget burn-rate alerts to notify the team when failures threaten that objective. This ties alerts to reliability impact rather than ordinary changes in traffic volume. </p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>To measure whether operations work, divide successful attempts by total attempts.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For request-based availability, look for a success ratio with a meaningful denominator. Counts describe volume, latency describes speed, and neither alone establishes the proportion of successful operations.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — Implementing SLOs:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Good-event ratios, request-based availability, and the relationship between SLIs and SLOs.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/\"><strong>Google SRE Book — Service Level Objectives:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Availability, latency, throughput, durability, and the limitations of averages.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring/alerting-on-budget-burn-rate\"><strong>Google Cloud Observability — Alerting on your burn rate:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Using SLO-based error-budget alerts to detect reliability risks.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Objective 3.1, covering the definition of SLIs, SLOs, and error budgets.</p>","lessons":["d3l1"]},"e3q30":{"id":"e3q30","exam":3,"n":30,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your team needs to debug a live issue affecting a workload running in a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster that has Binary Authorization enabled. To investigate, you want to launch a short-lived diagnostic Pod using an unsigned utility image pulled from Artifact Registry. When you apply the Pod manifest, the Binary Authorization policy blocks the deployment. You need the approach that lets the Pod run while keeping the deployment as secure and auditable as possible. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Change the enforcementMode setting in the Binary Authorization policy to ALWAYS_ALLOW.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Add the image-policy.k8s.io/break-glass label to the Pod specification.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Temporarily turn off Binary Authorization on the GKE cluster.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Add the diagnostic image to the admissionWhitelistPatterns entry of the Binary Authorization policy.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Add the image-policy.k8s.io/break-glass label to the Pod specification.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Start by separating the hard requirement from the preferences. The hard requirement is: the Pod must deploy despite failing the policy. The preferences are: scope the exception as narrowly as possible, and leave an audit trail. Every option satisfies the hard requirement — all four would get the Pod running. The question is decided entirely by the preferences. Break-glass satisfies both: it is applied per-Pod (not cluster-wide or policy-wide), it requires no edit to the stored policy object, and Google Cloud explicitly logs break-glass usage so security teams can review every emergency bypass after the fact. The other three options all widen the blast radius or remove the audit trail to varying degrees, so the decision collapses to “which option is scoped to exactly this one Pod and still gets logged” — and only break-glass is.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“short-lived diagnostic Pod”</strong> — a sequence/cardinality-style signal that the fix should be scoped to one Pod, not the cluster or the policy.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“unsigned utility image”</strong> — confirms the image genuinely fails attestation; this rules out any answer that claims the image would pass normally.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Binary Authorization policy rejects the deployment”</strong> — tells you the policy is doing its job correctly; the fix should not be “make the policy stop doing its job everywhere.”</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“most secure and auditable solution”</strong> — a decisive preference signal (a “BEST”-type qualifier): multiple options can technically work, so you must rank them by scope and logging, not just functionality.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>The break-glass label is a Binary Authorization-specific override mechanism built for exactly this scenario: a one-off, emergency deployment of an image that would otherwise be denied. Setting <code>image-policy.k8s.io/break-glass: \"true\"</code> on the Pod spec tells the admission webhook to let this single Pod through without touching the policy document itself, and Binary Authorization writes a log entry recording that the override was used. Once you delete the Pod and remove the label, there is nothing left over to clean up in the policy — the exception existed only for the lifetime of that one Pod.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Setting the policy to always allow (regardless of whether this is read as the evaluationMode or enforcementMode field) turns off attestation checking for every deployment the policy covers, not just your one diagnostic Pod. It trades “most secure” for “least secure” and has to be manually reverted, which is also the opposite of auditable-by-default.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Disabling Binary Authorization on the cluster removes image-signature enforcement for every workload on that cluster for as long as it stays off, including unrelated production Pods. It is the broadest possible exception and the easiest one to forget to re-enable.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> admissionWhitelistPatterns is a legitimate field, but it is a permanent, policy-level allowlist entry, normally reserved for known infrastructure images (for example, Google system images) that should always bypass checks. Using it for a one-time debug image means the exemption persists in the policy after your troubleshooting is done unless someone remembers to remove the pattern — less scoped and less self-cleaning than a per-Pod break-glass label.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “it will make the Pod deploy” as the only test. A/C/D all work mechanically, so a learner who stops at “does this unblock the deployment?” has no way to pick one. The exam is really testing whether you recognize break-glass as the purpose-built, narrowly scoped, auto-logged mechanism versus the broader, policy-level levers. A secondary trap in option A is the field name itself: ALWAYS_ALLOW is actually a value of a policy’s evaluationMode, not its enforcementMode — conflating the two is a realistic distractor pattern, and recognizing that mismatch is itself a clue that A isn’t describing a real, well-formed configuration.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Binary Authorization enforces a deploy-time policy (“only run images that meet these attestation requirements”) independent of how the image got into the cluster. Any override mechanism you choose should be evaluated the same way you’d evaluate least-privilege access in IAM: prefer the narrowest, most temporary, most observable exception over a broad or permanent one. Break-glass exists specifically to give you a narrow, logged escape hatch instead of forcing a choice between “can’t debug” and “turn off security.”</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team that standardizes on break-glass for debug Pods can alert on every break-glass log entry and review them weekly, catching both legitimate emergency use and any accidental or malicious misuse quickly. A team that instead reaches for admissionWhitelistPatterns or disabling enforcement tends to accumulate stale exceptions in the policy over time, quietly widening the cluster’s attack surface long after the original incident is resolved.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Debug one Pod → break-glass label; change the rules for everyone → anything else.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a Binary Authorization question asks for the “most secure and auditable” way to run one noncompliant image, look for the option scoped to a single Pod with built-in logging — that’s break-glass. Any option that edits the policy itself or turns enforcement off is a broader, less reversible change and is rarely the best answer for a temporary, single-use need.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs/using-breakglass\">Google Cloud — Use breakglass (GKE, Distributed Cloud): </a>Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (the <code>image-policy.k8s.io/break-glass</code> label and its audit logging behavior)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs/policy-yaml-reference\">Google Cloud — Binary Authorization Policy YAML reference: </a>Supports: the admissionWhitelistPatterns, enforcementMode, and evaluationMode field definitions used to eliminate options A and D</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l9"]},"e3q31":{"id":"e3q31","exam":3,"n":31,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your company is rolling out supply chain security controls for its containerized applications running on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). You need the ability to trace a container image running in production back to the exact source and build process that produced it, and your solution needs to follow Google-recommended practice for software supply chain security. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Store every build artifact in Artifact Registry, and turn on automatic vulnerability scanning.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Enable the Container Analysis API, configure Cloud Build to use only official base images, and turn on automatic vulnerability scanning.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Run every GKE node pool on Container-Optimized OS, enable Binary Authorization on the cluster, and turn on automatic security updates.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Enable the Container Analysis API, configure Cloud Build to generate and sign build provenance attestations for every build, and configure Binary Authorization to verify those attestations before deployment.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Enable the Container Analysis API, configure Cloud Build to generate and sign build provenance attestations for every build, and configure Binary Authorization to verify those attestations before deployment.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard requirement is traceability: proving which source commit and which build process produced a given production image, not just whether that image contains known vulnerabilities or runs on a hardened host. Only one mechanism actually records that chain of custody — build provenance, which Cloud Build can generate and cryptographically sign for every build, storing the metadata via the Container Analysis API. Vulnerability scanning (A, B) and node-level hardening (C) are both legitimate supply chain security practices, but neither one captures or proves where an image came from. Binary Authorization only becomes meaningful for traceability once it’s verifying a real provenance attestation; configured on its own (as in C), it enforces nothing specific about the build’s origin. D is the only option where generation (Cloud Build signs provenance) and enforcement (Binary Authorization verifies it) are both present.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“trace container images used in production back to their source and build processes”</strong> — a technical signal pointing specifically at build provenance, not vulnerability data or host configuration.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“supply chain security controls”</strong> — a scope clue broad enough to fit several Google Cloud features, which is why the exam relies on the traceability requirement to pick among them.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“follow Google-recommended practices”</strong> — points at Cloud Build’s documented SLSA-aligned provenance generation combined with Binary Authorization verification, which Google explicitly describes as its software supply chain security approach.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Google-recommended practices for software supply chain security”</strong> paired with “trace... back to... source and build processes” together rule out any option that stops at scanning or OS hardening without a provenance mechanism.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Build can generate build provenance for the images it produces, meeting SLSA Level 3 assurance, and sign that provenance as an attestation; this metadata (recording the source, build steps, and builder identity) is stored through the Container Analysis API. Configuring Binary Authorization to verify these attestations before deployment means only images with valid, verifiable provenance from your build process can run in GKE — giving you exactly the ability to trace a running production image back to the specific build that created it, and to block anything that lacks that proof.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong> Storing artifacts in Artifact Registry and scanning them for vulnerabilities tells you what known issues exist inside an image’s packages. It says nothing about which source commit or build pipeline produced the image, so it doesn’t satisfy the traceability requirement at all.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Enabling the Container Analysis API is a necessary underlying piece (it’s the same metadata store that provenance attestations use), and restricting to official base images improves supply chain hygiene at the base-layer level. But without Cloud Build actually generating and signing provenance, and without Binary Authorization verifying it, there’s no enforced record proving which build process produced a given image — the API is enabled but nothing is using it to establish traceability.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> Container-Optimized OS and automatic security updates harden the GKE node/host layer, which is a different part of the supply chain than the container image’s build history. Enabling Binary Authorization without pointing it at a provenance-based attestor enforces nothing specific about where the image came from — the cluster-level controls here address runtime infrastructure security, not image traceability.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “supply chain security” as satisfied by any combination of recognizable security features — vulnerability scanning, official base images, hardened nodes, Binary Authorization by name. The question’s actual requirement is narrower and specific (traceability to source and build process), and only the option that generates and verifies build provenance actually answers that, regardless of how many other legitimate security practices the distractors stack together.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Software supply chain security has several distinct layers: what’s inside an image (vulnerability scanning), what runs the image (node/OS hardening), and where the image came from (build provenance and attestation). A control from one layer doesn’t substitute for another — proving an image’s origin specifically requires generating verifiable provenance at build time and enforcing its presence at deploy time, which is what the SLSA framework and Binary Authorization’s attestation model are designed to provide together.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team that only scans images for vulnerabilities can tell you an image is currently “clean,” but if asked “did this exact image come from our official CI pipeline, built from the commit we think it was,” they have no answer. A team that generates signed build provenance and enforces it with Binary Authorization can answer that question for every image running in production, which is exactly what’s needed during an incident investigation or a supply chain audit.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Scanning tells you what’s inside an image; provenance plus Binary Authorization tells you where it came from — traceability needs the second one.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question specifically asks to trace a container image back to its source and build process, look for build provenance generation (Cloud Build) paired with Binary Authorization verification — vulnerability scanning and node-level hardening are supply chain practices too, but they don’t establish or enforce an image’s origin.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Build overview (build provenance and SLSA)</strong></a><strong>:</strong> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (Cloud Build generating SLSA Level 3 build provenance and signing it as an attestation)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs/deploy-cloud-build\"><strong>Google Cloud — Securing deployments by only allowing images built by Cloud Build (Binary Authorization):</strong></a>Supports: Binary Authorization verifying Cloud Build-signed attestations before deployment, and the elimination of option C’s unconfigured Binary Authorization</p>","lessons":["d2l9"]},"e3q32":{"id":"e3q32","exam":3,"n":32,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You are designing the hosting architecture on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) for a set of business-critical applications. These applications already expose custom metrics in Prometheus format for monitoring. You need to collect these application metrics so they can be used for alerting and troubleshooting. You want to minimize ongoing manual effort and maintenance while following Google-recommended practices. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Deploy your own Prometheus servers inside the GKE clusters, and configure them to scrape the custom metrics and forward the data to the Cloud Monitoring API.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Deploy the OpenTelemetry Collector inside the GKE clusters, and configure it to collect and export the custom metrics to Cloud Monitoring.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Change each application so it pushes its custom metrics directly to Cloud Monitoring through the Cloud Monitoring API.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Enable Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus collection on the GKE clusters to collect the custom metrics.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Enable Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus collection on the GKE clusters to collect the custom metrics.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Separate the hard requirement from the preferences. The hard requirement is: collect Prometheus-format custom metrics from GKE workloads for alerting and troubleshooting. The preferences are: minimize manual effort/maintenance, and follow Google’s recommended practice. All four options can technically get the metrics into Cloud Monitoring, so the preferences decide the answer. Managed Service for Prometheus is Google’s purpose-built, fully managed Prometheus-compatible service for exactly this scenario: it ingests standard Prometheus-exposed metrics from GKE workloads without you operating the Prometheus server stack yourself, and Google explicitly documents it as the solution that avoids manually managing and operating Prometheus at scale. That satisfies both preferences at once, which is why it beats the other three, each of which trades away one of them.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“provide custom metrics for monitoring with Prometheus”</strong> — a technical-signal clue: the applications already speak the Prometheus exposition format, so the answer should consume that format natively rather than requiring re-instrumentation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“for alerting and troubleshooting purposes”</strong> — tells you the destination needs to be Cloud Monitoring (where alerting policies and dashboards live), ruling out any option that stops short of getting data there.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“minimize the amount of manual effort and maintenance required”</strong> — a decisive preference/exclusion clue: it eliminates any option that makes you operate infrastructure (a self-run Prometheus server) or rewrite application code.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“following Google-recommended practices”</strong> — points directly at the option Google documents as its recommended, fully managed path for this exact use case.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus is Google’s fully managed Prometheus-compatible collection and storage backend. On GKE it can scrape Prometheus-format metrics, including your applications’ custom metrics, and send them into Cloud Monitoring, where they become available for alerting policies, dashboards, and PromQL-based troubleshooting. Because collection is managed by Google and is enabled by default on GKE clusters (with configuration needed only to pick up additional, non-system metrics like your custom ones), you avoid standing up, scaling, and operating a Prometheus server yourself.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Running your own Prometheus servers in-cluster absolutely works and is the traditional way to scrape Prometheus metrics, but you take on everything Managed Service for Prometheus exists to remove: sizing, scaling, storage, and high availability for the Prometheus server itself. That directly conflicts with “minimize manual effort and maintenance.”</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> The OpenTelemetry Collector can scrape Prometheus-format metrics and export them (including to Managed Service for Prometheus as a backend), so it’s a legitimate pattern in some architectures. But deploying and configuring your own collector is additional infrastructure you have to manage, and it isn’t Google’s primary recommended, lowest-effort path when your workloads already emit standard Prometheus metrics on GKE — enabling the managed collection directly is simpler and requires no collector of your own.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Rewriting every application to push metrics via the Cloud Monitoring API means touching application code for every service, discarding the Prometheus instrumentation you already have. This is the most manual-effort-intensive option of the four, the opposite of what the scenario asks for.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating A, B, and D as interchangeable because they all “get Prometheus metrics into Cloud Monitoring.” The question is decided by the maintenance-burden qualifier, not by capability — once you notice “minimize manual effort and maintenance” plus “Google-recommended,” the fully managed service is the only option that doesn’t require you to run or configure a collection component yourself.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>When a workload already speaks a standard metrics protocol (here, Prometheus exposition format), the lowest-maintenance integration path is almost always a managed, protocol-compatible collector service rather than (a) a self-hosted version of the same open-source tool, (b) a general-purpose collector you must configure yourself, or (c) changing the application to speak a different, proprietary API.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team that self-hosts Prometheus for dozens of GKE clusters typically ends up owning its own on-call rotation just for Prometheus storage and scaling issues. Switching the same workloads to Managed Service for Prometheus collection removes that operational surface entirely while keeping the same PromQL queries, dashboards, and alerting workflows the team already built.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Already emitting Prometheus metrics on GKE and want the lowest-maintenance path into Cloud Monitoring → Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Whenever a GKE question stresses “custom/application Prometheus metrics,” “minimize manual effort,” and “Google-recommended,” the answer is almost always Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus rather than a self-run Prometheus server, a generic collector, or application code changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/integrate/gke\">Google Cloud — Managed Service for Prometheus overview (metrics collection on GKE): </a>Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (fully managed, Google-recommended Prometheus-compatible collection on GKE)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/collect-specific-prometheus-metrics\">Google Cloud — Collect specific Prometheus metrics on GKE: </a>Supports: how custom/user-defined Prometheus metrics (beyond built-in system metrics) are configured for collection, distinguishing D from a default/no-config scenario</p>","lessons":["d4l7"]},"e3q33":{"id":"e3q33","exam":3,"n":33,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your team operates a high-traffic application that produces several terabytes of log data every day. You need to lower your Cloud Logging costs without losing critical operational and security information. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Configure an exclusion filter that drops DEBUG-level logs, and set up an export of ERROR-level logs to BigQuery.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Reduce the retention period for the _Default logging bucket from its current setting down to 15 days.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Turn on sampling across every log severity level in the _Default logging bucket.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Build log-based metrics for the key indicators you care about, and configure an exclusion filter that drops all non-ERROR logs.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Build log-based metrics for the key indicators you care about, and configure an exclusion filter that drops all non-ERROR logs.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Separate the hard requirement from the preference: the hard requirement is “retain critical operational and security information” (so nothing can silently drop ERROR-level or security-relevant entries), and the preference is “lower Cloud Logging costs” for a workload whose dominant cost driver is ingestion volume, not retention length. An exclusion filter on everything except ERROR logs attacks the actual volume driver directly — the non-error traffic/debug noise that makes up the bulk of “several terabytes daily” — while leaving ERROR logs flowing into Cloud Logging untouched. Log-based metrics then recover the operational visibility you’d otherwise lose from the excluded logs, because metric evaluation happens independently of exclusion filtering, so a metric can still count and track key indicators even from logs that are never stored or billed as ingested volume. That combination is the only option that meaningfully cuts the real cost driver while provably keeping both the critical logs and ongoing visibility intact.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“several terabytes of logs daily”</strong> — a technical signal that the cost problem is ingestion volume, which points toward exclusion/filtering rather than a retention-period tweak.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“retaining critical operational and security information”</strong> — a hard constraint/exclusion signal: any option that could drop ERROR-level or security-relevant entries, even partially, fails this requirement outright.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“reduce Cloud Logging costs”</strong> — favors the option that removes the largest share of billed log volume, not the option with the smallest or most indirect cost effect.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“non-ERROR logs”</strong> in option D vs <strong>“DEBUG logs”</strong> in option A — a scope clue: excluding only DEBUG leaves other high-volume levels (such as INFO-level request logs typical of a high-traffic app) fully billed, while excluding everything except ERROR removes far more of the volume.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Logging’s exclusion filters prevent matching log entries from being ingested into a bucket (and billed), so excluding all non-ERROR logs removes the great majority of a high-traffic application’s log volume at the source. Because ERROR logs are explicitly left out of the exclusion, they keep flowing into Cloud Logging exactly as before, satisfying the “critical operational and security information” requirement. Layering log-based metrics on top closes the remaining gap: a log-based metric’s filter is evaluated against the log stream independently of exclusion filters, so you can still track counts or trends for key indicators (for example, warning rates or specific event types) drawn from logs that are no longer stored, at a fraction of the cost of retaining the full log volume.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Excluding only DEBUG-level logs leaves every other non-error level (most notably INFO, which is typically where high-volume per-request logging lives) fully ingested and billed, so it addresses only a slice of the actual volume problem. Exporting ERROR logs to BigQuery is also reasonable for durable analysis, but on its own it adds a second storage destination rather than removing cost, and nothing in this option stops ERROR logs from also continuing to be stored (and billed) in the _Default bucket.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> Cloud Logging’s cost driver for a high-volume workload is ingestion, not how long data sits in the bucket afterward, so shortening retention from 30 to 15 days does little to address “several terabytes daily.” Worse, it shortens how long operational and security logs remain available for investigation, directly working against “retaining critical operational and security information.”</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Applying sampling indiscriminately across every severity level means a fraction of ERROR and security-relevant log entries gets dropped right along with the low-value noise. That’s the one outcome the scenario explicitly rules out — losing some of the critical information you were told to keep.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming “any filtering or sampling mechanism that cuts volume” is equally valid. The scenario’s real constraint is which logs you’re allowed to touch: DEBUG-only filtering (A) is too narrow, retention shortening (B) doesn’t touch volume at all, and blanket sampling (C) touches the one category you must not touch (ERROR/security). Only D’s “exclude everything except ERROR” scope satisfies both the cost goal and the retention constraint simultaneously.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>In Cloud Logging, cost control and information retention are solved by two different mechanisms that can be combined: exclusion filters decide what gets stored (and billed) at all, while log-based metrics let you keep lightweight, aggregated visibility into logs you’ve chosen not to store in full. Treating “what do I keep in full,” “what do I just count,” and “what do I drop entirely” as three separate decisions — rather than one blanket setting — is what lets you cut cost without losing the signal you actually need.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team that applies a single blunt setting, like reducing retention or sampling everything uniformly, often discovers the gap only during an incident or security review, when the exact log entries needed are missing. A team that excludes low-value logs by category while routing key indicators through log-based metrics keeps both a low Cloud Logging bill and a reliable audit trail for the entries that actually matter operationally and for security.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Cut cost by excluding everything except what must be kept (ERROR), and recover visibility into the rest with metrics, not by shortening retention or sampling indiscriminately.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a Cloud Logging cost question also demands you keep critical/security information intact, look for the option that scopes exclusion precisely to the non-critical log levels and recovers lost visibility through log-based metrics — a retention-period change or blanket sampling will almost always risk the exact data you were told to protect.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/exclusions\"><strong>Google Cloud — Exclusion filters in Cloud Logging</strong></a><strong>:</strong> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (excluding non-ERROR logs from ingestion to cut cost) and the option C distinction (sampling via exclusion filters is meant to be scoped away from critical severities, not applied to all of them)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/guides/filter-logs\">Google Cloud — Control Dataflow log ingestion (exclusion filters and logs-based metrics): </a>Supports: the key technical fact that exclusion filters do not apply to user-defined logs-based metrics, which is why option D still provides visibility into excluded logs</p>","lessons":["d4l2"]},"e3q34":{"id":"e3q34","exam":3,"n":34,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You’re designing a containerized CI pipeline and want to reduce how much the pipeline depends on public upstream container registries, while improving build reproducibility and security. You need a design that caches every containerized dependency the pipeline uses, while keeping ongoing manual effort and maintenance to a minimum. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A.</p>\n<p>Create a remote Docker repository in Artifact Registry for each upstream registry you depend on.</p>\n<p>Have the CI pipeline pull container images directly through these remote repositories.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B.</p>\n<p>Create a standard Docker repository in Artifact Registry for each upstream registry you depend on.</p>\n<p>Upload the container images used by the build pipelines into the appropriate repositories yourself.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C.</p>\n<p>Create a standard Docker repository in Artifact Registry for each upstream registry you depend on.</p>\n<p>Create a single, central virtual Docker repository that fronts these standard repositories.</p>\n<p>Have the CI pipeline pull container images through the central virtual repository.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D.</p>\n<p>Create a remote Docker repository in Artifact Registry for each upstream registry you depend on.</p>\n<p>Create a single, central virtual Docker repository that fronts these remote repositories.</p>\n<p>Have the CI pipeline pull container images through the central virtual repository.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Create a remote Docker repository in Artifact Registry for each upstream registry you depend on.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Create a single, central virtual Docker repository that fronts these remote repositories.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Have the CI pipeline pull container images through the central virtual repository.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Separate the two things the stem is asking for: caching needs to happen automatically (minimal manual effort), and the CI pipeline needs a simple, low-maintenance way to consume it (minimal ongoing maintenance). A remote repository solves the first part on its own — it’s a proxy that downloads and caches a package from its upstream the first time it’s requested, with no manual upload step. A virtual repository solves the second part — it gives every consumer one single address instead of one address per upstream source, which is what actually keeps maintenance low as the number of upstream registries grows. Only option D combines both: automatic caching per upstream, consolidated behind one endpoint the pipeline references.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“minimize reliance on public upstream container registries”</strong> — a technical signal pointing at a caching proxy layer (remote repositories) sitting between the pipeline and the public registries.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“cache all containerized dependencies”</strong> — a scope clue: the mechanism must handle every image automatically, ruling out anything requiring someone to manually populate a repository.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“minimizing the required amount of manual effort and maintenance”</strong> — a decisive preference clue that applies twice: once to rule out manual population (standard repos with manual uploads), and again to favor a single consolidated access point over one per upstream source.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“improve build reproducibility and security”</strong> — reinforces that the goal is a controlled, cached, Google-managed path to every dependency, rather than the pipeline reaching out to each public registry directly every time.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A remote Docker repository per upstream registry gives you automatic, no-touch caching: Artifact Registry fetches and stores each image the first time it’s requested, and subsequent requests are served from that cache rather than from the public internet, directly reducing reliance on upstream registries without anyone uploading anything. Layering a virtual Docker repository on top of those remote repositories gives the CI pipeline one single Docker endpoint to reference, regardless of how many different upstream registries are actually behind it — this is the documented pattern Google Cloud itself describes (a virtual repository serving as the single access point in front of remote, caching repositories), and it’s what keeps future maintenance low as upstream sources are added, removed, or reconfigured.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Remote repositories correctly provide automatic caching, so this option does satisfy the “minimal manual effort to populate the cache” part. But without a virtual repository, the CI pipeline and every Dockerfile or build config has to know and reference the specific remote repository address for each upstream source individually, which is more to configure and maintain as the number of upstream registries grows.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> Standard repositories don’t cache anything automatically; someone has to manually pull each image from its upstream source and push it into the right standard repository, and keep repeating that process every time a new version is needed. That’s the opposite of “minimizing manual effort and maintenance,” and it also means the cache isn’t guaranteed to be complete for every dependency the build actually uses.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Adding a virtual repository here only solves the “single endpoint” half of the problem. The standard repositories behind it still don’t cache anything on their own, so this design still requires the same manual upload process as option B — it just hides that manual process behind a consolidated address.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “virtual repository” and “remote repository” as interchangeable ways to get caching, when only remote repositories actually proxy and cache an external upstream. A virtual repository is purely a routing/consolidation layer — pairing it with standard repositories (option C) looks like a complete solution but silently reintroduces the manual-upload problem that remote repositories exist to eliminate.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>In Artifact Registry, each repository type serves a distinct purpose: standard repositories hold artifacts you explicitly publish, remote repositories automatically proxy and cache an external upstream, and virtual repositories provide one unified address across any mix of standard and remote repositories. Solving “cache external dependencies with minimal effort” requires remote repositories specifically; solving “give consumers one simple, low-maintenance endpoint” requires a virtual repository on top — and most real designs that need both properties combine the two, exactly as Google’s own documentation illustrates.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A CI pipeline built directly against several different remote-repository URLs (one per upstream vendor) works, but every new base image source means another endpoint to add to build configs across every repository that uses it. Fronting all of those remote repositories with one virtual repository means onboarding a new upstream source, or swapping which backing repository serves a given namespace, is a change made once in the virtual repository’s configuration, with no changes required in any individual pipeline or Dockerfile.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Caching needs a remote repository; one single address for everything needs a virtual repository on top of it — you typically want both together.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks to cache public upstream dependencies in Artifact Registry with minimal manual effort and maintenance, look for remote repositories (for the automatic caching) combined with a virtual repository (for the single, low-maintenance access point) — standard repositories alone always imply manual population, no matter what’s layered on top of them.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/repositories/remote-overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — About remote repositories:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (automatic download-and-cache behavior on first request, with no manual upload step)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/repositories\">Google Cloud — Repository overview (standard, remote, and virtual repositories): </a>Supports: the virtual-repository consolidation pattern (a single access point fronting remote and standard repositories) and the elimination of options B and C, where standard repositories require manual publishing</p>","lessons":["d2l5"]},"e3q35":{"id":"e3q35","exam":3,"n":35,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You manage a critical, high-traffic application running on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). You are about to release a new version that makes significant changes to the payment processing workflow. You need a deployment approach that minimizes risk and automatically rolls back the release if it turns out to be unstable. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use Cloud Deploy to run a canary deployment. Configure the delivery pipeline with a multi-phase canary sequence that starts at a 5% traffic split, define success criteria from application telemetry in Cloud Monitoring, and configure Cloud Deploy to automatically roll back the release if the new version fails verification.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Configure your GKE deployment manifest to follow a blue-green pattern: deploy the new version as a “green” deployment next to the existing “blue” one, then, once “green” is live, update the Service selector to gradually move user traffic over to the new version.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Edit the GKE Service manifest to add a selector for the v2 Pods, then create a second GKE Deployment for the new version, and gradually shift traffic by scaling the new Deployment’s replica count up while scaling the old Deployment’s replica count down.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Use the GKE Deployment’s default rolling update strategy, monitor logs and metrics in Cloud Monitoring while it rolls out, and if a problem appears, run kubectl rollout undo to revert the application to the previous stable version.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Use Cloud Deploy to run a canary deployment. Configure the delivery pipeline with a multi-phase canary sequence that starts at a 5% traffic split, define success criteria from application telemetry in Cloud Monitoring, and configure Cloud Deploy to automatically roll back the release if the new version fails verification.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>There are two hard requirements stacked in this stem: the release must minimize risk, and it must roll back automatically — not “be rollback-able” or “be rolled back once someone notices.” Every option can plausibly reduce risk through gradual traffic exposure, so the deciding factor is whether rollback is actually automated or depends on a human noticing a problem and taking action. Cloud Deploy’s canary strategy combined with its verification and automation features is the only option where both a defined, telemetry-based pass/fail check and the rollback action itself are handled by the platform rather than by a person — which is exactly what “automatically rolls back” requires.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“significant changes to its payment processing workflow”</strong> — signals high blast-radius risk, favoring a small initial exposure (a low traffic percentage) over an all-at-once or coarse-grained cutover.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“minimizes risk”</strong> — favors a progressive, percentage-based rollout over a strategy that exposes 100% of either version at once.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“automatically rolls back if the release proves to be unstable”</strong> — a decisive requirement/exclusion clue: it rules out any option whose rollback step depends on a person detecting the issue and running a command.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“5% traffic split”</strong> paired with <strong>“success criteria using application telemetry”</strong> — technical anchors that map directly to Cloud Deploy’s canary phases and deploy-analysis/verification features.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Deploy’s canary deployment strategy lets you define a sequence of traffic percentages (such as 5%, then larger phases) for a progressive rollout, and its verification (deploy analysis) feature lets you gate each phase on real telemetry, including metrics pulled from Cloud Monitoring. When you pair this with a Cloud Deploy automation rule configured to repair/roll back a failed rollout, a failed verification at any phase triggers rollback to the last known-good release without a human needing to intervene — satisfying both “minimizes risk” (small initial exposure, telemetry-gated progression) and “automatically rolls back” in one coherent, Google-recommended pipeline.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> Nothing in this option describes an automated verification step or an automated rollback trigger; “gradually switch” traffic via a Service selector is also not how plain Kubernetes Service selectors behave — a selector matches one set of Pods or another, so the actual traffic cutover here is a discrete switch, not a graduated one, and any rollback would have to be performed manually by reverting the selector.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> This is an entirely manual, hand-rolled approximation of a canary pattern, with no defined success criteria and no automated decision point. Nothing rolls anything back automatically; a person has to watch the rollout and decide when and whether to reverse the replica counts.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> This option explicitly describes a human detecting the issue from logs and metrics and then manually running kubectl rollout undo. That is a manual rollback by definition, directly contradicting the stated requirement that the release roll back automatically.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is that B, C, and D all sound like reasonable progressive-delivery or safety practices in isolation (blue-green, manual traffic shifting, rolling updates with monitoring). A learner who stops at “does this reduce risk somewhat?” can talk themselves into any of them. The question is actually testing whether you notice that only one option ties a defined, telemetry-based success/failure check directly to an automated rollback action, rather than to a person’s judgment call.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A deployment strategy that “automatically rolls back” needs three pieces working together: a gradual exposure mechanism (so a bad release affects few users), a defined, machine-evaluable success criterion (so “unstable” has a precise meaning), and an automation that acts on that criterion without waiting for a human. Progressive delivery tools like Cloud Deploy are built to wire these three pieces together; hand-built traffic-shifting with manual monitoring only ever provides the first piece.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A payments team that relies on “watch the dashboards and run rollout undo if something looks wrong” is exposed to detection lag — by the time a person notices elevated error rates and runs the command, a meaningful number of payment transactions may already have hit the broken code path. Wiring verification directly to an automation rule means the bad version is rolled back within the same phase window it was detected in, often before it has reached a large percentage of traffic.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Automatically rolls back” means the platform decides and acts — if a human has to notice and run a command, it isn’t automatic.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks for a deployment strategy that “automatically rolls back,” look for the option where both the success/failure check and the rollback action are configured as part of the delivery pipeline (Cloud Deploy verification plus an automation rule) — any option that ends in “a person monitors and then manually intervenes” fails that requirement regardless of how gradual the rollout itself is.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/deployment-strategies/canary\">Google Cloud — Use a canary deployment strategy (Cloud Deploy): </a>Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (percentage-based canary phases and deploy analysis using Cloud Monitoring/Google Cloud Observability metrics)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/automation\">Google Cloud — Automate your deployment (Cloud Deploy): </a>Supports: the automatic-rollback mechanism (a repair-rollout automation rule that acts on a failed verification without manual intervention), used to eliminate options B, C, and D</p>","lessons":["d2l4","d2l6"]},"e3q36":{"id":"e3q36","exam":3,"n":36,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You manage a complex microservices application running on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), and you recently instrumented your services with OpenTelemetry to produce traces. While investigating performance in Cloud Trace, you spot a transaction with unusually high latency. The trace waterfall doesn’t give you enough detail to pin down the root cause, and you suspect the delay originates in the service’s business logic. This service already sends structured logs to Cloud Logging. You need to quickly analyze the logs that correspond to this specific high-latency request. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. In the Cloud Trace waterfall view, note the trace ID of the slow request, then go to Logs Explorer and write a query filtering for log entries that contain that specific trace ID.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Configure a log-based metric in Cloud Logging to count error-message occurrences, create an alerting policy on that metric so you’re notified of future issues, and analyze the logs tied to those alerts.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Export all logs from Cloud Logging to BigQuery, then write a SQL query that joins the trace data with the log data on the trace ID field and filters for this specific transaction to find the related log entries.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Make sure your application’s logging configuration includes the trace ID from the OpenTelemetry context in every structured log entry, then, from the Cloud Trace details view for the slow request, click “Show logs” to see the correlated logs directly alongside the trace’s span waterfall.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Make sure your application’s logging configuration includes the trace ID from the OpenTelemetry context in every structured log entry, then, from the Cloud Trace details view for the slow request, click “Show logs” to see the correlated logs directly alongside the trace’s span waterfall.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard requirement is “quickly analyze the logs that correspond to the high-latency request,” which means both correctness (you need the logs for that exact request, not a broader set) and speed (minimal manual steps). The scenario tells you logs are already structured and sent to Cloud Logging, but it never confirms those log entries actually carry the trace context — and that’s the real gap keeping you from correlating them with the slow trace. D is the only option that names this missing piece explicitly (include the trace ID from the OpenTelemetry context in each log entry) and then uses the single built-in, one-click mechanism Cloud Trace provides for viewing exactly those correlated logs next to the waterfall. Every other option either assumes the correlation already works, solves a different problem, or reaches for a slower, heavier mechanism than the one Google Cloud already provides for this.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“trace waterfall lacks the detail needed”</strong> — tells you the trace data alone is insufficient; the fix must bring in a second data source (logs), not more trace configuration.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“sends structured logs to Cloud Logging”</strong> — a technical signal that logs exist, but doesn’t confirm they carry trace context, which is the actual precondition for correlation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“you need to quickly analyze the logs”</strong> — a decisive preference clue: it favors the fastest, most integrated path over one requiring exports, joins, or separately built queries.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“corresponds to the high-latency request”</strong> — a scope clue: the goal is the logs for one specific request, which rules out any option built around aggregate counts or alerts.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Logging and Cloud Trace correlate log entries to traces through a reserved trace field on the log entry; this only works if your logging configuration populates that field (here, from the OpenTelemetry trace context) on each structured log entry. Once that’s in place, the Cloud Trace details view for any request exposes a “Show logs” control on the waterfall graph that displays exactly the log entries associated with that trace, right alongside the spans — letting you go from “this request was slow” to “here’s what the business logic logged during this request” in essentially one click, with no separate query, export, or join required.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Manually copying the trace ID and writing a Logs Explorer query is a legitimate way to view correlated logs, but it silently assumes the log entries already contain the trace field — if they don’t, the query returns nothing. It also requires an extra manual step (building and running a query) in a situation that calls for speed, when the trace details view already offers the same correlated logs directly.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Counting error messages and alerting on them is a detection/monitoring pattern for catching future problems in aggregate. It does nothing to surface the logs for the one specific slow request you’ve already identified, and it doesn’t address the business-logic root cause you’re investigating right now.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Exporting the entire log volume to BigQuery and writing a SQL join can eventually get you the same correlated log entries, but it’s a heavyweight, batch-oriented path (export latency, storage/query cost, manual SQL) for a task the scenario explicitly asks you to do quickly, when the correlated view is already built into Cloud Trace.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “the logs mention the trace ID” as a given rather than a configuration step. A learner who jumps straight to “query Logs Explorer by trace ID” (A) or “join on trace ID in BigQuery” (C) is assuming correlation already works, when the scenario’s real obstacle is that the trace waterfall alone isn’t enough — which is a strong hint that the missing piece is getting trace context into the logs in the first place, not just querying for it.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Distributed tracing and structured logging are complementary signals: traces show you timing and call structure across services, while logs show you what the code actually did. They become powerful together only when log entries carry the same trace (and span) identifiers as the trace itself, which lets tooling like Cloud Trace present both side by side instead of leaving you to manually stitch them together.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team that instruments traces but never threads the trace ID into its structured logs ends up doing exactly the slow, manual correlation work options A and C describe every time they investigate a slow request. Teams that bake trace-ID propagation into their logging setup from the start get single-click, per-request log correlation for every future investigation, which is a large recurring time savings during incidents.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>No trace ID in the log entry, no correlation — fix the logging configuration first, then use Cloud Trace’s “Show logs,” not a manual query or export.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question pairs “trace waterfall isn’t detailed enough” with “structured logs already exist,” check whether the logs actually carry the trace context before assuming you can just query for it — the Google-recommended, fastest path is ensuring trace-ID propagation into logs and then using Cloud Trace’s built-in correlated log view.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/trace/docs/trace-log-integration\">Google Cloud — Link log entries with traces: </a>Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (including the trace field in structured log entries and using “Show logs” in the Trace details pane) and the precondition that eliminates option A when correlation isn’t yet configured</p>","lessons":["d4l10"]},"e3q37":{"id":"e3q37","exam":3,"n":37,"domain":1,"html":"<p>You manage a Google Cloud environment that serves 20 different business units, with 50 production projects and 50 development projects all sitting directly under the organization node. This flat layout makes it hard to apply security policies consistently across every production project, and it’s difficult to track the aggregated costs of the production and development environments separately. You need to restructure the resource hierarchy to fix both problems. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create a folder for the production environment and a folder for the development environment, then move the corresponding projects into each folder.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Create a separate folder for each business unit, then move that business unit’s projects into its folder.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Apply a label identifying the environment to every project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Create a common services project for each business unit.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Create a folder for the production environment and a folder for the development environment, then move the corresponding projects into each folder.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>There are two hard requirements here: security policies need to apply consistently across all production projects, and costs need to be tracked in aggregate separately for production versus development. In Google Cloud’s resource hierarchy, folders are the attachment point for IAM and Organization Policy inheritance, and they’re also a natural grouping boundary for billing and cost reports. Grouping all 50 production projects under one “Production” folder means one policy set at that folder cascades to every production project automatically, and grouping development projects under a separate folder gives you a clean boundary for aggregating development costs apart from production costs. Every other option either organizes along the wrong axis (business unit) or uses a mechanism (a label, a shared-services project) that isn’t actually a policy-inheritance or billing-aggregation boundary in Google Cloud.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“difficult to apply security policies consistently across all production projects”</strong> — a technical signal pointing at IAM/Organization Policy inheritance, which in Google Cloud flows down from folders, not from labels or project-level settings.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“track the aggregated costs of the production and development environments separately”</strong> — a scope clue: the grouping needed is by environment specifically, not by business unit or by individual project.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“this flat structure makes it difficult”</strong> — tells you the fix is structural (resource hierarchy), ruling out an option (labels) that leaves the hierarchy flat and only adds metadata.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“restructure the resource hierarchy”</strong> — explicitly points you at folders/projects as the mechanism, not at billing labels or a services-centralization pattern.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Folders in the Google Cloud resource hierarchy are both an IAM/Organization Policy inheritance point and a natural unit for grouping cost data. Moving all production projects into a single “Production” folder lets you set IAM bindings and organization policies once, at the folder, and have them apply to every current and future project placed in that folder — directly solving the “consistent security policy across production” problem. The same folder boundary lets you view and aggregate billing data at the folder level, so production and development costs can be tracked and reported separately without relying on per-project tagging discipline.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Business-unit folders solve a different problem — delegating ownership or administrative autonomy to each business unit — not the one described here. Google’s own landing zone guidance specifically cautions against mapping your corporate org chart onto the resource hierarchy as the primary structure, and with business-unit folders, production and development projects for the same unit would sit in the same folder, making it harder, not easier, to apply one consistent policy to “all production projects” or to cleanly separate production and development costs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> A label can help you filter or break down a billing export by environment, so it partially helps with cost visibility, but labels are not an IAM or Organization Policy attachment point in Google Cloud — you cannot apply a security policy to “every project labeled production” the way you can to a folder. This option fails the “apply security policies consistently” requirement outright.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> A common services project per business unit is a pattern for centralizing shared infrastructure (such as a Shared VPC host project, logging, or security tooling) for that business unit. It doesn’t create any environment-wide policy inheritance boundary and doesn’t help separate production and development costs in aggregate.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming that because the scenario mentions “20 business units,” the fix must be organized around business units. The scenario’s actual pain points — policy consistency across production, and separate environment-level cost tracking — are both environment problems, not business-unit problems, and Google Cloud’s own guidance explicitly favors environment-based (or sensitivity-based) folders over org-chart-based folders for exactly this kind of requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>In the Google Cloud resource hierarchy, IAM policies and Organization Policies flow downward from organization to folder to project, and folders are the recommended place to attach controls that should apply to a whole class of projects at once. Choosing the right folder axis — environment, sensitivity, business unit, or some combination — should be driven by which axis your policies and reporting actually need to group by, not by mirroring your company’s org chart by default.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>An organization that structures folders by business unit alone often finds itself reapplying the same “production-grade” IAM bindings and Organization Policy constraints separately inside each business unit’s folder, with a high risk of drift as units update independently. Structuring folders by environment first lets a central platform or security team maintain one authoritative production policy set and one aggregated production cost view, regardless of how many business units contribute projects to it.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Need one policy and one cost view across all production projects → a production folder (and a development folder), not a folder per business unit.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a resource-hierarchy question asks for consistent policy enforcement and separate cost tracking “across environments,” the answer is almost always environment-based folders — reach for business-unit folders only when the stated need is ownership, delegation, or billing isolation per business unit specifically.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/landing-zones/decide-resource-hierarchy\"><strong>Google Cloud — Decide your resource hierarchy (landing zone design)</strong></a><strong>:</strong> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (folders as the recommended grouping and policy-inheritance boundary) and the explicit caution against mapping business-unit/org-chart structure as the primary hierarchy axis, used to eliminate option B</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/security-foundations/organization-structure\"><strong>Google Cloud — Security foundations blueprint: organization structure:</strong></a> Supports: Google’s own reference architecture, which groups projects into folders by environment specifically for consistent policy application</p>","lessons":["d1l1"]},"e3q38":{"id":"e3q38","exam":3,"n":38,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You need a pipeline that builds a containerized application from GitHub and publishes its image to Artifact Registry. Each new Git tag must automatically submit a build, but execution must wait for approval from a member of the <code>gcp-developers-admins@mountkirk.com</code> Google group.</p>\n<p>Security policy prohibits static credentials and federation between Google Cloud IAM and external identity providers. Which configuration satisfies these requirements?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create a GitHub-connected Cloud Build trigger for new tag pushes and enable required approval. Grant <code>roles/cloudbuild.builds.approver</code> to <code>gcp-developers-admins@mountkirk.com</code> and <code>roles/artifactregistry.writer</code> to the service account used to execute the builds.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Create a GitHub Actions workflow that builds and publishes images on new tag pushes. Mirror the Google group’s membership into a GitHub team and require that team’s approval. Create a Google Cloud service account, download its key, and use the key to authenticate the workflow. Grant the service account <code>roles/artifactregistry.writer</code>.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Generate an SSH key pair and register its public key as a GitHub repository deploy key. Configure an approval-required Cloud Build trigger that retrieves the repository using this key. Grant <code>roles/cloudbuild.builds.editor</code> to <code>gcp-developers-admins@mountkirk.com</code> and <code>roles/artifactregistry.writer</code> to the build service account.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Create a GitHub Actions workflow that builds and publishes images on new tag pushes. Mirror the Google group’s membership into a GitHub team and require that team’s approval. Configure a Google Cloud workload identity pool that trusts the GitHub workflow identity, and grant the authorized federated principal <code>roles/artifactregistry.writer</code>.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Create a GitHub-connected Cloud Build trigger for new tag pushes and enable required approval. Grant</strong><code>**roles/cloudbuild.builds.approver**</code><strong>to</strong><code>**gcp-developers-admins@mountkirk.com**</code><strong>and</strong><code>**roles/artifactregistry.writer**</code><strong>to the service account used to execute the builds.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>First eliminate designs using static keys or external identity federation. Then verify that the remaining design provides approval <strong>before execution</strong> , the correct approver permission, and an authorized identity for publishing images. Option A satisfies all these requirements.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>New tag pushed:</strong> Configure a GitHub tag-push trigger.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Approval before execution:</strong> Enable Cloud Build’s approval gate.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google group approval:</strong> Grant the group the Cloud Build Approver role.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>No static credentials or federation:</strong> Execute within Cloud Build using its configured service account.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Build supports GitHub tag-push triggers with required approval. A matching event creates a <strong>pending build</strong> ; its steps execute only after an authorized approver accepts it. </p>\n<p>Grant the group <code>roles/cloudbuild.builds.approver</code> on the build project. Grant the execution service account <code>roles/artifactregistry.writer</code>, preferably scoped to the destination repository, so it can publish images. </p>\n<p>Cloud Build runs under the configured Google Cloud service account without requiring an exported service account key or a workload identity pool trusting GitHub. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> The downloaded service account key is a long-lived credential, directly violating the prohibition on static credentials. GitHub team approval does not resolve that authentication issue. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> The SSH private key introduces a static credential. Additionally, the exact role <code>roles/cloudbuild.builds.editor</code> does <strong>not</strong> include <code>cloudbuild.builds.approve</code>; the specified group therefore lacks the required approval permission.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Workload Identity Federation exchanges GitHub’s OIDC identity for short-lived Google Cloud credentials. Although it avoids service account keys, it establishes the external identity trust expressly prohibited here. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Keyless authentication can still involve federation.</strong> Option D satisfies one security condition while violating the other. Also, automatic triggering and approval gating coexist: submission is automatic, while execution waits.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Separate human authorization from workload permissions. Group members authorize execution through the Approver role; the build service account performs operations using its own permissions, including writing artifacts. </p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>An accidentally pushed release tag creates a pending build. An authorized group member can reject it before container-building or image-publishing steps execute. </p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>The trigger submits, the group approves, and the build service account publishes.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Choose <strong>A</strong> : a tag-triggered Cloud Build pipeline with required approval, the group’s Approver role, and the build service account’s Artifact Registry Writer role.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud — Gate builds on approval</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Pending builds, approval before execution, and approval permissions.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/securing-builds/gate-builds-on-approval\">Official documentation</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Build roles and permissions</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: The permissions in <code>roles/cloudbuild.builds.approver</code> and <code>roles/cloudbuild.builds.editor</code>.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/roles-permissions/cloudbuild\">Official documentation</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud — Store artifacts in Artifact Registry</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Granting Artifact Registry Writer to the build service account.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/building/store-artifacts-in-artifact-registry\">Official documentation</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Google Cloud — Run builds with a user-managed service account</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Configuring the build execution identity and applying least privilege.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/securing-builds/configure-user-specified-service-accounts\">Official documentation</a></p>","lessons":["d2l1","d2l2"]},"e3q39":{"id":"e3q39","exam":3,"n":39,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You’re responsible for a critical, user-facing application and have an SLO configured in Cloud Monitoring that targets 99% availability over a rolling 30-day window. A string of minor issues has recently driven up latency, and as a result the error budget is being consumed faster than expected. You want to be notified proactively if the service is at risk of breaching its SLO, ideally before the error budget runs out completely. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Configure a log-based metric in Cloud Monitoring that tracks the rate of application error log entries, then create an alerting policy on that metric that fires when the error rate shows a significant anomalous deviation from its historical baseline.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Build a custom dashboard in Cloud Monitoring that visualizes SLO compliance percentage and remaining error budget, and configure the dashboard to email a scheduled daily report.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. In Cloud Monitoring, create an alerting policy based on the SLO’s error budget burn rate, and configure it to fire when the current consumption rate is projected to exhaust the budget before the end of the 30-day compliance period.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. In Metrics Explorer, create an alerting policy based on the application load balancer’s average request latency, and configure it to fire when latency exceeds a predefined threshold.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. In Cloud Monitoring, create an alerting policy based on the SLO’s error budget burn rate, and configure it to fire when the current consumption rate is projected to exhaust the budget before the end of the 30-day compliance period.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard requirement is being warned before the SLO is actually violated, based on the trajectory of error-budget consumption, not just on a raw symptom crossing a fixed line. That rules out anything that measures a side signal (error-log anomalies, latency thresholds) instead of the budget itself, and anything that isn’t forward-looking (a periodic report instead of a real-time, projection-based alert). Cloud Monitoring’s SLO burn-rate alerting is purpose-built for exactly this: it continuously evaluates how fast the error budget is being spent and can warn you specifically when, at the current rate, the budget would run out before the compliance window ends — which is precisely “at risk of violating the SLO before the budget is depleted,” stated almost word for word.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“99% availability over a 30-day rolling window”</strong> — establishes the SLO and its compliance period, which is the denominator any proactive alert needs to reason about.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“error budget to be consumed at an accelerated rate”</strong> — a technical signal that the right alert should track the rate of consumption (burn rate), not a static error count or a fixed latency number.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“proactively notified when the service is at risk of violating its SLO before the error budget is fully depleted”</strong> — a decisive requirement clue: it specifically wants a forward-looking, budget-exhaustion-projecting alert, not a lagging report or a generic symptom alert.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“minor issues have increased latency”</strong> — tells you the root cause is latency-driven, but the thing you need to monitor and alert on is the SLO’s error budget, which already reflects the downstream effect of that latency on availability.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Monitoring’s SLO-based alerting computes a burn-rate metric: a normalized value where a rate greater than one means that, if sustained, the service will be out of SLO for the compliance period. Configuring an alerting policy with a burn-rate threshold — a “slow-burn” style alert — warns you specifically when the current rate of consumption, if it continues, will exhaust the error budget before the 30-day window ends. That is a direct, purpose-built answer to “notify me before the budget runs out,” independent of which underlying symptom (latency, errors, or anything else) is driving the consumption.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong> A log-based metric with anomaly detection flags statistical deviations in the error-log rate from a historical baseline, which is a general error-detection pattern, not a measure of how much of the SLO’s error budget remains or how fast it’s being spent. It also may not even fire here, since the scenario’s root cause is latency, which might not show up as application error log entries at all.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A dashboard with a scheduled daily email gives visibility into compliance and remaining budget, but it’s a periodic report, not a real-time, threshold-triggered alert. With the budget already burning faster than normal, a day’s delay between reports could mean the budget is exhausted before anyone sees the next email.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> Alerting on raw average load-balancer latency crossing a fixed threshold monitors the symptom causing the problem, not the SLO’s actual compliance measure. A latency threshold alert can’t tell you whether the service is on track to violate its 99% availability target over the 30-day window — it only tells you latency is currently high, with no connection to remaining error budget or time-to-exhaustion.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is picking an option that reacts to the symptom mentioned in the stem (latency, in option D) instead of the SLO itself. The scenario describes latency as the cause, but the thing you’re actually responsible for protecting is the SLO’s error budget, and Cloud Monitoring’s SLO alerting is designed to track that budget directly regardless of which symptom is driving its consumption.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An SLO’s error budget is the single number that already integrates every source of unreliability (latency, errors, availability) relative to your compliance target. Alerting directly on the budget’s burn rate — rather than on any one symptom that might be contributing to it — gives you a true, root-cause-agnostic early warning, and lets you set a consistent policy (“alert me if we’re on pace to blow the budget”) regardless of what breaks next.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team that only alerts on fixed latency thresholds has to separately guess at a different threshold for every new kind of incident; a team that alerts on burn rate gets one unified signal that fires whenever anything — latency, a bad deploy, a dependency outage — threatens the SLO, letting on-call engineers prioritize based on actual business risk (are we going to breach our commitment?) rather than chasing every individual metric spike.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>To know if you’ll run out of error budget before the window closes, alert on the burn rate — not on a symptom metric and not on a scheduled report.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks for a proactive warning tied to an SLO’s error budget and a compliance window, look for the option that explicitly configures a burn-rate alert projecting budget exhaustion — alerts on raw symptom metrics (latency, error counts) or passive reporting (dashboards, scheduled emails) are almost always the weaker distractors.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring/alerting-on-budget-burn-rate\"><strong>Google Cloud — Alerting on burn rate (SLO monitoring)</strong></a><strong>:</strong> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (burn-rate alerting and the “slow-burn” alert that warns of budget exhaustion before the compliance period ends)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring/ui/create-alert\">Google Cloud — Creating an alerting policy for an SLO: </a>Supports: how the burn-rate threshold is configured in an SLO-based alerting policy</p>","lessons":["d4l9"]},"e3q40":{"id":"e3q40","exam":3,"n":40,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your company runs applications in a private VPC that has no internet access, and it has internal APIs reachable only from within that VPC network. You need to monitor these internal APIs’ availability using a solution that follows Google-recommended practice and keeps manual effort and ongoing maintenance to a minimum. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Deploy the application on a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) instance with a liveness and a readiness probe configured.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Set up private uptime checks.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Build a Cloud Run function with a VPC connector that pings the internal endpoints, then configure public uptime checks against that Cloud Run function.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Set up a synthetic monitor, and use Cloud VPN to reach the private endpoints.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Set up private uptime checks.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard requirement is monitoring availability of endpoints that are reachable only inside the VPC, with the preference of minimal custom engineering and upkeep. Every other option either solves the wrong problem (A) or solves this one by building extra infrastructure to bridge into the private network (C’s relay function and VPC connector, D’s VPN tunnel). Cloud Monitoring’s private uptime checks exist specifically to send HTTP or TCP requests directly into a customer VPC network over the private network, using Service Directory to resolve the internal target — no relay function, no VPN tunnel, and no custom code required. That makes it the only option that directly satisfies both the “internal-only reachability” requirement and the “minimize manual effort and maintenance” preference at the same time.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“private VPC without internet access”</strong> and <strong>“only accessible within your VPC network”</strong> — a technical signal that rules out any monitoring approach assuming public reachability, and points toward a mechanism built for private-network targets specifically.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“monitor the availability”</strong> — a scope clue favoring a dedicated availability-monitoring feature (uptime checks) over a Kubernetes self-healing mechanism (liveness/readiness probes), which serves a different purpose.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Google-recommended practices”</strong> — points at Cloud Monitoring’s native, documented private uptime checks feature rather than a hand-built workaround.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“minimize the amount of manual effort and maintenance required”</strong> — a decisive preference clue: it eliminates any option requiring you to build and operate custom relay infrastructure (a Cloud Run function, a VPN connection) just to make the target reachable.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Private uptime checks let Cloud Monitoring send HTTP or TCP requests over the private network to resources like a VM or an internal load balancer, while enforcing IAM restrictions and VPC Service Controls perimeters, with the internal target registered through Service Directory. Because this capability is built directly into Cloud Monitoring, configuring a private uptime check against the internal API is a matter of registering the service and creating the check — no custom proxy application, no VPN tunnel, and no separate compute resource to build, deploy, or maintain, which is exactly the low-overhead, Google-native path to monitoring an internal-only endpoint’s availability.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong> Liveness and readiness probes are a Kubernetes mechanism the kubelet uses to decide whether to restart a container or route traffic to it; they don’t give you an external, alertable availability signal in Cloud Monitoring, and they’d require redeploying the application onto GKE specifically rather than monitoring the APIs where they already run. This solves pod health management, not availability monitoring.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Building a Cloud Run function with a VPC connector to relay requests, then pointing a public uptime check at that function, can work, but it requires you to write, deploy, and maintain a custom piece of software purely to bridge network reachability — exactly the ongoing maintenance burden the requirement asks you to avoid, when private uptime checks already provide that bridge natively.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Standing up a Cloud VPN connection specifically to let a synthetic monitor reach private endpoints is significant additional networking infrastructure to build and operate, when private uptime checks already reach into the VPC over the private network without requiring a VPN tunnel at all.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming that because the endpoints are private, you must build a bridge yourself (a relay function, a VPN) to make a monitoring tool reach them. Google Cloud Monitoring already has a purpose-built feature for exactly this case, and recognizing “private uptime checks” as that native capability is what separates the correct answer from the plausible-sounding, more labor-intensive workarounds.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>When a monitoring need involves a resource that standard, internet-facing tooling can’t reach, the lowest-maintenance solution is usually a provider-native feature designed for that specific reachability gap, rather than custom infrastructure built to bridge the gap yourself. Recognizing when such a native feature already exists — versus when it genuinely doesn’t and a workaround is necessary — is the core judgment this kind of question is testing.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team that builds a Cloud Run relay function or a dedicated VPN tunnel just to monitor an internal API takes on ownership of that bridge indefinitely: patching it, monitoring the monitor, and handling its own failure modes. A team that uses private uptime checks offloads all of that to Cloud Monitoring itself, leaving only the uptime check configuration to maintain.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Need to monitor an endpoint that’s reachable only inside your VPC → private uptime checks, not a custom relay function and not a VPN tunnel.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks how to monitor the availability of an internal-only endpoint with minimal custom effort, check whether Cloud Monitoring already has a native feature for that reachability gap — private uptime checks — before reaching for a hand-built bridge like a relay function or a VPN connection.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/uptime-checks/private-checks\"><strong>Google Cloud — Create private uptime checks:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (sending HTTP/TCP requests into a customer VPC over the private network via Service Directory, without a relay function or VPN)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide:</strong></a>Supports: domain alignment (Section 4.1, Instrumenting and collecting telemetry, explicitly names creating synthetic monitors/checks to proactively probe application endpoints)</p>","lessons":["d4l9"]},"e3q41":{"id":"e3q41","exam":3,"n":41,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You have a CI/CD pipeline in Cloud Build that builds container images, which are stored in Artifact Registry. You need to guarantee that only images built by this Cloud Build pipeline in your production project can be deployed to your Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster, while keeping the implementation and ongoing maintenance effort as low as possible. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Turn on Binary Authorization for the GKE cluster, and build a Cloud Run function that’s triggered by Artifact Registry INSERT events to check that pushed containers carry the projects/PROJECT_ID/attestors/built-by-cloud-build attestor.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Restrict Artifact Registry so that only the Cloud Build service account is permitted to push container images.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Turn on Binary Authorization for the GKE cluster. Create a Cloud Key Management Service (Cloud KMS) keyring and the built-by-cloud-build attestor yourself. After each Cloud Build push, use the key to generate an attestation, and update the Binary Authorization policy to require the projects/PROJECT_ID/attestors/built-by-cloud-build attestor.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Turn on Binary Authorization for the GKE cluster, and set the Binary Authorization policy to require the projects/PROJECT_ID/attestors/built-by-cloud-build attestor.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Turn on Binary Authorization for the GKE cluster, and set the Binary Authorization policy to require the projects/PROJECT_ID/attestors/built-by-cloud-build attestor.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard requirement is a hard deployment-time gate: only Cloud-Build-built images may run on the cluster. The preference is minimal setup and upkeep. Binary Authorization is the mechanism that actually enforces a gate at deploy time in GKE, so any option without it (B) is disqualified outright regardless of effort. Among the Binary Authorization options, Cloud Build already has a built-in, Google-managed integration: it automatically creates the built-by-cloud-build attestor in your project and automatically signs an attestation for every image it builds, with Binary Authorization verifying that attestation at deploy time. That means the entire “verify this was built by Cloud Build” problem is solved the moment you point your policy at that attestor — no keys, no custom code, no manual signing step required, which is exactly what makes D the lowest-effort option that still fully satisfies the hard requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“only images built by the Cloud Build pipeline in your production project are deployed”</strong> — a technical signal pointing at Binary Authorization’s attestor-verification model, specifically one tied to Cloud Build as the builder.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“deployed to your GKE cluster”</strong> — a scope clue: the control must act at GKE admission/deploy time, not merely at registry-push time.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“minimize the amount of manual effort required for implementation and maintenance”</strong> — a decisive preference clue: it favors whichever option offloads the most setup and ongoing work onto a managed/automatic mechanism.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“built-by-cloud-build attestor”</strong> named explicitly in every option — a technical anchor signaling that the exam wants you to recognize this as Google’s specific, automatically managed attestor rather than something you build yourself.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Build’s integration with Binary Authorization automatically provisions the built-by-cloud-build attestor in your project (created the first time you run a build) and automatically signs an attestation for each successfully built image — all without you creating a Cloud KMS key, managing signing keys, or writing any code. Once Binary Authorization is enabled on the GKE cluster and its policy requires the projects/PROJECT_ID/attestors/built-by-cloud-build attestor, the cluster’s admission controller will refuse to deploy any image that doesn’t carry a valid attestation from that attestor — which only Cloud Build can produce for that project. That delivers the exact guarantee asked for, with the setup reduced to two configuration steps.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> This requires designing, writing, and maintaining a custom Cloud Run function as a permanent piece of infrastructure, which directly conflicts with “minimize manual effort and maintenance.” It’s also the wrong enforcement point: reacting to an Artifact Registry INSERT (push) event checks images after they land in the registry, not at the moment GKE tries to deploy them, so it doesn’t actually function as the deployment-time gate the requirement calls for — Binary Authorization’s native admission control already does this natively.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> Limiting who can push to Artifact Registry controls one storage location, but it does nothing to control what GKE is allowed to pull and run. Without an enforcement mechanism at the cluster itself, there’s no guarantee against deploying images from elsewhere, so this doesn’t satisfy “ensure only images built by the Cloud Build pipeline are deployed to your GKE cluster.”</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> This reaches the same end state as D, but by manually rebuilding what Cloud Build’s integration already automates: creating your own Cloud KMS keyring and attestor, and manually generating an attestation after every build. That’s substantial duplicate setup and an ongoing manual (or custom-scripted) step on every single build, which is exactly the extra effort the requirement asks you to avoid.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming that because Binary Authorization “requires an attestor and attestations,” you must be the one who creates the Cloud KMS key and signs each attestation (option C) — that’s how Binary Authorization works in the general case, but Cloud Build has a specific, documented shortcut that automates exactly that workflow for images it builds, which is what the built-by-cloud-build attestor name is signaling.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Binary Authorization enforces policy at the point resources are deployed, by requiring one or more attestations — cryptographically signed claims that a specific verification step occurred — before an image is allowed to run. When the entity doing the verifying is also the platform enforcing the policy (here, Google-managed Cloud Build and Binary Authorization working together), the provider can automate both the attestor’s existence and the signing step, removing the key-management burden that a fully custom attestor setup would otherwise require.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team that hand-rolls its own KMS keyring, attestor, and per-build signing step takes on key rotation, access control for the signing key, and a scripted or manual signing step that has to run reliably on every single build or the pipeline silently stops producing deployable images. Using the built-by-cloud-build attestor removes all of that: Cloud Build keeps attesting every build automatically, and the only thing the platform team maintains is the one-line policy requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>If Cloud Build built it, let Cloud Build attest it — reach for your own Cloud KMS keyring and attestor only when you need to verify something Cloud Build’s built-in attestor doesn’t already cover.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks to restrict GKE (or Cloud Run) deployments to images built by Cloud Build with minimal setup, look for the option that simply points a Binary Authorization policy at the built-by-cloud-build attestor — any option involving your own Cloud KMS key, a hand-written attestation step, or a custom verification function is doing manual work Cloud Build already automates.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs/deploy-cloud-build\"><strong>Google Cloud — Securing deployments by only allowing images built by Cloud Build (Binary Authorization):</strong></a>Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (Cloud Build’s automatic creation of the built-by-cloud-build attestor and automatic attestation signing, verified by Binary Authorization at deploy time)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/securing-builds/use-binary-authorization\"><strong>Google Cloud — Securing image deployments to Cloud Run and GKE (Cloud Build docs):</strong></a> Supports: the detail that Cloud Build generates and signs attestations automatically, and the multi-project note relevant to “production project,” used to eliminate the manual Cloud KMS approach in option C</p>","lessons":["d2l9"]},"e3q42":{"id":"e3q42","exam":3,"n":42,"domain":3,"html":"<p>Your team manages a critical service with a 99.9% availability SLO. The quarterly error budget has nearly run out because of several recent incidents. Your team has a new set of features planned for release next week. You need to avoid exceeding the error budget while following Google-recommended practice. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Go ahead with the release, but put an automatic rollback in place in case new errors show up.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Release the features in stages, rolling out to one subset of users at a time to limit the potential impact on the SLO.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Postpone the release and have the team focus on reliability improvements until the error budget replenishes.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Postpone the release and have the team spend the time building up a backlog of features to deploy once the error budget replenishes.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Postpone the release and have the team focus on reliability improvements until the error budget replenishes.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The error budget exists specifically to govern this trade-off: it’s the amount of unreliability the service can absorb while still meeting its SLO, and it’s meant to be spent deliberately on things like feature launches when it’s healthy. With the budget nearly exhausted, any further risk-bearing change — even a carefully staged one — threatens to push the service out of SLO. Google’s own SRE guidance treats this situation as a trigger for a feature freeze specifically so that engineering effort shifts to reliability work, which is the only one of the four options that both avoids further budget risk and redirects effort toward actually fixing the underlying problem rather than just sitting on hands or stockpiling unrelated work.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“error budget for the quarter has been nearly depleted”</strong> — a decisive constraint clue: there is very little room left to absorb any additional errors, which argues against any option that still introduces new risk.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“due to several incidents”</strong> — signals that the service has an active reliability problem, which is the condition Google’s error budget policy explicitly treats as a reason to pause feature work and fix the underlying issues.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“planning on releasing new features next week”</strong> — frames the choice as whether to proceed, in some form, with new risk-bearing change right now.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“following Google-recommended practices”</strong> — points directly at the documented SRE error-budget policy response (a feature freeze focused on reliability), rather than at ad hoc risk-mitigation tactics.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Google’s SRE practice treats an exhausted or near-exhausted error budget as the signal to impose a freeze on new feature releases, specifically so that the team can redirect its attention to reliability improvements until the budget is replenished. Postponing the release removes the immediate source of additional risk, and spending that time on reliability work (fixing root causes, improving testing, hardening the release process) is what actually restores headroom in the budget and reduces the odds of repeating the recent incidents, rather than just waiting out the clock or doing unrelated work.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> An automatic rollback limits how long a bad release stays live, but it doesn’t prevent the release from consuming more of an already nearly-exhausted budget before the rollback triggers. Proceeding with a risk-bearing release at all runs directly counter to the recommended response to a strained budget.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Staged, subset-based rollout is a reasonable general technique for limiting blast radius, and it reduces risk relative to a full release. But it still introduces new change and new risk onto a service whose budget is nearly gone; Google’s documented guidance for this situation is a full pause on feature releases, not merely a more cautious version of the same release.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> This option correctly postpones the risky release, but it spends the freed time on building a feature backlog rather than on reliability work. That addresses a different goal (being ready to ship more features later) and does nothing to reduce the chance of repeating the incidents that burned the budget in the first place — which is the specific purpose Google’s guidance assigns to this kind of pause.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “postpone the release” as the whole answer and picking whichever postponement option is listed, without checking what the team is supposed to do with the freed time. Google’s guidance is explicit that a freeze is meant to create space for reliability work specifically, not simply to delay the release or redirect effort toward unrelated feature planning.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An error budget converts “how reliable should this service be” into a concrete, spendable quantity, which lets a team make principled trade-offs between shipping new risk-bearing changes and investing in stability. When the budget is nearly spent, the policy response isn’t just “be more careful” — it’s a deliberate, temporary rebalancing of where engineering effort goes, specifically toward the reliability work that restores the budget.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team that keeps shipping features, even cautiously, while its error budget is nearly gone tends to keep having the same category of incident recur, because nothing is actually being invested in root-cause fixes or hardening. A team that treats budget depletion as an explicit freeze trigger and spends that time on reliability work typically breaks the cycle and arrives at the next compliance period with both a healthier budget and a more resilient service.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Budget nearly gone → freeze features, spend the time on reliability — not on a safer release and not on a bigger feature backlog.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When an error-budget question asks what to do about an upcoming release while the budget is nearly or fully depleted, the Google-recommended answer is almost always to pause the release and redirect the team to reliability work until the budget refreshes — any option that still ships something, however carefully, or that spends the pause on non-reliability work, is the weaker distractor.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/error-budget-policy/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — Example Error Budget Policy (Appendix B):</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (freezing feature releases and dedicating the team to reliability work when the error budget is overspent)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/devops-sre/good-housekeeping-error-budgetscre-life-lessons\"><strong>Google Cloud Blog — Good housekeeping for error budgets (CRE life lessons)</strong></a><strong>:</strong> Supports: the canonical response to overspending an error budget being a feature freeze aimed specifically at reliability work, used to eliminate option D’s “build a feature backlog” framing</p>","lessons":["d3l3"]},"e3q43":{"id":"e3q43","exam":3,"n":43,"domain":1,"html":"<p>Your organization is bringing the marketing department onto Google Cloud. The marketing team will independently manage its own development work, but you need visibility into total departmental spending and want to receive an alert if spending exceeds the marketing budget as a whole. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create all of the marketing department’s projects independently, and configure a separate budget alert for each project using the full marketing budget divided by the number of projects as that project’s limit.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Create all of the marketing department’s projects under a dedicated marketing folder, and configure a separate budget alert for each project using the full marketing budget as that project’s limit.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create all of the marketing department’s projects under a dedicated marketing folder, and configure a single budget alert scoped to the folder using the full marketing budget as its limit.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Create all of the marketing department’s projects independently, and configure a separate budget alert for each project using the full marketing budget as that project’s limit.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Create all of the marketing department’s projects under a dedicated marketing folder, and configure a single budget alert scoped to the folder using the full marketing budget as its limit.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Must-have constraints: a resource structure that groups every current and future marketing project together, and one alert tied to the department’s real total budget. Not a requirement: granularity down to the individual project — the question never asks which project overspent, only whether the department as a whole did. Capability: folders in the resource hierarchy exist to group projects for IAM, policy, and billing purposes, and a Cloud Billing budget can be scoped to a folder, which sums spend from every project nested under it. Elimination: any option without a folder (A, D) has no structural anchor for department-wide scoping, and any option that assigns the full budget to each individual project (B, D) lets combined spend across several projects run well past the real budget while every individual alert still reads “under threshold.” Only the folder-plus-single-folder-alert combination satisfies both constraints at once.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“visibility into total departmental spending” — a hard constraint requiring aggregate monitoring, not per-project monitoring.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“alert if spending exceeds the marketing budget as a whole” — a cardinality signal: one ceiling, evaluated once, not a ceiling duplicated per project.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“will independently manage its own development work” — implies multiple, possibly growing, projects, which is why a grouping mechanism matters.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>No stated project count — a technical signal that the correct approach must keep working as projects are added, ruling out any option whose math depends on a fixed project count.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Grouping every marketing project inside one folder turns that folder into a single accounting unit in the resource hierarchy. A budget’s scope can be filtered to that folder, and Cloud Billing then totals the cost of every project beneath it before comparing the total to the configured amount. Setting that single budget’s amount to the full marketing allocation means the alert fires exactly when true cumulative departmental spend crosses the line — and because the scope is the folder, not a fixed list of projects, a new marketing project dropped into that folder later is automatically covered with no reconfiguration.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Dividing the total budget by project count creates an artificial per-project ceiling disconnected from how spend actually distributes. One project can blow past its slice while the department overall is still under budget (a false alarm), or every project can stay under its slice while combined spend exceeds the real budget (a missed alarm) — and the slice size breaks the moment a project is added or removed.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> The folder grouping is correct, but assigning the FULL marketing budget as each individual project’s limit means no alert fires until one project alone has consumed the entire department’s budget. Combined spend across several projects can be multiples of the real budget before anything triggers, which directly fails the “total spending” requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> This combines both weaknesses above — no folder to anchor department-wide visibility, and a full-budget threshold applied per project rather than in aggregate — so it neither organizes the projects nor catches cumulative overspend.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming more alerts means tighter cost control. What determines correctness here isn’t the number of alerts, it’s the scope each alert sums against. Options that create more alerts (A, B, D) can still fail to answer what the question actually asks — whether the department’s combined spend stayed within budget. A per-project alerting approach would only become correct if the question instead asked you to identify which individual project overspent.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>The organization → folder → project hierarchy exists so policies, IAM, and billing can be applied once at a grouping level instead of repeated per resource. Cloud Billing budgets extend that pattern to cost control: a budget’s scope can be the billing account, one or more folders, or one or more projects, and that scope choice determines what gets summed before comparison to the configured amount. Matching the scope to the actual question being asked — “how is this one project doing” versus “how is this whole department doing” — is the underlying skill, independent of console click-paths.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Organizations commonly mirror their org chart with one folder per department or cost center specifically so FinOps and engineering leads can watch a single dashboard and a single alert per department instead of reconciling numbers from dozens of per-project alerts. It also means a project spun up mid-quarter is automatically captured by the existing folder-level budget, keeping cost governance consistent as the environment grows without anyone having to remember to wire up a new per-project alert each time.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>One department, one folder, one budget — group first, then alert once at the level you actually need to monitor.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a scenario asks you to monitor combined spend across several projects and alert on one overall ceiling, pick the option that both groups those projects under a folder and scopes the budget alert to that folder — any option alerting per project, however the per-project amount is calculated, is solving a different problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/budgets\"><strong>Google Cloud — Create, edit, or delete budgets and budget alerts:</strong></a> Supports: folder-level budget scoping and the budget creation/scope-setting workflow</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/billing/docs/reference/budget/rest/v1/billingAccounts.budgets\"><strong>Google Cloud — Budget resource (REST reference), billingAccounts.budgets:</strong></a> Supports: confirms a budget’s filter can include a set of folder names, so usage from every project under that folder is aggregated into the budget</p>","lessons":["d1l1"]},"e3q44":{"id":"e3q44","exam":3,"n":44,"domain":1,"html":"<p>You’re working on a Google Cloud project that’s been running in production for several months, and you need to update an IAM policy to grant a team member additional permissions. Before applying the change to the production environment, you need to proactively surface any potential risks tied to this IAM policy change. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Apply the IAM policy change, then use the recent change recommendations to watch for any unintended consequences.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use the change risk recommendations to evaluate the potential impact of the IAM policy change and surface any associated risks before applying it.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Grant the new team member the Project Owner role, and reassess their permissions once their task is finished.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Use the Cloud deprecation and breaking changes general recommender to evaluate how the IAM policy change might affect any services that are deprecated or due to change soon.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Use the change risk recommendations to evaluate the potential impact of the IAM policy change and surface any associated risks before applying it.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard requirement is timing and scope: you need risk visibility for this specific IAM policy change, before it’s applied, not after. That rules out A outright, since it applies the change first and only observes afterward — the opposite of proactive. It also rules out D, which targets a completely different risk category (deprecated or soon-to-change services) rather than the risk of an IAM permission grant itself. C doesn’t attempt any risk assessment at all; it just grants broad access and defers the real work. Change risk recommendations is the Recommender/Active Assist feature purpose-built for exactly this scenario — it’s explicitly scoped to include IAM policy changes and is designed to warn you about the risk of a change before you make it, based on recent usage activity tied to that policy.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“been in production for several months”</strong> — a technical signal that the policy likely has real, active dependents whose access could be affected, which is exactly the kind of usage-based risk change risk recommendations is designed to surface.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“proactively identify any potential risks”</strong> — a decisive requirement/exclusion clue: it rules out any option where the change is made first and checked afterward (A).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“before applying them to the production environment”</strong> — reinforces the same timing requirement and rules out C’s “grant broad access now, fix it later” approach.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“associated risks associated with these IAM policy changes”</strong> — a scope clue naming IAM policy changes specifically, distinguishing the needed tool from one aimed at service deprecations (D).</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Change risk recommendations is a family of Active Assist recommendations within the Recommender service, specifically built to warn you about the risk of making a change — including an IAM policy change — based on that resource’s recent usage activity, before you actually apply it. Running this check against the proposed IAM policy update lets you see, ahead of time, whether the change could break an essential dependency or have other unintended consequences, which is precisely the proactive, pre-application risk assessment the scenario asks for.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> This applies the IAM policy change before assessing anything, then relies on a different, retrospective signal (recent change recommendations) to catch problems after the fact. That inverts the stated requirement to identify risks before applying the change, and by the time an unintended consequence is observed, the risky change is already live in production.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Granting Project Owner sidesteps the actual question entirely — it doesn’t assess risk at all, it just grants far more access than requested and pushes the real evaluation to some later point. This also conflicts with least-privilege practice, since Project Owner is a broad, high-privilege role for what was described as an incremental permission addition.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> The deprecation and breaking-changes recommender addresses a different risk category — whether a service or API your project depends on is being deprecated or changed — not the risk introduced by modifying an IAM policy. It wouldn’t surface anything about who currently depends on the policy being changed or what could break as a result of the permission grant itself.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is picking an option that includes the word “recommendations” or sounds proactive without checking whether its scope actually matches “risk of an IAM policy change.” Recent change recommendations and the deprecation/breaking-changes recommender are both real Google Cloud tools, but neither is scoped to pre-application IAM risk assessment the way change risk recommendations specifically is.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud’s Active Assist recommenders are organized by what they help you decide, not just by when they run: some (like change risk recommendations) are designed to be consulted before you make a change, using recent usage data to warn you of likely impact, while others are designed to surface issues after a change or event has already occurred, or to track an entirely different category of risk such as service lifecycle changes. Picking the right recommender means matching both its timing (before vs. after) and its subject (IAM policy vs. service deprecation) to what you actually need to evaluate.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team that applies IAM changes first and only reviews recent-change recommendations afterward tends to discover broken dependencies only once something has already failed in production. A team that runs change risk recommendations against a proposed IAM policy update before applying it can catch that, for example, a service account’s access is a load-bearing dependency for another team’s pipeline, and adjust the change before it ever goes live.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Need to know the risk of a change before you make it → change risk recommendations, not a broader role grant, not a post-hoc monitoring recommender, and not a deprecation recommender.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks for a proactive, pre-application risk assessment of an IAM policy change, look specifically for “change risk recommendations” — any option that applies the change first, grants broader access as a workaround, or points to a recommender scoped to a different kind of risk (like service deprecation) is the weaker distractor.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/recommender/docs/change-risk-recommendations\"><strong>Google Cloud — Change risk recommendations (Recommender):</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (proactive, usage-based risk warnings for IAM policy changes before they’re applied)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/recommender/docs/reference/rest/v1beta1/recommenders\">Google Cloud — Recommender reference (recommender and insight types): </a>Supports: the distinction between change risk recommendations and the separate recent change recommendations and deprecation-related recommenders, used to eliminate options A and D</p>","lessons":["d1l3"]},"e3q45":{"id":"e3q45","exam":3,"n":45,"domain":5,"html":"<p>A newly created Google Cloud project will host several applications on Compute Engine VM instances distributed across multiple regions. You expect the total instance count to remain steady or increase, although you might redistribute instances among regions over time. Which approach best minimizes compute costs for this multi-year project?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Purchase a three-year resource-based commitment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Purchase a three-year compute flexible commitment and create capacity reservations.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Purchase a three-year compute flexible commitment.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Purchase a one-year resource-based commitment.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Purchase a three-year compute flexible commitment.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The solution must accommodate changes in regional allocation. The stable overall demand supports committing to ongoing usage, while the multi-year horizon favors the longer term. Resource-based commitments introduce regional restrictions. Between the flexible options, reservations add capacity assurance that the scenario does not request, making C the best fit.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Distributed across multiple regions” — Technical signal:</strong> Evaluate the geographical scope of each commitment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Total instance count to remain steady or increase” — Technical signal:</strong> Indicates an ongoing consumption baseline.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Redistribute instances among regions” — Hard constraint:</strong> Savings must remain applicable as regional placement changes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Minimizes compute costs” — Optimization objective:</strong> Avoid adding capacity arrangements without a corresponding requirement.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Multi-year project” — Technical signal:</strong> Supports considering a three-year commitment.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Compute flexible commitments apply to eligible spending across regions and projects within the same Cloud Billing account. Their coverage can therefore follow eligible workloads as the regional distribution changes. A three-year term provides greater discounts than a one-year term for eligible Compute Engine resources.</p>\n<p>The commitment covers a minimum <strong>hourly spend</strong> , rather than a fixed number of instances. Actual savings depend on maintaining sufficient eligible consumption.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A three-year resource-based commitment can provide substantial savings for predictable regional usage. However, its coverage remains tied to the specified region. Moving workloads elsewhere can leave paid commitments underutilized.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Flexible commitments can cover eligible usage that consumes separately created reservations. Reservations hold capacity in specified zones; they do not independently increase the commitment discount. Unused reserved resources can incur charges, so this option introduces unnecessary cost exposure for the stated objective. Consuming a reservation does <strong>not</strong> create duplicate resource charges for the same VM usage. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A one-year resource-based commitment reduces the commitment duration but retains the regional restriction. It also provides a smaller discount than the corresponding three-year resource-based plan. It would be more appropriate for predictable regional demand with only a one-year commitment horizon. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Do not confuse a <strong>pricing commitment</strong> with a <strong>capacity reservation</strong>. Option B becomes appropriate if the requirements additionally include assured capacity for particular VM configurations in specified zones. Compute flexible commitments do not support attached reservations; any required reservations are created separately. </p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Evaluate commitment scope, duration, and capacity assurance separately. A favorable discount rate only produces useful savings when actual consumption continues to match the commitment’s eligibility and scope.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>An application team could redistribute eligible VMs between two regions while continuing to consume the same billing account’s flexible commitment. Before purchasing, it should forecast minimum hourly eligible spending: unchanged instance counts alone do not guarantee unchanged spending when VM sizes or regional prices differ.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Stable demand with changing regions favors flexible commitments; add reservations when capacity assurance is required.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Identify what remains predictable: total consumption or consumption within a particular region. For ongoing eligible compute usage whose regional distribution may change, prefer a compute flexible commitment and select the term supported by the workload horizon.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/committed-use-discounts-overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Committed use discounts for Compute Engine:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Cross-region flexible coverage, regional resource-based restrictions, commitment duration, and hourly spending obligations.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/reservations-with-commitments\"><strong>Google Cloud — Combine reservations with committed use discounts:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: The distinction between discounts and capacity assurance, including separate reservations with compute flexible commitments.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/reservations-overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — About reservations:</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Zonal capacity assurance, charges for unused reserved resources, and the absence of duplicate resource charges when reservations are consumed.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide:</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Alignment with “Optimizing performance and cost,” objective 5.2, which includes infrastructure cost planning and committed-use discounts. <a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com\">English</a></p>","lessons":["d5l1"]},"e3q46":{"id":"e3q46","exam":3,"n":46,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You’re designing a secure Cloud Build pipeline for a web application that needs access to a package manager. The package manager credentials are highly sensitive and must be protected from unauthorized access. You need a way to retrieve these credentials securely at build time, following Google-recommended practice, while keeping ongoing maintenance to a minimum. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Grant roles/cloudkms.cryptoKeyDecrypter to the Cloud Build service account. Store Cloud KMS-encrypted data in the build config file, specifying the secret under the secrets field.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Grant roles/cloudkms.cryptoKeyDecrypter to the Cloud Build service account. Store Cloud KMS-encrypted data in the build config file, specifying the secret under the availableSecrets.inline field.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Grant roles/secretmanager.secretAccessor to the service account used for the build, and specify the secret under the availableSecrets.secretManager field.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Store base64-encoded data in the web application’s source control repository, and decrypt and use the secret during the build.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Grant roles/secretmanager.secretAccessor to the service account used for the build, and specify the secret under the availableSecrets.secretManager field.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Separate the hard requirement from the preference: the hard requirement is real protection against unauthorized access, and the preference is low ongoing maintenance. D fails the hard requirement outright, since base64 is an encoding, not encryption, and provides no actual confidentiality once it’s sitting in source control. That leaves A, B, and C, which all genuinely protect the secret, so the preference decides between them. A and B both require you to manually encrypt the credential with Cloud KMS and embed the resulting ciphertext directly in the build config file — meaning every rotation of the credential requires re-encrypting and updating that file. Secret Manager, referenced through availableSecrets.secretManager, lets Cloud Build pull the current value directly from a dedicated secret store with no ciphertext embedded in the build config at all, which is both Google’s documented, purpose-built mechanism for this and the option with the least ongoing upkeep.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“package manager credentials are highly sensitive and need to be protected from unauthorized access”</strong> — a decisive requirement clue that rules out D immediately, since base64 offers no real protection.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“securely retrieve credentials at build time following Google-recommended practices”</strong> — a technical signal pointing at Cloud Build’s documented secret-handling guides, which treat Secret Manager integration as the primary path and the Cloud KMS-based top-level secrets field as legacy.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“keep maintenance overhead to a minimum”</strong> — a decisive preference clue: it favors whichever secret-retrieval mechanism requires the least manual work to keep current, which rules out the two options that embed a static ciphertext in the build config.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“secrets field” vs. “availableSecrets.inline field” vs. “availableSecrets.secretManager field”</strong> — technical anchors distinguishing the legacy mechanism, the current KMS-based mechanism, and the Secret Manager mechanism from one another.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Secret Manager is Google Cloud’s dedicated service for storing credentials like API keys and passwords, with built-in versioning and access control. Granting the build’s service account the Secret Manager Secret Accessor role and referencing the secret through the availableSecrets.secretManager field lets Cloud Build fetch the current secret value directly at build time, with the secret’s actual value living only in Secret Manager — never encoded or embedded in the build config. When the credential changes, you add a new version in Secret Manager; there’s no build config file to re-encrypt or edit, which is exactly what keeps maintenance low while still meeting Google’s documented, recommended approach for secret access in Cloud Build.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Outdated:</strong> Specifying the secret under the top-level secrets field is Cloud Build’s legacy mechanism for using Cloud KMS-encrypted data; it works, but it’s the superseded approach, and every credential rotation means manually re-running the Cloud KMS encryption and committing a new ciphertext into the build config.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> The availableSecrets.inline field is the current (non-legacy) way to use Cloud KMS-encrypted data directly in the build config, so this is a legitimate, secure option. But it still requires manually encrypting the credential and maintaining the resulting ciphertext inside the build config file, which is more ongoing effort than letting Secret Manager hold the live value.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Base64 is a reversible encoding scheme, not encryption — anyone who can read the repository can trivially decode the “encrypted” value. Storing it in source control also exposes the credential to everyone with repository access, which directly contradicts the requirement to protect it from unauthorized access.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating A, B, and C as functionally interchangeable because all three involve granting a role and referencing a field in the build config — on the surface, they look like minor syntax variations. The real distinction the question is testing is between a static, manually-maintained encrypted value living in the build config (A, B) and a live reference to a centrally managed secret store (C), which is where the “minimize maintenance” requirement actually bites.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>When a CI/CD system needs a sensitive value at build time, there are two fundamentally different patterns: encrypt the value once and embed the ciphertext in configuration (requiring you to manage encryption and re-embed ciphertext on every change), or store the value in a dedicated secret-management service and reference it by name and version (letting the service own versioning, rotation, and access control). The second pattern is lower-maintenance by design, because updating the secret never requires touching the pipeline configuration itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team using inline Cloud KMS-encrypted secrets in its build config has to coordinate a config change across every repository that uses the credential whenever it rotates, and risks builds breaking if that step is missed. A team using Secret Manager rotates the credential once, in one place, and every build referencing “latest” (or the updated version) picks it up automatically, with no pipeline changes required.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>A ciphertext embedded in the build config has to be re-embedded every time the secret changes; a Secret Manager reference doesn’t.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a Cloud Build question asks for secure secret retrieval with minimal maintenance, look for Secret Manager and the secretAccessor role over any option that embeds Cloud KMS-encrypted ciphertext directly in the build config (whether under the legacy secrets field or the newer inline field) — and treat base64 “encryption” as an automatic disqualifier.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/securing-builds/use-secrets\"><strong>Google Cloud — Use secrets from Secret Manager (Cloud Build):</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (the Secret Manager Secret Accessor role and the availableSecrets.secretManager field)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/securing-builds/use-encrypted-credentials\"><strong>Google Cloud — Use encrypted credentials from Cloud KMS (Cloud Build)</strong></a><strong>:</strong> Supports: the distinction between the legacy top-level secrets field and the current inline field, used to classify and eliminate options A and B</p>","lessons":["d2l8"]},"e3q47":{"id":"e3q47","exam":3,"n":47,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You’re setting up a Cloud Deploy pipeline to deploy an application to Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) as a Deployment. You need the Deployment to run two replicas in the development environment and five replicas in production, while keeping every other configuration parameter identical across both environments. You also want to minimize the ongoing effort of updating the manifests going forward. How should you configure the skaffold.yaml file?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Define multiple profiles, and configure the manifests.kustomize section.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Define a single profile, and configure the manifests.kustomize section.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Define multiple profiles, and configure the manifests.rawYaml section.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Define a single profile, and configure the manifests.rawYaml section.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Define multiple profiles, and configure the manifests.kustomize section.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>There are two separate requirements stacked here: the replica count must differ per environment while everything else stays identical, and future manifest updates should require minimal duplicate effort. Kustomize is built for exactly the first requirement — a shared base manifest plus small, targeted overlay patches (here, just the replica count) — which is what keeps “everything else” automatically consistent, since there’s only ever one copy of the non-varying configuration. The second requirement, differentiating dev from production, needs a profile per target, because Cloud Deploy activates a specific Skaffold profile for each target in the delivery pipeline, and each profile can point to its own Kustomize overlay. A single profile has no way to represent two different overlay paths for two different targets at once, so both “multiple profiles” and “kustomize” are necessary together.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“two replicas in the development environment and five replicas in the production environment”</strong> — a technical signal that per-target configuration differs, which requires some mechanism to select different values per target.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“all other configuration parameters are consistent between environments”</strong> — a decisive constraint: it favors a shared base manifest with small overlay patches over separate, independently maintained files per environment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“minimize developmental overhead when updating the manifests in the future”</strong> — a decisive preference clue: it rules out any approach that requires keeping multiple near-duplicate manifest files in sync by hand.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“skaffold.yaml”</strong> and the explicit manifests.kustomize vs. manifests.rawYaml naming — technical anchors pointing directly at Skaffold’s two distinct manifest-rendering mechanisms, one of which (Kustomize) supports overlay patching and one of which (raw YAML) does not.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Deploy renders manifests through Skaffold, and its documented pattern for per-target variation with Kustomize is to define one profile per target, each with its own manifests.kustomize entry pointing at a target-specific overlay directory (for example, one overlay for development, one for production). Because Kustomize overlays only need to specify the fields that differ — here, just the replica count — the base manifest holding every other setting is defined once and shared, so there’s nothing to keep manually synchronized across environments. Cloud Deploy’s delivery pipeline then references the matching profile for each target, so the dev target renders with two replicas and the production target renders with five, with a single source of truth for everything else.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> A single profile can only point to one Kustomize configuration. Since development and production need different replica counts, you need two separate overlay paths active for their respective targets — which requires two profiles. A single profile has no way to represent both outcomes at once.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Multiple profiles with raw YAML can technically achieve different replica counts by pointing each profile at an entirely separate manifest file (for example, one full file for development, another for production). But raw YAML has no overlay/patch mechanism, so every other configuration parameter has to be duplicated identically across both files and manually kept in sync — directly working against “all other configuration parameters are consistent” and “minimize overhead when updating manifests in the future.”</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> A single profile using raw YAML means exactly one static manifest file with no mechanism to vary anything — including the replica count — between development and production. This configuration can’t actually produce two replicas in one environment and five in the other at all.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “profiles” and “manifest format” as independent choices rather than recognizing they have to work together. A learner might reason “Kustomize handles the differences, so one profile is enough,” missing that Cloud Deploy’s target-to-profile association is itself what selects which overlay applies — the overlay mechanism and the per-target profile selection are two halves of the same solution.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Kustomize’s core design is a base configuration plus small, composable overlay patches per variant, which is precisely suited to “mostly identical configuration, with a few deliberate per-environment differences.” Pairing that with Skaffold profiles — one profile per Cloud Deploy target, each pointing at its own overlay — is the standard way to let a single pipeline definition serve multiple environments without duplicating the parts that should stay identical.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team maintaining separate full raw-YAML manifests per environment often finds that a change meant for every environment (a new label, an updated resource limit, a changed probe setting) gets applied to one file and forgotten in another, causing environments to drift apart silently. A team using a shared Kustomize base with per-environment overlays makes that same change once, in the base, and every environment picks it up automatically on the next render — the overlays only ever need to contain the replica-count difference.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Same manifest everywhere except one small per-environment difference → one shared base plus per-target Kustomize overlays, selected by one profile per target.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a Cloud Deploy/Skaffold question asks for consistent manifests across environments with one or two deliberate per-environment differences and low future maintenance, look for multiple profiles paired with Kustomize overlays — raw YAML forces full file duplication, and a single profile can’t represent more than one target’s configuration at a time.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/skaffold\"><strong>Google Cloud — Manage manifests in Cloud Deploy (rendering using Kustomize)</strong></a><strong>:</strong> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (one profile per target, each pointing to its own Kustomize configuration) and the raw-manifest alternative requiring separate full files per target, used to eliminate options C and D</p>","lessons":["d2l4"]},"e3q48":{"id":"e3q48","exam":3,"n":48,"domain":2,"html":"<p>A third-party application continuously uploads encrypted files to a Cloud Storage bucket. Each file is protected with an HMAC-SHA256 key and contains sensitive data that must be loaded into BigQuery for analysis every day shortly after midnight. You need to design a secure, fully automated pipeline that decrypts these files and loads them into BigQuery, following Google-recommended practices while keeping custom development effort to a minimum. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. 1. Upload the decryption key into the same Cloud Storage bucket that stores the encrypted files. 2. Build a Cloud Build pipeline that decrypts each file and then loads it into BigQuery. 3. Set up a Cloud Scheduler job that triggers the pipeline daily at the required time.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. 1. Create a Cloud Key Management Service (Cloud KMS) key ring and import the decryption key into it. 2. Containerize the decrypt-and-load script, then push the resulting image to an Artifact Registry repository. 3. Create a Cloud Run job that runs the image on a recurring schedule. 4. Grant the Cloud Run service agent the Cloud KMS CryptoKey Encrypter/Decrypter role.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. 1. Create a Cloud Key Management Service (Cloud KMS) key ring and import the decryption key into it. 2. Configure a Cloud Build trigger that calls Cloud KMS to decrypt each file before loading it into BigQuery. 3. Set up a Cloud Scheduler job that fires the trigger daily at the required time.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. 1. Upload the decryption key into the same Cloud Storage bucket that stores the encrypted files. 2. Write a script that retrieves the key from Cloud Storage and uses it to decrypt each file. 3. Containerize the script and push the resulting image to an Artifact Registry repository. 4. Create a Cloud Run job that runs the image on a recurring schedule.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. 1. Create a Cloud Key Management Service (Cloud KMS) key ring and import the decryption key into it. 2. Containerize the decrypt-and-load script, then push the resulting image to an Artifact Registry repository. 3. Create a Cloud Run job that runs the image on a recurring schedule. 4. Grant the Cloud Run service agent the Cloud KMS CryptoKey Encrypter/Decrypter role.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Must-have constraints: the key has to be managed securely (not just “available”), the job must run unattended on a daily schedule, and the solution should take minimal custom engineering while still following Google’s own recommended pattern. Preference, not requirement: which specific compute product runs the script, as long as it’s a managed, low-maintenance option. Capability: Cloud KMS exists specifically to store and broker cryptographic key material — including imported HMAC-SHA256 key material — without ever exposing the raw key to application code or storage; Cloud Run jobs exist specifically to run a container to completion on a schedule, with a built-in “Add Scheduler Trigger” integration that requires no pipeline authoring. Elimination: any option that puts the decryption key in the same Cloud Storage bucket as the data it protects (A, D) fails the security requirement outright, since anyone with read access to the bucket has both the ciphertext and the key. Any option that repurposes Cloud Build — a CI/CD build tool meant for compiling, testing, and deploying code — as the engine for a recurring data-decrypt-and-load job (A, C) is forcing a tool to do something it isn’t designed or optimized for, which works against “Google-recommended practices” and “minimal development effort.” Decision: Option B is the only one that stores the key correctly (Cloud KMS), runs the job with the purpose-built scheduled-execution service (Cloud Run jobs), and explicitly grants the minimum IAM permission needed to decrypt.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“HMAC-SHA256 key” and “sensitive data” — a hard constraint that key handling itself is part of what’s being graded, not incidental detail.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“secure and automated solution” — a compound hard constraint; an option that is automated but not secure (A, D) or that strains to be secure through an awkward mechanism (C) does not fully satisfy it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“follow Google-recommended practices” — a technical signal pointing toward purpose-built managed services (Cloud KMS for keys, Cloud Run jobs for scheduled containers) over repurposed tooling.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“minimizing the required development effort” — an elimination signal favoring the option with the least custom pipeline/trigger configuration, which favors Cloud Run’s native scheduler integration over building a Cloud Build trigger from scratch.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“every day after midnight” — a sequence/cardinality instruction confirming a recurring, unattended schedule is required; satisfied by all four options, so it narrows nothing on its own but rules out any manual or ad hoc process.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Importing the HMAC-SHA256 key into a Cloud KMS key ring means the raw key material lives inside a service designed to guard it — it can be used to decrypt without ever being readable in plaintext outside of an authorized call, and access is governed by IAM rather than by whoever can read a storage bucket. Packaging the decrypt-and-load logic as a container and publishing it to Artifact Registry is the standard, Google-recommended way to make a piece of automation portable and versioned. A Cloud Run job is purpose-built to execute that container to completion on a schedule — Cloud Run’s own console and CLI offer a one-step “Add Scheduler Trigger” that wires up Cloud Scheduler for you, which is precisely the “minimal development effort” path. Finally, granting the Cloud KMS CryptoKey Encrypter/Decrypter role (<code>roles/cloudkms.cryptoKeyEncrypterDecrypter</code>) is the least-privilege grant needed for the executing identity to call Decrypt against that specific key — nothing more, nothing less.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Placing the decryption key in the same bucket as the encrypted files defeats the purpose of encrypting them in the first place — anyone who can read the bucket can read both the ciphertext and the key. Using Cloud Build as the recurring processing engine is also a mismatch: Cloud Build is designed for build/test/deploy steps triggered by source changes, not as a standing daily data-decrypt-and-load job, making this a “Different Use Case” layered on top of the security failure.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> This option gets key management right by using Cloud KMS, so it isn’t insecure, but it still drives the daily decrypt-and-load work through a Cloud Build trigger. Cloud Build pipelines are optimized for short-lived build/deploy jobs rather than ongoing scheduled data processing, so while it could be made to work, it isn’t the Google-recommended, lowest-effort fit, and unlike Option B it never grants the executing identity permission to call Cloud KMS, which would leave decryption failing on IAM grounds as described.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> The execution pattern (containerize the script, publish to Artifact Registry, run as a scheduled Cloud Run job) is structurally identical to the correct answer’s pattern, but it inherits the same flaw as Option A — the decryption key sits in the same Cloud Storage bucket as the data it’s meant to protect, which is the one requirement this question is most directly testing.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “automated and running on a schedule” as sufficient to call a design secure. Two of the four options (A and D) are fully automated and correctly scheduled, yet both fail because of where the key lives, not how the job runs. The question would only reward storing the key in Cloud Storage if it were encrypted there by a separate, properly managed key — simply dropping the raw decryption key next to the ciphertext is never the secure choice, regardless of how clean the rest of the automation looks.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>The transferable idea is separation of secrets from data: a cryptographic key should live in a system designed to broker access to it under IAM control (Cloud KMS), never in the same storage location as the material it protects. Layered on top of that is tool fit: a scheduled, recurring execution engine purpose-built for running a container to completion (Cloud Run jobs) is the Google-recommended, low-effort choice for batch-style automation, while a CI/CD build tool (Cloud Build) is optimized for building and deploying code, not for standing in as a daily ETL engine. One nuance worth flagging: in production, the identity that actually needs the Cloud KMS CryptoKey Encrypter/Decrypter role is the Cloud Run job’s runtime service account — the identity your code executes as and uses to call other Google Cloud APIs — which is distinct from the Google-managed “Cloud Run service agent” that handles internal plumbing like pulling the container image. The exam phrasing uses “service agent” loosely to mean “the identity Cloud Run uses,” so for this question treat the two as the same target; just know the distinction exists if you’re wiring this up for real.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Teams building scheduled data-ingestion jobs like this one typically standardize on exactly this pattern — Cloud KMS for any key material, a container image in Artifact Registry, and a Cloud Run job on a Cloud Scheduler trigger — because it keeps the security boundary (who can decrypt) and the operational boundary (who can deploy a new version of the script) cleanly separate and auditable through Cloud Audit Logs, while avoiding the ongoing maintenance burden of a standing VM or a misapplied CI/CD pipeline.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Keys go in Cloud KMS, never in the bucket next to what they protect; recurring container jobs go in Cloud Run jobs, not Cloud Build.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Whenever a Google Cloud scenario combines “decrypt a file” with “run it on a schedule,” check two things independently: does the key live in Cloud KMS (not alongside the data), and does the job run on a tool built for scheduled execution (Cloud Run jobs) rather than a CI/CD build tool — an option can get one right and still be wrong on the other.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kms/docs/key-import\"><strong>Google Cloud — Import a key into Cloud KMS</strong></a><strong>:</strong> Supports: confirms HMAC-SHA256 key material can be imported into Cloud KMS for secure storage and use</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/execute/jobs-on-schedule\"><strong>Google Cloud — Execute Cloud Run jobs on a schedule:</strong></a> Supports: confirms Cloud Run jobs natively integrate with Cloud Scheduler as the low-effort, Google-recommended way to run a container on a recurring schedule</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kms/docs/reference/permissions-and-roles\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud KMS permissions and roles</strong></a><strong>:</strong> Supports: confirms the exact name and effect of the Cloud KMS CryptoKey Encrypter/Decrypter role granted in the correct answer</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Build overview:</strong></a> Supports: clarifies Cloud Build’s intended CI/CD build/deploy purpose, the basis for eliminating Options A and C as a tool mismatch</p>","lessons":["d2l1","d2l8"]},"e3q49":{"id":"e3q49","exam":3,"n":49,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You’re investigating slow Apigee responses for calls made to the orders proxy in the Production environment. This proxy relies on several services running in Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) behind Cloud Service Mesh. You need to find the root cause of the latency without affecting the performance of the other proxies exposed in that environment. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Turn on distributed tracing for the whole Production environment. Use Cloud Trace to inspect each span and pinpoint the service adding the most latency.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use Cloud Monitoring and the Apigee target latencies metric to pinpoint the service adding the most latency.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use Cloud Logging to review logs from the different backend services to pinpoint the service adding the most latency.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Turn on distributed tracing on Apigee, configured so that only the orders proxy is traced. Use Cloud Trace to inspect each span and pinpoint the service adding the most latency.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Turn on distributed tracing on Apigee, configured so that only the orders proxy is traced. Use Cloud Trace to inspect each span and pinpoint the service adding the most latency.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>There are two requirements stacked here: pinpoint exactly which service in a multi-hop chain is adding latency, and do it without adding overhead to every other proxy running in the same environment. Apigee’s distributed trace configuration applies by default to every API proxy in an environment, but it also supports a trace configuration override scoped to one specific API proxy. Scoping the trace to just the orders proxy satisfies the “don’t impact other services” constraint directly, while Cloud Trace’s span-level breakdown is what lets you see exactly which downstream service in the Cloud Service Mesh chain is responsible for the added latency. Options that use coarser signals (an aggregate latency metric, raw logs) can’t localize the problem as precisely, and enabling tracing environment-wide ignores the explicit “without impacting the other exposed services” constraint.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“multiple services running in GKE with Cloud Service Mesh”</strong> — a technical signal that the latency could originate at any of several hops, which calls for span-level breakdown rather than a single aggregate metric.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“determine the root cause”</strong> — a scope clue favoring a diagnostic tool that attributes latency to a specific service, not just confirms that latency exists.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“without impacting the performance of the other exposed services”</strong> — a decisive constraint clue: it rules out any approach that applies tracing (or its overhead) broadly across every proxy in the environment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“orders proxy”</strong> named specifically — points toward a mechanism that can be scoped to just that one proxy, which Apigee’s trace configuration override supports directly.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Apigee’s environment-level distributed trace configuration defines a default sampling behavior applied to every API proxy in that environment, but Apigee also supports a trace configuration override that targets one specific API proxy by ID, letting you enable (and sample) tracing for just that proxy while leaving every other proxy’s tracing configuration untouched. Scoping the configuration to the orders proxy means only calls through that proxy incur any tracing overhead, directly satisfying “without impacting the other exposed services.” Once that scoped trace data flows into Cloud Trace, you get a span per hop (through Apigee and through the chain of GKE/mesh services), letting you see exactly which span accounts for the bulk of the latency.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Enabling distributed tracing “in the Production environment” without scoping it to a specific proxy applies the sampling configuration to every API proxy in that environment by default. That adds tracing overhead across all exposed services, directly conflicting with the requirement to avoid impacting the other proxies.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> The Apigee target latencies metric can confirm that the backend target for the orders proxy is responding slowly in aggregate, but it doesn’t decompose that latency across the individual downstream services sitting behind Cloud Service Mesh. It tells you something is slow, not specifically which of the “multiple services” is the cause.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Manually reviewing logs from each backend separately, with no shared trace context tying them together, is a slower and less precise way to reconstruct a multi-hop request’s timing than a tool built specifically to show per-span duration across that same chain.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is picking the option that correctly identifies “distributed tracing + Cloud Trace” as the right tool category (A) but misses the scoping detail that the scenario explicitly calls for. Without noticing “without impacting the performance of the other exposed services,” enabling tracing environment-wide looks like the obvious, complete answer — the constraint is what separates it from the proxy-scoped version.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Observability tooling often supports both a broad, default configuration and a narrower, resource-specific override; choosing the broad default when only one resource is under investigation is a common way to introduce unintended overhead elsewhere. Scoping diagnostic instrumentation (tracing, enhanced logging, sampling) to the specific resource under investigation is the general pattern for getting the detail you need without disturbing unrelated, healthy traffic.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team that enables distributed tracing environment-wide to chase down one slow proxy risks adding latency and cost to every other API exposed from that environment, potentially masking the very problem they’re chasing with new noise elsewhere. A team that scopes the trace configuration override to just the proxy under investigation gets the detailed span data it needs while leaving the rest of production traffic completely unaffected.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>One proxy is slow → trace just that proxy, not the whole environment.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When an Apigee/GKE latency question asks you to find the root cause “without impacting other services,” look for the option that scopes distributed tracing to the specific proxy under investigation — environment-wide tracing, aggregate latency metrics, and raw log review are all weaker distractors by comparison.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/develop/enabling-distributed-trace\"><strong>Google Cloud — Enabling distributed tracing (Apigee):</strong></a>Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (Apigee’s Cloud Trace integration and span-level request breakdown)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/reference/apis/apigee/rest/v1/organizations.environments.traceConfig.overrides\"><strong>Google Cloud — TraceConfig and trace configuration overrides (Apigee API reference):</strong></a> Supports: the environment-wide default sampling configuration versus a per-API-proxy trace configuration override, which distinguishes option D from option A</p>","lessons":["d4l10"]},"e3q50":{"id":"e3q50","exam":3,"n":50,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You’re building a centralized platform to monitor applications that run on Kubernetes clusters both on-premises and across several Google Cloud projects. You want developers to be able to monitor both infrastructure and application metrics using OpenTelemetry across these on-premises and Google Cloud environments, while keeping day-to-day operation simple and bringing all the metrics together in one place. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Run a sidecar OpenTelemetry Collector alongside every Pod across all the Kubernetes clusters (on-premises and GKE), configured to export Prometheus-formatted metrics. Run a dedicated Prometheus instance in each cluster, federate them with Thanos in a central GKE cluster, install Grafana on a GKE cluster, and use Grafana to monitor the applications.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Create a scoping project and add all the Google Cloud projects to it. Use Bindplane to streamline OpenTelemetry metric collection from the on-premises Kubernetes clusters into Cloud Monitoring.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Create a new Google Cloud project to act as a scoping project, and add the other Google Cloud projects to it. Instrument the applications running on-premises so they export metrics to Cloud Monitoring as user-defined metrics, and use Cloud Monitoring to monitor the applications.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Enable Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus collection using the OpenTelemetry Collector on the GKE clusters, and run unmanaged Prometheus on the on-premises Kubernetes clusters. Install Grafana on a GKE cluster, and use Grafana to monitor the applications.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Create a scoping project and add all the Google Cloud projects to it. Use Bindplane to streamline OpenTelemetry metric collection from the on-premises Kubernetes clusters into Cloud Monitoring.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>There are three things to solve at once: aggregate metrics across several Google Cloud projects, bring in OpenTelemetry-based infrastructure and application metrics from on-premises, and do both with minimal self-managed infrastructure. Cloud Monitoring’s metrics scope, hosted by a scoping project, is the native, built-in mechanism for the first part — no extra software to run. Bindplane is Google’s own documented, OpenTelemetry-native pipeline specifically built to pull metrics, logs, and traces from hybrid and on-premises environments straight into Cloud Monitoring, and it’s offered at no additional cost to Cloud Observability customers, which covers the second and third parts simultaneously: real OpenTelemetry collection from on-prem infrastructure and applications, landing directly in the same single pane of glass as the Google Cloud projects, with no Prometheus servers, Thanos federation, or Grafana deployment for you to operate.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“multiple Google Cloud projects”</strong> — a technical signal pointing directly at Cloud Monitoring’s scoping project/metrics scope feature, which exists specifically to unify metrics across several projects.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“monitor both infrastructure and application metrics using OpenTelemetry across on-premises and Google Cloud environments”</strong> — a scope clue requiring a genuine OpenTelemetry collection path from on-premises infrastructure, not just hand-instrumented application code.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“keep the operation easy to support”</strong> — a decisive preference clue: it eliminates any option built around self-hosted Prometheus, Thanos, or Grafana stacks that the team would have to operate.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“integrate all metrics into a single place”</strong> — reinforces that the destination should be one existing, managed service (Cloud Monitoring) rather than a separately maintained visualization layer like a self-hosted Grafana instance.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A scoping project with a multi-project metrics scope lets every Google Cloud project’s metrics appear in one Cloud Monitoring view with no additional infrastructure — it’s a configuration setting, not a service you run. Bindplane complements this by deploying and managing OpenTelemetry collectors across on-premises and hybrid cloud sources, sending that telemetry directly to Cloud Monitoring’s APIs, so on-premises Kubernetes infrastructure and application metrics land in exactly the same place as the Google Cloud project metrics. Together, this gives developers a single, OpenTelemetry-based view across every environment, with Google managing both the central monitoring platform and the hybrid collection pipeline’s fleet management.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Running a sidecar collector per Pod, a dedicated Prometheus server per cluster, Thanos for federation, and a self-hosted Grafana instance is the heaviest possible stack to build and operate yourself. This is the opposite of “easy to support” — every one of those components needs its own scaling, patching, and high-availability story.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Using a scoping project correctly solves the multi-Google-Cloud-project aggregation, but instrumenting on-premises applications individually to push user-defined metrics only covers application-level data — it misses infrastructure metrics from the on-premises clusters entirely, and it means every application needs its own custom instrumentation work rather than a single, uniform OpenTelemetry collection layer.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Managed Service for Prometheus handles the GKE side with little operational burden, but falling back to unmanaged (self-hosted) Prometheus on-premises reintroduces the exact maintenance burden the requirement asks to avoid, and adding a self-hosted Grafana instance as the unifying view means operating yet another component instead of using the already-centralized, managed Cloud Monitoring.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming that because Prometheus and Grafana are familiar, industry-standard observability tools, building a stack around them is automatically the right answer. The scenario’s explicit “easy to support” and “single place” requirements are specifically testing whether you’ll recognize Google’s managed, purpose-built hybrid path (Bindplane into Cloud Monitoring, with a scoping project for multi-project aggregation) over a more familiar but self-operated alternative.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>When a monitoring architecture needs to span cloud-native and on-premises environments, the lowest-maintenance design usually pairs the cloud provider’s own managed collection pipeline for hybrid sources with the provider’s native multi-project aggregation feature, rather than building a parallel open-source observability stack (Prometheus, Thanos, Grafana) that duplicates capabilities the managed platform already provides.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team that stands up per-cluster Prometheus instances, Thanos federation, and a self-hosted Grafana deployment takes on the full operational lifecycle of all three — upgrades, storage scaling, and failure recovery — on top of the monitoring work itself. A team that configures a Cloud Monitoring scoping project and deploys Bindplane’s managed collector fleet gets the same cross-environment visibility while Google operates the aggregation and collection-management layers.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Multiple Google Cloud projects in one view → scoping project; on-premises OpenTelemetry metrics into that same view with the least effort → Bindplane into Cloud Monitoring, not a self-hosted Prometheus/Thanos/Grafana stack.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks for low-maintenance, unified monitoring across multiple Google Cloud projects and on-premises environments using OpenTelemetry, look for a Cloud Monitoring scoping project paired with Bindplane — options that build a self-hosted Prometheus/Thanos/Grafana stack, or that only manually instrument individual on-premises applications, are the weaker distractors.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/stackdriver/bindplane\"><strong>Google Cloud — About Bindplane:</strong></a> Supports: the correct-answer mechanism (an OpenTelemetry-native pipeline that collects metrics, logs, and traces from hybrid/on-premises environments into Cloud Monitoring, free for Cloud Observability customers)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/settings\"><strong>Google Cloud — Metrics scopes overview:</strong></a> Supports: the scoping project / multi-project metrics scope mechanism used to unify metrics across multiple Google Cloud projects</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide:</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment (Section 4.1, Instrumenting and collecting telemetry, explicitly names hybrid/multi-cloud environments and Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus)</p>","lessons":["d4l7"]},"e3q51":{"id":"e3q51","exam":3,"n":51,"domain":4,"html":"<p>While investigating a sustained spike in request volume, you build a custom dashboard in Cloud Monitoring and trace the unusual traffic to a single type of client. You now want your company’s development team to continue the investigation using that same custom dashboard. Following Google-recommended practices, what should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Export the custom dashboard as a JSON file and send the file to the development team.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Export the dashboard’s time series data in CSV format, and have the development team analyze it in BigQuery.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Grant the development team the Monitoring Viewer (roles/monitoring.viewer) IAM role at the project level, and share the custom dashboard’s URL with them.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Grant the development team the Viewer (roles/viewer) IAM role at the project level, and share the custom dashboard’s URL with them.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Grant the development team the Monitoring Viewer (roles/monitoring.viewer) IAM role at the project level, and share the custom dashboard’s URL with them.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Must-have constraints: the development team needs to go on using the <em>same live dashboard</em> you already built, and the chosen method has to match Google’s own documented recommendation. Not a requirement: the team editing the dashboard, managing alerts, or touching anything outside Monitoring. Capability: Cloud Monitoring dashboards are sharable in place — anyone with sufficient IAM permission on the project can open the dashboard’s URL directly and see live, filterable data, no export step needed. Elimination: options that export the dashboard or its data (A, B) hand the team a disconnected, static artifact instead of the live investigative tool you built, and require extra engineering (a JSON re-import flow, or a whole BigQuery analysis pipeline) that the scenario doesn’t call for. Option D grants access, but through <code>roles/viewer</code>, a broad basic role that reaches far beyond Monitoring into most of the project’s resources — more access than the task needs. Decision: Option C is the only one that preserves the live dashboard experience and grants exactly the scope required, nothing more.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“continue the investigation using that same custom dashboard” — a hard constraint that the team needs the live, interactive dashboard, not a copy or a derived dataset.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“following Google-recommended practices” — a technical signal pointing straight at Google’s own documented least-privilege sharing pattern for Monitoring dashboards.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>No mention of editing, alerting, or configuration needs — an exclusion signal ruling out any role broader than a viewer-level grant.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“development team” (a whole team, ongoing investigation) — favors a durable IAM grant over a one-time file export, since the team will likely need to keep coming back to the dashboard.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p><code>roles/monitoring.viewer</code> is the predefined Cloud Monitoring role scoped specifically to viewing Monitoring data and configuration — dashboards, metrics, alerting policies — without reaching into unrelated Google Cloud resources. Granting it at the project level, then sharing the dashboard’s URL, is exactly the workflow Google documents: the people who need to view a dashboard get the Monitoring Viewer role on the project that stores it, and from there they can open the URL and interact with the same live dashboard, including its filters and time ranges, with no rebuilding or re-importing involved.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A JSON export captures the dashboard’s <em>definition</em> (its widgets and layout), which is useful for recreating the dashboard in another project or checking it into version control, but it is not live data. The development team would need to import it somewhere they already have permissions, and even then they’d be looking at a copy, not the dashboard you’re actively using to investigate.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Exporting time series data to CSV and routing it through BigQuery is a legitimate pattern for long-term analysis or custom querying, but it’s a heavier, custom-built workflow for a task that Cloud Monitoring already solves natively. It also strips away the dashboard’s visual context (the charts and layout that already isolated the anomalous client), forcing the team to reconstruct that view themselves.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong><code>roles/viewer</code> does grant enough access to see the dashboard, so it isn’t outright broken — but it’s a basic/primitive role that provides view access across nearly all resource types in the project, far beyond what’s needed to look at one Monitoring dashboard. That’s more standing access than the task requires, which runs against the least-privilege principle Google’s own guidance calls out for this exact scenario.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming “a role that works” is the same as “the Google-recommended role.” <code>roles/viewer</code> would technically let the team see the dashboard, so it’s tempting to treat it as equivalent to the Monitoring-specific option. The exam is testing whether you reach for the narrowest predefined role that satisfies the need — here, Monitoring Viewer — rather than a broader basic role that happens to also cover it.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>The transferable principle is least privilege applied to sharing, not just to initial provisioning: when someone needs to view a specific Cloud Monitoring artifact, grant the Monitoring-scoped viewer role rather than a project-wide basic role, and let them reach the artifact through its own URL rather than exporting it into a different format. One precision note for real-world use: the correct role identifier is <code>roles/monitoring.viewer</code> (with a dot, under the <code>monitoring.*</code> permission namespace) — a role string like <code>roles/monitoringViewer</code> isn’t a real IAM role, so if you’re ever typing this into <code>gcloud</code> or Terraform, use the dotted form.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Teams running incident response commonly standardize on this exact pattern: grant a Monitoring-scoped viewer role to whoever needs visibility into an ongoing investigation, and share dashboard links directly, rather than emailing exports around. That keeps everyone looking at the same live, current data during the incident, and it means revoking access afterward is a single IAM change instead of tracking down every exported file that was sent out.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Sharing a live Monitoring dashboard = grant <code>roles/monitoring.viewer</code> + send the URL, not an export.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a Google Cloud scenario asks how to let another team view a Cloud Monitoring dashboard you built, look for the option that grants the narrow <code>monitoring.viewer</code> role and shares the dashboard’s URL — exporting the dashboard or its data, and granting a broad basic role like <code>viewer</code>, are both signs of the wrong answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/charts/share-dashboards\"><strong>Google Cloud — Share a custom dashboard:</strong></a> Supports: documents the exact recommended pattern of granting Monitoring Viewer and sharing the dashboard URL</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/roles-permissions/monitoring\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Monitoring roles and permissions:</strong></a> Supports: confirms the correct <code>roles/monitoring.viewer</code> identifier and its scope relative to other Monitoring roles</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/access-control\"><strong>Google Cloud — Control access with IAM (Monitoring):</strong></a> Supports: confirms <code>roles/viewer</code> is a broad, project-wide basic role, the basis for eliminating Option D on least-privilege grounds</p>","lessons":["d4l8","d4l13"]},"e3q52":{"id":"e3q52","exam":3,"n":52,"domain":1,"html":"<p>Your organization maintains separate Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) clusters for staging and production. You must select release channels according to Google-recommended practices, prioritizing cluster stability over early access to new features. Which configuration should you choose?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Enroll production in the Stable channel and staging in the Regular channel.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Enroll production in the Extended channel and staging in the Rapid channel.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Enroll both production and staging in the Stable channel.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Enroll both production and staging in the Regular channel.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Enroll both production and staging in the Stable channel.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Apply two decisions: choose the channel that prioritizes release maturity, then keep staging representative of production. Stable satisfies the stability preference. Using it in both environments supports consistent validation before production deployment. Selecting different channels introduces version differences without serving the stated priority.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Separate … clusters for staging and production” — Technical signal:</strong> Staging should provide meaningful validation of the production environment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Google-recommended practices” — Technical signal:</strong> Consider coordinated upgrades across environments alongside channel selection.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Prioritizing cluster stability over early access” — Preference:</strong> Favors Stable over the more feature-oriented channels.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Stable receives releases after they have accumulated validation in Rapid and Regular, making it the channel intended for prioritizing maturity over new features. </p>\n<p>Using the same channel in staging and production supports testing the versions intended for production. Google’s upgrade guidance recommends progressively validating upgrades across environments using a common release channel. <strong>Channel alignment alone does not guarantee identical versions or staging-first upgrades</strong> ; upgrade timing and sequencing must also be managed. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Stable suits production, and Regular staging can help evaluate upcoming changes. However, staging can move ahead of production, reducing its fidelity as the final validation environment. This arrangement is less aligned with the stated emphasis on stability and representative testing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Extended supports retaining a Kubernetes minor version longer, while Rapid provides earlier access to releases. Extended is useful when prolonged minor-version support is required; it is not simply a more thoroughly validated Stable channel. Rapid staging also emphasizes release freshness rather than the requested stability preference. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Matching channels supports consistency, and Regular is Google’s default recommendation for balancing feature availability with stability. However, the question explicitly favors stability over feature freshness, making Stable the stronger choice. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Do not assume staging must always use a faster channel than production. Early feature exploration and final production validation serve different purposes. If the priority changed to <strong>balancing feature freshness with stability</strong> , Regular for both environments—option D—would become the better fit.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Release maturity, support duration, and upgrade order are separate decisions. Select a channel for the desired maturity level, keep validation environments representative, and control when upgrades progress between environments.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team can place both clusters on Stable, validate an available upgrade in staging, observe workload health, and then advance production to the validated version. A controlled rollout reduces the risk that production encounters compatibility problems that staging never exercised. </p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For stability-focused production validation, align both environments on Stable and test upgrades in staging first.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Choose the channel from the stated maturity-versus-freshness preference, then evaluate staging alignment. Maximum release maturity favors Stable; representative validation favors matching staging and production channels.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/release-channels\"><strong>Google Cloud — About release channels:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Stable, Regular, Rapid, and Extended channel purposes and their distinctions.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/best-practices/upgrading-clusters\"><strong>Google Cloud — Best practices for GKE cluster upgrades:</strong></a></p>\n<p>Supports: Representative testing environments, release-channel selection, maintenance controls, and progressive upgrades.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/rollout-sequencing-custom-stages/about-rollout-sequencing\"><strong>Google Cloud — About rollout sequencing with custom stages:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Same-channel recommendations and qualifying upgrades before advancing to subsequent environments.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Objective 1.4, covering staging and production environments, GKE cluster management, and safe upgrading practices.</p>","lessons":["d1l9"]},"e3q53":{"id":"e3q53","exam":3,"n":53,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your organization is consolidating its monitoring and alerting systems. The centralized alerting platform exposes a webhook that is accessible only from within your VPC network. You need to deliver Google Cloud alert notifications to this endpoint while minimizing costs and following Google-recommended practices. Which approach should you choose?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create a Pub/Sub topic and a notification channel that publishes to it, then configure a pull subscription to consume the notifications.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Create a Pub/Sub topic and a notification channel that publishes to it, then configure a push subscription targeting the internal webhook.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create a webhook notification channel targeting the internal endpoint, then associate that channel with your alerting policies.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Create a Pub/Sub topic and notification channel, deploy a Cloud Run function that forwards notifications to the internal endpoint using Direct VPC egress, and configure a push subscription targeting the function.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Create a Pub/Sub topic and a notification channel that publishes to it, then configure a pull subscription to consume the notifications.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Private endpoint reachability is the decisive constraint. Eliminate direct delivery mechanisms that cannot reach the internal webhook. Google explicitly recommends Pub/Sub notifications with a pull subscription for private Monitoring destinations. This approach also avoids mandating the additional function introduced by option D. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Accessible only from within your VPC network” — Hard constraint:</strong> The destination cannot receive ordinary public webhook delivery.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Following Google-recommended practices” — Technical signal:</strong> Select the documented private-endpoint notification pattern.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Minimizing costs” — Optimization objective:</strong> Avoid unnecessary components, while accounting for the resources needed to consume messages.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Monitoring publishes notifications to the configured Pub/Sub topic. A consumer with access to Pub/Sub and network reachability to the webhook retrieves those notifications through the pull subscription and invokes the internal endpoint.</p>\n<p>The consumer initiates the Pub/Sub connection, so notification delivery does not require opening an inbound endpoint in the VPC. <strong>The consuming application must perform the webhook call; creating a pull subscription alone does not invoke it.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> Push delivery sends HTTPS requests to a reachable subscriber endpoint. An arbitrary webhook accessible only through private VPC addressing cannot serve directly as that endpoint. Pub/Sub does not acquire private network connectivity merely because the topic belongs to the same project. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> A webhook notification channel is attractive because it directly matches the destination’s interface. However, Cloud Monitoring webhook channels require public endpoints and cannot directly deliver to this private webhook. It would fit if the destination were appropriately reachable through a public endpoint. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A Cloud Run function can bridge Pub/Sub push delivery to the private webhook through Direct VPC egress. This is a valid architecture with appropriate invocation permissions and networking, but Google’s specific private-endpoint guidance recommends pull delivery. D becomes attractive when an event-driven serverless relay is an explicit architectural requirement. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Do not reject D as technically unsupported: Direct VPC egress supports second-generation Cloud Run functions. Conversely, do not assume A is automatically cheaper in every deployment. Its cost advantage is clearest when an existing integration service can consume the subscription; actual costs depend on subscriber hosting and notification volume.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Separate message delivery from private network access. A messaging subscription transports notifications, while an application with the necessary connectivity performs the internal action. Google’s documented integration recommendation does not establish a universal cost ranking for every possible implementation.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>If the centralized alerting service supports a Pub/Sub consumer, integrate consumption into that service and have it invoke the webhook locally. Reusing that runtime can avoid deploying and maintaining a separate forwarding function.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For private Monitoring destinations, use Pub/Sub pull and a consumer that can reach the internal endpoint.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Check destination reachability before choosing a notification channel. For a private webhook, Google recommends Pub/Sub with pull consumption; a serverless forwarding function remains a feasible alternative when its operational benefits justify it.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/alerts/troubleshoot-missing-notifications\"><strong>Google Cloud Monitoring — Troubleshoot missing notifications:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: The explicit private-endpoint recommendation, webhook restrictions, and pull-consumer connectivity.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/push\"><strong>Google Cloud Pub/Sub — Push subscriptions:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Push delivery behavior and subscriber endpoint reachability requirements.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/functions/docs/running/direct-vpc\"><strong>Google Cloud — Configure Direct VPC egress for 2nd gen functions:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: The technical feasibility of option D and its generation-specific prerequisite.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification Exam Guide:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Objective 4.3, covering alerting policies and integration with third-party alerting tools, including webhooks.</p>","lessons":["d4l9"]},"e3q54":{"id":"e3q54","exam":3,"n":54,"domain":5,"html":"<p>Your company offers a free photo-processing application. You are selecting compute infrastructure for its backend processing service, which:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Stores original and processed photos in Cloud Storage.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Can resume processing after a failure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Is unsuitable for containerization.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>There is no service level objective (SLO) for photo-processing completion time. You need to minimize compute costs. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Deploy the service on Cloud Run.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Deploy the service on standard Compute Engine VMs covered by a three-year committed use discount.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Deploy the service on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE).</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Deploy the service on Compute Engine Spot VMs.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Deploy the service on Compute Engine Spot VMs.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The workload tolerates interruptions, has flexible completion times, and requires a VM-based execution environment. These characteristics make Spot VMs the best fit among the available options. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Recovery capability:</strong> Processing can resume after a failure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scheduling flexibility:</strong> There is no processing-time SLO.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Hard constraint:</strong> The service is unsuitable for containerization.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Primary objective:</strong> Minimize costs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Google recommends Spot VMs for fault-tolerant workloads such as batch processing. They offer substantially discounted compute capacity in exchange for accepting possible interruption when Google reclaims resources. </p>\n<p>The service can run directly on the VM without containerization. Its recovery capability and flexible completion time accommodate the interruptions and capacity delays associated with Spot VMs.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Cloud Run executes workloads in containers. The service’s containerization constraint makes it unsuitable, even though Cloud Run supports batch jobs. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Standard VMs can run the service, and committed use discounts benefit predictable usage. However, this option requires a three-year commitment and does not exploit the workload’s suitability for discounted interruptible capacity. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>C — Violates Requirement:</strong> GKE is a container orchestration platform designed to deploy and operate containerized applications. It does not match the stated execution constraint. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Spot VMs can be interrupted and might not be immediately available again. The processing system must implement recovery and retry behavior; selecting Spot VMs does not provide application recovery automatically. </p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Spot pricing exchanges interruption tolerance for lower compute costs. Committed use discounts exchange a minimum usage or spending commitment for discounted prices. </p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Keep processing progress in durable storage and make retries safe. A replacement worker can then resume unfinished work without corrupting or unnecessarily duplicating completed outputs.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Resumable processing with flexible deadlines is a strong candidate for Spot VMs.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Choose <strong>D</strong>. The service can tolerate interruptions and delays, making Spot VMs the appropriate cost-saving option.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/spot\"><strong>Spot VMs</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Discounted capacity, preemption, availability limitations, and suitability for fault-tolerant batch workloads.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/committed-use-discounts-overview\"><strong>Committed use discounts for Compute Engine</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Commitment requirements and suitability for predictable usage.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/container-contract\"><strong>Cloud Run container runtime contract</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Cloud Run’s container execution requirements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/kubernetes-engine-overview\"><strong>GKE overview</strong></a><br />\nSupports: GKE’s role as a platform for containerized applications.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d5l1"]},"e3q55":{"id":"e3q55","exam":3,"n":55,"domain":1,"html":"<p>Your organization is moving its production workloads to Google Cloud. During the migration, you need to establish site reliability engineering (SRE) practices that reduce customer disruption when future incidents occur.</p>\n<p>Which two practices should you implement? <strong>Choose two.</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Maintain current playbooks that explain how to troubleshoot problems and mitigate their impact.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Allow every team to modify the production environment when resolving issues.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Configure alerts for the SRE team based on the system’s internal behavior.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Automate recurring tasks to analyze key impact information and recommend mitigation actions to on-call engineers.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>E. Grant full operational autonomy and permissions exclusively to the on-call team.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Maintain current playbooks that explain how to troubleshoot problems and mitigate their impact.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Automate recurring tasks to analyze key impact information and recommend mitigation actions to on-call engineers.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The objective is to reduce customer disruption during incidents. Select practices that help responders assess problems and restore service quickly. A provides prepared response procedures; D reduces repetitive analysis and supports faster decisions. Each is useful independently, and together they improve incident response readiness.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Production workloads” — Operational signal:</strong> Response procedures must support real service failures.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“During the migration” — Timing requirement:</strong> Establish readiness before incidents occur.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Reduce customer disruption” — Decisive objective:</strong> Prioritize effective mitigation and shorter recovery times.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Choose two” — Selection requirement:</strong> Select both documented procedures and operational automation.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A — Maintained playbooks:</strong> Playbooks capture incident impact, debugging guidance, and possible mitigation steps. Keeping them aligned with production changes helps responders act consistently. Google’s SRE Workbook explicitly connects playbooks with reduced repair time, stress, and human error.</p>\n<p><strong>D — Diagnostic and response automation:</strong> Automating repeatable information gathering and analysis reduces manual effort and gives responders useful context sooner. Recommendations can support human decisions for unfamiliar situations. Google’s SRE guidance identifies consistency, faster action, and faster repairs as benefits of appropriately designed automation. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Giving every team production modification capability does not establish a coordinated response process. Google recommends defined incident roles and an operations team responsible for system changes during an incident. Uncoordinated interventions can worsen an outage. </p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Internal monitoring is valuable and can support valid alerts. However, internal behavior alone does not establish that an alert is urgent, actionable, or associated with actual or imminent customer impact. Google recommends emphasizing symptoms and actionable problems when paging responders. A and D more directly describe effective mitigation practices. </p>\n<p><strong>Option E — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> On-call engineers need sufficient authority to respond, but exclusive, unrestricted permissions are not a general SRE requirement. Use scoped permissions and coordinated escalation to additional specialists when necessary. Google recommends least privilege, including temporary elevated access where appropriate. </p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Do not interpret C as proof that internal metrics are unsuitable for alerting. An internally measured request-error rate can reveal customer impact. The weakness is that the option specifies internal behavior without establishing alert actionability or impact.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Effective incident response combines prepared knowledge, timely evidence, clear responsibilities, and safe mitigation. Automation handles repeatable work; responders apply judgment when circumstances require it. Mitigation can restore service before the complete root cause is understood.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Before migration cutover, rehearse a failed deployment. An automated diagnostic tool can gather recent changes and error trends, while a maintained playbook guides rollback and recovery checks. This tests both the documentation and tooling before customers depend on them.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Prepare the response in playbooks; accelerate it with tested automation.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For reducing incident impact, choose maintained troubleshooting and mitigation playbooks plus automation that supports responders. Evaluate alerting by actionability and customer impact, and grant production access according to defined responsibilities.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/on-call/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — On-Call</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Playbook contents, maintenance, and operational automation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/automation-at-google/\"><strong>Google SRE — The Evolution of Automation at Google</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Consistency and faster incident recovery through automation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/\"><strong>Google SRE — Monitoring Distributed Systems</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Symptom-oriented, actionable alerting and valid internal monitoring.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/managing-incidents/\"><strong>Google SRE — Managing Incidents</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Coordinated production changes, incident roles, and delegation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/using-iam-securely\"><strong>Google Cloud — Use IAM securely</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Least privilege and appropriately scoped production permissions.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l13"]},"e3q56":{"id":"e3q56","exam":3,"n":56,"domain":3,"html":"<p>A new version of your Cloud Run application is being introduced through a canary rollout. An SRE colleague reports that the application is now breaching a service level objective (SLO). Your immediate priority is to restore healthy service as quickly as possible. Which action should you take <strong>FIRST</strong>?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Update the traffic allocation so that the latest revision receives 100% of requests.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Update the traffic allocation so that the previous revision receives 100% of requests.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Deploy a new revision from the application’s last known good version.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Investigate the source of the latency using Cloud Trace.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Update the traffic allocation so that the previous revision receives 100% of requests.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The immediate requirement is service recovery. The recent rollout provides a plausible mitigation target, and traffic reassignment offers a direct way to reverse exposure to that change. Prioritize restoring the previous serving revision over deploying another revision or completing a root-cause investigation. Google’s SRE guidance prioritizes mitigating user impact before investigating the underlying cause. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Canary rollout” — Technical signal:</strong> Traffic is being introduced to a candidate revision while an earlier revision remains available.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Breaching a service level objective” — Technical signal:</strong> Reliability has degraded sufficiently to require intervention.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Restore healthy service as quickly as possible” — Hard constraint:</strong> Favor the shortest available recovery path.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“FIRST” — Sequence instruction:</strong> Choose immediate mitigation before subsequent diagnosis and remediation.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Run supports rolling back by assigning 100% of service traffic to an existing previous revision. Changing traffic allocation does not require deploying the application again.</p>\n<p>This answer assumes the previous revision remains a healthy, compatible rollback target. Traffic changes are not instantaneous, and in-flight requests can continue during the transition, so confirm recovery after changing the allocation. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> This completes the rollout to the revision associated with the emerging reliability problem, potentially increasing user impact. It would suit promoting a successfully validated canary rather than responding to the reported degradation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Redeploying a known good application version can restore service, but it introduces another deployment when the existing previous revision can receive traffic directly. The additional deployment work delays the available mitigation. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Cloud Trace helps investigate request latency and performance bottlenecks. However, investigation alone does not redirect traffic or restore service. It is appropriate for diagnosing the problem after mitigation, or in parallel without delaying recovery.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Identifying the exact defect can feel like the necessary first step, but a safe rollback can reduce impact before that investigation finishes. If no usable previous revision remained available, deploying a new revision from the known good application version—option C—could become necessary.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Canary deployment limits exposure while evaluating a candidate release. Retaining the established baseline makes reversal practical; determining why the candidate failed is a separate task from restoring acceptable service.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>After redirecting traffic, monitor current error rates and latency to confirm that user experience improves. Preserve the unsuccessful revision’s logs and traces for investigation. This approach reduces recovery time while retaining evidence needed to prevent recurrence.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>When a canary degrades service, restore traffic to the healthy baseline before pursuing detailed diagnosis.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For <strong>FIRST</strong> -action questions during a rollout incident, look for an available mitigation that reduces user impact with minimal additional change. Prefer traffic rollback to an existing healthy revision over redeployment or extended troubleshooting.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/rollouts-rollbacks-traffic-migration\"><strong>Google Cloud Run — Rollbacks, gradual rollouts, and traffic migration:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Routing all traffic to a previous revision, changing allocation without redeployment, and transition behavior.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/incident-response/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — Incident Response:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Prioritizing mitigation before root-cause analysis and using rollback to reduce incident impact.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/trace/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Trace overview:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Cloud Trace’s role in latency analysis and performance troubleshooting.</p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e3q57":{"id":"e3q57","exam":3,"n":57,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Jenkins orchestrates application deployments to Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) and on-premises VMs. Following an expansion of the on-premises infrastructure, deployments to those VMs take longer to start, although resource utilization remains within normal levels. You need to reduce the delay before on-premises deployments begin. Which change should you make?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Increase the CPU performance and memory capacity of the on-premises VMs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Adopt blue-green deployments for applications running on the on-premises VMs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Increase the configured number of Jenkins executors.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Configure Jenkins to retrieve deployment artifacts from an on-premises artifact repository.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Increase the configured number of Jenkins executors.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The requirement concerns deployment <strong>start delay</strong> , while normal utilization provides no evidence that faster hardware is needed. Among the choices, increasing execution capacity most directly addresses tasks waiting to begin. Changing deployment strategy or artifact storage does not increase Jenkins scheduling capacity.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Expansion of the on-premises infrastructure” — Technical signal:</strong> The orchestration configuration might need to accommodate additional deployment work.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Take longer to start” — Hard constraint:</strong> Focus on initiation and scheduling rather than execution speed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Resource utilization remains within normal levels” — Technical signal:</strong> Hardware saturation is not the demonstrated bottleneck.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A Jenkins executor is a task-execution slot. Its configured count determines how many tasks can run concurrently on a node. Increasing that count on eligible agents allows additional deployment tasks to begin without waiting for an occupied slot.</p>\n<p>This helps when executor availability is the constraint and the agents have sufficient CPU, memory, I/O, and network capacity. Configure execution capacity on agents; Jenkins discourages running deployment work on the controller’s built-in node. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Faster CPUs and additional memory help resource-bound workloads. They do not directly increase the configured executor count, and the scenario reports normal utilization.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Blue-green deployment maintains alternate application environments and switches traffic between them to support safer releases and rollback. It does not resolve Jenkins tasks waiting for execution capacity. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> An artifact repository changes where deployment packages are stored and retrieved. It does not increase Jenkins execution slots, and the scenario provides no evidence that artifact retrieval causes the delay.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Normal CPU utilization does not guarantee that Jenkins has an available executor: concurrency limits can leave tasks waiting despite hardware headroom. If measurements instead showed CPU or memory saturation during deployment execution, upgrading resources could become appropriate.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Separate <strong>queue waiting time</strong> from <strong>task execution time</strong>. These symptoms favor an executor-capacity explanation, but normal resource utilization alone does not prove it. In production, inspect the queue reason and eligible-agent availability before tuning concurrency; offline agents or restrictive labels can also delay starts. </p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Increase agent executor capacity gradually, then compare queue waiting time and resource utilization. This can shorten deployment initiation while helping you avoid introducing resource contention through excessive parallelism.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Slow starts with hardware headroom point toward execution-slot availability; slow running tasks require a different diagnosis.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Determine whether work is waiting to start or running slowly. When Jenkins concurrency is the limiting factor, increase eligible-agent executor capacity within the resources available.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/managing/nodes/\"><strong>Jenkins — Managing Nodes:</strong></a>Supports: Executor semantics, concurrency limits, resource considerations, and separation of agent execution from controller responsibilities.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/using/executor-starvation/\"><strong>Jenkins — Executor Starvation:</strong></a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Queue delays caused by unavailable executors, offline agents, and agent-label restrictions.</p>","lessons":["d2l11"]},"e3q58":{"id":"e3q58","exam":3,"n":58,"domain":1,"html":"<p>You are deploying a web application to Cloud Run. Expected traffic varies from 10 requests per second during quiet periods to 1,000 requests per second during peak periods. You need automatic scaling that responds efficiently to demand while staying within the project’s resource quotas.</p>\n<p>What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Manually change the instance count throughout the day according to observed traffic.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Set appropriate resource limits for the Cloud Run service and verify that project quotas support the intended scaling range.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Configure CPU-based autoscaling with a target utilization of 80%.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Configure request-count-based autoscaling with a target of 500 requests per instance.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Set appropriate resource limits for the Cloud Run service and verify that project quotas support the intended scaling range.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The requirements combine automatic capacity adjustment with resource control. A scaling metric determines when more instances are needed; it does not establish how much capacity the service may consume. B addresses the service’s resource configuration and the quota needed to support its scaling range.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“10 … to 1,000 requests per second” — Workload signal:</strong> Capacity must adapt to substantial demand changes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Automatic scaling” — Hard requirement:</strong> Repeated manual instance adjustments are unsuitable.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Within the project’s resource quotas” — Decisive constraint:</strong> Resource sizing and scaling limits must align with available quota.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>In practice, choose suitable CPU and memory allocations per instance, configure a maximum instance count, and check the relevant regional project quotas. Cloud Run recommends service-level maximum instances unless revision-level limits are specifically needed. </p>\n<p>Review quota availability before peak demand and request increases where necessary. Available quota does not guarantee that regional capacity will always be available. </p>\n<p>Per-instance CPU and memory limits alone are insufficient: the instance-count limit also matters. Leave capacity headroom because Cloud Run can briefly exceed its configured maximum instance count in certain circumstances.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Manual adjustments do not meet the requirement to use autoscaling. Cloud Run already supports automatically adjusting instance counts as workload demand changes. </p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Current Cloud Run supports custom CPU utilization targets, including 80%. However, selecting that target does not establish resource ceilings or ensure sufficient quota. The appropriate utilization target also depends on the workload’s performance requirements. </p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Incorrect as Stated:</strong> Cloud Run’s request-based scaling considers <strong>concurrent requests</strong> , rather than an arbitrary cumulative request count. If “500 requests per instance” means concurrency, it could describe a configuration choice, but it still does not address the quota requirement. </p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Do not reject C by assuming Cloud Run’s CPU target cannot be customized; current documentation permits it. The distinction is between a <strong>scaling target</strong> and a <strong>resource boundary</strong>. CPU-target tuning becomes relevant after resource sizing, instance limits, and quotas have been established.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Autoscaling has three separate concerns: signals that indicate demand, limits that constrain scaling, and quotas that govern available resources. Requests per second alone cannot determine the required instance count; request duration and each instance’s processing capacity also matter.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Load-test the application at 1,000 requests per second, measure latency and resource usage, and select an appropriate instance configuration and scaling range. Check regional quotas and leave headroom for other workloads and deployment activity. This provides evidence that the planned configuration can support peak traffic.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Scaling targets guide growth; instance limits and quotas define its boundaries.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question emphasizes quota-aware autoscaling, choose resource sizing, maximum instances, and quota validation. A CPU or concurrency target alone does not satisfy that requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/about-instance-autoscaling\"><strong>Cloud Run instance autoscaling</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Automatic scaling and CPU/concurrency scaling signals.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/configuring/max-instances\"><strong>Set maximum instances for services</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Scaling limits, service-level configuration, and temporary overshoot.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/quotas\"><strong>Cloud Run quotas and limits</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Regional resource quotas and quota adjustments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/configuring/scaling-controls\"><strong>Configure custom scaling controls</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Configurable CPU targets and why option C is feasible but incomplete.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l12"]},"e3q59":{"id":"e3q59","exam":3,"n":59,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Several applications share a load balancer protected by Google Cloud Armor. You need a dashboard that shows blocked requests specifically for the HTTPS API URL prefix <code>mountkirk.com/api</code> and identifies the request parameter names associated with the blocking matches. Which approach should you use?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create a log-based counter metric filtered by <code>configuredAction=\"ALLOW\"</code> and the API <code>requestUrl</code> prefix. Extract <code>matchedFieldName</code> as a label and chart the counts in Cloud Monitoring grouped by that label.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Create a Boolean log-based metric filtered by <code>configuredAction=\"DENY\"</code>. Extract <code>matchedFieldName</code> as a label and chart the results in Cloud Monitoring grouped by that label.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create a log-based counter metric filtered by <code>configuredAction=\"DENY\"</code> and the API <code>requestUrl</code> prefix. Extract <code>matchedFieldName</code> as a label and chart the counts in Cloud Monitoring grouped by that label.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Chart the built-in <code>networksecurity.googleapis.com/https/request_count</code> metric in Cloud Monitoring and filter its <code>blocked</code> label to <code>true</code>.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Create a log-based counter metric filtered by</strong><code>**configuredAction=\"DENY\"**</code><strong>and the API</strong><code>**requestUrl**</code><strong>prefix. Extract</strong><code>**matchedFieldName**</code><strong>as a label and chart the counts in Cloud Monitoring grouped by that label.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The solution must provide three things: blocked-request counts, isolation of the API’s traffic, and a breakdown by matched parameter name. A filtered counter supplies the counts and URL scope; an extracted label supplies the breakdown. A total blocked-request metric lacks the required detail.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Several applications share a load balancer” — Technical signal:</strong> Load-balancer-wide totals can include unrelated application traffic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Specifically for …</strong><code>**mountkirk.com/api**</code><strong>” — Hard constraint:</strong> Filter the request URL before counting.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Blocked requests” — Hard constraint:</strong> Select denied traffic rather than allowed traffic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Request parameter names” — Hard constraint:</strong> Preserve the matched field name as a metric label.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A counter metric counts matching log entries. For enforced deny rules, the filter uses the documented action value and complete field paths:</p>\n<pre><code>      1. jsonPayload.enforcedSecurityPolicy.configuredAction=\"DENY\"\n  2. httpRequest.requestUrl=~\"^https://mountkirk[.]com/api\"\n</code></pre>\n<p>Extract a string label named <code>matchedFieldName</code> using:</p>\n<pre><code>      1. EXTRACT(jsonPayload.enforcedSecurityPolicy.matchedFieldName)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Group the dashboard’s series by this label and sum the counts within each group. Log fields become usable grouping dimensions only after extraction into metric labels. </p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisite:</strong> Relevant request logs must contain the matched-field details. Cloud Armor verbose logging supplies these details for applicable WAF matches. The field can be empty when the match does not identify a key-value parameter. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> The URL filter and grouping approach are useful, but <code>ALLOW</code> selects permitted requests. It cannot provide the required denied-request breakdown.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Incorrect:</strong> User-defined log-based metrics support counter and distribution types; Boolean is not a supported user-defined log-based metric type. This option also omits the API URL filter. A Boolean-valued <em>label</em> is supported, but that is different from creating a Boolean log-based metric. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> The built-in metric can show blocked-request totals through its <code>blocked</code> label. However, it does not expose <code>requestUrl</code> or <code>matchedFieldName</code> dimensions, so it cannot provide the requested URL-specific parameter breakdown. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The built-in metric’s <code>blocked</code> Boolean is tempting, but it identifies enforcement results without supplying parameter-level detail. D would fit a requirement for aggregate blocked-request totals. In request-log filters, use the documented <code>DENY</code> action value rather than treating the metric label as an action string.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A metric filter decides <strong>which events count</strong> ; extracted labels decide <strong>how those events can be categorized</strong>. Cloud Armor denies requests—the matched parameter identifies information that triggered a rule, rather than a parameter being independently blocked.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A dashboard showing increased matches for a parameter such as <code>search</code> can guide investigation of a possible WAF false positive. Keep label values controlled: highly variable values create additional time series and can increase monitoring costs. </p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Filter the denied requests, count the matching logs, and extract the field needed for grouping.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Choose built-in metrics when their existing dimensions satisfy the requirement. When the dashboard needs URL filtering and request-field detail available in logs, use a log-based counter with an extracted label.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/armor/docs/verbose-logging\"><strong>Google Cloud Armor — Verbose logging:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: The <code>DENY</code> action example, matched-field details, verbose logging prerequisites, and empty-field limitations.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/logs-based-metrics\"><strong>Cloud Logging — Log-based metrics overview:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Counter behavior, supported user-defined metric types, and charting log-based metrics.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/logs-based-metrics/labels\"><strong>Cloud Logging — Configure labels on log-based metrics:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Label extraction, <code>EXTRACT</code> syntax, grouping dimensions, and cardinality considerations.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/api/metrics_gcp_i_o#networksecurity\"><strong>Cloud Monitoring — Google Cloud metrics: networksecurity:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: The built-in request-count metric and its available labels.</p>","lessons":["d4l4"]},"e3q60":{"id":"e3q60","exam":3,"n":60,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your team builds an application from a Dockerfile using Cloud Build and stores the resulting container images in Artifact Registry. Developers report that builds take too long. You need to reduce build times using Google-recommended practices. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use <code>--cache-from</code> to reference a previously built image in Artifact Registry, and copy frequently modified files in later Dockerfile instructions.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Use <code>--cache-from</code> to reference a previously built image in Artifact Registry, and copy frequently modified files in earlier Dockerfile instructions.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Store the container-layer cache in Cloud Storage, and copy frequently modified files in earlier Dockerfile instructions.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Store the container-layer cache in Cloud Storage, and copy frequently modified files in later Dockerfile instructions.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Use</strong><code>**--cache-from**</code><strong>to reference a previously built image in Artifact Registry, and copy frequently modified files in later Dockerfile instructions.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The solution must combine cache reuse with an instruction order that preserves reusable work. Artifact Registry already holds the images, so use a previous image as the cache source and introduce frequently changing inputs later. A provides both optimizations. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“From a Dockerfile” — Technical signal:</strong> Docker layer caching applies.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Images in Artifact Registry” — Technical signal:</strong> Previously built images can supply the cache.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Reduce build times” — Required outcome:</strong> Avoid rebuilding unchanged work.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>“Google-recommended practices” — Preference:</strong> Favors the documented Docker-specific caching approach.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Google’s documented Cloud Build configuration pulls a previously built image from Artifact Registry and references it through <code>--cache-from</code>. An existing cache image must be available for reuse. </p>\n<p>Docker invalidates cached work when relevant instructions or inputs change. Placing frequently modified files later preserves earlier layers, such as dependency installation, when their inputs remain unchanged.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> The registry cache is appropriate, but frequently changing files introduced early invalidate subsequent work. This reduces the benefit of caching.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Cloud Storage can preserve build results through a custom save-and-restore process. However, it does not use the documented image-cache approach, and its early placement of changing files further limits reuse. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> The instruction order is appropriate. However, a Cloud Storage cache introduces an additional restoration workflow when Google’s Docker-specific guidance already supports using an image from Artifact Registry. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Choosing a cache source is only half the solution. A cache cannot preserve dependent layers after their inputs change. Copying stable dependency manifests early is useful; copying frequently changing application source before dependency installation is usually inefficient.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Build caching reuses unchanged work. Order stable, expensive operations before frequently changing inputs wherever dependencies allow. Here, “later” refers to Dockerfile instruction order, rather than requiring a separate multi-stage build.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Copy dependency manifests and lockfiles, install dependencies, and then copy application source. Routine source edits can then reuse the dependency layer, shortening the feedback cycle for developers. </p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Reuse the previous image, and place frequently changing inputs late.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For Docker builds in Cloud Build, combine <code>--cache-from</code> with effective layer ordering. Measure the result: retrieving a cache image also takes time, so the benefit depends on how much work is reused. </p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/optimize-builds/speeding-up-builds\"><strong>Google Cloud Build — Best practices for speeding up builds:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Artifact Registry images with <code>--cache-from</code>, cache prerequisites, transfer overhead, and Cloud Storage directory caching.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://docs.docker.com/build/cache/optimize/\"><strong>Docker — Optimize cache usage in builds:</strong></a></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Supports: Cache invalidation, instruction ordering, and separating dependency installation from frequently changing source files.</p>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e4q1":{"id":"e4q1","exam":4,"n":1,"domain":3,"html":"<p>Cloud Monitoring alerts you to potentially malicious activity on a node in your Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster. A container running on that node might be compromised.</p>\n<p>You need to isolate the affected node while investigating the incident and minimize disruption to applications running in the cluster. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Taint the suspicious node to prevent Pods that previously interacted with it from being scheduled on other cluster nodes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Scale the Deployment associated with the suspected container to zero replicas.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Restart the node to interrupt the malicious activity and force its Pods to be recreated on other nodes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cordon the node to prevent new Pod scheduling, then drain it gracefully so controller-managed workloads can be recreated on other nodes.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Cordon the node to prevent new Pod scheduling, then drain it gracefully so controller-managed workloads can be recreated on other nodes.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>D provides controlled evacuation of the affected node while preventing normal scheduling onto it. It is the best available option, although complete incident containment also requires network restrictions. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security signal:</strong> A container might be compromised.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Containment requirement:</strong> Isolate the affected node during investigation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Availability preference:</strong> Minimize disruption to other workloads.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cordoning marks the node as unschedulable. Draining gracefully evicts eligible Pods, allowing their controllers to create replacements elsewhere. Normal eviction respects PodDisruptionBudgets, helping limit simultaneous application disruption. </p>\n<p>Successful evacuation depends on suitable workload controllers, healthy replicas, and sufficient capacity on other nodes. It does not guarantee zero downtime.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A — Incorrect:</strong> Taints affect scheduling or eviction on the tainted node. They do not track which Pods interacted with it or automatically prevent those Pods from running elsewhere. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>B — Violates Requirement:</strong> Scaling to zero removes every replica of that Deployment, potentially taking the application offline. It also leaves the suspicious node available to other workloads.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>C — Incorrect:</strong> Restarting does not establish scheduling or network quarantine. It also does not guarantee that replacement Pods run on different nodes or that the underlying compromise is resolved. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Cordon and drain are not complete security isolation.</strong> Google’s incident-response guidance recommends retaining the suspected Pod for investigation, draining the other workloads, and restricting network access to the node’s VM. Do not blindly redeploy the suspected workload elsewhere. </p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>These controls serve different purposes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cordon:</strong> Prevent normal scheduling onto the node.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Drain:</strong> Gracefully evacuate eligible workloads.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Network quarantine:</strong> Restrict communication involving the suspected node.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Google documents this selective evacuation pattern:</p>\n<pre><code>      1. kubectl cordon NODE_NAME\n  2. kubectl label pod SUSPECT_POD -n APP_NAMESPACE quarantine=true\n  3. kubectl drain NODE_NAME --pod-selector='!quarantine' --ignore-daemonsets\n</code></pre>\n<p>This excludes the quarantined Pod from evacuation. DaemonSet Pods remain, and node-level network restrictions must be configured separately. </p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Cordon stops arrivals, drain evacuates workloads, and network controls restrict communication.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Choose <strong>D</strong> for controlled node evacuation with minimal disruption. During an actual compromise investigation, add network quarantine and preserve the suspected workload appropriately.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/security-mitigations\"><strong>Mitigate security incidents — GKE</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Selective draining, suspected-Pod quarantine, network restrictions, and incident-response tradeoffs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/generated/kubectl_drain/\"><strong>kubectl drain</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Unschedulable nodes, graceful eviction, workload-controller requirements, and DaemonSet exclusions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/taint-and-toleration/\"><strong>Taints and Tolerations</strong></a><br />\nSupports: What node taints control and why option A misrepresents their behavior.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/disruptions/\"><strong>Disruptions</strong></a><br />\nSupports: PodDisruptionBudgets and maintaining availability during voluntary disruptions.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e4q2":{"id":"e4q2","exam":4,"n":2,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You are configuring a continuous integration (CI) pipeline in Cloud Build with the following <code>cloudbuild.yaml</code> configuration:</p>\n<pre><code>      1. steps:\n  2. - name: foo\n  3.   id: foo\n  4. - name: bar\n  5.   id: bar\n  6. - name: baz\n  7.   id: baz\n</code></pre>\n<p>The <code>foo</code> and <code>bar</code> steps each take five minutes and can run independently. The <code>baz</code> step must wait until both have completed successfully. You need to reduce build time by running the independent steps concurrently. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Add the following machine configuration:</p>\n<pre><code>      1. options:\n  2.   machineType: 'E2_HIGHCPU_8'\n</code></pre>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Add the following machine configuration:</p>\n<pre><code>      1. options:\n  2.   machineType: 'E2_HIGHCPU_32'\n</code></pre>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Configure <code>foo</code> to start immediately and make <code>baz</code> wait for both preceding steps:</p>\n<pre><code>      1. steps:\n  2. - name: foo\n  3.   id: foo\n  4.   waitFor: ['-']\n  5. - name: bar\n  6.   id: bar\n  7. - name: baz\n  8.   id: baz\n  9.   waitFor:\n  10.     - foo\n  11.     - bar\n</code></pre>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Configure both independent steps to start immediately and make <code>baz</code> wait for their completion.</p>\n<pre><code>      1. steps:\n  2. - name: foo\n  3.   id: foo\n  4.   waitFor: ['-']\n  5. - name: bar\n  6.   id: bar\n  7.   waitFor: ['-']\n  8. - name: baz\n  9.   id: baz\n  10.   waitFor:\n  11.     - foo\n  12.     - bar\n</code></pre>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Configure both independent steps to start immediately and make</strong><code>**baz**</code><strong>wait for their completion.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Build executes steps sequentially by default. Option D explicitly allows <code>foo</code> and <code>bar</code> to run concurrently while preserving both dependencies required by <code>baz</code>. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Parallelism opportunity:</strong> <code>foo</code> and <code>bar</code> are independent.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Dependency constraint:</strong> <code>baz</code> requires both steps to finish successfully.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Optimization goal:</strong> Reduce elapsed build time through concurrency.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><code>waitFor: ['-']</code> makes each independent step eligible to start when the build begins.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><code>waitFor: ['foo', 'bar']</code> makes <code>baz</code> wait for successful completion of both identified steps. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A — Different Use Case:</strong> More vCPUs can accelerate workloads that benefit from additional CPU capacity. Changing the machine type does not change the step dependencies or enable concurrent execution. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>B — Different Use Case:</strong> A larger machine likewise leaves the sequential execution order unchanged. It does not implement the requested parallelism. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>C — Incorrect:</strong> <code>bar</code> has no <code>waitFor</code> setting, so it waits for all preceding steps—including <code>foo</code>—to finish. The two five-minute steps remain sequential. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Adding <code>waitFor: ['-']</code> only to the first step does not create concurrency. The first step already depends implicitly on the start of the build.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p><code>id</code> identifies a build step. <code>waitFor</code> references those identifiers to establish dependencies. An omitted or empty <code>waitFor</code> waits for all preceding steps; it does <strong>not</strong> mean “no dependencies.” </p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Assuming sufficient resources and unchanged execution times, the prerequisite phase decreases from approximately <strong>10 minutes to 5 minutes</strong>. The complete pipeline still includes the execution time of <code>baz</code> and any startup overhead.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Use <code>['-']</code> to start independently and step IDs to wait for prerequisites.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Choose <strong>D</strong>. Start <code>foo</code> and <code>bar</code> concurrently, then let <code>baz</code> run only after both succeed.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/configuring-builds/configure-build-step-order\"><strong>Configuring the order of build steps</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Concurrent execution, <code>waitFor: ['-']</code>, and explicit dependencies.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/build-config-file-schema\"><strong>Build configuration file schema</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Step identifiers, default sequential execution, and omitted <code>waitFor</code> behavior.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/optimize-builds/increase-vcpu-for-builds\"><strong>Increase vCPU for builds</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Machine-size configuration and its distinction from build-step scheduling.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e4q3":{"id":"e4q3","exam":4,"n":3,"domain":1,"html":"<p>You are designing a multi-tenant Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster. Your customer wants to avoid the risks of long-lived credentials and ensure that each workload receives only the IAM permissions it requires.</p>\n<p>You need to implement IAM service account impersonation following Google-recommended practices. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create an IAM service account and configure it as the node identity of a dedicated node pool. Restrict the workload to that pool using node selectors, taints, and tolerations. Repeat this configuration for each workload.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Create an IAM service account and configure it as the node identity of a node pool without taints. Grant the required IAM permissions to that service account.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create an IAM service account and a Kubernetes ServiceAccount in a cluster with Workload Identity Federation for GKE enabled. Grant the Kubernetes ServiceAccount <code>roles/iam.workloadIdentityUser</code> on the IAM service account, and link them using the <code>iam.gke.io/gcp-service-account</code> annotation. Configure the workload to use the Kubernetes ServiceAccount. Repeat for each workload.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Create an IAM service account and generate a service account key. Store the key in a Kubernetes Secret, mount it into the workload, and set <code>GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS</code> to the mounted key file’s path. Repeat for each workload.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Create an IAM service account and a Kubernetes ServiceAccount in a cluster with Workload Identity Federation for GKE enabled. Grant the Kubernetes ServiceAccount</strong><code>**roles/iam.workloadIdentityUser**</code><strong>on the IAM service account, and link them using the</strong><code>**iam.gke.io/gcp-service-account**</code><strong>annotation. Configure the workload to use the Kubernetes ServiceAccount. Repeat for each workload.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Workload Identity Federation for GKE provides workload-specific access to Google Cloud APIs without distributing service account key files. Its impersonation configuration matches the role binding and annotation described in C. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Credential requirement:</strong> Avoid long-lived credentials.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Authorization requirement:</strong> Apply least privilege separately to each workload.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Architecture:</strong> Multiple tenants share the cluster.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Explicit mechanism:</strong> IAM service account impersonation.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>The implementation requires:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>A dedicated workload identity with narrowly scoped resource permissions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>An impersonation binding restricted to the intended Kubernetes namespace and ServiceAccount.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The annotation linking that Kubernetes ServiceAccount to its IAM service account.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Assignment of the Kubernetes ServiceAccount through the Pod’s <code>serviceAccountName</code>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both the impersonation binding and annotation are required. Workload Identity Federation uses short-lived tokens rather than downloaded service account keys. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Dedicated pools can separate workload placement, but this ties identity to nodes and requires a pool per workload. Google recommends workload-level identities rather than relying on node service accounts for application access. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>B — Violates Requirement:</strong> Workloads sharing the node identity can inherit the same permissions. This does not establish separate least-privilege identities for each tenant workload. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Mounting a service account key in a Kubernetes Secret still distributes a persistent credential. Google recommends avoiding user-managed service account keys when a more secure authentication method is available.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Current guidance generally prefers direct IAM grants to federated workload principals where supported. However, this question explicitly requires <strong>IAM service account impersonation</strong> , making C the appropriate configuration. </p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>The <code>roles/iam.workloadIdentityUser</code> binding authorizes <strong>impersonation</strong>. Separate roles granted to the IAM service account determine <strong>which Google Cloud resources the workload can access</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Two applications can share a GKE node while using different identities: one reads objects from a specific Cloud Storage bucket, while another accesses a particular Secret Manager secret. Their permissions remain independently configurable.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Give each workload a scoped identity that uses short-lived credentials.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Choose <strong>C</strong>. Use Workload Identity Federation for GKE, the impersonation binding, the linking annotation, and a dedicated Kubernetes ServiceAccount assigned to each workload.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity\"><strong>Authenticate to Google Cloud APIs from GKE workloads</strong></a><br />\nSupports: IAM impersonation bindings, required annotations, workload assignment, and direct federation alternatives.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/workload-identity?\"><strong>About Workload Identity Federation for GKE</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Short-lived credentials and the risks of shared node identities.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/service-accounts\"><strong>About service accounts in GKE</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Dedicated, minimally privileged ServiceAccounts for individual workloads.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/best-practices-for-managing-service-account-keys\"><strong>Best practices for managing service account keys</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Avoiding user-managed keys and reducing credential leakage risks.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l3"]},"e4q4":{"id":"e4q4","exam":4,"n":4,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your application is running in production on Cloud Run. Your team has completed a new version, revision B, and wants to test it with 10% of your clients.</p>\n<p>You need to implement this test with the <strong>least operational effort</strong>. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Deploy revision B to the existing service without allocating production traffic. Assign it a revision tag and share its tagged URL with 10% of your clients.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Deploy revision B to a separate Cloud Run service. Update the existing application to forward a percentage of incoming requests to the new service.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Deploy revision B to a separate Cloud Run service. Configure a load balancer to distribute traffic between the existing and new services.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Deploy revision B to the existing service without initially allocating traffic. Configure Cloud Run traffic splitting to send 10% of traffic to revision B and 90% to the existing revision.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Deploy revision B to the existing service without initially allocating traffic. Configure Cloud Run traffic splitting to send 10% of traffic to revision B and 90% to the existing revision.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Run provides native percentage-based routing between revisions of the same service. This supports a canary rollout with minimal configuration and no application routing changes. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Existing platform:</strong> The application already runs on Cloud Run.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Limited exposure:</strong> Test the new revision before a complete rollout.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Primary preference:</strong> Use the least operational effort.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>Deploy revision B with no initial traffic allocation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Set the service’s traffic allocation to <strong>90% for the existing revision and 10% for revision B</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Assess revision B before increasing its allocation.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Cloud Run manages routing through the existing service endpoint. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Tagged URLs support direct testing of a specific revision. However, distributing a different URL requires coordinating client participation instead of automatically allocating traffic through the existing endpoint. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Application-level forwarding requires code changes and another service. Native revision traffic splitting provides the required rollout mechanism with less work. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> This introduces another service and load-balancing configuration, including serverless backends. Those additional components are unnecessary for splitting traffic between revisions of one service. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p><strong>10% of requests is not necessarily 10% of distinct clients.</strong> D is the intended answer for a percentage-based canary. If the requirement means a fixed, identifiable client cohort, traffic splitting alone does not guarantee that assignment. Session affinity is also best effort. </p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Traffic allocation controls the proportion of requests sent to each revision. A revision tag provides a separate URL that targets a particular revision directly.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>After deploying revision B without traffic, configure the split using actual revision names:</p>\n<pre><code>      1. gcloud run services update-traffic SERVICE \\\n  2.   --region=REGION \\\n  3.   --to-revisions=CURRENT_REVISION=90,REVISION_B=10\n</code></pre>\n<p>The command updates routing without requiring application changes. </p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For a simple Cloud Run canary, split traffic between revisions of the existing service.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Choose <strong>D</strong> for the lowest-effort 90/10 canary rollout. Remember that the allocation applies to traffic rather than a guaranteed fixed group of clients.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/rollouts-rollbacks-traffic-migration\"><strong>Rollbacks, gradual rollouts, and traffic migration</strong></a><br />\nSupports: No-traffic deployment, revision traffic splitting, and tagged testing URLs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/run/services/update-traffic\"><strong>gcloud run services update-traffic</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Configuring explicit 90/10 revision allocations.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/configuring/session-affinity?\"><strong>Set session affinity for services</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Request routing behavior and limitations of client affinity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/https/setup-global-ext-https-serverless\"><strong>Set up an external Application Load Balancer with serverless backends</strong></a><br />\nSupports: The additional configuration introduced by option C.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l7"]},"e4q5":{"id":"e4q5","exam":4,"n":5,"domain":1,"html":"<p>Your organization keeps a large amount of rarely accessed data in Cloud Storage. Separately, the projects under your CustomerService folder make frequent Cloud Storage calls but hold very little data of their own. You want to turn on Data Access audit logging company-wide so you can analyze data usage patterns, but the CustomerService folder’s projects need to be left out of that logging. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Enable Data Access audit logging for Cloud Storage at the organization level, and add the principals used by the CustomerService folder’s projects to the exempted principals list.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Enable Data Access audit logging for Cloud Storage at the organization level, and leave the configuration as is with no further changes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Enable Data Access audit logging for Cloud Storage individually on every project and folder except the CustomerService folder.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Enable Data Access audit logging for Cloud Storage individually on every project and folder, and add the principals used by the CustomerService folder’s projects to the exempted principals list.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Enable Data Access audit logging for Cloud Storage at the organization level, and add the principals used by the CustomerService folder’s projects to the exempted principals list.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Must-have constraints: Data Access logging has to reach every current and future project “across the company,” and the CustomerService folder’s projects specifically must not end up logged. Not a requirement: excluding any particular human reviewer or adding logging service by service — the scope is Cloud Storage, company-wide. Capability: enabling at the organization level cascades the configuration to every folder and project beneath it automatically, including ones created later, with no per-project setup. Elimination: Cloud Audit Logs’ hierarchy rules explicitly block a child resource from disabling a Data Access log type its parent already enabled, which rules out any plan built around “enable everywhere except this folder” (C) as a way to selectively disable logging downstream. Manually enabling resource by resource (C, D) also doesn’t extend to future projects the way an org-level setting does. Decision: only an org-level enablement paired with the exempted principals list — Google’s documented mechanism for excluding specific identities from an already-enabled configuration — satisfies both the company-wide requirement and the CustomerService exclusion at once.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“across the company” — a hard constraint pointing toward an organization-level configuration rather than a project-by-project one.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“exclude the CustomerService folder projects from Data Access audit logging” — a hard constraint requiring a reliable, complete exclusion for that folder specifically.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>CustomerService “accesses Cloud Storage frequently, but store[s] very little data” — background explaining why their activity would otherwise flood the audit logs with high-volume, low-value entries, motivating the exclusion rather than changing the mechanism used.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>No mention of excluding particular people, only the folder’s projects — a technical signal that the folder-level exclusion has to be translated into identity terms, since that’s the lever Cloud Audit Logs actually exposes.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Enabling the Data Access audit log for Cloud Storage at the organization level applies it immediately to every existing folder and project and automatically extends to any project created later, which is exactly what “across the company” calls for with the least ongoing maintenance. Google Cloud’s documented mechanism for narrowing who gets logged under an already-enabled configuration is the exempted principals list: adding the identities the CustomerService folder’s projects actually use (their service accounts, and any human users) means Cloud Storage activity from those projects stops producing Data Access log entries, while every other project in the organization keeps being logged normally.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> This enables logging everywhere, including the CustomerService folder, with no exclusion at all. It satisfies the “across the company” half of the requirement but directly ignores the explicit instruction to leave CustomerService out.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> This assumes you can scope initial enablement around a folder as a substitute for exclusion, but Cloud Audit Logs doesn’t work that way — there’s no single org-level action that says “enable for everyone except X”; you would have to configure each non-CustomerService project and folder individually, and a project created later anywhere else in the company wouldn’t be covered until someone remembers to configure it. It also doesn’t actually use the one supported exclusion mechanism (exempted principals) at all.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Manually enabling Data Access logging on every project and folder individually, then adding CustomerService’s principals to the exemption list, can produce the same end state as Option A for the resources configured today, but it carries all of Option C’s scaling problem — any new project outside CustomerService needs its own manual enablement rather than inheriting it from one org-level setting — making it considerably more administrative effort for no functional benefit over Option A.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is reading “exclude the CustomerService folder” and reaching for the option that scopes enablement away from that folder, since that looks like the most literal translation of the requirement (Option C). But Data Access log inheritance only allows a lower-level resource to add to what a parent enabled, never to disable or narrow it, and per-resource enablement doesn’t automatically cover future projects. The only way Google actually lets you narrow an enabled configuration is by principal, not by resource branch — so “exclude a folder” has to become “exempt that folder’s principals.”</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Data Access log configuration lives inside the IAM policy of organization, folder, and project resources and follows IAM’s inheritance model: the effective configuration across the hierarchy is a union, a descendant can add more logging or more exemptions, but it can never disable a log type or remove an exemption a parent already set. Because inheritance only ever adds, scoped exclusions have to be expressed as exempted principals rather than as selective non-enablement at certain branches of the resource hierarchy.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Centralizing an org-wide audit logging policy at the top of the resource hierarchy, then layering in narrow, documented exemptions for known high-volume, low-risk workloads, is the standard pattern for keeping Data Access log volume — and the Cloud Logging storage costs that come with it — manageable without creating silent blind spots elsewhere; it also means a security or compliance reviewer can audit the entire exception list in one place instead of discovering gaps caused by a project nobody remembered to configure.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Enable Data Access logging high, in the organization; exempt low, by principal — you can never disable what a parent resource already turned on.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a scenario wants Data Access audit logging turned on broadly but one part of the org left out, look for the option that enables at the top of the hierarchy and uses exempted principals for the exclusion — any option that tries to withhold enablement from one branch of the hierarchy, or re-enables resource by resource, is fighting how Cloud Audit Logs inheritance actually works.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/audit/configure-data-access\"><strong>Google Cloud — Enable Data Access audit logs:</strong></a> Supports: confirms a descendant resource can’t disable a Data Access log type enabled by a parent organization, that hierarchy configurations merge as a union, and that exempted principals is the supported mechanism for excluding specific identities from logging</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/audit\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Audit Logs overview:</strong></a> Supports: general context on Data Access audit logs versus Admin Activity logs and how audit logging is scoped across organizations, folders, and projects</p>","lessons":["d1l4"]},"e4q6":{"id":"e4q6","exam":4,"n":6,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your company operates an ecommerce business. The service that handles payment processing emits structured JSON log entries using this schema:</p>\n<pre><code>      1. {\n  2.   \"logName\": ...,\n  3.   \"resource\": ...,\n  4.   \"httpRequest\": ...,\n  5.   \"jsonPayload\": {\n  6.     \"user_email\" : ...,\n  7.     \"method\" : ...,\n  8.     \"code\" : ...,\n  9.   },\n  10.   ...\n  11. }\n</code></pre>\n<p>Capturing these payment-processing logs and making them available for operations is required, but the <code>jsonPayload.user_email</code> field holds personally identifiable information (PII). Your security team does not want the broader engineering team to be able to see that PII, and wants access to it limited to security team members. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Apply a conditional IAM role binding using the expression <code>resource.name.extract(\"locations/global/buckets/{bucket}/\") == \"_Default\"</code> against the <code>_Default</code> bucket.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Mark <code>jsonPayload.user_email</code> as a restricted field on the <code>_Default</code> bucket, and grant the Log Field Accessor role to the security team members.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Apply an exclusion filter on <code>jsonPayload.user_email</code> to the <code>_Default</code> bucket.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Change the application so that it only includes <code>user_email</code> in its logs when the <code>LOG_USER_EMAIL</code> environment variable is set to true, and restrict which engineering team members can modify that production environment variable by using a CODEOWNERS file.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Mark</strong><code>**jsonPayload.user_email**</code><strong>as a restricted field on the</strong><code>**_Default**</code><strong>bucket, and grant the Log Field Accessor role to the security team members.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Must-have constraints: log capture and general operational access to the payment-processing logs must keep working, the <code>user_email</code> field specifically must stop being visible to engineering at large, and visibility into that field must be limited to the security team. Not a requirement: changing whether the data is logged at all, or building a custom access gate outside Google Cloud’s own controls. Capability: Cloud Logging’s field-level access control lets you name a specific field path, such as <code>jsonPayload.user_email</code>, as restricted on a log bucket; once restricted, that field is hidden from anyone who doesn’t also hold the Log Field Accessor role (<code>roles/logging.fieldAccessor</code>) for that path, while the rest of the log entry, and log access generally, is unaffected. Elimination: any option that drops or withholds the underlying log data (C) breaks mandatory capture; any option that controls whether data is written rather than who can view it (D) can’t distinguish between engineering and security once the field is logged; an option that only gestures at a conditional binding without actually marking the field restricted or granting the field-accessor role to security (A) does nothing to hide the field at all. Decision: Option B is the only one that uses the purpose-built mechanism end to end — restrict the field, then grant the matching viewer role to exactly the group that should see it.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“Capture and access of logs... is mandatory for operations” — a hard constraint ruling out anything that prevents the data from being ingested or generally accessible.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“<code>jsonPayload.user_email</code> field contains personally identifiable information” — a technical anchor naming the exact field path, pointing directly at a field-level (not entry-level) control.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“does not want the entire engineering team to have access to PII... restrict access to security team members only” — a precise least-privilege, per-group constraint that calls for an IAM role grant scoped to specific principals, not a blanket on/off switch.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“stop exposing PII to the engineering team” — confirms the fix has to act on who can <em>see</em> the field, not on whether the field gets stored, which is what rules out dropping the data entirely.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Restricting <code>jsonPayload.user_email</code> on the <code>_Default</code> bucket tells Cloud Logging to hide that specific field’s value from anyone querying the bucket’s logs, independent of everything else in the log entry. Granting the Log Field Accessor role to the security team’s principals then gives exactly that group permission to see the real value, provided they also have ordinary log-viewing access to the bucket (which, as the team responsible for investigating this data, they would). Engineering continues to see and query every other field — satisfying “mandatory for operations” — while <code>user_email</code> itself comes back redacted or denied for them, satisfying the PII restriction, all without touching what gets captured or stored.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Incorrect:</strong> The CEL expression shown is a real piece of Cloud Logging’s own IAM Condition syntax — it’s the documented way to scope a role grant to a specific bucket like <code>_Default</code>. But on its own, with no field marked as restricted and no mention of granting the Log Field Accessor role to the security team, applying this condition accomplishes nothing: there is nothing yet for the condition to scope. Without first restricting <code>jsonPayload.user_email</code>, the field remains fully visible to anyone with ordinary log access, engineering included.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> An exclusion filter prevents matching log data from being ingested and stored in the first place, rather than hiding it from unauthorized viewers. Applying one here would mean the payment-processing logs are no longer fully captured, which directly contradicts the requirement that capture remain mandatory for operations — and it would also deny the security team any access to the PII they’re supposed to retain access to.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Gating whether <code>user_email</code> gets logged behind an application environment variable, and gating who can change that variable through a CODEOWNERS file, controls whether the data exists in logs at all, not who can view it once it’s there. Anyone with ordinary log access — engineering included — would see <code>user_email</code> whenever the flag happened to be on, so this never actually limits viewing to the security team, and it relies on a source-control review convention rather than any Google Cloud access control.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap in Option A is that the CEL expression is genuinely pulled from Google’s own documented workflow for field-level access control — it’s real syntax used when scoping a Log Field Accessor grant to a particular bucket. Seeing accurate, official-looking syntax can read as “this must be the right answer,” but a condition on a role binding is only half a mechanism: without the field actually being marked restricted and without that role being granted to the right group, the condition has nothing to act on.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Field-level access control is a narrower tool than bucket- or log-view-level access control: it hides individual named fields within a <code>LogEntry</code> from principals who lack the matching <code>logging.fieldAccessor</code> grant, while leaving the rest of the entry, and the entry’s existence, untouched. The transferable principle is matching the access-control mechanism to the actual shape of the requirement — when the ask is “keep the data, hide one field, let one group see it,” reach for field-level restriction plus a scoped viewer role, not an ingestion-time filter or an application-level toggle.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Teams handling regulated or sensitive fields — SSNs, emails, health identifiers — inside otherwise-operational logs commonly use exactly this pattern: keep full logs flowing for incident response and debugging, restrict the one or two sensitive fields, and grant the field-accessor role only to the security or compliance group that has a legitimate need to see the raw value, with everyone else’s dashboards and queries working exactly as before, just with that one field redacted.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>To hide one PII field without losing the log: restrict the field, then grant Log Field Accessor to the one group that should still see it.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a scenario needs a specific named log field hidden from most viewers while logs stay fully captured and a smaller group retains access, look for the option that configures a restricted field on the bucket and grants the Log Field Accessor role to that group — options that drop the data, toggle it at the application level, or apply an access condition with no restricted field behind it are all solving a different problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/field-level-acl\"><strong>Google Cloud — Configure field-level access:</strong></a> Supports: confirms how to mark a field such as <code>jsonPayload.user_email</code> as restricted on a bucket, and that only principals with the Log Field Accessor role (plus ordinary log-view access) can see the unredacted value</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/access-control\"><strong>Google Cloud — Access control with IAM (Logging)</strong></a><strong>:</strong> Supports: confirms the Log Field Accessor (<code>roles/logging.fieldAccessor</code>) role definition and how it differs from general log-viewing role</p>","lessons":["d4l2","d4l3","d4l13"]},"e4q7":{"id":"e4q7","exam":4,"n":7,"domain":1,"html":"<p>Your organization gives engineering teams autonomy to self-manage their own Google Cloud projects, including project-level Identity and Access Management (IAM) assignments. You are worried that the team that owns the Shared VPC host project could accidentally delete the project, so you have already attached a deletion lien to it. You now need a design that limits who can remove that lien on the Shared VPC project to only principals holding the resourcemanager.projects.updateLiens permission at the organization level. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Require every team to manage their IAM permission assignments exclusively through Terraform-based infrastructure as code.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Create a VPC Service Controls perimeter that covers the container.googleapis.com API service.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Strip the resourcemanager.projects.updateLiens permission from every user who is associated with the project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Turn on the compute.restrictXpnProjectLienRemoval organization policy constraint.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Turn on the compute.restrictXpnProjectLienRemoval organization policy constraint.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard constraint is that project-level IAM self-management already exists, so a project-level permission grant can never be trusted as the enforcement boundary — teams can simply grant themselves whatever project-level permission the control relies on. The preference is to raise the required permission scope specifically for lien removal on a Shared VPC host project, without disrupting normal self-service IAM elsewhere. Google Cloud exposes exactly one capability built for this: an organization policy constraint that re-scopes the permission check for lien removal from project level to organization level. Process controls (A), unrelated security perimeters (B), and blanket permission revocation (C) are eliminated because none of them changes which scope Google Cloud actually checks when a lien-removal request is evaluated, leaving D as the only option that implements the stated requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“self-manage Google Cloud projects, including project-level IAM” — hard constraint signaling that project-level permission grants cannot be relied on as a security boundary</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“a lien has been placed on the project” — confirms the lien already exists; the question is strictly about who can remove it, not whether to create one</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“restrict ... to those with ... permission at the organization level” — technical signal pointing directly to a mechanism that elevates a permission’s required scope from project to organization</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Shared VPC project” — narrows the answer to the XPN-specific lien-removal constraint rather than any generic project-lien behavior</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>compute.restrictXpnProjectLienRemoval is a boolean organization policy constraint purpose-built for Shared VPC host projects. By default, any principal holding resourcemanager.projects.updateLiens at any scope — including project level — can remove a lien. Enforcing this constraint changes that default: lien removal on a Shared VPC host project then requires the permission to be held at the organization level specifically. Because the scenario’s teams can self-manage project-level IAM, a project-level grant of updateLiens would otherwise let them clear the lien and delete the host project; this constraint closes exactly that gap without requiring any change to how teams normally operate.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Managing IAM as code improves auditability and review discipline, but it is a process convention, not an access-control mechanism. Nothing prevents a team from authoring Terraform that grants itself project-level updateLiens, so it does not change which scope Google Cloud checks.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> VPC Service Controls perimeters govern data exfiltration and API access boundaries for services like Cloud Storage or BigQuery; container.googleapis.com governs GKE API access. Neither the service named nor the VPC Service Controls mechanism has any bearing on project liens or project deletion.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Revoking the permission from everyone associated with the project removes the ability to manage the lien entirely rather than restricting it to organization-level holders as required, and it would also block legitimate org-level administrators. It establishes no organization-level control, and project self-management could simply re-grant the permission at any time.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is reaching for a direct IAM action — revoking or granting resourcemanager.projects.updateLiens (Option C) —**** because the question names that exact permission. But the scenario’s real obstacle is that project-level IAM is self-managed and therefore untrustworthy as an enforcement boundary; the decisive phrase is “at the organization level,” which only an organization policy constraint can enforce. If the scenario instead said project-level removal was acceptable, blanket revocation could become defensible — but as written, it is not.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Organization policies form a hierarchy-wide control plane that can tighten or override default IAM-permission checks for specific, high-impact operations, independent of how IAM roles are assigned on any individual resource. A project lien is a metadata marker blocking deletion until explicitly removed, and removal normally follows wherever resourcemanager.projects.updateLiens is granted. The Shared VPC-specific constraint is a targeted exception: it raises the required scope of that one permission just for Shared VPC host projects, because deleting a host project disrupts every service project attached to it.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team centralizes network administration in a Shared VPC host project while application teams keep project-level autonomy for their own service projects. Enforcing compute.restrictXpnProjectLienRemoval preserves that autonomy for day-to-day IAM work while guaranteeing that no application team — however broad its project-level grants become — can single-handedly trigger an organization-wide network outage by deleting the host project, reducing operational risk without adding review friction elsewhere.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Shared VPC host project + lien removal → compute.restrictXpnProjectLienRemoval forces the updateLiens check up to the organization level.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks you to raise an IAM permission’s required scope from project to organization for one specific, high-impact action, look for a purpose-built organization policy constraint before reaching for generic IAM grants/revocations or unrelated controls like VPC Service Controls.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/provisioning-shared-vpc\">Google Cloud — Provision Shared VPC (Prevent accidental deletion of host projects): </a>Supports: the compute.restrictXpnProjectLienRemoval mechanism, its enforcement command, and why it eliminates Option C’s blanket-revocation approach</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/organization-policy/reference/org-policy-constraints\"><strong>Google Cloud — Organization policy constraints reference:</strong></a> Supports: confirming compute.restrictXpnProjectLienRemoval as a current, valid Google-managed constraint and its classification as an organization-level control</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l2"]},"e4q8":{"id":"e4q8","exam":4,"n":8,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your company runs a GKE application composed of 12 microservices across development (Dev), staging (Staging), and production (Prod) environments. Multiple teams develop features concurrently. Deployments from different feature branches into the shared Dev cluster cause dependency test failures and delay releases.</p>\n<p>You need a cost-effective approach that gives developers stable environments for testing their microservice changes independently of other development work. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use Cloud Build to build container images and update Kubernetes manifests from main-branch merge requests. Use Config Sync to deploy the images for testing in dynamically created namespaces within the autoscaling Dev cluster. Delete the namespaces after testing.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Use Cloud Build to build container images and update Kubernetes manifests for each commit. Use Cloud Deploy to progressively deliver changes to the Dev, Staging, and Prod clusters. Use Config Sync to maintain consistent Kubernetes configurations across environments.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use Cloud Build to build container images and update Kubernetes manifests from feature-branch commits. Use Config Sync to deploy the images for testing in dynamically created namespaces within the autoscaling Dev cluster. Delete the namespaces after testing.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Use Cloud Build to build container images and update Kubernetes manifests from feature-branch commits. Use Config Sync to deploy the images into a separate, dynamically provisioned Dev cluster for each feature branch. Delete the clusters during the merge-request workflow.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Use Cloud Build to build container images and update Kubernetes manifests from feature-branch commits. Use Config Sync to deploy the images for testing in dynamically created namespaces within the autoscaling Dev cluster. Delete the namespaces after testing.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>C most explicitly combines <strong>feature-branch testing</strong> , <strong>separate test environments</strong> , and <strong>shared infrastructure</strong>. Dedicated namespaces reduce deployment collisions while avoiding a separate cluster for every branch. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Root cause:</strong> Concurrent feature deployments interfere with one another.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Testing requirement:</strong> Developers need stable, independent feature environments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cost preference:</strong> Reuse the existing Dev cluster.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Lifecycle requirement:</strong> Remove temporary resources after testing.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Configure Cloud Build triggers for feature-branch pushes. Give each feature branch a distinct namespace, and update its manifests with the appropriate images. Config Sync supports namespace-scoped configurations sourced from specified Git branches or revisions. </p>\n<p>Within each environment, use known versions of required dependencies and namespace-specific service endpoints. Apply resource quotas, RBAC, and network policies to reduce resource contention and unintended interactions. Clean up the environment through its GitOps configuration after testing. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A — Technically Possible but Ambiguous:</strong> Its namespace approach is appropriate, but “main-branch merge requests” does not clearly specify the intended feature-branch workflow. Importantly, pre-merge pull-request triggers can also test feature-branch commits, so this wording can overlap with C. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>B — Different Use Case:</strong> This describes promotion through shared environments. It does not establish separate environments for concurrent feature branches, leaving the stated Dev deployment conflicts unresolved.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Separate clusters provide stronger separation, but introduce more provisioning and management overhead. Shared-cluster multi-tenancy reduces resource fragmentation and avoids waiting for new clusters. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>A namespace alone does not guarantee stable dependencies or complete isolation. Dependency versions, service routing, shared test data, resource quotas, and policies must also be scoped appropriately.</p>\n<p>For an unambiguous question, A should explicitly distinguish mainline integration from pre-merge feature testing.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Build builds images and executes tests. Config Sync reconciles declared Kubernetes configuration. Namespaces provide separate scopes for feature workloads within shared infrastructure.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A checkout feature can run in <code>feature-checkout-123</code> with known versions of its inventory and payment dependencies. Another team’s deployment updates its own namespace without replacing those dependencies.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For economical feature testing, give each branch its own namespace before giving it its own cluster.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Choose <strong>C</strong> as the clearest intended answer: feature-branch triggers, temporary namespaces, stable dependencies, and cleanup. A’s merge-request wording should be clarified because pre-merge testing can implement the same pattern.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/isolate-workloads-dedicated-namespaces\"><strong>Isolate your workloads using namespaces</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Reducing naming collisions and applying namespace-scoped quotas, access controls, and network policies.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/config-sync/docs/how-to/multiple-repositories\"><strong>Configure syncing from more than one source of truth</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Namespace-scoped configuration and Git branch or revision selection.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/multitenancy-overview\"><strong>Cluster multi-tenancy</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Shared-cluster isolation, reduced management overhead, and reduced resource fragmentation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/automating-builds/create-manage-triggers\"><strong>Create and manage build triggers</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Branch-push and pull-request triggers, including the ambiguity in option A.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e4q9":{"id":"e4q9","exam":4,"n":9,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your organization runs its workloads on Google Cloud, with each team operating its applications inside its own dedicated project. New teams and new projects are provisioned on an ongoing basis. Your security team mandates that every log entry be forwarded to a security information and event management (SIEM) platform, which ingests data exclusively through Pub/Sub. You need an approach that guarantees logs from both current projects and any projects created later are automatically captured and sent to the SIEM. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Configure an aggregated sink at the organization level that routes to a Cloud Logging bucket named siem, and set its inclusion filter to match every log entry.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Configure an aggregated sink at the folder level that routes to a Pub/Sub topic named siem, set its inclusion filter to match every log entry, and repeat this configuration for each folder.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Configure an aggregated sink at the organization level that routes to a Pub/Sub topic named siem, and set its inclusion filter to match every log entry.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Configure a logging sink at the project level that routes to a Pub/Sub topic named siem, set its inclusion filter to match every log entry, and repeat this configuration for each project.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Configure an aggregated sink at the organization level that routes to a Pub/Sub topic named siem, and set its inclusion filter to match every log entry.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two hard constraints must both be satisfied: the destination has to be something the SIEM can actually ingest (Pub/Sub, not a bucket or dataset), and coverage has to extend to projects that don’t exist yet without ongoing manual work. Cloud Logging’s aggregated sink, scoped to the organization and set to include child resources, satisfies the coverage constraint because the organization is the ancestor of every project the company will ever create; pairing it with a Pub/Sub topic destination and an all-logs inclusion filter satisfies the ingestion constraint. Each distractor fails exactly one of the two constraints: A gets the scope right but the destination wrong, while B and D get the destination right but scope too low, forcing repeated manual setup as the org grows.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“New teams and projects are created regularly” — a hard constraint that eliminates any solution requiring per-resource repetition for resources that don’t exist yet</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“The SIEM ingests logs by using Pub/Sub” — a hard constraint on destination type, ruling out log buckets, BigQuery datasets, or Cloud Storage buckets</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“all existing and future logs” — the decisive phrase pointing to organization-level scope, since only the organization is guaranteed to be an ancestor of every future project</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Repeat for each folder” / “Repeat for each project” (in the distractors) — an explicit tell that those options require ongoing manual maintenance, directly conflicting with the “future” requirement</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>An aggregated sink defined at the organization level, with child resources included, evaluates its filter against log entries generated anywhere inside the organization’s resource hierarchy — including projects and folders created after the sink was configured. An inclusion filter that matches every log entry means nothing is dropped, and a Pub/Sub topic destination gives the SIEM the exact mechanism it needs to ingest the data. Because the sink lives at the top of the hierarchy, no further action is required as the company provisions new teams and projects.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> The scope is correct — an organization-level aggregated sink does cover current and future projects — but the destination is a log bucket. A SIEM that ingests exclusively through Pub/Sub has no way to consume a log bucket directly, so the integration requirement fails despite the coverage being right.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> The Pub/Sub destination is correct, and a folder-level aggregated sink does automatically cover every present and future project nested under that folder. But “repeat for each folder” means any folder created after this configuration is not covered until someone manually adds a sink for it, so future coverage is incomplete without ongoing maintenance that an organization-level sink avoids entirely.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> A project-level sink has no concept of child resources — it covers only the single project it’s created in. Repeating it for every existing project still leaves every future project unmonitored until a new sink is manually created for it, which directly contradicts the requirement to capture future logs automatically.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is validating only one of the two independent requirements — scope or destination — and assuming the option is correct. Option A passes the scope check but fails the destination check; Options B and D pass the destination check but fail the scope check. Only the organization-level-plus-Pub/Sub combination passes both. If the SIEM instead ingested from Cloud Storage or BigQuery, Option A’s destination would become the right choice, and the correct answer would change accordingly.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An aggregated sink is a Cloud Logging resource created at the folder or organization level that, when configured to include child resources, automatically evaluates its filter against log entries from every project nested anywhere beneath it, present and future, with no per-project configuration required — unlike a standard sink, which only ever processes logs generated inside the single resource it belongs to. A sink’s destination type (log bucket, BigQuery dataset, Cloud Storage bucket, or Pub/Sub topic) is a separate, independent choice from its scope, and both have to be correct for a given downstream consumer.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team centralizes security log collection with a single organization-level aggregated sink feeding a Pub/Sub topic that their SIEM consumes, instead of asking every application team to build and maintain its own export pipeline. As new initiatives spin up new projects, the security team never has to chase down and onboard each one individually — the aggregated sink already has it covered — which keeps audit and compliance coverage complete and removes a recurring operational burden from both security and application teams.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Existing and future, everywhere in the org” → organization-level aggregated sink; “ingests via Pub/Sub” → destination must be a Pub/Sub topic, not a bucket or dataset.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a scenario stresses that new projects or folders appear regularly and all of them must be covered without extra setup, that’s a signal for an organization-level aggregated sink rather than a per-project or per-folder sink — then separately verify the destination type actually matches what the downstream consumer requires.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/export/aggregated_sinks\"><strong>Google Cloud — Aggregated sinks (Cloud Logging):</strong></a> Supports: the aggregated-sink mechanism, include-children behavior that automatically captures future child projects/folders, and why organization scope beats folder or project scope for this requirement</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/install/logs_export\"><strong>Google Cloud — Route logs to supported destinations:</strong></a> Supports: the list of supported sink destination types (log bucket, Cloud Storage bucket, BigQuery dataset, Pub/Sub topic), underpinning why Option A’s log-bucket destination cannot serve a Pub/Sub-only SIEM</p>","lessons":["d4l1"]},"e4q10":{"id":"e4q10","exam":4,"n":10,"domain":1,"html":"<p>Your organization hosts its applications on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). Application developers routinely provision cloud resources to support those applications. You want to give developers a way to manage infrastructure as code that follows Google-recommended practices. The approach must let developers define infrastructure through Kubernetes Custom Resource Definitions (CRDs), and it must be a configuration that Google Cloud Support can officially assist with. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Set up Cloud Build with a Terraform builder step that runs terraform plan and terraform apply.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Deploy and configure Crossplane inside the GKE cluster.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Set up a GitHub Action that runs a Terraform builder step invoking terraform plan and terraform apply during the pull request workflow.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Deploy and configure Config Connector inside the GKE cluster.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Deploy and configure Config Connector inside the GKE cluster.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two independent constraints must both be satisfied: infrastructure has to be expressed through Kubernetes CRDs specifically, and the resulting setup has to be something Google Cloud Support can officially assist with. Both Terraform-based options define infrastructure in Terraform configuration executed by the Terraform CLI — not as Kubernetes CRDs — so they fail the first constraint regardless of which CI/CD system runs them. That leaves two CRD-based Kubernetes operators, Config Connector and Crossplane, which both satisfy the first constraint equally well. The second constraint breaks the tie: Config Connector is a Google-built product with official, documented Google support channels, while Crossplane is an independent, community-governed open-source project with no relationship to Google Cloud Support at all. Only Config Connector satisfies both constraints at once.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“manage infrastructure as code through Kubernetes Custom Resource Definitions (CRDs)” — hard constraint eliminating both Terraform-based options, since Terraform’s HCL configuration is not a Kubernetes CRD</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“ensure that your chosen setup can be supported by the Google Cloud Support Portal” — hard constraint that separates the two remaining CRD-based tools from each other</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“adhering to Google-recommended practices” — reinforces favoring the Google-built tool once two options are otherwise functionally equivalent</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Application developers frequently create cloud resources” — points toward a Kubernetes-native, GitOps-friendly workflow developers can use with the same tooling they already use for applications</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Config Connector is a Kubernetes add-on built and published by Google that provides CRDs and controllers mapping directly onto Google Cloud resources — for example, creating a StorageBucket or SQLInstance custom resource causes Config Connector to provision the corresponding Google Cloud resource. Running it inside GKE lets developers manage infrastructure with the same kubectl-based, declarative workflow they already use for application manifests. Because it is a Google-authored product with officially documented support and issue-tracking channels, it is the configuration that satisfies both the CRD requirement and the Google Cloud Support requirement together.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Running Terraform through Cloud Build is a legitimate, well-supported way to manage infrastructure as code, but it defines infrastructure in Terraform configuration files applied via the Terraform CLI, not Kubernetes CRDs. It fails the scenario’s explicit CRD requirement regardless of how well Cloud Build itself is supported.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> Crossplane extends Kubernetes with CRDs for managing cloud resources, so it satisfies the CRD requirement on its own merits. However, it is an independent, community-governed open-source project with no official relationship to Google Cloud, so a Crossplane deployment has no path into Google’s official support channels — failing the support requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Running Terraform through a GitHub Action during the pull request process has the same core problem as Option A — infrastructure is defined in Terraform, not CRDs — and additionally depends on tooling (GitHub Actions) entirely outside Google Cloud, moving further from a Google-supported configuration rather than closer to one.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is stopping as soon as an option satisfies the CRD requirement, which makes Crossplane look attractive since it is a legitimate, widely used CRD-based infrastructure tool. The scenario layers a second, independent constraint — an official Google support path — that only a Google-built product can satisfy, and the two Terraform options test the opposite failure mode of recognizing “infrastructure as code” without checking that the mechanism is CRD-based at all. Each option has to be checked against both constraints independently. If the support requirement were dropped, Crossplane would become an equally defensible answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Kubernetes Custom Resource Definitions let non-native objects behave like first-class Kubernetes resources, with a controller reconciling them the same way the control plane reconciles built-in objects like Deployments. Several tools apply this pattern to cloud infrastructure so developers can provision cloud resources declaratively with the same kubectl and GitOps tooling used for applications — but exposing resources as CRDs is a technical mechanism, not a support relationship. Whether a given CRD-based tool has an official vendor support path is a separate question that depends entirely on who builds and maintains it.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team standardizes on Config Connector so developers can request a Cloud SQL instance or a storage bucket using the same YAML manifests and GitOps pipeline they already use to deploy their services, rather than maintaining a separate Terraform workflow and state backend. Because Config Connector is a Google-authored product with documented support and issue-tracking channels, the platform team has a defined, Google-sanctioned path to escalate reconciliation failures or unexpected behavior — something they would not have with a third-party controller like Crossplane that Google has no official relationship with.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>CRDs plus an official Google support path together → Config Connector; either Terraform option fails on “CRDs,” Crossplane fails on “Google Cloud Support.”</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a scenario stacks a specific technical mechanism (like CRDs) with a support-channel requirement, check each option against both independently — a technically capable tool can still be wrong if it isn’t a Google-supported product.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/config-connector/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Config Connector overview:</strong></a> Supports: the CRD-based mechanism Config Connector uses to map Kubernetes objects to Google Cloud resources, and its status as a Google-built product</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/config-controller/docs/get-support\"><strong>Google Cloud — Get support (Config Controller / Config Connector):</strong></a> Supports: the documented, official Google support and issue-tracking channels for Config Connector, contrasted with Crossplane having no such relationship with Google Cloud</p>","lessons":["d1l7"]},"e4q11":{"id":"e4q11","exam":4,"n":11,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You are defining service level objectives for a high-traffic web application whose customers are currently satisfied with its performance and availability. Over a trailing 28-day measurement window, the application’s 90th-percentile latency is 160 ms and its 95th-percentile latency is 300 ms. Which latency SLO should you publish?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. A 90th-percentile latency SLO of 150 ms, paired with a 95th-percentile latency SLO of 290 ms.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. A 90th-percentile latency SLO of 160 ms, paired with a 95th-percentile latency SLO of 300 ms.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. A 90th-percentile latency SLO of 190 ms, paired with a 95th-percentile latency SLO of 330 ms.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. A 90th-percentile latency SLO of 300 ms, paired with a 95th-percentile latency SLO of 450 ms.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. A 90th-percentile latency SLO of 190 ms, paired with a 95th-percentile latency SLO of 330 ms.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The SLO must be achievable — not tighter than what the service already delivers — so the team isn’t immediately out of compliance. It should also stay close enough to what users already experience happily that it remains a meaningful signal rather than a number so loose it lets real degradation go unnoticed. Option A sets targets below current measured performance, which the service hasn’t demonstrated it can meet. Option B matches current performance exactly, leaving zero error budget — any ordinary variance breaches it immediately. Option D loosens the targets so far that the published 90th-percentile figure nearly matches today’s 95th-percentile figure, no longer reflecting real user experience. Option C loosens both figures by a modest, deliberate margin, preserving a workable error budget while staying anchored to today’s actual performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“Customers are currently happy with the application performance” — signals current measured performance is the right anchor point; the published SLO shouldn’t diverge sharply from it in either direction</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“90th percentile of latency is 160 ms and the 95th percentile of latency is 300 ms” — the baseline both figures in a correct option must sit at or modestly above, eliminating anything below these values</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“over a 28-day window” — a sustained measurement, not a single data point, making it a credible basis for the target rather than something needing heavy discounting</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Two coupled thresholds — a correct answer must get both the 90th- and 95th-percentile figures right simultaneously, not just one</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Publishing an SLO at or below current measured performance leaves no room for ordinary variance in traffic, infrastructure, or dependencies — any normal fluctuation would immediately put the service out of SLO and exhaust the error budget before a genuine problem even occurs. Setting the target a modest amount looser than current performance, as this option does, creates a workable error budget while staying close enough to today’s experience that a real regression will still breach the SLO and trigger a response. This balances defensibility with meaningfulness in a way none of the other options do.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Both figures (150 ms and 290 ms) are tighter than what the service currently delivers (160 ms and 300 ms). Publishing an SLO the service doesn’t already meet puts it out of compliance immediately, with no realistic path to meeting it without first improving the system — contradicting the premise that customers are already happy with current performance.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Matching the SLO exactly to current measured performance looks appealing because the service meets it by construction, but it leaves zero error budget. Any small fluctuation in traffic or dependency behavior pushes the service out of SLO, making the target brittle and difficult to defend operationally.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Loosening the targets this far (300 ms and 450 ms) means the published SLO no longer reflects the experience customers are actually happy with — the 90th-percentile target alone is nearly double today’s measured value. A target this loose would let a genuine, user-impacting regression occur without ever breaching the SLO, defeating its purpose as an early warning signal.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is reading “base the SLO on current measurement” as “set the SLO equal to current measurement” (Option B), rather than using the measurement as a starting anchor with deliberate headroom built in. The opposite trap is assuming more headroom is always safer, which makes Option D look reasonable — but excessive headroom turns the SLO into a number that no longer represents genuine user happiness, since real degradations could occur well within the published target. The correct answer sits between these two failure modes; further tightening or loosening comes later, from comparing the SLO against actual user-satisfaction signals, not from guessing a wide margin upfront.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A sound starting SLO is typically derived from a recent, sustained measurement window and then loosened by a modest, deliberate margin rather than published as an exact match to that measurement. This creates an error budget — the gap between “always meets target” and the published objective — that absorbs ordinary variance and gives engineering teams room to make changes without immediately breaching the SLO, while staying close enough to actual performance to remain a meaningful signal of user happiness. The target is refined further over time by comparing it against real indicators of user satisfaction, such as support tickets or sentiment data.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team publishing its first formal SLO for a service users already like takes the current 28-day latency percentiles as a baseline, adds a modest buffer, and publishes that as the objective rather than the raw measured numbers. This gives the on-call team a genuine error budget to absorb routine fluctuations from deploys, traffic spikes, or dependency hiccups without constant SLO-violation alerts, while keeping the target tight enough that a real regression — say, a bad release pushing 90th-percentile latency to 250 ms — still breaches the SLO and triggers the team’s error budget policy.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Anchor the SLO to current measured performance, then loosen it by a modest margin — never match it exactly (zero error budget) and never loosen it so far that it stops reflecting real user experience.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question gives current measured performance and asks what SLO to publish, rule out anything tighter than the measurement, rule out an exact match (zero error budget), and rule out an excessively loose target that would stop representing real user experience — the right answer sits a modest, deliberate margin above what’s already being delivered.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — Chapter 2: Implementing SLOs:</strong></a> Supports: the methodology of deriving a starting SLO from a recent measurement window and loosening it by a modest margin rather than publishing the exact measured value, illustrated by the book’s own worked example (90th-percentile latency measured at 432 ms, published SLO of 450 ms)</p>","lessons":["d3l2"]},"e4q12":{"id":"e4q12","exam":4,"n":12,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your organization is beginning to containerize its workloads on Google Cloud. You need a fully managed storage solution capable of holding both container images and Helm charts, and it must integrate natively with the Google Cloud services you already rely on — Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), Cloud Run, VPC Service Controls, and Identity and Access Management (IAM). What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Configure Docker’s registry storage driver to use a Cloud Storage bucket owned by your organization as the backing store.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Deploy an open source container registry server inside GKE, locked down with a restrictive role-based access control (RBAC) configuration.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Configure Artifact Registry as an OCI-based registry that stores both container images and Helm charts.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Configure Container Registry as an OCI-based registry for container images.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Configure Artifact Registry as an OCI-based registry that stores both container images and Helm charts.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Three constraints must all be satisfied: the solution has to be fully managed, it has to store both container images and Helm charts, and it has to integrate natively with GKE, Cloud Run, VPC Service Controls, and IAM specifically. Artifact Registry is Google’s fully managed artifact storage service; because it supports the OCI format, a single Docker-format repository can hold both container images and Helm charts, and as a first-party Google Cloud service it is already wired into GKE, Cloud Run, VPC Service Controls, and IAM. The Cloud Storage-backed Docker driver (A) and the self-run open source registry (B) both fail the “fully managed, natively integrated” requirement since they’re assembled or operated by the team itself. Container Registry (D) never supported Helm charts and has been deprecated in favor of Artifact Registry, so it fails the dual-artifact requirement outright.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“fully managed storage solution” — hard constraint eliminating any option that requires operating or assembling the registry infrastructure yourself</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“container images and Helm charts” — hard constraint requiring one solution to handle both artifact types, which only an OCI-capable registry provides</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“native integration into ... GKE, Cloud Run, VPC Service Controls, and ... IAM” — hard constraint pointing to a first-party Google Cloud service rather than a self-hosted or storage-bucket-backed workaround</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“starting to containerize” — signals the forward-looking, currently recommended choice rather than a legacy service</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Artifact Registry is Google Cloud’s fully managed artifact storage service. Its Docker-format repositories are OCI-compliant, and because Helm 3.8+ can push and pull charts as OCI artifacts, the same repository type that holds container images can also hold Helm charts — no separate chart-hosting system is needed. As a first-party service, Artifact Registry integrates natively with GKE and Cloud Run for pulling images and charts, with IAM for repository-level access control, and with VPC Service Controls for perimeter protection, covering every integration point the scenario names.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Pointing a Docker storage driver at a Cloud Storage bucket builds a registry backing store out of general-purpose object storage rather than using a purpose-built, managed registry service. It provides none of Artifact Registry’s native IAM repository permissions, VPC Service Controls perimeter support, or Helm chart handling, and leaves the team responsible for assembling and maintaining the registry layer itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> Running an open source container registry server inside GKE, however tightly locked down with RBAC, is a self-operated solution — the organization owns its availability, upgrades, and security, which fails the “fully managed” requirement outright. It also has no built-in integration with VPC Service Controls or Cloud Run, since it isn’t a first-party Google Cloud service.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Container Registry only ever stored container images; it never supported Helm charts, so it fails the dual-artifact requirement regardless of its other characteristics. It is also Google’s legacy registry service, deprecated and fully superseded by Artifact Registry, making it the wrong choice for an organization just starting to containerize.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is anchoring on the familiar name “Container Registry” because the scenario mentions container images, while overlooking that the requirement explicitly includes Helm charts — an artifact type Container Registry was never built to store. A second trap is treating any registry-shaped setup (a Cloud Storage-backed Docker driver, or a self-hosted open source registry) as equivalent to a managed service once it technically stores images; “fully managed” and “native integration” are there specifically to rule out anything you’d have to operate, patch, or wire up to IAM and VPC Service Controls yourself.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>The Open Container Initiative (OCI) format standardizes how registries store artifacts, which is why a single OCI-compliant Docker-format repository can hold both container images and Helm charts (pushed and pulled using Helm 3.8+’s native OCI support) rather than needing a separate chart repository. A fully managed, first-party registry service built on this standard — rather than a bucket repurposed as a registry, or a self-run open source registry — is what gives you integration with a cloud provider’s IAM, network security, and compute services without extra engineering effort.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team standardizing on Artifact Registry stores both its application container images and its internal Helm charts in the same repository type, grants developers push access through IAM roles scoped to individual repositories, and places the registry inside a VPC Service Controls perimeter to prevent exfiltration — all without operating any registry infrastructure themselves. When GKE or Cloud Run deploys a workload, it pulls images and charts directly from Artifact Registry using the same IAM identity and network controls already governing the rest of the environment.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Images plus Helm charts, fully managed, native GKE/Cloud Run/IAM/VPC-SC integration → Artifact Registry; Container Registry never did Helm charts and has been retired.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a scenario asks for a managed Google Cloud artifact store that must hold both container images and Helm charts, look for OCI-based Artifact Registry — Container Registry is images-only and deprecated, and anything self-hosted or storage-bucket-backed fails the “fully managed, natively integrated” requirement regardless of what it can technically store.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Artifact Registry overview:</strong></a> Supports: Artifact Registry’s fully managed nature and its native integration with IAM and VPC Service Controls</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/artifact-registry/docs/transition-from-gcr\"><strong>Google Cloud — Transition from Container Registry:</strong></a> Supports: Container Registry’s deprecation and its lack of support for non-container artifacts, versus Artifact Registry’s expanded, fully managed support for both container images and formats like Helm charts</p>","lessons":["d2l5"]},"e4q13":{"id":"e4q13","exam":4,"n":13,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your organization runs several Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) clusters within a single project. You need a highly available way to collect and query both your own domain-specific workload metrics and GKE’s default metrics across every cluster, while keeping operational overhead to a minimum. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Install Prometheus in every cluster using the Prometheus Operator, scrape the metrics there, configure remote-write to a single central Prometheus instance, and query that central instance.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Enable managed collection on every GKE cluster, and query the resulting metrics in BigQuery.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Install Prometheus in every cluster using the Prometheus Operator, scrape the metrics there, enable a Thanos sidecar on each Prometheus instance, deploy Thanos in a central cluster, and query that central Thanos instance.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Enable managed collection on every GKE cluster, and query the resulting metrics in Cloud Monitoring.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>D. Enable managed collection on every GKE cluster, and query the resulting metrics in Cloud Monitoring.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Enable managed collection on every GKE cluster, and query the resulting metrics in Cloud Monitoring.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Must-have constraints: native high availability with no DIY engineering, low ongoing operational burden, ingestion of two distinct metric sources — custom Prometheus-format workload metrics and GKE’s built-in default metrics — and one query surface spanning every cluster. Not a requirement: a particular query language or tool, only that both metric types are queryable together. Capability: Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus’s managed collection mode runs the scraping and collection machinery for you in each cluster, with nothing to deploy, scale, or shard yourself, and ships everything into the same backend Cloud Monitoring uses — a globally distributed, highly available time-series store. Cloud Monitoring then exposes GKE’s default metrics and any custom workload metrics side by side via PromQL, across every cluster, from one place. Elimination: Options A and C both require you to deploy and operate Prometheus (and, for C, Thanos) in every cluster plus a central aggregation layer you must keep highly available yourself — the opposite of minimizing operational overhead. Option B gets the collection step right but sends the query step to BigQuery, which isn’t where these metrics natively live or get queried. Decision: only D pairs zero-maintenance collection with the metrics’ already-highly-available, native query surface.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“highly available” — a hard constraint that must be satisfied by the platform itself, not something you engineer on top of it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“minimizing operational overhead” — a hard constraint eliminating any option where you deploy, scale, or federate Prometheus or Thanos yourself.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“both domain-specific workload metrics and GKE default metrics” — a technical signal requiring one solution that natively ingests both custom application metrics and GKE’s built-in system metrics.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“across all clusters” — requires a single, unified, multi-cluster query surface rather than per-cluster silos or manual federation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“query” — the solution needs a convenient native query path, not just storage, which is what eliminates routing to BigQuery.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Managed collection is Google’s own recommended approach for Kubernetes environments precisely because it removes the work of deploying, scaling, sharding, configuring, and maintaining Prometheus servers — you enable it per cluster and the collection runs itself. Because Managed Service for Prometheus shares its backend and APIs with Cloud Monitoring, both GKE’s default metrics and any custom, domain-specific workload metrics scraped under managed collection land in the same store and become queryable together with PromQL inside Cloud Monitoring, across every cluster in the project, with no central query tier for you to assemble. The backing store is Google’s own highly available, globally distributed time-series database, so the “highly available” requirement is met by the platform rather than by anything you design or operate.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Self-deployed Prometheus via the Prometheus Operator is a supported collection mode, but it puts you back in charge of deploying, scaling, and maintaining a Prometheus server in every cluster. Remote-writing everything into one central Prometheus instance also adds a component you must separately make highly available — a single Prometheus instance isn’t inherently HA — working against both “highly available” and “minimizing operational overhead.”</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Pairing self-deployed Prometheus with a Thanos sidecar and a central Thanos deployment is a well-known way to get multi-cluster federation and HA, but it means deploying, configuring, and operating an entire additional system — Thanos components, object storage, compaction — on top of Prometheus itself, which is substantially more operational overhead than enabling a managed feature.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Managed collection is the right ingestion choice, but Managed Service for Prometheus data isn’t natively stored in or queried from BigQuery — it’s queried through Cloud Monitoring (or any PromQL-compatible tool pointed at that backend). Routing to BigQuery would mean building and maintaining an export pipeline just to answer a question the native query surface already handles, adding overhead rather than minimizing it.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating the word “Prometheus” as a cue to reach for a traditional self-hosted architecture, especially when an option describes a legitimate, well-known HA pattern like Thanos federation. The decisive phrase is “minimizing operational overhead”: any option where you are the one deploying, scaling, or federating Prometheus or Thanos servers fails that constraint no matter how sound the architecture is otherwise.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus stays Prometheus-compatible at the collection and query layer — PromQL, scrape configs, standard exporters — while swapping the operational burden of running Prometheus servers for a managed collector and Google’s own highly available metrics backend. The transferable skill is recognizing “collect once, query everywhere, someone else runs the infrastructure” as the signature of a managed service, versus “I deploy and federate the infrastructure myself” as the signature of a self-hosted one — a distinction that applies to observability tooling generally, not just Prometheus.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>Organizations running many GKE clusters commonly standardize on managed collection specifically to avoid the recurring work of patching, resizing, and troubleshooting their own Prometheus/Thanos stack, freeing engineering time for the metrics and alerts that actually matter, while keeping full PromQL compatibility so existing dashboards, alerting rules, and Grafana integrations continue to work unchanged.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Managed collection in, Cloud Monitoring out” — if you’re the one running or federating Prometheus or Thanos yourself, you’ve already added the overhead the question asked you to minimize.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a multi-cluster GKE scenario asks for highly available metrics collection with minimal operational overhead, favor the option that enables Managed Service for Prometheus’s managed collection and queries through Cloud Monitoring — any option where you personally deploy, scale, or federate Prometheus or Thanos is solving the HA problem the hard way.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/managed-prometheus\"><strong>Google Cloud — Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus:</strong></a> Supports: confirms managed collection eliminates the operational burden of deploying, scaling, and maintaining Prometheus, and that the backend is a highly available, globally distributed time-series database</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/managed-prometheus\">Google Cloud — Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus overview (data collection methods): </a>Supports: confirms managed collection is Google’s recommended approach for Kubernetes environments and that metrics are queried via PromQL in Cloud Monitoring, not BigQuery</p>","lessons":["d4l7"]},"e4q14":{"id":"e4q14","exam":4,"n":14,"domain":5,"html":"<p>You monitor a service built on n2-standard-2 Compute Engine instances that serve large files to end users, and customers have been reporting slow downloads. Your Cloud Monitoring dashboard confirms the VMs are consistently hitting their maximum network throughput. You need to raise the network throughput ceiling for these instances. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Attach additional virtual network interface controllers (vNICs) to each VM.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Deploy a Cloud NAT gateway and attach it to the VMs’ subnet.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Resize the VMs to the n2-standard-8 machine type.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Install the Ops Agent on the VMs to export additional monitoring metrics.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Resize the VMs to the n2-standard-8 machine type.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The fix has to raise the actual network throughput ceiling, since the dashboard confirms the VMs are already saturating their current cap. Google Cloud enforces network bandwidth limits per compute instance based on machine type and vCPU count, not per network interface, so adding vNICs (A) does nothing to raise the cap. Cloud NAT (B) solves an unrelated problem — outbound internet access for instances without external IPs — and has no effect on bandwidth ceilings. The Ops Agent (D) only adds observability, not capacity. Increasing the vCPU count by moving to a larger machine type (C) directly raises the per-instance maximum egress bandwidth, since that limit scales with vCPU count up to the machine series’ cap, making it the only option that actually increases available throughput.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“running at peak network throughput” — the decisive signal that the bottleneck is the instance’s bandwidth cap, not an unrelated connectivity or configuration issue</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“n2-standard-2” — a small vCPU count, implying real headroom to grow within the N2 series before hitting its bandwidth ceiling</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“serve large files” / “downloads are slow” — frames this as a sustained egress-bandwidth problem rather than a one-off latency or routing issue</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“improve the network throughput performance” — specifically asks for a capacity fix, ruling out anything that only adds visibility (Ops Agent) or addresses outbound internet access for private instances (Cloud NAT)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud’s per-instance maximum egress bandwidth is generally around 2 Gbps per vCPU, up to a cap defined by the machine series. Moving from n2-standard-2 (2 vCPUs) to n2-standard-8 (8 vCPUs) quadruples the vCPU count, directly raising the per-instance bandwidth ceiling within the N2 family’s limits. Since the VMs are already measured at their current peak, this change actually gives them room to serve files faster rather than leaving them capped where they already sit.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Incorrect:</strong> Google Cloud accounts for network bandwidth per compute instance, not per virtual network interface. Adding more vNICs does not raise a VM’s aggregate egress or ingress bandwidth limit — the instance remains capped at the same total throughput regardless of how many interfaces that traffic is split across.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Cloud NAT provides outbound internet connectivity for instances without external IP addresses; it has no effect on an instance’s network bandwidth ceiling. Since the VMs here are already serving traffic — just slowly — the problem isn’t connectivity, it’s throughput capacity, which Cloud NAT doesn’t address.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> The Ops Agent exports additional monitoring and logging metrics; it’s an observability tool, not a performance capability. Deploying it would give more visibility into the problem but do nothing to raise the network throughput ceiling the scenario asks you to improve.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming that because traffic flows through NICs, adding more NICs must increase available bandwidth — but Google Cloud explicitly caps bandwidth at the instance level based on machine type, not per interface, so Option A looks intuitively correct while being technically wrong. A secondary trap is reaching for Cloud NAT whenever “network” and “VM” appear together, without noticing that NAT addresses outbound connectivity for private instances, not throughput capacity for instances that are already reachable and transferring data.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Compute Engine enforces a maximum egress (and roughly similar ingress) bandwidth per instance, derived primarily from vCPU count and machine series — generally around 2 Gbps per vCPU up to a series-specific ceiling. This limit applies to the instance as a whole regardless of how many network interfaces or IP addresses it has, which is why scaling vCPUs — by resizing to a larger machine type, or enabling a high-bandwidth Tier_1 networking configuration on qualifying machine types — are the mechanisms that actually raise available throughput.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team serving large media or software downloads from Compute Engine VMs notices download speeds plateauing as demand grows, and monitoring confirms the VMs are pinned at their network bandwidth cap. Rather than adding more interfaces or spinning up unrelated networking components, they resize the instances to a larger machine type (or enable Tier_1 networking on a qualifying series) to raise the per-instance throughput ceiling, directly improving the download experience without re-architecting the service.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Bandwidth caps are per-instance (vCPU-based), not per-NIC — to raise the ceiling, scale the machine type, not the interface count.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a VM is confirmed to be at its network throughput ceiling, look for a fix that raises the per-instance bandwidth cap — typically a larger machine type or Tier_1 networking — and rule out adding NICs, which Google Cloud explicitly does not credit toward instance bandwidth, along with unrelated tools like Cloud NAT or monitoring agents.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/network-bandwidth\"><strong>Google Cloud — Network bandwidth:</strong></a> Supports: bandwidth being accounted per compute instance rather than per vNIC, vCPU-based scaling of the per-instance egress cap, and the N2 machine series bandwidth limits</p>","lessons":["d5l2","d5l4"]},"e4q15":{"id":"e4q15","exam":4,"n":15,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You recently migrated an ecommerce application onto Google Cloud, and you now need to get it ready for the approaching peak shopping season. You want to follow Google-recommended practices for this preparation. What is the first thing you should do to get ready for the high-traffic period?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Re-platform the application onto Cloud Run and rely on its built-in autoscaling.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Write a Terraform configuration for the application’s infrastructure so you can quickly stand it up in additional regions.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Run load tests against the application to profile how it performs and scales.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Pre-provision the same amount of additional compute capacity used during last year’s peak season, on the assumption that this year’s growth will be similar.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Run load tests against the application to profile how it performs and scales.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The “first” step must be the one that generates the data every other decision depends on. Options A, B, and D each commit to a specific architectural or capacity decision — re-platforming to Cloud Run, building multi-region deployment automation, or copying last season’s provisioning numbers — without first knowing how the newly migrated application actually performs or where it breaks under load. Google’s own peak-event preparation guidance places load testing early in the preparation phase, months ahead of the event, precisely because it reveals the bottlenecks and scaling behavior that then inform every subsequent architecture and capacity decision. Only load testing produces that foundational data, making it the correct first action; the other three are plausible later steps that are premature without it.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“recently migrated” — the application’s behavior on Google Cloud is unproven, so prior performance data (and, by extension, last season’s provisioning figures) can’t be trusted to transfer directly</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“what should you do first” — signals a sequencing question where several options could eventually be reasonable, but only one belongs at the very start</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“follow Google-recommended practices” — points to Google’s own documented peak-event methodology, which places load testing in the preparation phase ahead of architecture changes</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“prepare for the upcoming peak traffic season” — a known, anticipated event, which Google’s guidance treats as something to test for well in advance rather than react to</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Load testing reveals how the newly migrated application actually behaves under increasing demand — where it bottlenecks, how its components scale, and what capacity it genuinely needs — using real data from the current Google Cloud deployment rather than assumptions carried over from before the migration. That data is the necessary input for every subsequent decision: whether to re-platform a component, how many regions to deploy to, or how much capacity to provision. Doing it first, well ahead of the peak season, is also explicitly how Google’s own peak-event preparation guidance sequences this work.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Moving to Cloud Run and relying on autoscaling can be a reasonable architectural choice, but committing to it before understanding the application’s current bottlenecks and scaling behavior is premature — you don’t yet know whether the application’s architecture even suits Cloud Run’s execution model, or where its real constraints lie.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Automating infrastructure deployment to additional regions is valuable, but it assumes multi-region expansion is the right response to peak load without first confirming that regional capacity, rather than some other bottleneck entirely, is actually what limits the application under load.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Pre-provisioning based on last season’s compute usage assumes the application’s resource needs on its new Google Cloud infrastructure mirror its needs on whatever platform it ran on before migration, and assumes a growth figure without any current data to support it. This is guesswork rather than the data-driven approach Google recommends, and it skips the step that would reveal whether last season’s numbers are even relevant anymore.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is picking an option that sounds like solid peak-season preparation — more autoscaling, more automation, more pre-provisioned capacity — without noticing that each one presupposes knowledge the team doesn’t have yet, since the application was only just migrated. The “first” in the question is the tell: it’s asking which action produces the information the other actions would need to be well-informed, not which action sounds the most proactive in isolation.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Capacity planning and architectural decisions for a known, high-stakes event should be grounded in current, measured performance data rather than assumptions carried over from a previous environment or a previous season. Load testing supplies that data by simulating expected (and above-expected) traffic against the actual production environment, surfacing bottlenecks and scaling limits before they become customer-facing incidents, and giving every later decision — scaling strategy, regional footprint, provisioned capacity — a factual basis instead of a guess.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A retailer that just migrated its storefront to Google Cloud runs load tests simulating its expected Black Friday traffic months in advance, discovering that a particular database connection pool, not raw compute capacity, is the actual bottleneck at scale. That finding changes their entire preparation plan — instead of blindly provisioning more VMs or committing to a platform migration, they fix the specific constraint the test revealed, then validate the fix with another round of testing before the season arrives.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Just migrated, peak season coming → load test first; everything else (autoscaling platform, multi-region automation, pre-provisioning) is a decision that load-test data should inform, not precede.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a scenario asks what to do “first” to prepare a recently migrated or newly deployed application for a known high-traffic event, look for the option that generates performance data — load testing — rather than one that commits to a specific architecture or capacity decision before that data exists.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/support/docs/checklists/plan-peak-capacity-event\"><strong>Google Cloud — Prepare for a peak capacity event:</strong></a> Supports: load testing and capacity planning as part of the documented preparation phase for a known high-traffic event, ahead of architecture or capacity commitments</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/support/docs/peak-events/conduct-load-testing\"><strong>Google Cloud — Conduct load testing:</strong></a> Supports: load testing as an early step (recommended three to five months before the event) that determines whether the system will scale successfully and surfaces bottlenecks before other preparation decisions are made</p>","lessons":["d3l7"]},"e4q16":{"id":"e4q16","exam":4,"n":16,"domain":1,"html":"<p>You are setting up a continuous integration pipeline whose integration-testing build step needs to reach APIs that live inside your private VPC network. Your security team has mandated that API traffic must never be exposed publicly. You also need a solution that keeps management overhead to a minimum. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Run the build on Cloud Build private pools, peered into the private VPC.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Deploy Spinnaker for Google Cloud and connect it to the private VPC.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Run the build on Cloud Build’s default pool, and front the APIs with an Internal HTTP(S) Load Balancer.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Run the build on Cloud Build’s default pool, and front the APIs with an External HTTP(S) Load Balancer protected by a Google Cloud Armor policy.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Run the build on Cloud Build private pools, peered into the private VPC.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two constraints must both be satisfied: API traffic must never be exposed publicly, and the solution should minimize management overhead. Cloud Build’s default worker pool runs outside the customer’s VPC with no inherent path into private resources, so “default pool plus a load balancer” doesn’t actually solve connectivity on its own — an external load balancer (D) directly violates the no-public-exposure requirement regardless of Cloud Armor, and an internal load balancer (C) doesn’t give default-pool workers, which sit outside the VPC, any way to reach it without extra networking work. Spinnaker (B) is a deployment-orchestration tool, not a mechanism for private build-time network access, and self-hosting it adds overhead rather than removing it. Cloud Build private pools (A) are purpose-built for exactly this problem: fully managed, scaling to zero with no infrastructure to maintain, and connected to the customer’s VPC via private peering so builds reach internal resources without any traffic touching the public internet — satisfying both constraints at once.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“requires access to APIs inside your private VPC network” — hard constraint that the build execution environment itself needs network reachability into the VPC, not just that the API exists somewhere</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“do not expose API traffic publicly” — hard constraint eliminating any option that fronts the API with a public-facing component, however well protected</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“minimizes management overhead” — hard constraint favoring a fully managed, Google-operated solution over anything self-deployed or requiring extra infrastructure</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“CI pipeline” / “build step” — frames this as a Cloud Build execution-environment problem specifically, not a general application traffic-routing problem</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Build private pools are a fully managed, dedicated worker pool hosted and automatically scaled by Google — including to zero — with no infrastructure for the customer to set up, patch, or scale. Because the private pool’s network is peered directly with the customer’s VPC, builds running in the pool can reach private resources, including internal APIs, using only internal IP addressing, with no traffic ever crossing the public internet. This satisfies both the no-public-exposure requirement and the low-overhead requirement in a single, purpose-built configuration.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Spinnaker is a continuous delivery and deployment-orchestration tool, not a mechanism for giving a CI build step private network access. Standing it up to solve this specific connectivity problem means deploying and operating an additional system, increasing management overhead rather than minimizing it, and it still wouldn’t natively solve how build steps reach private VPC resources.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> An internal load balancer keeps the API off the public internet, which is a step in the right direction, but Cloud Build’s default worker pool runs outside the customer’s VPC and has no inherent path to an internal, VPC-only IP address. Making this work requires additional networking configuration layered on top of the default pool — complexity that private pools avoid entirely by being peered into the VPC from the start.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> An external HTTP(S) load balancer is, by definition, internet-facing. A Cloud Armor policy improves the security posture of that public endpoint, but doesn’t change the fact that the API becomes reachable over the public internet, directly contradicting the security team’s explicit requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming that any load balancer paired with a security control — an internal LB, or an external LB with Cloud Armor — is an equally valid way to expose an API to a CI pipeline, without checking where the pipeline’s build workers actually run. Cloud Build’s default pool executes outside the customer’s VPC, so neither load-balancer option solves the fundamental reachability problem on its own, and the external option additionally breaks the explicit “no public exposure” requirement regardless of how well it’s protected.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>By default, Cloud Build executes builds in a Google-managed environment with public internet access but no connectivity into a customer’s private VPC. Private pools solve this by running builds in a dedicated, customer-specific pool hosted in a Google-owned service producer network that is peered directly with the customer’s VPC, giving builds internal-IP-only access to private resources without any additional load balancing, VPN, or custom networking layer — all while remaining fully managed by Cloud Build itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team running integration tests that call internal microservices during every CI build switches their build step from Cloud Build’s default pool to a private pool peered with their VPC. Their build steps can now reach internal APIs directly over private IPs, satisfying the security team’s no-public-exposure policy, while the platform team avoids having to design, secure, or operate any additional networking infrastructure — Cloud Build handles the pool’s scaling and lifecycle automatically.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>CI build needs private VPC access, no public exposure, low overhead → Cloud Build private pools (peered into the VPC); an external load balancer is public by definition, and an internal one alone doesn’t get a default-pool build worker into the VPC in the first place.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a CI/CD build step needs to reach resources inside a private VPC without public exposure and with minimal operational overhead, look for a fully managed, VPC-peered execution environment — like Cloud Build private pools — rather than adding load balancers or standing up additional orchestration tools around a build runner that was never on the VPC to begin with.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/private-pools/private-pools-overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Private pools overview:</strong></a> Supports: private pools being fully managed with no infrastructure to maintain, and their VPC-peering mechanism that gives builds private-only access to internal resources</p>","lessons":["d1l8"]},"e4q17":{"id":"e4q17","exam":4,"n":17,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You are investigating a failed deployment in your CI/CD pipeline. Deployment logs show that the application container could not start because a required environment variable was missing.</p>\n<p>You need to identify the configuration error and add a safeguard to the CI/CD workflow to prevent similar failures. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Adopt a canary deployment strategy.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Add static code analysis to the CI pipeline.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Add integration tests to the CI pipeline.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Enable Cloud Audit Logs for deployment operations.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Add integration tests to the CI pipeline.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The failure occurs when the application starts with its runtime configuration. An integration test that launches the container and validates startup can reproduce this problem before deployment. Google’s SRE guidance specifically recommends integration testing configuration files. </p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Failure stage:</strong> The container fails during startup.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Root-cause signal:</strong> A required environment variable is missing.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Prevention requirement:</strong> Add validation to the CI/CD workflow.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>First, trace the missing variable through the deployment configuration and pipeline’s variable mappings, then correct the omission.</p>\n<p>Add an integration test that starts the built container with representative deployment configuration and verifies that it becomes ready and handles a basic request. Make successful testing a prerequisite for deployment. Cloud Build supports integration tests as pipeline steps. </p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A — Different Use Case:</strong> Canary deployments limit exposure to a faulty release and support validation during rollout. They do not, by themselves, detect missing configuration before deployment or correct its source. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Static checks can catch some configuration mistakes. However, executing the application with its configuration more directly tests the startup behavior that failed here. </p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>D — Different Use Case:</strong> Audit logs help establish who performed deployment or configuration operations. They do not validate that the application can start or prevent deployment of incomplete configuration. </p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>Integration tests must cover the relevant configuration. A test that supplies a hardcoded variable can pass even when the deployment template omits that variable. </p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Integration testing checks whether assembled application components work together. For this scenario, that includes the container, its configuration, and its required dependencies.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>For example, if the missing variable is <code>DATABASE_URL</code>, correct its deployment mapping and add a startup test using configuration generated from the deployment templates. An omitted mapping should then fail the pipeline before release.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>A runtime configuration failure needs a test that runs the application with its configuration.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>Choose <strong>C</strong>. Correct the missing configuration and add a startup-focused integration test that blocks deployment when it fails.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/testing-reliability/\"><strong>Testing for Reliability — Google SRE</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Integration testing, configuration testing, and blocking releases when tests detect failures.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/overview\"><strong>Overview of Cloud Build</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Running integration tests and static analysis as build steps.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/deployment-strategies\"><strong>Use a deployment strategy</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Canary deployments as a method for reducing rollout risk.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/audit\"><strong>Cloud Audit Logs overview</strong></a><br />\nSupports: Audit logs recording administrative operations and caller identities.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e4q18":{"id":"e4q18","exam":4,"n":18,"domain":1,"html":"<p>Your company recently completed a migration to Google Cloud. You now need to design a fast, reliable, and repeatable way for the organization to provision new projects and their foundational resources in Google Cloud. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create each new project manually through the Google Cloud console.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Write a gcloud CLI script that accepts the request’s parameters and provisions the project, and store that script in a Git repository.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Author your own Terraform module from scratch, store it in your source control repository, and run terraform apply against a copy of it for each new project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Adopt the Terraform modules provided by the Cloud Foundation Toolkit, and apply them with the appropriate parameters to create each Google Cloud project and its related resources.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Adopt the Terraform modules provided by the Cloud Foundation Toolkit, and apply them with the appropriate parameters to create each Google Cloud project and its related resources.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The solution has to be fast, reliable, and repeatable for provisioning projects and their basic resources. Manual console creation (A) is inherently non-repeatable and slow at scale, dependent on a person correctly redoing every step each time. A custom gcloud CLI script (B) codifies the steps but is imperative — the team must build and maintain their own idempotency, error handling, and drift detection. Writing a custom Terraform module from scratch (C) gets declarative, state-managed infrastructure as code, but the team still has to design and validate their own best-practice provisioning logic before it’s trustworthy. The Cloud Foundation Toolkit (D) already provides that logic as Google-maintained, best-practice Terraform modules purpose-built for exactly this task, so applying it with the team’s own parameters delivers speed (no module to design), reliability (maintained, widely used code), and repeatability (a standard Terraform workflow) in one step.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“fast, reliable, and repeatable” — the three criteria every option must be checked against; manual work fails “fast” and “repeatable” immediately</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“recently migrated to Google Cloud” — implies the team doesn’t yet have mature internal tooling, favoring a solution that supplies that maturity rather than requiring them to build it</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“provision new projects and basic resources” — a standard, well-understood task, exactly what a pre-built, parameterized toolkit is designed to handle</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“new projects” implying an ongoing need — signals this must scale as a repeatable process, not a one-off action</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>The Cloud Foundation Toolkit provides Google-maintained Terraform modules — such as a project-factory-style module — that already encode best practices for provisioning a new Google Cloud project and its foundational resources: IAM bindings, API enablement, networking attachment, and similar basics, as parameterized, reusable code. Applying this existing code with the appropriate parameters is faster than designing equivalent logic from scratch, more reliable because the modules are actively maintained, and fully repeatable because each new project is just another parameterized apply of the same proven module — satisfying all three stated criteria at once.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Manually creating projects through the console depends entirely on someone correctly repeating every configuration step each time. It doesn’t scale, isn’t fast beyond a handful of projects, and offers no built-in guarantee of consistency between projects — failing “reliable” and “repeatable” outright.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A gcloud CLI script is more repeatable than manual console work, but it’s an imperative script the team must design, test, and maintain themselves, including retries, partial failures, and drift — concerns a declarative, state-tracked tool like Terraform manages natively. It also means building provisioning best practices from scratch instead of reusing Google’s.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A custom Terraform module is a real improvement — declarative and state-managed — but it still requires the team to design and validate their own best-practice provisioning logic before it can be trusted at scale, which is slower to get right and more prone to missing edge cases than adopting the Cloud Foundation Toolkit’s existing, maintained modules for the same purpose.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating any Terraform-based option as equally good once it’s declarative and version-controlled, without noticing that one option (C) still requires building the provisioning logic yourself while the other (D) lets you reuse logic Google already built and maintains. The fastest, most reliable path to a repeatable process is rarely to author it from scratch when a purpose-built, well-tested toolkit already exists for exactly this task.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Infrastructure as code becomes genuinely fast, reliable, and repeatable not merely by being declarative and version-controlled, but by being built on modules that are already tested, maintained, and aligned with best practices — so provisioning a new instance of a standard resource, like a project, is a matter of supplying parameters to proven code rather than designing that code anew each time it’s needed.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team standardizes new-project requests by wiring an intake process to a Cloud Foundation Toolkit project module: a requester supplies a project name, owning team, and a few other parameters, and the team applies the module to produce a correctly configured project — with the right IAM bindings, enabled APIs, and network attachments — in minutes, consistently, without hand-crafting provisioning logic for every request or risking configuration drift between projects created months apart.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Fast, reliable, repeatable project provisioning → reuse the Cloud Foundation Toolkit’s existing Terraform modules, don’t build equivalent logic yourself from a script or a module you author from scratch.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a scenario asks for a fast, reliable, repeatable way to provision standard Google Cloud resources like new projects, favor an existing, Google-maintained toolkit of modules over writing custom scripts or custom Terraform from scratch — reusing proven, parameterized code beats building equivalent logic yourself.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/terraform/blueprints/terraform-blueprints\">Google Cloud — Terraform blueprints and modules for Google Cloud (Cloud Foundation Toolkit): </a>Supports: the Cloud Foundation Toolkit’s production-ready, best-practice Terraform modules for automating the provisioning of Google Cloud projects and related resources</p>","lessons":["d1l6"]},"e4q19":{"id":"e4q19","exam":4,"n":19,"domain":3,"html":"<p>Your company is planning a major marketing event for an online retail site during the holiday shopping season, and you expect the web application to receive a large volume of traffic in a short window. You need to prepare the application for potential failures during the event. What should you do? (Choose two.)</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Set up Anthos Service Mesh on the application so you can spot issues on its topology map.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Confirm that the relevant system metrics are being collected in Cloud Monitoring, and configure alerts at thresholds that matter to you.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Review your expected increase in capacity requirements and plan out the quota management you’ll need to support it.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Track your services’ latency using an average percentile latency measurement.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>E. Configure Cloud Monitoring alerts for every common failure your application experiences.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Confirm that the relevant system metrics are being collected in Cloud Monitoring, and configure alerts at thresholds that matter to you.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Review your expected increase in capacity requirements and plan out the quota management you’ll need to support it.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Preparing for a known, high-stakes traffic event has two independent needs: having enough capacity to absorb the expected load, and having the visibility to detect and respond when something does go wrong. C directly addresses capacity — without reviewing increased needs and planning quota management in advance, the application can be throttled or fail outright no matter how well it’s monitored. B directly addresses observability — meaningful, well-tuned alerts on relevant metrics let the team detect and react to real problems during the event. Option A proposes deploying new infrastructure tooling shortly before a high-stakes event, adding risk rather than readiness. Options D and E each sound like reasonable monitoring practices but describe flawed implementations: averaged latency hides the tail-latency problems that actually hurt users, and alerting on every common failure produces noise rather than actionable signal. B and C work independently — one is a capacity action, the other an observability action — and together they form a complete readiness picture: enough capacity, plus the ability to detect trouble if capacity or anything else still goes wrong.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“prepare your application for potential failures during the event” — frames this as readiness with two distinct angles: having enough capacity, and being able to detect problems</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“large volume of traffic in a short period” — signals a capacity/quota concern specifically, since everyday quotas may not cover a sudden spike</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“alerts at levels of interest” (B) versus “all common failures” (E) — a deliberate contrast between targeted, meaningful alerting and indiscriminate alerting</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“average percentile latency” (D) — an internally inconsistent phrase signaling a flawed metric choice, since averages and percentiles summarize a distribution in different, non-interchangeable ways</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Reviewing increased capacity requirements and planning quota management (C) ensures the application has enough underlying resource headroom to actually handle the expected spike — without it, the event could fail regardless of monitoring quality, since requests could be throttled or rejected at the infrastructure or API level. Ensuring relevant metrics are captured with alerts set at meaningful thresholds (B) ensures that if something does go wrong, the team finds out quickly enough to respond rather than discovering it through customer complaints. Together these cover both halves of event readiness — sufficient capacity, and the ability to detect trouble when it happens — and neither one substitutes for the other, which is why both are needed and the set is complete at two.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Service mesh topology visibility can eventually be useful, but introducing new infrastructure tooling into the application shortly before a known high-stakes event adds operational risk and complexity precisely when stability matters most, rather than directly strengthening readiness for the event itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Monitoring latency as an “average” obscures the tail latency that actually affects user experience — a service with a low average but a long tail of slow requests can still be delivering a poor experience to a meaningful share of users. SRE practice favors percentile-based latency metrics specifically because they surface this tail behavior that averages hide.</p>\n<p><strong>Option E — Violates Requirement:</strong> Alerting on every common failure generates a high volume of low-value notifications, many of which may be routine or self-correcting. This creates alert fatigue, making it harder, not easier, for the on-call team to notice and act on the alerts that actually matter during a high-traffic event.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is picking any option containing “monitor” or “alert” without checking whether it describes monitoring and alerting done well. D sounds like good observability practice but names an inferior metric; E sounds like proactive readiness but describes a noisy, unfocused alerting strategy. The correct pair requires recognizing that readiness for a known traffic spike needs both adequate capacity and well-targeted detection — not just “more monitoring” or “more infrastructure” in any form.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Preparing a service for a known period of elevated demand has two largely independent prerequisites: confirming the underlying infrastructure and quotas can absorb the expected load, and ensuring the team has the observability to detect and respond to problems that do occur. Within observability itself, implementation quality matters — percentile-based metrics over averages, and targeted alerts over blanket ones, are what make monitoring actually useful during an incident rather than just noisy or misleading.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>An online retailer preparing for a major holiday sale reviews its expected traffic increase, requests the necessary quota increases for compute, networking, and API calls well ahead of the event, and separately tunes its Cloud Monitoring dashboards to alert on signals like p99 latency and error rate crossing meaningful thresholds — rather than paging engineers for every minor, self-resolving blip. When a backend dependency does slow down during the sale, the team is alerted promptly on a signal that actually reflects degraded user experience, and they already have the quota headroom to scale additional capacity in response.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Preparing for a traffic spike needs two things: enough capacity (review and raise quotas) and good visibility (meaningful metrics and targeted alerts) — not new infrastructure tooling, not averaged latency, and not alerts on every failure.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a scenario asks how to prepare a service for a known high-traffic event, look for one action that secures adequate capacity/quota headroom and one that ensures well-targeted observability — and be suspicious of any monitoring-sounding option that uses averages instead of percentiles, or alerts on everything instead of what matters.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/support/docs/checklists/plan-peak-capacity-event\"><strong>Google Cloud — Prepare for a peak capacity event:</strong></a> Supports: capacity planning and quota review as a core part of preparing for a known high-traffic event</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/\"><strong>Google SRE Book — Chapter 6: Monitoring Distributed Systems:</strong></a> Supports: the four golden signals framework, and latency being tracked as a percentile-based distribution rather than an average, underpinning why targeted, meaningful alerting beats averaged metrics and blanket alerting</p>","lessons":["d3l1","d3l7"]},"e4q20":{"id":"e4q20","exam":4,"n":20,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your Cloud Run application currently writes its logs as plain unstructured text strings, which land in Cloud Logging. You want those logs to become JSON-based structured logs instead. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Update the application code to use the Cloud Logging client library and emit log entries whose payload is structured JSON.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Add a Fluent Bit sidecar container to the service and configure it with a JSON parser.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Bundle a logging agent into the Cloud Run container image and have the agent forward logs to Cloud Logging.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Configure the logging agent to convert the existing text payload of each log entry into a JSON payload.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Update the application code to use the Cloud Logging client library and emit log entries whose payload is structured JSON.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Run already automatically captures anything written to stdout/stderr and forwards it to Cloud Logging, with no agent to install or configure by the customer. Whether a line lands in textPayload or jsonPayload is determined entirely by whether that line is a single, valid, serialized JSON object at the moment it’s written — Cloud Logging doesn’t retroactively convert plain text into JSON. That means the only way to get structured logs is to change what the application itself emits, which is exactly what using the Cloud Logging client library to construct and write JSON-formatted entries accomplishes. Options B, C, and D each propose some kind of agent transforming the logs after the fact, which doesn’t match how Cloud Run’s logging pipeline works and doesn’t solve the stated problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“writes unstructured logs as text strings” — identifies the root cause as the application’s own output format, not a transport or forwarding problem</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“convert the unstructured logs to JSON-based structured logs” — the desired end state is specifically the jsonPayload field, which Cloud Logging only populates from a genuinely JSON-formatted log line</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Cloud Run application” — a serverless, fully managed environment where the customer doesn’t install or manage logging agents the way they might on a VM or in a self-managed Kubernetes cluster</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Three of the four options revolve around an “agent,” which should prompt checking whether Cloud Run’s logging architecture even has a customer-configurable agent step to intervene in</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Logging treats a log line as structured only when that line is a single, valid serialized JSON object, placing it in jsonPayload instead of textPayload. The Cloud Logging client library gives the application a direct way to construct such JSON-formatted entries — including fields like severity and message — and write them to stdout, stderr, or directly via the API, which Cloud Run automatically picks up. Because the transformation has to happen at the point the log is produced, modifying the application to emit structured entries through the client library is the only option that changes the log format at its source.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A Fluent Bit sidecar with a JSON parser could theoretically reformat log output, but it adds an extra container, configuration surface, and point of failure to parse and restructure text that the application could simply emit as JSON in the first place — solving the same problem with substantially more complexity than using the Cloud Logging client library directly.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Incorrect:</strong> Cloud Run is fully managed and serverless; log capture from stdout, stderr, and the Cloud Logging client libraries is already built in and automatic. There is no customer-installable logging agent in the Cloud Run execution environment, and even if there were, forwarding unstructured text unchanged would not make it structured.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Incorrect:</strong> There is no agent-level feature in Cloud Logging that retroactively rewrites an existing text payload into a JSON payload. Whether a log entry is structured is determined at write time by whether the line was valid JSON, not by any downstream agent configuration applied after ingestion.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming that because “agents” commonly appear in other Google Cloud logging contexts — such as the Ops Agent on Compute Engine or Fluent Bit in GKE — a similar agent-based approach must apply to Cloud Run too. Cloud Run’s fully managed nature means there’s no agent for the customer to install or configure in the execution environment; stdout/stderr capture is automatic, and the only lever available is what the application itself writes, which is exactly what the client-library option controls.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>In Cloud Logging, a log entry is structured specifically when its payload is a JSON object stored in the jsonPayload field, as opposed to a plain string stored in textPayload. This distinction is determined when the log line is written, not through any later transformation step, which is why structured logging guidance centers on writing a single line of serialized JSON to stdout/stderr or using a Cloud Logging client library, rather than on any agent-based post-processing.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team running a Cloud Run service switches from ad hoc print statements to the Cloud Logging client library, emitting each log entry as a JSON object with fields like severity, message, and a request trace identifier. Doing so lets them filter and query logs in the Logs Explorer by those structured fields — for example, isolating all ERROR-severity entries for a specific request — something that was impossible when every log entry was just an opaque block of text.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Structured logs come from what the app writes, not from an agent — use the Cloud Logging client library to emit JSON, since Cloud Run has no customer-installable agent to convert text after the fact.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a Cloud Run (or other serverless) application needs structured JSON logs instead of plain text, look for the option that changes what the application itself writes — typically via the Cloud Logging client library — and be skeptical of any answer that proposes installing or configuring an agent, since Cloud Run’s managed logging pipeline doesn’t work that way.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/logging\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Run logging:</strong></a> Supports: automatic stdout/stderr log capture, the textPayload-versus-jsonPayload distinction, and writing structured logs via the Cloud Logging client library</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/structured-logging\"><strong>Google Cloud — Structured logging:</strong></a> Supports: the jsonPayload mechanism and the client-library/API approach as the documented way to write structured logs, as opposed to any agent-based conversion</p>","lessons":["d4l5"]},"e4q21":{"id":"e4q21","exam":4,"n":21,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You are choosing a deployment approach for your applications on Google Cloud, and as part of that planning you want to gather performance metrics for new application versions using live traffic. The new versions need to be tested against the full production load before they are actually launched. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Combine A/B testing with a blue/green deployment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Combine canary testing with continuous deployment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Combine canary testing with a rolling updates deployment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Combine shadow testing with continuous deployment.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Combine shadow testing with continuous deployment.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Three constraints must all be satisfied: use live production traffic to gather performance metrics, test against the full production load, and do this before the new version is actually launched. A/B testing (A) and canary testing (B, C) both route real user requests directly to the new version, meaning real users are actually served by it — that’s a form of launch, and canary specifically limits traffic to a fraction, not the full load. Shadow testing (D) mirrors the complete incoming request stream to the new version in parallel with the current version, but only returns the current version’s responses to users — the new version processes the full real traffic volume and pattern without ever being responsible for what a user actually sees, satisfying “full production load” and “before launched” simultaneously. Pairing it with continuous deployment keeps this evaluation a repeatable, automated pipeline step.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“use live traffic to gather performance metrics” — rules out synthetic load in a staging environment, points toward a technique operating on real production requests</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“test against the full production load” — a hard constraint eliminating any technique that only exposes the new version to a fraction of traffic (canary) or splits users between versions (A/B)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“before your applications are launched” — a hard constraint eliminating any technique where real users are actually served responses from the new version, since that is itself a form of launch</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“deployment technique” paired with a strategy — signals a combination of a testing technique with a deployment mechanism, matching every option’s paired structure</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Shadow testing duplicates the full stream of live production requests and sends a copy to the new version at the same time the current version handles the real request, but only the current version’s response is ever returned to the user. The new version is exercised by the complete, real-world volume and pattern of production traffic — exactly what’s needed for meaningful performance metrics — while remaining entirely invisible to users, meaning it has not actually been launched. Running this as part of continuous deployment lets the team evaluate every new version this way automatically and repeatedly, rather than as a manual, one-time test.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> A/B testing routes real users to the new version based on some condition, meaning those users are actually served by it — a form of launch to part of the audience, contradicting testing “before” launch. Blue/green’s typical instantaneous full-traffic cutover also doesn’t provide the gradual or mirrored evaluation the scenario implies.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> Canary testing intentionally limits the new version’s exposure to a small percentage of live traffic to contain risk, so it’s never tested against the full production load — only a deliberately reduced subset. It also serves real responses to the users whose traffic lands on the canary, meaning the new version has effectively been launched to them.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> This shares Option B’s core issues — only a portion of traffic reaches the new version, so it isn’t validated against the full load, and the users on that portion are actually served by it, meaning it isn’t tested purely “before” launch. Pairing canary with rolling updates doesn’t change canary’s fundamental traffic-splitting behavior.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “canary” as the default answer whenever a question mentions testing new versions with real traffic, since canary is the most commonly discussed technique for this general idea. But canary and A/B testing both expose some real users to the new version, and canary specifically limits traffic volume rather than using the full load — two details this scenario’s wording (“full production load,” “before... launched”) is specifically designed to rule out. Shadow testing is the less commonly reached-for option that actually satisfies both constraints.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Deployment and release-testing techniques differ along two independent dimensions: how much of the real traffic volume reaches the new version, and whether the new version’s output is ever actually shown to real users. Canary and A/B testing both expose a portion of real users to real responses from the new version. Shadow testing is distinct because it decouples traffic volume from user exposure entirely: the new version can see 100% of the traffic pattern without ever being responsible for a single response a user actually sees.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team preparing to replace a core backend service mirrors all production requests to the new version for several days using traffic mirroring on their load balancer, comparing its latency, error rates, and resource usage against the current version under the exact same real-world load — all while users continue to be served exclusively by the existing version. Only after confirming the new version performs acceptably under full production conditions do they begin a separate, gradual rollout to actually expose users to it.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Full production load, zero user exposure, before launch → shadow testing; canary and A/B both actually serve some real users, so neither is “before launch.”</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a scenario wants a new version validated against the complete, real production traffic pattern without any real user ever seeing its response, that’s shadow testing (traffic mirroring) — canary and A/B testing both involve serving at least some real users, and canary specifically limits traffic volume rather than using the full load.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/https/setting-up-global-traffic-mgmt\"><strong>Google Cloud — Set up traffic management for global external Application Load Balancers:</strong></a> Supports: the traffic-mirroring mechanism underlying shadow testing, described as sending an identical request to a mirror backend without client-facing impact, “useful for testing a new version of a backend service”</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/deployment-strategies\">Google Cloud — Cloud Deploy deployment strategies: </a>Supports: canary deployment being a progressive rollout to a portion of infrastructure/traffic rather than the full production load, contrasting with shadow testing’s full-traffic, no-user-exposure approach</p>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e4q22":{"id":"e4q22","exam":4,"n":22,"domain":1,"html":"<p>You are designing a new Google Cloud organization on behalf of a client who is concerned about the risks posed by long-lived credentials in Google Cloud. You need a solution that completely removes the risk associated with JSON service account keys while keeping ongoing operational overhead to a minimum. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Enforce the constraints/iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation organization policy constraint at the organization level.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Build custom versions of the relevant predefined roles that strip out every iam.serviceAccountKeys.* permission.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Enforce the constraints/iam.disableServiceAccountKeyUpload organization policy constraint at the organization level.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Grant the roles/iam.serviceAccountKeyAdmin role solely to organization administrators.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Enforce the constraints/iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation organization policy constraint at the organization level.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The solution must completely eliminate the risk of JSON service account key creation — not merely reduce exposure or restrict who can do it — while adding minimal ongoing operational burden. Option B requires building and maintaining custom IAM roles org-wide, which is high-overhead and incomplete, since any principal retaining a broader role could still create keys. Option D restricts who can create keys to administrators, but those administrators can still create JSON keys — the risk is concentrated, not eliminated, and gatekeeping adds process overhead. Option C enforces a constraint that blocks a different, narrower action — uploading an externally generated public key — which does nothing to stop the standard JSON key creation flow the client is worried about. Option A enforces the constraint purpose-built to block creation of new user-managed service account keys org-wide, in a single, centrally enforced setting with no per-role or per-principal maintenance, fully satisfying both requirements.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“completely eliminate the risks” — a hard constraint ruling out anything that merely restricts or reduces who can create keys (D) or requires comprehensive, error-prone permission auditing to guarantee coverage (B)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“JSON service account keys” specifically — points to the standard key-creation flow, as opposed to uploading externally generated key material, which is the distinction between the two similarly named constraints</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“minimizing operational overhead” — a hard constraint favoring a single, centrally enforced organization policy over custom IAM roles or administrative gatekeeping that require ongoing maintenance</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“designing a new Google Cloud organization” — frames this as foundational setup, where an org-wide policy constraint is naturally applied once at the top of the hierarchy</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>The constraints/iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation organization policy constraint, enforced at the organization level, prevents any principal anywhere in the organization from creating new user-managed (including JSON) service account keys, regardless of what IAM roles they otherwise hold. Because this is a single policy enforced centrally by the Organization Policy Service, it requires no per-role redesign, no list of approved administrators to manage, and no ongoing auditing to confirm coverage — it applies uniformly and automatically, satisfying “completely eliminate” and “minimizing operational overhead” together.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Custom roles excluding iam.serviceAccountKeys.* permissions could reduce key creation if applied everywhere correctly, but require identifying and modifying every predefined role in use, maintaining those custom roles going forward, and ensuring no principal is ever granted a broader role that reintroduces the permission — a much higher, more error-prone burden than a single org policy, with no guarantee of complete coverage.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> constraints/iam.disableServiceAccountKeyUpload blocks uploading an externally generated public key to a service account, not the creation of new JSON keys through Google’s standard key-creation flow. Enforcing it alone leaves the primary risk the client is worried about completely unaddressed.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Restricting roles/iam.serviceAccountKeyAdmin to organization administrators narrows who can create keys but doesn’t eliminate the ability to create them — those administrators can still generate JSON keys. It also adds overhead, since anyone else who legitimately needs a key must now go through the restricted admin group.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is confusing constraints/iam.disableServiceAccountKeyUpload with constraints/iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation, since their names are nearly identical and both relate to service account keys. Upload blocks associating an externally generated key with a service account; creation blocks generating a new key through Google’s own key-creation process — the action this scenario actually describes. A second trap is assuming that restricting a role (D) or redesigning permissions (B) is equivalent to eliminating a capability outright, when only an enforced organization policy constraint guarantees that outcome regardless of what roles anyone holds.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Organization policy constraints operate above and independently of IAM role grants: even a principal with a broad IAM role like Owner cannot perform an action that an enforced organization policy constraint blocks. This makes constraints the appropriate tool whenever a requirement is to eliminate a capability organization-wide with certainty, as opposed to IAM role design, which only shapes what a specific principal can do and depends on every relevant role being correctly and consistently configured to achieve the same guarantee.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team migrating their organization toward keyless authentication, such as Workload Identity Federation, enforces constraints/iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation at the organization level as a first step, immediately and permanently closing off new JSON key creation across every current and future project without redesigning a single IAM role or manually policing who holds which permissions — freeing the team to focus on migrating existing workloads off any keys created before the policy was enforced.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>JSON key creation risk, zero overhead, complete elimination → constraints/iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation at the org level; “Upload” blocks a different action, and custom roles or admin-only grants don’t eliminate the capability outright.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a scenario wants service account key creation eliminated organization-wide with minimal ongoing effort, reach for the organization policy constraint built for exactly that (disableServiceAccountKeyCreation) rather than IAM role redesign or role-grant restrictions, and don’t confuse it with the similarly named upload-blocking constraint, which addresses a different action entirely.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/service-account-creds\"><strong>Google Cloud — Service account credentials:</strong></a> Supports: the distinction between disableServiceAccountKeyCreation (blocks creating user-managed keys) and disableServiceAccountKeyUpload (blocks uploading external public keys)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/organization-policy/restrict-service-accounts\"><strong>Google Cloud — Restrict IAM service account usage:</strong></a> Supports: the constraint’s function of disabling creation of new external service account keys at the organization level</p>","lessons":["d1l2"]},"e4q23":{"id":"e4q23","exam":4,"n":23,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your company runs its applications on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), and several of them rely on ephemeral volumes. You’ve noticed that some applications have become unstable because worker nodes are reporting a DiskPressure condition. You need to identify which Pods are responsible, but you don’t have execute access into either the workloads or the nodes. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Inspect the node/ephemeral_storage/used_bytes metric in Metrics Explorer.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Inspect the container/ephemeral_storage/used_bytes metric in Metrics Explorer.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Find every Pod that uses an emptyDir volume, and run the df -h command to measure that volume’s disk usage.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Find every Pod that uses an emptyDir volume, and run the df -sh * command to measure that volume’s disk usage.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Inspect the container/ephemeral_storage/used_bytes metric in Metrics Explorer.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two constraints must both be satisfied: identifying specifically which Pods are responsible for the DiskPressure condition, and doing so without execute access to workloads or nodes. Options C and D both require running a shell command inside or against a Pod or node, which needs exec or SSH access — directly violating the stated constraint regardless of the exact df syntax used. That leaves the two Metrics Explorer options, which differ only in which metric they check. The node-level metric (A) reports aggregate ephemeral storage usage for an entire node, confirming a node is under pressure but not attributing that usage to any particular Pod. The container-level metric (B) reports ephemeral storage usage per container, which can be filtered and grouped by Pod and namespace in Metrics Explorer, directly revealing which specific Pods are the likely cause.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“identify which Pods are causing the issue” — the central requirement, which only a per-container/per-Pod metric can satisfy; a node-level metric cannot attribute usage to an individual workload</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“you do not have execute access to workloads and nodes” — a hard constraint eliminating any option that requires running a command inside a Pod or on a node</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“ephemeral volumes” / “DiskPressure” — identifies ephemeral storage specifically as the resource under pressure, pointing to the ephemeral_storage metric family rather than memory or CPU metrics</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“using Metrics Explorer” appears in two of the four options — signals that the real decision point is which metric to select, not which tool to use</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>The container/ephemeral_storage/used_bytes metric reports local ephemeral storage usage at the individual container level, and because GKE’s Cloud Monitoring metrics carry labels such as pod_name and namespace_name, this metric can be filtered or grouped in Metrics Explorer to show exactly which Pods are consuming the most ephemeral storage. This gives a direct, per-Pod view of resource consumption entirely through the monitoring console, with no need to exec into a container or SSH into a node — satisfying both the identification requirement and the access constraint.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> The node/ephemeral_storage/used_bytes metric confirms that a given node’s overall ephemeral storage usage is high, which is useful for seeing that a node is under pressure, but it’s an aggregate across every container on the node and cannot attribute that usage to any specific Pod — it answers “which nodes,” not “which Pods.”</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Running df -h against a Pod’s emptyDir volume requires exec access into the Pod, or SSH access to the node hosting it, which the scenario explicitly states is unavailable. This approach is also far more labor-intensive, requiring each Pod to be checked individually rather than viewing consumption across the fleet at once.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> This has the same fundamental problem as Option C — df -sh * still requires executing a command inside a Pod or on a node. Changing the specific df flags doesn’t change the fact that this approach is blocked by the stated access constraint.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is reflexively reaching for a hands-on shell command (df) to check disk usage, since that’s the natural instinct for a storage question, without registering that the scenario explicitly removes execute access as an option. A second, more subtle trap is picking the node-level metric (A) because it’s the one most directly tied to the DiskPressure condition’s name, without noticing the question asks specifically which Pods are responsible — something only a container-level metric can answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>GKE automatically exports both node-level and container-level resource metrics to Cloud Monitoring, and the two serve different diagnostic purposes: node-level metrics tell you that a resource constraint exists on a given node, while container-level metrics carry Pod and namespace labels that let you attribute that constraint to specific workloads. When the question is “which workload is responsible,” the container-level metric is the one that can actually answer it — all without needing shell access to anything.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team notices DiskPressure alerts firing on several GKE nodes and, without needing to exec into any Pod, opens Metrics Explorer, selects the container/ephemeral_storage/used_bytes metric, and groups the results by pod_name to immediately see a handful of Pods consuming far more ephemeral storage than the rest — typically ones writing large temporary files to an emptyDir volume without limits. They can then set ephemeral storage requests and limits on those specific workloads to prevent them from starving other Pods on the same node.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Which Pods?” needs a container-level metric, not a node-level one — and “no exec access” rules out any df command; container/ephemeral_storage/used_bytes in Metrics Explorer answers both.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a GKE troubleshooting question asks which specific Pods are responsible for a resource-pressure condition and rules out shell/exec access, look for the container-level Cloud Monitoring metric (filterable by Pod) rather than the node-level aggregate or any command that requires running something inside a Pod or node.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/troubleshooting/introduction-monitoring\"><strong>Google Cloud — Introduction to monitoring your GKE environment</strong></a><strong>:</strong> Supports: the definitions and troubleshooting significance of container/ephemeral_storage/used_bytes (per-container, Pod-attributable) versus node/ephemeral_storage/used_bytes (node-level aggregate), and the DiskPressure node condition</p>","lessons":["d4l6","d4l12"]},"e4q24":{"id":"e4q24","exam":4,"n":24,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You have a Cloud Build job configured to deploy Terraform code whenever a Git branch is updated. During testing, the job fails, and the build logs show the following error:</p>\n<p>Initializing the backend...</p>\n<p>Error: Failed to get existing workspaces: querying Cloud Storage failed: googleapi: Error 403</p>\n<p>You need to resolve this by following Google-recommended practices. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Update the Terraform configuration to use local state instead of a remote backend.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Create a Cloud Storage bucket matching the name referenced in the Terraform configuration.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Grant the Cloud Build service account the roles/owner Identity and Access Management (IAM) role on the project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Grant the Cloud Build service account the roles/storage.objectAdmin Identity and Access Management (IAM) role, scoped to just the state file’s storage bucket.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Grant the Cloud Build service account the roles/storage.objectAdmin Identity and Access Management (IAM) role, scoped to just the state file’s storage bucket.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The fix must resolve a 403 permission error encountered while Terraform’s GCS backend queries the state bucket, and it must follow Google-recommended, least-privilege practice. The error text — “querying Cloud Storage failed: googleapi: Error 403” — is specifically an authorization failure, not a not-found error, which rules out Option B; creating the bucket addresses a missing-resource scenario, not a permissions one. Option A sidesteps the problem rather than fixing it, and abandoning a shared remote backend for local state contradicts recommended CI/CD practice for team-shared infrastructure as code. Option C would technically resolve the 403 by granting the Cloud Build service account unrestricted project-wide permissions, but this is explicitly against the least-privilege principle Google recommends for automated pipeline service accounts. Option D grants exactly the permissions Terraform’s GCS backend needs — reading, listing, and writing objects — scoped to only the specific bucket holding the state file, resolving the 403 while following least-privilege practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“Error 403” paired with “querying Cloud Storage” — specifically identifies an authorization failure against the GCS backend, not a missing-resource (404) condition</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“following Google-recommended practices” — a hard constraint ruling out any fix that technically works but over-grants permissions, such as a broad project-level Owner grant</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Cloud Build job that deploys Terraform code” — identifies the Cloud Build service account as the identity whose permissions need examining, since that’s the principal executing terraform init</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Initializing the backend...” — pinpoints the failure to the backend initialization step, where Terraform first needs to list and read objects in the configured state bucket</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Terraform’s Google Cloud Storage backend needs to list and read objects in the configured bucket during terraform init to check for existing state, and a 403 at this exact step means the identity running Terraform — the Cloud Build service account — lacks sufficient permissions on that bucket. The roles/storage.objectAdmin role grants the read, write, and list permissions Terraform’s GCS backend needs to manage state objects, and granting it specifically on the state file’s bucket, rather than at the project level, gives Cloud Build exactly the access required without extending broader privileges, matching Google’s recommended least-privilege approach for pipeline service accounts.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Switching to local state avoids the permission error rather than fixing it, and abandons the shared, lockable remote state a team-oriented CI/CD pipeline depends on — contradicting recommended Terraform practice for collaborative infrastructure management generally, not just this one error.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Incorrect:</strong> A 403 error means the caller was denied access to a resource it could reach, not that the resource doesn’t exist; a genuinely missing bucket would produce a different, not-found-style error. Creating a new bucket doesn’t address a permissions problem and risks creating confusion or duplicate infrastructure if the intended bucket already exists.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Granting roles/owner at the project level would resolve the 403, since Owner includes every needed Cloud Storage permission, but it also hands the Cloud Build service account unrestricted control over the entire project — a significant over-grant for a pipeline that only needs to read and write objects in one specific bucket, and a clear violation of least-privilege best practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is picking the option that would “work” (C) without checking whether it follows the recommended practice the question explicitly asks for — a broad Owner grant does fix the symptom, but the real test is recognizing that scoped, least-privilege access is the Google-recommended way to fix it. A second trap is misreading “403” as if it could mean the resource is missing, which would make creating a bucket (B) seem plausible; distinguishing authorization failures from not-found errors is the key diagnostic skill being tested.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>When a service account hits an authorization error against a specific resource, the Google-recommended remediation is to grant that service account the narrowest IAM role that covers the required permissions, scoped to the smallest resource that makes sense — here, the individual bucket rather than the project — rather than reaching for a broad, predefined role like Owner that happens to include the needed permissions along with many others the task doesn’t require.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team running Terraform through Cloud Build grants the Cloud Build service account roles/storage.objectAdmin on only the bucket holding their Terraform state, rather than broader project-level access. When a new pipeline later needs similar access for a different state bucket, the precedent of scoping grants to individual buckets makes it easy to extend the same least-privilege pattern, keeping the blast radius of any single service account’s compromise or misconfiguration limited to the specific state files it’s actually responsible for.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>403 on the state bucket during backend init → grant roles/storage.objectAdmin on that bucket specifically, not roles/owner on the whole project and not a workaround like local state.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a Cloud Build/Terraform pipeline fails with a 403 while querying its GCS state bucket, the Google-recommended fix is a least-privilege IAM grant scoped to that bucket — never a project-wide role like Owner, and never abandoning the remote backend to dodge the error.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/terraform/resource-management/store-state\">Google Cloud — Store Terraform state in a Cloud Storage bucket: </a>Supports: the object-level permissions Terraform’s GCS backend requires, and the recommendation to control access to the state bucket</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/terraform/best-practices/security\"><strong>Google Cloud — Terraform security best practices:</strong></a> Supports: applying least-privilege IAM principles to service accounts used for Terraform automation, rather than broad roles like Owner</p>","lessons":["d2l2","d2l10"]},"e4q25":{"id":"e4q25","exam":4,"n":25,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You are setting up Cloud Logging for a new application running on a Compute Engine instance that has a public IP address and a user-managed service account attached. You’ve confirmed the required logging agents are running on the instance, but no log entries from it are showing up in Cloud Logging. You want to fix this following Google-recommended practices. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Export a key for the service account, and configure the agents to authenticate using that key.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Switch the instance to use the default Compute Engine service account instead.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Grant the service account the Logs Writer IAM role.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Turn on Private Google Access for the subnet the instance resides in.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Grant the service account the Logs Writer IAM role.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The fix must restore the already-running agent’s ability to write log entries to Cloud Logging, and it must align with Google-recommended practice, which rules out anything introducing long-lived credentials or weakening the security posture. Option D addresses a networking path relevant only to instances without a public IP that need internal routing to reach Google APIs; this instance already has a public IP and a route to those APIs, so it isn’t the blocker. Option A would technically let the agent authenticate, but exporting and distributing a key is explicitly a secondary, less-recommended method compared to using the attached service account’s identity directly. Option B might fix the symptom if the default service account happens to carry broader permissions, but replacing a purpose-built custom service account with the default one is a step backward from least privilege and doesn’t target what’s actually missing. Option C grants exactly the permission the agent needs — the Logs Writer role — resolving the issue at its root.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“you confirmed that the necessary agents are running” — rules out an agent installation or configuration problem, narrowing the cause to authorization</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“user-managed service account” — a custom service account doesn’t automatically carry the Logs Writer role the way some default-account scenarios do, making IAM the first thing to check</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“public IP address” — rules out Private Google Access as relevant, since the instance already has a direct path to Google APIs</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“following Google-recommended practices” — a hard constraint eliminating the service-account-key option, which Google positions as a fallback rather than the preferred approach when an attached service account is available</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Both the Ops Agent and the legacy Logging agent authenticate using application default credentials tied to the VM’s attached service account, and that service account needs the Logs Writer (roles/logging.logWriter) IAM role to actually write entries to Cloud Logging. Newly created Compute Engine VMs often have sufficient access scopes and a service account already holding this role, but a user-managed service account isn’t guaranteed to have it granted automatically. Since the agent is confirmed running and simply failing to deliver log entries, granting this specific role to the attached service account directly closes the authorization gap, which is the documented, Google-recommended way to authorize the agent via an attached service account.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Service account keys are a supported authentication method for the agent, but Google documents attaching a service account directly as the preferred path and keys as the fallback for cases where an attached service account isn’t an option — introducing a key here adds credential-management overhead the scenario’s existing setup doesn’t require.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Switching to the default Compute Engine service account could resolve the symptom if that account carries the necessary role, but it abandons a purpose-built custom service account for a broader, less-scoped identity, running counter to least-privilege practice and sidestepping the actual gap — a missing role grant on the account already in use.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> Private Google Access provides a path to Google APIs for instances without a public IP by routing traffic internally. This instance already has a public IP and can already reach Google’s APIs, so enabling it addresses nothing relevant to a failure that’s about authorization, not connectivity.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is reaching for a networking fix (Private Google Access) simply because the instance has a public IP and logging can feel like a connectivity concern, without checking whether connectivity was ever actually the problem. A second trap is treating a key-based or default-service-account fix as equally valid because it would also make the symptom disappear, without weighing it against the explicit “Google-recommended practices” requirement, which favors a scoped IAM role grant on the existing attached service account over credential export or a broader, less-scoped identity.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Any identity writing log entries to Cloud Logging — a default service account, a custom service account, or an application client library — needs the Logs Writer IAM role on the relevant resource. This requirement is independent of network reachability or whether agents are installed and running: an agent that can reach the Cloud Logging API endpoint will still fail to write entries if the identity it authenticates as lacks this specific role, which is why confirming the agent is running doesn’t rule out an authorization problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team provisions Compute Engine instances with purpose-built, minimally scoped service accounts rather than the broad default service account, and as part of their standard instance template explicitly grants each custom service account the Logs Writer role so every instance’s logs reliably reach Cloud Logging. When a new application’s logs don’t show up despite the agent running correctly, checking IAM role assignments on the attached service account is the first and most direct place to look, rather than suspecting agent configuration or network routing.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Agent running, public IP present, no logs appearing → check the service account’s IAM roles first; grant Logs Writer rather than exporting keys, switching to the default service account, or touching network access.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a Compute Engine instance’s logging agent is confirmed running but logs never reach Cloud Logging, suspect a missing Logs Writer IAM role on the attached service account before looking at network configuration or reaching for a service account key — especially when the service account is user-managed, since it won’t carry that role automatically.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/agent/ops-agent/authorization\">Google Cloud — Authorize the Ops Agent: </a>Supports: authorizing the agent via an attached service account granted the Logs Writer role as the primary method, with service account keys documented as a secondary alternative</p>","lessons":["d4l5","d4l13"]},"e4q26":{"id":"e4q26","exam":4,"n":26,"domain":1,"html":"<p>You are designing a platform with three environments — development, quality assurance (QA), and production. Each environment is provisioned with Terraform and includes its own Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster where application teams deploy their workloads. Anthos Config Management, using templated Kustomize manifests, deploys infrastructure-level resources into each GKE cluster. Every user — infrastructure operators and application owners alike — works through GitOps. How should you structure your source control repositories for both infrastructure as code (IaC) and application source code?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Cloud infrastructure (Terraform) repository is shared, with different directories representing different environments. GKE infrastructure (Anthos Config Management Kustomize manifests) repository is shared, with different overlay directories representing different environments. Application (app source code) repositories are separate per application, with different branches representing different features.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Cloud infrastructure (Terraform) repository is shared, with different directories representing different environments. GKE infrastructure (Anthos Config Management Kustomize manifests) repositories are separate, with different branches representing different environments. Application (app source code) repositories are separate per application, with different branches representing different features.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Cloud infrastructure (Terraform) repository is shared, with different branches representing different environments. GKE infrastructure (Anthos Config Management Kustomize manifests) repository is shared, with different overlay directories representing different environments. Application (app source code) repository is shared, with different directories representing different features.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Cloud infrastructure (Terraform) repositories are separate per environment, with different branches also representing different environments within each. GKE infrastructure (Anthos Config Management Kustomize manifests) repositories are separate, with different overlay directories representing different environments. Application (app source code) repositories are separate per application, with different branches representing different features.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Cloud infrastructure (Terraform) repository is shared, with different directories representing different environments. GKE infrastructure (Anthos Config Management Kustomize manifests) repository is shared, with different overlay directories representing different environments. Application (app source code) repositories are separate per application, with different branches representing different features.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The structure must support GitOps for every user, keep each tooling layer consistent with how that tool is actually designed to be used, and avoid double-encoding the same dimension in two places. Option D fragments both Terraform and ACM/Kustomize into separate repositories per environment while also layering branches or overlay directories for environment inside each already-environment-specific repository — a redundant, internally inconsistent double-encoding that undermines a single source of truth. Option C combines a legitimate branch-per-environment Terraform pattern with a single shared application-source repository using directories for features, which collapses every application team’s code into one repository and treats “feature” as a static folder. Option B structures ACM/Kustomize with separate repositories and branches per environment, fighting against Kustomize’s own base-and-overlay design, which already expresses environment differences through overlay directories in one repository. Option A keeps each infrastructure layer in a single shared repository with environments as directories/overlays — matching how Kustomize is built and a well-documented Terraform pattern — while giving each application team its own repository with standard feature branches.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“Anthos Config Management... templated to deploy infrastructure level resources” — points directly at Kustomize’s base-and-overlay design, where overlay directories, not branches or separate repos, are the standard way to express environment differences</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“application teams can deploy their applications” (plural teams) — signals application source code belongs to distinct teams, favoring separate per-team repositories over one shared application repository</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“All users... will use GitOps” — reinforces that each layer needs a single source of truth that changes promote through, which overlay directories and environment branches both support, but double-fragmenting a layer does not</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Three named environments (dev, QA, production) — frames “environment” as the dimension each infrastructure layer’s structure needs to cleanly represent</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Terraform and Anthos Config Management/Kustomize are both infrastructure layers best kept in shared repositories with environments expressed as directories (Terraform’s root configurations) or overlays (Kustomize’s purpose-built base-plus-overlay pattern), keeping one source of truth per layer and making promotion between environments a simple, reviewable diff between directories rather than a cross-repository or cross-branch operation. Application source code belongs to individual teams and follows ordinary software development practice, where features are developed on branches and merged back into a main line — not expressed as folders, and not mixed with other teams’ unrelated code in one repository. This combination matches both infrastructure-as-code structuring guidance and standard application development workflow at once.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Separate repositories with environment branches for ACM/Kustomize works against Kustomize’s own design, which already differentiates environments through overlay directories on a shared base within one repository; splitting both repository and branch by environment adds fragmentation with no corresponding benefit.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Branch-per-environment for the Terraform root configuration is a legitimate, documented pattern, but pairing it with a single shared application source repository where directories represent features breaks down once there are multiple distinct application teams — their codebases, release cadences, and ownership boundaries don’t fit naturally into folders of one shared repository.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Separating both Terraform and ACM/Kustomize repositories by environment and then also using branches or overlay directories for environment within each already-environment-specific repository encodes the same distinction twice, which is redundant and undermines the single-source-of-truth structure GitOps depends on.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming “more separation” is always safer or more organized — splitting repositories by environment feels thorough, but when a tool like Kustomize is explicitly designed around one shared base with environment-specific overlays in a single repository, adding repository- or branch-level environment separation on top just duplicates what overlays already do. A second trap is treating “shared repository” as always correct without checking ownership — infrastructure benefits from a shared repository with directory-based environment separation, but application source code belongs to individual teams and shouldn’t be collapsed into one shared repository just because the infrastructure layers are.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>GitOps repository structure should match the actual design and ownership model of what’s being managed: Kustomize’s base-and-overlay pattern is built for a single repository with per-environment overlay directories, Terraform root configurations are commonly structured with environment-specific directories within a shared repository (environment branches are a documented alternative for directly-deployed root configs), and application source code should follow standard team-based ownership with feature branches rather than being folded into shared infrastructure repositories.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team sets up one Terraform repository with dev, qa, and prod directories for foundational cloud infrastructure, and one Anthos Config Management repository with a base configuration and dev/qa/prod overlay directories for cluster-level resources, so promoting a change to production is a reviewable pull request that carries a directory’s state forward. Each application team keeps its own source repository and follows an ordinary feature-branch workflow, merging into main and letting the CI/CD pipeline — not a repository or branch per environment — handle deploying the resulting build to each environment’s GKE cluster.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Infrastructure layers (Terraform, Kustomize/ACM): one shared repo, directories/overlays for environments. Application code: one repo per app, branches for features — not the reverse.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a GitOps repository-structure question involves both infrastructure (Terraform, Kustomize) and application code, expect infrastructure to live in shared repositories with directory- or overlay-based environment separation, and application code to live in per-team repositories using ordinary feature branches — watch for options that double-encode environment separation or collapse multiple application teams into one shared repository.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/terraform/best-practices/version-control\">Google Cloud — Best practices for version control (Terraform): </a>Supports: directory-based component and environment organization, the default feature-branch workflow for application-style repositories, and environment branches as a documented alternative pattern for directly-deployed root configurations</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/docs/terraform/resource-management/managing-infrastructure-as-code\"><strong>Google Cloud — Manage infrastructure as code with Terraform, Cloud Build, and GitOps:</strong></a> Supports: the environments/ directory pattern (dev, prod subfolders) for expressing environment separation within a single shared Terraform repository</p>","lessons":["d1l6"]},"e4q27":{"id":"e4q27","exam":4,"n":27,"domain":1,"html":"<p>Your company runs applications on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), deployed using a GitOps methodology. Application developers frequently need to create cloud resources to support their applications, and you want to give them a way to manage infrastructure as code that follows Google-recommended practices. The solution also needs to reconcile the infrastructure periodically so configuration drift doesn’t go uncorrected. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Deploy and configure Config Connector inside the GKE cluster.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Set up Cloud Build with a Terraform builder step that runs terraform plan and terraform apply.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create a standalone Pod running a Terraform container image that executes terraform plan and terraform apply.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Create a Kubernetes Job running a Terraform container image that executes terraform plan and terraform apply.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Deploy and configure Config Connector inside the GKE cluster.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two constraints must both be satisfied: developers manage infrastructure as code following Google-recommended practices, and the infrastructure continuously reconciles on its own to correct drift, not just on a one-time or triggered basis. A Pod (C) runs its process once, with no built-in mechanism to re-run itself or detect drift afterward. A Job (D) is Kubernetes’ primitive for a single run-to-completion task and doesn’t repeat by design. Cloud Build with Terraform (B) runs on triggers, typically a Git push, producing a single apply per trigger rather than an ongoing reconciliation process; achieving periodicity would require additional scheduling the option doesn’t describe. Config Connector (A) is architected as a Kubernetes controller, and controllers are fundamentally built around a continuous reconciliation loop — Config Connector re-evaluates each managed resource’s actual state against its desired state on every update and on a recurring interval, automatically correcting drift as a built-in property of the tool itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“reconciles periodically to avoid configuration drift” — the decisive requirement, ruling out anything that only runs once or only runs in response to a Git push</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Create a Pod resource” / “Create a Job resource” — both name Kubernetes primitives that run to completion rather than continuously operate, signaling they lack built-in repetition</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Application developers frequently create cloud resources” — points toward a Kubernetes-native, CRD-based tool fitting naturally into a GitOps workflow developers already use</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“following a GitOps methodology” already in place — favors a tool whose resources are ordinary Kubernetes manifests that slot into the existing pipeline, rather than a separate build-triggered or batch mechanism</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Config Connector is a Kubernetes controller, built around a reconciliation loop by design: it continuously compares each managed resource’s declared state against the actual Google Cloud resource state, re-checking on every update and again on a recurring interval, and automatically correcting any detected drift. Because Config Connector resources are ordinary Kubernetes YAML, they fit directly into the GitOps pipeline already used for applications, letting developers manage infrastructure the same way they manage workloads, while the controller itself — not a scheduled job or pipeline trigger — guarantees the periodic reconciliation the scenario requires.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A Cloud Build Terraform pipeline is legitimate for managing infrastructure as code, but it executes on triggers, most commonly a Git push, producing a single terraform apply per trigger rather than an ongoing reconciliation process. Periodic execution would require separate scheduling, and even then each run is a discrete apply, not a continuously operating controller.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> A standalone Pod runs its container’s process once, or keeps a single process running without re-executing it; it has no native mechanism to periodically re-run terraform plan and apply or detect drift afterward, so it fails “reconciles periodically” entirely.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> A Kubernetes Job is specifically designed to run to completion exactly once and doesn’t repeat on a schedule by itself. At best it produces a single apply, with no periodic reconciliation unless a different resource type (a CronJob) were used — and even that wouldn’t provide the continuous drift detection a controller offers natively.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “run Terraform somewhere in Kubernetes” (Pod or Job) as equivalent to a reconciling controller, when neither primitive is designed to repeat or recheck state on its own — a Job in particular sounds like it might be scheduled or recurring, but Kubernetes Jobs are explicitly single-run resources. A second trap is assuming a CI/CD pipeline inherently provides ongoing reconciliation because it’s automated, when its automation is trigger-based rather than a continuously running state-comparison loop.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A reconciliation loop — continuously comparing a system’s actual state to its declared desired state and correcting differences — is the foundational pattern behind every Kubernetes controller, and it’s what distinguishes an operator like Config Connector from a one-shot execution mechanism like a Pod, a Job, or a triggered CI/CD pipeline step. Tools built on this pattern provide drift correction as an inherent property of simply running, rather than something separately scheduled or re-triggered.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team lets application developers request Cloud SQL instances, Pub/Sub topics, and storage buckets by committing Config Connector YAML manifests through the same GitOps pipeline and pull-request review process they already use for application deployments. If someone later changes a resource’s configuration directly in the Google Cloud console, Config Connector’s next reconciliation pass detects the mismatch against the manifest in Git and reverts it automatically, keeping Git as the enforced source of truth without the platform team building or maintaining any separate drift-detection mechanism.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Reconciles periodically” is a controller behavior, not a Pod/Job/pipeline behavior — Config Connector’s built-in reconciliation loop continuously corrects drift; Pods and Jobs run once, and Cloud Build runs on triggers.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a requirement specifically calls for infrastructure as code to reconcile periodically and self-correct drift, look for a Kubernetes-native controller like Config Connector rather than a one-time execution resource (Pod, Job) or a trigger-based CI/CD pipeline step, since only a controller’s reconciliation loop provides that ongoing behavior natively.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/config-connector/docs/concepts/reconciliation\"><strong>Google Cloud — Config Connector reconciliation:</strong></a> Supports: the controller’s reconciliation-loop behavior, re-checking resource state on every update and on a recurring interval to automatically correct drift</p>","lessons":["d1l7"]},"e4q28":{"id":"e4q28","exam":4,"n":28,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You are deploying an application to Cloud Run that needs a password to start up. Your organization mandates that every password be rotated every 24 hours, and the application must always be working with the current password. You need to achieve this without any downtime. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Store the password in Secret Manager and expose it to the application as an environment variable.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Store the password in Secret Manager and mount it as a volume inside the application’s container.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Have Cloud Build bake the password into the application container image at build time, and make sure Artifact Registry is locked down from public access.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Hard-code the password directly in the application’s source code, and use Cloud Build to rebuild and redeploy the application whenever the password changes.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Store the password in Secret Manager and mount it as a volume inside the application’s container.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Three things must hold: the application must always have the current, 24-hour-rotated password, there must be no downtime, and the approach should follow sound secrets-handling practice. Options C and D both bake the password into something static — a container image or the source code itself — meaning every rotation requires a full rebuild-and-redeploy cycle, and D additionally hardcodes a secret in source control, a serious anti-pattern on its own. Option A exposes the secret as an environment variable, which Cloud Run resolves only once, at instance startup; already-running instances keep serving the old password until replaced by a new deployment. Option B mounts the secret as a volume, and Cloud Run reads volume-mounted secrets directly from Secret Manager on each access rather than caching them at startup, so when the secret rotates, currently running instances pick up the new value the next time they read the file — no redeploy, no downtime.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“your application must have the latest password” — a hard constraint ruling out any mechanism resolved only once at startup, since that leaves running instances holding a stale value after rotation</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“rotated every 24 hours” — establishes rotation as a recurring, ongoing event the solution must handle automatically</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“deploy the application with no downtime” — rules out any approach requiring a rebuild-and-redeploy cycle every time the password changes, reintroducing deployment risk every 24 hours</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“requires a password to start” paired with secret rotation — points toward Cloud Run’s two secret-delivery mechanisms (environment variable vs. volume) as the real decision point</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Run reads a volume-mounted secret directly from Secret Manager each time it’s accessed, rather than resolving it once when the instance starts, which is why this method is documented as working well with secret rotation. When the password rotates in Secret Manager, the application’s already-running Cloud Run instances read the new value the next time they access the mounted file — no new revision, no redeploy, no downtime — satisfying both “latest password” and “no downtime” at once.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Cloud Run resolves environment-variable secrets once, at instance startup, and Google explicitly recommends pinning environment-variable secrets to a specific version rather than “latest” for exactly this reason. After a rotation, already-running instances keep the old password until replaced by a new deployment — an additional step the scenario doesn’t call for.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Baking the password into the container image at build time makes it a fixed part of that image; picking up a rotated password requires rebuilding and redeploying the container every 24 hours. It also embeds a secret directly in a container image, a weaker practice than referencing it from Secret Manager at runtime, regardless of how well Artifact Registry access is restricted.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Hardcoding the password in source code is a severe security anti-pattern on its own — exposed in version control history to anyone with repository access — and compounds the redeployment problem, since every rotation requires a code change, rebuild, and redeploy.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “store the password in Secret Manager” as the whole answer and overlooking that Cloud Run offers two delivery mechanisms with meaningfully different rotation behavior — an environment variable looks like the simpler, more familiar choice, but it’s resolved only at startup and won’t reflect a rotated secret in already-running instances. The decisive detail is “must have the latest password” combined with a 24-hour rotation cadence, which specifically favors the mechanism Cloud Run reads live rather than the one resolved once at startup.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Run offers two ways to deliver a Secret Manager secret to a container: as an environment variable, resolved once when the instance starts and fixed for that instance’s lifetime, or as a mounted volume, read directly from Secret Manager on each access. A secret that rotates on a recurring schedule needs the volume-mount approach so running instances stay current without requiring a new deployment every time the value changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team running a Cloud Run service against a database that rotates its credentials daily mounts the database password as a Secret Manager volume rather than an environment variable, and configures the application to read the password file on each connection attempt rather than caching it once at startup. When the credential rotates at midnight, every running instance seamlessly picks up the new password on its next database connection, with no coordinated redeploy and no window where the service is unavailable or authenticating with an outdated credential.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Secret rotates, app must always have the latest value, no downtime → mount as a Secret Manager volume (read live on each access); environment variables are resolved once at startup and go stale after rotation.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a Cloud Run application needs a Secret Manager value that rotates and must always reflect the current version without redeploying, mount the secret as a volume rather than exposing it as an environment variable — volumes are read live from Secret Manager on each access, while environment variables are fixed for the life of the instance.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/configuring/secrets\"><strong>Google Cloud — Configure secrets for services (Cloud Run):</strong></a> Supports: volume-mounted secrets reading live from Secret Manager (“works well with secret rotation”) versus environment-variable secrets being resolved only once, at instance startup</p>","lessons":["d2l8"]},"e4q29":{"id":"e4q29","exam":4,"n":29,"domain":5,"html":"<p>As a Site Reliability Engineer, you support a Go application running in production on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). After a new release, you notice the application runs for roughly 15 minutes before restarting. You add Cloud Profiler to the application and observe that heap usage climbs steadily until each restart. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Raise the CPU limit configured on the application’s deployment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Add nodes with more memory to the cluster’s node pool.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Raise the memory limit configured on the application’s deployment.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Instrument the application with Cloud Trace, and redeploy it.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Raise the memory limit configured on the application’s deployment.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The fix must address the memory-growth-then-restart pattern Cloud Profiler has already confirmed, and it must actually change what determines whether the container gets OOMKilled. Option A targets CPU, a different resource than the one Profiler identified as the problem, so it can’t affect a memory-driven restart. Option D adds Cloud Trace, a distributed-tracing tool for request latency and call paths, not a memory-diagnostic tool — Profiler has already supplied the relevant heap data, and Trace adds nothing toward solving a memory problem. Option B adds nodes with more physical memory, but a container’s OOMKill is governed by its own configured memory limit, not by how much memory is available on the node it runs on — more node capacity doesn’t change when that specific container gets killed. Option C directly raises the limit the container is actually measured against, giving the application room to run without hitting that ceiling — the mechanism that determines restart behavior.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“heap usage grows constantly until the application restarts” — identifies this specifically as a memory-consumption problem, ruling out any fix targeting a different resource (CPU)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“runs for about 15 minutes and then restarts” — a classic OOMKill/crash-loop pattern, pointing toward resource limits as the mechanism to examine</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“add Cloud Profiler... and now notice” — establishes profiling has already successfully diagnosed the issue, so the next step should act on that finding rather than add a different, unrelated diagnostic tool</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“application deployment” appears in two of the four options — signals the decision is about which deployment-level resource setting to change, not whether to change one at all</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Profiler’s heap data has already identified memory, not CPU, as the resource driving the restarts, and in Kubernetes a container is terminated with an out-of-memory kill specifically when it exceeds the memory limit configured for that container in its deployment spec — regardless of how much memory the underlying node has available. Raising the memory limit directly changes that ceiling, giving the application room to operate without triggering a restart, targeting the exact mechanism responsible for the crash-loop behavior Profiler has already diagnosed.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Profiler specifically showed heap (memory) usage climbing, not CPU usage. Raising the CPU limit changes a resource unrelated to the diagnosed problem and would have no effect on when the container gets OOMKilled.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A container’s OOM kill is triggered by its own configured memory limit, not the physical memory available on its node. Adding higher-memory nodes increases cluster capacity in general, but without also raising the container’s own memory limit, that same container is still killed at the same threshold regardless of how much memory the surrounding node has.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Cloud Trace captures distributed request latency and call-path data, not memory allocation information, so it contributes nothing new toward diagnosing or resolving a heap-growth problem Cloud Profiler has already clearly identified. Redeploying with an unrelated diagnostic tool also doesn’t address the restarts themselves.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “add more resources somewhere in the cluster” (B) as equivalent to “fix the container’s resource limit” (C) — they sound similar but operate at different layers: node capacity versus the cgroup-enforced limit on the individual container, and only the latter determines whether that specific container gets OOMKilled. A second trap is reaching for a familiar observability tool (Cloud Trace) just because the scenario already involves observability, without checking whether that specific tool measures the resource in question; Trace addresses latency and request flow, not memory.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>In Kubernetes, a container’s memory limit is enforced independently of the node’s total memory capacity: the container is killed once its own usage crosses its configured limit, even if the node still has free memory elsewhere, and adding memory to the node doesn’t raise that container-specific ceiling. This is why diagnosing a memory-related restart requires checking not just whether there’s “enough memory in the cluster,” but specifically whether the container’s own configured limit is appropriate for how much memory the application actually needs.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>An SRE investigating a Go service that restarts every 15 minutes adds Cloud Profiler, confirms the heap grows continuously rather than plateauing, and raises the container’s memory limit in the deployment manifest so it has enough headroom to run without hitting its ceiling — buying stability immediately while a parallel workstream uses the same profiler data to track down and fix whatever code change in the new release caused the excess allocation in the first place.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Profiler shows memory, not CPU, climbing until restart → raise the container’s own memory limit; more node memory or a tracing tool doesn’t touch the limit that’s actually triggering the kill.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When profiling data shows a container’s heap climbing until an OOM-style restart, fix the resource limit Kubernetes actually enforces against that container — its configured memory limit — rather than adding node-level capacity (which doesn’t change the container’s own ceiling) or reaching for a tool, like Cloud Trace, that measures a different kind of problem entirely.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/troubleshooting/introduction-example\">Google Cloud — Put it all together: Example GKE troubleshooting scenario: </a>Supports: the exact diagnostic pattern of climbing container memory usage leading to an OOMKilled restart, and raising the container’s configured memory limit as a documented response</p>","lessons":["d5l8"]},"e4q30":{"id":"e4q30","exam":4,"n":30,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You are building a CI/CD pipeline natively on Google Cloud, and you want every build deployed to a pre-production Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) environment to be automatically load-tested before it can be promoted to production. Only builds that pass this test should ever reach production, and you want to follow Google-recommended practices for doing this with Binary Authorization. How should you configure the pipeline?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Have the lead quality assurance engineer manually sign the attestation for each passing build, using their own personal private key.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Have the pipeline sign the attestation for each passing build with a private key stored in Cloud Key Management Service (Cloud KMS), authenticating with a service account JSON key stored as a Kubernetes Secret.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Have the pipeline sign the attestation for each passing build with a private key stored in Cloud Key Management Service (Cloud KMS), authenticating through Workload Identity.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Have the lead quality assurance engineer manually sign the attestation for each passing build, using a key stored in Cloud Key Management Service (Cloud KMS).</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Have the pipeline sign the attestation for each passing build with a private key stored in Cloud Key Management Service (Cloud KMS), authenticating through Workload Identity.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two things must both hold: the attestation must be created automatically once a build passes the load test, with no manual human gate for every build, and the signing key and its authentication method must follow Google-recommended security practices. Options A and D both require the lead QA engineer to personally sign every attestation, turning an automated pipeline step into a manual approval bottleneck — contradicting the premise that builds are automatically tested and promoted based on that result. A additionally uses a personal private key rather than any centrally managed key, compounding the problem. Option B keeps the signing key in Cloud KMS correctly, but authenticates to it with an exported service account JSON key stored as a Kubernetes Secret — a long-lived credential Google explicitly recommends avoiding when a keyless alternative exists. Option C uses Cloud KMS for the signing key and Workload Identity for authentication, letting the pipeline’s identity securely assume a Google Cloud service account without exported key material, fully automating attestation creation while following Google’s recommended practices.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“automatically load-tested before being promoted” — establishes that the gate is based on an automated test result, which should flow directly into an automated attestation step rather than requiring human sign-off</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“only builds that have passed this test are deployed to production” — frames the attestation as the automated proof that gates promotion, not a separate manual approval layered on top</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“personal private key” versus “a key stored in Cloud Key Management Service” — a direct signal distinguishing an anti-pattern key-management choice from a properly centralized one</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“service account JSON key stored as a Kubernetes Secret” versus “authenticated through Workload Identity” — the precise detail distinguishing the two Cloud-KMS-based options, determining which follows Google’s recommended keyless authentication practice</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A fully automated Binary Authorization pipeline has the CI/CD system itself create and sign the attestation as soon as a build passes its load test, using a signing key securely held in Cloud KMS rather than a locally managed or personal key. For a pipeline component running in GKE to authenticate to Cloud KMS and use that key, Workload Identity lets it assume a Google Cloud service account’s permissions without ever exporting or storing a long-lived JSON key — the Google-recommended way to grant Google Cloud API access to GKE-based workloads. Cloud KMS for the key plus Workload Identity for authentication keeps the entire attestation step automated and keyless.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Requiring the lead QA engineer to manually sign every attestation turns an automated gate into a human bottleneck. Signing with a personal private key compounds the problem, since personal keys aren’t centrally managed, rotated, or revocable the way a Cloud KMS key is.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Storing the signing key in Cloud KMS is correct, but authenticating to it with an exported service account JSON key stored as a Kubernetes Secret introduces a long-lived credential that must be stored, rotated, and protected manually — exactly the risk Workload Identity is designed to eliminate when it’s available, as it is here.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Moving the signing key into Cloud KMS improves on Option A, but the attestation still depends on the lead QA engineer manually signing every passing build, keeping a human approval step in what should be a fully automated promotion gate driven by the load test’s result.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “the key is now in Cloud KMS” as sufficient to make an option correct, without separately checking whether the attestation process is actually automated and whether the authentication method avoids exported credentials. Option D shows this clearly — a properly managed key paired with a manual human signer is still wrong, because the automation requirement is just as binding as the key-management requirement, and both must be satisfied together.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An automated Binary Authorization pipeline needs two things working together: a securely managed signing key, typically held in Cloud KMS rather than with any individual or embedded in long-lived files, and a way for the pipeline’s own identity to authenticate to that key without relying on exported credentials. Workload Identity is the mechanism that lets a workload running in GKE securely assume a Google Cloud service account’s permissions for exactly this purpose, completing the fully automated, keyless chain from “test passed” to “attestation created” to “deployment permitted.”</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team wires their CI/CD pipeline so that, immediately after a build passes its automated load test in the pre-production GKE cluster, a pipeline step running under Workload Identity signs an attestation using a Cloud KMS key, and Binary Authorization’s production policy only admits images carrying that attestation. No person reviews individual builds, no service account key ever needs to be generated or rotated, and a bad build simply never accumulates the attestation it needs to reach production.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Automated gate + Google-recommended key practice → Cloud KMS for the signing key, Workload Identity for authentication; a human signer (personal key or not) breaks automation, and a service account JSON key breaks the keyless-authentication practice.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a Binary Authorization question asks how to attest builds automatically based on a passed test, look for the option pairing a centrally managed key (Cloud KMS) with keyless authentication (Workload Identity) — eliminate anything that inserts a manual human signer, and eliminate anything that authenticates with an exported service account key when Workload Identity is available.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs/making-attestations\"><strong>Google Cloud — Create attestations (Binary Authorization):</strong></a> Supports: Cloud KMS as the recommended store for the attestation signing key, used by an automated pipeline step rather than a manual signer</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/workload-identity\">Google Cloud — About Workload Identity Federation for GKE: </a>Supports: Workload Identity Federation being the recommended way for GKE workloads to authenticate to Google Cloud services, in place of service account keys stored as Kubernetes Secrets</p>","lessons":["d2l2","d2l8","d2l9"]},"e4q31":{"id":"e4q31","exam":4,"n":31,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You are the Operations Lead handling an ongoing incident on one of your services. The service typically operates at around 70% capacity, and you’ve noticed that one node is returning 5xx errors on every request it receives, along with a noticeable rise in customer support cases. You need to pull the offending node out of the load balancer pool so you can isolate and investigate it, while following Google-recommended practices to manage the incident and limit the impact on users. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A.</p>\n<p>Communicate your intent to the incident team.</p>\n<p>Perform a load analysis to determine whether the remaining nodes can absorb the traffic that would be offloaded from the removed node, and scale the pool if the analysis shows it’s needed.</p>\n<p>Once any new nodes report healthy, drain traffic from the unhealthy node and remove it from service.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B.</p>\n<p>Communicate your intent to the incident team.</p>\n<p>Add a new node to the pool, and wait for it to report as healthy.</p>\n<p>Once traffic is being served from the new node, drain traffic from the unhealthy node and remove the old node from service.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C.</p>\n<p>Drain traffic from the unhealthy node and remove it from service.</p>\n<p>Monitor traffic to confirm the error is resolved and that the remaining nodes in the pool are handling the load appropriately.</p>\n<p>Scale the pool as necessary to handle the new load.</p>\n<p>Communicate your actions to the incident team.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D.</p>\n<p>Drain traffic from the unhealthy node and remove the old node from service.</p>\n<p>Add a new node to the pool, wait for it to report as healthy, and begin serving traffic from it.</p>\n<p>Monitor traffic to confirm the pool is healthy and handling traffic appropriately.</p>\n<p>Communicate your actions to the incident team.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>Communicate your intent to the incident team.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Perform a load analysis to determine whether the remaining nodes can absorb the traffic that would be offloaded from the removed node, and scale the pool if the analysis shows it’s needed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Once any new nodes report healthy, drain traffic from the unhealthy node and remove it from service.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Three things must hold: the incident team stays informed as part of coordinated response rather than finding out after the fact; removing the unhealthy node must not leave the remaining pool unable to absorb its traffic, since the service already runs at a substantial 70% baseline; and the node should be removed gracefully, by draining it, rather than abruptly. Options C and D both act first and communicate last, running against coordinated incident response, and both remove the unhealthy node before confirming sufficient remaining capacity, risking a cascading overload in the window before new capacity is added. Option B communicates first and ensures a new node is healthy before removing the bad one, protecting capacity, but it unconditionally adds a new node without first checking whether the existing 30% of spare capacity is actually enough — less analysis-driven than evaluating first. Option A communicates first, performs a load analysis to determine whether scaling is actually needed given the service’s normal 70% utilization, scales only if that analysis shows a genuine gap, and only then drains and removes the unhealthy node — satisfying all three requirements with the most measured, analysis-driven approach.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“The service usually runs at around 70% capacity” — a deliberately given detail establishing headroom in the system, prompting an analysis of whether that headroom is sufficient rather than an automatic decision to add capacity</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“follow Google-recommended practices to manage the incident” — points toward structured incident response, including communication to the incident team as coordination happens, not as a final report</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“remove the offending node... so that you can isolate and investigate” — frames node removal as the goal, but one that still needs to happen safely, which is why the order of operations matters</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Communication appearing as either the first or last step across the four options — a clear signal that when communication happens is one of the things being tested</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Communicating intent to the incident team first keeps everyone coordinated before a production-affecting action is taken, core to structured incident response. Performing a load analysis before deciding whether to scale directly uses the fact that the service normally runs at 70% capacity — rather than assuming new capacity is automatically required, the analysis determines whether existing headroom is enough to absorb the unhealthy node’s traffic, scaling only if it isn’t. Waiting until any new nodes report healthy before draining and removing the unhealthy node ensures the pool never dips below the capacity needed to serve current demand, and draining lets in-flight requests complete safely rather than being abruptly cut off.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Communicating first and ensuring a new node is healthy before removing the bad one protects capacity, but it skips the load analysis step and always adds a new node regardless of whether the service’s existing spare capacity would have been sufficient on its own — a less deliberate response than evaluating first.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Removing the unhealthy node before confirming the remaining nodes can handle the offloaded traffic risks overloading the pool during the gap before scaling happens reactively in step 3, and communicating to the incident team only as the final step means coordination happens after the mitigating action has already been taken.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> This shares Option C’s core problems — the unhealthy node is removed before any replacement capacity exists, creating a window where the pool may be under-provisioned, and communication again comes last, after the node has already been pulled and a new one added.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “remove the bad node quickly” as the priority, when the real priority is removing it <em>safely</em> , without creating a second, broader problem by overloading the remaining capacity — Options C and D both act first and reason about capacity afterward, reversing the safer order. A second trap is assuming any option that adds a new node before removing the old one is equally good; Option B does this unconditionally, while the specifically called-out 70% baseline capacity detail rewards recognizing that a load analysis might show scaling isn’t even necessary, which only Option A captures.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Mitigating an incident that involves removing a capacity-serving component from production should follow a sequence that protects users throughout: communicate with the incident team as actions are planned and taken, assess whether remaining capacity is sufficient using the real utilization data already known about the system, scale only if that assessment shows a genuine gap, and perform the actual removal gracefully only once any necessary additional capacity is confirmed healthy. This ordering keeps both the team and the user-facing system in a known-good state at every step.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>An on-call engineer managing a node reporting 5xx errors on all requests tells the incident channel what they’re about to do, checks current utilization against the service’s normal 70% baseline, confirms the remaining nodes have enough headroom to absorb the bad node’s share of traffic without needing to scale, and then drains and removes the unhealthy node — all while the incident team has visibility into each step as it happens, and users experience a smooth transition rather than a second wave of errors from an overloaded remaining pool.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Communicate first, analyze capacity before acting, scale only if the analysis shows it’s needed, then drain and remove — never remove the bad node before confirming the rest of the pool can handle the load, and never save communication for last.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question sequences incident mitigation steps around removing a capacity-serving node, look for the option that communicates to the incident team first, performs a capacity/load analysis before deciding whether to scale, and only drains and removes the unhealthy node once sufficient capacity is confirmed — options that act before analyzing or communicate only after acting are the wrong order.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/incident-response/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook — Chapter 9: Incident Response:</strong></a> Supports: the “3Cs” of incident management (coordinate, communicate, control) that make early communication with the incident team a core part of a well-run mitigation, rather than a final report after action has already been taken</p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e4q32":{"id":"e4q32","exam":4,"n":32,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You are developing reusable infrastructure as code modules. Each module includes integration tests that launch the module in a test project, and your source control is GitHub. You need to test your feature branches continuously and make sure all code is tested before any change is accepted. You need to automate the integration tests. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use a Jenkins server for your CI/CD pipelines, and run all of the tests on the feature branch on a periodic schedule.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Have the pull request reviewers run the integration tests before they approve the code.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use Cloud Build to run the tests, and trigger the full set of tests after a pull request has been merged.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Use Cloud Build to run the tests in a specific folder, and trigger a Cloud Build run for every GitHub pull request.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Use Cloud Build to run the tests in a specific folder, and trigger a Cloud Build run for every GitHub pull request.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Three requirements must hold together. The tests must run automatically, each change must be tested continuously as it is proposed, and the tests must complete before a change is accepted. That makes the pull request the right point to run them, because it sits between the feature branch and the code being accepted. Option D ties an automated Cloud Build run to every pull request, so no change reaches review untested. The other options fail one requirement each: they run on a schedule, depend on a person, or run only after the change has already been accepted.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“automate the integration tests” is a hard constraint that rules out any option relying on people to run them</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“continuously test your feature branch” is a hard constraint that rules out scheduled or occasional runs</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“ensure that all code is tested before changes are accepted” is a hard constraint on timing: the tests must be a gate before merge, not a check afterward</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Each module contains integration tests” is a technical signal that tests are organized per module, which suits tests scoped to a specific folder</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“You are using GitHub for source control” is a technical signal that pull request events can start the pipeline</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Build triggers can start a build in response to events on a GitHub repository, including commits to a pull request. A pull request trigger therefore runs the integration tests automatically whenever a change is proposed or updated, and the build result is shown on the pull request so reviewers see it before approving. Because each module keeps its tests in its own folder, the build can run the tests in that folder. A trigger can also be limited with an included-files filter so it starts only when matching files change, which keeps runs focused on the affected module. One prerequisite applies: any shared files that modules depend on must also be covered by the filters, or a change to them could skip the tests it affects.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Running tests on a schedule is not continuous testing of every change, so a change could be reviewed or merged between runs without having been tested. It also means operating and maintaining a Jenkins server when the scenario is already built around GitHub and Google Cloud, which adds management effort without meeting the requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> Asking reviewers to run the tests by hand is not automation. It also depends on every reviewer running the full tests every time, so coverage is inconsistent and slow, and it makes a human step part of the gate.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Cloud Build is the right tool, but triggering the tests after the pull request is merged means the change has already been accepted when the tests run. A failure then lands on the main branch instead of blocking the change, which defeats the requirement that code be tested before it is accepted.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is option C, because it uses the right service and sounds thorough by running all tests. The failure is timing: a post-merge run reports problems after acceptance and can’t act as a gate. Option A is a second trap, since Jenkins is a legitimate CI server, but the option’s periodic schedule is what breaks it. Post-merge builds are still useful for deploying or running a final regression on the main branch, so the answer would favor a post-merge trigger only if the requirement were about what happens after acceptance.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Continuous integration gates change by running automated tests against each proposed change before it is merged, with results reported on the pull request, so only passing code reaches the main branch. In a repository that holds several independent modules, scoping tests to the changed module’s folder keeps runs short. That matters here because infrastructure integration tests create real resources in a test project, so they are slower and cost money. Because these builds run code against a real project, Cloud Build’s comment control on pull request triggers is relevant: builds from external contributors can be set to run only after an owner or collaborator comments /gcbrun.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team that maintains a repository of Terraform modules creates a Cloud Build trigger for each module folder, set to run on pull requests and filtered to that folder’s files. When an engineer opens a pull request that changes one module, Cloud Build runs that module’s integration tests in the test project and reports the result on the pull request. Reviewers see a passing or failing result before they approve, and broken changes never reach the main branch.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>To test every change before it is accepted, trigger Cloud Build on each pull request and scope the build to the folder being changed.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks how to automatically test changes before they are accepted in GitHub, look for a pull request trigger, not a scheduled job, a manual step, or a post-merge build. Scoping the test run to the changed folder keeps it efficient for repositories that contain several modules.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/run-builds-on-github\"><strong>Google Cloud Build — Building repositories from GitHub:</strong></a> Supports: triggers that run builds in response to GitHub events such as pull requests, comment control for pull request builds, and build results being reported on GitHub</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/build/docs/api/reference/rest/v1/projects.locations.triggers\"><strong>Google Cloud Build — REST Resource: projects.locations.triggers:</strong></a> Supports: the pull request event filter on triggers and the includedFiles and ignoredFiles filters that limit a trigger to changes in specific paths</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\">Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification exam guide (Section 2.1, designing pipelines: CI/CD pipeline triggers; continuous testing): </a>Supports: domain and objective alignment</p>","lessons":["d2l1","d2l3","d2l10"]},"e4q33":{"id":"e4q33","exam":4,"n":33,"domain":1,"html":"<p>You need to enforce several constraint templates across your Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) clusters, with policy parameters that include restrictions on the Kubernetes API. The policy parameters must live in a GitHub repository and be applied automatically whenever that repository changes. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Configure a GitHub Action that triggers Cloud Build whenever a parameter changes, and have Cloud Build run a gcloud CLI command to apply the change.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Configure a webhook that fires on GitHub changes and sends a request to Anthos Service Mesh to apply the change.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Configure Anthos Config Management to sync with the GitHub repository, so it automatically applies the change whenever the repository is updated.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Configure Config Connector to sync with the GitHub repository, so it automatically applies the change whenever the repository is updated.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Configure Anthos Config Management to sync with the GitHub repository, so it automatically applies the change whenever the repository is updated.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The solution must enforce Kubernetes-level policy constraint templates, keep parameters in a Git repository, and apply changes automatically with no manual steps. Policy Controller, the Anthos Config Management component built for exactly this, lets you define and parameterize constraint templates — such as ones restricting Kubernetes API usage — as Kubernetes custom resources, while Config Sync continuously syncs those resources from a Git repository to registered clusters, applying changes automatically as the repository updates. Config Connector (D) is architecturally built to manage Google Cloud resources, not Kubernetes-level policy constraints, so it’s the wrong tool for this object type regardless of being wired to a repository. Anthos Service Mesh (B) is a traffic-management product with no role in applying Kubernetes policy objects. A custom Cloud Build pipeline (A) could technically apply changes via gcloud/kubectl, but means building and maintaining a bespoke sync mechanism rather than using the Google-managed tool designed for this. Anthos Config Management (C) satisfies every part of the requirement as a single, purpose-built solution.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“enforce several constraint templates... policy parameters, such as restricting the Kubernetes API” — specifically names the Policy Controller/Gatekeeper constraint-template pattern, pointing directly at ACM’s policy-enforcement component</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“stored in a GitHub repository and automatically applied when changes occur” — describes a continuous GitOps sync pattern, Config Sync’s defining function</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“across your... GKE clusters” (plural) — reinforces a tool built for consistently applying the same policy set across a fleet from one source of truth, not a one-off scripted pipeline</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Four very different tool categories across the options (CI/CD pipeline, service mesh, config/policy management, cloud-resource management) — signals this is a “match the capability to the tool” question</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Anthos Config Management combines Policy Controller, which lets you define parameterized constraint templates — including ones restricting Kubernetes API usage — as Kubernetes custom resources, with Config Sync, which continuously reads configuration from a connected Git repository and applies it across registered clusters as a single source of truth. Pointing Config Sync at the GitHub repository means any committed change to the constraint parameters is automatically detected and applied to every configured cluster, with no custom pipeline logic and no manual steps.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A GitHub Action triggering Cloud Build to run a gcloud command could eventually apply the same changes, but requires designing, securing, and maintaining a custom sync pipeline, duplicating functionality Anthos Config Management already provides as a managed, purpose-built capability.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> Anthos Service Mesh manages traffic routing, mutual TLS, and observability between services; it has no mechanism for applying Kubernetes constraint templates or policy objects, so routing a webhook to it accomplishes nothing related to the described policies.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Different Use Case:</strong> Config Connector’s custom resources map to Google Cloud resources like databases and storage buckets, not Kubernetes policy constraint templates. It isn’t built to enforce API restrictions or other cluster-level policy constraints, and syncing it to a Git repository doesn’t change what kind of objects it manages.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is seeing “Kubernetes CRDs synced from a Git repo” and reaching for Config Connector, since it sounds structurally similar, without checking that Config Connector’s CRDs represent Google Cloud infrastructure resources, not Kubernetes policy constraints. The actual tool for constraint templates and policy parameters is Policy Controller, bundled with Config Sync inside Anthos Config Management — a distinction the question tests by pairing two superficially similar “GitHub repo plus GKE-adjacent tool” options that solve different problems.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Anthos Config Management is Google’s combined configuration-and-policy management service for Kubernetes: Config Sync continuously applies configuration from a Git repository as a single source of truth across one or many clusters, while Policy Controller defines and enforces constraint templates and their parameters, built on the open source Gatekeeper project. Together they implement a GitOps workflow specifically for Kubernetes configuration and policy, distinct from Config Connector’s separate role of managing Google Cloud resources through Kubernetes custom resources.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team maintains a Git repository containing Policy Controller constraint templates that restrict which Kubernetes API groups and verbs are allowed across the fleet, and configures Config Sync on every GKE cluster to pull from that repository. When a security engineer tightens a policy parameter and merges the change, every connected cluster picks up and enforces the new restriction automatically, with no per-cluster manual application and a clear, auditable Git history of every policy change over time.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Constraint templates and policy parameters, synced automatically from Git, across many GKE clusters → Anthos Config Management (Policy Controller + Config Sync); Config Connector manages Google Cloud resources, not Kubernetes policy constraints.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question describes enforcing Kubernetes constraint templates or policy parameters from a Git repository across GKE clusters, that’s Anthos Config Management’s Policy Controller and Config Sync — don’t confuse it with Config Connector (Google Cloud resource management), Anthos Service Mesh (traffic management), or a custom CI/CD pipeline built to replicate functionality Google already provides natively.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/anthos-config-management/docs/config-sync-overview\">Google Cloud — Config Sync overview: </a>Supports: Config Sync applying Policy Controller constraints from a Git repository consistently across registered clusters</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/anthos-config-management/docs/reference/gcloud-apply-fields\"><strong>Google Cloud — gcloud apply fields reference (Anthos Config Management):</strong></a> Supports: the configSync.syncRepo and policyController configuration fields used to wire a Git repository to automatic constraint-template enforcement</p>","lessons":["d1l7"]},"e4q34":{"id":"e4q34","exam":4,"n":34,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You have just noticed that one of your services has used up its error budget for the current rolling window. Meanwhile, your company’s product team is about to launch a new feature. You want to follow Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) practices. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Tell the team there is no error budget left, and make sure all of their tests pass so the launch doesn’t put any further strain on the budget.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Tell the team their error budget is used up. Negotiate with them between a launch freeze and accepting a somewhat worse user experience.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Escalate the situation and ask for additional error budget.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Search the product’s other metrics for SLOs that still have error budget left, reallocate that budget, and let the feature launch proceed.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Tell the team their error budget is used up. Negotiate with them between a launch freeze and accepting a somewhat worse user experience.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The error budget exists to give product and SRE teams a shared, data-driven way to trade reliability against feature velocity. Once the budget is spent, the data says reliability should take priority, and the decision about releases is made transparently with the team that wants to launch. Option B does this. It surfaces the fact that the budget is exhausted and puts the actual trade-off on the table: freeze the launch, or knowingly accept a degraded user experience. Options A, C, and D each try to get around the signal instead of acting on it. A assumes passing tests protect the budget, but tests do not stop production errors. C treats the budget as something to request more of, which defeats its purpose. D moves budget between unrelated SLOs, which does not reflect the reliability of the service that has actually exceeded its budget.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“exceeded the error budget for the current rolling window” is the trigger condition that the SRE error budget policy exists to handle</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“You want to follow Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) practices” points to the error budget policy and its release-versus-reliability trade-off, not to workarounds</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“about to launch a new feature” sets up the tension between velocity and reliability that error budgets are designed to arbitrate</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The options that reallocate or request more budget (C, D) treat the budget as negotiable arithmetic, which should stand out as incompatible with how SLOs work</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Google’s example error budget policy states that when a service has exceeded its error budget for the preceding four-week window, all changes and releases other than P0 issues or security fixes are halted until the service is back within its SLO. The same policy says it is not meant as a punishment, and that it gives teams permission to focus on reliability when the data indicates it matters more than new features. Notifying the product team and negotiating between a launch freeze and tolerating a slightly worse user experience follows this approach. It treats the exhausted budget as a decision point for the whole organization, to be resolved openly with the team that owns the launch, rather than as something to hide, bypass, or inflate.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A: Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> Passing tests is good practice, but it does not address the situation. The service has already overspent its budget, and tests cannot guarantee that a launch won’t add production errors. It also leaves the trade-off implicit and doesn’t give the product team the information needed to decide about the launch.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C: Violates Requirement.</strong> An error budget is derived directly from the SLO, which is 1 minus the target, so it is not something that can be granted on request. Asking for more budget turns the budget from a decision-making tool into a negotiable allowance and removes the signal that reliability needs attention.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D: Violates Requirement.</strong> Each SLO measures a different aspect of user experience, so spare budget on one SLO does not offset overspending on another. Reallocating budget hides the fact that the affected service is unreliable and lets the launch go ahead while users are already seeing degraded reliability on that dimension.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is choosing an option that sounds cooperative, such as being helpful by ensuring the launch is safe (A), finding more budget (C), or reallocating it (D). All three let the launch proceed without confronting the exhausted budget. The correct answer is the one that surfaces the exhausted budget to the product team and has them decide between a freeze and a worse user experience, because the error budget is meant to inform that decision, not be engineered around. The option wording is also a trap: “tolerate a slightly worse user experience” can look like a poor choice, but it is the honest alternative to a freeze that the policy expects teams to weigh.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An error budget is the allowed amount of unreliability implied by an SLO, calculated as 100% minus the SLO target. Error budgets give product and SRE teams a shared way to balance reliability against feature velocity. While budget remains, teams can take risks and release features. When it is exhausted, an error budget policy agreed in advance by the stakeholders governs what happens next, typically halting non-critical releases until the service is back within its SLO. Disputes about how the policy applies are escalated to a defined decision maker rather than resolved by changing the budget.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team’s checkout service has used all of its four-week error budget after a series of incidents, and the product team plans to ship a promotion feature next week. The SRE lead meets with the product manager, shows that the budget is exhausted and which incidents consumed it, and lays out two options: delay the launch while engineering fixes the causes, or launch on schedule and accept that checkout reliability may stay below target for a while. The product manager decides to slip the launch by two weeks. The decision is made with the data in front of everyone, and nobody has altered the SLO or moved budget around to make the numbers look acceptable.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Budget spent means the launch decision goes back to the team: freeze it or knowingly accept worse reliability. Don’t ask for more budget, don’t move budget between SLOs, and don’t treat passing tests as a substitute.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a scenario says a service has exhausted its error budget and a launch is coming, choose the answer that communicates the exhausted budget and has the team decide between halting the launch and accepting reduced reliability. Reject any option that creates, requests, or reallocates budget to make the launch possible.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/error-budget-policy/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook, Appendix B: Example Error Budget Policy:</strong></a> Supports: halting changes and releases other than P0 issues or security fixes once the four-week error budget is exceeded, the policy’s non-goal of punishing teams for missing SLOs, and escalation to a named decision maker when parties disagree</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook, Chapter 2: Implementing SLOs:</strong></a> Supports: the definition of an error budget as 100% minus the SLO, and the use of an error budget policy to decide what to do once the budget is exhausted</p>","lessons":["d3l3"]},"e4q35":{"id":"e4q35","exam":4,"n":35,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You are creating a Cloud Monitoring SLO for a service that will be published soon. You want to verify that requests to the service are handled in under 300 ms at least 90% of the time over each calendar month. You need to choose the metric and the evaluation method. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Select a latency metric and use the request-based evaluation method.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Select a latency metric and use the windows-based evaluation method.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Select an availability metric and use the request-based evaluation method.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Select an availability metric and use the windows-based evaluation method.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Select a latency metric and use the request-based evaluation method.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two independent choices have to be right: which metric measures the thing the SLO is about, and which evaluation method counts the right kind of event. The goal is about how fast requests are served, so the metric must be latency, which removes both availability options. The goal is also stated as a share of requests that meet a threshold (“fewer than 300 ms at least 90% of the time”), so each request is the unit being counted, and that is the request-based method. “Per calendar month” is a separate SLO setting, the compliance period, and does not influence either choice. Only A is correct on both.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“requests... addressed in fewer than 300 ms” is a technical signal that the SLI is latency, with a 300 ms threshold deciding which requests count as good</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“at least 90%” is the performance goal, expressed as a proportion of requests that meet the threshold, which is how a request-based SLI is defined</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“per calendar month” is a compliance-period setting, not a clue about the metric or evaluation method</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“identify the metric and evaluation method” tells you the answer needs one choice for each, so both must be checked</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A latency SLI in Cloud Monitoring is the ratio of calls under a latency threshold to all calls. With request-based evaluation, the SLO is met when the fraction of good requests, here those faster than 300 ms, is at or above the goal over the compliance period. Cloud Monitoring’s own example is “Latency is below 100 ms for at least 95% of requests”, which has the same structure as this requirement. You set the 300 ms threshold in the SLI details, the 90% goal as the performance goal, and a calendar month as the compliance period.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B, Violates Requirement.</strong> Windows-based evaluation counts time intervals that meet a goodness criterion, for example 10-minute windows in which the 95th-percentile latency is under a threshold. That measures the share of time a service performed well, which is not the share of requests under 300 ms. It also gives a quiet interval the same weight as a busy one, which changes what is being measured.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C, Different Use Case.</strong> Request-based evaluation is the right method, but an availability metric measures the ratio of successful responses to all responses, not how fast they were. A fast failure or a slow success would both be misjudged, so this option cannot verify a latency target.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D, Violates Requirement.</strong> It is wrong on both counts: availability does not measure speed, and windows-based evaluation does not count requests.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The phrase “90% of the time” tempts people toward windows-based evaluation, since “of the time” sounds like time intervals. In this scenario, though, “of the time” is shorthand for “of the requests”, because the subject of the sentence is requests and the threshold applies to each of them. A second trap is treating “per calendar month” as a hint about the evaluation method, when it is only the compliance period. The answer would change to windows-based if the goal were stated per interval, for example “95th-percentile latency under 300 ms in at least 90% of 10-minute windows”, or if the only raw metric available were a latency percentile, since Google’s documentation says a percentile metric requires a windows-based SLO.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A Cloud Monitoring SLO is built from three parts: an SLI that measures performance, a performance goal, and a compliance period, either calendar-based or rolling. The SLI is chosen in two steps, the metric (such as availability or latency) and the evaluation method. Request-based evaluation treats each request as the event and reports the fraction of good requests. Windows-based evaluation treats each time interval as the event and reports the fraction of good intervals.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team preparing to launch an API defines its latency SLO in the Cloud Monitoring console by choosing the latency metric, selecting request-based evaluation, setting the threshold to 300 ms, setting the goal to 90%, and using a calendar month as the compliance period. Once the service is live, the console shows what share of requests came in under 300 ms. The team can also use the remaining error budget to decide whether a risky release is acceptable.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Choose the metric for what you measure (speed means latency), choose the evaluation method for what you count (requests means request-based), and treat the calendar month as the compliance period.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a Cloud Monitoring SLO is worded as “X% of requests faster than N ms”, use a latency metric with request-based evaluation. Pick windows-based only when the goal is about the share of time intervals that meet a criterion, or when the raw metric is a percentile.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring\"><strong>Google Cloud Observability: Concepts in service monitoring:</strong></a> Supports: the definition of a latency SLI as the ratio of calls below a threshold to all calls, request-based versus windows-based SLO compliance, the example “Latency is below 100 ms for at least 95% of requests”, and calendar-based compliance periods</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring/ui/create-slo\"><strong>Google Cloud Observability: Creating an SLO:</strong></a> Supports: the console workflow of choosing a metric, then a request-based or windows-based evaluation method, setting the latency threshold, and choosing a calendar or rolling compliance period</p>","lessons":["d3l2"]},"e4q36":{"id":"e4q36","exam":4,"n":36,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your application runs on Cloud Run. You want to test a new version using live production traffic, and at the same time let the quality assurance (QA) team test it manually. You need to limit the potential impact of any problems found during testing, and you must be able to roll back to a previous version of the application if necessary. How should you deploy the new version? (Choose two.)</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Deploy the application as a new, separate Cloud Run service.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Deploy a new Cloud Run revision that has a tag, using the --no-traffic option.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Deploy a new Cloud Run revision that has no tag, using the --no-traffic option.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Deploy the new application version using the --no-traffic option, and route production traffic to the revision’s URL.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>E. Deploy the new application version, and split traffic to the new version.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Deploy a new Cloud Run revision that has a tag, using the --no-traffic option.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>E. Deploy the new application version, and split traffic to the new version.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The scenario has two testing needs that call for two separate mechanisms. QA needs to reach the new version directly and manually, without real users being sent to it, and the live-traffic test needs a controlled share of real users on the new version so that any problem affects only a small portion of them. Deploying a tagged revision with --no-traffic gives QA a dedicated URL for the new revision while production traffic stays on the existing revision (B). Splitting traffic then sends a limited percentage of live requests to the new revision (E). The old revision stays deployed throughout, so rollback is a matter of moving traffic back to it. Options A, C, and D each fail one of the needs. A creates a separate service that production traffic never reaches. C leaves QA without a stable URL for the revision. D sends production traffic to a URL meant for direct testing, which doesn’t work as a traffic-splitting mechanism. B and E work together, with B covering QA’s manual testing and E covering the live-traffic test with limited exposure.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“while you let the quality assurance team perform manual testing” requires a way to reach the new revision directly, which a revision tag provides through a dedicated URL</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“use live production traffic to test a new version” requires routing real requests to the new revision, which traffic splitting does</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“limit the potential impact of any issues” favors sending only a fraction of traffic to the new version rather than all of it</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“roll back to a previous version” favors keeping the earlier revision deployed within the same service so traffic can be moved back</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“(Choose two.)” with two distinct needs (QA access and live traffic) signals two complementary actions</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Run keeps each deployment as a revision and lets you control which revisions receive traffic. Deploying with --no-traffic creates the new revision without sending any requests to it, and adding a tag gives it a dedicated URL that QA can use to test it directly without serving production traffic. Once the revision looks healthy, traffic splitting lets you direct a small percentage of live traffic to the new revision, with the rest still going to the existing one, so any problem reaches only a limited share of users. Because the previous revision remains deployed, you can roll back by routing all traffic to it.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A: Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> A new service gets its own URL and its own traffic configuration, so production traffic never reaches it unless you add other routing in front of the two services. It also gives up the revision history within one service that makes traffic splitting and rollback simple.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C: Violates Requirement.</strong> Without a tag, the new revision has no dedicated URL, so the QA team has no stable address for testing it directly while it receives no traffic. The revision is deployed but effectively unreachable for manual testing.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D: Violates Requirement.</strong> A revision URL created by a tag is meant for direct testing, not for sending production traffic. Pointing production traffic at it is not traffic splitting, and doing so would not limit the impact in a controlled way. It also leaves the main service URL without a defined split, so it does not provide the gradual exposure the scenario requires.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is picking the options that look safe in isolation. A no-traffic deployment (B, C, D) sounds cautious, but the scenario also requires live production traffic to test the new version, which only traffic splitting supplies. The reverse trap is choosing traffic splitting alone (E) and forgetting that QA needs a direct way to reach the revision, which the tag provides. A third trap is reading “tag” as optional. Without it, the revision has no URL of its own, which is what separates B from C.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Run services are made up of revisions. Each deployment creates an immutable revision, and a service’s traffic can be assigned to one or more revisions by percentage. A revision tag creates a dedicated URL that reaches that revision directly, even when it is receiving no production traffic. Combining no-traffic deployment, tags, and percentage-based traffic splitting supports gradual rollouts: validate directly, expose a small share of users, then increase or roll back by changing the traffic assignment.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team deploys a new version of its API as a tagged, no-traffic revision and gives the QA team the tagged URL for manual testing. After QA signs off, the team sends 5% of production traffic to the new revision and watches error rates and latency. If metrics look healthy, they raise the share in steps. If something goes wrong, they move all traffic back to the previous revision, which was never removed, and affected users see the problem for only a short time.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Tagged revision with no traffic is for QA, and a traffic split is for live users in limited numbers. The old revision stays deployed, so rollback is just moving traffic back.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For a Cloud Run test that needs both direct manual testing and limited live traffic, choose a tagged --no-traffic revision for the direct-access part and traffic splitting for the live-traffic part. Avoid separate services, untagged revisions, and pointing production traffic at a test URL.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/serverless/cloud-run-now-supports-gradual-rollouts-and-rollbacks\"><strong>Google Cloud: Cloud Run gradual rollouts and rollbacks:</strong></a> Supports: deploying a new revision with --no-traffic and a tag to test it at a specific URL without serving traffic, then migrating traffic gradually and rolling back to an earlier revision</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://codelabs.developers.google.com/codelabs/cloud-run/revisions-cloud-run-traffic-splitting-gradual-rollout-rollbacks\"><strong>Google Codelabs: Using revisions in Cloud Run for traffic splitting, gradual rollouts, and rollbacks:</strong></a> Supports: tagging a revision to test it at a dedicated URL without serving traffic, splitting traffic by percentage, and rolling back to a previous revision</p>","lessons":["d2l7"]},"e4q37":{"id":"e4q37","exam":4,"n":37,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You are investigating problems in a production application that runs on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). You’ve traced the problem to a recently updated container image, but you haven’t been able to identify which code change caused it. The deployment currently references the image by its latest tag. You need to get the cluster running a version of the container that works as intended. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create a new tag named stable that points to the previously working container image, and change the deployment to reference that new tag.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Change the deployment to reference the sha256 digest of the previously working container image.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Build a new container image from an earlier Git tag, and perform a rolling update of the deployment to that new image.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Reapply the latest tag to the previous container image, and perform a rolling update of the deployment.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Change the deployment to reference the sha256 digest of the previously working container image.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two things must hold. The deployment must run exactly the image that was previously working, since the code change that broke it hasn’t been found and there is nothing to fix forward. The reference the deployment uses must also stay unambiguous, so the rollback does not depend on what a registry label happens to point to at the moment a Pod starts. Tags are mutable pointers, so any tag-based reference, whether a new tag or the old latest tag, can later point at a different image. A digest is a cryptographic hash of the image’s contents and cannot be repointed. Pointing the deployment at the digest of the known-good image restores that exact artifact without a rebuild.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“recently updated container image” identifies the artifact as the cause, which points toward reverting to the earlier artifact</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“the exact change in code was not identified” is a constraint that rules out fixing forward, since the faulty change isn’t known</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“currently pointing to the latest tag” highlights the mutable reference that created the ambiguity</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“a version of the container that functions as intended” means a previously working image exists and should be reused as-is</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A container image digest uniquely and immutably identifies a container image. Google’s guidance recommends deploying by digest to avoid the downsides of tags. Tags are mutable, and Google notes that deploying by tag in Kubernetes can lead to unexpected results, because new Pods created from the same Deployment spec can end up using either an old or a new image. Referencing the previously working image by its sha256 digest makes the Deployment spec point at exactly that content, and the update to that spec triggers a rolling update that replaces the faulty Pods. No rebuild is needed because the working image is already in the registry.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A, Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> A new stable tag would work until someone moves it, and it adds a mutable label that must be managed. It also repeats the problem that caused the incident. The deployment depends on a pointer that can change, instead of on the image itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C, Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> Rebuilding from an earlier Git tag does not guarantee the same image as before, since base image or dependency versions may have changed since the original build. It is also slower than reusing the existing known-good image, and it produces a new artifact that has never run in production.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D, Violates Requirement.</strong> Moving the latest tag keeps the mutable-reference problem, and it doesn’t change the Deployment spec because the image string is still “:latest”. Kubernetes starts a rolling update only when the Pod template changes, so the rollout may never start, and nodes that already hold a cached latest image may not pull the retagged one.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating a tag as a stable name for an image. Options A and D both rely on tags, and D looks tidy because it keeps the deployment unchanged, but that is exactly why it fails. A second trap is that rebuilding from a Git tag (C) sounds like the standard way to roll back code. Here the working artifact already exists, and a rebuild produces a different artifact even if the source is the same. The answer would change to C only if the previous image had been deleted from the registry, so that a rebuild was the only way to recreate it.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>An image tag is a human-readable, mutable label that can be moved to a different image at any time. An image digest is the hash of the image manifest or image index, and it is a content-addressed, immutable identifier. When a rollback has to restore a specific known-good artifact, a digest reference guarantees that Kubernetes pulls that exact image regardless of any retagging in the registry.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>An on-call engineer finds that an incident began right after a new image was pushed under the latest tag. They look up the digest of the last known-good image from the registry or the Cloud Build history, update the Deployment to reference it by digest, and let the rolling update replace the faulty Pods. The deployment then stays pinned to that content, so a later push to latest can’t alter it, and the team can investigate the code change without time pressure.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Tags can move and digests cannot, so roll back to the known-good image by its digest.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks how to run a specific known-good container image in Kubernetes, choose the immutable digest reference over any tag, and prefer reusing the existing image over rebuilding it. Be wary of options that move or add tags, because a tag can be repointed.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/about-container-images\"><strong>Google Cloud: About container image digests (GKE):</strong></a> Supports: image digests uniquely and immutably identifying an image, tags being mutable, and the downsides of deploying by tag in Kubernetes, including Pods created from the same Deployment spec ending up with different images</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/tutorials/using-container-image-digests-in-kubernetes-manifests\"><strong>Google Cloud: Using container image digests in Kubernetes manifests:</strong></a> Supports: referencing images by digest in Kubernetes manifests as the recommended way to identify container images</p>","lessons":["d2l5"]},"e4q38":{"id":"e4q38","exam":4,"n":38,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You are the Site Reliability Engineer responsible for your company’s data services and products. You regularly deal with operational challenges, such as unpredictable data volume and high cost, in your company’s data ingestion processes. You have just learned that a new data ingestion product will be developed in Google Cloud. You need to work with the product development team to give them operational input on the new product. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Deploy the prototype product to a test environment, run a load test, and share the results with the product development team.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. After the initial version passes the quality assurance phase and compliance assessments, deploy the product to a staging environment, and share error logs and performance metrics with the product development team.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Once at least one internal customer is using the new product in production, share error logs and monitoring metrics with the product development team.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Review the product’s design with the product development team, and give feedback early in the design phase.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Review the product’s design with the product development team, and give feedback early in the design phase.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The operational problems named in the scenario, unpredictable data volume and high cost, are largely determined by architecture and design choices. The input you provide is therefore worth the most when it arrives while those choices are still being made. Options A, B, and C each offer evidence and feedback at a later stage of the lifecycle, after the design has been built and more commitments exist. Only D puts operational experience into the design itself, where changes are cheapest. The later options also use test, staging, or production data to describe problems that earlier input could have prevented.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“unpredictable data volume and high cost” are design-level concerns, such as how the system absorbs bursts and how it uses resources, which are hard to fix after the architecture is set</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“a new data ingestion product will be developed” means the product is at the start of its lifecycle, so there is still a design phase to influence</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“collaborate with the product development team to provide operational input” points to working together on the product’s design, not delivering test or monitoring reports</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Options A, B, and C describe progressively later lifecycle stages (prototype test, staging, production), while D is the only one at the design stage</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Google’s SRE engagement model describes the architecture and design phase as the first place SRE can contribute, for example by sharing best practices, giving early engagement consulting on architectures and design choices, and helping validate assumptions with targeted prototypes. The SRE Workbook notes that early SRE engagement can help avoid costly redesigns that become necessary once a system meets real-world users and has to scale as the service grows. That is the same set of problems you face with unpredictable volume and cost. Reviewing the design with the development team lets you bring the lessons from your existing ingestion processes into the new product before they are expensive to apply.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A, Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> A prototype load test gives useful evidence, but it comes after a prototype exists, so major design decisions have already been made. Results that show a scaling or cost problem may then force rework that earlier design input could have avoided.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B, Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> Waiting until the product has passed quality assurance and compliance assessments means the design is largely fixed and changes are expensive. Error logs and performance metrics from staging describe what was built, not what should be built.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C, Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> Sharing logs and metrics only after an internal customer uses the product in production is the most reactive choice. It treats SRE as a recipient of problems instead of a contributor to the product, and real users are already exposed to any issue before the first piece of input is given.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is picking a hands-on validation step, especially the load test in A, because it sounds concrete and data-driven. A load test is valuable, but it answers whether a design copes after the design exists. The scenario’s cues, unpredictable volume and high cost, are design problems that are best handled before building. The answer would change to A if the product were already built and the question asked how to verify capacity before launch.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>The cost of changing a decision rises as a service moves through its lifecycle. Characteristics such as how a system handles variable load and how efficiently it uses resources are set during architecture and design. Engaging early, sometimes called shifting left, lets operational experience shape those decisions instead of being applied as fixes later. SRE can take part through design reviews, best practices, and consulting, and still engage at later stages when a team arrives late.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>An SRE with experience of bursty ingestion volumes is invited to the new product’s design review. They raise questions about what happens when input spikes, how cost grows with volume, and how operators will see and control both. The development team revises the design before any code is written, and the team avoids discovering the problem later through a failed load test or a production incident.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>Operational problems that come from design are best fixed in the design phase, so review the design early.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a new product is just starting development and SRE wants to contribute operational input, choose early engagement in the design phase over any option that waits for a prototype, staging, or production. Later steps validate or react to design choices, but only early input can change them cheaply.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/engagement-model/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook, Chapter 18: SRE Engagement Model:</strong></a> Supports: Phase 1 (Architecture and Design) engagement activities, such as early engagement consulting and best practices, and the point that early SRE engagement can help avoid costly redesigns when systems meet real users and need to scale</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/evolving-sre-engagement-model/\"><strong>Google SRE Book, Chapter 32: The Evolving SRE Engagement Model:</strong></a> Supports: the Early Engagement Model and the Production Readiness Review process as ways for SRE to partner with development teams across a service’s lifecycle</p>","lessons":["d3l8"]},"e4q39":{"id":"e4q39","exam":4,"n":39,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You need to introduce postmortems into your organization, and you want the postmortem process to be well received. What should you do? (Choose two.)</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Encourage new employees to learn the practice by conducting postmortems for the team.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Set up a dedicated team that is responsible for conducting every postmortem.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Encourage senior leadership to acknowledge and take part in postmortems.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Make sure that writing effective postmortems is a practice that is both rewarded and celebrated.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>E. Give the organization a forum in which to critique earlier postmortems.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Encourage senior leadership to acknowledge and take part in postmortems.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Make sure that writing effective postmortems is a practice that is both rewarded and celebrated.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The goal is cultural adoption: people must see the postmortem process as valuable and safe rather than as a burden or a punishment. Two options describe the reinforcement mechanisms that build that perception. Visible senior-leadership participation signals that the practice is important and that the whole organization, including those at the top, takes part in learning from failure (C). Rewarding and celebrating effective postmortem writing gives the people who do the work a positive reason to do it well and to keep doing it (D). The remaining options each undermine the goal. Handing every postmortem to a designated team (B) takes ownership away from the engineers who understand the incident. Assigning new employees to conduct postmortems (A) puts the least-equipped people in charge of the process. Opening earlier postmortems to critique (E) risks making authors feel judged. C and D act independently, one through top-down modeling and the other through bottom-up recognition, and together they cover the two main levers of cultural change.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“introduce postmortems into your organization” frames this as a culture-change question, not a process-design or staffing question</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“well received” is the success criterion: the right answers are the ones that build acceptance, not the ones that merely get postmortems written</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“(Choose two.)” signals two complementary actions, here one leadership-driven and one recognition-driven</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“designated team that is responsible for conducting all postmortems” is a centralization pattern that conflicts with ownership by the engineers involved</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Google’s SRE guidance says that introducing a postmortem culture takes continuous cultivation and reinforcement, and it lists specific strategies for winning people over. Two of them are to encourage senior leadership’s acknowledgment and participation in postmortems, and to make sure that writing effective postmortems is a rewarded and celebrated practice, both publicly and through individual and team performance management. Leadership participation shows that postmortems matter. Visible rewards show that the effort is valued, which counters the common objection that postmortems cost too much time for too little benefit.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A: Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> Practice has value for newcomers, but having new employees conduct the team’s postmortems puts the least experienced people in charge of a sensitive process whose goal is to build trust. Google’s guidance instead has new SREs learn through exercises that reenact past postmortems, such as the Wheel of Misfortune, which is practice without ownership of real incidents.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B: Violates Requirement.</strong> A dedicated team that conducts every postmortem separates the analysis from the engineers closest to the incident. It makes the process feel imposed from outside and cuts against the collaborative, engineer-owned approach that Google describes, in which blameless postmortems are ideally the product of engineer self-motivation.</p>\n<p><strong>Option E: Technically Possible but Not Best.</strong> Feedback on the postmortem process is valuable, and Google surveys teams about its effectiveness. A forum to critique previous postmortems, though, invites judgment of individual authors’ work, which can make people reluctant to write them. It is not one of the recommended strategies for building acceptance.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is picking options that sound rigorous or process-oriented, such as a dedicated postmortem team (B) or a critique forum (E), when the question asks what makes the process well received. Acceptance comes from signals of safety and value, namely leadership participation and visible recognition, not from adding oversight or centralizing ownership. Option A is a subtler trap, because training newcomers sounds constructive but gives them a sensitive responsibility they are not yet equipped to carry.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Introducing postmortems is a cultural change as much as a technical one. Cultures shift through what leaders visibly do and what the organization visibly rewards. If senior people participate and good postmortem work is celebrated, others conclude that the practice is safe, valued, and expected. Mandates, centralized ownership, or critique mechanisms can produce documents without producing the openness that makes postmortems useful.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A new engineering director starts joining postmortem review sessions and says openly what they learned from each one. The team also starts a monthly newsletter that highlights a well-written postmortem and its author, and managers cite strong postmortem work in performance reviews. Engineers who initially saw postmortems as paperwork begin to see them as a way to gain recognition, and they start volunteering to write them.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>To make postmortems well received: leaders take part, and good postmortem work gets visibly rewarded. Do not centralize them, hand them to newcomers, or open them to critique.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks how to get a postmortem culture accepted, choose the options that build safety and perceived value: visible senior-leadership participation and public and performance-based recognition for writing good postmortems. Avoid options that centralize ownership, assign the process to inexperienced staff, or expose authors to critique.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/\"><strong>Google SRE Book, Chapter 15: Postmortem Culture: Learning from Failure:</strong></a> Supports: the “Introducing a Postmortem Culture” strategies of rewarding and celebrating effective postmortem writing and encouraging senior leadership’s acknowledgment and participation, plus the collaborative, engineer-owned approach that rules out a dedicated postmortem team</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/postmortem-culture/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook, Chapter 10: Postmortem Culture: Learning from Failure:</strong></a> Supports: the framing of introducing postmortems as a cultural change that can start small and be tuned to the organization</p>","lessons":["d3l5"]},"e4q40":{"id":"e4q40","exam":4,"n":40,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You have deployed a fleet of Compute Engine instances in Google Cloud. You need to make sure the instances’ monitoring metrics and logs appear in Cloud Logging and Cloud Monitoring, where your company’s operations and cybersecurity teams can see them. You must grant the required roles to the Compute Engine service account through Identity and Access Management (IAM), following the principle of least privilege. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Grant the logging.logWriter and monitoring.metricWriter roles to the Compute Engine service accounts.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Grant the logging.admin and monitoring.editor roles to the Compute Engine service accounts.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Grant the logging.editor and monitoring.metricWriter roles to the Compute Engine service accounts.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Grant the logging.logWriter and monitoring.editor roles to the Compute Engine service accounts.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Grant the logging.logWriter and monitoring.metricWriter roles to the Compute Engine service accounts.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The roles in question go to the service account that the instances run as. That identity only needs to send telemetry, meaning log entries to Cloud Logging and metrics to Cloud Monitoring. The operations and security teams’ ability to view the data depends on their own identities’ access, which this question does not ask you to configure. Because the stated requirement is least privilege, the right choice is the narrowest write-only role for each service. Any role that adds read, edit, or administrative abilities to the service account is wrong. A is the only option made up of two write-only roles.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“grant the required roles for the Compute Engine service account” is a hard constraint: the identity being configured is the one that sends telemetry, not the people who read it</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“following the principle of least privilege” is a hard constraint that rules out admin and editor roles when a write-only role exists</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“monitoring metrics and logs” is a technical signal that two separate write permissions are needed, one for each service</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Every option pairs one logging role with one monitoring role, so both halves must be checked for excess privilege</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Google’s Ops Agent authorization guidance names Logs Writer and Monitoring Metric Writer as the roles for the service account the agent uses. The Logs Writer role (logging.logWriter) provides the permissions to write log entries. The Monitoring Metric Writer role (monitoring.metricWriter) is write-only and is intended for service accounts and agents. Together they let the instances send logs and metrics and do nothing beyond that. The same guidance says to grant the service account the least privileged IAM roles possible.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> Logging Admin gives full access to all features of Cloud Logging, and Monitoring Editor gives read-write access to Monitoring, including creating and editing configurations such as dashboards and alerting policies. A service account that only sends telemetry needs neither, so this pair breaks least privilege on both sides.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Monitoring Metric Writer is correct, but it is paired with an editor-style logging role, which implies broader access than writing log entries. Google documents Logs Writer as the role that provides the permission to write log entries, so the editor-style role adds privilege without adding capability the agent needs.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Logs Writer is correct, but Monitoring Editor grants read-write access to Monitoring and the ability to create and edit configurations. The service account only has to write metrics, which Monitoring Metric Writer already covers.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is choosing a role pair that would certainly make everything work, such as editor or admin roles, instead of the narrowest pair that does the job. Options B, C, and D each contain one plausible role, which makes them look reasonable. Both halves have to be write-only. A second trap is reading “visible by the operations and security teams” as a reason to give the service account viewing roles, when visibility is a matter of the teams’ own access. The answer would change only if the question asked what the teams themselves need to read the data, in which case viewer roles would apply to them and not to the service account.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A telemetry agent on a VM authenticates as the VM’s attached service account and needs permission only to write data. Cloud Logging and Cloud Monitoring each have a purpose-built write-only role for this, Logs Writer and Monitoring Metric Writer, which are separate from the viewer, editor, and admin roles meant for people. IAM roles are one part of authorization. For VMs that use the default Compute Engine service account, the instance’s access scopes must also permit writing, and Google notes that new VMs usually have adequate scopes but older VMs or VMs with modified scopes might not.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team builds an instance template for a fleet and attaches a service account that holds only Logs Writer and Monitoring Metric Writer. The Ops Agent on each VM sends logs and metrics, and the operations and security teams read them using their own viewer roles. If an instance were compromised, the service account could do little beyond writing telemetry, so the potential damage is limited.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>An instance’s service account only writes telemetry, so give it Logs Writer and Monitoring Metric Writer.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks which roles an instance’s service account needs for logs and metrics under least privilege, choose the two write-only roles, Logs Writer and Monitoring Metric Writer. Reject admin and editor roles, which add abilities the service account never uses.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/agent/ops-agent/authorization\"><strong>Google Cloud Observability — Authorize the Ops Agent:</strong></a> Supports: the Logs Writer and Monitoring Metric Writer roles for the service account the agent uses, the guidance to grant the least privileged roles possible, and the access-scope caveat for older VMs</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/monitoring/access-control\"><strong>Google Cloud Monitoring — Control access with IAM:</strong></a> Supports: Monitoring Metric Writer as the write-only role for service accounts and agents, Monitoring Editor’s read-write access to configurations, and Logging Admin’s full access to Cloud Logging</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/roles-permissions/logging\"><strong>Google Cloud IAM — Cloud Logging roles and permissions:</strong></a> Supports: Logs Writer (roles/logging.logWriter) providing the permissions to write log entries</p>","lessons":["d3l6"]},"e4q41":{"id":"e4q41","exam":4,"n":41,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your company runs its services on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). The GKE clusters in the development environment run applications with verbose logging turned on. Developers read logs with the <code>kubectl logs</code> command and do not use Cloud Logging. The applications do not follow a uniform logging structure. You need to minimize the cost of application logging while still collecting GKE operational logs. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Run the <code>gcloud container clusters update</code> command with <code>--logging=SYSTEM</code> on the development cluster.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Run the <code>gcloud container clusters update</code> command with <code>--logging=WORKLOAD</code> on the development cluster.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Run the <code>gcloud logging sinks update _Default --disabled</code> command in the project that hosts the development environment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Add an exclusion filter of <code>severity &gt;= DEBUG resource.type = \"k8s_container\"</code> to the <code>_Default</code> log sink in the project that hosts the development environment.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Run the</strong><code>**gcloud container clusters update**</code><strong>command with</strong><code>**--logging=SYSTEM**</code><strong>on the development cluster.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two requirements must both hold: the cost of application logs in Cloud Logging has to drop, and GKE operational logs must keep being collected. Developers already read logs with <code>kubectl logs</code>, and the applications have no uniform log structure, so the verbose application logs have little value in Cloud Logging. GKE lets you choose which log sources a cluster sends, and the <code>--logging</code> flag separates operational (system) logs from application (workload) logs. Setting the cluster to system-only collection removes the application logs at the source and keeps the operational logs. The other options either collect the wrong source or cut logs after collection with a wide net that can also drop operational logs.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“minimize the costs associated with application logging” is a hard constraint, and application logs are the specific thing to cut</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“while still collecting GKE operational logs” is a hard constraint that rules out any option that stops or filters system logs</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Developers view logs by using the <code>kubectl logs</code> command and do not use Cloud Logging” is context showing that nobody consumes the application logs in Cloud Logging</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Applications do not have a uniform logging structure” is context showing the application logs have limited value for querying and that field-based filtering is not reliable</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><code>--logging=SYSTEM</code> versus <code>--logging=WORKLOAD</code> is a technical signal that the decision is about which log source the cluster collects</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>The <code>--logging</code> flag controls which logs a GKE cluster sends to Cloud Logging. The <code>SYSTEM</code> value collects logs from Pods in system namespaces such as kube-system, from non-containerized services such as containerd and kubelet, and Kubernetes events. Google documents it as required for all cluster types. The <code>WORKLOAD</code> value collects all logs from non-system containers on user nodes and is on by default. Google’s documentation states that a Standard cluster can be configured to capture only system logs and not collect application logs. Setting <code>--logging=SYSTEM</code> therefore stops the verbose application logs from being collected at all, so they are not ingested or billed, and the operational logs continue to flow.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> <code>WORKLOAD</code> is the log source that collects application logs, which is the opposite of what is needed, and it is already on by default. Google also documents <code>SYSTEM</code> as required for all cluster types, so selecting only <code>WORKLOAD</code> does not express the intended configuration.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Disabling the <code>_Default</code> sink stops it from routing log entries into the <code>_Default</code> bucket for the whole project, not only the application logs of the development cluster. That includes the GKE system logs the scenario says must still be collected.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Exclusion filters are a legitimate way to reduce log volume, but this filter selects by resource type and severity, not by whether a container is an application or a system Pod. <code>k8s_container</code> also covers system Pods, so it can drop operational logs, and it filters after collection instead of avoiding collection. Google also cautions that exclusion filters which keep GKE logs from being ingested can leave you without logs to share with Google Cloud support.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is reaching for an exclusion filter (D) because it is the most familiar log-cost tool. When a whole category of logs has no consumer, turning off its collection is cleaner than ingesting and then filtering. The answer would change to an exclusion filter if you needed to keep part of the application logs in Cloud Logging, such as only higher-severity entries or specific namespaces. It would also change if the clusters were Autopilot, because Google’s documentation describes system-only collection for Standard clusters and exclusion filters for reducing volume on both modes.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>You can control logging cost at two layers. At the source, you configure what the cluster’s logging agent collects. At the routing layer, the Log Router applies sinks and exclusion filters to logs that were already collected. Source-level control suits cases where an entire category of logs is not needed, and routing-level control suits partial reductions. In GKE, system logs are the operational baseline and cannot be turned off while Cloud Logging is on, while workload logs are optional.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team sets <code>--logging=SYSTEM</code> on its development clusters. Developers keep reading application output with <code>kubectl logs</code>, node and system-component logs stay in Cloud Logging for troubleshooting and for Google Cloud support, and the logging bill falls because the verbose application logs are no longer ingested. If a team later needs application logs in Cloud Logging for a specific test, the team can re-enable <code>WORKLOAD</code> for that cluster.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>To drop application logs but keep operational ones, set the cluster’s <code>--logging</code> to SYSTEM instead of filtering or disabling at the Log Router.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a GKE logging-cost question asks to cut application logs while keeping operational logs, choose the cluster-level <code>--logging=SYSTEM</code> setting. Avoid disabling the <code>_Default</code> sink or using broad exclusion filters, which can also remove system logs.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/about-logs\"><strong>Google Cloud — About GKE logs:</strong></a> Supports: the <code>--logging</code> values <code>SYSTEM</code> and <code>WORKLOAD</code>, what each collects, that <code>SYSTEM</code> is required for all cluster types, that a Standard cluster can capture only system logs, and the caution about exclusion filters affecting GKE logs</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/install/logs_export\"><strong>Google Cloud — Route logs to supported destinations:</strong></a> Supports: sinks belonging to a project, and the <code>_Default</code> sink routing matching log entries into the <code>_Default</code> bucket, which shows why disabling it affects every log the project routes there</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification exam guide (Section 4.1, optimizing logs: filtering, exclusions, cost management, source considerations):</strong></a> Supports: domain and objective alignment</p>","lessons":["d4l2","d4l5"]},"e4q42":{"id":"e4q42","exam":4,"n":42,"domain":5,"html":"<p>Your company processes IoT data at scale using Pub/Sub, the App Engine standard environment, and an application written in Go. You have noticed that performance degrades intermittently at peak load, and you could not reproduce the problem on your workstation. You need to monitor the application continuously in production to find the slow paths in the code, while keeping both performance impact and management overhead to a minimum. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Use Cloud Monitoring to review the App Engine CPU utilization metric.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Install a continuous profiling tool on Compute Engine, and configure the application to send its profiling data to that tool.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Periodically run the go tool pprof command against the application instance, and analyze the output by using flame graphs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Set up Cloud Profiler, and initialize the cloud.google.com/go/profiler library in the application.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Set up Cloud Profiler, and initialize the cloud.google.com/go/profiler library in the application.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Three requirements must hold together. The problem can’t be reproduced locally and appears only intermittently under peak load, so the monitoring has to run continuously in production. You need to find slow paths in the code, so the data must be attributed to functions in the source, not just to resource totals. Finally, both performance impact and management overhead must be minimal, which favors a managed, low-overhead tool over anything you operate yourself. A metric only shows that a resource is busy, and the self-managed and manual profiling options fail the overhead or continuity requirement. Cloud Profiler is the only option that meets all three.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“could not reproduce this issue on your workstation” is a technical signal that profiling has to happen in production</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“continuously monitor the application in production” is a hard constraint that rules out occasional manual captures</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“identify slow paths in the code” is a technical signal that code-level attribution is needed, not infrastructure metrics</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“minimize performance impact and management overhead” is a hard constraint that favors a managed, low-overhead service</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“App Engine standard environment” and “written in Go” together point to a supported Cloud Profiler configuration</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Profiler is a statistical, low-overhead profiler that continuously gathers CPU usage and memory-allocation information from production applications and attributes it to the source code that generated it. Google states that the overhead of CPU and heap profiling at collection time is under 5 percent, and amortized across the execution time and replicas of a service it is commonly under 0.5 percent, which makes it suitable for always-on use in production. Go on the App Engine standard environment is a supported configuration. You enable it by calling profiler.Start from the cloud.google.com/go/profiler package early in main, and on App Engine the service name and version are inferred from the environment. Google manages the backend and the interface, so you don’t run profiling infrastructure.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> CPU utilization shows how busy the instances are at peak load, which fits the symptom, but it does not show which functions or code paths consume the CPU. It tells you that something is slow, not where in the code.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A continuous profiling tool does fit the continuity requirement, but installing and running it on Compute Engine means provisioning, operating, and securing your own profiling infrastructure and wiring the application to it. That is the management overhead the scenario asks you to avoid, when a managed service already supports this environment.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Running go tool pprof periodically is manual and point-in-time, so it can miss an intermittent problem that appears only at peak load, and it is not continuous monitoring. Collecting and analyzing samples by hand is also ongoing operational work.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is picking option A because the symptom appears at peak load and CPU is the obvious resource to check. A resource metric can confirm saturation, but it can’t tell you which code is responsible, which is the actual goal. Option C is a second trap, since pprof is a real Go profiling tool, but it is manual and not continuous. The answer would change to option A only if the goal were to confirm resource saturation or alert on it, and different tooling again (distributed tracing) if the goal were to find which downstream call in a request is slow.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Metrics and profiles answer different questions. Metrics such as CPU utilization show that a service is slow or saturated, while profiles show where in the code the CPU time or memory goes. Statistical (sampling) profiling takes brief samples at intervals instead of tracing every operation, which keeps overhead low enough to leave on permanently in production. That is what makes it suitable for problems that are intermittent, load-dependent, and impossible to reproduce on a workstation.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team adds a single profiler.Start call to its Go service’s main function and deploys to App Engine. After the next peak-load slowdown, they open Profiler, look at the profile data for the period when the degradation occurred, and find that one message-handling function dominates CPU time. They fix that function and redeploy. They never provisioned profiling servers, and the overhead stayed small enough to leave profiling on for good.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For slow code paths in production with minimal overhead, use Cloud Profiler, because metrics show that a resource is busy and profiles show which code is using it.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question needs continuous, low-overhead, code-level performance analysis of a production application, choose Cloud Profiler. Reject resource metrics, which don’t point to code, and reject self-hosted or manual profiling, which adds management effort or misses intermittent problems.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/about-profiler\"><strong>Google Cloud Profiler — Cloud Profiler overview:</strong></a> Supports: Cloud Profiler as a statistical, low-overhead continuous profiler for production applications, the overhead figures (under 5 percent at collection, commonly under 0.5 percent amortized), and Go on the App Engine standard environment as a supported configuration</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/profiling-go\"><strong>Google Cloud Profiler — Profiling Go applications:</strong></a> Supports: initializing the cloud.google.com/go/profiler library with profiler.Start and App Engine inferring the service name and version</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification exam guide (Section 5.1, collecting performance information: application performance monitoring):</strong></a> Supports: domain and objective alignment</p>","lessons":["d5l8"]},"e4q43":{"id":"e4q43","exam":4,"n":43,"domain":3,"html":"<p>Your CTO has asked you to put a postmortem policy in place for every incident, for internal use. You want to define what a good postmortem looks like so that the policy succeeds at your company. What should you do? (Choose two.)</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Require every postmortem to state what caused the incident, name the person or team responsible for causing it, and describe how to prevent it from recurring.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Require every postmortem to state what caused the incident, describe how the incident could have been worse, and describe how to prevent it from recurring.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Require every postmortem to state the severity of the incident, describe how to prevent it from recurring, and describe what caused it without naming internal system components.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Require every postmortem to state how the incident was resolved and what caused it, without naming customer information.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>E. Require every postmortem to involve all incident participants in authoring it, and share postmortems as widely as possible.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Require every postmortem to state what caused the incident, describe how the incident could have been worse, and describe how to prevent it from recurring.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>E. Require every postmortem to involve all incident participants in authoring it, and share postmortems as widely as possible.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>A successful postmortem policy needs two things: a clear standard for what a postmortem contains, and a process that keeps postmortems honest and useful. The content standard must produce learning and prevention, which means a real root cause, the near-misses that show hidden risk, and actions that prevent recurrence, without blaming anyone. The process standard must bring in everyone who saw the incident and make the result visible to the people who can learn from it. B supplies the content, and E supplies the process. A, C, and D each contain a flaw that would weaken the policy: blame, withheld technical detail, or missing preventive action. B and E work independently of each other, and together they cover both halves of what the CTO asked for.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“define what a good postmortem is” is a hard constraint that points to both the content and the practices of a quality postmortem</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“to ensure that the policy is successful” is a hard constraint favoring practices that build trust and adoption, such as blamelessness and open sharing</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“for internal use” is a technical signal that postmortems can name specific systems and include specific data, which weighs against options that remove technical detail</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“(Choose two.)” is a cardinality instruction, so one selected option should cover content and the other process</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“identify the person or team responsible” is an exclusion, since it describes the blame-oriented pattern the policy needs to avoid</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Option B matches how Google describes a good postmortem. It identifies the root cause and trigger, it includes a “Where we got lucky” lesson, which records the factors that kept the incident from being worse and exposes latent risk, and it ends in preventive action items. The Workbook states that a postmortem with no action items is ineffective, and it describes root-cause sections that focus on what went wrong, not who caused it. Option E matches the Workbook’s recommendations to include all incident participants in postmortem authoring, because a postmortem written in isolation or by one team can overlook contributing factors, and to share postmortems as widely as possible, because the value of a postmortem is proportional to the learning it creates. The two work independently, since one governs what is written and the other governs how it is written and shared, and together they form a complete definition of a good postmortem.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Naming the person or team responsible is the blame-oriented pattern the Workbook lists as a failure mode. It makes people risk-averse and tempts them to hide facts that are critical to preventing recurrence. It is tempting because it names a cause and a prevention step, but the policy’s success depends on blamelessness.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> Severity and prevention are useful, but banning internal system component names removes the technical specifics needed to explain the root cause and write measurable action items. Google’s good example names the systems involved and explains unfamiliar terms in a glossary, and a policy for internal use should allow that detail.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Describing resolution and cause is useful, and keeping end-user information out of postmortems matches Google’s practice. The option omits follow-up and preventive actions, though, which Google treats as essential, and it says nothing about collaboration or sharing, so it leaves the policy’s success to chance.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is option A, because identifying who caused an incident feels like accountability. A blameless postmortem pursues accountability through owned, tracked action items and through fixing system gaps, not through naming individuals. Option D is a quieter trap, since protecting customer information is a real concern and the option sounds responsible, but it is incomplete without preventive actions. D would come closer to correct if it added action items, and C would be acceptable if it withheld only end-user details instead of internal components.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A blameless postmortem is a learning tool that focuses on contributing causes and gaps in systems and processes, assuming people acted with good intentions on the information they had. Its quality has two parts. The content part covers impact, root cause and trigger, resolution, lessons including what went well, what went poorly, and where the team got lucky, and action items that have an owner, a priority, and a measurable end state. The process part covers collaborative authoring, timely publication, and wide sharing. A policy that sets standards for both is more likely to produce postmortems that people trust and learn from.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A company adopts a postmortem template that requires a root cause, a “where we got lucky” section, and prioritized, owned action items filed as tracked bugs. Each postmortem has one owner and collaborators from every team involved in the incident, and the result is published to all engineering employees within about a week. Engineers outside the incident read the postmortems and fix similar weaknesses in their own systems, so repeat incidents decline and people are willing to share information because nobody is blamed.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>A good postmortem is blameless in content (cause, how it could have been worse, prevention) and open in process (everyone involved writes it and everyone can read it).</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks how to define a good postmortem, choose the options that keep the content blameless and focused on cause, near-misses, and prevention, and keep the process collaborative and widely shared. Eliminate options that name individuals, remove technical specifics, or leave out preventive actions.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/postmortem-culture/\"><strong>Google SRE Workbook, Chapter 10: Postmortem Culture: Learning from Failure:</strong></a> Supports: the good-postmortem example with root cause, “Where we got lucky”, and prioritized owned action items, blamelessness, including all incident participants in authoring, and sharing postmortems as widely as possible</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/\"><strong>Google SRE Book, Chapter 15: Postmortem Culture: Learning from Failure:</strong></a> Supports: the definition of a postmortem as a record of impact, mitigation, root causes, and follow-up actions, blameless postmortems, sharing to the widest useful audience, and keeping end-user-identifying information out of postmortems</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification exam guide (Section 3, applying site reliability engineering practices):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment</p>","lessons":["d3l5"]},"e4q44":{"id":"e4q44","exam":4,"n":44,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your company operates in a highly regulated industry, and its security team requires that only trusted container images can be deployed to Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE). You need a solution that meets this requirement while keeping management overhead to a minimum. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Configure Binary Authorization on your GKE clusters to enforce deploy-time security policies.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Grant the roles/artifactregistry.writer role to the Cloud Build service account, and verify that no employee has write permission on Artifact Registry.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Use Cloud Run to build and deploy a custom validator, and enable an Eventarc trigger so that validation runs whenever a new image is uploaded.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Configure Kritis in your GKE clusters to enforce deploy-time security policies.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Configure Binary Authorization on your GKE clusters to enforce deploy-time security policies.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The requirement is about what can be deployed to GKE, so the control has to act at deploy time, on the path where images enter the cluster. It also has to be a managed capability, because the scenario asks for minimal management overhead. Controlling who can push images, or validating images when they are uploaded, acts earlier in the supply chain and does not stop an untrusted image from reaching the cluster through another route. Between the two deploy-time options, the managed Google Cloud product wins over the open-source tool that you would deploy and operate yourself. Binary Authorization is the only option that meets all of these requirements.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“only trusted container images can be deployed to GKE” is a hard constraint that places enforcement at the deployment step, not at the registry</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“minimizing management overhead” is a hard constraint that favors a managed product over custom code or a self-run tool</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“highly regulated” is a technical signal that favors centrally defined, auditable policy enforcement</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“enforce deploy-time security policies” appears in both A and D, which signals that the real decision is between Google’s managed product and an open-source implementation</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Binary Authorization is a Google Cloud product for software supply-chain security. It acts as a deploy-time enforcer that prevents images that violate your policy from being deployed. It integrates with the GKE control plane to allow or block deployment based on policies you define. A policy can require attestations, which are signed statements that an image passed a step such as a build or test, and attestors verify them at deploy time. Policy enforcement events can be viewed in Cloud Audit Logs, and Google describes dry-run support for testing a policy before enforcing it. One prerequisite applies: if the policy requires attestations, you must set up attestors and have your pipeline create the attestations.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Incorrect:</strong> Restricting who can write to Artifact Registry controls who can publish images, not which images can be deployed. An untrusted image from another registry, or an image pushed through an allowed identity without any checks, could still be deployed to the cluster. Limiting write access is good practice, but it does not enforce trust at deployment.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> A custom validator triggered when an image is uploaded checks images at the registry, not at deployment, so it does not block images that arrive another way. It is also custom code you must build, secure, and maintain, which works against the minimal-overhead requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Kritis is an open-source implementation of deploy-time enforcement that uses an admission controller, so it fits the enforcement point. You deploy and operate it yourself in the clusters, though, while Binary Authorization is the Google-managed product natively integrated with GKE and based on the Kritis specification. That makes Kritis the higher-overhead choice.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is option D. Binary Authorization is based on the Kritis specification, so Kritis looks like the same capability. The difference is who runs it: Kritis is an open-source tool you install and maintain, and Binary Authorization is a managed Google Cloud service. Kritis would become a candidate only if the requirement were open-source, vendor-neutral enforcement on Kubernetes clusters that a managed Google Cloud service does not cover, and the team accepted operating it. A second trap is option B, because least-privilege access to the registry is a sound practice, but it addresses publishing and not deployment.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Container trust can be controlled at three points: build (who builds and signs), registry (who can push), and deploy (what the cluster admits). Only a deploy-time admission control guarantees that what runs matches policy, because images can reach a cluster from any registry. Controls at the earlier points reduce risk but do not enforce. Attestations let earlier stages record that an image passed checks so that the deploy-time check only needs to verify the signature, and least-privilege registry access complements deploy-time enforcement.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A regulated company’s Cloud Build pipeline builds each image, runs tests and scans, and signs an attestation for images that pass. The GKE clusters have a Binary Authorization policy requiring that attestation. When someone tries to deploy an unsigned image from a public registry, the deployment is blocked and the event is logged to Cloud Audit Logs, which gives the security team evidence for auditors. The team ran the policy in dry-run mode first to see what it would block before turning on enforcement.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>For “only trusted images can be deployed” with low overhead, choose the managed deploy-time enforcer, Binary Authorization, and not registry permissions, custom validators, or a self-run open-source tool.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question requires that only trusted images be deployed to GKE with minimal management overhead, choose Binary Authorization. Registry write restrictions and upload-time validators act before deployment, and Kritis is an open-source implementation you would have to run yourself.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Binary Authorization overview:</strong></a> Supports: Binary Authorization as a Google Cloud product for software supply-chain security, the deploy-time policy enforcer, attestations and attestors, its basis in the Kritis specification, and enforcement events in Cloud Audit Logs</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/identity-security/deploy-only-what-you-trust-introducing-binary-authorization-for-google-kubernetes-engine\"><strong>Google Cloud Blog — Deploy only what you trust: introducing Binary Authorization for Google Kubernetes Engine:</strong></a> Supports: Binary Authorization as a policy enforcement point integrated into GKE deployment, and Kritis as an open-source implementation of deployment-time enforcement that uses an admission controller</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer Certification exam guide (Section 2.4, securing the deployment pipeline: software supply chain security such as Binary Authorization):</strong></a> Supports: domain and objective alignment</p>","lessons":["d2l9","d2l11"]},"e4q45":{"id":"e4q45","exam":4,"n":45,"domain":5,"html":"<p>You support a set of Java applications running in production, and every application already has Cloud Profiler and Cloud Trace enabled by default. You need a way to identify which of these applications actually require performance tuning. What should you do? (Choose two.)</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Compare the application’s wall-clock time with its CPU time; if the gap between them is large, increase its CPU resource allocation</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Compare the application’s wall-clock time with its CPU time; if the gap between them is large, increase its memory resource allocation</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Compare the application’s wall-clock time with its CPU time; if the gap between them is large, increase its local disk storage allocation</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Compare the application’s latency, wall-clock time, and CPU time together; if latency is steadily consuming the error budget while the gap between wall-clock time and CPU time is small, mark the application for optimization</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>E. Check the application’s heap usage; if usage is low, mark the application for optimization</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Compare the application’s wall-clock time with its CPU time; if the gap between them is large, increase its CPU resource allocation</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Compare the application’s latency, wall-clock time, and CPU time together; if latency is steadily consuming the error budget while the gap between wall-clock time and CPU time is small, mark the application for optimization</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Profiler reports CPU time (time actually executing on a CPU) and wall-clock time (total elapsed time, including everything the thread wasn’t executing) separately, so comparing the two tells you something different depending on which way the gap points. A must-have constraint is that the chosen signals have to actually distinguish “needs tuning” from “doesn’t,” rather than pointing to an unrelated resource. Working the chain: requirement (use Profiler/Trace data to flag applications that genuinely need tuning) → capability (the wall-clock/CPU gap reveals two distinct conditions depending on its size, and Trace’s latency/error-budget data adds the user-impact dimension) → elimination (B and C swap in resources that have no causal link to CPU/wall-time accounting, and E’s “low heap usage” has no established link to a performance problem) → decision (A and D are the two conditions where the Profiler/Trace signals actually justify action, each addressing a different root cause).</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“the gap between them is large” (A) is a technical signal that a large share of elapsed time was not spent executing on CPU, which is consistent with the application not getting enough CPU time to run when it has work ready to do.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“latency... steadily consuming the error budget” combined with “the gap... is small” (D) is a compound hard constraint: the small wall-clock/CPU gap shows the thread is genuinely CPU-bound (actively computing nearly the whole time), and the error-budget erosion confirms that this CPU-bound behavior is measurably hurting users — together these justify flagging the app.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“(Choose two.)” signals the answer set should cover two distinct, non-overlapping diagnostic conditions rather than two phrasings of the same one.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>For A: when wall-clock time is much larger than CPU time, the thread spent most of its elapsed duration not actively running on a CPU core. One concrete cause of this pattern is that the application isn’t being allocated enough CPU capacity to run as often as it’s ready to — it’s sitting ready but not scheduled. In that situation, giving the application more CPU resource allocation directly closes the gap, because the limiting factor is CPU availability, not the code itself.</p>\n<p>For D: when wall-clock time and CPU time are close together, the thread is spending nearly all of its elapsed time actively executing — it is genuinely CPU-bound, not waiting on anything. Pairing that with Cloud Trace data showing latency is steadily burning the error budget confirms this CPU-bound behavior is a real, user-facing reliability problem rather than a harmless inefficiency. That combination is exactly the signal that should mark an application for optimization work (such as algorithmic or code-level tuning), since simply adding more of the same resource won’t change code that’s already fully using what it has.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Incorrect:</strong> a wall-clock/CPU time gap reflects how much of an application’s elapsed time was spent executing versus not, which has no causal connection to how much memory it has. Increasing memory allocation changes how much data the application can hold, not how promptly its threads get CPU time, so it doesn’t address what the gap is actually showing.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Incorrect:</strong> the same reasoning applies to local disk storage. Disk capacity governs how much data can be stored and accessed on disk, not whether a thread is executing or waiting, so it has no logical relationship to a wall-clock/CPU time comparison.</p>\n<p><strong>Option E — Incorrect:</strong> heap usage reflects how much memory is currently allocated to live objects, not how efficiently or how slowly the application is running. Low heap usage simply means the application isn’t using much memory — on its own that says nothing about latency, throughput, or CPU behavior, so it isn’t a reliable signal that an application needs performance tuning.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming that any “substantial difference” between wall-clock and CPU time always means the same thing, or that it can be fixed the same way regardless of direction. A large gap and a small gap point to opposite conditions — one suggests the application isn’t getting enough CPU, the other confirms it’s already fully using the CPU it has — so the correct response depends entirely on which direction the comparison points, and in the small-gap case, the fix is code-level optimization rather than scaling a resource at all.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Application performance monitoring works by correlating multiple signals rather than reading any single metric in isolation. CPU time versus wall-clock time tells you how much of an operation’s duration was spent actively computing, which separates “waiting for a resource” problems from “genuinely compute-intensive” ones; latency and error-budget trends from distributed tracing tell you whether that behavior is actually affecting users enough to matter. Combining both is what turns raw profiling data into an actionable judgment about which applications deserve tuning effort.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team notices one service’s wall-clock time is consistently far above its CPU time and finds the service is capped at a low CPU limit even though demand has grown; raising that limit lets the service actually use the CPU time it needs, and the gap closes. A different service shows wall-clock and CPU time nearly identical, and its latency has been quietly eating into its SLO’s error budget for weeks; because the code is already fully CPU-bound, the team profiles the hot path and optimizes an inefficient algorithm rather than scaling resources, which is what actually reduces latency and stops the budget burn.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Big gap, give it CPU. Small gap plus a shrinking budget, fix the code.”</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question pairs Cloud Profiler’s CPU-time-versus-wall-time comparison with Cloud Trace’s latency/error-budget data, read the size and direction of the gap as the diagnosis: a large gap points toward insufficient CPU allocation, while a small gap combined with budget-burning latency points toward genuinely CPU-bound code that needs optimization rather than more resources.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/concept\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Profiler concepts (CPU time and wall time definitions):</strong></a> Supports: correct-answer mechanism — what CPU time versus wall time measure and why their gap is diagnostic</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 5.1, Collecting performance information in Google Cloud):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment — application performance monitoring as the basis for identifying optimization candidates</p>","lessons":["d5l8"]},"e4q46":{"id":"e4q46","exam":4,"n":46,"domain":1,"html":"<p>Your organization routes application logs from several Google Cloud projects into a single central Cloud Logging project. The security team requires that each project team be able to see only their own project’s logs, while the operations team is the only group that can see every project’s logs, and the solution needs to keep costs as low as possible. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Grant each project team access to the central logging project’s _Default view, and grant the operations team Logging Viewer access on the central logging project</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Define a custom IAM role for each project team scoped to the _Default log view in their own individual Google Cloud project, and grant the operations team viewer access on the central logging project</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create a separate log view for each project team that exposes only that team’s application logs, and grant the operations team access to the central logging project’s built-in _AllLogs view</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Export each project team’s logs to its own BigQuery dataset and grant that team access to it, then grant the operations team Logs Writer access on the central logging project</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Create a separate log view for each project team that exposes only that team’s application logs, and grant the operations team access to the central logging project’s built-in _AllLogs view</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The must-have constraints are per-team visibility scoped to each project’s own logs, full visibility for operations, and no added storage cost. Preferences like administrative simplicity matter less than meeting those three hard constraints together. Walking the chain: requirement (segment one shared log store by team without duplicating data) → capability (log views filter a subset of a single log bucket and are individually IAM-grantable, with an automatic _AllLogs view for full visibility) → elimination (A relies on a view that doesn’t scope by project, B abandons the central project and adds unnecessary custom roles, D duplicates storage and grants the wrong access direction) → decision (C uses the one mechanism purpose-built for this exact scenario at no extra storage cost).</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“central Cloud Logging project” collecting logs from multiple source projects is a technical signal pointing to a single shared log bucket, which is exactly where log views apply.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“each project team can only view their respective logs” and “only the operations team can view all the logs” are hard constraints requiring both narrow, per-project read access and one group with full access from the same store.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“minimizing costs” is a hard constraint that eliminates any option that copies or duplicates log data into a separate storage destination.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Log views are a Cloud Logging resource that filter a subset of entries within a single log bucket and can be granted individually through IAM, letting a team see only the log entries matching a filter — such as those originating from their own project — without copying or moving any underlying data. Every log bucket also automatically includes a built-in _AllLogs view exposing every entry in that bucket, which is the natural, no-setup grant for the operations team that needs complete visibility. Because both the per-team views and the _AllLogs view operate on the same underlying bucket, there is no additional storage, export, or duplication cost, which satisfies the cost-minimization requirement as well as the access-segregation requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Incorrect:</strong> a “_Default” view is not a generic view available on any bucket; Cloud Logging automatically creates a _Default view only for the _Default log bucket specifically, and that view shows all non-Data-Access audit logs in that bucket to anyone granted it. It has no built-in mechanism to scope visibility down to a single project’s logs within a shared central bucket, so it cannot deliver “only view their respective logs.”</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Different Use Case:</strong> pointing each project team back to the _Default view in their own individual project sidesteps the central logging project entirely, undermining the point of centralizing logs for controlled access in the first place. It also requires creating and maintaining a custom IAM role per team, adding management overhead that the purpose-built log views feature is designed to avoid.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> exporting logs into a separate BigQuery dataset per team duplicates every log entry’s storage, directly conflicting with “minimizing costs.” It compounds the problem by granting the operations team Logs Writer access, which is the wrong direction of permission for a team that needs to view logs, not create them.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “_Default” as a flexible, filterable view that can be scoped per project the way a custom log view can — in reality it is a single, automatically created view tied specifically to the _Default bucket, and it surfaces everything in that bucket rather than a filtered subset. If the requirement instead were about independent retention or export policies per team rather than simply viewing logs, per-team exports (closer to option D) could become more relevant, though the cost trade-off would still need to be weighed.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Logging separates storage (log buckets) from access control (log views). A single bucket can hold logs from many sources, and log views let you carve that bucket into filtered, independently access-controlled slices without moving or copying the underlying data. This lets an organization centralize storage for operational simplicity while still enforcing least-privilege, per-team visibility — the same general pattern of separating “where data lives” from “who can see what part of it” that recurs across Google Cloud’s access-control design.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team aggregates application logs from dozens of projects into one central bucket using an aggregated log sink, then creates one log view per application team filtered to that team’s source project, and grants the operations team the bucket’s _AllLogs view. The result is that each application team gets a focused, noise-free view limited to its own logs, reducing accidental exposure of other teams’ data, while operations retains the full cross-project picture needed for incident response — all without any additional storage spend beyond the single central bucket.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“One bucket, many windows” — log views slice a single bucket into per-team visibility without copying any data.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks how to let different teams see only their own slice of logs stored in one shared bucket without added cost, think log views paired with the automatic _AllLogs view for full visibility, rather than exporting to a separate store or relying on a bucket’s fixed default view.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/logs-views\"><strong>Google Cloud — About log views (Cloud Logging):</strong></a> Supports: correct-answer mechanism, including the documented example of centralizing multi-project logs with one log view per project, and the automatically created _AllLogs and _Default views</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 1.1, Designing the overall resource hierarchy for an organization):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment — multi-project monitoring and logging, and IAM roles and organization-level policies</p>","lessons":["d1l4"]},"e4q47":{"id":"e4q47","exam":4,"n":47,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your organization’s shift-left security initiative requires every team to add guardrails to their Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) clusters so that only trusted, approved container images can be deployed. Which approach best satisfies this requirement?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Turn on Artifact Analysis for your Artifact Registry repositories to scan container images for known common vulnerabilities and exposures (CVEs)</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Require Binary Authorization to attest container images as part of your CI/CD pipeline before they can be deployed</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Set up Identity and Access Management (IAM) policies that enforce a least-privilege model across your GKE clusters</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Run Falco or Prisma Cloud on your GKE clusters to monitor running Pods for vulnerabilities</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Require Binary Authorization to attest container images as part of your CI/CD pipeline before they can be deployed</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The must-have constraint is that only trusted/approved images may be deployed, enforced as a guardrail — meaning automatically, not advisorially — and “shift left” means the check has to happen before or at deploy time, not after. Detecting CVEs, applying least privilege, and runtime monitoring are all useful, but none of them stop an unapproved image from reaching the cluster. Tracing requirement → capability → elimination → decision: the requirement is “block untrusted images at deploy”; Binary Authorization supplies a deploy-time admission policy requiring attestations; A only scans and reports, C only governs who can act, D only observes after the fact; so B is the only option that gates the deployment itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“guard rails... to only allow the deployment of trusted and approved images” is a hard constraint pointing to an enforcement/admission-control mechanism rather than a detection tool.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“shift left on security” is a sequence/timing signal: the control must act before or at deployment, which rules out anything that only observes afterward.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“on all the GKE clusters” signals an org-wide, policy-based guardrail rather than a one-off manual check in a single pipeline.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Binary Authorization is a deploy-time policy engine for GKE (and other supported platforms) that only allows an image to run if it carries attestations proving it passed required steps, such as a build or vulnerability-scan gate earlier in the CI/CD pipeline. Because the policy is enforced by the admission controller at deploy time, an image lacking the required attestation is rejected before it ever starts — which is precisely what “guardrails to only allow trusted and approved images” is asking for, and it acts at the earliest possible point in the deployment lifecycle, satisfying “shift left.”</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Artifact Analysis scans images in Artifact Registry and surfaces CVEs, which is valuable input, but scanning by itself does not stop an unapproved image from being deployed; nothing blocks the deployment unless those findings are wired into an enforcement mechanism such as Binary Authorization.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> least-privilege IAM policies control which identities are allowed to perform which actions, such as who may deploy to a cluster. That says nothing about whether a specific image is trusted; a fully authorized engineer could still deploy an unapproved image.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> Falco and Prisma Cloud are runtime security tools that detect and alert on suspicious behavior in Pods that are already running. By definition this happens after deployment, which satisfies neither “guardrail that prevents deployment” nor “shift left.”</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating vulnerability scanning (Option A) as equivalent to a deployment guardrail. Scanning answers “is this image risky?” while the question asks for a mechanism that answers “can this image run at all?” The distinction is enforcement versus detection. If the requirement were instead “identify which images contain known CVEs” rather than “only allow approved images to deploy,” Option A would become the correct answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Shift-left security means moving verification earlier in the software delivery lifecycle so problems are caught before production rather than after. In practice this splits into two complementary capabilities: scanning/detection (find issues early, e.g., CVE scanning) and enforcement/admission control (block anything that hasn’t proven it meets policy, e.g., Binary Authorization). A guardrail, by definition, belongs to the enforcement category — it has to be able to say “no.”</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team configures its CI/CD pipeline so that after a build passes vulnerability scanning and tests, Cloud Build signs an attestation; a Binary Authorization policy on the GKE clusters then requires that attestation before admitting any Pod. The operational result is that an engineer can no longer deploy an untested or unscanned image directly to production even by accident, which reduces the risk of a known-vulnerable or unverified image causing an incident and gives InfoSec an auditable, cluster-wide enforcement point instead of relying on manual review.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Guardrail means gate, not gauge” — if the requirement is to block deployment, think Binary Authorization; if it’s to measure or report risk, think scanning tools like Artifact Analysis.</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks for a control that only allows trusted/approved artifacts to be deployed, look for an admission-control or attestation mechanism rather than a scanning, IAM, or runtime-monitoring tool — those inform or restrict activity around the deployment but don’t gate the deployment decision itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs/overview\"><strong>Google Cloud — Binary Authorization overview:</strong></a> Supports: correct-answer mechanism — deploy-time attestation and admission-control enforcement on GKE</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/artifact-analysis/docs/container-analysis\"><strong>Google Cloud — Artifact Analysis and vulnerability scanning:</strong></a> Supports: why Option A is a scanning/detection capability rather than a deployment guardrail (also confirms the current product name, formerly Container Analysis)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 2.4, Securing the deployment pipeline):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment — Binary Authorization is explicitly named under securing the deployment pipeline</p>","lessons":["d2l9"]},"e4q48":{"id":"e4q48","exam":4,"n":48,"domain":4,"html":"<p>Your team supports production systems that frequently become unhealthy and are automatically restarted within a minute, yet you still receive an alert every time this happens. You want to reduce the risk of staff burnout while staying consistent with Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) practices. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Stop sending alerts for conditions that don’t require a human to take action</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Adjust the service’s SLO so the error budget isn’t consumed by these events</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Route the alerts to on-call engineers across different time zones</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. File an incident report every time one of these alerts fires</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Stop sending alerts for conditions that don’t require a human to take action</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard constraint is that the system already resolves the problem on its own within a minute, so there is nothing a human can actually do when the alert fires — it can only be acknowledged and dismissed. The preference is to protect genuine incident-response capacity rather than erode it further. Walking the chain: requirement (cut unnecessary interruptions while staying within real SRE discipline) → capability (SRE alerting philosophy pages humans only for conditions requiring action) → elimination (B changes the reliability target instead of the alert, C spreads the same non-actionable load across more people, D adds more non-actionable work) → decision (A removes the root cause: paging humans for something they neither need nor are able to fix).</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“automatically restarted within a minute” is a hard constraint showing the condition already self-remediates, so a human alert adds nothing actionable.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“alerts... every time this happens” paired with “reduce the risk of staff burnout” signals the core problem is alert volume/noise, not reliability or staffing capacity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“staying consistent with Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) practices” is an exclusion cue: the answer must align with the SRE principle that paging is reserved for conditions needing human judgment or action.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Core SRE monitoring philosophy holds that a page should interrupt a human only when the situation requires timely judgment or action; everything else belongs in logs, dashboards, or tickets rather than the paging path. Because these systems self-heal automatically in under a minute, there is nothing for an engineer to do when the alert fires — it is pure noise. Stopping (or down-routing) alerts for self-healing, non-actionable conditions directly reduces the interruption load on staff, which is the mechanism that prevents burnout while remaining aligned with SRE practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> adjusting the SLO so the error budget isn’t consumed by these events changes how reliability is measured, not how often a human is paged. The alerts keep firing and staff keep being interrupted; loosening an SLO purely to protect the error budget also undermines the SLO’s purpose as an honest reliability signal rather than something tuned to avoid discomfort.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> distributing alerts to engineers in other time zones is a follow-the-sun staffing pattern that can help on-call coverage generally, but it does nothing to reduce the total number of non-actionable alerts — it just spreads the same noise across more people, redistributing burnout risk instead of reducing it.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> filing an incident report for every alert adds manual work on top of an already non-actionable event, increasing toil instead of reducing it — moving further from the stated goal rather than toward it.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is mistaking “document every occurrence” (Option D) or “share the load” (Option C) for sound operational hygiene, when the real SRE lever is reducing alert volume at the source by reserving paging for actionable conditions. If the scenario instead described a self-healing mechanism that was unreliable or masking a worsening trend, suppressing the alert outright would be the wrong move, and tracking occurrences as a metric (distinct from an incident report per event) would matter more.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>In SRE monitoring philosophy, every page should be actionable, every page response should require intelligence, and pages should be reserved for problems that are either happening now or clearly about to happen — not routine, self-resolving events. Conditions a system already auto-remediates are, by definition, not actionable for a human on receipt, so they belong in logs, dashboards, or aggregated metrics rather than the paging pipeline. Keeping the signal-to-noise ratio of pages high is itself a toil-reduction and burnout-prevention practice.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team notices their on-call rotation is paged dozens of times a night for Pods that crash and automatically restart within seconds. By reclassifying those events as non-paging log entries — while still tracking their frequency as a metric that can trigger a real investigation if the rate spikes — the team cuts nighttime pages dramatically. The operational result is fewer interrupted nights for on-call engineers, lower attrition risk, and a cleaner signal so that when a page does fire, it’s trusted and acted on quickly rather than reflexively dismissed.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“If a human can’t do anything about it, it shouldn’t page a human.”</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question describes alerts firing for conditions a system already resolves on its own, look for the option that stops paging on non-actionable conditions rather than one that changes reliability targets, redistributes the same noise, or adds more manual work — SRE treats a high signal-to-noise alerting pipeline as a core defense against toil and burnout.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/\"><strong>Google SRE Book — Monitoring Distributed Systems:</strong></a> Supports: correct-answer mechanism — the “every page should be actionable” alerting philosophy</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/practical-alerting/\"><strong>Google SRE Book — Practical Alerting:</strong></a> Supports: why non-actionable, self-healing conditions belong outside the paging path (logs/tickets vs. pages)</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 4.3, Managing metrics, dashboards, and alerts):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment — configuring alerting and alerting policies</p>","lessons":["d4l9"]},"e4q49":{"id":"e4q49","exam":4,"n":49,"domain":2,"html":"<p>Your organization runs Jenkins on a Google Cloud Compute Engine VM for CI/CD and wants to extend its pipelines to manage infrastructure as code with Terraform. The Terraform runs that execute on this Jenkins instance need to be authorized to create Google Cloud resources, and you want to follow Google-recommended practices for granting that authorization. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Verify that the Jenkins VM has an attached service account granted the appropriate Identity and Access Management (IAM) permissions</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Configure a Terraform module that has Secret Manager supply the authentication credentials at runtime</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create a separate service account, download its key file, and set its value in the GOOGLE_CREDENTIALS environment variable on the Jenkins server</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Add a gcloud auth application-default login step to the Jenkins job before the Terraform commands run</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Verify that the Jenkins VM has an attached service account granted the appropriate Identity and Access Management (IAM) permissions</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard constraint is that the authorization method must follow Google-recommended practice, not merely “work.” Terraform on Google Cloud authenticates through Application Default Credentials (ADC), and when Terraform runs on a Compute Engine VM, ADC automatically picks up the VM’s attached service account via the metadata server — no key file or login step required. Walking the chain: requirement (authorize Terraform to create resources, the Google-recommended way) → capability (ADC + attached service account is Google’s documented preferred method for production code on Google Cloud) → elimination (B adds an unnecessary credential-retrieval layer, C introduces a long-lived downloaded key, D is designed for interactive human sign-in) → decision (A simply confirms the native, keyless mechanism is correctly configured).</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“authorized to create Google Cloud resources” is the core requirement: the solution must grant Terraform an identity with the right permissions, not just make Terraform technically runnable.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“follow Google-recommended practices” is an exclusion cue that rules out any option involving a manually downloaded, long-lived key or an interactive login flow.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Jenkins running on Google Cloud VM instances” is a technical signal that the workload already runs on Compute Engine, which is exactly the environment where attached-service-account authentication applies natively.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Google documents Application Default Credentials as the recommended way to authenticate Terraform to Google Cloud, and specifically states that attaching a user-managed service account is the preferred way to supply ADC with credentials for production code running on Google Cloud. Because the Jenkins VM is already a Compute Engine instance, Terraform executing there automatically discovers and uses that attached service account’s credentials through the metadata server. Confirming the VM has an attached service account with the correct IAM permissions is therefore all that’s needed — no credential file to create, store, or rotate.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> having a Terraform module pull credentials from Secret Manager at runtime still means managing a stored secret and the logic to retrieve it, which is more moving parts than necessary when the VM can already supply credentials natively through its attached identity; it doesn’t match Google’s stated preferred method for this exact scenario.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> downloading a service account key and placing it in the GOOGLE_CREDENTIALS environment variable is a documented authentication method, but Google’s own guidance treats service account keys as the less secure, fallback option for cases where attaching a service account isn’t possible (local development, on-premises, other clouds) — not for a workload already running on a GCE VM, so it fails the “Google-recommended practices” requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Incorrect:</strong> gcloud auth application-default login creates local ADC credentials tied to a human user’s sign-in session for development workstations; it isn’t intended for unattended, automated CI/CD execution and doesn’t represent how production workloads on Google Cloud are meant to authenticate.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming that because options B, C, and D all result in Terraform being able to authenticate, they’re equally valid — the question isn’t asking what works, it’s asking what Google recommends. The moment a scenario already places the workload on a GCE VM, that’s the signal to reach for attached-service-account credentials rather than any form of manually managed or interactive credential. If the Jenkins server instead ran outside Google Cloud (on-premises or another cloud), a downloaded key or Workload Identity Federation would become the relevant choice instead.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Application Default Credentials is Google Cloud’s unified strategy for letting client libraries and tools like Terraform find credentials automatically based on where code is running, without changing application code between environments. On Google Cloud compute resources, the preferred credential source is an attached service account resolved through the instance metadata server; everywhere else (local workstations, on-premises, other clouds), ADC falls back to user credentials, impersonation, downloaded keys, or Workload Identity Federation. Recognizing which environment you’re in tells you which ADC source is “recommended” for that case.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team migrates their Terraform runs from a developer’s laptop (using a downloaded service account key) to a Jenkins job running on a GCE VM. By removing the key file and instead attaching a purpose-built service account to the VM with only the IAM roles Terraform needs, they eliminate a long-lived credential that could leak through logs, source control, or a compromised laptop, and they gain centralized control: revoking or auditing access now means managing the VM’s service account rather than hunting down who has a copy of the key.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“On a GCP VM, let the VM’s own identity do the work — don’t hand Terraform a key it doesn’t need.”</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a workload authenticating to Google Cloud is already running on a Google Cloud compute resource, the Google-recommended answer is almost always an attached service account with Application Default Credentials — treat downloaded keys and interactive login commands as fallback options reserved for environments outside Google Cloud.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/terraform/authentication\"><strong>Google Cloud — Authentication for Terraform:</strong></a> Supports: correct-answer mechanism — ADC as the recommended authentication method and attached service accounts as the preferred credential source for production code on Google Cloud; also clarifies why the downloaded-key and <code>gcloud auth application-default login</code> options are not the best fit here</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 2.3, Managing pipeline configuration and secrets):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment — build versus runtime secret injection and configuration/secret management in CI/CD pipelines</p>","lessons":["d2l2","d2l10"]},"e4q50":{"id":"e4q50","exam":4,"n":50,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You’re preparing to launch a new production service that will autoscale through a managed instance group and run across several regions. Each instance will require a substantial amount of resources, and you need to plan for that capacity before launch. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Monitor Cloud Trace output over time to determine the right instance sizing</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Configure the managed instance group to use the n2-highcpu-96 machine type</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Deploy the service across multiple regions behind an internal load balancer that routes traffic between them</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Confirm that the instance group’s resource requirements fit within the project’s available quota in each target region</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Confirm that the instance group’s resource requirements fit within the project’s available quota in each target region</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard constraint is that this question is specifically about capacity planning, not architecture choice or sizing methodology. Walking the chain: requirement (plan for capacity for a resource-heavy, autoscaling, multi-region deployment) → capability (Google Cloud enforces allocation quotas — CPU, instances, IP addresses, and the instance-group resources themselves — at the regional level, and scaling cannot exceed what a region’s quota allows) → elimination (A addresses latency monitoring rather than capacity, B picks an implementation detail that was never justified by the scenario and still doesn’t confirm availability, C restates an already-given requirement and adds an unaddressed routing decision) → decision (D is the step that directly verifies the deployment can actually obtain the resources it needs in every target region).</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“need to plan for capacity” is the hard constraint that points directly at capacity-planning practice, not at sizing, routing, or monitoring tooling.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“a large number of resources for each instance” combined with “multiple regions” signals that per-region resource consumption could be substantial, making quota exhaustion a realistic risk.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“should be deployed across multiple regions” and “automatically scale using a managed instance group” are design decisions the scenario has already made, so any option that simply restates them isn’t answering what’s being asked.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud enforces allocation quotas at the regional level — CPU quota, instance-group resource quota, IP addresses, and disk are all capped per region, and using an instance group requires having enough available quota for every resource it consumes. Because this service needs a large amount of resources per instance and will autoscale across several regions, the amount of quota consumed in each region could be significant, and an autoscaler that hits a quota ceiling simply stops growing rather than failing loudly. Confirming that each target region’s available quota can support the planned resource requirements before launch is what capacity planning concretely means here, and it’s the one option that directly prevents a silent scaling ceiling in production.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong> Cloud Trace reports on request latency and the behavior of distributed calls; it has no role in determining machine sizing or confirming quota availability, so monitoring it doesn’t address planning for capacity.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> choosing a specific machine type is a real configuration step that has to happen at some point, but nothing in the scenario establishes this workload is specifically CPU-optimized, and selecting a machine type says nothing about whether that scale of resource is actually obtainable in each region — it isn’t capacity planning on its own.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> the scenario already states the service will run across multiple regions, so repeating that isn’t new guidance, and adding an internal load balancer introduces a traffic-routing decision intended for private, internal-only traffic that isn’t established as appropriate here; neither part addresses resource or capacity planning.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is reaching for an architecture or tooling decision — a machine type, a load balancer, a tracing tool — when the question explicitly asks about capacity planning, which in Google Cloud’s documented sense centers on quotas, limits, and reservations. If the scenario instead asked how to pick the right machine shape for a known workload profile, option B’s reasoning would become the relevant one; here, the stated task is specifically to plan for capacity.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Capacity planning on Google Cloud means confirming, ahead of scaling, that the quotas and limits governing a project in each target region can actually support the resource consumption you expect. Because quotas for things like CPUs, IP addresses, and instance-group resources are enforced per region, a workload that scales smoothly in one region can still stall in another if that region’s quota was never checked or raised in advance.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team launches an autoscaling, resource-heavy service into a new region and finds the managed instance group stalls well below its target size during a traffic spike — not because of an application bug, but because the project’s CPU quota in that region was never raised to match the expected scale. After requesting a quota increase ahead of the next regional launch, the same spike is absorbed without incident, which is why checking quota is treated as a pre-launch capacity-planning step rather than something discovered during an outage.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Before you scale wide, check what each region will actually let you have.”</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question explicitly asks about planning for capacity for an autoscaling workload spread across multiple regions, look for the option addressing project quotas and limits — that’s Google Cloud’s documented meaning of capacity planning, distinct from sizing, routing, or monitoring decisions.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/quotas\"><strong>Google Cloud — Resource quotas for Compute Engine:</strong></a> Supports: correct-answer mechanism — instance groups require available quota for every resource they consume, and quotas are enforced regionally</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\">Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 3.2, Managing service lifecycle): </a>Supports: domain alignment — capacity planning (quotas, limits, reservations) and autoscaling with managed instance groups</p>","lessons":["d3l7"]},"e4q51":{"id":"e4q51","exam":4,"n":51,"domain":2,"html":"<p>While reviewing your delivery pipeline in Google Cloud Deploy, you determine that you need to cut down the toil built into the pipeline and shorten how long an end-to-end deployment takes to complete. What should you do? (Choose two.)</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Set up a trigger that notifies the responsible team as soon as a manual intervention step is reached</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Break the pipeline’s automation steps down into a larger number of smaller tasks</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Write a script that automates how the delivery pipeline itself is created in Google Cloud Deploy</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Bring in additional engineers to work through the manual steps</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>E. Automate the promotion approval that moves a release from the development environment to the test environment</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Set up a trigger that notifies the responsible team as soon as a manual intervention step is reached</strong></p>\n<p><strong>E. Automate the promotion approval that moves a release from the development environment to the test environment</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The two hard constraints are that toil must actually be reduced (not redistributed) and that end-to-end deployment time must actually shorten. Walking the chain: requirement (cut toil and wall-clock time together) → capability (Cloud Deploy supports both removing an approval step entirely for low-risk promotions, and notifying a team the instant a still-required manual step is reached) → elimination (B restructures already-automated steps without removing manual work, C automates a different, infrequent activity — pipeline setup, not per-release execution, D keeps the same manual work in place and simply adds people to it) → decision (A and E are the two documented levers that actually shrink either the manual-work surface or the wait time around it).</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“cut down the toil” is a hard constraint that rules out any option that keeps the same manual work in place, however it’s staffed or restructured.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“shorten how long an end-to-end deployment takes to complete” is a second hard constraint requiring the chosen actions to reduce actual wall-clock duration, not just configuration effort.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“(Choose two)” signals the intended pair should cover two distinct categories of delay: a step that can be removed entirely, and a step that still needs a human but shouldn’t sit waiting.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A: notifying the responsible team the moment a manual step is reached closes the gap between “work is ready” and “someone notices,” which is itself a form of toil (the overhead of checking or polling for whether action is needed), and it directly cuts the dead time the pipeline spends waiting for a human to notice — shortening the end-to-end duration even for steps that genuinely still require a person.</p>\n<p>E: automating the approval that promotes a release from development to test removes a manual gate entirely for a lower-risk promotion, eliminating both the toil of a person clicking approve and the wait time for that approval to happen — exactly the kind of safe-to-automate step continuous delivery pipelines are designed to remove.</p>\n<p>Together, these cover different parts of the pipeline rather than overlapping: E eliminates an entire manual approval step at one promotion stage, while A addresses steps that still require a human (such as a higher-risk later promotion) by minimizing how long the pipeline waits on that person. Between them, the set addresses both the parts of the pipeline that can be automated away and the parts that still need a human but shouldn’t be left idle.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Incorrect:</strong> subdividing automation steps into smaller tasks doesn’t remove any manual work and doesn’t inherently shorten the pipeline; it can just as easily add coordination overhead between more steps, and nothing about step granularity on its own reduces toil or wall-clock duration.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> scripting the creation of the delivery pipeline automates a setup or configuration activity that typically happens once, or only when the pipeline definition itself changes — not the recurring, per-release toil and delay the question is asking about.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> adding more engineers to the manual steps keeps the same manual work in place and simply spreads it across more people; it doesn’t eliminate the work itself, so it directly fails the “reduce toil” requirement rather than satisfying it.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “add more people” or “add more granular steps” as if they address toil, when the defining feature of toil is manual, repetitive work that can be automated away — the fix is removing the need for a human, or speeding up the handoff when a human is genuinely unavoidable, not staffing it up or restructuring steps that are already automated. If a step truly can’t be automated yet, such as a production approval requiring a compliance sign-off, the right lever is still minimizing wait time with a notification, not adding people to it.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Reducing toil in a delivery pipeline means either eliminating a manual step by automating it outright, or, where a human genuinely must be involved, minimizing the delay between “action needed” and “action taken.” Cloud Deploy supports both patterns directly: approval requirements can be automated away for low-risk promotions by driving the next promotion from a Pub/Sub event (such as a successful test run), and for steps that still require a human, Cloud Deploy’s Pub/Sub notifications — including its dedicated approvals topic — let a team be notified the instant action is needed instead of relying on someone to check manually.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team configures their Cloud Deploy pipeline so that promotion from development to test happens automatically once tests pass, removing a manual approval that used to sit idle overnight waiting for someone to notice. For the still-required production approval, they subscribe their on-call system to the pipeline’s approval notifications so the approver is paged the moment a release is ready, cutting what used to be a multi-hour wait for someone to check their email down to minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Automate the gate you can remove; page the human for the gate you can’t.”</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks how to cut toil and shorten end-to-end deployment time together, look for one option that removes a manual approval step entirely through automation and another that minimizes the wait time for approvals that still genuinely require a human — adding staff or restructuring already-automated steps addresses neither goal.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/promote-release\"><strong>Google Cloud — Promote a release in Cloud Deploy:</strong></a> Supports: correct-answer mechanism for Option A — the clouddeploy-approvals Pub/Sub topic notifies teams or systems the moment a rollout needs manual approval</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/integrating\"><strong>Google Cloud — Integrating Cloud Deploy with other systems:</strong></a> Supports: correct-answer mechanism for Option E — using Pub/Sub notifications (such as successful test results) to automatically promote a release to the next target</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\">Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 2.1, Designing pipelines): </a>Supports: domain alignment — CI/CD pipeline triggers and configuring deployment processes such as approval flows</p>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e4q52":{"id":"e4q52","exam":4,"n":52,"domain":3,"html":"<p>An application running on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), made up of several microservices deployed with Deployments and Services, has one microservice whose Pods start returning 403 errors once they’ve been running for more than five hours. Your development team expects it will take about a month to fix the underlying cause, and in the meantime you need to keep the service operating normally, using the fewest steps and following Google-recommended practice. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Create a scheduled job that terminates any Pod that has been running for more than five hours</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Add an HTTP liveness probe to the microservice’s Deployment</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Have someone watch the Pods and manually terminate any that have been running for more than five hours</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Set up an alert that notifies you whenever a Pod starts returning 403 errors</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Add an HTTP liveness probe to the microservice’s Deployment</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard constraint is that whatever solution is chosen has to keep working automatically, unattended, for about a month, using as little new infrastructure as possible. Walking the chain: requirement (keep a known, time-based failure from affecting operations, with the fewest steps) → capability (Kubernetes’ liveness probe is a built-in mechanism precisely for containers that degrade after running for a while, triggering an automatic restart) → elimination (A duplicates that built-in capability with a separate custom resource, C is unscalable manual toil, D only reports the problem without fixing it) → decision (B is the single, declarative addition that uses Kubernetes’ own self-healing feature exactly as designed).</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“Pod returns 403 errors after... running for more than five hours” is a technical signal describing a container that degrades over time and needs a restart to recover — the exact condition liveness probes exist to detect and fix.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“will not be resolved for a month” paired with “ensure continued operations” is a hard constraint requiring the solution to keep working automatically and unattended for weeks, ruling out anything that needs a person to keep intervening.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“fewest number of steps” and “Google-recommended practices” together form an exclusion cue favoring Kubernetes’ built-in, declarative self-healing feature over custom external automation.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>An HTTP liveness probe lets the kubelet periodically check whether a container is still healthy; once the probe starts failing, Kubernetes automatically restarts that container. This matches the documented pattern for long-running processes that work correctly at first but degrade after running for a while and need a periodic restart to keep functioning. Configuring the probe is a single, declarative addition to the existing Deployment’s Pod spec, requiring no new resources, no custom scripts, and no ongoing human attention — giving the service a self-healing cycle that can run unattended for the month it takes the development team to ship the real fix.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> a scheduled job that finds and deletes Pods older than five hours can produce a similar outcome, but it duplicates functionality Kubernetes already provides natively through liveness probes, and it means building and maintaining a separate resource with its own logic and permissions — more steps than the built-in mechanism requires.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Incorrect:</strong> manually watching Pods and terminating them is pure human toil that doesn’t scale, and it directly conflicts with both “fewest number of steps” and the month-long duration this workaround would need to survive unattended.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Incorrect:</strong> an alert only notifies that the problem occurred; it doesn’t restart anything or restore the Pod to a healthy state, so on its own it doesn’t “ensure continued operations” — someone would still have to manually act on every alert for a month.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is reaching for custom automation, like a scheduled job, or for observability, like an alert, as if either were a complete fix, when Kubernetes already has a built-in, declarative mechanism purpose-built for “a container that degrades after running for a while and needs a restart.” Any option that adds infrastructure Kubernetes doesn’t require, or that only reports on the problem instead of fixing it, misses what “fewest steps” and “Google-recommended practice” are pointing at here.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Kubernetes’ self-healing model relies on probes to decide when a container needs to be restarted, rather than requiring external tooling to watch and intervene. A liveness probe is specifically designed for processes that run fine initially but can enter a broken state over time, since it keeps checking the container’s health on an ongoing basis and lets the kubelet act the moment the probe starts failing, with no outside system required.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team discovers that one of their services develops a slow memory leak and starts returning errors after running for several hours, with a proper fix still weeks away. Adding a liveness probe against a simple health endpoint lets Kubernetes automatically cycle affected Pods before users are impacted, buying the team time to ship the real fix without anyone having to babysit the cluster or build throwaway automation in the meantime.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“A container that breaks after a while needs a liveness probe, not a babysitter or a timer job.”</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a GKE Pod reliably becomes unhealthy after running for a while and a permanent fix is still some time away, the Google-recommended, fewest-steps answer is almost always an HTTP liveness probe — it lets Kubernetes restart the Pod automatically rather than relying on custom jobs, manual monitoring, or alerts that only report the problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/configure-pod-container/configure-liveness-readiness-startup-probes/\"><strong>Kubernetes documentation — Configure Liveness, Readiness and Startup Probes:</strong></a> Supports: correct-answer mechanism — liveness probes automatically restarting containers that become unhealthy after running for a while</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 3.3, Mitigating incident impact on users):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment — keeping a service operating while a known issue’s root cause is still being resolved</p>","lessons":["d3l6"]},"e4q53":{"id":"e4q53","exam":4,"n":53,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You build and operate client-facing applications on Cloud Run and Cloud Run functions. Your client needs every log to remain available for a full year so they can later import it into their own logging platform, and you must keep required code changes to a minimum. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Modify every Cloud Run image and every Cloud Run function so that it writes logs to both Cloud Logging and the client’s logging service, and open the VPC firewall ports needed to send that traffic</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Create a Pub/Sub topic and subscription along with a logging sink that routes all logs into the topic, then give the client access to the topic so they can pull the logs</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create a Cloud Storage bucket with the necessary VPC firewall rules, and modify every Cloud Run image and every Cloud Run function so that it writes its logs to a file in that bucket</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Create a custom log bucket with its retention set to 365 days, configure a logging sink that routes logs into that bucket, and give the client access to the bucket so they can retrieve the logs</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Create a custom log bucket with its retention set to 365 days, configure a logging sink that routes logs into that bucket, and give the client access to the bucket so they can retrieve the logs</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two hard constraints must be satisfied together: the logs need to stay available for a full year, and the solution can’t require rewriting application code. Walking the chain: requirement (one year of log availability, minimal code changes) → capability (Cloud Run and Cloud Run functions already send stdout/stderr output to Cloud Logging automatically, and a custom log bucket can be set to retain data for exactly 365 days, with a logging sink routing logs into it entirely at the platform level) → elimination (A and C both require touching every service’s code to add new logging destinations, B requires no code changes but can’t retain messages anywhere near a year) → decision (D is the only option where both constraints are met by configuration alone).</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“available for one year” is a hard, specific duration constraint that the chosen storage mechanism must actually be capable of supporting.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“MUST minimize required code changes” is a hard constraint that eliminates any option requiring every Cloud Run image and Cloud Run function to be modified.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“import the logs into their logging service” signals the client needs read access to a durable store of the log data on their own schedule, not a live push integration into their specific external system.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Run and Cloud Run functions already write anything sent to stdout or stderr into Cloud Logging automatically, with no application code changes required. Creating a custom log bucket, setting its retention to 365 days, and configuring a logging sink to route logs into that bucket all happen at the platform and infrastructure layer, completely outside the application code, so required code changes stay at zero. Granting the client access to the bucket lets them retrieve and import a full year of logs whenever suits their own process, satisfying every stated requirement at once.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> modifying every Cloud Run image and every Cloud Run function to add a second logging destination, plus managing the networking that destination needs, requires touching every service’s code and configuration — directly conflicting with the instruction to minimize required code changes.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> a logging sink into Pub/Sub requires no code changes, but Pub/Sub message retention tops out at 31 days even at its maximum configurable duration, far short of the one year the client needs — logs would be purged from the topic or subscription long before a year’s worth could be imported.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Violates Requirement:</strong> having every Cloud Run image and Cloud Run function write its own log file into a storage bucket means rewriting each service’s logging logic from scratch, which is exactly the kind of code change the requirement is trying to avoid, and it ignores that Cloud Logging already captures this output for free.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “minimize code changes” and “available for one year” as separate problems that can be solved independently, when the correct answer has to satisfy both together with a single, code-free configuration. An option that solves one constraint well — no code changes, as with Pub/Sub — but silently fails the other, with retention far short of a year, is just as wrong as an option that nails the retention duration but requires rewriting every service.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Logging already captures stdout and stderr output from Cloud Run and Cloud Run functions without any application involvement, and routing and retaining that data is handled entirely through platform configuration — log buckets for storage and retention, and logging sinks for routing logs into a bucket, a Pub/Sub topic, or another destination. Recognizing that the logs already exist in Cloud Logging by default, and that retention and routing are infrastructure concerns rather than application concerns, points directly at a sink-and-bucket solution instead of any approach that touches application code.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team supporting dozens of Cloud Run services for a client needs to guarantee a year of log history without asking every service owner to change their code. By standing up one custom log bucket with 365-day retention and a single sink that captures logs from all the services, the team meets the requirement with one piece of infrastructure to maintain, and the client simply pulls from that bucket on whatever schedule suits their own import process.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Logs already flow into Cloud Logging for free — retention and sharing are a bucket-and-sink problem, not a code problem.”</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks for long-term log retention from Cloud Run or Cloud Run functions with minimal code changes, look for a custom log bucket with the needed retention period paired with a logging sink — Cloud Logging already captures the logs automatically, so the work belongs entirely on the infrastructure side.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/buckets\"><strong>Google Cloud — Configure log buckets:</strong></a> Supports: correct-answer mechanism — custom log buckets with retention configurable from 1 to 3650 days, routed to by a logging sink</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/subscription-message-retention\"><strong>Google Cloud — Configure message retention for a Pub/Sub subscription:</strong></a> Supports: why Option B fails — Pub/Sub message retention maxes out at 31 days, far short of one year</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 4.2, Managing and analyzing logs):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment — exporting and retaining logs</p>","lessons":["d4l1","d4l2"]},"e4q54":{"id":"e4q54","exam":4,"n":54,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You want to share a custom Cloud Monitoring dashboard with a partner team. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Send the partner team the dashboard’s URL so they can use it to create their own copy</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Export the underlying metrics to BigQuery, build an equivalent dashboard in Data Studio, and share that new dashboard with the partner team</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Copy the Monitoring Query Language (MQL) query used in the dashboard and send that query to the partner team</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Export the dashboard’s JSON definition and send that JSON file to the partner team</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Export the dashboard’s JSON definition and send that JSON file to the partner team</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard constraint is handing over the complete dashboard — its widgets, queries, and layout — to a team that doesn’t already have standing access to the project that owns it. Walking the chain: requirement (transfer a full, working dashboard to an outside team) → capability (Cloud Monitoring lets you export a dashboard’s entire configuration as JSON, which can be pasted or imported into a new dashboard in another project) → elimination (A assumes access the partner team likely doesn’t have, B rebuilds something different in another tool, C captures only a fragment of the dashboard’s configuration) → decision (D is the one option that transfers the actual, complete dashboard definition).</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“partner team” is a contextual signal suggesting a team outside your project’s existing IAM boundary, which rules out any approach that silently assumes they already have, or should be given, direct access to your project.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“share a Cloud Monitoring custom dashboard” — not “share the underlying metrics” or “build a new reporting view” — is a hard constraint: the goal is to hand over the dashboard itself, not a subset of it or a reimplementation elsewhere.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The option describing a “JSON definition” lines up directly with Cloud Monitoring’s own documented export/import mechanism for moving a dashboard between projects.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Monitoring custom dashboards can be exported as a complete JSON definition that captures every widget, query, and layout choice in the dashboard. Sending that JSON file to the partner team lets them paste or import it into a new dashboard in their own project, recreating the exact same dashboard without requiring you to grant them IAM access to your project or rebuild anything by hand. This matches Google’s documented path for moving a dashboard from one project to another.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Incorrect:</strong> a raw dashboard URL only works for someone who already has viewing permissions on the project that owns the dashboard. A partner team on a different project typically doesn’t have that access, and simply sending a link neither grants it nor otherwise lets them create their own copy.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> exporting metrics to BigQuery and rebuilding the visualization in Data Studio produces a different artifact, not the original dashboard, and introduces considerably more setup — a metrics export pipeline plus a report built from scratch — than the task of sharing an existing dashboard calls for.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Incorrect:</strong> a custom dashboard is typically made up of multiple widgets, each with its own query, layout, and formatting. Copying a single MQL query only captures a fragment of that configuration and loses everything else that makes up the dashboard.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is treating “sharing a dashboard” as equivalent to “sharing a link,” the way you might with a typical collaborative document, when Cloud Monitoring dashboards are scoped to a project and governed by that project’s IAM. The documented way to hand a dashboard’s actual configuration to someone outside that boundary is to export its definition, not merely point them at its URL.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>A Cloud Monitoring custom dashboard is a saved configuration — widgets, queries, layout — rather than a dataset. Moving that configuration to a different project is a configuration-transfer problem, which Cloud Monitoring solves by letting you export and import the dashboard’s JSON definition, rather than an access-control or data-export problem, which is what granting a URL or standing up a parallel reporting pipeline would actually solve.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team wants a partner team in a separate Google Cloud project to use the same on-call dashboard they rely on. Rather than granting the partner team IAM access into their project, they export the dashboard’s JSON and send the file over; the partner team pastes it into a new dashboard in their own project, and from then on each team can independently customize, filter, or extend its own copy using data scoped to its own project.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“A dashboard is configuration, not a link — export the JSON to hand someone their own copy.”</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks how to share a Cloud Monitoring custom dashboard with a team outside your project, think export/import of the dashboard’s JSON definition — that’s the one option that transfers the complete, original configuration without requiring cross-project IAM access or rebuilding it elsewhere.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/charts/dashboards\"><strong>Google Cloud — Create and manage custom dashboards:</strong></a> Supports: correct-answer mechanism — exporting a dashboard’s JSON and pasting/importing it into a new dashboard in another project</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 4.3, Managing metrics, dashboards, and alerts):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment — managing dashboards, including sharing</p>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e4q55":{"id":"e4q55","exam":4,"n":55,"domain":3,"html":"<p>Your team is building a service that runs compute-intensive processing on batches of data, where processing speed depends directly on the number and speed of CPUs available. These batches vary in size and can arrive at any time from several third-party sources, which need a secure way to upload their data. You want to keep costs as low as possible while still processing each batch as quickly as possible. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Stand up a secure file transfer protocol (SFTP) server on a Compute Engine instance for third parties to upload their batches to, and issue them the appropriate credentials. Create a Cloud Run function with a Cloud Storage trigger on the object-finalized event, and have that function scale up a Compute Engine autoscaling managed instance group. Use an instance image pre-loaded with the processing software that terminates its instances once processing finishes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Provide a Cloud Storage bucket for third parties to upload their batches to, with Identity and Access Management (IAM) access granted appropriately. Run a Standard Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster with two services: one that processes the batches and another that watches Cloud Storage for new batches. Stop the processing service whenever there are no batches left to process.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Provide a Cloud Storage bucket for third parties to upload their batches to, with Identity and Access Management (IAM) access granted appropriately. Create a Cloud Run function with a Cloud Storage trigger on the object-finalized event, and have that function scale up a Compute Engine autoscaling managed instance group. Use an instance image pre-loaded with the processing software that terminates its instances once processing finishes.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Provide a Cloud Storage bucket for third parties to upload their batches to, with Identity and Access Management (IAM) access granted appropriately. Use Cloud Monitoring to detect new batches landing in the bucket and have it trigger a Cloud Run function that processes the data directly. Configure that Cloud Run function to use the largest available CPU allocation to minimize its runtime.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Provide a Cloud Storage bucket for third parties to upload their batches to, with Identity and Access Management (IAM) access granted appropriately. Create a Cloud Run function with a Cloud Storage trigger on the object-finalized event, and have that function scale up a Compute Engine autoscaling managed instance group. Use an instance image pre-loaded with the processing software that terminates its instances once processing finishes.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The must-have constraints are a secure, low-overhead upload path for outside parties, processing that scales with CPU count and speed, triggering that reacts instantly to unpredictable arrivals, and compute that doesn’t cost anything once the work is done. Walking the chain: requirement (secure upload + CPU-scalable processing + fast reaction + minimal cost) → capability (Cloud Storage with IAM gives secure, serverless uploads; an object-finalized trigger reacts the instant a batch lands; a Compute Engine autoscaling group lets the heavy lifting run on machines sized for real CPU needs; a self-terminating image stops billing once processing ends) → elimination (A adds an unnecessary self-managed SFTP server, B keeps a cluster and a polling service running even when idle, D does the heavy compute inside a resource-capped function and detects uploads indirectly through Monitoring) → decision (C is the only path where every piece matches a stated requirement without adding overhead the scenario didn’t ask for).</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“processed faster based on the speed and number of CPUs” is a technical signal that the heavy computation needs to run on infrastructure you can size with CPUs, not inside a resource-capped serverless function.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“vary in size and may arrive at any time” favors an event-driven trigger that reacts the instant a batch appears, over a scheduled or polling-based detection mechanism.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“upload their data securely” points toward a native, access-controlled Google Cloud storage destination rather than a self-managed server that has to be secured and maintained.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“minimize costs, while... processed as quickly as possible” is a dual constraint requiring both an instant trigger and compute that doesn’t sit running, or idle, once the work is finished.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>A Cloud Storage bucket with IAM gives third parties a secure, access-controlled upload destination with nothing to patch or operate. The object-finalized Cloud Storage trigger fires a lightweight Cloud Run function the instant an upload completes, with no polling delay, and that function’s only job is to scale up a Compute Engine autoscaling managed instance group — handing the actual compute-heavy work to machines that can be sized with as many, or as fast, CPUs as the workload needs. Because the instance image is pre-loaded with the processing software and configured to terminate once processing completes, the project pays for compute only while a batch is actively being processed, which minimizes cost while keeping the time from “upload finishes” to “processing starts” as short as possible.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> standing up a self-managed SFTP server on a Compute Engine instance means an always-running machine that has to be secured, patched, and paid for continuously, when Cloud Storage with IAM already provides a secure, serverless upload path at lower operational cost and overhead — the rest of the path is identical to the correct answer, so this is purely a worse choice for the upload step.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> a Standard GKE cluster running two always-on services — one processing, one watching Cloud Storage — keeps compute (and a polling loop) running even when there’s nothing to process. Stopping the processing service when idle still requires something to be continuously watching and deciding that, which is less efficient and less immediate than a native, push-based event trigger, and it doesn’t scale compute to match genuinely CPU-heavy, variable-sized batches the way a right-sized Compute Engine group can.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> performing the compute-heavy processing inside the Cloud Run function itself, even with its largest CPU setting, ignores the stated requirement that speed depends on “the number and speed of CPUs on the machine” — serverless functions have bounded CPU and memory ceilings well below what a purpose-sized Compute Engine machine can offer. Using Cloud Monitoring to detect new uploads is also an indirect, less immediate substitute for the native Cloud Storage event trigger used in the correct path.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming any combination of a Cloud Storage bucket with a Cloud Run function is equally good, without checking where the actual heavy computation happens. A function that does the processing itself — even dialed up to its largest CPU allocation — is still bounded by serverless compute ceilings, so it fails a requirement explicitly tied to CPU count and speed. The correct pattern uses the function only as a lightweight trigger that hands off to appropriately sized Compute Engine capacity, never as the processor itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Event-driven architectures separate “detecting that work has arrived” from “doing the work.” A lightweight, serverless function is well suited to the first job — reacting instantly to a storage event — but a compute-heavy workload belongs on infrastructure that can be sized to the job and that scales down to zero cost once the job is done, which is exactly what an autoscaling managed instance group paired with a self-terminating image provides.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A media-processing service lets outside partners drop large video files into a Cloud Storage bucket; the moment an upload finishes, a lightweight Cloud Run function fires and resizes a Compute Engine instance group to match the batch, with each VM pre-loaded to start transcoding immediately and shut itself down once its job is done. Partners get a simple, secure upload target, transcoding starts within seconds of the upload completing rather than on the next polling cycle, and the team’s bill reflects only the minutes of actual transcoding rather than a cluster or server running around the clock.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Let the trigger be tiny; let the compute be big — and let the big compute turn itself off.”</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>For compute-heavy, event-driven batch work with unpredictable arrival, look for the pattern of a lightweight serverless trigger (never doing the heavy lifting itself) handing off to right-sized, autoscaling Compute Engine capacity that terminates when finished — that combination is what actually minimizes processing latency and cost together.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/functions/docs/calling/storage\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Storage triggers for Cloud Run functions:</strong></a>Supports: correct-answer mechanism — reacting to the object-finalized event the instant an upload completes</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 3.2, Managing service lifecycle):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment — autoscaling with managed instance groups as part of service lifecycle management</p>","lessons":["d3l7"]},"e4q56":{"id":"e4q56","exam":4,"n":56,"domain":1,"html":"<p>Your company’s security team needs read-only access to your Google Cloud project’s Data Access audit logs. You want to grant that access following the principle of least privilege and Google-recommended practices. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Grant the roles/logging.viewer role to each individual member of the security team</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Grant the roles/logging.viewer role to a group that contains the security team’s members</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Grant the roles/logging.privateLogViewer role to each individual member of the security team</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Grant the roles/logging.privateLogViewer role to a group that contains the security team’s members</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Grant the roles/logging.privateLogViewer role to a group that contains the security team’s members</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>Two hard constraints have to be satisfied together: the role itself must actually include permission to view Data Access audit logs, and the assignment method must follow Google’s documented IAM guidance. Walking the chain: requirement (read-only access to Data Access audit logs, granted the recommended way) → capability (Private Logs Viewer specifically includes Data Access audit log read permission, and Google explicitly recommends binding roles to groups rather than individuals) → elimination (A and B both use a role that can’t see Data Access audit logs regardless of assignment method, C uses the right role but the wrong assignment method) → decision (D is the only option where both the role and the assignment method match what’s required).</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“read-only access to... Data Access audit logs” is a hard, specific technical signal: the standard Logs Viewer role does not include Data Access audit log visibility, so the role choice itself is what’s being tested.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“principle of least privilege” favors the role that grants exactly the read access needed for the required log categories, no more and no less.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Google-recommended practices” is an exclusion cue pointing toward granting the role to a group rather than to individual accounts, which is Google’s documented IAM guidance for managing access at scale.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>The Logs Viewer role doesn’t include permission to view Data Access audit logs; only the Private Logs Viewer role includes that permission, in addition to everything Logs Viewer already grants, so it’s the only role of the two that actually satisfies “read-only access to Data Access audit logs.” Google’s own IAM security guidance separately recommends granting roles to groups rather than to individual users, because updating a group’s membership is simpler and less error-prone than updating many individual policy bindings. Granting Private Logs Viewer to a group containing the security team satisfies both the specific permission requirement and the recommended management practice at the same time.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Violates Requirement:</strong> Logs Viewer doesn’t grant visibility into Data Access audit logs regardless of who it’s assigned to, so this fails the core access requirement outright, independent of the assignment method.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> using a group is the right practice, but Logs Viewer still can’t surface Data Access audit logs, so the fundamental permission gap remains even with the correct assignment approach.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> Private Logs Viewer is the correct role and does grant the needed access, but assigning it to each team member individually works against Google’s own guidance to manage access through groups, making it harder to onboard, offboard, or audit the team’s access over time.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming Logs Viewer is “enough” because it’s the more commonly used, general-purpose log-access role — Data Access audit logs are deliberately walled off behind the more privileged Private Logs Viewer role because they can reveal sensitive details about who accessed what data, so a plain Viewer role is insufficient no matter how it’s assigned.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Logging separates general log visibility from visibility into more sensitive audit log categories. Private Logs Viewer is effectively a superset of Logs Viewer that adds the ability to see Data Access and other sensitive audit log entries, reflecting the principle that broader visibility into who accessed what data should require a deliberately broader grant. Separately, in IAM generally, roles are meant to be bound to groups rather than individuals so that access reviews, onboarding, and offboarding happen by managing group membership rather than editing a resource’s IAM policy every time someone joins or leaves a team.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A security team needs to review who accessed sensitive customer records, which only appears in Data Access audit logs. Granting Private Logs Viewer to a dedicated security-team Google group lets every analyst query those logs immediately, and when someone joins or leaves the team, an administrator only has to update the group’s membership rather than hunting down and editing IAM bindings tied to that person across every project.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Data Access logs need the ‘Private’ role; access for a team needs a group, not a list of names.”</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks for read-only access to Data Access audit logs, reach for Private Logs Viewer rather than Logs Viewer, and when it’s for a team, grant that role to a group rather than to each person individually — both are explicit, documented Google-recommended practices.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/audit\"><strong>Google Cloud — Cloud Audit Logs overview:</strong></a> Supports: correct-answer mechanism — Private Logs Viewer versus Logs Viewer and which role is required to read Data Access audit logs</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/using-iam-securely\"><strong>Google Cloud — Use IAM securely:</strong></a> Supports: Google-recommended practice of granting roles to groups rather than individual users</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\">Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 1.1, Designing the overall resource hierarchy for an organization): </a>Supports: domain alignment — IAM roles and organization-level policies</p>","lessons":["d1l3","d1l4"]},"e4q57":{"id":"e4q57","exam":4,"n":57,"domain":3,"html":"<p>You’ve deployed a stateless application, running as multiple pods at once, on a large Standard Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster. Traffic to the application is unpredictable, and you need the user experience to stay consistent through those traffic swings while also keeping the cluster’s resource usage efficient. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Set up a cron job that scales the deployment on a fixed schedule</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Set up a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler for the deployment</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Set up a Vertical Pod Autoscaler for the deployment</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Enable cluster autoscaling on the node pool</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Set up a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler for the deployment</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The must-have constraint is that the mechanism has to react to actual, unpredictable traffic in real time, while also avoiding wasted capacity — a schedule-based or slow-to-react approach fails the first half, and anything that doesn’t change how much capacity is actually running fails the second. Walking the chain: requirement (keep performance steady under unpredictable load while staying resource-efficient) → capability (the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler continuously tracks live demand signals and adjusts replica count to match) → elimination (A follows a fixed schedule unrelated to real traffic, C reacts too slowly and disruptively for inconsistent load, D only reacts to pod-scheduling pressure that something else has to create first) → decision (B is the single mechanism that directly and continuously ties pod capacity to actual demand).</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“inconsistent traffic” is a hard constraint that rules out any schedule-based approach, since a fixed timetable can’t track unpredictable demand.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“stateless... multiple pods run at the same time” is a technical signal that the workload is naturally suited to horizontal (replica-count) scaling rather than resizing individual pods.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“user experience... stay consistent” paired with “resource usage... efficient” is a dual requirement: the right mechanism must add capacity under load and shed it when load drops, in the same motion.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>The Horizontal Pod Autoscaler watches real-time signals such as CPU or memory utilization, or custom and external metrics tied to actual demand like request rate, and adjusts the deployment’s pod replica count accordingly. Adding pods as traffic rises keeps response times and throughput steady for users, while removing pods as traffic falls stops the deployment from holding capacity it no longer needs. Because this responds continuously to the live traffic pattern rather than a predetermined timetable, and operates at exactly the layer that fits a stateless, horizontally scalable application, it’s the mechanism built for this combination of unpredictable load and resource efficiency.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Incorrect:</strong> a cron job scales on a fixed schedule, which has no way to track traffic that is genuinely inconsistent; the deployment could easily be over-provisioned during a scheduled-up window with little actual demand, or under-provisioned during a scheduled-down window that happens to see a spike, directly undermining a consistent user experience.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Different Use Case:</strong> the Vertical Pod Autoscaler adjusts a pod’s own CPU and memory requests and limits based on observed usage over time, which suits workloads with predictable, steadily trending resource needs rather than fast, unpredictable swings. Applying a new recommendation typically requires evicting and recreating the pod, so it reacts too slowly and disruptively for inconsistent traffic, and Google’s own guidance cautions against running it together with the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler on the same resource dimension.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> cluster autoscaling adds or removes nodes in response to pods that can’t be scheduled due to insufficient node capacity, which is a valuable complement once something else is changing how many pods need to run. On its own, with a fixed pod count, it has nothing to react to as traffic changes, so by itself it cannot keep the user experience consistent as load fluctuates.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is reading “optimize cluster resource usage” as a cue to reach for cluster-level autoscaling (option D) rather than recognizing that right-sizing the pod replica count is itself what prevents wasted resources at this layer — cluster autoscaling only has something to act on once pod-level scaling, like the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler, changes the demand for nodes. If the scenario instead described pods failing to schedule because a fixed replica count occasionally can’t fit on the available nodes, cluster autoscaling would become directly relevant in its own right.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Kubernetes offers scaling at different layers for different needs: horizontal scaling changes how many replicas of a stateless workload are running to track changing demand, vertical scaling changes how much CPU and memory a single pod is allocated to match its own resource profile, and cluster-level scaling changes how many nodes exist to host whatever pods are currently scheduled. A stateless application facing unpredictable traffic is the textbook case for horizontal scaling, because adding and removing identical replicas is faster and safer than resizing or rescheduling individual pods.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A retail team’s stateless checkout service sees large, unpredictable traffic swings around promotions and social-media mentions. Configuring a Horizontal Pod Autoscaler against request-based metrics lets the deployment automatically grow from a handful of replicas to dozens during a surge and shrink back afterward, keeping checkout latency steady for shoppers during the spike while freeing up capacity, and the cost that comes with it, once traffic subsides — without anyone having to predict the next surge in advance.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Unpredictable traffic, stateless pods: scale the replica count, not the schedule or the pod’s own size.”</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a stateless, horizontally scalable GKE workload faces unpredictable traffic and the goal is both consistent performance and efficient resource use, reach for the Horizontal Pod Autoscaler first — it’s the mechanism built to react to live demand at the replica-count layer, with the Vertical Pod Autoscaler and cluster autoscaling serving more specialized or complementary roles.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/horizontalpodautoscaler\"><strong>Google Cloud — Horizontal Pod autoscaling in GKE:</strong></a> Supports: correct-answer mechanism — reacting to real-time resource/demand signals by adjusting replica count, and why it isn’t combined with VPA on the same resource dimension</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 3.2, Managing service lifecycle):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment — autoscaling (including GKE) as part of service lifecycle management</p>","lessons":["d3l7"]},"e4q58":{"id":"e4q58","exam":4,"n":58,"domain":5,"html":"<p>Your organization runs a monolithic application on Compute Engine, and you need to choose a machine type that optimizes CPU utilization, basing that choice on historical system metrics and reaching it in as few steps as possible. You want to follow Google-recommended practices. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Query the Recommender API for the VM’s machine type recommendation and apply what it suggests</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Set up an Agent Policy that automatically installs the Ops Agent on every VM</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Manually install the Ops Agent across the fleet of VMs using the gcloud CLI</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Inspect the VM’s Cloud Monitoring dashboard yourself and pick a machine type based on its lowest observed CPU utilization</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Query the Recommender API for the VM’s machine type recommendation and apply what it suggests</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The must-have constraints are using historical metrics, reaching a decision in the fewest steps, and following Google-recommended practice — all three have to be satisfied together, not traded off against each other. Walking the chain: requirement (pick an optimal machine type from historical CPU data with minimal effort) → capability (the VM instance rightsizing recommender already monitors CPU and memory utilization automatically and surfaces a ready-to-apply suggestion through the Recommender API) → elimination (B and C both add agent-installation steps that aren’t required for a base CPU recommendation, D substitutes manual review and judgment for an automated calculation) → decision (A is the single call that goes directly from existing historical data to an actionable recommendation).</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“fewest number of steps” is a hard constraint that rules out any option requiring setup or configuration work before a recommendation exists.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“historical system metrics” is a technical signal pointing to a feature that already analyzes a window of past utilization automatically, rather than one requiring manual interpretation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“follow Google-recommended practices” is an exclusion cue favoring the documented, purpose-built mechanism over an ad hoc or tangential configuration task.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>The VM instance rightsizing recommender, accessed through the Recommender API, automatically monitors a Compute Engine instance’s CPU and memory utilization over the trailing eight days and produces a specific, ready-to-apply recommendation for a better-fitting machine type — no manual configuration or agent installation is required to get this CPU-based recommendation, since Compute Engine already collects the underlying utilization data by default. Querying the Recommender API and applying its suggestion is the single, documented, lowest-step path from historical metrics to a chosen machine type, satisfying every constraint in the scenario at once.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> an Agent Policy that installs the Ops Agent fleet-wide can improve recommendation accuracy or provide richer guest-level telemetry, but it’s an optional enhancement rather than a prerequisite for a base CPU-utilization recommendation, and it adds setup steps the scenario explicitly asks to avoid.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> manually installing the Ops Agent through the gcloud CLI has the same issue as B — it’s an extra configuration step that doesn’t by itself identify a machine type, and it isn’t necessary for CPU-based rightsizing, which Compute Engine already monitors without it.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Violates Requirement:</strong> manually reviewing a dashboard and selecting a machine type by eye is a multi-step, subjective process rather than Google’s documented, repeatable mechanism, and it substitutes a vague heuristic (“lowest observed CPU utilization”) for the recommender’s actual sizing calculation — which can lead to choosing a machine type that doesn’t actually fit the workload.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming that better recommendations must start with manually installing an agent, since Compute Engine already collects the CPU (and memory) utilization used for rightsizing by default — the Ops Agent steps look relevant but are an unnecessary detour for this specific, already-available CPU-based recommendation. If the scenario instead needed more granular guest-OS or application-level metrics to sharpen a memory-based recommendation, installing the Ops Agent would become a genuinely necessary step.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud’s Active Assist recommenders continuously analyze operational telemetry Google already collects and turn it into specific, actionable suggestions exposed through the Recommender API, console, or gcloud CLI, removing the need for an operator to manually collect, graph, and interpret metrics before making an infrastructure decision. Recognizing that a recommender already exists for a given optimization goal is usually the fastest, most Google-aligned path, compared to building the same analysis by hand.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A platform team managing hundreds of Compute Engine VMs across a global organization uses the VM instance rightsizing recommender to surface overprovisioned and underprovisioned machines automatically, instead of having an engineer manually chart CPU usage for each VM. Applying the suggested machine type changes directly from the recommendation reduces wasted spend on oversized VMs and protects performance on undersized ones, without a separate metrics-collection effort to design and maintain.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Before you build your own dashboard-and-judgment process, check whether a recommender already does the sizing for you.”</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question asks for the fastest, Google-recommended way to pick a resource size or configuration based on data Google already collects, look first for the matching Active Assist recommender exposed through the Recommender API, rather than agent installation or manual dashboard analysis.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/recommender/docs/vm-instance-rightsizing-recommender\"><strong>Google Cloud — Apply machine type recommendations to VM instances (VM instance rightsizing recommender):</strong></a> Supports: correct-answer mechanism — automatic CPU/memory utilization analysis over the trailing 8 days and ready-to-apply recommendations via the Recommender API</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 5.1, Collecting performance information in Google Cloud):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment — Active Assist insights and recommendations</p>","lessons":["d5l6"]},"e4q59":{"id":"e4q59","exam":4,"n":59,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You’re building the CI/CD pipeline for an application on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), deployed with a Kubernetes Deployment, Service, and Ingress. The application team has asked for blue/green deployments, and you now need to implement the rollback step. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Run kubectl rollout undo</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Delete the new container image and delete its running Pods</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Update the Kubernetes Service’s selector to point back at the previous Deployment</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Scale the new Kubernetes Deployment down to zero replicas</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Update the Kubernetes Service’s selector to point back at the previous Deployment</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard constraint is that the rollback mechanism has to match the deployment strategy actually in use, and blue/green keeps two independently running Deployments side by side rather than progressively updating one. Walking the chain: requirement (roll back a blue/green deployment) → capability (a Service’s label selector is what decides which Deployment’s Pods receive traffic, and switching it back to the previous Deployment restores service instantly since those Pods never stopped running) → elimination (A reverts a single Deployment’s own revision history, which is the rolling-update model, not two separate Deployments; B and D either remove capacity or fail to redirect traffic without also touching the Service) → decision (C is the one action that matches how blue/green is actually structured).</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“blue/green deployment methodology” is a hard constraint that determines which rollback mechanism applies, since blue/green and rolling updates use fundamentally different rollback mechanics.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“Deployment, Service, and Ingress” is a technical signal confirming the Service’s label selector is the layer that actually controls which set of Pods receives traffic — exactly what a blue/green switch manipulates.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“implement the rollback actions,” rather than “fix the bug” or “investigate the failure,” is a scope signal: the task is to restore traffic to the known-good version, not to diagnose or rebuild anything.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>In a blue/green setup, the old (“blue”) and new (“green”) versions run as two separate, independently running Deployments at the same time, and a single Kubernetes Service’s label selector decides which one actually receives traffic. Because both Deployments keep running after the cutover, rolling back is simply a matter of updating the Service’s selector so it matches the previous Deployment’s Pod labels again — traffic returns to the known-good version instantly, with no redeployment, rebuild, or Pod deletion needed, and the problematic new Deployment is left intact for investigation.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option A — Different Use Case:</strong><code>kubectl rollout undo</code> reverts a single Deployment back to an earlier revision in its own rollout history, which is the rollback mechanism for a rolling-update strategy. In blue/green, the previous version isn’t a prior revision of the same Deployment — it’s a separate, still-running Deployment — so this command doesn’t accomplish the kind of switch blue/green rollback requires.</p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Violates Requirement:</strong> deleting the new image and its running Pods removes the broken version but does nothing to point the Service back at Pods that can actually serve traffic, risking an outage rather than restoring service to the previous version.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> scaling the new Deployment to zero stops it from running, but unless the Service’s selector is also switched back to the old Deployment, there’s nothing left for the Service to point at, so this step alone doesn’t complete a working rollback.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is reaching for <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> out of habit, since it’s the familiar Kubernetes rollback command — but that command is scoped to a Deployment’s own revision history, which is the rolling-update model, not the two-Deployment, Service-switch model that defines blue/green. The deployment strategy named in the scenario is the signal that determines which rollback mechanism actually applies.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Blue/green deployments keep two complete, independent environments running side by side and use a single switch — here, a Service’s label selector — to decide which one is “live.” That design is what makes rollback instant and low-risk: because the previous environment was never torn down, reverting is just flipping the switch back, rather than rebuilding or restoring anything.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team cuts their Service over to a newly deployed “green” version and immediately starts seeing errors in production. Because the “blue” Deployment’s Pods are still running untouched, they patch the Service’s selector back to the blue labels, and traffic returns to the stable version within seconds — giving the team time to debug the green Deployment without any user-facing downtime or a rushed redeploy.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“Blue/green rollback is a selector flip, not a rollout undo — the old version never left.”</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a question specifies blue/green deployments on Kubernetes and asks how to roll back, look for the option that repoints the Service’s selector to the previous Deployment — <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> belongs to rolling updates, not to a strategy built around two independently running Deployments.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/\">Kubernetes documentation — Service: </a>Supports: correct-answer mechanism — a Service’s label selector determines which Deployment’s Pods receive traffic, and switching it is how blue/green cutover and rollback work</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 2.2, Implementing and managing pipelines):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment — deployment strategies, including blue/green</p>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e4q60":{"id":"e4q60","exam":4,"n":60,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You’re building a Cloud Run application that needs an API key to call a third-party API, and you want to store and use that key securely, following Google-recommended practices. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Store the API key as a secret in Secret Manager, and expose that secret to the Cloud Run service as an environment variable</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Store the API key as a secret in Secret Manager, mount it as a file under a custom directory, and have the application decrypt it before use</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Store the API key as a key in Cloud Key Management Service (Cloud KMS), and expose it to the Cloud Run service as an environment variable</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Encrypt the API key with Cloud Key Management Service (Cloud KMS), pass the resulting ciphertext to Cloud Run as an environment variable, and have the application decrypt it before use</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Store the API key as a secret in Secret Manager, and expose that secret to the Cloud Run service as an environment variable</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Exam Reasoning Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The hard constraint is that the storage and retrieval mechanism must be both secure and the one Google specifically documents as recommended for this exact scenario — a Cloud Run workload needing a credential. Walking the chain: requirement (securely store and use an API key in Cloud Run, the Google-recommended way) → capability (Secret Manager is the purpose-built secret store, and Cloud Run has native support for exposing a Secret Manager secret as an environment variable at startup, with no application-level cryptography involved) → elimination (B misdescribes that native integration by adding an unnecessary manual decryption step, C misapplies Cloud KMS as if it were a secret store, D manually rebuilds an encrypt/decrypt flow that Secret Manager’s integration already handles) → decision (A is the simplest path that matches exactly what Google documents).</p>\n<p><strong>Key Exam Clues</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>“secure way to store and use the API key... following Google-recommended practices” is an exclusion cue pointing to the purpose-built secret-storage service and its native Cloud Run integration, rather than a general-purpose key-management service or a manually engineered encrypt/decrypt flow.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>“API key” — an arbitrary credential string, not a cryptographic key used to encrypt other data — is a technical signal distinguishing this from what Cloud KMS actually manages.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The repeated phrase “decrypt... before use” appearing in two of the four options is worth noticing: Secret Manager’s native Cloud Run integration never requires the application to perform its own decryption step, so any option describing that extra step is describing unnecessary complexity.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why This Is Correct</strong></p>\n<p>Google explicitly recommends storing sensitive values like API keys for Cloud Run workloads in Secret Manager, and Cloud Run has built-in, native support for exposing a Secret Manager secret to a service as an environment variable. Cloud Run retrieves the secret’s value securely at instance startup and makes it directly available to the application, while Secret Manager itself handles encryption at rest and access control through IAM. No custom decryption code, encryption key management, or special mount path is required, which makes this the simplest, most secure, and most Google-aligned way to meet the requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option B — Incorrect:</strong> this starts from the right service, Secret Manager, but misrepresents how the integration works. Cloud Run and Secret Manager already handle secure delivery and decryption of the secret’s value, so an application-level “decrypt the key” step is unnecessary and reflects a misunderstanding of the feature; the described mount path also isn’t a standard, documented convention.</p>\n<p><strong>Option C — Incorrect:</strong> Cloud KMS manages cryptographic keys used to encrypt, decrypt, or sign data — it isn’t designed to store arbitrary secret values such as an API key, so treating an API key as something to “save in Cloud KMS as a key” misapplies the service to a job it isn’t built for.</p>\n<p><strong>Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best:</strong> manually encrypting the API key with Cloud KMS, passing the ciphertext as an environment variable, and decrypting it inside the application is a workable but needlessly complex pattern that duplicates work Secret Manager’s native Cloud Run integration already performs automatically and securely — it isn’t the practice Google documents as recommended for this scenario.</p>\n<p><strong>Exam Trap</strong></p>\n<p>The trap is assuming that because Cloud KMS is an “encryption” service, it must be the more secure or more rigorous choice for protecting an API key. Cloud KMS solves a different problem — managing the keys used to encrypt other data — while Secret Manager is the service purpose-built for storing and retrieving secret values like API keys, passwords, and certificates, with its own encryption and access control handled internally.</p>\n<p><strong>Foundation Concept</strong></p>\n<p>Google Cloud deliberately separates secret storage (Secret Manager, for values an application needs to read, like API keys and credentials) from key management (Cloud KMS, for cryptographic keys used to encrypt, decrypt, or sign data elsewhere). Recognizing which category a piece of sensitive data falls into points directly at which service to use, and Cloud Run’s native integrations with Secret Manager mean the correct choice usually requires no custom cryptography code in the application at all.</p>\n<p><strong>Real-World Connection</strong></p>\n<p>A team deploying a Cloud Run service that calls a paid weather API stores that API key as a Secret Manager secret and references it as an environment variable in the service’s configuration. When the key needs to rotate, they add a new secret version and update the reference; the application code never changes, never handles raw ciphertext, and the service account’s IAM permissions determine exactly who and what can read the key, giving the team both operational simplicity and a clean audit trail.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory Hook</strong></p>\n<p>“An API key is a secret to store, not a key to manage — reach for Secret Manager, not Cloud KMS.”</p>\n<p><strong>30-Second Exam Takeaway</strong></p>\n<p>When a Cloud Run workload needs to securely hold a credential like an API key, the Google-recommended answer is almost always storing it in Secret Manager and exposing it through Cloud Run’s native secret integration — Cloud KMS and manual encrypt/decrypt flows solve a different problem and add unnecessary complexity here.</p>\n<p><strong>Official References</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/configuring/secrets\"><strong>Google Cloud — Configure secrets for Cloud Run services:</strong></a> Supports: correct-answer mechanism — Google’s recommendation to store sensitive values in Secret Manager and expose them to Cloud Run natively as environment variables</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://services.google.com/fh/files/misc/professional_cloud_devops_engineer_exam_guide_english.pdf\"><strong>Google Cloud — Professional Cloud DevOps Engineer exam guide (Section 2.3, Managing pipeline configuration and secrets):</strong></a> Supports: domain alignment — configuration and secret management (Secret Manager) versus key management (Cloud KMS)</p>","lessons":["d2l8"]},"e5q1":{"id":"e5q1","exam":5,"n":1,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You created a <strong>Cloud Monitoring chart for CPU utilization</strong> in a <strong>dashboard</strong> within your <strong>workspace project</strong>. You want to <strong>share the chart with your Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) team only</strong>. You want to ensure that you <strong>follow the principle of least privilege</strong>. What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p><strong>I.</strong> Share the <strong>workspace Project ID</strong> with the <strong>SRE team</strong>. Assign the <strong>SRE team</strong> the <strong>Monitoring Viewer IAM role</strong> in the workspace project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>II.</strong> Share the <strong>workspace Project ID</strong> with the <strong>SRE team</strong>. Assign the <strong>SRE team</strong> the <strong>Dashboard Viewer IAM role</strong> in the workspace project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>III.</strong> Click <strong>“Share chart by URL”</strong> and provide the <strong>URL</strong> to the <strong>SRE team</strong>. Assign the <strong>SRE team</strong> the <strong>Monitoring Viewer IAM role</strong> in the workspace project.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p><strong>IV.</strong> Click <strong>“Share chart by URL”</strong> and provide the <strong>URL</strong> to the <strong>SRE team</strong>. Assign the <strong>SRE team</strong> the <strong>Dashboard Viewer IAM role</strong> in the workspace project.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C (III. Click “Share chart by URL” and provide the URL to the SRE team. Assign the SRE team the Monitoring Viewer IAM role in the workspace project.)</strong></p>\n<p>Justification:</p>\n<p>Cloud Monitoring supports sharing <strong>individual charts from Metrics Explorer</strong> using the <em>Share chart by URL</em> feature</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/charts/metrics-explorer#share-charts\">Share charts in Metrics Explorer</a></p>\n<p>For the SRE team to view the chart and underlying metric data, they need the <strong>Monitoring Viewer role (</strong><code>**roles/monitoring.viewer**</code><strong>)</strong> , which grants <strong>read-only access to monitoring data and configurations</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/access-control#predefined_roles\">Monitoring IAM roles</a></p>\n<p>More restrictive roles like <strong>Dashboard Viewer/Configuration Viewer</strong> only allow viewing dashboard <em>configurations</em> and don’t allow access to metric data. That’s why they cannot be used for chart sharing.</p>\n<p>Since the requirement is to <strong>share the chart</strong> , not the entire dashboard, option C best matches both the technical capability and the principle of least privilege achievable in this scenario.</p>\n<p><strong>Why other options are not a perfect fit</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A (Project ID + Monitoring Viewer)</strong> :<br />\nTechnically valid (they can see dashboards and charts), but it shares <strong>all monitoring dashboards and metrics in the project</strong> , not just the specific chart. This goes beyond the requirement and does not strictly follow “share the chart only.”</p>\n<p><strong>B (Project ID + Dashboard Viewer)</strong> :<br />\nThis role (actually “Dashboard Configuration Viewer” in practice) only provides access to dashboard definitions. It does not allow viewing chart <strong>data</strong>. Users will see errors like <em>“Invalid resource type/metric type”</em>. Not valid.</p>\n<p><strong>D (Share URL + Dashboard Viewer)</strong> :<br />\nSame problem as B — Dashboard Viewer/Config Viewer cannot access metric data, so the shared chart URL fails with errors. Not valid.</p>\n<p><strong>Final Answer: C is correct.</strong></p>\n<p>Because:</p>\n<p>It leverages the official <strong>“share chart by URL”</strong> feature in Metrics Explorer.</p>\n<p>It grants the <strong>Monitoring Viewer role</strong> , the minimum role that allows access to metric data needed for charts.</p>\n<p>Other options either over-share (A) or don’t work at all (B, D).</p>","lessons":["d4l8","d4l13"]},"e5q2":{"id":"e5q2","exam":5,"n":2,"domain":2,"html":"<p>You use a <strong>multi-step Cloud Build pipeline</strong> to build and deploy your application to <strong>Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE)</strong>. You want to <strong>integrate with a third-party monitoring platform</strong> by performing an <strong>HTTP POST of the build information to a webhook</strong>. You want to <strong>minimize development effort</strong>.</p>\n<p>What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p><strong>A.</strong> Add logic to <strong>each Cloud Build step</strong> to <strong>HTTP POST the build information</strong> to a webhook.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>B.</strong> Add a <strong>new step at the end of the pipeline</strong> in <strong>Cloud Build</strong> to <strong>HTTP POST the build information</strong> to a webhook.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>C.</strong> Use <strong>Cloud Logging</strong> to create a <strong>logs-based metric</strong> from the <strong>Cloud Build logs</strong>. Create an <strong>alert</strong> with a <strong>Webhook notification type</strong>.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>D.</strong> Create a <strong>Cloud Pub/Sub push subscription</strong> to the <strong>Cloud Build</strong><code>**cloud-builds**</code><strong>Pub/Sub topic</strong> to <strong>HTTP POST the build information</strong> to a webhook.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D.</strong> Create a <strong>Cloud Pub/Sub push subscription</strong> to the <strong>Cloud Build</strong><code>**cloud-builds**</code><strong>Pub/Sub topic</strong> to <strong>HTTP POST the build information</strong> to a webhook.</p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Build <strong>automatically publishes build events</strong> to the <code>**cloud-builds**</code><strong>Pub/Sub topic</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The most efficient approach is to <strong>subscribe to this topic</strong> and trigger an <strong>HTTP POST to the webhook</strong> whenever a build completes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>This <strong>decouples</strong> the build pipeline from the monitoring integration, reducing complexity and <strong>minimizing development effort</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Steps to implement:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Create a Cloud Pub/Sub subscription</strong> for the <code>cloud-builds</code> topic.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Set up a push subscription</strong> that <strong>sends build events</strong> to the <strong>third-party monitoring webhook</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Incorrect:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Add logic to each Cloud Build step</strong> – This is inefficient because <strong>each step would need to be modified</strong> , leading to <strong>code duplication and maintenance overhead</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>B. Add a new step at the end of the pipeline</strong> – While <strong>this works</strong> , it <strong>ties webhook integration to the build process</strong> , making it <strong>less flexible</strong> and <strong>more effort to maintain</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>C. Use Cloud Logging and logs-based metrics</strong> – This is <strong>not the best approach</strong> because:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Cloud Logging metrics are <strong>aggregated over time</strong> and not <strong>real-time event triggers</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>It introduces <strong>additional configuration steps</strong> without improving efficiency.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/automate-builds-pubsub-events\">Cloud Build and Cloud Pub/Sub integration</a><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/create-push-subscription\"> Creating a Pub/Sub push subscription</a></p>","lessons":["d2l1"]},"e5q3":{"id":"e5q3","exam":5,"n":3,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You have a pool of application servers running on Compute Engine. You need to provide a secure solution that requires the least amount of configuration and allows developers to easily access application logs for troubleshooting.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>How would you implement the solution on Google Cloud?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p><strong>A.Deploy the Cloud Logging agent</strong> to the application servers.</p>\n<p><strong>Give the developers the IAM Logs Viewer role</strong> to access <strong>Cloud Logging</strong> and view logs.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p><strong>B.Deploy the Cloud Logging agent</strong> to the application servers.</p>\n<p><strong>Give the developers the IAM Logs Private Logs Viewer role</strong> to access <strong>Cloud Logging</strong> and view logs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>C.Deploy the Cloud Monitoring agent</strong> to the application servers.</p>\n<p><strong>Give the developers the IAM Monitoring Viewer role</strong> to access <strong>Cloud Monitoring</strong> and view metrics.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>D.Install the</strong><code>**gsutil**</code><strong>command-line tool</strong> on your application servers.</p>\n<p><strong>Write a script using</strong><code>**gsutil**</code> to upload application logs to a <strong>Cloud Storage bucket</strong> , and then schedule it to run via <code>cron</code> every 5 minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Give the developers IAM Object Viewer access</strong> to view the logs in the specified bucket.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Deploy the Cloud Logging agent</strong> to the application servers.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Give the developers the IAM Logs Viewer role</strong> to access <strong>Cloud Logging</strong> and view logs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>best and easiest</strong> approach to allow developers to access logs is to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Deploy the Cloud Logging agent</strong> – This automatically <strong>collects logs from Compute Engine VMs</strong> and <strong>sends them to Cloud Logging</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Grant the IAM Logs Viewer role</strong> – This allows developers to <strong>view logs in the Cloud Logging UI</strong> without needing full admin access.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Least configuration effort</strong> – The Cloud Logging agent <strong>integrates natively with Compute Engine</strong> and <strong>requires minimal setup</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Incorrect:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Logs Private Logs Viewer Role</strong> – This role <strong>is used for viewing private logs</strong> , which include <strong>admin activity and system logs</strong>. It <strong>is not required for regular application logs</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>C. Cloud Monitoring Agent + Monitoring Viewer Role</strong> – The <strong>Cloud Monitoring agent</strong> is for <strong>metrics (CPU, memory, network, etc.)</strong> , <strong>not logs</strong>. Developers need access to <strong>Cloud Logging, not Cloud Monitoring</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>D. Using</strong><code>**gsutil**</code><strong>to upload logs manually</strong> – This is <strong>overly complex and inefficient</strong> :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Requires <strong>writing and maintaining a log upload script</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Logs will only be available in <strong>Cloud Storage</strong> , making <strong>real-time log analysis difficult</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Logging already provides native log storage and filtering</strong> , eliminating the need for manual uploads.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/agent/logging/install-index\">Installing the Cloud Logging Agent</a><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/access-control\"> IAM Roles for Cloud Logging</a></p>","lessons":["d4l5","d4l13"]},"e5q4":{"id":"e5q4","exam":5,"n":4,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>You manage the backend infrastructure of a mobile game running on a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster, serving HTTP requests from users. To optimize network expenses, what step should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Establish the VPC as a Shared VPC Host project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Set up your network services on the Standard Tier.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Configure your Kubernetes cluster as a Private Cluster.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Implement a Google Cloud HTTP Load Balancer as Ingress.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct option: II</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why option II is correct</strong></p>\n<p><strong>II. Set up your network services on the Standard Tier.</strong></p>\n<p>When the explicit goal is to <strong>optimize network expenses</strong> , the most direct and impactful lever in Google Cloud is the <strong>Network Service Tier</strong> selection.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Standard Tier</strong> is priced lower than Premium Tier because traffic is routed over the public internet instead of Google’s global private backbone.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>For a <strong>mobile game backend</strong> where traffic is typically <strong>regional</strong> and ultra-low global latency is not the primary requirement, Standard Tier provides <strong>significant cost savings</strong> on:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Network egress</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Load-balancing data processing</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>This choice requires <strong>no changes to application code or cluster design</strong> , making it the cleanest cost-optimization step.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is a classic <strong>exam signal</strong> : when cost optimization is the focus (not latency, availability, or security), <strong>Standard Tier</strong> is the expected answer.</p>\n<p>Authoritative documentation:<br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/network-tiers/docs/overview\">https://cloud.google.com/network-tiers/docs/overview</a></p>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are not correct</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Establish the VPC as a Shared VPC Host project.</strong><br />\nShared VPC improves <strong>network governance and IAM separation</strong> , not runtime network costs. It does not reduce egress or traffic processing charges.</p>\n<p><strong>III. Configure your Kubernetes cluster as a Private Cluster.</strong><br />\nPrivate clusters improve <strong>security</strong> by removing public node IPs. They do not reduce inbound HTTP traffic costs and may even introduce additional NAT costs.</p>\n<p><strong>IV. Implement a Google Cloud HTTP Load Balancer as Ingress.</strong><br />\nWhile this is architecturally recommended for HTTP workloads, <strong>external HTTP(S) Load Balancing runs on Premium Tier by default</strong> , which is <strong>more expensive</strong> , not cheaper. It optimizes performance, not cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer:</strong> <strong>II</strong></p>","lessons":["d5l2"]},"e5q5":{"id":"e5q5","exam":5,"n":5,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Your company encounters frequent bugs, outages, and performance issues in its production systems. Developers often utilize the production environment for new feature development and bug fixes, alongside conducting configurations and experiments, causing disruptions for users. Moreover, testers conduct load testing in the production environment, impacting system performance. How should you redesign the environment to mitigate bugs and outages in production while enabling effective load testing of new features?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Deploy an automated testing script within the production environment to promptly identify failures as they occur.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Establish a dedicated development environment with scaled-down server capacity, granting access solely to developers and testers.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Strengthen security measures within the production environment to prevent unauthorized alterations by developers, and implement a regulated update schedule, permitting only one update annually.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Create separate environments: one for development purposes focused on code creation, and another for testing configurations, experiments, and load testing.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct option: IV</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why option IV is correct</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IV. Create separate environments: one for development purposes focused on code creation, and another for testing configurations, experiments, and load testing.</strong></p>\n<p>This option directly addresses the <strong>root causes</strong> of the production instability while aligning with <strong>SRE and DevOps best practices</strong>.</p>\n<p>Core reliability principles at play:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Production must be protected</strong> : SRE strongly discourages using production for development, experimentation, or load testing because it introduces uncontrolled risk and user-facing impact.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Environment isolation</strong> : Separating <strong>development</strong> , <strong>testing (including load testing and experiments)</strong> , and <strong>production</strong> environments ensures that failures, misconfigurations, and performance tests do not affect real users.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Realistic testing without user impact</strong> : A dedicated testing environment allows load tests, chaos experiments, and configuration validation under controlled conditions that resemble production, without risking outages.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Reduced blast radius</strong> : Bugs and performance regressions are caught before deployment to production, significantly reducing incidents.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This redesign improves:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>System reliability</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>User experience</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Developer velocity</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Confidence in releases</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Google SRE explicitly promotes isolating environments to reduce risk and prevent accidental production impact.</p>\n<p><strong>Authoritative documentation:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/release-engineering/\">https://sre.google/sre-book/release-engineering/</a></p>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are not suitable</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Deploy automated testing scripts in production.</strong><br />\nAutomated testing is valuable, but running tests <em>in production</em> does not prevent outages caused by experiments, load testing, or unsafe changes. It detects failures after they impact users rather than preventing them.</p>\n<p><strong>II. Establish a single scaled-down development environment for both developers and testers.</strong><br />\nThis partially helps but is insufficient. Load testing and configuration experiments require an environment closer to production scale and behavior. Mixing development and testing still increases risk and reduces test accuracy.</p>\n<p><strong>III. Restrict production changes and allow only one update per year.</strong><br />\nThis is an extreme and impractical approach that harms agility and does not solve the underlying problem of improper environment usage. SRE encourages <em>frequent, safe releases</em> , not infrequent ones.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer:</strong> <strong>IV</strong></p>","lessons":["d1l9"]},"e5q6":{"id":"e5q6","exam":5,"n":6,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You're on-call for an infrastructure service with numerous dependent systems. An alert notifies you of widespread failure in serving requests, impacting all dependent systems with hundreds of thousands of users. Following Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) incident management protocols, you designate yourself as Incident Commander (IC) and assign two experienced team members as Operations Lead (OL) and Communications Lead (CL). What's your immediate next step?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Initiate actions to mitigate user impact and implement the mitigations in production.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Contact the owners of the affected services and keep them informed about the incident status.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Establish a dedicated communication channel for incident responders and leads to ensure seamless coordination.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Begin a postmortem process, incorporating incident details, share the draft internally, and solicit input from internal stakeholders.#</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct option: III</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why option III is correct</strong></p>\n<p><strong>III. Establish a dedicated communication channel for incident responders and leads to ensure seamless coordination.</strong></p>\n<p>Under <strong>Google SRE incident management</strong> , once an incident is declared and roles are assigned (IC, Operations Lead, Communications Lead), the <strong>very next step</strong> is to <strong>create a single, dedicated communication channel</strong> (for example, a Slack channel or conference bridge).</p>\n<p>Core SRE fundamentals behind this step:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Single source of truth:</strong> All responders coordinate in one place, avoiding fragmented updates across emails, chats, or calls.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Clear command structure:</strong> The IC can direct actions efficiently, the OL can report mitigation progress, and the CL can prepare consistent external/internal updates.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Reduced cognitive load:</strong> Responders focus on fixing the issue rather than tracking scattered communications.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> As more responders join, they can quickly get context without interrupting mitigation work.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Google SRE explicitly defines this as an <em>early, mandatory step</em> before mitigation actions begin.</p>\n<p><strong>Authoritative documentation:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/managing-incidents/\">https://sre.google/sre-book/managing-incidents/</a></p>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are not correct</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Initiate actions to mitigate user impact immediately.</strong><br />\nMitigation is critical, but acting <em>before</em> establishing a coordination channel leads to duplicated effort, missed context, and confusion—especially in large, multi-team incidents.</p>\n<p><strong>II. Contact owners of affected services right away.</strong><br />\nStakeholder communication is important, but it is the responsibility of the <strong>Communications Lead</strong> <em>after</em> coordination mechanisms are in place.</p>\n<p><strong>IV. Begin a postmortem process.</strong><br />\nPostmortems occur <strong>after incident resolution</strong> , not during active response.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer:</strong> <strong>III</strong></p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e5q7":{"id":"e5q7","exam":5,"n":7,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>To diminish costs for a stable, business-critical workload running on a fixed set of Compute Engine instances over several months, without compromising performance, what's the recommended approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Opt for Committed Use Discounts to avail discounted pricing.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Transition the instances to a Managed Instance Group for enhanced management and potential cost efficiencies.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Convert the instances to preemptible virtual machines to benefit from lower pricing for non-essential workloads.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Set up an Unmanaged Instance Group dedicated to running the workload on instances.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct option: I</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why option I is correct</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Opt for Committed Use Discounts to avail discounted pricing.</strong></p>\n<p>For a <strong>stable, business-critical workload</strong> running on a <strong>fixed set of Compute Engine instances over several months</strong> , <strong>Committed Use Discounts (CUDs)</strong> are the <strong>recommended and exam-correct approach</strong>.</p>\n<p>Core cost-optimization principles:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Designed for predictable workloads:</strong> CUDs provide significant discounts (up to ~55–70%) in exchange for a <strong>1-year or 3-year commitment</strong> on vCPU, memory, or specific machine types.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>No performance impact:</strong> Unlike preemptible/Spot VMs, committed instances <strong>retain full availability and performance guarantees</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>No architectural changes required:</strong> You continue running the same instance types; the discount is applied automatically to matching usage.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>SRE-aligned:</strong> Reduces cost while maintaining reliability for business-critical services.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This exactly matches the scenario: <em>long-running, stable, fixed capacity, performance-sensitive workload</em>.</p>\n<p>Authoritative documentation:<br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/committed-use-discounts-overview\">https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/committed-use-discounts-overview</a></p>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are not suitable</strong></p>\n<p><strong>II. Transition the instances to a Managed Instance Group (MIG).</strong><br />\nMIGs improve <strong>availability, scaling, and lifecycle management</strong> , but they <strong>do not reduce compute pricing</strong> by themselves. Cost savings come from discounts, not grouping.</p>\n<p><strong>III. Convert the instances to preemptible virtual machines.</strong><br />\nPreemptible (Spot) VMs are <strong>not suitable for business-critical workloads</strong> because they can be terminated at any time. This violates the “without compromising performance” requirement.</p>\n<p><strong>IV. Set up an Unmanaged Instance Group.</strong><br />\nUnmanaged instance groups offer <strong>no cost benefits</strong> and add operational complexity. They are not a pricing optimization mechanism.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer:</strong> <strong>I</strong></p>","lessons":["d5l1"]},"e5q8":{"id":"e5q8","exam":5,"n":8,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>In managing Terraform templates for your infrastructure, you're joined by two new engineers. How can you establish a process and adopt a tool to prevent code conflicts while ensuring all updates are incorporated into the latest version?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Store the code in a Git-based version control system. Implement a process for developers to merge changes daily. Upload code to a versioned Cloud Storage bucket, marking it as the latest master version.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize a Git-based version control system for code storage. Introduce a process involving peer code reviews and unit testing to validate integrity and functionality before code integration. Designate the fully integrated code in the repository as the latest master version.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Organize code as text files in Google Drive within a defined folder structure. Ensure daily changes are captured in the files and rename the folder structure using a predefined version naming convention.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Structure code as text files in Google Drive within a defined folder hierarchy. Confirm daily changes are captured, creating a new .zip archive with a predefined naming convention. Upload the .zip archive to a versioned Cloud Storage bucket, declaring it as the latest version.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct option: II</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why option II is correct</strong></p>\n<p><strong>II. Utilize a Git-based version control system for code storage. Introduce a process involving peer code reviews and unit testing to validate integrity and functionality before code integration. Designate the fully integrated code in the repository as the latest master version.</strong></p>\n<p>This option reflects <strong>industry-standard Terraform and SRE practices</strong> for collaborative Infrastructure as Code (IaC) development.</p>\n<p>Core fundamentals behind this choice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Git as the source of truth:</strong> Git is explicitly recommended for Terraform to manage concurrent changes, maintain history, and resolve conflicts through merges.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Conflict prevention through workflow, not timing:</strong> Peer reviews and pull requests prevent conflicting or unsafe changes from being merged, instead of relying on manual coordination (such as “daily merges”).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Quality gates before merge:</strong> Code reviews and automated tests (e.g., <code>terraform validate</code>, <code>terraform plan</code>) ensure correctness and prevent broken infrastructure changes from reaching main/master.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Clear latest state:</strong> The main/master branch represents the <strong>current desired state of infrastructure</strong> , aligning perfectly with declarative IaC principles.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This approach scales cleanly as more engineers join and is the <strong>exam-expected, production-grade solution</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Authoritative documentation:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/tutorials/teamwork/teamwork-remote\">https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/tutorials/teamwork/teamwork-remote</a></p>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are not suitable</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Daily merges + Cloud Storage as master copy</strong><br />\nDaily merging does not prevent conflicts and Cloud Storage is not a version control system. This introduces race conditions and lacks review, rollback, and auditability.</p>\n<p><strong>III. Google Drive with renamed folders</strong><br />\nGoogle Drive offers no native merge conflict resolution, code review, or change history suitable for IaC. This is unsafe for infrastructure management.</p>\n<p><strong>IV. Zipped versions uploaded to Cloud Storage</strong><br />\nArchiving code removes real-time collaboration, prevents incremental review, and makes conflict resolution nearly impossible.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer:</strong> <strong>II</strong></p>","lessons":["d1l6"]},"e5q9":{"id":"e5q9","exam":5,"n":9,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>In managing your CI/CD pipeline for building and deploying application artifacts, ensuring secure access to secrets and simplifying their rotation in the event of a security breach is paramount. What action should you take to address this concern?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Require developers to input secrets during the build process and emphasize avoiding storage of secrets at rest.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Store secrets in a distinct configuration file within Git, granting specific developers access to this file.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Securely store secrets in Cloud Storage encrypted with a key from Cloud KMS, providing the CI/CD pipeline access via IAM.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Encrypt the secrets and store them within the source code repository, while safeguarding the decryption key in a separate repository accessible to the pipeline.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Securely store secrets in Cloud Storage encrypted with a key from Cloud KMS, providing the CI/CD pipeline access via IAM.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this stands out as the most secure and manageable approach:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Centralized Secret Management:</strong> Cloud Storage provides a dedicated location for storing secrets, separate from application code.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Robust Encryption:</strong> Cloud KMS offers strong encryption with granular key management capabilities.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Identity-Based Access Control:</strong> IAM allows fine-grained permissions, ensuring only the CI/CD pipeline has authorized access to secrets.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Secret Isolation:</strong> Separates secret management from source code repositories, reducing the risk of accidental exposure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Secret Rotation:</strong> Cloud KMS simplifies secret rotation, enhancing security in case of a breach.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Let's analyze the shortcomings of the other options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Require developers to input secrets during the build process...</strong> This introduces potential for human error and makes secret management cumbersome within a CI/CD pipeline.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Store secrets in a distinct configuration file within Git...</strong> Storing secrets within version control, even with limited access, increases the risk of accidental exposure in the history or through compromised credentials.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Encrypt the secrets and store them within the source code repository...</strong> Even encrypted, directly storing secrets within the repository presents a greater risk compared to a dedicated secret management system.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/security-key-management\">https://cloud.google.com/security-key-management</a></p>","lessons":["d2l8"]},"e5q10":{"id":"e5q10","exam":5,"n":10,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You support an application that stores product information in cached memory. For every cache miss, an entry is logged in Cloud Logging.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>You want to visualize how often a cache miss happens over time.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p><strong>A.</strong> <strong>Link Cloud Logging</strong> as a source in <strong>Looker Studio (formerly Data Studio)</strong>. <strong>Filter the logs on cache misses</strong>.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>B.</strong> <strong>Configure Cloud Profiler</strong> to identify and visualize when the <strong>cache misses occur</strong> based on the logs.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>C.</strong> <strong>Create a logs-based metric in Cloud Logging</strong> and a <strong>dashboard for that metric in Cloud Monitoring</strong>.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p><strong>D.</strong> <strong>Configure BigQuery as a sink</strong> for <strong>Cloud Logging</strong>. <strong>Create a scheduled query</strong> to filter the <strong>cache miss logs</strong> and write them to a <strong>separate table</strong>.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C.</strong> <strong>Create a logs-based metric in Cloud Logging</strong> and a <strong>dashboard for that metric in Cloud Monitoring</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Logging allows you to create logs-based metrics</strong> , which can track specific log patterns such as <strong>cache misses</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Monitoring can visualize these metrics in real-time</strong> via a <strong>custom dashboard</strong> , providing insights into <strong>cache performance over time</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>This approach <strong>requires minimal configuration</strong> and is the <strong>most efficient solution</strong> for tracking cache misses.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Steps to Implement:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Create a logs-based metric</strong> in <strong>Cloud Logging</strong> that <strong>counts occurrences of cache miss logs</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>View the logs-based metric in Cloud Monitoring</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Create a dashboard</strong> in Cloud Monitoring to <strong>visualize cache misses over time</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Incorrect:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Linking Cloud Logging to Looker Studio and filtering logs</strong> – <strong>Looker Studio is not optimized for real-time log-based monitoring</strong>. <strong>Logs-based metrics in Cloud Monitoring are more efficient</strong> for tracking cache misses.</p>\n<p><strong>B. Using Cloud Profiler</strong> – <strong>Cloud Profiler is for analyzing CPU and memory performance</strong> , <strong>not tracking log-based events like cache misses</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>D. Exporting logs to BigQuery and creating scheduled queries</strong> – While <strong>BigQuery is useful for deeper historical analysis</strong> , it <strong>introduces unnecessary complexity</strong> for simple <strong>real-time monitoring</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Reference Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring/sli-metrics/logs-based-metrics\"> Using Logs-Based Metrics in Cloud Monitoring</a></p>","lessons":["d4l4"]},"e5q11":{"id":"e5q11","exam":5,"n":11,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>In line with Site Reliability Engineering principles, you're drafting a postmortem for an incident triggered by a software change that significantly impacted users. How can you prevent similar severe incidents in the future?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Hold the engineers involved accountable and escalate the issue to their senior management.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Ensure that test cases detecting errors of this nature are successfully executed before every new software release.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Conduct follow-up discussions with personnel responsible for reviewing the changes, prescribing best practices for future implementations.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Implement a policy mandating immediate engagement of on-call teams to convene engineers and management for prompt incident resolution planning.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Best Option:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>II. Ensure that test cases detecting errors of this nature are successfully executed before every new software release.</strong> This approach directly targets the root cause and promotes proactive issue prevention.</p>\n<p><strong>Reasoning:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Preventing Recurrence:</strong> By focusing on testing, you establish a robust barrier to prevent similar errors from reaching production and impacting users.</p>\n<p><strong>SRE Alignment:</strong> Thorough testing is a core element of SRE, emphasizing a proactive approach to reliability.</p>\n<p><strong>Tangible Improvement:</strong> This action item leads to concrete changes in your development and release processes.</p>\n<p><strong>Why Others Are Less Optimal:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Hold the engineers involved accountable ...</strong> While understanding the causes is important, focusing solely on accountability fosters a blame culture, which is counterproductive for long-term improvement and contradicts SRE principles.</p>\n<p><strong>III. Conduct follow-up discussions ...</strong> Postmortem discussions are vital for learning from the incident, but they are not a direct preventive measure against future similar failures.</p>\n<p><strong>IV. Implement a policy mandating immediate engagement ...</strong> While incident response speed is crucial, the policy doesn't address the root cause of the issue, making it less effective in preventing similar incidents.</p>\n<p><strong>Important Considerations</strong></p>\n<p>While improving test coverage is a top priority, consider these additional SRE-aligned actions:</p>\n<p><strong>Blameless Culture:</strong> Promote a culture where errors are seen as opportunities for learning and improvement. This encourages open communication and problem-solving rather than fear.</p>\n<p><strong>Code Review:</strong> Strengthen code review processes to enhance the identification of potential issues before deployment.</p>\n<p><strong>Chaos Engineering:</strong> Experiment with deliberate disruptions in testing or staging environments to uncover hidden vulnerabilities and build resilience.</p>","lessons":["d3l5"]},"e5q12":{"id":"e5q12","exam":5,"n":12,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Your team has recently deployed an NGINX-based application into Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) and has exposed it to the public via an HTTP Google Cloud Load Balancer (GCLB) ingress.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>You want to scale the deployment of the application's frontend using an appropriate Service Level Indicator (SLI).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p><strong>A.</strong> Configure the <strong>horizontal pod autoscaler</strong> to use the <strong>average response time</strong> from the <strong>Liveness and Readiness probes</strong>.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>B.</strong> Configure the <strong>vertical pod autoscaler in GKE</strong> and enable the <strong>cluster autoscaler</strong> to scale the cluster as pods expand.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>C.</strong> <strong>Install the Cloud Monitoring (formerly Stackdriver) custom metrics adapter</strong> and configure a <strong>horizontal pod autoscaler (HPA) to use the number of requests</strong> provided by the <strong>Google Cloud Load Balancer (GCLB)</strong>.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p><strong>D.</strong> Expose the <strong>NGINX stats endpoint</strong> and configure the <strong>horizontal pod autoscaler</strong> to use the <strong>request metrics</strong> exposed by the <strong>NGINX deployment</strong>.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C.</strong> <strong>Install the Cloud Monitoring (formerly Stackdriver) custom metrics adapter</strong> and configure a <strong>horizontal pod autoscaler (HPA) to use the number of requests</strong> provided by the <strong>Google Cloud Load Balancer (GCLB)</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Horizontal Pod Autoscaler (HPA) is the best approach for scaling stateless applications</strong> like <strong>NGINX-based frontends</strong> based on real traffic metrics.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google Cloud Load Balancer (GCLB) provides HTTP request count metrics</strong> , which are <strong>more relevant SLIs</strong> for scaling than CPU/memory alone.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The <strong>Cloud Monitoring custom metrics adapter</strong> enables <strong>HPA to use external HTTP request rate metrics</strong> from GCLB, ensuring <strong>demand-based autoscaling</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Incorrect:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Using Liveness and Readiness probe response times for HPA</strong> –</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Liveness and readiness probes are <strong>used for health checks</strong> , not for <strong>measuring real traffic load</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Response time <strong>does not accurately represent the need for scaling</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>B. Using Vertical Pod Autoscaler (VPA) and Cluster Autoscaler</strong> –</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>VPA optimizes individual pod resource allocation</strong> , <strong>not the number of replicas</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cluster Autoscaler only adjusts node counts</strong> , which is <strong>not sufficient</strong> for handling increasing request loads dynamically.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>D. Using the NGINX stats endpoint for autoscaling</strong> –</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>While <strong>NGINX exposes request metrics</strong> , these <strong>are not natively integrated with Cloud Monitoring or HPA</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>GCLB already provides request count metrics</strong> in <strong>Cloud Monitoring</strong> , making it <strong>a better choice</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Recommended Approach:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Install the Cloud Monitoring custom metrics adapter for Kubernetes</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/custom-metrics/creating-metrics\"> Cloud Monitoring Custom Metrics Adapter</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Use GCLB's HTTP request metrics for autoscaling</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/custom-and-external-metrics\"> Autoscaling with External HTTP Request Metrics</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Configure the HPA to use request count per second from GCLB</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/horizontal-pod-autoscaling\"> Scaling Workloads with HPA</a></p>\n</li>\n</ol>","lessons":["d3l7"]},"e5q13":{"id":"e5q13","exam":5,"n":13,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You are managing an application that exposes an HTTP endpoint without using a load balancer. The latency of HTTP responses is important for the user experience.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>You want to understand what HTTP latencies all of your users are experiencing. You use Cloud Monitoring.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p><strong>A. In your application, create a custom metric with metricKind set to DELTA and valueType set to DOUBLE.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>In Cloud Monitoring's Metrics Explorer, use a Stacked Bar graph to visualize the metric.</strong></p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>B. In your application, create a custom metric with metricKind set to CUMULATIVE and valueType set to DOUBLE.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>In Cloud Monitoring's Metrics Explorer, use a Line graph to visualize the metric.</strong></p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>C. In your application, create a custom metric with metricKind set to GAUGE and valueType set to DISTRIBUTION.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>In Cloud Monitoring's Metrics Explorer, use a Heatmap graph to visualize the metric.</strong></p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p><strong>D. In your application, create a custom metric with metricKind set to METRIC_KIND_UNSPECIFIED and valueType set to INT64.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>In Cloud Monitoring's Metrics Explorer, use a Stacked Area graph to visualize the metric.</strong></p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>In your application, create a <strong>custom metric</strong> with <code>metricKind</code> set to <code>GAUGE</code> and <code>valueType</code> set to <code>DISTRIBUTION</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>In <strong>Cloud Monitoring's Metrics Explorer</strong> , use a <strong>Heatmap graph</strong> to visualize the metric.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>HTTP response latency varies over time</strong> and across requests.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The <strong>best way to track user latency experience</strong> is to <strong>capture individual latency values</strong> and store them in a <strong>custom distribution metric</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The <code>**GAUGE**</code><strong>metricKind</strong> is appropriate because <strong>latency represents a real-time value</strong> at a given point in time.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The <code>**DISTRIBUTION**</code><strong>valueType</strong> allows <strong>storing multiple latency measurements</strong> , making it easier to analyze <strong>percentiles and trends</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A <strong>Heatmap graph</strong> helps <strong>visualize latency patterns over time</strong> and <strong>identify spikes or trends</strong> in user experience.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Incorrect:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Using a</strong><code>**DELTA**</code><strong>metricKind and</strong><code>**DOUBLE**</code><strong>valueType with a Stacked Bar Graph</strong> –</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><code>DELTA</code> is used for <strong>rate-based metrics over time</strong> , but <strong>latency should be measured as a point-in-time value (GAUGE)</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Stacked Bar graphs are not ideal for latency distributions</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>B. Using a</strong><code>**CUMULATIVE**</code><strong>metricKind and</strong><code>**DOUBLE**</code><strong>valueType with a Line Graph</strong> –</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><code>CUMULATIVE</code> metrics track values <strong>increasing over time</strong> (e.g., total number of requests).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Latency is not cumulative</strong> , so this <strong>does not provide accurate insights</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>D. Using</strong><code>**METRIC_KIND_UNSPECIFIED**</code><strong>and</strong><code>**INT64**</code><strong>with a Stacked Area Graph</strong> –</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><code>METRIC_KIND_UNSPECIFIED</code> is <strong>not a valid selection</strong> for Cloud Monitoring.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><code>INT64</code> is <strong>not appropriate for capturing floating-point latency values</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/custom-metrics/creating-metrics\"> Creating Custom Metrics in Cloud Monitoring</a><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/charts/charting-distribution-metrics#:~:text=Heatmap%20charts%20are%20designed%20to,color%20indicates%20a%20higher%20value.\"> Using Heatmaps for Distribution Metrics</a></p>","lessons":["d4l6"]},"e5q14":{"id":"e5q14","exam":5,"n":14,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>Your organization recently shifted to a container-based workflow for application development. Within this setup, your team continuously deploys multiple applications via an automated build pipeline to the production environment. However, a recent security audit flagged concerns about potential vulnerabilities in the code deployed to production. The conventional tooling for virtual machine (VM) vulnerabilities is inadequate for the containerized environment. How can you ensure the security and patch level of all code processed through the pipeline?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Implement Container Analysis to scan and report Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVEs).</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Configure the containers within the build pipeline to autonomously update themselves before release.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Adapt existing operating system vulnerability software to function within the containerized environment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Integrate static code analysis tooling to scrutinize the Docker files utilized in creating the containers.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Implement Container Analysis to scan and report Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVEs).</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this stands out:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Container-Focused:</strong> Container Analysis is specifically designed to identify vulnerabilities within container images, offering a more accurate and targeted approach than traditional VM-based tools.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Pipeline Integration:</strong> Seamlessly integrates into your CI/CD pipeline, providing security checks at multiple stages of the development process.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Actionable Insights:</strong> Provides detailed reports on discovered CVEs, enabling timely remediation.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Configure the containers within the build pipeline...</strong> While auto-updating can help in some cases, it doesn't guarantee patching of known CVEs and might lead to compatibility issues.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Adapt existing operating system vulnerability software...</strong> Adapting VM-focused tools for containers is often cumbersome and may not offer comprehensive coverage.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Integrate static code analysis tooling...</strong> While valuable for overall code quality, static code analysis primarily focuses on custom code, not necessarily external library vulnerabilities.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Key Points:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Proactive Security:</strong> Implement vulnerability scanning early in your build pipeline to discover issues before they reach production.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Image Repository Integration:</strong> Consider integrating Container Analysis with your image repository (e.g., Google Container Registry) for continuous monitoring of stored images.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Additional Considerations:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Base Images:</strong> Regularly update base images to incorporate OS-level security patches.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Dependency Scanning:</strong> Use tools to scan for outdated dependencies with known vulnerabilities in your container images.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/container-analysis/docs/container-analysis\">https://cloud.google.com/container-analysis/docs/container-analysis</a></p>","lessons":["d2l9"]},"e5q15":{"id":"e5q15","exam":5,"n":15,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You support a web application hosted on Compute Engine that provides a booking service for thousands of users.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Shortly after the release of a new feature, your monitoring dashboard shows that all users are experiencing latency at login.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>You want to mitigate the impact of the incident on the users of your service.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What should you do first?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p><strong>A. Roll back the recent release.</strong></p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p><strong>B. Review Cloud Monitoring.</strong></p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>C. Upsize the virtual machines running the login services.</strong></p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>D. Deploy a new release to see whether it fixes the problem.</strong></p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Roll back the recent release.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The <strong>latency issue started immediately after the new release</strong> , indicating that the <strong>new deployment is likely the root cause</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The <strong>fastest and safest way to mitigate impact</strong> is to <strong>roll back to the previous stable version</strong> while investigating the issue.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>This follows <strong>Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) best practices</strong> , where a rollback is the <strong>primary mitigation strategy</strong> for post-release incidents.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Incorrect:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Reviewing Cloud Monitoring logs before rolling back</strong> –</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>While <strong>Cloud Monitoring helps diagnose the issue</strong> , <strong>it does not mitigate user impact immediately</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Rollback first</strong> to reduce user disruption, then analyze the cause.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>C. Upsizing VMs running the login service</strong> –</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scaling resources does not fix application-level issues</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>If the new release introduced <strong>inefficient code or database locks</strong> , <strong>increasing VM size won't resolve the root cause</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>D. Deploying another release without investigation</strong> –</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>This is risky</strong> because it might <strong>worsen the issue</strong> or introduce new bugs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Rollback first</strong> , verify the issue is resolved, then debug and prepare a <strong>proper fix</strong> for a future deployment.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Recommended Approach:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Rollback to the last stable release immediately</strong> to restore service availability.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Investigate logs in Cloud Logging &amp; Monitoring</strong> to find the root cause.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Run a post-mortem</strong> to ensure the issue is resolved before the next deployment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Test the fix in a staging environment</strong> before rolling out again.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Reference Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/resources/practices-and-processes/anatomy-of-an-incident/\"> Google SRE Handbook: Managing Incidents</a><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/roll-back\"> Rolling Back Deployments on Compute Engine</a></p>","lessons":["d3l4"]},"e5q16":{"id":"e5q16","exam":5,"n":16,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>You're responsible for deploying application releases to Google Cloud Platform (GCP) using Jenkins to streamline processes, reduce overhead, and ensure data security. What's the best course of action?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Deploy Jenkins on local workstations.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Implement Jenkins on an on-premises Kubernetes cluster.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Utilize Jenkins within Google Cloud Functions.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Set up Jenkins on Compute Engine virtual machines.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Set up Jenkins on Compute Engine virtual machines.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this choice is optimal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Resource Control:</strong> Compute Engine provides dedicated VMs, giving you full control over Jenkins' environment, resources, and configuration.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Customization:</strong> VMs offer flexibility for installing plugins, managing dependencies, and integrating with other GCP services.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security:</strong> You can implement robust security measures on the VMs, aligning with your data security requirements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> If needed, you can adjust the resources allocated to your Jenkins setup as build demands change.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Deploy Jenkins on local workstations.</strong> This lacks scalability, consistency, and is vulnerable to local machine failures.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Implement Jenkins on an on-premises Kubernetes cluster.</strong> Managing a hybrid cloud setup adds complexity and might not be necessary if your application already resides in GCP.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Utilize Jenkins within Google Cloud Functions.</strong> Cloud Functions are designed for short-lived tasks, not a persistent CI/CD tool like Jenkins.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Important Considerations:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>High Availability:</strong> For mission-critical pipelines, consider setting up multiple Jenkins instances in a master-agent configuration.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>GCP Integration:</strong> Utilize GCP features like Cloud Build for some parts of your pipeline, where appropriate, for cost-efficiency and simplicity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security Best Practices:</strong> Harden your Jenkins VMs, implement proper access control, and encrypt sensitive data.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://plugins.jenkins.io/google-compute-engine/\">https://plugins.jenkins.io/google-compute-engine/</a></p>","lessons":["d2l11"]},"e5q17":{"id":"e5q17","exam":5,"n":17,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You provide support for a large service with a clearly defined Service Level Objective (SLO). The development team frequently deploys new releases of the service each week. In the event of a significant incident causing the service to miss its SLO, you aim for the development team to pivot their focus from feature development to enhancing service reliability. What action should you take proactively before a major incident arises?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Collaborate with all service stakeholders to formulate an appropriate error budget policy.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Advocate for prioritizing service reliability over introducing new features in discussions with the product team.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Discuss with the development team to potentially reduce the frequency of releases to once a week or less.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Implement a plugin within your Jenkins pipeline to halt new releases when the service is out of compliance with its SLO.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Collaborate with all service stakeholders to formulate an appropriate error budget policy.</strong></p>\n<p>Let's break down why this is the most effective starting point:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Shared Responsibility:</strong> An error budget policy creates a framework for making decisions around reliability vs. feature development. It aligns stakeholders (development, product, operations) on priorities.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Proactive:</strong> Establishes a clear mechanism for when to shift focus <em>before</em> a major incident disrupts the service.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Data-Driven:</strong> Error budgets are based on your SLO. They translate a reliability goal into tangible terms that everyone can understand.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal (Initially):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Advocate for prioritizing service reliability ...</strong> While important, a general prioritization doesn't provide the same specific guidance or shared understanding as an error budget policy.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Discuss with the development team ...</strong> Slowing down releases might be a consequence of an error budget policy, but it's a blunt instrument and could hinder necessary fixes or improvements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Implement a plugin within your Jenkins pipeline ...</strong> Halting releases automatically can be a tool to enforce an error budget policy, but the policy itself needs to be defined first.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Important Considerations</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Communication:</strong> An error budget policy is only effective if well-communicated and agreed upon across the organization.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Review and Adjust:</strong> The error budget should be reviewed periodically and adjusted based on changing business needs and service performance.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/error-budget-policy/\">https://sre.google/workbook/error-budget-policy/</a></p>","lessons":["d3l3"]},"e5q18":{"id":"e5q18","exam":5,"n":18,"domain":5,"html":"<p>You manage <strong>several production systems</strong> that run on <strong>Compute Engine</strong> in the same <strong>Google Cloud Platform (GCP) project</strong>.</p>\n<p>Each system has its <strong>own set of dedicated Compute Engine instances</strong>.</p>\n<p>You want to know <strong>how much it costs to run each system</strong>.</p>\n<p>What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p><strong>A.</strong> In the <strong>Google Cloud Console</strong> , use the <strong>Cost Breakdown section</strong> to visualize the <strong>costs per system</strong>.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>B.</strong> <strong>Assign all instances a label specific to the system they run</strong>. Configure <strong>BigQuery billing export</strong> and query <strong>costs per label</strong>.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p><strong>C.</strong> <strong>Enrich all instances with metadata</strong> specific to the system they run. Configure <strong>Cloud Logging (formerly Stackdriver Logging) to export to BigQuery</strong> , and query <strong>costs based on the metadata</strong>.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>D.</strong> <strong>Name each virtual machine (VM) after the system it runs</strong>. Set up a <strong>usage report export</strong> to a <strong>Cloud Storage bucket</strong>. Configure the bucket as a source in <strong>BigQuery</strong> to <strong>query costs based on VM name</strong>.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B.</strong> <strong>Assign all instances a label specific to the system they run</strong>. Configure <strong>BigQuery billing export</strong> and <strong>query costs per label</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google Cloud billing supports resource labels</strong> that allow you to <strong>track costs based on specific attributes</strong> like <strong>which system a VM belongs to</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>BigQuery billing export</strong> provides <strong>detailed billing data</strong> , which can be queried <strong>by labels to determine per-system costs</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>This approach <strong>follows Google Cloud best practices for cost allocation</strong> and allows for <strong>granular cost analysis</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Steps to Implement:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Assign labels to Compute Engine instances</strong> for each system:</p>\n<pre><code>            1. bash\n    2. CopyEditgcloud compute instances add-labels instance-name --labels=system=my-system\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Enable BigQuery billing export</strong> to <strong>store billing data</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Query costs per system using BigQuery</strong> :</p>\n<pre><code>            1. sql\n    2. CopyEditSELECT service.description, labels.system, SUM(cost) AS total_cost\n    3. FROM `billing_dataset.gcp_billing_export`\n    4. WHERE usage_start_time &gt;= TIMESTAMP_SUB(CURRENT_TIMESTAMP(), INTERVAL 30 DAY)\n    5. GROUP BY service.description, labels.system\n    6. ORDER BY total_cost DESC;\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Incorrect:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Using the Cost Breakdown section in Google Cloud Console</strong> –</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>The Cost Breakdown section does not provide detailed per-system costs</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>It is not as granular as querying BigQuery exports with labels</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>C. Exporting logs to BigQuery and querying based on metadata</strong> –</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Logging does not track cost-related data</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Billing data is stored in Cloud Billing, not Cloud Logging</strong> , so this approach <strong>won't provide accurate cost breakdowns</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>D. Naming VMs after the system and using usage report exports</strong> –</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>VM names are not included in detailed billing exports</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Labels are the recommended approach for cost allocation</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/cost-management/use-labels-to-gain-visibility-into-gcp-resource-usage-and-spending\"> Using Labels for Cost Allocation</a><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/export-data-bigquery\"> Exporting Cloud Billing Data to BigQuery</a></p>","lessons":["d5l5"]},"e5q19":{"id":"e5q19","exam":5,"n":19,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>You're set to deploy a new feature of a web-based application to production and wish to utilize Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) for a phased rollout to half of the web server pods. What is the optimal course of action?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Employ a partitioned rolling update method.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Implement Node taints with NoExecute.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Utilize a replica set within the deployment specification.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Opt for a stateful set with parallel pod management policy.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Employ a partitioned rolling update method.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Controlled Rollout:</strong> Partitioned rolling updates allow you to release your new feature in increments. You can update half of your web server pods, validate functionality, and then proceed with the remaining pods if everything looks good.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Kubernetes Native:</strong> This method is directly supported within Kubernetes Deployment objects, ensuring a streamlined process.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Phase Control:</strong> You have granular control over the percentage of pods updated in each phase, enabling the targeted 50% rollout.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Implement Node taints with NoExecute.</strong> Node taints are used to control pod scheduling, not for incrementally updating existing pods.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Utilize a replica set within the deployment specification.</strong> Replica sets simply control the number of running pods, not the rollout strategy of updates.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Opt for a stateful set with parallel pod management policy.</strong> Stateful sets are used for workloads requiring persistent storage and stable identifiers, not for phased rollouts of web servers.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/statefulset#partitioning_rolling_updates\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/statefulset#partitioning_rolling_updates</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/statefulset/#partitions\">https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/statefulset/#partitions</a></p>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e5q20":{"id":"e5q20","exam":5,"n":20,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>As you craft and allocate action items in a postmortem following an outage, your goal is to efficiently address the root causes. Although the outage has concluded, it's vital to swiftly tackle the action items. How should you designate owners and collaborators for these action items?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Assign a single owner for each action item, along with any necessary collaborators.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Allocate multiple owners for each item to expedite the team's response.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Assign collaborators to the items without designating individual owners to maintain a blameless postmortem approach.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Designate the team lead as the owner for all action items, given their oversight of the SRE team.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Assign a single owner for each action item, along with any necessary collaborators.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Clarity &amp; Accountability:</strong> A single owner ensures there's no confusion about who is ultimately responsible for driving the action item to completion.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Collaboration:</strong> Collaborators provide support, expertise, and diverse perspectives while maintaining accountability with the designated owner.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Urgency:</strong> Given the need for swift action, having clear ownership fosters a sense of responsibility and helps prioritize tasks.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Allocate multiple owners for each item...</strong> While well-intentioned, multiple owners can lead to diffusion of responsibility and potential delays if it's unclear who's driving the resolution.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Assign collaborators to the items without designating individual owners...</strong> Collaboration is vital, but without clear ownership, tasks risk falling through the cracks or lacking the necessary drive for quick resolution.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Designate the team lead as the owner for all action items...</strong> This places too much burden on the team lead and may not leverage the specific expertise of other team members who are better suited to own individual action items.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/example-postmortem/\">https://sre.google/sre-book/example-postmortem/</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://devops.com/when-it-disaster-strikes-part-3-conducting-a-blameless-post-mortem/\">https://devops.com/when-it-disaster-strikes-part-3-conducting-a-blameless-post-mortem/</a></p>","lessons":["d3l5"]},"e5q21":{"id":"e5q21","exam":5,"n":21,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>During the migration of an on-premises containerized web application to Google Cloud, considering specific traffic direction and testing needs, which deployment strategy and platform should you opt for?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Implement the canary release on Cloud Run, employing traffic splitting to route 10% of user traffic to the canary release based on the revision tag.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Choose App Engine for deploying the canary release, utilizing traffic splitting to direct a subset of user traffic to the new version based on IP address.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Deploy the canary release on Compute Engine, utilizing Anthos Service Mesh with Compute Engine to direct 10% of user traffic to the canary release via virtual service configuration.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Opt for Google Kubernetes Engine with Anthos Service Mesh for deploying the canary release, employing traffic splitting to direct 10% of user traffic to the new version based on the user-agent header configured in the virtual service.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct option: IV</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why option IV is correct</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IV. Opt for Google Kubernetes Engine with Anthos Service Mesh for deploying the canary release, employing traffic splitting to direct 10% of user traffic to the new version based on the user-agent header configured in the virtual service.</strong></p>\n<p>This is the <strong>most appropriate and exam-accurate choice</strong> given all constraints in the question:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The application is <strong>containerized</strong> and being migrated from <strong>on-premises</strong> → GKE is the natural target platform.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The question explicitly mentions <strong>specific traffic direction and testing needs</strong> , which implies <strong>advanced traffic control</strong> , not just percentage-based splitting.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Anthos Service Mesh (ASM)</strong> provides <strong>Layer-7 traffic management</strong> using Istio, enabling:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Header-based routing (such as <code>User-Agent</code>)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Canary testing for specific client groups (internal testers, beta users, bots, QA devices)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Fine-grained control without modifying application code</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>This allows <strong>safe validation of new versions</strong> under real traffic conditions while tightly controlling exposure.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>From an SRE and DevOps perspective, this is the <strong>gold standard</strong> for canary deployments requiring deterministic traffic selection rather than random sampling.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are not suitable</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Canary on Cloud Run using revision-based traffic splitting</strong><br />\nCloud Run supports <strong>percentage-based traffic splitting only</strong>. It does <strong>not support header-based routing</strong> , so you cannot direct traffic based on user-agent or other request attributes.</p>\n<p><strong>II. App Engine with IP-based traffic splitting</strong><br />\nIP-based routing is coarse, unreliable (NAT, mobile users), and not suitable for controlled canary testing. Additionally, App Engine is less aligned with migrating an existing <strong>containerized</strong> on-prem application.</p>\n<p><strong>III. Compute Engine with Anthos Service Mesh</strong><br />\nAnthos Service Mesh is designed for <strong>Kubernetes workloads</strong>. While traffic shaping on Compute Engine is possible, ASM does not manage non-GKE VM workloads for application-level canary routing.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer:</strong> <strong>IV</strong></p>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e5q22":{"id":"e5q22","exam":5,"n":22,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>When configuring the frontend tier of an application deployed in Google Cloud, which serves only users from the United Kingdom within the europe-west2 region, utilizing nginx and a managed instance group with an Envoy-based external HTTP(S) load balancer, what network tier and load balancing setup offer the most cost-effective solution?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Premium Tier with a global load balancer</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Premium Tier with a regional load balancer</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Standard Tier with a global load balancer</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Standard Tier with a regional load balancer</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct option: IV</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why option IV is correct</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IV. Standard Tier with a regional load balancer</strong></p>\n<p>Your users are <strong>only in the UK</strong> and the application is served from <strong>a single region (europe-west2)</strong>. In this case, the most cost-effective design is to use a <strong>regional external Application Load Balancer</strong> (Envoy-based external HTTP(S) load balancer) with the <strong>Standard Network Service Tier</strong> :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>In <strong>Standard Tier</strong> , the external Application Load Balancer is intended for <strong>single-region</strong> delivery (it distributes traffic to backends in a single region).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Google’s pricing guidance explicitly calls out the <strong>regional external Application Load Balancer</strong> as a cost-effective option for single-region workloads because it uses <strong>Standard Tier</strong> , which has lower outbound data transfer charges.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Since you are not serving global users, paying for global premium backbone/edge features is unnecessary for the requirement.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Documentation:</strong> <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/network-tiers/docs/overview\">https://cloud.google.com/network-tiers/docs/overview</a></p>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are not suitable</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Premium Tier with a global load balancer</strong><br />\nPremium Tier is optimized for global users and multi-region/backbone performance. For a UK-only, single-region serving model, it is typically <strong>more expensive than needed</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>II. Premium Tier with a regional load balancer</strong><br />\nEven though regional is correct for the geography constraint, choosing <strong>Premium</strong> defeats the cost goal when <strong>Standard Tier regional</strong> is available and intended for single-region delivery.</p>\n<p><strong>III. Standard Tier with a global load balancer</strong><br />\nFor Envoy-based external HTTP(S) / external Application Load Balancing, <strong>Standard Tier effectively operates in a single region</strong> (not true global multi-region Anycast behavior). If you want global external Application Load Balancing behavior, it requires Premium Tier.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer: IV</strong></p>","lessons":["d5l2"]},"e5q23":{"id":"e5q23","exam":5,"n":23,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>As a member of a global organization managing a service with a 99% availability target and limited engineering resources, you've noted a 99.5% availability for the current month. To meet the set availability objectives, adapt to business changes, including forthcoming feature launches, and diminish technical debt while minimizing operational expenses, which Google-endorsed approach should take precedence?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Bolster service resilience by implementing N+1 redundancy through the incorporation of extra computing resources.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Streamline operations by identifying, quantifying, and automating repetitive tasks to eradicate unnecessary manual effort.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Establish an error budget for service-level availability and actively work to reduce any remaining errors.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Assign available engineers to feature backlog development, ensuring adherence to the availability target.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The Priority:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>II. Streamline operations by identifying, quantifying, and automating repetitive tasks to eradicate unnecessary manual effort.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why this takes precedence:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Efficiency Optimization:</strong> Google's Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) philosophy prioritizes efficient use of available resources. Automating manual processes reduces the potential for human error and frees up engineers for higher-value tasks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Addressing Root Issues:</strong> Manual operations are a common source of delays and errors. Automation tackles these problems at their core, leading to inherent reliability improvements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Resource Maximization:</strong> Reducing manual work allows your limited engineers to focus on proactive improvements, new features, and reducing technical debt, all leading to a better service.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cost-effectiveness:</strong> Operational streamlining through automation often leads to significant cost savings.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Others are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Bolster service resilience by implementing N+1 redundancy...</strong> Redundancy is valuable but can be expensive. It doesn't address existing operational inefficiencies, which could still lead to outages.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Establish an error budget...</strong> Error budgets are an important SRE tool, but a sole focus on reduction might discourage necessary development work and innovation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Assign available engineers to feature backlog development...</strong> Features are important, but a service that fails to meet its availability targets due to operational problems won't be useful, regardless of feature additions.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l3","d3l6"]},"e5q24":{"id":"e5q24","exam":5,"n":24,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>To ensure consistency across your Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) environments - development, staging, and production - while following Google-recommended practices for enforcing network policies and deploying a logging DaemonSet, what steps should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Employ Google Cloud Deploy to enforce network policies and deploy the DaemonSet. Utilize Cloud Monitoring to trigger alerts for any deviations from the repository source.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Use Google Cloud Deploy for the DaemonSet deployment and Policy Controller to configure network policies. Leverage Cloud Monitoring to identify drifts and Cloud Functions to rectify them.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Utilize Cloud Build to generate and deploy network policies and the DaemonSet. Implement Config Sync to synchronize configurations across all three environments.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize Cloud Build to generate and deploy network policies and the DaemonSet. Set up a Policy Controller to enforce configurations across the three environments.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct option: III</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why option III is correct</strong></p>\n<p><strong>III. Utilize Cloud Build to generate and deploy network policies and the DaemonSet. Implement Config Sync to synchronize configurations across all three environments.</strong></p>\n<p>This option most closely follows <strong>Google-recommended GitOps practices for GKE</strong> when the goal is <strong>consistency across dev, staging, and prod</strong>.</p>\n<p>Key fundamentals behind this choice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Config Sync (Anthos Config Management)</strong> is Google’s <strong>authoritative solution</strong> for enforcing configuration consistency across multiple GKE clusters and environments. It continuously reconciles the desired state stored in Git with the actual cluster state.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Network policies and DaemonSets</strong> are classic cluster-level configurations that should be enforced declaratively and continuously, not deployed ad hoc.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Build</strong> integrates naturally with Git-based workflows to validate and promote configuration changes before they reach the repository that Config Sync watches.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>This approach <strong>prevents configuration drift automatically</strong> , rather than merely detecting it after the fact.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is the canonical pattern Google documents for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>GitOps-driven configuration management</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Multi-environment consistency</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Policy and system workload enforcement in GKE</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Authoritative documentation:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/anthos-config-management/docs/concepts/config-sync-overview\">https://cloud.google.com/anthos-config-management/docs/concepts/config-sync-overview</a></p>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are not suitable</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Google Cloud Deploy + Cloud Monitoring alerts</strong><br />\nCloud Deploy is for <strong>application delivery</strong> , not enforcing cluster-level configurations like network policies or DaemonSets. Monitoring detects drift but does <strong>not prevent or correct it</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>II. Cloud Deploy + Policy Controller + Cloud Functions</strong><br />\nThis combines tools with overlapping responsibilities and introduces unnecessary operational complexity. Drift remediation via Cloud Functions is not Google’s recommended approach.</p>\n<p><strong>IV. Cloud Build + Policy Controller only</strong><br />\nPolicy Controller enforces <strong>policy constraints</strong> , not the <strong>deployment and synchronization</strong> of resources like DaemonSets. It cannot replace Config Sync for full configuration consistency.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer:</strong> <strong>III</strong></p>","lessons":["d1l7"]},"e5q25":{"id":"e5q25","exam":5,"n":25,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>To facilitate trunk-based development in a central Git repository, how can you establish a robust CI/CD pipeline for a containerized application in Google Cloud, ensuring thorough testing for new versions to enhance its quality</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Enforce a Git hook to mandate developers execute unit tests before pushing code to the central repository. Subsequently, initiate Cloud Build to construct the application container. Upon completion, deploy the container to a testing environment and execute integration tests. If successful, proceed to deploy the application container to the production environment, followed by the execution of acceptance tests.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Implement a Git hook to enforce developers execute unit tests before pushing code to the central repository. Upon successful unit tests, trigger Cloud Build to build a container. Subsequently, deploy the application container to a testing environment, execute integration tests, and acceptance tests. If all tests pass, designate the code as production-ready and deploy the application container to the production environment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Initiate Cloud Build to build the application container and conduct unit tests within the container. Upon successful completion, deploy the application container to a testing environment and execute integration tests. Following successful integration tests, deploy the application container to the production environment, and execute acceptance tests.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Trigger Cloud Build to execute unit tests upon code push. If all unit tests pass, proceed to build and push the application container to a central registry. Subsequently, deploy the container to a testing environment, execute integration tests, and acceptance tests. Upon passing all tests, deploy the application to the production environment, and conduct smoke tests.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The most appropriate approach is:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IV. Trigger Cloud Build to execute unit tests upon code push. If all unit tests pass, proceed to build and push the application container to a central registry. Subsequently, deploy the container to a testing environment, execute integration tests, and acceptance tests. Upon passing all tests, deploy the application to the production environment, and conduct smoke tests.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>This approach outlines a well-structured CI/CD pipeline that leverages Cloud Build and ensures the application goes through multiple stages of testing before deployment to production:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Unit tests upon code push</strong> ensure that basic functionality is verified immediately.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Building and pushing the container to a central registry</strong> ensures that the built container image is version-controlled and available for subsequent deployments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Deploying to a testing environment</strong> for further integration and acceptance testing ensures that the application is thoroughly tested before it reaches production.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Smoke tests in production</strong> provide an extra layer of verification in the live environment.</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This process aligns well with trunk-based development and ensures quality by testing in incremental stages.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are incorrect:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Enforce a Git hook to mandate developers execute unit tests before pushing code to the central repository, then deploy to production after tests:</strong> While this approach does involve unit and integration tests, relying on developers to run unit tests locally before a push is less reliable. Automated testing in CI (via Cloud Build) ensures consistent execution of tests in a controlled environment. Additionally, deploying directly to production before running acceptance tests in the production environment poses a risk.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Enforce a Git hook and designate code as production-ready after passing tests:</strong> Like the first option, this relies on local unit tests, which can be inconsistent. Also, labeling code as production-ready without actually deploying to production or running post-deployment tests (like smoke tests) increases risk.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Initiate Cloud Build for unit tests and deploy to production before acceptance tests:</strong> Deploying to production before running <strong>acceptance tests</strong> is risky. Acceptance tests should be executed in a controlled pre-production environment to ensure the application is fully functional before impacting production users.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Documentation:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build?hl=en\">Cloud Build CI/CD Pipelines</a></p>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e5q26":{"id":"e5q26","exam":5,"n":26,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>In your organization's multi-cloud setup, you're tasked with setting up a CI/CD pipeline for an application deployed using custom Compute Engine images across various cloud platforms. The solution should enable seamless building and deployment of images in the current environment while remaining adaptable to future changes. Which solution stack should you choose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Opt for Cloud Build integrated with Packer.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Choose Cloud Build combined with Google Cloud Deploy.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Implement Google Kubernetes Engine alongside Google Cloud Deploy.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize Cloud Build integrated with kpt (Kubernetes Package Manager).</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Opt for Cloud Build integrated with Packer.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this choice stands out:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Image Focus:</strong> Packer is specialized in building images across multiple platforms, making it ideal for your multi-cloud setup.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Flexibility:</strong> Cloud Build can orchestrate the image building process using Packer and deploy the resulting images to the various cloud platforms.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Future-Proof:</strong> This combination provides a versatile foundation that can easily adapt as you add more cloud environments or change deployment strategies.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Others Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Choose Cloud Build combined with Google Cloud Deploy.</strong> Cloud Deploy is primarily focused on continuous delivery to Kubernetes environments, making it less relevant for a scenario centered around custom Compute Engine images.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Implement Google Kubernetes Engine alongside Google Cloud Deploy.</strong> While Kubernetes and Cloud Deploy are powerful, they add complexity if your core need is building and deploying images to VMs across clouds.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Utilize Cloud Build integrated with kpt (Kubernetes Package Manager).</strong> kpt is a great tool for managing Kubernetes configurations but is less relevant to building multi-platform VM images.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/building/build-vm-images-with-packer\">https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/building/build-vm-images-with-packer</a></p>","lessons":["d2l4","d2l10"]},"e5q27":{"id":"e5q27","exam":5,"n":27,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>To optimize costs, you've deployed a serverless application on Cloud Run in your production environment. How can you effectively identify the application's resource utilization?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Utilize Cloud Trace with distributed tracing to monitor the application's resource utilization.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Use Cloud Profiler with Ops Agent to track the CPU and memory utilization of the application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Monitor the container CPU and memory utilization of the application using Cloud Monitoring.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Employ Cloud Ops to establish logs-based metrics for monitoring the resource utilization of the application.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>III. Monitor the container CPU and memory utilization of the application using Cloud Monitoring.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this is the most direct and effective approach:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Container-Level Metrics:</strong> Cloud Run resources are allocated at the container level, making container-specific metrics essential for understanding utilization.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Monitoring Integration:</strong> Cloud Monitoring provides built-in metrics for tracking CPU, memory, and other resources for Cloud Run instances.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Optimization Focus:</strong> Monitoring container resource usage directly helps you make informed decisions about right-sizing your Cloud Run instances to reduce costs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Others Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Utilize Cloud Trace with distributed tracing...</strong> Cloud Trace is primarily for investigating latency and performance issues in distributed systems, less so for granular resource monitoring.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Use Cloud Profiler with Ops Agent...</strong> Cloud Profiler is designed for code-level profiling, which might be less relevant than container-level resource tracking in a pure serverless context.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Employ Cloud Ops to establish logs-based metrics...</strong> While logs hold valuable information, extracting detailed resource metrics from them can be complex compared to using Cloud Monitoring's pre-built metrics.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/monitoring?hl=ja\">https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/monitoring?hl=ja</a></p>","lessons":["d5l6"]},"e5q28":{"id":"e5q28","exam":5,"n":28,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>Your web application, deployed on a Compute Engine managed instance group with Ops Agent installed on all instances, has detected suspicious activity from a specific IP address. To monitor the number of requests from that IP address with minimal operational complexity, what's the most effective approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Set up the Ops Agent with a logging receiver and establish a logs-based metric.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Develop a script to extract IP address request metrics from the web server log and transmit them to the Cloud Monitoring API.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Enhance the application to directly transmit IP address request metrics to the Cloud Monitoring API.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Configure the Ops Agent with a metrics receiver.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The Best Approach:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Set up the Ops Agent with a logging receiver and establish a logs-based metric.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reasons:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Low Operational Overhead:</strong> The Ops Agent you already have installed can easily collect web server logs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Logs-Based Metrics:</strong> Creating a logs-based metric in Cloud Monitoring allows you to track requests from the suspicious IP without modifying your application.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Flexibility:</strong> You can filter, aggregate, and analyze this metric alongside other Ops Agent data.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Develop a script to extract IP address request metrics...</strong> This adds development &amp; maintenance overhead, especially if your web server log format is complex.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Enhance the application to directly transmit IP address request metrics...</strong> Modifying production code for monitoring purposes introduces risks and complexity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Configure the Ops Agent with a metrics receiver.</strong> While the Ops Agent can collect custom metrics, this likely requires more configuration and potentially custom code than a logs-based solution.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/agent/ops-agent/configuration\">https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/agent/ops-agent/configuration</a></p>","lessons":["d4l4"]},"e5q29":{"id":"e5q29","exam":5,"n":29,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>While setting up the CI/CD pipeline for an application deployed on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) utilizing Kubernetes Deployment, Service, and Ingress, the application team desires deployment using the blue/green methodology. What are the appropriate rollback actions to implement?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Perform a rollback using the kubectl rollout undo command.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Delete the new container image and subsequently terminate the running Pods.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Update the Kubernetes Service to revert to the previous Kubernetes Deployment.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Scale down the new Kubernetes Deployment to zero.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Best Rollback Approach:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>III. Update the Kubernetes Service to revert to the previous Kubernetes Deployment.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reasons:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Blue/Green Alignment:</strong> This directly embodies blue/green strategy, where you switch traffic between fully provisioned deployments, allowing for instantaneous rollback.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Simplicity and Speed:</strong> Updating the service selector is efficient and ensures traffic is quickly directed back to the previous, stable version.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Control:</strong> You retain both deployments, giving you the option to investigate the new deployment if needed.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Perform a rollback using the kubectl rollout undo command.</strong> While feasible, this mutates the current deployment, deviating from the pure blue/green approach where both versions remain intact.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Delete the new container image and subsequently terminate...</strong> This is destructive and can disrupt troubleshooting efforts if issues need to be analyzed in the new deployment.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Scale down the new Kubernetes Deployment to zero.</strong> Scaling down still leaves the new deployment in place, and scaling back up to serve traffic could be slower than simply switching the Service.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e5q30":{"id":"e5q30","exam":5,"n":30,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>In developing an application on Cloud Run requiring access to a third-party API via an API key, adherence to Google-recommended practices for secure storage and usage of the key is paramount. What's the recommended approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Store the API key securely in Secret Manager as a secret, then reference it as an environment variable within the Cloud Run application.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Save the API key in Secret Manager as a secret key, then mount it under the /sys/api_key directory and decrypt it within the Cloud Run application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Secure the API key in Cloud Key Management Service (Cloud KMS) as a key, then reference it as an environment variable within the Cloud Run application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Encrypt the API key using Cloud Key Management Service (Cloud KMS), pass it as an environment variable to Cloud Run, and decrypt it for usage within the applicationX</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct option: I</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why option I is correct</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Store the API key securely in Secret Manager as a secret, then reference it as an environment variable within the Cloud Run application.</strong></p>\n<p>This is the <strong>Google-recommended and exam-accurate approach</strong> for handling sensitive data such as API keys in <strong>Cloud Run</strong>.</p>\n<p>Core security and platform fundamentals:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Secret Manager is purpose-built for secrets</strong> (API keys, tokens, passwords). It provides encryption at rest, fine-grained IAM access control, versioning, and audit logging.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Run natively integrates with Secret Manager</strong> , allowing secrets to be injected securely as environment variables <strong>without embedding secrets in code or images</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>No custom cryptography required</strong> : Google manages encryption and decryption transparently, reducing operational risk and complexity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Least privilege</strong> : The Cloud Run service account can be granted access to only the specific secret it needs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This aligns perfectly with Google’s guidance: <em>use Secret Manager for application secrets and reference them securely at runtime</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Documentation:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/configuring/secrets\">https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/configuring/secrets</a></p>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are not suitable</strong></p>\n<p><strong>II. Mount the key under</strong><code>**/sys/api_key**</code><strong>and decrypt it in the app.</strong><br />\nCloud Run does not support arbitrary filesystem secret mounts like this, and manual decryption is unnecessary and error-prone. This is not a supported or recommended pattern.</p>\n<p><strong>III. Store the API key directly in Cloud KMS and reference it as an environment variable.</strong><br />\nCloud KMS is designed for <strong>encryption keys</strong> , not for storing application secrets. Google explicitly recommends using <strong>Secret Manager on top of KMS</strong> for secret storage.</p>\n<p><strong>IV. Encrypt the API key with KMS and decrypt it inside the application.</strong><br />\nThis adds needless complexity and increases the attack surface. Application-managed cryptography is discouraged when managed secret services are available.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer:</strong> <strong>I</strong></p>","lessons":["d2l8"]},"e5q31":{"id":"e5q31","exam":5,"n":31,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>In optimizing your deployment pipeline with Google Cloud Deploy to minimize toil and reduce deployment time, what steps should you take? (Choose two.)</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Implement a trigger system to alert the relevant team for manual intervention when necessary during the deployment process.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Break down automation tasks into smaller, more manageable components to streamline deployment.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Develop a script to automate the setup of the deployment pipeline within Google Cloud Deploy, reducing manual configuration efforts.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Increase the number of engineers to expedite completion of manual steps in the deployment process.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>V. Automate approval procedures for promoting deployments from the development environment to the testing environment.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answers: I and V</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>The goal is explicitly:<br />\n<strong>Minimize toil</strong> + <strong>Reduce end-to-end deployment time</strong> in <strong>Google Cloud Deploy</strong>.</p>\n<p>The two actions that directly reduce <em>human bottlenecks</em> and optimize <em>pipeline performance</em> are:</p>\n<p><strong>I. Implement a trigger system to alert the relevant team for manual intervention when necessary.</strong></p>\n<p>Why this is correct</p>\n<p>Manual approvals introduce <strong>human latency</strong> , which is one of the biggest contributors to long deployment times.</p>\n<p>A trigger (Pub/Sub → Slack, email, webhook, PagerDuty, etc.) ensures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The right person is notified immediately</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The manual step completes as quickly as possible</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Pipeline idle time is minimized</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This <strong>reduces deployment time</strong> and <strong>reduces toil</strong> caused by missed or delayed approvals.</p>\n<p><strong>V. Automate approval procedures for promoting deployments from dev → test.</strong></p>\n<p>Why this is correct</p>\n<p>If your pipeline already includes automated quality checks, then automatically approving the promotion:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Eliminates a manual step</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Removes human bottlenecks</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Reduces toil</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Speeds up deployment across environments</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Follows CI/CD best practices (automation + gating via tests)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Google Cloud Deploy supports automated approvals when the risk is low and automated tests ensure safety.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Incorrect</strong></p>\n<p><strong>II. Break down automation tasks into smaller pieces — Not impactful</strong></p>\n<p>This improves maintainability but <strong>does not reduce toil or accelerate deployments</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>III. Script the creation of the pipeline — One-time setup</strong></p>\n<p>Automating <em>pipeline creation</em> does not affect:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>ongoing deployment time</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>repetitive toil<br />\nIt's a setup optimization, not a deployment optimization.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>IV. Add more engineers — Not helpful</strong></p>\n<p>Increasing people does not solve CI/CD delays.<br />\nAutomation, not headcount, reduces toil.</p>\n<p><strong>Doc:</strong><a href=\"https://docs.cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/automation\"><strong>Google Cloud Deploy – Approvals &amp; Automation</strong></a></p>\n<p><strong>Final Answer: I and V</strong></p>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e5q32":{"id":"e5q32","exam":5,"n":32,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>In preparation for deploying a new service to production, you aim to achieve automatic scalability across multiple regions using a managed instance group. This service requires significant resources per instance, necessitating meticulous capacity planning. What actions should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Evaluate Cloud Trace insights to determine the most effective resource sizing strategy for the service.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Specify the n2-highcpu-96 machine type within the managed instance group configuration to accommodate the service's resource requirements adequately.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Implement deployment across multiple regions and utilize an internal load balancer to efficiently manage traffic distribution.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Validate that the resource demands align with the available project quota limits in each region before initiating deployment.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct option: IV</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why option IV is correct</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IV. Validate that the resource demands align with the available project quota limits in each region before initiating deployment.</strong></p>\n<p>When deploying a <strong>multi-region managed instance group (MIG)</strong> for a <strong>resource-intensive service</strong> , the most critical prerequisite is <strong>quota validation</strong>.</p>\n<p>Core infrastructure fundamentals behind this choice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Managed instance groups scale automatically</strong> , but <strong>scaling is hard-blocked by quotas</strong> (vCPU, CPU families, memory, IP addresses, regional limits).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>For <strong>large machine types</strong> and <strong>multi-region deployments</strong> , quota exhaustion is one of the <strong>most common production blockers</strong> , even when configuration is correct.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Capacity planning in Google Cloud is quota-driven</strong>. If quotas are insufficient in <em>any</em> target region, autoscaling will fail silently or partially, breaking availability guarantees.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Google explicitly recommends <strong>verifying and increasing quotas before production rollout</strong> , especially for high-resource workloads.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This step ensures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Predictable autoscaling behavior</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>No partial regional failures</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>No last-minute deployment blockers</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are not suitable</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Evaluate Cloud Trace insights for resource sizing.</strong><br />\nCloud Trace is for <strong>latency analysis and request tracing</strong> , not for determining instance sizing or capacity planning.</p>\n<p><strong>II. Specify a large machine type (n2-highcpu-96).</strong><br />\nSelecting a machine type alone does not ensure availability. Without confirming <strong>regional quota capacity</strong> , instances may fail to launch entirely.</p>\n<p><strong>III. Deploy across multiple regions using an internal load balancer.</strong><br />\nInternal load balancers are <strong>regional</strong> , not global. They cannot distribute traffic across regions and are inappropriate for multi-region production access.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer:</strong> <strong>IV</strong></p>","lessons":["d3l7"]},"e5q33":{"id":"e5q33","exam":5,"n":33,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Your organization employs Jenkins hosted on Google Cloud VM instances for CI/CD processes. To expand functionality, you want to integrate infrastructure as code automation using Terraform. Ensuring the Terraform module within Jenkins can create Google Cloud resources with proper authorization is essential. How should you proceed to align with Google-recommended practices?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Ensure the Jenkins VM instance is associated with a service account holding appropriate Identity and Access Management (IAM) permissions.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Implement the Terraform module to enable Secret Manager for secure credential retrieval.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Establish a dedicated service account for the Terraform instance. Then, configure the GOOGLE_CREDENTIALS environment variable on the Jenkins server, injecting the secret key value.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Integrate the gcloud auth application-default login command into Jenkins as a preliminary step before executing Terraform commands.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>I. Ensure the Jenkins VM instance is associated with a service account holding appropriate Identity and Access Management (IAM) permissions.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this is the most secure and easily managed method:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Principle of Least Privilege:</strong> Grant the service account only the specific IAM permissions needed for Terraform to manage resources, minimizing potential security risks.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Workload Identity:</strong> Workload Identity is specifically designed for VM-to-Google Cloud service communication. It doesn't involve long-lived credentials or manual key management.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Operational Ease:</strong> You manage permissions centrally through IAM instead of handling credentials directly within Jenkins jobs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Implement the Terraform module to enable Secret Manager...</strong> Managing secrets with Secret Manager is recommended, but it doesn't directly address how Jenkins obtains authorization to perform actions on GCP.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Establish a dedicated service account ...</strong> Creating a service account is necessary, but storing the key as an environment variable introduces risks if the Jenkins server is compromised.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Integrate the gcloud auth application-default login ...</strong> This necessitates manual key rotation and places sensitive credentials on your Jenkins server</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d1l3"]},"e5q34":{"id":"e5q34","exam":5,"n":34,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>In alignment with your company's security strategy to shift left on security, the InfoSec team requires all teams to enforce guardrails on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) clusters, allowing only the deployment of trusted and approved images. How can you fulfill this requirement effectively?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Implement Container Analysis in Artifact Registry to scan container images for common vulnerabilities and exposures (CVEs) during your CI/CD pipeline.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize Binary Authorization to verify and attest images during the CI/CD pipeline, ensuring only approved images are deployed to GKE clusters.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Establish Identity and Access Management (IAM) policies to establish a least privilege model on GKE clusters, restricting image deployments based on predefined permissions.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Deploy Falco or Twistlock on GKE to actively monitor running Pods for vulnerabilities and security issues.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct option: II</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why option II is correct</strong></p>\n<p><strong>II. Utilize Binary Authorization to verify and attest images during the CI/CD pipeline, ensuring only approved images are deployed to GKE clusters.</strong></p>\n<p>This is the <strong>Google-recommended and exam-correct mechanism</strong> for enforcing <strong>preventive (shift-left) security guardrails</strong> on GKE.</p>\n<p>Core security fundamentals:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Binary Authorization is an admission control system</strong> for GKE that enforces <strong>deploy-time policies</strong> , not just detection.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>It ensures <strong>only container images that are cryptographically attested and explicitly approved</strong> can be deployed to a cluster.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Policies can require:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Images to come from trusted registries</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Images to be signed by approved attestors</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>CI/CD pipelines to complete security checks before deployment</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>This directly fulfills the requirement of <strong>allowing only trusted and approved images</strong> , blocking everything else <strong>before it reaches production</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Binary Authorization is the canonical Google Cloud solution for <strong>shift-left container security on GKE</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Authoritative documentation:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs/overview\">https://cloud.google.com/binary-authorization/docs/overview</a></p>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are not suitable</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Container Analysis image scanning</strong><br />\nImage scanning detects vulnerabilities but <strong>does not prevent deployment</strong>. It is advisory, not an enforcement guardrail.</p>\n<p><strong>III. IAM-based restrictions on image deployment</strong><br />\nIAM controls <em>who</em> can deploy, not <em>what</em> images are deployed. It cannot validate image trust or integrity.</p>\n<p><strong>IV. Runtime security tools (Falco / Twistlock)</strong><br />\nThese tools detect issues <strong>after pods are already running</strong> , which is <strong>shift-right</strong> , not shift-left. They do not prevent untrusted images from being deployed.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer:</strong> <strong>II</strong></p>","lessons":["d2l9"]},"e5q35":{"id":"e5q35","exam":5,"n":35,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>In your company's Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) setup, the development environment's clusters are logging applications with verbosity, accessed by developers via kubectl logs without utilizing Cloud Logging. Additionally, there's no standardized logging structure across applications. To minimize logging-related costs while retaining GKE operational logs, what's the optimal approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Update the development cluster using the command: gcloud container clusters update --logging=SYSTEM.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Modify the logging configuration for the development cluster with: gcloud container clusters update --logging=WORKLOAD.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Disable the default logging sink within the project associated with the development environment using: gcloud logging sinks update _Default --disabled.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Implement an exclusion filter on the default logging sink within the project tied to the development environment with: severity &gt;= DEBUG resource.type = \"k8s_container\".</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IV. Implement an exclusion filter on the default logging sink within the project tied to the development environment with: severity &gt;= DEBUG resource.type = \"k8s_container\".</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Targeted Filtering:</strong> Directly addresses the verbose application logs by filtering out DEBUG and lower severity log entries from Kubernetes containers, significantly reducing log volume.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Retains Operational Logs:</strong> Keeps essential GKE system logs flowing to Cloud Logging for operational visibility.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Minimal Change:</strong> Minimizes disruption, requiring just a filter update on the existing logging sink.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cost-Effective:</strong> Reduces the volume of ingested logs, directly impacting logging costs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Update the development cluster using the command: gcloud container clusters update --logging=SYSTEM.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Too Broad:</strong> Disables application logging entirely, potentially hindering necessary debugging for developers.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Loses Valuable Developer Insights:</strong> May eliminate useful troubleshooting logs generated by developer-side applications.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Modify the logging configuration for the development cluster with: gcloud container clusters update --logging=WORKLOAD.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No Filtering:</strong> Doesn't address the verbosity issue. Logs would still be sent to Cloud Logging, incurring costs.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Disable the default logging sink within the project associated with the development environment using: gcloud logging sinks update _Default --disabled.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Extreme Measure:</strong> Completely disables all logging, including crucial GKE operational logs, hindering monitoring and troubleshooting.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d5l7"]},"e5q36":{"id":"e5q36","exam":5,"n":36,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You're tasked with establishing a Cloud Monitoring SLO for an upcoming service launch, aiming to ensure that requests are handled within 300 ms for at least 90% of the time per calendar month. How should you select the metric and evaluation method to meet this objective?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Opt for a latency metric using a request-based evaluation approach.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Opt for a latency metric using a window-based evaluation approach.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Opt for an availability metric using a request-based evaluation approach.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Opt for an availability metric using a window-based evaluation approach.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>I. Opt for a latency metric using a request-based evaluation approach.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Matches the SLO Requirement:</strong> Directly aligns with the performance goal of requests being handled within 300 ms.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Focus on User Experience:</strong> Request-based evaluation emphasizes the individual user experience with each request, ensuring a majority of users see acceptable performance.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Typical for Latency SLOs:</strong> This combination is commonly used for latency-based SLOs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Opt for a latency metric using a window-based evaluation approach.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Less User-Centric:</strong> While relevant, window-based SLOs can mask significant latency issues within short time periods, potentially not reflecting the actual user experience.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Opt for an availability metric using a request-based evaluation approach.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Measures a Different Aspect:</strong> Availability deals with whether a service can respond at all, not how fast it responds. It wouldn't accurately measure the desired 300ms performance.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Opt for an availability metric using a window-based evaluation approach.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Even Less Granular:</strong> This combines the wrong metric type (availability) with the less user-centric evaluation (window-based).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring#slo-type-request\">https://cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring#slo-type-request</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring#slo-types\">https://cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring#slo-types</a></p>","lessons":["d3l2"]},"e5q37":{"id":"e5q37","exam":5,"n":37,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>To enforce multiple constraint templates, including policies such as restricting the Kubernetes API, across your Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) clusters, while ensuring these parameters are stored in a GitHub repository and automatically applied upon changes, what approach should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Implement a GitHub action to trigger Cloud Build upon parameter changes, then use Cloud Build to execute a gcloud CLI command for applying the modifications.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Utilize a webhook to notify Anthos Service Mesh of changes in GitHub, triggering the application of modifications.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Configure Anthos Config Management with the GitHub repository to automatically apply changes from the repository.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Configure Config Connector with the GitHub repository to automatically apply changes from the repository.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>III. Configure Anthos Config Management with the GitHub repository to automatically apply changes from the repository.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Purpose-Built Tool:</strong> Anthos Config Management (ACM) is designed specifically for managing configurations across multiple Kubernetes clusters, including policies like constraint templates.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>GitOps Workflow:</strong> ACM integrates seamlessly with Git, enabling a GitOps workflow where configuration changes in the repository trigger automatic updates to clusters.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Centralized Management:</strong> Provides a centralized way to enforce multiple constraint templates consistently across GKE clusters.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Implement a GitHub action to trigger Cloud Build upon parameter changes, then use Cloud Build to execute a gcloud CLI command for applying the modifications.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>More Complexity:</strong> This approach requires building custom automation with GitHub Actions and Cloud Build. It's less streamlined than a dedicated config management tool like ACM.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Utilize a webhook to notify Anthos Service Mesh of changes in GitHub, triggering the application of modifications.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mismatched Focus:</strong> Anthos Service Mesh is primarily focused on service networking and traffic management, not enforcing cluster policies across multiple clusters.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Configure Config Connector with the GitHub repository to automatically apply changes from the repository.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Specific Use Case:</strong> Config Connector is suitable for managing external GCP resources via Kubernetes style definitions, but not the primary choice for enforcing intra-cluster Kubernetes policies.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://medium.com/@kasiarun/introduction-to-anthos-config-management-1a43917c26ae\">https://medium.com/@kasiarun/introduction-to-anthos-config-management-1a43917c26ae</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/anthos-config-management/docs/concepts/policy-controller\">https://cloud.google.com/anthos-config-management/docs/concepts/policy-controller</a></p>","lessons":["d1l7"]},"e5q38":{"id":"e5q38","exam":5,"n":38,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Your company manages applications in Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) using a GitOps approach. Developers frequently create cloud resources for their applications. To enable developers to handle infrastructure as code while adhering to Google-recommended practices and preventing configuration drift, what should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Implement and configure Config Connector in Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE).</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Set up Cloud Build with a Terraform builder to execute <code>**terraform plan**</code> and <code>**terraform apply**</code> commands.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Deploy a Pod resource with a Terraform Docker image to execute <code>**terraform plan**</code> and <code>**terraform apply**</code> commands.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Configure a Job resource with a Terraform Docker image to execute <code>**terraform plan**</code> and <code>**terraform apply**</code> commands.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Option:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Implement and configure Config Connector in Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>GitOps Alignment:</strong> Config Connector is explicitly designed to manage GCP resources using Kubernetes-style declarative configurations, fitting seamlessly into a GitOps workflow.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google-Recommended:</strong> Config Connector is Google's recommended tool for enabling Infrastructure as Code practices on GKE, ensuring compatibility and support.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Prevents Configuration Drift:</strong> By defining cloud resources as Kubernetes manifests, tracked in Git, Config Connector enforces consistency and detects/corrects drift.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Set up Cloud Build with a Terraform builder to execute terraform plan and terraform apply commands.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Less Integrated:</strong> While workable, it introduces an additional layer (Cloud Build) for managing cloud resources, increasing complexity compared to Config Connector's native Kubernetes integration.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Deploy a Pod resource with a Terraform Docker image to execute terraform plan and terraform apply commands.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Manual Management:</strong> Running Terraform in a Pod requires managing the Pod's lifecycle, state persistence, and ensuring it can run to completion, adding operational overhead.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Configure a Job resource with a Terraform Docker image to execute terraform plan and terraform apply commands.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Temporary Solution:</strong> Jobs in Kubernetes are suitable for one-time tasks, not continuous management of cloud resources like Config Connector provides.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/config-connector/docs/overview\">https://cloud.google.com/config-connector/docs/overview</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/anthos-config-management-config-controller-available-on-gke\">https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/containers-kubernetes/anthos-config-management-config-controller-available-on-gke</a></p>","lessons":["d1l7"]},"e5q39":{"id":"e5q39","exam":5,"n":39,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>Your company operates applications in Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), with several relying on ephemeral volumes. Recently, you've observed instability in some applications due to the DiskPressure node condition on the worker nodes. However, you lack execute access to workloads and nodes. What should you do to identify which Pods are causing the issue?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Use Metrics Explorer to examine the node/ephemeral_storage/used_bytes metric.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Use Metrics Explorer to examine the container/ephemeral_storage/used_bytes metric.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Identify all Pods with emptyDir volumes and employ the df -h command to measure volume disk usage.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Identify all Pods with emptyDir volumes and use the df -sh * command to measure volume disk usage.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct option: II</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why option II is correct</strong></p>\n<p><strong>II. Use Metrics Explorer to examine the</strong><code>**container/ephemeral_storage/used_bytes**</code><strong>metric.</strong></p>\n<p>You explicitly <strong>do not have execute access</strong> to workloads or nodes, so the solution must rely on <strong>observability data</strong> , not shell commands.<br />\nTo identify <strong>which Pods (containers)</strong> are contributing to <strong>DiskPressure</strong> , you need <strong>container-level visibility</strong> into ephemeral storage usage.</p>\n<p>Key fundamentals:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>DiskPressure</strong> on a node is often caused by <strong>excessive ephemeral storage consumption by one or more Pods</strong> (for example, large <code>emptyDir</code> usage, logs, or temporary files).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The metric <code>**container/ephemeral_storage/used_bytes**</code> provides <strong>per-container (and therefore per-Pod)</strong> ephemeral storage usage.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Using <strong>Metrics Explorer</strong> , you can:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Group by <code>pod_name</code> / <code>container_name</code></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Identify the top consumers</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Correlate spikes with DiskPressure events</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>This works <strong>without any exec access</strong> , fully satisfying the constraint.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is the <strong>Google-recommended, SRE-aligned approach</strong> for diagnosing resource pressure issues when direct access is unavailable.</p>\n<p><strong>Documentation:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/api/metrics_gcp#gcp-kubernetes\">https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/api/metrics_gcp#gcp-kubernetes</a></p>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are not suitable</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I.</strong><code>**node/ephemeral_storage/used_bytes**</code><br />\nThis metric shows <strong>node-level usage only</strong>. It tells you <em>that</em> a node is under pressure, but not <strong>which Pods</strong> are responsible.</p>\n<p><strong>III. Use</strong><code>**df -h**</code><strong>on Pods with emptyDir volumes</strong><br />\nThis requires <strong>exec access to workloads or nodes</strong> , which you explicitly do not have.</p>\n<p><strong>IV. Use</strong><code>**df -sh ***</code> <strong>on Pods with emptyDir volumes</strong><br />\nSame issue as III—this is a command-line, in-Pod approach and is not possible without execute access.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer:</strong> <strong>II</strong></p>","lessons":["d4l6","d4l12"]},"e5q40":{"id":"e5q40","exam":5,"n":40,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Your company has recently migrated to Google Cloud. To efficiently provision new projects and essential resources in Google Cloud, you aim to design a solution that is both swift and dependable. What should be your approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Opt for manual creation of projects using the Google Cloud console.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Develop a script utilizing the gcloud CLI, capable of accepting relevant parameters from the request, and store it in a Git repository.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Create a Terraform module and store it in your version control repository. Execute the terraform apply command to instantiate the new project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Utilize the Terraform repositories provided by the Cloud Foundation Toolkit, applying the code with appropriate parameters to create the Google Cloud project and related resources.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct option: IV</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why option IV is correct</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IV. Utilize the Terraform repositories provided by the Cloud Foundation Toolkit, applying the code with appropriate parameters to create the Google Cloud project and related resources.</strong></p>\n<p>This is the <strong>most Google-recommended, scalable, and reliable approach</strong> for rapidly provisioning Google Cloud projects and foundational resources after a migration.</p>\n<p>Core fundamentals behind this choice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Foundation Toolkit (CFT)</strong> provides <strong>production-ready, opinionated Terraform modules</strong> designed by Google specifically for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Project creation</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>IAM setup</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Networking</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Logging, billing, and org-level governance</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The modules follow <strong>Google Cloud best practices</strong> and are continuously maintained to stay aligned with platform changes.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Using CFT significantly <strong>reduces risk, time, and design errors</strong> compared to building everything from scratch.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Terraform ensures:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Idempotent and repeatable provisioning</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Version control and auditability</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Fast, parameter-driven project creation</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This approach is ideal when the goal is <strong>speed + reliability + standardization</strong> at scale.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are not suitable</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Manual project creation via the console</strong><br />\nManual workflows are slow, error-prone, non-repeatable, and unsuitable for enterprise-scale provisioning.</p>\n<p><strong>II. Custom gcloud CLI script stored in Git</strong><br />\nWhile better than manual steps, scripts lack:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Strong state management</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Dependency tracking</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Built-in drift detection<br />\nTerraform is explicitly recommended over imperative scripting for infrastructure provisioning.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>III. Create a custom Terraform module from scratch</strong><br />\nThis is viable, but unnecessary. Google already provides <strong>battle-tested modules</strong> via CFT, making this option slower and riskier than IV.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer:</strong> <strong>IV</strong></p>","lessons":["d1l6"]},"e5q41":{"id":"e5q41","exam":5,"n":41,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>As you monitor a service employing n2-standard-2 Compute Engine instances for delivering large files, users complain of slow download speeds. Cloud Monitoring indicates that VMs are operating at maximum network throughput. How can you improve network throughput performance?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Enhance the VMs by adding more network interface controllers (NICs).</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Implement a Cloud NAT gateway and link it to the VMs' subnet.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Upgrade the VMs' machine type to n2-standard-8.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Deploy the Ops Agent to access supplementary monitoring metrics.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct option: III</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why option III is correct</strong></p>\n<p><strong>III. Upgrade the VMs' machine type to n2-standard-8.</strong></p>\n<p>In Google Compute Engine, <strong>network throughput scales with machine type size</strong>. When Cloud Monitoring shows that VMs are already operating at <strong>maximum network throughput</strong> , it means the instances have hit their <strong>per-VM network bandwidth ceiling</strong> , not a software or configuration issue.</p>\n<p>Key fundamental concepts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Network performance is tied to vCPU count</strong> : Larger machine types provide higher <strong>egress and ingress bandwidth caps</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>n2-standard-2</strong> instances have relatively low maximum network throughput, which is insufficient for serving <strong>large file downloads</strong> at high speed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>By upgrading to <strong>n2-standard-8</strong> , you significantly increase:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Available network bandwidth</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Packet processing capacity</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Sustained throughput per VM</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>This improves download speeds <strong>without changing application logic or architecture</strong> , making it the most direct and effective fix.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is a classic <strong>capacity scaling</strong> scenario where vertical scaling is the correct response.</p>\n<p><strong>Relevant documentation:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/network-bandwidth\">https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/network-bandwidth</a></p>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are not suitable</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Add more network interface controllers (NICs).</strong><br />\nMultiple NICs are used for <strong>network isolation or multi-network connectivity</strong> , not for increasing outbound throughput of a single workload. They do not aggregate bandwidth for standard traffic flows.</p>\n<p><strong>II. Implement a Cloud NAT gateway.</strong><br />\nCloud NAT provides <strong>outbound internet access</strong> for private VMs. It does not increase VM network throughput and can actually introduce additional limits.</p>\n<p><strong>IV. Deploy the Ops Agent.</strong><br />\nThe Ops Agent improves observability only. It does not affect performance and does not remove network bandwidth limits.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer:</strong> <strong>III</strong></p>","lessons":["d5l4"]},"e5q42":{"id":"e5q42","exam":5,"n":42,"domain":4,"html":"<p>You support an <strong>e-commerce application</strong> that runs on a <strong>large Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) cluster</strong> deployed <strong>both on-premises and on Google Cloud</strong>. The application consists of <strong>microservices running in containers</strong>.</p>\n<p>You want to <strong>identify containers that are using the most CPU and memory</strong>.</p>\n<p>What should you do?</p>","options":[{"html":"<p><strong>A.</strong> Use <strong>Cloud Operations for GKE (Cloud Monitoring and Cloud Logging)</strong>.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p><strong>B.</strong> Use <strong>Prometheus</strong> to collect and aggregate logs per container, and then analyze the results in <strong>Grafana</strong>.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>C.</strong> Use the <strong>Cloud Monitoring API</strong> to create <strong>custom metrics</strong> , and then organize your containers using <strong>groups</strong>.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p><strong>D.</strong> Use <strong>Cloud Logging</strong> to export application logs to <strong>BigQuery</strong> , aggregate logs per container, and then analyze <strong>CPU and memory consumption</strong>.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer: A. Use Cloud Operations for GKE (Cloud Monitoring and Cloud Logging).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation</strong></p>\n<p>To identify which containers are consuming the most <strong>CPU</strong> and <strong>memory</strong> across a hybrid GKE deployment (on-prem + Google Cloud), the most efficient and fully integrated solution is <strong>Cloud Operations for GKE</strong>.</p>\n<p>Cloud Operations for GKE provides:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Per-container CPU usage metrics</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Per-container memory usage metrics</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Node and pod-level resource dashboards</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Built-in <strong>workload monitoring</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Automatic telemetry collection with minimal setup</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You can directly view the highest resource-consuming containers using built-in charts and dashboards—no need for custom metrics, log exports, or additional tooling.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Incorrect</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Prometheus + Grafana</strong></p>\n<p>Although technically possible and common in Kubernetes environments, it introduces:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>More infrastructure to manage</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Higher operational overhead</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>No need for Grafana when Cloud Operations already provides dashboards<br />\nNot the simplest or most integrated solution.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>C. Create custom metrics with Cloud Monitoring API</strong></p>\n<p>Not required—CPU and memory metrics for containers are <strong>already provided automatically</strong> by GKE + Cloud Monitoring.<br />\nCustom metrics add unnecessary work.</p>\n<p><strong>D. Export logs to BigQuery</strong></p>\n<p>Application logs do <strong>not</strong> include CPU/memory usage.<br />\nYou can't infer resource consumption from logs, so this approach will not work.</p>\n<p><strong>Authoritative Google Documentation</strong></p>\n<p>Cloud Operations for GKE – Observability and Container Metrics<br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/gke\">https://cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/gke</a></p>\n<p><strong>Final Answer: A. Use Cloud Operations for GKE</strong></p>","lessons":["d4l6"]},"e5q43":{"id":"e5q43","exam":5,"n":43,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>Following your organization's recent adoption of a container-based workflow for application development, your team consistently deploys numerous applications to a Kubernetes cluster in the production environment through an automated build pipeline. However, the security auditor has expressed concerns regarding the potential for developers or operators to bypass automated testing and push unauthorized code changes to production. How can you enforce approvals in this scenario?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Implement protected branches in the build system, mandating approval of pull requests.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Deploy an Admission Controller to validate that incoming requests originate from approved sources.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Utilize Kubernetes Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) to restrict access exclusively to approved users.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Enable binary authorization within the Kubernetes cluster and configure the build pipeline as an attestor.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>IV. Enable binary authorization within the Kubernetes cluster and configure the build pipeline as an attestor.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this approach is the strongest:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Image-Level Enforcement:</strong> Binary authorization provides granular control at the container image level. It ensures only images that have passed the required validation and approval steps (automated tests, security scans, human sign-off, etc.) within your build pipeline can be deployed into the Kubernetes cluster.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Build Pipeline Integration:</strong> Configuring the build pipeline as an attestor provides a tamper-resistant mechanism to track the approval state of an image along its creation process.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security Focus:</strong> This approach is specifically tailored to prevent the deployment of unauthorized code, directly addressing the auditor's concern.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>While other options provide security benefits, they're either too broad or indirect:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Implement protected branches...</strong> Enforces code review but doesn't prevent unauthorized image builds and deployments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Deploy an Admission Controller...</strong> Useful for validating various properties, but would require defining complex rules and doesn't intrinsically track the approval status of an image.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Utilize Kubernetes RBAC...</strong> Vital for access control, but it doesn't enforce code approval within the build process.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/binary-authorization\">https://cloud.google.com/binary-authorization</a></p>"},"e5q44":{"id":"e5q44","exam":5,"n":44,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You manage a service with a well-defined Service Level Objective (SLO) that has consistently been achieved for the past 6 months, resulting in high customer satisfaction. With most operational tasks automated and only a few repetitive tasks occurring frequently, you aim to optimize the balance between reliability and deployment velocity while adhering to site reliability engineering best practices. What steps should you take? (Select two.)</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Consider tightening the service's SLO criteria.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Potentially accelerate the service's deployment velocity and/or risk profile.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Allocate engineering resources to other services requiring greater reliability.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Collaborate with the product team to prioritize reliability improvements over new features.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>V. Adjust the implementation of your Service Level Indicators (SLIs) to broaden coverage.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The two best steps for this scenario are:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Potentially accelerate the service's deployment velocity and/or risk profile.</strong> Your service's track record demonstrates the ability to maintain a reliable SLO. This allows for experimentation with quicker iterations and potentially more complex changes to boost innovation.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Allocate engineering resources to other services requiring greater reliability.</strong> Capitalize on your current service's success by shifting resources to areas that need attention. This promotes a balanced and efficient approach across your systems.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why these options are the most suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Utilizing Success:</strong> Your service's reliability creates space for focusing efforts on other areas or pushing for greater feature development velocity.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>SRE Alignment:</strong> This approach aligns with SRE principles by allocating resources strategically across systems to maximize overall user experience.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why other options are less ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Consider tightening the service's SLO criteria.</strong> While a possibility, it might be unnecessary given the consistent success and could even introduce undue pressure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Collaborate with the product team to prioritize reliability improvements over new features.</strong> With the current service already performing well, this might unnecessarily stifle product development.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>V. Adjust the implementation of your Service Level Indicators (SLIs) to broaden coverage.</strong> While always beneficial to improve monitoring, it doesn't directly address the opportunity to balance reliability and velocity.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/#slo-decision-matrix\">https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/#slo-decision-matrix</a></p>","lessons":["d3l2"]},"e5q45":{"id":"e5q45","exam":5,"n":45,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>You're utilizing Terraform to manage infrastructure as code within a CI/CD pipeline. However, you notice that multiple copies of the entire infrastructure stack persist in your Google Cloud project, with a new copy created each time a change is made to the existing infrastructure. To optimize your cloud spend following Google-recommended practices, what steps should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Implement a new pipeline to automatically delete outdated infrastructure stacks when they're no longer required.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Ensure that the pipeline stores and retrieves the terraform.tfstate file from Cloud Storage using the Terraform gcs backend.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Verify that the pipeline stores and retrieves the terraform.tfstate file from a source control repository.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Update the pipeline to remove any existing infrastructure before applying the latest configuration.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>II. Ensure that the pipeline stores and retrieves the terraform.tfstate file from Cloud Storage using the Terraform gcs backend.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reasoning:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>State Management:</strong> The <code>terraform.tfstate</code> file is vital as it tracks the mapping between your Terraform configuration and the actual deployed infrastructure. Storing it remotely in Cloud Storage allows Terraform to correctly identify changes needed, instead of always creating an entirely new stack.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Integration:</strong> The GCS backend integrates seamlessly with Google Cloud, ensuring secure and reliable state storage.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cost Optimization:</strong> This approach directly addresses the issue of redundant stacks proliferating, reducing the need for unnecessary resources and associated costs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Implement a new pipeline...</strong> While deleting old infrastructure could help, it doesn't address the root cause of the issue – incorrect state management.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Verify that the pipeline stores...</strong> Source control systems are excellent for code versioning, but less ideal for handling the dynamic <code>terraform.tfstate</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. Update the pipeline to remove...</strong> Destructive actions can lead to accidental data loss if applied without careful state tracking.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/docs/terraform/resource-management/managing-infrastructure-as-code#configuring_terraform_to_store_state_in_a_cloud_storage_bucket\">https://cloud.google.com/docs/terraform/resource-management/managing-infrastructure-as-code#configuring_terraform_to_store_state_in_a_cloud_storage_bucket</a></p>","lessons":["d1l6"]},"e5q46":{"id":"e5q46","exam":5,"n":46,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Your organization aims to elevate the availability target of an application from 99.9% to 99.99% with a $2,000 investment. The current revenue generated by the application is $1,000,000. You need to assess whether the increased availability justifies the investment for a single year of usage. What should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Evaluate the value of enhanced availability at $900 and conclude that the investment is not justified.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Assess the value of enhanced availability at $1,000 and conclude that the investment is not justified.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Determine the value of enhanced availability at $1,000 and conclude that the investment is justified.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Calculate the value of enhanced availability at $9,000 and conclude that the investment is justified.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct choice — I. “Evaluate the value of enhanced availability at $900 and conclude that the investment is not justified.”</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why option I is correct</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>How many outage hours are removed?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>99.9 % (“three-nines”) permits ≈ 8 h 45 m 57 s of downtime per year. <a href=\"https://uptime.is/\">uptime.is</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>99.99 % (“four-nines”) permits ≈ 52 m 36 s of downtime per year. <a href=\"https://uptime.is/99.99\">uptime.is</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Δ downtime = 8.76 h − 0.876 h ≈ 7.884 h saved.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>How much money does that save?</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Annual revenue = $1 000 000.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Revenue/hour = $1 000 000 ÷ 8 760 h ≈ $114.16 per hour.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Value of avoided downtime = 7.884 h × $114.16 ≈ <strong>$900</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Compare benefit to cost.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Benefit ≈ $900 / year &lt; Cost = $2 000 / year → financially <strong>unfavorable</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Therefore option I, which identifies the benefit as $900 and rejects the $2 000 spend, matches the calculation.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are wrong</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. “Assess the value at $1 000 and conclude the investment is not justified.”</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The conclusion (not justified) is fine, but the <em>benefit figure</em> is wrong. Correct math gives ≈ $900, not $1 000; an extra $100 appears from rounding far beyond two significant digits.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. “Determine the value at $1 000 and conclude the investment is justified.”</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Two errors: the benefit is overstated (should be ≈ $900) <strong>and</strong> even at $1 000 the cost still exceeds the benefit, so calling it “justified” is logically inconsistent.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV. “Calculate the value at $9 000 and conclude the investment is justified.”</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The $9 000 figure is off by an order of magnitude (likely mis-placing a decimal). Because the correct benefit is only about $900, this option both mis-states the savings and reaches an incorrect conclusion.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> Elevating availability from 99.9 % to 99.99 % yields ~7.9 hours of extra uptime worth roughly <strong>$900</strong> in annual revenue protection—far short of the proposed <strong>$2 000</strong> outlay. Only option I reflects that reality.</p>","lessons":["d3l2"]},"e5q47":{"id":"e5q47","exam":5,"n":47,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>You encounter an error \"The organization policy constraint iam.disableServiceAccounKeyCreation is enforced\" when attempting to export a service account key for a third-party application. How can you resolve this while adhering to Google's security recommendations?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Activate the default service account key and obtain it.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Eliminate the iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation policy at the organization level, then generate the key.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Deactivate the service account key creation policy within the project's folder and obtain the default key.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Include a directive to disable the iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation policy in your project, then proceed to create a key.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct answer: D — Add a project-level exception to the</strong><code>**iam.disableServiceAccountKeyCreation**</code><strong>constraint, create the one key you need, then keep the exception as narrow and short-lived as possible.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Google recommends enforcing <strong>Disable service account key creation</strong> at the <strong>organization</strong> root to block long-lived user-managed keys everywhere by default. <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/organization-policy/restricting-service-accounts?utm_source=chatgpt.com\">cloud.google.com</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>When a workload truly cannot use a stronger alternative (for example, Workload Identity Federation), you should <strong>override the constraint only on the affected project or even a single service account</strong>. In the policy editor you choose <em>Override parent’s policy</em> and switch <strong>Enforcement</strong> off just for that scope. <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/troubleshoot-org-policies?utm_source=chatgpt.com\">cloud.google.com</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This keeps the global protection intact while letting you create exactly one key for the third-party integration—and nothing else.</p>\n<p><strong>Why the other options fall short</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option A – “Activate the default service-account key and obtain it.”</strong><br />\nGoogle-managed keys are rotated automatically and are <strong>not downloadable</strong> ; only newly created <strong>user-managed</strong> keys can ever be exported. <a href=\"https://www.googlecloudcommunity.com/gc/Developer-Tools/Issue-with-user-managed-GCP-public-key-for-Service-Account/td-p/780657?utm_source=chatgpt.com\">googlecloudcommunity.com</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option B – “Remove the constraint at the organization level.”</strong><br />\nDisabling the policy for the whole organization re-opens key creation for every project, defeating the very control Google recommends you keep enabled. <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/organization-policy/restricting-service-accounts?utm_source=chatgpt.com\">cloud.google.com</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Option C – “Turn the policy off on the folder.”</strong><br />\nA folder-wide exemption is narrower than option B but still broader than necessary. Best practice is the smallest possible scope—usually a single project or service account. <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/troubleshoot-org-policies?utm_source=chatgpt.com\">cloud.google.com</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Extra security tip</strong></p>\n<p>Before creating any key, verify whether the third-party product can authenticate with <strong>Workload Identity Federation</strong> , which “eliminates the maintenance and security burden associated with service-account keys.” <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/workload-identity-federation?utm_source=chatgpt.com\">cloud.google.com</a></p>","lessons":["d1l2"]},"e5q48":{"id":"e5q48","exam":5,"n":48,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Your company adheres to Site Reliability Engineering principles. Following a severe incident triggered by a software change, you're drafting a postmortem to prevent similar incidents in the future. What steps should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Identify the engineers accountable for the incident and escalate the matter to senior management.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Ensure that test cases capable of catching errors like this are successfully executed before every new software release.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Conduct follow-ups with the staff who reviewed the changes and establish best practices for future adherence.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Develop a policy mandating that on-call teams promptly engage engineers and management in discussions to devise an action plan upon incident occurrence.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The best answer here is</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Ensure that test cases capable of catching errors like this are successfully executed before every new software release.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why and a breakdown of the other options:</p>\n<p><strong>Why Option B is the Best Approach:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Focus on Prevention:</strong> SRE is about building resilient systems and preventing failures. While other options have value, this option directly addresses the root cause by creating a safety net to catch similar errors before they impact production.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Systems-Based Approach:</strong> It emphasizes improving processes rather than focusing on individuals, aligning with SRE and fostering a blameless culture.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A. Identify the engineers accountable for the incident and escalate the matter to senior management.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blame Culture:</strong> This promotes a culture of fear and discourages open communication, undermining SRE principles and making it harder to uncover the true systemic causes of failure.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>C. Conduct follow-ups with the staff who reviewed the changes and establish best practices for future adherence.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>While valuable, it's reactive:</strong> This option is important for learning from the incident, but it doesn't directly prevent a similar error from happening again.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>D. Develop a policy mandating that on-call teams promptly engage engineers and management in discussions to devise an action plan upon incident occurrence.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Focus on Response, Not Prevention:</strong> This optimizes incident response, which is vital, but the main goal of the postmortem is to proactively avoid repeating the incident.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l5"]},"e5q49":{"id":"e5q49","exam":5,"n":49,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>You're maintaining a user-facing web application and analyzing its error budget over the past six months. Despite occasional fluctuations, the application has consistently stayed below 5% of its error budget. After a Service Level Objective (SLO) review with stakeholders, you confirm that the current SLO is appropriate. How can you better align the application's SLO with its actual reliability, considering velocity, reliability, and business needs? (Select two.)</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Increase serving capacity across all application zones.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Opt for more frequent or potentially risky application releases.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Adjust the SLO to better match the observed reliability of the application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Introduce and assess additional Service Level Indicators (SLIs) for the application.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>E. Schedule planned downtime to utilize more of the error budget, ensuring users aren't reliant on a stricter SLO.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answers: B and D</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Reasoning</strong></p>\n<p>Your application has consistently consumed <strong>less than 5%</strong> of its error budget over six months.<br />\nThis means:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The service is <strong>more reliable than required</strong> by the current SLO.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>You are <strong>under-utilizing your error budget</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>You can afford to <strong>move faster</strong> without hurting users or violating SLOs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Google SRE guidance states:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>If your error budget is consistently under-consumed</strong> , you should <strong>increase the release velocity</strong>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>If your SLO is appropriate and not too strict</strong> , you should <strong>NOT tighten it</strong> , but instead improve value delivery or observability by adding meaningful SLIs.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This leads to the two correct choices.</p>\n<p><strong>Correct Answer 1: B. Opt for more frequent or potentially risky application releases.</strong></p>\n<p>Why this is correct</p>\n<p>Underused error budget means:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>You can ship faster</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>You can take more risk</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>You can accelerate innovation</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is exactly what Google’s <strong>Error Budget Policy</strong> recommends.</p>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong><br />\n<strong>Google SRE Book - Error Budget Policy</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://sre.google/sre-book/availability-table/\">https://sre.google/sre-book/availability-table/</a></p>\n<p><strong>Correct Answer 2: D. Introduce and assess additional Service Level Indicators (SLIs) for the application.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why this is correct</strong></p>\n<p>If your system appears <em>too reliable</em> , you might not be measuring <strong>all</strong> important aspects of user experience.</p>\n<p>Adding or refining SLIs can:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Improve understanding of real reliability</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Ensure all critical user interactions are measured</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Align reliability signals with business needs</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is a standard SRE practice when reliability seems artificially high.</p>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong><br />\n<strong>Google SRE Workbook – Implementing SLIs</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/\">https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/</a></p>\n<p><strong>Why the Other Options Are Incorrect</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Increase serving capacity</strong></p>\n<p>This increases cost and does not improve alignment with SLOs.<br />\nYour system is already exceeding expectations.</p>\n<p><strong>C. Adjust the SLO</strong></p>\n<p>You <strong>already confirmed</strong> the SLO is appropriate.<br />\nTightening it would reduce error budget artificially and may slow velocity without business need.</p>\n<p><strong>E. Schedule planned downtime</strong></p>\n<p>This is anti-SRE and harmful.<br />\nNever reduce reliability intentionally just to “use up” error budget.</p>\n<p><strong>Final Answer: B and D</strong></p>","lessons":["d3l2"]},"e5q50":{"id":"e5q50","exam":5,"n":50,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>What steps should you take to deploy the new versions of your service's API with minimal disruption to third-party developers and end users of third-party installed applications?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Implement the new API version, announce deprecation of the old version, contact remaining users of the old API, provide best effort support to users, and finally, deactivate the old version.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Announce the old API version deprecation, introduce the new version, contact users still on the old version, deactivate the old version, and offer best effort support to old API users.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Notify the deprecation of the old API version, contact remaining users of the old version, introduce the new version, deactivate the old version, provide best effort support, and finally, turn down the old version.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Introduce the new API version, contact users still on the old API version, announce the deprecation of the old version, deactivate the old version, and provide best effort support to old API users.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct option: A</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Why option A is correct</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Implement the new API version, announce deprecation of the old version, contact remaining users of the old API, provide best effort support to users, and finally, deactivate the old version.</strong></p>\n<p>This sequence follows <strong>Google-recommended API lifecycle and SRE best practices</strong> for minimizing disruption to <strong>external developers and dependent applications</strong>.</p>\n<p>Core fundamentals behind this approach:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Backward compatibility first:</strong> You must <strong>introduce the new API version before deprecating the old one</strong> , ensuring developers have a working alternative.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Clear deprecation signaling:</strong> Announcing deprecation early gives third-party developers sufficient time to migrate.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Active communication:</strong> Proactively contacting remaining users of the old API reduces surprise outages and helps identify blockers.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Grace period + support:</strong> Providing best-effort support during the deprecation window aligns with reliability and developer-experience principles.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Controlled shutdown:</strong> Only after adoption and communication should the old API be deactivated.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is the standard <strong>safe rollout → deprecation → migration → retirement</strong> lifecycle.</p>\n<p><strong>Documentation:</strong><br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/apis/design/versioning\">https://cloud.google.com/apis/design/versioning</a></p>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are not suitable</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Deprecates before introducing the new version.</strong><br />\nThis creates risk because users are warned before a viable replacement exists, increasing disruption and confusion.</p>\n<p><strong>C. Contacts users and deactivates before a clear migration path exists.</strong><br />\nThis violates backward-compatibility expectations and can break third-party applications.</p>\n<p><strong>D. Contacts users before formally announcing deprecation.</strong><br />\nThis leads to inconsistent messaging and does not follow a transparent, documented lifecycle process.</p>\n<p><strong>Final answer:</strong> <strong>A</strong>.</p>","lessons":["d2l3"]},"e5q51":{"id":"e5q51","exam":5,"n":51,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>As the SRE engineer for a microservice deployed to a Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) Autopilot cluster, you oversee an online store's functionality. The system publishes order messages to Pub/Sub, and a microservice processes these messages to update stock information in the warehousing system. Due to a surge in orders during a sales event, stock updates are lagging, leading to orders being accepted for out-of-stock products. Reviewing the microservice metrics against typical levels reveals:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Current Microservice Metrics:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Average CPU usage across all Pods: 30% of Pod limit (typical state: 20% of Pod limit)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>B. Average memory usage across all Pods: 10% of Pod limit (typical state: 10% of Pod limit)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Pub/Sub subscription:A. Average oldest unacknowledged message age: 8074 milliseconds (typical state: 347 milliseconds)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Average undelivered messages: 14705 messages (typical state: 5 messages)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>E. Average acknowledgment latency: 354 milliseconds (typical state: 312 milliseconds)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Your objective is to ensure accurate inventory representation in the warehouse system during order placement while minimizing customer impact. What course of action should you take to resolve the current situation?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Decrease the acknowledgment deadline on the subscription.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Introduce a virtual queue to manage typical traffic levels on the online store.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Scale up the number of Pod replicas.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>IV. Increase the Pod CPU and memory limits.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Correct Answer: III. Increase the number of Pod replicas.</strong></p>\n<p>Reasoning</p>\n<p>The metrics clearly indicate a <strong>processing throughput bottleneck</strong> , not a resource constraint on individual Pods.</p>\n<p>Key observations from the metrics:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>CPU usage:</strong> 30% of Pod limit (only slightly higher than normal 20%)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Memory usage:</strong> 10% of Pod limit (same as normal)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This shows that <strong>individual Pods are not resource-constrained</strong>.</p>\n<p>However, Pub/Sub metrics show a <strong>large backlog</strong> :</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Oldest unacknowledged message age:</strong> 8074 ms (vs 347 ms normally)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Undelivered messages:</strong> 14,705 (vs 5 normally)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Acknowledgment latency:</strong> slightly increased</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These metrics indicate that <strong>messages are accumulating faster than the microservice can process them</strong> , meaning the system <strong>needs more parallel consumers</strong>.</p>\n<p>In a Pub/Sub-driven architecture, the standard scaling strategy is:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Increase the number of <strong>consumer instances</strong> (Pods)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Each Pod pulls messages independently</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Pub/Sub distributes messages across consumers automatically</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In <strong>GKE Autopilot</strong> , scaling Pods increases message processing concurrency without managing nodes manually.</p>\n<p>Increasing replicas will:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Increase message processing throughput</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Reduce backlog</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Ensure inventory updates keep pace with order intake</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Prevent overselling inventory</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This aligns with Google Cloud’s guidance for Pub/Sub consumer scaling.</p>\n<p>Reference:<br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/pull#scaling_subscribers\">https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/pull#scaling_subscribers</a></p>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are not the best choice</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Decrease the acknowledgment deadline on the subscription</strong></p>\n<p>The acknowledgment deadline determines <strong>how long the subscriber has to acknowledge a message</strong> before Pub/Sub redelivers it.<br />\nReducing it would <strong>increase duplicate deliveries</strong> and worsen load rather than increasing processing throughput.</p>\n<p>Reference:<br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/subscriber#ack_deadline\">https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/subscriber#ack_deadline</a></p>\n<p><strong>II. Add a virtual queue to the online store that allows typical traffic levels</strong></p>\n<p>Introducing a queue or throttling layer at the store would <strong>delay or reject orders</strong> , negatively impacting customers during a sales event.<br />\nIt does not solve the underlying issue of <strong>insufficient consumer throughput</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>IV. Increase the Pod CPU and memory limits</strong></p>\n<p>Current usage shows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>CPU ~30%</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Memory ~10%</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This indicates Pods <strong>are not CPU or memory bound</strong>. Increasing limits would not increase message processing throughput.</p>\n<p>Reference:<br />\n<a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/autopilot-overview\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/autopilot-overview</a></p>\n<p><strong>Key Concept to Remember</strong></p>\n<p>When <strong>Pub/Sub backlog increases while CPU/memory usage remains low</strong> , the issue is typically <strong>insufficient consumer parallelism</strong> , not resource exhaustion.</p>\n<p>The recommended solution is to <strong>scale horizontally (more subscriber Pods)</strong> rather than vertically scaling Pod resources.</p>","lessons":["d3l7"]},"e5q52":{"id":"e5q52","exam":5,"n":52,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>You oversee an application operating within Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE) and employing the blue/green deployment approach. Portions of the Kubernetes manifests are provided below:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>apiVersion: apps/v1</strong></p>\n<p><strong>kind: Deployment</strong></p>\n<p><strong>metadata:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>name: green-app</strong></p>\n<p><strong>labels:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>app: my-app</strong></p>\n<p><strong>version: green</strong></p>\n<p><strong>---</strong></p>\n<p><strong>apiVersion: apps/v1</strong></p>\n<p><strong>kind: Deployment</strong></p>\n<p><strong>metadata:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>name: blue-app</strong></p>\n<p><strong>labels:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>app: my-app</strong></p>\n<p><strong>version: blue</strong></p>\n<p><strong>---</strong></p>\n<p><strong>apiVersion: v1</strong></p>\n<p><strong>kind: Service</strong></p>\n<p><strong>metadata:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>name: appservice</strong></p>\n<p><strong>spec:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>selector:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>app: my-app</strong></p>\n<p><strong>version: green</strong></p>\n<p><strong>---</strong></p>\n<p><strong>apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1</strong></p>\n<p><strong>kind: Ingress</strong></p>\n<p><strong>metadata:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>name: ingress-app</strong></p>\n<p><strong>spec:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>defaultBackend:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>service:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>name: appservice</strong></p>\n<p><strong>The green-app Deployment was recently updated to incorporate a new application version. However, after the update, a substantial portion of user requests is failing, a behavior not observed during testing. Your task is to minimize the impact on users and facilitate developers in diagnosing the issue. What action should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Update the Deployment blue-app to revert to the previous version of the application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Roll back the Deployment green-app to use the prior version of the application.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Modify the selector on the Service appservice to app: my-app.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Adjust the selector on the Service appservice to app: my-app, version: blue.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Given the scenario, the correct action to minimize the impact on users and facilitate developers in diagnosing the issue is:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>D. Adjust the selector on the Service appservice to app: my-app, version: blue.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Justification:</strong></p>\n<p>Adjusting the selector on the Service to target the blue version specifically would effectively route traffic only to the blue version of the application. Since the green version is experiencing issues after the recent update, directing traffic to the stable blue version ensures that users are not impacted by the failures in the green version. Meanwhile, developers can investigate the issue with the green version without disrupting user experience.</p>\n<p><strong>Explanation for why other options are not suitable:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Updating the Deployment blue-app to revert to the previous version of the application:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>This option doesn't directly address the issue with the failing green version. Reverting the blue version won't resolve the problem with the green version, and it may introduce further complications.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>B. Rolling back the Deployment green-app to use the prior version of the application:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>While rolling back the green version would address the immediate issue, it may impact user experience as the application would revert to a potentially outdated version. Additionally, it doesn't provide developers with a controlled environment to diagnose the issue.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>C. Modifying the selector on the Service appservice to app: my-app:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Modifying the selector to a generic value won't effectively route traffic to a specific version of the application. It doesn't address the need to isolate traffic from the failing green version while maintaining user experience with the stable blue version.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References</strong> :</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/application-deployment-and-testing-strategies  https://blog.devgenius.io/blue-green-deployment-with-kubernetes-b7595b17fe17\">https://cloud.google.com/architecture/application-deployment-and-testing-strategies</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/architecture/application-deployment-and-testing-strategies  https://blog.devgenius.io/blue-green-deployment-with-kubernetes-b7595b17fe17\">https://blog.devgenius.io/blue-green-deployment-with-kubernetes-b7595b17fe17</a></p>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e5q53":{"id":"e5q53","exam":5,"n":53,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>You're managing an application on Google Cloud Platform (GCP) using Terraform. The application leverages a managed instance group for deployment on instances defined by an instance template. Your CI/CD pipeline deploys the Terraform code. When you attempt to update the machine type within the instance template via Terraform, the pipeline fails during the terraform apply stage, encountering the following error message:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Error waiting for deleting instance template: The instance_template resource 'project/demo-project/global/instanceTemplates/demo-instance-2024010000010001' is already being used by 'projects/demo-project/regions/us-central1/instanceGroupManagers/demo-mig'</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Your objective is to update the instance template while minimizing disruptions to both the application and the CI/CD pipeline execution frequency.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What's the most appropriate course of action?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Delete the managed instance group entirely, then recreate it after updating the instance template.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Introduce a new instance template, update the managed instance group to use this new template, and subsequently delete the old instance template.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Eliminate the managed instance group from your Terraform state file, update the instance template, and then re-import the managed instance group.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Employ the create_before_destroy meta-argument within the instance template's lifecycle block, setting it to <code>true</code>.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p>The best course of action in this situation is</p>\n<p><strong>D. Employ the create_before_destroy meta-argument within the instance template's lifecycle block, setting it to true.</strong></p>\n<p>Let's break down why this is the most suitable solution and discuss the other options:</p>\n<p><strong>D. Employ the create_before_destroy meta-argument within the instance template's lifecycle block, setting it to true.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>How it works:</strong> create_before_destroy forces Terraform to create a new instance template (with the updated machine type) <em>before</em> destroying the old template. This allows the managed instance group to seamlessly transition to the new resource, avoiding downtime.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Advantages</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Minimizes application disruption:</strong> The managed instance group can continue serving traffic from the old instances while new ones with the updated machine type are spun up.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Maintains CI/CD pipeline consistency:</strong> Your pipeline won't need major modifications, allowing for smooth continuous deployment.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Incorrect Options:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A. Delete the managed instance group entirely, then recreate it after updating the instance template.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Too disruptive. This would delete all running instances, leading to significant application downtime.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>B. Introduce a new instance template, update the managed instance group to use this new template, and subsequently delete the old instance template.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>While less disruptive, it involves more manual steps and could cause temporary inconsistencies due to a mix of instance types.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>C. Eliminate the managed instance group from your Terraform state file, update the instance template, and then re-import the managed instance group.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Risky – Managing resources outside of Terraform's control can lead to state inconsistencies. It's best to let Terraform handle resource lifecycles directly.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/meta-arguments/lifecycle#create_before_destroy\">https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language/meta-arguments/lifecycle#create_before_destroy</a></p>","lessons":["d2l10"]},"e5q54":{"id":"e5q54","exam":5,"n":54,"domain":4,"html":"<p><strong>You are devising a plan to present Cloud Monitoring metrics for your organization's Google Cloud projects. The organizational structure includes three folders (Development, Staging, and Production), each containing two projects (app-1-dev, app-1-staging, app-1-prod, app-2-dev, app-2-staging, and app-2-prod).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Folders:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Development: app-1-dev, app-2-dev</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Staging: app-1-staging, app-2-staging</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Production: app-1-prod, app-2-prod</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Your objective is to configure Cloud Monitoring dashboards to exhibit metrics solely from the projects within a designated folder (e.g., the Development folder). It's crucial to adhere to Google's recommended practices for this setup.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What course of action should you pursue?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Establish a single new scoping project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Establish new scoping projects for each folder (Development, Staging, and Production).</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Utilize the current app-1-prod project as the scoping project.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Employ the current projects (app-1-dev, app-1-staging, and app-1-prod) as the scoping project for the Development folder.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The most appropriate course of action for configuring Cloud Monitoring dashboards to exhibit metrics solely from the projects within a designated folder is:</p>\n<p><strong>B. Establish new scoping projects for each folder (Development, Staging, and Production).</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>Establishing new scoping projects for each folder aligns with Google's recommended practices for organizing resources and managing access control within Google Cloud Platform. By creating separate scoping projects for each folder, you can effectively segregate the monitoring metrics based on the environment (Development, Staging, and Production). This approach provides better isolation, scalability, and control over the monitoring setup for each environment.</p>\n<p><strong>Justification for why other options are not suitable:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Establishing a single new scoping project:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>This option would result in a lack of segregation between the different environments (Development, Staging, and Production), making it challenging to manage access control and monitor metrics specific to each environment effectively.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>C. Utilizing the current app-1-prod project as the scoping project:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Using an existing production project as the scoping project could lead to issues with access control and resource isolation. Mixing production monitoring with development or staging environments may not adhere to best practices and could potentially expose sensitive production metrics to inappropriate access.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>D. Employing the current projects (app-1-dev, app-1-staging, and app-1-prod) as the scoping project for the Development folder:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>This option suggests using individual projects within a folder as the scoping project, which may not provide the desired level of organization and segregation. It's more practical to have separate scoping projects for each folder to ensure better management and control over the monitoring setup.</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d4l8"]},"e5q55":{"id":"e5q55","exam":5,"n":55,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Your organization is leading a DevOps project. The current responsibilities are as follows:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>The DevOps team manages the service infrastructure and is on-call for incidents.</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>The Software Development team writes, submits, and reviews code.</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Currently, neither team has published Service Level Objectives (SLOs) for the service. You are tasked with designing a new joint-ownership model where both teams share responsibility for the service.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>How should you allocate the responsibilities between the DevOps team and the Software Development team in this new model?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A: Limited Collaboration</p>\n<p>DevOps: Management of service infrastructure and response to incidents.</p>\n<p>Software Development: Code development, submission of code for review, and conducting reviews of code.</p>\n<p>Shared: No shared responsibilities.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B: Complete Role Reversal</p>\n<p>DevOps: Code development, submission of code for review, and conducting reviews of code.</p>\n<p>Software Development: Management of service infrastructure and response to incidents.</p>\n<p>Shared: No shared responsibilities.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C: SLO-Focused Collaboration</p>\n<p>DevOps: Management of service infrastructure and response to incidents.</p>\n<p>Software Development: Code development, submission of code for review, and conducting reviews of code.</p>\n<p>Shared: Defining and making public Service Level Objectives (SLOs) for the service.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D: Expanded Joint Ownership</p>\n<p>DevOps: Management of service infrastructure, response to incidents, definition and publication of SLOs.</p>\n<p>Software Development: Code development, submission of code for review, conducting reviews of code, monitoring of application performance metrics.</p>\n<p>Shared: No explicit shared responsibilities (though collaboration on SLOs is likely).</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>Out of the provided options, the best approach for the new joint-ownership model is</p>\n<p><strong>C: SLO-Focused Collaboration</strong>. Here's why:</p>\n<p><strong>Why SLOs Matter</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Shared Understanding:</strong> SLOs create a common language around service health and reliability, providing clear targets for both teams to work towards.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Customer Focus:</strong> SLOs directly tie service performance to user experience, ensuring the focus remains on delivering a reliable product.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Proactive Approach:</strong> Monitoring performance against SLOs allows teams to identify and address potential issues before they escalate into major incidents.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Breakdown of Responsibilities</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>DevOps:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Manage Infrastructure:</strong> DevOps remains responsible for maintaining the underlying infrastructure supporting the service.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Incident Response:</strong> As the specialists in infrastructure and operations, DevOps continues to handle incident resolution.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Software Development:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Code Ownership:</strong> Software devs focus on writing, submitting, and reviewing code to ensure quality and maintainability.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Shared:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Defining SLOs:</strong> Both teams collaborate to determine appropriate SLOs based on user expectations, business needs, and the capabilities of the system.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Publishing SLOs:</strong> Making SLOs visible to stakeholders improves transparency and builds trust.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Advantages of SLO-Focused Collaboration</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Balance:</strong> Maintains core strengths of each team while creating a clear overlap in responsibility around service reliability.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Mutual Accountability:</strong> Both teams are incentivized to work together to meet the agreed-upon SLOs.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Data-Driven Improvement:</strong> SLO monitoring leads to insights that drive improvements across development and operational practices.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>A: Limited Collaboration:</strong> No shared goals or responsibilities make it hard to drive holistic service improvements.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>B: Complete Role Reversal:</strong> Forcing teams into unfamiliar domains is disruptive and inefficient. It's better to leverage existing expertise.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>D: Expanded Joint Ownership:</strong> While good in theory, it risks overextending team responsibilities. SLOs provide a more manageable starting point for shared ownership.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>","lessons":["d3l3"]},"e5q56":{"id":"e5q56","exam":5,"n":56,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>In the context of managing a high-priority workload on an autoscaled managed instance group within a single zone, with the need to validate alterations before finalizing changes and ensuring uninterrupted capacity for handling requests during testing, what is the recommended action to achieve this?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Modify autoscaling settings to \"Only scale out\" during the modification process.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Transform the managed instance group to function across multiple zones.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Temporarily disable autoscaling while implementing changes.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Enable predictive autoscaling to anticipate resource requirements.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The recommended action to achieve uninterrupted capacity for handling requests during testing while validating alterations before finalizing changes on an autoscaled managed instance group within a single zone is:</p>\n<p><strong>I. Modify autoscaling settings to \"Only scale out\" during the modification process.</strong></p>\n<p>Explanation:</p>\n<p>By modifying the autoscaling settings to \"Only scale out\" during the modification process, the managed instance group will only add new instances as needed to handle increased workload, ensuring uninterrupted capacity for handling requests. This approach allows you to validate alterations before finalizing changes while maintaining the necessary capacity to handle requests without interruption.</p>\n<p><strong>Justification for why other options are not suitable:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>II. Transform the managed instance group to function across multiple zones.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>While spreading instances across multiple zones can improve availability and fault tolerance, it doesn't directly address the need to ensure uninterrupted capacity during testing or the requirement to validate alterations before finalizing changes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>III. Temporarily disable autoscaling while implementing changes.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Disabling autoscaling may lead to capacity constraints during testing, potentially causing service interruptions or degraded performance. It's crucial to maintain autoscaling functionality to ensure the service can dynamically adjust to workload changes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>IV. Enable predictive autoscaling to anticipate resource requirements.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Predictive autoscaling can help anticipate resource requirements based on historical data, but it may not be suitable for ensuring uninterrupted capacity during testing or validating alterations before finalizing changes. Predictive autoscaling relies on historical patterns and may not accurately predict resource needs during testing or when making significant alterations to the workload.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/autoscaler/managing-autoscalers\">https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/autoscaler/managing-autoscalers</a></p>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e5q57":{"id":"e5q57","exam":5,"n":57,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Your company mandates adherence to a standardized development environment template, including popular integrated development environments (IDEs), with a focus on maintaining security patches and updates. Additionally, you aim to implement a reliable method, recommended by Google, to ensure consistent development environments across all team members. Which approach should you choose?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Implement daily Compute Engine snapshots of a 'golden' machine with all security patches, allowing developers to update their own machines from these snapshots.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Create a comprehensive checklist of software updates along with update scripts for developers to apply updates on their own machines.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Develop an image containing all current dependencies and deploy this image on developers' laptops for their use.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Establish Cloud Workstations configurations customized to each team's needs.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Out of the options provided, the best approach to achieve your goals is</strong></p>\n<p><strong>IV. Establish Cloud Workstations configurations customized to each team's needs.</strong> Here's why:</p>\n<p><strong>Advantages of Cloud Workstations:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Standardized Environments:</strong> Cloud Workstations provide centrally managed development environments, ensuring consistency across your team and easier adherence to your company's template.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Easy Updates and Security:</strong> You can maintain and update a base configuration with all the required tools, dependencies, and security patches, which developers then access through Cloud Workstations.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability and Portability:</strong> Cloud Workstations eliminates setup issues on individual machines and offers flexibility for developers to work from different locations or devices.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Google Recommended:</strong> Cloud Workstations is a solution natively integrated with Google Cloud Platform, making it a recommended and well-supported choice for GCP users.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>I. Compute Engine Snapshots:</strong> While snapshots can be useful for backups, they are not efficient for frequently updated development environments. Developers would need to manually restore snapshots regularly, which is time-consuming.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II. Checklists and Scripts:</strong> Relies heavily on individual developers to consistently apply updates and configurations. This is error-prone and can lead to inconsistencies across environments.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III. Deploying images on laptops:</strong> This can work for smaller teams with homogeneous hardware. However, it introduces management overhead for different hardware types and doesn't scale easily.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/workstations/docs/overview\">https://cloud.google.com/workstations/docs/overview</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/workstations/docs/create-configuration\">https://cloud.google.com/workstations/docs/create-configuration</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/workstations/docs/create-workstation\">https://cloud.google.com/workstations/docs/create-workstation</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=C6Dpmujxp9Q\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=C6Dpmujxp9Q</a></p>","lessons":["d1l11"]},"e5q58":{"id":"e5q58","exam":5,"n":58,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>Your team has been alerted to a critical security vulnerability requiring a patch on Compute Engine instances serving millions of global customers. The goal is to swiftly deploy the security patch while minimizing costs and impact on end customers. What is the recommended course of action?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Perform a canary rollout of the security patch with a brief duration for testing.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>II. Simultaneously update all Compute Engine instances worldwide.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Conduct a 24-hour rolling update during each region's overnight hours.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Implement an A/B rollout by duplicating all Compute Engine instances, applying the security patch to one set, and then transitioning users.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>Choose I — start with a short-lived canary rollout of the patched instance template, verify that the first few VMs stay healthy, and then let the Managed Instance Group (MIG) updater finish rolling the patch out everywhere.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Google Cloud’s MIG updater is designed for exactly this scenario: it lets you <strong>canary a new template on a small slice of instances</strong> (for example, 1-5 %) and, if health checks stay green, automatically continue to a full rollout. This gets the security fix into production much faster than a region-by-region schedule, yet still limits blast radius if something goes wrong. <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instance-groups/rolling-out-updates-to-managed-instance-groups?utm_source=chatgpt.com\">cloud.google.com</a> | <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instance-groups?utm_source=chatgpt.com\">cloud.google.com</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>A canary update is markedly cheaper than running an entire duplicate fleet (the A/B pattern) and avoids the customer-visible risk of a simultaneous global restart. Google’s documentation calls canary deployment a <strong>“progressive rollout that splits traffic between the existing and new versions before ramping up to 100 %.”</strong> <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/deploy/docs/deployment-strategies/canary?utm_source=chatgpt.com\">cloud.google.com</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Because this is a <em>critical</em> vulnerability, waiting until each region’s overnight window (24 h) leaves too large an exposure window; a tight canary followed immediately by an automated rollout closes the gap quickly while still providing a safety valve.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why the other options are not ideal</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>II — update all instances at once:</strong> fastest, but any unforeseen issue would take the whole service down simultaneously, violating high-availability principles. Google recommends staged rollouts (canary or rolling) rather than “all-at-once” for production MIGs. <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/release-notes?utm_source=chatgpt.com\">cloud.google.com</a></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>III — 24-hour regional rolling update:</strong> safer than II but too slow for an urgent fix; attackers could exploit the vulnerability in regions that have not yet been patched.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IV — duplicate the fleet and do an A/B cut-over:</strong> provides safety but <strong>doubles VM cost</strong> during the patch window and still requires a traffic switch. Google highlights canary updates as a lighter-weight alternative that meets the same safety goals without the extra spend. <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instance-groups/rolling-out-updates-to-managed-instance-groups?utm_source=chatgpt.com\">cloud.google.com</a></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In short, a brief canary rollout (option I) best balances <strong>speed, safety, and cost</strong> in line with Google’s recommended practices for managed instance groups.</p>","lessons":["d2l6"]},"e5q59":{"id":"e5q59","exam":5,"n":59,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>Your Cloud Run-deployed application caters to users worldwide, and the goal is to configure Cloud Run to direct users to their nearest region for optimal response times. What is the recommended approach?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Deploy the application to a single region and serve it from a global anycast IP address routed behind a global external HTTP(S) load balancer.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Deploy the application to each region and serve it from a global anycast IP address routed behind a global external HTTP(S) load balancer.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>III. Deploy the application to one region per continent, utilizing a regional external HTTP(S) load balancer in each region, and assign the same global IP address to all regional load balancers.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Deploy the application to a single region and configure Google Cloud Armor in the frontend to permit traffic from all IP addresses.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The recommended approach to configure Cloud Run to direct users to their nearest region for optimal response times is:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>II. Deploy the application to each region and serve it from a global anycast IP address routed behind a global external HTTP(S) load balancer.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>Deploying the application to each region and serving it from a global anycast IP address routed behind a global external HTTP(S) load balancer allows users to be directed to their nearest region based on latency, ensuring optimal response times. This approach leverages Google's global network infrastructure to route traffic efficiently to the closest region, minimizing latency and improving user experience.</p>\n<p><strong>Justification for why other options are not suitable:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Deploy the application to a single region and serve it from a global anycast IP address routed behind a global external HTTP(S) load balancer.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>This approach does not utilize the benefits of deploying the application to multiple regions to minimize latency and improve response times for users worldwide.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>III. Deploy the application to one region per continent, utilizing a regional external HTTP(S) load balancer in each region, and assign the same global IP address to all regional load balancers.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>While deploying the application to one region per continent can improve latency compared to a single region deployment, it may not provide the most optimal response times as users within continents may still experience latency due to regional distances.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>IV. Deploy the application to a single region and configure Google Cloud Armor in the frontend to permit traffic from all IP addresses.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>This approach does not optimize response times by directing users to their nearest region. Instead, it allows traffic from all IP addresses, which may not necessarily result in the best user experience in terms of latency.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/multiple-regions\">https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/multiple-regions</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/container-contract\">https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/container-contract</a></p>","lessons":["d5l3"]},"e5q60":{"id":"e5q60","exam":5,"n":60,"domain":3,"html":"<p><strong>Your company relies on a legacy database hosted on Google Kubernetes Engine (GKE), which experiences significant delays in responding to Pod evictions and rescheduling. Any interruptions to the database's operation are deemed unacceptable. To ensure continuous operation, you need to select an appropriate GKE maintenance strategy. What should you do?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>I. Set the GKE maintenance channel to \"Stable\".</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>II. Define a node pool upgrade strategy with the max-surge-upgrade parameter set to 0.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>III. Establish a \"PodDisruptionBudget\" object with maxUnavailable specified as 100%.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>IV. Configure a maintenance exclusion window with a scope of \"No minor or node upgrades\".</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p>The most appropriate GKE maintenance strategy to ensure continuous operation of the legacy database hosted on Google Kubernetes Engine is:</p>\n<p><strong>IV. Configure a maintenance exclusion window with a scope of \"No minor or node upgrades\".</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>Configuring a maintenance exclusion window with a scope of \"No minor or node upgrades\" allows you to prevent GKE from performing any minor or node upgrades during the specified window. This ensures that no interruptions occur due to upgrades that may lead to Pod evictions and rescheduling, which could impact the database's operation. By defining this exclusion window, you can maintain continuous operation of the legacy database without the risk of disruptions caused by GKE maintenance activities.</p>\n<p><strong>Justification for why other options are not suitable:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>I. Setting the GKE maintenance channel to \"Stable\":</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Setting the maintenance channel to \"Stable\" provides predictability in terms of when upgrades occur, but it doesn't prevent upgrades from happening altogether. It may still lead to interruptions if upgrades occur during critical times.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>II. Defining a node pool upgrade strategy with the max-surge-upgrade parameter set to 0:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>While setting max-surge-upgrade to 0 ensures that no additional nodes are added during node pool upgrades, it doesn't prevent the upgrades themselves. Node pool upgrades could still result in Pod evictions and rescheduling, potentially impacting the database's operation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>III. Establishing a \"PodDisruptionBudget\" object with maxUnavailable specified as 100%:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Specifying maxUnavailable as 100% in a PodDisruptionBudget would effectively prevent any Pod disruptions during maintenance activities. However, it wouldn't prevent node upgrades or other maintenance tasks that could still impact the database's operation. Additionally, this approach may not align with best practices for managing Pod disruptions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/maintenance-windows-and-exclusions\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/maintenance-windows-and-exclusions</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/maintenance-windows-and-exclusions#scope_of_maintenance_to_exclude\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/maintenance-windows-and-exclusions#scope_of_maintenance_to_exclude</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/release-channels\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/release-channels</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/best-practices/upgrading-clusters\">https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/best-practices/upgrading-clusters</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eqJQTANI_RI\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eqJQTANI_RI</a></p>","lessons":["d3l7"]},"e5q61":{"id":"e5q61","exam":5,"n":61,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>Your organization conducts numerous builds on Google Cloud daily. To protect against data exfiltration and ensure VMs lack public IP addresses, you aim to implement Google-recommended practices to create a customizable, easily-managed solution for your builds capable of scaling to your organization's needs. What action should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Deploy private pools in Cloud Build with VPC service control.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Configure and execute the builds in Cloud Build within a default pool to enforce necessary privacy measures.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Create a build environment using Compute Engine VMs within a custom VPC, utilizing Jenkins to execute the builds.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Establish a build environment using Compute Engine Spot VM instances within a custom VPC, employing Jenkins to execute the builds.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The best way to achieve your goals is </p>\n<p><strong>A. Deploy private pools in Cloud Build with VPC service control.</strong> Here's why this aligns with Google's recommended practices:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Security:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Private Pools:</strong> Isolated environments prevent unauthorized access and data exfiltration.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>VPC Service Control:</strong> Adds a layer of protection by restricting communication with Google services only within your designated perimeter.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Scalability:</strong> Cloud Build automatically scales worker nodes up and down within your private pool based on demand.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Ease of Management:</strong> Cloud Build's integrated solution reduces the overhead of managing infrastructure compared to custom setups.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Customization:</strong> Private pools offer flexibility for fine-grained configuration and security settings.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options Are Less Ideal:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>B. Default Pools:</strong> Lack the enhanced security and isolation provided by private pools.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>C &amp; D. Custom Compute Engine VMs with Jenkins:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Management Overhead:</strong> You become responsible for setting up, managing, and scaling the underlying infrastructure.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Spot VM Risk:</strong> While cost-effective, Spot VMs are preemptible and might disrupt builds.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/private-pools/private-pools-overview\">https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/private-pools/private-pools-overview</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/private-pools/using-vpc-service-controls\">https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/private-pools/using-vpc-service-controls</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/private-pools/set-up-private-pool-to-use-in-vpc-network\">https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/private-pools/set-up-private-pool-to-use-in-vpc-network</a></p>","lessons":["d2l1","d2l11"]},"e5q62":{"id":"e5q62","exam":5,"n":62,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Your company is gearing up for a significant sales event scheduled for September, during which a designated group of employees will support operations. To comply with Google-recommended practices, how can you provide these employees with access to Google Cloud resources for the duration of September only?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Set up a firewall rule allowing traffic from the identified employees' machines to the resources, then adjust the firewall rule to block traffic at the end of September.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Create a group containing the identified employees, grant the group permissions to access the resources, and configure an Identity and Access Management (IAM) condition with a date/time expression for September.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>C. Establish a group comprising the identified employees, assign the group a custom Identity and Access Management (IAM) role granting permissions to access the resources, and remove the custom role at the end of September.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Allocate a custom Identity and Access Management (IAM) role, granting permissions to access the resources, to each of the identified employees. Set up a Cloud Scheduler task to revoke the custom role at the end of September.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p>The action that aligns with Google-recommended practices to provide employees with access to Google Cloud resources for the duration of September only is:</p>\n<p><strong>B. Create a group containing the identified employees, grant the group permissions to access the resources, and configure an Identity and Access Management (IAM) condition with a date/time expression for September.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p>Option B involves creating a group containing the identified employees and granting the group permissions to access the resources. By configuring an IAM condition with a date/time expression for September, you can ensure that the group's access is limited to the specified timeframe. This approach follows Google-recommended practices for access management by leveraging IAM conditions to enforce time-based access controls. At the end of September, access will automatically be revoked based on the IAM condition, ensuring security and compliance.</p>\n<p><strong>Justification for why other options are not suitable:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Setting up a firewall rule allowing traffic from the identified employees' machines to the resources, then adjusting the firewall rule to block traffic at the end of September.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Firewall rules control network traffic and are not suitable for managing access to Google Cloud resources at the IAM level. Additionally, manually adjusting firewall rules introduces complexity and potential for human error.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>C. Establishing a group comprising the identified employees, assigning the group a custom Identity and Access Management (IAM) role granting permissions to access the resources, and removing the custom role at the end of September.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>While this option grants access to the identified employees, it requires manual intervention to remove the custom role at the end of September. It is preferable to leverage IAM conditions for automated and more granular access control.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>D. Allocating a custom Identity and Access Management (IAM) role, granting permissions to access the resources, to each of the identified employees, and setting up a Cloud Scheduler task to revoke the custom role at the end of September.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>While this option automates the revocation of access at the end of September, it involves assigning custom roles to individual employees, which can be more complex to manage compared to using groups. Additionally, managing access at the individual level may not scale well for larger organizations. Using groups and IAM conditions provides a more scalable and manageable solution.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Reference:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/conditions-overview\">https://cloud.google.com/iam/docs/conditions-overview</a></p>","lessons":["d1l3"]},"e5q63":{"id":"e5q63","exam":5,"n":63,"domain":1,"html":"<p><strong>Your company operates applications serving users located in Europe. Regulatory requirements mandate that resources be instantiated exclusively in specific regions. To ensure compliance across all projects within your Google Cloud organization, what action should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Set up a log-based alert to identify resources created outside the permitted list of regions, subsequently deleting any non-compliant resources.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Perform an Asset Inventory scan hourly to catalog all resources, removing any instances outside of the permitted list of regions.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Designate a restricted group of users with authorization to create new instances, implementing processes to verify compliance.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Configure an organizational policy specifying the allowed regions within an \"in: allowed_values\" list.</p>","correct":true}],"explanation":"<p>The action that aligns with Google-recommended practices to ensure compliance across all projects within your Google Cloud organization regarding the instantiation of resources exclusively in specific regions is:</p>\n<p><strong>D. Configure an organizational policy specifying the allowed regions within an \"in: allowed_values\" list.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option D involves configuring an organizational policy that specifies the allowed regions within an \"in: allowed_values\" list. This approach allows you to define and enforce regional restrictions across all projects within your Google Cloud organization. By implementing this organizational policy, you can ensure that resources are instantiated only in the permitted regions, thus ensuring compliance with regulatory requirements. This method provides a centralized and standardized approach to enforce regional compliance across all projects.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Justification for why other options are not suitable:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Setting up a log-based alert to identify resources created outside the permitted list of regions, subsequently deleting any non-compliant resources</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>While log-based alerts can help identify non-compliant resources, relying solely on alerts for enforcement may not be sufficient. Manually deleting non-compliant resources based on alerts can also be time-consuming and error-prone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>B. Performing an Asset Inventory scan hourly to catalog all resources, removing any instances outside of the permitted list of regions.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Performing Asset Inventory scans hourly and manually removing non-compliant instances is not an efficient or scalable approach to enforce regional compliance. It may also lead to delays and inconsistencies in compliance enforcement.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>C. Designating a restricted group of users with authorization to create new instances, implementing processes to verify compliance.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>While restricting user access and implementing verification processes can help enforce compliance, it relies heavily on manual intervention and oversight. It may not provide a scalable or automated solution for enforcing regional compliance across all projects within the organization. Configuring organizational policies is a more robust and centralized approach to ensure compliance.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/organization-policy/defining-locations#gcloud\">https://cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/organization-policy/defining-locations#gcloud</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/organization-policy/overview\">https://cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/organization-policy/overview</a></p>","lessons":["d1l2"]},"e5q64":{"id":"e5q64","exam":5,"n":64,"domain":5,"html":"<p><strong>Your application contains a module responsible for executing a computationally intensive task. The development team aims to optimize this module by experimenting with different algorithms. To facilitate testing, the team wants to evaluate the performance of each algorithm in both staging and production environments, gathering detailed performance data. What action should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Integrate the code with profiling libraries to collect detailed performance data. Visualize the data using Cloud Profiler, presenting it as flame graphs for analysis.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>B. Implement code instrumentation to track the flow of requests and responses. Analyze request latencies using Cloud Trace to assess algorithm performance.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Integrate the code with log statements at various algorithmic stages. Utilize log-based notifications to capture data on algorithm execution.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>D. Embed log statements within the code at various algorithmic stages. Use log-metric-based notifications to capture data when specific control structures are invoked multiple times.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The best course of action to achieve this goal is</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Integrate the code with profiling libraries to collect detailed performance data. Visualize the data using Cloud Profiler, presenting it as flame graphs for analysis.</strong></p>\n<p>Here's why this approach is ideal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Focused on performance:</strong> Profiling libraries are specifically designed to gather in-depth performance metrics about code execution, such as function call times, memory usage, and CPU consumption.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Cloud Profiler Integration:</strong> Cloud Profiler seamlessly integrates with GCP, collecting profiling data from your running application in both staging and production.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Visual Analysis with Flame Graphs:</strong> Flame graphs provide an intuitive, hierarchical visualization of where the code spends the most time, making bottlenecks easily identifiable.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Algorithm-Specific Insights:</strong> Profiling allows fine-grained analysis within your algorithms, helping compare their performance characteristics.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why Other Options are Less Suitable:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><strong>B. Cloud Trace:</strong> Cloud Trace is primarily for tracing distributed requests and identifying latency issues across services, less focused on algorithmic performance within a module.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>C &amp; D. Log statements &amp; notifications:</strong> While logging can provide some information, it's less efficient and accurate than profiling for detailed performance analysis. This approach can also become overly verbose.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/concepts-profiling\">https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/concepts-profiling</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/measure-app-performance\">https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/measure-app-performance</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/concepts-flame\">https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs/concepts-flame</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://codelabs.developers.google.com/codelabs/otel-cloudtrace-go#0\">https://codelabs.developers.google.com/codelabs/otel-cloudtrace-go#0</a></p>","lessons":["d5l8"]},"e5q65":{"id":"e5q65","exam":5,"n":65,"domain":2,"html":"<p><strong>Your code is hosted on GitHub and built using Cloud Build, both manually and via webhooks. Building the code requires accessing confidential information. To adhere to Google-recommended practices and protect the confidentiality of this information, what action should you take?</strong></p>","options":[{"html":"<p>A. Initiate the builds from the command line each time, providing the necessary confidential information as parameters during the build process.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>B. Create an encrypted file within your GitHub repository containing only the confidential information, and reference this encrypted file within your Cloud Build configuration file.</p>","correct":false},{"html":"<p>C. Store the confidential secrets in Secret Manager, specify the URI for the secrets within the Cloud Build configuration file, and reference the secret as parameters in the build steps.</p>","correct":true},{"html":"<p>D. Maintain the code in GitHub and establish a separate repository within Cloud Source Repositories solely for storing confidential information. Reference this new repository within your Cloud Build configuration file.</p>","correct":false}],"explanation":"<p><strong>The action that aligns with Google-recommended practices to protect the confidentiality of confidential information required during builds hosted on GitHub and built using Cloud Build is:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>C. Store the confidential secrets in Secret Manager, specify the URI for the secrets within the Cloud Build configuration file, and reference the secret as parameters in the build steps.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Explanation:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Option C involves storing confidential secrets securely in Google Cloud's Secret Manager. Secret Manager provides a centralized and secure storage solution for sensitive information such as API keys, passwords, and other credentials. By storing confidential information in Secret Manager, you can ensure that it is encrypted at rest and accessible only to authorized users and services.</strong></p>\n<p>In the Cloud Build configuration file, you can specify the URI for the secrets stored in Secret Manager and reference these secrets as parameters in the build steps. This allows Cloud Build to securely access the confidential information during the build process without exposing it in plaintext in the configuration files or build logs.</p>\n<p><strong>Justification for why other options are not suitable:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A. Initiating the builds from the command line each time, providing the necessary confidential information as parameters during the build process.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Manually providing confidential information as parameters during the build process introduces potential security risks, as the information may be exposed in plaintext in the command history or build logs. This approach is also not scalable and may not be suitable for automated builds triggered by webhooks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>B. Creating an encrypted file within your GitHub repository containing only the confidential information, and referencing this encrypted file within your Cloud Build configuration file.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>While encrypting sensitive information within the repository can provide some level of protection, managing encryption keys and decryption mechanisms adds complexity and may not offer the same level of security as dedicated secret management solutions like Secret Manager. Additionally, storing encrypted files within the repository may still expose sensitive information to authorized repository users.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>D. Maintaining the code in GitHub and establishing a separate repository within Cloud Source Repositories solely for storing confidential information, referencing this new repository within your Cloud Build configuration file.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>While separating code and sensitive information into different repositories may provide some level of isolation, it does not offer the same level of security and centralized management as using Secret Manager. Additionally, managing multiple repositories for different types of information may increase complexity and administrative overhead.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>References:</strong></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/securing-builds/use-secrets\">https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/securing-builds/use-secrets</a></p>\n<p><a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/configuring-builds/substitute-variable-values\">https://cloud.google.com/build/docs/configuring-builds/substitute-variable-values</a></p>","lessons":["d2l8"]}}};
